ns1:43831 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The bill also would have extended the authority of U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) to collect travel promotion fees from certain foreign individuals traveling to the United States of America. The bill was introduced and passed in the United States of America House of Representatives, but was not passed in the Senate. The bill would revise requirements for the Corporation's annual report to the United States of America Secretary of Commerce (Secretary) to require a description of and rationales for: (1) the Corporation's efforts to focus on specific countries and populations, and (2) its combination of media channels employed in meeting the promotional objectives of its marketing campaign. The bill would amend the Immigration and Nationality Act to extend through FY2020 also the authority of the United States of America Secretary of Homeland Security (DHS) to charge a fee for use of the electronic travel authorization system to determine, in advance, an alien's eligibility to travel to the United States of America. The bill also would extend the authority of U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) to collect travel promotion fees from certain foreign individuals traveling to the United States of America. The Travel Promotion, Enhancement, and Modernization Act of 2014 Act was introduced into the United States of America House of Representatives on April 10, 2014 by Rep. Gus M. Bilirakis (R, FL-12). The bill was referred to the United States of America House Committee on Energy and Commerce, the United States of America House Committee on Homeland Security, the United States of America House Energy Subcommittee on Commerce, Manufacturing and Trade, the United States of America House Homeland Security Subcommittee on Border and Maritime Security. ASTA President Zane Kerby said that the bill \"is essential to marketing the United States of America as a desirable destination for international tourists, conferences and business.\"" .

