ns1:44508 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Shaktism (Sanskrit: Śāktaḥ, lit., \"doctrine of energy, power, the eternal goddess\") is a major sect of Hinduism, wherein the metaphysical reality is considered metaphorically a woman and Shakti is regarded as the supreme godhead. The Sruti and Smriti texts of Hinduism are an important historical framework of the Shaktism tradition. Shaktism has at times been dismissed as a superstitious, black magic-infested practice that hardly qualifies as a true religion at all. And indeed it can scarcely be doubted that Shaktism is Hinduism arrived at its worst and most corrupt stage of development.\" These traditions compare with Vaishnavism, Shaivism and Smartism as follows: There is no census data available on demographic history or trends for Shaktism or other traditions within Hinduism. Estimates vary on the relative number of adherents in Shaktism compared to other traditions of Hinduism." .

