ns1:45450 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Women's liberation movement (WLM) was a political alignment of women and feminism intellectualism that emerged in the late 1960s and continued into the 1980s primarily in the industrialized nations of the Western world, which effected great change (political, intellectual, cultural) throughout the world. Though challenging patriarchy and the anti-patriarchal message of the Women's liberation movement was considered radical, it was not the only, nor the first, radical movement in the early period of second-wave feminism." .

