ns1:46052 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Ghana-Nigeria football rivalry is a sports rivalry that exists between the national association football teams of Ghana and Nigeria, as well as their respective sets of fans. At that time the modern nation of Ghana was known as the Gold Coast and members of the team from Nigeria, prior to adopting their national colors of green and white, wore scarlet tops over white shorts and were known as the \"Red Devils\". Gold Coast's association football federation, founded in 1920, was over twenty years older than Nigeria's and its team was more respected among Britain's colonies. The two nations played to relative parity for much of the rest of the 1950s, mostly trading victories on respective home soil, but Ghana would go on to dominate much of the competition between the 1960s and the early 2000s and win the Africa Cup of Nations four times to Nigeria's two in that time period. During the 1950s and 1960s, Nigeria and Ghana would compete in three cup competitions. Despite these issues, the tournament would see two matches played between Ghana and Nigeria, the high point being when the two sides met in the final of the 1960 tournament in Lagos, Nigeria in which Ghana won 3–0." .

ns1:46054 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Philip Morris USA v. Williams, 549 U.S. 346 (2007), 556 U.S. 178 (2009), was a decision by the Supreme Court of the United States, which held that the due process clause of the Fourteenth Amendment limits punitive damages, and ordered a lower court to reconsider its damages awards on that basis." .

