ns1:48428 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1969 Kansas City Royals season was the Kansas City Royals' inaugural season. However, Symington was not willing to have Kansas City wait three years for another team, and renewed his threat to have baseball's antitrust exemption revoked unless the teams began play in 1969. The Kansas City Royals and Seattle Pilots, along with the two National League expansion teams set to debut in 1969, the Montreal Expos and San Diego Padres, were allowed to participate in the June 1968 MLB first-year player draft, although the new teams were barred from the lottery's first three rounds." .

ns1:48429 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 Independence Cup was the inaugural men's association football friendly tournament organised by the Albanian Football Association on 28 November to commemorate Albanian Independence Day. Flamurtari Vlorë of Albania, Shkëndija of Republic of Macedonia and Prishtina of Kosovo were invited to compete in the tournament, which was held at the Flamurtari Vlorë Stadium in Vlorë, near the site of the Albanian Declaration of Independence on 28 November 1912. All three matches finished in goalless draws and were all decided through penalty shootouts, with Flamurtari Vlorë winning the tournament after defeating Shkëndija and Prishtina 11–10 and 3–2 on penalties, respectively." .

ns1:48430 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Nigeria at the 2012 Summer Paralympics in London, United Kingdom from 2012 – 2012. Powerlifting was Nigeria's dominant sport in London. Babagana Ali was quoted by the BBC as saying of the 2012 Paralympics, \"Paralympics is a clear indication to disabled persons in Nigeria and Africa alike that they can something good with their lives better than begging. Nigeria was also a beneficiary of London Organising Committee of the Olympic and Paralympic Games (LOCOG)'s International Inspiration project as part of the LOCOG's efforts to leave a lasting impact as a result of the city hosting the Olympic and Paralympic Games. Nigeria's Paralympic team in London won more medals than Nigeria's Olympic team in London." .

ns1:48431 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 WTA Tour was the elite professional tennis circuit organized by the Women's Tennis Association (WTA) for the 2014 tennis season. The 2014 WTA Tour calendar comprises the Grand Slam tournaments (supervised by the International Tennis Federation (ITF)), the WTA Premier tournaments (Premier Mandatory, Premier 5, and regular Premier), the WTA International tournaments, the Fed Cup (organized by the ITF), and the year-end championships (the WTA Tour Championships and the WTA Tournament of Champions). These tables present the number of singles (S), doubles (D), and mixed doubles (X) titles won by each player and each nation during the season, within all the tournament categories of the 2014 WTA Tour: the Grand Slam tournaments, the year-end championships (the WTA Tour Championships and the Tournament of Champions), the WTA Premier tournaments (Premier Mandatory, Premier 5, and regular Premier), and the WTA International tournaments. Following is a list of notable players (winners of a main tour title, and/or part of the WTA Rankings top 100 (singles) or (doubles) for at least one week) who announced their retirement from professional tennis, became inactive (after not playing for more than 52 weeks), or were permanently banned from playing, during the 2014 season: Following are notable players who will come back after retirements during the 2014 WTA Tour season: The winners of the 2014 WTA Awards were announced throughout the last two weeks of November." .

