ns1:48740 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Brazil–Iran relations are the bilateral relations between the Brazil and the Iran. Iran has a productive trade balance with Brazil. However, since the election of Brazilian president, Dilma Rousseff, relations between the two countries recently have deteriorated greatly, following Rousseff shifting Brazil away from Iran due to Iran's violation of human and civil rights. With the end of hostilities, Brazil decided to pursue Iran to sign a memorandum of understanding that would establish a high-level commission between the two countries. Iran became Brazil's second largest importer in the region. In recent years, Brazil has continued to engage in normal diplomatic relations with Iran, despite the international sanctions against Iran's nuclear program. The Brazilian government's view was reaffirmed in November 2008 when Foreign Minister Celso Amorim stated that \"Brazil does not recognize unilateral sanctions imposed on Iran, whether by the United States or the European Union, the Iranian government should fully cooperate with the agency because it is the best way to avoid sanctions.\" In February 2010, there was some speculation that Brazil could have been involved in direct bilateral talks to provide Iran with weapons-grade uranium, which was denied by Brazilian Foreign Minister Celso Amorim. On May 17, 2010, Brazil, Iran and Turkey issued the \"Tehran Nuclear Declaration\", a joint declaration \"in which Iran agreed to send low-enriched uranium to Turkey in return for enriched fuel for a research reactor.\" The election of Dilma Rousseff as president of Brazil has brought a change to the Brazilian policy towards Iran, leading to a cooling in relations between the two nations. Brazil has since supported a resolution for nominating a U.N. Special Rapporteur for human rights in Iran, whose eventual report condemned Iranian human rights abuses. Brazil remains Iran's main trading partner and exporter in Latin America with a total trade of $2.33 billion in 2011, up 5% from the previous year. Brazil's top exports to Iran include food, medication, minerals and automobiles. Iran was Brazil's largest export market for beef in 2011. Brazil supports Iran's program to develop nuclear technology for peaceful purposes, though both countries have agreed to stop the proliferation of nuclear weapons. Brazil and Turkey agreed to a fuel-swap deal with Tehran during the G-15 Summit in May 2010 in a failed attempt to avoid further international sanctions against Iran." .

