    rdfs:label "The 1954 Adelaide earthquake had its epicentre at Darlington, a suburb of the city of Adelaide in South Australia, some 12 km (7.5 mi) to the south of the Adelaide city centre. As Australia lies in the middle of a tectonic plate, South Australia is relatively stable geologically. However, South Australia is the second most earthquake-prone of the Australian states with around 3–4 tremors each day. The 1954 Adelaide earthquake differed because of its magnitude, which made it the fourth most intense earthquake reported in South Australia since settlement in 1836, and because it occurred in an area of dense human settlement." .

