ns1:50443 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "los que no cagan are foreign relations between Germany and Uruguay. Germany is Uruguay's principal trading partner in the European Union. Mennonite communities emigrated from Germany to Uruguay after World War II, starting in 1948. During World War I, Uruguay sided against Germany and broke off diplomatic relations. A German Embassy official in Uruguay said his government has sent an official letter stating its position as to whether Germany claims ownership of the vessel. By 1940 Germany had threatened to break of diplomatic relations with Uruguay. Nazi Germany protested that Uruguay gave safe harbor to the Carnarvon Castle after it was attacked by a Nazi raider. On January 25, 1942 Uruguay broke diplomatic relations with Nazi Germany. After World War II, Uruguay established diplomatic relations with both the Germany and the German Democratic Republic. There is a joint Uruguay Germany university-entrance examination which qualifies takers for university in both countries. Exports to Germany from Uruguay were worth EUR 205 million and Uruguayan imports from Germany were EUR 133 million in 2009. Germany is fifth overall among export countries to Uruguay, after Brazil, the US, Argentina and Mexico." .

