ns1:51293 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The AFC Challenge Cup was an international association football competition for Asian Football Confederation (Asian Football Confederation) member countries that was categorized as \"emerging countries\" in the \"Vision Asia\" programme. \"Vision Asia\", created by former Asian Football Confederation president, Mohammed Bin Hammam, is the Asian Football Confederation's plan for a continent wide programme to raise the standards of Asian association football at all levels. The AFC Challenge Cup, which reflected the philosophy of Vision Asia, was created for teams to experience playing in a continental competition with the possibility to win an Asian Football Confederation trophy and to potentially discover new talent. The inaugural tournament was hosted by Bangladesh in 2006 and was held biennially. An amendment to men's national team competitions in 2006, meant that starting with the 2008 and 2010 editions of the AFC Challenge Cup, the winners automatically qualify for the Asian Football Confederation Asian Cup. In the 2011 and 2015 Asian Football Confederation Asian Cup tournaments, two qualification spots have been allocated to the two most recent AFC Challenge Cup winners. The Asian Football Confederation initially divided their 46 member nations into three groups in 2006." .

ns1:51295 a ns1:context_narration ;
