ns1:52751 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1997 Red River Flood was a major flood that occurred in April and 1997 along the Red River of the North in Minnesota, North Dakota, and Southern Manitoba. As a result of the 1997 flood and its extensive property losses, the United States and state governments made additional improvements to the flood protection system in North Dakota and Minnesota. In Grand Forks, thousands of people, including Air Force personnel from Grand Forks Air Force Base, tried to prepare for the 1997 flood by building sandbag dikes. Grand Forks, North Dakota and its counterpart East Grand Forks, Minnesota, were the pair most severely affected by the 1997 flood. Even with these flood protection measures, in 1997 the province had a flood cresting at 21.6 ft (6.6 m). The 1997 flood was a 100-year flood." .

