ns1:55238 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "China–Ireland relations are interstate relations of People's Republic of China and Ireland. Ireland and People's Republic of China first established their bilateral foreign relations after they signed the Communique on the Establishment of Diplomatic Relations on 22 June 1979. Ireland has an embassy in Beijing, a general consulate in Shanghai and an honorary consulate in Hong Kong; People's Republic of China has an embassy in Dublin. The first historical meeting for the two headers of People's Republic of China and Ireland governments took place in November 1996 when Premier Li Peng met with Taoiseach John Bruton at the World Food Summit (Rome, 1996). The trading volumes in People's Republic of China and Ireland have substantially improved after the establishment of diplomatic relations. By 2000, Ireland had invested 34 projects in People's Republic of China and offered pledged investment of US$89.30 million with real input of US$19.28 million\". Irish mainly exports food and dairy to People's Republic of China — trade has almost doubled to 8 billion euros ($9 billion) during Enda Kenny's tenure, who was the Prime Minister of Ireland during 2014–2017. However, the US–People's Republic of China trade war in 2019 negatively affected Ireland's export goods to People's Republic of China. Ireland is known for exporting high-end chips that mainly export to People's Republic of China to make final products which are frequently shipped on to the US. People's Republic of China and Ireland signed the Agreement on Education Cooperation between the Government of the People's Republic of China and the Government of Ireland in October 2000. In April 2000, Ireland and People's Republic of China initiated an “Agreement for the Avoidance of Double Taxation \"; they also signed \"Agreement on Scientific and Technological Cooperation\" in the following September. The friendly diplomatic relations between People's Republic of China and Ireland promote cultural exchange and traveling over the years. In Ireland, beginning in 2012, more than 93 percent of immigrants are from People's Republic of China." .

