ns1:57267 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Canada–Haiti relations are relations between Canada and the Haiti. Canada participated in various international interventions in Haiti between 1994 and 2004, and continues to provide substantial aid to Haiti. Canada is a member of the Francophonie, or community of French-speaking countries, as is Haiti. Canada and Haiti only officially established diplomatic relations in 1954 when Edward Ritchie Bellemare was appointed Chargé d'Affaires of the Canadian Embassy in Haiti. Though government bilateral aid had plunged, Haiti was still seeing aid from Canada in the form of NGO bilateral assistance. In June 1993, Prime Minister Brian Mulroney of Canada told President Bill Clinton that Canada was ready to send troops to enforce a blockade around Haiti. Canada's decision to intervene in Haiti in this time period was largely propelled by the demands and concerns of the Haitian-Francophonie population living in Quebec at the time. Later that year, Canada assisted in the United Nations Mission in Haiti, a peacekeeping operation carried out by the United Nations between September 1993 and June 1996. Canada also provided the Police Commissioners between October 1993 and February 1996, and contributed police officers to the United Nations Civilian Police Mission in Haiti (1997–2000). In 2006 Canada assisted in international efforts to help Haiti complete its first full electoral cycle since its constitution entered into force in 1987. As of 2008, Canada had committed $555 million to Haiti over five years. In addition to official aid, Haitian immigrants in Canada sent about $248 million of remittances to their families in Haiti in 2006. In January 2013, Canada's Minister of International Cooperation, Julian Fantino, announced that the government would be reviewing the aid being remitted to Haiti, citing concerns regarding the results of the use of the funds. There remains a security warning from the government of Canada to exercise caution when travelling to the country, however there is not presently an advisory in effect for Haiti." .

