ns1:58030 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Italy–Serbia relations are diplomatic relations between Italy and Serbia. Kingdom of Italy established formal bilateral relations with the Principality of Serbia on 18 January 1879. The strategic partnership between the Republic of Serbia and the Republic of Italy was established in Rome on 13 November 2009. Italy is one of the member states of the European Union which strongly support Accession of Serbia to the European Union. Until the 1860s Serbia and Italy both pursued anti-Austrian politics, which made the two countries closer. During the Congress of Berlin Italy supported Serbia's request for independence but did not support other request made by Serbian leadership. Kingdom of Italy established formal bilateral relations with the Principality of Serbia on 18 January 1879. Relations between Italy and the Federal Republic of Yugoslavia (Serbia-Montenegro) under Slobodan Milosevic's rule were cold but nevertheless continued. The Italian government bought shares in Telekom Serbia, but also took part in the 1999 NATO bombing of Yugoslavia when the NATO-member states used the Aviano Air Base in Italy from where military aeroplanes dropped bombs onto Yugoslavia. Italy and Serbia quickly normalized relations after the overthrow of Slobodan Milošević and Rome becoming one of the first supporters of Serbia’s European integrations. On April 25 Serbia sent 8 planes with medical aid to Italy, due to the COVID-19 pandemic." .

