ns1:59108 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Burkina Faso–Taiwan relations referred to the historical relationship between the Taiwan (Taiwan) and Burkina Faso. Taiwan had an embassy in Ouagadougou, and Burkina Faso had an embassy in Taipei. In May 2018, Burkina Faso switched to recognize the People's Taiwan, thus ending diplomatic ties with Taiwan. The last ambassador of Burkina Faso to Taiwan, appointed in August 2017, was Aminata Sana Congo. Burkina Faso, known as the Republic of Upper Volta prior to 1984, entered diplomatic relations with the Taiwan in 1961, the year after the country gained independence from its former colonial power, France. Following Burkina Faso's switch to recognizing the People's Taiwan in 2018, the mayor of Taipei, Ko Wen-je, announced the sister city relationship between the two cities would still continue. Other aid measures include the donation of large amounts of condoms and medicines by Taiwan to help prevent the spread of HIV/AIDS in Burkina Faso. After the termination of relations between São Tomé and Príncipe and Taiwan in 2016, Burkina Faso was one of only two states in Africa to recognize the Taiwan over the People's Taiwan, the another one being Eswatini (formerly Swaziland). On May 24, 2018, Burkina Faso derecognized the Taiwan." .

