ns1:59913 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Brazil–Cape Verde relations refer to the bilateral relations between the Brazil and the Cape Verde. Both Brazil and Cape Verde were united for three hundred years as part of the Portuguese Empire. From 1815 to 1822, Cape Verde was administered by Brazil during the Transfer of the Portuguese court to Brazil. In 1975, Cape Verde obtained its Independence from Portugal. That same year, Brazil and Cape Verde established diplomatic relations. Diplomats and military officers from Cape Verde have also traditionally attended training courses in Brazil. High-level visits from Brazil to Cape Verde. High-level visits from Cape Verde to Brazil." .

ns1:59916 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Tauromenium was laid down by Dionysius, tyrant of Syracuse, in the winter of 394 BC, in the course of the Sicilian Wars against Carthage. Himilco first stormed Motya, where the mostly Sicel garrison under Biton was easily overcome, then lifted the siege of Segesta, and Dionysius retired to Syracuse instead of offering battle in western Sicily against a superior army. After the defeat at Catana the Sicilian Greek soldiers returned to their respective homes when Dionysius decided to withstand a siege in Syracuse against their wishes. The Sicels had also turned against Dionysius after the establishment of Tauromenium and had sent soldiers to help Himilco during the siege of Syracuse in 397 BC. The Carthaginians caught a break when Dionysius chose not to invade the Punic territories in western Sicily immediately lifting the siege of Syracuse. Dionysius did not immediately attack Punic Sicily after lifting the siege of Syracuse in 396 BC, although no formal treaty had been made ending the war with Carthage." .

