ns1:41733 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Maritime Sign Language (MSL) is a sign language and used in Canada Atlantic provinces descended from British Sign Language. Maritime Sign Language is descended from British Sign Language through the convergence of deaf communities from the Northeastern United States and the United Kingdom who immigrated to Canada during the 18th and 19th centuries. As late as the mid-20th century, it was the dominant form of sign language in The Maritimes and the language of instruction at the Halifax School for the Deaf (1857–1961) and the Atlantic Provinces Special Education Authority in Amherst, Nova Scotia (1961–1995). The number of MSL speakers is unknown and was estimated to have been fewer than 100 in 2009; most were concentrated in Nova Scotia, some in New Brunswick, while almost none were thought to remain in Newfoundland and Labrador (only 3 were said to exist) or Prince Edward Island ASL and MSL have 'blended' in the region. for MSL speakers are largely lacking, but a grant to the Nova Scotia Cultural Society of the Deaf produced VHS tapes documenting the language, and in the 2010s a project was started to document placenames in Atlantic Canada in both MSL and ASL and has resulted in interactive online maps." .

ns1:41735 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Biathlon World Championships 2017 – men's sprint competition at the Biathlon World Championships 2017 was held on 2017." .

ns1:41740 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Recapture of Bahia (Spain: Jornada del Brasil; Portuguese: Jornada dos Vassalos) was a Spain-Portuguese military expedition in 1625 to retake the city of Salvador in Brazil from the forces of the Dutch West India Company (WIC). Sailing from the port of Lisbon, under the command of Fadrique Álvarez de Toledo y Mendoza, who was appointed Captain General of the Army of Brazil, the fleet crossed the Atlantic Ocean, and arrived at Salvador on April 1 of 1625. There Willekens was revealed that his objective was the capture of the city of Salvador, on the coast of Brazil, in order to use its port as a commercial base to ensure the Dutch trade with the East Indies. These intentions to invade Brazil were soon reported to the court of Madrid by the Spain spies in the Netherlands, but Count-Duke of Olivares did not give them credit. The Portuguese governor of Salvador, Diogo de Mendonça Furtado, tried to organized the defense of the town with 3,000 men hastily recruited, mostly Portuguese militia of peasant levees and black slaves, all of them resentful to Spain rule. When news of the loss of Salvador arrived to Spain in August 1624, Philip IV ordered to assemble a joint Spain-Portuguese fleet under Admiral Fadrique Álvarez de Toledo y Mendoza with the mission to retake the city. One of them returned to Holland with the supplies and ammunition for the garrison of Salvador; the other two attacked respectively the Spain Caribbean colonial town of San Juan de Puerto Rico and the Portuguese African trading post of the Castle of Elmina but were both decisively defeated." .

ns1:41742 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1997 Denver Broncos season was the Denver Broncos's 28th season in the National Football League, and the 38th overall. The 1997 season saw the new addition of the Denver Broncos' newest wordmark and logo." .

ns1:41752 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011–12 Basketball Bundesliga was the 46th season of the Basketball Bundesliga (BBL)." .

