ns1:44705 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Trans-Gaule is a trans-national ultramarathon the second longest multiday race in France after Le Tour de France Footrace (43days 2776km).It consists of eighteen stages in eighteen consecutive days with a total of 1,150 km, from Roscoff in the English Channel to the Mediterranean." .

ns1:44707 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Al Punto (Spanish pronunciation: , To the Point) is a Sunday morning talk show hosted by Noticiero Univision anchor Jorge Ramos on the Univision network. Al Punto debuted on Univision on 09 September 2007 to coincide with the first Spanish language U.S. Presidential Debate hosted by Univision at the University of Miami on the same date." .

ns1:44717 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1984 Winston 500 was a NASCAR Winston Cup Series racing event that took place on 06 May 1984, at Talladega Superspeedway in Talladega, Alabama. Talladega Superspeedway, originally known as Alabama International Motor Superspeedway (AIMS), is a motorsports complex located north of Talladega, Alabama." .

ns1:44722 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Tangiwai disaster occurred at 10:21 p.m. on 24 December 1953 when the Whangaehu River bridge collapsed beneath Wellington-to-Auckland express passenger train No. 626 at Tangiwai, in the central North Island of New Zealand." .

ns1:44730 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "United We Stand: What More Can I Give was a benefit concert led by Michael Jackson held on 21 October 2001 at Robert F. Kennedy Memorial Stadium in Washington, D.C. The concert was the third major concert held in tribute to the victims of the September 11 attacks." .

ns1:44735 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1991 Pontins Professional was the eighteenth edition of the professional invitational snooker tournament which took place in 11 May 1991 in Prestatyn, Wales." .

ns1:44739 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "japonism is a French term that refers to the popularity and influence of Japanese art and design among a number of Western European artists in the nineteenth century following the forced reopening of trade of Japan in 1858. japonism began as a craze for collecting Japanese art, particularly ukiyo-e." .

