ns1:45707 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Robinson v. Florida, 378 U.S. 153 (1964), was a case in which the Supreme Court of the United States reversed the convictions of several white and African American persons who were refused service at a restaurant based upon a prior Court decision, holding that a Florida regulation requiring a restaurant that employed or served persons of both races to have separate lavatory rooms resulted in the state becoming entangled in racial discriminatory activity in violation of the Equal Protection Clause of the Fourteenth Amendment to the United States Constitution. Robinson v. Florida was one of five cases involving segregation protests decided on 22 June 1964." .

ns1:45713 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1980 Pittsburgh Panthers football team represented the University of Pittsburgh in the 1980 NCAA Division I-A football season. Selectors that named Pitt the 1980 national champion: at Pitt Stadium • Pittsburgh, PA. at Memorial Stadium • Lawrence, KS." .

ns1:45724 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1997 U.S. Men's Clay Court Championships was an Association of Tennis Professionals men's tennis U.S. Men's Clay Court Championships held in Orlando in the United States that was paert of the World Series of the 1997 ATP Tour." .

ns1:45726 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1974 Philadelphia Eagles season was the Philadelphia Eagles's 42nd season in the National Football League." .

ns1:45730 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Meridian Ridge Campaign was a series of battles fought between the nationalists and the communists in the Shaanxi province of northwest China during the Chinese Civil War in the post World War II era, resulting in a nationalist victory. However, the rapid response of the nationalist cavalry ruined his plan by following closely, and by the afternoon of August 17, 1947, Wang Shitai (王世泰) still could not shake off the nationalists when they reached the Eagle's Feather's Pass (Diao Ling Guan, 雕翎关) at the border of Shaanxi and Gansu. However, communists had obviously not learnt anything from their defeat and thus paid a heavy price in later conflicts: In 1948, during the Western Shaanxi-Eastern Gansu Campaign, the communist 3rd Garrison Brigade was tasked to protect the right flank of the attacking communist force but it was unable to stop the nationalist counterattack led by the nationalist general Pei Changhui (裴昌会) and the former-guerrilla turned regular unit withdrew into Linyou (麟游) mountains, exposing the Peng Dehuai's headquarters and the communist main force at Baoji. Peng Dehuai and his headquarters of the communist First Field Army would meet a similar danger several days later when the communist 3rd Garrison Brigade once again made the same debacle: when the attacking communists withdrew to Northern Shaanxi via Eastern Gansu, Peng Dehuai ordered the 5th Regiment of the communist 3rd Garrison Brigade to guard the region from Duzi (屯字) town to Xiaojing (萧金) to secure the safe passage for the headquarters and the communist 1st, 4th and 6th columns. After the Western Shaanxi-Eastern Gansu Campaign, Peng Dehuai angrily criticized Huang Luobing (黄罗斌), claiming that the mistake he had made would be sufficient to have him executed. Had the communists learned the problem of the former-guerillas earlier during the Meridian Ridge Campaign and remedied the problem back then, they would not have to face the danger later on in Western Shaanxi-Eastern Gansu Campaign." .

ns1:45731 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1912 Challenge Cup was the 16th staging of rugby league's oldest knockout competition, the Challenge Cup. The final was played on Saturday, 1912, where Dewsbury Rams caused an upset to beat Oldham 8–5 at Headingley in front of a crowd of 15,271." .

ns1:45737 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1952 PGA Championship was the 34th PGA Championship, held June 18–25 at Big Spring Country Club in Louisville. The PGA Championship returned to the state 44 years later in 1996, at Valhalla Golf Club east of Louisville. The match play format at the PGA Championship in 1952 called for 12 rounds (216 holes) in seven days: Heavy rains on Saturday postponed the third round until Sunday; the final was played on Wednesday, June 25." .

ns1:45738 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1961 Titleholders Championship was the 22nd Titleholders Championship, held April 27–30 at Augusta Country Club in Augusta, Georgia." .

ns1:45744 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1913–14 Georgetown Hoyas men's basketball team represented Georgetown University during the 1913-14 NCAA Division I college basketball season. The Hoyas began to play their home games at Ryan Gymnasium 1914–15 Georgetown Hoyas men's basketball team, so this was the last Georgetown men's basketball team to play its home games off campus until the team left Ryan to return to the Arcade Rink for the 1927-28 season." .

ns1:45745 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Tennessee Centennial and International Exposition was an exposition held in Nashville from May 1 – 1897 in what is now Centennial Park." .

ns1:45749 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1996 Vermont gubernatorial election took place on 1996. Incumbent Democrat Howard Dean ran successfully for re-election to a third full term as Governor of Vermont, defeating Republican nominee John L. Gropper." .

ns1:45750 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1997–98 UC Irvine Anteaters men's basketball team represented the University of California, Irvine during the 1997–98 NCAA Division I men's basketball season." .

ns1:45751 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008–09 Toto Cup Leumit was the twenty-seventh season of the third most important association football tournament in Israel since its introduction and fifth under the current format. On 3 February 2009, Hapoel Be'er Sheva F.C. won the 2008–09 Toto Cup Leumit making it their first Toto Cup title." .

ns1:45752 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "German auxiliary cruiser Orion (HSK-1) was an auxiliary cruiser of Nazi Germany's Kriegsmarine which operated as a merchant raider in World War II. Blohm + Voss built her in Hamburg in 1930–31 as the cargo ship Kurmark. The navy requisitioned her at the start of World War II, had her converted into the auxiliary cruiser German auxiliary cruiser Orion, and commissioned her on 9 December 1939. Blohm + Voss in Hamburg built the ship as a freighter for the Hamburg America Line (HAPAG). One of Germany's first auxiliary cruisers in World War II, German auxiliary cruiser Orion left Germany on 6 April 1940, under the command of Korvettenkapitän (later Fregattenkapitän) Kurt Weyher. It was dispatched on board the supply ship Münsterland to rendezvous with German auxiliary cruiser Orion at the Maug Islands in the Northern Marianas. They met on 1 February 1941, and German auxiliary cruiser Orion thus became the only German naval ship of World War II to use a Japanese float plane. In January 1945 she was again renamed German auxiliary cruiser Orion and was used to take refugees from Germany's eastern provinces across the Baltic Sea to ports in northern Germany and occupied Denmark." .

ns1:45753 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1955 All-Ireland Senior Hurling Championship Final was the 68th All-Ireland Final and the culmination of the All-Ireland Senior Hurling Championship 1955, an inter-county hurling tournament for the top teams in Ireland. The match was held at Croke Park, Dublin, on 1955, between Wexford and Galway." .

ns1:45763 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1923 Imperial Conference met in London in the autumn of 1923, the first attended by the new Irish Free State. In addition, prior to the Imperial Conference, Canada negotiated the Halibut Treaty with the United States and did so without involving the United Kingdom or allowing the British government to sign on Canada's behalf." .

ns1:45769 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 Djiboutian protests were widespread demonstrations and riots that took place between January and 2011 in Djibouti, situated in the Horn of Africa. A member of the Arab League, the protests in Djibouti showed a clear influence from the concurrent Arab Spring protests in North Africa and the Arabian peninsula. On January 25, thousands of people turned out to protest in Djibouti City. On March 3, Djibouti ordered its opposition party to cancel its anti-government protests which were to be held on 2011 due to a previous rally a month earlier turning violent." .

ns1:45782 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1992 Slovenian presidential election were held in Slovenia on 06 December 1992." .

ns1:45785 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1985 NBA All-Star Game was played on 10 February 1985, at the RCA Dome in Indianapolis." .

ns1:45803 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Special Olympics Leicester were held in Leicester between the 2009 and 2009. There are some of the sporting venues hosted the 2009 Games, including Abbey Park (cycling), New College Leicester (gymnastics), Hollywood Bowl (ten pin bowling) and Saffron Lane Sports Centre (athletics)." .

ns1:45809 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 2013 Russia Open Badminton Championships was the eleventh grand prix gold and grand prix tournament of the 2013 BWF Grand Prix Gold and Grand Prix. The tournament was held in Sports Hall Olympic, Vladivostok, Russia 24 September 2013 until 29 September 2013 and had a total purse of $50,000." .

ns1:45816 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The World Military Track & Field Championships are the world championship of athletics organized by the International Military Sports Council (CISM)." .

ns1:45819 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 ICC World Twenty20 was the fifth ICC World Twenty20 competition, an international Twenty20 cricket tournament, that took place in Bangladesh from 16 to 2014. The International Cricket Council announced Bangladesh as host in 2010. The top eight Full Member nations in the International Cricket Council T20I Championship rankings as of 8 October 2012 automatically progressed to the Super 10 stage of 2014 ICC World Twenty20. The official theme song for the 2014 ICC World Twenty20 Char Chokka Hoi Hoi was released on 2014." .

ns1:45820 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Liberation of Strasbourg took place on 23 November 1944 during the Alsace campaign (23 November 1944 - March 1945) in the last months of the World War II. After the liberation of Mulhouse on 21 23 November 1944 by the 1st Armored Division, General Philippe Leclerc de Hauteclocque and the 2nd Armored Division entered Strasbourg after having liberated Sarrebourg and La Petite-Pierre, which cleared the way to the city of Strasbourg. On 23 November 1944, units of the French 2nd Armored Division entered the city and raised the Free French tricolore over Strasbourg cathedral at 2:30 pm. The oath was fulfilled on 23 November 1944, when the 2nd French Armoured Division under Leclerc's command liberated Strasbourg." .

ns1:45823 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Vantage was a British military operation in 1961 to support the newly independent state of Kuwait against territorial claims by its neighbour, Iraq. By 01 July 1961 Britain had half of a brigade group in Kuwait ready for action." .

ns1:45827 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Bob Cook Memorial Mount Evans Hill Climb or Mount Evans Hill Climb is a bicycle race situated on Mount Evans near Idaho Springs, Colorado." .

ns1:45832 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1976 European Athletics Indoor Championships – women's 1500 metres event at the 1976 European Athletics Indoor Championships was held on 22 February in Munich." .

ns1:45835 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1984 Santista Textile Open was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor carpet courts in Rio de Janeiro, Brazil that was part of the 1984 Virginia Slims World Championship Series. The tournament was held from 09 July 1984 through 15 July 1984." .

ns1:45849 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 II liiga was the 24th season of the Second League of Estonia, fourth-highest league for association football clubs in Estonia. The following clubs are competing in Second League of Estonia North/East during the 2018 season." .

