ns1:48480 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2004 United States presidential election in New Mexico took place on 02 November 2004, and was part of the 2004 United States presidential election. New Mexico was won by incumbent Republican President of the United States George W. Bush by a 0.79% margin of victory. Exit polling showed that incumbent George W. Bush performed better among Hispanic Americans in 2004 than in 2000 United States presidential election in New Mexico. New Mexico was one of the only three states which swung between 2000 United States presidential election in New Mexico and 2004 (Iowa also flipped from Gore to Bush, while New Hampshire flipped from Bush to Kerry), although Bush only won with a margin of less than 1% of the vote. As of the 2020 presidential election, this is the last time that the Republican nominee carried New Mexico, as well as Los Alamos County and Sandoval County. Although Bill Richardson, the Democratic governor, was very popular, the state, which voted for Al Gore by 300 votes in 2000 United States presidential election in New Mexico, chose George W. Bush in 2004, by 6,000 votes. As of 2020 this is the last time New Mexico chose a Republican for President of the United States." .

ns1:48488 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Marshfield Fair is a traditional, American country fair that has taken place at the end of August every year since 1867. The fair is located on the 62-acre (250,000 m2) Marshfield Fairgrounds in Marshfield, approximately 30 miles (48 km) south of Boston in Massachusetts' South Shore region." .

ns1:48498 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "United Nations Security Council Resolution 1999 was adopted without a vote on 13 July 2011 after examining the application of the Republic of South Sudan for membership into the United Nations." .

