ns1:49197 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Greensboro massacre is the term for an event which took place on 03 November 1979, in Greensboro. Defendants included the city of Greensboro, state of North Carolina, the Justice Department and the FBI. On August 15, 2017 and on October 6, 2020, the Greensboro City Council formally apologized for the massacre. It planned to stage a rally and a march against the Klan on 03 November 1979, in Greensboro. With support from the North Carolina ACLU, the CWP proceeded to commemorate these four with the following inscription: On 03 November 1979 the criminal monopoly capitalist class murdered Jim Waller, César Cauce, Mike Nathan, Bill Sampson, and Sandi Smith with government agents, Klan, and Nazis. The FBI started an investigation which it called GREENKIL (Greensboro Killings), turning over evidence it gathered to the state of North Carolina for its murder trial. A Federal jury in Winston-Salem, North Carolina, found two Klansmen, three Nazis, two Greensboro police officers, and a police informant liable for the wrongful death of Dr. Michael Nathan, a non-CWP demonstrator, and for injuries to survivors Paul Bermanzohn and Tom Clark, who had been wounded." .

ns1:49202 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Sandakan Death Marches were a series of death march in Borneo from Sandakan to Ranau which resulted in the deaths of 2,434 Allied prisoners of war held captive by the Empire of Japan during the Pacific campaign of World War II in the Sandakan POW Camp." .

ns1:49205 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Etiwanda Fire was a wildfire in the San Bernardino National Forest in the foothills north of Rancho Cucamonga." .

