ns1:51025 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "\"Day of Independence\") is the national day of Israel, commemorating the Israeli Declaration of Independence in 1948. Because Israel declared independence on 14 May 1948, which corresponded with the Hebrew date 5 Iyar in that year, Yom Ha'atzmaut was originally celebrated on that date. Independence Day (Israel) is founded on the declaration of the establishment of the Israel by the Jewish leadership headed by future Prime Minister David Ben-Gurion on 14 May 1948. In response to widespread public feeling, the Chief Rabbinate in Israel decided during 1950–51 that Independence Day (Israel) should be given the status of a minor Jewish holiday on which Hallel be recited. People affiliated to the Edah HaChareidis mourn the establishment of Israel on Independence Day (Israel), claiming that the establishment of a Jewish state before the coming of the Messiah is a sin and heresy. In 2015, Rabbi Shlomo Riskin of Efrat founded Day to Praise, a global initiative which calls on Christians around the world to join in reciting the Hallel (Psalms 113–118), with the Jewish people, on Israel's Independence Day (Israel)." .

ns1:51026 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Kinburn was fought on 12 October 1787 (N.S." .

ns1:51027 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Partial 1896 Belgian general election were held in Belgium on 05 July 1896 and 12 05 July 1896." .

ns1:51036 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 Commonwealth Youth Games were held in Pune, India, a city in the state of Maharashtra. The III Commonwealth Youth Games were conducted from 12 to 2008." .

ns1:51037 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "In the Illinois state capitol referendum, 1834 voters in the state of Illinois were asked on 04 August 1834 to choose the next state capitol." .

ns1:51052 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1904 United States presidential election in California took place on 08 November 1904 as part of the 1904 United States presidential election." .

ns1:51054 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Halberd was a British naval operation that took place on 27 September 1941, during the World War II. Nine merchant ships carrying 81,000 tons of military equipment and supplies sailed from Liverpool on 16 September and from the Clyde on 17 September as part of Malta Convoys WS (Winston Specials) 11X, passing Gibraltar on 24 27 September 1941, with a close escort under the command of Rear-Admiral Harold Burrough. Force H rejoined the Malta Convoys at 07:10 27 September 1941." .

ns1:51056 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1938 Norwegian Football Cup was the 37th season of the Norway annual knockout association football tournament. The tournament was open for all members of Norwegian Football Federation, except those from Northern Norway. The 1938 Norwegian Football Cup Final was played at Briskeby in Hamar on 1938, and was contested by the defending champions Mjøndalen and the three-time former winners Fredrikstad FK. Fredrikstad FK secured their fourth title with a 3-2 win after extra time in the 1938 Norwegian Football Cup Final. Fredrikstad FK's cup-victory completed the first Norway double, having also won the inaugural League of Norway." .

ns1:51064 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Bliss-Leavitt Mark 9 torpedo was a Bliss-Leavitt torpedo developed and produced by the E. W. Bliss Company and the Naval Torpedo Station in Newport, Rhode Island in 1915." .

ns1:51067 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1986 British Columbia general election was the 34th general election in the Province of British Columbia, Canada. The governing British Columbia Social Credit Party (Socreds) had seen a leadership change just months before the election, with Bill Bennett standing down in favour of Bill Vander Zalm." .

ns1:51069 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Sejny took place in 01 September 1920 during the Polish–Soviet War and Polish–Lithuania War. The 1st Legions Infantry Division of Colonel Dab-Biernacki attacked Lithuania 2nd Infantry Division at Sejny, capturing the town after a six-hour battle." .

ns1:51072 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1993 South African Grand Prix was a Formula One motor race held at Kyalami on 14 March 1993. The race, contested over 72 laps, was the first race of the 1993 Formula One season and was won from pole position by Alain Prost, driving a Williams-Renault, with Ayrton Senna second in a McLaren-Ford and Mark Blundell third in a Ligier-Renault." .

ns1:51077 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Edmonton bid for the 2022 Commonwealth Games was an attempt to bring the 2022 Commonwealth Games to the city of Edmonton, Alberta, Canada. On 10 February 2015, Edmonton withdrew its bid for the Games. On March 31, 2014, The City of Edmonton announced its intent to bid for 2022 Commonwealth Games. On July 1, 2014, The City of Edmonton, in partnership with the Government of Alberta and Commonwealth Games Canada, named former Edmonton Airports President and CEO Reg Milley as the Chair of the Bid Committee for the 2022 Commonwealth Games. The logo for the Edmonton bid was also unveiled during the Canada Day celebrations at City Hall. Edmonton is Canada's fifth-largest city and the second-largest city in as well as the capital of the Province of Alberta. On February 11, 2015, Edmonton announced it was withdrawing its bid to host the 2022 Commonwealth Games, citing financial reasons and a global fall in oil prices." .

ns1:51082 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "UFC 144 was a mixed martial arts pay-per-view event held by the Ultimate Fighting Championship on 26 February 2012 at the Saitama Super Arena in Saitama, Japan. UFC 144 was held at the Saitama Super Arena in the Chūō-ku central ward of Saitama City in Saitama Prefecture. UFC 144 marked the UFC's fifth appearance in Japan, their first event in Japan since UFC 29 in 2000, and its first event in Japan since the 2007 purchase of Pride Fighting Championships, which held many of its cards at Saitama Super Arena." .

ns1:51085 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of the strait of Messina was fought in 276 BC when a Ancient Carthage fleet attacked the Sicilian fleet of Pyrrhus of Epirus, who was crossing the strait to Italy. Pyrrhus had left Italy for Sicily on the Autumn of 278 BC and scored several major victories against the Ancient Carthage armies, but Roman successes against Pyrrhus' Italian allies convinced him to return to Italy." .

ns1:51090 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1979 Dino Ferrari Grand Prix was a non-championship Formula One motorsport held at the Autodromo Dino Ferrari, Imola on 16 September 1979." .

ns1:51102 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1965 Lady Wigram Trophy was a motor race held at the Wigram Airfield Circuit on 1965. It was the fourteenth Lady Wigram Trophy to be held and was won by Jim Clark driving the Lotus 32B in what was the fastest race in the history of the Lady Wigram Trophy." .

ns1:51103 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The IWOCL (IWOCL – \"eye-wok-ul\") is an annual conference that brings together the community of OpenCL users, researchers, developers and suppliers to share OpenCL best practise and help advance the use of the Khronos OpenCL standard for the parallel programming of heterogeneous systems. Participation at IWOCL is open to anyone who is interested in contributing to, and participating in the OpenCL community including developers working with the many APIs, tools and libraries built on OpenCL, including the Khronos SPIR (Standard Portable Intermediate Representation) and SYCL (C++ abstraction layer). The IWOCL (IWOCL) is now in its 7th year with the first event taking place at Georgia Tech, USA on May 13–14, 2013." .

ns1:51108 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The San Diego Film Critics Society Awards 2006, honoring the best in film for 2006, were given in 2006 by the San Diego Film Critics Society Award." .

ns1:51109 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The group stage of the 2013 AFC Cup group stage was played from 26 February to 2013." .

ns1:51117 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Saskatchewan general election was held on 2007; the writ was dropped on 2007. The Saskatchewan New Democratic Party government of Premier Lorne Calvert was defeated by the Saskatchewan Party, led by Brad Wall. Lorne Calvert, Premier of Saskatchewan and leader of the New Democratic Party (NDP), advised the Lieutenant Governor of Saskatchewan to call an election for 2007. This strategy paid off; by the spring of 2007 the Saskatchewan Party was well ahead in most opinion polling. 1 Dan Harder, the Saskatchewan Party candidate in Regina Walsh Acres, withdrew his candidacy on 2007 after the party learned the details of a complaint of inappropriate conduct made against him by employees of Big Brothers of Regina in 2006 while he was executive director of the organization." .

ns1:51123 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1966 Xingtai earthquake (Chinese: 邢台大地震; pinyin: Xíngtái Dà Dìzhèn) was a sequence of major earthquakes that took place between 08 March 1966 and March 29, 1966, in the area administered by the prefecture-level city of Xingtai in southern Hebei province, People's Republic of China. The first earthquake with magnitude 6.0 on the Moment magnitude scale and epicenter in Longyao County occurred in the early morning of 08 March 1966." .

ns1:51126 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Formula Three, also called Formula 3, abbreviated as F3, is a third-tier class of open-wheel formula racing. (1213 kiB) Until the launch of the FIA Formula 3 Championship in 2019, there has never been a World Championship for Formula Three. In the 1970s and into the 1980s the European Formula Three Championship and British Formula 3 Championship (once one series had emerged from the competing British series in the 1970s) were the most prominent, with a number of future Formula One champions coming from them. France, Germany, and Italy also had important Formula Three series, but interest in these was originally subsidiary to national formulae – Formula Renault in France and Formula Super Vee in Germany. Brazil's SudAm Formula Three Championship, which now has the most powerful engine of all Formula Three series, was known for producing excellent drivers who polished their skills in the British Formula 3 championship." .

ns1:51129 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "United Nations Security Council Resolution 2021 was unanimously adopted on 29 November 2011." .

ns1:51146 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "World Bee Day is celebrated on May 20." .

ns1:51153 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1947 ISSF World Shooting Championships was the contemporary name of the ISSF World Shooting Championship held in Stockholm, Sweden in the summer of 1947." .

ns1:51166 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Men's skeet event at the shooting at the 2016 Summer Olympics – men's skeet took place on 12 13 August 2016 and 13 August 2016 at the National Shooting Center." .

ns1:51167 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Szuperkupa is an annual association football match between the League (Nemzeti Bajnokság I) champions and the Cup (Magyar Kupa) winners." .

ns1:51175 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Chilean War of Independence was a war between pro-independence Chile Criollo peoples seeking political and economic independence from Spain and royalist creoles supporting continued allegiance to the Captaincy General of Chile and membership of the Spanish Empire. The Chilean War of Independence was part of the more aroused Spanish American wars of independence. The independence wars in Chile (1810–1818) and Peru (1809–1824) had a negative impact on the Chile wheat industry." .

ns1:51183 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010–11 Olympiacos F.C. season was Olympiacos F.C.' 52nd consecutive season in the Super League Greece. Olympiacos F.C. finished first in the Greek Super League, winning the title after an unsuccessful 2009–10 Olympiacos F.C. season." .

ns1:51184 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 Green Soccer Bowl (reported by some media outlets as the Obama Cup) was a proposed association football tournament." .

ns1:51185 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "wheelchair tennis at the wheelchair tennis at the 1996 Summer Paralympics consisted of four events, singles and doubles competitions for men and women." .

ns1:51187 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010–11 ISU Short Track Speed Skating World Cup was a multi-race tournament over a season for short track speed skating. The season began on 22 October 2010 and ended on 19 20 February 2011." .

ns1:51199 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Bombardment of Tripoli in 1828 was a naval engagement fought between the navies of Tripoli and the Kingdom of the Two Sicilies. As a result of the Pasha's declaration of war the Neapolitan squadron offshore began a blockade of the coast of Tripoli on 1828." .

ns1:51206 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1955 Prince Edward Island general election was held in the Canada province of Prince Edward Island on 1955. The governing Liberals of Premier Alexander Wallace Matheson increased their majority in the Legislature, winning three more seats over the opposition Progressive Conservatives led by Reginald Bell, who would resign as leader in 1957 following this election. This election marked the first and only time (as of 2016) that a party has won six consecutive general elections on Prince Edward Island." .

ns1:51207 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Conference National play-off Final, known as the 2012 Blue Square Bet Premier play-off Final for sponsorship purposes, was a association football match between Luton Town F.C. and York City F.C. on 2012 at Wembley Stadium in London." .

ns1:51208 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 Campeonato Ecuatoriano de Fútbol Serie A (known as the 2011 Copa Credife Ecuadorian Liga Pro for sponsorship reasons) was the 53rd season of the Ecuadorian Liga Pro, Ecuador's premier association football league. After a session of the preliminary meeting of the Congress of Professional Football of the Ecuadorian Football Federation (Spanish: Congreso Ordinario del Fútbol Profesional de la Federación Ecuatoriana de Fútbol), it was determined that the format for the 2011 season will be the same as the 2010 Campeonato Ecuatoriano de Fútbol Serie A. Twelve teams will compete in the 2011 Ecuadorian Liga Pro season, ten of whom remain from the 2010 Campeonato Ecuatoriano de Fútbol Serie A. The winner will be the Ecuadorian Liga Pro champion and earned the Ecuador 1 berth in the 2012 Copa Libertadores." .

ns1:51209 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1909–10 Ottawa Hockey Club season saw the Ottawa Senators (1883-1954) secede from the Eastern Canada Hockey Association (ECHA), and join the new Canadian Hockey Association (CHA), only to abandon that group and join the National Hockey Association (NHA) a few weeks later." .

ns1:51211 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2000 World Junior Figure Skating Championships was held from 05 March 2000 to 12 in Oberstdorf, Germany." .

ns1:51213 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "A sucker punch (American English), also known as a dog shot, coward punch, king hit or one-punch attack (Australian English) or cold-cock (American English), is a punch made without warning or while the recipient is distracted, allowing no time for preparation or defense on the part of the recipient." .

ns1:51214 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 US Open Badminton Championships was the eighth grand prix gold and grand prix tournament of the 2014 BWF Grand Prix Gold and Grand Prix. The tournament was held in Suffolk County Community College, New York, United States 08 July 2014 until 13 July 2014 and had a total purse of $120,000." .

ns1:51221 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Himara revolt of 1912 (Greek: Εξέγερση της Χειμάρρας), was a Greek uprising during the First Balkan War that took place in the region of Himarë (Himarë, today southern Albania), on November 18  1912. The landing in Himarë, in the rear of the Ottoman Army was planned as an independent operation from the rest of the First Balkan War." .

ns1:51222 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Rio–São Paulo high-speed rail (Portuguese: Trem de Alta Velocidade Rio-São Paulo; Abbreviation: TAV RJ-SP) is a planned high-speed rail project to connect São Paulo and Rio de Janeiro. The original project, named Expresso Bandeirantes, was to build a high-speed rail line between São Paulo and Campinas. The project was canceled in 2007 because the Brazilian government concluded that it was more viable to connect Campinas, São Paulo and Rio de Janeiro with a single line 518 kilometres (322 mi) long. Later in March, Hyperloop TT announced that the company has plans to implement a hyperloop railway line in Brazil, which may replace the current high-speed train and connect connect São Paulo and Rio de Janeiro in 25 minutes. The first stretch of high-speed train line in Brazil will be between its main cities of Rio de Janeiro and São Paulo. The distance of 412 km between the two city terminals: Campo de Marte in São Paulo and the Barão de Mauá station in Rio de Janeiro will be covered in one hour and twenty-five minutes at a maximum speed of 360 km/h. It would link Belo Horizonte, Divinópolis, Varginha and Pocos de Caldas (all in the state of Minas Gerais); Campinas, São Paulo, Sorocaba, Itapetininga and Apiaí (all in the state of São Paulo); and Curitiba (in the state of Paraná). The line would be around 1,150 kilometres long, about twice the length of the line between Rio de Janeiro and Campinas. The resultant network, centred on São Paulo, would serve an area containing more than half of Brazil's economic output and population. The line is scheduled to be built after the completion of the line between Rio de Janeiro, São Paulo, and Campinas." .

ns1:51223 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Sacriporto also called the Battle of Scariportus took place in April of 82 BC during Sulla's second civil war. After signing the peace treaty at Dardanos, Sulla returned to Italy at the head of his battle-hardened army with the intention of confronting his political opponents, the Populares." .

ns1:51226 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 ACC Championship Game featured the Boston College Eagles and the Virginia Tech Hokies in a regular-season college football game that determined the conference's champion for the 2007 season. In the off-season, the Gator Bowl Association declared that if sales did not improve for the 2007 ACC Championship game, the game's Jacksonville future would be in jeopardy. Attendance for the 2006 game was low due to high travel costs stemming from Jacksonville's distance from the participating schools, and the 2007 participants—Boston College and Virginia Tech—faced the same problem. The 2007 ACC Championship Game kicked off at 13:10 EST in Jacksonville. The 2007 game injected approximately $10 million into the Jacksonville economy." .

ns1:51227 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1955 Paris-Tours was the 49th edition of the Paris–Tours cycle race and was held on 09 October 1955. The race started in Paris and finished in Tours." .

ns1:51233 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "A. R. Rahman Jai Ho Concert: The Journey Home World Tour is the first world tour by Indian musician A. R. Rahman." .

ns1:51241 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Guadeloupe Division d'Honneur (French: Guadeloupe Division d'Honneur) is the top association football league in Guadeloupe. It was created in 1952 and is headed by the Guadeloupean League of Football." .

ns1:51248 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Deildabikar was the 18th season of the Iceland League Cup, a pre-season professional association football competition in Iceland. The competition started on 15 February 2013 and concluded on 27 April 2013. The games were played from 15 February 2013 to 2013." .

ns1:51251 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1962 Liège–Bastogne–Liège was the 48th edition of the Liège–Bastogne–Liège cycle race and was held on 06 May 1962." .

ns1:51264 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Copa Ciudad de Tigre is a professional tennis tournament played on hard courts. It is held annually in Tigre, Buenos Aires, Argentina since 2017." .

ns1:51265 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2002 European Athletics Indoor Championships – men's 60 metres hurdles event at the 2002 European Athletics Indoor Championships was held on March 1–2." .

ns1:51266 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Brest European Short Film Festival (French: Festival européen du film court de Brest) is a film festival dedicated to short films, happening every year in Brest, in the Brittany region in France. Known as the second best short film festival in France, Brest unites around 30.000 professionals and filmgoers. In 1984, Brest film director Olivier Bourbeillon organizes a short film night at the Mac Orlan theater. In 1986, Gilbert Le Traon, who became director of Brittany Film Archive in 2000, joins Olivier Bourbeillon in the organization of the first edition of \" Brest Short Film Festival \"." .

ns1:51270 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Widerøe Flight 839, also known as the Værøy Accident (Norwegian: Værøy-ulykken), was a de Havilland Canada DHC-6-300 Twin Otter that crashed shortly after take-off from Værøy Airport in Norway." .

ns1:51272 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Neomodern or Neomodern architecture architecture is a reaction to the complexity of postmodern architecture and eclecticism in architecture, seeking greater simplicity. Neomodern architecture continues Modern Movement as a dominant form of architecture in the 20th and 21st centuries, especially in corporate offices." .

ns1:51273 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1920 UCI Track Cycling World Championships were the World Championship for track cycling. They took place in Antwerp, Belgium from 6 to 08 August 1920." .

ns1:51284 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Enschede fireworks disaster (Dutch: vuurwerkramp in Enschede) was a catastrophic fireworks explosion occurring at the S.E. A fire led to an enormous explosion which killed 23 people including four firefighters, and injured nearly 1,000. However, after the explosion, residents from the affected district of Roombeek—a poor, working-class neighbourhood—complained of government inaction and lack of interest, saying the whole disaster was just waiting to happen. Fireworks disaster led to intensified safety regulations in the Netherlands concerning the storage and sale of fireworks." .

ns1:51294 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1988 Houston Oilers season was the Houston Oilers's 19th season in the National Football League and the 29th overall." .

ns1:51296 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "A August 2007 lunar eclipse occurred on 28 August 2007, lasting just over 90 minutes." .

ns1:51299 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "A Contrat nouvelle embauche (abbreviated CNE), known as a new employment contract, new recruitment contract or new-job contract in English is a France employment contract proposed by Prime Minister Dominique de Villepin (UMP) which came into force by ordinance on August 2, 2010." .

ns1:51301 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014–15 South Carolina Gamecocks women's basketball team represented University of South Carolina during the 2014–15 NCAA Division I women's basketball season. 2014–15 South Carolina Gamecocks men's basketball team." .

ns1:51302 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Hong Kong Tennis Open (also known as the Prudential Hong Kong Tennis Open for sponsorship reasons) was a professional tennis tournament played on hard courts. It took place in Victoria Park, Hong Kong, from 10 October 2016 to 16." .

ns1:51307 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 China Open Super Series Premier was a top level badminton competition contested from 22 November 2011 to 27 November 2011 in Shanghai, China." .

ns1:51309 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The June 1988 Haitian coup d'état took place on 20 June 1988, when Henri Namphy overthrew Leslie Manigat. On 20 June 1988 Namphy ousted Manigat in a coup d'état, declaring himself President with Col. Jean-Claude Paul at his side." .

ns1:51312 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The equestrian at the 1992 Summer Olympics – team jumping event, part of the equestrian at the 1992 Summer Olympics at the 1992 Summer Olympics was held on 1992 and 1992 at the Royal Polo Club in Barcelona." .

