ns1:51898 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Deportation of the Chechens and Ingush, also known as Aardakh (Chechen: Аардах), Operation Lentil (Russian: Чечевица, Chechevitsa; Chechen: Вайнах махкахбахар Vaynax Maxkaxbaxar) was the Soviet forced transfer of the whole of the Vainakh (Chechen and Ingush) populations of the North Caucasus to Central Asia on 1944, during World War II. According to a correspondence dated 1944, at least 19,000 officers and 100,000 NKVD soldiers from all over the Soviet Union were sent to implement this operation. Throughout the North Caucasus, about 650,000 people (according to Dalkhat Ediev, 724,297) were deported in 1943 and 1944 by the Soviet forces. In 1944, Beria issued a directive ordering the NKVD to browse the entire Soviet Union in search for any remaining members of that nation, \"not leaving a single one\"." .

