ns1:53951 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Saint-Florent (or Siege of Saint-Florent ) was a British military operation, supported by Corsican partisans early in the French Revolutionary Wars against the French-held town of Saint-Florent on the Mediterranean island of Corsica. Paoli rapidly defeated his political rivals to become de facto ruler of the island once more, but resisted efforts by the National Convention to exercise control over Corsica and in 1793, as the Reign of Terror swept France, orders were issued for his arrest. Linzee therefore determined to attack Saint-Florent, on the northwestern coast of Corsica at the southern end of Cap Corse." .

ns1:53952 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 SEAT León Eurocup season was the fourth season of the SEAT León Eurocup, and the first since 2010." .

ns1:53953 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Euro Players Tour Championship 2010/2011 – Event 3 (also known as the 2010 Rhein–Main Masters and the 2010 Russelsheim Cup) was a professional minor-ranking snooker tournament that took place between 22 October 2010–24 October 2010 at the Walter Kobel Sporthalle in Rüsselsheim, Germany." .

ns1:53954 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1994 Gator Bowl was an American college football bowl game between the Virginia Tech Hokies and the Tennessee Volunteers at Ben Hill Griffin Stadium in Gainesville, Florida, on 1994. The game was moved to Gainesville in 1994 due to renovations to the Gator Bowl for the Jacksonville Jaguars. Instead of beginning in early 1995, work would have to begin in 1994, leaving the site of the 1994 Gator Bowl (played on Dec. 30) in doubt. The Hokies entered the 1994 college football season having finished the 1993 season with a win in the 1993 Independence Bowl, and hopes were high that they would return to a bowl game again in 1994. The 1994 Gator Bowl kicked off on 1994, at 8 p.m. EST at Ben Hill Griffin Stadium in Gainesville, Florida." .

ns1:53980 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1988 U.S. Men's Clay Court Championships was a 1988 Grand Prix circuit men's tennis U.S. Men's Clay Court Championships held in Charleston in the United States. It was the 20th edition of the U.S. Men's Clay Court Championships and was held on outdoor clay court from April 25 through 1988." .

ns1:53985 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1971–72 Paris Saint-Germain F.C. season was Paris Saint-Germain F.C.'s 2nd season in existence." .

ns1:53986 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "WEC 31 was a mixed martial arts event held by World Extreme Cagefighting. The event took place on Wednesday, December 12, 2007 at the Hard Rock Hotel and Casino in Las Vegas, Nevada and was aired live on the Versus Network." .

ns1:53993 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Rimsha Masih blasphemy case (Urdu: رمشا مسیح  — some early reports used the first name \"Rifta\" or \"Riftah\") is a Pakistan girl from Islamabad, who was arrested by the Pakistan police on blasphemy charges on August 2012 when she was 14 years old. The alleged charges included desecrating pages of the Quran (or a book containing verses from the Quran) by burning—a crime punishable by death under Pakistan's blasphemy law. France \"urged the Pakistan authorities to release this young girl\" and has reaffirmed that \"the very existence of the crime of blasphemy infringes upon fundamental freedoms, namely the freedom of religion or belief, as well as the freedom of expression. On September 7, Rimsha Masih blasphemy case was released on bail, on a surety of 1,000,000 Pakistan rupees (US$10,570 on that date). Paul Bhatti, the Pakistan Minister for National Harmony, who had earlier stated his hopes that the case might help end the widespread abuse of the blasphemy laws, expressed \"joy and satisfaction\" at the development. Despite the fact that the case against her was thrown out, people in Pakistan accused of blasphemy are often subject to vigilante justice." .

ns1:53994 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "artistic swimming at the 2018 Asian Games at the 2018 Asian Games was held at the Gelora Bung Karno Sports Complex, Jakarta, Indonesia from 27 August to 2018." .

ns1:53996 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Independence Day in the Niger is commemorated on August 3, marking the nation's independence from France in 1960. Independence Day is celebrated in Niger with official festivals and appearances of political leaders, an official broadcast by the President as well as the tradition (since 1975) that every Nigerien plant a tree." .

ns1:53997 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1983 Talladega 500 was a NASCAR Winston Cup Series event on 31 July 1983, at Talladega Superspeedway in Talladega, Alabama. Talladega Superspeedway, originally known as Alabama International Motor Superspeedway (AIMS), is a motorsports complex located north of Talladega, Alabama." .

ns1:53998 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Kandy Esala Perahera (the Esala Perahera procession of Kandy) also known as The Festival of the Tooth is a festival held in July and August in Kandy, Sri Lanka. The festival ends with the traditional diya-kepeema ritual, a water cutting ceremony which is held at the Mahaweli River at Getambe, Kandy. It was also through the efforts of Upali Thera that the Kandy Esala Perahera came into being. The Kandy Esala Perahera begins with the Kap Situveema or Kappa, in which a sanctified young Jackfruit tree (Artocarpus integrifolia) is cut and planted in the premises of each of the four Devales dedicated to the four guardian gods Natha, Vishnu, Katharagama and the goddess Pattini. 2018 Kandy Esala Maha Perahera (Randoli Perahera) was held on August, the full moon poya day with the participation of hundreds thousands people. The following are some of the notable tusker elephants who participated as the main casket bearer of the Kandy Esala perehera festival." .

