ns1:56196 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Malvern Hill, also known as the Battle of Poindexter's Farm, was fought on 01 July 1862, between the Confederate Army of Northern Virginia, led by Gen. Robert E. Lee, and the Union Army of the Potomac under Maj. Gen. George B. McClellan. It was the final battle of the Seven Days Battles during the American Civil War, taking place on a 130-foot (40 m) elevation of land known as Malvern Hill, near the Confederate capital of Richmond, Virginia and just one mile (1.6 km) from the James River. In spring 1862, Union commander Maj. Gen. George B. McClellan developed an ambitious plan to capture Richmond, the Confederate capital, on the Virginia Peninsula. When Union troops encountered the Confederate rearguard at Williamsburg, the two armies fought an inconclusive battle. On the first day, as Lee led the Army of Northern Virginia toward the Union lines, McClellan preempted him with an attack at Oak Grove. In the resulting battle, the Confederates launched numerous failed charges, until a final concerted attack broke the Union line, resulting in the only clear Confederate victory during the Seven Days. Early the next day, Tuesday, 01 July 1862, McClellan, having come from nearby Haxall's Landing the night before, examined his army's battle line on Malvern Hill. McClellan did not delegate an interim commander; Porter, who was in command during the initial attack, became the de facto leader on the Union side of the battle. Two veteran generals rejoined the Army of Northern Virginia for the battle, Brig. The Army of the Potomac's disposition in the lead-up to the battle was more orderly than Lee's Army of Northern Virginia; all of McClellan's forces would be concentrated in one place, save for Erasmus Keyes and one of his two divisions, which were stationed across the James River. With the infantry part of the battle over, Union artillery continued to boom across the hill. The battle on Malvern Hill was a resounding Union tactical victory. The Union batteries and McClellan's engineers began moving to Harrison's Landing soon after the end of the Battle of Malvern Hill. Its efforts have been bolstered by the Virginia Land Conservation Fund, the Association for the Preservation of Virginia Antiquities, and officials from Henrico County." .

ns1:56197 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "OU812 Tour (pronounced \"Oh You Eight One Two\") is the eighth studio album by United States of America hard rock band Van Halen. was more enthusiastic and called OU812 Tour \"loud, rude, dirty and very much a Van Halen album\". Canadian journalist Martin Popoff defined OU812 Tour music as \"cynical corporate rock\" and found the album \"over-produced and actually more commonplace\" than its predecessor 5150, implying that \"the philosophical soul and warmth\" of Van Halen \"evaporated when David Lee Roth packed it in.\"" .

ns1:56198 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1917 Clemson Tigers football team represented the Clemson Tigers of Clemson Agricultural College during the 1917 Southern Intercollegiate Athletic Association football season." .

ns1:56201 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The athletics at the 2016 Summer Olympics – women's triple jump competition at the 2016 Summer Olympics in Rio de Janeiro, Brazil. The event was held at the Olympic Stadium on 13 August 2016–14 August 2016. Before the competition, 2015 World Champion Caterine Ibargüen of Colombia had the longest jump of the season with 15.04 m. Kazakhstan's Olga Rypakova entered as the defending athletics at the 2012 Summer Olympics – women's triple jump and had been the first athlete to beat Ibargüen that year, ending her rival's 34-meet-long winning streak." .

ns1:56212 a ns1:context_narration ;
