ns1:56255 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 FIFA U-20 World Cup was the 21st edition of the FIFA U-20 World Cup, the biennial international men's youth association football championship contested by the under-20 national teams of the member associations of FIFA, since its inception in 1977 as the FIFA World Youth Championship. The tournament was hosted by South Korea from 20 May 2017 to 11 June 2017. Along with Canada, Chile, Japan, and Mexico, South Korea became the fifth nation to have hosted all of FIFA men's international competitions, namely the 2002 FIFA World Cup, the 2001 FIFA Confederations Cup, and the 2007 FIFA U-17 World Cup. The following were key milestones in the organization of the tournament: The draw was held on 2017, 15:00 KST (UTC+9), at the Suwon Artrium in Suwon, South Korea. The 24 teams were drawn into six groups of four teams, with hosts South Korea being allocated to position A1. From the preliminary squad, the team had to name a final squad of 21 players (three of whom must be goalkeepers) by the FIFA deadline. The squads were announced by FIFA on 2017. This was England's first appearance and victory in the final of a global association football tournament since their senior side's 1966 FIFA World Cup victory, ending 51 years of waiting for a global tournament trophy." .

