    rdfs:label "The 1959 USAC Road Racing Championship season was the second season of the USAC Road Racing Championship." .

ns1:56305 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Ephesus was a naval battle off the coast of Asia Minor near Ephesus in which the Rhodes fleet, commanded by Agathostratus, defeated a Ptolemaic fleet under the Athenian admiral Chremonides. It is also possible that the battle was not part of a wider imperial conflict between the great Hellenistic powers, but a confrontation between Rhodes and Ptolemaic Egypt alone. Along with Cos and Andros, Ephesus was instrumental in breaking Ptolemaic sea-power in the Aegean Sea, and replacing it with Macedonian and Rhodes predominance." .

ns1:56306 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1958 Hungarian parliamentary election were held in Hungary on 16 November 1958." .

ns1:56322 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Kogi prison break was an attack on Koto-Karffi Federal Medium Security Prisons in Kogi State, in north-central Nigeria by unknown gunmen suspected to be members of the terrorist group, Boko Haram." .

ns1:56326 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Cabezón was an engagement early in the Peninsular War on 12 June 1808 between a small Spain militia force (grandiloquently styled the \"Army of Castile\"), based in Valladolid, and a detachment of Marshal Bessières' French Army Corps under General Lasalle. The battle took place when General Cuesta's small army, scraped together almost from scratch to defend Old Castile, deployed itself at the bridge over the Pisuerga at Cabezón, just 13 kilometres (8.1 mi) outside Valladolid, to bar the road from Burgos against oncoming French divisions." .

ns1:56327 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "United Nations Security Council Resolution 722, adopted unanimously on 29 November 1991, after considering a report by the Secretary-General regarding the United Nations Disengagement Observer Force (UNDOF), the Council noted its efforts to establish a durable and just peace in the Middle East." .

ns1:56329 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1947 U.S. Women's Open Golf Championship was the second United States Women's Open Championship, held June 26−29 at Starmount Forest Country Club in Greensboro. Betty Jameson won her only United States Women's Open Championship, six strokes ahead of runners-up Polly Riley and Sally Sessions, both amateurs." .

ns1:56336 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 24th annual Señorita México pageant, was held 1978. Thirty-two contestants competed for the national title, which was won by Señorita México 1978 from Yucatán who competed in Miss Universe 1978 where she was a Semifinalist. The Señorita México title was won by Martha Eugenia Ortiz from Distrito Federal who competed in Miss World 1978 where she was 3rd Runner-up." .

ns1:56343 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Shanghai Rolex Masters was a tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts. It was the seventh edition of the Shanghai Masters, classified as an ATP World Tour Masters 1000 event on the 2015 ATP World Tour. It took place at Qizhong Forest Sports City Arena in Shanghai, People's Republic of China from 11 October 2015 to 18 October 2015." .

ns1:56345 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2002 Women's Pro Softball League season was a year after the WPSL suspended play in 2001 and before it rebranded, toured, and resumed competitive play as National Pro Fastpitch in 2003 and 2004. The roster of the 2002 WPSL All-Star softball team is listed below: Head Coach Tim Kiernan." .

ns1:56346 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1980 Honduras earthquake struck just offshore Honduras on 09 August 1980 at 05:45 UTC. The earthquake severed communications with rural hill and coastal villages in Honduras, and it frightened thousands of people into leaving their homes. The earthquake did not extend to Tegucigalpa, Honduras' capital, or Guatemala's capital, Guatemala City. The earthquake's epicenter lay in the Gulf of Honduras, just offshore." .

ns1:56348 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1920 Milan–San Remo was the 13th edition of the Milan – San Remo cycle race and was held on 25 March 1920. The race started in Milan and finished in Sanremo." .

ns1:56353 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Lake Ptolemy is a former lake in Sudan. Lake Ptolemy is located on the territory of present-day Sudan. Shorelines developed on Lake Ptolemy's northern margin and the lake submerged two tributary valleys there; the development of dune fields on the western shore makes identification of the shores there difficult and their absence has raised questions about whether the lake actually existed at such large sizes. This runoff reached the lake through various wadis, many of which entered Lake Ptolemy from the north, such as Wadi Fesh-Fesh. The presence of Asphataria indicates that Lake Ptolemy was a freshwater lake especially close to its inflows, although with occasional brackish phases. Lake Ptolemy existed as a freshwater lake already around 9,180 ± 185 years before present. Lake Ptolemy is related to the Nubian Sandstone Aquifer; in simulations maximum water levels in the aquifer reached the surface of the lake, and about 3 cubic kilometres (0.72 cu mi) of water from the lake entered the aquifer every year." .

ns1:56362 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Alcheringa, also known as \"Alcher\", is the annual cultural festival of Indian Institute of Technology (IIT), Guwahati. The Campus Princess is another beauty pageant of Alcheringa which started in its 20th edition, was conducted in association with the Miss India Organisation. Alcheringa has also had the jazz band Tropic Green from Singapore, the Portuguese horn player Mickael Faustino, the Algeria Dance Company from Malta, Hungarian flutist David Simon and pianist Janos Palojtay, the German Classical jazz performer Mito, alternate pop music band BETTY from the USA and English hypnotist Andrew Newton and extreme technical metal band Meta Stasis. Udaan is a social initiative by Alcheringa wherein the students of IIT Guwahati visit underprivileged children in various corners of India. Alcheringa has in the past associated with freecharge, Swiggy, OnePlus, Hero MotoCorp, Wipro, State Bank of India, Indian Oil, Maruti Suzuki, Ola, FastTrack, Daikin, Viber, Coca-Cola, KitKat and Baskin Robbins. In the media sector Alcheringa has been associated with the RED FM, The Telegraph, The Assam Tribune, Business India, North East Today, Metalbase India etc." .

ns1:56371 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "United Nations Security Council Resolution 28, adopted on 06 August 1947, appointed a sub-committee composed of all the representatives who proposed solutions for the Greek question to attempt to combine all of them into a new draft resolution." .

ns1:56374 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "La Route du Rock is a biannual music festival that occurs in the city of Saint-Malo." .

ns1:56376 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013–14 California Golden Bears women's basketball team will represent University of California, Berkeley during the 2013–14 NCAA Division I women's basketball season. 2013–14 California Golden Bears men's basketball team." .

ns1:56379 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Italy at the 1985 Summer Universiade competed at the 1985 Summer Universiade in Kobe, Japan and won 15 medals." .

ns1:56383 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Relations between the European Union (EU) and the People's Republic of China (PRC) or China–European Union relations are bilateral relations that were established in 1975 between the PRC and the European Community. In March 2019, the European Union has referred to People's Republic of China as a \"systemic rival\". Relations between the European Union and People's Republic of China have experienced a cool down after People's Republic of China canceled the EU-People's Republic of China yearly summit in November 2008. The European Union representing its 28 Member States and People's Republic of China are both members of the World Trade Organisation (WTO) and participate in the ongoing discussions about the Trade in Services Agreement (TiSA). The SSTL project was launched in 2006 as a pilot project between the European Union and People's Republic of China as the first Asian country." .

ns1:56386 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Return of TLC tour was a concert tour of American group TLC." .

ns1:56405 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011–12 Slovenian Hockey League season was the 21st season of the Slovenian Ice Hockey League, the top level of ice hockey in Slovenia." .

ns1:56407 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1992–93 Ukrainian Football Amateur League was the first season of the nationwide amateur competitions in the independent Ukraine and was known as the football competitions of fitness collectives or KFK for short. The tournament was conducted under auspices of the Football Federation of Ukraine before creation of the Ukrainian Football Amateur Association." .

ns1:56420 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010–11 Armenian Hockey League season was the tenth season of the Armenian Hockey League." .

ns1:56422 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1996–97 Czech 2. Liga was the fourth season of the Czech 2. Liga, the second tier of the Czech football league." .

ns1:56424 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Baghdad (2003), also known as the Battle of Baghdad, was a military invasion of Baghdad that took place in early 03 April 2003, as part of the invasion of Iraq. Three weeks into the invasion of Iraq, Coalition Forces Land Component Command elements, led by the U.S. Army 3rd Infantry Division moved into Baghdad. After the fall of Baghdad, Coalition forces entered the city of Kirkuk on 10 April and Tikrit on 2003. On 2003, the 2nd Tank Battalion Marine Corps had a stiff fight with the Al Nida Division of the Republican Guards and foreign Islamist fighters on the outskirts of Baghdad. After several hours of combat, the First Brigade, Third Infantry Division succeeded in taking control of Baghdad International Airport, which would become the hub of American logistics in Iraq for the next seven years. During the softening up bombardment of Baghdad Airport on 03 April 2003, an Air Force F-15E fighter mistakenly attacked Battery C, First Battalion, 3-13th Field Artillery (supporting Third Infantry Division), destroying two Humvees and killing Sergeant 1st Class Randy Rehn and Sergeants Donald Oaks and Todd Robins. Sergeant 1st Class Wilbert Davis is also reported to have been killed along with American journalist Michael Kelly on 03 April 2003 after coming under fire, during operations to secure Baghdad Airport. This portion of the battle was described in detail in the book Thunder Run: The Armored Strike to Capture Baghdad by David Zucchino, published 22 March 2004. Within hours of a palace seizure and with television coverage of this spreading through Iraq, US forces ordered Iraqi forces within Baghdad to surrender, or the city would face a full-scale assault. On 2003, some 500 Iraqi soldiers, including Republican Guard mounted a fierce counterattack across the Jumhuriya Bridge, forcing part of the US forces on the western side of Baghdad to initially retreat, but the Iraqis reportedly lost 50 soldiers in the fighting that included the use of A-10 Warthogs on the part of the US forces. Serious looting was described at National Museum of Iraq, and the Saddam Arts Center, the University of Baghdad, three five-star hotels: the Al-Rashid, the Al-Mansour and Babel Hotel, state-owned supermarkets, many embassies, and state-owned factories. Within eight days following the 2003 invasion, only 35, or 5% of the 700 animals in the Baghdad Zoo survived. On 2003, Baghdad was formally occupied by Coalition forces. The Marines fought a fierce four-hour battle at a Baghdad mosque where senior Iraqi leaders had been thought to be holed up, as American warplanes attacked areas of the city under the control of Iraqi fighters." .

ns1:56427 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1991 NBA Draft took place on June 26, 1991, in New York City, New York. Larry Johnson won the 1992 NBA Rookie of the Year award and was a two-time All-Star, the first player to represent the Charlotte Hornets franchise at an All-Star game." .

ns1:56429 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1984 European Athletics Indoor Championships – men's 400 metres event at the 1984 European Athletics Indoor Championships was held on 3 March and 4 March." .

ns1:56433 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operação Prato (Portuguese: Operação Prato; literally, Operation Plate) was an investigation carried out between 1977 and 1978 by the Brazilian Air Force following alleged UFO sightings in the city of Colares." .

ns1:56440 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2014 IIHF World Championship Division II was a pair of international ice hockey tournaments organized by the International Ice Hockey Federation. Group A was contested in Belgrade, Serbia, running from 9 to 15 April 2014 and Group B was contested in Jaca, Spain, running from 5 to 2014." .

ns1:56444 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Little Blue River was fought on 21 October 1864, as part of Price's Raid during the American Civil War. At the start of the American Civil War in 1861, the state of Missouri was a slave state, but did not secede. The Confederates struck at about 07:00 on the morning of 21 October 1864, with the advance led by Company D of the 5th Missouri Cavalry Regiment." .

ns1:56452 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1937 Argentine presidential election of 1937 was held on 05 September 1937." .

ns1:56458 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Dalat Flower Festival (Vietnamese: Festival Hoa Da Lat) is a cultural festival which takes place every other year in Da Lat city, Lâm Đồng, Vietnam and some other localities in Lâm Đồng Province. The festival is held with the purpose of exhibiting flowers, vegetables and ornamental plants from the local as well as other regions within the country and many other countries in the world in order to attract visitors to come to Da Lat, promote economic development of the city. Dalat Flower Festival is also an activity honoring the value of flowers and floriculture, calling for investment in Da Lat flower industry as well as promoting a positive image of the city, the culture and the people of Da Lat." .

ns1:56462 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Libertarianism in the United States is a political philosophy and political movement promoting individual liberty. The split was finalized in 1971 when conservative leader William F. Buckley Jr. attempted to divorce libertarianism from the political movement, writing in a New York Times article as follows: \"The ideological licentiousness that rages through America today makes anarchy attractive to the simple-minded. Wendy McElroy has stated that she would leave the political movement if thick libertarianism prevails. Anthony Gregory points out that within the libertarian political movement, \"just as the general concepts \"left\" and \"right\" are riddled with obfuscation and imprecision, left- and right-libertarianism can refer to any number of varying and at times mutually exclusive political orientations\"." .

ns1:56465 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 Liga de Ascenso Bicentenario is the second association football tournament of the 2009–10 Ascenso MX season." .

ns1:56472 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012–13 Fenerbahçe S.K. season is Fenerbahçe FC 55th consecutive season in the Süper Lig and their 105th year in existence." .

ns1:56473 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1989–90 Biathlon World Cup was a multi-race tournament over a season of biathlon, organised by the UIPMB (Union Internationale de Pentathlon Moderne)." .

ns1:56475 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Batoo is a Korean go variant of the board game Go." .

ns1:56476 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1st series of benefit concert were held on 2007. The 2nd Live Earth concert was scheduled for 2008 at the Andheri Sports Complex on Veera Desai Road in Andheri West, Mumbai, India and was managed by Kevin Wall who, after a request from former U. S. Vice President Al Gore, planned to have the entire event take place in India. Jethro Tull and Anoushka Shankar, who also cancelled their 29 November Mumbai concert after the 2008 Mumbai attacks, reorganized the performance as A Billion Hands Concert, a benefit concert for victims of the attacks, and held it in Mumbai on 2008. The Dow Live Earth Run for Water took place 2010 and consisted of a series of 6 km run/walks (the average distance many women and children walk every day to secure water) taking place over the course of 24 hours in countries around the world, featuring concerts and water education activities aimed at igniting a tipping point to help solve the water crisis. In New Delhi, the event was protested by a group of activists who disguised their involvement by creating a fictitious front organization, the Hindustan Sea Turtle Alliance, to register their event with Live Earth." .

ns1:56478 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Tim Wilkison defeated Peter Feigl 6–3, 4–6, 6–4, 2–6, 6–2 to win the 1979 Heineken Open – singles singles competition." .

ns1:56481 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2001 Britannic Asset Management International Championships was a women's tennis tournament played on grass courts at the Eastbourne Tennis Centre in Eastbourne in the United Kingdom that was part of WTA Tier II tournaments of the 2001 WTA Tour. It was the 27th edition of the tournament and was held from 18 June 2001 through 2001." .

ns1:56484 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The gradual is part of the proper of the Mass." .

ns1:56490 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Kristin Chenoweth In Concert was the debut concert tour by American actress and singer Kristin Chenoweth." .

ns1:56491 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 Asian Five Nations, known as the 2011 HSBC Asian 5 Nations due to the tournament's sponsorship by the HSBC, was the 4th series of the Asian Five Nations rugby union tournament. The 2011 Asian 5 nations consisted of the best 5 teams in Asia; Hong Kong, Japan, Kazakhstan, Sri Lanka and the United Arab Emirates." .

ns1:56494 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1972 Cambodian presidential election were held for the first, and to date only time in Cambodia on 04 June 1972, following the toppling of the monarchy and the declaration of the Khmer Republic in the 1970 coup." .

ns1:56497 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008–09 UCI Oceania Tour was the fifth season of the UCI Oceania Tour. The season began on 12 October 2008 with the Herald Sun Tour and ended on 2009 with the Oceania Cycling Championships. Peter McDonald of Australia was crowned as the 2008–09 UCI Oceania Tour champion." .

ns1:56499 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Virginia Ruano Pascual and Paola Suárez won the title by defeating Tina Križan and Katarina Srebotnik 7–5, 6–1 in the final." .

ns1:56505 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Zong massacre was a mass killing of more than 130 enslaved Africans by the crew of the British slave ship Zong on and in the days following 29 November 1781. When news of the Zong massacre reached Great Britain, the ship's owners claimed compensation from their insurers for the loss of the enslaved people. Following Sharp's efforts, the Zong massacre became an important topic in abolitionist literature and the massacre was discussed in works by Thomas Clarkson, Ottobah Cugoano, James Ramsay and John Newton." .

ns1:56514 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1974 Canadian federal election was held on 08 July 1974, to elect members of the House of Commons of Canada of the 30th Parliament of Canada." .

ns1:56518 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018–19 FC Academica Clinceni season is FC Academica Clinceni's 12th season of competitive football by FC Academica Clinceni, and the 6th consecutive in Liga II." .

ns1:56523 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2005 NATO Headquarters summit was a NATO summit held in the NATO Headquarters, Brussels, Belgium on 22 February 2005." .

ns1:56528 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Severe Tropical Storm Jebi (pronounced ), known in the Philippines as Tropical Storm Jebi, caused loss of life and moderate damage across Vietnam and South China in 28 July 2013." .

ns1:56529 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 AAA Texas 500 was a NASCAR Sprint Cup Series stock car race held on 03 November 2013, at Texas Motor Speedway in Fort Worth, Texas, United States of America." .

ns1:56538 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Vilna offensive was a campaign of the Polish–Soviet War of 1919–1921. Vilnius, the historical capital of the Grand Duchy of Lithuania, became part of the Lithuanian Soviet Socialist Republic and was soon proclaimed capital of the Lithuanian–Belorussian Soviet Socialist Republic (Lit-Bel) on February 27, 1919. In 1920, the Soviets recaptured Vilnius, followed by the Poles' establishment of the short-lived Republic of Central Lithuania. The Polish capture of Vilnius set the stage for further escalation of Polish conflicts with Soviet Russia and Lithuania." .

ns1:56539 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The swimming at the 1936 Summer Olympics – men's 4 × 200 metre freestyle relay was a swimming event held as part of the swimming at the 1936 Summer Olympics programme." .

ns1:56540 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The athletics at the 2014 Commonwealth Games – men's 4 × 100 metres relay, as part of the athletics at the 2014 Commonwealth Games, took place at Hampden Park on 01 August 2014 and 2 01 August 2014." .

ns1:56543 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Blackburn's Ford (also known as the Skirmish at Blackburn's Ford) took place on 18 July 1861, in the Confederate state of Virginia, as part of the Manassas campaign of the American Civil War. Union general Irvin McDowell's Army of Northeastern Virginia was marching south towards the Confederate capitol of Richmond, and encountered the Confederate Army of the Potomac under the command of P. G. T. Beauregard. On July 16, 1861, the untried Union Army of Northeastern Virginia under Brigadier General (Brig. The Union advance met initial resistance from the 1st Virginia Infantry, 11th Virginia Infantry, and 17th Virginia Infantry regiments of Longstreet's brigade. The resulting battle ended in a defeat for McDowell's Union army, although the Confederates were in too much of disarray to provide a strong pursuit." .

ns1:56546 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 KPIT MSLTA Challenger was a professional tennis tournament played on hard courts. It took place at Shree Shiv Chhatrapati Sports Complex in Pune, India from 24 to 2016." .

ns1:56555 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1989 Great American Bank Classic was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts at the Omni La Costa Resort & Spa in San Diego, California in the United States that was part of the Category 3 tier of the 1989 WTA Tour. The tournament's purse doubled to $200,000 in 1989, when the sponsor changed to San Diego-based Great American Bank from Virginia Slims, a cigarette brand owned by Philip Morris." .

ns1:56557 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "National Heroes Day in Angola is a holiday in Angola on 17 September, the birthday of the national hero Agostinho Neto. National Heroes Day in the Bahamas has been a public holiday since 2013. National Heroes Day is a public holiday in Barbados on April 28. Heroes' Day (Hungarian: Hősök napja), National Heroes Day or the Memorial Day of Hungarian Heroes in Hungary is celebrated on the 31 May as a public holiday. National Heroes Day, a national public holiday in the Philippines, is held on the last Monday of every August (formerly last Sunday of August) to mark the anniversary of the Cry of Pugad Lawin, the beginning of the Philippine Revolution by the Katipunan and its Supremo Andrés Bonifacio in 1896. National Heroes Day is a public holiday in Rwanda on 1 February. National Heroes Day is a holiday in Saint Kitts and Nevis that is observed annually on 16 September." .

ns1:56558 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 4th Goya Awards were presented in Madrid, Spain on 10 March 1990." .

ns1:56559 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Day's Gap, fought on April 30, 1863, was the first in a series of American Civil War skirmishes in Cullman County, Alabama, that lasted until May 2, known as Streight's Raid. These regiments participated in Streight's raid: The Civil War Trust, a division of the American Battlefield Trust, and its partners have saved 40 acres of the battlefield at Hog Mountain, an associated battle that occurred on the same day as the Battle of Day's Gap." .

ns1:56565 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 Atlantic Coast Conference men's soccer season is the 29th season of men's college soccer in the Atlantic Coast Conference." .

ns1:56570 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "TEDxBermuda is an independently organized, licensed TED conference, held each year in Bermuda since 2011." .

ns1:56571 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1962 Uruguayan general election were held in Uruguay on 25 November 1962, alongside a constitutional referendum." .

ns1:56573 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Moncontour occurred on 03 October 1569 between the Catholic forces of King Charles IX of France, commanded by Henry, Duke of Anjou, and the Huguenots commanded by Gaspard de Coligny." .

ns1:56576 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015–16 Indiana State Sycamores women's basketball team represents Indiana State University during the 2015–16 NCAA Division I women's basketball season. 2015–16 Indiana State Sycamores men's basketball team." .

ns1:56577 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Eurovision Song Contest 1983 was the 28th edition of the annual Eurovision Song Contest. It was held in Munich, then West Germany, on 23 April 1983." .

ns1:56584 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Bengal-ITC SRA Classical Music Festival is a major South Asian classical music festival held in Dhaka, the capital of Bangladesh." .

ns1:56585 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Death Before Dishonor XI was the 11th Death Before Dishonor professional wrestling pay-per-view event produced by Ring of Honor (ROH). It took place on 20 September 2013 at the Pennsylvania National Guard Armory in Philadelphia. Death Before Dishonor XI will feature professional wrestling matches, which involved different wrestlers from pre-existing scripted feuds, plots, and storylines that played out on ROH's television programs." .

ns1:56590 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The rowing at the 1932 Summer Olympics – men's coxless four competition at the 1932 Summer Olympics in Los Angeles took place at the Long Beach Marine Stadium." .

ns1:56591 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1903 Chorley by-election was held on 04 November 1903 when the incumbent Conservative MP, David Lindsay, was made Lord Commissioner of the Treasury." .

ns1:56593 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 Metro Manila Film Festival (MMFF) is the 36th edition of the annual film festival in Manila, held from 2010 until January 7, 2011. On 2010, multi-awarded Filipino blogger \"The Professional Heckler\" wrote with sarcasm: \"Several people are questioning the results of the MMDA-organized Metro Manila Film Festival 'Gabi ng Parangal'." .

ns1:56594 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The World Mixed Doubles Curling Championship are annual curling tournaments featuring the world's best teams of mixed doubles curlers. With the popularity of curling, and specifically mixed doubles, growing this policy of open entry led to 48 teams participating in the 2019 championship, the final year of open entry." .

ns1:56599 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Football at the 2000 Summer Olympics – Men's African Qualifiers began in December 1998 and ended in 2000 and was held to determine the African national teams for under 23 that will participate at the football at the 2000 Summer Olympics held in Sydney." .

ns1:56603 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1967–68 Michigan State Spartans men's basketball team represented Michigan State University in the 1967–68 NCAA Division I men's basketball season as members of the Big Ten Conference. They played their home games at Jenison Fieldhouse in East Lansing, Michigan and were coached by John E. Benington in his third year as head coach of the 1966–67 Michigan State Spartans men's basketball team." .

ns1:56606 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "International Sign (IS) is a pidgin sign language which is used in a variety of different contexts, particularly at international meetings such as the World Federation of the Deaf (WFD) congress, events such as the Deaflympics and the Miss & Mister Deaf World, and informally when travelling and socialising. International Sign has been described as a highly variable type of signed communication used between two signers who lack a common sign language. Supalla and Webb (1995) describe IS as a kind of a pidgin, but conclude that it is \"more complex than a typical pidgin and indeed is more like that of a full sign language\"." .

ns1:56611 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 The Bahamas Women's Open was a professional tennis tournament played on hard courts. It took place in Nassau, Bahamas between 14 March 2011 and 20 March 2011." .

ns1:56612 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The case of R v Baillie, a prosecution for criminal libel, opened on 23 November 1778 at 1 pm before Lord Mansfield, the Lord Chief Justice, in the Court of King's Bench." .

ns1:56613 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Baku-Rostov highway bombing was an incident which occurred near the village of Shaami-Yurt in Chechen Republic, on 29 October 1999. The Government in their submission of 28 March 2003 explained that on 29 October 1999 the roadblock “Kavkaz-1” on the administrative border between Chechen Republic and Ingushetia had been closed, because it could not cope with the substantial number of refugees wishing to cross (see § 26 above)." .

ns1:56614 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle off Barbados was fought in 07 March 1778 during the American Revolutionary War. While escorting a fleet of United States of America ships in the West Indies, the frigate USS Randolph was attacked by the Great Britain ship-of-the-line HMS Yarmouth. However, after sailing out to meet the Great Britain off Charleston on February 14, the enemy was nowhere in sight, so the United States of America fleet headed for the West Indies where Biddle would raid commerce. Three days after that, at about 5:30 pm, on 07 March 1778, the Americans were sailing off the eastern coast of Barbados when lookouts spotted a large ship to the windward." .

ns1:56616 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Curtis Cup was played from 8 to 2012 at Nairn Golf Club in Nairn, Scotland." .

ns1:56619 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "United Nations Security Council Resolution 672, adopted unanimously on 12 October 1990, after reaffirming resolutions 476 (1980), 478 (1980), the Council expressed alarm at the 1990 Temple Mount riots in Jerusalem on 8 12 October 1990, resulting in the death of over 20 Palestinians and the injury of more than 150 people, including Palestinian civilians and worshippers. Resolution 672 also authorised a mission to be sent to the region to investigate the incident, reporting back by the end of 12 October 1990." .

ns1:56620 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 IFSC Climbing World Championships, the 9th edition, were held in Avilés, Spain from 17 to 2007." .

