ns1:58826 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Forty Stars (Persian: عملیات چلچراغ), also known as Operation Forty Lights, or Chelcheraq, was a military operation conducted by the PMOI and the Iraqi military at the closing stages of the Iran–Iraq War on 18 June 1988. On 18 June 1988, Operation Forty Stars (Persian: Chehel Cheragh) was one of those battles, planned in conjunction with the Iranian opposition group the Mujahedeen-e-Khalq (MEK), which was actively collaborating with Iraq." .

ns1:58827 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The French conquest of Algeria took place between 1830 and 1903. In 1827, an argument between Hussein Dey, the ruler of the Ottoman Empire Regency of Algiers, and the French consul escalated into a naval blockade, following which July Monarchy invaded and quickly seized Algiers in 1830, and seized other coastal communities. Finally driven into Morocco in 1842, by large-scale and heavy-handed French military action, he continued to wage a guerrilla war until the Moroccan government, under French diplomatic pressure following its defeat in the First Franco-Moroccan War, drove him out of Morocco. The territory now known as Algeria was only partially under the Ottoman Empire's control in 1830. The territory was bordered to the west by the Morocco and to the east by the Ottoman Empire Regency of Tunis. The conquest of Algeria began in the last days of the Bourbon Restoration by Charles X of July Monarchy. The merchants, who had debts to Hussein Dey, the Ottoman Empire ruler of Algiers, claimed inability to pay those debts until July Monarchy paid its debts to them. Following a plan for the invasion of Algeria originally developed under Napoleon in 1808, General de Bourmont then landed 34,000 soldiers 27 kilometres (17 mi) west of Algiers, at Sidi Ferruch, on 1830. The news of the capture of Algiers had hardly reached Paris when Charles X was deposed during the Three Glorious Days of 1830, and his cousin Louis-Philippe, the \"citizen king\", was named to preside over a July Monarchy. On 22 June 1834, July Monarchy formally annexed the occupied areas of Algeria, which had an estimated Muslim population of about two million, as a military colony. Both battles were fought in 1830 and July Monarchy was only able to capture Blida in 1839. A map of \"Ottoman Empire Africa\" from 1905 still shows the empire as possessing a border with Morocco to the west of the \"region\" (hitta, a term for a territory with vague borders) of Algeria. The conquest of the Saharan lands in Algeria finished in 1903 when July Monarchy conquered the kingdom of Kel Ahaggar." .

