ns1:59006 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Fair Access to Science and Technology Research Act (FASTR) is a bill in the United States that would mandate earlier public release of taxpayer-funded research. The bill has been introduced in 2013, 2015, and 2017. FASTR has been described as \"The Other Aaron's Law\", named for open-access activist Aaron Swartz who died in a dramatic case in support of open access research in 2013. The bill is often compared to and discussed in conjunction with the Public Access to Public Science (PAPS) Act, also introduced in 2013. Days after FASTR was introduced in 2013, the Executive Branch's Office of Science and Technology Policy (OSTP) issued a memorandum that \"hereby directs each Federal agency with over $100 million in annual conduct of research and development expenditures to develop a plan to support increased public access to the results of research funded by the Federal Government.\"" .

ns1:59022 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Challenge Provincia di Varese was a golf tournament on the Challenge Tour that is played at the Golf Club Varese in Luvinate, Italy." .

ns1:59024 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Pan Am Flight 73 was a Pan Am flight from Bombay, India, to New York, United States with scheduled stops in Karachi, Pakistan and Frankfurt, West Germany. On 05 September 1986, the Boeing 747-121 serving the flight was hijacked while on the ground at Karachi by four armed Palestinian men of the Abu Nidal Organization. Pan Am Flight 73 originated in Mumbai and stopped at the Jinnah International Airport for a scheduled stopover at 4:30 AM. Safarini negotiated with officials, in particular Viraf Daroga, the head of Pan Am's Pakistan operation, stating that if the crew was not sent on the plane within 30 minutes, then Kumar would be shot. In August 2003, Libya accepted responsibility for \"the actions of its officials\" in respect of the bombing Pan Am Flight 103, but was silent on the question of the Pan Am Flight 73 hijacking." .

ns1:59025 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "United States v. Glaxo Group Ltd., 410 United States Reports 52 (1973), is a 1973 decision of the Supreme Court of the United States in which the Court held that (1) when a patent is directly involved in an competition law violation, the Government may challenge the validity of the patent; and (2) ordinarily, in patent-competition law cases, “andatory selling on specified terms and compulsory patent licensing at reasonable charges are recognized competition law remedies.”. Text of United States v. Glaxo Group Ltd., 410 United States Reports 52 (1973) is available from:  Findlaw  Justia  Library of Congress  Oyez (oral argument audio)" .

