ns1:63219 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The International Documentary Film Festival Amsterdam is the world's largest documentary film festival held annually since 1988 in Amsterdam. Since 2007, the festival's New Media program IDFA DocLab showcases the best interactive non-fiction storytelling and explores how the digital revolution is reshaping documentary film art. At co-financing and co-production market IDFA Forum filmmakers and producers pitch their plans to financiers; at Docs for Sale new documentaries are on offer to programmers and distributors; the IDFA Bertha Fund supports filmmakers and documentary film projects in developing countries; and the IDFAcademy offers an international training programs for up-and-coming doc talents. IDFA was founded by Ally Derks, who remained at the helm from 1988 until 2017, when she stepped down. In addition to the competitions, IDFA presents several non-competitive film programs: In this program section, the festival presents the latest documentaries by renowned documentary film auteurs." .

ns1:63221 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "United Nations Security Council Resolution 1596, adopted unanimously on 18 April 2005, after recalling all previous United Nations Security Council resolution on the situation in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, including United Nations Security Council resolution 1493 (2003), 1533 (2004), 1552 (2004), 1565 (2004) and 1592 (2005), the Council expanded the arms embargo to include all recipients of weapons in the country, and imposed a travel ban and asset freeze on those violating the embargo." .

ns1:63223 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Prosperous is the name given to a military engagement between the forces of the British Crown and the United Irishmen during the Irish Rebellion of 1798. Prosperous, a recently founded (1780) cotton-manufacturing village, was attacked shortly after the outbreak of the Irish Rebellion of 1798 at 2 a.m on 24 May 1798 by a rebel force about 20 strong under Dr. John Esmonde which targeted the British garrison consisting of Cork militia and a detachment of a Welsh regiment, the \"Ancient Britons\"." .

