ns1:63538 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Marshall Islands–United States relations are bilateral relations between Marshall Islands and the United States of America. During World War II, the United States of America took control of the islands from Japan (which governed them as part of the South Pacific Mandate) in the 1944 Gilbert and Marshall Islands campaign. The Republic of the Marshall Islands is now a sovereign state in free association with the United States of America. After more than a decade of negotiation, the Marshall Islands and the United States of America signed a Compact of Free Association on June 25, 1983. Under the Compact, the United States of America has full authority and responsibility for security and defense of the Marshall Islands, and the Government of the Marshall Islands is obligated to refrain from taking actions that would be incompatible with these security and defense responsibilities. In the United Nations, the Marshall Islands shows overwhelming support for the United States of America. On 13 key issues in 2008, the Marshall Islands matched the vote of the United States of America 100% of the time. In December 2017, the Marshall Islands was one of just nine countries (including the United States of America and Israel) to vote against a motion adopted by the United Nations General Assembly condemning the United States of America' recognition of Jerusalem as the capital of Israel. The United States of America and the Marshall Islands have full diplomatic relations. The United States of America and the Marshall Islands governments both claim Wake Island, which puts the United States of America armed forces in the ambiguous position of defending US territory while acting as guarantor (under the Compact of Free Association) of the territorial integrity of a state with which it is involved in a territorial dispute." .

