ns1:41701 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Asian Men's Club Volleyball Championship was the 19th edition of the Asian Men's Club Volleyball Championship, an annual international volleyball club tournament organised by the Asian Volleyball Confederation (AVC) with Myanmar Volleyball Federation (MVF). The tournament was held in Naypyidaw, Myanmar from 30 July 2018 to 06 August 2018. The 13 AVC member associations submitted their men's club to the 2018 Asian Club Championship." .

ns1:41720 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 Faroe Islands Cup was the 57th edition of the Faroe Islands domestic association football cup. It started on 26 March 2011 and ended with the final on 2011. EB/Streymur were the defending champions, having won their third cup title 2010 Faroe Islands Cup. These ties will be played over two legs and will be played on 19 and 2011." .

ns1:41738 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2005 Hurricane Relief 400 was the eleventh round of the 2005 Champ Car season, held on 24 September 2005 at the Las Vegas Motor Speedway in Las Vegas, Nevada." .

ns1:41757 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2003 Lexmark Indy 300 was the eighteenth round of the 2003 CART season, held on 26 October 2003 on the Surfers Paradise Street Circuit, Queensland, Australia. The event was to have been the penultimate race of the 2003 season, but it later became the final round after the 2003 King Taco 500 was canceled because of a wildfire near to the California Speedway venue." .

ns1:41762 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Racławice was one of the first battles of the Polish-Lithuanian Kościuszko Uprising against Russian Empire. It was fought on 04 April 1794 near the village of Racławice in Lesser Poland. The battle site is one of Poland's official national Historic Monuments (Pomnik historii), as designated May 1, 2004. After the battle, Kościuszko paraded before his troops in a sukmana, a traditional attire worn in Lesser Poland, in honour of the bravery of the peasants, whose charge ensured the quick capture of the Russian artillery He also praised Wojciech Bartosz Głowacki, a peasant who was the first to capture the cannon (he is visible in Matejko's painting, above). The victory was subsequently promoted in Poland as a major success and helped in spreading the Kościuszko Uprising to other areas of Poland and instigating the Warsaw Uprising of 1794." .

ns1:41768 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The FIS Nordic World Ski Championships 1925 was the 1st official FIS Nordic World Ski Championships and took place between February 4–14, 1925 in Johannisbad (Janské Lázně), Czechoslovakia." .

ns1:41775 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Prague Cup is an international, multi-level synchronized skating competition, held in Prague, Czech Republic. Prague Cup is organized by the Czech Figure Skating Association and sanctioned by the International Skating Union." .

ns1:41778 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The second World Youth Day 1987 took place on 06 April 1987 and 12 April 1987 in Buenos Aires, Argentina and was presided by John Paul II. The headquarters of the 2nd edition of World Youth Day was made official by John Paul II on June 8, 1986. The World Youth Day of 1987 took place as part of the John Paul's apostolic trip to Uruguay and Argentina which took place from March 31 to 12 April 1987. The Pope arrived in Argentina on 06 April 1987. The Cross of the World Youth Day was a wooden crucifix of 3.8 m height given to young Catholics by John Paul II at the end of the Holy Year of 1984 with the words \"Take it for the world as a sign of the love of the Lord Jesus.\" For the first time since its institution, the Cross out of Rome to be one of the main symbols of World Youth Day in Buenos Aires 1987. The World Youth Day Hymn of Buenos Aires 1987, popularly known as \"Un Nuevo Sol\" was composed by Alberto Croce and Eugenio Perpetua." .

ns1:41793 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1976 United States presidential election in Utah took place on 02 November 1976. Utah was won by incumbent Republican President Gerald Ford over the Democratic nominee, Jimmy Carter. Ronald Reagan would repeat Nixon’s 1972 United States presidential election in Utah clean sweep in 1980 United States presidential election in Utah his elections, and no county in Utah except Carbon and Tooele would ever vote against a Republican until 2008." .

ns1:41795 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 Guangzhou International Women's Open (also known as the Landsky Lighting Guangzhou International Women's Open for sponsorship reasons) was a tennis tournament on outdoor hard courts. It was the 7th edition of the Guangzhou International Women's Open, and part of the WTA International tournaments of the 2010 WTA Tour. It took place in Guangzhou, People's Republic of China, from September 13 through 19 September 2010." .

ns1:41824 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006 World Baseball Classic was the inaugural tournament between national baseball teams that included players from Major League Baseball. It was held from March 3 to 20 in stadiums that are in and around Tokyo; San Juan, Puerto Rico; Lake Buena Vista, Florida; Phoenix, Arizona; Scottsdale, Arizona; Anaheim, California; and San Diego. The first World Baseball Classic featured 16 teams in a round-robin. Seven stadiums were used during the tournament: The teams selected for the inaugural World Baseball Classic were chosen because they were judged to be the \"best baseball-playing nations in the world and provide global representation for the event.\"" .

ns1:41832 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Continental Airlines Flight 1713 was a commercial airline flight that crashed while taking off in a snowstorm from Stapleton International Airport in Denver, Colorado on 15 November 1987." .

ns1:41847 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "In Catholicism, the Miracle of Lanciano is a Eucharistic miracle which is alleged to have occurred in the eighth century in the city of Lanciano, Italy. The alleged miracle is usually described roughly as follows: In the city of Lanciano, Italy, then known as Anxanum, some time in the 700s, a Basilian hieromonk was assigned to celebrate Mass at the monastery of St. Longinus." .

ns1:41856 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Welwyn Tunnel rail crash took place in Welwyn North Tunnel, north of Welwyn (now Welwyn North) station on the Great Northern Railway, on 09 June 1866." .

ns1:41872 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Lathen train collision occurred on 22 September 2006 when a Transrapid magnetic levitation (or \"maglev\") train collided with a maintenance vehicle near Lathen, Germany, killing 23 people." .

ns1:41880 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1966 Daytona 500, the 8th running of the Daytona 500, was won by Richard Petty driving a 1966 Plymouth on 27 February 1966. The 1966 Daytona 500 was the fifth Daytona 500 of 49 in the 1966 NASCAR Grand National Series, which included the two qualifying races for the 500. The 1966 NASCAR Grand National Series opened in Augusta with Petty taking the win in the season inaugural Daytona 500. Dan Gurney followed with a win in Riverside before the drivers and their teams ventured to Daytona International Speedway for the 500-mile (800 km) Daytona 500. David Pearson won the NASCAR Grand National Series Championship after winning 15 events while 168 drivers competed in at least one Daytona 500 during the 1966 NASCAR Grand National Series. Richard Petty captured the pole position for the Daytona 500 with a speed of 175.165 miles per hour (281.901 km/h). After starting on the 1966 Daytona 500 pole, Petty went on to lead the first six laps of the Daytona 500, before relinquishing the lead to Paul Goldsmith, who had started in the third position. The victory by Petty was his second at the Daytona International Speedway, having won the Daytona 500 in 1964. Richard Petty's victory at the 1966 Daytona 500 was the first and only time that he captured the pole position, despite winning the Daytona 500 a record seven times? As of 2009, Richard Petty is the only driver to win the Daytona 500 seven times: 1964, 1966, 1971, 1973, 1974, 1979, and 1981. The 1966 Daytona 500 was the first time that a driver won the Daytona 500 for a second time." .

ns1:41881 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "AAA When Worlds Collide (Spanish: Cuando Los Mundos Chocan) was a professional wrestling pay-per-view (PPV) event that took place on 06 November 1994, at the Los Angeles Memorial Sports Arena in Los Angeles. For the match, Hijo del Santo and Octagón would \"bet\" their masks against the hair of Barr and Guerrero, a challenge that La Pareja del Terror eagerly accepted, and the match was set for the AAA When Worlds Collide show in Los Angeles. After Triplemanía III-A, Aguayo began teaming with his son Perro Aguayo Jr. as the latter began his professional wrestling career." .

ns1:41888 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1992 WTA Tour Championships were held in New York City, United States of America between 16 November 1992 and 22 November 1992." .

ns1:41890 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 BET Hip Hop Awards are a recognition ceremony held on 2009 at the Atlanta Civic Center in Atlanta, Georgia. The 2009 show was hosted by Mike Epps. Kanye West led the BET Hip-Hop Awards 2009 with nine nominations, followed by Jay-Z and Lil Wayne with seven and T.I." .

ns1:41892 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Babang (12 July 1965) was the fourth and last in a series of successful ambushes conducted between in May and 12 July 1965 by Australian troops from 3rd Battalion, Royal Australian Regiment (3 RAR), this time at Babang in Kalimantan (Indonesian Borneo), during the Indonesia–Malaysia confrontation." .

ns1:41934 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1989 World Snooker Championship (also referred to as the 1989 Embassy World Snooker Championship for the purposes of sponsorship) was a professional ranking snooker tournament that took place between 15 15 April 1989 and 1 01 May 1989 at the Crucible Theatre in Sheffield, England." .

ns1:41938 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 World Junior Figure Skating Championships was an international figure skating competition in the 2014–2015 figure skating season. The event was held on 02 March 2015–08 March 2015 at the newly constructed Tallinn Arena in Tallinn, Estonia." .

ns1:41941 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Second Battle of Zurich (25 September 1799–26 September 1799) was a key victory by the Republican French army in Switzerland led by André Masséna over an Austrian and Russian force commanded by Alexander Korsakov near Zürich. The French troops which took part in the battle included: On 22 August Korsakov and Hotze agreed that the Russians with 22,000 men would line the lower reaches of the River Limmat (Aare), and Hotze with 20,000 men would occupy the Obersee region below Lake Zürich from the Linth to Glarus. Korsakov himself arrived in Zürich the following day, quickly displaying a vain over-confidence in the capabilities of his troops and disregard both for the French and his allies the Austrians: The presumption and arrogance of Korsakoff were carried to such a pitch, that in a conference with the Archduke Charles, shortly before the battle , when that great general was pointing out the positions which should in an especial manner be guarded, and said, pointing to the map, \"Here you should place a battalion.\" Shortly before the battle Korsakov detached Sacken's 5,000 man Reserve Division to Rapperswil to reinforce Hotze in anticipation of Suvorov's approach, consequently weakening his defensive line along the Limmat before Zürich and exposing his line of communications. The War of the Second Coalition War had greatly weakened the Helvetic Republic, the consequent loss of popular support led ultimately to the 1803 Act of Mediation." .

ns1:41945 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Cravant was fought on 31 July 1423, during the Hundred Years' War between English and French forces at the village of Cravant in Burgundy, at a bridge and ford on the banks of the river Yonne, a left-bank tributary of the Seine, southeast of Auxerre." .

ns1:41950 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 ITM Auckland SuperSprint was a motor racing event for Supercars, held on the weekend of 4 to 2016. The event was held at Pukekohe Park Raceway near Pukekohe, New Zealand, and consisted of four races at 100 kilometres in length." .

ns1:41952 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Reichenberg was a battle of the Third Silesian War (part of the Seven Years War), fought on 21 April 1757 near the town of Liberec (Czech: Liberec) in Bohemia. At Liberec he encountered Königsegg's Austria corps. The full Austria corps consisted of 18,000 infantry and 4,900 cavalry, but only about 14,000 of them had been concentrated at Liberec. Austria Forces during the battle were: Prussian Forces during the battle were (cavalry maintained 4 sqns and infantry had 2 battalions):" .

ns1:41953 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Grand Paris Express is a group of new rapid transit lines being built in the Île-de-France region of France." .

ns1:41973 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Big Revival Tour was the fourteenth headlining concert tour by American country music artist Kenny Chesney, in support of his sixteenth studio album The Big Revival (2014)." .

ns1:41981 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1961 Monaco Grand Prix was a Formula One motor race held on 14 May 1961 on the Circuit de Monaco in Monte Carlo, Monaco. It was race 1 of 8 in both the 1961 World Championship of Drivers and the 1961 Formula One season." .

ns1:41984 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Ashleigh Barty and Demi Schuurs won the title, defeating Makarova and Latisha Chan in the final, 4–6, 6–3, ." .

ns1:41996 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 Toray Pan Pacific Open was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts. It was the 25th edition of the Pan Pacific Open, and was part of the WTA Tier I tournaments of the 2008 WTA Tour. It took place at the Ariake Coliseum in Tokyo, Japan, from 15 September 2008 through 21 September 2008." .

ns1:42005 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1979 La Flèche Wallonne was the 43rd edition of La Flèche Wallonne cycle race and was held on 10 April 1979. The race started in Esneux and finished in Marcinelle." .

ns1:42018 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014–15 RFU Championship, known for sponsorship reasons as the Greene King IPA Championship, is the sixth season of the professionalised format of the RFU Championship, the second tier of the English rugby union league system run by the Rugby Football Union. Ten of the teams that competed in the 2013–14 RFU Championship remain in the competition for the 2014–15 RFU Championship." .

ns1:42034 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Zimbabwe Premier Soccer League season (known as the Castle Larger Zimbabwe Premier Soccer League for sponsorship reasons) will be the thirty second season of the Zimbabwe Premier Soccer League since its establishment in 1980. Dynamos F.C. are the defending champions, having won the previous 2011 Zimbabwe Premier Soccer League season." .

ns1:42036 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Papillon was a large scale air and ground assault on the Viet Minh at Hòa Bình by the French Army and Air Force in 01 April 1947, during the early stages of the First Indochina War." .

ns1:42041 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1951 edition of Mitropa Cup was unofficial and only for this tournament was named Zentropacup. The tournament crushed by the contemporary and most prestigious Latin Cup and Rio Cup, which eluded the national champions club, was won by the Austrians of SK Rapid Wien. 03 July 1951, Praterstadion, Vienna. 05 July 1951, Praterstadion, Vienna. 05 July 1951, Praterstadion, Vienna." .

ns1:42086 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Egyptian revolution of 1952 (Arabic: ثورة 23 يوليو 1952), also known as the 1952 coup d'état d'état (Arabic: إنقلاب 23 يوليو 1952) or 23 July 1952 revolution, began on 23 July 1952, by the Free Officers Movement, a group of army officers led by Mohammed Naguib and Gamal Abdel Nasser to overthrow King Farouk and the Muhammad Ali Dynasty, and aimed at changing the political leadership in Egypt. However, the movement had more political ambitions, and soon moved to abolish the constitutional monarchy and aristocracy of Egypt and Sudan, establish a republic, end the British occupation of the country, and secure the independence of Sudan (previously governed as an Anglo-Egyptian Sudan). The revolution was faced with immediate threats from Western imperial powers, particularly the United Kingdom, which had occupied Egypt since 1882, and France, both of whom were wary of rising nationalist sentiment in territories under their control throughout Africa and the Arab world. These two issues conflated four years after the revolution when Egypt was invaded by Britain, France, and Israel in the Suez Crisis of 1956. The revolution is commemorated each year on 23 July 1952. The anniversary of the coup d'état is commemorated on Revolution Day, an annual public holiday in Egypt, on 23 July 1952." .

ns1:42096 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1999 Westel 900 Budapest Open was a women's tennis tournament played on clay court in Budapest in Hungary that was part of WTA Tier IV tournaments of the 1999 WTA Tour. It was the fourth edition of the tournament and was held from 19 April 1999 until 25 April 1999." .

ns1:42141 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Typhoon Noul (pronounced ), known in the Philippines as Typhoon Dodong, was a relatively small but powerful tropical cyclone that affected several areas but caused minor damage. The sixth named storm and third typhoon of the 2015 Pacific typhoon season, Noul formed as a tropical depression over the eastern Caroline Islands on May 2, 2015. On May 5, an eye developed in the center of the storm while Noul was near Yap State, prompting the JTWC to upgrade the storm to typhoon status. At 18:00 UTC that day, the JMA upgraded Noul to a severe tropical storm, and later to typhoon status at 06:00 UTC on May 6. Due to radial outflow, low vertical wind shear, and water temperatures above 30°C, Noul started rapid deepening on May 9 while approaching the Philippine island of Luzon, forming a sharply well-defined 35 km (20 mi) eye embedded in a deepening symmetric core; therefore, the JTWC upgraded it to a super typhoon late on the same day. On May 11, Noul impacted the Ryukyu Islands of Japan as a weakening typhoon." .

ns1:42152 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1991 World Table Tennis Championships were held in Chiba from 24 April 1991 to 06 May 1991. On 1991, they agreed to the creation of the unified table tennis team to compete at the 1991 World Table Tennis Championships." .

ns1:42188 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 29 March 2007 Baghdad bombings was a 29 March 2007, double suicide attack on the al-Shaab market in a Shiite district of northeastern Baghdad. The death toll eventually climbed to 82 killed and 138 people were also wounded." .

ns1:42193 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Strong Heart (Korean: 강심장; Hanja: 强心臟; RR: Gang Sim Jang; MR: Kang Sim Jang) is a South Korean talk show or talk battle broadcast by Seoul Broadcasting System." .

ns1:42213 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015–16 Luxembourg National Division was the 102nd season of top-tier football in Luxembourg. The league season started on 02 August 2015 and ended on 22 May 2016, with a promotion/relegation playoff following on 27 May. F91 Dudelange claimed the title on goal difference over 2014–15 Luxembourg National Division Fola Esch. A match was played between Wiltz, the twelfth-placed team in the 2015–16 Luxembourg National Division and Jeunesse Canach, the third-placed team in the 2015–16 Luxembourg Division of Honour." .

ns1:42215 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Rafah massacre occurred on 12 November 1956, during Israel's occupation of the Sinai Peninsula and All-Palestine Protectorate following the Suez Crisis." .

ns1:42216 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 Zengcheng riot (6•11事件) began on 10 June 2011 in Xintang town (新塘镇), Zengcheng District, Guangdong, People's Republic of China and is ongoing. The incident started late Friday 10 June 2011 with the riot lasting several days at Xintang." .

ns1:42221 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1989 French Open – Men's Doubles was held from 29 May until 1989 on the outdoor clay courts at the Stade Roland Garros in Paris, France. Jim Grabb and Patrick McEnroe won the title, defeating Mansour Bahrami and Éric Winogradsky in the final." .

ns1:42226 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Medal Winners Open is an invitational pro–am figure skating competition organized by the Japan Skating Federation and sanctioned by the International Skating Union (ISU). In place of Medal Winners Open, the promoters presented Medal Winners Gala on January 14, 2017 in Yokohama, Japan. The inaugural competition was held on 2012 in Saitama, Japan." .

ns1:42251 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1804 United States presidential election in Vermont took place between November 2 and 1804, as part of the 1804 United States presidential election." .

ns1:42257 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Rennae Stubbs and Todd Woodbridge won the title by defeating Lisa Raymond and Leander Paes 6–4, 5–7, in the final." .

ns1:42265 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1903 World Figure Skating Championships is an annual figure skating competition sanctioned by the International Skating Union in which figure skating compete for the title of World Champion. The competition took place from 20 February 1903 to 21 at the Yusupovsky Garden in Saint Petersburg, Russian Empire and was timed to 200-year Jubilee of Saint Petersburg." .

ns1:42281 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Alexandretta was the first clash between the forces of the Byzantine Empire and the Fatimid Caliphate in Syria. It was fought in early 971 near İskenderun, while the main Fatimid army was besieging Antioch, which the Byzantines had captured two years previously. The defeat at İskenderun, coupled with the Qarmatian invasion of southern Syria, forced the Fatimid caliphate to lift the siege and secured Byzantine Empire control of Antioch and northern Syria. Seized with the spirit of jihad and aiming to legitimize their rule, the Fatimid caliphate used the Byzantine Empire advance on Antioch and the \"infidel\" threat as a major item in their propaganda aimed towards the newly conquered region, along with promises to restore just government. At some point, a Fatimid detachment—according to Ibn al-Dawadari 4,000 men under a Berber chieftain called Aras and a former emir of Tarsus, Ibn az-Zayyat—moved north against İskenderun, where the Byzantine Empire relief army had camped. The first clash between the eastern Mediterranean's two foremost powers thus ended in a Byzantine Empire victory, which on the one hand strengthened the Byzantine Empire position in northern Syria and on the other weakened the Fatimid caliphate, both in lives lost and in morale and reputation. In the end, Ja'far was unable to resist the Qarmatians and their Arab Bedouin allies; making the fatal choice of confronting them in the desert, he was defeated and killed in battle in August 971. Although the emperor advanced deep into Muslim lands and even threatened to take Jerusalem, his death in January 976 lifted the Byzantine Empire danger for the Fatimid caliphate: the Byzantines would never again try to advance far beyond their northern Syrian possessions around Antioch." .

ns1:42291 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Linlithgow Bridge is a battle that took place on 04 September 1526 in the village of Linlithgow Bridge, outside the Scottish town of Linlithgow." .

ns1:42294 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 China International Guangzhou was a professional tennis tournament played on hard courts. It took place in Guangzhou, People's Republic of China between 09 March 2015 and 15 March 2015." .

ns1:42325 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1959–60 Alpha Ethniki was the 24th season of the highest association football league of Greece and the first season of the nationwide league after the league replaced the Panhellenic Championship. The season began on 25 October 1959 and ended on 31 July 1960 with the play-off matches. The Hellenic Football Federation took the decision to reduce the number of teams to 16 and as a consequence a promotional play-off round was formed to decide the last two places of the championship." .

ns1:42329 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1990 Monaco Grand Prix was a Formula One motor race held on 27 May 1990 at Circuit de Monaco. It was the fourth race of the 1990 Formula One season and the 48th Monaco Grand Prix." .

ns1:42330 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 London Marathon was the 34th running of the annual marathon race in London, England, which took place on Sunday, 13 April 2014. The 2014 London Marathon was held on 13 April 2014. The men's elite race featured a particularly strong field, including marathon world-record holder Wilson Kipsang, reigning Olympic and world marathon champion Stephen Kiprotich, 2013 London Marathon champion Tsegaye Kebede, and London course-record holder Emmanuel Kipchirchir Mutai." .

ns1:42348 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1834 Hebron pogrom occurred in early 01 August 1834, when the forces of Ibrahim Pasha launched an assault against Hebron to crush the last pocket of significant resistance in Palestine during the Peasants' revolt in Palestine." .

ns1:42357 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 53rd running of the 1969 Tour of Flanders cycling race in Belgium was held on Sunday 30 March 1969. Belgian cycling legend Eddy Merckx won ahead of Italians Felice Gimondi and Marino Basso, winning his first of two in the Flemish classic. Eddy Merckx won practically every major cycling event in 1969, including the 1969 Tour of Flanders. He arrived in Gentbrugge with a 5' 36\" lead over runner-up Felice Gimondi, the biggest margin in the history of the 1969 Tour of Flanders." .

ns1:42361 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1995 Empress's Cup Final was the 17th final of the Empress's Cup competition. The final was played at Nishigaoka Soccer Stadium in Tokyo on March 3, 1996. Fujita SC Mercury won their 1st title, by defeating Nippon TV Beleza 3–2." .

ns1:42379 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Hurricane Cleo was the strongest hurricane of the 1964 North Atlantic tropical cyclone season. Early on 21 August 1964 hurricane warnings were in effect for Barbados and the Windward Islands." .

ns1:42390 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Clonfin Ambush was an ambush carried out by the Irish Republican Army (IRA) on 02 February 1921, during the Irish War of Independence." .

ns1:42402 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 8th Golden Horse Film Awards (Mandarin:第8屆金馬獎) took place on 30 October 1970 at Zhongshan Hall in Taipei, Taiwan." .

ns1:42409 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1930 German football championship, the 23rd edition of the competition, was won by Hertha BSC, defeating Holstein Kiel 5–4 in the final. The teams qualified through the regional championships: The round of 16, played on 18 May 1930: The quarter-finals, played on 1 22 June 1930, with the replay held on 9 June: The semi-finals, played on 15 22 June 1930: The final, played on 22 June 1930:" .

ns1:42438 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Edict of Potsdam (German: Edikt von Potsdam) was a proclamation issued by Frederick William and Duke of Prussia, in Potsdam on 29 October 1685, as a response to the revocation of the Edict of Nantes by the Edict of Fontainebleau. Kein Neid ward wach, Nicht Eifersucht, - man öffnete die Thür Und hieß als Glaubensbrüder uns willkommen...... Theodor Fontane, 1885, ' Encouraged by the French theologian-philosopher Jakob Abbadie, on 29 October 1685, Frederick William issued the Edict of Potsdam." .

ns1:42457 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006 Air New Zealand Screen Awards were held on Thursday 24 August 2006 at SkyCity Theatre in Auckland, New Zealand. Previously known as the 2005 New Zealand Screen Awards, the awards were renamed when airline Air New Zealand became the naming-rights sponsor, signing for five years of sponsorship." .

ns1:42461 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Convoy HX 79 was an Allied North Atlantic convoy of the HX convoy which ran during the Battle of the Atlantic in World War II. Convoy HX 79 was an east-bound convoy of 50 ships which sailed from Halifax on 8 20 October 1940 making for Liverpool with war materials. On 19 October, 4 days from landfall, Convoy HX 79 was entering the Western Approaches, and had caught up with the position of SC 7, which was under attack." .

ns1:42481 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1916 South Shields by-election was a parliamentary by-election held for the House of Commons constituency of South Shields on 18 March 1916." .

ns1:42493 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of the Aguadores was a sharp skirmish on the banks of the Aguadores River near Santiago de Cuba, on 01 July 1898, at the height of the Spanish–American War." .

ns1:42497 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Vaal Krantz (05 February 1900 to 07 February 1900) was the third failed attempt by General Redvers Buller's British army to fight its way past Louis Botha's army of Boer irregulars and lift the Siege of Ladysmith. The battle occurred during the Second Boer War." .

ns1:42515 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1972 Spanish Grand Prix was a Formula One motor race held at Circuito del Jarama on 01 May 1972. The 90-lap race was won by Lotus driver Emerson Fittipaldi after he started from third position." .

ns1:42555 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1985 Florida Federal Open was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts at the Bardmoor Country Club in Largo, Florida in the United States and was part of the 1985 Virginia Slims World Championship Series. It was the 13th edition of the tournament and was held from 04 November 1985 through 10 November 1985." .

ns1:42594 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Upshot-Knothole Grable was a nuclear weapons test conducted by the United States of America as part of Operation Upshot-Knothole." .

ns1:42601 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1911 Maltese general election were held in Malta on 13 October 1911 and 14 October 1911." .

ns1:42602 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018–19 Russian Cup, also known as the Olimp Russian Cup was the 26th season of the Russian association football knockout tournament since the dissolution of Soviet Union. The Russian Cup is organized by the Russian Football Union. The competition started on 21 July 2018 and concluded on 22 01 May 2019." .

ns1:42605 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017–18 SVB Topklasse is the 85th season of top tier Suriname association football league and 2nd season of the Topklasse, the highest association football league competition of Suriname. The season began on 2017 and ended on 24. Joining the league was F.C. West United and S.V. Papatam who played the previous season in the second-tier SVB Eerste Klasse." .

ns1:42627 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Almedalen Week (Almedalsveckan, also known as Politician's Week in Almedalen, Politikerveckan i Almedalen) is an annual event taking place in week 27 in and around Almedalen, a park in the city of Visby on the Swedish island Gotland. The origin of the Almedalen Week was the speeches made by Olof Palme during several summers in Almedalen. Because of the origin of the Almedalen Week, Almedalen is sometimes nicknamed \"Palmedalen\". These are the official numbers: Almedalen Week is an annual event taking place in week 27 in and around Almedalen, a park in the city of Visby. In 2007, a South Korean delegation came to Visby to study the concept of the Almedalen Week. Almedalen Week visitor Dennis Kucinich commented on the event: When you see the kind of internecine conflict that happens in the United States— the partisan divide, the dichotomous thinking, the separation from each other—there is a different thing happening here in Sweden at Almedalen, which is a sense of a common bond as citizens with a common purpose for the nation." .

ns1:42635 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1922 World Figure Skating Championships is an annual figure skating competition sanctioned by the International Skating Union in which figure skating compete for the title of World Champion. Men's competitions took place from February 5 to 6 in Stockholm, Sweden. Ladies' competitions took place from 04 February 1922 to 6 in Stockholm, Sweden. Pairs' competition took place on 29 January 1922 in Davos, Switzerland." .

ns1:42637 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Steele County Free Fair is an annual week-long summer county fair held in Owatonna, United States, the county seat of Steele County." .

ns1:42649 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Use of Sarum (or Use of Salisbury, also known as the Sarum Rite) is the Latin liturgical rite developed at Salisbury Cathedral and used from the late eleventh century until the English Reformation." .

ns1:42654 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Seacroft Moor took place in Whinmoor moor near the village of Seacroft, north-east of Leeds in West Riding on 30 March 1643 during the First English Civil War." .

ns1:42658 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Verismo (Italian for '\"realism\"', from vero, \"true\") was an Italian literary movement which peaked between approximately 1875 and the early 1900s. The first author to theorize on Italian verismo was Capuana, who theorized the \"poetry of the real\" – thus Verga, at first part of the late Romantic literary movement (he was called the poet of the duchesses and had considerable success), later shifted to verismo with his short story collections Vita dei campi and Novelle rusticane and finally with the first novel of the 'Ciclo dei Vinti' cycle, I Malavoglia in 1881." .

ns1:42669 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2019 Bolivian general election were held in Bolivia on 20 October 2019. As a result, the second vice president of Bolivia of the Senate, Jeanine Áñez of the opposition Democrat Social Movement, assumed the interim presidency of Bolivia on 2019. On 2019 the TSE cancelled the redo session after neither MAS nor 'Bolivia Dice No' protested the inclusion of the annulled ballots. The day after the election the vice-president of Bolivia of the TSE Antonio Costas resigned, citing his disagreement with the decision to stop transmitting results." .

ns1:42675 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Reinado Internacional del Café 2012, was held in Manizales, Colombia, on 2012. 24 beauty queens competed for the title.- The winner was Ximena Vargas, from Bolivia." .

ns1:42681 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Talas or Battle of Artlakh (Chinese: 怛羅斯戰役; pinyin: dáluósī zhànyì; Arabic: معركة نهر طلاس) was a military engagement between the Abbasid Caliphate along with its ally, the Tibetan Empire, against the Chinese Tang dynasty. The exact location of the battle has not been confirmed but is believed to be near Trainville Taraz and Talas on the border of present-day Kazakhstan and Kyrgyzstan. The war was the contention between Tang dynasty and the Abbasid Caliphate at the Transoxiana area. The Tang dynasty Chinese defeated the Umayyad invaders at the battle of Aksu. According to ancient Arabic sources, Chinese prisoners of war are responsible for a technology transfer to the Islamic world, because they engaged in the craft of papermaking while living on land occupied by the Abbasid Caliphate following the lost battle of Talas." .

ns1:42719 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1999 Copa del Rey de Baloncesto was the 63rd edition of the Copa del Rey de Baloncesto. It was organized by the ACB and was held in Valencia at the Pabellón Municipal Fuente de San Luis between 29 January 1999 and 01 February 1999." .

ns1:42721 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2001 Virginia gubernatorial election was held on 06 November 2001. Incumbent Republican Governor Jim Gilmore was barred from seeking a second term; Democratic nominee Mark Warner, the 1996 Democratic nominee for the U.S. Senate, defeated Republican nominee Mark Earley, the Attorney General of Virginia." .

ns1:42741 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Asian Junior Badminton Championships – women's singles tournament of the 2013 Asian Junior Badminton Championships was held from July 10–14 in Kota Kinabalu, Malaysia." .

ns1:42747 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The First Moscow Conference (Codename: Caviar) of World War II took place from 29 September 1941 to 01 October 1941. The initial contact with the Soviet Union came with Presidential Envoy and Director of the Lend-Lease programme Harry Hopkins with Soviet leader Joseph Stalin in Moscow. The Moscow conference was proposed following the meeting between British Prime Minister Winston Churchill and US President Franklin D. Roosevelt at Placentia Bay. An announcement on Radio Moscow said - Comrade Stalin requested the American Ambassador (Laurence Steinhardt) and the British Ambassador (Sir Stafford Cripps) to convey to President Roosevelt and Mr. Churchill respectively the heartfelt thanks of the peoples of the Soviet Union and of the Soviet Government for their readiness to aid the U.S.S.R. in its war of liberation against Hitlerite Germany. W. Averell Harriman representing the United States of America and Lord Beaverbrook representing the United Kingdom met with Vyacheslav Molotov (Soviet Minister of Foreign Affairs) presiding. In a speech of 1941 to mark the 24th anniversary of the October Revolution Joseph Stalin stated - ... the three power conference in Moscow with the participation of Mr. Beaverbrook, the representative of Great Britain, and Mr. Harriman, representative of the United States of America, decided upon systematic assistance to our country with tanks and airplanes. If to this is added the fact that a few days ago the United States of America decided to grant a loan of one billion dollars to the Soviet Union, one can say with assurance that the coalition of the United States of America, Great Britain and the U. S. S. R., is a reality (stormy applause) which is increasing and will increase for the good of our common cause." .

ns1:42762 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2005 Fukuoka earthquake struck Fukuoka Prefecture, Japan at 10:53 am JST on 20 March 2005, and lasted for approximately 1 minute. The Japan Meteorological Agency (JMA) measured it as peaking at a magnitude of 7.0 (6.6) and named it and its subsequent aftershocks the Fukuoka Prefecture western offshore earthquakes (福岡県西方沖地震, Fukuoka-ken Seihō Oki Jishin). Fukuoka is not as seismically active as many other parts of Japan, and was known prior to the earthquake as one of Japan's safest locations in terms of natural disasters; the previous earthquake, a magnitude 5, had occurred over a hundred years ago and it had been centuries since the city had experienced a serious earthquake. Kyodo news agency, citing Japan's meteorological agency, said it was the strongest earthquake to hit kyushu since May 1997, and the first to exceed magnitude 6 since 1898. Earthquake experts in Japan said it is rare for a strong earthquake to strike the area. When a center is located at a depth of 10 km, it would cause an earthquake of a lower-6 magnitude (similar to the 20 March 2005 earthquake) in downtown Fukuoka. The probability of an earthquake along the known length of the Kego fault occurring within 30 years was estimated at 0.4% prior to the 20 March 2005 earthquake, but this probability has been revised upwards since. Following reports that the city has only prepared for earthquakes up to a magnitude of 6.5, the aftershock renewed fears that the quakes might cause the Kego fault to become active again beneath Fukuoka, leading to an earthquake as big as, or bigger than, the 20 March 2005 quake." .

ns1:42766 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Slammiversary XI was a professional wrestling pay-per-view (PPV) event produced by the Total Nonstop Action Wrestling (TNA) promotion, which took place on 02 June 2013 at the Agganis Arena in Boston. During the Lockdown Fanfest held in San Antonio, Texas on March 9, 2013, TNA President Dixie Carter announced that this year's Slammiversary would take place at the Agganis Arena in Boston on 02 June 2013. In the press release that followed, Carter stated, \"Slammiversary is our biggest show of the summer, and we're so excited to bring it to Boston for the first time. Slammiversary featured nine professional wrestling matches that involved different wrestlers from pre-existing scripted feuds and storylines." .

ns1:42770 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The M+M's Tour was the sixth concert tour by American recording artist Britney Spears,in support upcoming fifth studio album Blackout (2007). On 01 May 2007, a front-page story on The San Diego Union-Tribune fueled the speculation." .

ns1:42771 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Tauragė Revolt (Lithuanian: Tauragės sukilimas) was an anti-government revolt that took place in Tauragė, Lithuania on 09 September 1927. The rebels took control of Tauragė, the revolt spread to neighboring villages and their forces grew to 200 men. In the local press, the rebels printed 5,000 copies of a proclamation, signed by reserve captain Antanas Majus as the new head of the Tauragė District, informing locals about the revolt and ordering to surrender all weapons to the new commandant. Antanas Majus was not present in Tauragė and later claimed that he knew nothing of the revolt." .

ns1:42786 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016–17 Men's Volleyball Thailand League was the 12th season of the Thai League, the top Thai professional league for association volleyball clubs, since its establishment in 2005. Wing 46 Phitsanulok are the defending champions, having won the Volleyball Thailand League title the 2015–16 Men's Volleyball Thailand League." .

ns1:42811 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 United States Grand Prix (formally the 2014 Formula 1 United States Grand Prix) was a Formula One motor race held on 02 November 2014 at the Circuit of the Americas in Travis County near Austin. It was the 17th round of the 2014 Formula One World Championship and the 36th running of the United States Grand Prix held as part of the series. The 56-lap race was won by Mercedes driver Lewis Hamilton after starting from second position. The 2014 United States Grand Prix was the 17th of the 19 rounds of the 2014 Formula One World Championship, and the 36th running of the United States Grand Prix in Formula One. It was held on 02 November 2014 at the 5.513-kilometre (3.426 mi) 20-turn Circuit of the Americas in Travis County, Austin. The United States Grand Prix's official name was the 2014 Formula 1 United States Grand Prix. Before the race, Mercedes driver Lewis Hamilton led the World Drivers' Championship with 291 points, ahead of teammate Nico Rosberg in second and Daniel Ricciardo third. It was Hamilton's tenth win of 2014, his second in Austin, and the 32nd of his career, breaking Nigel Mansell's record for the highest number of race victories for a British driver." .

ns1:42815 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2008 Northern Territory general election were held in the Northern Territory of Australia on 2008. The incumbent centre-left Labor Party (ALP), led by Chief Minister of the Northern Territory Paul Henderson won a narrow third term victory against the opposition centre-right Country Liberal Party (CLP), led by Terry Mills." .

ns1:42831 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Samsung 500, the seventh race of the 2007 NASCAR Nextel Cup Series Series, was held on Sunday, 15 April 2007 of that year at the 1.5-mile Texas Motor Speedway in Fort Worth. In the end, the race came down to former Texas Motor Speedway winners and ex-teammates Matt Kenseth and Jeff Burton. While Burton won his second race, being the first repeat winner, still the past twelve races have been won by twelve different drivers, and it was the fourth different winning team at Texas Motor Speedway in the past four races (Roush, Evernham, Gibbs, Childress), with the past three teams winning at Texas winning for the first time." .

ns1:42835 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014–15 ISU Speed Skating World Cup, officially the Essent ISU World Cup Speed Skating 2014–2015, was a series of international speed skating competitions that ran the entire season. Additionally, skaters placed among the top 32 in the final 2013–14 ISU Speed Skating World Cup, or included in the final qualifying list (including the reserve list) of the 2014 Winter Olympics, for the distance category concerned, was allowed entry." .

ns1:42843 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1976–77 FIBA European Cup Winner's Cup was the eleventh edition of FIBA Europe's 2nd-tier level European-wide professional club basketball competition, contested between national domestic cup champions, running from 20 October 1976, to 29 March 1977. It was contested by 25 teams, five more than in the 1975–76 FIBA European Cup Winner's Cup. Pallacanestro Cantù was the fourth Italian club to win the FIBA Europe Cup Winners' Cup, after Ignis Varese, Fides Napoli, and Olimpia Milano." .

ns1:42866 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 U.S. Open Cup Final was played on 16 September 2014, at Subaru Park in Chester, Pennsylvania. Seattle Sounders FC won the match, defeating the Philadelphia Union. The United States Soccer Federation determined the host of the final with a coin flip on August 4, 2014. The winner of the toss was whoever was victorious in the FC Dallas/Philadelphia Union semifinal, meaning that the game would take place at Subaru Park in Chester, Pennsylvania. Attendance at Subaru Park was 15,256, the lowest for an Open Cup final in six years." .

ns1:42871 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1954 Rugby Union European Cup was the fifth rugby union European championship, organised by Rugby Europe and the second (and last) with this name. As in the 1952 Rugby Union European Cup, France won the title after a victory over Italy in the final." .

ns1:42883 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Four Days' Battle, also known as the Four Days' Fight in some English sources and as Vierdaagse Zeeslag in Dutch, was a naval battle of the Second Anglo-Dutch War. Fought from 1 June to 4 14 June 1666 in the Julian or Old Style calendar that was then used in England, in the southern North Sea, it began off the Flemish coast and ended near the English coast. Dutch accounts referred to its dates as 11 June 1666 to 14 June 1666 by using the New Style calendar. Second Anglo-Dutch War arose from an escalation of existing commercial tensions between England and the Netherlands in 1664, involving English provocations in North America and West Africa. After Battle of Lowestoft, and concerned that the complete destruction of the Dutch fleet would leave the English fleet in a position to interfere with his plans in the Spanish Netherlands, Louis again offered mediation, but as he had already sent French troops into the Netherlands to assist the Dutch, and had also attempted to bring Denmark into an alliance with the Dutch Republic and France that was designed to put pressure on England, his credibility as mediator was undermined. The biggest sea battle of the Second Anglo-Dutch War and in the age of sail was undoubtedly a Dutch victory although both sides initially claimed they had won. After this battle, while the Dutch fleet was being repaired an English squadron, entered the Vlie estuary and burned 150 out of a fleet of 160 merchant ships, inflicting severe economic damage on the Netherlands. England had gone to war in the expectation of an early victory that would not overstretch its government's fragile financial position, but both it and the Netherlands had put such unprecedented efforts into providing ships and men that no decisive naval victory was possible, and severe English financial difficulties and the Dutch need to resume unrestricted commercial activity created the conditions for peace without resolving all the underlying causes for the conflict. The Four Days' Battle is dramatized in the Dutch film Michiel de Ruyter (2015), although it is not clear which phase of the battle is shown." .

ns1:42886 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 8th Indie Series Awards were held on 05 April 2017 at The Colony Theatre in Los Angeles, with the ceremony hosted by EastSiders producers and stars Kit Williamson and John Halbach." .

ns1:42896 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2000 ANZ Tasmanian International was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts at the Hobart International Tennis Centre in Hobart in Australia that was part of WTA Tier IV tournaments of the 2000 WTA Tour. It was the seventh edition of the tournament and was held from 09 January 2000 until 15 January 2000." .

ns1:42904 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Ostrovno (French: Combat d'Astroŭna) was a military engagement that took place on 25 July 1812, between French forces under the command of King of Naples Joachim Murat and Russian forces under General Ostermann-Tolstoy and ended with the Russian forces retreating from the battlefield. With French forces moving through different routes in the direction of Vitebsk, a first major engagement took place on 25 July 1812 near Astroŭna, 30 kilometers west of Vitebsk, when General Etienne de Nansouty's 1st Cavalry Reserve Corps encountered the forces of Russian General Alexander Ivanovich Ostermann-Tolstoy." .

ns1:42919 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of the Ice (Russian: Ледовое побоище, Ledovoye poboishche; German: Schlacht auf dem Eise; Estonian: Jäälahing; German: Schlacht auf dem Peipussee) was fought between the Novgorod Republic led by Prince Alexander Nevsky and the forces of the Livonian Order and Bishopric of Dorpat led by Bishop Hermann of Dorpat on 05 April 1242, at Lake Peipus. The Crusaders' defeat in the battle marked the end of their campaigns against the Orthodox Novgorod Republic and other Slavic territories for the next century. Hoping to exploit Novgorod's weakness in the wake of the Mongol and Swedish invasions, the Teutonic Order attacked the neighboring Novgorod Republic and occupied Pskov, Izborsk, and Koporye in autumn 1240. The legacy of the battle, and its decisiveness, came because it halted the eastward expansion of the Teutonic Order and established a permanent border line through the Narva River and Lake Peipus dividing Eastern Orthodoxy from Western Catholicism. The movie, bearing propagandist allegories of the Teutonic Order as Nazi Germans, with the Teutonic infantry wearing modified World War I German Stahlhelm helmets, has created a popular image of the battle often mistaken for the real events. The kingdoms of Sweden, Denmark and the Teutonic Order built up an alliance in June 1238, under the auspices of Danish king Valdemar II." .

ns1:42938 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The unseeded pair of Piet Norval and Katarina Srebotnik won in the final over sixth seeds Rick Leach and Larisa Neiland, 6–3, 3–6, 6–3." .

ns1:42949 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 World Rowing Championships were World Rowing Championships that were held from 31 October 2010 to 07 November 2010 on Lake Karapiro near Cambridge, New Zealand. The annual week-long rowing regatta was organised by World Rowing Federation (the International Rowing Federation). The World Rowing Championships were previously held at Lake Karapiro in 1978. Rowing's international body said Lake Karapiro's 2010 World Rowing Championships raised the bar for the rest of the world and more international events would be held there. The event lost $2.2m and a report by SPARC (Sport And Recreation New Zealand, rebranded as Sport New Zealand in 2012) found that a lack of clarity around roles and responsibilities of the Karapiro 2010 Board was a factor, alongside others mostly relating to shortcomings in governance, inadequate financial management, and less revenue than expected from ticket sales. With the roaring success of the world championships behind them, Rowing New Zealand is eager to make further use of their world-class facility at Lake Karapiro." .

ns1:42950 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Perth was an Australian military operation in Urozgan undertaken in 01 July 2006 during the Afghanistan War." .

ns1:42967 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Whose Line is it Anyway? (abbreviated to Whose Line? or WLIIA) is a short-form improvisational comedy radio programme." .

ns1:42972 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Kilmacrennan was a skirmish fought near Kilmacrenan, County Donegal in 1608 during O'Doherty's Rebellion." .

ns1:42974 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Gap Fire was a fire that burned 9,443 acres (38.21 km2) of the Santa Ynez Mountains above Goleta, California between July 1 and July 28, 2008." .

ns1:42980 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2008 Egypt Cup Final, was the final match of 2007–08 Egypt Cup, when Zamalek SC played ENPPI Club at Cairo International Stadium in Cairo." .

ns1:42997 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Judicial Procedures Reform Bill of 1937, frequently called the \"court-packing plan\", was a legislative initiative proposed by U.S. President Franklin D. Roosevelt to add more justices to the U.S. Supreme Court in order to obtain favorable rulings regarding New Deal legislation that the Court had ruled unconstitutional. The bill came to be known as Roosevelt's \"court-packing plan,\" a phrase coined by Edward Rumely. The legislation was unveiled on 05 February 1937, and was the subject of Roosevelt's ninth Fireside chat on 1937. This reversal came to be known as \"the switch in time that saved nine\"; however, recent legal-historical scholarship has called that narrative into question as Roberts' decision and vote in the Parrish case predated both the public announcement and introduction of the 1937 bill. A series of Gallup Polls conducted between February and 1937 showed that the public opposed the proposed bill by a fluctuating majority. Reaction against the bill also spawned the National Committee to Uphold Constitutional Government, which was launched in 05 February 1937 by three leading opponents of the New Deal. The administration began making its case for the bill before the Senate Judiciary Committee on 1937." .

ns1:43005 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1997 NCAA Rowing Championships were contested at the first annual NCAA-sanctioned regatta to determine the team national champions of women's rowing in the United States of America during the. This year's regatta was held on 31 May 1997 at Lake Natoma near Rancho Cordova." .

ns1:43022 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Philippine Senate election was the 31st election to the Senate of the Philippines. The senators elected in 2010 Philippine Senate election will serve until June 30, 2016 Philippine Senate election, while the senators elected in this election will serve up to June 30, 2019. Team PNoy campaign manager and senator Franklin Drilon was elected Senate President upon the convening of the 16th Congress of the Philippines on late July, after emerging as the frontrunner in the Senate Presidency, at the expense of incumbent Juan Ponce Enrile of UNA. Poe broke the record of Bong Revilla in 2010 Philippine Senate election for the most votes received in any election in the Philippines, by winning more than 20 million votes. As early in April, Miriam Defensor Santiago said that the Senate President for the 16th Congress of the Philippines will come from Team PNoy, and that it will shall come from its oldest members; she tightened the contenders to Sergio Osmeña III, Franklin Drilon, TG Guingona and Cynthia Villar." .

ns1:43032 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "During the Siege of Mantua, which lasted from 4 July 1796 to 02 February 1797 with a short break, French forces under the overall command of Napoleon Bonaparte besieged and blockaded a large Austrian garrison at Mantua for many months until it surrendered. The siege occurred during the War of the First Coalition, which is part of the French Revolutionary Wars. Mantua, a city in the Lombardy region of Italy, lies on the Mincio River. After driving the Austrian army out of northwest and north-central Italy, the French invested the fortress of Mantua starting in early June 1796. Mantua was reached and the French were forced to abandon the siege. By requisitioning cannon from all over northern Italy, Bonaparte assembled a siege train of 179 heavy guns. Bonaparte, who had only 44,000 soldiers, posted his divisions in an arc to protect his siege of Mantua. Wurmser pushed Masséna back and Quosdanovich quickly seized Brescia, forcing Bonaparte to lift the siege on 01 August 1796." .

ns1:43045 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1986 Cerritos mid-air collision was a scheduled commercial flight from Mexico City, Mexico, to Los Angeles, California, United States, with several intermediate stops. On Sunday, 31 August 1986, the McDonnell Douglas DC-9 operating the flight was clipped in the tail section by N4891F, a Piper PA-28-181 Archer owned by the Kramer family, and crashed into the Los Angeles suburb of Cerritos, killing all 67 on both aircraft and an additional fifteen on the ground. On Sunday, 31 August 1986 at about 11:46 am PDT, Flight 498 began its descent into Los Angeles with 58 passengers and six crew members on board." .

ns1:43058 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Rock Avalanche was a six-day, US-led offensive from 19 25 October 2007–25 October 2007, with the purpose of hunting Taliban fighters in the Korangal Valley of Afghanistan. Operation Rock Avalanche began in the early-morning hours of 19 25 October 2007 when U.S. and Afghan forces boarded UH-60 and CH-47 helicopters at the Korengal Outpost (KOP) to be inserted in the southern portion of the Korangal Valley, near the village of Yaka Chine." .

ns1:43064 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Old Town Square execution (Czech: Staroměstská exekuce) was the execution of 27 Bohemian leaders (three noblemen, seven knights and 17 burghers) of the Bohemian Revolt by the Austrian House of Habsburg that took place on June 21, 1621 at the Old Town Square in Prague. The execution of the 27 leaders of the Bohemian Estates Uprising began on 21 June 1621 in Prague on the Old Town Square." .

ns1:43083 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1906 International Lawn Tennis Challenge was the sixth edition of what is now known as the Davis Cup. The \"World Group\" ties were played at the Rodney Parade in Newport, Monmouthshire, England from 07 June 1906–9 June, and the final was played at Worple Road (the former site of the All England Club) in Wimbledon, London, Englan15 Juneo18 June June." .

ns1:43091 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The November Storm of 1995 (Swedish: Novemberstormen 1995) was a snowstorm, coming from the North Sea in mid 17 November 1995. Beginning on Thursday evening, 16 17 November 1995, the storm culminated on Friday morning, 17 November 1995, striking against the southern and western parts of Sweden." .

ns1:43101 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Señorita Panamá 1999, the 17th Señorita Panamá pageant and 34th celebration of the Miss Panama contest, was held in Atlapa Convention Centre, Panama city, Panama, on 1999. About 12 contestants from all over Panama competed for the prestigious crown. At the conclusion of the final night of competition, outgoing titleholder Yamani Esther Saied Calviño of Panama Centro crowned Analía Núñez of Chiriquí as the new Señorita Panamá. Señorita Panamá 1998 Lorena del Carmen Zagía Miró of Panama Centro crowned Jessenia Casanova Reyes of Panama Centro as the new Señorita Panamá." .

ns1:43102 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Asian Junior Badminton Championships – men's singles tournament of the 2012 Asian Junior Badminton Championships was held from 03 July 2012–7 in Gimcheon, South Korea." .

ns1:43130 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1952 Tour de Suisse was the 16th edition of the Tour de Suisse cycle race and was held from 14 June 1952 to 21 June 1952." .

ns1:43132 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1918 Republic of China presidential election were the elections held on 04 September 1918 in Beijing for the second term of the President of the Republic of China of China." .

ns1:43150 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Fort Ticonderoga occurred between the 2nd and 6th of 06 July 1777 at Fort Ticonderoga, near the southern end of Lake Champlain in the state of New York. Some gunfire was exchanged, and there were some casualties, but there was no formal siege and no pitched battle. Burgoyne's army occupied Fort Ticonderoga and Mount Independence, the extensive fortifications on the Vermont side of the lake, without opposition on 06 July 1777." .

ns1:43168 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Los Angeles Open (tennis) was a former tennis tournament held in Los Angeles, United States from 1927 until 2012. The inaugural edition of the event, known as the Pacific Southwest Championships, was organized by Perry T. Jones and held at the Los Angeles Tennis Club (LATC) starting in 1927. The tournament quickly became a prestigious event on the tennis calendar. 1 tennis players: Bill Tilden, Ellsworth Vines, Don Budge, Fred Perry, Jack Kramer, Pancho Gonzales and amateur champions Roy Emerson and Barry MacKay. In the open era the event was known by various (sponsored) names including Farmers Classic, Countrywide Classic, Los Angeles Tennis Open, Pacific South West Open and Jack Kramer Open. Beginning in 1984, the tournament was held at the Los Angeles Tennis Center at UCLA, which was built to host the 1984 Summer Olympics tennis event. In its last years it was an ATP World Tour 250 series tournament on the Association of Tennis Professionals and had a 28-player singles draw and 16-team doubles draw. The tournament, with prize money of $557,550 in 2012, was one of the events included in the US Open Series. Colombian investors purchased the tournament's license for $1.5 million at the end of 2012 and moved the tournament to Bogotá." .

ns1:43203 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Electronic Entertainment Expo 2016 (E3 2016) was the 22nd Electronic Entertainment Expo, during which several hardware manufacturers and software developers and publishers from the video game industry presented new and upcoming products to the attendees, primarily retailers and members of the video game press. The event, organized by the Entertainment Software Association, took place at the Los Angeles Convention Center from June 14–16, 2016. For E3 2016, the ESA planned a separate three-day E3 Live event held from June 14–16, 2016 alongside the main E3 event for members of the public at L.A. Live, which neighbors the Convention Center." .

ns1:43205 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Who I Am Tour was the first headlining concert tour by American band Nick Jonas & the Administration, showcasing their debut album, Who I Am." .

ns1:43206 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Valor Ecclesiasticus (Latin: \"church valuation\") was a survey of the finances of the church in England, Wales and English controlled parts of Ireland made in 1535 on the orders of Henry VIII. In 1535, the government appointed commissions throughout the country to conduct the survey." .

ns1:43207 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Fort Gaines occurred between 03 August 1864 and 8, 1864, during the American Civil War. Union forces under the command of Maj. Gen. Gordon Granger landed on Dauphin Island, about 7 miles from Fort Gaines, on 03 August 1864, and moved against Fort Gaines guarding the western edge of Mobile Bay." .

ns1:43246 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1988 Tour of the Basque Country was the 28th edition of the Itzulia Basque Country cycle race and was held from 04 April 1988 to 08 April 1988." .

ns1:43249 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Sabha was a battle between forces loyal to Libya leader Muammar Gaddafi and rebel anti-Gaddafi forces for control of the desert oasis city of Sabha and a part of the Libya Civil War. Sabha is located by an oasis in the Libya Desert and is home to an important military base. On 19 September 2011, spokesman for the NTC Ministry of Defence, Col. Ahmed Bani, announced at a press conference that NTC fighters managed to capture Sabha airport and fort. On 21 September, the NTC announced that almost the whole of Sabha was under their control, with widespread defections from pro-Gaddafi elements helping to end the battle, and NTC forces were encountering only sporadic resistance from few individuals. On 22 September 2011 near Sabha, NTC forces discovered two warehouses containing thousands of blue barrels marked with tape saying \"radioactive\" and plastic bags of yellow powder sealed with the same tape. The International Atomic Energy Agency (IAEA) stated, \"We can confirm that there is yellowcake stored in drums at a site near Sabha ... which Libya previously declared to the IAEA." .

ns1:43254 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Sacking of Osceola was a Kansas Jayhawker initiative on 23 September 1861, to push out pro-slavery Southerners at Osceola, Missouri. The climax of the campaign was on 23 September 1861, at Osceola, where Lane's forces drove off a small Southern force and then looted and burned the town." .

ns1:43268 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Vrbanja bridge was an armed confrontation which occurred on 27 May 1995, between United Nations (UN) peacekeepers from the French Army, and elements of the Bosnian Serb Army of Republika Srpska (VRS). The fighting occurred at the Suada and Olga bridge crossing of the Miljacka river in Sarajevo, Bosnia and Herzegovina, during the Bosnian War. The Suada and Olga bridge was located in no-man's-land between the besieged Army of the Republic of Bosnia and Herzegovina (Armija Republike Bosne i Hercegovine, ARBiH) and the surrounding Army of Republika Srpska (Vojska Republike Srpske, VRS) during the 1992–1996 Siege of Sarajevo in Bosnia and Herzegovina. The VRS then seized 377 United Nations Protection Force (United Nations Protection Force) hostages and used them as human shields for a variety of potential NATO airstrike targets in Bosnia and Herzegovina, forcing NATO to end air operations against the VRS. On 27 May 1995 at 04:30, VRS soldiers posing as French troops captured the UN observation posts on both ends of the Suada and Olga bridge without firing a shot. The second French soldier to die in the battle, Private Marcel Amaru, was killed by a sniper while supporting the assault from Sarajevo's Jewish cemetery. According to the top French officers involved in the battle, the action at the Suada and Olga bridge showed the VRS that United Nations Protection Force's attitude had changed." .

ns1:43280 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Miss Universe Japan 2017 (Japanese: 2017 ミス・ユニバース・ジャパン), the Miss Universe Japan pageant, took place in the Hotel Chinzanso Tokyo, Bunkyō, Tokyo, Japan, on 2017. The winner represented Japan during Miss Universe 2017." .

ns1:43302 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Louis Vuitton Cup held in Valencia, Spain, from April 16 to June 6 was the event used to select the challenger for the 2007 America's Cup. The winner was Team New Zealand, who challenged the defender Alinghi in 2007 for the America's Cup. As winner of the round-robin events Team New Zealand won the right to choose their opponent in the semi-finals and chose Desafío Español 2007. In their previous meetings, Team New Zealand had a 2–0 record against Desafío Español 2007 with winning margins of 1:12 and 0:43. In their semi-final competition Team New Zealand always led at the 1st mark and beat Desafío Español 2007 5–2, advancing to the finals. The finals of the 2007 Louis Vuitton Cup pitted Luna Rossa Challenge against Team New Zealand. Both boats had decisive results in the semi-finals, where Luna Rossa Challenge defeated BMW Oracle Racing 5–1, and Team New Zealand defeated Desafío Español 2007 5–2. Team New Zealand won their race in round robin 2 on 2007 by 36 seconds." .

ns1:43307 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The men's long jump was an event at the athletics at the 1984 Summer Olympics – men's long jump in Los Angeles. There were 31 participating athletes from 25 nations, with two qualifying groups, and the final held on 06 August 1984." .

ns1:43312 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1898 Sheriff of London Charity Shield was the first edition of the Sheriff of London Charity Shield. The match and subsequent replay were both drawn and hence the honour was shared between Corinthian F.C. and Sheffield United F.C.. Sheffield United F.C. were league champions for the first time in their history in the 1897–98 Football League and Corinthian F.C. were the premier amateur side of the time providing many of the England national football team players." .

ns1:43317 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Panipat (1556) was fought on 05 November 1556, between the Hindu emperor of north India, Hemu, and the forces of the Mughal emperor Akbar. On 05 November 1556, the Mughal army met Hemu's army at the historic battlefield of Panipat. The spoils from the battle at Panipat included 120 of Hemu's war elephants whose destructive rampages so impressed the Mughals that the animals soon became an integral part of their military strategies." .

ns1:43324 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "He and his infant daughter Lynching of Julia and Frazier Baker died at his house after being fatally shot during a white mob attack on 22 February 1898. The lynching was defended by those who agreed with South Carolina Senator Benjamin Tillman, who said the \"proud people\" of Lake City refused to receive \"their mail from a nigger.\"" .

ns1:43336 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Asylum Tour was a concert tour by American heavy metal band Disturbed in support of the group's fifth studio album, Asylum, which was released in 13 August 2010." .

ns1:43357 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Sinope in 1214 was a successful siege and capture of Sinop by the Seljuq Turks under their Sultan, Kaykaus I (r. 1211–1220). Upon this, he and his beys gathered those who had been to Sinop and drew up a plan for the conquest, deciding that a long siege would be required. Although the primary sources consistently named the leader of the Trapezuntine forces as Alexios, beginning with Fallmerayer earlier scholars used to place the death of David Komnenos, Alexios' younger brother and co-founder of the Trapezuntine empire, during the siege of Sinop. But since the name of David never occurs in the sources after 1214, we may positively conclude that it was David who was slain at the first Turkish capture of Sinop.\" The Seljuq capture of Sinop had important consequences: apart from a short period of Trapezuntine recovery in 1254–1265, the city henceforth remained in Turkish hands, cutting the small Trapezuntine state off from overland contact with the metropolitan Byzantine lands of the Empire of Nicaea in western Anatolia." .

ns1:43363 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 8th Nuestra Belleza México 2001 pageant, was held at the Lic. Adolfo López Mateos International Airport of Toluca, Estado de México, Mexico on 2001. Forty-seven contestants of the Mexico competed for the national title, which was won by Ericka Cruz from Yucatán, who later competed in Miss Universe 2002 in Puerto Rico. The Mexicana Universal title was won by Tatiana Rodríguez from Campeche, who later competed in Miss World 2001 in South Africa. For the second consecutive time and for the four time in the history of the pageant, two events were held separately to select the two winners for the titles Nuestra Belleza México 2001 and Mexicana Universal. One week before the final competition was held the semi-final competition with a live show entitled \"Nuestra Belleza Mundo Mexico\" in which was announced the winner of the Mexicana Universal title Tatiana Rodríguez from Campeche who represented the country in Miss World 2001. The Mexicana Universal pageant was held at the Lic. Adolfo López Mateos International Airport of Toluca, Estado de México and was hosted by Mónica Noguera and Julio Bracho. It was the 4th edition of the \"Mexicana Universal\" contest and as an official separate pageant to choose Mexico's representative to Miss World." .

ns1:43371 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1585 Siege of Ōta Castle (太田城の戦い, Ōta Jō no Tatakai) was one of a series of assaults made by Toyotomi Hideyoshi against the Ikkō-ikki religious zealots towards the end of Japan's Sengoku period. This battle followed the siege of Negoro-ji, in which Hideyoshi's forces burnt a temple complex to the ground; a number of the Saiga Ikki zealots escaped to nearby Ōta Castle, which was held by Ōta Munemasa." .

ns1:43375 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Taça da Liga Final was the final match of the 2014–15 Taça da Liga, the eighth season of the Taça da Liga. Trophy holders S.L. Benfica beat C.S. Marítimo (2–1) to win a record sixth title in the competition. Goal-line technology was introduced in the final, for the first time in Portugal." .

ns1:43383 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Nordic Figure Skating Championships was held from 26 February 2014 to 02 March 2014 at the Upplands Bilforum Arena in Uppsala, Sweden." .

ns1:43404 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Murder of Claire Tiltman was a 16-year-old girl who was murdered on 18 January 1993 in Greenhithe, Kent, England. Murder of Claire Tiltman, a Year 11 pupil at Dartford Grammar School for Girls, took a short cut down an alleyway on her way to meet a friend near her home in Greenhithe. The confession proved to be crucial to bringing him to justice for the murder of Murder of Claire Tiltman. Ash-Smith was already a known violent offender, and was first identified as a suspect in the murder of Murder of Claire Tiltman almost 20 years before he was finally convicted." .

ns1:43412 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Malaysian motorcycle Grand Prix was the eighteenth round of the 2018 MotoGP season. It was held at the Sepang International Circuit in Sepang on 04 November 2018." .

ns1:43421 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "FC Term gained promotion to the 2018–19 Albanian Second Division." .

ns1:43426 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1979 Imperial Valley earthquake occurred at 16:16 Pacific Daylight Time (23:16 UTC) on 15 October 1979 just south of the Mexico–United States border. The Imperial Valley is surrounded by a number of interconnected fault systems and is vulnerable to both moderate and strong earthquakes as well as earthquake swarms. The pattern of displacement along the Imperial Fault was very similar to that observed for the northern part of the rupture during the 1940 El Centro earthquake, although on this occasion the rupture did not extend across the border into Mexico. The United States Geological Survey operates a series of strong motion stations in the Imperial Valley and while the majority of stations in the array recorded ground accelerations that were not unexpected, station number six registered an unusually high vertical component reading of 1.74g which, at the time, was the highest yet recorded as the result of an earthquake. The earthquake caused damage to the Californian cities of El Centro and Brawley, and in the Mexican city of Mexicali Mexico. The earthquake shaking also led to extensive damage to the irrigation systems of the Imperial Valley, leading to breaches in some canals, particularly the All-American Canal that brings water to the valley from the Colorado River. Slumping of the foundations there resulted in severe damage, and occurred as a result of the 15 October 1979 main shock, though the Brawley earthquake's epicenter was nearby." .

ns1:43447 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2002 European Grand Prix (formally the XLVI Allianz Grand Prix of Europe) was a Formula One motor race held on 23 June 2002 at the Nürburgring, Nurburg, Rhineland-Palatinate, Germany. Going into the race, Ferrari driver Michael Schumacher led the Drivers' Championship with 70 points, ahead of Williams teammates Ralf Schumacher and Juan Pablo Montoya who were tied for second place on 27 points. Michael Schumacher had won the previous two European Grands Prix held at the Nürburgring and wanted to concentrate on the potential challenge from the Williams and McLaren teams at the race rather than worry about a possible disqualification from the Austrian Grand Prix where he was given victory after Barrichello was ordered by Ferrari to allow Schumacher past. After the previous race held at the Nürburgring (the 2001 European Grand Prix) the layout of the circuit was changed, its first major change since it was reopened in 1984." .

ns1:43452 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Suva Reka massacre (Albanian: Masakra e Suharekës, Serbian: Masakr u Suvoj Reci) refers to the mass murder of Kosovo Albanian civilians committed by Serbian police officers on 26 March 1999 in Suva Reka, Kosovo, during the 1999 NATO bombings of Yugoslavia. There were 48 victims of the Suva Reka massacre, including fourteen who were under 15 years old." .

ns1:43465 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 UCI World Tour was the sixth edition of the ranking system launched by the Union Cycliste Internationale (UCI) in 2009. The series started with the opening stage of the 2014 Tour Down Under on 21 January, and concluded with the final stage of the 2014 Tour of Beijing on 14 October. The Movistar Team rider finished 66 points clear of his closest rival and compatriot Alberto Contador of Tinkoff–Saxo, while Australian rider Simon Gerrans was third for the Orica–GreenEDGE team, but was over 200 points in arrears of Valverde." .

ns1:43468 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The squash at the 1997 World Games competition at the World Games 1997 took place from 16 August to 1997, in Lahti in Finland." .

ns1:43476 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1902 Scottish Cup Final was played on 26 April 1902 at Celtic Park in Glasgow and was the deciding match of the 29th season of the Scottish Cup. This meant that the final was delayed by two weeks and moved to Celtic Park, although Celtic F.C. were one of the finalists. Hibernian F.C. and Celtic F.C. contested the match. Hibernian F.C. won the match 1–0, by the 75th-minute goal from Andy McGeachan." .

ns1:43499 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Indian Open was a professional ranking snooker tournament that took place between 10 March 2015–14 March 2015 at the Grand Hyatt Mumbai in Mumbai, India. The breakdown of prize money for this year is shown below: These matches were played in Mumbai on 10 March 2015." .

ns1:43524 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2019 World Men's Curling Championship (branded as the 2019 Pioneer Hi-Bred World Curling Championships for sponsorship reasons) was held from March 30 to 07 April 2019 at the ENMAX Centre in Lethbridge, Alberta, Canada." .

ns1:43560 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "CBS News Sunday Morning (also simply titled Sunday Morning) is an United States of America news magazine television program that has aired on CBS since 28 January 1979. Since 2016, the show has been hosted by Jane Pauley, who also hosts news segments, after the retirement of Charles Osgood. CBS News correspondent Steve Hartman periodically revisits some of Charles Kuralt's memorable personal profiles." .

ns1:43578 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Gura was fought on March 7–9, 1876 between the Ethiopian Empire and the Khedivate of Egypt near the town of Gura, Eritrea in Eritrea. It was the decisive battle of the Ethiopian–Egyptian War." .

ns1:43605 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Böhmermann affair (also known as Erdogate) was a political affair following an experimental poem on German satirist Jan Böhmermann's satire show Neo Magazin Royale in late March 2016 that deliberately insulted Turkish president Recep Tayyip Erdoğan using profane language. Days after a music video titled \"Erdowie, Erdowo, Recep Tayyip Erdoğan\" in another German satire show had infuriated Recep Tayyip Erdoğan, prompting Ankara to summon the German ambassador, Böhmermann went on to find the line between satire, which is protected by freedom of speech legislation, and \"abusive criticism\" (German: Schmähkritik) of a foreign state leader (Lèse-majesté), which was still a punishable offense in Germany at that time. \"Discussions about and criticism of Jan Böhmermann's Recep Tayyip Erdoğan poem belong in the newspaper arts sections of the country and not in a courtroom in Mainz\", they wrote." .

ns1:43614 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1980 Turkish coup d'état (Turkish: 12 Eylül Darbesi), headed by Chief of the General Staff General Kenan Evren, was the third coup d'état in the history of the Republic, the previous having been the 1960 coup and the 1971 coup by memorandum. On 12 September 1980, the National Security Council (Turkish: Milli Güvenlik Konseyi, MGK), headed by Evren declared coup d'état on the national channel." .

ns1:43626 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Deal barracks bombing was an attack by the Provisional Irish Republican Army (IRA) on the Royal Marine Depot, Deal, England. The building collapsed, killing 11 marines from the Royal Marines Band Service and wounding another 21. Originally created at Portsmouth in 1930, it moved to Deal in 1950 and in 1989 was still there as part of the Royal Marine Depot, Deal. Another 21 were seriously injured and received treatment at hospitals in Dover, Deal and Canterbury. Deal Parochial Church of England School now stands in the former barracks' Drill Fields." .

ns1:43641 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Montgisard was fought between the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the Ayyubid dynasty on 25 November 1177 at Montgisard, in the Levant (present day Israel) between Ramla and Yibna. Meanwhile, Saladin was planning his own invasion of the Kingdom of Jerusalem from Ayyubid dynasty. Saladin's chronicler Imad ad-Din al-Isfahani refers to the battle taking place by the mound of Al-Safiya, potentially modern Tell es-Safi near the village of Menehem, not far from Ashkelon, and within the contemporary Ramla province." .

ns1:43642 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "SoFlo Superflat describes an art genre started in Miami in the 1990s. It is an outcrop of the Japanese Superflat movement, founded by the artist Takashi Murakami." .

ns1:43655 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Cumae was a naval battle in 474 BCE between the combined navies of Syracuse and Cumae against the Etruscans. In 474, they met and defeated the Etruscan fleet at Cumae in the Gulf of Naples." .

ns1:43657 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "In 1114, an 1113–1115 Balearic Islands expedition, then a Muslim taifa, was launched in the form of a Crusades. Founded on a treaty of 1113 between the Republic of Pisa and Ramon Berenguer III, Count of Barcelona, the expedition had the support of Pope Paschal II and the participation of many lords of Catalonia and Occitania, as well as contingents from northern and central Italy, Sardinia, and Corsica." .

ns1:43678 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Festival Internacional de Benicàssim (Valencian pronunciation: ), commonly abbreviated to simply FIB, is an annual music festival which takes place in the town of Benicàssim, in the Valencian Community (Spain). The first 'FIB' took place in 1995, and since then Benicàssim has been dedicated to rock, pop and electronic music as well as featuring artists who are a reference on the world stage. 'FIB' is held in the seaside resort of Benicàssim (Castellón province) on Costa del Azahar, Valencia, Spain, 92.2 km away from Valencia Airport. FIB has received criticism from nationalist sectors in Spain, aimed at the fact that it does not support Valencian culture, even though it takes place in the Valencian Community and receives a high amount of public money to finance itself." .

ns1:43696 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Google Cloud Storage is a RESTful online file storage web service for storing and accessing data on Google Cloud Platform infrastructure. Contrary to Google Drive and according to different service specifications, Google Cloud Storage appears to be more suitable for enterprises." .

ns1:43704 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 2013 Indonesia Open Grand Prix Gold was the tenth grand prix gold and grand prix tournament of the 2013 BWF Grand Prix Gold and Grand Prix. The tournament was held in Among Rogo Sports Hall, Yogyakarta City, Indonesia 24 September 2013 until 29 September 2013 and had a total purse of $120,000." .

ns1:43710 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Thrividha Balaya was a combined military operation, the first of its kind, launched by the Sri Lankan Military in Jaffna. The Operation was carried out to break the siege of the Sri Lanka Army garrison of the old Dutch Jaffna Fort in Jaffna. While a causeway linked Mandaitivu with the Jaffna peninsula as the causeway could be heavily mined Fiberglass Dinghies were to be used despite the navy at that time lacking any experience in major amphibious assaults using small boats under high intensity fire.The operation to transport troops across the lagoon commenced in the early hours of 13 September 1990 with use of Sri Lanka Navy Fibre Glass Dinghies (FGD) under heavy LTTE fire." .

ns1:43715 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Ramsar Ramsar Convention of International Importance Especially as Waterfowl Habitat is an international treaty for the conservation and sustainable use of wetlands. Martha Rojas Urrego is the sixth secretary general of the Ramsar Ramsar Convention. The conference which adopted the terms of the agreement was held in the Iranian Caspian Sea resort of Ramsar on 02 February 1971." .

ns1:43717 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1894 San Juan earthquake took place in the province of San Juan Province, Argentina, on 27 October 1894, at about 07:30 PM." .

ns1:43718 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2002 Skate America was the first event of six in the 2002–03 ISU Grand Prix of Figure Skating, a senior-level international invitational competition series. It was held at the Spokane Veterans Memorial Arena in Spokane, Washington on 23 October 2002–27." .

ns1:43722 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Iconium (sometimes referred as the Battle of Konya) took place on 18 May 1190 during the Third Crusade, in the expedition of Frederick Barbarossa to the Holy Land. As a result, Konya, the capital city of the Sultanate of Rûm under Kilij Arslan II, fell to the Holy Roman Empire forces. After passing through todays Hungary, Serbia, Bulgaria and the Byzantine Empire, the forces arrived in Anatolia, held by the Sultanate of Rum. The Turks lost 3,000 killed at the field battle on 18 May 1190 according to the History of the Expedition of the Emperor Frederick (Historia de Expeditione Friderici Imperatoris), a contemporary German chronicle relying on eyewitness accounts from the participating Crusaders and completed by an Austrian cleric called Ansbert no later than 1200." .

ns1:43743 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Australasian Performing Right Association Awards of 2018 (generally known as APRA Awards) are a series of related awards which include the APRA Music Awards of 2018, Art Music Awards, and Screen Music Awards. The APRA Music Awards of 2018 was the 36th annual ceremony by the Australasian Performing Right Association (APRA) and the Australasian Performing Right Association (AMCOS) to award outstanding achievements in contemporary songwriting, composing and publishing." .

ns1:43745 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Webb Farm disaster was an industrial disaster that occurred on 27 May 1983, on a farm near Benton. Between December 1982 and the date of the explosion, a total of 1,542,880 M-series fireworks were reported to have been manufactured at the factory and distributed to at least twelve states. On 27 May 1983, at approximately 9:15 AM, eleven workers were on duty when a cache of M-80 and M-100 explosives, flash powder, and other chemicals in the barn detonated, producing a powerful explosion and instantly killing all eleven workers and leveling the barn." .

ns1:43781 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 Viva World Cup was the second Viva World Cup, an international tournament for association football, that took place in 2008. The competition, organized by the Sami people, took place from 7 to 13 July, in Gällivare, Sweden. The men's teams that took part were: The women's teams that took part were: As a small tournament, like its European counterpart in Europeada 2008, this Viva World Cup was not expected to bring in the numbers and financial support of UEFA Euro 2008 earlier this summer; the stadia venues are thus rather small. The hosts of the games were: Padania became the 2008 Viva World Cup champions beating Arameans Suryoye in the final 2-0." .

ns1:43808 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1909 Maltese general election were held in Malta on 11 October 1909 and 12 October 1909." .

ns1:43810 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The On My Way Home Tour was the fourth headlining concert tour by American a cappella group Pentatonix to promote their EP PTX, Vol. III. On December 7, 2014, Pentatonix announced the On My Way Home Tour." .

ns1:43821 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Battle of Camas Creek, 20 August 1877, was a raid by the Nez Perce Indians on a U.S. Army encampment and a subsequent battle during the Nez Perce War." .

ns1:43834 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Six Nations Championship, known as the 2015 RBS 6 Nations because of the tournament's sponsorship by The Royal Bank of Scotland national rugby union team, was the 16th series of the Six Nations Championship, the annual rugby union tournament. It was contested by England national rugby union team, France national rugby union team, Ireland national rugby union team, Italy national rugby union team, Scotland national rugby union team and Wales national rugby union team. Ireland national rugby union team retained their title from 2014 Six Nations Championship, their 13th triumph in the competition. This was the first time that Ireland national rugby union team had retained their title outright since 1949, having shared the 1983 championship with France national rugby union team after winning in 1982. * Except the final match at home to Wales national rugby union team, when he was ruled out after an injury sustained in the round four match against France national rugby union team. The 2015 Six Nations Championship commenced with a Friday night fixture, once again held at the Millennium Stadium in Cardiff, this time between Wales national rugby union team and England national rugby union team. Notes: Notes: Notes: Notes: Notes: Notes: Notes: Notes: Notes: Notes: Notes: Notes: Notes: In the United Kingdom, BBC One televised all the matches live, although for viewers in Scotland national rugby union team the week 4 match between Italy national rugby union team and France national rugby union team was shown on BBC Two Scotland national rugby union team. BBC Two also televised live the buildup of the opening match between Wales national rugby union team and England national rugby union team and after the match, an extra between the two sides on BBC Two but only for viewers in Wales national rugby union team. On the morning of the final week of matches, BBC Two televised classic Five Nations matches including Scotland national rugby union team vs Ireland national rugby union team in 1991 and France national rugby union team vs England national rugby union team in 1992. The final match of the tournament between England national rugby union team and France national rugby union team was watched live on BBC One by 9.63 million people, beating the previous record for that fixture of 9.56 million, set in 2011. This followed audiences of 4.1 million for the match between Italy national rugby union team and Wales national rugby union team and 5.1 million for the match between Scotland national rugby union team and Ireland national rugby union team earlier in the day." .

ns1:43853 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Bjelovar Barracks, also known by the codename Operation Bilogora (Croatian: Operacija Bilogora), was the blockade and capture of the Yugoslav People's Army (JNA) barracks and other facilities in and around the city of Bjelovar, a part of the JNA 32nd (Varaždin) Corps, during the Croatian War of Independence. A general blockade of the JNA facilities in Croatia was ordered on 14 September 1991, and it continued until 29 September 1991 when the JNA garrison was captured by Croatian forces. It was part of the Battle of the Barracks—an effort by Croatian armed forces to isolate JNA units based at barracks in Croatia, or capture the barracks to provide arms for Croatia's nascent army. Since 1988, the JNA's Bjelovar garrison had been included in the 32nd Corps, which was headquartered in Varaždin, and was the second largest JNA corps in Croatia. Croatia established a company-sized special police unit in Bjelovar on 23 February 1991. The first significant conflict involving the JNA in the Bjelovar area occurred on 1 September, when 14 JNA officers and soldiers were disarmed at a Croatian checkpoint. Preparations to seize the JNA facilities in Bjelovar took place on 21 September–29 September 1991. The crisis headquarters was to coordinate all activities of the Croatian armed forces based in Bjelovar, as well as reinforcements received from Varaždin after the JNA garrison based there surrendered, but Colonel Želimir Škarec, a member of the General Staff of the Armed Forces of the Republic of Croatia, was appointed as the commanding officer of the military operation. Despite the ceasefire agreement signed on 22 September between the JNA and Croatia, which provided for the resumption of supplies to the JNA barracks, the authorities in Bjelovar refused to restore utilities, claiming that the agreement allowed for the supply of JNA officers and soldiers only, but there were also civilians sheltering in the barracks. On the morning of 29 September 1991, the ZNG and Croatian police attacked the JNA facilities in Bjelovar. The 5th Military District instead pressured the central Croatian authorities to order the ZNG in Bjelovar to observe a comprehensive ceasefire previously agreed between Croatia and the JNA on 22 September. The JNA suffered 14 killed, and 30 wounded during the siege and capture of the Bjelovar barracks. The capture of the JNA barracks in Bjelovar also affected the ceasefire agreement reached between the JNA and Croatia in Igalo, specifically a provision regarding the lifting of the blockade of the JNA barracks there. According to the charges filed by the County Court of Bjelovar, Šimić or several persons directly commanded by him killed Kovačević and two other JNA officers after they surrendered on 29 September 1991." .

ns1:43879 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017–18 Euro Hockey League was the eleventh season of the Euro Hockey League, Europe's premier club field hockey tournament. A total of 24 teams from 12 of the 45 European Hockey Federation member associations participate in the 2018–19 Euro Hockey League." .

ns1:43885 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Dongnae was a siege that occurred on 15 April 1592 (Gregorian: May 25, 1592) during the Japanese invasions of Korea. After the fall of Busan, the Japanese army had to secure their bridgehead, because ten kilometres to the north of Busan lay the fortress of Dongnae District, a certain type of castle that was situated within a very strong position atop a hill, that dominates the main road north towards Hanseong. Dongnae District was a walled citadel on a hilltop commanded by the 41-year-old prefect, Song Sang-hyeon, and the castle was packed with over 20,000 soldiers, poorly trained and equipped conscripts, and panicked civilian refugees from Busan and surrounding areas. After resting overnight at Busan, the First Division left at 6:00 AM the following morning, marched around the bay without delay, and arrived at Dongnae District around 8 AM two hours later. Gyeongsang Right Navy Commander Won Gyun was in a state of panic on learning of the fall of Busan and Dongnae District and the self-destruction of the Gyeongsang Left Navy fleet by Commander Bak Hong. Busan and Dongnae District fortress were quickly garrisoned, and the harbor of Busan began to provide a safe and almost unchallenged landing stage to disembark more than 100,000 Japanese soldiers with their equipment, horses, and supplies over the next month." .

ns1:43913 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Miss Russia 2017 was the 25th Miss Russia pageant, and was held in the concert hall Barvikha Luxury Village in Moscow on 2017. The pageant was hosted by Miss Russia Victoria Lopyreva and Maxim Privalov, and featured guest performances from Elka, Stas Piekha, Nyusha, Ani Lorak, and Dmitry Malikov." .

ns1:43922 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1924 British Lions tour to South Africa was the tenth tour by a British Isles team and the fifth to South Africa. Led by England's Ronald Cove-Smith and managed by former Wales international Harry Packer, the tour took in 21 matches." .

ns1:43933 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of El Brazito took place on 25 December 1846 between the United States Army and the Mexican Army during the Mexican-American War. En route to Chihuahua, Doniphan's regiment was attacked by a Mexican army about thirty miles from El Paso del Norte, about 9 miles south of Las Cruces, New Mexico, at Brazito on the Rio Grande Since it was Christmas, Doniphan had halted his men's march at 1 PM that day However, they spotted the dust cloud of a Mexican scouting party to the south and Colonel Doniphan promptly ordered his men to prepare for battle." .

ns1:43950 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013–14 Serie B (known as the Serie B Eurobet for sponsorship reasons) was the 82nd season since its establishment in 1929. A total of 22 teams contested the league: 15 of which were returning from the 2012–13 Serie B, 4 of which were promoted from Lega Pro Prima Divisione, and three relegated from Serie A." .

ns1:43952 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Jallikattu (or sallikkattu), also known as eru thazhuvuthal and mañcuvirattu, is a disputed traditional event in which a bull (Bos indicus), such as the Pulikulam or Kangayam breeds, is released into a crowd of people, and multiple human participants attempt to grab the large hump on the bull's back with both arms and hang on to it while the bull attempts to escape. Jallikattu is typically practised in the Indian state of Tamil Nadu as a part of Pongal celebrations on Mattu Pongal day, which occurs annually in January. Jallikattu has been known to be practiced during the Tamil classical period (400-100 BCE). With the introduction of the Regulation of Jallikattu Act, 2009, by the Tamil Nadu legislature, the following activities were done in preparation of the event: Between 2008 and 2014, 43 humans and 4 bulls were killed in the jallikattu events. On 27 November 2010, the Supreme Court permitted the Government of Tamil Nadu to allow jallikattu for five months in a year and directed the District Collectors to make sure that the animals that participate in jallikattu are registered to the Animal Welfare Board and in return the Board would send its representative to monitor the event. The Government of Tamil Nadu ordered that ₹2 lakh (US$2,800) be deposited by the organizers in case of an accident or injury during the event and enacted a rule to allow a team of veterinarians be present at the venue for certifying the bulls for participation in the event and to provide treatment for bulls that get injured. The Ministry of Environment and Forests issued a notification in 2011 that banned the use of bulls as performing animals, thereby banning the event But the practice continued to be held under Tamil Nadu Regulation of Jallikattu Act No 27 of 2009. However, on 14 January 2016, the Supreme Court of India issued a stay on this order, upholding the ban, after a petition filed by the Animal Welfare Board of India and PETA India, leading to protests all over Tamil Nadu. After hearing the petitions which were led by the Animal Welfare Board of India challenging central government's notification, the Supreme Court of India on 12 January ordered a stay, issued notices to the central government and the Tamil Nadu government and later refused to lift the stay. The Jallikattu Premier League is a professional league in Tamil Nadu for jallikattu. The league was announced on 24 February 2018, to be organized in Chennai by the Tamil Nadu Jallikattu Peravai and the Chennai Jallikattu Amaippu. Jallikattu is often shown in Tamil cinema where the hero tames the bull to prove his gallantry." .

ns1:43955 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Hungerford massacre was a series of random shootings in Hungerford, England, United Kingdom on 19 August 1987, when 27-year-old Michael Robert Ryan, an unemployed antiques dealer and handyman, shot dead 16 people, including an unarmed police officer and his own mother, before shooting himself. The first shooting occurred seven miles (11 km) to the west of Hungerford in Savernake Forest in Wiltshire, at 12:30 in the afternoon of 19 August 1987. The dead were: Hungerford was policed by two sergeants and twelve constables, and on the morning of 19 August 1987 the duty cover for the section consisted of one sergeant, two patrol constables and one station duty officer. The Hungerford massacre remains, along with the 1996 Dunblane school massacre, and the 2010 Cumbria shootings, one of the worst criminal atrocities involving firearms to occur in the United Kingdom." .

ns1:43957 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "In 2004 the Israeli Defense Forces launched Operation Days of Penitence (Hebrew: מבצע ימי תשובה), otherwise known as Operation \"Days of Repentance\" in the northern Gaza Strip. The operation lasted between 29 September 2004 and 16 October 2004." .

ns1:43963 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Bolivarian Games (Spanish: Juegos Bolivarianos), officially the XVII Bolivarian Games, was a major international multi-sport event that was held from November 16–30, 2013, in Trujillo, Peru, with some events held in Lima and Chiclayo. These Games was the third Bolivarian Games that was hosted by Peru. Previously, Peru hosted the 1947–48 Bolivarian Games and the 1997 Bolivarian Games. Since 2011, Trujillo was preparing the sport buildings for the Bolivarian Games. Early 2011, Trujillo began to bid, to replace Panama City, as host of the 2013 Bolivarian Games. The review found that Trujillo is capable of hosting the Games and unofficially stated that the city will host the 2013 Bolivarian Games." .

ns1:43981 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1963 UCI Road World Championships took place on 11 August 1963 in Ronse, Belgium." .

ns1:43983 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Tropical Storm Vamei (also known as Typhoon Vamei) was a Pacific tropical cyclone that formed closer to the equator than any other tropical cyclone on record. The last storm of the 2001 Pacific typhoon season, Vamei developed on 26 December 2001 at 1.4° N in the South China Sea. At 0600 UTC, the JMA first classified the system as Tropical Storm Vamei, about 65 km (40 mi) northeast of Singapore, and the agency estimated the storm attained peak winds of 85 km/h (50 mph) at the same time." .

ns1:44000 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The UCI Road World Championships – women's time trial at the 2001 UCI Road World Championships – Women's time trial took place over a distance of 19.2 kilometres (11.9 miles) in Lisbon, Portugal on 10 October 2001." .

ns1:44013 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The perpetrator, 29-year-old Richard Russell, was a Horizon Air ground service agent with no piloting experience. It described the perpetrator, identified as 29-year-old Richard Russell, as suicidal and said his actions did not constitute a \"terrorist incident\"." .

ns1:44015 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Indonesia Masters, officially the Daihatsu Indonesia Masters 2018, was a badminton tournament which took place at Istora Gelora Bung Karno in Jakarta, Indonesia, from 23 to 28 January 2018 and had a total prize of US$350,000. The 2018 Indonesia Masters was the third tournament of the 2018 BWF World Tour and also part of the Indonesia Masters championships which had been held since 2010. This international tournament was held at Istora Gelora Bung Karno in Jakarta, Indonesia." .

ns1:44016 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "United Nations General Assembly Resolution 3236, adopted by the 29th Session of the General Assembly on 22 November 1974 recognizes the Palestinian people's right to self-determination, officializes United Nations contact with the Palestine Liberation Organization and added the \"Question of Palestine\" to the U.N." .

ns1:44032 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Penco, on 12 March 1550 was a battle between 60,000 Mapuche under the command of their toqui Ainavillo with his Araucan and Tucapel allies and Pedro de Valdivia's 200 Spaniards on horse and afoot with many yanakuna including 300 Mapochoes auxiliaries under their leader Michimalonco, defending their newly raised fort at Penco. On 12 March 1550, Ainavillo's army of sixty thousand warriors advanced against the fort at Penco in three separate bodies with 5,000 skirmishers covering their advance and deployment." .

ns1:44041 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 OFC Champions League was the 16th edition of the Oceanian Club Championship, Oceania's premier club association football tournament organized by the Oceania Football Confederation (Oceania Football Confederation), and the 11th season under the current OFC Champions League name. In the 2017 OFC Champions League Final, Auckland City FC defeated Team Wellington 5–0 on aggregate and won the OFC Champions League seven years in a row and nine times in total, and qualified as the Oceania Football Confederation representative at the 2017 FIFA Club World Cup in the United Arab Emirates. The Oceania Football Confederation decided to expand the tournament and change the format for the 2017 edition: A total of 18 teams from all 11 Oceania Football Confederation member associations entered the competition. The draw of the qualifying stage and group stage was held on 24 August 2016, 12:30 NZST (UTC+12), at the Oceania Football Confederation Headquarters in Auckland, New Zealand. Matches were played between 28 January 2017 – 2017 in Nukuʻalofa, Tonga. The draw for the knockout stage was held on 2017, 11:30 NZDT (UTC+13), at the Oceania Football Confederation Headquarters in Auckland, New Zealand, to decide the matchups and the order of legs of the semi-finals, and the order of legs of the 2017 OFC Champions League Final. Matches were30 April 2017on th7 06 May 2017 and 2017." .

ns1:44042 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1987–88 Cypriot First Division was the 49th season of the Cypriot top-level association football league. The champions ensured their participation in the 1988–89 European Cup and the runners-up in the 1988–89 UEFA Cup. They were replaced by the first two teams of the 1986–87 Cypriot Second Division, APEP F.C. and Anagennisi Deryneia FC." .

ns1:44043 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Vaikal occurred in 10 April 1465 in the valley of Vaikal, southeastern Albania. A new Ottoman Empire sanjakbey of the Sanjak of Ohrid, Ballaban Badera (of Albanian origin), was sent by Sultan Mehmed II to inflict heavy losses on the Albanian forces. The Sultan Mehmed II gave Ballaban Badera, who was a sanjakbey of the Sanjak of Ohrid, the reins of the Ottoman Empire campaigns in Albania. A bloody battle soon followed with heavy casualties on both sides, but the Albanians managed to hold their ground and soon, the frightened Ottoman Empire soldiers began to flee. These men were overcome by the heat of the battle and did not follow Skanderbeg's orders to stay below the hills and were captured by the reformed Ottoman Empire forces." .

ns1:44049 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2011 FIBA Africa Championship was the 26th FIBA Africa Championship, played under the auspices of the Fédération Internationale de Basketball, the basketball sport governing body, and the African zone thereof. However, in 2011 Madagascar was chosen as host replacement due to a political crisis in Côte d'Ivoire. On 2011, it was announced that Madagascar would host the tournament in its capital Antananarivo after the political unrest in Côte d'Ivoire put them irreparably behind schedule in outfitting their arenas. These teams included the host nation, the top four sides at the FIBA Africa Championship 2009 in Libya (including Côte d'Ivoire, which finished second at the 2009 tournament) and the top twelve sides at the 2009 Zone preliminary basketball competitions." .

ns1:44051 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1954 Taça de Portugal Final was the final match of the 1953–54 Taça de Portugal, the 14th season of the Taça de Portugal, the premier Portuguese association football cup competition organized by the Portuguese Football Federation (FPF). The match was played on 27 June 1954 at the Estádio Nacional in Oeiras, and opposed two Primeira Liga sides: Sporting CP and Vitória F.C.. Sporting CP defeated Vitória F.C. 3–2 to claim their fifth Taça de Portugal." .

ns1:44076 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Zealand Point was a naval battle of the English Wars and the Gunboat War. The Danish ship of the line HDMS Prinds Christian Frederik was stationed in Kristiansand, Norway from 7 August 1807, patrolling waters between Norway and Denmark where Britain had imposed a blockade." .

ns1:44090 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Eurovision Young Dancers 1985 was the first edition of the Eurovision Young Dancers, held at Teatro Municipale in Reggio nell’Emilia, Italy on 16 June 1985. Teatro Municipale (also since the 1980s called the Teatro Municipale Valli due to being named after the actor Romolo Valli), a theatre in Reggio nell’Emilia, Northern Italy was the host venue for the 1985 edition of the Eurovision Young Dancers." .

ns1:44095 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2001 Tokyo Sevens was an international rugby sevens tournament that was part of the World Sevens Series in the 2000–01 World Sevens Series season. It was the Japan Sevens leg held at Chichibunomiya Rugby Stadium in Tokyo on 2001–2001." .

ns1:44112 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Hokuetsu (北越戦争, Hokuetsu senso) was a battle of the Boshin War of the Meiji Restoration, which occurred in 1868 in the northwestern part of Japan, in the area of modern Niigata Prefecture. Makino Tadakuni, the daimyō of Nagaoka, in Echigo Province (modern day Niigata Prefecture) was a supporter of the Tokugawa shogunate, and refused to submit to the new government even after the fall of Edo Castle to the imperial armies. After an unsuccessful effort at stopping the progress of the Imperial armies at the Battle of Bonari Pass, the next key battle in the Boshin War was the Battle of Aizu." .

ns1:44122 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1997 Danamon Open was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts at the Gelora Senayan Stadium in Jakarta, Indonesia and was part of the Tier IV category of the 1997 WTA Tour. It was the fifth and last edition of the tournament and was held from 22 April 1997 through 27 April 1997." .

ns1:44134 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1987 Portuguese legislative election took place on 19 July 1987. For these elections, and compared with the 1985 elections, the MPs distributed by districts were the following: The table below lists the parties represented in the Assembly of the Republic during the 4th legislature (1985-1987) and that also partook in the election: No debates between the main parties were held as the PSD leader and Prime Minister, Aníbal Cavaco Silva, refused to take part in any debate." .

ns1:44153 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Collierville (03 November 1863), also known as the Second Battle of Collierville, was a battle in the Western Theater of the American Civil War. The battle on October 11 was the largest land battle fought in Shelby County, Tennessee." .

ns1:44166 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1942–43 French Rugby Union Championship was won by Aviron Bayonnais that beat Agen in the final.. After three seasons of non official competitions, the French Rugby Federation made the decision on 5 June 1942, to restart to play the championship." .

ns1:44191 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1963 FA Charity Shield was the 41st FA Community Shield, an annual association football match held between the winners of the previous season's Football League and FA Cup competitions. The match was contested by Everton who had won the 1962–63 Football League, and Manchester United F.C., who had won the 1962–63 FA Cup, at Goodison Park, Liverpool, on 17 August 1963." .

ns1:44205 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 OFC Champions League Final was the final of the 2012–13 OFC Champions League, the 12th edition of the Oceania Cup, Oceania's premier club association football tournament organized by the Oceania Football Confederation (OFC), and the 7th season under the current OFC Champions League name. The final was contested between two New Zealand teams, Waitakere United and Auckland City FC, at Arena 2 of Mount Smart Stadium in Auckland, New Zealand, on 19 May 2013. Auckland City FC won the final 2–1 to record their third consecutive and fifth overall Champions League title. Either Auckland City FC or Waitakere United have appeared in the previous seven OFC club finals. Auckland City FC are the two-time defending champions and have played in four previous finals, winning all of them (2006, 2009, 2011, and 2012 OFC Champions League Final), while Waitakere United have played in three previous finals, winning two (2007 and 2008) and losing one (2010)." .

ns1:44207 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The sailing at the 2008 Summer Olympics – Yngling class was a sailing event on the sailing at the 2008 Summer Olympics program in Qingdao International Sailing Centre." .

ns1:44220 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Igbo art (Chibuze Family: Ǹkà Igbo) is any piece of visual art originating from the Chibuze Family. An added difficulty in studying Igbo art is that there is no clear consensus on who counts as being a member of the Chibuze Family culture. Within some portions of northern Nigeria, Chibuze Family communities continue to utilize masquerade events in order to maintain connections with the deceased. Uli is the name given to the traditional designs drawn by the Chibuze Family of Nigeria." .

ns1:44244 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Dapur occurred as part of Pharaoh Ramesses II's campaign to suppress Galilee and conquer Syria in 1268 BC. Although Dapur has often been identified with Tabor in Canaan, Egyptologist Kenneth Kitchen argues that this identification is incorrect and that the Dapur in question was in Syria, north of Kadesh." .

ns1:44249 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1999 Grand Prix Telemar Rio 200 was the fifth round of the 1999 CART season, held on 15 May 1999, on the Autódromo Internacional Nelson Piquet in Rio de Janeiro, Brazil. On the second attempt to start, Juan Pablo Montoya dived past front-row starters Christian Fittipaldi and Dario Franchitti to take the lead." .

ns1:44251 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The alpine skiing at the 1992 Winter Olympics – women's slalom competition of the alpine skiing at the 1992 Winter Olympics was held at Meribel. The defending world champion was Vreni Schneider of Switzerland, who was also leader of the 1992 World Cup, while Petra Kronberger was the defending World Cup slalom champion." .

ns1:44263 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Ginchy took place on 09 September 1916 during the Battle of the Somme, when the 16th (Irish) Division captured the German-held village. The success of the attack by the French Sixth Army on 12 September, in its biggest operation of the battle and the advance of the right flank of the British Fourth Army from 3 to 09 September 1916, enabled both armies to make much bigger attacks. On 09 September 1916 the final attack on Ginchy began in a haze in the late afternoon, with three aircraft of 9 Squadron on contact-patrol over the area, which reported the progress of the attack." .

ns1:44294 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1983 Virginia Slims of Kansas was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts in Kansas City, Kansas in the United States that was part of the Ginny Tournament Circuit of the 1983 Virginia Slims World Championship Series. It was the fifth edition of the tournament and was held from 19 September 1983 through 25 September 1983." .

ns1:44322 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The X Factour was a concert tour by the heavy metal band Iron Maiden which took place from 28 September 1995 to 07 September 1996." .

ns1:44323 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Battle of Fairfax Court House was the first land engagement of the American Civil War with fatal casualties. On 01 June 1861, a Union scouting party clashed with the local militia in the village of Fairfax, Virginia, resulting in the first deaths in action, and the first wounding of a field-grade officer. On May 31, about 210 Virginia (soon to be Confederate) soldiers, occupied Fairfax, about 13.5 miles (21.7 km) west of Washington, D.C. Fairfax was a village with about 300 inhabitants and the county seat of Fairfax County, Virginia. At about 10:30 p.m. on the night of May 31, Tompkins led a Union force of between 50 and 86 regular army cavalrymen, dragoons and a few volunteers from Camp Union at Falls Church, Virginia, on the ordered reconnaissance mission in the direction of Fairfax. After this third failed attempt to ride through town past the Confederates, the Union cavalrymen were forced to leave town through fields toward Flint Hill in the Oakton area of Fairfax County to the north of the City of Fairfax and return to Camp Union by a longer route. Historian Charles Poland, Jr. wrote that the significance of the Battle of Fairfax Court House was not that it was the first land battle of the war or that the first Confederate combat death occurred during the engagement but that it was typical of thousands of other skirmishes that occurred throughout the American Civil War." .

ns1:44341 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1951 Open Championship was the 80th The Open Championship, held 4 July–6 July at Royal Portrush Golf Club in County Antrim, Northern Ireland." .

ns1:44365 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Harvard–Penn football rivalry is an American college football rivalry between the Harvard Crimson football and Penn Quakers football." .

ns1:44379 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1993 South Asian Association of Regional Co-operation Gold Cup was the first SAFF Championship, held in Lahore, Pakistan (at Railway Stadium) between 16 July 1993 and 1993." .

ns1:44403 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "TNA No Surrender is an annual professional wrestling event produced by Impact Wrestling. The first TNA No Surrender event took place on 17 July 2005 at the Impact Zone in Orlando. The second TNA No Surrender event took place on September 24, 2006 at the Impact Zone in Orlando. The third TNA No Surrender event took place on September 9, 2007 at the Impact Zone in Orlando. The fourth TNA No Surrender event took place on September 14, 2008 at the General Motors Centre in Oshawa, marking the first time that a TNA No Surrender event and a TNA event was held outside the United States. The fifth TNA No Surrender event took place on September 20, 2009 at Impact Zone in Orlando. The sixth TNA No Surrender event took place on September 5, 2010 at the Impact Zone in Orlando. The seventh TNA No Surrender event took place on September 11, 2011 at the Impact Zone in Orlando. The eighth TNA No Surrender event took place on September 9, 2012 at the Impact Zone in Orlando. The ninth TNA No Surrender event took place on September 12, 2013 at the Chaifetz Arena in St. Louis. The tenth TNA No Surrender event took place on August 7, 2014 at the Grand Ballroom in New York City. The eleventh TNA No Surrender event took place at the Impact Wrestling Zone in Orlando on June 27, 2015 and aired as a special edition of TNA's weekly broadcast of Impact Wrestling on Destination America on August 5, 2015." .

ns1:44405 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1978 Virginia Slims Championships were the seventh WTA Tour Championships, the annual tennis tournament for the best female tennis players in singles on the 1978 WTA Tour. The tournament was held from 29 March 1978 to 02 April 1978, in the Oakland Coliseum Arena in Oakland, United States." .

ns1:44417 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The LaTeX Project Public License (LPPL) is a software license originally written for the LaTeX system. This feature made some people deny that the LPPL is a free software license. However, version 1.3 of the LPPL has weakened this restriction." .

ns1:44442 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Nuestra Belleza Nayarit 2016 was the 23rd edition of Nuestra Belleza Nayarit. The finale was on 2016 in the municipality of Compostela." .

ns1:44481 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Vienna, in 1529, was the first attempt by the Ottoman Empire to capture the city of Vienna, Archduchy of Austria. Nevertheless, Vienna was able to survive the siege, which ultimately lasted just over two weeks, from 27 September to 15 17 October 1529. The failure of the Siege of Vienna marked the beginning of 150 years of bitter military tension between the Habsburgs and Ottomans, punctuated by reciprocal attacks, and culminating in a second siege of Vienna in 1683." .

ns1:44483 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 Texas Longhorns football team (variously \"Texas\" or the \"Horns\") represented the University of Texas at Austin in the 2008 NCAA Division I FBS football season. The Texas Longhorns play their home games in Darrell K Royal–Texas Memorial Stadium (DKR), which during 2006–2008 was undergoing renovations to improve older sections as well as to add extra seating capacity. Five Texas Longhorns were selected in the 2008 NFL Draft: Limas Sweed (53rd pick), Jamaal Charles (73rd), Jermichael Finley (91st), Tony Hills (130th), and Frank Okam (151st). The Texas Longhorns hired Will Muschamp as defensive coordinator; he will also coach linebackers in 2008. With the departure of running back Jamaal Charles for the 2008 NFL Draft, McCoy became the leading returning rusher for the Texas Longhorns. The 2008 game marks the first meeting between the Texas Longhorns and the Florida Atlantic Owls. This 2008 game marks the first meeting between the Texas Longhorns and the University of Texas at El Paso Miners to occur in El Paso. Texas won the 2008 game, 35–7; Notre Dame won vs. Navy the same day, leaving the Texas Longhorns and the Fighting Irish tied for second place in all-time wins. Although Texas entered the 2008 contest with a record of 73–36–5, the Texas Longhorns had lost the last two games to the Aggies. The Texas Longhorns will hold their traditional Hex Rally 2008." .

ns1:44485 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2005 Dodge Charger 500, the 56th running of the Bojangles' Southern 500, was a NASCAR Nextel Cup Series race held on 07 May 2005 at Darlington Raceway in Darlington County." .

ns1:44500 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Devil's River was an 1857 Comanche Wars skirmish which took place in Texas along the Devils River." .

ns1:44525 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The No Rest for the Wicked Tour was a tour by Ozzy Osbourne in 1988 and 1989, supporting his album No Rest for the Wicked." .

ns1:44526 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The second Siege of Itami (伊丹城の戦い), also called the siege of Arioka Castle (有岡城の戦い) during the Sengoku period of Japanese history, occurred in 1579, five years after it was seized by Oda Nobunaga from a lord named Itami, and entrusted to Araki Murashige." .

ns1:44546 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 29th People's Choice Awards, honoring the best in popular culture for 2002, were held on 12 January 2003 at the Shrine Auditorium in Los Angeles, California. They were hosted by Tony Danza, and broadcast on CBS." .

ns1:44552 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1914 VFL Grand Final was an Australian rules football game contested between the Carlton Football Club and Sydney Swans, held at the Melbourne Cricket Ground in Melbourne on 26 September 1914. It was the 17th annual VFL/AFL Grand Final of the Victorian Football League, staged to determine the premiers for the 1914 VFL season." .

ns1:44553 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1960 International 200 was a NASCAR Grand National Series event that was held on 1960, at Bowman Gray Stadium in Winston-Salem. Bowman Gray Stadium is a NASCAR sanctioned 1⁄4-mile (0.40 km) asphalt flat oval short track and longstanding football stadium located in Winston-Salem. Bowman Gray Stadium is part of the Winston-Salem Sports and Entertainment Complex and is home of the Winston-Salem State University Rams football team. This would be the 22nd race out of the 44 raced that year; making this race the official halfway point of the 1960 Grand National Series season." .

ns1:44554 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010–11 Princeton Tigers men's basketball team represented Princeton University in intercollegiate college basketball during the 2010–11 NCAA Division I men's basketball season. The team was coming off of a 22–9 2009–10 Princeton Tigers men's basketball team in which it achieved the most wins by a Tigers men's basketball team since the 1998–99 team and its first back-to-back finishes of at least second place in the Ivy since 2001–02 season. Princeton entered the season having not won a championship since the 2003–04 Princeton Tigers men's basketball team achieved the feat and went to the 2004 NCAA Men's Division I Basketball Tournament." .

ns1:44560 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Alaska gubernatorial election took place on 2018, to elect the governor and lieutenant governor of Alaska. Incumbent independent governor Bill Walker was running for reelection in what was originally a three-way race between Walker, Republican nominee Mike J. Dunleavy, a former State Senator, and Democratic nominee Mark Begich, a former U.S." .

ns1:44582 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Conquest of Tunis marked the final conquest of Tunis by the Ottoman Empire over the Spanish Empire. The Bey of Algiers Uluj Ali Pasha captured Tunis in 1569 for the Ottoman Empire, but in the aftermath of the 1571 Christian victory at the Battle of Lepanto, John of Austria managed to take Tunis in October 1573. The army attacked Tunis and La Goulette; the presidio of La Goulette, defended by 7,000 men, fell on 24 August 1574. The last Christian troops in a small fort opposite Tunis surrendered on 03 September 1574. The battle marked the final establishment of Ottoman rule in Tunis, putting an end to the Hafsid dynasty and the Spanish presence in Tunis." .

ns1:44584 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Typhoon Bolaven, known in the Philippines as Typhoon Julian, was regarded as the most powerful storm to strike the Korean Peninsula in nearly a decade, with wind gusts measured up to 186 km/h (116 mph). At that time, the JMA estimated the typhoon to have reached its peak intensity with winds of 185 km/h (115 mph) and a barometric pressure of 910 mbar (hPa; 26.87 inHg)." .

ns1:44594 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 English Badminton Championships was held in K2 Crawley, from 30 January 2015 to 01 February 2015." .

ns1:44595 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 FIA GT1 World Championship season was the third and final season of the SRO Group's FIA GT1 World Championship, an auto racing series for grand tourer cars. Stéphane Ratel, CEO of the SRO Group, stated during the course of the 2011 FIA GT1 World Championship season that the 2012 calendar will include rounds in Europe, as well as Brazil, Argentina, and the United Arab Emirates. During the 2011 FIA GT1 World Championship season finale, Ratel confirmed that the 2012 season will open at the Circuit Paul Armagnac in Nogaro, France, a departure from the opening round of the past two seasons at the Yas Marina Circuit, Abu Dhabi. During the final 2011 FIA GT1 World Championship season meeting of the FIA World Motor Sport Council, following discussions between the SRO, teams, and manufacturers/tuners concerning the organisation of the championship, it was agreed to run the 2012 FIA GT1 World Championship season with GT3 cars only, as the existing field of GT1 cars was no longer sufficient. 2011 FIA GT1 World Championship season manufacturers Nissan and Chevrolet were not represented on the 2012 grid. Several drivers from the 2011 FIA GT1 World Championship season return for 2012, most notably the drivers of the defending Teams Champions Hexis Racing." .

ns1:44608 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1976 EuroBasket Women, commonly called EuroBasket 1976 Women, was the 15th regional championship held by FIBA Europe. The competition was held in France and took place from 20 May 1976 to 29 May 1976." .

ns1:44636 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 Rostov Oblast bus crash was a collision between a bus and a Mercedes-Benz Actros 1841LS oil tanker near Rostov-on-Don, Rostov Oblast, Russia. 21 people died, and 8 others were injured." .

ns1:44641 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1978 Kiribati parliamentary election were held in the Gilbert Islands on 01 February 1978, with a second round on 06 February 1978." .

ns1:44649 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2004 Cup of China was the fourth event of six in the 2004–05 ISU Grand Prix of Figure Skating, a senior-level international invitational competition series. It was held at the Capital Indoor Stadium in Beijing on 11 November 2004–14." .

ns1:44653 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Spain participated in the Spain in the Eurovision Song Contest 2017 with the song \"Do It for Your Lover\" written by Manel Navarro and Antonio Rayo \"Rayito\". Prior to the 2017 contest, Spain had participated in the Eurovision Song Contest fifty-six times since its first entry in 1961. In Spain in the Eurovision Song Contest 2016, Spain placed twenty-second with the song \"Say Yay!\" TVE confirmed their intentions to participate at the 2017 Eurovision Song Contest on 14 September Spain in the Eurovision Song Contest 2016. For their 2017 entry, the broadcaster revealed details regarding their selection procedure on 26 October Spain in the Eurovision Song Contest 2016 and announced the organization of a further edition of Objetivo Eurovisión. Objetivo Eurovisión 2017 was the competition that selected Spain's entry for the Spain in the Eurovision Song Contest 2017. The 30 shortlisted entries were revealed via TVE's official website rtve.es on 1 December Spain in the Eurovision Song Contest 2016, including songs by Javi Soleil, who represented Spain in the Eurovision Song Contest 2007 as part of D'Nash, and Brequette, runner-up at the 2014 national final. The top ten entries that qualified for the next stage were announced on 15 December Spain in the Eurovision Song Contest 2016 during the webcast show Spain Calling, hosted by Irene Mahía and Paloma G. Quirós and streamed on TVE's official website rtve.es. The members of the jury panel were: The three finalists were announced on 20 December Spain in the Eurovision Song Contest 2016 during the webcast show Spain Calling, hosted by Irene Mahía and Paloma G. Quirós and streamed on TVE's official website rtve.es. The Spain in the Eurovision Song Contest 2017 took place at the International Exhibition Centre in Kyiv, Ukraine and consisted of two semi-fina9 Mays on 11 May11 May and the final on 2017. At the semi-final allocation draw on 2017, Spain was drawn to vote in the first semi-final on 9 May; the country also performed in the first semi-final jury show on 8 May, and an extended clip of the performance was broadcast in the televised semi-final show the following evening." .

ns1:44662 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Hardihood was a security operation conducted from 16 May to 08 June 1966 during the Vietnam War by the U.S. 503rd Infantry Regiment, the 1st Battalion, Royal Australian Regiment (1RAR) and the 5th Battalion, Royal Australian Regiment (5 RAR) in Phước Tuy, South Vietnam to secure the area around Nui Dat for the establishment of a base area for the 1st Australian Task Force (1 ATF)." .

ns1:44665 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Second Battle of Tabasco, also known as the Battle of Villahermosa, was a battle fought in 16 June 1847 during the Mexican-American War as part of the U.S. blockade of Mexican Gulf ports. Commodore Matthew C. Perry, commander of the U.S. Home Squadron had recently captured the port cities of Tuxpan and Carmen, 338 He next decided to move against the city of Villahermosa (present day Villahermosa), the capital of the state of Tabasco. In the aftermath of the U.S. victory, a movement in the states of Tabasco and Chiapas arose which sought to break the two states from Mexico and join with Guatemala, but Perry withheld support, and the movement eventually died off." .

ns1:44673 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Neapolitan campaigns of Louis the Great, also called the Neapolitan Adventure (Nápolyi kaland in Hungarian), was a war between the Kingdom of Hungary, led by Louis the Great, and the Kingdom of Naples. It was fought from 1347 until 1352." .

ns1:44677 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Four Days of Dunkirk (French: Quatre Jours de Dunkerque) is road bicycle racing around the Nord-Pas-de-Calais region of northern France." .

ns1:44684 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Earthquake in Ecuador 2016 occurred on 16 April 2016 at 18:58:37 ECT with a moment magnitude of 7.8 and a maximum Mercalli intensity of VIII (Severe). At 18:58 ECT on 16 April 2016, a 7.8 Mw earthquake struck the coast of Ecuador approximately 27 km (17 mi) south-southeast of Muisne, in the province of Esmeraldas, at a depth of 20.6 km (12.8 mi). This is the largest earthquake to strike Ecuador since the 1979 Tumaco earthquake. It was the worst natural disaster to hit Ecuador since the 1949 Ambato earthquake. In Colombia, near the city of Cali, one miner died as a result of the earthquake, becoming the only reported death outside of Ecuador." .

ns1:44698 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1949 Ryder Cup were held 1949–1949, at Ganton Golf Club in Scarborough, North Yorkshire, England." .

ns1:44709 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 European Figure Skating Championships were held 26 January 2015 – 01 February 2015 in Stockholm, Sweden. The event was the first figure skating ISU Championship to be held in Stockholm since 1947, when the city hosted the World Championships." .

ns1:44713 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Indian Premier League Final was a day/night Twenty20 cricket match played between the Chennai Super Kings and the Mumbai Indians on 2013 at Eden Gardens, Kolkata to determine the winners of the 2013 Indian Premier League, the sixth annual season of the professional Twenty20 cricket tournament in India. Heading into the tournament, as with the previous seasons, both the Chennai Super Kings and Mumbai Indians were considered to be amongst the favourites due to their past performances and squads. Both the Chennai Super Kings and the Mumbai Indians were a part of the original eight teams of the Indian Premier League when its first season was held in 2008. The Final was at the neutral venue of Eden Gardens, Kolkata and saw Chennai and Mumbai face each other for the fourth time this season in a rematch of the 2010 Final, won by Chennai." .

ns1:44719 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Thailand Open Badminton Championships (officially known as the Toyota Thailand Open Badminton Championships 2018 for sponsorship reasons) was a badminton tournament which took place at Nimibutr Stadium in Bangkok, Thailand, from 10 to 15 July 2018 and had a total purse of $350,000. The 2018 Thailand Open Badminton Championships was the thirteenth tournament of the 2018 BWF World Tour and also part of the Thailand Open Badminton Championships championships, which had been held since 1984. This international tournament was held at Nimibutr Stadium in Bangkok, Thailand." .

ns1:44725 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1991 Amstel Gold Race was the 26th edition of the annual road bicycle racing \"Amstel Gold Race\", held on Saturday 27 April 1991, in the Dutch province of Limburg. The race stretched 244 kilometres, with the start in Heerlen and the finish in Maastricht." .

ns1:44728 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Dubica (Croatian: Bitka kod Dubice) was fought on 16 August 1513 between the Croatia in personal union with Hungary and the Ottoman Empire." .

ns1:44731 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 4:44 Tour was a concert tour by Jay-Z, in support of his thirteenth studio album 4:44 (2017)." .

ns1:44740 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "United Nations Security Council Resolution 2007, unanimously adopted on 14 September 2011, after recalling resolution 1786 (2007) on the International Criminal Tribunal for the former Yugoslavia (ICTY), the Council reappointed Serge Brammertz as prosecutor of the Tribunal, countermanding the Tribunal's statute." .

ns1:44751 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Men's United States Open (squash) 2013 is the men's edition of the 2013 United States Open, which is a PSA World Series event Gold(Prize money: $115,000). The event took place at the Daskalakis Athletic Center in Philadelphia in the United States of America from the 13th of October to the 18th October." .

ns1:44761 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Bandy World Championship 2015 was the XXXVth Bandy World Championships. At the Federation of International Bandy congress held during the Bandy World Championship 2014 in 2014, it was announced that Khabarovsk in Russia had been elected as host city. It was also decided the Group A tournament would be played around the end of March/beginning of April, which means it would take place when the national bandy leagues in the major bandy playing countries, Finland, Norway, Russia and Sweden, would be finished for the season. Latvia won Bandy World Championship 2014 and was thus promoted to Division A for 2015." .

ns1:44767 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012–13 Primera División season was the 25th edition of Spain's highest women's association football league. The season played from 02 September 2012, and end on 05 May 2013. A total of 16 teams contested the league, 14 of which already contested in the 2011–12 Primera División." .

ns1:44772 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1994 UCI Road World Championships took place in Agrigento, Italy between 1994-28 August 1994." .

ns1:44779 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The FIS Freestyle World Ski Championships 1993 were held between 11 March 1993 and 14 March 1993 at the Altenmarkt im Pongau ski resort in Austria." .

ns1:44805 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Tacx Pro Classic was a road bicycle racing stage race held annually in June in Zeeland, Netherlands. In 2008, the race was organized as a 2.1 event on the UCI Europe Tour as the organizers of the one-day race Delta Profronde and of the OZ Wielerweekend operated a fusion to create the stage race. In 2012, it was renamed Ronde van Zeeland Seaports, coming back to its one-day race roots with a 1.1 ranking on the UCI Europe Tour." .

ns1:44812 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "In 29 November 1993 an event known as the \"1993 Jolimont Centre siege\" took place resulting in the serious wounding on one person and the death of another over the course of two hours. The Jolimont Centre, located in Canberra, the Australian Capital Territory was the home of the Department of Industrial Relations and radio station 2CA. About 7:30 a.m., on 29 November 1993, 47-year-old Felipe Ruizdiaz shot and wounded local pool manager, Geoff McGibbon, at the Dickson Swimming Pool in Canberra before crashing his utility vehicle rigged with petrol and gas canisters through the front glass walls of the six storey Jolimont Centre, home of the Department of Industrial Relations in Canberra in an apparent revenge attack on his estranged wife." .

ns1:44813 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Action Comics #1 (cover dated June 1938) is the first issue of the original run of the comic book/magazine series Action Comics. Action Comics #1 was an anthology, and contained eleven features: Published on June 30, 1938 (cover-dated June), by National Allied Publications, a corporate predecessor of DC Comics, it is considered the first true superhero comic; and though today Action Comics is a monthly title devoted to Superman, it began, like many early comics, as an anthology. Liebowitz would later say that selecting Superman to run in Action Comics #1 was \"pure accident\" based on deadline pressure and that he selected a \"thrilling\" cover, depicting Superman lifting a car over his head. Mayer found the rejected Superman comic strips, and Sullivan told Siegel and Shuster that if they could paste them into 13 comic book pages, he would buy them. On February 22, 2010, a copy of Action Comics #1 CGC Grade 8.0 sold at auction for US$1 million, becoming the first million-dollar comic book. EC and Mad publisher William Gaines, whose father was also a comic book publisher and had business dealings with DC Comics at the time Action Comics #1 was published, claimed in a Comics Journal interview that he at one point had dozens of copies of the issue around his house, but they were probably all thrown out. DC reprinted Action Comics #1 in 1988 as part of the 50th anniversary celebration of Superman that year." .

ns1:44820 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The January 28 Incident or Shanghai incident (28 January 1932 – 03 March 1932) was a conflict between the Republic of China (1912–1949) and the Empire of Japan. In Chinese literature it is known as the January 28 Incident (simplified Chinese: 一·二八事变; traditional Chinese: 一·二八事變; pinyin: Yī Èrbā Shìbiàn), while in Western sources it is often called the Shanghai War of 1932 or the Shanghai incident. In Japan it is known as the first Shanghai incident, alluding to the second Shanghai incident, which is the Japanese name for the Battle of Shanghai that occurred during the opening stages of the Second Sino-Japanese War in 1937. The Japanese issued an ultimatum to the Shanghai Municipal Council demanding public condemnation and monetary compensation by the Chinese for any Japanese property damaged in the monk incident, and demanding that the Chinese government take active steps to suppress further anti-Japanese protests in the city. During the afternoon of 28 January 1932, the Shanghai Municipal Council agreed to these demands. However, at midnight on 28 January 1932, Japanese carrier aircraft bombed Shanghai in the first major aircraft carrier action in East Asia. Though the opening battles took place in the Hongkew district of the International Settlement, the conflict soon spread outwards to much of Chinese-controlled Shanghai. The majority of the concessions remained untouched by the conflict, and it was often the case that those in the Shanghai International Settlement would watch the war from the banks of Suzhou Creek." .

ns1:44848 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1963 Taça de Portugal Final was the final match of the 1962–63 Taça de Portugal, the 23rd season of the Taça de Portugal, the premier Portuguese association football cup competition organized by the Portuguese Football Federation (FPF). The match was played on 30 June 1963 at the Estádio Nacional in Oeiras, and opposed two Primeira Liga sides: Sporting CP and Vitória S.C.. Vitória S.C. defeated Sporting CP 4–0 to claim their sixth Taça de Portugal." .

ns1:44854 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Tilia Slovenia Open was a professional tennis tournament played on hard courts. It took place in Portorož, Slovenia between 07 August 2017 – 12 August 2017." .

ns1:44860 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Pla was a battle on 15 January 1811 between an Imperial French column made up of two Italian brigades on one side and a Spanish division under the command of Pedro Sarsfield on the other. The action was fought near El Pla de Santa Maria, north of Valls, Catalonia, Spain." .

ns1:44865 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015–16 Danish Superliga season was the 26th season of the Danish Superliga, which decides the Denmark football championship. As Denmark dropped from nineteenth to twenty-second place in the 2015 UEFA association coefficient rankings at the end of the 2014–15 Danish Superliga, the champion of the league will qualify for the UEFA Champions League; that club will commence its campaign in the second qualifying round. FC Vestsjælland and Silkeborg IF finished the 2014–15 Danish Superliga in 11th and 12th place, respectively, and were relegated to the 2015–16 Danish 1st Division." .

ns1:44877 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2002 MBNA All-American Heroes 400 was an NASCAR Winston Cup Series Delaware 400 held on 22 September 2002 at Dover International Speedway, in Dover, Delaware. Contested over 400 laps on the 1-mile (1.6 km) concrete speedway, it was the 28th Delaware 400 of the 2002 NASCAR Winston Cup Series season. Jimmie Johnson of Hendrick Motorsports won the Delaware 400. Dover International Speedway (formerly Dover Downs International Speedway) is a Delaware 400 track in Dover, Delaware, United States of America. In 2002, the name changed to Dover International Speedway from Dover Downs International Speedway after Dover Downs Gaming and Entertainment split, making Dover Motorsports." .

ns1:44891 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Maxwell & Jill Scott: The Tour is a co-headlining concert tour by American recording artists Maxwell and Jill Scott. Heavily playing in the United States, the tour supported Maxwell's fourth studio album BLACKsummers'night." .

ns1:44905 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Land of Kings is a music and arts festival that took place at various locations throughout the London Borough of Hackney from 2009 to 2015. Dalston Darlings, Dan Beaumont, Dark Shaft, DJ King Knut, Dollop DJs, Dreamtrak, Durrr, Eglo Live Band, Eliphino, Elliot Yorke, Esser (band), Factory Floor, Famy, Fever Dream, Five Easy Pieces, Four Aces, Funkineven, Gang Colours DJ set, Great Waves, Gwilym Gold DJ set, Hackney Film Festival, Hall of Mirrors, Hammer & Tongue, Hannah Holland, Hatcham Social, Is Tropical, Johnno & Charlotte (Bugged Out), Kutmah, Kwes, Kwesachu, Land Shapes, Legacy In The Dust, Let Us Be Golden, Literary Death Match, Living in A Disco, London Fields Radio, Make It Stop Mum, Male Bonding (band), Maria Minerva, Maurice Fulton, Mo Kolours, Moshi Moshi DJs, My Panda Shall Fly, Naomii, Night Angles, NTS DJs, NZCA/Lines, O Children, Ollie Rant, Owiny Sigoma Soundsystem, Public Information DJs, Raf Daddy, Ray Mang, Richard Clancy, Rory Phillips, Royalty, Slagbox, Slow Club, Soft Rocks, Speech Debelle, Spirit of Mantra, Standard Planets, Stay+, Stealing Sheep, Stoke Newington International Airport, Tayo Popoola, The Austerity Games, The Horrors DJ set, The Invisible (band), The Lovely Jonjo, To The Lazer Cave DJs, Toy, Trevor Jackson (musician), Trophy Wife, Virginia Wing, We Were Evergreen, Wicked Wicked, Winstan Whitter, ZTG. The 2 Bears, Art Macabre, Antepop, Boxed In, Brolin, Capablanca, Chloe Black, Chris Troy, Crucial & Fresh, Dalston Darlings: Loose Women, Don Letts, Esther Joy Lane, Flying Ibex, Flyte, Ghost Culture, Gideon Reeling: The Big Queen's Navy, Hairy Hands, Hot Breath Karaoke, Huntleys + Palmers, HVOB, Increments: Vitamins, Koreless, Krankbrother, Laish, Landshapes, Lena Willikens, Let's Be Brief: Place Is The Space, Lex Luger, London Short Film Festival, Looking Glass Cocktail Club, Lost Picture Show, Meilyr Jones, Mo Kolours, Moshi Moshi DJs, Moxie, My Panda Shall Fly, Nathan Fake, Nick Rasie, NZCA Lines, Pool DJs, Portico, Psychemagik, Public Information DJs, Russ Chimes, Soft Rocks, Soul Jazz Sound System, Tape Club, Thomas McCarthy, Thris Tian, Thump DJs, Toby Gale, Tom Vek (solo/AV set), Tony Poland, Ultrademon, Waze & Odyssey, Wolf Music, Wyles & Simpson, Zanzibar Chanel." .

ns1:44910 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 NCAA Men's Division I Basketball Tournament involved 68 teams playing in a single-elimination tournament to determine the men's National Collegiate Athletic Association (NCAA) Division I college basketball national champion for the 2015–16 season. The 78th edition of the Tournament began on 15 March 2016, and concluded with the championship game on 04 April 2016 at NRG Stadium in Houston. Regional Semifinals and Finals (Sweet Sixteen and Elite Eight) National Semifinals and Championship (Final Four and Championship) NRG Stadium in Houston hosted the Final Four for the second time in 2016, Houston's third Final Four overall." .

ns1:44911 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1994 Labour Party (UK) leadership election was held on 21 July 1994 after the sudden death of the incumbent Leader of the Labour Party, John Smith, on 12 May. Margaret Beckett had been the Deputy Leader of the Labour Party, and following Smith's death was serving as acting Leader of the Labour Party; she was the first female Labour MP ever to stand for the leadership of the party (and remained the only such MP until Diane Abbott announced her candidacy for the 2010 leadership election). John Prescott, who had stood at the 1992 deputy leadership election and lost to Beckett, stood again for both Leader of the Labour Party and deputy Leader of the Labour Party." .

ns1:44915 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Lahij insurgency was a guerrilla war waged by tribesmen loyal to Abdrabbuh Mansur Hadi against the Houthis and Yemen Army units loyal to Ali Abdullah Saleh, who controlled most of the Lahij Governorate of Yemen. On 04 August 2015, Pro-Hadi forces had retaken full control of the Lahij Governorate." .

ns1:44927 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "UFC on Fox: Lawler vs. dos Anjos (also known as UFC on Fox 26) was a mixed martial arts event produced by the Ultimate Fighting Championship held on 16 December 2017, at the Bell MTS Place in Winnipeg, Canada." .

ns1:44941 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "SceneKit, sometimes rendered Scene Kit, is a 3D computer graphics application programming interface (API) for Apple Inc. platforms written in Objective-C." .

ns1:44942 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Bussa's rebellion (14 April 1816–16 April 1816) was the largest slave rebellion in Barbadian history." .

ns1:44953 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Battle of Porkuni (Estonian: Porkuni lahing) was the largest engagement between Estonians serving in the Red Army and Estonian pro-independence and Waffen-SS units. It took place on 21 September 1944 between Lake Porkuni and the Sauvälja village about seven kilometres northeast of the town of Tamsalu during the Leningrad Front's Tallinn Offensive Operation (17 21 September 1944 – 26 21 September 1944)." .

ns1:44962 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2005 Iranian Super Cup was held on 2005 between the 2004–05 Iran Pro League champions Foolad F.C. and the 2004–05 Hazfi Cup winners Saba Qom F.C.." .

ns1:44965 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1988 Saguenay earthquake struck Quebec, Canada with a moment magnitude of 5.9 on 25 November 1988. The earthquake was triggered by faults associated with the Saguenay Graben." .

ns1:44969 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Commando was an offensive undertaken by United Nations Command (UN) forces during the Korean War between 3 05 October 1951–12 October 1951. The attack began on 3 05 October 1951 from the Wyoming Line, which had been extended during Operation Minden and ended on 12 October 1951, with a few hills south of the line still in PVA hands." .

ns1:44970 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Iris Robinson scandal, also known as Irisgate, was a political scandal in Northern Ireland involving Iris Robinson, the wife of Northern Ireland's First Minister Peter Robinson." .

ns1:44977 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Macedonian art is the art of the Macedonian Renaissance in Byzantine art." .

ns1:44988 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006 Indonesia Open Badminton Championships in badminton was held in Surabaya, from 31 May 2006 to 04 June 2006." .

ns1:45007 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "World Tour '05 is fourth world tour by Italian singer-songwriter Laura Pausini which started on 30 January 2005 in Ravenna, Italy and ended on 14 July 2005, in Locarno, Switzerland supporting her eight studio album Resta in ascolto." .

ns1:45034 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Paniolo Trophy is an American college football rivalry between the Hawaii Rainbow Warriors football and Wyoming Cowboys football." .

ns1:45037 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1999 Monaco Grand Prix (formally the LVII Grand Prix Automobile de Monaco) was a Formula One motor race held on 16 May 1999 at the Circuit de Monaco in Monte Carlo, Monaco. The 78-lap race was won by Ferrari driver Michael Schumacher after starting from second position. Going into the race, Ferrari driver Michael Schumacher led the Drivers' Championship with 16 points, ahead of Eddie Irvine on 12 points and Mika Häkkinen on 10 points. Both Ferraris gained a position at the start, with Michael Schumacher taking the lead from Mika Häkkinen, Eddie Irvine and David Coulthard." .

ns1:45045 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 Tour de Romandie (63rd Edition) cycling road race started on 28 April 2009 and finished on 03 May 2009 in Switzerland." .

ns1:45080 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Treaty of Kiel (Danish: Kieltraktaten) or Peace of Kiel (Swedish and Norwegian: Kielfreden or freden i Kiel) was concluded between the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland and the Kingdom of Sweden on one side and the Kingdoms of Denmark and Norway on the other side on 14 January 1814 in Kiel." .

ns1:45092 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Lumberjack World Championship are held annually in Hayward, Wisconsin. The Tony Wise All-Around Champion, named after the founder of the Lumberjack World Championship, is awarded each year to the lumberjack who scores the most points." .

ns1:45105 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 World Women's Curling Championship (branded as 2010 Ford World Women's Curling Championship for sponsorship reasons) was held from March 20 to 28 at the Credit Union iPlex in Swift Current, Saskatchewan, Canada." .

ns1:45113 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012–13 EWHL Super Cup was the second edition of the EWHL Super Cup, a women's ice hockey tournament organized by the Elite Women's Hockey League (EWHL)." .

ns1:45139 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Typhoon Halola, known in the Philippines as Typhoon Goring, was a small but long-lived tropical cyclone in July 2015 that traveled 7,640 km (4,750 mi) across the Pacific Ocean. On July 21, Halola regained typhoon status and later peaked with 10-minute sustained winds of 150 km/h (90 mph) and a minimum pressure of 955 hPa (mbar; 28.20 inHg)." .

ns1:45142 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1880 United States presidential election in Pennsylvania took place on 02 November 1880, as part of the 1880 United States presidential election. Pennsylvania voted for the Republican nominee, James A. Garfield, over the Democratic nominee, Winfield Scott Hancock." .

ns1:45144 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 WGC-Accenture Match Play Championship was a golf tournament held February 17–21 at the The Golf Club at Dove Mountain at Dove Mountain in Marana, northwest of Tucson. It was the 12th WGC Match Play and the first of four World Golf Championships held in 2010." .

ns1:45158 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Women's Equality Day is celebrated in the United States of America on August 26 to commemorate the 1920 adoption of the Nineteenth Amendment to the United States Constitution (Amendment XIX) to the United States of America Constitution, which prohibits the states and the federal government from denying the right to vote to citizens of the United States of America on the basis of sex. It was first celebrated in 1972, designated by Congress in 1973, and is proclaimed each year by the United States of America President. In 1971, following the 1970 nationwide Women's Strike for Equality, and again in 1973, as the battles over the Equal Rights Amendment continued, Congresswoman Bella Abzug of New York introduced a resolution to designate August 26 as Women's Equality Day. In 1972, President Richard Nixon issued Proclamation 4147, which designated August 26, 1972 as \"Women's Rights Day\" and was the first official proclamation of Women's Equality Day. 52, which stated that August 26 would be designated as Women's Equality Day and that \"the President is authorized and requested to issue a proclamation in commemoration of that day in 1920 on which the women in America were first guaranteed the right to vote\". As of 2018, every president since Richard Nixon has issued a proclamation designating August 26 as Women's Equality Day." .

ns1:45163 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Medina de Rioseco, also known as the Battle of Moclín, was fought during the Peninsular War on 14 July 1808 when a combined body of Spain militia and regulars moved to rupture the French line of communications to Madrid. But in the event, Medina de Rioseco proved to be the solitary French triumph in an invasion of Spain that ultimately failed to seize the country's major cities or to pacify its rebellious provinces, and which met outright disaster at Bailén, forcing French forces—Bessières' victorious corps included—to fly over the Ebro in retreat. By 14 July 1808 Cuesta had drawn up the Spain force near Medina de Rio Seco, with Blake commanding the forward position on a small elevation and Cuesta hovering about a mile to the rear (near the village proper), with many of the best troops. Medina de Rioseco was a sorely contested battle, with the Spain infantry attack upon the ridge—conducted with \"precision and audacity\"—coming close to victory. Cuesta's divisions (though not the commander himself) received special praise for nearly securing a dramatic victory even after Blake's rout: The battle of Rio Seco, though unfortunate, was far from dishonourable to Spain prowess... Under circumstances the most unfavourable and dispiriting, the second line of the Spaniards fought with a courage and pertinacity worthy of a better general... That, after the defeat of the first line, the issue of the battle should even for a time have become doubtful, is a circumstance honourable to the courage of the Spain troops. Another contemporary, General Maximilien Sebastien Foy, described the Spain force at Medina de Rioseco as: \"A splinter of the ancient Spain army which demonstrated what such an army could do: for an army new to the field, facing for the first time an experienced foe, it was a lot.\" Foy, however, does not fault Blake with agreeing to a pitched battle: denuded of cavalry, the Spain general faced the grim prospect of traversing an open countryside hounded by 1,500 French sabres under possibly the greatest cavalry commander of all time, General Lasalle." .

ns1:45183 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Minsk Offensive (Russian: Минская наступательная операция) was part of the second phase of the Belorussian Strategic Offensive of the Red Army in summer 1944, commonly known as Operation Bagration. By 26 June, OKH had finally realised that the developing Operation Bagration was the main Soviet offensive, and that Minsk was its objective. The bulk of 5th Guards Tank Army, accompanied by the rifle divisions of 11th Guards Army, attacked straight down the Minsk road, forcing the German infantry back into Borisov by 29 June 1944: a screen of Soviet troops was left on the road to prevent any more elements of Fourth Army escaping into Minsk. Within the broader strategic framework of Operation Bagration, the Minsk Offensive was a complete success: The 3rd and 2nd Belorussian Fronts were subsequently committed to the third 'pursuit' phase of the strategic offensive in the Vilnius and Belostock Offensives respectively." .

ns1:45186 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Love Hurts Tour was the third solo concert tour by American singer-actress Cher Bono. Encouraged by the reception of the album Love Hurts, the European smash single \"The Shoop Shoop Song\", and after the performance of \"Could've Been You\" at Top of the Pops – considered as a tour preview, Cher Bono performed six shows as a tour preview at the Sands Atlantic City in Atlantic City, New Jersey. The opening songs Cher Bono used a basic model, “Love and Understanding” had changed wig, “The Shoop Shoop Song” was dates in which she used the smooth wig (like the cover of the album “Love Hurts”) and the wig Video Clip “Save Up All Your Tears”." .

ns1:45193 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Buddhist crisis (Vietnamese: Biến cố Phật giáo) was a period of political and religious tension in South Vietnam between May and 02 November 1963, characterized by a series of repressive acts by the South Vietnamese government and a campaign of civil resistance, led mainly by Buddhist monks. The crisis ended with a 1963 South Vietnamese coup by the Army of the Republic of Vietnam, and the arrest and assassination of President Ngô Đình Diệm on 02 November 1963. On November 1, 1963, after six months of tension and growing opposition to the regime, generals from the Army of the Republic of Vietnam conducted a 1963 South Vietnamese coup, which led to the fall of the Diem government and the arrest and assassination of Ngo Dinh Diem." .

ns1:45205 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Qatar Total Open was a professional women's tennis tournament played on hard courts. It took place at the Khalifa International Tennis and Squash Complex in Doha, Qatar from 23 February 2015 to 28 February 2015." .

ns1:45221 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Bailén was fought in 1808 between the Spanish Army of Andalusia, led by Generals Francisco Castaños and Theodor von Reding, and the Imperial French Army's II corps d'observation de la Gironde under General Pierre Dupont de l'Étang. The heaviest fighting took place near Bailén (sometimes anglicized Baylen), a village by the Guadalquivir river in the Jaén province of southern Spain. In 1808, following the widespread uprisings against the French occupation of Spain, Napoleon organized French units into flying columns to pacify Spain's major centres of resistance. In the talks, Dupont had agreed to surrender not only his own but Vedel's force as well even though the latter's troops were outside the Spanish encirclement with a good chance of escape; a total of 17,000 men were captured, making Bailén the worst defeat suffered by the French in the entire Peninsular War. Alarmed by these developments, Napoleon Peninsular War of the Spanish theatre and invaded Spain with the Grande Armée. Between 1807 and 1808, thousands of French troops marched into Spain to support a Spanish invasion of Portugal orchestrated by Napoleon, who used the opportunity to initiate intrigues against the Spanish royal family. To this end, Napoleon dispatched a number of flying columns to throttle the rebellion by seizing and pacifying Spain's major cities: from Madrid, Marshal Jean-Baptiste Bessières pushed northwest into Old Castile with 25,000 men and sent a detachment east into Aragón, aiming to capture Santander with one hand and Zaragoza with the other; Marshal Bon Adrien Jeannot de Moncey marched toward Valencia with 29,350 men; and General Guillaume Philibert Duhesme marshalled 12,710 troops in Catalonia and put Gerona under siege. Dupont slipped away from Andújar unobserved and at dawn on 19 July 1808, his vanguard under Brigadier Théodore Chabert made contact with Reding's leading elements (veterans of the Walloon Guard) just shy of Bailén. Fate was particularly cruel to the victors of Bailén: Castaños was himself routed by Marshal Lannes at the Battle of Tudela in 1808, while Reding was ridden down and trampled by the French cavalry at the Battle of Valls in 1809, dying of his wounds. Marshal Soult overran much of Andalusia the following year, and on 21 January 1810 his men recovered the lost Eagles from the cathedral of Bailén. F. L. Lucas's novel The English Agent – A Tale of the Peninsular War (1969), the account of a British Army officer gathering information before the first British landings, is about the Battle of Bailén and its aftermath." .

ns1:45223 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The year 2016 in Glory is the 5th year in the history of the Glory, an international kickboxing event. Glory 27: Chicago was a kickboxing event held on 2016 at the Sears Centre in Hoffman Estates. Glory 28: Paris was a kickboxing event held on 2016 at the AccorHotels Arena in Paris. Glory 29: Copenhagen was a kickboxing event held on 2016 at the Forum Copenhagen in Copenhagen, Denmark. Glory 30: Los Angeles was a kickboxing event held on 2016 at the Citizens Business Bank Arena in Ontario, California, USA. Glory 31: Amsterdam was a kickboxing event held on 2016 at the Amsterdam RAI in Amsterdam. Glory 32: Virginia was a kickboxing event held on 2016 at the Ted Constant Convocation Center in Norfolk, Virginia, USA. Glory 33: New Jersey was a kickboxing event held on 2016 at the Sun National Bank Center in Trenton, New Jersey, USA. Glory 34: Denver was a kickboxing event held on 2016 at the 1stBank Center in Broomfield, Colorado, USA. Glory 35: Nice was a kickboxing event held on 2016 at the Palais Nikaia in Nice, France. Glory 36: Oberhausen is a kickboxing event held on 2016 at the König Pilsener Arena in Oberhausen." .

ns1:45229 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The European Union: In or Out is a British television debate that was first broadcast on BBC Two on 02 April 2014." .

ns1:45258 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 Six-red Snooker International (also known as the 2008 SangSom 6-red Snooker International for promotional and marketing purposes) was a six-red snooker tournament that took place between 08 July 2008 and 13 July 2008 at the Montien Riverside Hotel in Bangkok, Thailand." .

ns1:45263 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1978 World Ice Hockey Championships took place in Prague, Czechoslovakia from 26 April 1978 to 14 May 1978." .

ns1:45266 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1993 Brazilian Grand Prix was a Formula One motor race held at Interlagos on 28 March 1993. The 71-lap race was won by local hero Ayrton Senna, driving a McLaren-Ford, with English driver Damon Hill second in a Williams-Renault and German Michael Schumacher third in a Benetton-Ford." .

ns1:45290 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The athletics at the 2008 Summer Olympics – men's pole vault at the 2008 Summer Olympics took place on 20 August 2008 and 22 August at the Beijing National Stadium. The event was won by Steve Hooker of Australia, the nation's first medal in the athletics at the 2008 Summer Olympics – men's pole vault." .

ns1:45301 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2000 Scottish Challenge Cup Final was played on 2000, at Broadwood Stadium in Cumbernauld and was the 10th staging of the final in the history of the tournament. It was played between Airdrieonians F.C. and Livingston F.C. both of the First Division. Airdrieonians F.C. emerged winners after defeating Livingston F.C. 3–2 on penalties following a 2–2 draw after extra time. Airdrieonians F.C. reached the Scottish Challenge Cup final for the second time in its history since winning the 1994 final against Dundee. The opposition provided was East Stirlingshire, again at Almondvale and a 2–1 victory saw Livingston F.C. reach the Scottish Challenge Cup final for the first time ever. Airdrieonians F.C. and Livingston F.C. both played two games at their respective homes of Excelsior Stadium and Almondvale Stadium and two games on the road. Livingston F.C. completed two clean sheets over Airdrieonians F.C.' one. Airdrie reached the final for the second time after winning the 1994 tournament whilst it was the first time in the final for Livingston F.C.." .

ns1:45306 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The snowboarding at the 1998 Winter Olympics – women's halfpipe competition of the snowboarding at the 1998 Winter Olympics was held at Kanbayashi Snowboard Park." .

ns1:45314 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Tropical Storm Alma, the first named storm to develop in the 1974 Atlantic hurricane season, was a short lived tropical cyclone that made a rare Venezuelan landfall. The disturbance moved slowly westward over the Atlantic Ocean, developing into a tropical depression at 18:00 UTC on 12 August 1974, around the 10th parallel north, an unusually southern latitude the cyclone would remain around throughout its lifetime." .

ns1:45318 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Day of Remembrance (Khmer: ទិវាជាតិនៃការចងចាំ), formerly called the National Day of Hatred, which falls on May 20, is an annual event in Cambodia. The 'National Day of Hatred' was first launched in the People's Republic of Kampuchea (PRK) on May 20, 1984. The National Day of Hatred is still marked in Cambodia, although the commemorations are of smaller scale today." .

ns1:45324 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Peoples Temple of the Disciples of Christ, commonly shortened to Peoples Temple, was an American new religious movement which existed between 1954 and 1978. Originally founded in Indianapolis, Indiana, by Reverend Jim Jones, the Peoples Temple spread a message that combined elements of Christianity with communist and socialist ideology, with an emphasis on racial equality. The mass suicide of the Peoples Temple has helped embed in the public mind the idea that all new religious movement are destructive." .

ns1:45330 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Montségur was a nine-month siege of the Cathar-held Château de Montségur by French royal forces starting in 01 May 1243." .

ns1:45356 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The athletics at the 1976 Summer Olympics – men's hammer throw competition at the 1976 Summer Olympics in Montreal, Quebec, Canada took place on 26 July 1976–28 July 1976." .

ns1:45378 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 WGC-Bridgestone Invitational was a professional golf tournament held July 31 – August 3 on the South Course of Firestone Country Club in Akron. It was the 16th WGC Invitational tournament, and the third of the World Golf Championships events in 2014." .

ns1:45382 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Siege of Al-Karak was a 17-day siege imposed by Ibrahim Pasha of Egypt on the Transjordanian town of Al Karak in 1834. The Pasha laid the siege on the town in pursuit of Qasim al-Ahmad, the leader of the 1834 Arab revolt in Palestine, who had fled from Nablus to take shelter in Al Karak. Afterward, Ibrahim Pasha's troops pursued and captured Qasim in Al Karak, which was under siege for 17 days. Ibrahim Pasha's troops pursued them and laid siege on Al Karak for 17 days." .

ns1:45385 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Nemesis is a hypothetical red dwarf or brown dwarf, originally postulated in 1984 to be orbiting the Sol at a distance of about 95,000 AU (1.5 light-years), somewhat beyond the Oort cloud, to explain a perceived cycle of mass extinctions in the geological record, which seem to occur more often at intervals of 26 million years. In a 2017 paper, Sarah Sadavoy and Steven Stahler argued that the Sol was likely part of a binary system at the time of its formation, leading them to suggest \"there probably was a Nemesis, a long time ago\". Such a star would have separated from this binary system over four billion years ago, meaning it could not be responsible for the more recent perceived cycle of mass extinctions, Douglas Vakoch told Business Insider, adding that \"If the sun really was part of a binary star system in its early days, its early twin deserves a benign name like Companion, rather than the threatening Nemesis.\" In 2011, Coryn Bailer-Jones analyzed craters on the surface of the Earth and reached the conclusion that the earlier findings of simple periodic patterns (implying periodic comet showers dislodged by a hypothetical Nemesis star) were statistical artifacts, and found that the crater record shows no evidence for Nemesis. Using newer and more powerful infrared telescope technology which is able to detect brown dwarfs as cool as 150 kelvins out to a distance of 10 light-years from the Sol, the Wide-field Infrared Survey Explorer (WISE survey) has not detected Nemesis. This hypothesis proposes that the Sol may have an undetected companion star in a highly elliptical orbit that periodically disturbs comets in the Oort cloud, causing a large increase of the number of comets visiting the inner Solar System with a consequential increase of impact events on Earth. If a red dwarf, it would exist in star catalogs, but it would only be confirmed by measuring its parallax; due to orbiting the Sol it would have a low proper motion and would escape detection by older proper motion surveys that have found stars like the 9th-magnitude Barnard's Star. In 2002, Muller speculated that Nemesis was perturbed 400 million years ago by a passing star from a circular orbit into an orbit with an eccentricity of 0.7. However, because Nemesis is so distant from the Sol, it is expected to be subject to perturbations by passing stars, and therefore its orbital period should shift by 15–30%. In particular, if Nemesis is a red dwarf or a brown dwarf, the WISE mission (an infrared sky survey that covered most of our Sol neighborhood in movement-verifying parallax measurements) was expected to be able to find it." .

ns1:45389 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1994 All-Ireland Senior Club Hurling Championship Final was a hurling match played at Croke Park on 1994 to determine the winners of the 1993–94 All-Ireland Senior Club Hurling Championship, the 24th season of the All-Ireland Senior Club Hurling Championship, a tournament organised by the Gaelic Athletic Association for the champion clubs of the four provinces of Ireland. The final was contested by Sarsfields GAA of Galway and Toomevara GAA of Tipperary, with Sarsfields GAA winning by 1-14 to 3-6. The All-Ireland final was a unique occasion as it was the first ever championship meeting between Sarsfields GAA and Toomevara GAA. Sarsfields GAA were hoping to become the first the first team to retain the title while Toomevara GAA were hoping to make their own history by winning their first All-Ireland title. Goals in the 13th, 29th and 44th minutes gave Toomevara GAA a three-point lead, however, Sarsfields GAA equalized with a 65th-minute goal from Michael Kenny after a Joe Cooney pass." .

ns1:45398 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Apamea was fought on 19 July 998 between the forces of the Byzantine Empire and the Fatimid Caliphate. Qalaat al-Madiq), surrounded by mountains and located near the Lake of Apamea, on 19 July 998. Arriving in Syria in mid-September, the emperor's army buried their fallen in the field of Apamea and then captured Shayzar, sacked the fortress of Masyaf and Rafaniya, torched Arqa, and raided the environs of Baalbek, Beirut, Tripoli and Jubayl." .

ns1:45407 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Search for Everything World Tour was a concert tour by American recording artist John Mayer in support of his seventh studio album The Search for Everything (2017)." .

ns1:45415 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Summis desiderantes affectibus (Latin for \"desiring with supreme ardor\"), sometimes abbreviated to Summis desiderantes was a papal bull regarding witchcraft issued by Innocent VIII on 5 14 December 1484." .

ns1:45423 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1975 World Table Tennis Championships were held in Kolkata, The entire tournament was held at the newly constructed Netaji Indoor Stadium, from 06 February 1975 to 16 February 1975." .

ns1:45435 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Vyborg took place in the spring of 1710 during the Great Northern War (1700–1721), as a second attempt by the Russians to capture the fortress port of Vyborg (Vyborg), near the modern border between Russia and Finland, after a failed attempt in 1706. Thirteen thousand troops under General-Admiral Fyodor Apraksin marched to Vyborg and laid siege on 22 01 March 1710. The selection of the command staff destined for the siege of Vyborg led to the main authority being given to General-Admiral Fyodor Apraksin, while his subordinates were Major-Generals Robert Bruce and Wilhelm Bergholtz. Russia had made a previous attempt to capture Vyborg; in October 1706, a siege corps was sent there under the command of Robert (Roman) Bruce. Peter's plan for the second siege of Vyborg involved a combination of land and sea forces, and the fort was to be taken by a prolonged siege. On 2 01 March 1710, General Apraksin received orders to attack Vyborg, and he planned to move out by 15 March. When the siege corps arrived at Vyborg on the 22nd, they occupied the town's outskirts and countryside, forcing the Swedes to regroup behind the town's inner stone wall. Thus, Myshlaevsky concluded that there were originally 12 cannon brought to Vyborg, but only ten of them were used in the siege, as two of them were disabled. However, other sources do not mention the truce-bearer or the naval battle; Fyodor Apraksin even wrote to the czar saying that he \"could not imagine\" how a Swedish fleet could be sent to Vyborg, although he did take precautions. Russia's occupation of Vyborg allowed the creation of a base to supply troops and build ships, and expanded the zone of action of the Baltic Fleet. Thus, in effect, the capture of Vyborg and Karelia served to determine the outcome of the Great Northern War by establishing a staging area for further military actions. Thus, after the Treaty of Nystad, Vyborg officially became incorporated into the Russian Empire as Vyborg." .

ns1:45524 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The athletics at the 1920 Summer Olympics – men's 3000 metres steeplechase event was part of the athletics at the 1920 Summer Olympics at the 1920 Summer Olympics. The competition was held on Wednesday, 18 August 1920 and on Friday, 20 August 1920." .

ns1:45539 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 AFC Champions League was the 34th edition of Asia's premier club association football tournament organized by the Asian Football Confederation (AFC), and the 13th under the current AFC Champions League title. Guangzhou Football Club won the tournament after defeating Al-Ahli in the 2015 AFC Champions League Final. The member associations are ranked based on their national team's and clubs' performance over the last four years in AFC competitions, with the allocation of slots for the 2015 and 2016 editions of the AFC club competitions determined by the 2014 rankings: The AFC Competitions Committee finalised the slot allocation for the 2015 and 2016 editions of the AFC Champions League based on the criteria, including AFC's MA rankings and the implementation of club licensing regulations, on 28 November 2014." .

ns1:45566 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1904 Cape Turnagain earthquake struck 10 kilometres (6.2 mi) north of Cape Turnagain on the morning of 09 August 1904 with a magnitude estimated at 6.8 Ms and 7.0–7.2 Mw It was felt throughout the North Island and upper South Island, with many communities within a 500 kilometres (310 mi) radius reporting noticeable ground shaking. It was the largest to strike New Zealand since the 7.0 Mw  North Canterbury earthquake in 1888. Seismograph data of the earthquake was held at stations in Melbourne and Honolulu, as well as various sites across New Zealand." .

ns1:45568 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Capture of Oaxaca was a battle during the Mexican War of Independence that occurred on 25 November 1812 at Oaxaca de Juárez, Oaxaca de Juárez. This army proceeded to march to Oaxaca de Juárez on 10 25 November 1812." .

ns1:45590 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Second Encirclement Campaign against Hubei-Henan-Anhui Soviet was an encirclement campaign launched by the Chinese Nationalist Government that was intended to destroy communist Hubei–Henan–Anhui Soviet and its Chinese Red Army in the local region. It was responded by the Communists' second counter-encirclement campaign at Hubei–Henan–Anhui Soviet (Chinese: 鄂豫皖苏区第二次苏区反围剿), also called by the communists as the second counter-encirclement campaign at Hubei–Henan–Anhui Revolutionary Base (Chinese: 鄂豫皖革命根据地第二次反围剿), in which the local Chinese Red Army successfully defended their soviet republic in the border region of Hubei, Henan, and Anhui provinces against the Nationalist attacks from 01 April 1931 to 01 July 1931." .

ns1:45592 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014–15 Derby County F.C. season is the 109th season in the Football League and 116th season overall of association football played by Derby County F.C., an English football club based in Derby County F.C., Derbyshire. Derby County F.C. retained 21 players from the 2013–14 Derby County F.C. season, these were players still under contract going into the new season. Several of Derby County F.C.'s players had come to the end of their contracts in the 2013–14 Derby County F.C. season and were therefore released by the club." .

ns1:45597 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Provisional Tungus Central National Government (PT-CNG; Russian: Временное Центральное Тунгусское Национальное Управление, Vremennoye Tsentral'noye Tungusskoye Natsional'noye Upravleniye), more commonly known as Tungus Republic (Russian: Тунгусская республика, Tungusskaya Respublika) was a short-lived unrecognized secessionist state covering mostly Okhotsk region and the eastern regions of the Yakut ASSR from 01 July 1924 to 01 May 1925." .

ns1:45619 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Bileća was fought in August 1388 between the forces of the Kingdom of Bosnia led by Grand Duke of the kingdom, Vlatko Vuković, and the Ottoman Turks under the leadership of Lala Şahin Pasha. King Tvrtko's army, led by his most capable and trusted general, Grand Duke of Bosnia, Vlatko Vuković, allowed the Turks to penetrate as far as the town of Bileća." .

ns1:45628 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Yokozuna Memorial Show was a professional wrestling event produced by World Xtreme Wrestling (WXW) promotion which took place on 29 November 2001, at the Lehigh Valley Sports Zone (WXW Arena) in Allentown, Pennsylvania. 29 November 2001 in Allentown, Pennsylvania (WXW Arena)" .

ns1:45647 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The alpine skiing at the 1998 Winter Olympics – men's Super-G competition of the alpine skiing at the 1998 Winter Olympics was held at Hakuba on Monday, February 16." .

ns1:45659 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Blondes 'Ave More Fun Tour was a worldwide concert tour held by British singer-songwriter Rod Stewart to promote his album Blondes Have More Fun. The tour began on 20 November 1978 in Paris and ended on 28 June 1979 in Los Angeles, California." .

ns1:45667 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 5th edition of Miss Diva 2017 took place on 2017. At the finale held on 2017, Roshmitha Harimurthy along with the reigning Miss Universe 2016 Iris Mittenaere crowned Shraddha Shashidhar as Miss Diva , while Srinidhi Shetty crowned Peden Ongmu as Miss Diva and Aradhana Buragohain crowned Apeksha Porwal as Miss Diva 2017 2nd runner up. The two winners represented India at Miss Universe 2017, Miss Supranational 2017." .

ns1:45671 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Lion Air Flight 904 was a scheduled domestic passenger flight from Husein Sastranegara International Airport in Bandung to Ngurah Rai International Airport in Bali, Indonesia. In January 2017, Budi Waseso, the chief of Indonesia's national narcotics agency, said that the pilot of Lion Air Flight 904 was under the influence of drugs at the time of the accident, and had hallucinated that the sea was part of the runway." .

ns1:45672 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1934 Air France Wibault 282T crash occurred on 09 May 1934 when Wibault 282T-12 F-AMHY of Air France crashed into the English Channel off Dungeness, Kent, United Kingdom while operating an international scheduled passenger flight from Paris–Le Bourget Airport, Paris, France to Croydon Airport, Surrey, United Kingdom. The aircraft was operating a scheduled international passenger flight from Paris–Le Bourget Airport, Paris, France to Croydon Airport, Surrey, United Kingdom." .

ns1:45689 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Supercoppa Italiana was the 27th edition of the Supercoppa Italiana, Italian football supercup, played on 22 December 2014 at the Jassim Bin Hamad Stadium in Doha, Qatar. It was held between 2013–14 Serie A champions, Juventus F.C., and the winners of the 2013–14 Coppa Italia, S.S.C. Napoli. The game was tentatively scheduled to be played on 2014, but due to S.S.C. Napoli's participation in the 2014–15 UEFA Champions League play-off round, the team asked that the game be rescheduled during Serie A's winter break. S.S.C. Napoli emerged as the victorious side 6–5 in a penalty shootout, following a 2–2 draw, to pick up their second trophy in the tournament. Qatar became the fifth different country to host a Supercoppa Italiana. Both of S.S.C. Napoli's prior appearances had been against Juventus F.C., beating them 5–1 in 1990 but losing 2–4 after extra time in 2012. Carlos Tevez put Juventus F.C. ahead in the fifth minute following defensive errors by S.S.C. Napoli." .

ns1:45712 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 European Darts Trophy was the eighth of ten PDC European Tour events on the 2016 PDC Pro Tour. The tournament took place at RWE Arena in Mülheim an der Ruhr, Germany, between 09 September 2016–11 September 2016." .

ns1:45718 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 Hong Kong Super Series was a top level badminton competition contested from 15 November 2011 to 20 November 2011 in Hong Kong." .

ns1:45719 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Glasgow School was a circle of influential artists and designers that began to coalesce in Glasgow, Scotland in the 1870s, and flourished from the 1890s to around 1910. Glasgow experienced an economic boom at the end of the 19th century, resulting in an increase in distinctive contributions to the Art Nouveau movement, particularly in the fields of architecture, interior design and art of painting." .

ns1:45733 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Le Mesnil-Patry during the World War II, was the last attack by an armoured battle group conducted by Canadian troops in Normandy in 11 June 1944. On 6 11 June 1944, Allied forces invaded France in Operation Neptune, the landings of Operation Overlord. following the action at Le Mesnil-Patry, troops of the 12th SS-Panzer Division captured seven Canadians, who had been wandering around no-man's land since the battle, all being tired and hungry. With the murders of prisoners taken at Le Mesnil-Patry on 11 June 1944, 147 Canadians are known to have been killed by the 12th SS-Panzer Division." .

ns1:45735 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The alpine skiing at the 1988 Winter Olympics – men's downhill competition of the alpine skiing at the 1988 Winter Olympics was held at the newly-developed Nakiska on Mount Allan on Monday, February 15." .

ns1:45746 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Atlantic Coast Conference football season was an NCAA football season that was played from 29 August 2013, to 2014." .

ns1:45748 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "LAN Chile Flight 160 was a Boeing 727-116 on a flight from Buenos Aires, Argentina to Santiago, Chile carrying 8 crew and 52 passengers which crashed during approach to Santiago on 28 April 1969. Flight 160 took off from Ministro Pistarini International Airport at 23:56 GMT (20:56 local time), but when nearing Santiago the Boeing 727 descended below the minimum height of 2829 feet and kept on descending until it struck the ground in farm land north of Colina, Chile." .

ns1:45754 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 South Point 400 was a Monster Energy NASCAR Cup Series South Point 400 held on 16 September 2018 at Las Vegas Motor Speedway in Las Vegas. Contested over 272 laps -- extended from 267 laps due to an overtime finish, on the 1.5 mi (2.4 km) asphalt intermediate speedway, it was the 27th South Point 400 of the 2018 Monster Energy NASCAR Cup Series season, first South Point 400 of the Playoffs, and the first South Point 400 of the Round of 16. Erik Jones scored the pole for the South Point 400 with a time of 28.705 and a speed of 188.121 mph (302.751 km/h)." .

ns1:45757 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1994 Tour of the Basque Country was the 34th edition of the Itzulia Basque Country cycle race and was held from 04 April 1994 to 08 April 1994." .

ns1:45759 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Omloop Het Nieuwsblad was a road cycling one-day race that took place on 24 February 2018 in Belgium. It was the 73rd edition of the Omloop Het Nieuwsblad and the fourth event of the 2018 UCI World Tour. It was won by Danish rider Michael Valgren for the Astana team, soloing to victory by 12 seconds ahead of Team Sky's Łukasz Wiśniowski of Poland, with the podium completed by the highest-placed home rider Sep Vanmarcke (EF Education First–Drapac p/b Cannondale)." .

ns1:45774 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1972 European Athletics Indoor Championships were held between 11 March 1972–12 March 1972 in Grenoble, France." .

ns1:45801 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Seamen's strike of 1922 began on 12 January 1922, when Chinese seamen from Hong Kong and Canton (now Guangzhou) went on strike for higher wages. Though the strike was declared illegal by the Hong Kong government, negotiations eventually took place after 52 days, with employers capitulating on 5 08 March 1922 and agreeing to wage increases of 15–30%. The shipping companies refused to increase their salary, and the union reacted by going on strike starting in 12 January 1922." .

ns1:45806 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Stockholm Bloodbath (Swedish: Stockholms blodbad, Danish: Det Stockholmske Blodbad) was a trial that led to a series of executions in Stockholm between 07 November 1520 and 09 November 1520." .

ns1:45813 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Combined Bomber Offensive (CBO) was an Allied offensive of strategic bombing during World War II in Europe. The Combined Bomber Offensive began on 10 June 1943 during the British bombing campaign against German industry in the Ruhr area known as the \"Battle of the Ruhr\". After Arnold submitted the October 9, 1943 \"Plan to Assure the Most Effective Exploitation of the Combined Bomber Offensive\" on October 22 the \"Allied Joint Chiefs of Staff\" signed orders to raid \"the aircraft industries in the southern Germany and Austria regions\". Tedder's proposal to keep oil targets as the highest priority and place \"Germany's rail system in second priority was approved by the CSTC on November 1 On 12 April 1945, Strategic Bombing Directive No." .

ns1:45818 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 Pétion-Ville school collapse occurred on 07 November 2008, in Pétion-Ville, a suburb of Port-au-Prince, Haiti, when the church-operated Collège La Promesse Évangélique (\"The Evangelical Promise School\") collapsed at around 10:00 a.m. local time (15:00 GMT)." .

ns1:45829 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "George Lott and Anna McCune Harper defeated Ian Collins and Joan Ridley in the final, 6–3, 1–6, 6–1 to win the Mixed Doubles tennis title at the 1931 Wimbledon Championships." .

ns1:45843 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "According to some early medieval sources, the Siege of Exeter or Siege of Caer-Uisc was a military conflict that took place in or around 630 CE, between the Mercians, led by Penda of Mercia, and the Britons occupying Caer-Uisc (Exeter) in the kingdom of Dumnonia. The heathen Penda besieges Exeter in the year 634, and the siege is raised by the Briton Cadwalla." .

ns1:45868 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Mi Delirio World Tour (better known as \"MDWT\") is the first worldwide concert tour by Mexican singer Anahí, in support of her fifth studio album, Mi Delirio (2009). In June 2010, was officially announced by Guillermo Rosas, Anahí's manager, and RRPRO (Rafael Reisman Productions) that MDWT was planned to have a second stage called Mi Delirio World Tour Reloaded." .

ns1:45872 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Tabriz took place in 1501 just after the Safavids had defeated the Aq Qoyunlu in the Battle of Sarur. After the siege Ismail I chose Tabriz as his capital and proclaimed himself Shahanshah of Iran." .

ns1:45873 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Peteneras is a flamenco palo in a 12-beat metre, with strong beats distributed as follows: ." .

ns1:45880 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Tour Over Europe 1980 was the last concert tour by the English rock band Led Zeppelin. The tour ran from 17 June 1980 to 07 July 1980. The final full-length concert Led Zeppelin played until 2007 was on 07 July 1980, with \"Whole Lotta Love\" being the closing number at this gig." .

ns1:45886 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "A special election was held in 2006 California's 50th congressional district special election to choose a new member of the U.S. Representative to fill the vacancy caused by the resignation of Republican Randy Cunningham, who resigned November 28, 2005 after pleading guilty to bribery, wire fraud, mail fraud, and tax evasion charges. As no candidate won a simple majority, the top vote-getters in each party, Democrat Francine Busby and Republican Brian Bilbray, competed in a runoff special general election held on 06 June 2006, the same day that primaries were held for the November 2006 general election. In the 06 June 2006 primary election for the November 2006 election, both candidates won their party's nomination. The 06 June 2006 run-off was held at the same time as the primary election for the biennial November 7, 2006 general election. She faced the leading vote getter from the two other parties participating: Republican Brian Bilbray and Libertarian Paul King, as well as independent candidate William Griffith, in a 06 June 2006 runoff. For the new term beginning in 2007, the primaries were held on 06 June 2006, concurrent with the special election." .

ns1:45890 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The World Infestation Tour was a concert tour by American glam metal band Ratt in support of their latest album Invasion of Your Privacy." .

ns1:45899 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Singapore Open (officially known as the 2018 Singapore Open Badminton Championships) was a badminton tournament which took place at the Singapore Indoor Stadium in Singapore from 17 to 22 July 2018 and had a total purse of $355,000. The 2018 Singapore Open was the fourteenth tournament of the 2018 BWF World Tour and also part of the Singapore Open Badminton Championships championships, which had been held since 1987. This international tournament was held at Singapore Indoor Stadium in Singapore." .

ns1:45920 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The archery at the 2008 Summer Olympics – men's team at the 2008 Summer Olympics was part of the archery at the 2008 Summer Olympics and took place at the Olympic Green Archery Field. As the defending archery at the 2004 Summer Olympics – men's team, South Korea defended the title with two archers from the past Games, Im Dong-hyun and Park Kyung-Mo." .

ns1:45930 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Copa do Brasil Final was the final two-legged tie that decided the 2018 Copa do Brasil, the 30th season of the Copa do Brasil, Brazil's national cup association football tournament organised by the Brazilian Football Confederation. The finals were contested in a two-legged home-and-away format between the defending champions Cruzeiro E.C., from Minas Gerais, and S.C. Corinthians Paulista, from São Paulo. Cruzeiro E.C. and S.C. Corinthians Paulista reached the Copa do Brasil finals for the eighth and sixth time, respectively. The first leg was hosted by Cruzeiro E.C. at Mineirão in Belo Horizonte on 2018, while the second leg was hosted by S.C. Corinthians Paulista at Arena S.C. Corinthians Paulista in São Paulo on 2018. Cruzeiro E.C. defeated S.C. Corinthians Paulista 3–1 on aggregate in the finals to win their sixth title. In the finals, the teams played a single-elimination tournament with the following rules: In the second leg of the semi-finals, Douglas (S.C. Corinthians Paulista) picked up a yellow card and Sassá (Cruzeiro E.C.) was sent off which meant they were suspended for the first leg of the Finals. A goal from Thiago Neves gave Cruzeiro E.C. a 1–0 win over S.C. Corinthians Paulista in the first leg. Egídio (Cruzeiro E.C.), booked in the first leg, Ángelo Araos (S.C. Corinthians Paulista) sent off in the first leg, and Sassá (Cruzeiro E.C.), suspended six games, were ruled out of the second leg. In the second leg, Cruzeiro E.C. defeated S.C. Corinthians Paulista 1–2 to lift the Copa do Brasil." .

ns1:45960 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1993 Bombay bombings were a series of 13 terrorist bombings that took place in Mumbai, India, then known as Bombay, on 12 March 1993. After India's three-judge Supreme Court bench rejected his curative petition, saying the grounds raised by him do not fall within the principles laid down by the apex court in 2002, the Maharashtra state government executed Yakub Memon on 30 July 2015. On 9 12 March 1993, three days before the bombings took place, a small-time criminal from the Behrampada slum in Northeast Mumbai named Gul Noor Mohammad Sheikh (Gullu) was detained at the Nag Pada police station. The locations attacked: The official number of fatalities was 257 with 1,400 others injured (some sources reported that 317 people died; this misreport was due to a bomb which killed 69 in Calcutta on 16 March and was not part of 12 March 1993 Bombay bombings). On 10 July 2006, the Chief Minister of Maharashtra, Sharad Pawar, admitted that he had \"deliberately misled\" people following the 1993 Mumbai bombings by saying there were \"13 and not 12\" explosions and had added the name of a Muslim-dominated locality to show that people from both communities had been affected." .

ns1:45973 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Brookledge Follies is a variety show presented in a small theater located behind a historic Spanish-style mansion near Hancock Park in Los Angeles, California. Created by Erika Larsen in 2009, the invitation-only show was designed to showcase a wide range of variety talent on an occasional basis." .

ns1:45974 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1891 Chilean presidential election took place through a system of electors, and resulted in the election as President of Chile of Jorge Montt, who was the sole candidate." .

ns1:45988 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "In the Battle of Cape Finisterre (22 July 1805) off Galicia, Spain, the British fleet under Admiral Robert Calder fought an indecisive naval battle against the combined Franco-Spanish fleet which was returning from the West Indies. So with some ships in bad condition, tired crews and scarce victuals, the combined fleet sighted land near Cape Finisterre on 22 July 1805." .

ns1:46006 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "sailing at the 2012 Summer Paralympics in London was held from August in Weymouth and Isle of Portland. Boats were prepared and launched from the docks of the Weymouth and Isle of Portland Sailing Academy." .

ns1:46031 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Orleans took place in the year 463 pitting the forces of the Kingdom of Soissons, under the command of the magister militum Aegidius, against those of the Visigoths who were commanded by the Visigoth King Theodoric II and his brother Federico. The two armies met at Orléans in 463." .

ns1:46033 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of the Argeş was a battle of the Romania during World War I of World War I. Taking place on 01 December 1916, the battle was fought along the line of the Argeș River in Romania between Austro-German forces of the Central Powers and Romanian forces with the Russian imperial forces. After the battle, minor actions were fought in the fortifications surrounding Bucharest between the invading Germans and the Romanian reserves which had failed to arrive and the remnants of the defenders of the Sereth–Putna line, yet it was occupied by the Germans on 6 01 December 1916, which was the same day that southern Romania capitulated, as the monarchy had fled to Iași." .

ns1:46038 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "In these perspectives, criticism of the welfare state often goes alongside criticism of the structural issues of capitalism and the inability for social welfare measures to solve fundamental economic issues which Marxists consider inherent to the capitalist mode of production." .

ns1:46050 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Ethias Trophy was a professional tennis tournament played on hard courts. It took place in Mons, Belgium between 29 September 2014 and 05 October 2014." .

ns1:46065 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007–08 European Challenge Cup was the 12th year of the European Challenge Cup, the second tier rugby union cup competition below the Heineken Cup. The tournament was held between 08 November 2007 and 25 May 2008." .

ns1:46074 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "neo-Luddism or new Luddism is a philosophy opposing many forms of modern technology." .

ns1:46075 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Nybøl was fought on the 28 May 1848 between the Danish Army and Germans in Sundeved." .

ns1:46076 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "A German referendum, 1929 was held in Weimar Republic on 22 December 1929." .

ns1:46079 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1988 Internationaux de Strasbourg was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor clay court in Strasbourg, France, and was part of the Category 2 tier of the 1988 WTA Tour. It was the second edition of the tournament and ran from 16 May 1988 until 22 May 1988." .

ns1:46083 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013–14 Swedish Hockey League season was the 39th season of the Swedish Hockey League (SHL). It was the first season since the league changed name from Elitserien to Swedish Hockey League in June 2012–13 Elitserien season. Media related to 2012–13 Elitserien season-2014 Swedish Hockey League season at Wikimedia Commons." .

ns1:46085 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1st Grey Cup game was played on 04 December 1909, between the Intercollegiate Rugby Football Union champion University of Toronto Varsity Blues and the Ontario Rugby Football Union champion Toronto Parkdale Canoe Club." .

ns1:46086 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Arnaud Clément and Sébastien Grosjean won in the final 6–3, 4–6, 7–5 against Wayne Black and Kevin Ullyett." .

ns1:46087 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Renfrew was fought between the Kingdom of the Isles and the Kingdom of Scotland in 1164, near Renfrew, Scotland. The fact that the battle is said to have been fought at Renfrew, the seat of one of Walter's lordships, could indicate that he was a specific target. The stated location of Renfrew could be evidence that the target of Somairle's strike was Walter fitz Alan, Steward of Scotland." .

ns1:46093 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Incumbent leader and Prime Minister Mirek Topolánek faced Mayor of Prague Pavel Bém who was supported by President Václav Klaus. Topolánek was re-elected when he received votes from 284 delegates while Bém received only 162 votes. He received 284 votes against Bém's 162 and won the election." .

ns1:46106 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The judo at the 2016 Summer Olympics – women's 78 kg competition in judo at the 2016 Summer Olympics in Rio de Janeiro was held on 11 August 2016 at the Carioca Arena 2." .

ns1:46112 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Frontier Middle School shooting was a school shooting that occurred on 02 February 1996, at Frontier Middle School in Moses Lake, United States." .

ns1:46119 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Death of a Bachelor Tour was a concert tour by Panic! At The Disco, in support of the group's fifth studio album Death of a Bachelor (2016)." .

ns1:46125 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "black anarchism is a term applied to a group of people of African descent who identify with the principles of anarchism. black anarchism has had a major influence on the anarchist movement, black anarchists have a 100 year history in black led anti fascist and anti-racism history." .

ns1:46126 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Het Bijltjesoproer (\"Hatchet Riot\") or Kattenburgoproer (\"Kattenburg Riot\") of 30 May 1787 was an uprising of Dutch Orangist ship carpenters in the Kattenburg quarter of Amsterdam against the city's Patriots regenten. On the other hand, the Patriots movement was gaining strength since 1780, started arming themselves in 1783 and eventually seized control in several cities throughout the Netherlands, such as Utrecht. On 9 30 May 1787, the first pitched battle occurred near Jutphaas in the province of Utrecht, resulting in a Patriots victory." .

ns1:46136 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "taekwondo at the taekwondo at the 2016 Summer Olympics in Rio de Janeiro took place from 17 to 20 August 2016 at the Carioca Arena 3 inside the Barra Olympic Park in Barra da Tijuca. Around 128 taekwondo fighters competed in eight weight categories; four for men, and four for women. taekwondo competition at these Games featured a total of 128 athletes, 64 in each gender, and 16 in each of the eight weight categories. After finishing in third in taekwondo at the 2012 Summer Olympics, this Olympics marks South Korea's return to the top of the taekwondo medal standings." .

ns1:46144 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The \"The Keep Calm & Play Louder Tour\" was the second theatre tour undertaken by British pop-rock band McFly. The tour began on 07 March 2012 and ended on 21 April 2012." .

ns1:46149 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1999–2000 Prva HNL was the ninth season of the Prva HNL, the national championship for men's association football teams in Croatia, since its establishment in 1992. The season started on 24 July 1999 and ended on 13 May 2000. GNK Dinamo Zagreb (Croatia Zagreb in fall season) were the defending champions, having won their ninth championship title the previous season, and they defended the title again, after a win against Rijeka on 6 13 May 2000." .

ns1:46163 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2004 Lexmark Indy 300 was the thirteenth and penultimate round of the 2004 Champ Car season, held on 24 October 2004 on the Surfers Paradise Street Circuit, Queensland, Australia. Paul Tracy won the pole and Bruno Junqueira won the race." .

ns1:46167 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Gijón International Film Festival (Asturian: Festival Internacional de Cine de Xixón or FICXixón) is an annual film festival held in Gijón, a city in northwest Spain. In the beginning it was an initiative of the City Council of Gijón in co-operation with Caja de Ahorros de Asturias (Cajastur)." .

ns1:46193 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Switzerland at the 2015 Summer Universiade participated at the 2015 Summer Universiade in Gwangju, South Korea." .

ns1:46197 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1995 German Grand Prix (formally the XXIV Grosser Mobil 1 Preis von Deutschland) was a Formula One motor race held on 30 July 1995 at the Hockenheimring, Hockenheim. The 45-lap race was won by Benetton driver Michael Schumacher after he started from second position. Damon Hill started the race from the pole position alongside Michael Schumacher." .

ns1:46200 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Tipton's Island was an engagement between a Shawnee war party and Indiana militia under command of John Tipton in 01 April 1813 on the White River near Seymour, Indiana. In 01 April 1813, during the War of 1812, a Shawnee war party killed two white settlers eight miles from Fort Vallonia." .

ns1:46222 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1970 Federation Cup was the eighth edition of what is now known as the Billie Jean King Cup. 22 nations participated in the tournament, which was held at the Freiburg Tennis Club in Freiburg, West Germany from 19 May 1970–24 May 1970. Australia Fed Cup team defeated West Germany in the final. West Germany vs. Australia Fed Cup team." .

ns1:46224 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 AFC Champions League was the 33rd edition of the top-level Asian club association football tournament organized by the Asian Football Confederation (AFC), and the 12th under the current AFC Champions League title. In the 2014 AFC Champions League Final, Western Sydney Wanderers FC of Australia defeated Al-Hilal of Saudi Arabia 1–0 on aggregate, to become the first Australian team to win the title, and in doing so qualified for the 2014 FIFA Club World Cup. The AFC laid out the procedure for deciding the participating associations and the allocation of slots, with inspection of the associations interested in participating in the AFC Champions League to be done in 2013, and the 2014 AFC Champions League Final decision to be made by the AFC on 26 November 2013. The AFC Competitions Committee proposed the following participating criteria for the 2014–2016 editions of the AFC Champions League on 12 March 2013: On 26 November 2013, the AFC Executive Committee approved the slots for the 2014 edition of the AFC Champions League." .

ns1:46246 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Execution of Louis XVI by guillotine, a major event of the French Revolution, took place on 21 January 1793 at the Place de la Révolution (\"Revolution Square\", formerly Place Louis XV, and renamed Place de la Concorde in 1795) in Paris. At Trial of Louis XVI on 17 21 January 1793, the National Convention had convicted the king of high treason in a near-unanimous vote; while no one voted \"not guilty\", several deputies abstained." .

ns1:46250 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Delaware Bay, or the Battle of Cape May, was a naval engagement fought between the Great Britain and United States of America during the American Revolutionary War. The ensuing combat in Delaware Bay, near Cape May, ended with an American victory over a superior British force." .

ns1:46254 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2011–12 Egyptian parliamentary election were held in Egypt held from 28 November 2011 to 11 January 2012, following the revolution that ousted President Hosni Mubarak, after which the Supreme Council of the Armed Forces (SCAF) dissolved Parliament. On 2011, an administrative court in Mansoura ruled that former NDP members were not allowed to stand in the election as independent candidates. The election for the upper house, the Shura Council (\"the Consultative Council\") are to follow on 2012, and will take place in 3 stages as well between 29 January and 22 February. Only then will presidential election be held, \"no later than 2012\" according to Hussein Tantawi's statement. The liberal New Wafd Party announced on 2011 that it would contest the election in an alliance with the Freedom and Justice Party. On 2012, the Supreme Constitutional Court of Egypt ruled that the election was unconstitutional, and one third of the winners were illegitimate. Mohamed Morsi, sworn as Egypt's new president on 30 June, has initially refrained from speaking publicly on that (crucial to the extent of his own real power) issue." .

ns1:46259 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1998 FA Trophy Final was the 29th final of The Football Association's cup competition for levels 5–8 of the English football league system. It was contested by Cheltenham Town F.C. and Southport F.C. on 1998 at Wembley Stadium, London." .

ns1:46269 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Mrs. Carter Show World Tour was the fifth concert tour by American singer Beyoncé. It began in Belgrade, Serbia on 15 April 2013, and concluded in Lisbon, Portugal on 27 March 2014. The set list of the 2013 portion of the tour included approximately twenty five songs from Beyoncé's four solo studio albums: Dangerously in Love (2003), B'Day (2006), I Am... Sasha Fierce (2008), and 4 (2011). Tickets for Rock in Rio became available for general sale on April 4, 2013 and within two hours, the first day of the festival, in which Beyoncé was headlining, had sold out all 65,000 tickets. A video titled \"Goodbye to The Mrs. Carter Show World Tour\" was uploaded by the singer on her official YouTube channel on 27 March 2014 showing portions of live performances from the final leg of the tour, behind the scenes moments and visits to cities as \"XO\" was played in the background." .

ns1:46288 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The June 2015 Turkish general election took place on 07 June 2015 in all 85 electoral districts of Turkey to elect 550 members to the Grand National Assembly. In the end, no such initiative was taken and Supreme Electoral Council of Turkey (YSK) went on with the date 07 June 2015. The candidate lists were drawn up such that BBP candidates were placed top in electoral districts in which they won more votes than the SP in 2011 Turkish general election, while SP candidates were placed top in province of Turkey in which the SP had beaten the BBP in 2011 Turkish general election. On 5 January 2015, the Supreme Electoral Council of Turkey announced that the general election would take place on 07 June 2015 and also released a decision in regards to the process for standing as a candidate. The party's leader Masum Türker claimed that the abolition of the Presidency of Religious Affairs would cause division in Turkey and also predicted that an early election would occur after 07 June 2015 since no party would win a majority. The Supreme Electoral Council of Turkey announced the fully confirmed results on 18 07 June 2015. The 25th Parliament of Turkey was sworn in on 23 07 June 2015, chaired temporarily by the new parliament's oldest member Deniz Baykal." .

ns1:46291 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Maria Bueno and Darlene Hard defeated Robyn Ebbern and Margaret Smith in the final, 8–6, 9–7 to win the Ladies' Doubles tennis title at the 1963 Wimbledon Championships." .

ns1:46324 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1965 Rhodesian general election were held in Southern Rhodesia on 07 May 1965." .

ns1:46333 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1996 West Virginia gubernatorial election took place on 1996 to elect the Governor of West Virginia. Republican Cecil H. Underwood, who had previously been the Governor of West Virginia from 1957 to 1961, defeated Democratic State Senator Charlotte Pritt." .

ns1:46339 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1932 French Grand Prix (official name: XVIII Grand Prix de l'Automobile Club de France) was a Grand Prix motor race held at Reims-Gueux on 03 July 1932." .

ns1:46340 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Pro Bowl (branded as the 2017 Pro Bowl presented by Aquafina for sponsorship reasons) was the National Football League's all-star game for the 2016 NFL season, which was played at Camping World Stadium in Orlando on 29 January 2017. The game was the first in a three-year deal to host the Pro Bowl in Orlando, which also included cross-promotional events (such as a newly-established skills competition) held at the Walt Disney World Resort (which is owned by the primary parent company of the game's broadcaster, ESPN). On June 1, 2016, the NFL announced that it had awarded the next three Pro Bowl games to Orlando. The game format was nearly the same for 2017 as it had been in 2016, with some exceptions: at Camping World Stadium in Orlando." .

ns1:46341 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 Copa Libertadores Finals was the final two-legged series that determined the 2010 Copa Libertadores champion. It was contested between Mexican club C.D. Guadalajara and Brazil club S.C. Internacional. The first leg was played on 11 August in C.D. Guadalajara's home field, while the second leg was played in S.C. Internacional's. S.C. Internacional, as the champions, played the winners of the 2010 Copa Sudamericana in the 2011 Recopa Sudamericana. S.C. Internacional, by virtue of being the last South American team in the competition, was predetermined to enter the semifinals of the 2010 FIFA Club World Cup before winning the finals. Prior to the 2010 final, Chivas and S.C. Internacional had previously met two times in South American competition. S.C. Internacional, on the other hand, are appearing on their third final after losing in 1980 to Nacional." .

ns1:46346 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "taekwondo at the 2004 Summer Olympics were held in the Sports Pavilion at the Faliro Coastal Zone Olympic Complex in Athens, Greece where 124 competitors competed in eight events, four each for men and women. A total of 124 taekwondo jins from 60 nations competed at the Athens Games:" .

ns1:46347 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Hexham, 15 May 1464, marked the end of significant Lancastrian resistance in the north of England during the early part of the reign of Edward IV. The battle was fought near the town of Hexham in Northumberland. The two sides met outside Hexham on 14 15 May 1464. Neville showed little of Edward's conciliatory spirit, and had thirty leading Lancastrians executed in Hexham on the evening following the battle, including Henry Beaufort, 3rd Duke of Somerset, and Lord Roos. Following his defeat in the Battle of Hexham on 15 May 1464, Henry VI found refuge, sheltered by Lancastrian supporters, at houses across the north of England." .

ns1:46387 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "A Egyptian constitutional referendum, 2014 was held in Egypt on 14 January 2014 and 15 January 2014 and with Egyptians abroad voting between 8 January and 12 January. The Freedom and Justice Party and the Islamic Bloc, which had won 65.3% of the vote in Egypt's parliamentary elections in 2011–2012, opposed the new Constitution and the referendum as being the fruits of an illegal military coup." .

ns1:46392 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Little Muddy Creek, also known as the Lame Deer Fight, was fought on 07 May 1877–8, 1877, by United States soldiers and scouts against a village of Miniconjou Lakota and Northern Cheyenne. The battle occurred near Little Muddy Creek in Montana Territory, near present-day Lame Deer, Rosebud County." .

ns1:46407 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1985 Israel Super Cup was the 15th Israel Super Cup (20th, including unofficial matches, as the competition wasn't played within the Israel Football Association in its first 5 editions, until 1969), an annual Israel football match played between the winners of the previous season's Top Division and Israel State Cup. The match was played between Maccabi Haifa F.C., champions of the 1984–85 Liga Leumit and Beitar Jerusalem F.C., winners of the 1984–85 Israel State Cup. This was Maccabi Haifa F.C.'s 3rd Israel Super Cup appearance (including unofficial matches) and Beitar's 4th. At the match, played at Thomas D'Alesandro Stadium, Maccabi Haifa F.C. won 5–2." .

ns1:46416 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siamese coup d'état of 1947 (Thailand: รัฐประหาร 8 พฤศจิกายน พ.ศ. 2490) was a Thailand coup d'état that took place on the evening of 07 November 1947, ending in the early morning hours of 08 November 1947." .

ns1:46425 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1963–64 Football League Cup was the fourth season of the EFL Cup, a knockout competition for England's top 92 football clubs. The competition began on 04 September 1963, and ended with the two-legged final on 15 22 April 1964 and 22 April 1964. Leicester City F.C. win 4–3 on aggregate." .

ns1:46430 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Miss Universe 1976, the 25th Miss Universe pageant, was held on 11 July 1976 at the Lee Theatre in Hong Kong." .

ns1:46438 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Our Version of Events Tour was a debut global concert tour by Scottish singer Emeli Sandé, in support of her 2012 debut album, Our Version of Events." .

ns1:46456 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Sibiu Open was a professional tennis tournament played on clay courts. It took place in Sibiu, Romania between 17 September 2018 and 23 September 2018." .

ns1:46466 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1952 Tour de Romandie was the sixth edition of the Tour de Romandie cycle race and was held from 17 April 1952 to 20 April 1952." .

ns1:46473 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The American Music Awards of 2016 took place on 20 November 2016, at the Microsoft Theater in Los Angeles. It was broadcast on American Broadcasting Company and was hosted by Gigi Hadid and Jay Pharoah." .

ns1:46497 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "LoveMusik is a musical written by Alfred Uhry, using a selection of music by Kurt Weill. The show was directed by Harold Prince with musical staging by Patricia Birch and starred Michael Cerveris as Kurt Weill, Donna Murphy as Lotte Lenya, David Pittu as Bertolt Brecht and John Scherer as George Davis. This bio-musical about the marital and professional relationship of the German-born composer Kurt Weill and the actress Lotte Lenya, directed by Harold Prince, is sluggish, tedious and (hold your breath) unmissable.\"" .

ns1:46500 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems (CHI) series of academic conferences is generally considered the most prestigious in the field of human–computer interaction and is one of the top-ranked conferences in computer science. CHI has been held annually since 1982 and attracts thousands of international attendees. The CHI conference series started with the Human Factors in Computer Systems conference in Gaithersburg, Maryland, US in 1982, organized by Bill Curtis and Ben Shneiderman. ACM SIGCHI became the sponsor of the Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems." .

ns1:46519 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1981 BMW Championships was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor grass courts at Devonshire Park in Eastbourne in the United Kingdom that was part of the Toyota Series category of the 1981 WTA Tour. It was the eighth edition of the tournament and was held from 15 June 1981 through 21 June 1981." .

ns1:46525 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 52nd Guldbagge Awards ceremony, presented by the Swedish Film Institute, honoring the best Swedish films of 2016 and took place on 2017 at Cirkus in Stockholm. The nominees for the 52nd Guldbagge Awards were announced on 2017 in Stockholm, by the Swedish Film Institute." .

ns1:46542 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Sandown 500 was an annual endurance motor race which was staged at the Sandown Raceway, near Melbourne, Victoria, Australia from 1964 to 2019. The second hiatus in the history of the race commenced in 1999 when a Queensland government-supported bid saw the Sandown 500 replaced on the Supercars calendar by the Queensland 500, held at Queensland Raceway. By 2003, new owners of Queensland Raceway had tired of the relative expense of the 500 kilometre endurance race format, resulting in the Sandown 500 again being contested by V8 Supercars. After a change of promoter of Sandown Raceway's motorsport activities, a changed V8 Supercars calendar resulted in the 500 kilometre event moving to the Phillip Island Grand Prix Circuit for the 2008 season, while Sandown reverted to hosting a sprint round, an event which became known as the Sandown Challenge. The Sandown 500 returned to the V8 Supercars calendar in 2012, replacing the Phillip Island 500 to again become the traditional lead-in race to the Bathurst 1000. 2015 saw Winterbottom win the Sandown 500 for a second time, having first tasted success in 2006, leading home a Prodrive Racing Australia one-two finish. It was also announced in the months leading up to the event that the Sandown 500 would not return in 2020, to be replaced by The Bend 500 at The Bend Motorsport Park." .

ns1:46546 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Día internacional de darileidy cortorreal (French: Journée internationale de la Francophonie) is observed within the Organisation internationale de la Francophonie's 77 member states every 20 March 1988 to celebrate the French language and Francophone culture. According to the Canadian then-minister for La Francophonie Steven Blaney's speech in 2013, the Día internacional de darileidy cortorreal is to \"celebrate our commitment to not only the French language and the rich and diverse francophone culture but also the values of peace, democracy and respect for human rights that unite all members of the Organisation internationale de la Francophonie\"." .

ns1:46547 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 ECM Prague Open was a tennis tournament played on outdoor clay courts. It was the 13th edition of the Prague Open, and was part of the Tier IV Series of the 2008 WTA Tour and of the 2008 ATP Challenger Tretorn Serie+. It took place at the I. Czech Lawn Tennis Club in Prague, Czech Republic, from April 28 through 04 May 2008." .

ns1:46560 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1997 Indian vice-presidential election was held on 16 August 1997 to elect Vice President of India. Krishan Kant defeated Surjit Singh Barnala to become 10th Vice President of India." .

ns1:46574 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Fort Lauderdale–Tampa Bay rivalry, also known as the Florida Derby, refers to the suspended association football rivalry that most recently involved the Fort Lauderdale Strikers and the Tampa Bay Rowdies, both of whom played in the North American Soccer League through the 2016 season." .

ns1:46580 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1981 Budweiser NASCAR 400 was a NASCAR Winston Cup Series racing event that took place on 1981, at Texas World Speedway in College Station, Texas." .

ns1:46589 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Tro-Bro Leon was the 24th edition of the Tro Bro Leon cycle race and was held on 22 April 2007." .

ns1:46591 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Buttevant Rail Disaster was a train crash that occurred at Buttevant Railway Station, County Cork in Ireland, 220 kilometres (137 mi) on the Dublin to Cork main line on 1 August 1980." .

ns1:46637 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "In May 2005, an unregistered editor posted a hoax article onto Wikipedia about journalist John Seigenthaler. In 2013, the hoax article was archived to Wikipedia:List of hoaxes on Wikipedia." .

ns1:46639 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The fifty-sixth edition of the 2014 Caribbean Series (Serie del Caribe) was played in 2014. It was held from February 1 through 08 February 2014 of 2014 with the champion baseball teams of Cuba, Naranjas de Villa Clara; Dominican Republic, Tigres del Licey; Mexico, Naranjeros de Hermosillo; Puerto Rico, Indios de Mayagüez, and Venezuela, Navegantes del Magallanes." .

ns1:46640 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Cheriyal scroll painting is a stylized version of Nakashi art, rich in the local motifs peculiar to the Telangana. They are at present made only in Hyderabad, Telangana, India. In the same way, Cheriyal scrolls must have been popular across Telangana in earlier times, though with the advent of television, cinemas and computers it has been fenced into its last outpost, the Cheriyal town. While the above-mentioned traditions could have significantly influenced the Cheriyal scroll paintings and artists, the Cheriyal paintings were and continue to be a distinctly local invention, peculiar to the Telangana region, drawing mainly on local traditions. It can safely be said that the local temple art traditions and the Kalamkari tradition across Telangana in particular, and the graphic art traditions of the Deccan and South India in general were the major influences that shaped and guided the art of scroll paintings." .

ns1:46645 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The athletics at the 2016 Summer Olympics – women's hammer throw competition at the 2016 Summer Olympics in Rio de Janeiro, Brazil. The event was held at the Olympic Stadium on 12 August 2016–15 August 2016. Anita Włodarczyk, the reigning World Champion and European Champion, was one of the stand-out gold medal favourites in the Olympic athletics programme, given that she held all ten of the best throws that year and a season's best of 80.26 m – the third best ever." .

ns1:46654 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2000 European Figure Skating Championships was a senior international figure skating competition in the 1999–2000 season. The event was held at the Wiener Stadthalle in Vienna, Austria from 06 February 2000 to 13, 2000." .

ns1:46658 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1941 Colima earthquake occurred on 15 April 1941 at 19:09 UTC (13:09 local time). The intensities were X (Extreme) in Colima, VIII (Severe) in Manzanillo, and V (Moderate) in Mexico City. This earthquake was very close to where the 1973 Colima earthquake occurred." .

ns1:46680 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "United Nations Security Council Resolution 355, adopted on 01 August 1974, recalled its resolutions 186, 353 and United Nations Security Council Resolution 354, noted that all States have declared their respect for Cyprus' sovereignty, independence and territorial integrity and requested the Secretary-General take appropriate action with regard to a possible cease-fire and report back to the Council." .

ns1:46710 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1995 Castilian-Leonese regional election was held on Sunday, 28 May 1995, to elect the 4th Cortes of the autonomous community of Castile and León. The 1991 Castilian-Leonese regional election was held on 26 May 1991, setting the election date for the Cortes on Sunday, 28 May 1995." .

ns1:46717 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "On 13 July 2011, Noar Linhas Aéreas Flight 4896, a Let L-410 Turbolet passenger aircraft on a domestic service from Recife to Mossoró, Brazil, crashed shortly after take-off in the Boa Viagem neighbourhood of Recife, after suffering an engine failure. The aircraft took off from Recife/Guararapes–Gilberto Freyre International Airport's runway 18 at 06:50 local time (09:50 UTC) bound for Augusto Severo International Airport, Natal and then Mossoró Airport, its final destination." .

ns1:46719 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Taeyunkak Hotel Fire was a skyscraper fire in Seoul, South Korea on 25 December 1971, that killed 164 people and injured 63 people." .

ns1:46720 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Second Battle of the Corunna Road (Spanish: Batalla de la Carretera de Coruña) was a battle of the Spanish Civil War that took place from 13 December 1936 to 15 January 1937, northwest of Madrid. In 13 December 1936, the Nationalists launched an offensive in order to cut the Corunna Road and isolate Madrid, but a Republican counter-offensive stopped the Nationalist advance. However, by 15 January 1937 both sides were exhausted and the battle ceased. After the Third Battle of the Corunna Road in 15 January 1937 with similar results, the next Nationalist attempt to encircle Madrid was the Battle of Jarama, taking place between 6 February and 27 February." .

ns1:46741 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Novara was a battle of the War of the League of Cambrai fought on 06 June 1513, near Novara, in Northern Italy. Nevertheless, the French under King Louis XII were driven out of the city of Milan the following month by the War of the League of Cambrai. In 06 June 1513, the French army, consisting of more than 20,000 under Louis de la Trémoille, besieged the city of Novara, which was held by Swiss mercenaries. By 06 June 1513, most of the western part of the duchy of Milan had been occupied by the French. The French defeat forced Louis XII to withdraw from Milan and Italy." .

ns1:46742 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Estepona was a naval battle that occurred in the year 1342 between the armada of the Crown of Aragon, commanded by Admiral Pere de Montcada, against the fleet of the Marinid Dynasty. The battle took place in the Bay of Estepona (Málaga) in the Strait of Gibraltar and resulted in an Aragonese victory and a rout of the Marinid fleet." .

ns1:46744 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Upton was fought on 28 August 1651 when a New Model Army detachment under the command of Colonel John Lambert made a surprise attack on Royalists defending the river Severn crossing at Upton-upon-Severn, 6 miles (9.7 km) below Worcester. To do this, on the next day, 28 August 1651, a strong detachment, both horse (cavalry) and foot (infantry), under the command of Colonel John Lambert, was ordered to march from Evesham to Upton-upon-Severn and drive Major General Edward Massey from there, by taking possession of the bridge and occupying the town (which is located on the west bank of the Severn)." .

ns1:46745 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2007 Senegalese parliamentary election were held in Senegal on 03 June 2007. In the 2007 presidential elections, turnout had been 70%; in the 2001 Senegalese parliamentary election it had been 67.4%." .

ns1:46761 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 Next Generation Adelaide International was a men's tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts. It was the 31st edition of the event known that year as the Australian Hard Court Championships, and was part of the International Series of the 2008 ATP Tour. It took place at the Memorial Drive Park in Australian Hard Court Championships, Australia, from 31 December 2007 through 06 January 2008." .

ns1:46770 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2004 Coca-Cola 600, the 45th running of the Coca-Cola 600, was a NASCAR Nextel Cup Series Coca-Cola 600 held on 30 May 2004 at Charlotte Motor Speedway in Charlotte, North Carolina. Contested at 400 laps on the 1.5 mile (2.4 km) speedway, it was the twelfth Coca-Cola 600 of the 2004 NASCAR Nextel Cup Series season. Jimmie Johnson of Hendrick Motorsports won the Coca-Cola 600, his second win of the season and also at Charlotte. 80) Charlotte Motor Speedway is a motorsports complex located in Concord, North Carolina, United States 13 miles from Charlotte, North Carolina." .

ns1:46779 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2000 NHK Trophy was the final event of six in the 2000–01 ISU Grand Prix of Figure Skating, a senior-level international invitational competition series. It was held at the Asahikawa Taisetsu Ice Arena in Asahikawa on 30 November 2000 – 03 December 2000." .

ns1:46797 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 Grand Prix Cycliste de Québec was the second edition of the Grand Prix Cycliste de Québec, a single-day professional bicycle road race. It was held on 09 September 2011, over a distance of 201.6 km (125.3 mi), starting and finishing in Quebec City, Quebec, Canada." .

ns1:46802 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "New Democrats, also known as centrism Democrats, Clinton Democrats, or moderate Democrats, are a centrism ideological faction within the Democratic Party in the United States. Bill Clinton is the Democratic politician most identified with the New Democrats due to his promise of welfare reform in the 1992 United States presidential campaign and its subsequent enactment, his 1992 promise of a middle-class tax cut and his 1993 expansion of the Earned Income Tax Credit for the working poor. Clinton presented himself as a centrism candidate to draw White middle-class voters who had left the Democratic Party for the Republican Party. Columnist Michael Lind argued that neoliberalism for New Democrats was the \"highest stage\" of left liberalism. Many leading New Democrats, including Bill Clinton, started out in the George McGovern wing of the Democratic Party and gradually moved toward the right on economic and military policy." .

ns1:46804 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Mascot was an unsuccessful British carrier air raid conducted against the German battleship Tirpitz at her anchorage in Kåfjorden, Norway, on 17 July 1944. The improved defences in place by the time of Operation Mascot included a cliff-top observation post near Kåfjorden, which was capable of directing the battleship's anti-aircraft guns if necessary." .

ns1:46805 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Northern Ireland Football League Cup (BetMcLean League Cup for sponsorship purposes), also known as the Irish League Cup, is a national association football knock-out cup competition in Northern Ireland open to the 36 Northern Ireland Football League clubs. The cup is operated by the Northern Ireland Football League, who in 2013 took over the administration from the Irish Football Association (IFA) for the 2013–14 season onwards, after which the cup was renamed as the Northern Ireland Football League (NIFL) Cup." .

ns1:46808 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Agram Trial or Zagreb Trial (known as the \"High treason trial\" in Serbo-Croatian, veleizdajnički proces) was the trial of 53 Serbs in Austria-Hungary, who were accused of conspiracy to overthrow the state and place Croatia-Slavonia under Serbian rule." .

ns1:46810 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The debut season of One in a Million, season 1 premiered on 26 May 2006 and was aired on 8TV." .

ns1:46831 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2002–03 Bosnia and Herzegovina Football Cup was the ninth season of the Bosnia and Herzegovina's annual association football cup, and a third season of the unified competition. The competition started on 06 November 2002 with the First Round and concluded on 27 May 2003 with the Final." .

ns1:46847 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1901 Argentine Primera División was the tenth season of top-flight football in Argentina. The season began on 19 May 1901 and ended on 30 August 1901." .

ns1:46850 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "United Nations Security Council Resolution 581, adopted on 13 February 1986, after hearing representations from Sudan and the front-line states and reaffirming resolutions 567 (1985), 568 (1985), 571 (1985), 572 (1985) and United Nations Security Council Resolution 580 (1985), the Council strongly condemned \"racist South Africa\" for its recent threats to perpetrate acts of aggression against neighbouring countries in southern Africa." .

ns1:46853 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011–12 Bosnia and Herzegovina Football Cup is the seventieth season of Bosnia and Herzegovina's annual association football cup, and a twelfth season of the unified competition. The competition started on 14 September 2011 and concluded on 16 May 2012. The following 32 teams competed in Round 1: (Team in bold is the winner) 1 Of the 32 participants, the Federation of Bosnia and Herzegovina has 20 clubs, while the Republika of Srpska has 11. The draws for the round of 32 was conducted in Sarajevo at 12:00 (CEST) on 6 14 September 2011 in hotel \"Art\". All 32 clubs entered the competition from this round, while the matches were played on 14 September 2011." .

ns1:46868 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Bulgarian coup d'état of 1934, also known as the 1934 Bulgarian coup d'état (Bulgarian: Деветнадесетомайски преврат, Devetnadesetomayski prevrat), was a coup d'état in the Kingdom of Bulgaria carried out by the Zveno military organization and the Military Union with the aid of the Bulgarian Army. Taking advantage of the discord in the Popular Bloc in the spring of 1934, the Zveno activists carried out the coup on the eve of 19 May 1934, ahead of Aleksandar Tsankov's supporters, who had planned a coup for 20 May. In foreign policy, the Zveno's most notable act was to establish diplomatic relations with the USSR on 23 July 1934 and steer Bulgaria towards France." .

ns1:46875 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Murder of Ofir Rahum was a shooting attack which occurred on 17 January 2001, in which Palestinian militants from the Tanzim faction of Fatah killed 16-year-old Israeli high school student Ofir Rahum on the outskirts of Ramallah." .

ns1:46881 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Skirmish of Arisaig took place on 16 17 May 1746 at Arisaig, Scotland and was the last armed conflict of the Jacobite rising of 1745." .

ns1:46885 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1842 United States House of Representatives elections for the 28th Congress were held during President John Tyler's term at various dates in different states between 1842 and February 1844. Maryland's elections to the next Congress were 1844 United States House of Representatives elections, after the 1842–1843 election cycle was passed and almost after the next Congress completed." .

ns1:46902 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Artscape Nordland (Skulpturlandskap Nordland) is an international art project that aims to bring art to people where they live, art museums being few and far apart in the sparsely populated county of Nordland. The project has been celebrated by the composers Kari Beate Tandberg and Karsten Brustad who working together wrote «Skulpturlandskap Nordland – en musikalsk dialog» (\"Artscape Nordland: a musical dialogue\")." .

ns1:46904 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Malacca occurred in 1568, when the Sultan of Aceh Alauddin attacked the Portuguese-held city of Portuguese Malacca." .

ns1:46916 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 KNVB Cup Final was a association football match between AZ Alkmaar and AFC Ajax on 2007 at De Kuip, Rotterdam. It was the final match of the 2006–07 KNVB Cup competition. AFC Ajax beat AZ Alkmaar on penalties after the match finished 1–1 after extra time." .

ns1:46926 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010–11 Taça de Portugal, also known as Taca de Portugal Millennium for sponsorship reasons, was the 71st season of the Taca de Portugal. A total of 172 clubs from all four tiers of Portugal association football took part in this tournament." .

ns1:46954 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "symbiogenesis, or endosymbiotic theory, is the leading evolutionary theory of the origin of eukaryotic cells from prokaryotic organisms. Essay on the Theory of symbiogenesis, he wrote, \"The theory of symbiogenesis is a theory of selection relying on the phenomenon of symbiosis.\"" .

ns1:46962 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Malaysia Open Grand Prix Gold was the first grand prix gold and grand prix tournament of the 2015 BWF Grand Prix Gold and Grand Prix. The tournament was held at the Stadium Perpaduan in Kuching, Malaysia from 13 January 2015–18 January 2015 and had a total purse of $120,000." .

ns1:46966 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Rügen was a naval battle near the island of Rügen (in modern Germany), that took place on 21 May 1565 between an allied fleet of 6 Danish and 3 Lübeck ships, and a Swedish fleet of 48 ships with a total of 1,638 guns and 8,000 men under Klas Horn." .

ns1:46968 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Wayne Ferreira and Yevgeny Kafelnikov were the defending champions but lost in the first round to Mark Knowles and Daniel Nestor." .

ns1:46972 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Mallavelly (also spelled Malvilly or Malavalli) was fought on 27 March 1799 between forces of the British East India Company and the Kingdom of Mysore during the Fourth Anglo-Mysore War." .

ns1:46973 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Tudor Revival architecture (also known as mock Tudor in the UK) first manifested itself in domestic architecture in the United Kingdom in the latter half of the 19th century. Outside North America, Tudorbethan is also used synonymously with Tudor architecture Revival and mock Tudor." .

ns1:46986 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Group B of the 2008 Fed Cup Asia/Oceania Zone Group II – Pool B was one of two pools in the Asia/Oceania Zone Group II of the 2008 Fed Cup." .

ns1:46995 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 44th edition of the annual 2004 Clásico RCN was held from 17 October 2004 to 24 October 2004 in Colombia." .

ns1:46996 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Fourteenth Ordinary General Assembly of the Synod of Bishops, popularly referred to as the Synod on the Family, took place from 4 to 25 October 2015 with the theme of \"the vocation and mission of the family in the Church and in the contemporary world.\" On Sunday, 18 04 October 2015 Francis canonized Louis and Marie-Azélie Guérin Martin." .

ns1:46998 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1959 Volta a Catalunya was the 39th edition of the Volta a Catalunya cycle race and was held from 06 September 1959 to 13 September 1959." .

ns1:47000 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1970 European Rowing Championships were rowing championships held on Lake Öreg in Tata, Hungary." .

ns1:47005 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1840 United States presidential election in Pennsylvania took place between October 30 and 1840, as part of the 1840 United States presidential election. Pennsylvania voted for Whig challenger William Henry Harrison over Democratic incumbent Martin Van Buren by just 334 votes, a margin of 0.12%. It is the narrowest margin of victory in a presidential election in Pennsylvania history, with Donald Trump's 2016 win following close behind at 0.72%." .

ns1:47007 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Guangzhou Uprising, Canton Uprising or Canton Riots of 1927 was a failed communist uprising in the city of Guangzhou (Canton) in southern China. On 11 December 1927, the political leadership of the CPC ordered about 20,000 communist-leaning soldiers and armed workers to organize a \"Red Guard\" and take over Guangzhou." .

ns1:47016 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1994 Australian FAI Indycar Grand Prix was the opening round of the 1994 IndyCar season, held on 20 March 1994 on the Surfers Paradise Street Circuit, Australia." .

ns1:47017 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Battle of Tomaszów Mazowiecki (Polish: Bitwa pod Tomaszowem Mazowieckim) refers to the battle on 06 September 1939 near the town of Tomaszów Mazowiecki, Second Polish Republic, during the Invasion of Poland." .

ns1:47023 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Council of Constantinople was convened in late 1094 by the Byzantine emperor Alexios I Komnenos and held in Constantinople at Palace of Blachernae in order to resolve the case of Leo of Chalcedon." .

ns1:47039 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Denmark participated in the Denmark in the Eurovision Song Contest 2017. Prior to the 2017 contest, Denmark had participated in the Eurovision Song Contest forty-five times since their first entry in 1957. In the Denmark in the Eurovision Song Contest 2016, \"Soldiers of Love\" performed by Lighthouse X failed to qualify Denmark to the final, making it the second consecutive year that Denmark missed out on qualification. Dansk Melodi Grand Prix 2017 was the 47th edition of Dansk Melodi Grand Prix, the music competition that selects Denmark's entries for the Eurovision Song Contest. Denmark was placed into the second semi-final, to be held on 2017, and was scheduled to perform in the first half of the show." .

ns1:47050 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Myanmar National League (known as the Ooredoo Myanmar National League for sponsorship reasons) is the 6th season of the Myanmar National League, the top Burmese professional league for association football clubs. The Myanmar National League will be temporarily suspended for three months from 8 March to 22 June due to the 2015 Southeast Asian Games held in Singapore. The 2015 transfer window for Myanmar National League clubs will open from 2015 to 2015. This season will be the first Myanmar National League season to have Ooredoo as the title sponsor, after penning a deal worth US $1.5 million per year on 2015. A total of 12 teams will compete in the league: 10 sides from the 2014 season and two promoted teams from the 2014 Myanmar National League 2. Fixtures and Results of the Myanmar National League 2015 season." .

ns1:47051 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Illinois–Missouri football rivalry, also known as the Arch Rivalry, is an American college football rivalry between the Illinois Fighting Illini football team of the University of Illinois and Missouri Tigers football team of the University of Missouri." .

ns1:47068 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2003 Tushino bombing was a terrorist attack that occurred on 05 July 2003, at Tushino airfield in Moscow, Russia, killing 15 people and injuring up to 60 more. Only a few meters away from where Elikhadzhiyeva had detonated, 26-year-old Zinaida Aliyeva detonated her explosives 15 minutes later, killing 11 people on the spot while at least 60 people were injured, with four of them later dying in hospital. The Tushino bombing was part of a string of suicide attacks in Russia that had occurred within the previous four months, killing 165 people in total, in the context of the Second Chechen War." .

ns1:47095 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Sinhalese–Portuguese War was a series of conflicts waged from 1527 to 1658 during the Transitional and Kandyan periods in Sri Lanka between the Sinhalese kingdoms and their allies, against the Portuguese Empire. The last Portuguese forces were expelled from Sri Lanka entirely in 1658." .

ns1:47102 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Drakenburg (German: Schlacht bei Drakenburg) took place on 23 May 1547 to the north of Nienburg, between the Protestant army of the Schmalkaldic League and the Holy Roman Empire troops of Eric II, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg, Prince of Calenberg. After Eric was informed of the approach of the enemy near Drakenburg, he ordered his soldiers to get into battle formation. The units under the command of Wrisberg reached the battlefield on 23 May 1547, but the battle was already over." .

ns1:47104 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The papal bull Aeterni regis was issued on 21 June 1481 by Sixtus IV." .

ns1:47120 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006 Norwegian Premier League was the 61st completed season of top division association football in Norway. The season began on 09 April 2006 and ended on 05 November 2006." .

ns1:47129 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Korea National League, also known as the Incheon International Airport Korea National League due to sponsorship reasons, was the 14th season of the Korea National League, the third tier of South Korea's football league system. The 2016 season operated in the same system as in 2015 Korea National League, with the ten member clubs playing each other a total of three times each before the top four sides compete in the post-season championship playoff series." .

ns1:47133 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Bangka Island massacre (also spelled Banka Island massacre) was committed during World War II in the Pacific, on Bangka Island, east of Sumatra in the Indonesian archipelago. On 16 February 1942, Imperial Japanese soldiers machine-gunned 22 Australian Army nurses and 60 Australian and British soldiers and crew members who had survived the sinking of Vyner Brooke by Japanese bombers. About 100 survivors reunited near Radji Beach at Bangka Island in the Dutch East Indies (now Indonesia), including 22 of the original 65 nurses." .

ns1:47135 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Carmen de Patagones school shooting occurred on 28 September 2004 at the \"Islas Malvinas\" Institute in Carmen de Patagones, Buenos Aires Province, Argentina. The morning of Tuesday 28 September 2004, in the middle school N°202 \"Islas Malvinas\" in Carmen de Patagones, a 15-year-old student named Rafael Solich, known as \"Junior\", opened fire upon his classmates in their classroom." .

ns1:47158 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 ATP Challenger China International – Nanchang was a professional tennis tournament played on hard courts. It took place in Nanchang, China between 12 September 2016 and 17 September 2016." .

ns1:47159 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The football (soccer) 2001 Campeonato Brasileiro Série B, the second level of Brazilian National League, was played from August 11 to 2001. The competition had 28 clubs and two of them were promoted to Série A and six were relegated to Campeonato Brasileiro Série C. Paysandu S.C. finished the final phase group with the most points, and was declared 2001 Campeonato Brasileiro Série B champions, claiming the promotion to the 2002 Série A along with Figueirense, the runners-up." .

ns1:47171 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Solheim Cup was the 13th edition of the Solheim Cup matches, held on August 16–18 at the Colorado Golf Club in Parker, Colorado, southeast of Denver. Points were earned beginning with the 2011 Solheim Cup LPGA Hana Bank Championship and concluding with the 2013 Women's British Open. 1Age at the start of the 2013 Solheim Cup matches on 2013 (Pepper turned 48 during the matches). 1Age at the start of the 2013 Solheim Cup matches on 2013." .

ns1:47174 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Versailles rail accident occurred on 08 May 1842 in the cutting between Meudon and Bellevue stations on the railway between Versailles and Paris following celebrations at the Palace of Versailles marking King Louis Philippe's saint's day." .

ns1:47184 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Arkansas–Texas A&M football rivalry is an American college football rivalry between the Arkansas Razorbacks football and Texas A&M Aggies football, which started in 1903." .

ns1:47199 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 British Academy Scotland New Talent Awards were held on 25 March 2014 at The Arches." .

ns1:47200 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Cap de la Roque was a naval battle that took place on 22 May 1703 between a Dutch convoy protected by captain Roemer Vlack and a French squadron under Alain Emmanuel de Coëtlogon, during the War of the Spanish Succession. This battle is sometimes called \"The Battle of the Bay of Biscay\", but this is geographical not correct, as Cabo da Roca is situated in the southern part of Portugal." .

ns1:47219 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Mount Dhaulagiri avalanches occurred in central Nepal during the month of October and resulted in the deaths of at least 43 people of various nationalities, including at least 21 trekkers. On 14 October 2014, a snowstorm and series of avalanche occurred on and around Annapurna and Dhaulagiri in the Manang District and Mustang District of Nepal within the Himalaya range. Twenty-one trekkers and guides from Nepal, Slovakia and Germany were rescued on 15 October after the avalanche the previous day. When search and rescue operations ceased on 19 October, up to 400 people had been rescued from various areas including Thorong La, the Manang District and Mustang District areas and the Tukuche basecamp of Mustang District." .

ns1:47223 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "UFC Fight Night: Lewis vs. Abdurakhimov (also known as UFC Fight Night 102) was a mixed martial arts event held by the Ultimate Fighting Championship on 09 December 2016, at Times Union Center in Albany, New York." .

ns1:47249 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Nietjärvi (15 July 1944–17 July 1944) was part of the Continuation War between Finland and the Soviet Union, which occurred during World War II. Finnish field artillery fired in U-line during five days period (11 July -15 July 1944) more rounds than in any other battle during summer 1944, total 54,300, while mortars fired 24,400." .

ns1:47253 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Anqing (安慶之戰) was a prolonged siege of the prefecture-level city of Anqing in Anhui, China, initiated by Hunan Army forces loyal to the Qing Dynasty against the Taiping Rebellion of the Taiping Heavenly Kingdom." .

ns1:47281 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 Invercargill mayoral election finished on Saturday, 09 October 2010 and was conducted under the First Past the Post system using the postal voting system. The candidates for mayor included the incumbent Tim Shadbolt who contested a sixth consecutive term. Tim Shadbolt won the mayoral election and won a sixth term as Mayor of Invercargill, making him one of the longest-serving mayors in New Zealand." .

ns1:47292 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2001 Armenian Cup was the tenth edition of the Armenian Independence Cup, a football competition. In 2001, the tournament had 16 participants, none of which were reserve teams. The first legs were played on 07 April 2001 and 2001. The firs3 27 May 2001 legs4 May 2001ed on 3 May and 2001." .

ns1:47295 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1989 Irish Professional Championship was a professional invitational snooker tournament, which took place in 01 February 1989 at the Antrim Forum in Antrim, Northern Ireland." .

ns1:47312 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "A 1946 Albury state by-election was held for the New South Wales Legislative Assembly seat of Albury on Saturday, 1946. The seat was subsequently won by John Hurley of the Australian Labor Party (New South Wales Branch). At the 1946 federal election held on 28 September, Mair was unsuccessful with the Australian Labor Party (New South Wales Branch) winning all three NSW seats." .

ns1:47318 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The men's 400 metre individual medley competition at the 1999 Pan Pacific Swimming Championships – men's 400 metre individual medley took place on August 23 at the Sydney Olympic Park Aquatic Centre. The 1997 Pan Pacific Swimming Championships – men's 400 metre individual medley champion was Matthew Dunn of Australia." .

ns1:47329 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Pure Michigan 400 was a NASCAR Sprint Cup Series stock car Consumers Energy 400 held on 28 August 2016 at Michigan International Speedway in Brooklyn, Michigan. Contested over 200 laps on the two-mile (3.2 km) D-shaped oval, it was the 24th Consumers Energy 400 of the 2016 NASCAR Sprint Cup Series season. Kyle Larson won his first career Cup Series Consumers Energy 400, the Consumers Energy 400 had thirteen lead changes among different drivers and four cautions for 17 laps. Michigan International Speedway (MIS) is a two-mile (3.2 km) moderate-banked D-shaped speedway located off U.S. Highway 12 on more than 1,400 acres (5.7 km2) approximately four miles (6.4 km) south of the village of Brooklyn, in the scenic Irish Hills area of southeastern Michigan. Joey Logano scored the pole for the Consumers Energy 400 with a time of 35.697 and a speed of 201.698 mph (324.601 km/h)." .

ns1:47341 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1958 Ugandan general election were held in Uganda between 20 October 1958 and 24 October 1958." .

ns1:47372 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Edict of Restitution, passed eleven years into the Thirty Years' War on 06 March 1629, in the city of Vienna, following Catholic successes at arms, was a belated attempt by Ferdinand II to restore the religious and territorial situations reached in the Peace of Augsburg (1555), whose \"Ecclesiastical Reservation\" had impeded the secularization of Catholic church lands after 1555, as no further Catholic church lands could legally be transferred to Protestant control. With that principle confirmed by the Treaty at Augsburg, the violence of that earlier day between the Lutherans and the Catholics in Germany ended—at least for the generation, until renewed tensions brought about the Thirty Years' War. Behind all this were the inheritance practices in Europe as a whole and in particular the German states within the Holy Roman Empire." .

ns1:47376 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1995 Women's EuroHockey Nations Championship was the fourth edition of the field hockey championship organised by the European Hockey Federation. It was held in Amstelveen, Netherlands from 14 June 1995 to 25 June 1995." .

ns1:47386 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Sama (Turkish: Sema, Persian, Urdu and Arabic: سَمَاع - samā‘un) is a Sufi ceremony performed as dhikr." .

ns1:47411 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1997 Papua New Guinean general election were held in Papua New Guinea between 14 June 1997 and 28 June 1997." .

ns1:47429 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1953 World Table Tennis Championships were held at the Floreasca and Dynamo Halls in Bucharest from 20 March 1953 to 29 March 1953." .

ns1:47431 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1991 Critérium du Dauphiné Libéré was the 43rd edition of the cycle race and was held from 03 June 1991 to 10 03 June 1991. The race started in Chamonix-Mont-Blanc and finished in Aix-les-Bains." .

ns1:47434 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1928 Tour of the Basque Country was the fifth edition of the Itzulia Basque Country cycle race and was held from 01 August 1928 to 05 August 1928. The race started in Bilbao and finished in Getxo." .

ns1:47436 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Turkish Cup Final was a association football match between Beşiktaş J.K. and Kayseri Erciyesspor. Beşiktaş won 1–0 after extra time. Beşiktaş beat Vestel Manisaspor 4-0 at home, and lost 0–2 away. In the Semi Finals Beşiktaş faced off, with one of their Istanbul rivals, Fenerbahçe S.K.. At home, Beşiktaş won 1–0. Away Beşiktaş ended the match down 1–0. In extra time though, Mert Nobre scored a goal for Beşiktaş making it 1–1. The 1st match ended 0–0 at home, and away 1-1. The results were a 1–0 win away and a 1–1 draw at home." .

ns1:47517 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "West Germany vs France was a FIFA World Cup semi-final match that took place in the Estadio Ramón Sánchez Pigone stadium in Seville, Spain on 08 July 1982 and is in both countries known as Night of Seville (German: Nacht von Sevilla, France national association football team: Nuit de Séville). Four minutes later, Germany national association football team began their comeback, with Rummenigge flicking home an outside-of-the-foot volley from six yards that cut France's lead to 3–2. The penalty shootout began with Giresse converting the first kick for France, which was answered by Germany national association football team's Manfred Kaltz. Amoros for France and Paul Breitner for Germany national association football team both converted, but in the third round, Uli Stielike's shot was blocked by Ettori, following Rocheteau's successful strike, giving France a 3–2 lead. However, in the fourth round, France failed to capitalise: Schumacher blocked Didier Six's shot, and Littbarski scored for Germany national association football team." .

ns1:47528 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 AFC Champions League Finals was the final match of the 2013 AFC Champions League, the thirty-second round of the top grade Asian club association football tournament. The first leg was hosted by FC Seoul at the Seoul World Cup Stadium in Seoul on 2013, while the second leg was hosted by Guangzhou Evergrande at the Tianhe Stadium in Guangzhou on 2013." .

ns1:47530 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Philippsburg was a siege of the fortress of Philippsburg during the Franco-Dutch War. This made it a constant threat to the Holy Roman Empire's west flank and at the outbreak of the Franco-Dutch War it became the jumping off point for several French incursions into the Palatinate and Neckar area. Charles V, Duke of Lorraine began the siege with a 40,000 strong imperial force on 01 May 1676." .

ns1:47535 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1896 United States presidential election in New York took place on 03 November 1896. New York was won by the Republican nominees, former Governor William McKinley of Ohio and his running mate corporate lawyer Garret Hobart of New Jersey. McKinley would lose New York City to Bryan in their 1900 United States presidential election in New York rematch four years later and Bryan would later win the city again against William Howard Taft in 1908." .

ns1:47549 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Tientsin, or the Relief of Tianjin, occurred on 13 July 1900–14 July 1900, during the Boxer Rebellion in Northern China. On 13 July 1900, the allied force to assault the walled city of Tianjin consisted of about 6,900 soldiers: 2,500 Russians, 2,000 Japanese, 900 Americans, 800 British, 600 French, and 100 Germans and Austrians. For the Eight-Nation Alliance, Tianjin was the bloodiest battle of the Boxer Rebellion. In the Nankai District of Tianjin in present-day China, a \"Nie Shicheng Martyrs Monument\" is in the area, to General Nie Shicheng who died in battle at the Battle of Tientsin." .

ns1:47552 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Sexual abuse scandal in the Catholic archdiocese of Boston was part of a series of Catholic Church sexual abuse cases in the United States that revealed widespread crimes in the American Roman Catholic Church. The events in the Roman Catholic Archdiocese of Boston exploded into a national Catholic Church sexual abuse cases. In June 2004, much of the land around the Roman Catholic Archdiocese of Boston headquarters was sold to Boston College, in part to raise money for legal costs associated with the scandal. Ward was suspended by the Roman Catholic Archdiocese of Boston in 2002 and dismissed by the Vatican in 2005." .

ns1:47557 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Miss Earth México 2012, the 11th annual national beauty pageant of Miss Earth México, will be held late summer of 2012. The winner of Miss Earth México 2012 will represent Mexico in the international Miss Earth 2012 pageant which will be held on 2012 in Philippines. Special awards were given during the Miss Earth México 2012 coronation night as follows: The following is the list of delegates that represented the 32 States of Mexico as listed in the Miss Earth Mexico 2012 website including their height and age during the pageant:" .

ns1:47581 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Prison Fatal (Spanish for \"Deadly Prison\") was a major lucha libre event produced and scripted by the Mexican International Wrestling Revolution Group (IWRG) professional wrestling promotion on 03 February 2000. The Prison Fatal show was the first confirmed IWRG show that used the name, with it becoming a regular event with the 2012 Prisión Fatal show. The main event was the eponymous Prisión Fatal Steel cage match where the last person remaining in the cage was forced to unmasked per the match stipulation. The Prison Fatal show was the first time that IWRG promoted a show under that name, and it was not used again until the 2012 Prisión Fatal show. The event featured at least two, and most likely more, professional wrestling matches with different wrestlers involved in pre-existing scripted feuds, plots and storylines. The main event Prisión Fatal steel cage match included 12 wrestlers in total and had a 10-minute time limit, where all wrestlers had to remain in the ring before they would be allowed to climb over the top of the cage to safety." .

ns1:47587 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Witch's Cradle (1944), sometimes billed as Witches' Cradle, is an unfinished, silent, experimental short film written and directed by Maya Deren, featuring Marcel Duchamp, and filmed in Peggy Guggenheim's Art of This Century gallery. The Witch's Cradle was written and directed by experimental filmmaker Maya Deren." .

ns1:47601 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 Trani Cup was a professional tennis tournament played on clay court. It took place in Trani, Italy between 09 August 2010 and 15 August 2010." .

ns1:47611 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2005 Asian Junior Badminton Championships is an Asia continental junior championships to crown the best U-19 badminton players across Asia. This tournament were held in Tennis Indoor Senayan, Gelora Bung Karno Sports Complex, Jakarta, Indonesia from 11 July 2005–17 July 2005." .

ns1:47624 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The September 2010 Quetta bombing occurred on 03 September 2010 in Quetta, Pakistan." .

ns1:47626 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Stampex is a twice yearly British Philatelic exhibition held at the Business Design Centre (BDC) at Islington, London, every Spring and Autumn." .

ns1:47628 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1969–70 FIBA European Cup Winner's Cup was the fourth edition of FIBA Europe's 2nd-tier level European-wide professional club basketball competition, contested between national domestic cup champions, running from 04 December 1969, to 26 April 1970." .

ns1:47635 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Anthony Joshua vs. Joseph Parker, billed as Road to Undisputed, was a professional boxing heavyweight unification match contested between Anthony Joshua and Joseph Parker. The event took place on 31 March 2018 at Millennium Stadium in Cardiff, Wales, with Joshua's WBA (Super), IBF, and IBO titles and Parker's WBO title on the line." .

ns1:47646 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Relief of Montgomery Castle took place during the First English Civil War of 1642–1646. On 17 18 September 1644, a Parliamentarian force commanded by Sir John Meldrum advanced to engage a Royalist army led by Lord Byron which was besieging Montgomery Castle in mid Wales." .

ns1:47653 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "South Korea at the 2012 Asian Beach Games participated in the 2012 Asian Beach Games in Haiyang, China on 2012–2012." .

ns1:47656 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Tughlaqabad (also known as the Battle of Delhi) was a notable battle fought on 07 October 1556 between Hem Chandra Vikramaditya, also known as Hemu and the forces of the Mughal emperor Akbar led by Tardi Beg Khan at Tughlaqabad Fort near Delhi. The battle ended in a victory for Hem Chandra who took possession of Delhi and claimed royal status, assuming the title of Raja Vikramaditya. Hemu, who had set off in pursuit of Agra's governor, reached Tughlaqabad Fort, a village just outside Delhi where he ran into Tardi Beg Khan's forces. He took possession of Delhi after a day's battle on 07 October 1556." .

ns1:47670 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Jaji occurred in 17 April 1987, during the first stage of withdrawal of Soviet forces from Soviet–Afghan War. Remaining Soviet troops supported the Democratic Republic of Afghanistan's operations in Paktia against the Mujahideen, hoping to relieve a besieged garrison at Ali Sher, and cut off supply lines to the Mujahideen from Pakistan." .

ns1:47672 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1976 VFL Grand Final was an Australian rules football game contested between the Hawthorn Football Club and North Melbourne Football Club at the Melbourne Cricket Ground on 25 September 1976. It was the 80th annual VFL/AFL Grand Final of the Victorian Football League, staged to determine the premiers for the 1976 VFL season." .

ns1:47688 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2004 DFB-Ligapokal Final decided the winner of the 2004 DFB-Ligapokal, the 8th edition of the reiterated DFB-Ligapokal, a knockout association football cup competition. The match was played on 2004 at the Stadion am Bruchweg in Mainz. FC Bayern Munich won the match 3–2 against SV Werder Bremen for their 5th title." .

ns1:47696 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1998 Cup of Russia was the fifth event of six in the 1998–99 ISU Grand Prix of Figure Skating, a senior-level international invitational competition series. It was held in Moscow on 26 November 1998–29." .

ns1:47697 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Irish presidential election, 1959 was held on 17 June 1959. Éamon de Valera, then Taoiseach, was elected as President of Ireland." .

ns1:47705 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Democratic Party (Italy) leadership election was an open primary election held in November–08 December 2013. After having come first in the vote by party members, Matteo Renzi was elected by a landslide 68% in a three-way primary on 08 December 2013." .

ns1:47708 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Minolta A-mount camera system was a line of photographic equipment from Minolta introduced in 1985 with the world's first integrated autofocus system in the camera body with interchangeable lenses. In Europe, early Minolta A-mount cameras were initially identified by a 4 digit number followed by Minolta AF. The last film-based Minolta AF SLRs produced by Minolta were the Maxxum 50 (a.k.a." .

ns1:47709 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2000 Northern Cyprus presidential election were held in Turkish Republic of Northern Cyprus on 15 April 2000." .

ns1:47711 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Ironman World Championship was a long distance triathlon competition held on 2012 in Kailua-Kona, Hawaii. The Ironman 2012 series consisted of 28 Ironman races plus the 2011 Ironman World Championship which was itself a qualifier for the 2012 Championship." .

ns1:47715 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2009–10 Eurocup Basketball was the eighth edition of Europe's second-tier level transnational competition for men's professional basketball clubs, the EuroCup Basketball. The competition was won by Spanish club Valencia BC, who won their second EuroCup Basketball title, and also secured a place in the 2010–11 edition of the EuroCup Basketball's parent competition, the EuroLeague." .

ns1:47722 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 EBSA European Under-21 Snooker Championships took place in Bucharest, Romania between 18 March 2014-23 March 2014. For winning the tournament, Oliver Lines was rewarded with a place in the 2014/15 snooker season." .

ns1:47748 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "In version control systems, a commit is an operation which sends the latest changes to the source code to the repository, making these changes part of the head revision of the repository. In this context, a commit within a version control system is protected as it is easily rolled back, even after the commit has been applied. To commit a change in git on the command line, assuming git is installed, the following command is run: git commit -m 'commit message' This is also assuming that the files within the current directory have been staged as such: git add . The above command adds all of the files in the working directory to be staged for the git commit. Also, a shortcut to add all the unstaged files and make a commit at the same time is: git commit -a -m 'commit message' To commit a change in Mercurial on the command line, assuming hg is installed, the following command is used: hg commit --message 'Commit Message' This is also assuming that the files within the current directory have been staged as such: hg add. The above command adds all of the files in the working directory to be staged for the Mercurial commit." .

ns1:47755 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Women's Pan American Cup was the fifth edition of the Women's Pan American Cup, the quadrennial international women's field hockey championship of the Americas organised by the Pan American Hockey Federation. It was held between 2017 and 2017 in Lancaster, Pennsylvania, United States, simultaneously with the men's tournament." .

ns1:47760 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Candomblé Jejé, also known as Brazilian Vodum, is one of the major branches (nations) of Candomblé." .

ns1:47768 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 All-Ireland Senior Club Hurling Championship Final was a hurling match played at Croke Park, Dublin, the headquarters of the Gaelic Athletic Association, on Wednesday, 2010, to determine the winners of the 2009–10 All-Ireland Senior Club Hurling Championship. The match was won by Ballyhale Shamrocks GAA of Kilkenny, who beat Portumna GAA of Galway by 1-19 to 0-17. Prior to the 2010 final, Ballyhale Shamrocks GAA and Portumna GAA had previously met only once in the All-Ireland championship." .

ns1:47811 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "KotoriCon was an annual two-day anime convention held during January at Rowan College at Gloucester County (formerly known as Gloucester County College) in Sewell, New Jersey. The convention in 2010 started under the name Gloucester County Anime Convention. The third KotoriCon was in two locations, Pitman High School and Gloucester County College; in the first location, a concert by Eyeshine took place on January 6, 2012." .

ns1:47812 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Typhoon Longwang, known in the Philippines as Typhoon Maring, was the deadliest tropical cyclone to impact China during the 2005 Pacific typhoon season. Typhoon Longwang, weakened from its passage of Taiwan, struck mainland China late on October 2 as a low-end typhoon." .

ns1:47815 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 33rd Grey Cup was played on 01 December 1945, before 18,660 fans at Varsity Stadium at Toronto. The Toronto Argonauts defeated the Winnipeg Blue Bombers 35 to 0." .

ns1:47816 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "In the 2008 Agartala bombings at least 4 people were killed and a 100 injured on 01 October 2008 in a series of 5 blasts set off in Agartala, capital of the Indian state of Tripura, within 45 minutes, while two unexploded bombs were defused." .

ns1:47826 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Jürgen Melzer and Philipp Petzschner won the title, defeating the Polish team of Mariusz Fyrstenberg and Marcin Matkowski in the final, 6–2, 6–2." .

ns1:47832 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Ladies Tour of Qatar was the 8th edition of the Ladies Tour of Qatar. It took place from 02 February 2016 until 05 February 2016 and consisted of 4 stages. A year after her last stage win, also at Al Shamal, Ellen van Dijk made the best of her time-trialling skills to conquer her third success in Qatar." .

ns1:47856 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1950 Wimbledon Championships took place on the outdoor grass courts at the All England Lawn Tennis and Croquet Club in Wimbledon, United Kingdom. The tournament was held from Monday 26 June 1950 until Saturday 08 July 1950. It was the 64th staging of the Wimbledon Championships, and the third Grand Slam tennis event of 1950." .

ns1:47869 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "In 03 November 2015, Turkish Security Forces launched the Siege of Silvan (2015) - an operation and a curfew in the city of Silvan, directed against the PKK-affiliated group YDG-H. The curfew was started on 03 November 2015 and lifted on 14 November 2015, during the time the military and police forces held anti-terror operations in the three neighborhoods of Konak, Mescit and Tekel." .

ns1:47870 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 58th Edition of Vuelta a España (Tour of Spain), a long-distance bicycle stage race and one of the three grand tours, was held from 6 September to 28 September 2003. It consisted of 21 stages covering a total of 2,957 km (1,837 mi), and was won by Roberto Heras of the US Postal cycling team. Isidro Nozal lead the general classification for much of the race until succumbing to the pressure posed by Roberto Heras who closed the gap to Nozal over the final days and took the jersey in the final time trial. The points classification was won by Erik Zabel from Germany, the mountains classification was won by Félix Cárdenas from Colombia and the combination classification was won by Alejandro Valverde." .

ns1:47912 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Greater Poland Civil War (Polish: Wojna domowa w Wielkopolsce) refers to the conflict that took place during 1382–1385 in the Greater Poland province of the Kingdom of Poland during the interregnum period following the transition of power between the Piast dynasty, Angevin dynasty and the Jagiellon dynasty." .

ns1:47940 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1917–18 Austrian football championship season was the seventh season of top-tier football in Austria. It was won by Floridsdorfer AC who would finish ahead of SK Rapid Wien by goal difference." .

ns1:47942 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 CAF Confederation Cup Final was the final of 2012 CAF Confederation Cup, the 9th edition of the CAF Confederation Cup, Africa's secondary club association football competition organized by the Confederation of African Football (CAF). The final was played between Djoliba AC from Mali and AC Léopards from the Republic of the Congo. As the winner of the 2012 CAF Confederation Cup, they qualified to participate in the 2013 CAF Super Cup against the winner of the 2012 CAF Champions League." .

ns1:47943 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Russian Cup Final decided the winner of the 2014–15 Russian Cup, the 23rd season of Russia's main football cup. It was played on 21 May 2015 at the Central Stadium in Astrakhan, between FC Lokomotiv Moscow and FC Kuban Krasnodar. FC Lokomotiv Moscow came back from behind and emerged victorious with a 3-1 win in extra time. The Russian Cup final was held for the first time ever at Central Stadium in Astrakhan. Kuban Stadium in Krasnodar and Otkrytie Arena in Moscow were touted to be other potential alternatives but ultimately the stadium was announced by the Russian Football Union as the venue of the final through an official statement on Twitter on 2015. FC Lokomotiv Moscow played their 7th Russian Cup final, second only to CSKA's 10. Prior to the 2015 final, they have won five, against the seven won by their cross town rivals. As a side note, Leonid Kuchuk, the current manager of Kuban, faced FC Lokomotiv Moscow in the final after he was sacked from the same position earlier this season following a disappointing start at the helm of the Muscovite club." .

ns1:47945 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2019 ASB Classic (sponsored by ASB Bank) was a joint ATP and WTA tennis tournament, played on outdoor hard courts. It took place at the ASB Tennis Centre in Auckland, New Zealand, from 31 December 2018 to 2019 for the women, and from 7 to 12 January 2019 for the men." .

ns1:47968 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 Heineken Open was a men's tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts. It was the 33rd edition of the event known that year as the Heineken Open, and was part of the ATP International Series of the 2008 ATP Tour. It took place at the ASB Tennis Centre in Auckland, New Zealand, from 5 January through 13 January 2008." .

ns1:47973 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1947 Open Championship was the 76th The Open Championship, held 2 July–4 July at Royal Liverpool Golf Club in Hoylake. Fred Daly became the first Irish winner of the The Open Championship, one stroke ahead of runners-up Reg Horne and amateur Frank Stranahan." .

ns1:47977 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2002 Paris-Tours was the 96th edition of the Paris–Tours cycle race and was held on 06 October 2002. The race started in Saint-Arnoult-en-Yvelines and finished in Tours." .

ns1:47984 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1869 Cachar earthquake occurred on 10 January 1869 with an estimated moment magnitude of 7.4 and a maximum EMS-98 intensity of VII (Damaging). The earthquake was felt in Upper Burma, Bihar, Jharkhand, Bengal and Northeast India." .

ns1:47985 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Turkish presidential election refers to two attempts to elect the country's President of Turkey, to succeed Ahmet Necdet Sezer. Turkey President of Turkey office is regarded as the guardian of the country's secular system; the fact that Gül's wife wears the Islamic headscarf, as well as his own history in political Islam, turned the elections into a political crisis. Abdullah Gül's candidacy was announced by Erdoğan on 2007 while calling Gül as his brother. He was the official candidate of the Justice and Development Party, thus making him the strongest candidate to be the President of Turkey of Turkey. On 2007, Foreign Minister Abdullah Gül withdrew his candidacy after the Parliament failed to achieve a quorum for a second time. On 2007, two days before the start of the nominations announcement for the President of Turkey elections, over one million protesters marched in the centre of Ankara, chanting slogans such as \"Turkey is secular, and it will remain secular\", and \"We do not want an imam for President\" to protest against the possibility of Prime Minister Erdoğan or another member of the Justice and Development Party standing in the President of Turkey elections. A second rally was organised for 2007 opposing the candidacy of Abdullah Gül from the Justice and Development Party, which has its roots in political Islam. On 2007, the Turkish Armed Forces issued a statement of its interests on its official website, later termed the \"e-memorandum\" by Ural Akbulut: \"...The problem that emerged in the President of Turkey election process is focused on arguments over secularism. The first round of voting took place on 2007, which resulted in Abdullah Gül, the Minister of Foreign Affairs and the official candidate of the Justice and Development Party, achieving 357 votes. As of 2007 and 2007, the following parties were represented in the Turkish Grand National Assembly and therefore could vote: Votes taken by Abdullah Gül was below the two-thirds of the vote needed, and so, there would be another round of voting in the following days. On 2007, the President of Turkey elections were postponed due to the lack of a candidate after the pullout of Abdullah Gül." .

ns1:47995 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1997 Welsh Open was a professional ranking snooker tournament that took place between 24 January 1997–01 February 1997 at the Newport Centre in Newport, Wales. Stephen Hendry defeated Mark King 9–2 in the final to win his second Welsh Open title." .

ns1:48002 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The December 2011 Jabal al-Zawiya massacres took place on 19 20 December 2011–20 December 2011, in the Idlib province of Syria during the 2011-2012 Idlib Governorate clashes between the Syrian Army and opposition forces, within the larger scope of the 2011-2012 Syrian uprising." .

ns1:48003 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Cavalese cable car disaster of 1998, also known as the Strage del Cermis (Italy: Massacre of Cermis), occurred on 03 February 1998, near the Italy town of Cavalese, a ski resort in the Dolomites some 25 miles (40 km) northeast of Trento." .

ns1:48014 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Prince of Ning rebellion or rebellion of the Prince of Ning (Chinese: 寧王之亂) was a rebellion that took place in China between 10 July 1519 and 20 August 1519 during the Ming dynasty." .

ns1:48019 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Weber Cup, named after bowling legend Dick Weber, is a men's ten-pin bowling competition between Team Europe and Team USA. The Weber Cup is the annual Europe vs United States ten-pin bowling championships and is currently broadcast live from start to finish on Sky Sports in the UK and in many other international channels around the world. The 2008 Weber Cup took place once again at the Barnsley Metrodome, from 3 to 5 October 2008." .

ns1:48032 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1995 Goodwrench 500, was a NASCAR Winston Cup Series race held on 26 February 1995 at Rockingham Speedway in Richmond County, North Carolina." .

ns1:48036 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Myall Creek Massacre involved the killing of at least twenty-eight unarmed Indigenous Australians by twelve colonists on 10 June 1838 at the Myall Creek near the Gwydir River, in northern New South Wales. The poem expresses Dunlop's sorrow over the massacre and expresses sympathy for the Aboriginals of Australia. The Myall Creek Massacre is often cited as the only massacre of its kind in colonial Australia for which white people were subsequently executed. A memorial to the victims of the massacre was unveiled on 10 June 1838 2000, consisting of a granite rock and plaque overlooking the site of the massacre. The Myall Creek Massacre and Memorial Site was included on the Australian National Heritage List on 7 June 2008 and the New South Wales State Heritage Register on 12 November 2010." .

ns1:48038 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1991 Asian Men's Volleyball Championship was the 6th Asian Volleyball Championship, which took place from 11 August 1991 to 1991, in Perth, Western Australia." .

ns1:48039 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Garowe Principles were a set of agreements between the Transitional Federal Government (TFG) of Somalia and various Somali stakeholders outlining a framework for the interim administration's scheduled political transition in August 2012 to a permanent, representative government. The agreements were signed during the Somali National Consultative Constitutional Conference held in Garoowe, the administrative capital of the autonomous Puntland region." .

ns1:48056 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Miss Teen USA 2016 was the 34th Miss Teen USA pageant, was held at The Venetian in Las Vegas, Nevada on 2016." .

ns1:48059 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Scottish Women's Premier League (SWPL) was the fourteenth season of the Scottish Women's Premier League, the highest division of women's association football in Scotland since its inception in 2002. After 12 games the top two clubs, Glasgow City F.C. and Hibernian had moved 12 points clear of third placed Aberdeen." .

ns1:48074 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "401 (1791), was the first United States Supreme Court decision and the earliest case calling for oral argument. Van Staphorst v. Maryland (1791) was docketed prior to West v. Barnes but settled before the Court heard the case: West was argued on 1791 and decided on 1791. Despite lack of formal training, West represented himself pro se in the circuit court in 1791 before Chief Justice John Jay, Associate Justice William Cushing, and Henry Marchant. The court's opinion was extensively covered by period newspapers as no official court reporter was yet published in 1791, and the seriatim opinions were republished in the newspapers and are currently accessible in James R. Perry's The Documentary History of the Supreme Court of the United States, 1789-1800, Volume 6, \"West v. Barnes,\" pp." .

ns1:48077 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Loncomilla was the decisive battle of the 1851 Chilean Revolution between conservative government and liberal rebel forces on 08 December 1851." .

ns1:48095 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "In the Netherlands, the Boekenweek (Dutch pronunciation: (listen); English: Book Week) is an annual \"week\" of ten days dedicated to Dutch literature. The beginning of the Boekenweek is marked by the Boekenbal (book ball), a gathering that is attended by writers and publishers. Since 2002, the Boekenweekgeschenk can be used as a ticket to travel for free by train with the Nederlandse Spoorwegen on the final Sunday of the Boekenweek. The curriculum vitae of the author of the Boekenweekgeschenk – called the Boekenweek-cv – is distributed by public libraries among their members. In 1932, the first Boekenweek was held and the first Boekenweekgeschenk was published. Despite competition from other media, including new technology since 1932, the Boekenweekgeschenk has grown in popularity over the years. In 2010, during the 75th Boekenweek, 958.000 copies of the Boekenweekgeschenk were produced." .

ns1:48099 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Kufra conflict started in the aftermath of the Libyan civil war, and involved armed clashes between the Tobu and Zuwayya tribes in the Kufra area of Cyrenaica, Libya." .

ns1:48114 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Battle of the Vozha River (Russian Битва на реке Воже) was a battle fought between the Grand Duchy of Moscow and the Golden Horde on 11 August 1378." .

ns1:48152 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 British Virgin Islands general election was held in the British Virgin Islands on 07 November 2011. On 2011 Governor Boyd McCleary officially appointed Orlando Smith as the Premier under section 52(1) the constitution." .

ns1:48154 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The National Defence revolt (Greek: Κίνημα της Εθνικής Αμύνης) was a military uprising in Thessaloniki on 17 August 1916, by Greek Army officers opposed to the neutrality followed by the royal government in Athens during World War I, and sympathetic to former Prime Minister Eleftherios Venizelos and the Entente Powers." .

ns1:48158 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "dudum siquidem (Latin for \"A short while ago\") is a papal bull issued by Pope Alexander VI on 26 September 1493, one of the Bulls of Donation addressed to the Catholic Monarchs Isabella I of Castile and Ferdinand II of Aragon which supplemented the bull Inter caetera and purported to grant to them \"all islands and mainlands whatsoever, found and to be found, discovered and to be discovered, that are or may be or may seem to be in the route of navigation or travel towards the west or south, whether they be in western parts, or in the regions of the south and east and of India\". In August 1493 the Portuguese ambassadors Pêro Dias and Rui de Pina were in Barcelona negotiating with the Spanish over several issues, including the Portuguese intention to send a fleet to the New World to capture the islands discovered by Columbus, when Ferdinand and Isabella sent an appeal to the pope which resulted in the granting of dudum siquidem, dated 26 September 1493." .

ns1:48160 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Jaipur Literature Festival, or JLF, is an annual literary festival which takes place in the India city of Jaipur each January. The Festival is an Initiative of the Jaipur Virasat Foundation (JVF) founded by Faith Singh, originally as a segment of the Jaipur Heritage International Festival in 2006, and developed into a free-standing festival of literature standing on its own feet in 2008. The 2006 inaugural Jaipur Literature Festival featured 18 writers, including Hari Kunzru, William Dalrymple, Shobhaa De and Namita Gokhale and 14 others. Police said that Ruchir Joshi, Jeet Thayil, Hari Kunzru and Amitava Kumar fled Jaipur on the advice of officials at the Jaipur Literature Festival after reading excerpts from The Satanic Verses, which is banned in India. Rushdie expressed disappointment \"on behalf of India\", \"an India in which religious extremists can prevent free expression of ideas at a literary festival, in which the politicians are too, let's say, in bed with those groups...for narrow electoral reasons, in which the police forces are unable to secure venues against demonstrators even when they know the demonstration is on its way\". The festival also expanded beyond the four walls of Diggi Palace, holding more than 300 events in 10 venues, including the Music Stage at Clarks Amer, the Jaipur BookMark at Narain Niwas, and two special sessions at Amer Fort and Hawa Mahal to focus on heritage and culture, supported by Rajasthan Tourism. Notable sessions of the festival in 2015 included two packed sessions each for Nobel Laureate Sir V. S. Naipaul and former President of India, Dr APJ Abdul Kalam, who drew a 5,000-strong crowd to the Front Lawns of Diggi Palace. The Jaipur Lit Fest 2016 began at the Diggi Palace as scheduled, with Gair dance from Barmer, Rajasthan accompanied by a crowd that had been waiting since early morning. The 2018 Jaipur Literature Festival was organised from 25 to 29 January at the Diggi Palace in Jaipur. The Jaipur literature festival 2019 was organised in royal Diggi Palace Jaipur from 24 to 28 January 2019. Around 300 speakers were expected to visit Jaipur literature festival 2018–2019, including prominent writers such as Shobha de (known for her bold writing style), Anuradha Roy (India novelist, journalist), Chitra Banerjee (best known for her novel The palace of illusions). Here are few more names of Jaipur literature festival 2018–2019 are: The Jaipur Literature Festival 2020 took place at the royal Diggi Palace from 23 to 27 January 2020." .

ns1:48162 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Gavinana was a battle in the War of the League of Cognac. It was fought on 03 August 1530 between the city of Florence and the army of the Holy Roman Empire." .

ns1:48169 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Elwood Cooke and Bobby Riggs defeated Charles Hare and Frank Wilde in the final, 6–3, 3–6, 6–3, 9–7 to win the Gentlemen' Doubles tennis title at the 1939 Wimbledon Championship." .

ns1:48171 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1992 Yukon general election (32nd general election) was held on 19 October 1992 to elect members of the 28th Legislative Assembly of the territory of Yukon, Canada." .

ns1:48176 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Canadian Open Badminton Championships was the eighth grand prix gold and grand prix tournament of the 2012 BWF Grand Prix Gold and Grand Prix. The tournament was held in Richmond Olympic Oval, Richmond, British Columbia, Canada 10 July 2012 until 15 July 2012 and had a total purse of $50,000." .

ns1:48185 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Glasgow (1544) was fought on 16 March 1544, between Matthew Stewart, 4th Earl of Lennox and the Scottish Regent James Hamilton, 2nd Earl of Arran, and their adherents, during the minority of Mary, Queen of Scots. There was a second battle at Glasgow Muir in May 1544 between Arran and the Earl of Glencairn. According to an English messenger, Edward Storye, it was reported that Hamilton then took the town of Glasgow and laid siege to Bishop's Castle on Wednesday 26 March 1544. Around 24 May 1544, Hamilton fought another battle on Glasgow Moor with the Earl of Glencairn." .

ns1:48191 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1995 UEFA Champions League Final was the 40th edition and took place in Vienna between AFC Ajax and A.C. Milan. It was A.C. Milan's third consecutive UEFA Champions League final, a feat which has since been matched in the Champions League era by Juventus between 1996 UEFA Champions League Final and 1998 and Real Madrid between 2016 and 2018, A.C. Milan was also aiming to tie Real Madrid's record of having won the European Cup/UEFA Champions League six times." .

ns1:48198 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Born and raised in Reykjavík, Björk began her music career at the age of 11 and first gained international recognition as the lead singer of the alternative rock band the Sugarcubes, whose 1987 single \"Birthday\" was a hit on UK and US indie stations and a favourite among music critics. Björk was born on 21 November 1965 in Reykjavík, where she grew up. At six, Björk enrolled at Reykjavík school Barnamúsíkskóli, where she studied classical piano and flute. The group was featured in the documentary Rokk í Reykjavík, with Björk being featured on the cover of the VHS release. Björk was featured on two tracks of the soundtrack for the 1992 film Remote Control (known as Sódóma Reykjavík in Iceland). Björk's former band, the Sugarcubes, reunited for a one-night-only concert in Reykjavík on 17 November 2006. Björk is currently set to embark on her eleventh concert tour, called Björk Orkestral, in which she will perform orchestral arrangements of songs from her career so far. On 21 May 2010, Björk wrote an open letter in The Reykjavík Grapevine, calling on the Icelandic government to \"do everything in its power to revoke the contracts with Magma Energy\", the Canadian company which now has complete ownership of Icelandic geothermal company HS Orka. In 1998, Björk invited Stína to perform as her support act on the Homogenic Tour, and in 2004 Stína contributed to the production of Medúlla." .

ns1:48208 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2002 China Open was a professional ranking snooker tournament that took place between 24 February 2002 – 03 March 2002 at the International Gymnastic Centre in Shanghai, People's Republic of China." .

ns1:48216 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006 European Athletics Championships – women's 1500 metres at the 2006 European Athletics Championships were held at the Ullevi on August 11 and August 13." .

ns1:48222 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Kaiserswerth (18 April 1702 – 15 June 1702), was a siege of the War of the Spanish Succession." .

ns1:48231 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1997 Grand Prix was a professional snooker tournament and the first of eight WPBSA ranking events in the Snooker season 1997/1998 season, preceding the UK Championship. It was held from 14 to 26 October 1997 at the Bournemouth International Centre in Bournemouth, England." .

ns1:48248 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1999 European Youth Summer Olympic Days was the fifth edition of multi-sport event for European youths between the ages of 12 and 18. It was held in Esbjerg, Denmark from 10 to 16 July 1999." .

ns1:48258 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Maryland gubernatorial election was held on 2018. The date included the election of the Governor of Maryland, lieutenant Governor of Maryland, and all members of the Maryland General Assembly. Hogan thus became the second Republican Governor of Maryland of Maryland to win reelection, the first since 1954. Hogan was elected Governor of Maryland in 2014 Maryland gubernatorial election, defeating then-lieutenant Governor of Maryland Anthony G. Brown by a margin of 52–47; the result was considered one of the biggest election upsets that year. In 2018, Hogan had a 68% approval rating, the second-highest approval of any Governor of Maryland in the country, only behind Governor Charlie Baker of Massachusetts, who had a 71% approval rating. Following the Maryland Green Party's nominating procedure, the delegates of the Coordinating Council, which is the party's State Central Committee, made the decision to nominate the gubernatorial ticket as no other candidate had filed by the party's 2018 deadline." .

ns1:48268 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Alberta Winter Games is a multi-sport event that was hosted in Banff and Canmore, Alberta on February 6–9, 2014." .

ns1:48271 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Zieleńce was a battle in the Polish–Russian War of 1792, in defence of the Polish Constitution of 3 May 1791. The battle took place on 18 June 1792, between the Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth Army of Józef Poniatowski and an Imperial Russian Army group under the command of General Irakly Morkov, which was a part of General Mikhail Krechetnikov's Russian forces invading the Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth from the south." .

ns1:48299 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1963 Men's European Volleyball Championship was the sixth edition of the event, organized by Europe's governing volleyball body, the European Volleyball Confederation. It was hosted in various cities in Romania from 21 October 1963 to 02 November 1963, with the final taking place in Bucharest." .

ns1:48301 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Blumenau or Battle of Lamač was the last battle fought in the Austro-Prussian War, on 22 July 1866 (on the day of the conclusion of peace), with the Austrians defending against the Kingdom of Prussia army. Kingdom of Prussia scout patrols were in Malacky on 19 22 July 1866, and in two days the Kingdom of Prussia army occupied Stomfa (Stupava). On Sunday, the morning of 22 July 1866, the Kingdom of Prussia army began the attack on Lamacs." .

ns1:48328 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1993 Taça de Portugal Final was the final match of the 1992–93 Taça de Portugal, the 53rd season of the Taça de Portugal, the premier Portuguese association football cup competition organized by the Portuguese Football Federation (FPF). The match was played on 10 June 1993 at the Estádio Nacional in Oeiras, and opposed two Primeira Liga sides: S.L. Benfica and Boavista F.C.. S.L. Benfica defeated Boavista F.C. 5–2 to claim the Taça de Portugal for a twenty-second time in their history. In Portugal, the final was televised live on RTP. As a result of S.L. Benfica winning the Taça de Portugal, they qualified for the 1993 Supertaça Cândido de Oliveira where they took on 1992–93 Primeira Divisão winners Porto." .

ns1:48332 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Grito de Dolores (Spanish: Grito de Dolores) occurred in Dolores Hidalgo Municipality, Mexico, on 16 September 1810, when Roman Catholic priest Miguel Hidalgo y Costilla rang his church bell and gave the call to arms that triggered the Mexican War of Independence. His speech became known as the \"Grito de Dolores\". Mexico's independence from Spain took a decade of Mexican War of Independence. The President then recites a shout of patriotism (a Grito Mexicano) based upon the \"Grito de Dolores\", with the names of the important heroes of the Mexican War of Independence who were there on that historic day. On the morning of 16 September 1810, or Independence Day, the national military parade in honor of the holiday starts in the Zócalo and its outskirts, passes the Hidalgo Memorial and ends on the Paseo de la Reforma, Mexico City’s main boulevard, passing \"El Ángel de la Independencia\" memorial column and other places along the way. President Felipe Calderón made an exception by re-enacting the Grito in Dolores Hidalgo Municipality Hidalgo as part of the bicentennial celebrations on 16 September 1810 2010, even though he had already done so the night before from the National Palace balcony to launch the celebrations. Similar celebrations to the Presidential one occur in cities and towns throughout Mexico, and in Mexican embassies and consulates worldwide on 15 or 16 September 1810." .

ns1:48334 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Festival de Gramado (Portuguese: Festival de Gramado) is an international film festival held annually in the Brazil city of Gramado, Rio Grande do Sul, since 1973. Formed by the National Cinema Institute (Instituto Nacional de Cinema - INC) in 1973, the Festival de Gramado was originally launched at the Hydrangeas Festivity (Festa das Hortênsias), where film exhibitions were promoted between 1969 and 1971. By the 1980s, it was already the most important film festival of Brazil." .

ns1:48349 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018–19 Big Bash League season or BBL|08 was the eighth season of the KFC Big Bash League, the professional men's Twenty20 domestic cricket competition in Australia. The title was won by Melbourne Renegades, who defeated Melbourne Stars at the Marvel Stadium. This was the first BBL season featuring the new cricket broadcasting rights deal made between Cricket Australia, Fox Sports Australia and Seven Network." .

ns1:48351 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1895 European Rowing Championships were rowing championships held on the Bruges-Ostend Canal in the Belgian city of Ostend on 15 September 1895. At the World Rowing Federation Congress held on 14 September and 15 September 1895, five nations were represented." .

ns1:48374 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Oregon–Oregon State rivalry (known as the Civil War until 2020) is a college rivalry between the University of Oregon and Oregon State University. The football rivalry is the seventh longest running rivalry in the United States of America, being first contested in 1894." .

ns1:48380 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1999 Wimbledon Championships was a tennis tournament played on grass courts at the All England Lawn Tennis and Croquet Club in Wimbledon, London in the United Kingdom. It was the 113th edition of the Wimbledon Championships and were held from 21 June 1999 to 04 July 1999." .

ns1:48384 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 World Amateur Boxing Championships was held at the Heydar Aliyev Sports and Exhibition Complex in Baku, Azerbaijan from 2011 to 2011. The competition ran under the supervision of the world's governing body for amateur boxing, the International Boxing Association, and followed on from the biggest World Championships in International Boxing Association history which was held in Milan, Italy in 2009." .

ns1:48393 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Glinsky's Rebellion was a revolt in 1508 in the Grand Duchy of Lithuania by a group of aristocrats led by Prince Michael Glinski in 1508. In the early 16th century, the Grand Duchy of Lithuania lost about one third of its territory as a result of the Russian–Lithuanian Wars from 1500 to 1503. Historians Volodymyr Antonovych, Matvey Liubavsky, Mykhailo Hrushevsky and Mitrofan Dovnar-Zapol'skiy while noting the rebellion's narrowness, described it as a struggle between those of \"Russian\" and \"Lithuanian origins\" in the Grand Duchy of Lithuania and as an Orthodox Christian struggle against Catholic proselytism. Meanwhile, Polish historians Ludwik Finkel, Oskar Halecki, Ludwik Kolankowski, and the Russian historian Alexander Presnyakov denied that the rebellion had ethnic and religious aspects and considered Michael Glinski a shady dealer, supported only by his dependents." .

ns1:48399 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Pinkpop Festival or PINKPOP is a large, annual music festival held at Landgraaf, Netherlands. Starting in 1970, at Burgemeester Damen Sportpark in the city of Geleen, Pinkpop is the oldest and longest running annual dedicated pop and rock music festival in the world. Pinkpop 2008 was held May 30 – June 1 in the Landgraaf Megaland Park." .

ns1:48419 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2002 Africa One Antonov An-26 crash occurred on 26 July 2002 when an Africa One Antonov An-26 (9Q-CMC) faced a rejected takeoff at Kinshasa-N'Djili Airport in Kinshasa, Democratic Republic of the Congo." .

ns1:48420 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Borgo was a battle between Corsican and French forces over control of the town of Borgo on 8 10 October 1768. In 10 October 1768, Pasquale Paoli tried to recapture U Borgu (Borgo), where a French force of 700 men under De Ludre was entrenched awaiting reinforcements." .

ns1:48424 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Eurovision Young Dancers (French: L'Eurovision des Jeunes Danseurs), often shortened to EYD, or Young Dancers, was a biennial dance competition, organised by the European Broadcasting Union (EBU) and is broadcast on television throughout Europe. Performers between the ages of 16 and 21, from member countries of the European Broadcasting Union, compete as solo of couples to dance routines of their choice. The inaugural Eurovision Young Dancers contest, then known as Eurovision Competition for Young Dancers, took place in Reggio Emilia, Italy, on 1985. Eurovision Young Dancers is also the only Eurovision event to feature a country whose broadcaster is neither a full nor European Broadcasting Union of the EBU, as Kosovo made their sole appearance in 2011 (although they have broadcast other Eurovision events for many years). Florence Clerc, Irek Mukhamedow, Christopher Bruce and Piotr Nardelli were the dance teachers selected to work with the participants and tasked to select the 10 finalists out of the 13 participating countries. In addition, several transcontinental countries with only part of their territory in Europe have competed: Russia, since 1995; Canada in the North America continent, despite only being an European Broadcasting Union of the EBU, have competed twice, in 1987 and 1989. Sixteen performances have won the Eurovision Young Dancers competition, a biennial dance contest organised by member countries of the European Broadcasting Union." .

ns1:48456 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Groenlo was a siege of Groenlo during the Eighty Years' War and the Anglo–Spanish War by a Dutch and English army led by Maurice of Nassau after it had followed an unsuccessful siege by Maurice in 1595. The siege lasted from 11 to 28 September 1597 and ended in the town's capture from its Spanish garrison. Groenlo was then held by the States until a siege in 1606 by Ambrosio Spinola." .

ns1:48459 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The badminton competition at the badminton at the 2006 Central American and Caribbean Games was held in the Pavilion of Parque del Este in Santo Domingo as a sub-cities for Cartagena, Colombia. The tournament was scheduled to be held from 15 July 2006–30 July 2006." .

ns1:48460 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1996–97 Slovenian PrvaLiga season started on 04 August 1996 and ended on 01 June 1997." .

ns1:48473 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1998–99 Danish Superliga season was the 9th season of the Danish Superliga league championship, governed by the Danish Football Union. It took place from the first match on 26 July 1998, to final match on 16 June 1999." .

ns1:48481 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1913 Asmara earthquake took place outside Asmara, Eritrea on 27 February 1913. The data as to the magnitude of the earthquake is imprecise due to the frequency and magnitude of aftershocks, but a maximum felt intensity of VI (Strong) on the Mercalli intensity scale was recorded at Asmara. The earthquake caused significant damage in Asmara (VI), Keren (IV), Massawa (V) and Adi Ugri (V)." .

ns1:48482 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1986 Alaska gubernatorial election took place on November 4, 1986, for the post of Governor of Alaska. In the general election, Democratic state Representative Steve Cowper defeated Republican candidate Arliss Sturgulewski and Alaska Independence candidate Joe Vogler." .

ns1:48501 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Global Observer is a concept for a high-altitude, long endurance unmanned aerial vehicle, designed by AeroVironment (AV) to operate as a stratospheric geosynchronous satellite system with regional coverage. In 05 August 2010, Aerovironment announced that the full-sized Global Observer wing had passed wing load testing. The Global Observer performed its first flight on 05 August 2010, taking off from Edwards AFB and reaching an altitude of 4,000 ft (1,200 m) for one hour. AeroVironment is currently in possession of the second prototype Global Observer. On 6 February 2014, AeroVironment announced that it had teamed with Lockheed Martin to sell the Global Observer to international customers." .

ns1:48503 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1992 Roermond earthquake occurred on 13 April 1992, around 3:20 AM (1:20 UTC) with a moment magnitude of 5.3 and a maximum Mercalli intensity of VII (Very strong). Striking on the Peel Boundary Fault, a normal fault near Roermond, it was the strongest recorded earthquake in the Netherlands and in Northwestern Europe, and caused substantial damage to older buildings in the Netherlands and adjacent countries of Belgium and Germany. The earthquake was recorded as 5.8 on the Richter magnitude scale (5.3 on the moment magnitude scale) and a maximum intensity of VII (Very strong) on the Mercalli intensity scale. The economic cost of the earthquake was estimated to be around 275 million guilders (around 125 million euros), of which 170 million guilders (around 77 million euros) of damage in the Netherlands." .

ns1:48514 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Zirve Publishing House massacre, called the missionary massacres by Turkish media, took place on 18 April 2007, in Zirve Publishing House, Malatya, Turkey." .

ns1:48516 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 World Netball Series was the inaugural tournament of the Fast5 Netball World Series. The 2009 World Netball Series was a new and exciting opportunity for those that have wanted to support netball to assist in making the series a success. The following were the sponsors for the 2009 World Netball Series: -The Co-operative Group who assists with the netball development in the UK. It is sometimes described as the ‘voice of Manchester’ for anything that is occurring there, like the 2009 world netball series." .

ns1:48517 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Robin White and Shelby Cannon won in the final 3–6, 6–2, 7–5 against Meredith McGrath and Rick Leach." .

ns1:48531 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Ladeira da Velha was fought on 03 August 1831, between Portuguese Liberal and Miguelite forces on São Miguel Island in the Azores, as part of the Liberal Wars." .

ns1:48533 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 IMSA SportsCar Championship season was the third season of the United SportsCar Championship and first to be under the name as the IMSA SportsCar Championship organized by the International Motor Sports Association (IMSA)." .

ns1:48542 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Bulgarian–Serbian wars of 917–924 (Bulgarian: Българо–сръбски войни от 917–924) were a series of conflicts fought between the Bulgarian Empire and the Principality of Serbia as a part of the greater Byzantine–Bulgarian war of 913–927. On 20 August 917 the Byzantines suffered a devastating defeat at the hands of the Bulgarian army in the Battle of Achelous which de facto brought the Balkans under Bulgarian control." .

ns1:48567 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Kharkiv Operation (November 24 - 12 December 1919) was an offensive during the Russian Civil War by the Southern Front of the Red Army under command of Alexander Yegorov, against the White Guard troops of Anton Denikin. In the night of 12 December 1919, the Latvian and 8th Cavalry divisions entered the outskirts of Kharkiv, and in the afternoon the remaining White troops encircled in the city capitulated." .

ns1:48568 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2000 NBA All-Star Game was an exhibition basketball game which was played on 13 February 2000 at Oakland Arena in Oakland, home of the Golden State Warriors." .

ns1:48577 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Mexican Grand Prix (formally known as the Formula 1 Gran Premio de México 2018) was a Formula One motor race held on 28 October 2018 at the Autódromo Hermanos Rodríguez in Mexico City. The race was the nineteenth round of the 2018 Formula One World Championship and marked the 20th running of the Mexican Grand Prix, and the 19th time that the race had been run as a World Championship event since the inaugural season in 1950. Daniel Ricciardo took pole position from team mate Max Verstappen and with Hamilton in third." .

ns1:48594 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "pwn symbols, especially the number 1337, are Internet meme that have spilled over into popular culture. pwn has its owned colloquialisms, many of which originated as jokes based on common typing errors, habits of new computer users, or knowledge of cyberculture and history." .

ns1:48596 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "On 11 February 2016, 2016 Ohio machete attack, a native of Guinea who was a permanent resident in the United States and had been working in computer programming and information technology, entered the Nazareth Restaurant in Columbus, Ohio, and began to attack customers with a machete, injuring four. In November 2016, following a car ramming and stabbing attack at Ohio State University, Columbus Police Deputy Chief Michael Woods compared it to the restaurant attack committed by Barry as also a potential act of terrorism." .

ns1:48606 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Kilvenmani massacre (or Keezhvenmani massacre) was an incident in Kizhavenmani village, Tamil Nadu on 25 December 1968 in which a group of around 44 people, the families of striking Dalit (oppressed community) village labourers, were murdered by a gang, allegedly led by their landlords. The massacre resulted in death of 44, including 5 aged men, 16 women and 23 children. The Communist Party of India (Marxist) organises an annual \"Venmani Martyrs Day\" to commemorate the massacre." .

ns1:48610 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Łódź took place from 11 November 1914 to 06 December 1914, near the city of Łódź in Poland. The Scheidemann's Russian Empire Second army was deployed around Łódź, with his IV, I and XXIII Corps on the Warta, and his II Corps at Łęczyca. The enlarged Ninth Army would then attack the Russian Empire right flank, cutting Łódź off from Warsaw, and eliminating any troops thus encircled. On 15 November, the Germans captured Kutno, and on 16 November, crossed the Bzura, while Scheidemann ordered his Russian Empire I and IV Corps to retreat towards Łódź. It was Plehve's Russian Empire Fifth Army which prevented German encirclement of Łódź from the south. On 16 November he ordered Wenzel von Plehve's Russian Empire Fifth Army to abandon the proposed offensive into Silesia and to move northward towards Łódź; they marched 116 km (72 mi) in only two days. By 19 November, the Germans were facing a numerically superior Russian Empire force, with the intention of the Russians to attack the German penetration east of Łódź on 21 November. Yet, Mackensen still ordered his XX and XXV Reserve Corps to continue their effort to outflank the Russian Empire Łódź defenses. For the Russian Empire Army, Łódź became the furthest point of advance towards Germany's heartland." .

ns1:48615 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 ITF Women's Circuit UBS Thurgau was a professional tennis tournament played on indoor carpet courts. It took place in Kreuzlingen, Switzerland, on 22 February 2016–28 February 2016." .

ns1:48620 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 20th 1965 Vuelta a España (Tour of Spain), a long-distance bicycle stage race and one of the three grand tours, was held from 29 April 1965 to 16 May 1965." .

ns1:48641 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "In the Battle of al-Buqaia in 1163, the Crusaders and their allies inflicted a rare defeat on Nur ad-Din Zangi, the Emir of Aleppo and Damascus. King Amalric I led the army of the Kingdom of Jerusalem, together with contingents from the northern Latin states, a substantial body of pilgrims who had just arrived from France, and a force brought by the Byzantine Empire governor of Cilicia. Nur ad-Din was no match for such a formidable combination of enemies and his army suffered a defeat near al-Buqaia (located between Krak des Chevaliers and Beqaa Valley, in modern-day Akkar District, Lebanon)." .

ns1:48643 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Eastern China smog was a severe air pollution episode that affected East China, including all or parts of the municipalities of Shanghai and Tianjin, and the provinces of Hebei, Shandong, Jiangsu, Anhui, Henan, and Zhejiang, during 01 December 2013." .

ns1:48654 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Soy Luna In Concert was the debut concert tour by the cast of the popular Disney Channel television series Soy Luna, in support of the soundtracks Soy Luna (2016), Música en ti (2016) and La vida es un sueño (2017). The tour began on 24 March 2017 in Buenos Aires at Tecnopolis Arena. For the first leg, the cast of Soy Luna performed on forty two shows across nineteen cities of Latin America, finishing on 2017 in Guayaquil." .

ns1:48677 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Theyyam (Teyyam, Theyam, Theyyattam) is a popular ritual form of dance worship in Kerala and Karnataka, India. The Devakoothu is the only Theyyam ritual performed by women.It is performed only on the Thekkumbad Kulom temple. Briefly, Theyyam provides a good example for the religious evolution of, and the subsequent different stages in modern Hinduism, with the overall understanding that within Hindu syncretism lay propitiation as ancient practices and rituals of ancient worship intended for the blessings of the supernatural not unlike, \"in Indus Valley and other ancient civilizations, mother goddess had been invoked for fertility and prosperity\"." .

ns1:48681 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Chilembwe uprising was a rebellion against British colonial rule in Nyasaland (modern-day Malawi) which took place in 31 January 1915. The rebellion had lasting effects on the British system of administration in Nyasaland, and some reform was enacted in its aftermath." .

ns1:48689 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 23rd Miss Chinese International, Miss Chinese International Pageant 2012 was held on 2012. Miss Chinese International Pageant 2010 Eliza Sam of Vancouver, British Columbia, Canada crowned her successor, Kelly Cheung of Chicago, USA at the end of the pageant. Cheung represented Hong Kong, China at Miss World 2012, becoming the first Miss Chinese International titleholder to represent the region in a Miss World Pageant that is not a Miss Hong Kong Pageant titleholder." .

ns1:48694 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The football at the 1976 Summer Olympics started on 18 July 1976 and ended on 31 July 1976. The following 13 teams qualified for the 1976 Olympics association football tournament: With six goals, Andrzej Szarmach of Poland is the top scorer in the tournament." .

ns1:48705 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Starmus Festival is an international gathering focused on celebrating astronomy, space exploration, music, art, and allied sciences such as biology and chemistry. It was founded by Garik Israelian, an astronomer at the Institute for Astrophysics in Tenerife, Canary Islands, Spain. The festival has occurred in 2011, 2014 and 2016 in Tenerife. The first Starmus Festival took place June 20–25, 2011, on Tenerife, and La Palma, Canary Islands. The second Starmus Festival occurred September 22–27, 2014, again on Tenerife, and La Palma, Canary Islands. The Government of Tenerife has announced that the equivalent publicity value from Starmus Festival II (2014) exceeds 170 Mln. The third STARMUS festival took place in 2016 in the Canary Islands, on Tenerife and La Palma from June 27 to July 2. In addition to the scientists in attendance the festival also featured a vast array of musical talent including: keynote speaker Brian Eno, Hans Zimmer, Brian May, Sarah Brightman, Anathema, the Symphonic Orchestra of Tenerife, and MC Hawking. The festival closed with the Sonic Universe Concert featuring Sarah Brightman singing with the Symphonic Orchestra of Tenerife as conducted by Hans Zimmer. Open to the general public at large as well as the Starmus Festival delegates, the two Sonic Universe Concerts were held at the Magma Arte & Congresos, an arena in Tenerife. It is the result of the unique Starmus meeting in 2011 on Tenerife, where the legendary Russian and American pioneers of the space age met up for the first time to share the moments that electrified the human race." .

ns1:48707 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Romanian Navy during World War II was the main Axis naval force in the Black Sea campaigns and fought against the Soviet Union's Black Sea Fleet from 1941 to 1944. The naval war in the Black Sea commenced with the Raid on Constanța on 26 June 1941, the only encounter between major warships during the entire campaign." .

ns1:48735 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1995 Hong Kong legislative election for members of the Legislative Council of Hong Kong (LegCo) was held on 17 September 1995. It was the first, and only, fully elected legislative election in the British Hong Kong before transferring Hong Kong's sovereignty to China two years later." .

ns1:48736 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Victoria's Secret Fashion Show is an annual promotional event sponsored by and featuring Victoria's Secret, a brand of lingerie. From 1995 to 2018, Victoria's Secret uses the show to market its goods in high-profile settings. The first fashion show, introduced by Stephanie Seymour, was held at the Plaza Hotel in New York City in 1995. In 1999, during Super Bowl XXXIII, Victoria's Secret announced a 72-hour countdown to the webcast of their fashion show, which resulted in over 2 million viewers. In place of the fashion show, the company sent the Victoria's Secret (Tyra Banks, Heidi Klum, Gisele Bündchen, Adriana Lima, and Alessandra Ambrosio) on an Victoria's Secret Across America Tour in 2004 to promote the brand through visit to four major cities: New York City, Miami, Las Vegas, and Los Angeles. Victoria's Secret sister brand PINK made its debut on the fashion show starting in 2006 and was regularly featured in a segment of the fashion show each year afterward. In 2009, the fashion show returned to the Lexington Avenue Armory and featured the “Victoria's Secret Model Search”, a competition to find a new fashion show Angel with the winner announced as Kylie Bisutti. On November 21, 2019, L Brands CFO Stuart Burgdoerfer officially confirmed that the 2019 fashion show had been canceled, citing its declining viewership and lack of immediate \"material impact\" on Victoria's Secret sales post-broadcast. The same models who were featured in the campaigns for the \"Angel\"-bra line, with the exception of Chandra North filling in the spot for Christensen, walked the fashion show with Angel wings on.. Over the years, the show featured various forms and sizes of wings, such as butterfly, peacock, or devil wings, which have become emblematic of the Victoria's Secret brand. Prior to each fashion show, Victoria's Secret contracts a renowned jewelry designer to craft the bra to be used as a focal point for promoting the fashion show and as a centerpiece within it. Victoria's Secret had worked with Swarovski for 9 years prior to the Swarovski items worn on the fashion show in 2011." .

ns1:48747 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Malaysia Super Series Premier was the third super series tournament of the 2016 BWF Super Series. The tournament took place in Shah Alam, Malaysia from 05 April 2016–10 April 2016 and had a total purse of $550,000." .

ns1:48764 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 European Cup Winter Throwing was held on 16 March and 17 March at the Pista de Atletismo Universitat Jaume I and Complejo Deportivo Gaetà Huguet in Castelló de la Plana, Spain. It was the thirteenth edition of the athletics competition in throwing events and was jointly organised by the European Athletic Association and the Real Federación Española de Atletismo." .

ns1:48782 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1852 United States presidential election in Delaware took place on 02 November 1852, as part of the 1852 United States presidential election." .

ns1:48784 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1950 Tour de Suisse was the 14th edition of the Tour de Suisse cycle race and was held from 24 June 1950 to 01 July 1950." .

ns1:48787 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Typhoon Damrey was a compact tropical cyclone, which became the strongest to affect the area north of the Yangtze River since 1949. It was the tenth named storm and the fifth typhoon of the 2012 Pacific typhoon season. Damrey finally intensified into a typhoon early on August 2, a half of day before it made landfall over the northern Jiangsu. According to JTWC Best Track Data, Damrey became a typhoon at 12Z. Based on RSMC Best Track Data, Damrey intensified into a typhoon west of Jeju at 00Z on August 2, when it just arrived at the Yellow Sea. Unlike JTWC analyses that Damrey began to gradually weaken after 00Z on August 2, JMA analyses that the typhoon reached peak intensity at 06Z, with ten-minute maximum sustained winds at 70 knots (130 km/h, 80 mph) and the atmospheric pressure at 965 hPa (14.00 psi). After slightly weakening, Damrey made landfall as a minimal typhoon over Xiangshui County in Jiangsu, China at 13:30 UTC (21:30 CST)." .

ns1:48808 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Pink Friday Tour is the debut concert tour by Trinidadian singer/songwriter Nicki Minaj. The tour then continued on to Europe and North America in June, July, and 14 August 2012. The tour came to a close on 14 August 2012 in New York City at the Roseland Ballroom. Alexis Petridis from The Guardian attended the show at the HMV Hammersmith Apollo in London, he gave the Pink Friday Tour an average score with 3 out of 5 stars, saying \"It seems a strange thing to say about a performance that opens with a woman rapping \"I'm a bad bitch, I'm a cunt\", but there's something oddly restrained about Nicki Minaj's live show." .

ns1:48811 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "ofo (/ˈoʊfoʊ/), stylised as ofo, is a Beijing-based bicycle-sharing system company founded in 2014. The company was founded in 2014 by five members of the Peking University cycling club as a project that initially focused on bicycle tourism before deciding on bicycle-sharing system. ofo was launched in June 2015 in Beijing, gaining 20,000 users and 2,000 bicycles by October with investment funding from a Peking University alumnus. After missing the opportunity to merge with Mobike and failing to reach an acquisition deal with Didi, ofo now suffers from consistently high operational costs and lack of additional funding to expand its business. It was reported in January 2019 that ofo had dissolved its international division and offered its 50 remaining employees the option to leave immediately or take a 50% pay cut and transfer to the Chinese business." .

ns1:48852 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Whitby Gothic Weekend, abbreviated to WGW or nicknamed Whitby, is a twice-yearly music festival for the gothic subculture, in Whitby, North Yorkshire, England, organised by Jo Hampshire. Whitby Gothic Weekend is an alternative music festival held in Whitby. The term \"Whitby Gothic Weekend\" is sometimes used as a generic term to describe events during the week in Whitby as a whole, although the name of the event and its associated logo are registered trademarks of Jo Hampshire of Top Mum Promotions." .

ns1:48854 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1919 VFL Grand Final was an Australian rules football game contested between the Collingwood Football Club and Richmond Football Club, held at the Melbourne Cricket Ground in Melbourne on 11 October 1919. It was the 22nd annual VFL/AFL Grand Final of the Victorian Football League, staged to determine the premiers for the 1919 VFL season." .

ns1:48873 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Fine Air Flight 101 was a scheduled cargo flight from Miami International Airport to Las Américas International Airport, operated by McDonnell Douglas DC-8-61F N27UA, that crashed after take-off on 07 August 1997, at Miami International Airport." .

ns1:48874 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Bin Jawad was a battle in the 2011 Libyan Civil War between forces loyal to Libya leader Muammar Gaddafi and anti-Gaddafi forces for control of the town of Bin Jawad. On 5 06 March 2011, following the Battle of Ra's Lanuf, rebel forces advanced along the Mediterranean coast and captured the town of Bin Jawad. On the morning of 06 March 2011, the rebels were on the move again and were passing through Bin Jawad, when they realized that loyalist forces had entered the town the previous night and taken up positions in houses and on rooftops in preparation for an ambush." .

ns1:48891 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1993 Challenge Bell was a tennis tournament played on indoor carpet courts at the Club Avantage Multi-Sports in Quebec City in Canada that was part of Tier III of the 1993 WTA Tour. It was the 1st edition of the National Bank Cup, and was held from 01 November 1993 through 07 November 1993." .

ns1:48895 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1979 Amstel Gold Race was the 14th edition of the annual road bicycle racing \"Amstel Gold Race\", held on Sunday 14 April 1979, in the Dutch province of Limburg. The race stretched 237 kilometres, with the start in Heerlen and the finish in Meerssen. There were a total of 137 competitors, and 32 cyclists finished the race." .

ns1:48910 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Fort Henry (1777) was an attack on American militiamen during the American Revolutionary War near the Virginia outpost known as Fort Henry by a mixed band of Indians in 21 September 1777. The battle is reported in some sources to have taken place on 01 September 1777, and in others on 21 September 1777." .

ns1:48928 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The shell game (also known as thimblerig, three shells and a pea, the old army game) is portrayed as a gambling game, but in reality, when a wager for money is made, it is almost always a confidence trick used to perpetrate fraud. In practice, however, the shell game is notorious for its use by confidence tricksters who will typically rig the game using sleight of hand to move or hide the ball during play and replace it as required." .

ns1:48947 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Tan-Sahsa Flight 414 was a scheduled flight from Juan Santamaría International Airport, San José, Costa Rica to Toncontin Airport in Tegucigalpa, Honduras, with a stopover at Augusto C. Sandino Airport in Managua, Nicaragua on 21 October 1989. Flown with a Boeing 727-200, the flight crashed into a hill of the mountain range after the pilots failed to follow a special landing procedure required for the arrival to the airport, killing 131 passengers, leaving 15 survivors." .

ns1:48961 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "A panel discussion, or simply a panel, involves a group of people gathered to discuss a topic in front of an audience, typically at scientific, business, or academic conferences, fan conventions, and on television shows. The typical format for a discussion panel includes a moderator in front of an audience. Television shows in the English-speaking world that feature a discussion panel format include Real Time with Bill Maher, Loose Women, The Nightly Show with Larry Wilmore, as well as segments of the long-running Meet the Press." .

ns1:48968 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Tatiana Golovin and Richard Gasquet won the title, defeating Cara Black and Wayne Black in the final." .

ns1:48969 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Credit Agricole Friuladria Tennis Cup was a professional tennis tournament played on clay courts. It took place in Cordenons, Italy between 12 August 2013 and 18 August 2013." .

ns1:48976 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Asia Tour was planned to be the eleventh concert tour by Canadian singer Celine Dion. The tour was organized to support the English language album Loved Me Back to Life, and was scheduled to take place in October and 29 November 2014. The tour was organized to support the English language album Loved Me Back to Life, and was scheduled to take place in October and 29 November 2014." .

ns1:48979 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 GP Miguel Induráin was the 59th edition of the GP Miguel Indurain cycle race and was held on 31 March 2012." .

ns1:48986 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1999 World Horticultural Exposition (昆明世博会) was an A1 category international garden festival recognised by the Bureau International des Expositions (BIE). Organised under the auspices of the International Association of Horticultural Producers, the event was held in Kunming, People's Republic of China." .

ns1:48990 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1880 United States presidential election in New York took place on 02 November 1880. New York was won by the Republican nominees, Congressman James A. Garfield of Ohio and his running mate former Collector of the Port of New York Chester A. Arthur of New York." .

ns1:49024 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Güstow, also known as the Battle of Tornow, was a battle during the Pomeranian War in the Seven Years' War fought between Prussia and Sweden near the village Güstow in Germany on 18 November 1758." .

ns1:49027 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Gideon v. Wainwright, 372 U.S. 335 (1963), is a landmark Supreme Court of the United States case in which the Court unanimously held that in criminal cases states are required under the Sixth Amendment of the U.S. Constitution to provide an attorney to defendants who are unable to afford their own attorneys. The Supreme Court's decision was announced on 18 March 1963, and delivered by Justice Hugo Black." .

ns1:49044 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Salses (1639–1640) was a double siege during the Franco-Spanish War (1635-1659), starting with a French success, but ending with a Spanish victory. Now Salses-le-Château was alone and hunger forced the French to surrender on 06 January 1640. The presence of a large number of troops in Catalonia contributed to the outbreak of the Catalan Revolt a few months later and the murder of Dalmau de Queralt, Count of Santa Coloma, second in command at the siege of Salses-le-Château." .

ns1:49052 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Imam Abbas Mosque bombing occurred on 28 April 2007 when a suicide car bomb exploded in front of the al-‘Abbās Mosque in Karbala, Iraq. Karbala is considered the second most important shrine city for the Shia Islam." .

ns1:49056 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Delray Beach International Tennis Championships is an Association of Tennis Professionals World Tour 250 series men's professional tennis tournament held each year in Delray Beach, Florida, United States of America, and played on hard courts. The event was held in Coral Springs from 1993–1998; in 1999, it was relocated to the Delray Beach Tennis Center." .

ns1:49064 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Revelation of John describes a War in Heaven between angels led by the Archangel Michael against those led by \"the dragon\", identified as the devil or Satan, who will be defeated and thrown down to the earth. 7 Then war broke out in heaven. As a result of linking this motif with the cited passage of the Revelation of John, the casting of Satan down from heaven, which other versions of the motif present as an action of God himself, has become attributed to the archangel Michael at the conclusion of a war between two groups of angels, of whom (because of the mention of the dragon's tail casting a third of the stars of heaven to the earth) one third are supposed to have been on the side of Satan, in spite of the fact that the casting down of the stars (Revelation 12:4) is recounted as occurring before the start of the \"War in Heaven\" (Revelation 12:7). He added that Michael's name \"was the war-cry of the good angels in the battle fought in heaven against the enemy and his followers\". The concept of a War in Heaven at the end of time became an addendum to the story of Satan's fall at the genesis of time—a narrative which included Satan and a third of all of heaven's angels." .

ns1:49094 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 IIHF World U18 Championships was the 15th IIHF World U18 Championship and was hosted in Sochi, Russia. The IIHF selected 12 referees and 10 linesmen to work the 2013 IIHF World U18 Championships. The Division I A tournament was played in Asiago, Italy, from 7 to 2013." .

ns1:49125 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Silje Case (26 June 1989 – 15 October 1994) was a Norwegian girl who was killed by two boys in the village of Rosten near Trondheim on 15 October 1994." .

ns1:49137 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Danish Figure Skating Championships (Danish: Danmarks Mesterskaberne 2007) was held in Frederikshavn from 01 December 2006 through 3, 2006." .

ns1:49169 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "OTI Festival 1977 was the sixth edition of the OTI Song Festival that was held in Madrid on November 12, the same city where the inaugural edition of the Latin-American song contest was held back in 1972. The construction of this building started in 1973 and was finished in 1977 few months before the OTI Song Festival was held again in this city. Guayo González, the Nicaraguan contestant, sang \"Quincho Barrilete\", a song addressing poverty, composed by the renowned Carlos Mejía Godoy." .

ns1:49173 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The wrestling at the 1992 Summer Olympics – men's freestyle 130 kg as part of the wrestling at the 1992 Summer Olympics were held at the Institut National d'Educació Física de Catalunya from August 4 to August 6." .

ns1:49180 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1992 Irish Professional Championship was a professional invitational snooker tournament, which took place in 18 May 1992 at Jury's Hotel in Cork, Ireland." .

ns1:49192 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006–07 La Liga season, the 76th since its establishment, started on 27 August 2006 and finished on 17 June 2007. Real Madrid CF won La Liga on the better head to head against Barcelona in one of its most thrilling seasons to date." .

ns1:49228 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1998 Russian Figure Skating Championships (Russian: Чемпионат России по фигурному катанию на коньках 1998) were held in Moscow on 11 December 1997–14 December 1997." .

ns1:49245 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1992–93 Umaglesi Liga was the fourth season of top-tier football in Georgia. It began on 09 August 1992 and ended on 21 June 1993." .

ns1:49274 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 25th Congress of the Communist Party of the Soviet Union met in Moscow from 24 February 1976 to 05 March 1976." .

ns1:49281 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Indiana gubernatorial election took place on 2012. The Republican candidate, Congressman Mike Pence; the Democratic candidate, former Speaker of the Indiana House of Representatives John R. Gregg; and the Libertarian candidate, youth mentor, small business owner and reality TV personality, Rupert Boneham, were all unopposed in their respective primaries or conventions and contested the general election. This is the first open Indiana gubernatorial election since 1996 and the first gubernatorial election since 1972 without the Governor of Indiana or LT. Governor of Indiana as a nominee. The Indiana Debate Commission organized three televised debates between Indiana Gubernatorial candidates Republican Mike Pence, Democrat John R. Gregg and Libertarian Rupert Boneham. The first debate was held on Wednesday, 2012 at the Zionsville Performing Arts Center in Zionsville, Indiana and was moderated by former Indianapolis Star editor Dennis Ryerson. The second debate was held on Wednesday, 2012 at the DeBartolo Performing Arts Center in South Bend, Indiana and was moderated by Indiana Fiscal Policy Institute president John Ketzenberger. The third debate was held on Thursday, 2012 at the WFWA PBS 39 studio in Fort Wayne, Indiana and was moderated by DePauw University Executive Director of Media Relations Ken Owen." .

ns1:49283 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Big Week or Operation Argument was a sequence of raids by the United States Army Air Forces and RAF Bomber Command from 20 to 25 February 1944, as part of the European strategic bombing campaign against Nazi Germany. Prior to the Big Week, throughout 1943, the US 8th Air Force had been growing in size and experience and started pressing attacks deeper into Germany." .

ns1:49302 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1998 WDF Europe Cup was the 11th edition of the WDF Europe Cup darts tournament, organised by the World Darts Federation. It was held in Oslo, Norway from August 6 to 9." .

ns1:49304 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Asian Players Tour Championship 2012/2013 – Event 2 was a professional minor-ranking snooker tournament that took place between 23 September 2012–27 September 2012 at the Yixing Sports Centre in Yixing, People's Republic of China." .

ns1:49329 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Barcelona (09 June 1359–11, 1359) was a naval engagement fought in the coastal region of Barcelona, Catalonia, Spain, between the navies of the Crowns of Aragon and Castile, during the War of the Two Peters. Consisting of 128 warships including royal vessels, ships from the King of Castile's vassals, and several others that had been sent by the Castilian-allied monarchs of Kingdom of Portugal and Emirate of Granada, this large fleet had been entrusted to the Genoese admiral, Egidio Boccanegra, who was seconded by two of his relatives, Ambrogio and Bartolome. Traversing the coast of Valencia and forcing the surrender of the Castle of Guardamar, it appeared before Barcelona on 09 June 1359. The king, Peter I of Kingdom of Portugal, and Muhammed V, Sultan of Emirate of Granada, also contributed ships to the increase in Peter's fleet. On 09 June 1359 it was within sight of Barcelona." .

ns1:49331 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1883 Ontario general election was the fifth general election held in the Province of Ontario, Canada, to elect the 88 Members of the 5th Legislative Assembly of Ontario (\"MLAs\"). Held on 27 February 1883, the fifth Ontario provincial election was the only one ever contested in the month of February. The Ontario Liberal Party, led by Oliver Mowat, won a fourth consecutive term in government, despite losing nine seats in the Legislature." .

ns1:49338 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Bowling Green massacre is a fictitious incident of terrorism mentioned by Kellyanne Conway, U.S. A day later, on January 31, two days before the Hardball interview with Conway, Kentucky senator Rand Paul referenced the 2011 case in an interview with MSNBC by mentioning \"the possibility or the attempted bombing in Bowling Green, where I live\". On 29 January 2017, in an interview with Cosmopolitan, Conway said that Obama had limited Iraqi immigration because \"two Iraqi nationals came to this country, joined ISIS, traveled back to the Middle East to get trained and refine their terrorism skills, and come back here, and were the masterminds behind the Bowling Green massacre of taking innocent soldiers' lives away\". On February 3, the day after her MSNBC interview, Conway said she misspoke and that she meant to use the term \"Bowling Green terrorists\" instead of \"Bowling Green massacre\", referring to the two Iraqi men arrested in 2011. In a March 2017 interview with New York, Conway said she intended to say \"Bowling Green masterminds\" rather than \"Bowling Green massacre\", in reference to \"would-be terrorists who were apprehended before they staged an attack\"." .

ns1:49346 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of the Echinades was fought in 1427 among the Echinades islands off western Greece between the fleets of Carlo I Tocco and the Byzantine Empire." .

ns1:49347 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Oulu International Children's and Youth Film Festival (Finnish: Oulun kansainvälinen lasten- ja nuortenelokuvien festivaali) is an annual children's film festival held in November in Oulu, Finland. The festival has been founded in 1982 by the Oulu Film Centre. Other awards are the ECFA Award for the best European film, the For Tomorrow Award to the best youth film and a prize of the Media Foundation of the Evangelical Lutheran Church of Finland for a Finnish feature or short film at the film festival." .

ns1:49350 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Thirty-second Amendment of the Constitution Bill 2013 was a proposal to amend the Constitution of Ireland to abolish Seanad Éireann, the upper house of the Irish parliament, the Oireachtas. The next day, the Local Government Minister Phil Hogan made the necessary order establishing 04 October 2013 as the date for the referendum on the Seanad bill, and for a separate referendum on Thirty-third Amendment of the Constitution of Ireland to establish a Court of Appeal." .

ns1:49351 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Netflix Prize was an open competition for the best collaborative filtering algorithm to predict user ratings for films, based on previous ratings without any other information about the users or films, i.e. The competition was held by Netflix, an online DVD-rental and video streaming service, and was open to anyone who is neither connected with Netflix (current and former employees, agents, close relatives of Netflix employees, etc.) On 21 September 2009, the grand prize of US$1,000,000 was given to the BellKor's Pragmatic Chaos team which bested Netflix's own algorithm for predicting ratings by 10.06%. The competition began on 02 October 2006. The Netflix Prize competition then entered the \"last call\" period for the Grand Prize. On July 26, 2009, Netflix stopped gathering submissions for the Netflix Prize contest. On September 18, 2009, Netflix announced team \"BellKor's Pragmatic Chaos\" as the prize winner (a Test RMSE of 0.8567), and the prize was awarded to the team in a ceremony on 21 September 2009. On March 12, 2010, Netflix announced that it would not pursue a second Prize competition that it had announced the previous August." .

ns1:49352 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "UFC Live: Vera vs. Jones (also known as UFC on Versus 1) was a mixed martial arts event held by the Ultimate Fighting Championship on 21 March 2010 in Broomfield, Colorado, United States of America at the 1stBank Center." .

ns1:49353 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1983 Guilin Airport collision was a ground collision between a People's Liberation Army Air Force (PLAAF) Harbin H-5 bomber and a CAAC Hawker-Siddeley Trident at the military – civilian Guilin Qifengling Airport, killing 11 passengers. On 14 September 1983, a military aircraft collided into a CAAC Hawker-Siddeley Trident at the Guilin Qifengling Airport." .

ns1:49368 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1991 Halloween blizzard was a powerful storm that caused a period of heavy snowfall and ice accumulation, which affected parts of the Upper Midwest of the United States, from 31 October 1991 to 03 November 1991. By the time the precipitation stopped falling, many cities in the eastern half of Minnesota and northwestern Wisconsin had witnessed record early-season snowfall accumulations, while parts of southern Minnesota and northern Iowa were crippled by a large ice storm. The precipitation from this system began falling as snow in Iowa late on October 30, and by the morning of 31 October 1991 it had changed over to ice and spread northward across southeast Minnesota. The heavy snow began falling across east central Minnesota and northwest Wisconsin during the late afternoon hours of 31 October 1991. As the storm system pushed its way northward, more communities in eastern Minnesota and northwestern Wisconsin began to experience similar conditions; and thundersnow occurred as far north as Duluth. By the time the snow ended on 03 November 1991 the storm had dropped 36.9 in (93.7 cm) on Duluth, the largest single snow storm total in Minnesota history at that time. In all, at least one foot (30 cm) of snow fell in a swath approximately 100 mi (160 km) wide from south central Minnesota, northeastward into northwestern Wisconsin and into the Minnesota Arrowhead. Areas of southern Minnesota and Iowa along and south of the I-90 corridor did not see as much snow, but instead saw a major ice storm. Most of Minnesota, western Wisconsin and northern Iowa were heavily impacted by the storm. Eleven counties in southern Minnesota were declared federal disaster areas, and 52 counties in Iowa were declared disaster areas. Many roads were closed both from the snow and ice, including long stretches of I-90, I-35 and I-29 in Iowa, Minnesota and South Dakota. November 1 saw similar daily records fall, but with a more wide reaching area from most of central Minnesota eastward to La Crosse and Eau Claire, Wisconsin." .

ns1:49385 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013–14 Eredivisie was the 58th season of Eredivisie since its establishment in 1955. It began on 02 August 2013 with the first match of the season and ended on 18 May 2014 with the returns of the finals of the European competition and relegation playoffs. A total of 18 teams took part in the league: The best fifteen teams from the 2012–13 Eredivisie, two promotion/relegation playoff winners and the 2012–13 Eerste Divisie champions." .

ns1:49386 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1975 Davis Cup was the 64th edition of the Davis Cup, the most important tournament between national teams in men's tennis. Sweden Davis Cup team then defeated Czechoslovakia in the final to win their first title and become the sixth nation to win the Davis Cup. The final was held at the Kungliga tennishallen in Stockholm, Sweden Davis Cup team on 19 December–21 December 1975." .

ns1:49407 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Rolling Stones' The Rolling Stones US Tour 1978 was a concert tour of the United States that took place during June and 26 July 1978, immediately following the release of the group's 1978 album Some Girls." .

ns1:49422 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006 Huntsville bus crash involved a school bus carrying 40 students from Lee High School to the Huntsville Center for Technology and occurred on 20 November 2006, on an elevated portion of Interstate 565 in Huntsville. On 20 November 2006, around 10 AM, a school bus carrying forty students from Lee High School to the Huntsville Center for Technology was traveling in the left lane on the elevated two-lane I-565." .

ns1:49429 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Ma'arra, or Ma'arra, occurred in late 1098 in the city of Maarrat al-Nu'man, in what is modern-day Syria, during the First Crusade." .

ns1:49458 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1987 Winter Universiade, the XIII Winter Universiade, took place in Štrbské pleso, Czechoslovakia." .

ns1:49462 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The national commemorative Exhibition of the centenary of the opening of the Ports of Brazil, also known as Brazilian National Exposition of 1908 or the National Exposition of Brazil at Rio de Janeiro, marked a hundred years since the opening of the Brazilian ports acelebrated Brazil's trade and development. It opened in Urca, Rio de Janeiro on 11 August 1908, stayed open for 3 months and received over 1 million visitors." .

ns1:49490 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Turkish Airlines Flight 1951 (also known as the Poldercrash or the Schiphol Polderbaan incident) was a passenger flight that crashed during landing at Amsterdam Schiphol Airport, Netherlands, on 25 February 2009, resulting in the deaths of nine passengers and crew, including all three pilots. The aircraft, a Turkish Airlines Boeing 737-800, crashed into a field approximately 1.5 kilometres (0.9 mi) north of the Amsterdam Airport Schiphol (18R), prior to crossing the A9 motorway inbound, at 09:26 UTC (10:26 CET), having flown from Istanbul, Turkey." .

ns1:49509 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Canucks–Flames rivalry is a rivalry that takes place in the Pacific Division of the National Hockey League between the Vancouver Canucks and the Calgary Flames. The Vancouver Canucks started play in the 1970–71 season as an expansion team and the Calgary Flames started play during the 1980–81 season as a relocated team from Atlanta (Atlanta Flames)." .

ns1:49521 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Keep Moving Tour is a cancelled concert tour by Australian hard rock musician Andrew Stockdale in support of his debut solo album Keep Moving. In July 2013, it was announced that all Andrew Stockdale Keep Moving tour dates had been cancelled, and that his old band Wolfmother would reunite to perform a number of shows in the United States. As of 21 July 2013, it is not yet known whether any of the Keep Moving shows will be rescheduled, either as Andrew Stockdale or as Wolfmother." .

ns1:49528 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The triathlon at the 2000 Summer Olympics – men's was part of the triathlon at the 2000 Summer Olympics programme. The competition was held on Sunday, 2000 at the Sydney Opera House in Sydney. 52 athletes competed in triathlon at the 2000 Summer Olympics – men's; only four of them dropped out of the cycling phase." .

ns1:49533 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1938 Kyeema crash occurred on 25 October 1938 when the Australian National Airways Douglas DC-2 Kyeema, tail number VH-UYC, flying from Adelaide to Melbourne, commenced final approach to Essendon Airport through heavy fog and crashed into the western slopes of Mount Dandenong, also known as Mount Corhanwarrabul, killing all 18 on board instantly." .

ns1:49540 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017–18 Northern Kentucky Norse men's basketball team represented Northern Kentucky University (NKU) during the 2017–18 NCAA Division I men's basketball season. The 2016–17 Northern Kentucky Norse men's basketball team, led by third-year head coach John Brannen, played their home games at BB&T Arena in Highland Heights, Kentucky as members of the Horizon League. The 2016–17 Northern Kentucky Norse men's basketball team shared their home arena, BB&T Arena, with the University of Cincinnati's men's basketball team during the season while Cincinnati's home arena, Fifth Third Arena, underwent renovations during the season." .

ns1:49542 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Battle of Natural Bridge was fought during the American Civil War in what is now Woodville, Florida near Tallahassee on 06 March 1865. As people in Tallahassee tell the story, handed down since the civil war, a small band of Confederate troops and volunteers, mostly composed of teenagers from the nearby Florida Military and Collegiate Institute that would later become Florida State University, and the elderly, protected by breastworks, prevented a detachment of United States Colored Troops from crossing the Woodville on the St. Marks River. The Union Army force, however, had advanced and, after finding one bridge destroyed, started before dawn on 06 March 1865 to attempt to cross the river at Woodville. Based on the involvement of the students from the Florida Military and Collegiate Institute, the Florida State University Reserve Officer Training Corps (ROTC) program is one of only four Army ROTC programs to have a battle streamer for their actions in the Civil War. The site of the battle is now Woodville Battlefield State Historic Site, a Florida State Park, and contains a monument with the inscription: This monument erected under authority of an act of the legislature of Florida of 1921 as a just tribute of the people of Florida to commemorate the victory of the battle of Woodville. A ceremony honoring the combatants on both sides of the Battle of Natural Bridge, followed by a reenactment of the battle featuring Union, Confederate, and civilian reenactors, is held at the park the first weekend of March every year." .

ns1:49551 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 Sunoco Red Cross Pennsylvania 500 was a NASCAR Sprint Cup Series stock car race held on 01 August 2010 at Pocono Raceway in Long Pond, Pennsylvania. Greg Biffle, driving for Roush Fenway Racing, won the race while Tony Stewart finished second, and Carl Edwards, who started twenty-fifth, clinched third." .

ns1:49561 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Swedish Open Championships, also known as the Swedish Open Championships (SOC), is an annual table tennis tournament in Sweden, run by the International Table Tennis Federation (ITTF)." .

ns1:49564 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 World Series of Poker Europe (WSOPE) took place from 19 October 2017 to 10 November 2017 at King's Casino Rozvadov in Rozvadov, Czech Republic. Source: Final standings as of 10 November 2017 (end of WSOPE): The 2017 World Series of Poker Europe Main Event began on November 4 and finished 10 November 2017." .

ns1:49578 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1908–09 FA Cup was the 38th staging of the world's oldest association football competition, the Football Association Challenge Cup (more usually known as the FA Cup). Manchester United F.C. won the competition for the first time, beating Bristol City 1–0 in the 1909 FA Cup Final at Crystal Palace, through a goal from Sandy Turnbull. The format of the FA Cup for the season had two preliminary rounds, five qualifying rounds, four proper rounds, and the semi finals and 1909 FA Cup Final. Bristol City won this tie, and so went on to meet the other semi-1909 FA Cup Final winner, Manchester United F.C., in the 1909 FA Cup Final. The Final was played on 24 April 1909 at Crystal Palace, and was contested by Manchester United F.C. and Bristol City, both of the First Division." .

ns1:49603 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1957 Stanley Cup Finals was the championship series of the National Hockey League's (NHL) 1956–57 season, and the culmination of the 1957 Stanley Cup playoffs. It was contested between the defending champion Montreal Canadiens and the Boston Bruins. The Canadiens were making their seventh consecutive Final appearance, while Boston was making their first appearance since their 1953 loss to Montreal. 1957 Montreal Canadiens." .

ns1:49632 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1981 Paris–Roubaix was ultimately settled in a 6-man sprint on the velodrome in Roubaix with Bernard Hinault prevailing in front of former four times winner Roger De Vlaeminck and reigning three times winner Francesco Moser. With about 8 kilometres to go, Bernard Hinault attempted to break away, but was hauled in by Hennie Kuiper acting for Roger De Vlaeminck. In the end, he was able to resist the charge from Roger De Vlaeminck, and became the first French winner of the Paris–Roubaix for 25 years." .

ns1:49648 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Take Us Alive World Tour was a concert tour by hard rock band Extreme, taking place in from mid to late 2008, in support of Saudades de Rock, their first album of original material in 13 years, and the first since their reunion in 2007." .

ns1:49667 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1957 Saint Lucian general election were held in Saint Lucia on 18 September 1957." .

ns1:49681 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Berlin ePrix (formally the 2018 BMW i Berlin E-Prix) was a Formula E electric car race held at the Tempelhof Airport Street Circuit at Tempelhof Airport in the outskirts of Berlin on 19 May 2018. It was the ninth round of the 2017–18 Formula E season and the fourth edition of the Berlin ePrix as part of the championship. Daniel Abt won the pole position by recording the fastest lap in qualifying and he maintained his start line advantage for virtually the entire race, with teammate di Grassi in second at Audi's home ePrix despite Abt having a slow mandatory mid-Berlin ePrix pit stop to switch into a second car as he had to let e.Dams-Renault's Sébastien Buemi enter his pitbox. The ePrix was held at the 2.375 km (1.476 mi) clockwise ten-turn Tempelhof Airport Street Circuit at Berlin Tempelhof Airport on 19 May 2018." .

ns1:49696 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Elephant Point was an airborne operation at the mouth of the Yangon River conducted by a composite Gurkha airborne battalion that took place on 01 May 1945. At 02:30 on 01 May 1945, a C-47 of 317 Tactical Control Squadron USAAF departed for Yangon carrying a twenty-man pathfinder team, followed by a CCG plane carrying a pair of VCP, to mark and defend the landing zone at Tawhai. Operation Dracula was a complete success, as Japanese forces had actually vacated Yangon several days prior to the amphibious landing; 36th Indian Infantry Brigade was able to occupy the city without encountering any Japanese opposition." .

ns1:49703 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Sasun Uprising or Sason rebellion of 1904 (Armenian: Սասունի երկրորդ ապստամբութիւնը, Sasuni yerkrord apstambut'yunĕ, literally Second Sassoun resistance) was an uprising by Armenian fedayees against the Ottoman Empire in Turkey's Sason region in 1904. The first Sason resistance was led by the Armenian national movement's Armenian fedayees, which belonged to Hunchak. In May 1903, a Armenian fedayees led by Gorgos \"Marrik\" arrived in Sason. Andranik suggested inciting a general revolt in Armenia which would disperse the Turkish forces; Hrayr objected that an Armenian revolt, unsupported by other factions of the Ottoman Empire, would be doomed and suggested concentrating on the defense of Sason." .

ns1:49708 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Sinjar offensive was a combination of operations of Kurdish Peshmerga, PKK and People's Protection Units forces in 17 December 2014, to recapture regions formerly lost to the Islamic State of Iraq and the Levant in their August offensive. In August 2014, the Islamic State of Iraq and the Levant launched an offensive in Northern Iraq and pushed into Kurdish held areas of the Nineveh Governorate, capturing the city of Sinjar, among others." .

ns1:49714 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "United States v. Manning was the court-martial of former United States Army Private First Class Bradley E. Manning, known now as Chelsea Manning." .

ns1:49722 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1986 Miller High Life 400 was a NASCAR Winston Cup Series race that was held on 23 February 1986, at Richmond Raceway (now Richmond Raceway) in Richmond, Virginia. Kyle Petty would defeat Joe Ruttman in his 1986 Ford Thunderbird, finishing under caution in front of twenty-five thousand fans." .

ns1:49733 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The fencing at the 2016 Summer Olympics – women's épée competition in fencing at the 2016 Summer Olympics in Rio de Janeiro was held on 06 August 2016 at the Carioca Arena 3." .

ns1:49752 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1966 Syrian coup d'état refers to events between 21 February 1966 and 23 February 1966 during which the government of the Syria was overthrown and replaced." .

ns1:49756 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Go Bowling 400 is a Monster Energy NASCAR Cup Series Kansas 400 held on 13 May 2017, at Kansas Speedway in Kansas City, Kansas. Contested over 267 laps on the 1.5 mile (2.4 km) asphalt speedway, it was the 11th Kansas 400 of the 2017 Monster Energy NASCAR Cup Series season. Kansas Speedway is a 1.5-mile (2.4 km) tri-oval Kansas 400 track in Kansas City, Kansas. Ryan Blaney scored the pole for the Kansas 400 with a time of 28.481 and a speed of 189.600 mph (305.132 km/h). Ryan Blaney led the field to the green flag at 7:53 p.m. Martin Truex Jr. shot past his outside exiting Turn 2 to take the lead on lap 11. Fox Sports covered their seventh Kansas 400 at the Kansas Speedway." .

ns1:49765 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The short track speed skating at the 2006 Winter Olympics – men's 5000 metre relay in short track speed skating at the 2006 Winter Olympics began with the semifinals, on 15 February, and concluded with the final on 25 February, at the Torino Palavela." .

ns1:49810 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015–16 Top 14 season competition was a France domestic rugby union club competition operated by the National Rugby League (LNR). Having defeated Toulouse and table-topping Clermont en route to the final, Racing 92 won their sixth France championship title, and first since 1990, with a 29–21 win over Toulon, despite playing over three-quarters of the game with 14 men after scrum-half Maxime Machenaud was sent off for a dangerous tackle on Matt Giteau. The winners then face the top two teams in the semi-finals, with the winners meeting in the final, held this season at Camp Nou because the traditional site of Stade de France was not available in 2015–16 due to conflict with UEFA Euro 2016." .

ns1:49812 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "UFC 123 was a mixed martial arts event held by the Ultimate Fighting Championship on 20 November 2010 at the The Palace of Auburn Hills in Auburn Hills. It was the first Ultimate Fighting Championship event in the Metro Detroit area since Ultimate Fighting Championship 9." .

ns1:49844 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Colombian Championship 1962 was the 15th season of Colombia's top-flight football league. Millonarios won the league for the 7th time in its history, defending successfully the title won in Colombian Championship 1961. Source: RSSSF.com Colombia 1962." .

ns1:49847 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "murder of Jenna Lepomäki was an 18-year-old Finnish woman, who was found dead in Fuengirola, Spain, on 06 October 2011." .

ns1:49849 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Lennox Lewis vs. Tommy Morrison, billed as \"Laying It All on the Line\", was a professional boxing match contested on 07 October 1995. A Lennox Lewis–Tommy Morrison fight had been in the making for two years." .

ns1:49866 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013–14 LNB Pro A season was the 92nd season of the LNB Pro A and the 27th season since inception of the Ligue Nationale de Basket." .

ns1:49869 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2000 UCI Cyclo-cross World Championships were held in Sint-Michielsgestel, Netherlands on Saturday January 28 and Sunday 29 January 2000." .

ns1:49888 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Eva Blanco Puig (February 17, 1981 – 20 April 1997) was a Spain high school student who was murdered in Algete, Community of Madrid. While it was unsolved, the case was popularly known in Spain as the Eva Blanco Case (Caso Eva Blanco), the murder of Eva Blanco (Crimen de Algete) and Operation Gang (Operación Pandilla), the code name given to the investigation by the Spain Civil Guard. The murder weapon was a navaja between 8 and 10 centimeters long and 1 cm wide. All attempts to discover the meaning of \"343110\" were unsuccessful, with some hypothesis revolving around the fact that \"34\" is the phone prefix of Spain, and \"110\" is Algete's postal code. The proposition was unanimously rejected by Spain judicial organizations who considered it \"simple-minded,\" \"disproportionate,\" \"useless,\" and a potential stigma for Algete residents who refused to provide a DNA sample without court order and were in their legal right to do so. Six years later, in 2013, the number of people investigated had risen to 1,503, and 208 men from Algete and other towns had had their DNA tested, including criminals who were on leave at the time of the murder." .

ns1:49913 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 Norwegian Figure Skating Championships was held in Tønsberg from 11 January 2008 to 13, 2008." .

ns1:49935 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "It is commonly used interchangeably with the terms such as Political Islam or Islamic fundamentalism, giving this term more than one sense and referring to different concepts. There was no need for any other term, until the rise of an ideological and political interpretation of Islam challenged scholars and commentators to come up with an alternative, to distinguish Islam as modern ideology from Islam as a faith... To all intents and purposes, Islamic fundamentalism and Islamism have become synonyms in contemporary American usage. A writer for the International Crisis Group maintains that \"the conception of 'Political Islam'\" is a creation of Americans to explain the Iranian Islamic Revolution and apolitical Islam was a historical fluke of the \"short-lived era of the heyday of secular Arab nationalism between 1945 and 1970\", and it is quietist/non-Political Islam, not Islamism, that requires explanation." .

ns1:49948 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Cal-UCLA rivalry is an American college football rivalry between the California Golden Bears football team of the University of California, Berkeley and UCLA Bruins football team of the University of California, Los Angeles." .

ns1:49953 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1975 Winter Universiade, the VIII Winter Universiade, took place in Livigno, Italy." .

ns1:49962 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Juno Awards of 1975, representing Canadian music industry achievements of the previous year, were awarded on 24 March 1975 in Toronto at a ceremony in the Canadian National Exhibition." .

ns1:49966 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Germany's Aims in the First World War (German title: Griff nach der Weltmacht: Die Kriegzielpolitik des kaiserlichen Deutschland 1914–1918) is a book by German Historian Fritz Fischer. The book included a memorandum by the then German Chancellor Bethmann-Hollweg dated 9 September 1914 which set out a plan for Germany to dominate Europe. On publication, the book caused controversy in West Germany as it challenged the view that Hitler was an aberration by emphasising the continuity in German foreign policy in 1914 and 1939." .

ns1:49971 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1893 Virginia gubernatorial election was held on 07 November 1893 to elect the Governor of Virginia." .

ns1:49973 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Typhoon Fengshen was the strongest storm of the 2002 Pacific typhoon season. By July 15, Fengshen attained typhoon status, and after initially moving to the north, it turned toward the northwest. The Joint Typhoon Warning Center estimated peak winds of 270 km/h (165 mph 1‐minute winds), and the agency estimated that Fengshen was a typhoon for five days. While near peak intensity, Typhoon Fengshen underwent the Fujiwhara effect with 2002 Pacific typhoon season, causing the latter storm to loop to its south. The cyclone quickly strengthened into Tropical Storm Fengshen just six hours after forming. After a 13 km (8 mi) wide eye developed, the Japan Meteorological Agency (JMA) upgraded Fengshen to a typhoon on July 15 to the southwest of Wake Island; the JTWC also upgraded the storm the same day. By late on July 18, the JMA estimated that Fengshen attained maximum sustained winds of 185 km/h (115 mph 10‐minute), around the same time the JTWC upgraded the storm to a typhoon. Around that time of its weakening, Fengshen began undergoing the Fujiwhara effect with 2002 Pacific typhoon season; the latter looped to the south of Fengshen and dissipated on July 29. On July 21, Fengshen began re-intensifying, and the JMA reported that the typhoon again reached winds of 185 km/h (115 mph 10‐minute). Fengshen retained much of its intensity until July 22, being a typhoon for five days. After several days as a powerful typhoon, Fengshen began a weakening trend due to decreased outflow and dry air. On July 24, Fengshen turned more to the west while passing to the south of mainland Japan, and the next day it weakened below typhoon intensity." .

ns1:49979 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "A Malawian democracy referendum, 1993 was held in Malawi on 14 June 1993." .

ns1:49983 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 Copa Centroamericana (English: 2011 Central American Cup) was the eleventh edition of Copa Centroamericana (formerly known as the Copa Centroamericana), an international association football championship for national teams affiliated with the Central American Football Union (UNCAF) of the CONCACAF region. It took place in Panama from 14 January 2011 to 23 January 2011." .

ns1:49985 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2001 Cypriot legislative election were held in Cyprus on 27 May 2001. The result was a victory for Progressive Party of Working People, which won 20 of the 56 seats." .

ns1:49991 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Women's European Water Polo Championship took place at the Pieter van den Hoogenband Swim Stadium in Eindhoven, the Netherlands, from January 18 to 28, 2012." .

ns1:49994 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The athletics at the 2000 Summer Olympics – men's 110 metres hurdles as part of the athletics at the 2000 Summer Olympics were held at Stadium Australia on Sunday 24 September 2000 and Monday 25 September 2000." .

ns1:50001 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006 Copa del Rey Final was the 104th final since its establishment. The match took place on 2006 at the Estadio Santiago Bernabéu in Madrid. The match was contested by Espanyol and Real Zaragoza, and it was refereed by Luis Medina Cantalejo." .

ns1:50028 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2020 IIHF World Championship would have been hosted from 8 to 24 May 2020 by Switzerland, as the International Ice Hockey Federation announced on 15 May 2015. The seedings in the preliminary round are based on the 2019 International Ice Hockey Federation World Ranking, as of the end of the 2019 IIHF World Championship, using the serpentine system." .

ns1:50039 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The first season of American Idol premiered on June 11, 2002 (under the full title American Idol, season 1) and continued until September 4, 2002. The winner, Kelly Clarkson, signed with RCA Records, the label in partnership with American Idol's 19 Recordings. Controversy erupted when the winner of Season 1, Kelly Clarkson, was invited to sing \"The Star-Spangled Banner\" in a deal arranged by her then management company, 19 Entertainment, at a special commemoration for the first anniversary of September 11 attacks at Washington's Lincoln Memorial. Re-edited episodes of American Idol season 1 were being shown in syndication as American Idol Rewind. Rewind also showcased footage that was not seen in the initial run of season 1, including Kelly Clarkson's full audition." .

ns1:50070 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 Lower Austrian state election was held on 09 March 2008 to elect the members of the Landtag of Lower Austria." .

ns1:50071 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 Toppserien was the twenty-fourth season of top-tier women's association football in Norway since its establishment in 1987. The season ran from 05 April 2010 to 11 November 2010, and was won by Stabæk Fotball Kvinner, their first ever title. As of 2010, the women's association football club Team Strømmen are affiliated with Lillestrøm SK and have changed their name to LSK Kvinner. This is the second instance in Norway of a leading women's club becoming affiliated with a leading men's club, the first occurring in 2009 when Asker Fotball relocated to Bærum and became Stabæk Fotball Kvinner Fotball." .

ns1:50075 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Cage to Rattle Tour is a current concert tour by American rock band Daughtry in support of their fifth studio album, Cage to Rattle." .

ns1:50085 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Charleston Open, also known by its sponsored name Volvo Car Open and formerly the Family Circle Cup from 1973 to 2015, is a Women's Tennis Association-affiliated professional tennis tournament for women, held every year since 1973. The tournament is played on the green clay court at the Family Circle Tennis Center (which contains the 10,200-seat Volvo Car Stadium) on Daniel Island in Charleston, South Carolina, USA. From its inception in 1973 to 2000, the tournament was held at the Sea Pines Plantation on Hilton Head Island with the exception of 1975 and 1976 when it was played on Amelia Island off the coast of Florida. The event moved to Charleston in 2001. From 1973 to 2015, the title sponsor was Family Circle magazine, which had made it the longest-running title sponsor in professional tennis." .

ns1:50109 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "United Nations Security Council Resolution 1835 was adopted unanimously by United Nations Security Council on 27 September 2008. The 5984th meeting of the UN United Nations Security Council took place on 27 September 2008. The provisions of Resolution 1835 were terminated by United Nations Security Council Resolution 2231 effective on Implementation Day of the Joint Comprehensive Plan of Action, 16 January 2016." .

ns1:50111 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "cross-country skiing at the 2013 Winter Universiade was held at the Lago di Tesero Cross-Country Ski Stadium in Tesero from 12 December 2013 to 21 December 2013." .

ns1:50112 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Cyzicus was fought in 193 between the forces of Septimius Severus and his rival for the empire, Pescennius Niger, who was defeated. The battle took place in the context of the Year of the Five Emperors, a tumultuous period in the Roman Empire when Emperor Pertinax was assassinated by the Praetorian Guards. The battle was fought near Cyzicus in Asia Minor." .

ns1:50128 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1991 FIFA Women's World Cup was the inaugural FIFA Women's World Cup, the world championship for women's national association football teams. It was won by the United States women's national soccer team, whose captain April Heinrichs formed a forward line dubbed the \"triple–edged sword\" with Carin Jennings and Michelle Akers. The United States women's national soccer team defeated Norway 2–1 in the final in front of a crowd of 65,000 people at Guangzhou's Tianhe Stadium. The draw for the group stage was held on 1991 at the Tianhe Stadium in Guangzhou, China. There were 99 goals scored in 26 matches, for an average of 3.81 goals per match. Michelle Akers of the United States women's national soccer team won the FIFA Women's World Cup award for scoring ten goals." .

ns1:50130 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Six Gallery reading (also known as the Gallery Six reading or Six Angels in the Same Performance) was an important poetry event that took place on Friday, 07 October 1955, at 3119 Fillmore Street in San Francisco. Conceived by Wally Hedrick, this event was the first important public manifestation of the Beat Generation and helped to herald the West Coast literary revolution that continued the San Francisco Renaissance." .

ns1:50141 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The sailing at the 2004 Summer Olympics – women's sailboard was a sailing event on the sailing at the 2004 Summer Olympics program in Agios Kosmas Olympic Sailing Centre." .

ns1:50151 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Sis Hores de Cançó in Canet de Mar was a mass festival of Catalan singing in a time when Catalan, as a language and as a culture, was somehow persecuted by the Spanish Francoist dictatorship. The first edition of Sis Hores de Cançó took place on 18 September 1971." .

ns1:50152 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Deutschland Cup was a one-off football competition played in 27 November 1990, to celebrate German reunification. The match was played one week after the dissolution of the East German Football Association and its merger with the German Football Association, and featured the reigning champions of East and West Germany, Dynamo Dresden and FC Bayern Munich respectively. The match featured the reigning cup winners of East and West Germany, Dynamo Dresden and 1." .

ns1:50162 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006–07 Umaglesi Liga was the eighteenth season of top-tier football in Georgia. It began on 26 29 July 2006 and ended on 20 May 2007." .

ns1:50166 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Cape Sarych was a naval engagement fought off the coast of Sarych in the Black Sea during the World War I." .

ns1:50176 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Thirty Meter Telescope protests are a series of protests and demonstrations that began on the Hawaii in the United States over the choosing of Mauna Kea for the site location of the Thirty Meter Telescope. Protests began locally within the state of Hawaii on 07 October 2014 but went global within weeks of the April 2, 2015 arrest of 31 people who had blockaded the roadway to keep construction crews off the summit. While opposition against the observatories on Mauna Kea has been ongoing since the first telescope, built by the University of Hawaii, this protest may be the most vocal. On the \"Big Island\" of Hawaii, Mauna Kea is considered the highest island mountain in the world, measuring roughly 33,000 feet from the base deep under the Pacific Ocean. Although polls, some of them highly criticized, indicate that a majority of Hawaii residents support the Thirty Meter Telescope, opposition to the project and other observatories has existed since 1964. The Thirty Meter Telescope (TMT) is a proposed extremely large, segmented mirror telescope, planned for the summit of Mauna Kea. However, Mauna Kea's summit is considered the most sacred of all the mountains in Hawaii to many Native Hawaiian people. The Hawaii Board of Land and Natural Resources conditionally approved the Mauna Kea site for the TMT in February 2011. A planned ceremony at the base of the mountain was scheduled by the group, Mauna Kea Anaina Hou, in opposition of the telescope and in a press release dated that day, the organization Sacred Mauna Kea stated: \"Native Hawaiians and non-Hawaiians will gather for a peaceful protest against the Astronomy industry and the State of Hawaii’s ground-breaking ceremony for a thirty-meter telescope (TMT) on the summit of Mauna Kea.\" Construction was halted for one week at the request of Hawaii state governor David Ige on April 7, 2015 after the protest on Mauna Kea continued and demonstrations began to appear over the state. The days actions were described in a court declaration filed in connection to a Mauna Kea access case by Hawaii County Police, which claims that there was a \"significant risk\" that certain protesters would \"respond with violence\" if officers forcefully separated protesters blocking the road. The protest continued into August 2019 at the entrance of the Mauna Kea access road, in front of Pu'u Huluhulu on Hawaii Route 200. On July 20, 2019 an online petition titled \"A Call to Divest Canada's Research Funding for the Thirty Meter Telescope on Mauna Kea\" has been posted on Change.org." .

ns1:50190 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "A 1932 Honduran general election (Spanish: Elecciones generales de Honduras de 1932) was held in Honduras on 28 October 1932. As a result, the National Party of Honduras candidate, Tiburcio Carías Andino, won the election by a margin of some 20,000 votes\"." .

ns1:50191 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Screaming Bloody Murder Tour is a concert tour by rock band Sum 41, taking place between 2010–11 and resuming again in 2012, in support of their fifth full-length studio album Screaming Bloody Murder." .

ns1:50197 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Diu was a naval battle fought on 03 February 1509 in the Arabian Sea, in the port of Diu, India, between the Portuguese Empire and a joint fleet of the Sultan of Gujarat, the Mamlûk Burji Sultanate of Egypt, and the Zamorin of Calicut with support of the Republic of Venice and the Ottoman Empire. A cunning and pragmatic ruler, Malik Ayyaz turned the city into the main port of Gujarat (known to the Portuguese as Cambaia) and one of the main entrepôts between India and the Persian Gulf, avoiding Portuguese hostility by pursuing a policy of appeasement and even alignment – up until Hussain unexpectedly sailed into Diu. On 2 03 February 1509, the Portuguese sighted Diu from atop the crow nests." .

ns1:50217 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Volta a Catalunya was the 87th edition of the Volta a Catalunya road bicycle racing, which took place from 21 May 2007 to 27 May 2007, in Catalonia. The race began in Salou with a team time trial and ended in Barcelona. Twenty-five teams of up to eight riders started the race: 21 May 2007: Salou to Salou, 15.7 km (9.8 mi) (TTT) 22 21 May 2007: Salou to Perafort, 170 km (110 mi) 23 21 May 2007: Perafort to Tàrrega, 182.1 km (113.2 mi) 24 21 May 2007: Tàrrega to Vallnord-Arinsal (Andorra), 201.1 km (125.0 mi) 25 21 May 2007: Sornas to Vallnord-Arcalís (Andorra), 17.1 km (10.6 mi) (ITT) 26 21 May 2007: Llívia to Lloret de Mar, 177.1 km (110.0 mi) 27 May 2007: Lloret de Mar to Barcelona, 119.3 km (74.1 mi) As of 27 May 2007, after the Volta a Catalunya." .

ns1:50222 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Revolt of the Languedoc winegrowers was a mass movement in 1907 in Languedoc and the Pyrénées-Orientales of France that was repressed by the government of Georges Clemenceau. On 11 March 1907 the signal for the revolt was given by a group of Minervois vine growers in the village of Argeliers. On 31 11 March 1907 there were 500–600 protesters in Bize-Minervois." .

ns1:50226 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The World Championship runs alongside the 2009 IIHF InLine Hockey World Championship Division I tournament and took place between 2009 and 2009 in Ingolstadt, Germany and the Saturn Arena. Seven of the eight teams automatically qualified for the 2009 IIHF InLine Hockey World Championship while the eighth spot was awarded to the winner of the 2008 IIHF Men's InLine Hockey World Championship Division I tournament. The World Championship groups are named Group A and Group B while the 2009 IIHF InLine Hockey World Championship Division I tournament uses Group C and Group D, as both tournaments were held in Ingolstadt, Germany." .

ns1:50258 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1956 United States presidential election in Massachusetts took place on 06 November 1956, as part of the 1956 United States presidential election, which was held throughout all contemporary 48 states. Massachusetts voted decisively for the Republican nominee, incumbent President of the United States Dwight D. Eisenhower of Pennsylvania, over the Democratic nominee, former Governor Adlai Stevenson of Illinois. This remains the last presidential election in which Massachusetts voted more Republican than the nation, as the state would trend dramatically toward the Democratic Party beginning in 1960 United States presidential election in Massachusetts. In his initial 1952 United States presidential election in Massachusetts campaign, Eisenhower won back Massachusetts by a closer 54–45 margin, but the popular incumbent, who governed in a very moderate way that appealed to New England voters, was able to more than double his margin of victory in the state in the 1956 election. No Republican would carry Massachusetts in a presidential election again until Ronald Reagan won the state in 1980." .

ns1:50264 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "After Miklós Horthy was chosen Regent of Hungary on 1 March 1920, Charles I of Austria, who reigned in Hungary as Charles IV of Habsburg's attempts to retake the throne of Hungary, made two unsuccessful attempts to retake the throne." .

ns1:50275 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1991 FIFA U-17 World Championship, the fourth edition of the tournament, was held in the cities of Florence, Montecatini Terme, Viareggio, Massa, Carrara, and Livorno in Italy between 16 August 1991 and 31 August 1991." .

ns1:50288 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Petropavlovsk was a military operation in the Pacific Theatre of the Crimean War. There were three potential bases for the Russians: the island of Novo-Arkhangelsk, capital of Russian America (modern Alaska), Okhotsk on the Sea of Okhotsk, and the largest Russian settlement on the Pacific Coast, Petropavlovsk-Kamchatsky on the Kamchatka Peninsula." .

ns1:50293 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010–11 Austrian Football First League (German: Erste Liga, also known as ADEG Erste Liga due to sponsorship) was the 37th season of the Austria second-level association football league. It began on 14 13 July 2010 and ended on 28 27 May 2011. The size of the league was reduced from twelve to ten teams for this season after a reform of the second- and third-level tiers of the Austria association football league pyramid. Wacker were originally to be replaced by SK Austria Kärnten, who finished the 2009–10 Bundesliga season in last place; however, Kärnten were not awarded a professional licence by the Bundesliga licensing board and hence relegated to the Austria Regionalliga. As part of the reform of the Austria second and third levels, the reserve teams of Bundesliga clubs Red Bull Salzburg and Austria Vienna were both relegated to the Regionaliga at the end of the 2009–10 season." .

ns1:50304 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Black Arrow(Hebrew: מבצע חץ שחור Mivtza Ḥetz Shaḥor) was an Israeli military operation carried out in Gaza City (while under Egyptian control) on 28 February 1955. On 28 February 1955, Ariel Sharon, commander of the Paratroop Brigade was issued the go-ahead to initiate Operation Black Arrow." .

ns1:50308 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 35th International Emmy Awards took place on 2007, at the Hilton New York in New York City, United States and hosted by American actor Roger Bart. The nominees for the 35th International Emmy Awards were announced by the International Academy of Television Arts & Sciences, in 2007, at a press conference at MIPCOM in Cannes." .

ns1:50316 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1975 Australian Open was a tennis tournament played on outdoor grass courts at the Kooyong Stadium in Melbourne in Australia and was held from 21 December 1974 to 1975." .

ns1:50336 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 World Rowing Championships were World Rowing Championships that were held from 15 to 19 August 2012 at Plovdiv, Bulgaria. The annual week-long rowing regatta is organized by World Rowing Federation (the International Rowing Federation), and held at the end of the northern hemisphere summer. Because the 2012 Summer Olympics was the major rowing event in 2012, the programme was limited to non-Olympic events, and the World Rowing Junior Championships were held at the same time." .

ns1:50338 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1876 United States presidential election in California was held on 07 November 1876 as part of the 1876 United States presidential election." .

ns1:50341 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 Emilia-Romagna regional election took place on 28 March 2010–29 28 March 2010. Errani (–10.6% compared to 2005 Emilia-Romagna regional election) and the Democrats (–7.7%) lost ground to the Five Star Movement, whose candidate won a surprising 7.0% of the vote, in what was the worst result for the centre-left in a regional election in Emilia–Romagna. The other surprise of the election was Lega Nord, which gained 13.7% of the vote, up from 4.8% in 2005 Emilia-Romagna regional election." .

ns1:50350 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Hong Kong Sevens was the 41st edition of the Hong Kong Sevens tournament, and the seventh tournament of the 2015–16 World Rugby Sevens Series. The tournament was played on 2016–2016 at Hong Kong Stadium in Hong Kong." .

ns1:50364 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Third Encirclement Campaign against Hubei-Henan-Anhui Soviet was an encirclement campaign launched by the Chinese Nationalist Government that was intended to destroy the communist Hubei–Henan–Anhui Soviet and its Chinese Red Army in the local region. It was responded by the Communists' third counter-encirclement campaign at Hubei–Henan–Anhui Soviet (Chinese: 鄂豫皖苏区第三次苏区反围剿), also called by the communists as the third counter-encirclement campaign at Hubei–Henan–Anhui Revolutionary Base (Chinese: 鄂豫皖革命根据地第三次反围剿), in which the local Chinese Red Army successfully defended their soviet republic in the border region of Hubei, Henan, and Anhui provinces against the Nationalist attacks from 01 November 1931 to 17 June 1932." .

ns1:50367 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1920 Victorian state election was held in the Australia state of Victoria on Thursday 1920 to elect the 65 members of the state's Legislative Assembly. The Nationalist party had reunited after the 1917 Victorian state election in which the Nationalist members supporting John Bowser defeated those supporting the former Premier of Victoria Alexander Peacock over his decision to increase country rail fares, and formed a majority government with 40 members." .

ns1:50378 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Théodore Gouvy International Festival (Festival international Louis Théodore Gouvy) is an annual festival of classical music held in honour of Franco-German composer, Louis Théodore Gouvy. The festival has been established by the Institut Louis Théodore Gouvy and the Choeur d'hommes de Hombourg-Haut (male choir founded in 1865 with Gouvy's support)." .

ns1:50394 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Rock Springs massacre, also known as the Rock Springs Riot, occurred on 02 September 1885, in the present-day United States city of Rock Springs in Sweetwater County, Wyoming. The massacre in Rock Springs was one among several instances of violence culminating from years of anti-Chinese sentiment in the United States. The massacre in Rock Springs touched off a wave of anti-Chinese violence, especially in the Puget Sound area of Washington Territory. At the time of the massacre, there were about 150 white miners and 331 Chinese miners in Rock Springs. In the two years before the massacre, a \"Whitemen's Town\" was established in Rock Springs. No evidence has been uncovered to prove that the national Knights of Labor organization was behind the massacre at Rock Springs. At 7:00 a.m. on 02 September 1885, ten white men, in ordinary garb and miner's uniforms, arrived at coal pit number six at the Rock Springs mine. The situation in Rock Springs was stabilized as early as 15, when Warren first requested the removal of federal troops, but the mines at Rock Springs remained closed for a time. The massacre at Rock Springs led to other incidents of anti-Chinese aggression, primarily in Washington Territory, though there were incidents in Oregon and other states as well. Other states reported incidents as well: As far away as Augusta, Georgia, anger was expressed against the Chinese in response to the massacre at Rock Springs. In general, the locations in Rock Springs associated with the massacre have been surrounded and absorbed by the city's growth." .

ns1:50395 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1971 Motor Trend 500 was the first race in NASCAR's Winston Cup era (also known as the Winston Cup Grand National Series) that took place on 10 January 1971. 191 laps on a road course at Riverside International Raceway in Riverside, California that spanned a total distance of 2.620 miles (4.216 km). Defending NASCAR Grand National West series champion Ray Elder won the race; making it the first time that the 500-mile event at Riverside was won by a manufacturer other than Ford." .

ns1:50407 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 Men's European Volleyball League was the 5th edition of the European Volleyball League, organised by Europe's governing volleyball body, the European Volleyball Confederation. The Final Four was held in Bursa, Turkey from 19 to 20 July 2008." .

ns1:50420 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Commonwealth Fencing Championships was held in Largs, Scotland between the 10th and 15 November 2014 at the Inverclyde National Sports Training Centre." .

ns1:50430 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Manzikert in 1054 was a successful defense of the city of Manzikert by Byzantine forces under Basil Apocapes against the Seljuk dynasty led by sultan Toğrül." .

ns1:50435 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Royal Charter Storm (also known as the Great storm of 1859) of 25 October 1859 and 26 October 1859 was considered to be the most severe storm to hit the Irish Sea in the 19th century, with a total death toll estimated at over 800. The storm drifted northwards, hitting Anglesey by about 8 p.m. and not reaching maximum force at the River Mersey until midday on 26 October 1859, then continued northwards to affect Scotland." .

ns1:50447 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Festival International de Programmes Audiovisuels (FIPADOC) or International Documentary Festival FIPADOC (French: Festival International de Programmes Audiovisuels (FIPA)), founded in 1987 by Michel Mitrani (1930-1996), was first held in Cannes in 1987., In 2019, the FIPA became FIPADOC,,, an international festival specializing in non-fiction films for all screens and all formats. The festival was moved to Nice in its eighth year (1995), and, since 1997, has been held in Biarritz, France." .

ns1:50466 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The athletics at the 2008 Summer Olympics – men's high jump at the 2008 Olympic Games took place on 17 August 2008–19 August at the Beijing National Stadium. The event was won by Andrey Silnov of Russia, the nation's second victory (after 2000) in the athletics at the 2008 Summer Olympics – men's high jump." .

ns1:50477 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "NHK News Ohayō Nippon (Japanese: NHKニュース おはよう日本) is a Japanese morning television show on NHK General TV, anchored mainly by Kozo Takase and Maho Kuwako, from the NHK studios at NHK Broadcasting Center in Tokyo, Japan. It is simulcast on NHK World Premium to countries other than Japan (except weekday edition, from 4:30–6:00 a.m. JST)." .

ns1:50479 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Bob Bryan and Mike Bryan won in the final 6–2, 6–3 against Juan Ignacio Chela and Nicolás Massú." .

ns1:50481 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Hurricane Erika was the strongest and longest-lasting tropical cyclone in the 1997 Atlantic hurricane season. It developed from a tropical wave on 03 September 1997 and moved west-northwestward across the tropical Atlantic Ocean, steadily intensifying until it attained hurricane status on September 4, becoming the fifth named storm and third hurricane of the season. Erika was the only tropical cyclone in the Atlantic Ocean in the months of August and September, the first occurrence of such event in an Atlantic hurricane season since 1929." .

ns1:50497 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Rosmalen Grass Court Championships, current sponsored name the Libéma Open (formerly known as the Ricoh Open, Heineken Trophy, Ordina Open, UNICEF Open, and Topshelf Open), is a professional tennis tournament held in the town of Rosmalen, on the outskirts of the city of 's-Hertogenbosch (Den Bosch), the Netherlands. The men's and women's tennis matches are played on outdoors grass court at the Autotron convention center, and constitute a stage on the Association of Tennis Professionals and the Women's Tennis Association. The next year, 1990, the tournament became part of the newly founded Association of Tennis Professionals and was officially called the Continental Grass Court Championships." .

ns1:50510 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2000 UK Championship was a professional ranking snooker tournament that took place at the Bournemouth International Centre in Bournemouth, England. The event started on 2000 and the televised stages were shown on BBC between 25 November 2000 and 03 December 2000. This was to be the last UK Championship to be held in Bournemouth, where the event was hosted since the 1998." .

ns1:50522 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1984 World Indoor Bowls Championship was held at the Coatbridge indoor bowling club, North Lanarkshire, Scotland, from 1984–1984." .

ns1:50533 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1937 All-Ireland Senior Hurling Championship Final was a hurling match played at Fitzgerald Stadium in Killarney, County Kerry on 1937 to determine the winners of the All-Ireland Senior Hurling Championship 1937, the 51st season of the All-Ireland Senior Football Championship, a tournament organised by the Gaelic Athletic Association for the champions of the three hurling provinces of Ireland." .

ns1:50534 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013–14 Hessenliga of the Hessenliga, the highest association football league in the German state of Hesse, was the sixth season of the league at tier five (V) of the German football league system and the 36th season overall since establishment of the league in 1978, then as the Oberliga Hessen. No team was promoted from the Hessenliga to the Regionalliga Südwest above as 2013–14 champions TGM SV Jügesheim decided to withdraw from the league while 1." .

ns1:50553 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Cooke's Spring was a typical Apache Wars skirmish which took place in 1857. Apache raiders were tracked through the frontier by a party of United States of America Army cavalry who intercepted the native warriors at Cooke's Spring in the Black Range of New Mexico." .

ns1:50571 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 FA Women's Cup Final was the 47th final of the FA Women's Cup, England's primary cup competition for women's association football teams. The final was contested between Birmingham City L.F.C. and Manchester City W.F.C. on 13 May 2017 at Wembley Stadium in London." .

ns1:50573 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1979 Women's Nordic Football Championship was the sixth edition of the Women's Nordic Football Championship tournament. It was held from 05 July 1979 to 08 July 1979 in Oslo, Fredrikstad and Setskog in Norway." .

ns1:50595 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Kentucky Apple Festival is an annual festival held in Paintsville, Kentucky during the first Friday and Saturday in October. Eventually, the Kentucky Apple Festival became the more popular of the two and the Johnson County Fair became nonexistent." .

ns1:50599 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Action of 4 September 1782 was a small naval engagement which was fought off the Île-de-Batz between a French naval frigate Hébé and a Royal Naval frigate HMS Rainbow. On 04 September 1782 the 44 gun frigate HMS Rainbow under Captain Henry Trollope armed entirely with carronades was off the French coast near the Île-de-Batz when a frigate was sighted." .

ns1:50604 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2003 UEFA Intertoto Cup finals were won by FC Schalke 04, Villarreal Club de Fútbol, and A.C. Perugia Calcio." .

ns1:50610 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Flugplatzrennen (known alternately as either 1961 Flugplatzrennen or 5th Flugplatzrennen) was a motor race, run to Formula One rules, held on 17 September 1961 at Zeltweg Air Base, Austria. The race was run over 80 laps of the circuit, and was dominated by British driver Innes Ireland in a Lotus 21." .

ns1:50611 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The athletics at the 1962 British Empire and Commonwealth Games – men's 4 × 110 yards relay as part of the athletics at the 1962 British Empire and Commonwealth Games was held at the Perry Lakes Stadium on Saturday 1962." .

ns1:50625 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2005 Equatorial Express crash, also known as the 2005 Baney plane crash occurred on 16 July 2005 after an Equatorial Express Airlines Antonov An-24 crashed into a side of a mountain near Baney, Equatorial Guinea." .

ns1:50626 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1994–95 Frauen DFB Pokal was the 15th season of the cup competition, Germany's second-most important title in women's football. In the final which was held in Berlin on 24 June 1995, FSV Frankfurt defeated TSV Siegen 3–1, thus winning their fourth cup title." .

ns1:50632 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Sambat (Filipino: Labanan sa Sambat, Spanish: Batalla de Sambat) was the culminating battle of the first revolts of the Katipunan in Laguna. The battle was the final major action for the Katipunan chapter of \"Maluningning\" ending in the defeat of the rebels and martial law in Laguna province. The attacks on the Laguna towns of Lumban, Paete, Pakil, Siniloan, Cavinti, Santa Cruz, Magdalena and Pagsanjan were tactically successful in gaining arms and recruiting the cuadrilleros or local native police of each town." .

ns1:50640 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Glory By Honor XII was the 12th Glory By Honor professional wrestling event produced by Ring of Honor (ROH). It took place on 26 October 2013, at the Frontier Fieldhouse in Chicago Ridge. Glory By Honor XII featured seven professional wrestling matches, which involved different wrestlers from pre-existing scripted feuds, plots, and storylines that played out on ROH's television programs." .

ns1:50658 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012–13 Scottish Cup was the 128th season of Scotland's most prestigious football knockout competition. The tournament began on 04 August 2012 and ended on 26 May 2013. From round four onwards, selected matches from the Scottish Cup are broadcast live in Ireland and the United Kingdom by BBC Scotland and Sky Sports." .

ns1:50666 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Carson Sink UFO incident is a unidentified flying object incident alleged by John L. McGinn and John R. Barton, two United States of America Air Force Colonels, to have taken place near Carson Sink in western Nevada in the United States of America while they were en route from California to Colorado in a B-25 on 24 July 1952." .

ns1:50667 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Yokohama-e (横浜絵; literally “Yokohama pictures”) are ukiyo-e Japanese woodblock printing depicting non-East Asian foreigners and scenes of Yokohama. The port of Yokohama was opened to foreigners in 1859 and ukiyo-e artists, primarily of the Utagawa school, produced more than 800 different woodblock printing in response to a general curiosity about these strangers." .

ns1:50675 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Victory Boogie-Woogie is the last, unfinished creative work work of the Dutch abstract painter Piet Mondrian. Left incomplete in 1944, since 1998 it has been in the collection of the Kunstmuseum Den Haag in The Hague." .

ns1:50677 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Scuttling of SMS Cormoran off Guam on 07 April 1917 was the result of the United States entry into World War I and the internment of the German merchant raider SMS Cormoran. Captain Zuckschwerdt pulled into Apra Harbor, Guam on December 14 with the intention of receiving coal from the Americans on the island. When war was declared on 07 April 1917, the United States Marines and sailors on Guam were notified and set out from their base." .

ns1:50682 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Strikeforce: Heavy Artillery was a mixed martial arts event held by Strikeforce on 15 May 2010, in St. Louis, Missouri, United States of America at the Enterprise Center. Bobby Lashley, who was originally set to fight on the Strikeforce: Nashville card, was rumored to have fought on this card, however he was moved to the card for Strikeforce: Los Angeles." .

ns1:50703 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 U.S. Women's Open Golf Championship was the 71st United States Women's Open Championship, held 07 July 2016–10 at CordeValle Golf Club in San Martin, California, southeast of San Jose. The United States Women's Open Championship is the oldest of the five current major championships and the third of the 2016 season." .

ns1:50704 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Aer Lingus Flight 164 was a scheduled Boeing 737 passenger flight that was hijacked on 02 May 1981, en route from Dublin Airport in Ireland to Heathrow Airport in the United Kingdom." .

ns1:50705 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Red Ford or Battle of the String of Lorne was a battle in 1294 or else after September 1296 between Clan Campbell and Clan MacDougall in Lorne, Scotland. The battle was on the borders of Loch Awe and Lorne, with the site and battle named Red Ford (Scottish Gaelic: Ath Dearg) after the ford which ran red with blood where the battle took place." .

ns1:50706 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 U.S. Women's Open Golf Championship was the 62nd United States Women's Open Championship, held June 28 to July 1 at the Pine Needles Lodge and Golf Club in Southern Pines." .

ns1:50711 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 European Aquatics Championships took place in Glasgow, Edinburgh and Luss in the central belt of Scotland, from 3 to 12 August 2018. The Tollcross International Swimming Centre hosted the swimming events with the diving being hosted by the Royal Commonwealth Pool in Edinburgh. The open water swimming competition took place at Loch Lomond, while Scotstoun Sports Campus hosted the synchronised swimming events." .

ns1:50716 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Eruption of Mount Tarawera occurred in the early hours of 10 June 1886 in the North Island near Rotorua then extended to Waimangu, New Zealand." .

ns1:50745 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "These are the results for the girls' singles event at the tennis at the 2014 Summer Youth Olympics – girls' singles. Xu Shilin of People's Republic of China won the gold medal, defeating Iryna Shymanovich of Belarus in the final, 6–3, 6–1." .

ns1:50759 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2001 Family Circle Cup was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor clay courts at the Family Circle Tennis Center in Charleston in the United States of America and was part of WTA Tier I tournaments of the 2001 WTA Tour. It was the 29th edition of the tournament and ran from 16 April 2001 through 22 April 2001." .

ns1:50760 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2013 Cebu local elections was held in the Province of Cebu last 2013 within the Philippine general election. On September 28, 2012, Hilario Davide III, was announced by the Liberal Party as its candidate for governor." .

ns1:50786 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Evonne Goolagong Cawley and Peggy Michel were the defending champions." .

ns1:50796 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1974 Southeastern 500 was a NASCAR Winston Cup Series racing event that was held on 17 March 1974, at Bristol Motor Speedway in Bristol, Tennessee. Bristol Motor Speedway is a NASCAR short track venue located in Bristol, Tennessee. Donnie Allison, Cale Yarborough, Bobby Isaac, Benny Parsons, and Bobby Allison would be the respective leaders for certain parts of the race." .

ns1:50798 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1979 Avon Championships of Florida was a women's tennis tournament played on indoor carpet courts at the Sportatorium in Hollywood, Florida in the United States that was part of the 1979 Avon Championships circuit. It was the third and final edition of the tournament and was held from 22 January 1979 through 28 January 1979." .

ns1:50820 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Emotional Roadshow World Tour (stylized as EMØTIØNAL RØADSHØW WØRLD TØUR) was the fifth concert tour by American music duo Twenty One Pilots, in support of their album Blurryface." .

ns1:50821 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2003 Asturian parliamentary election was held on Sunday, 25 May 2003, to elect the 6th General Junta of the Principality of Asturias. The 1999 Asturian regional election was held on 13 June 1999, setting the election date for the General Junta on Sunday, 25 May 2003." .

ns1:50832 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1960 Bulgarian Cup Final was the 20th final of the Bulgarian Cup (in this period the tournament was named Cup of the Soviet Army), and was contested between FC Septemvri Sofia and PFC Lokomotiv Plovdiv on 1960 at Vasil Levski National Stadium in Sofia." .

ns1:50834 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Meridional Gothic (French: gothique méridional) is a specific and militant style of Gothic architecture developed in the South of France, especially in the Toulouse region. Meridional Gothic, as its name suggests, is found in the southern part of France, mainly in the regions where Catharism had developed, and which were subjected to religious and military repression from the North. Romanesque architecture persisted for longer in the south of France than in the north, and the transition to Gothic was gradual." .

ns1:50841 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle for No.3 Post (28 May 1915–30 May 1915) was fought during the Gallipoli Campaign in the World War I, between the forces of the New Zealand Mounted Rifles Brigade and the Turkish 19th Division. The battle for No.3 Post seemed a major event to those involved, but in General Ian Hamilton's dispatches the fight only received a brief mention: \"On 28 May 1915, at 9 p.m., a raid was made on a Turkish post overlooking the beach 1,200 yards north of Kaba Tepe, H.M.S." .

ns1:50856 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1970 Air Canada Silver Broom, the men's World Curling Championships, was held at the Utica Memorial Auditorium in Utica, New York, United States of America between 1970-1970." .

ns1:50861 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Bow Mistress Stakes is a Tasmanian Racing Club Group 3 Thoroughbred horse race held under Weight for age conditions, for fillies and mares aged three years old and upwards, over a distance of 1,200 metres at the Elwick Racecourse, Glenorchy, Australia in February." .

ns1:50866 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "An Nevis independence referendum was held in Nevis on 10 August 1998. If it had passed, Nevis would have seceded from the Saint Kitts and Nevis." .

ns1:50882 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Miss Universe 1961, the 10th Miss Universe pageant, was held on 15 July 1961 at the Miami Beach Auditorium in Miami Beach, United States." .

ns1:50910 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Baduhenna Wood was a battle, possibly fought (but not proven) near Heiloo, Netherlands, in 28 AD between the Frisii and a Roman army led by the Roman general Lucius Apronius." .

ns1:50936 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Perch was a British offensive of the Second World War which took place from 7 to 14 June 1944, during the early stages of the Battle of Normandy." .

ns1:50937 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1994 UCI Mountain Bike World Championships were held in Vail, Colorado, United States of America from 18 to 19 September 1994." .

ns1:50949 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 FIFA Beach Soccer World Cup was the fifth edition of the FIFA Beach Soccer World Cup, governed by FIFA. It took place in Dubai, the United Arab Emirates between 16 November 2009 and 22 November 2009. All matches are listed as local time in Dubai, (UTC+4) There were 284 goals scored in 32 matches, for an average of 8.88 goals per match." .

ns1:50959 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Pemaquid (02 August 1689–3, 1689) was a successful attack by a large band of Abenaki Indians on the English fort at Pemaquid, Fort Charles, then the easternmost outpost of colonial Massachusetts (present-day Bristol, Maine)." .

ns1:50976 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Mr Gay World 2010, the 2nd Mr Gay World pageant, was held at the Nedre Vollgate 11, in Oslo, Norway on 2010. Charl Van Den Berg of South Africa, was crowned Mr Gay World 2010 by outgoing titleholder Max Krzyzanowski from Ireland." .

ns1:50987 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Xingyang was a battle fought in 190 in the late Eastern Han dynasty as part of the Campaign against Dong Zhuo." .

ns1:51018 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Pan American Women's Handball Championship was the ninth edition of the Pan American Women's Handball Championship, which took place in Santo Domingo from 31 May 2007 to 04 June 2007." .

ns1:51038 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2011 Singaporean general election was held on 07 May 2011 to elect the next Government of Singapore. The governing People's Action Party (PAP) has been in power since Singapore's independence in 1965, and is currently led by the Prime Minister Lee Hsien Loong. The election saw a heavier use of social and online media compared to 2006 Singaporean general election, especially to evade censorship in Singapore. Lee Kuan Yew was also quoted as saying that a younger generation was required to \"carry Singapore forward in a more difficult and complex situation\" while Lee Hsien Loong declared the party \"would change the way it governs\" and do some \"soul-searching\"." .

ns1:51039 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Beirut (12 July 1941) marked the end of hostilities in the Syria–Lebanon campaign of World War II." .

ns1:51079 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Traditional Berber religion is the ancient and native set of beliefs and deities adhered to by the Berbers (Berbers autochthones) of North Africa. Many ancient Berbers beliefs were developed locally, whereas others were influenced over time through contact with other traditional African religions and ancient Egyptian religion, or borrowed during antiquity from the Punic religion from Carthage, Judaism, Iberian mythology, and the Hellenistic religion. Some of the ancient Berbers beliefs still exist today subtly within the Berbers popular culture and tradition. Syncretic influences from the traditional Berbers religion can also be found in certain other faiths. The veneration (not worship) of saints which exists among the modern Berbers in the form of Maraboutism—which is widespread in northwest Africa—may or may not contain traces of prior beliefs or customs concerning the dead. Some scholars distinguish the relationships between the Phoenicians and the Berbers in two phases: When the Phoenicians established in Northwest Africa, they stayed in the coastal regions to avoid wars with the Berbers." .

ns1:51086 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "They were defeated in the semifinals by eighth seed Dinara Safina and Katarina Srebotnik who, in turn, had to give way in the finals to the unseeded Nathalie Dechy and Vera Zvonareva, the new champions." .

ns1:51097 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Philippines earthquake occurred on 15 October 2013 at 8:12:31 PST in Bohol, an island province located in Central Visayas, Philippines. It was the deadliest earthquake in the Philippines in 23 years since the 1990 Luzon earthquake. The 15 October 2013 magnitude (Mw) 7.2 Bohol earthquake produced an approximate 50 kilometres (31 mi) long, 12 kilometres (7.5 mi) wide northeast trending zone of uplift with an approximate 8 kilometres (5.0 mi) long discontinuous ground rupture indicating predominantly reverse‐slip movement on a southeast dipping fault. The earthquake struck as the Philippines was observing the Muslim holiday of Eid-al-Adha." .

ns1:51098 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Cyclone Grant was a tropical cyclone that affected Queensland and the Northern Territory in late 21 December 2011 and early 02 January 2012." .

ns1:51099 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of the Ailette was a battle that took place during the First World War in 23 August 1918, on the banks of the Ailette between Laon and Aisne. Though it was a largely unknown battle of the Hundred Days Offensive during the First World War, the Ailette was the scene of fierce fighting between the French and German forces for its control from August to September 1918, before it returned to the French in October." .

ns1:51107 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1978 Virginia Slims of Houston was a women's tennis tournament played on indoor carpet courts at the NRG Arena in Houston, Texas in the United States that was part of the 1978 Virginia Slims World Championship Series. It was the eighth edition of the tournament and was held from 16 January 1978 through 22 January 1978." .

ns1:51111 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 Price Chopper 400 was the 29th of 36 scheduled stock car races of the 2009 NASCAR Sprint Cup Series and the third in the ten-race season-ending Chase for the Sprint Cup. It was held on 04 October 2009, in Kansas City, Kansas, at Kansas Speedway, before a crowd of 100,000 spectators. The 2009 Price Chopper 400 was the twenty-ninth of thirty-six scheduled stock car races of the 2009 NASCAR Sprint Cup Series, and the third in the ten-race season-ending Chase for the Sprint Cup. It was held on 04 October 2009, in Kansas City, Kansas, at Kansas Speedway, an intermediate track that holds NASCAR races." .

ns1:51116 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 45th Grey Cup was the Canadian Football Council's (CFC) championship game of the 1957 season, which was played on 30 November 1957. The Hamilton Tiger-Cats defeated the Winnipeg Blue Bombers 32–7 at Varsity Stadium in Toronto." .

ns1:51118 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Copa del Rey de Baloncesto 2003–04 was the 68th edition of the Copa del Rey de Baloncesto. It was organized by the ACB and was disputed in Seville in the Palacio Municipal de Deportes San Pablo between 26 February 2004 and 29 February 2004." .

ns1:51149 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "figure skating at the 2016 Winter Youth Olympics at the 2016 Winter Youth Olympics took place at the Hamar Olympic Amphitheatre in Hamar, Norway from 12 to 21 February 2016. The overall quota for the figure skating competition was 76 total skaters, consisting of 38 men and 38 ladies. The International Skating Union published a complete list of entries on 4 12 February 2016. figure skating at the 2016 Winter Youth Olympics events will take place 13 12 February 2016–16 12 February 2016." .

ns1:51150 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Beach Soccer World Cup 1995 was the first edition of the Beach Soccer World Championships, the most prestigious competition in international beach soccer contested by men's national teams until 2005, when the competition was then replaced by the second iteration of a world cup in beach soccer, the better known FIFA Beach Soccer World Cup. The tournament took place at Copacabana Beach in Rio de Janeiro, Brazil. The hosts and heavy favourites Brazil won the tournament by beating the United States 8–1 in the final, coming from behind to claim their first world title. The event was notable for featuring many high profile ex-association football, fuelling its popularity, including the likes of the Brazilians Zico, Júnior and Cláudio Adão, Italian 1982 World Cup winners Alessandro Altobelli and Claudio Gentile, Franco Causio, England's Gary Stevens and Luther Blissett, West German international Rainer Bonhof and brothers René and Willy van de Kerkhof of the Netherlands' 1978 World Cup runners-up squad. The presence of Zico as part of the Brazilian squad, who made over 70 appearances for the Brazilian national association football team, gained considerable attention in the local press prior to the start of the championship. Koch Tavares, the tournament organisers, decided that as the first World Cup of beach soccer, since the sport is a derivative of association football, it would be fitting for the six winners of the FIFA World Cup of football throughout history (as of 1995) to field a team in Rio (being hosts Brazil, Argentina, Italy, Uruguay, England and Germany) and as such the aforementioned nations were invited to play, all of which accepted the opportunity." .

ns1:51169 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "SuperBrawl III was the third SuperBrawl professional wrestling pay-per-view (PPV) event produced by World Championship Wrestling (WCW). It took place on 21 February 1993 from the Asheville Civic Center in Asheville." .

ns1:51173 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Almond Blossom Cross Country (Portuguese: Cross Internacional das Amendoeiras em Flor) is an annual international cross country running competition which takes place in Albufeira, Portugal, in early March. The competition was first organised in 1977 through a joint partnership of the Federação Portuguesa de Atletismo (Portuguese Athletics Federation), the District of Faro and Turismo de Portugal, with the aim of promoting both sport and tourism in the region. The Almond Blossom Cross Country competition is international in its nature, attracting a large number of foreign athletes each year, but it is also a significant draw for Portugal's top domestic runners. Only two Portuguese athletes have won at the World Cross Country Championships (Carlos Lopes and Albertina Dias) and both have been victorious in Albufeira: Lopes won the first ever Almond Blossom race in 1977 while Dias won the 1989 women's race." .

ns1:51188 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Invicta International Airlines Flight 435 was a Vickers Vanguard 952, flying from Bristol Lulsgate to Basel-Mulhouse, which crashed into a forested hillside near Hochwald, Switzerland on 10 April 1973. The aircraft somersaulted and broke up, killing 108 people, with 37 survivors. 108 passengers and crew were killed, while 37 others survived the crash; 36 of these were injured, while one air hostess was unhurt." .

ns1:51200 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1992 Taça de Portugal Final was the final match of the 1991–92 Taça de Portugal, the 52nd season of the Taça de Portugal, the premier Portuguese association football cup competition organized by the Portuguese Football Federation (FPF). The match was played on 24 May 1992 at the Estádio Nacional in Oeiras, and opposed two Primeira Liga sides from the same city: Boavista F.C. and Porto. Boavista F.C. defeated Porto 2–1 to claim the Taça de Portugal for a fourth time in their history. In Portugal, the final was televised live on RTP. As a result of Boavista F.C. winning the Taça de Portugal, the Panteras qualified for the 1992 Supertaça Cândido de Oliveira where they took on their cup opponents who won the 1991–92 Primeira Divisão." .

ns1:51202 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Melodifestivalen 2005 was the selection for the 45th song to represent Sweden at the Eurovision Song Contest. Alcazar and Alcastar got to number one the week before the final, Jimmy Jansson got to the number one some weeks after that with Vi kan gunga, Martin Stenmarck's winner Las Vegas hit the top spot the week after that, while Nanne Grönvall and Håll om mig topped the charts the week after that. The semifinals for Melodifestivalen 2005 began on 12 February 2005." .

ns1:51218 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Set The World On Fire Tour was the fifth concert tour by American recording artist Alicia Keys in support of her fifth album, Girl on Fire." .

ns1:51219 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011–12 Polish Cup was the fifty-eighth season of the annual Poland association football knockout tournament. It began on 19 July 2011 with the first matches of the Extra Preliminary Round and ended on 24 April 2012 with the Final." .

ns1:51225 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The winner was Lena Philipsson with \"Det gör ont\" who later that year came in 5th place in Eurovision Song Contest 2004 in Turkey with the English-language version Det gör ont. The semifinals for Melodifestivalen 2004 began on 2004." .

ns1:51228 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 BWF World Tour, known as 2018 HSBC BWF World Tour for sponsorship reasons, was the first season of the BWF World Tour of badminton, a circuit of 26 tournaments which led up to the World Tour Finals tournament. Below is the schedule released by Badminton World Federation: Below are the 2018 BWF World Tour performances by country." .

ns1:51240 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1961 Solitude Grand Prix was a non-Championship motor race, run to Formula One rules, held on 23 July 1961 at the Solitudering, near Stuttgart. The race was run over 25 laps of the circuit, and was won by Innes Ireland in a Lotus 21." .

ns1:51249 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2001 Food City 500 was the sixth stock car race of the 2001 NASCAR Winston Cup Series season. It was held on 25 March 2001 at the Bristol Motor Speedway in Bristol, Tennessee." .

ns1:51252 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Football League One play-off Final was a football match contested by Brentford F.C. and Yeovil Town F.C.. It was played on 2013 at Wembley Stadium to decide the third team to be promoted from League One to the Championship for the 2013–14 season. Brentford F.C. secured a place in the final by beating Swindon Town 5–4 on penalties, after the two-legs finished 4–4 on aggregate. Yeovil Town F.C. secured a place in the final by beating Sheffield United 2–1 on aggregate. Yeovil went on to win the final 2–1 against Brentford F.C.." .

ns1:51271 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1993 FIVB World Grand Prix was the first edition of the women's volleyball tournament, annually arranged by FIVB. It was played by eight countries from 28 May 1993 to 1993. 1993 Fédération Internationale de Volleyball World League." .

ns1:51324 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Pro Bowl is the all-star game of the National Football League (NFL). The first official Pro Bowl was played in 1951, three weeks after the 1950 NFL Championship Game (between 1939 and 1942, the NFL experimented with all-star games pitting the league's champion against a team of all-stars). The concept of an all-star game was not revived until June 1950, when the newly christened \"Pro Bowl\" was approved. Immediately prior to the Pro Bowl, following the 1949 season, the All-America Football Conference, which contributed three teams to the NFL in a partial merger in 1950, held its own all-star game, the Shamrock Bowl. The early Pro Bowl, contested by the National Football League's Eastern and Western Division stars and played at the Los Angeles Memorial Coliseum, featured the same uniforms from the 1950s to mid-1960s; the Eastern team wore scarlet jerseys with white numerals and a white crescent shoulder stripe, white pants with red stripe, red socks, and a plain red helmet. As the NFC–AFC format was not used between 2014 through 2016, team 1 sported a white uniform with bright orange and team 2 sported a gray uniform with volt green. While players are financially compensated for participating in the Pro Bowl, for a star player, the pay can be less than 1% of their salary. Voting by fans makes up 1/3 of the vote for Pro Bowl players." .

ns1:51327 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2002 Asian Men's Handball Championship was the tenth Asian Men's Handball Championship. It took place from 10 to 19 February 2002 in Isfahan, Iran." .

ns1:51334 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Sant'Egidio was fought on 12 July 1416 at Sant'Egidio, near Umbertide (central Italy) between the condottiere Braccio da Montone and the troops of Perugia, under Carlo I Malatesta." .

ns1:51338 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1959 All England Badminton Championships was a badminton tournament held at Wembley, London, England, from 18 March 1959–21 March 1959." .

ns1:51340 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Tour of Hainan was the eighth edition of the Tour of Hainan cycling stage race. It was rated as a 2.HC event on the UCI Asia Tour, and was held between 20 October 2013 and 28 October 2013, in Hainan." .

ns1:51341 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1999 La Flèche Wallonne was the 63rd edition of La Flèche Wallonne cycle race and was held on 14 April 1999. The race started in Charleroi and finished in Huy." .

ns1:51344 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Australian federal election was held in Australia on 24 November 2007. Considering two-party estimates going back to the 1949 election, the swing to Labor in 2007 was the fourth-largest two-party-preferred swing, behind John Curtin and Labor in 1943 on 7.9 per cent, Malcolm Fraser and the Coalition in 1975 on 7.4 per cent, and Gough Whitlam and Labor in 1969 on 7.1 per cent. Post-election, ALP secretary Tim Gartrell commented on pre-election campaign billboard ads featuring a picture of John Howard stating \"Working families in Australia have never been better off\", which looked like Liberal Party advertisements, were actually paid for by the Labor Party. The first meeting of the 41st Parliament after the 2004 Australian federal election was on 16 November 2004, hence the parliament would have expired on 2007 had it not been dissolved earlier. On 14 October, John Howard gained the agreement of the governor-general, Major-General Michael Jeffery, to dissolve the House of Representatives and hold a general election for the House and half the Senate on 24 November 2007. During the last term of parliament before the 2007 election, the deadline for new voter enrolment was brought forward from 7 working days after the issue of the writ to the same day. On 14 October, Howard announced a 24 November 2007 election. The Reserve Bank of Australia adjusted interest rates upwards by another 0.25 per cent, the sixth rise since the last election, to a 10-year high of 6.75 per cent, and the first time the Bank had been changed rates during an election campaign. 2007 saw, for the first time during an election campaign, a 0.25-point interest-rate rise to 6.5 per cent by the Reserve Bank, the sixth rise since the last election in 2004. In 24 November 2007 interest rates were raised for the sixth time since the 2004 Australian federal election, to a 10-year high of 6.75 per cent. In response to Labor criticism of the government on the rate rises, Howard stated in 2007 \" can scour every transcript, and I will make them available, of every interview that I gave during that election campaign and he will find no such commitment. During 2007 Immigration Minister Kevin Andrews's decision to cut Australia's refugee intake and expressing public concern about Sudanese migrants was branded as racist." .

ns1:51356 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Samarra, also called Operation Baton Rouge, took place in 2004 during the Iraq War. The city of Samarra in central Iraq had fallen under the control of insurgents shortly after insurgents had seized control of Fallujah and Ramadi. In preparation for an offensive to retake Fallujah, on 01 October 2004, 5,000 American and Iraqi troops assaulted Samarra and secured the city after three days of fighting." .

ns1:51366 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1972 Federation Cup was the tenth edition of the most important competition between national teams in women's tennis. 31 nations participated in the tournament, which was held at Ellis Park in Johannesburg, South Africa from 20 March 1972–25 March 1972. South Africa became the first team other than United States or Australia to win the Fed Cup. All ties were played at Ellis Park in Johannesburg, South Africa on hard courts." .

ns1:51370 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1984 U.S. Women's Open Golf Championship was the 39th United States Women's Open Championship, held July 12–15 at Salem Country Club in Peabody. Hollis Stacy won her third United States Women's Open Championship, one stroke ahead of runner-up Rosie Jones." .

ns1:51376 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Miracle of Cordoba (known in Austria national association football team as Das Wunder von Córdoba (The Miracle of Córdoba) and in Germany as Die Schmach von Córdoba or Die Schande von Córdoba (The Disgrace of Córdoba)), describes the game of association football between Austria national association football team and defending World Champions Germany national association football team that occurred on 21 June 1978 at the conclusion of the second round of the 1978 FIFA World Cup, in Córdoba, Argentina. The game is fondly remembered by fans of the Austrian national team for being the first time in 47 years that Austria national association football team had defeated Germany (during partition, called Germany national association football team). A draw between the Dutch and the Italians combined with a decisive five goal victory by Germany national association football team over Austria national association football team would give the West Germans the advantage over the Dutch, and a berth in the Final. If the Netherlands–Italy game had a winner, Germany national association football team could still qualify for the third place match by beating Austria national association football team, or in case of an Italian loss, even with a draw. Hans Krankl then scored to put Austria national association football team ahead, but Bernd Hölzenbein equalised for Germany national association football team a few minutes later. Then in 1982, at the next World Cup in Spain, Germany national association football team and Austria national association football team met again, in a final group-stage game in Gijón." .

ns1:51386 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "United Nations Operation in Somalia II (UNOSOM II) was the second phase of the United Nations intervention in Somalia, from 01 March 1993 until 28 March 1995, after the country had become involved in civil war in 1991. It did not formally take over operations in Somalia until UNITAF was dissolved on 1993. UNOSOM II's mandate ended in 28 March 1995 when US ships off the coast of Somalia assisted in the safe departure of the remaining UNOSOM troops. The withdrawal of UN troops from Somalia was completed on 28 March 1995." .

ns1:51394 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Piltown took place near Piltown, County Kilkenny in 1462 as part of the Wars of the Roses." .

ns1:51403 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Hurricane Joaquin (Spanish: , wah-KEEN) was a powerful tropical cyclone that devastated several districts of the Bahamas and caused damage in the Turks and Caicos Islands, parts of the Greater Antilles, and Bermuda. The tenth named storm, third hurricane, and second major hurricane of the 2015 Atlantic hurricane season, Joaquin evolved from a non-tropical low to become a tropical depression on 28 September 2015, well southwest of Bermuda. With all 34 deaths attributed to the storm occurring at sea, Joaquin has the highest offshore death toll for any Atlantic hurricane since the Escuminac hurricane in 1959, which killed 35 people in the Northumberland Strait. Convective showers and thunderstorms steadily increased on September 27, and at 03:00 UTC on 28 September 2015 the NHC assessed the system to have become a tropical depression, situated roughly 405 mi (650 km) southwest of Bermuda." .

ns1:51404 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Junior men's race at the 2001 IAAF World Cross Country Championships – junior men's race was held at the Hippodrome Wellington in Ostend, Belgium, on 2001." .

ns1:51406 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1993 Island Games were the fifth Island Games, and were held on the Isle of Wight, from 03 July 1993 to 1993." .

ns1:51408 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Sunrise Over Tiananmen Square (French: Le jour se lève sur la place Tienanmen) is a 1998 National Film Board of Canada short film animated documentary directed by Shui-Bo Wang." .

ns1:51418 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 Three Nationals Figure Skating Championships included the Czech Figure Skating Championships, Slovakia, and Polish Figure Skating Championships. The event was held on 16 December 2010–18, 2010 at the DOXXbet Arena in Žilina, Slovakia." .

ns1:51429 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1922 UCI Road World Championships was the second edition of the UCI Road World Championships, the annual world championships for road bicycle racing. The championships took place in Liverpool, United Kingdom on 03 August 1922." .

ns1:51430 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "This page shows the results of the diving for men and women at the diving at the 1991 Pan American Games, held from 02 August 1991 to 18 August 1991 in Havana, Cuba." .

ns1:51433 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 Beach Volleyball World Championships is a beach volleyball event, that was held from 26 June 2009 to 2009 in Stavanger, Norway. The Beach Volleyball World Championships are organized every two years, and Norway is the first Northern European country to host the event." .

ns1:51442 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 Premios Juventud (Youth Awards) were broadcast by Univision on 17 July 2008." .

ns1:51447 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Jibrieni was an attack on 17 December 1941 by a Soviet submarine on an Axis convoy and its Romanian escorts off the coast of the Romanian village of Prymorske (today Prymorske, Ukraine). On 17 December 1941, the Soviet M-class submarine M-59 carried out an attack against an Axis convoy near the coastal town of Prymorske in 17 December 1941." .

ns1:51463 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Jorma Panula Conducting Competition, is a music competition for young European conductors held in Vaasa, (Finland). The first competition, named after the conductor and conducting professor Jorma Panula, was organized in 1999 with the idea of being a platform for young conductors starting their professional careers." .

ns1:51465 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Kraśnik started on 23 August 1914, in the province of Galicia and the adjacent areas across the border in the Russian Empire, in northern Austria (in present-day Poland), and ended two days later. The battle was also the first of a series of engagements between Austria-Hungary and Russian Empire all along the Galicia front. Going into the battle of Kraśnik, the Austro-Hungarian forces enjoyed two key advantages over their Russian opponents: superior numbers and a better strategic position." .

ns1:51475 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Tiburon Challenger was a professional tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts. It took place in Tiburon, United States of America between 26 September 2016 and 02 October 2016." .

ns1:51488 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 IRB Tbilisi Cup was the second edition of this international rugby union tournament, created by the International Rugby Board. It was played from 14 to 22 June 2014 at the Avchala Stadium in Tbilisi. The hosts Georgia were joined by Spain and two teams that regularly take part in the IRB Nations Cup, Argentina XV and Emerging Italy." .

ns1:51496 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Wilberforce Eaves and Ernest Lewis defeated Walter Bailey and Charles Simond 6–4, 6–4, 6–3 in the All Comers' Final, but the reigning champions Herbert Baddeley and Wilfred Baddeley defeated Eaves and Lewis 8–6, 5–7, 6–4, 6–3 in the Challenge Round to win the Gentlemen' Doubles tennis title at the 1895 Wimbledon Championships." .

ns1:51498 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Point Pleasant — known as the Battle of Kanawha in some older accounts — was the only major action of Dunmore's War. Along the Ohio River near modern Point Pleasant, forces under the Shawnee Chief Cornstalk attacked Virginia militia under Colonel Andrew Lewis, hoping to halt Lewis's advance into the Ohio Valley. After the battle, the Virginians, along with a second force led by Lord Dunmore, the Royal Governor of Virginia, marched into the Ohio Valley and compelled Cornstalk to agree to a treaty, ending the war. The Battle of Point Pleasant forced Cornstalk to make peace in the Dunmore's War, ceding to Virginia the Shawnee claims to all lands south of the Ohio River (today's states of Kentucky and West Virginia). By the end of that year, the same militiamen who had fought at Point Pleasant managed to drive Lord Dunmore and the British troops supporting him out of Virginia. It cites Point Pleasant as a \"battle of the Revolution\". Nevertheless, the Battle of Point Pleasant is honored as the first engagement of the American Revolution during \"Battle Days\", an annual festival in modern Point Pleasant, now a city in West Virginia." .

ns1:51511 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Test 219 was a nuclear test conducted by the Soviet Union in the atmosphere via ICBM. The test was performed on 24 December 1962 over the Novaya Zemlya test range." .

ns1:51520 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1144 papal election followed the death of Pope Celestine II and resulted in the election of Lucius II. Pope Celestine II died on 1144 at Rome, after a pontificate of only five months. The elect took the name Lucius II and received episcopal consecration on 1144." .

ns1:51523 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Auburn–Georgia Tech football rivalry is an American college football rivalry between the Auburn Tigers and Georgia Tech Yellow Jackets football Yellow Jackets. On 25 November 1892, 1920, Georgia Tech Yellow Jackets football defeated Auburn at Grant Field by a score of 34 to 0 for a share of the SIAA title." .

ns1:51532 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2009 Namibian general election were held in Namibia on 27 November 2009–28 November 2009. Voting ended on 28 November 2009 and official election results, released on 4 December, showed that Hifikepunye Pohamba and his SWAPO Party were re-elected, each with over 75% of the vote." .

ns1:51537 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Bangkok Challenger II was a professional tennis tournament played on hard courts. It took place in Bangkok, Thailand between 09 January 2017 and 14 January 2017." .

ns1:51543 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1964 United Kingdom general election was held on 15 October 1964, five years after the previous election, and thirteen years after the Conservative Party, first led by Winston Churchill, had entered power. It resulted in the Conservatives, led by its fourth leader, Alec Douglas-Home, narrowly losing the election to the Labour Party, led by Harold Wilson, with Labour having an overall majority of four seats. It resulted in Labour ending its thirteen years in opposition and led Harold Wilson to become, at the time, the youngest Prime Minister of the United Kingdom since Lord Rosebery in 1894. Both major parties had changed leadership in 1963; after the sudden death of Hugh Gaitskell early in the year, Labour chose Harold Wilson (who was then thought of as being on the party's centre-left), while Alec Douglas-Home (then the Earl of Home) had taken over as Conservative leader and Prime Minister of the United Kingdom in the autumn after Harold Macmillan announced his resignation. This proved to be unworkable, and Harold Wilson called a snap election in 1966 United Kingdom general election. Harold Wilson became Prime Minister of the United Kingdom, replacing Alec Douglas-Home. The four-seat majority was not sustainable for a full Parliament, and Harold Wilson called another 1966 United Kingdom general election." .

ns1:51545 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The murder of Shalhevet Pass was a shooting attack carried out in Hebron, West Bank, on 26 March 2001, in which a Palestinian sniper killed 10-month-old Israel infant Shalhevet Pass. According to Deborah Sontag of the New York Times, the murder became a \"potent Israel symbol as an innocent victim of the raging violence.\" At 4:00 pm on 26 March 2001, Shalhevet was shot in her stroller while accompanied by her parents from a parking lot by Hebron's Avraham Avinu neighborhood, where the family lived. The murder, which occurred during the Second Intifada, produced vocal outrage in Israel and abroad." .

ns1:51556 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "One of the first streams of Romanesque architecture in Europe from the 10th century and the beginning of 11th century is called First Romanesque or Lombard Romanesque. Catalan architect Josep Puig i Cadafalch suggested that what was formerly considered the late form of pre-Romanesque architecture in Catalonia bore features of Romanesque and thus classified it as First Romanesque (primer romànic). To avoid the term Pre-Romanesque, which is often used with a much broader meaning than is generally suited to refer to early Medieval and early Christian art, and in Spain may also refer to the Visigothic, Asturias, Mozarabic and Repoblación art forms, Puig i Cadafalch preferred to use the term \"First Romanesque\" or \"first Romanesque art\" to designate those Catalan anticipations of the Romanesque itself." .

ns1:51558 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Kaleidoscope World Tour was a Club Life Tour tour in support of his album Kaleidoscope." .

ns1:51561 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1931 Fuyun earthquake (富蕴地震) occurred on 10 August 1931 at 21:18 UTC. The epicenter was near Fuyun County of northern Xinjiang, People's Republic of China." .

ns1:51562 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1963 Aintree 200 was a motor race, run to Formula One rules, held on 27 April 1963 at Aintree Motor Racing Circuit, England. The race was run over 50 laps of the circuit, and was won by British driver Graham Hill in a BRM P57." .

ns1:51575 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1996 South Ossetian presidential election were held in South Ossetia on 10 November 1996." .

ns1:51579 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1963 Tony Bettenhausen 200 was the seventh round of the 1963 USAC Championship Car season, held on 18 August 1963, at the 1-mile (1.6 km) Milwaukee Mile, in West Allis, Wisconsin. Jim Clark and Team Lotus had finished second at the 1963 Indianapolis 500 after a controversy surrounding the lack of a black flag for winner Parnelli Jones, whose car was leaking oil." .

ns1:51594 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Rewind Tour was the twelfth headlining concert tour by American country music trio Rascal Flatts, in support of their ninth studio album Rewind (2014)." .

ns1:51611 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011–12 UCI Africa Tour was the eighth season of the UCI Africa Tour. Adil Jelloul of Morocco was the defending champion of the 2010–11 UCI Africa Tour. Tarik Chaoufi of Morocco was crowned as the 2011–12 UCI Africa Tour champion." .

ns1:51624 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018–19 Biathlon World Cup was a multi-race series over a season of biathlon, organised by the International Biathlon Union. The season started on 02 December 2018 in Pokljuka, Slovenia and ended on 24 March 2019 in Oslo Holmenkollen, Norway." .

ns1:51625 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1967 ICF Canoe Slalom World Championships were held in Lipno nad Vltavou, Czechoslovakia under the auspices of International Canoe Federation. The mixed C2 team event was not held after having been done so at the 1965 ICF Canoe Slalom World Championships." .

ns1:51630 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Coppa Federale Siciliana (which translates as the Sicilian Federal Cup) was a Sicilian football competition which took place during 23 May 1920. Sicilian football clubs were not entered into the Italian Football Championship after the war and the tournament was organized in an attempt to set up a viable alternative for the four main clubs on the island at the time; Palermo FC, Messinese, Calcio Catania and S.C. Marsala 1912." .

ns1:51644 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1986 Virginia Slims of Indianapolis was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts at the Indianapolis Racquet Club in Indianapolis, Indiana in the United States and was part of the 1986 Virginia Slims World Championship Series. It was the seventh edition of the tournament and ran from 27 October 1986 through 02 November 1986." .

ns1:51671 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The First Battle of Algeciras was a naval battle fought on 06 July 1801 (17 messidor an IX of the French Republican Calendar) between a squadron of British Royal Navy ships of the line and a smaller French Navy squadron at anchor in the fortified Spanish port of Algeciras in the Strait of Gibraltar. Discovering the French at anchor in Algeciras on the morning of 06 July 1801, Saumarez launched an immediate attack on the anchorage through the complicated shoals of Bay of Gibraltar." .

ns1:51701 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "John Newcombe and Tony Roche beat Rosewall and Stolle in the final, 3–6, 8–6, 5–7, 14–12, 6–3 to win the Gentlemen's Doubles title at the 1968 Wimbledon Championships." .

ns1:51718 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Fayal was a naval engagement between the United States and the United Kingdom fought in 27 September 1814 during the War of 1812 just off the Portuguese city of Horta, Faial Island, in the Azores." .

ns1:51722 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Wrocław Open was a professional tennis tournament played on hard courts. It took place in Wroclaw, Poland between 16 February 2015 and 22 February 2015." .

ns1:51726 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1980 Florida Federal Open was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts at the East Lake Woodlands Racquet Club in Tampa, Florida in the United States that was part of the Colgate Series circuit of the 1980 WTA Tour and classified as category AAA. It was the eighth edition of the tournament and was held from 10 November 1980 through 16 November 1980." .

ns1:51735 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Señorita Panamá 1993, the 11th Señorita Panamá pageant, was held in Atlapa Convention Centre, Panama city, Panama, 1993, after weeks of events. About 15 contestants from all over Panama competed for the prestigious crown. At the conclusion of the final night of competition, outgoing titleholder Giselle González crowned María Sofía Velásquez as the new Señorita Panamá." .

ns1:51744 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Épila was fought on 21 July 1348 near Zaragoza, in what is now Spain, between the supporters of the Union of Aragon and King Peter IV, led by Don Lope de Luna. There followed the final battle on 21 July 1348. 'In Épila' 'Army of Lope de Luna' The battle began on the morning of 21 July 1348 with an attempt by Union troops, led by Juan Ximénez de Urrea, Jr., to occupy Épila. The victory of the royalist troops at the battle of Épila was complete and final." .

ns1:51751 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 EuroBasket Women, commonly called EuroBasket Women 2013, was the 34th regional championship held by FIBA Europe. The competition was held in France from 15 June 2013–30 June 2013. This was the fourth time that the EuroBasket Women has been hosted by France. Spain women's national basketball team defeated France 70–69 in the final to win the title. The draw took place on September 21, 2012 at 16:30 local time at Paris, France." .

ns1:51752 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Norman Brookes and Anthony Wilding defeated Karl Behr and Beals Wright 6–4, 6–4, 6–2 in the All Comers' Final to win the Gentlemen' Doubles tennis title at the 1907 Wimbledon Championships." .

ns1:51760 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Human World Tour is the second solo concert tour by American recording artist, Brandy Norwood. The tour primarily visited North America and Europe, with a few dates in Asia. During the tour, Brandy Norwood performed her hits from previous albums along with a few selections from Human." .

ns1:51773 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Ninety-Six was a siege in western South Carolina late in the American Revolutionary War. From 22 May 1781 to June 18, 1781, Continental Army Major General Nathanael Greene led 1,000 troops in a siege against the 550 Loyalists in the fortified village of Ninety Six. The British Army's \"southern strategy\" for winning the American Revolutionary War, which had been successful in taking Charleston and winning submission of much of South Carolina and Georgia, hit a stumbling block in March 1781, after General Lord Cornwallis defeated Continental Army General Nathanael Greene at the Battle of Guilford Courthouse in Greensboro, North Carolina. The order of battle for the forces involved in the battle was: The British outpost at Ninety Six was garrisoned by 550 experienced Loyalists, such as De Lancey's Brigade, formed into Provincial regiments (regular army troops who had been recruited from Loyalists in New York, New Jersey, and South Carolina) under the command of Lieutenant Colonel John Cruger. Greene and about 1,000 men arrived outside Ninety Six on 22 May 1781, the same day that Andrew Pickens and Henry \"Light-horse Harry\" Lee began to besiege nearby Augusta, Georgia." .

ns1:51776 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 Slovenian Supercup was a association football that saw the 2008–09 PrvaLiga champions Maribor face off against Slovenian Cup winners NK Interblock. The match was held on 2009 at Ljudski vrt in Maribor." .

ns1:51785 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1926 Volta a Catalunya was the eighth edition of the Volta a Catalunya cycle race and was held from 22 August 1926 to 29 August 1926." .

ns1:51788 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Boxers Tour was a concert tour by Morrissey, taking place in 03 February 1995." .

ns1:51795 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Toan Thang I (\"Complete Victory\") was a U.S. Army, Army of the Republic of Vietnam (ARVN), 1st Australian Task Force and Royal Thai Volunteer Regiment operation conducted between 08 April 1968 and 31 May 1968 in the Vietnam War." .

ns1:51797 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Challenger Banque Nationale de Saguenay was a professional tennis tournament played on indoor hard courts. It took place in Saguenay, Canada between October 17 and 23 October 2016." .

ns1:51807 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Monaco Grand Prix (formally known as the Formula 1 Grand Prix de Monaco 2018) was a Formula One motor race that was held on 27 May 2018 at the Circuit de Monaco, a street circuit that runs through the Monaco. It was the 6th round of the 2018 Formula One World Championship, the 76th time the Monaco Grand Prix had been held, and the 65th time it had been a round of the Formula One World Championship since the inception of the series in 1950. Daniel Ricciardo led going into the first corner with the top 6 staying in the same order." .

ns1:51823 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Navratilova and Fleming lost in the final 6–4, 6–4 against Raffaella Reggi and Sergio Casal." .

ns1:51827 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018–19 Serie A is the 52nd season of the women's football top level league in Italy. The season is scheduled to run from 15 21 September 2018 to 20 April 2019. Brescia, who finished second in Serie A last season, and Res A.S. Roma Women, who finished eighth, sold their licenses respectively to Milan and A.S. Roma Women." .

ns1:51828 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2001 FIFA Confederations Cup Final was a association football match to determine the winners of the 2001 FIFA Confederations Cup. The match was held at International Stadium Yokohama, Yokohama, Japan national football team, on 10 June 2001 and was contested by Japan national football team and France national association football team. France national association football team won the match 1–0 with the only goal coming after thirty minutes when Patrick Vieira headed in over the advancing keeper Yoshikatsu Kawaguchi from the edge of the penalty area after a long pass from Frank Leboeuf in midfield. This was Japan national football team's first-ever final in a senior FIFA competition, and is also the one of three senior FIFA final with an appearance from an AFC team up to date." .

ns1:51832 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 Yevpatoria gas explosion took place on 24 December 2008 with an explosion in an apartment block in Yevpatoria, a Ukraine Black Sea resort town. On 27 the Ukraine Cabinet of Ministers ordered the central government agencies and the Yevpatoria administration to provide the victims of the blast and the families of those killed with new housing and all financial compensations they are entitled to by January 1, 2009." .

ns1:51858 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 Albanian Cup Final was the 57th final of the Albanian Cup. The final was played at Niko Dovana Stadium in Durres on 2009. The match was contested by KF Tirana, who beat Elbasani in their semi-final, and Flamurtari Vlorë who beat Shkumbini." .

ns1:51861 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The In Rock World Tour was a successful worldwide concert tour by British hard rock group Deep Purple. In 04 July 1970, Deep Purple's MKII released In Rock." .

ns1:51887 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Shopian took place on 03 July 1819 between an expeditionary force from the Sikh Empire and Jabbar Khan, the governor of the Durrani Empire province of Kashmir. On 03 July 1819, the Sikh army attempted to march through Shupiyan to Srinagar but was stopped by a Durrani army headed by Jabbar Khan." .

ns1:51891 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1993 Indonesian Women's Open Tennis Championships was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts at the Gelora Senayan Stadium in Jakarta, Indonesia and was part of Tier IV of the 1993 WTA Tour. It was the inaugural edition of the tournament and was held from 26 April 1993 through 02 May 1993." .

ns1:51897 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Sparrsätra, fought between the forces of King Eric XI of Sweden and rebels led by Holmger Knutsson, took place in 1247 near Enköping in Sweden. The main source for what was at stake during the battle is the Annals of Sigtuna, which relates that at Sparrsätra, in 1247, the rural community of Uppland lost its freedom and was charged with paying taxes, including taxes on grain production and the right of laying up a ship: The archaeologist Mats G. Larsson has commented that it is hardly surprising that such fundamental changes in the structure of society would meet fierce resistance. There may have been additional reasons why the battle took place in 1247. The battle took place at Sparrsätra, which is located in Uppland, north of Enköping." .

ns1:51932 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Bilbao, part of the War in the North in the Spanish Civil War, had the Nationalist Army captured Bilbao and the rest of the Basque Country that was still being held by the Republic. The Basque people in Spain generally inhabit four provinces: Navarre, Álava, Gipuzkoa and Biscay. By 11 19 June 1937 the Basque forces had fallen back to the city of Bilbao, which was defended by a series of rushed fortifications called the \"Bilbao's Iron Ring\"." .

ns1:51941 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 European Curling Championships were held November 22 to 29 at the Palladium de Champéry in Champéry, Switzerland. Switzerland last hosted the European Curling Championships in 2010, when it was also held in Champéry. This edition of the European Curling Championships marks the first appearance of Israel at an international curling event." .

ns1:51949 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1989 Tirreno–Adriatico was the 24th edition of the Tirreno–Adriatico cycle race and was held from 09 March 1989 to 15 March 1989. The race started in Bacoli and finished in San Benedetto del Tronto." .

ns1:51967 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1979 Gould Twin Dixie 125 were the second and third rounds of the 1979 IndyCar season, held on 22 April 1979, at Atlanta Motor Speedway, Hampton, Georgia." .

ns1:51979 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Brobacka was a battle fought in Alingsås between Sweden and Denmark, on 09 August 1566." .

ns1:51990 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Who concert disaster occurred on 03 December 1979, when British rock band the Who performed at Heritage Bank Center (now known as Heritage Bank Center) in Cincinnati, Ohio, United States, and a crush of concert-goers outside the Coliseum's entry doors resulted in the deaths of 11 people." .

ns1:51992 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Bellator MMA: Season Ten was the tenth season for Bellator MMA, a mixed martial arts promotion. It began on 28 February 2014 and aired on Paramount Network." .

ns1:51993 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 NHK Trophy was the final event of six in the 2014–15 ISU Grand Prix of Figure Skating, a senior-level international invitational competition series. It was held at the Namihaya Dome in Ōsaka Prefecture on 28 November 2014–30." .

ns1:51994 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1999 Scottish Parliament general election, to fill 129 seats, took place on 06 May 1999. Following the election, the Labour Party and the Liberal Democrats formed the Scottish Executive, with Labour Member of the Scottish Parliament (MSP) Donald Dewar becoming First Minister. The Conservative Party, still recovering from their wipeout in the 1997 UK general election across Scotland, failed to win a single constituency seat but did manage to win 18 seats through the Additional Member System. Following the election the new parliament met in the General Assembly Hall of the Church of Scotland in Edinburgh for the first time on Wednesday 12 06 May 1999 although the actual devolution of powers from Westminster to the Scottish Parliament did not take place until midnight on Thursday 1 July 1999, almost two months later." .

ns1:51997 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1961 Burundian legislative election were held in Burundi on 08 September 1961 in order to install a government to rule the country following independence from Belgium on 1 July 1962." .

ns1:52001 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Southeast Asian Games (Thai: กีฬาแห่งเอเชียตะวันออกเฉียงใต้ 2007, romanized: Kila haeng echiye tawan oak chiyeng tai 2007), officially known as the 24th Southeast Asian Games, was a Southeast Asian multi-sport event held in Nakhon Ratchasima (Korat), Thailand. This was the sixth time Thailand hosted the Southeast Asian Games and its first time since 1995. It was opened by Prince Vajiralongkorn, the Crown Prince of Thailand at the 80th Birthday Stadium. A general assembly of the SEA Games Federation Council during the 2003 Southeast Asian Games in Hanoi, Vietnam approved Thailand's hosting of the games. On 24, northeast Thailand's Nakhon Ratchasima Province authorities met to discuss the 2007 SEA Games schedule, and progress on the province's under-construction US$65 million sports complex. On 2007, Prince Vajiralongkorn, on behalf of his father, King Bhumibol Adulyadej presented the royal flame to the games organising committee chairman, Suvit Yodmani. After that, the flame was relayed from Bangkok to provinces such as Chonburi, Samut Songkhram, Prachuap Khiri Khan, Ratchaburi, Suphanburi, Nakhon Sawan, Phichit, Phitsanulok, Phetchabun, Loei, Nong Khai, Sakhon Nahkhon, Mukdahan, Ubon Ratchatthani, Surin, Roi Et and Khon Kaen before arrived in the host province, Nakhon Ratchasima on 2007. In Nakhon Ratchasima, the flame was relayed across its districts such as Phimai, Choke Chai, Pak Thong Chai, Pak Chong and Soongnern before arrived at Nakhon Ratchasima City, the host city of the games on 2007. The shape of the boats that resemble the Pimai castle, represents the host city Nakhon Ratchasima, the Northeast region of Thailand and its civilisation one thousand years ago. It is one of the oldest stable cat breeds in Thailand and named after the Nakhon Ratchasima. The24th Southeast Asian Games had 39 venues for the games, 28 in Nakhon Ratchasima, 6 in Bangkok and 5 in Chonburi. The Host Thailand performance was its best ever yet in Southeast Asian Games history and emerged as overall champion of the games." .

ns1:52013 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010–11 ISU Grand Prix of Figure Skating was the ISU Grand Prix of Figure Skating series of the 2010–2011 figure skating season. The Grand Prix series began on 22 October 2010 and ended on 12 December 2010. Skaters who reached the age of 14 by 2010 were eligible to compete on the senior Grand Prix circuit." .

ns1:52017 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Berlin state election, 2001 was held on 21 October 2001 to elect the members of the 15th Abgeordnetenhaus of Berlin. Prior to the election, Mayor Klaus Wowereit had led a minority government of the Social Democratic Party (SPD) and The Greens since June, which had replaced a coalition between the Christian Democratic Union (CDU) and SPD which collapsed in June. Summary of the 21 October 2001 election results for the Abgeordnetenhaus of Berlin." .

ns1:52018 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The swimming at the 2014 South American Games – women's 1500 metre freestyle competition at the 2014 South American Games took place on March 10 at the Estadio Nacional Julio Martínez Prádanos. The swimming at the 2010 South American Games – women's 1500 metre freestyle champion was Kristel Köbrich of Chile." .

ns1:52026 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Rolling Stones' The Rolling Stones 3rd European Tour 1965 was a concert tour by the band. The tour commenced on 15 June 1965 and concluded on 29 June 1965." .

ns1:52029 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 Indonesia Super Series Premier was the sixth super series tournament of the 2011 BWF Superseries. The tournament was held in Jakarta, Indonesia from 21 June 2011–26 June 2011 and had a total purse of $600,000." .

ns1:52039 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Ironman World Championship was a long distance triathlon competition that was held on 2014 in Kailua-Kona, Hawaii. The 2014 Ironman series consisted of 29 Ironman races plus the 2013 Ironman World Championship which was itself a qualifier for the 2014 Championship." .

ns1:52040 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Greek raid on Alexandria (1825) was an unsuccessful attempt organized by Greek bruloteer Constantine Kanaris to destroy the Egyptian fleet at its base in Alexandria in 1825 during the Greek War of Independence." .

ns1:52044 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Shaba I was a conflict in Zaire's Katanga Province lasting from March 8 to 26 May 1977. The banks feared that the war would now bankrupt Zaire's government. Observers were unsure of whether the war had ended with a victory for the Zaire government or had devolved into a guerrilla war. The poor performance of Zaire's military during Shaba I gave evidence of chronic weaknesses." .

ns1:52063 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The We Are Pioneers World Tour is the first headlining tour by American Country music group, The Band Perry. The tour began on 08 November 2013 in Gothenburg, Sweden and ended on 03 October 2014 in Primm, Nevada." .

ns1:52077 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "5th Thailand National Games (Thai: กีฬาเขตแห่งประเทศไทย ครั้งที่ 5, also known as the 1971 National Games and 1971 Interprovincial Games) were held in Nakhon Sawan, Thailand from 2 to 1971, with contests in 13 sports. The 1971 Thailand National Games featured 10 Olympic sports contested at the 1971 Southeast Asian Peninsular Games, 1974 Asian Games and 1972 Summer Olympics." .

ns1:52089 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 59th Technology & Engineering Emmy Awards was held on 08 January 2008 at the 2008 International Consumer Electronics Show in Las Vegas." .

ns1:52097 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "24 Hours in Cyberspace (1996) was \"the largest one-day online event\" up to that date, headed by photographer Rick Smolan with Jennifer Erwitt, Tom Melcher, Samir Arora and Clement Mok. 24 Hours in Cyberspace was an online project which took place on the then-active website, cyber24.com (and is still online at a mirror website maintained by Georgia Tech). The goal was not to show pictures of website and computer monitors, but rather images of people whose lives were affected by the use of the growing Internet." .

ns1:52108 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1989 Copa do Brasil was the 1st staging of the Copa do Brasil. The competition started on 19 July 1989, and concluded on 02 September 1989, with the second leg of the final, held at the Estádio Olímpico Monumental in Porto Alegre, in which Grêmio Football Porto Alegrense lifted the trophy for the first time with a 2-1 victory over Sport Recife." .

ns1:52110 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Naval Battle of the Han River (28 September 1951–30 September 1951) was fought during the Korean War." .

ns1:52127 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Danish Cup Final were the final and deciding match of the 2006–07 Danish Cup. It took place on Thursday 17 May 2007 at Parken Stadium in Copenhagen." .

ns1:52130 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Welsh Open was a professional ranking snooker tournament that took place between 13 February 2012–19 February 2012 at the Newport Centre in Newport, Wales. The breakdown of prize money for this year is shown below: These matches were8 February 2012held 2012 13 February 2012 at the World Snooker Academy, Sheffield, England." .

ns1:52133 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 25th Golden Raspberry Awards, or Razzies, were held on 26 February 2005 at the Ivar Theatre in Hollywood, California to honor the worst films the film industry had to offer in 2004. To celebrate the 25th anniversary of the Golden Raspberry Awards, four special categories—Worst Razzie Loser of Our First 25 Years, Worst \"Comedy\" of Our First 25 Years, Worst \"Drama\" of Our First 25 Years, and Worst \"Musical\" of Our First 25 Years—were created." .

ns1:52149 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2023 Africa Cup of Nations (also referred to as AFCON 2023 or CAN 2023) is scheduled to be the 34th edition of the Africa Cup of Nations, the biennial international men's association football championship of Africa organized by the Confederation of African Football (CAF). Bids: Rejected Bids: After the final vote at the CAF Executive Committee meeting, on 20 September 2014, the CAF announced the hosts for the 2019, 2021 Africa Cup of Nations and 2023 AFCON tournaments: 2019 to Cameroon, 2021 Africa Cup of Nations to Côte d'Ivoire, and 2023 to Guinea. Consequently, Côte d'Ivoire, original hosts of 2021 Africa Cup of Nations, would host the 2023 Africa Cup of Nations, and Guinea, original hosts of 2023, would host the 2025 Africa Cup of Nations." .

ns1:52150 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Poznań Open was a professional tennis tournament played on clay courts. It took place at the Park Tenisowy Olimpia in Poznań, Poland from 9 to 11 July 2016 2015, including the qualifying competition in the first two days. The prize money has decreased to €42,500 + Hospitality, and because of calendar congestion caused by the 2016 Summer Olympics no top 100 player entered that year, which had not happened in Poznań Open before." .

ns1:52155 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The War of the Mantuan Succession (1628–31) was a peripheral part of the Thirty Years' War. The war, fought among the backers of rival claimants, pitted France against the Habsburgs in a contest for control of northern Italy. Duchy of Mantua is the ancestral city where the male line of the Gonzaga dynasty ruled, first as marquesses, then after 1540 as dukes, in vassalage to the Holy Roman Empire. He sought to re-attach the Duchy of Mantua to the Holy Roman Empire; Ferrante being in the Imperial-Spanish camp, was a useful tool to that purpose. The resulting French-Habsburg war over the succession was just one of the many theatres of the Thirty Years' War, fought all over Europe." .

ns1:52160 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of the Trough (March or 1756) was a skirmish of the early French and Indian War (1754–63) fought between Native Americans and United Kingdom settlers in the valley of the South Branch Potomac River in what is now northern Hardy County, USA." .

ns1:52166 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Hungarian Challenger Open is a professional tennis tournament played on indoor hard courts. It takes place in Budapest, Hungary between 06 February 2017 and 12 February 2017." .

ns1:52187 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Hialeah Turf Cup Handicap is a discontinued American Thoroughbred horse race open to horses aged three and older that was run each year at Hialeah Park Race Track in Hialeah until the track closed at the end of the 2001 racing season." .

ns1:52194 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of María (15 June 1809) saw a small Spanish army led by Joaquín Blake y Joyes face an Imperial French corps under Louis Gabriel Suchet. María de Huerva is located 17 kilometres (10.6 mi) southwest of Zaragoza, Spain." .

ns1:52198 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "On the morning of 04 September 2005, six days after Hurricane Katrina struck New Orleans, members of the New Orleans Police Department (NOPD) who were allegedly responding to a call of an officer under fire shot and killed two civilians at the Danziger Bridge: 17-year-old James Brissette and 40-year-old Ronald Madison. On 04 September 2005, almost a week after Hurricane Katrina struck New Orleans, several New Orleans Police Department (NOPD) officers arrived at the Danziger Bridge. The officers involved in the shooting were taken into custody on January 2, 2007, and were indicted for murder and attempted murder. On December 19, 2016, New Orleans Mayor Mitch Landrieu announced a settlement agreement between the city and the families of the Danziger Bridge shootings, plus two other cases involving \"lethal confrontations between officers and civilians in the aftermath of Hurricane Katrina." .

ns1:52205 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Pride Shockwave is the international PPV broadcast name for the mixed martial arts and kickboxing event co-promoted by the Pride Fighting Championships and K-1 on 28 August 2002. It was held at the Japan National Stadium in Tokyo." .

ns1:52207 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Aberto de Tênis do Rio Grande do Sul was a professional tennis tournament played on clay courts. It took place in Porto Alegre, Brazil between 28 September 2015 and 04 October 2015." .

ns1:52212 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Finnish League Cup is the 16th season of the Finnish League Cup, Finland's second-most prestigious cup association football tournament. The group stage matches will be played from 13 January 2012 to 2012." .

ns1:52221 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Germany participated in the Germany in the Eurovision Song Contest 2016 with the song \"Ghost\" written by Thomas Burchia, Anna Leyne and Conrad Hensel. ARD and NDR had initially announced on 19 November Germany in the Eurovision Song Contest 2015 that they had selected the soul and R&B singer-songwriter Xavier Naidoo to represent Germany in Stockholm. Performing in position 10, Germany placed twenty-sixth (last) out of the 26 participating countries with 11 points. Prior to the 2016 Contest, Germany had participated in the Eurovision Song Contest fifty-nine times since its debut as one of seven countries to take part in 1956. NDR confirmed that Germany would participate in the 2016 Eurovision Song Contest on 27 May Germany in the Eurovision Song Contest 2015. Following NDR's announcement on 19 November Germany in the Eurovision Song Contest 2015 that Naidoo had been selected to represent Germany, negative reactions were expressed by both the German public and media. Unser Lied für Stockholm (English: Our Song for Stockholm) was the competition that selected Germany's entry for the Germany in the Eurovision Song Contest 2016. As a member of the \"Big 5\", Germany automatically qualified to compete in the final on 2016. This would have been regularly decided via a draw held during the semi-final allocation draw on 2016, however, prior to the draw, ARD requested of the European Broadcasting Union that Germany be allowed to broadcast and vote in the second semi-final on 2016, which was approved by the contest's Reference Group. Germany was subsequently placed to perform in position 10, following the entry from Sweden and before the entry from France. Germany placed twenty-sixth (last) in the final, scoring 11 points: 10 points from the televoting and 1 point from the juries. Voting during the three shows was conducted under a Eurovision Song Contest 2016 that involved each country now awarding two sets of points from 1-8, 10 and 12: one from their professional jury and the other from televoting." .

ns1:52232 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 GP2 Series season was the forty-sixth season of the second-tier of Formula One feeder championship and also eighth season under the GP2 Series moniker and also the first season after merging with the GP2 Asia Series. The title was won by Italian Davide Valsecchi, driving for DAMS." .

ns1:52239 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1993 European Open Water Swimming Championships was the third edition of the European Open Water Championships and took part from 1993-1993 in Slapy, Czech Republic." .

ns1:52254 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 PDC European Tour Event 1 was the first of eight PDC European Tour events on the 2013 PDC Pro Tour. The tournament took place at the Butlins Minehead in Minehead, England, from 2013–2013." .

ns1:52257 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The MTV Australia Awards 2008 is the new title for the Australian MTV Awards previously known as the MTV Australia Video Music Awards. The show was held at Sydney's Australian Technology Park in Redfern on 2008 and aired live on MTV Australia and Fox8 on the Foxtel, Austar and Optus platforms in Australia and 28 April on SKY Digital in New Zealand." .

ns1:52274 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1952 German Grand Prix was a Formula Two race held on 03 August 1952 at the Nürburgring. It was race 6 of 8 in the 1952 Formula One season, in which each Grand Prix was run to Formula Two rules rather than the Formula One regulations normally used. The 18-lap race was won by Ferrari driver Alberto Ascari after he started from pole position. The date was 03 August 1952, the earliest anyone would claim the Championship until Jim Clark seized the crown on 1 August in 1965, also at the Nürburgring." .

ns1:52277 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1947 PGA Championship was the 29th PGA Championship, held June 18–24 at Plum Hollow Country Club in Southfield, Michigan, a suburb northwest of Detroit. The match play format at the PGA Championship in 1947 called for 12 rounds (216 holes) in seven days: Runyan did not advance in the eleven-way playoff for the one final spot in the match play field." .

ns1:52291 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1974 FIFA World Cup knockout stage was a single-elimination tournament involving the four teams that qualified from 1974 FIFA World Cup. The knockout stage began with the third place play-off on 6 July and ended with the 1974 FIFA World Cup Final on 1974, both at the Olympiastadion in Munich." .

ns1:52306 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The athletics at the 2004 Summer Olympics – men's 400 metres hurdles at the 2004 Summer Olympics as part of the athletics at the 2004 Summer Olympics were held at the Olympic Stadium of Athens from 23 August 2004 to 26. The qualification period for athletics was 1 January 2003 to 2004." .

ns1:52307 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1998 Texaco/Havoline 200 was the fourteenth round of the 1998 CART FedEx Champ Car World Series season, held on 16 August 1998, at Road America in Elkhart Lake, Wisconsin." .

ns1:52314 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 FIVB Volleyball World League was the 28th edition of the annual men's international volleyball tournament played by 36 teams between 02 June 2017 and 08 July 2017. The previous highest set score was 44–42, which had happened twice (in the 1999 FIVB Volleyball World League match between Canada and Brazil, and in the 2016 FIVB Volleyball World League World Olympic Qualifying Tournament match between France and Australia)." .

ns1:52317 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2013 Turkish Super Cup (Turkish: TFF Süper Kupa) was the 40th edition of the Turkish Super Cup since its establishment as Presidential Cup in 1966. The match was contested between the 2012 Turkish Super Cup–13 Süper Lig champions Galatasaray S.K. and the 2012 Turkish Super Cup–13 Turkish Cup winners Fenerbahçe FC, making the game the 375th edition of Kıtalar Arası Derbi. The game was a rematch of the 2012 Turkish Super Cup, which was won by title holders Galatasaray S.K.. Galatasaray S.K. last won the cup in 2012 Turkish Super Cup against Fenerbahçe FC, and Fenerbahçe FC last won the cup in 2009 against Beşiktaş. Galatasaray S.K. in 1996 and 2012 Turkish Super Cup, and Fenerbahçe FC in 1973 and 1985. Galatasaray S.K. were champions in the regular season, finishing ten points ahead of Fenerbahçe FC. In the 2013 Turkish Cup Final they were crowned champions against rivals Trabzonspor by a decisive 1–0 win. This was a consecutive win for Fenerbahçe FC, as they were title holders of the 2012 Turkish Super Cup Turkish Cup Final." .

ns1:52323 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 European Championship was the tenth edition of the Professional Darts Corporation tournament, the European Championship, which saw the top players from the twelve European tour events compete against each other. The tournament took place from 2017–2017 at the Ethias Arena in Hasselt, Belgium." .

ns1:52330 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006 California gubernatorial election occurred on 07 November 2006. The incumbent Republican Governor of California, Arnold Schwarzenegger, won re-election for his first and only full term. Under the California Constitution, the Governor of California serves a four-year term, with a maximum limit of two consecutive terms. Arnold Schwarzenegger was elected during the 2003 California gubernatorial recall election and served out the remainder of Gray Davis's term; he was eligible to serve until 2011." .

ns1:52340 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Sino-Burmese War (Chinese: 清緬戰爭; Burmese: တရုတ်-မြန်မာ စစ် (၁၇၆၅–၆၉)), also known as the Qing invasions of Burma or the Myanmar campaign of the Qing dynasty, was a war fought between the Qing dynasty of China and the Konbaung Dynasty of Burma (Myanmar). Nonetheless, the war, which claimed the lives of over 70,000 Chinese soldiers and four commanders, is sometimes described as \"the most disastrous frontier war that the Qing dynasty had ever waged\", and one that \"assured Burmese independence\". At first, the Emperor envisaged an easy war, and sent in only the Green Standard troops stationed in Yunnan. The Qing kept a heavy military lineup in the border areas of Yunnan for about one decade in an attempt to wage another war while imposing a ban on inter-border trade for two decades. The Burmese control of the Shan States (which covered the present-day Kachin State, Shan State and Kayah State) came in 1557 when King Bayinnaung of the Toungoo dynasty conquered all of the Shan States. The annexed border states ranged from Mogaung and Bhamo in present-day Kachin State to Hsenwi State (Theinni) and Kengtung State (Kyaingtong) in present-day Shan State to Sipsongpanna (Kyaingyun) in present-day Xishuangbanna Dai Autonomous Prefecture, Yunnan. In 1758–59, King Alaungpaya, the founder of the dynasty, sent an expedition to the farther Shan States (present-day Kachin State and northern and eastern Shan State), which had been annexed by the Qing over two decades earlier, to reestablish Burmese authority. Nonetheless, the Emperor kept a heavy military lineup in the border areas of Yunnan for about one decade in an attempt to wage another war while imposing a ban on inter-border trade for two decades. The war is considered the peak of Konbaung Dynasty military power." .

ns1:52345 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1904 Prince Edward Island general election was held in the Canada province of Prince Edward Island on 1904. The election was won by the governing Liberals, led by incumbent Premier Arthur Peters." .

ns1:52346 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Haguenau (18 November 1793 – 22 December 1793) saw a Republican French army commanded by Jean-Charles Pichegru mount a persistent offensive against a Coalition army under Dagobert Sigmund von Wurmser during the War of the First Coalition. Haguenau is a city in Bas-Rhin department of France, located 29 kilometres (18 mi) north of Strasbourg." .

ns1:52351 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2000 Jarafa mosque massacre was an attack on members of Ansar al-Sunna praying at a mosque in Jarafa, a village in the outskirts of Omdurman, Sudan on 08 December 2000." .

ns1:52364 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "athletics at the 2008 Summer Olympics were held during the last ten days of the games, from 15 August 2008 to 24 August 2008, at the Beijing National Stadium. Despite a haul of 100 medals at the games as a whole, People's Republic of China athletes took home two bronze medals from the athletics events. During the 2008 Summer Olympics, 17 new Olympic records and 5 new world records were set in the athletics events." .

ns1:52365 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Proximus Diamond Games was a women's professional tennis Diamond Games played on indoor hard courts at the Sportpaleis in Antwerp, Belgium that was part of the Tier II category of the 2006 WTA Tour. It was the sixth edition of the Diamond Games and was held from 10 February until 18 February 2007 2002." .

ns1:52372 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 IPP Open and the Orto-Lääkärit Open were professional tennis tournaments played on indoor hard courts. The two events took place together at the Tali Tennis Center in Helsinki, Finland, on 11 November 2013–17 November 2013." .

ns1:52387 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1800 United States presidential election was the fourth quadrennial United States presidential election. In what is sometimes referred to as the \"Revolution of 1800\", Vice President of the United States Thomas Jefferson of the Democratic-Republican Party defeated incumbent President of the United States John Adams of the Federalist Party. The Democratic-Republicans nominated a ticket consisting of Vice President of the United States Thomas Jefferson of Virginia and former Senator Aaron Burr of New York. While the 1800 election was a re-match of the 1796 United States presidential election, it ushered in a new type of American politics, a two-party republic and acrimonious campaigning behind the scenes and through the press. Although the congressional election of 1800 turned over majority control of the House of Representatives to the Democratic-Republicans by 68 seats to 38, the United States presidential election had to be decided by the outgoing House that had been elected in the congressional election of 1798 (at that time, the new presidential and congressional terms all started on March 4 of the year after a national election). True or not, House Whigs, who from the start of the 1800 campaign viewed Jefferson as their candidate for president and Burr for vice president, faced two abhorrent possible outcomes when they met to vote: A–the Federalists manage to engineer a victory for Burr, or, B–the Federalists refuse to break the deadlock leaving a Federalist—Secretary of State John Marshall—as Acting President of the United States." .

ns1:52388 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Claudy bombing occurred on 31 July 1972, when three car bombs exploded mid-morning on the Main Street of Claudy in County Londonderry, Northern Ireland." .

ns1:52402 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1999 Nepalese legislative election were held in Nepal on 03 May 1999 and 17 May 1999." .

ns1:52422 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Cara Black and Leander Paes won the title, defeating Liezel Huber and Jamie Murray in the final 7–6(8–6), 6–4." .

ns1:52425 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Immae was fought in 272 between the Roman army of Emperor Aurelian, and the armies of the Palmyrene Empire, whose leader, Queen Zenobia, had usurped Roman control over the eastern provinces. Both armies took the field \"near\" Antioch on the Orontes at Immae (close by Reyhanli, Turkey) in traditional battle formations with infantry in the center and cavalry on the flanks. His infantry was no match for the battle-hardened legionaries and immediately ordered a full retreat to Antioch on the Orontes." .

ns1:52430 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 77th running of the 1993 Tour of Flanders cycling race in Belgium was held on Sunday 04 April 1993. Belgian Johan Museeuw won his first 1993 Tour of Flanders in a two-man sprint against Frans Maassen. The race started in Sint-Niklaas and finished in Meerbeke (Ninove)." .

ns1:52438 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2002 Challenge Bell was a tennis tournament played on indoor carpet courts at the Club Avantage Multi-Sports in Quebec City in Canada that was part of WTA Tier III tournaments of the 2002 WTA Tour. It was the 10th edition of the National Bank Cup, and was held from 16 September 2002 through 22 September 2002." .

ns1:52443 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Verizon Ladies First Tour was a co-headlining concert tour by American recording artists Beyoncé, Alicia Keys and Missy Elliott. ; Keys' second studio album, The Diary of Alicia Keys and Beyoncé's first studio album, Dangerously in Love. That's exactly the aura Beyoncé gave off as she began her closing set of the Verizon Ladies First Tour, which also features Alicia Keys, Missy Elliott and Tamia\"." .

ns1:52444 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Inauguration of George H. W. Bush as the 41st President of the United States was held on Friday, 20 January 1989, at the West Front of the United States Capitol in Washington, D.C. This was the 51st United States presidential inauguration and marked the commencement of the only term of both George H. W. Bush as President and Dan Quayle as Vice President." .

ns1:52474 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1975 Rugby League World Cup (also referred to as the World Series) was the seventh tournament for the Rugby League World Cup. The Kangaroos showed they were worthy World Champions with a comprehensive 25–0 win at Headingley in front of a disappointing crowd of 7,680 which was over 11,000 less than had attended the 1970 World Cup Final between Great Britain and Australia national rugby league team at the same venue." .

ns1:52488 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "A Bumps race is a form of rowing race in which a number of boats chase each other in single file, each crew attempting to catch and ‘bump’ the boat in front without being caught by the boat behind. Bumps racing in fours is also the format of inter-house rowing at Eton College and Shrewsbury School." .

ns1:52491 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 IFK Göteborg season was IFK Göteborg 108th in existence, their 81st season in Allsvenskan and their 37th consecutive season in the league. IFK Göteborg also participated in one competition in which the club continued playing in for the 2014 IFK Göteborg season, 2013–14 Svenska Cupen. IFK Göteborg qualified for the 2013 Svenska Supercupen by winning the 2012–13 Svenska Cupen on 2013. IFK Göteborg qualified for the 2013–14 UEFA Europa League by merit of winning the 2012–13 Svenska Cupen." .

ns1:52507 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Prosperita Open was a professional tennis tournament played on clay courts. It took place in Ostrava, Czech Republic between 25 April 2016 and 01 May 2016." .

ns1:52514 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1938 World Men's Handball Championship was the first ever handball World Championship. It was played in Germany on 05 February 1938 and 06 February 1938. Contested by just 4 national teams, the tournament was won by hosts Germany." .

ns1:52527 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1985 VFL Grand Final was an Australian rules football game contested between the Essendon Football Club and Hawthorn Football Club, held at the Melbourne Cricket Ground in Melbourne on 28 September 1985. It was the 89th annual VFL/AFL Grand Final of the Victorian Football League, staged to determine the premiers for the 1985 VFL season." .

ns1:52542 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "This Battle of Megiddo is recorded as having taken place in 609 BC when Pharaoh Necho II of Ancient Egypt led his army to Carchemish (northern Syria) to join with his allies, the fading Neo-Assyrian Empire, against the surging Neo-Babylonian Empire. Jehoahaz was taken captive to Ancient Egypt, where he became the first king of Judah to die in exile. From being wounded by an arrow to his burial in Jerusalem, the story resembles perhaps too closely stories from I and II Kings about Kings Ahab of Israel and Ahaziah of Judah, events which occurred at least two centuries before Josiah's death." .

ns1:52551 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Northern European Gymnastics Championships was an artistic gymnastics competition held in the city of Limerick in the mid-west of the Ireland. The event was held between the 18th and 20 September 2015 at the University of Limerick. Saturday 18 September 2015 (Team and Individual All-Around competition) Sunday 2015 (Men's and Women's Individual Apparatus Finals)" .

ns1:52558 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "WrestleMania VIII was the eighth annual WrestleMania professional wrestling pay-per-view (PPV) event produced by the World Wrestling Federation (WWF, now WWE). It took place on 05 April 1992, at the RCA Dome in Indianapolis. WrestleMania VIII would also be the first time since the inaugural WrestleMania that the closing match was not contested for the WWF Championship." .

ns1:52565 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1994 CONCACAF Women's Championship determined the CONCACAF's two qualifiers for the FIFA Women's World Cup 1995 — the winner the United States women's national soccer team and the runner-up Canada. The tournament took place in Montreal, Quebec, Canada between 13 August 1994 & 21 1994 and consisted of 5 teams. The 1994 CONCACAF's Women's Championship was the third staging of the CONCACAF's Women's Championship." .

ns1:52567 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Drumclog was fought on 01 June 1679, between a group of Covenanters and the forces of John Graham of Claverhouse, at Drumclog, in South Lanarkshire, Scotland. The Battle of Drumclog is celebrated by some in Scotland as a victory for religious freedom. The Covenanters' flag from this battle at Drumclog now resides in a museum in Scotland." .

ns1:52574 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2013 Rwandan parliamentary election were held in Rwanda between 16 September 2013 and 18 September 2013." .

ns1:52580 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Hunger War or Famine War was a brief conflict between the allied Kingdom of Poland, and Grand Duchy of Lithuania, against the Teutonic Order in summer 1414 in an attempt to resolve territorial disputes. According to Johann von Posilge, 86 knights of the Teutonic Order died from plague following the war. After the Polish–Lithuanian–Teutonic War of 1410–1411 not all issues between the Grand Duchy of Lithuania and Teutonic Order were settled. In the summer of 1414, armies of King Jogaila and Grand Duke Vytautas invaded Prussia, ruled by the State of the Teutonic Order." .

ns1:52583 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 AFC Champions League was the 30th edition of the top-level Asian club association football tournament organized by the Asian Football Confederation (Asian Football Confederation), and the 9th under the current AFC Champions League title. The winners, Al Sadd Sports Club qualified for the 2011 FIFA Club World Cup in Japan. The Asian Football Confederation approved criteria for participation in the 2011 and 2012 seasons. On 30, the Asian Football Confederation announced 12 more MA's that were keen to join the ACL, in addition to ten participating national associations. All losers from the qualifying play-off entered the 2011 Asian Football Confederation Cup group stage." .

ns1:52586 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The alpine skiing at the 1960 Winter Olympics – men's downhill competition of the alpine skiing at the 1960 Winter Olympics was held at Squaw Valley Ski Resort on Monday, February 22." .

ns1:52615 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Petit Le Mans was the 2014 edition of the Petit Le Mans automotive endurance race, held on September 30–2014, at the Road Atlanta circuit in Braselton, Georgia, United States of America." .

ns1:52620 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Sevenoaks railway accident occurred on 24 August 1927 between Dunton Green railway station and Sevenoaks railway station." .

ns1:52627 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1923 French Grand Prix (formally the XVII French Grand Prix) was a Grand Prix motor race held at Tours on 02 July 1923. The race was run over 35 laps of the 22.83 km circuit for a total distance of just under 800 km and was won by Henry Segrave driving a Sunbeam." .

ns1:52640 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1966 FIBA Africa Championship for Women was the 1st FIBA Africa Championship for Women, played under the rules of FIBA, the world governing body for basketball, and the FIBA Africa thereof. The tournament was hosted by the Guinea from 11 April 1966 to 14, 1966." .

ns1:52653 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Die Wende and Peaceful Revolution (German: Friedliche Revolution) was the process of sociopolitical change that led to the opening of East Germany's borders with the west, the end of the Socialist Unity Party of Germany (SED) in the East Germany (East Germany) and the transition to a parliamentary democracy, which enabled the German reunification in 1990. After the fall of the Berlin Wall, in demonstrations calling for German reunification, this morphed into \"Wir sind ein Volk\" (\"We are one people\")." .

ns1:52661 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 5th Independent Spirit Awards, honoring the best in independent filmmaking for 1989, were announced on 24 March 1990 at the Hollywood Roosevelt Hotel in Los Angeles." .

ns1:52677 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1989 Memorial Cup occurred May 6–13 at the brand new Saskatchewan Place in Saskatoon, Saskatchewan. It was the 71st annual Memorial Cup competition and determined the major junior ice hockey champion of the Canadian Hockey League (CHL). In the first ever all-WHL final (in fact, an all-Saskatchewan final), an overflow, bi-partisan crowd of more than 11,000 saw Swift Current beat Saskatoon in overtime to win their first Memorial Cup. The Saskatoon Blades represented the Western Hockey League as the host team at the 1989 Memorial Cup. The Broncos swept Saskatoon in four games, ending their season, however, the club qualified for the 1989 Memorial Cup as the host team of the event." .

ns1:52682 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Gumbinnen Operation, also known as the Goldap Operation (or Goldap-Gumbinnen Operation, Russian: Гумбиннен-Гольдапская наступательная операция), was a Soviet offensive on the Eastern European theatre of World War II late in 1944, in which forces of the 3rd Belorussian Front attempted to penetrate the borders of East Prussia." .

ns1:52692 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012–13 Superleague Greece was the 77th season of the highest association football league of Greece and the seventh under the name Super League. Olympiacos F.C. were the defending champions, having won their 39th Greek championship in the 2011–12 Superleague Greece." .

ns1:52711 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Vienna Offensive was launched by the Soviet 2nd and 3rd Ukrainian Fronts in order to invade Vienna, Austria, during World War II. Joseph Stalin reached an agreement with the Western Allies prior to 13 April 1945 concerning the relative postwar political influence of each party in much of Eastern and Central Europe; however, these agreements said virtually nothing about the fate of Austria, then officially considered to be merely the Ostmark area of Greater Nazi Germany after the Anschluss. After they took Sopron and Nagykanizsa, they crossed the border between Hungary and Austria. Having secured his right wing by 2nd Ukrainian Front, Tolbukhin was now ready to advance into Austria and take Vienna. On 02 April 1945, Vienna Radio denied that the Austrian capital had been declared an open city. Central Vienna was now cut off from the rest of Austria. Austrian politician Karl Renner astutely set up a Provisional Government in Vienna sometime in April with the tacit approval of the victorious Soviet forces, and declared Austria's secession from the Third Reich. This order of battle shows what remained \"on paper\" of the German armies that fought in Hungary and Austria." .

ns1:52720 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The swimming at the 2000 Summer Olympics – men's 100 metre breaststroke event at the 2000 Summer Olympics took place on 16 September 2000–17 September at the Sydney Olympic Park Aquatic Centre in Sydney, Australia. Domenico Fioravanti made an Olympic milestone to become Italy's first ever gold medalist in swimming." .

ns1:52735 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Clausura 2018 Copa MX (officially the Clausura 2018 Copa Corona MX for sponsorship reasons) was the 79th staging of the Copa MX, the 51st staging in the professional era and is the twelfth tournament played since the 1996–97 edition. The tournament began on 09 January 2018 and ended on 2018. As winners, Necaxa earned a spot to face Monterrey (winners of Apertura 2017 Copa MX edition), in the 2018 Supercopa MX. The Liga MX teams in Pot 1 are the four best teams in the Apertura 2017 Copa MX classification table not participating in the 2018 CONCACAF Champions League as well as the defending champion. Pot 2 contains the next four best Liga MX clubs in the Apertura 2017 Copa MX classification table not participating in the 2018 CONCACAF Champions League. Pot 3 contains the next five best Liga MX clubs in the Apertura 2017 Copa MX classification table not participating in the 2018 CONCACAF Champions League." .

ns1:52737 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "anti-revisionism is a position within Marxism–Leninism which emerged in the 1950s in opposition to the reforms of Soviet leader Nikita Khrushchev. Because different political trends trace the historical roots of revisionism to different eras and leaders, there is significant disagreement today as to what constitutes anti-revisionism. anti-revisionism is a position within Marxism–Leninism based on its interpretation by Stalin, who supported the dictatorship of the proletariat, drastic and fast-paced economic transformation in the short-term, and violent confrontation with capitalist powers." .

ns1:52741 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Helsingborg was fought on 08 July 1362 between the Danish and Hanseatic fleets. As part of the ongoing trading and territorial disputes between the Hanseatic League, Sweden, and Denmark, the Hanseatic cities made an agreement with Sweden and Holstein to jointly attack Denmark, the agreed targets being Helsingborg and Copenhagen. As Wittenborg's fleet sailed through the narrow Øresund en route for Copenhagen he was persuaded to attack the town of Helsingborg and its fortified citadel." .

ns1:52758 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Bandy World Championship 1973 was the eighth Bandy World Championships and was contested between four men's bandy playing nations. The championship was played in Moscow and Krasnogorsk (Soviet Union national bandy team) on 17 February 1973–24, 1973." .

ns1:52768 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2003 ICF Canoe Slalom World Championships were held in Augsburg, Germany under the auspices of International Canoe Federation for the record-tying third time. Augsburg hosted the championships previously in 1957 and 1985 when the city was part of West Germany, and matches the times hosted by Spittal, Austria (1963, 1965, 1977), Meran, Italy (1953, 1971, 1983), and Bourg St.-Maurice, France (1969, 1987, 2002 ICF Canoe Slalom World Championships)." .

ns1:52773 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Itamar attack, also called the Itamar massacre, was an attack on a Jewish family in the Israeli settlement of Itamar in the West Bank that took place on 11 March 2011, in which five members of the same family were murdered in their beds. At midday on Friday, 11 March 2011, they decided that they would enter Itamar later that night, and carry out an attack armed with knives. In response to the Itamar attack, on 13 March, the Israeli cabinet approved the construction of 500 housing units in Gush Etzion, Ma'ale Adumim, Ariel and Modi'in Illit, areas of the West Bank that Israel intends to keep under any permanent accord with the Palestinians. An opinion poll conducted by the Harry S. Truman Research Institute for the Advancement of Peace and the Palestinian Center for Policy and Survey Research found, according to the groups, \"In light of the attack in Itamar, 59% among Israelis oppose and 33% support the government policy to relax the security measures in the West Bank such as the removal of road blocks.\" The groups interviewed 1,270 adults face-to-face in the West Bank, East Jerusalem, and Gaza Strip from 17 to 19 11 March 2011. Gary Rosenblatt, editor-in-chief of The Jewish Week, commented that reactions to the Itamar killings may have been more muted given that the victims were religious Israelis living in a small West Bank settlement." .

ns1:52775 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Davis Cup was the 105th edition of the Davis Cup, a tournament between national teams in men's tennis. Argentina Davis Cup team won their first Davis Cup title, after 4 runner-up finishes, defeating Croatia in the final. Federico Delbonis defeated Ivo Karlović in the final match to give Argentina Davis Cup team its first Davis Cup title, after a huge comeback from Juan Martín del Potro against Marin Čilić in the fourth match. Seeding: The seeding was based on the Davis Cup Rankings of 2016 (shown in parentheses below). Seeding: The seeding was based on the Davis Cup Rankings of 2016 (shown in parentheses below). Seeding: The seeding was based on the Davis Cup Rankings of 2016 (shown in parentheses below). Seeding: The seeding was based on the Davis Cup Rankings of 2016 (shown in parentheses below)." .

ns1:52779 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Tuyuhun invasion of Gansu was a battle between the Tuyuhun Kingdom and the Tang Dynasty in 623. In 623, the Tuyuhun departed from their homeland in the pastures surrounding Qinghai Lake, known in Mongolian as Kokonur, and invaded Gansu." .

ns1:52785 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017–18 Eerste Divisie, known as Jupiler League for sponsorship reasons, was the sixty-second season of Eerste Divisie since its establishment in 1955. 2016–17 Eerste Divisie champions VVV-Venlo gained promotion to the 2017–18 Eredivisie, and was replaced by Go Ahead Eagles, who finished bottom in the 2016–17 2017–18 Eredivisie. Ten teams, two from the 2017–18 Eredivisie and eight from the Eerste Divisie, played for two spots in the 2018–19 2017–18 Eredivisie, the remaining eight teams playing in the 2018–19 Eerste Divisie." .

ns1:52786 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 All England Badminton Championships (officially known as the Yonex All England Badminton Championships 2007 for sponsorship reasons) was a badminton tournament which took place at Arena Birmingham in Birmingham, England, from 6 to 11 March 2007 and had a total purse of $200,000. The 2007 All England Badminton Championships was the third tournament of the 2007 BWF Super Series and also part of the All England Badminton Championships championships, which had been held since 1899. This international tournament was held at Arena Birmingham in Birmingham, England." .

ns1:52799 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Wintour is Coming was a headlining concert tour by American rock band Fall Out Boy in support of their 2015 album, American Beauty/American Psycho." .

ns1:52801 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 German Open Badminton Championships was the first grand prix gold and grand prix tournament of the 2012 BWF Grand Prix Gold and Grand Prix. The tournament was held in innogy Sporthalle, Mulheim an der Ruhr, Germany from 28 February 2012 until 04 March 2012 and had a total purse of $125,000." .

ns1:52812 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1916 Irondale earthquake struck in the north–central region of the U.S. state of Alabama on 18 October 1916. Even an earthquake similar in size to the 1916 Irondale event today could damage thousands of buildings and cause up to $1 billion in damage. On February 4, 1886, the first recorded earthquake from Alabama hit the western part of the state, jolting people in Sumter and Marengo counties. Following minor events in 1917, 1927, 1931, and 1939, threatening activity resumed in the late 1950s, when, in 1957, an earthquake near Birmingham extended from northern Alabama to Georgia and Tennessee, cracking and displacing small objects. The Geological Survey of Alabama predicts a 90 percent chance of an earthquake of 6.0 or greater in the area by 2040. The shaking from such an earthquake would spread again into the northern segment of Alabama. The South Carolina seismic zone has produced earthquakes as powerful as 7.3 on the Richter scale, and a large earthquake originating from it could easily reach Alabama. The earthquake originated near the city of Irondale, 3 miles (5 km) north of Birmingham, striking just after 4 P.M. local time. Because the earthquake took place before Alabama had a seismograph network, its Richter scale magnitude of 5.1 is an estimate, but it is still considered the most powerful earthquake in state history, affecting seven other states. He claims that \"If there is an earthquake in one of the zone areas, North Alabama is certainly going to feel it." .

ns1:52821 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Gulf Coast campaign or the Spanish conquest of West Florida in the American Revolutionary War, was a series of military operations primarily directed by the governor of Spanish Louisiana, Bernardo de Gálvez against the British province of West Florida. Begun with operations against British positions on the Mississippi River shortly after Britain and Spain went to war in 1779, Gálvez completed the conquest of West Florida in 1781 with the successful siege of Pensacola. Spain officially entered the American Revolutionary War on 1779, with a formal declaration of war by King Charles III." .

ns1:52831 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1952 Paris–Nice was the tenth edition of the Paris–Nice cycle race and was held from 25 March 1952 to 30 March 1952. The race started in Paris and finished in Nice." .

ns1:52840 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Triplemanía XIV was the fourteenth Triplemanía professional wrestling show promoted by AAA. The show took place on 18 June 2006 in Naucalpan, Mexico. The following year Peña and AAA held their first Triplemanía event, building it into an annual event that would become AAA's Super Bowl event, similar to the WWE's WrestleMania being the biggest show of the year. The Triplemanía XIV show featured seven professional wrestling matches with different wrestlers involved in pre-existing scripted feuds, plots and storylines." .

ns1:52846 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2002 Miller Lite 250 was the fourth round of the 2002 CART season, held on 02 June 2002 at the Milwaukee Mile oval in West Allis, Wisconsin." .

ns1:52857 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1974 Old Dominion 500 was a NASCAR Winston Cup Series race that took place on 29 September 1974, at Martinsville Speedway in Martinsville, Virginia. Today, the race is a part of the First Data 500 annual series of Martinsville races that occur in the autumn for the NASCAR Sprint Cup Series. Canadian Earl Ross (driving for Junior Johnson) defeated his United States of America opponent Buddy Baker by more than one lap, lapping the entire field in the process. Petty, Yarborough and Pearson managed to win 27 out of 30 races in 1974 and the only other drivers to win a race were Bobby Allison, who won twice, and Earl Ross." .

ns1:52876 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Live Bootleg! Tour was a 1978 United States concert tour by the rock group Aerosmith. The tour began on 09 August 1978 in Boston, Massachusetts and ended on 12 December 1978 in Wichita, Kansas." .

ns1:52878 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 10 January 2010 Battle of Beledweyne began on 10 January 2010 when Ahlu Sunna Waljama'a forces attacked and captured East Beledweyne which was being held by the Hizbul Islam insurgent group. It became the first battle in months were al-Shabaab and Hizbul Islam groups cooperated against the Transitional Federal Government. Fighting started on early 10 January 2010, as forces loyal to the Transitional Federal Government-aligned Ahlu Sunnah Waljama'a (which controls large parts of central Somalia) attacked the city of Beledweyne which was held by Islamist Insurgents loyal to Hizbul Islam. It was also reported that Transitional Federal Government forces led by Tawane Hure joined the battle to support Ahlu Sunnah Waljama'a and forces loyal to Harakat al-Shabaab Mujahedeen (Somalia's most powerful insurgent group), which had been at war with Hizbul Islam since September 2009 joined to back Hizbul Islam fighters for the first time in months." .

ns1:52882 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Revolution Technologies Pro Tennis Classic was a professional tennis tournament played on outdoor clay courts. It took place in Indian Harbour Beach, United States, on 2016–08 May 2016." .

ns1:52910 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Quebec Conference, codenamed \"QUADRANT\", was a highly secret military conference held during World War II by the governments of the United Kingdom, Canada, and the United States. It took place in Quebec City on 17 August 1943–24, 1943, at both the Citadelle and the Château Frontenac. Couture found a leather portfolio with a gold inscription \"Churchill-Roosevelt, Quebec Conference, 1943.\"" .

ns1:52913 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2012 Faroe Islands Premier League was the seventieth season of top-tier football on the Faroe Islands. The season began on 24 March 2012 with a match between B36 Tórshavn and FC Suðuroy, and ended on 06 October 2012." .

ns1:52922 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Surfer is a integrated advertising campaign launched in 1999 by Diageo to promote Guinness-brand draught stout in the United Kingdom." .

ns1:52942 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Ghost Stories Tour was an intimate concert tour by British band Coldplay, consisting of nine shows at a handful of low-key venues, during eight non-consecutive months in 2014, reaching North America, Europe, Asia and Oceania. The concerts were part of Coldplay's campaign in support of their sixth studio album Ghost Stories (2014). In 21 March 2014, Coldplay debuted the album Ghost Stories at an intimate gig in Los Angeles. The band performed at E-Werk in Cologne, Germany on 25 April 2014 and performed at the Beacon Theatre in New York City, New York on 5 May, prior to the release of Ghost Stories. On 20 October, Coldplay announced the release of a new concert film and live album, Ghost Stories Live 2014, which was released on 24 November." .

ns1:52956 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Bundesvision Song Contest 2012 was the eighth edition of the annual Bundesvision Song Contest musical event. The contest was held on 2012 at the Max-Schmeling-Halle, Berlin, following Tim Bendzko's win in the 2011 contest in North Rhine-Westphalia with the song \"Wenn Worte meine Sprache wären\". This was the second time that the Max-Schmeling-Halle arena had hosted the contest after previously hosting in 2010, and Berlin's third time of hosting the contest, after previously hosting in 2007, and 2010." .

ns1:52975 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Ha My Massacre was a massacre purportedly conducted by the Republic of Korea Marine Corps on 25 February 1968 of unarmed citizens in Hà My village, Điện Dương commune, Điện Bàn District, Quảng Nam in South Vietnam." .

ns1:52977 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1950–51 FA Cup was the 70th season of the world's oldest association football cup competition, the Football Association Challenge Cup, commonly known as the FA Cup. Newcastle United F.C. won the competition for the fourth time, beating Blackpool 2–0 in the 1951 FA Cup Final at Wembley, London. The 1951 FA Cup Final took place on Saturday, 28 April 1951 at Wembley and ended in a victory for Newcastle United F.C. over Blackpool by 2–0, with both goals scored by Jackie Milburn." .

ns1:52984 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Anglo-Egyptian treaty of 1936 (officially, The Treaty of Alliance Between His Majesty, in Respect of the United Kingdom, and His Majesty, the King of Egypt) was a treaty signed between the United Kingdom and the Kingdom of Egypt. Under the terms of the treaty, the United Kingdom was required to withdraw all its troops from Egypt, except those necessary to protect the Suez Canal and its surroundings, numbering 10,000 troops plus auxiliary personnel. The treaty was to last for 20 years; it was negotiated in the Zaafarana palace, signed in London on 26 August 1936 and ratified on 22 December. The 1936 treaty did not resolve the question of Sudan, which, under the terms of the existing Anglo-Egyptian Condominium Agreement of 1899, stated that Sudan should be jointly governed by Egypt and United Kingdom, but with real power remaining in British hands." .

ns1:52989 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Ramsele witch trial, which took place in 1634, is one of the few known Swedish witch trials before the great witch mania of 1668–1676. In the year of 1634 a man and several women were put on trial in the city of Ramsele in Ångermanland in Norrland in Sweden." .

ns1:52992 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "United Nations Security Council Resolution 27, adopted on 01 August 1947, called for the opposing Netherlands and Indonesia Republicans in the Indonesian National Revolution to lay down their arms and to allow a peaceful mediation of the conflict." .

ns1:53003 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1989 French Open – Mixed Doubles was held from 29 May until 1989 on the outdoor clay courts at the Stade Roland Garros in Paris, France. Manon Bollegraf and Tom Nijssen won the title, defeating Horacio de la Peña and Arantxa Sánchez Vicario in the final." .

ns1:53015 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "In the Battle of Lake Huleh in 26 June 1157, a Crusader army led by King Baldwin III of Jerusalem was ambushed and badly defeated by Nur ad-Din Zangi, the emir of Aleppo and Damascus. The Hula Valley is located in the northeast part of modern-day Israel. At the time of the battle, the area belonged to the Kingdom of Jerusalem." .

ns1:53017 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 FEI World Equestrian Games were held in Mill Spring, North Carolina, U.S. at the Tryon International Equestrian Center, from September 11 to 2018. This is the eighth edition of the FEI World Equestrian Games, which are held every four years and run by the International Federation for Equestrian Sports (FEI)." .

ns1:53018 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1994 Portuguese Grand Prix (formally the XXIII Grande Premio de Portugal) was a Formula One motor race held on 25 September 1994 at the Circuito do Estoril. Which also left Gerhard Berger to take pole position for Ferrari ahead of Hill, David Coulthard in the other Williams followed by Hakkinen 4th in the leading McLaren-Peugeot, followed by Alesi who was 5th in the second Ferrari, Ukyo Katayama 6th for Tyrrell-Yamaha, as the top 10 was completed by Brundle 7th in the second McLaren-Peugeot, Barrichello 8th for Jordan ahead of Frentzen 9th for Sauber and Jos Verstappen 10th for Benetton. Soon afterwards, Jos Verstappen was able to pass Martin Brundle's McLaren for 5th and the order after the second round of pit stops had seen no change of the order as it was Damon Hill who made it 3 wins on the trot ahead of Williams teammate David Coulthard who was 2nd which also gave Williams the lead in the Constructors Championship with their 1-2 result ahead Mika Hakkinen who third for McLaren, Barrichello 4th for Jordan, Verstappen 5th for Benetton & Martin Brundle 6th in the second McLaren rounding out the top 6." .

ns1:53030 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "A 2013 Norwegian parliamentary election was held in Norway on 8 09 September 2013 and 09 September 2013 to elect all 169 members of the unicameral Norwegian Parliament. The election was the fourth for incumbent Prime Minister of Norway Jens Stoltenberg, whose party was previously defeated in the 2001 2013 Norwegian parliamentary election, but who won both the 2005 2013 Norwegian parliamentary election and the 2009 Norwegian parliamentary election (though in the latter election, the opposition narrowly received more votes than the coalition) leading the red–green coalition." .

ns1:53037 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2019 Kentucky gubernatorial election took place on 05 November 2019, to elect the Governor of Kentucky and lieutenant Governor of Kentucky of Kentucky. The Democratic nominee, Kentucky Attorney General Andy Beshear, defeated Republican incumbent Matt Bevin by just over 5,000 votes, or 0.37%, making this the closest gubernatorial election in Kentucky since 1899 by total votes, and the closest ever by percentage. No Republican Governor of Kentucky of Kentucky has ever been elected to more than one term, even after the state's constitutional prohibition of governors serving consecutive terms was repealed in 1992. Bevin is only the second Republican Governor of Kentucky of Kentucky in the last 50 years. Andy Beshear is the first Governor of Kentucky of Kentucky to be a direct relative of a former Governor of Kentucky. According to the Kentucky state constitution, the swearing in of a Kentucky Governor of Kentucky must be held on the fifth Tuesday following the election (December 10). This process, which is enforced through the Goebel Election Law, has only been used once, during the 1899 Kentucky gubernatorial election. On November 11, U.S. Senate Majority Leader Mitch McConnell, a Kentucky Republican, announced that \"all indications are\" Beshear would be the next Governor of Kentucky. Beshear was sworn in as Governor of Kentucky on 2019." .

ns1:53052 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1895 United Kingdom general election was held from 13 July 1895 to 07 August 1895." .

ns1:53067 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006 AMD at The Glen was a NASCAR Nextel Cup Series Go Bowling at The Glen that was held on 13 August 2006 at Watkins Glen International in Watkins Glen, New York. Contested over 90 laps on the 2.45 mile (3.942 km) road course, it was the 22nd Go Bowling at The Glen of the 2006 NASCAR Nextel Cup Series. Kevin Harvick of Richard Childress Racing won the Go Bowling at The Glen. Watkins Glen International (nicknamed \"The Glen\") is an automobile Go Bowling at The Glen track located in Watkins Glen, New York at the southern tip of Seneca Lake. In 1968 the Go Bowling at The Glen was extended to six hours, becoming the 6 Hours of Watkins Glen. Kurt Busch had the pole position for this Go Bowling at The Glen, in Penske Racing's #2 Miller Lite-sponsored Dodge. Busch was sent to the rear of the field and the Go Bowling at The Glen then got controlled by Tony Stewart and Kevin Harvick who passed Tony during the final 3 laps to win the Go Bowling at The Glen." .

ns1:53068 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2002 Eurocard German Open was a women's tennis event that was played from 06 May 2002 to 12 May 2002. It was played at the Rot-Weiss Tennis Club Berlin in the German capital of Berlin." .

ns1:53094 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Noordhorn, fought on 30 September 1581, was a pitched battle of the Dutch Revolt, fought between a Spanish army commanded by Colonel Francisco Verdugo – consisting of Walloon, German, Spanish, and Albanian soldiers – and a Dutch States rebel army under the Englishman John Norreys – comprising English, Scottish, Dutch, and Walloon troops – in the province of Friesland. On 30 September 1581 Verdugo forced Norreys to give battle using a strategy of attrition. In early September, after a failed raid on his camp, Verdugo was sure of Norreys' intentions of giving battle, and thus he advanced his army to near Norreys' camp near the village of Noordhorn." .

ns1:53095 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2001 Danish Figure Skating Championships (Danish: Danmarks Mesterskaberne 2001) was held in Aalborg from 05 January 2001 to 7, 2001." .

ns1:53101 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Prairie III was a U.S. Marine Corps operation in Quảng Trị Province, South Vietnam that sought to eliminate People's Army of Vietnam (PAVN) forces south of the Vietnamese Demilitarized Zone (DMZ) that took place from 19 March to 19 April 1967. Operation Prairie III concluded on 19 April 1967, the Marines had lost 56 killed and claiming that the PAVN suffered 252 killed and 4 captured." .

ns1:53105 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Gwangju Open was a professional tennis tournament played on hard courts. It took place in Gwangju, South Korea between 13 August 2018 and 19 August 2018." .

ns1:53110 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Battle of Dhala took place in 20 March 1965 during the Aden Emergency." .

ns1:53115 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Vítkov Hill was a part of the Hussite Wars. Vítkov Hill was located on the edge of the city of Prague and the battle occurred in a vineyard established by Sigismund's father, Charles IV. One of the most important points in the fortifications of Prague was Vítkov Hill. In honor of this battle, Vítkov Hill was renamed Žižkov after Jan Žižka." .

ns1:53117 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Helen Gourlay and Kerry Harris won in the final 6–0, 6–4 against Coleman and Krantzcke." .

ns1:53124 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Fei River, also known as \"Feishui\" (simplified Chinese: 淝水之战; traditional Chinese: 淝水之戰; pinyin: Féishŭi zhī zhàn) was a battle in AD 383, where Fu Jiān (Chinese: 苻堅) of the Di Former Qin Empire was decisively defeated by the outnumbered Jin (Han Chinese-led) army of Eastern Jin. (The location of the battle, the Fei River, no longer exists, but is believed to have flowed through modern Lu'an, Anhui, near the Huai River.) The aftermath of the battle included the Former Qin Empire falling into massive civil war and its eventual destruction, ensuring the survival of Eastern Jin and other Chinese regimes south of the Yangtze River (长江). In October 383, the Former Qin forces under Fu Rong captured the important Jin city of Shouyang (壽陽, in modern Lu'an, Anhui)." .

ns1:53132 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Ethias Trophy was a professional tennis tournament played on hard courts. It took place in Mons, Belgium between 30 September 2013 and 06 October 2013." .

ns1:53138 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 5th Hum Awards ceremony, presented by PEL, powered by QMobile and brought by Kashmir Cooking Oil & Banaspati took place in Lahore on 29 April 2017 at 7:30pm PST." .

ns1:53140 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1998 Lower Saxony state election was held on 01 March 1998 to elect the members of the 14th Landtag of Lower Saxony. Summary of the 01 March 1998 election results for the Landtag of Lower Saxony." .

ns1:53154 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Knockshinnoch Disaster was a mining accident that occurred in September 1950 in the village of New Cumnock, Ayrshire, Scotland." .

ns1:53177 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Atlanta Open (known for sponsorship reasons as the Truist Atlanta Open), is a professional men's tennis tournament that has been played in the Atlanta area in the United States of America since 2010, usually during July or August. The event was previously held in Indianapolis from 1988 until it was moved to Atlanta after 2009. The Atlanta Open was known as the Atlanta Tennis Championships for its first two years before signing BB&T as a title sponsor in 2012. In December 2009 it was announced that the ATP had sold the license to a group in Atlanta, Georgia, where it would be held at the Atlanta Athletic Club. Prior to 2010 Atlanta had previously held a tennis tournament known as the Verizon Tennis Challenge from 1992 to 2001. In 2012, the tournament gained BB&T as title sponsor and changed its name to the BB&T Atlanta Open. The 2012 and later editions have been held in Atlantic Station in midtown Atlanta." .

ns1:53182 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2015 Vincentian general election were held in Saint Vincent and the Grenadines on 09 December 2015." .

ns1:53201 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Monterrey Challenger was a professional tennis tournament played on hard courts. It took place in Monterrey, Mexico from 26 to 31 of 26 October 2015." .

ns1:53206 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label ": ‘Η Ἁγία καὶ Μεγάλη Σύνοδος τῆς ’Ορθοδόξου ’Εκκλησίας) (also sometimes called the Council of Crete), was a synod of set representative bishops of the universally recognised autocephalous local churches of Eastern Orthodox Christianity held in Kolymvari, Crete. On 27 19 June 2016, the Synod of the Church of Antioch issued a statement concerning the Crete Council that stated that the documents adopted by in Crete were not binding for the Patriarchate of Antioch; the Church of Antioch recognized the Synod as \"a preliminary gathering on the way to a Pan-Orthodox Council\", while the documents it adopted as not final and open for discussion." .

ns1:53207 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 MTV Millennial Awards were held on 2017 at the Palacio de los Deportes in Mexico City, and was broadcast on 2017 through MTV Latin America." .

ns1:53210 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Summer Tour was a co-headlining tour by American bands Matchbox Twenty and the Goo Goo Dolls. Beginning in 2013, the tour supported the band's albums, North and Magnetic respectively." .

ns1:53217 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Linea Aeropostal Venezolana Flight 253 (November 1956) was being operated by a Lockheed L-749 Constellation, registration YV-C-AMA and named Jose Marti, on an international scheduled Passenger flight that took off from Idlewild International Airport (modern John F. Kennedy International Airport) bound for Caracas International Airport on 27 November 1956." .

ns1:53226 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 6 Hours of Spa-Francorchamps, formally the WEC 6 Heures de Spa-Francorchamps, was an endurance sports car racing 6 Hours of Spa-Francorchamps held at the Circuit de Spa-Francorchamps, Stavelot, Belgium on 2017–2017. It was the second race of the 2017 FIA World Endurance Championship, and was the sixth running of the 6 Hours of Spa-Francorchamps as part of the championship." .

ns1:53261 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2001 Clásica de Almería was the 16th edition of the Clásica de Almería cycle race and was held on 04 March 2001. The race started in Vícar and finished in Vera." .

ns1:53289 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "'the Green Mountain War' or Arabic: حرب عمان, romanized: Ḥarb ʻumān), also known as Jebel Akhdar rebellion broke out in 1954 and again in 1957 in Oman, as an effort by the local Omanis in the interior of Oman led by their elected Imam, Ghalib Alhinai, to protect the Imamate of Oman from the occupation plans of sultan Said bin Taimur, backed by the British government, who were eager to gain access to the oil wells in the interior lands of Oman. On 1954, the Muscat and Oman Field Force (MOFF), later renamed Sultan of Oman's Armed Forces (SAF), which had eight British officers among its troops, marched from Fahud to Adam and occupied it. On 29 July 1957, the House of Commons debated the Jebel Akhdar War under the title \"Muscat and Oman\"." .

ns1:53315 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1932 Jalisco earthquake began on 03 June 1932 at 10:36 UTC with a megathrust event that registered 8.1 on the moment magnitude scale. With a maximum perceived intensity of X (Extreme) on the Mercalli intensity scale, at least 400 deaths were caused in Mexico and neighboring Guatemala. It was the first of a series of seismic events that affected parts of western Mexico during the month of 03 June 1932, all reaching magnitude 7 or greater. Carlos Valdez, director of the National Seismology Center at the National Autonomous University of Mexico, called the earthquake the most powerful quake in modern Mexican history. The initial earthquake of 03 June 1932 off of the coast of Colima and Jalisco caused extensive damage along western Mexico, with much of the deaths and injuries concentrated in the area of Guadalajara." .

ns1:53325 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Stångebro, or the Battle of Linköping, took place at Linköping, Sweden, on 25 September 1598 (O.S.) In the battle, an army of c. 8, 000–12,000 commanded by Duke Charles defeated a mixed force of c. 5,000–8,000 consisting of an invading army of mercenaries in the king's employ and diverse but poorly co-ordinated supporting Swedish noblemen's forces commanded by King of both Sweden and the Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth Sigismund III Vasa, who was acting to maintain and restore his personal union against anti-Catholic forces in Lutheran Sweden. The force won a battle against the troops of Duke Charles at Stegeborg but soon found itself encircled and retired to Linköping." .

ns1:53345 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1979 European Competition for Women's Football was a women's association football tournament contested by European nations. It took place in Italy from 19 to 27 July 1979. Denmark women's national football team won the tournament, beating hosts Italy 2–0 in the final at Stadio San Paolo. The European Confederation, Union of European Football Associations (UEFA), displayed little enthusiasm for women's association football and were particularly hostile to Italy's independent women's association football federation." .

ns1:53349 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Miss World 2001, the 51st edition of the Miss World pageant, was held on 16 November 2001 at the Super Bowl of Sun City, North West Entertainment Centre in Sun City, North West, South Africa. The Miss World 2001 contest was originally set to be held in Durban, but later changed the venue to Sun City, North West." .

ns1:53357 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Trois-Rivières was fought on 08 June 1776, during the American Revolutionary War. A site near the Le Jeune bridge was designated a National Historic Site of Canada in 1920 in order to commemorate the battle. There are three plaques in the city of Trois-Rivières commemorating aspects of the battle. During the American retreat from Quebec, and in this battle, wounded soldiers were treated at the Ursuline convent in Trois-Rivières." .

ns1:53361 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "luge at the 1992 Winter Olympics consisted of three events at La Plagne bobsleigh, luge, and skeleton track. Twenty-three nations participated in luge at the Albertville Games." .

ns1:53365 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1935–36 Palestine League was the fourth season of league football in the Mandatory Palestine. It began on 07 December 1935 and ended on 18 19 December 1936." .

ns1:53367 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "United Nations Security Council Resolution 683, adopted on 22 December 1990, after recalling Resolution 21 (1947) which approved the Trusteeship Territory of the Japanese Mandated Islands (since known as the Trust Territory of the Pacific Islands) as well as Chapter XII of the United Nations Charter which established the United Nations Trusteeship system, the Council determined that, in the light of entry into force of new status agreements for Micronesia, the Marshall Islands and the Northern Mariana Islands, the objectives of the Trusteeship Agreement had been completed and therefore ended the Trusteeship Agreement with those entities." .

ns1:53377 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2003 Winter Universiade, the XXI Winter Universiade, took place in Tarvisio, Italy." .

ns1:53378 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1941 Iraqi coup d'état (Arabic: ثورة رشيد عالي الكيلاني, Thawrah Rašīd ʿAlī al-Kaylānī), also called the Rashid Ali Al-Gaylani coup or the Golden Square coup, was a nationalist and pro-Nazi coup d'état in Iraq on 01 April 1941 that overthrew the pro-British regime of Regent 'Abd al-Ilah and his Prime Minister Nuri al-Said and installed Rashid Ali al-Gaylani as Prime Minister." .

ns1:53380 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1980 Svenska Cupen Final took place on 20 June 1984 at Råsunda Stadium in Solna. The match was contested by Allsvenskan sides Malmö FF and IK Brage. Brage played their first cup final ever, Malmö FF played their first final since 1978 and their 13th final in total." .

ns1:53387 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1926 Horta earthquake (Portugal: Sismo da Horta de 1926/Terramoto da Horta), occurred at 8:42 a.m. (local time) on 31 August 1926. It caused the destruction of many of the buildings located in the city of Horta, the central group of the Portugal autonomous region of the Azores, resulting in the death of nine and the partial or complete destruction of 4,138 buildings. On 31 August 1926, at about 8:40 in the morning, the islands were raised by a violent earthquake with its epicentre centralized in the Faial-Pico Channel, at a depth of 1.6–4 kilometres (0.99–2.49 mi) and with a body wave magnitude of 5.3–5.9. The earthquake progressed to a Mercalli Intensity of X (Extreme) in the northern part of Horta (around Conceição)." .

ns1:53391 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Ovid's exile is related by the poet himself, and also in brief references to the event by Pliny the Elder and Statius. Ovid wrote that the cause of his exile was carmen et error: \"a poem and an error\", probably the Ars Amatoria and a personal indiscretion or mistake. Ovid's poems in exile have been seen as of fundamental importance for the study of Roman aristocracy under Augustus and Tiberius, furnishing \"precious pieces of information about events and persons\". In modern times, classicists have questioned whether the exile was merely a farce, a misrepresentation by Ovid, or a rhetorical and literary device. Ovid wrote later that the reason for his exile was carmen et error – \"a poem and a mistake\", claiming that what he did was nothing illegal, but nevertheless worse than murder, more harmful than poetry. The carmen to which Ovid referred has been identified as Ars Amatoria (The Art of Love), written some seven years before his exile, although Ovid expresses surprise that only he has been exiled for such a reason, though many others also wrote obscene verse, seemingly with the emperor's approval. A more recent thesis has been that Ovid's exile was not real. Early in the 20th century, J. J. Hartman argued that Ovid never left Rome for exile, and that all of his works referring to it are an imaginative and humorous fiction. In a 1985 article, A.D. Fitton Brown also argued that Ovid's exile was fictional. According to Pliny the Elder, Ovid wrote another poem about fishing while in exile. In his works from exile, principally the Tristia and the Epistulae, Ovid tried to do three things that he hoped would help convince the emperor to end his exile: Much has been written suggesting that what Ovid wrote during this period is entirely different from his earlier works. According to Ovid himself, his exile ruined his former poetic genius." .

ns1:53404 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2004 Copa Sudamericana Finals was a two-legged football match-up to determine the 2004 Copa Sudamericana champion. It was contested by Bolivian club Club Bolívar and Argentinian club Club Atlético Boca Juniors." .

ns1:53419 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 Honda Indy Toronto was the second running of the Honda Indy Toronto (it had been conducted for 22 previous years under different sponsorship) and the tenth round of the 2010 IndyCar Series season." .

ns1:53451 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Tian Shan (simplified Chinese: 天山之战; traditional Chinese: 天山之戰; pinyin: Tiānshān zhī zhàn) was a battle fought in Tian Shan (in modern-day Xinjiang) in 99 BC during the Han–Xiongnu War." .

ns1:53468 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1984 Virginia Slims of Los Angeles was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts at the Manhattan Country Club in Manhattan Beach, California in the United States that was part of the 1984 Virginia Slims World Championship Series. The tournament was held from 01 October 1984 through 07 October 1984." .

ns1:53471 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 California lieutenant gubernatorial election was held on 02 November 2010 to choose the Lieutenant Governor of California. Incumbent Republican Lieutenant Governor of California Abel Maldonado, who was appointed to the office, ran for election to a full term and was defeated by Democratic Mayor Gavin Newsom of San Francisco." .

ns1:53474 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 English Badminton Championships was held in University of Derby Sports Centre, from 05 February 2016 to 07 February 2016." .

ns1:53500 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 WGC-Dell Match Play Championship was the 18th WGC Match Play, played March 23–27 at Austin Country Club in Austin." .

ns1:53512 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015–16 Austrian Cup (German: ÖFB-Samsung-Cup) was the 82nd season of Austria's nationwide football cup competition. It began with the matches of the First Round on 17 July 2015 and ended on 20 19 May 2016 with the final at Wörthersee Stadion in Klagenfurt." .

ns1:53513 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1962 Tour de Suisse was the 26th edition of the Tour de Suisse cycle race and was held from 14 June 1962 to 20 June 1962." .

ns1:53529 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The women's curling tournament of the curling at the 2018 Winter Olympics – women's tournament was held between 2018 and 2018 at the Gangneung Indoor Ice Rink." .

ns1:53534 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Orange Open Guadeloupe was a professional tennis tournament played on hard courts. It took place in Le Gosier, Guadeloupe between 2012 – 01 April 2012." .

ns1:53536 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Hillary Clinton presidential campaign, 2008, then junior United States Senator from New York, was announced on her website on 20 January 2007. On the final day of primaries on 2008, Obama had gained enough pledged- and super-delegates to become the presumptive nominee; she then suspended her campaign on 07 June 2008, and endorsed Barack Obama. On 2008, Solis Doyle ceased duties as campaign manager, and become a senior adviser, traveling with Clinton. Maggie Williams was appointed campaign manager; she had been Clinton's chief of staff at the White House. A 2008, NPR article stated (with regard to mailing lists) that \"Last year, New York Sen. Hillary Clinton took the unusual step of renting out some of her lists.\" Later in 2008, Clinton backed out of a cover photo shoot with Vogue over concerns by the Clinton camp that she would appear \"too feminine,\" which prompted the magazine's editor-in-chief, Anna Wintour, to write, \"Imagine my amazement, then, when I learned that Hillary Clinton, our only female presidential hopeful, had decided to steer clear of our pages at this point in her campaign for fear of looking too feminine. In mid 2008, a flurry of articles circulated announcing that \"Hillary Clinton and Sarah Palin plan to appear next week at the same rally in New York City – perhaps the closest the two history-making women will be to each other before Election Day.\"" .

ns1:53546 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Stirling Castle took place from 8 January to 01 February 1746, during the Jacobite rising of 1745, when a Jacobite force besieged Stirling Castle, held by a government garrison under William Blakeney. Despite defeating a relief force under Henry Hawley at Falkirk Muir on 17 January, the siege made little progress; when Cumberland's army began advancing north from Edinburgh, it was abandoned and on 01 February 1746 the Jacobites withdrew to Inverness. One of the strongest fortifications in Scotland, Stirling Castle controlled access between the Highlands and the Lowlands. De Gordon arrived at Stirling on 6 January to supervise siege operations, but his artillery did not arrive until 14 January and in the end never saw action. Charles reluctantly complied, but this destroyed the last remnants of trust between the two parties; on 01 February 1746, the siege was abandoned, and the Jacobite army withdrew." .

ns1:53551 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The two-man women's bobsleigh competition at the bobsleigh at the 2018 Winter Olympics – two-woman was held on 20 February 2018 and 21 February 2018 at the Alpensia Sliding Centre near Pyeongchang, South Korea. The first two runs were held on 20 February 2018 and the last runs on 21 February 2018." .

ns1:53560 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The FIS Freestyle World Ski Championships 2003 were held between January 31 and February 2 at the Deer Valley ski resort in northern Utah near Park City, United States of America." .

ns1:53585 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Wisniowiec (also known as the Battle of Lopuszno) took place on 28 April 1512. The battle was fought in Lopushne near Vyshnivets." .

ns1:53587 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1990 LPGA Championship was the 36th Women's PGA Championship, played July 26–29 at Bethesda Country Club in Bethesda, a suburb northwest of Washington, D.C. Five strokes back, Beth Daniel shot a final round 66 (−5) for 280 (−4) to win her sole major title, a stroke ahead of runner-up Rosie Jones, the third round leader." .

ns1:53595 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Action of 15 July 1805 was a small naval action that took place off Chausey, on the coast of Normandy, involving two British gun-brigs on one side, and vessels on the other. In 1805 Plumper, together with her sister-ship Teazer, were part of the Royal Navy force blockading the coat of northern France." .

ns1:53596 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1981 Chengdu-Kunming Railway Train Crash occurred on 09 July 1981, when train number 442 passed the failed Liziyida bridge near Ganluo County, Sichuan province." .

ns1:53597 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The naval Battle of Matapan took place on 19 July 1717 off the Cape Matapan, on the coast of the Mani Peninsula in southern Greece, between the Armada Grossa of the Republic of Venice, supported by a mixed squadron of allied ships from Portugal, the Papal States and Malta, and the Ottoman fleet, under Kapudan Pasha Eğribozlu İbrahim Pasha. The 24 Republic of Venice sailing ships under Marcantonio Diedo, commander of the Republic of Venice fleet, met up with another Republic of Venice squadron of 24 galleys under the Capitano generale da Mar Andrea Pisani and a small squadron of 9 mixed Portugal-Malta ships under the Malta knight Bellefontaine near Cape Matapan on 2 July. As a result of the battle, the Republic of Venice attempt to recapture the Morea was foiled and the Ottoman reconquest of the peninsula was confirmed. 13 Republic of Venice 5 Malta 4 Papal 2 Tuscan." .

ns1:53606 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 6 Hours of Donington was an auto racing held at the Donington Park, Leicestershire, United Kingdom on 13–2012. It was the second round of the 2012 European Le Mans Series season, and the first time that the European Le Mans Series held the 6 Hours of Donington since 2006." .

ns1:53614 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1990 WTA Tour Championships was the season-ending women's tennis held at the Madison Square Garden in New York City, United States between 12 November 1990 and 18 November 1990." .

ns1:53620 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1988 Taiwan Open was a women's tennis tournament played on indoor carpet courts in Taipei, Taiwan and was part of the Category 1 tier of the 1988 Virginia Slims World Championship Series. It was the third edition of the tournament and was held from 18 April 1988 through 24 April 1988." .

ns1:53622 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Carbisdale (also known as Invercarron) took place close to the village of Culrain, Sutherland, Scotland on 27 April 1650 and was part of the Wars of the Three Kingdoms. Although Carbisdale is the name of the nearest farm to the site of the battle, Culrain is the nearest village." .

ns1:53623 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Blockade of Veracruz was a conflict during the Mexican-American War. The blockade of Veracruz was extremely important in the Mexican-American War in stopping the trade of contraband. On 1846, Conner proclaimed Veracruz, Alvarado, Tampico and Matamoros under blockade Conner sent the sloop-of-war St. Mary's to Tampico, the paddle frigate Mississippi to Veracruz, and the sloop-of-war Falmouth to Alvarado." .

ns1:53627 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Martinair Flight 138 was a chartered flight from Surabaya, Indonesia, to Colombo, Sri Lanka. On 04 December 1974, the aircraft, a McDonnell Douglas DC-8-55CF, crashed into a mountain shortly before landing, killing all 191 people aboard – 182 Indonesian hajj pilgrims bound for Mecca, and 9 crew members. Sri Lanka aviation authorities later took the blame for their poor communication with the tech crew of the flight \" The aircraft took off from Juanda International Airport Indonesia at approximately 1200 hours on 04 December 1974 to proceed to Jeddah, Saudi-Arabia with a programmed stop at Bandaranaike Airport, near Colombo." .

ns1:53644 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "wrestling at the 2015 European Games – men's Greco-Roman 75 kg competition at the wrestling at the 2015 European Games in Baku, Azerbaijan, took place on 14 June at the Heydar Aliyev Sports and Exhibition Complex." .

ns1:53645 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1973-74 Cypriot Cup was the 32nd edition of the Cypriot Cup. It began on 06 March 1974 with the preliminary round and concluded on 23 June 1974 with the final which was held at GSP Stadium. AC Omonia won their 3rd Cypriot Cup trophy after beating Enosis Neon Paralimni FC 2–0 in the final." .

ns1:53658 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Erzurum Offensive (Russian: Эрзурумское сражение Erzurumskoe srazhenie, Turkish: Erzurum Taarruzu) or Battle of Erzurum (Turkish: Erzurum Muharebesi) was a major winter offensive by the Imperial Russian Army on the Caucasus Campaign, during the World War I that led to the capture of the strategic city of Erzurum. As of 10 January 1916, Ottoman Empire forces were 126,000 men, only 50,539 of whom were combat soldiers. On January 17, at Battle of Koprukoy The forces at Köprüköy, the main town on the road to Erzurum, were forced to leave. On the same day, Russian forces captured Muş after the Battle of Mush, 70 mi from Erzurum. Erzurum was considered as the second best-defended town in the Ottoman Empire. The Ottoman Empire units began to retreat from the fortified zones at the front and to evacuate the town of Erzurum. The Ottoman Empire did not have a chance to enjoy its victory at the Battle of Gallipoli since its loss of Erzurum changed the atmosphere in an instant. The Battle of Erzurum forms the climax of John Buchan's novel Greenmantle." .

ns1:53668 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Wielkopolska Chronicle (or Chronicle of Greater Kingdom of Poland, Polish: Kronika wielkopolska) is an anonymous medieval chronicle describing supposed history of Kingdom of Poland from legendary times up to the year 1273. The chronicle was written from the point of view of the region of Wielkopolska (Greater Kingdom of Poland)." .

ns1:53670 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 FIBA Intercontinental Cup was the 23rd edition of the FIBA Intercontinental Cup for men's professional basketball clubs and the 22nd edition of the tournament being in the form of a true intercontinental tournament for clubs. The 2 game aggregate score tournament took place at the Ginásio José Corrêa arena in Barueri, São Paulo, Brazil, on October 4 and 2013, in order to determine the world club champion. The tournament was contested between the 2012–13 season EuroLeague champions, Olympiacos B.C., and the 2013 FIBA Americas League champions, Pinheiros Sky. Olympiacos B.C. won the series 2-0." .

ns1:53707 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "During the 2002–03 Leicester City F.C. season English football season, Leicester City F.C. competed in the Football League First Division after being relegated from the FA Premier League 2001–02 Leicester City F.C. season." .

ns1:53735 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Ottoman conquest of Adrianople, a major Byzantine Empire city in Thrace, was conquered by the Ottoman Empire sometime in the 1360s, and eventually became the Ottoman capital, until the Fall of Constantinople in 1453. The main target of the advance was Ottoman conquest of Adrianople, which was the third most important Byzantine Empire city (after Constantinople and Thessalonica). The date of Ottoman conquest of Adrianople's fall to the Turks has been disputed among scholars due to the differing accounts in the source material, with the years 1361 to 1362, 1367 and 1371 variously proposed. Following sources dating from long after the events, earlier scholarship generally placed the conquest between 1361 and 1363, in accordance with the report in Ottoman sources that a solar eclipse occurred in the year of Ottoman conquest of Adrianople's fall. Thus later Turkish sources report that Lala Shahin Pasha defeated the Byzantine Empire ruler (tekfur) of the city at a battle in Sazlıdere southeast of the city, forcing him to flee secretly by boat. Based on Elisabeth Zachariadou's examination of previously unregarded Byzantine Empire sources, most modern scholars have moved to the view that the city was captured in 1369. Thus a poem from the city's metropolitan bishop to Emperor John V Palaiologos shows Ottoman conquest of Adrianople to have still been in Byzantine Empire hands in Christmas 1366, while a series of Byzantine Empire short chronicles place the date of its capture in 1369. Edirne did not immediately become the Ottoman Empire' capital; Murad's court continued to reside in Bursa and in nearby Demotika, as well as Edirne. The conquest of Ottoman conquest of Adrianople was a turning point in the history of the Ottoman Empire in Europe: prior to this the Balkan peoples had regarded them as transient raiders, like so many that came before them in centuries prior. Instead, the transformation of Ottoman conquest of Adrianople into the new Ottoman capital of Edirne signalled to the local populace that the Ottoman Empire intended to settle permanently in Europe." .

ns1:53737 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006 Maryland gubernatorial election was held on 07 November 2006. Elected to his first term in 2002 Maryland gubernatorial election, incumbent Republican Governor Bob Ehrlich ran for a second term as governor, opposed by the Democratic nominee, Martin O'Malley, the Mayor of Baltimore." .

ns1:53740 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "UFC 32 was a mixed martial arts event held by the Ultimate Fighting Championship at the Meadowlands Arena in East Rutherford on 29 June 2001." .

ns1:53755 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 Türk Telecom İzmir Cup was a professional tennis tournament played on hard courts. It took place in İzmir, Turkey between 19 September 2011 and 25 September 2011." .

ns1:53756 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1991 Indian general election were held in India in 1991 to elect the members of the 10th Lok Sabha. The remaining election days were postponed until mid-June and voting finally took place on 12 June 1991 and 15 June 1991." .

ns1:53759 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Kentucky Cup Turf Stakes is a Grade III American Thoroughbred horse race for three years olds and older, over a distance of 1 1⁄2 miles on the turf held annually in September at Kentucky Downs racetrack in Franklin, Kentucky during their short turf meeting." .

ns1:53767 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Michelob Championship at Kingsmill was a golf tournament on the PGA Tour from 1968 to 2002. It was played in Virginia at the River Course of Kingsmill Golf Club outside of Williamsburg, from 1981 to 2002. The event was founded in 1968 as the Kaiser International Open Invitational, which was played in northern California at Silverado Country Club in Napa through 1980." .

ns1:53774 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Nutuk (Modern Turkish: Söylev; The Speech) was a speech delivered by Mustafa Kemal Atatürk from 15 to 20 01 October 1927, at the second congress of Cumhuriyet Halk Partisi." .

ns1:53807 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1921 Copa del Rey was the 21st staging of the Copa del Rey, the Spanish association football cup competition. The competition started on 09 April 1921, and concluded on 08 May 1921, with the Final, held at the San Mamés in Bilbao, in which Athletic Club lifted the trophy for the 8th time ever with a 4–1 victory over Athletic Madrid." .

ns1:53812 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1987 UK Championship (also known as the 1987 Tennent's UK Championship for sponsorship reasons) was a professional ranking snooker tournament that took place at the Preston Guild Hall in Preston, England. The event started on 13 13 November 1987 and the televised stages were shown on BBC between 21 1987 and 29 13 November 1987." .

ns1:53818 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1996 United States presidential election in Kansas took place on 05 November 1996, as part of the 1996 United States presidential election. Kansas was won by former home state Senator Bob Dole (R-Kansas) over President of the United States Bill Clinton (D), with Dole winning 54.29% to 36.08% by a margin of 18.21%. Billionaire businessman Ross Perot (Reform Party of the United States of America-TX) finished in third, with 8.62% of the popular vote, a sharp decline from 1992 United States presidential election in Kansas, when Perot captured 27% of the state’s votes and held George H.W." .

ns1:53819 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Badminton at the All-Africa Games at the badminton at the 2011 All-Africa Games in Maputo was held between 06 September 2011–12, 2011." .

ns1:53821 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1999 Japan Open Tennis Championships – Women's Doubles was the women's doubles event of the twenty-fifth edition of the Japan Open; a WTA Tier III tournament held in Tokyo, Japan. Corina Morariu and Kimberly Po won in the final 6–3, 6–2 against Catherine Barclay and Kerry-Anne Guse." .

ns1:53823 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Dolinskoye (Dolinskoe, Dolinsky), which took place 25 kilometers northwest of the Chechen Republic capital of Grozny, was the first major ground engagement of the First Chechen War. The battle began on 12 01 December 1994, when six officers (including two colonels) and 13 enlisted men of the Russian Airborne Troops died in a surprise 9K51 Grad multiple rocket launcher attack on an advancing column of armored vehicles of the 106th Airborne Division and 56th Airborne Brigade. The Russians lost 21 soldiers killed in the initial attack and admitted losing up to 200 men in the overall battle., According to the Chechen Republic commander Hussein Ikshanov." .

ns1:53825 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1925 Australian federal election was held in Australia on 1925. The incumbent Nationalist–Country coalition, led by Prime Minister of Australia Stanley Bruce, defeated the opposition Labor Party led by Matthew Charlton in a landslide. Prime Minister of Australia Stanley Bruce was a supporter of the White Australia Policy, and made it an issue in his campaign for the 1925 Australian Federal election." .

ns1:53831 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1991 DFB-Pokal Final decided the winner of the 1990–91 DFB-Pokal, the 48th season of Germany's premier knockout association football cup competition. It was played on 22 June 1991 at the Olympiastadion Berlin in Berlin. SV Werder Bremen won the match 4–3 on penalties against 1. FC Köln, following a 1–1 draw after extra time, to claim their second cup title." .

ns1:53856 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2001 Indonesia Open Badminton Championships in badminton was held in Jakarta, from 25 July 2001 to 29 July 2001." .

ns1:53875 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1943 Paris–Roubaix was the 41st edition of the Paris–Roubaix, a classic one-day cycle race in France. The single day event was held on 25 April 1943 and stretched 250 km (155 mi) from Paris to the finish at Roubaix Velodrome." .

ns1:53890 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Monte Carlo Rally (formally known as the 81ème Rallye Automobile Monte-Carlo) was a motor racing event for rally cars that was held over four days between 16 January and 19 January, and marked the eighty-first running of the Monte Carlo Rally. Thirteen World Rally Car crews were entered in the event, including the defending World Drivers' Champion Sébastien Loeb, prior to the start a six-time winner of the Monte Carlo Rally. The rally was won by Sébastien Loeb, the seventy-seventh of his career and his seventh victory on the Monte Carlo Rally. The itinerary for the 2013 rally remained relatively unchanged from 2012 Monte Carlo Rally. With the cars running in the order that they finished the 2012 Monte Carlo Rally season, Sébastien Loeb was the first driver through the stages, and on his arrival at the end of Le Moulinon—Antraigues it was observed that the dry conditions had torn the studs free of his front tyres." .

ns1:53904 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 CAF Confederation Cup Final was the final of the 2014 CAF Confederation Cup, the 11th edition of the CAF Confederation Cup, Africa's secondary club association football competition organized by the Confederation of African Football (CAF). The final was contested in two-legged home-and-away format between Pedro Sangome of Côte d'Ivoire and Al Ahly SC of Egypt. The first leg was hosted by Pedro Sangome at the Stade Robert Champroux in Abidjan on 2014, while the second leg was hosted by Al Ahly SC at the Cairo International Stadium in Cairo on 2014. This was the first African final for Pedro Sangome. While Al Ahly SC had reached the finals of the African Champions Cup/CAF Champions League ten times and the African Cup Winners' Cup four times respectively, this was their first final in the CAF Confederation Cup. In their head-to-head meetings, the home match of Pedro Sangome finished in a 1–1 draw, while Al Ahly SC won their home match 1–0. Both teams finished with nine points, and Al Ahly SC won the group over Pedro Sangome on head-to-head record." .

ns1:53922 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Deir Yassin massacre took place on 09 April 1948, when around 130 fighters from the Far-right wing Zionist paramilitary groups Irgun and Lehi killed at least 107 Palestinian Arabs, including women and children, in Deir Yassin, a village of roughly 600 people near Jerusalem. Israeli military historian Uri Milstein alleged in 1998 that Pa'il was not in Deir Yassin on 09 April 1948. Pa'il, who died in 2015, defended himself in an interview in 2007: \"What the Lehi and Etzel people did in Deir Yassin in 09 April 1948 was a despicable act." .

ns1:53935 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Quart Festival was an annual popular music festival that took place in Kristiansand, Norway in the beginning of July. It was the most visited music festival in Norway. It was first named Qvadradurmusivalen in 1991, but changed into the more ear-catching Quart Festival the following year. For several years Quart was the largest music festival in Norway, but went bankrupt in 2008 in part due to tough competition from the Hovefestivalen in Arendal and some Oslo-based festivals. The Quart Festival was revived by one of the original organisers and was held on 30 June to 2009." .

ns1:53937 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Miss America 1939, the 13th Miss America pageant, was the last pageant to be held at the famed Steel Pier in Atlantic City, New Jersey. The finals were held on Saturday, 1939, and Miss Michigan, Patricia Donnelly, was crowned Miss America 1939." .

ns1:53940 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 Europe Cup in Badminton was the 33rd edition of the Europe Cup in badminton. It was held between June 23–27, 2010, in the ZBC-Hal, in Zwolle, Netherlands. The draw was held on 2010 at Zwolle, Netherlands." .

ns1:53962 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 Singapore Grand Prix, formally known as the 2008 Formula 1 SingTel Singapore Grand Prix, was a Formula One race held on 28 September 2008 at 20:00 SST at the newly built Marina Bay Street Circuit in Marina Bay, Singapore. This was also the first time Singapore hosted a Formula One race, as the last Singapore Grand Prix was a Formula Libre event in 1973. The 61-lap race was won by Fernando Alonso for the Renault team from 15th on the grid, after his teammate deliberately crashed on lap 14 to bring out the safety car after his first pit stop." .

ns1:53976 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The battle of Kolding (Slaget ved Kolding) on 09 January 1644 was the opening engagement of the Torstenson War between the Swedish Empire and Denmark-Norway. The battle was primarily a cavalry engagement between Swedish and Danish cavalry near the Danish seaport of Kolding." .

ns1:53987 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Battle of Schwyz occurred on 14 August 1799–15 August 1799 between French forces commanded by General of Division André Masséna and Major General von Franz Jellachich's brigade." .

ns1:53989 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Una (Croatian: Operacija Una) was a military offensive conducted by the Croatian Army (Hrvatska vojska – HV) against the Army of Republika Srpska (Vojska Republike Srpske – VRS) in western Bosnia and Herzegovina on 18 September 1995–19 September 1995, during the Bosnian War. The ARBiH drive towards Sanski Most and Novi Grad, where one of the bridgeheads had been established by the HV, came to a halt on 18 September 1995–19 September 1995, as the ARBiH encountered 14,000 previously undetected VRS troops in the area, supported by 2,000 troops that had arrived from Serbia. On 20 19 September 1995, Červenko submitted his report on Operation Una to Tuđman, divorcing responsibility from himself, and blaming Vrbanac and Defence Minister Gojko Šušak." .

ns1:54000 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Tripoli airport clashes occurred on 04 June 2012, after members of the Al-Awfea militia stormed the Tripoli International Airport as a measure to prompt the government's release of its jailed leader, Abu Oegeila al-Hebshi." .

ns1:54005 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1983 WTA German Open, also known by its sponsored name Fila German Open, was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor clay courts at the Rot-Weiss Tennis Club Berlin in West Berlin in West Germany that was part of the 1983 Virginia Slims World Championship Series. The tournament was held from 16 May 1983 through 22 May 1983." .

ns1:54045 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1975 Virginia Slims of Houston was a women's tennis tournament played on indoor carpet court at the Sam Houston Coliseum and Music Hall in Houston, Texas in the United States that was part of the 1975 WTA Tour." .

ns1:54051 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1976 U.S. Open was the 76th U.S. Open, held June 17–20 at the Highlands Course of the Atlanta Athletic Club in Duluth, Georgia, a suburb northeast of Atlanta." .

ns1:54086 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle on Vrtijeljka (Serbian: бој на Вртијељци, romanized: boj na Vrtijeljci) was fought on the hill of Vrtijeljka near Cetinje between a Venetian irregular force and an advancing Ottoman force on 07 May 1685 at the start of the Morean War. The two met at the hill of Vrtijeljka on 07 May 1685." .

ns1:54090 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 Indy Japan: The Final was the ninth and final running of the Indy Japan 300 and the sixteenth round of the 2011 IndyCar Series season. The race was contested over 63 laps at the 2.983-mile (4.801 km) Twin Ring Motegi road course in Motegi, Tochigi, Japan." .

ns1:54097 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Istria Cup was the fourth edition of the Istria Cup, an invitational women's association football tournament held annually in Istria, Croatia. It took place from 02 March 2016–07 March 2016; at the same time as the 2016 Algarve Cup, 2016 SheBelieves Cup and 2016 Cyprus Cup." .

ns1:54107 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Commission of Inquiry into the Crimes and Misappropriations Committed by Ex-President Habré, His Accomplices and/or Accessories (French: Commission d'Enquête du Ministère Chadien de la Justice sur les Crimes du Régime de Hissène Habré) was established on 29 December 1990 by the President of Chad, Idriss Déby. Habré began a programme of widespread repression including torture and killings, until he was himself overthrown by one of his former generals, the Libyan-backed Idriss Déby, in 29 December 1990." .

ns1:54113 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Miss World 1981, the 31st edition of the Miss World pageant, was held on 1981 at the Royal Albert Hall in London, UK. Another Venezuelan, Irene Sáez, had been crowned Miss Universe 1981 in July, making Venezuela one of the few countries to hold both major international titles (Miss Universe and Miss World) in the same year." .

ns1:54126 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1996 Ukrainian Cup Final is a association football match that took place at the Olimpiyskiy National Sports Complex on 26 May 1996. The match was the 5th Ukraine Cup Final and it was contested by FC Dynamo Kyiv and PFC Nyva Vinnytsia. The 1996 Ukrainian Cup Final was the fifth to be held in the Ukraine capital Kiev." .

ns1:54138 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Bandy World Championship 2009 was held between 18 January 2009 and 25 January 2009 in Västerås, Sweden. Men's teams from 13 countries participated in the 2009 competition: Belarus, Finland, Kazakhstan, Norway, Russia, Sweden (group A) and Canada, Estonia, Hungary, Latvia, Mongolia, the Netherlands and the United States (group B). The main venues were ABB Arena Syd in Västerås, Sweden's largest indoor arena for bandy, and Hakonplan, an outdoor stadium. Statistics for goalscorers during Bandy World Championships 2009." .

ns1:54142 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "sailing at the 2020 Summer Olympics competitions at the 2020 Summer Olympics in Tokyo are scheduled to take place from 25 July to 2021 at the Enoshima Yacht Harbor in Enoshima." .

ns1:54153 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2005 Tour Down Under was the seventh edition of the Tour Down Under stage race. It took place from 18 January 2005 to 23 January 2005 in and around Adelaide, South Australia. 18 January 2005 – Adelaide - Adelaide, 50 km. 23 January 2005 – Adelaide to Adelaide, 81 km." .

ns1:54158 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Government Pension Fund Global comprises two entirely separate sovereign wealth fund owned by the government of Norway. Its name changed in 2006 from the Petroleum Fund of Norway. Norway's sovereign wealth fund is taking steps to become more active in company governance. Norway's Government Pension Fund Global (GPFG) has the potential to influence the corporate governance market in Europe, and possibly China as well, greatly. As a result of the large size of the fund relative to the low number of people living in Norway (5.2 million people in 2017), the Oil Fund has become a hot political issue, dominated by three main issues: There are diverse concerns and predicted effects of sovereign wealth fund on international financial markets and the global economy as a whole, with experts expressing strong fears regarding destabilization and protectionism stemming from sovereign wealth fund. The Ministry of Finance in Norway stated that it would not withdraw investing in these companies or discuss an eventual exclusion of surveillance industry companies from its investments." .

ns1:54161 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Zacatecas was a military action of the Mexican War of Independence fought on 15 April 1811 at Zacatecas, Zacatecas. The victory was not decisive and the Royalist army continued to harass the insurgents until they were defeated again at Zacatecas on 15 April 1811." .

ns1:54177 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Miss Paraguay 2007 pageant was won by María José Maldonado. In took place on 2007 at the Municipal Theatre Ignacio A. Pane in Asunción, Paraguay." .

ns1:54189 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "World Festival of Animated Film Zagreb (Croatian: Svjetski festival animiranog filma), also known as Animafest Zagreb, is a film festival entirely dedicated to animated film held annually in Zagreb, Croatia. Zagreb's candidacy for holding a permanent animated film festival was accepted at the 1969 ASIFA meeting in London." .

ns1:54195 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Cotagaita was fought on 27 October 1810 during the Campaign of Upper Peru in the Argentine War of Independence when the troops of the Army of the North were sent by the Primera Junta government in Buenos Aires to the Alto Perú to fight the Royalists. The battle was fought near the town of Santiago de Cotagaita, nowadays in Potosí Department in Bolivia." .

ns1:54203 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Noahidism (/ˈnoʊə.haɪd.ɪsm/) or Noachidism (/ˈnoʊə.xaɪd.ɪsm/) is a monotheistic Jewish religious movement based upon the Noahide laws and their traditional interpretations within Orthodox Judaism." .

ns1:54215 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Rakshasbhuvan was fought on 10 August 1763. However, the Marathas were being led by Peshwa Madhavrao (until the skirmish of Chambhar Gonda, Senasahebsubha Janoji Bhonsle and Raghunathrao, uncle of Peshwa were also in the camp), who was assisted by Visaji Tryambak, Sardar Naro Shankar Dani, Babuji Naik (A Chief of Senasahebsubha's Nagpur Army who, prior to battle a few months before, had made a friendship treaty with Nizam),Sardar Yashwantrao Wable (Kopergaon) and Sardar Malojiraje III Ghorpade of Mudhol trounced the Nizam's forces at Rakshasbhuvan after the battle was fought for five to six months." .

ns1:54220 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1969 Georgia 500 was a NASCAR Grand National Series event that was held on November 17, 1968, at Middle Georgia Raceway in Byron, Georgia. It is not to be confused with another running of the 1969 Georgia 500 which actually took place in 1969." .

ns1:54236 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Bosnia and Herzegovina Football Cup Final will be the 17th final of the Bosnia and Herzegovina Football Cup, the highest association football cup competition in Bosnia and Herzegovina. The date of both 2012 Final matches, as in all the recent years, is set so that it avoids clashes with Premier League of Bosnia and Herzegovina fixtures. That goal, as proved later, will be the only one F.K. Željezničar Sarajevo concede on his route to final. F.K. Željezničar Sarajevo met Borac, a team they faced also in the last two season of this competition (once in final). Krajišnik got in lead in 38-minute over Šaban Pehilj, Željezničars footballer on loan, but Hrvoje Mišić scored the equalizer for NK Široki Brijeg in 86-minute of the game for the final 1–1. In the last hurdle before the final, NK Široki Brijeg faced Velež. Mario Kvesić scored for 0–1 in the 69-minute, sealing the fate of Velež and securing his team the final of the Bosnia and Herzegovina Football Cup for the fourth time. This final marked the eight appearance in final of Bosnia and Herzegovina Football Cup for F.K. Željezničar Sarajevo and ninth final in domestic cups, since they were once in final of Yugoslav Cup. On the other side, this will be the fourth final for NK Široki Brijeg and they won it once (in 2007), with two finals lost (in 2005 and 2006). On 2012, a draw to decide who will be the first host was held with F.K. Željezničar Sarajevo hosting the first-leg on 25 April and NK Široki Brijeg the rematch three weeks later on 17 May, with both matches aired on BHT1. Before the match, F.K. Željezničar Sarajevo was in a more than a 3-year unbeaten streak in national cup, with the last lost match dating to 24 September 2008 against Zvijezda in the first round of 2008–09 Bosnia and Herzegovina Football Cup. They won two of the last three national cups, since they lost the final in 2009–10 Bosnia and Herzegovina Football Cup to Borac on an aggregate score of 3:3 after two draw matches. Nevertheless, manager of NK Široki Brijeg, Marijan Bloudek, admitted that F.K. Željezničar Sarajevo is the better side at the moment, with the many records and the league position proving that, but with his team very close behind and with realistic chances to win. Many managers and sport workers came to the final, not only from Bosnia and Herzegovina but also from the region. While NK Široki Brijeg didn't have any problems with suspended or injured players, Patrick Nyema Gerhardt, F.K. Željezničar Sarajevo's standard player, would miss the game because of booking." .

ns1:54246 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Mitzvah Day International is an annual day of faith-based social action that takes place in November each year, primarily in the United Kingdom. Mitzvah Day International was founded by Laura Marks in 2005, who, upon returning to her native UK from a period spent in Los Angeles, decided to bring the 'Mitzvah Day' concept she became familiar with through the Temple Israel of Hollywood synagogue back to the UK. Mitzvah Day was originally associated with the Jewish Community Centre for London, and became registered as an independent charity in the United Kingdom in 2008. Mitzvah Day has been publicly endorsed by many public figures in the United Kingdom, including former UK Prime Minister David Cameron, Deputy Prime Minister and Leader of the Liberal Democrats Nick Clegg, Leader of the Labour Party Ed Miliband Former UK Prime Minister Tony Blair, UK Prime Minister Boris Johnson, Chief Rabbi of the United Kingdom Jonathan Sacks, and The Archbishop of Canterbury. In 2012, the name of the organization officially changed from 'Mitzvah Day' to 'Mitzvah Day International' to reflect the growing participation of community groups outside the UK." .

ns1:54247 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Tyrepower Tasmania SuperSprint was a motor racing event for V8 Supercars, held on the weekend of 1 to 2016. The event was held at Symmons Plains Raceway in Launceston, Tasmania, and consisted of one race of 120 kilometres and one race of 200 km in length." .

ns1:54260 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1901–02 FA Cup was the thirty-first staging of the world's oldest association football competition, the Football Association Challenge Cup (more usually known as the FA Cup). Sheffield United F.C. won the competition for the second time, beating Southampton 2–1 in the replay of the 1902 FA Cup Final at Crystal Palace. The format of the FA Cup for the season had a preliminary round, five qualifying rounds, an intermediate round, three proper rounds, and the semi finals and 1902 FA Cup Final. Sheffield United F.C. eventually won and went on to meet Southampton in the 1902 FA Cup Final." .

ns1:54262 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Hancock, also called the Romney Campaign, was a battle fought during the Romney Expedition, occurred January 5–6, 1862, in Washington County, and Morgan County, West Virginia, as part of Maj. Gen. Thomas J." .

ns1:54269 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Power Horse Cup was a men's tennis tournament played on clay courts. It was the first edition of the Power Horse Cup as an ATP World Tour 250 series event on the 2013 ATP World Tour and replaced the World Team Cup, a team event previously held at the same venue. It took place at the Rochusclub in Düsseldorf, Germany, from May 19 through 25 May 2013." .

ns1:54275 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Access All Areas Tour (advertised as the Access All Areas: North American Tour) was the second concert tour by supergroup, The AAA Girls." .

ns1:54276 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Budapest-Bamako or Great African Run is a charity car race in Africa, and the largest amateur rally in the world. It is a low-budget version of the Dakar Rally, and goes from Budapest, Hungary to Bamako, Mali through the Sahara. On 2005, forty-two teams lined up in Budapest's Hősök tere for the inaugural run of the Budapest-Bamako. The Budapest–Bamako is primarily a charity event, that brings direct donations to communities in Mali and Mauritania." .

ns1:54277 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "UFC 202 was a mixed martial arts event produced by the Ultimate Fighting Championship that was held on 20 August 2016, at T-Mobile Arena in Paradise, Nevada, part of the Las Vegas metropolitan area." .

ns1:54279 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Campeonato Ecuatoriano de Fútbol Serie A (officially known as the Copa Banco del Pacífico Ecuadorian Liga Pro for sponsorship reasons) is the 58th season of the Ecuadorian Liga Pro. Twelve teams are competing in the 2016 Ecuadorian Liga Pro season, ten of whom remain from the previous season. LDU Loja and Deportivo Quito were relegated from the Ecuadorian Liga Pro after accumulating the fewest points during the 2015 Campeonato Ecuatoriano de Fútbol Serie A." .

ns1:54288 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1994 Dutch Open was an ATP tennis tournament held in Hilversum, Netherlands and played on outdoor clay court. It was the 36th edition of the tournament and was held from 25 July 1994 to 01 August 1994." .

ns1:54309 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Netscape Public License (NPL) is a free software license, the license under which Netscape Communications Corporation originally released Mozilla." .

ns1:54326 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "A October 2004 lunar eclipse took place on 28 October 2004, the second of two total lunar eclipses in 2004, the first being on May 4, 2004. The last October 2004 lunar eclipse will be on July 7, 2419 and the last partial lunar eclipse on October 3, 2563. The final lunar eclipse of Lunar Saros 136 will be on June 1, 2960." .

ns1:54335 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Appleton Handicap is an American Thoroughbred horse race held annually near the end of March at Gulfstream Park in Hallandale Beach." .

ns1:54345 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The sailing at the 1912 Summer Olympics – 12 metre class was a sailing event on the sailing at the 1912 Summer Olympics program in Nynäshamn." .

ns1:54374 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1957 Ramnad riots or the Mudukulathur riots were a series of violent clashes that occurred between July and 01 September 1957 in the Ramanathapuram district and in southern Tamil Nadu, India." .

ns1:54377 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010–11 Toto Cup Al was the twenty-nine season of the third most important association football tournament in Israel since its introduction and seventh under the current format. On 19 January 2011, Hapoel Ironi Kiryat Shmona F.C. won the 2010–11 Toto Cup Al, a year after winning the Leumit version of the cup, it was their first Toto Cup Al title overall." .

ns1:54378 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1920–21 Slovenian Republic League was the second season of the Slovenian Republic Football League. The final was played between the 1920 Slovenian Republic League winners ND Ilirija Ljubljana and Athletik." .

ns1:54400 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1995 was not officially billed as 'Monsters of Rock Tour 1988' but as 'Escape from the Studio' due to Metallica's decision to headline while recording the Load album. USA: 23 May 1988 – 30 July 1988." .

ns1:54415 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Aanayoottu (feeding of elephants) is a festival held in the precincts of the Vadakkunnathan temple in City of Thrissur, in Kerala. Elephants are an integral part of Kerala culture, and elephants are integral to all festivals, including the Aanayoottu." .

ns1:54422 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1999 UNCAF Nations Cup was a Copa Centroamericana held in San Jose, Costa Rica in 17 March 1999." .

ns1:54423 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Cranston Fire was a wildfire that burned in southwest Riverside County, in the United States. The Cranston Fire allegedly was started on 25 July 2018, around noon, by Brandon M. McGlover, who allegedly started numerous fires in the region that day. On 25 July 2018, the same day the fire was reported, CAL FIRE arrested Brandon N. McGlover for setting multiple fires in Riverside County." .

ns1:54448 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Opening on 05 February 1989 at the National Art Museum of China (previously called the National Art Gallery), this exhibition included over 186 artists and approximately 300 artworks from all over China. China/Avant-Garde Exhibition — Exhibition Information, 1989." .

ns1:54449 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1968 Emperor's Cup Final was the 48th final of the Emperor's Cup competition. Cerezo Osaka won their 1st title, by defeating Urawa Red Diamonds 1–0." .

ns1:54454 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 Wirral Metropolitan Borough Council election took place on 2011 to elect members of Wirral Metropolitan Borough Council in England. Prior to the election the composition of the council was: After the election the composition of the council was: Results compared directly with the last local election in 2010 Wirral Metropolitan Borough Council election." .

ns1:54462 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1901–02 British Home Championship was an international football tournament between the British Home Nations which was meant to herald the arrival of the full professional game of association football as both England and Scotland national football team fielded fully professional teams for the first time." .

ns1:54477 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Air France Flight 152 (AF152) was a scheduled international passenger flight which made an emergency water landing in the Mediterranean Sea, off Fethiye, South-Western Turkey on 03 August 1953. The scheduled flight AF152 departed from Orly Airport in Paris, France at 18:38 hours on 2 03 August 1953, destination Tehran, with two planned stopovers in Rome, and Beirut. At 10:30 the stopover office of Air France in Athens asked Orly Airport for news about F-BAZS. At 17:30 hours, a telegram from the airliner's captain, sent from Fethiye, arrived at Air France stating that 38 people survived the accident." .

ns1:54496 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The wrestling at the 2004 Summer Olympics – women's freestyle 63 kg as part of the wrestling at the 2004 Summer Olympics were held at the Ano Liosia Olympic Hall, August 22 to August 23." .

ns1:54500 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2003 Women's European Volleyball Championship was the 23rd edition of the event, organised by Europe's governing volleyball body, the European Volleyball Confederation. It was hosted in Ankara, Turkey from 20 to 28 September 2003. The two finalists Poland and Turkey claimed a ticket for the 2003 FIVB Women's World Cup and the first Olympic Qualification Tournament for the 2004 Summer Olympics held in Japan in November. Semifinalists Turkey, Germany, Poland and the Netherlands also automatically qualified for the 2005 Women's European Volleyball Championship, which took place in Croatia." .

ns1:54504 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1999 Five Nations Championship (sponsored by Lloyds TSB) was the seventieth series of the rugby union Six Nations Championship. Ten matches were played over five weekends from 06 February 1999 to 11 April 1999. The tournament was won by Scotland national rugby union team, who beat England national rugby union team on points difference. Scotland national rugby union team scored sixteen tries in the tournament, to England national rugby union team's eight. Scotland national rugby union team had staged their own remarkable upset the previous day, scoring five first-half tries to beat France in Paris for only the second time in thirty years. The other men to achieve the feat were Carston Catcheside (England national rugby union team 1924), Patrick Estève (France 1983) and Phillipe Sella (France 1986)." .

ns1:54510 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1961 New York City mayoral election occurred on Tuesday, 07 November 1961, with incumbent Democratic Mayor Robert F. Wagner going on to win a decisive re-election victory for a third term in office." .

ns1:54514 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Major League Rugby (MLR or USMLR) is a professional rugby union competition and the top-level championship for clubs in North America. In the 2021 season it will be contested by thirteen teams: twelve from the United States of America and one from Canada. Officially sanctioned by USA Rugby, which is a member union of Rugby Americas North (RAN), and consequently part of World Rugby, the league was founded in 2017, and is headquartered in Dallas, Texas. In September 2016, with at least five amateur rugby union clubs across the United States of America discussing a possible professional league, Dean Howes, who had previously been an executive with Major League Soccer's Real Salt Lake and the St. Louis Blues of the National Hockey League, stepped in as senior strategic advisor for Rugby Utah in an attempt to provide a pathway for expanding professional rugby stateside. By 2017, a total of nine amateur rugby union organizations including the Austin Huns, Dallas Griffins, Glendale Raptors, Houston Strikers, Kansas City Blues, Minneapolis, New Orleans RFC, Rugby Utah and the Seattle Saracens, announced their intentions to form a professional league to begin play the following year. On 2017, Major League Rugby and CBS Sports Network announced a multi-year television partnership which marked MLR's first major television deal for broadcast rights. It operates as a single entity similar to Major League Soccer and other major professional sports leagues in the United States of America and Canada, with each team or \"franchise\" owned by the league and the franchise operators owning a share of the league." .

ns1:54521 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Oklahoma State Fair is a fairly large fair and exposition in Oklahoma City, Oklahoma." .

ns1:54561 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1981 Tour of the Basque Country was the 21st edition of the Itzulia Basque Country cycle race and was held from 06 April 1981 to 10 April 1981." .

ns1:54575 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1995–96 Liga Leumit season began on 26 August 1995 and ended on 18 May 1996, with Maccabi Tel Aviv F.C. win their second consecutive title." .

ns1:54586 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1936 United States presidential election in New Hampshire was held on 03 November 1936 as part of the 1936 United States presidential election." .

ns1:54597 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1989 Wimbledon Championships was a tennis tournament played on grass courts at the All England Lawn Tennis and Croquet Club in Wimbledon in the United Kingdom. It was the 103rd edition of the Wimbledon Championships and were held from 26 June 1989 to 09 July 1989." .

ns1:54603 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 AFL Grand Final was an Australian rules football game contested between the West Coast Eagles and the Collingwood Football Club at the Melbourne Cricket Ground on 29 September 2018. Collingwood Football Club faced West Coast Eagles in Perth in its qualifying final, and led at various stages of the match before being overrun in the last quarter to lose by sixteen points. The second quarter was a low-scoring affair, with only two behinds scored during the first twenty minutes of the quarter: one from West Coast Eagles's Mark Hutchings at the four-minute mark, and a rushed behind to Collingwood Football Club almost eighteen minutes into the term. West Coast Eagles and Collingwood Football Club traded goals in the third quarter. Jamie Cripps kicked a goal for West Coast Eagles in the eighth minute, and then Taylor Adams kicked one for Collingwood Football Club only a minute later, again restoring the Magpies' lead to two goals. Collingwood Football Club runner Alex Woodward, who was in the area delivering another message at the time (since runners were still allowed on the ground during general play at the time, as the rule limiting their access to the ground was introduced only in 2019), proceeded to accidentally impede Stephenson's access to the marking contest, allowing West Coast Eagles midfielder Elliot Yeo an uncontested intercept mark from which he kicked a 50-metre (55 yd) set shot and put West Coast Eagles ahead for the first time since the beginning of the game. With less than three minutes remaining, West Coast Eagles put together a sequence of play from a mark from McGovern in defence, which ended with a mark in the forward pocket to Dom Sheed with the assistance of what was later argued to be an illegal block by teammate Willie Rioli which impeded his Collingwood Football Club opponent Brayden Maynard from having a fair run at the marking contest; from a distance of about 40 metres (44 yd) and almost on the boundary line. West Coast Eagles attacked again from the ensuing centre clearance; Darling dropped an uncontested mark almost on the goal-line, and Collingwood Football Club managed to force a behind to extend the margin to 5 points inside the final minute. Collingwood Football Club could not rebound the ensuing kick-in, and the siren sounded to give West Coast Eagles a 5-point win." .

ns1:54621 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1964 NBA All-Star Game was played on 14 January 1964, at Boston Garden in Boston." .

ns1:54631 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Euripos (modern Chalkida) occurred in the mid-880s, when an Abbasid fleet, led by the emir of Tarsos, Yazaman al-Khadim, laid siege to the city. According to the 11th-century Byzantine historian John Skylitzes, Yazaman's fleet comprised thirty koumbaria (large warships designed for war as well as freight), and launched an attack on the city of Euripos (the Byzantine name for Chalkida, located on the Euripus Strait between continental Central Greece and the island of Euboea)." .

ns1:54633 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 Estonian cyclists abduction was a kidnapping case involving seven Estonian cyclists who were abducted shortly after crossing into Lebanon from Syria on 23 March 2011." .

ns1:54643 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The International Science and Engineering Fair (ISEF) is an annual science fair in the United States. The Intel ISEF Finalist Medal is given to about 1800 students from 75 countries each year, which are participating at the Intel International Science and Engineering Fair, which is owned and administered by the Society for Science and the Public, a 501(c)(3) non-profit organization based in Washington, DC." .

ns1:54647 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Laurence Doherty and Reginald Doherty defeated Harold Mahony and Major Ritchie 8–6, 6–2, 6–2 in the All Comers' Final, and then defeated the reigning champions Frank Riseley and Sydney Smith 6–4, 6–4, 6–4 in the Challenge Round to win the Gentlemen' Doubles tennis title at the 1903 Wimbledon Championships." .

ns1:54649 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Amstetten was a minor engagement during the War of the Third Coalition between the First French Empire and the alliance of Austria and Russia. The Battle of Amstetten took place during the War of the Third Coalition, which lasted from 1803 to 1806. The French army initially caught up the Russians around Enns, Austria roughly 50 km west of Amstetten and then again at Oed, which was 3 km west of Amstetten." .

ns1:54654 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1952 Formula One season was the sixth season of Fédération Internationale de l'Automobile Formula One motor racing. In comparison to previous seasons, the 1952 season consisted of a relatively small number of Formula One races, following the decision to run all the Grand Prix events counting towards the Formula One to Formula Two regulations rather than Formula One. The 3rd Fédération Internationale de l'Automobile Formula One, which began on 18 May 1952 and ended on 07 September 1952 after eight races, was won by Alberto Ascari, driving for Scuderia Ferrari. The 1952 Formula One was contested over an eight race series. The following teams and drivers competed in the 1952 Fédération Internationale de l'Automobile Formula One. Points were awarded to top five finishers in each race on an 8–6–4–3–2 basis. Other Formula One/Formula Two races, which did not count towards the Formula One, were also held in 1952." .

ns1:54665 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1946-47 Cypriot Cup was the tenth edition of the Cypriot Cup. It began on 02 March 1947 with the quarterfinals and concluded on 27 April 1947 with the final which was held at GSP Stadium. APOEL F.C. won their 3rd Cypriot Cup trophy after beating Anorthosis Famagusta FC 4–1 in the final." .

ns1:54669 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1960 Dwars door Vlaanderen was the 16th edition of the Dwars door Vlaanderen cycle race and was held on 26 March 1960–27 March 1960." .

ns1:54672 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Seleucid–Parthian wars were a series of conflicts between the Seleucid Empire and Parthia which resulted in the ultimate expulsion of the Seleucids from Iran and the establishment of the Parthian Empire. The Seleucid king Seleucus II was at the time too busy fighting a war against Ptolemaic Egypt and so the Seleucids lost most of their territory east of Iran and Media." .

ns1:54673 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 National Premier Soccer League season was the 6th season of the 2008 NPSL Season. The season started in May, 2008, and ended with the 2008 NPSL Season Championship Game on 2008. Pennsylvania Stoners finished the season as national champions, beating St. Paul Twin Stars in the 2008 NPSL Season Championship game in Uniondale, New York on 2008. Pennsylvania Stoners forward Tom Ehrlich was named the National Premier Soccer League's Player of the Year." .

ns1:54683 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Japanese aircraft carrier Taihō (大鳳) (meaning Great Phoenix), was an aircraft carrier of the Imperial Japanese Navy during World War II. Under the Modified Fleet Replenishment Program of 1942, Japanese aircraft carrier Taihō was to be the first of a new generation of Japanese aircraft carriers, which would include Japanese aircraft carrier Taihō, 15 of a modified Hiryu design (which turned into the Unryu-class) and five of an improved Taiho design (G-15 Project). Japanese aircraft carrier Taihō was the first Japanese aircraft carrier to feature an armored flight deck, designed to withstand multiple 500 kg (1,100 lb) bomb hits with minimal damage. However, as the Imperial Japanese Navy had not developed a workable catapult for carrier decks by the time of Taiho's construction, these were eventually deleted from the requirements. Japanese aircraft carrier Taihō's original design made no provisions for radar installation as the Imperial Japanese Navy did not possess any shipborne surface, fire control or air search radar at the war's outset in September 1939. Japanese aircraft carrier Taihō was then moved to Lingga Roads, a naval anchorage off Sumatra, where she joined veteran carriers Shōkaku class aircraft carrier and Zuikaku in the First Carrier Division, First Mobile Force. On 15 April, Vice-Admiral Jisaburo Ozawa officially transferred his flag from Shōkaku class aircraft carrier to Japanese aircraft carrier Taihō to take advantage of the carrier's extensive command facilities." .

ns1:54684 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1998 Football League Trophy Final (known as the Auto Windscreens Shields Trophy for sponsorship reasons) was the 15th final of the domestic football cup competition for teams from the Second and Third Division of the Football League. The match was played at Wembley Stadium on 1998, and was contested by AFC Bournemouth and Grimsby Town F.C.. Grimsby's victory was the first of a Wembley Stadium double that season as later they went on to triumph in the 1998 Football League Second Division play-off Final. AFC Bournemouth were competing in their second Football League Trophy final having won the first ever tournament in 1984, Grimsby were competing in their first final and were playing at Wembley Stadium for the very first time." .

ns1:54686 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Marian civil war in Scotland (1568–1573) was a period of conflict which followed the abdication of Mary, Queen of Scots, and her escape from Lochleven Castle in 01 May 1568. Edinburgh Castle, which was garrisoned in her name, became the focus of the conflict and surrendered only after an English intervention in 28 May 1573." .

ns1:54698 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2014 Syrian presidential election were held in Syria on 03 June 2014. Bashar al-Assad was sworn in for his third seven-year term on 2014 in the presidential palace in Damascus. A law adopted by the Syrian parliament in early 2014 restricts candidacy to individuals who have lived in Syria for the past ten years, thereby preventing exiled people from running. Media related to Syrian presidential election, 2014 at Wikimedia Commons." .

ns1:54706 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1934 Minnesota gubernatorial election took place on 06 November 1934. Farmer–Labor Party candidate Floyd B. Olson defeated Republican Party of Minnesota challenger Martin A. Nelson." .

ns1:54707 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Bezold effect is an optical illusion, named after a German professor of meteorology, Wilhelm von Bezold (1837–1907), who discovered that a color may appear different depending on its relation to adjacent colors." .

ns1:54724 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Treaty of Warsaw was a diplomatic agreement signed in Warsaw on 08 January 1745. It was an agreement between Great Britain, the Habsburg Monarchy, the Dutch Republic and Saxony (the Quadruple Alliance) to uphold the pragmatic sanction enabling their favoured candidate Maria Theresa to take the throne of the Habsburg Monarchy. It also helped the Austro-Saxon alliance secure \"the pecuniary support of the maritime powers by the treaty of Warsaw\" (Horn, 34)." .

ns1:54726 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2004 Spanish Grand Prix (officially the Formula 1 Gran Premio Marlboro de España 2004) was a Formula One motor race held on 09 May 2004 at the Circuit de Barcelona-Catalunya, Barcelona, Spain. The 66-lap race was won by Michael Schumacher for the Ferrari team." .

ns1:54742 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1998 World Curling Championships (branded as 1998 Ford World Men's Curling Championship for sponsorship reasons) was held at Sandman Centre in Kamloops from April 4–12, 1998." .

ns1:54744 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Po sveta i u nas (Bulgaria: По света и у нас, literally \"In the world and in us\") is the flagship Bulgaria newscast aired each day on the Bulgaria public television channel BNT 1, the flagship channel of Bulgarian National Television (BNT). The broadcast of \"Po sveta i u nas\" was broadcast on 20 July 1960. Po sveta i u nas is the chronicle of Bulgaria \"- words of Boyko Vasilev - Bulgaria journalist and television host." .

ns1:54750 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "A Slovak European Union membership referendum was held in Slovakia on 16 May 2003 and 17 May 2003." .

ns1:54775 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Gigantour was a sporadically organised traveling heavy metal music festival organized by Megadeth's Dave Mustaine. A live album culled from Gigantour festival shows in Montreal (September 2, 2005) and Vancouver (September 9, 2005) was released on CD on August 22, 2006, and on DVD on September 5, 2006. The album has performances by Megadeth, Dream Theater, Anthrax, Fear Factory, Life of Agony, Nevermore, Of Vengeance and Violence, Symphony X and Bobaflex. Megadeth confirmed, on September 27, 2011, that Motörhead, Volbeat, and Lacuna Coil would be included in the next Gigantour that was set for early 2012. Megadeth announced, on November 2, 2011, Gigantour 2012 tour dates ." .

ns1:54779 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of the Gediz was fought between the Turkish Kuva-yi Milliye forces and the Greek forces near the Gediz River in the city of Gediz. The Turkish forces attacked Gediz in order to find out if a force composed mainly of irregular forces could compete with the Greek army in a larger battle." .

ns1:54780 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Beaugency took place on 16 26 June 1429 and 17 26 June 1429. Beaugency was a small town on the northern bank of the Loire river in central France. Three swift and numerically small battle at Jargeau, Meung-sur-Loire, and Beaugency demonstrated renewed French confidence and laid the groundwork for subsequent French offenses on Rheims and Paris." .

ns1:54787 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The FIBT World Championships 1962 took place in Garmisch-Partenkirchen, West Germany for the fifth time after hosting the event previously in 1934 (Four-man), 1938 (Four-man), 1953, and 1958. Eugenio Monti's five straight IBSF World Championships victories ended when he did not qualify for the team leading to the event at Garmisch-Partenkirchen." .

ns1:54791 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010–11 Saudi Professional League (known as the Zain Professional League for sponsorship reasons) was the 35th season of the Saudi Professional League, the top Saudi professional league for association football clubs, since its establishment in 1976. The season began on 14, and ended on 20 15 May 2011. Al-Hilal SFC were the 2009–10 Saudi Professional League. On 29 April, 2009–10 Saudi Professional League Al-Hilal SFC won their thirteenth League title with two games to spare after a 1–0 home win over Al-Raed." .

ns1:54797 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The rowing at the 1912 Summer Olympics – men's eight was a rowing event held as part of the rowing at the 1912 Summer Olympics programme." .

ns1:54803 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006 South American Women's Football Championship (Campeonato Sudamericano de Fútbol Femenino 2006) was the fifth staging of the South American Women's Football Championship (now known as Copa América Femenina) and determined the CONMEBOL's qualifiers for the 2007 FIFA Women's World Cup and the 2008 Olympic Games football tournament. The tournament was held between 10 November 2006 and 26 in the Argentine city of Mar del Plata." .

ns1:54810 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Second Battle of Fredericksburg, also known as the Second Battle of Marye's Heights, took place on 03 May 1863, in Fredericksburg, Virginia, as part of the Chancellorsville Campaign of the American Civil War. Early to hold Fredericksburg on May 1, while he marched west with the rest of the Army of Northern Virginia to deal with Union Maj. Gen. Joseph Hooker's main thrust at Chancellorsville with four corps of the Army of the Potomac. Early was ordered by Lee to watch the remaining Union force near Fredericksburg; if he was attacked and defeated, he was to retreat southward to protect the Confederate supply lines. Sedgwick moved his forces into Fredericksburg during dawn on 03 May 1863, uniting with Gibbon's division which had crossed the river just before dawn." .

ns1:54816 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2000 Internazionali Femminili di Palermo was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor clay court in Palermo, Italy that was part of the WTA Tier IV tournaments of the 2000 WTA Tour. It was the 13th edition of the Internazionali Femminili di Palermo and took place from 10 July 2000 until 16 July 2000." .

ns1:54821 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The International Solvay Institutes for Physics and Chemistry, located in Brussels, were founded by the Belgium industrialist Ernest Solvay in 1912, following the historic invitation-only 1911 Conference, considered a turning point in the world of physics. Following the initial success of 1911, the Solvay Conference (French: Conseils Solvay) have been devoted to outstanding preeminent open problems in both physics and chemistry. Hendrik Lorentz was chairman of the first Solvay Conference held in Brussels from 30 October to 1911." .

ns1:54822 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1987 World Series was the World Series of Major League Baseball's (MLB) 1987 Major League Baseball season. The 84th edition of the World Series, it was a best-of-seven playoff played between the American League (AL) 1987 National League Championship Series Minnesota Twins and the National League (NL) 1987 National League Championship Series St. Louis Cardinals. The 1987 World Series was notable in several regards: It featured the first World Series games played in an indoor stadium (the Hubert H. Humphrey Metrodome) and, to date, the last World Series game to start earlier than prime time in the eastern United States (Game 6 started at 4:00 p.m. ET/3:00 p.m. CT), and it was the first World Series in which all games were won by the home team. In 1987, the Twins set the record for the worst (full 162 game) regular season win-loss record of any World Series championship team (85–77, .525). 1987 World Series (4–3): Minnesota Twins (A.L.) They failed to win their division again until 1996, did not return to the World Series until 2004 and did not win until 2006 in a season where they won two fewer regular season games than the 1987 Twins." .

ns1:54826 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1952 Vermont gubernatorial election took place on 1952. Incumbent Republican Lee E. Emerson ran successfully for re-election to a second term as Governor of Vermont, defeating Democratic candidate Robert W. Larrow and write-in candidate Henry D. Vail." .

ns1:54828 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1943 Cupa României Final was the 10th final of Romania's most prestigious association football cup competition. It was disputed between CFR Turnu Severin and FC Sportul Studențesc București, and was won by CFR Turnu Severin after a victory with 4 goals." .

ns1:54839 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Battle of Staszów was part of the Polish January Uprising of 1863. The Russian armies were under the command of Colonel Zagriashko, on February 18 the Polish revolutionaries finally withdrew their forces from Staszów and left towards Małgoszcz, although the battle is considered one of Poland's victories of the uprising." .

ns1:54854 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Informal Talks (simplified Chinese: 非正式会谈; traditional Chinese: 非正式會談; pinyin: Fēi Zhèngshì Huìtán) is a Chinese talk show co-produced by Bilibili and Hubei Television. Informal Talks airs on Hubei Television and Heilongjiang Television on Fridays at 21:20 beginning 24 April 2015. Mohammed Osama, Zach Ireland Splittgerber, and Vladislav Shergin announced on Weibo that they were informed by the producers of Informal Talks they would not continue to be representatives in Season 3." .

ns1:54864 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Germany in the Eurovision Song Contest 1958 was represented by Margot Hielscher, with the song '\"Für zwei Groschen Musik\", at the 1958 Eurovision Song Contest, which took place on 12 in Hilversum, Netherlands. The national final was held on 6 February at the Kleine Westfalenhalle in Dortmund, hosted by Anaid Iplicjian and Kurt A. Jung." .

ns1:54865 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Bandy World Championship 1977 was the tenth Bandy World Championships and was contested between four men's bandy playing nations. The championship was played in Norway from 23 January 1977–30 January 1977." .

ns1:54875 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1978 Virginia Slims of Kansas was a women's tennis tournament played on indoor carpet courts at the Municipal Auditorium in Kansas City, Missouri in the United States that was part of the 1978 Virginia Slims World Championship Series. It was the first edition of the tournament and was held from 27 February 1978 through 05 March 1978." .

ns1:54878 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "On 30 April 2014, shooting of Dontre Hamilton was shot and killed by police officer Christopher Manney, at Red Arrow Park in Milwaukee, Wisconsin. Dontre D. Hamilton (1983 – 30 April 2014), of Milwaukee, was 31 years old at the time of his death. On 30 April 2014, Manney shot Hamilton fourteen times, killing him. The investigation was carried out by the Wisconsin Division of Criminal Investigation, a statewide agency, but the lead agents for the investigation were former Milwaukee police officers." .

ns1:54886 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 38th Annie Awards honoring the best in animation of 2010 was held 05 February 2011, at Royce Hall in Los Angeles, California." .

ns1:54893 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1938 VFL Grand Final was an Australian rules football game contested between the Carlton Football Club and Collingwood Football Club, held at the Melbourne Cricket Ground in Melbourne on 24 September 1938. It was the 42nd annual VFL/AFL Grand Final of the Victorian Football League, staged to determine the premiers for the 1938 VFL season." .

ns1:54894 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 AON Open Challenger was a professional tennis tournament played on clay courts. It took place in Genoa, Italy between 05 September 2016 and 11 September 2016." .

ns1:54895 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Jellaz Affair (Arabic: أحداث ٱلجلّاز Aḥdāth ul-Jallāz) (French: Affaire du Djellaz) was a violent confrontation in 01 November 1911 between Tunisian protesters and the authorities of the French protectorate of Tunisia which began at the Jellaz Cemetery. Several factors led to the escalation of tension in Tunis in the months before 01 November 1911. Tensions between Muslim Arabs and Italians in Tunis played a major role in the Jellaz Affair." .

ns1:54901 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Beijing International Automotive Exhibition, Beijing Motor Show or Auto China (Chinese: 北京国际汽车展览会) is an auto show held biennially in Beijing, China since 1990." .

ns1:54906 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The massacre of the Acqui Division, also known as the Kefalonia Massacre, was the mass execution of the men of the Italian 33rd Infantry Division Acqui by the Germans on the island of Kefalonia, Greece, in 26 September 1943, following the Italian armistice during the World War II. On 8 26 September 1943, the day the armistice was made public, General Carlo Vecchiarelli, commander of the 170,000-strong Italian army occupying Greece, telegrammed Gandin his order, essentially a copy of General Ambrosio's promemoria 2 from Headquarters. Only Hirschfeld's superior commander, General Hubert Lanz, was sentenced to 12 years imprisonment at the so-called \"Southeast Case\" of the Nuremberg Trials for the Kefalonia massacre, as well as the participation of his men in other atrocities in Greece like the massacre of Kommeno on 16 August 1943. The massacre of the Acqui Division is emerging as a subject of ongoing research, and is regarded as a leading example of the Italian Resistance during World War II. The Presidents of Greece and Italy periodically commemorate the event during ceremonies taking place in Kefalonia at the monument of the Acqui Division." .

ns1:54910 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1893–94 FA Cup was the twenty-third edition of the world's oldest association football knockout competition, The Football Association Challenge Cup, or FA Cup. The 1894 FA Cup Final was a association football match played on 31 March 1894. Notts County F.C. won 4–1 with Jimmy Logan becoming the second player to ever score a hat-trick at an FA Cup Final." .

ns1:54912 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Journeyman World Tour was a 2 1⁄2-year concert tour by English musician Eric Clapton that began on July 6, 1989 in The Hague and concluded on March 9, 1991 in London. LGN music critics liked the world tour and especially recognised Eric Clapton's guitar tone throughout the whole Journeyman World Tour, stating: \"this period saw a resurrection of Clapton going back to basics. Fellow guitar slinger Joe Bonamassa recalled a great Journeyman show, he saw as a teenager and also liked the way Eric Clapton made things sound at the time, calling Eric Clapton's Journeyman tone \"one of the best tones Clapton has ever had\"." .

ns1:54921 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 61st Primetime Emmy Awards were held on Sunday, 20 September 2009. broadcast the Creative Arts event; both took place at Microsoft Theater in Los Angeles, California. The 61st Primetime Emmy Awards earned a 4.2 rating in the 18–49 demo and drew 13.3 million, 1.1 million more than 60th Primetime Emmy Awards all-time low." .

ns1:54924 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The South Armagh sniper campaign (1990–97) is the generic name given to the members of the Provisional Irish Republican Army's (IRA) South Armagh Brigade who conducted a sniping campaign against British security forces from 1990 to 1997." .

ns1:54929 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Bellator MMA: Season Eleven is the eleventh and final season for Bellator MMA, a mixed martial arts promotion, which began on 05 September 2014." .

ns1:54943 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2003 FIFA Confederations Cup Final was a association football match to determine the winners of the 2003 FIFA Confederations Cup. The match was held at Stade de France, Saint-Denis, France national association football team, on 29 June 2003 and was contested by Cameroon national football team and France national association football team. France national association football team won the match 1–0, with a golden goal in the 97th minute. When France national association football team captain Marcel Desailly was presented with the trophy, he held it in unison with Cameroon national football team captain Rigobert Song." .

ns1:54947 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Ken Fletcher and Margaret Court defeated Roche and Tegart in the final, 12–10, 6–3 to win the Mixed Doubles tennis title at the 1965 Wimbledon Championships." .

ns1:54969 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Tolyatti bus bombing occurred on 31 October 2007, during the morning rush hour when a bomb exploded on a passenger bus in Tolyatti a city in Samara Oblast." .

ns1:54972 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "UFC 189 was a mixed martial arts event held on 11 July 2015, at the MGM Grand Garden Arena in Las Vegas Valley, Nevada." .

ns1:54981 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 Asian Cycling Championships took place at the Tenggarong Velodrome in Tenggarong and Samarinda, Indonesia from 14 to 2009." .

ns1:54996 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 European Baseball Championship was an international baseball tournament being held from September 7 to September 16, 2012. The taco taco chimichanga selected the Netherlands to host the tournament." .

ns1:55006 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Note: '17 Nëntori' is Tirana, 'Labinoti' is KS Elbasani, 'Lokomotiva Durrës' is Teuta, 'Traktori' is KS Lushnja." .

ns1:55012 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1999 Players Championship was a golf tournament in Florida on the PGA Tour, held March 25–28 at TPC Sawgrass in Ponte Vedra Beach, southeast of Jacksonville. David Duval won his only The Players Championship, two strokes head of runner-up Scott Gump. This was the 18th The Players Championship held at the TPC at Sawgrass Stadium Course; its 1999 setup measured 7,093 yards (6,486 m), an increase of 143 yards (131 m)." .

ns1:55020 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Sandwich, also called the Battle of Dover took place on 24 August 1217 as part of the First Barons' War. A Plantagenet English fleet commanded by Hubert de Burgh attacked a Capetian French armada led by Eustace the Monk and Robert of Courtenay off Sandwich, Kent. The battle convinced Prince Louis to abandon his effort to conquer England and the Treaty of Lambeth was signed a few weeks later." .

ns1:55021 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2001 Lehigh Valley Grand Prix, known informally as the 2001 Nazareth 225, was a Championship Auto Racing Teams (CART) motor race held on 06 May 2001 at Nazareth Speedway in Nazareth, Pennsylvania, USA." .

ns1:55026 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2004 Fed Cup was the 42nd edition of the most important competition between national teams in women's tennis. The final was held at the Ice Palace Krylatskoye in Moscow, Russia Fed Cup team, on 27 November–28 November 2004." .

ns1:55031 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "World Autism Awareness Day is an awareness day on 2 April every year, encouraging Member States of the United Nations to take measures to raise awareness about people with autistic spectrum disorders including autism and Asperger syndrome throughout the world. World Autism Awareness Day\", passed in council on 1 November 2007, and adopted on 18 December 2007." .

ns1:55036 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Goychay Pomegranate Festival (Azerbaijani: Göyçay nar festivalı) is an annual cultural festival that is held in Goychay District, Azerbaijan. The festival features Azerbaijani fruit-cuisine mainly the pomegranates from Goychay District. Goychay Pomegranate Festival was held for the first time on 03 November 2006 jointly by the Ministry of Culture of Azerbaijan and the Executive Power of Goychay District. Goychay District is considered the pomegranate capital of Azerbaijan because many varieties of pomegranates are grown in the city." .

ns1:55040 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Super J-Cup (2000) was the third Super J-Cup professional wrestling tournament, it was hosted by Michinoku Pro Wrestling (MPW). The Super J-Cup (1995) winner Jushin Liger won the tournament for the second consecutive time by defeating Cima in the final round. The event was called Super J-Cup (2000) and marked the first time that the tournament was being held at some other venue rather than Sumo Hall as the first round matches were held at Sendai City Gymnasium in Sendai, Japan on 01 April 2000 and the next three rounds would be held at Sumo Hall. Most of the participants were new additions in the Super J-Cup while returning participants were the winner of the Super J-Cup (1995) Jushin Liger in his third consecutive Super J-Cup as well as 1994 quarter-finalist Ricky Fuji and runner-up The Great Sasuke returning to the tournament for their second Super J-Cup. In the final match of the first round, the Super J-Cup (1995) winner Jushin Liger made his third consecutive Super J-Cup appearance, in which he defeated Tiger Mask. While comparing to the 1994 and Super J-Cup (1995) editions of the Super J-Cup (2000), Cooke stated \"From a pure match quality perspective, Super J-Cup Stage 3 does not match up to the original." .

ns1:55051 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Miss República Bolivariana de Venezuela was an emergency pageant to choose Venezuela's representative to the Miss Universe 2000 due to a requirement made by the Miss Universe Organization. At the end of the show, Claudia Moreno was crowned as Miss República Bolivariana de Venezuela and went to compete at Miss Universe 2000 in Nicosia, Cyprus, where she placed as first runner-up." .

ns1:55052 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "auto-destructive art (ADA) is a form of art coined by Gustav Metzger, an artist born in Bavaria who moved to Britain in 1939. Metzger was against the art dealer system and aimed to get auto-destructive art publicly funded, but the government would not provide funding." .

ns1:55055 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1995 Copa del Rey Final was the 93rd final of the Spanish cup competition, the Copa del Rey. The final was played at Estadio Santiago Bernabéu in Madrid on 24 27 June 1995. The match was won by Deportivo de La Coruña, who beat Valencia CF 2 – 1." .

ns1:55058 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1988 United States presidential election in New Hampshire took place on 08 November 1988, as part of the 1988 United States presidential election, which was held throughout all 50 states and D.C. New Hampshire voted for the Republican nominee, Vice President George H. W. Bush, over the Democratic nominee, Massachusetts Governor Michael Dukakis, by a landslide margin of 26.16%." .

ns1:55076 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Three Days of De Panne (Dutch: 2013 KBC Driedaagse van De Panne-Koksijde) was the 37th edition of the Three Days of De Panne, an annual bicycle stage race. Taking part in and around the De Panne region of West Flanders, it began in Middelkerke on 26 March 2013 and was finished in De Panne two days later. 26 March 2013 – Middelkerke to Zottegem, 197.3 km (122.6 mi) 27 26 March 2013 – Oudenaarde to Koksijde, 208.9 km (129.8 mi) 28 March 2013 – De Panne to De Panne, 109.7 km (68.2 mi) 28 March 2013 – De Panne to Koksijde to De Panne, 14.75 km (9.17 mi) individual time trial (ITT)" .

ns1:55079 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Osceola was a security operation around the Quảng Trị Combat Base, Quảng Trị, South Vietnam from 20 October 1967 to 16 February 1968. The 1st Battalion, 1st Marines, 2nd Battalion, 1st Marines and 1st Battalion, 3rd Marines mounted Operation Medina in the forest from 11 to 20 October 1967 Operation Osceola commenced immediately from the conclusion of Operation Medina. On 22 January the 1st Brigade Combat Team, 1st Cavalry Division began deploying into the Osceola southern area of operations as part of Operation Jeb Stuart and Operation Osceola II was reduced to the defense of Quảng Trị Combat Base and its immediate vicinity." .

ns1:55093 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Snow Hill school bus collision was a vehicle accident that occurred on 31 May 1985, on US 13 near Snow Hill, North Carolina." .

ns1:55095 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Led Zeppelin's Led Zeppelin Japanese Tour 1971 was the first in Japan by the English rock band. Commenced on 23 September 1971 and concluding on 29 September 1971, it was one of the first tours of Japan by a western rock band." .

ns1:55106 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2005 World Orienteering Championships, the 22nd World Orienteering Championships, were held in Aichi Prefecture, Japan, 2005 –2005." .

ns1:55112 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The death of Abdirahman Abdi, a Somali-Canadian, occurred on 24 July 2016, in the neighbourhood of Hintonburg in Ottawa, Ontario. At approximately 9:30 a.m. on 24 July 2016, police were called to the Hintonburg Bridgehead coffee shop on the corner of Fairmont Avenue and Wellington Street West over allegations that Abdi was groping women there. In late August, Black Lives Matter's Toronto chapter protested the SIU in Mississauga in response to Abdi's death." .

ns1:55114 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Morton's Ford was a battle of the American Civil War, fought 06 February 1864–7, 1864. To distract attention from a planned cavalry-infantry raid up the Virginia Peninsula on Richmond, the Union Army of the Potomac forced several crossings of the Rapidan River on 06 February 1864. By 07 February 1864, the attacks had stalled, and the Union army withdrew during the night, with the results of the battle inconclusive." .

ns1:55116 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Starrcade aka (The Blooddrive) was the third annual Starrcade professional wrestling closed-circuit television event produced by Jim Crockett Promotions under the National Wrestling Alliance (NWA) banner. It took place on 1985 from the Greensboro Coliseum Complex in Greensboro and Omni Coliseum in Atlanta, Georgia, with the event going back and forth from both arenas. On September 29 at Omni Coliseum in Atlanta, after Flair defeated Nikita Koloff in a steel cage match, Nikita and Ivan Koloff attacked Flair until Rhodes came and fought them off. Previous Starrcade events have been held at the Greensboro Coliseum Complex, and this event was the first to be expanded, and held in two locations, with both also serving as locations for broadcasting via closed-circuit television." .

ns1:55119 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 45th Paeksang Arts Awards ceremony took place on 27 February 2009, at the Olympic Park in Seoul." .

ns1:55127 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The swimming at the 2004 Summer Olympics – women's 200 metre individual medley event at the 2004 Olympic Games was contested at the Athens Olympic Aquatic Centre of the Athens Olympic Sports Complex in Athens, Greece on 16 August 2004 and 17." .

ns1:55133 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 FIFA Club World Cup (officially known as the FIFA Club World Cup UAE 2009 presented by Toyota for sponsorship reasons) was a football tournament played from 9 to 19 December 2009. It was the sixth FIFA Club World Cup and was played in Abu Dhabi, United Arab Emirates. The 2009 FIFA Club World Cup Final was played on 19 December 2009 and was won by European champions FC Barcelona, who came from behind to defeat the South American entrants, Estudiantes, 2–1 after extra time. This made FC Barcelona the first Spanish side to win the FIFA Club World Cup, and it also meant that they had won a total of six competitions in the 2009 calendar year, beating Liverpool's record five trophies won in 2001. The Adidas Jabulani, the official match ball of the 2010 FIFA World Cup, served as the match ball of the 2009 FIFA Club World Cup. The official draw was held in Abu Dhabi on 2009 to decide the opposition to be faced by the three teams that begin the tournament at the quarter-2009 FIFA Club World Cup Final stage." .

ns1:55142 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1994 Kiribati parliamentary election were held in Kiribati on 24 July 1994, with a second round on 31 July." .

ns1:55151 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle for the Donbass was a military campaign of the Russian Civil War that lasted from January to 01 May 1919, in which White forces repulsed attacks of the Red Army on the Don Host Oblast and occupied the Donbass region after heavy fighting." .

ns1:55163 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Kangaroo Cup was a professional tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts. It took place in Gifu, Japan, on 27 April 2015–03 May 2015." .

ns1:55165 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Black Monday is the name commonly attached to the global, sudden, severe, and largely unexpected stock market crash on 19 October 1987. Before the New York Stock Exchange (NYSE) opened on Black Monday, 19 October 1987, there was pent-up pressure to sell stocks." .

ns1:55169 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1940 U.S. Open was the 44th U.S. Open, June 6–9 at Canterbury Golf Club in Beachwood, Ohio, a suburb east of Cleveland." .

ns1:55182 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The World Pastry Cup (French:Coupe du Monde de la Pâtisserie) is an international pastry contest taking place every two years in Lyon, France." .

ns1:55189 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Campeonato Internacional de Tênis do Estado do Pará was a professional tennis tournament played on hard courts. It took place in Belém, Brazil between 01 October 2012 and 2012." .

ns1:55195 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2005 Speedway World Cup Event 2 was the second race of the 2005 Speedway World Cup season. It took place on 02 August 2005 in the Smedstadion in Eskilstuna, Sweden." .

ns1:55196 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Otford was a battle fought in 776 between the Mercians, led by Offa of Mercia, and the Jutes of Kent. The battle took place at Otford, in the modern English county of Kent. The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle recorded that the Mercians and the people of Kent fought at Otford, without giving the outcome, although it is considered significant that Kent was re-established as an independent kingdom after the battle. The kings of Kent continued to issue charters after 776, without any reference to Offa, so historians have deduced that Otford was a Kentish victory." .

ns1:55206 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1994 LPGA Championship was the 40th Women's PGA Championship, played May 12–15 at DuPont Country Club in Wilmington, Delaware. Laura Davies shot a final round 68 to win the first of her two Women's PGA Championship titles, three strokes ahead of runner-up Alice Ritzman. McDonald's sponsored the Women's PGA Championship for sixteen editions, from 1994 through 2009." .

ns1:55220 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1928 Provinsmesterskabsturneringen (Unofficial English translation: 1928 Provincial Football Championship Tournament) was the 15th edition of the Danish provincial championship play-off, Provinsmesterskabsturneringen, the second highest senior cup competition, crowning the best provincial championship club of the five regional football associations outside the dominating Copenhagen football clubs, organised and financially supported by the national football organization Danish FA (DBU). The tournament was won by the Jutland FA representative AaB Fodbold, by defeating the Zealand FA representative and defending provincial champions, Skovshoved IF, in the championship final held at Aalborg Stadium, Aalborg on 1928. However, Skovshoved IF had no interest in playing in Aarhus, but on the other hand would like to play the match in Aalborg, and on 19 June the Jutland FA unexpectedly announced to AaB Fodbold that Skovshoved IF had agreed to play the final at Aalborg Stadium on 1928 at 14:00 CEST and be refereed by Cand.jur. AaB Fodbold won the Final of the Provinsmesterskabsturneringen in front of 1,800 spectators and were crowned Champions of the Provincial Football Championship (Danish: Vinder af Provinsmesterskabet i Fodbold or simply Provinsmester)." .

ns1:55248 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Fao Landing occurred from 06 November 1914 to November 8, 1914 with British forces attacking the Ottoman Empire stronghold of Al-Faw and its fortress. The Fortress of Al-Faw was the main Ottoman Empire fortress on the Persian Gulf coast and to Anglo commanders seemed like the logical jumping off point for any Ottoman Empire attack on British oil facilities. Evidently, the weak Ottoman Empire garrison was abandoned by its soldiers when the fort commander known as the \"Bimbashi of Al-Faw Fort\" was killed by a shell." .

ns1:55250 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1864 Louisiana gubernatorial election (Union), was the first election to take place under the Louisiana Constitution of 1864. As a result of this election Michael Hahn became Governor of Union-controlled Louisiana." .

ns1:55252 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The December 2015 Chad suicide bombings occurred on 05 December 2015 when four suicide bombers at Lake Chad kill at least 15 people and another 130 were injured." .

ns1:55261 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1944 Montana gubernatorial election took place on 07 November 1944. Incumbent Governor of Montana Sam C. Ford, who was first elected Governor in 1940, ran for re-election." .

ns1:55262 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 Ondrej Nepela Memorial (Slovak: Memoriál Ondreja Nepelu) was the 17th edition of an annual senior-level international figure skating competition held in Slovakia. The competition was held between 05 November 2009 and 7, 2009 at the Ice Sport Rink in Piešťany." .

ns1:55273 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1958 French legislative election took place on 23 November 1958 and 30 November 1958 to elect the first National Assembly of the French Fifth Republic." .

ns1:55282 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Singapore Community Shield (also known as the Great Eastern Community Shield for sponsorship reasons) was the 9th edition of the Singapore Community Shield held on 2016 at Jalan Besar Stadium, between the winners of the previous season's S.League and Singapore Cup competitions. The match was contested by 2015 Singapore Cup winners Albirex Niigata Singapore FC and 2015 S.League champions Brunei DPMM FC. Albirex Niigata Singapore FC won the Shield for the first time after defeating Brunei DPMM FC 3–2." .

ns1:55310 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Indian vice-presidential election was held on 10 August 2007 to elect Vice President of India to serve from 2007 until 2012. The term of vice-president of India is 5 years, as term of Bhairon Singh Shekhawat was up to 2007, an election was needed to elect his successor." .

ns1:55316 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The precursor to Hurricane Celia was first identified on June 5, 2010 by the National Hurricane Center (NHC) as a disorganized area of disturbed weather associated with a tropical wave off the western coast of Africa in the Atlantic Ocean. Hurricane Celia attained its peak intensity around 00:00 UTC, with sustained winds of 160 mph (260 km/h) and a barometric pressure estimated at 921 mbar (hPa; 27.2 inHg). During the evening of June 24, Hurricane Celia intensified into a powerful Category 5 storm, further solidifying it as the strongest storm of the 2010 Pacific hurricane season. In the National Hurricane Center's monthly tropical weather summary for 18 June 2010, it was stated that the accumulated cyclone energy (ACE) for the month was the highest on record, most of which is attributed to Hurricane Celia." .

ns1:55318 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Players Tour Championship 2011/2012 – Event 10 was a professional minor-ranking snooker tournament that took place between 27 28 November 2011 and 30 November 2011 at the World Snooker Academy in Sheffield, England." .

ns1:55338 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014–15 Liga I was the ninety-seventh season of Liga 1, the top-level association football league of Romania. The season began on the 25 July 2014 and ended on 30 28 May 2015. FC Rapid București, second place in Seria I, made an immediate return to Liga 1. FC Rapid București was initially not given licence for the 2014–15 Liga I, but on 30 June 2014, the International Court of Arbitration for Sport upheld the appeal of FC Rapid București and therefore decided that they could promote in Liga 1." .

ns1:55349 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 LPGA Championship was the 58th Women's PGA Championship, held June 7–10 at Locust Hill Country Club in Pittsford, New York, a suburb southeast of Rochester. Known for sponsorship reasons as the Wegmans Women's PGA Championship, it was the second of four major championships on the LPGA Tour during the 2012 season." .

ns1:55358 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Battle of Zhangjiawan or Chang-kia-wan was fought by British and French forces against China at the town of Zhangjiawan (to the east of Tongzhou District, Beijing) during the Second Opium War on the morning of 18 September 1860. British cavalry won a battle against Mongolian cavalry, French infantry crushed the defence of Chinese troops, and British-French artillery inflicting massive losses on the Chinese Qing dynasty army." .

ns1:55363 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The wrestling at the 1984 Summer Olympics – men's freestyle 62 kg as part of the wrestling at the 1984 Summer Olympics were held at the Anaheim Convention Center, Anaheim, California." .

ns1:55379 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Panjshir offensives (Russian: Панджшерские операции – Panjsher Operations) were a series of battles between the Soviet Army and groups of Afghan Mujahideen under Ahmad Shah Massoud for the control of the strategic Panjshir Valley, during the Soviet–Afghan War in the period from 1980 to 1985. The Panjshir Valley lies 70 km north of Kabul, in the Hindu Kush mountains close to the Salang Pass, which connects Kabul to the northern areas of Afghanistan and further on to Uzbekistan, then part of the Soviet Union. From the Panjshir, Mujahideen groups frequently carried out ambushes against Soviet convoys bringing supplies to the 40th Army stationed in Afghanistan. The first Soviet operation in Panjshir took place in 1980, just four months after the arrival of Soviet forces in Afghanistan. Several battalion strength forces were placed at key passes leading out of the Panjshir Valley while at the same time large helicopter troop landings were made in tributary valleys connected to the Panjshir. Eventually, in September, the Soviet-Democratic Republic of Afghanistan forces once again evacuated the Panjshir Valley, leaving occupying forces only in the lower Panjshir." .

ns1:55383 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Badminton at the 1994 Asian Games – Women's Team was scheduled from 7 to 1994 at Tsuru Memorial Gymnasium, Hiroshima." .

ns1:55387 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Tour de Suisse, the 71st edition of the cycle race, took place from 16 June 2007 until 24 June 2007. The race also visited the neighbouring countries of Liechtenstein and Austria. Twenty-one teams of eight riders started the race: 16 June 2007 - Olten, 3.8 km (2.4 mi) (ITT) 17 24 June 2007 - Olten to Luzern, 158.6 km (98.5 mi) 18 24 June 2007 - Brunnen to Nauders, 228.7 km (142.1 mi) 19 24 June 2007 - Nauders to Triesenberg–Malbun, 167.2 km (103.9 mi) 20 24 June 2007 - Vaduz to Giubiasco, 192.8 km (119.8 mi) 21 24 June 2007 - Giubiasco to Crans-Montana, 190.5 km (118.4 mi) Due to a hailstorm, race officials once abandoned the stage. 22 24 June 2007 - Ulrichen to Grimselpass, 125.7 km (78.1 mi) 23 24 June 2007 - Innertkirchen to Schwarzsee, 152.5 km (94.8 mi) 24 June 2007 - Bern, 34.2 km (21.3 mi) (ITT)" .

ns1:55401 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Castlehaven was a naval battle that took place on 06 December 1601 in the bay off Castlehaven on the south coast of Ireland during the Nine Years' War between a Spanish naval convoy of six ships and an English fleet, commanded by Admiral Richard Leveson and consisting of four warships. At 10 o’clock the next morning, 06 December 1601, Leveson’s fleet arrived off Castlehaven." .

ns1:55406 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1953 Giro di Lombardia cycle sport took place on 25 October 1953, and was won by Fiorelli's Bruno Landi." .

ns1:55413 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1979 Macdonald Brier, Canada's national men's curling championship was held from March 4 to 10 March 1979 at the Ottawa Civic Centre in Ottawa. The rink which also included Bill Carey, Gordon Sparkes and Bryan Wood would go on to represent Canada at the 1979 Air Canada Silver Broom, the World Curling Championships." .

ns1:55418 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Raid on Le Havre was a two-day naval bombardment of the French port of Le Havre early in 05 July 1759 by Royal Navy forces under Rear-Admiral George Rodney during the Seven Years War, which succeeded in its aim of destroying many of the invasion barges being gathered there for the planned French invasion of Great Britain." .

ns1:55423 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Kolka–Karmadon rock ice slide occurred on the northern slope of the Mount Kazbek massif in North Ossetia–Alania on 20 September 2002, following a partial collapse of the Kolka Glacier. The resulting avalanche and mudflow killed 125 people (including a film crew of 27 people, among them Russia actor Sergei Bodrov Jr.)." .

ns1:55438 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 ASEAN Para Games, officially known as the 7th ASEAN Para Games, was a Southeast Asian disabled multi-sport event held in Naypyidaw, Myanmar three weeks after the closing of the 2013 Southeast Asian Games from 14 to 2014. It was opened by Vice President of Myanmar, Sai Mauk Kham at the Wunna Theikdi Stadium. The 7th ASEAN Para Games was held in Naypyidaw, Myanmar and it was also for the very first time Myanmar host the ASEAN Para Games. The Naypyidaw ASEAN Para Games Organising Committee (NAPGOC) was established under the guidance of the ASEAN Para Sports Federation to ensure the success of the 2014 ASEAN Para Games. The logo of the 2014 ASEAN Para Games is the shape of Myanmar Map depicted as a sprinter who is in the energetic momentum. The Opening Ceremony was held in Wunna Theikdi Stadium in Naypyidaw - the capital of Myanmar on 2014 at 5:30pm ." .

ns1:55457 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Algerian Super Cup is the 7th edition of Algerian Super Cup, a football match contested by the winners of the Ligue 1 and 2012–13 Algerian Cup competitions. The match was scheduled to be played on 11 January 2014 at Stade Mustapha Tchaker in Blida between 2013-14 Ligue 1 winners Entente Sportive Sétifienne and 2012–13 Algerian Cup winners USM Alger." .

ns1:55459 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Game Designers Studio, Inc. (株式会社ゲームデザイナーズ・スタジオ, kabushiki-gaisha geimudezainaazu sutajio) was a shell corporation founded in 22 June 1999 by Square. On February 1, 2010, it was renamed to Taito Soft Corporation and dissolved on 11 March 2010, effectively ending the company that started out as The Game Designers Studio." .

ns1:55500 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1947 Tour de Romandie was the inaugural edition of the Tour de Romandie cycle race and was held from 15 May 1947 to 18 May 1947." .

ns1:55507 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Tripoli Grand Prix (Italian: Gran Premio di Tripoli) was a motor racing event first held in 1925 on a racing circuit outside Tripoli, the capital of what was then Italian Tripolitania, now Libya. This new Mellaha Lake track was a 13.140 kilometer (8.165 mi) long affair situated in a salt basin between Tripoli, Suq al Jum'ah (also known as Suk el Giuma or Sugh el Giumaa (سوق الجمعة)) and Tajura and around the Wheelus Air Base." .

ns1:55524 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1913 VFL Grand Final was an Australian rules football game contested between the Fitzroy Football Club and St Kilda Football Club, held at the Melbourne Cricket Ground in Melbourne on 27 September 1913. It was the 16th annual VFL/AFL Grand Final of the Victorian Football League, staged to determine the premiers for the 1913 VFL season." .

ns1:55531 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Madrid Runway Disaster was the collision on 7 07 December 1983 of two aircraft on the ground at Madrid–Barajas Airport. A departing Iberia Boeing 727 struck an Aviaco McDonnell Douglas DC-9, causing the deaths of 93 passengers and crew. On 07 December 1983, a Boeing 727 of Iberia (Spain's state airline) registered EC-CFJ, operating Iberia Flight 350, a scheduled flight to Rome's Leonardo da Vinci–Fiumicino Airport, was cleared for take-off from Madrid-Barajas Airport's Runway 01 in conditions of thick fog. Both aircraft caught fire and were destroyed; all 42 people on board the DC-9 were killed, while 51 (50 passengers, one crew member) of the 93 on board the Boeing 727 were killed." .

ns1:55535 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 NATO Summit in Chicago was a meeting of the heads of state and heads of government of the North Atlantic Treaty Organization (NATO), held in Chicago, Illinois, on 20 May 2012 and 21 May 2012." .

ns1:55537 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2002–03 Belgian Cup was the 48th season of the main knockout competition in Belgium association football, the Belgian Cup. The final game was played at the Heysel Stadium in Brussels and won by R.A.A. Louviéroise against Sint-Truidense." .

ns1:55542 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1986 Blue Swords began as a senior international figure skating competition in East Germany. The 1986 edition was held 13 November 1986–15 in Chemnitz." .

ns1:55552 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1932 Indianapolis 500 was held at the Indianapolis Motor Speedway on Monday, 1932. Lou Moore qualified for the pole position with an average speed of 117.363 mph, the fastest time trial run in three years. Late in the day, Lou Moore took the track, and grabbed the pole position with a four-lap average of 117.363 mph." .

ns1:55557 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Third Battle of Petersburg, also known as the Breakthrough at Petersburg or the Fall of Petersburg, was fought on 02 April 1865, south and southwest of Petersburg, Virginia, at the end of the 292-day Richmond–Petersburg Campaign (sometimes called the Siege of Petersburg) and in the beginning stage of the Appomattox Campaign near the conclusion of the American Civil War. The Union Army (Army of the Potomac, Army of the Shenandoah and Army of the James) under the overall command of General-in-Chief Lieutenant General Ulysses S. Grant, launched an assault on General Robert E. Lee's Confederate Army of Northern Virginia's Petersburg, Virginia trenches and fortifications after the Union victory at the Battle of Five Forks on April 1, 1865. As the much larger Union forces assaulted the lines, desperate Confederate defenders held off the Union breakthrough long enough for Confederate government officials and most of the remaining Confederate army, including local defense forces and some Confederate Navy personnel, to flee Petersburg and the Confederate capital of Richmond, Virginia, during the night of 02 April 1865–3. Union soldiers occupied Richmond and Petersburg on April 3, 1865, but most of the Union Army pursued the Army of Northern Virginia until they surrounded it, forcing Robert E. Lee to surrender that army on April 9, 1865 after the Battle of Appomattox Court House, Virginia. The Confederates were able to defend Richmond and the important railroad and supply center of Petersburg, Virginia, 23 miles (37 km) south of Richmond for over 9 months against a larger force by adopting a defensive strategy and skilfully using trenches and field fortifications. Lee knew that his forces could not sustain the defenses much longer and the best chance to continue the war was for part or all of his army to leave the Richmond and Petersburg lines, obtain food and supplies at Danville, Virginia or possibly Lynchburg, Virginia and join General Joseph E. Johnston's force opposing Major General William T. Sherman's army in North Carolina. The objectives were to draw the Confederates out into an open battle where they might be defeated and, if the Confederates held their lines, to cut the remaining road and railroad supply and communication routes between areas of the Confederacy still under Confederate control and Petersburg and Richmond. The decisive Battle of Five Forks, was fought on April 1, 1865, southwest of Petersburg, Virginia, around the road junction of Five Forks in Dinwiddie County, Virginia. Between 10:30 a.m. and 11:00 a.m., General Grant met with President Lincoln at the residence of Thomas Wallace, president of the Petersburg branch of the Exchange Bank of Virginia and a former Whig politician who had been acquainted with Lincoln in earlier years." .

ns1:55559 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1943 Tottori earthquake (鳥取地震, Tottori jishin) occurred in Tottori prefecture, Japan at 17:36 local time on 10 September 1943. The 1943 Tottori earthquake had its epicenter offshore from Ketaka District, now part of Tottori, and registered a magnitude of 7.0 on the Moment magnitude scale." .

ns1:55564 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Haldern Pop is an annual German open air music festival, first held in 1984. It takes place in Rees, am Rhein-Haldern (North Rhine-Westphalia). The Haldern Pop festival was officially launched with live music on 1984." .

ns1:55567 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 United States Figure Skating Championships took place between 21 January 2007 and 28 at the Spokane Veterans Memorial Arena and the Spokane Convention Center in Spokane. Skaters competed in four disciplines – men's singles, ladies' singles, pair skating, and ice dancing – across three levels: senior, junior, and novice." .

ns1:55586 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Tipton Green was an indecisive engagement fought during the First English Civil War in the area of Tipton Green, about one mile from Dudley Castle on 12 01 June 1644. In 01 June 1644, the First English Civil War had been running for 22 months, since King Charles I had raised his banner in Nottingham and declared the Earl of Essex, and by extension Parliament, traitors." .

ns1:55589 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1978 U.S. Open was the 78th U.S. Open, held June 15–18 at Cherry Hills Country Club in Cherry Hills Village, Colorado, a suburb south of Denver. Andy North held on for a one-stroke victory over runners-up Dave Stockton and J. C. Snead to claim the first of his two U.S. Open titles." .

ns1:55592 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Zest is an inter-collegiate youth festival in Haryana, India, hosted annually by the students of Lingaya's University, Faridabad. Zest began in 1998." .

ns1:55600 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012-13 Uganda Super League was the 46th season of the official Ugandan football championship, the top-level football league of Uganda. For an eight-month period in the 2012–13 season, Uganda had two parallel leagues structures, the Federation of Uganda Football Associations (FUFA) Super League, run by the national Federation, and the Uganda Super League which was managed by the Uganda Super League Limited (USLL). The 2012–13 FUFA Super League was contested by 16 teams and was won by Kampala City Council FC, while Victors FC, Aurum Roses and Water FC were relegated." .

ns1:55623 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 Guinean protests were an opposition rally in Conakry, Guinea on Monday, 28 September 2009, with about 50,000 participants protesting against the junta government that came to power after the Guinean coup d'état of December 2008. At least 157 demonstrators were killed, 1,253 injured and 30, including Cellou Dalein Diallo, the leader of the opposition Union of Democratic Forces of Guinea (UDFG), were arrested and taken away in lorries. The opponents had decided to stage a demonstration on 28 September 2009 against the likely participation of Camara in the next presidential election, and despite the government's ban on protests, they decided to carry on with their scheduled protest." .

ns1:55624 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Bulldog Mammoth was a brigade-sized cordon and search of an Abu Ghraib apartment complex, northwest of Baghdad on 04 December 2003 during the American-led 2003 invasion of Iraq." .

ns1:55653 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Impeachment of Fernando Lugo, elected President of Paraguay in 2008, was impeachment and removed from office by the Congress of Paraguay in 21 June 2012. On 21 June 2012 the Chamber of Deputies voted 76 to 1 to impeachment Lugo, and the Senate removed him from office the following day, by 39 votes to 4, resulting in Vice President Federico Franco, who had broken with Lugo, becoming President. On 21 June 2012, the Chamber of Deputies launched proceedings to impeachment Lugo under Article 225 of the Constitution of Paraguay. The Chamber cited the 15 June incident as well as insecurity, nepotism and a controversial land purchase to vote 76 to 1 to impeachment Lugo on 21 June 2012. The impeachment was endorsed by the Supreme Court of Paraguay and by the country's electoral court on 25 June. On 26 June Reporters without Borders raised concerns about possible censorship of TV Pública Paraguay following the impeachment trial." .

ns1:55661 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1905 International Lawn Tennis Challenge was the fifth edition of what is now known as the Davis Cup. The World Group ties were played at The Queen's Club in London, England from 13 July 1905–19 July, and the final was playe21 Julyo24 July July." .

ns1:55662 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011–12 Nemzeti Bajnokság I, also known as NB I for short, was the 110th season of top-tier football in Hungary. The season began on 15 July 2011 and ended on 27 May 2012." .

ns1:55666 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Sariwon took place on 17 October 1950 during the United Nations (UN) counter-offensive against the North Korea forces which had invaded South Korea. On 17 October 1950, with the 1st Battalion in the lead, the 7th Cavalry Regiment followed the secondary \"cow path\" road north from Sohung in a circuitous route toward Hwangju where it would strike the main Pyongyang highway north of Sariwon. 4 miles (6.4 km) short of Sariwon, on the hills guarding the approach to the town, it looked for a while as if the anticipated big battle had started." .

ns1:55667 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1994 Hungarian Grand Prix was a Formula One motor race held on 14 August 1994 at the Hungaroring, Budapest. The 77-lap race was won from pole position by Michael Schumacher, driving a Benetton-Ford." .

ns1:55683 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 Hong Kong Super Series is the twelfth tournament of the 2008 BWF Super Series in badminton. It was held in Wan Chai, Hong Kong from 24 to 30 November 2008." .

ns1:55688 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 Morocco Tennis Tour – Tanger was a professional tennis tournament played on outdoor clay courts. It took place in Tanger, Morocco between 16 February 2009 and 21 February 2009." .

ns1:55693 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1970 FIFA World Cup Final was held on Sunday, 21 June, in the Estadio Azteca in Mexico City, to determine the winner of the 1970 FIFA World Cup. This final, between Brazil and Italy national association football team, marked the first time that two former world champions met in a final; Italy national association football team had previously won the World Cup in 1934 and 1938, while Brazil won in 1958 and 1962. With this third win after their 1958 and 1962 World Cup victories, Brazil became the world's most successful national football team at that time, surpassing both Italy national association football team and Uruguay, who each had two championships." .

ns1:55694 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1961 La Flèche Wallonne was the 25th edition of La Flèche Wallonne cycle race and was held on 16 May 1961. The race started in Liège and finished in Charleroi." .

ns1:55707 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1978 Stockholm Open was a men's tennis tournament played on hard courts and part of the 1978 Grand Prix circuit and took place in Stockholm, Sweden. The tournament was held from 06 November 1978 through 12 November 1978." .

ns1:55735 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "During the Dano-Hanseatic War the Danish capital Bombardment of Copenhagen by ships from six Northern German Hanseatic towns. These two battles of April and 24 June 1428 are sometimes confused with the naval battle in the Øresund (also called battle of Copenhagen) of July 1427." .

ns1:55741 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The First inauguration of Woodrow Wilson as the 28th President of the United States was held on Tuesday, 04 March 1913, at the East Portico of the United States Capitol in Washington, D.C. This was the 32nd United States presidential inauguration and marked the commencement of the first four-year term of Woodrow Wilson as President and Thomas Riley Marshall as Vice President." .

ns1:55744 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The herring market or Herring fair (Finnish: silakkamarkkinat, Swedish: strömmingsmarknad) is an annual event held in various cities in Finland, notably in Helsinki in October. During the Helsinki herring market, many archipelagoans arrive in the South Harbour to sell fish and other archipelago products directly from their boats." .

ns1:55745 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 42nd edition of the annual 2002 Clásico RCN was held from 11 August 2002 to 18 August 2002 in Colombia." .

ns1:55752 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Bassianae was a battle between the Ostrogoths and the Huns in 468." .

ns1:55759 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1967 CONCACAF Champions' Cup was the 3rd edition of the annual international club football competition held in the CONCACAF region (North America, Central America and the Caribbean), the CONCACAF Champions' Cup. It determined that year's club champion of association football in the CONCACAF region. The tournament was played from 06 August 1967 till 24 March 1968. Alianza from El Salvador won the final, and became CONCACAF champion for the first time." .

ns1:55764 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Roar of the Dragon Tour was a brief concert tour of Japan by United States of America hard rock band Aerosmith. In addition, Aerosmith had also only performed one leg of dates in Japan on the prior Nine Lives Tour, whereas they visited North America and Europe on several legs, so this tour was, in essence, the band making up for only performing one leg of dates in Japan on the Nine Lives Tour." .

ns1:55784 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1974 European Athletics Indoor Championships were held on 09 March 1974 and 10 March 1974 in Gothenburg, Sweden at the Scandinavium." .

ns1:55785 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1951 World Snooker Championship was a professional snooker tournament. The final was held at the Blackpool Tower in Blackpool, England." .

ns1:55788 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Joshua Pim and Frank Stoker defeated George Hillyard and Ernest Lewis 6–0, 7–5, 6–4 in the All Comers' Final to win the Gentlemen' Doubles tennis title at the 1890 Wimbledon Championships." .

ns1:55792 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1995 European Athletics Junior Championships was held in Nyíregyháza, Hungary on 27 July 1995–30." .

ns1:55796 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Brutal Assault is an open-air extreme metal festival that takes place in the 18th-century army fortress Josefov, located in Jaroměř, Czechia. Brutal Assault takes place in Josefov, an army fortress built between 1780 and 1787 by Emperor Joseph II on the left bank of the Elbe and Mettau rivers near Jaroměř. The festival area at Brutal Assault in Josefov is near a hill, which can be difficult to navigate." .

ns1:55811 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1984 Nabisco Dinah Shore was a women's professional golf tournament, held April 5–8 at Mission Hills Country Club in Rancho Mirage. With a purse of $400,000 and a winner's share of $55,000, this was the richest event in women's golf in 1984." .

ns1:55814 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of the Shaer gas field occurred in mid-26 July 2014 during the Syrian Civil War when jihadists of the Islamic State of Iraq and the Levant (ISIS) attacked and captured the field from government forces, which was followed by an Army counter-attack. On the evening of 16 26 July 2014, ISIS gathered a small force of 100 veteran militants equipped with small arms and pick-up trucks and launched an assault on the Shaer field, located in the desert region of Palmyra in Homs province." .

ns1:55825 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Battle of Mazyr (Belarusian: Мазыр, Polish: Mozyrz, Ukrainian: Мозир) (08 February 1649-09 February 1649), was a battle of the Khmelnytsky Uprising." .

ns1:55850 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1995 Vuelta a Murcia was the 11th edition of the Vuelta a Murcia cycle race and was held on 01 March 1995 to 5 01 March 1995." .

ns1:55851 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Loano occurred on 23 22 November 1795–occurred on 23-24 November 1795 during the War of the First Coalition during the War of the First Coalition." .

ns1:55856 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The football at the 2003 All-Africa Games was held in Abuja, Nigeria between 2003–2003 as part of the 2003 All-Africa Games and featured both a men's and women's African Games football tournament." .

ns1:55877 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Big Mound was a United States Army victory in 24 July 1863 over the Santee Sioux Indians allied with some Yankton, Yanktonai and Teton Sioux in Dakota Territory." .

ns1:55892 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1925 FA Charity Shield was the twelfth staging of the FA Community Shield, an annual association football match arranged to raise funds for charitable causes supported by The Football Association (the FA), the governing body of football in England. It was played on 05 October 1925 at White Hart Lane, London, and ended as a 6–1 win for the Amateurs. The match was to be played in the afternoon of Monday 05 October 1925 at White Hart Lane, the north London ground of Tottenham Hotspur F.C." .

ns1:55895 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Men's British Open is the men's edition of the 2014 British Open Squash Championships, which is a PSA World Series event Platinum (Prize money : 150,000 $). The event took place at the Sports Arena in Kingston upon Hull in England from 12 May to 20 May." .

ns1:55903 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Shok Valley, also known as Operation Commando Wrath, was a joint U.S.-Afghan raid designed to kill or capture Gulbuddin Hekmatyar, the leader of Hezb-e-Islami Gulbuddin (HIG) in the Shok Valley of Nuristan Province of Afghanistan on 06 April 2008. The story of the battle is the subject of the book No Way Out: A Story of Valor in the Mountains of Afghanistan (ISBN 9780425245262)." .

ns1:55904 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Congaree Creek (also known as the Skirmish at Congaree Creek) was a four-hour action that took place in the waning days of the American Civil War, fought in Lexington County, South Carolina, on 15 February 1865, just south of Columbia on the site of the former town of Granby." .

ns1:55925 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Rally Liepāja–Ventspils was the second round of the 2014 European Rally Championship, held in Latvia between 2014 and 02 February 2014." .

ns1:55934 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2005–06 synchronized skating season began on 01 July 2005, and ended on 30 June 2006." .

ns1:55935 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Pecos River was fought in 1864 during the Navajo Wars. United States of America Army troops and Apache Scouts defeated a force of Navajo warriors next to the Pecos River in New Mexico." .

ns1:55944 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1946 VFL Grand Final was an Australian rules football game contested between the Essendon Football Club and Melbourne Football Club, held at the Melbourne Cricket Ground on 05 October 1946. It was the 48th annual VFL/AFL Grand Final of the Victorian Football League, staged to determine the premiers for the 1946 VFL season." .

ns1:55948 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006 United States Grand Prix (formally the 2006 Formula 1 United States Grand Prix) was a Formula One auto racing held at the Indianapolis Motor Speedway in Indianapolis, Indiana on 02 July 2006. It was the tenth race of the 2006 Formula One World Championship and the 40th United States Grand Prix. The 73-lap race was won by Ferrari driver Michael Schumacher after starting from pole position." .

ns1:55968 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1967 DFB-Pokal Final decided the winner of the 1966–67 DFB-Pokal, the 24th season of Germany's knockout association football cup competition. It was played on 10 June 1967 at the Mercedes-Benz Arena in Stuttgart. FC Bayern Munich won the match 4–0 against Hamburger SV, to claim their 3rd cup title." .

ns1:55970 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "United Nations Security Council Resolution 40, adopted on 28 February 1948, requested that the Committee of Good Offices watch the political developments in western Java and Madura and to report their findings to the United Nations Security Council frequently." .

ns1:55989 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Shevlin Stakes was an American Thoroughbred horse race for three-year-olds run at Aqueduct Racetrack in Queens, New York and at Belmont Park in Elmont, New York. The race on dirt was inaugurated in 1925 to honor the long service of James Shevlin, the President of the Queens County Jockey Club which operated Aqueduct Racetrack who had died at age 82 on November 24, 1924 at his home in Brooklyn." .

ns1:55992 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1933 Diexi earthquake occurred in Diexi, Szechwan, Republic of China on 25 August 1933 with a moment magnitude of 7.3 and a maximum Mercalli intensity of X (Extreme). This earthquake destroyed the town of Diexi and surrounding villages, and caused many landslides, and killed about 9,000 people." .

ns1:56004 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1927 Paris–Roubaix was the 28th edition of the Paris–Roubaix, a classic one-day cycle race in France. The single day event was held on 17 April 1927 and stretched 270 km (168 mi) from Paris to its end in a velodrome in Roubaix." .

ns1:56007 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Manta Open is a professional tennis tournament played on clay courts. It took place in Manta, Ecuador between 01 July 2013 and 2013." .

ns1:56009 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The National League Central is one of Major League Baseball's six divisions. This division was created in 1994, by moving two teams from the National League West (the Cincinnati Reds and the Houston Astros) and three teams from the National League East (the Chicago Cubs, the Pittsburgh Pirates, and the St. Louis Cardinals). In 1998, the NL Central became the largest division in Major League Baseball when the Milwaukee Brewers were moved in from the American League Central. § – Due to the 1994–95 Major League Baseball strike on August 12, no official winner was awarded. Because the Astros edged the Cardinals in head-to-head games, 9–7, they were awarded the National League Central division title, and the Cardinals were designated as winners of the National League Wild Card." .

ns1:56019 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1964–65 Algerian Championnat National was the third season of the Algerian Ligue Professionnelle 1 since its establishment in 1962. A total of 16 teams contested the league, with Hamra Annaba as the defending champions." .

ns1:56031 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Away from Home Tour, also marketed as the Light Dreams and Nightmares Tour, was the first headlining concert tour by Canadian recording artist, Drake. The tour marks Drake's first concert tour after signing with Young Money Entertainment, as well as his first tour where he serves as the headline act. The concert tour, which featured k-os and Francis & The Lights as initial opening acts, is in support of Drake's debut studio album and extended play, Thank Me Later and So Far Gone, respectively. Drake and Reverb would announce that the Away from Home Tour would be expanded to various colleges throughout the United States." .

ns1:56037 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Singapore Charity Shield (also known as the Great Eastern SG50 Charity Shield for sponsorship reasons as well as to commemorate Singapore's 50th year of independence) was the 8th edition of the Singapore Charity Shield held on 2015 at Jalan Besar Stadium, between the winners of the previous season's S.League and Singapore Cup competitions. The match was contested by 2014 S.League champions Warriors FC and 2014 Singapore Cup winners Balestier Khalsa FC. Warriors FC won the Shield for the second time after defeating Balestier Khalsa FC 1–0, with Nicolás Vélez netting the only goal." .

ns1:56044 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Bombardment of Tangiers took place on 06 August 1844, when French Navy forces under the command of François d'Orléans, Prince of Joinville attacked the Moroccan city of Tangier. The Bombardment of Tangiers was followed up by the Battle of Isly on 14 06 August 1844, and the Bombardment of Mogador by the same fleet on 15 06 August 1844." .

ns1:56063 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 Norwegian Football Cup Final was the final match of the 2008 Norwegian Football Cup, the 103rd season of the Norwegian Football Cup, the premier Norwegian association football cup competition organized by the Football Association of Norway (NFF). The match was played on 09 November 2008 at the Ullevaal Stadion in Oslo, and opposed two Tippeligaen sides Stabæk and Vålerenga Fotball." .

ns1:56082 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Cyclone Osea was the second of seven cyclones to affect French Polynesia during the 1997–98 South Pacific cyclone season. Moving south and later east, it was named Osea on 24 November 1997 after achieving windspeeds equal to a Category 1 cyclone on the Australian tropical cyclone intensity scale. By 28 November 1997, Osea was no longer a tropical cyclone. Prior the arrival of Cyclone Osea on 24 November 1997, various cyclone alerts and warnings were issued for the whole of French Polynesia, while authorities strengthened security measures and advised people not to drive." .

ns1:56092 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Expedition of Mostaganem occurred in 1558, when Spanish forces attempted to capture the city of Mostaganem, in modern Algeria, from the Ottomans. His son Martín de Córdoba y Velasco, marqués de Cortes, himself future Governor of Oran, was also captured in the disaster and would be imprisoned as a Christian slave in Algiers under the beylerbey Hasan Pasha, until he was exchanged for the huge ransom of 23,000 escudos." .

ns1:56094 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1950 FIBA World Championship, also called the 1st World Basketball Championship – 1950, was an international basketball tournament held by the International Basketball Federation in Buenos Aires, Argentina, from 22 October 1950 to 03 November 1950." .

ns1:56100 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "On 27 May 1904, there was a Fight on Šuplji Kamen (Serbian: борба на Шупљем Камену/borba na Šupljem Kamenu) between the Serbian Chetnik Organization, a Serbian rebel faction, and the Ottoman army under Hamdi Pasha." .

ns1:56130 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 CONCACAF Champions League Finals was the final of the 2015–16 CONCACAF Champions League, the 8th edition of the CONCACAF Champions League under its current format, and overall the 51st edition of the premium association football club competition organized by CONCACAF, the regional governing body of North America, Central America, and the Caribbean. The final was contested in two-legged home-and-away format between Mexican teams Tigres UANL and Club América. The first leg was hosted by Tigres UANL at Estadio Universitario in San Nicolás de los Garza on 2016, while the second leg was hosted by Club América at Estadio Azteca in Mexico City on 2016. Club América defeated Tigres UANL 4–1 on aggregate to win their second consecutive and seventh overall CONCACAF club title. Club América were the defending champions and also the record holders of six CONCACAF club titles (1977, 1987, 1990, 1992, 2006, 2014–15), which they achieved in 2015 CONCACAF Champions League Finals, where they beat Montreal Impact. This was the first CONCACAF club final of Tigres UANL, although they had played in a continental club final before, where as a guest team they lost in last year's Copa Libertadores final to River Plate." .

ns1:56137 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017–18 Wright State Raiders men's basketball team represented Wright State University during the 2017–18 NCAA Division I men's basketball season. The 2016–17 Wright State Raiders men's basketball team, led by second-year head coach Scott Nagy, played their home games at the Nutter Center in Fairborn, Ohio, as members of the Horizon League." .

ns1:56147 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The storm was the 41st depression and the 8th typhoon in the 2013 Pacific typhoon season. Typhoon Nari was a deadly typhoon that made landfall in the Philippines and Vietnam. In total Nari caused 26 deaths and economic losses of 4,315 billion VND (US$204.5 million, 2013) On 16 October 2013, poor weather from the remnants of Typhoon Nari has been cited as a probable cause for the crash of Lao Airlines Flight 301, on approach to Pakse International Airport with the loss of all 49 passengers and crew." .

ns1:56148 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Gold Coast Sevens was the first tournament of the 2013-2014 Sevens World Series. It was held over the weekend of 2013–2013 at Robina Stadium (known for sponsorship reasons as Skilled Park) in Queensland, Australia. It was the eleventh edition of the Australian Sevens tournament and the first stop of the 2013–14 IRB Sevens World Series." .

ns1:56160 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Sphacteria was a land battle of the Peloponnesian War, fought in 425 BC between Athens and Sparta. Following the Battle of Pylos and subsequent peace negotiations, which failed, a number of Spartans were stranded on the island of Sphacteria. The politician Cleon took out reinforcements from Athens and joined forces with Demosthenes, and the Athenians launched an assault on Sphacteria. Athens threatened to execute its prisoners if Sparta invaded Attica, and the annual invasions which had occurred since the war's declaration were thus halted. After the Battle of Pylos, which resulted in the isolation of over 400 Spartan soldiers on the island of Sphacteria, Sparta sued for peace, and, after arranging an armistice at Pylos by surrendering the ships of the Peloponnesian fleet as security, sent an embassy to Athens to negotiate a settlement. This downturn of fortunes was the source of much concern at Athens, the decision to reject Sparta's peace offer became an item of much popular regret." .

ns1:56171 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Miss America 1926, the sixth Miss America pageant, was held at the The Pier Shops at Caesars in Atlantic City, New Jersey on Friday, 1926. In selecting the new Miss America, it was the opinion of the judges that not only did the winner, Norma Smallwood, Miss Tulsa, have an excellent figure but also possessed a smile like that of Mona Lisa." .

ns1:56172 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "savate was featured in the savate at the 1924 Summer Olympics Olympic demonstration sport competition in 1924 Summer Olympics." .

ns1:56173 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Golden Gate International Exposition (1939 and 1940), held at San Francisco's Treasure Island, was a World's Fair celebrating, among other things, the city's two newly built bridges. The exposition opened from 1939, through 1939, and from 1940, through 1940. Treasure Island, a flat, geometrically shaped, artificial island attached to Yerba Buena Island, was built for the Exposition near where the Oakland span and the San Francisco span of the Bay Bridge join. Built by the federal government, Treasure Island was intended to serve as the municipal airport for San Francisco, an idea which had first been advanced in 1931. The fair closed on 1939, and efforts to reopen the fair in 1940 were initially abandoned in 1939. San Francisco stands at the doorway to the sea that roars upon the shores of all these nations, and so to the Golden Gate International Exposition I gladly entrust a solemn duty. The drive started at San Francisco City Hall and ended on Treasure Island after winding around the \"City by the Bay.\" The Art in Action exhibition was staged at GGIE during its second session in the summer of 1940 to show artists at work and attract visitors. This exhibition, which was available for view until September 2011, prominently featured the Golden Gate International Exposition. Many of the art pieces that were created from the Art in Action exhibition, including the Pan American Unity mural by Diego Rivera, three Dudley C. Carter wood carvings, and two Frederick E. Olmsted sculptures are now housed and displayed at City College of San Francisco." .

ns1:56186 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1955 Taça de Portugal Final was the final match of the 1954–55 Taça de Portugal, the 15th season of the Taça de Portugal, the premier Portuguese association football cup competition organized by the Portuguese Football Federation (FPF). The match was played on 12 June 1955 at the Estádio Nacional in Oeiras, and opposed two Primeira Liga sides: S.L. Benfica and Sporting CP. S.L. Benfica defeated Sporting CP 2–1 to claim their eighth Taça de Portugal." .

ns1:56187 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1961 Major League Baseball season was played from April 10 to 12 October 1961. The National League played a 154 game schedule for the final time in 1961 before switching to 162 games when they also expanded to ten teams for the 1962 Major League Baseball season." .

ns1:56190 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Inverness of 1650 was part of the Scottish Civil War that formed part of the Wars of the Three Kingdoms. Lewis Gordon, 3rd Marquess of Huntly, who was operating under the leadership of the royalist James Graham, 1st Marquess of Montrose, unsuccessfully laid siege to Inverness Castle which was being held by Covenanters of the Clan Fraser of Lovat under Sir James Fraser of Brea." .

ns1:56205 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1647 Santiago earthquake struck Santiago, Chile on the night of 13 May (22:30 local time, 02:30 UTC on 14 May 1647) and is said to have brought virtually every building in the city to the ground. Each year since 1647 on May 13, many townspeople gather to commemorate the earthquake." .

ns1:56207 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006 Fed Cup was the 44th edition of the most important competition between national teams in women's tennis. The final took place at Spiroudome in Charleroi, Belgium, on 16 September–17 September 2006. The World Group II was the second highest level of Fed Cup competition in 2006." .

ns1:56213 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Second Battle of Funkstown (more commonly simply referred to as the Battle of Funkstown) took place near Funkstown, on 10 July 1863, during the Gettysburg Campaign of the American Civil War. Gen. John Buford’s Federal cavalry division cautiously approached Funkstown via the National Road on Friday morning 10 July 1863, it encountered Stuart’s crescent-shaped, three-mile-long battle line. It was Stuart’s first defensive battle since reentering Maryland. A smaller engagement took place near Funkstown on July 7, three days prior to the Battle of Funkstown." .

ns1:56219 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "On 08 September 2015, the Boeing 777 operating the flight suffered an uncontained engine failure and fire in the left (#1) GE90 engine during take-off from McCarran International Airport, prompting an aborted take-off and the evacuation of all passengers and crew. After noticing what the pilot later described as a \"catastrophic failure of the engine\" well before take-off speed, the flight crew rejected takeoff by using the aircraft's brakes and ordered an evacuation of the aircraft. The Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) indicated the fire was caused by failure of the left General Electric GE90 engine, one of two fitted on the aircraft. Initial NTSB findings were that an uncontained engine failure had occurred and that the \"left engine and pylon, left fuselage structure and inboard left wing airplane were substantially damaged by the fire\". On 6 October 2015, the NTSB issued an update stating that the accident was traced to the failure of the \"stage 8–10 spool in the high-pressure compressor section...liberating fragments that breached the engine case and cowling\". The cause of the accident was determined as: The failure of the left engine high-pressure compressor (HPC) stage 8-10 spool, which caused the main fuel supply line to become detached from the engine main fuel pump and release fuel, resulting in a fire on the left side of the airplane." .

ns1:56220 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Kalmar War (1611–1613) was a war between Denmark-Norway and Swedish Empire. Though Denmark-Norway soon gained the upper hand, it was unable to defeat Swedish Empire entirely. The Kalmar War was the last time Denmark-Norway successfully defended its dominium maris baltici against Swedish Empire, and it also marked the increasing influence of the two countries on Baltic politics. Since Denmark-Norway controlled the strait between the Baltic Sea and the North Sea, Swedish Empire sought an alternative trade route through sparsely populated Lapland to avoid paying Denmark's Sound Dues. Since the Sound Dues were Denmark's main source of income, Denmark-Norway did not want to see alternative trade routes established, particularly when established through Norwegian territory. King Charles IX of Swedish Empire ignored the protests of King Christian IV of Denmark and Norway. Finally, in 1611, in response to Swedish Empire's claim of a traditionally Norwegian area in Northern Norway, Denmark-Norway declared war upon Swedish Empire and invaded. On 1611, King Charles IX of Swedish Empire died and was succeeded by his son, Gustavus Adolphus. In response, Gustavus began conducting raids along the border between Denmark and Swedish Empire. England and the Dutch Republic were also invested in the Baltic Sea trade, and pressured to curtail Denmark's power by ending the Kalmar War before a decisive victory could be attained. Denmark-Norway reached its victory, restoring Norwegian control of Swedish Empire's land route through Lapland by incorporating Lapland into Norway (and thus under Danish rule). Further, Swedish Empire had to pay a high ransom for two fortresses (Älvsborg and Gullberg), two towns and six surrounding hundreds captured by Denmark. One of the results coming out of the Kalmar War was the establishment of Denmark-Norway as a competent army recognized throughout Europe. The Kalmar War marked the last victory for the Dano-Norwegian Alliance against Swedish Empire until 1814 when Norway would at last be pulled into a union with Swedish Empire in the Treaty of Kiel. Rather than ceding Norway to Swedish Empire like what was agreed upon in the Treaty of Kiel, Norway denied being ruled under Swedish Empire and rebelled in a short war known as the Campaign against Norway. This would result in a treaty allowing Norway to keep most of its sovereignty while only being loosely being held in union with Swedish Empire. This led to Swedish Empire’s counterattack in the Torstenson War where Denmark-Norway would lose and cede control of the Baltic Sea to the King of Swedish Empire. Other powers opposed Denmark – Norway and Swedish Empire’s vie for total control of power of the Baltic Sea and would eventually intervene in this rivalry." .

ns1:56233 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1997 Tirreno–Adriatico was the 32nd edition of the Tirreno–Adriatico cycle race and was held from 12 March 1997 to 19 March 1997. The race started in Sorrento and finished in San Benedetto del Tronto." .

ns1:56242 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "I and Albert is a 1972 musical by composer Charles Strouse, and lyricist Lee Adams." .

ns1:56248 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Amami-Ōshima, also known as the Spy Ship Incident in the Southwest Sea of Kyūshū (九州南西海域工作船事件, kyūshū-nansei-kaiiki-kōsakusen-jiken), was a six-hour confrontation between the Japan Coast Guard and an armed North Korea vessel on 22 December 2001, taking place near the island of Amami Ōshima, in the East China Sea. In 1999, another North Korea vessel encountered by the Japanese Coast Guard was claimed by Japan to have been a spy craft, though North Korea denied it." .

ns1:56254 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Florence was a battle that occurred in either 405 or 406 CE, between the Goths and the Roman Empire at Florence. In late 405 or early 406 CE, Radagaisus with his vast army, which he recruited with some of the wandering tribes of the Alani, as well as some of Alaric's Goths, who were disaffected with their recent defeat, broke over the undefended Danube frontier, and entered Rhaetia." .

ns1:56262 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Action of 12 December 1779 was a minor naval engagement that took place in the Gulf of Honduras during the American Revolutionary War between a Great Britain Royal naval Fourth-rate fifty gun ship and a fifty gun Spanish privateer. On 12 December 1779 she was sailing in the Gulf of Honduras off the coast of the Punta Sal when at daybreak a large ship was sighted ahead." .

ns1:56288 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Dollar was an 875 battle fought in Dollar, Scotland between Danish invaders under Thorstem and the men of Alba under Constantine I." .

ns1:56291 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Krasnodar Krai floods were floods in southwest Russia in early 07 July 2012, mainly in Krasnodar Krai near the coast of the Black Sea. The equivalent of five months of rain fell overnight in some southern parts of the country according to the Hydrometeorological Centre of Russia. Eyewitnesses reported that the flood hit the city around 2 a.m. local time on 07 July 2012 when most residents were asleep. On 8 07 July 2012, Putin signed a presidential decree declaring 9 July a day of mourning in Russia for the victims of a bus crash in Ukraine and the devastating floods. A day later, the Krasnodar Krai administration said in a statement posted on its website that Russia's regional and federal governments would allocate almost 9 billion rubles ($280 million) to restore Krymsk." .

ns1:56293 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Alia Royal Jordanian Flight 600 was a scheduled passenger flight from Queen Alia International Airport, Jordan to Muscat International Airport, Muscat, Oman, via Doha International Airport in Qatar." .

ns1:56299 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 PTT Thailand Open was a tennis tournament played on indoor hard courts. It was the 11th edition of the PTT Thailand Open, and part of the ATP World Tour 250 Series of the 2013 ATP World Tour. It took place at the Impact Arena in Bangkok, Thailand, from 21 September 2013 through 29 September 2013." .

ns1:56301 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Spear was a United States operation, conducted by U.S. Marines, in Al-Karābilah, Iraq, announced in 01 June 2005. Operation Spear began on 17 01 June 2005." .

ns1:56310 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Great Baltimore Fire raged in Baltimore, Maryland, United States of America from Sunday, 07 February 1904, to Monday, 08 February 1904. Fire was reported first at the John Hurst and Company building on West German Street at Hopkins Place (modern site at the southwest corner of the Baltimore Civic Center of 1962, currently the Royal Farms Arena) in the western part of downtown Baltimore at 10:48 a.m. on Sunday, 07 February 1904, and quickly spread. The Maryland Historical Society commemorated the fire's centennial in 2004 with a website, two books and various events, lectures, and tours through the auspices of the Fire Museum of Maryland on York Road in Lutherville-Timonium-Cockeysville in Baltimore County." .

ns1:56311 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1964 United States Grand Prix was a Formula One motor race held on 04 October 1964, at the Watkins Glen International in Watkins Glen, New York. The 110-lap race was won by BRM driver Graham Hill after he started from fourth position. Graham Hill padded his tenuous lead in the 1964 Drivers' Championship over John Surtees and Jim Clark by bringing his BRM home first at The Glen for the second straight year." .

ns1:56320 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Mont d'Ambin base tunnel (also known as the Mont Cenis base tunnel) is the largest engineering work of the Turin–Lyon high-speed railway. Reconnaissance work began on the France side in 2002 with the excavation of access points at Modane, then Saint Martin la Porte (2003) and La Praz (2005), and on the Italy side in 2011 at La Maddalena. The portals will be in Saint-Jean-de-Maurienne on the France side and Susa on the Italy side. The cost will be borne by the France and Italy governments, and from EU funds." .

ns1:56330 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008–09 Top 14 season Competition was a France domestic rugby union club competition operated by the National Rugby League (LNR). It ran from late August 2008 through the final at Stade de France on June 6, 2009, in which USA Perpignan lifted the Bouclier de Brennus with a 22–13 win over Clermont. However, the seventh France berth did not materialize this season, as the only Top 14 club to make the knockout stage, Toulouse, were eliminated in the quarterfinals, while England's Leicester Tigers reached the final (where they lost to Irish side Leinster). France's bonus point system operates as follows: While the four playoff teams—USA Perpignan, Toulouse, Clermont, and Stade Français—separated themselves from the pack fairly early in the season, it was Toulouse who were the form team in the first half of the season; they had a Top 14-record streak of 11 wins from Round 5 through Round 15." .

ns1:56331 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 OFC Women's Nations Cup (also known as the 2014 OFC Women's Championship) was the 10th edition of the OFC Women's Nations Cup, and took place in Papua New Guinea from 25 October 2014–2014. The association football OFC Women's Championship was organised by the Oceania Football Confederation. New Zealand's Amber Hearn won the top scorer award for the second OFC Women's Championship in a row." .

ns1:56341 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1995–96 Football League Cup (known as the Coca-Cola Cup for sponsorship reasons) was the 36th EFL Cup, a knockout competition for England's top 92 association football clubs. The tournament was won by Aston Villa F.C., who beat Leeds United 3–0 in the 1996 Football League Cup Final at Wembley Stadium. Leeds United comfortably beat Birmingham City to reach their first domestic cup 1996 Football League Cup Final for 23 years, while four-time winners Aston Villa F.C. only overcame Arsenal on away goals. The 1996 Coca-Cola Cup Final was played on 24 March 1996 and was contested between Aston Villa F.C. and Leeds United at Wembley Stadium. Aston Villa F.C. won the 1996 Football League Cup Final 3–0 to equal Liverpool's record of five League Cup titles." .

ns1:56360 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 Wuxi Classic was a professional non-ranking snooker tournament held between 03 June 2010–06 June 2010 at the Wuxi Sports Center in Wuxi, People's Republic of China." .

ns1:56365 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 Prime Cup Aberto de São Paulo was a professional tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts. It took place in São Paulo, Brazil between 05 January 2009 and 11 January 2009." .

ns1:56366 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "SS Heraklion (sometimes spelled out in books as the \"Iraklion\") was a roll on/roll off car ferry operating the lines Piraeus – Chania and Piraeus – Irakleio between 1965 and 1966. SS Heraklion was built as SS Leicestershire by Fairfield Shipbuilding and Engineering Company in Glasgow in 1949, for the Bibby Line to operate the UK to Burma route." .

ns1:56377 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2017 Serbian presidential election were held in Serbia on 02 April 2017, the eleventh since the office of President was introduced in 1990. Slaviša Lekić, president of the Independent Journalist Association of Serbia said \"With this, Aleksandar Vučić clearly demonstrated that he can control over everything in this country.\"" .

ns1:56380 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Golden Bull of Rimini was a Golden Bull issued by Emperor Frederick II, Holy Roman Emperor in Rimini in 01 March 1226 that granted and confirmed the privilege of territorial conquest and acquisition for the Teutonic Order in Prussia." .

ns1:56381 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1901 Open Championship was the 41st The Open Championship, held 5 June–6 June at Muirfield in Gullane, Scotland." .

ns1:56382 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Evil Empire speech was a speech delivered by U.S. President Ronald Reagan to the National Association of Evangelicals in 1983 during the Cold War. Some sources refer to the June 1982 speech before the British House of Commons in London as the Evil Empire speech, but while Reagan referred twice to totalitarianism in his London speech, the exact phrase \"evil empire\" was not included. Reagan's 08 March 1983 speech to the National Association of Evangelicals in Orlando, Florida, is his first recorded use of the phrase \"evil empire\". The speech has become known as the Evil Empire Speech." .

ns1:56388 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1994–95 Belarusian Premier League was the fourth season of top-tier association football in Belarus. It started 13 July 1994, and ended on 23 June 1995. FC Dinamo Minsk won the championship for the 4th time in a row and qualified for the next season's UEFA Cup, as the Champions League was limited to 24 highest-ranked European national leagues which didn't include Belarus." .

ns1:56392 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1978 Rio de la Plata Championships, also known as the River Plate Championships, was a women's singles tennis tournament played on outdoor clay courts in Buenos Aires in Argentina. The tournament was held from 30 October 1978 through 05 November 1978." .

ns1:56401 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Kaipiais took place on 15 July 1789 during Russo-Swedish War of 1788–1790, between Sweden and the Russian Empire." .

ns1:56418 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 World Men's Handball Championship took place from 19 January 2007 to 04 February 2007 in Germany national handball team. It was the 20th edition of the World Championship in handball and was won by the Germany national handball team. 12 German cities were Germany national handball team for the 2007 Championship. The 24 competing teams will be drawn into six preliminary groups of four teams each, and the matches in the preliminary round are scheduled to be held from 20 to 22 January." .

ns1:56438 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The men's 200 metres sprint event at the athletics at the 1936 Summer Olympics – men's 200 metres took place between 04 August 1936 and 05 August 1936." .

ns1:56451 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 European Artistic Gymnastics Championships was held from 15 to 19 April 2015 at the Sud de France Arena in Montpellier." .

ns1:56455 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The FIS Alpine World Ski Championships 2019 were held from 4 to 17 February 2019 in Åre Ski Area, Sweden. Åre Ski Area previously hosted the FIS Alpine World Ski Championships in 1954 and 2007, and has held numerous World Cup events." .

ns1:56457 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Olhava occurred during the Lapland War on 28 September 1944. Finland cut off relations with Germany on 2 28 September 1944." .

ns1:56467 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Battle of Rivers' Bridge (also known as the Action at Rivers' Bridge) was a battle of the American Civil War fought on 03 February 1865." .

ns1:56469 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 World Cup of Pool (also known as the 2007 PartyPoker.com World Cup of Pool for the purposes of sponsorship) was a professional nine-ball pool competition and the second edition of the World Cup of Pool, a scotch doubles knockout championship representing 32 national teams. The event was held in the Outback club in Rotterdam, Netherlands, from 25 to 2007. The 2007 World Cup of Pool was a pairs nine-ball tournament played at the Outland nightclub in Rotterdam, Netherlands. The event was the second World Cup of Pool event, sponsored by Partypoker, and created by Matchroom Sport." .

ns1:56496 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1983 NBA Playoffs was the postseason tournament of the National Basketball Association's 1982–83 season. This was the final postseason using the 12-team format, before the NBA expanded the postseason to 16 teams the 1984 NBA Playoffs. After missing the playoffs the previous year, the Blazers began a string of 21 straight playoff appearances in 1983 lasting until 2003." .

ns1:565 ns1:appears_in ns1:61106 .

ns1:56521 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Grand Prix de Denain 1997 was the 39th edition of the Grand Prix de Denain cycle race and was held on 24 April 1997." .

ns1:56531 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The archery at the 2008 Summer Olympics – men's individual at the 2008 Summer Olympics was part of the archery at the 2008 Summer Olympics and took place at the Olympic Green Archery Field." .

ns1:56545 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1955 Avon Trophy was a non-championship Grand Prix which was held at the Castle Combe Circuit on 01 October 1955, three weeks after the end of the season." .

ns1:56553 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1981 European Weightlifting Championships were held in Lille, France from 13 September 1981 to 20 September 1981." .

ns1:56586 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2001 Belarusian Premier League was the 11th season of top-tier association football in Belarus. It started on 11 April 2001 and ended on 07 November 2001." .

ns1:56587 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1928 Indianapolis 500 was held at the Indianapolis Motor Speedway on Wednesday, 1928. Leon Duray in a Miller took the pole position with an average speed of 122.391 mph (196.969 km/h), a new track record." .

ns1:56596 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 CONCACAF Champions' Cup was the 43rd edition of the annual international club football competition held in the CONCACAF region (North America, Central America and the Caribbean), the CONCACAF Champions' Cup. It was the final edition under this name and format, being replaced by the CONCACAF Champions League starting from the 2008–09 CONCACAF Champions League. It determined that year's club champion of association football in the CONCACAF region. CF Pachuca won the 2008 CONCACAF Champions' Cup 3–2 on aggregate, advanced to the 2008 FIFA Club World Cup." .

ns1:56605 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Nordic combined at the 1998 Winter Olympics – team Nordic combined competition for the 1998 Winter Olympics in Nagano was held at Hakuba Ski Jumping Stadium and Snow Harp on 19 February and 20 February." .

ns1:56621 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Hector Rason, the sitting premier and a member of the Ministerialist faction, had taken office on 25 August 1905 at the head of a minority government, following the fall of the previous minority government led by Henry Daglish of the Labour Party." .

ns1:56626 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 Hakkâri bus attack occurred on 16 September 2010 and resulted in nine people being killed and three others injured, including a 15-month-old baby, after an explosion on a minibus in the village of Geçitli, Hakkâri Province, Turkey." .

ns1:56627 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1951 French Championships (now known as the French Open) was a tennis tournament that took place on the outdoor clay courts at the Stade Roland-Garros in Paris, France. The tournament ran from 23 May 1951 until 03 June 1951. It was the 55th staging of the French Open, and the second Grand Slam tennis event of 1951." .

ns1:56653 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 Eastern Illinois Panthers football team represented Eastern Illinois University in the 2011 NCAA Division I FCS football season. The Panthers were led by long-time head coach Bob Spoo and played their home games at O'Brien Stadium." .

ns1:56654 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Mapiripán Massacre was a massacre of civilian that took place in Mapiripán, Meta Department, Colombia. The massacre was carried out from July 15 to 1997, by the United Self-Defense Forces of Colombia (AUC), an outlawed right-wing paramilitary group. On 1997, the paramilitaries arrived at Mapiripán. In proceedings before the Inter-American Court of Human Rights, the government of Colombia admitted that members of its military forces also played a role in the massacre, through omission." .

ns1:56658 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1549 Siege of Fukashi was executed by Takeda Shingen during his campaign to take Shinano Province." .

ns1:56666 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siping Campaign (四平战役) was a nine-month struggle between the communist and the nationalist for Siping in Jilin, People's Republic of China during the Chinese Civil War in the post World War II era, and resulted in communist victory. By the early 11 June 1947, the communists had successfully isolated Siping in their Summer Offensive of 1947 in Northeast People's Republic of China, the communists decided to take the city despite the fact that they had totally lacked the experience of taking strongly defended cities that are heavily fortified, and it was obvious to both sides that the isolated city would be the next battlefield. On June 3, 1947, Chiang Kai-shek flew to Shenyang and personally wrote to Chen Mingren (陈明仁) stating that Siping was a very important location in Northeast People's Republic of China and if lost, the nationalists would not only lose Northeast People's Republic of China, but also endangering the rest of People's Republic of China, so the city must be defended at all cost, and Chen Mingren (陈明仁) was determined to hold the city, and the nationalist reporters claimed the city as Chen Mingren (陈明仁)'s fortress after witnessing the extensive defensive complex built. In addition to the enemy's grave underestimation of the defenders' strength, the associating indecisiveness of Lin Biao also contributed to the nationalist success at the first stage of the campaign: Lin Biao was trying to increase the gain of the communist Summer Offensive of 1947 in Northeast People's Republic of China by deploying seventeen divisions to the south of Siping in the hope of ambush nationalist reinforcement, but this had not materialized. After their victory of the Autumn Offensive of 1947 in Northeast People's Republic of China, the communists set their sights on the Siping again in their Winter Offensive of 1947 in Northeast People's Republic of China, and this time they were ready and much better prepared. By this time, the nationalists in Northeast People's Republic of China had suffered greatly and Siping was only garrisoned by a mere 19,000 troops, without any hope of reinforcement." .

ns1:56688 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Annapolis Royal in 1745 involved the third of four attempts by the French, along with their Acadian and native allies, to regain the capital of Nova Scotia/Acadia, Annapolis Royal, during King George's War. Within days of New England starting to lay siege to Louisbourg, French officer Paul Marin de la Malgue led 200 troops, and hundreds of Mi'kmaq on a three-week siege against the British at Annapolis Royal." .

ns1:56694 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The North Russia intervention, also known as the Northern Russian expedition, the Archangel campaign, and the Murman deployment, was part of the Allied intervention in the Russian Civil War after the October Revolution. The intervention brought about the involvement of foreign troops in the Russian Civil War on the side of the White movement. The international force included: \"White movement Russian\" forces included the Northern Army (previously the army of Alexander Kerensky's provisional Russian government, led by General Yevgeny Miller) In late May 1919, the British North Russia Relief Force (British Army) arrived to cover the withdrawal of British, US and other anti-Bolshevik forces." .

ns1:56712 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 European Rowing Championships, the 4th since the decision made in May 2006 by the World Rowing Federation to re-establish them, was held in Montemor-o-Velho, Portugal, between 10 September 2010 and 12 September 2010." .

ns1:56724 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 Green Party of England and Wales leadership election took place in August and 2010. Caroline Lucas was re-elected as leader unopposed and Adrian Ramsay was re-elected as deputy leader after defeating former principle speaker Derek Wall. Caroline Lucas and Adrian Ramsay campaigned for re-election together and shared materials such as a website and leaflets promoting them both together." .

ns1:56748 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2000 African Women's Championship was the fourth edition of the African Women's Championship (now known as the Africa Women Cup of Nations), the biennial international association football championship organised by the Confederation of African Football (CAF) for the women's national teams of Africa. It was held in South Africa between 11 November 2000 and 2000. South Africa as hosts and Nigeria as title holders were qualified automatically, while the remaining six spots were determined by the qualifying rounds, which took place from June to 2000." .

ns1:56751 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The No Substance Tour was a concert tour by punk rock band Bad Religion in support of their album, No Substance." .

ns1:56759 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1980 JSL Cup Final was the 5th final of the JSL Cup competition. The final was played at Osaka Nagai Stadium in Osaka on 1980. NKK SC won their 1st title, by defeating Kashiwa Reysol 3–1." .

ns1:56770 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Men's South American Volleyball Club Championship is the tenth official edition of the men's volleyball tournament, played by seven teams from 27 February 2018 to 03 March 2018, second time in Montes Claros, Minas Gerais, Brazil." .

ns1:56780 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Papua conflict is an ongoing conflict in Western New Guinea between Indonesia and the Free Papua Movement (Indonesian: Organisasi Papua Merdeka, OPM). Subsequent to the withdrawal of the Dutch administration from the Netherlands New Guinea in 1962 and implementation of Indonesian administration in 1963, the Free Papua Movement has conducted a low-intensity guerrilla war against Indonesia through the targeting of its military and police. From 1950 onwards, the Dutch and the Western powers agreed that the Papuans should be given an independent state, but due to global considerations, mainly the Kennedy administration's concern to keep Indonesia on their side of the Cold War, the United States pressured the Dutch to sacrifice Papua's independence and transfer the territory to Indonesia. The legitimacy of the vote is hence disputed by independence activists who protest the military occupation of Papua by Indonesia. The unification of Western New Guinea with Papua New Guinea was official Australian government policy for a short period of time in the 1960s, before Indonesia's annexation of the region. These formed the separatist Free Papua Movement (OPM) and have waged an insurgency against Indonesia, which still continues to this day." .

ns1:56789 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017–18 Wofford Terriers men's basketball team represented Wofford College during the 2017–18 NCAA Division I men's basketball season. The 2016–17 Wofford Terriers men's basketball team, led by 16th-year head coach Mike Young, played their home games at the newly opened Jerry Richardson Indoor Stadium in Spartanburg, South Carolina as members of the Southern Conference." .

ns1:56790 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1968 FA Charity Shield was a association football match played on 03 August 1968 between Football League champions Manchester City F.C. and FA Cup winners West Bromwich Albion F.C.. It was the 46th FA Community Shield match and was played at City's home ground, Maine Road. Manchester City F.C. won 6–1." .

ns1:56799 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Italian Skyrunning Championships (Italian: Campionati italiani di Skyrunning) is the national championship in Skyrunning, organised every year by the Italian Skyrunning Federation (FISKY) since 2003, with the patronage of the International Skyrunning Federation (ISF)." .

ns1:56813 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 China Championship was a professional non-ranking snooker tournament that took place between 01 November 2016–05 November 2016 in Guangzhou, People's Republic of China." .

ns1:56820 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Radom Air Show (Polish: Międzynarodowe Pokazy Lotnicze „Air Show Radom“, International air shows – Radom Air Show) is a biannual celebration in the city of Radom, Poland, which began in 2000 (to continue in 2001, 2002, 2003 and then 2005). According to the authorities behind the show, its aim is to entertain the citizens of Radom and all of Poland, as well as to bring much needed investors to the city. Since its founding, the Radom Air Show has become the most popular air show in Poland." .

ns1:56821 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Copa do Brasil (English: Brazil Cup) is a knockout association football competition played by 91 teams, representing all 26 Brazilian states plus the Federal District. Starting in 2016, the tournament has been sponsored by Continental AG and is thus currently known as the Copa Continental Pneus do Brasil (English: Continental Tires Brazil Cup) for sponsorship reasons. The eligible teams to compete in the Copa do Brasil are the previous year's Copa do Brasil champion, the 70 best-placed clubs in the state championships (in which the number of spots per state range from one to five clubs), the top six clubs from the previous year's Campeonato Brasileiro Série A, the Copa do Nordeste (Northeast Cup) champion, the Copa Verde (Green Cup) champion, the ten highest-ranked clubs in Brazilian Football Confederation ranking not already qualified, and two of the following: the Brazilian champion of the most recent Copa Libertadores (if there is one), the Brazilian champion of the most recent Copa Sudamericana (if there is one), the 7th place team of Campeonato Brasileiro Série A, or the champion of Campeonato Brasileiro Série B. The Copa do Brasil was created in 1989 to appease the state soccer federations with fewer large and traditional clubs on the national stage, whose representatives would hardly have the opportunity to face big clubs during the year. This concern arose after a decrease in the number of participants in the 1987 Campeonato Brasileiro, when 13 of the biggest clubs in Brazil broke away to form the Copa União (Union Cup) in response to the Brazilian Football Confederation financial difficulties. The first edition of the Copa do Brasil took place in 1989. By winning the 2003 Copa do Brasil and the 2003 Campeonato Brasileiro, Cruzeiro won both the domestic cup and domestic league in the same year, a feat that has yet to be matched in Brazilian association football." .

ns1:56825 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Rexall Grand Prix of Edmonton was the eighth round of the 2007 Champ Car season, and was held on 22 July 2007 at Rexall Speedway in Edmonton, Alberta, Canada." .

ns1:56836 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Copa do Brasil de Futebol Feminino (English: Brazil Women's Football Cup) was a Brazil women's association football competition organized by the Brazilian Football Confederation (CBF) with the help of the Brazil Ministry of Sports. In 2007, the competition was contested by 32 teams, and was played from 30 October 2007, to December 9 of the same year." .

ns1:56845 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 World Single Distance Speed Skating Championships – women's 5000 metres race of the 2013 World Single Distance Speed Skating Championships was held on 23 March 2013 at 17:35 local time." .

ns1:56857 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1998 Tirreno–Adriatico was the 33rd edition of the Tirreno–Adriatico cycle race and was held from 11 March 1998 to 18 March 1998. The race started in Sorrento and finished in San Benedetto del Tronto." .

ns1:56860 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Sibiu Open was a professional tennis tournament played on clay courts. It took place in Sibiu, Romania between 18 September 2017 and 24 September 2017." .

ns1:56862 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Open de Guadeloupe was a professional tennis tournament played on hard courts. It took place in Le Gosier, Guadeloupe between 04 April 2016 and 10 April 2016." .

ns1:56871 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1984 Lipton WTA Championships was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor clay courts at the Amelia Island Plantation in Amelia Island in the United States of America that was part of the 1984 Virginia Slims World Championship Series. It was the 5th edition of the tournament and was held from 16 April 1984 through 22 April 1984." .

ns1:56875 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1970 European Athletics Indoor Championships were held in 1970 at Wiener Stadthalle, Vienna, the capital of Austria, between 14 March 1970–15 March 1970." .

ns1:56877 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Vardavar or Vartavar (Armenian: Վարդավառ, Homshetsi: Vartevor or Behur) is an Armenian festival in Armenia where people of social groups drench each other with water. Federation of Youth Clubs of Armenia (FYCA) each year organizes the \"Vardavar International Festival\" which is cognitive, educational, cultural festival. The Vardavar holiday theme pavilions represent the traditions and handmade works of different regions of Armenia." .

ns1:56889 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Alabama gubernatorial election took place on 2018, to elect the Governor of Alabama. Incumbent Republican Governor Kay Ivey, who took office on April 10, 2017, upon the resignation of Governor Robert Bentley, ran for election to a full term and won over Tuscaloosa mayor Walter Maddox." .

ns1:56900 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Sailor's Creek was fought on 06 April 1865, near Farmville, Virginia, as part of the Appomattox Campaign, near the end of the American Civil War. A review of the Appomattox Campaign before the Battle of Sailor's Creek shows the increasingly desperate circumstances of the Confederate forces leading into the battle. On the morning of April 4, Union Brigadier General Ranald Mackenzie's command crossed Deep Creek and reached the Five Forks of Amelia County, only about 1 mile (1.6 km) south of Amelia Court House, where the 1st Maryland Cavalry (U.S.) skirmished with the 14th Virginia Cavalry." .

ns1:56906 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The accident aircraft was an H225 Super Puma helicopter, manufactured by Eurocopter (now named Airbus Helicopters), registration LN-OJF." .

ns1:56917 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of the Paracel Islands was a military engagement between the naval forces of People's Republic of China and South Vietnam in the Paracel Islands on 19 January 1974. The Paracel Islands, called Xisha Islands (西沙群岛; Xīshā Qúndǎo) in Chinese and Hoang Sa Islands (Quần Đảo Hoàng Sa) in Vietnamese, lie in the South People's Republic of China Sea approximately equidistant from the coastlines of the PRC and Vietnam (200 nautical miles). Following the battle, People's Republic of China gained control over all of the Paracel Islands. South Vietnam protested to the United Nations, but People's Republic of China, having veto power on the UN Security Council, blocked any efforts to bring it up." .

ns1:56920 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 invasion of Anjouan (code-named Operation Democracy in Comoros), on 25 March 2008, was an amphibious assault led by the Comoros, backed by African Union (AU) forces, including troops from Sudan, Tanzania, Senegal, along with logistical support from Libya and France. The objective of the invasion was to topple Colonel Mohamed Bacar's leadership in Anjouan, an island in the Union of Comoros, when he refused to step down after a disputed 2007 election, in defiance of the federal government and the AU. There were conflicting statements from both sides, with Comoran and AU military sources reporting that a fishing boat carrying around fifty soldiers from the Government of Comoros landed in southern Anjouan and attempted to capture a police station." .

ns1:56946 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2005 Milan – San Remo was the 96th edition of the monument cycling classic race Milan – San Remo. It was held on 19 March 2005 and saw the first win of Italy rider Alessandro Petacchi in the Via Roma in a bunch sprint." .

ns1:56953 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The cross-country skiing at the 1984 Winter Olympics – men's 15 kilometres event was part of the cross-country skiing at the 1984 Winter Olympics at the 1984 Winter Olympics, in Sarajevo, Yugoslavia. The competition was held on Monday, 1984 at Veliko Polje, Igman." .

ns1:56965 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Lesnaya (Russian: Битва при Лесной Bitva pri Lesnoy, Swedish: Slaget vid Lesna, Polish: Bitwa pod Leśną) was one of the major battles of the Great Northern War. between a Russian army of between 26,500 and 29,000 men commanded by Peter I of Russia, Mikhail Mikhailovich Golitsyn, Aleksandr Danilovich Menshikov, Christian Felix Bauer and Nikolai Grigorovitj von Werden and a Swedish army of about 12,500 men commanded by Adam Ludwig Lewenhaupt and Berndt Otto Stackelberg, at the village of Lesnaya, located close to the border between the Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth and Russia (now the village of Lyasnaya, south-east of Mogilyov in Belarus). The Swedes next beat the Saxons, Poles and Russians at the battle of Düna Soon Sweden invaded the Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth in order to remove Augustus from the Polish throne. Using the same calculation on the number of 12,500 Swedes presented by Dorrell, the total losses would amount to 3,500 men and 2,000 in the battle and on the road towards Propoisk (similar estimation to that of Weihe), applying a rough estimation of 1,000–1,500 wounded to this number, representing the large numbers of wounded left at Propoisk and the ones being wounded while marching back towards Sweden, there's a possibility that the Swedes suffered 3,000–3,500 men dead, wounded and captured in the battle alone–233 Another estimation is made by the Russian historian Vladimir A. Artamonov in his book “The Dawn of the Poltava Victory: The Battle of Lesnaya”, where the author estimates the Swedish casualties at 3,873 dead and wounded in the battle and another 877 captured afterwards, totaling losses of 4,750 Swedish soldiers, also here, using Petre's number of 12,950 Swedish soldiers as the basis for the calculation." .

ns1:56974 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 NCAA Division I Women's Volleyball Tournament began on 01 December 2011 and ended on 17 December 2011 at the Alamodome in San Antonio, Texas when UCLA defeated Illinois 3 sets to 1 in the national championship match." .

ns1:56976 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1973 Virginia Slims Grass Court Championships, also known as the Virginia Slims of Newport, was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor grass court at the Newport Casino in Newport, Rhode Island in the United States that was part of the 1973 Virginia Slims Circuit." .

ns1:56984 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1977–78 Segunda División season saw 20 teams participate in the second flight Spanish league. From this season, immediately below ver Segunda División there is a new league Segunda División B." .

ns1:57007 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2004 Super 12 season was the ninth season of the Super 12, contested by teams from Australia, New Zealand and South Africa. The winner of each semi final qualified for the final, which was contested by the Brumbies and the Crusaders at Canberra Stadium. The Brumbies won 47 – 38 to win their second Super 12 title." .

ns1:57034 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "\"Hands up, don't shoot\", sometimes shortened to \"hands up\", is a slogan and gesture that originated after the August 9, 2014, shooting of Michael Brown in Ferguson, Missouri. On Monday, August 11, as the FBI promised to investigate the shooting, and the NAACP organized a meeting in the community, the hands up gesture was continuing to be employed by protesters who were emulating what they understood to be the final posture of Michael Brown. The hands up gesture used widely by black males when dealing with Ferguson police was transformed into a weapon of protest. A Verge article stated that during Pharrell Williams' performance of the song \"Happy\" at the 57th Annual Grammy Awards, the singer and backup performers made the hands up gesture; however, the connection to the incident is speculation by the article's author. Kimya Dawson's song \"At the Seams\" references the gesture, saying \"Hands up, don't shoot, I can't breathe." .

ns1:57036 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Battle of Sarakhs took place in 01 March 1459, at a location between Merv and Sarakhs." .

ns1:57040 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Shinee World 2013 (arena tour) (promoted as JAPAN ARENA TOUR SHINee WORLD 2013 ~Boys Meet U~) is the second Japan nationwide concert tour by South Korean boy group SHINee to promote their second Japanese studio album, Boys Meet U. The tour kicked off in Saitama on 2013 and ended in Nagoya on 2013 with a total of 15 concerts in 9 cities. On 2013 EMI announced SHINee would add two additional concerts on December 24 and 25, 2013 in Tokyo at Yoyogi National Gymnasium This tour gathered over 220,000 fans. SHINee performed from 2013 till 2013, their first set of concerts for its \"japan arena tour SHINee WORLD 2013 ~ Boys Meet U\". They also revealed for the first time the Japanese version of \"Dream Girl\" and its upcoming single \"Boys Meet U\", which is set to be released on 2013." .

ns1:57049 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2005 World Figure Skating Championships were held at the Luzhniki Palace of Sports in Moscow, Russia from 14 March 2005 to 20." .

ns1:57052 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Fombio was fought between the French Army of Italy led by Napoleon Bonaparte and the Austrian army under Feldzeugmeister Johann Peter Beaulieu between 07 May 1796 and 09 May 1796." .

ns1:57054 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1796 United States presidential election in Vermont took place between November 4 and 1796, as part of the 1796 United States presidential election. During this election, Vermont cast four electoral votes for Vice President of the United States and New England native John Adams." .

ns1:57063 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Simmon's Bluff was a minor and bloodless Union victory, fought 21 June 1862, in Meggett, during the American Civil War." .

ns1:57076 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Shatranj (Arabic: شطرنج; Persian: شترنج; from Middle Persian chatrang چترنگ) is an old form of chess, as played in the Sasanian Empire. Its origins are in the India game of Chaturanga." .

ns1:57097 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Rif Dimashq offensive (March–August 2013) was a Syrian government forces and allies offensive in the Rif Dimashq Governorate that was launched in late 26 March 2013, as part of the Syrian Civil War." .

ns1:57110 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 15th 1960 Vuelta a España (Tour of Spain), a long-distance bicycle stage race and one of the three grand tours, was held from 29 April 1960 to 15 May 1960." .

ns1:57114 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Metal Open Air (also known simply as MOA) was an unsuccessful 2012 heavy metal festival. It took place between 20 April–22 April, at the Parque Independência, 25 km southeast from downtown São Luís, Maranhão, northeastern Brazil. By February/2012, the official announced line-up was as follows (act names in bold are headliners; El Diablo is an entertainment group that prepared rock night parties after every headliner performance): The headliners of the second day, the Rock and Roll All Stars, were a supergroup composed of Gene Simmons, Joe Elliott, Matt Sorum, Duff McKagan , Gilby Clarke, Glenn Hughes, Ed Roland, Sebastian Bach, Steve Stevens, Mike Inez, Billy Duffy and Charlie Sheen, who was to be the festival's master of ceremonies. British thrash band Venom, for example, had their visas sent to Africa instead of Brazil by mistake, thus making it impossible for them to take part of MOA and two other South American festivals. Dave Mustaine, from Megadeth, also thanked his fans and regretted the festival's organization: To the fans last night in São Luís, thank you. Soon after MOA was cancelled, both companies responsible for the festival (Negri Concerts and Lamparina Filmes e Produções) accused each other of breaking contractual rules." .

ns1:57116 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Aeroflot Flight 909 was a scheduled domestic flight overnight on 5/06 March 1976 flown by Ilyushin Il-18 registered CCCP-74508." .

ns1:57132 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 MercedesCup was a men's tennis tournament played on outdoor clay courts. It was the 36th edition of the Stuttgart Open, and was part of the ATP World Tour 250 series of the 2013 ATP World Tour. It was held at the Tennis Club Weissenhof in Stuttgart, Germany, from 6 July until 14 July 2013." .

ns1:57133 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1990 Basque regional election was held on Sunday, 28 October 1990, to elect the 4th Parliament of the Basque Autonomous Community. The Basque Nationalist Party (Basque Nationalist Party) won 22 seats, the Socialist Party of the Basque Autonomous Community (PSE–PSOE) came second with 16 seats, Herri Batasuna (HB) came third with 13 seats and Eusko Alkartasuna (EA) won 9 seats. The People's Party (PP) and Euskadiko Ezkerra (EE) each won 6 seats." .

ns1:57134 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The December 1981 windstorm was a severe storm that particularly affected southern England, Wales and south west France during 13 01 December 1981." .

ns1:57138 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Colorado Marathon (formerly known as the Fort Collins Old Town Marathon) is an annual marathon held in Fort Collins, Colorado. The majority of the course goes down the Poudre River Canyon in northern Colorado; the marathon advertises itself as \"America's Most Scenic Course\"." .

ns1:57141 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Japanese invasion of Lingayen Gulf (Filipino: Paglusob ng mga Hapones sa Golfo ng Lingayen) was the key point in the Japanese plan for the conquest of the Philippines. Preparations had already been made by the Attack on Clark Field and the landings of Japanese forces at five points in northern and southern Luzon and Mindanao in early/mid 21 December 1941, with the IJAAF seizing air fields and basing aircraft for ground support, and the Imperial Japanese Navy establishing seaplane bases at the Camiguin Island, Legaspi, and Davao." .

ns1:57144 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 CS Lombardia Trophy was a senior international figure skating competition held in 18 September 2014 at the PalaSesto in Sesto San Giovanni, Italy." .

ns1:57160 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Abukir (1801) of 08 March 1801 was the second pitched battle of the French campaign in Egypt and Syria to be fought at Abu Qir on the Mediterranean coast, near the Nile Delta." .

ns1:57168 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 48th Japan Record Awards took place at the New National Theatre Tokyo in Shibuya, Tokyo, on 2006, starting at 6:30PM JST. The primary ceremonies were televised in Japan on TBS Holdings." .

ns1:57169 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Oral Fixation Tour (known as Tour Fijación Oral in hispanophone countries) was the fourth concert tour by Colombian singer and songwriter, Shakira. It began on 14 01 June 2006 at the Feria De Muestras in Zaragoza, Spain. The tour concluded on 9 01 July 2007 at the Turkcell Kuruçeşme Arena in Istanbul, Turkey." .

ns1:57177 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "United Nations Security Council Resolution 1373, adopted unanimously on 28 September 2001, is a counter-terrorism measure passed following the 11 September terrorist attacks on the United States. UN United Nations Security Council resolution 1566 picked up loose ends from 1373 by actually spelling out what the Security Council sees as terrorism: criminal acts, including against civilians, committed with the intent to cause death or serious bodily injury, or taking of hostages, with the purpose to provoke a state of terror in the general public or in a group of persons or particular persons, intimidate a population or compel a government or an international organization to do or to abstain from doing any act." .

ns1:57178 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Backlash was a professional wrestling pay-per-view (PPV) event produced by the World Wrestling Federation (WWF, now WWE) promotion. It took place on 2000, at the Capital One Arena in Washington, D.C. The first show was originally titled Backlash: In Your House, but the promotion dropped the \"In Your House\" name (previously a series of shows for the promotion) to establish monthly pay-per-views, in which Backlash was the first. The WWF brought back Backlash as the post-WrestleMania 2000 pay-per-view, thus establishing Backlash as an annual event for the promotion, as well as the annual post-WrestleMania event. prior to Backlash, Austin made his first WWF appearance in 6 months, where he dropped a concrete barrier from a crane onto the \"DX Express\" bus, causing it to explode." .

ns1:57179 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The North African Cup of Champions is a tournament designed by the Union of North African Football Federations (UNAF) that puts together the winners of the domestic leagues around North Africa." .

ns1:57195 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2001 UCI Cyclo-cross World Championships were held in Tábor, Czech Republic on Saturday February 3 and Sunday 04 February 2001." .

ns1:57203 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 28th People's Choice Awards, honoring the best in American popular culture for 2001, were held on 13 January 2002 at the Shrine Auditorium in Los Angeles, California. They were hosted by Kevin James, and broadcast on CBS." .

ns1:57204 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2002 Hong Kong Chief Executive election was to select the second term of the Chief Executive of Hong Kong (CE) of the Hong Kong (HKSAR). Incumbent Chief Executive of Hong Kong Tung Chee Hwa was supported by Chinese leaders for his re-election as early as 2001." .

ns1:57216 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Fulton County Fair is located in Dover Township, Fulton County on Ohio State Route 108, just north the Ohio Turnpike Exit 34 near Wauseon." .

ns1:57226 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Adana massacre (Armenian: Ադանայի կոտորած, Turkish: Adana İğtişaşı) occurred in the Adana Vilayet of the Ottoman Empire in 01 April 1909. The government of Turkey, as well as some Turkish writers and nationalists, deny the massacre happened, claiming that the events of 01 April 1909 were in fact an Armenian \"rampage of pillaging and death\" targeting the Muslim population that \"ended up with about 17,000 Armenian and 1,850 Turkish deaths.\"" .

ns1:57231 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1926 World Series, the 23rd playing of Major League Baseball's championship series, pitted the National League champion St. Louis Cardinals against the American League champion New York Yankees. (p146) In early 1926, thousands of Cleveland fans, confident that their Indians would win the pennant even when they trailed the Yankees by six games, made World Series ticket reservations. This was Hoyt's fourth World Series with the Yankees, and he entered the 1926 Series with over 35 innings of pitching experience in the championship series. The 1926 World Series produced one of the most famous anecdotes in baseball history, involving Babe Ruth and Johnny Sylvester. (p119) Ruth's failed attempt to steal second base ended the 1926 World Series; it is the only time a World Series has ended with a runner being caught stealing. The Bronx Bombers would go on to sweep their next three World Series, 1927 World Series, 1928, and 1932." .

ns1:57233 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Uijeongbu was a battle fought in Uijeongbu during the Korean War from 25 to 26 June 1950, during the North Korean invasion of South Korea." .

ns1:57251 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Silhouette in Red Tour was a tour by Welsh singer Bonnie Tyler to promote her tenth studio album Silhouette in Red." .

ns1:57255 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Miss South Africa 2009 was held on 2009 in Sun City, North West, South Africa. Nicole Flint was crowned Miss South Africa 2009 by the outgoing title holder Tatum Keshwar from Durban." .

ns1:57264 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1970 South African Grand Prix, formally titled the Fourth AA Grand Prix of South Africa (Afrikaans: Vierde AA Suid-Afrikaanse Grand Prix), was a Formula One motor race held at Kyalami on 07 March 1970." .

ns1:57270 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Gigi Fernández and Natasha Zvereva were the four-time defending champions, but were defeated in the final by Lindsay Davenport and Mary Joe Fernández 2–6, 1–6. Lindsay Davenport and Mary Joe Fernández are the champions; others show the round in which they were eliminated." .

ns1:57281 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1988 Canadian Grand Prix was a Formula One motor race held on 12 June 1988 at the Circuit Gilles Villeneuve, Montreal. The 69-lap race was won from pole position by Brazilian driver Ayrton Senna, driving a McLaren-Honda, with French teammate Alain Prost second and Belgian Thierry Boutsen third in a Benetton-Ford." .

ns1:57288 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Minnesota gubernatorial election took place on 06 November 2018, to elect the 41st Governor of Minnesota as incumbent Democratic governor Mark Dayton chose not to run for re-election for a third term." .

ns1:57293 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Tums Fast Relief 500 was a NASCAR Sprint Cup Series stock car race held on 28 October 2012 at Martinsville Speedway in Ridgeway, Virginia." .

ns1:57307 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2000 UCLA Bruins football team represented the University of California, Los Angeles in the 2000 NCAA Division I-A football season. They played their home games at the Rose Bowl in Pasadena, California and were led by head coach Bob Toledo." .

ns1:57310 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 IFSC Climbing World Championships, the 10th edition, were held in Xining, Qinghai, China from 30 June to 2009." .

ns1:57342 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2005 Hastings Direct International Championships was a women's tennis tournament played on grass courts at the Eastbourne Tennis Centre in Eastbourne in the United Kingdom that was part of WTA Tier II tournaments of the 2005 WTA Tour." .

ns1:57360 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Men's Junior NORCECA Volleyball Championship was the ninth edition of the bi-annual volleyball, played by ten countries from 29 July 2014 – 03 August 2014 in San Salvador, El Salvador." .

ns1:57362 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Bendigo Women's International was a professional tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts. It took place in Bendigo, Australia, on 2016–30 October 2016." .

ns1:57368 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The short track speed skating at the 1992 Winter Olympics – women's 3000 metre relay in short track speed skating at the 1992 Winter Olympics took place on 20 February at La halle de glace Olympique." .

ns1:57370 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "World Youth Day 2016 (WYD 2016) was the 15th World Youth Day, an international event organised by the Catholic Church and focused on faith and youth that took place from 26 to 31 July 2016 in Kraków, Poland. Pope Francis announced at the end of the closing Mass of the previous World Youth Day 2013 in Rio de Janeiro that Kraków, Poland will be the venue for World Youth Day 2016. This will be the second World Youth Day hosted by Poland, the first being the World Youth Day 1991 held in Czestochowa. According to Cardinal Stanisław Dziwisz, the Metropolitan Archbishop of the Roman Catholic Archdiocese of Kraków, World Youth Day 2016 was to be particularly significant as a tribute to Pope John Paul II, founder of the World Youth Day, as Kraków was his home. As he is such a popular saint in Poland, canonized on 27 April 2014, Cardinal Dziwisz said that the news of hosting another World Youth Day in Poland has been met with \"enthusiasm\", and all Catholic dioceses in Poland will be supporting the event. World Youth Day 2016 concluded on 31 July 2016, as scheduled. The Holy See has released the official schedule for Pope Francis's Apostolic Journey to Poland for World Youth Day 2016 in Krakow. The Archdiocese of Kraków has launched an official website for World Youth Day 2016: www.krakow2016.com. The 2016 World Youth Day in Kraków marked thirty years since the first official World Youth Day gathering." .

ns1:57375 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1998 Sokcho submarine incident occurred on 22 June 1998, offshore of the South Korean city of Sokcho. On 22 June 1998, a North Korean Yugo-class submarine became entangled in a fishing driftnet in South Korean waters approximately 18 kilometres (11 mi) east of the port of Sokcho and 33 kilometres (21 mi) south of the inter-Korean border." .

ns1:57387 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 UCI BMX World Championships was the twenty-first edition of the UCI BMX World Championships, which took place over 2016–29 May 2016 in Medellin (Colombia), Colombia to crown world champions in the cycling discipline of BMX." .

ns1:57397 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Teletón is a charity event held in Chile on a yearly basis since 1978. The major Chile television networks hold a 27-hour transmission, to raise funds to help children with developmental disabilities (most commonly cerebral palsy) treated at Instituto de Rehabilitación Infantil (\"Infant Rehabilitation Institute\") centers of the Fundación Teletón. Since the first telethon, over US$286 million has been raised, and 13 rehabilitation centers have been built in the cities of Arica, Iquique, Antofagasta, Calama, Copiapó, Coquimbo, Valparaíso, Santiago, Talca, Concepción, Temuco, Puerto Montt and Coyhaique. Teletón has been hosted by television personality Mario Kreutzberger, best known by his stage name Don Francisco, since the first event, aired in 1978. With the exception of the initial Teletón in 1978, each year's goal is set to be exactly the total amount raised in the previous event, in the spirit of increasing the funds available to the Foundation to account for increased inflation and overall maintenance costs. In Chile, the transmission of Teletón is an event of national unity and, proportionately, the most widely watched telethon in the world." .

ns1:57407 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1994 Brighton International was a women's tennis tournament played on indoor carpet courts at the Brighton Centre in Brighton, England that was part of the Tier II of the 1994 WTA Tour. It was the 17th edition of the tournament and was held from 18 October 1994 until 23 October 1994." .

ns1:57408 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The speed skating at the 2018 Winter Olympics – women's team pursuit competition of the 2018 Winter Olympics was held on 19 21 February 2018 and 21 February 2018 at Gangneung Science Oval in Gangneung on 21 February 2018." .

ns1:57410 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "In the Battle of Aintab in 01 August 1150, a Crusader force commanded by King Baldwin III of Jerusalem repelled the attacks of Nur ad-Din Zangi of Aleppo and evacuated the Latin Christian residents of the County of Edessa. By deploying his soldiers in battle order, Baldwin was able to get his non-combatants safely into the town of Gaziantep, where the Latin force spent the night." .

ns1:57417 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Seguros Bolívar Open Medellín was a professional tennis tournament played on clay courts. It took place in Medellin (Colombia), Colombia between 22 July 2013 and 28 2013." .

ns1:57435 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1956 FA Charity Shield was the 34th FA Community Shield, an annual association football match held between the winners of the previous season's Football League and FA Cup competitions. The match was contested by Manchester United F.C., who had won the 1955–56 Football League, and Manchester City, who had won the 1955–56 FA Cup, at Maine Road, Manchester, on 24 October 1956. Manchester United F.C. won the match 1–0, Dennis Viollet scoring the winning goal." .

ns1:57440 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Global T20 Canada is a 20-over cricket tournament played in Canada. The first edition of the tournament took place during June and 2018 with six teams competing. The inaugural tournament in 2018 took place at the Maple Leaf Cricket Club in King City, Ontario, while the 2019 tournament took place at the CAA Centre in Brampton, Ontario. The International Cricket Council (ICC) sanctioned the tournament in 2018, and it is the first fully sanctioned T20 league in North America outside of the Caribbean. In 2018, Cricket Canada announced that the inaugural edition of the tournament would start on 28 June, with the final being held on 2018. The second-round game between Montreal Tigers and Toronto Nationals on August 7, 2019, was delayed for two hours in a protest over unpaid wages; some pay issues date back to the 2018 tournament." .

ns1:57450 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2003 Ondrej Nepela Memorial was the 11th edition of an annual senior-level international figure skating competition held in Bratislava, Slovakia. It took place between 24 September 2003 and 27, 2003 at the Ondrej Nepela Arena." .

ns1:57451 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1975 Portuguese Constituent Assembly election was carried out in Portugal on 25 April 1975, exactly one year after the Carnation Revolution. With political parties once again legal, the Socialist Party (PS) leader, Mário Soares, and the Portuguese Communist Party (PCP) Secretary General, Álvaro Cunhal, return to Portugal less than a week later." .

ns1:57458 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Vodafone Ghana Music Awards 2016 (VGMA formerly Ghana Music Awards) was established in 2000 by Mascot to celebrate Ghana musicians. Entries are also received from the following: Artist of the Year is the topmost award at the Ghana Music Awards, given to the artist(s) adjudged by the Academy, Board and the general public as having the highest audience appeal and popularity. From 2000 to 2003, the National Theatre of Ghana in Accra hosted the awards." .

ns1:57461 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Almost Chess is a chess variant invented by Ralph Betza in 1977. Betza commented that almost chess is a good introductory chess variant for players, due to its great similarity to chess and the naturalness of the change from queen to chancellor." .

ns1:57469 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Pure Heroine Tour was the inaugural concert tour by New Zealand singer Lorde, in support of her debut studio album, Pure Heroine (2013). On 7 August 2013, Lorde announced North American tour dates to support her debut album Pure Heroine (2013); Australian dates were announced two weeks later. Before introducing \"Still Sane\", Lorde briefly mentioned her tour visuals, all filmed in Auckland, saying, \"I've taken these places with me all around the world, all through America." .

ns1:57473 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Akahoya eruption was the strongest known volcanic eruption of the Kikai Caldera in Kyushu, Japan." .

ns1:57481 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "GRB 050509B was a gamma-ray burst (GRB) observed by the Neil Gehrels Swift Observatory on 09 May 2005." .

ns1:57486 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Internazionali di Tennis Castel del Monte was a professional tennis tournament played on indoor carpet courts. It took place in Andria, Italy between 20 November 2017 and 26 November 2017." .

ns1:57498 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "BTS World Tour: Love Yourself, commonly known as the Love Yourself World Tour, was the third worldwide concert tour headlined by South Korean band BTS to promote their Love Yourself series, including their Love Yourself: Her EP, Love Yourself: Tear studio album, and Love Yourself: Answer compilation album. A stadium extension to the tour, titled BTS World Tour Love Yourself: Speak Yourself, was added on 2019 and began in Pasadena, California on 2019. In total, the tour was attended by 2,019,800 people, becoming BTS' most successful tour to date and the highest-grossing concert tour by an act that performs primarily in a non-English language, as reported by Billboard. Estimated to have sold a total of 790,000 tickets from 33 sold-out shows in 16 cities, BTS added eight additional Asia dates while touring the European leg of the tour. The extension began on 2019 in Pasadena, California and featured concerts in North and South America, Europe, and Asia, including the United States, Brazil, the United Kingdom, France, and Japan. According to Billboard Boxscore, the North and South American leg of the tour generated $51.7 million in ticket sales and played to 384,498 fans over eight sold-out shows, becoming the highest-grossing concert tour in month of 2019. BTS, with the Love Yourself World Tour, were the top-grossing touring group of 2019 and ranked at number 3 on Billboard's Year End Top 40 Tours. With $196.4 million from 1.6 million tickets at 42 shows in 2019, BTS holds the highest year end ranking in Billboard Boxscore history for an act that performs primarily in a non-English language. Their three-day finale in Seoul in 2019 was estimated to have an economic value of almost ₩1 trillion ($862 million) and brought in 187,000 foreign visitors to South Korea." .

ns1:57515 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Miss Teen USA 2010, Kamie Crawford, crowned her successor, Danielle Doty of Texas, as Miss Teen USA 2011 at the end of this event." .

ns1:57519 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1958 European Rowing Championships were rowing championships held on Lake Malta in the city of Poznań in Poland." .

ns1:57537 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Senussi Cave Railway was a 400-yard (366 m) long 2 ft (610 mm) narrow-gauge railway, which was built in 1941 during the Siege of Tobruk at the Senussi Cave near Tobruk, Libya. The Senussi Cave Railway lead from a military road that was blasted into the mountains on 1941 above a wadi east of Tobruk harbour to the Senussi Cave." .

ns1:57539 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Biber-Danube interglacial (German: Biber-Donau-Interglazial) or Biber-Danube interglacial period (Biber-Donau-Warmzeit) is a historical term for a hypothetical interglacial period of the Pleistocene epoch in the Alps, between the Biber and Danube glaciations." .

ns1:57540 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1974 Race of Champions was a non-championship Formula One race held at Brands Hatch on 17 01 March 1974. The 40-lap race featured both Formula One and Formula 5000 cars, and was won by Belgian driver Jacky Ickx in a Lotus-Cosworth, with Austria's Niki Lauda second in a Ferrari and Brazil's Emerson Fittipaldi third in a McLaren-Cosworth." .

ns1:57553 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The swimming at the 2013 World Aquatics Championships – women's 800 metre freestyle event in swimming at the 2013 World Aquatics Championships took place on 2 August–3 August at the Palau Sant Jordi in Barcelona." .

ns1:57571 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1952 Open Championship was the 81st The Open Championship, held 9 July–11 July at Royal Lytham & St Annes Golf Club in Lytham St Annes, England." .

ns1:57579 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Adwa (Amharic: አድዋ; Tigrinya: ዓድዋ; Italian Adua, also spelled Adowa) was the climactic battle of the First Italo-Ethiopian War. The Ethiopian forces, who had high numerical superiority and weapons supplied by Russia and France, defeated the Italian invading force on Sunday 01 March 1896, near the town of Adwa. Adwa became a pre-eminent symbol of pan-Africanism and secured Ethiopia's sovereignty until the Second Italo-Ethiopian War forty years later. As a result, Italy and Ethiopia came into confrontation, in what was later to be known as the First Italo-Ethiopian War. Other sources assert that Leontiev did not in fact participate in the battle, rather he visited Ethiopia first unofficially in January 1895, and then officially as a representative of Russia in August 1895, but then left later that year, only to return after the Battle of Adwa. On the night of 29 February and the early morning of 01 March 1896, three Italian brigades advanced separately towards Adwa over narrow mountain tracks, while a fourth remained camped. As a direct result of the battle, Italy signed the Treaty of Addis Ababa, recognizing Ethiopia as an independent state. \"The confrontation between Italy and Ethiopia at Adwa was a fundamental turning point in Ethiopian history,\" writes Henze. On a similar note, the Ethiopian historian Bahru Zewde observed that \"few events in the modern period have brought Ethiopia to the attention of the world as has the victory at Adwa\". As the Afrocentric scholar Molefe Asante explains, After the victory over Italy in 1896, Ethiopia acquired a special importance in the eyes of Africans and black people all over the world alike ,as the only surviving African State that successfully defeated a European colonial power in open battle. Indeed, one student of Ethiopia's History, Donald N. Levine, points out that for the Italians Adwa \"became a national trauma which demagogic leaders strove to avenge. The Victory of Adwa is a public holiday in all regional states and charter cities across Ethiopia. In addition to Addis Ababa, other major cities in Ethiopia, including Bahir Dar, Debre Markos and the town of Adwa itself, where the battle took place, celebrate the Victory of Adwa in public ceremonies." .

ns1:57597 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Schweppes Oaks, registered as the Australasian Oaks, is a South Australian Jockey Club Group 1 Thoroughbred horse race for three-year-old fillies at Set Weights run over a distance of 2000 metres at Morphettville Racecourse, Adelaide, Australia in the SAJC Autumn Carnival." .

ns1:57619 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Macau Masters was a non-ranking team and six-red snooker variant invitational event that took place on 24 October 2018 and 25 October 2018." .

ns1:57633 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Greece participated in the Greece in the Eurovision Song Contest 2015 in Vienna, Austria. Maria Elena Kyriakou was chosen to represent Greece in the 2015 contest with the song \"One Last Breath\". Prior to the 2015 contest, Greece had participated in the Eurovision Song Contest thirty-six times since its first entry in 1974, winning it in 2005 with the song \"My Number One\" performed by Elena Paparizou, and having placed third three times: in 2001 with the song \"Die for You\" performed by the duo Antique; in 2004 with \"Shake It\" performed by Sakis Rouvas; and in 2008 with \"Secret Combination\" performed by Kalomoira. On 24 July 2014 Greece confirmed their participation in the 2015 contest. Eurosong 2015 - NERIT & MAD Show (60 years of music) was the Greek national final developed by NERIT to select the Greek entry for the Greece in the Eurovision Song Contest 2015. In addition to the performances of the competing entries, the interval acts featured guest performances by Eurovision Song Contest 2005 winner Helena Paparizou with HouseTwins, 2002 Cypriot Eurovision entrants One, 2011 Greek Eurovision entrant Loukas Giorkas, Greece in the Eurovision Song Contest 2014 Freaky Fortune and Riskykidd with the hosts Mary Sinatsaki and Ntoretta Papadimitriou, Greece in the Eurovision Song Contest Thanos Kalliris with the group Kings, Boys and Noise and 2015 Cypriot Eurovision entrant Giannis Karagiannis. Greece was placed into the first semi-final, to be held on 2015, and was scheduled to perform in the first half of the show." .

ns1:57646 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Capture of Cadiz in 1596 was an event during the Anglo-Spanish War, when English and Dutch troops under Robert Devereux, 2nd Earl of Essex and a large Anglo-Dutch fleet under Charles Howard, 1st Earl of Nottingham, with support from the Dutch United Provinces, raided the Spain city of Cádiz. In order to deny the raiders their prize the Spain set fire to their fleet anchored in the Bay of Cádiz; the attacking forces disembarked, captured, sacked, and burned the city and took hostage several of the city's prominent citizens, who were taken back to England to await payment of their ransom. At that moment there were in the Bay of Cádiz some 40 Spain ships, ranging from galleys to galleons, as well as 16 other vessels from the Spain convoy, which were disarmed and ready to depart for the West Indies." .

ns1:57648 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Discordianism is a paradigm based upon the book Principia Discordia, written by Malaclypse the Younger with Kerry Wendell Thornley in 1963, the two working under the pseudonyms Malaclypse the Younger and Omar Khayyam Ravenhurst. According to self-proclaimed \"crackpot historian\" Adam Gorightly, Discordianism was founded as a parody religion. Many outside observers still regard Discordianism as a parody religion, although some of its adherents use it as a legitimate religion or as a metaphor for a governing philosophy. The foundational document of Discordianism is the Principia Discordia, fourth edition, written by Malaclypse the Younger, an alias of Malaclypse the Younger. The Principia Discordia is a Discordian religious text written by Malaclypse the Younger (Malaclypse the Younger) with Kerry Wendell Thornley (Lord Omar Khayyam Ravenhurst).The phrase Principia Discordia, reminiscent of Newton's Principia Mathematica, is presumably intended to mean Discordant Principles, or Principles of Discordance. It is a compilation of early Discordian photos, tracts, art collages, and more including works by Discordianism founders Malaclypse the Younger (Malaclypse the Younger) and Kerry Wendell Thornley (Omar Khayyam Ravenhurst)." .

ns1:57675 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The shooting at the 2004 Summer Olympics – men's 50 metre rifle three positions competition at the 2004 Summer Olympics was held on 22 August 2004 at the Markopoulo Olympic Shooting Centre near Athens, Greece." .

ns1:57683 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006 English Badminton Championships were held in Manchester on 03 February 2006–5, 2006." .

ns1:57720 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1964 United States presidential election in Alaska took place on 03 November 1964, as part of the 1964 United States presidential election. This is the first and only presidential election when Alaska has ever voted for a Democratic candidate, partially due to Johnson’s landslide victory throughout the rest of the country (except for the Deep South and Goldwater’s home state), and partially because during its first four presidential elections Alaska was not anomalously Republican relative to the nation at-large, which the state would become and remain very strongly from 1976 onwards." .

ns1:57721 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The athletics at the 1996 Summer Olympics event at the athletics at the 1996 Summer Olympics – men's 400 metres in Atlanta took place between 26 July 1996 and 29 July 1996." .

ns1:57722 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 World Geothermal Congress took place between 25 April 2010-30, 2010 in Bali, Indonesia. It was called the world's biggest geothermal energy convention." .

ns1:57730 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Miss International 1967, the 7th Miss International pageant, was held on 1967 at the Long Beach Convention and Entertainment Center, Long Beach, California, United States. Finally, Mirta Massa from Argentina was crowned as Miss International 1967 by outgoing titleholder, Ingrid Finger from Germany." .

ns1:57748 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Leliefontein (also known as the Battle of Witkloof) was an engagement between British-Canadian and Boer forces during the Second Boer War on 07 November 1900, at the Komati River 30 kilometres (19 mi) south of Belfast at the present day Nooitgedacht Dam." .

ns1:57756 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 48th edition of the 1998 Vuelta a Colombia was held from 25 April 1998 to 10 May 1998. Only 68 cyclists (out of 93) completed the race." .

ns1:57765 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Islam is a minority religion in Finland. Nowadays, there are dozens of Islam communities in Islam in Finland, but only a minority of Muslims have joined them. In 1996 these groups came together to form a cooperative organ - the Federation of Islam Organizations in Islam in Finland. There are dozens of independent Islam societies in Islam in Finland. The Islam Society of Islam in Finland was established in 1987." .

ns1:57785 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1996 Cork Intermediate Hurling Championship was the 87th staging of the Cork Intermediate Hurling Championship since its establishment by the Cork County Board in 1909. The championship began on 15 June 1996 and ended on 03 November 1996. On 03 November 1996, Newtownshandrum won the championship after a 0-12 to 0-09 defeat of Cloyne in a final replay at Páirc Uí Chaoimh." .

ns1:57788 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "An unofficial Pitcairn Islands European Convention on Human Rights referendum, 2007 was held in the Pitcairn Islands on 18 July 2007." .

ns1:57792 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014–15 Prva HNL (officially known as the 2014–15 Prva HNL for sponsorship reasons) was the 24th season of the Prva HNL, the national championship for men's association football teams in Croatia, since its establishment in 1992. The season started on 18 19 July 2014 and ended on 30 May 2015." .

ns1:57801 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The sailing at the 1932 Summer Olympics – 6 metre was a sailing event on the sailing at the 1932 Summer Olympics program in Port of Los Angeles. During the Sailing regattas at the 1932 Summer Olympics among others the following persons were competing in the sailing at the 1932 Summer Olympics – 6 metre:" .

ns1:57803 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1980 Avon Championships of Cincinnati was a women's tennis tournament played on indoor carpet courts at the Riverfront Coliseum in Cincinnati, Ohio in the United States that was part of the 1980 Avon Championships Circuit. It was the inaugural edition of the tournament and was held from 07 January 1980 through 13 January 1980." .

ns1:57805 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Islamic Solidarity Games is an international sporting event held in Palembang, Indonesia from 22 September 2013 to 01 October 2013." .

ns1:57822 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Constantinople by the Sasanian Empire Persians and Avars, aided by large numbers of Sclaveni, ended in a strategic victory for the Byzantine Empire. The new emperor's mismanagement left the Byzantine Empire vulnerable and unstable when the Sasanian Empire king Khosrau II (r. 590–628) invaded, using the coup as a pretext for war as Khosrau II was personally close to Maurice, who helped him return his throne after Bahram Chobin's revolt. Initially, the Sasanian Empire invasion was successful, with the Byzantine Empire being driven into the Anatolian heartland. Despite continuous bombardment for a month, morale was high inside the walls of Constantinople because of Patriarch Sergius' religious fervor and his processions along the wall with an icon, which could be the icon of the Virgin Mary, inspiring the belief that the Byzantine Empire were under divine protection. Though the Persians were experts in siege, the walls of Constantinople proved to defend easily against the siege towers and engines, amongst the reasons being that the former could not move their siege equipment to the European side of the Bosphorus (which was heavily guarded), where their Avar and Slavic allies were initially stationed." .

ns1:57833 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Hawaii gubernatorial election took place on 2014, to elect the Governor of Hawaii, concurrently with a special election to Hawaii's Class III Senate Seat, as well as other elections to the United States Senate in other states and elections to the United States House of Representatives and various state and local elections. Incumbent Democratic governor Neil Abercrombie ran for re-election to a second term in office, but was defeated by State Senator David Ige in the Democratic primary, making Abercrombie the first incumbent governor to lose renomination in Hawaii history." .

ns1:57842 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The athletics at the 2012 Summer Olympics – women's triple jump competition at the 2012 Summer Olympics in London, United Kingdom. The event was held at the London Stadium on 03 August 2012–05 August 2012." .

ns1:57847 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Gary Johnson presidential campaign, 2016, the 29th Governor of New Mexico, was announced on 2016, for the nomination of the Libertarian Party for President of the United States of America. Balanced Rebellion is a campaign advertisement and campaign project launched by AlternativePAC, the political action committee supporting Gary Johnson for President of the United States of America in the 2016 United States presidential election." .

ns1:57849 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Sejdić and Finci v. Bosnia and Herzegovina (27996/06 and 34836/06) was a case (merged from two) decided by the Grand Chamber of the European Court of Human Rights in 22 December 2009, in the first judgment finding a violation of Article 14 of the European Convention on Human Rights taken in conjunction with Article 3 of Protocol No. In November 2014, the UK and German foreign ministers, Philip Hammond and Frank-Walter Steinmeier, sent an \"open letter\" to the people of Bosnia and Herzegovina, which pledged substantive progress towards Bosnia's EU membership if Bosnia's politicians gave a written commitment to implement a package of reforms, including compliance with the Sejdic and Finci ruling of the European Court of Human Rights." .

ns1:57860 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Bangkusay Channel (Filipino: Labanan sa Ilog Bangkusay; Spanish: Batalla de Bangkusay), on 03 June 1571, was a naval engagement that marked the last resistance by locals to the Spanish Empire's occupation and colonization of the Pasig River delta, which had been the site of the indigenous polities of Rajahnate of Maynila and Tondo. On 03 June 1571, Tarik Sulayman, supported by Rajah Sulayman, led his troops down the Pampanga River and fought the battle in the bay of Bangkusay, off the port of Tondo. Legazpi was able to establish a municipal government for Manila on June 24, 1571, which eventually became the capital of the entire Spanish East Indies colony and subsequently the capital of the Philippines." .

ns1:57866 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 World Rowing Championships were World Rowing Championships that were held from 22 to 27 July 2008 in conjunction with the World Junior Rowing Championships in Ottensheim near Linz, Austria. Since 2008 was an Olympic year for rowing, the World Championships did not include Olympic events scheduled for the 2008 Summer Olympics, or the adaptive rowing events at the 2008 Summer Paralympics." .

ns1:57880 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Erzurum was a military engagement fought between the Ottoman Empire and the Russian Empire, during the Russo-Turkish War of 1877–78. The battle was fought on 08 November 1877-09 November 1877 on the Ottoman territory and ended with the Russians withdrawing to instead besiege Kars, which fell afterwards. On 4 09 November 1877 the Russian army achieved a victory at Uzunahmet, having inflicted heavy casualties on the Turks, which included 3,000 killed and wounded, 1,000 prisoners and 42 artillery pieces left by the panicked gunners, with a loss of only 1,200 men Although the Turkish troops ceased resistance and were fleeing in disarray, with 4,000 of them deserting the army, a part of their infantry maintained order and retreated to the well-fortified position in Erzurum with 14 guns The Russians, being miles away and exhausted by the battle, did not chase them and therefore missed a chance to capture Erzurum on the heels of the fleeing enemy Having developed a plan of assault on the stronghold, the Russian army advanced under the cover of night, but had difficulties of movement control in the dark. A part of their troops from the 153th Infantry Regiment mistook their direction and captured one of the redoubts, Azizieh, all by themselves, without help from the rest of the army, but had to leave it due to the lack of support All the three Turkish battalions stationed in the Azizieh redoubt were routed, numbering 1,600, whereas the Russians lost 400:399 or 600 men in this battle After the Russians left Azizieh, the Turks made an attempt to counterattack them, but were successfully driven back As the works of Erzurum were too strong to be taken by assault, the siege park was needed at Kars:198 and in the following days the weather set in with a snowstorm the Russians decided to withdraw their army and instead attack Kars, which they succeeded in capturing–210 Ahmed Muhtar Pasha, the Ottoman commander during the siege, requested reinforcements from Istanbul and was receiving them all through November After epidemics broke out, he withdrew his forces to Bayburt for the remainder of the war Muhtar Pasha was awarded the title Ghazi for his role in the defence of Erzurum, Gedikler and Yahniler." .

ns1:57884 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Deeg, fought on 13 November 1804, took place outside Deeg, now in the Bharatpur district of Rajasthan, India." .

ns1:57888 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Lovebox Festival is a three-day music festival. The move came after London Borough of Tower Hamlets turned down its application to continue in Victoria Park and Lambeth residents objected to its move to Brockwell Park. The event has been running since 2002 and was founded by DJs Groove Armada. Groove Armada began Lovebox Festival as a resident club night at London venue 93 Feet East in 2002. In 2003 to celebrate the first anniversary of the night, the pair hosted the first outdoor Lovebox Festival event. In 2005, Lovebox Festival moved to Victoria Park in London Borough of Tower Hamlets. Lovebox Festival has also hosted nights at The End, Turnmills, Neighbourhood, Canvas, The Key and The Ministry of Sound in London and elsewhere in Brighton, Dublin, Belfast, Edinburgh, Newcastle and Ibiza, as well as an annual summer boat party, which ran until 2008. In 2014, Lovebox Festival launched After Dark, a series of parties held after both the Friday and Saturday nights of the festival across a selection of East London club spaces, including Oval Space, The Laundry and Village Underground. Being based in central London, yet still encompassing many of the features of country-set events such as Glastonbury and Secret Garden Party, Lovebox Festival has developed a reputation for being an “urban-based-yet-rural-feeling, multi-dimensional festival – without the camping or the endless tramping about.”." .

ns1:57901 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Euroleague Final Four is the final four format championship of the European-wide top-tier level EuroLeague professional club basketball competition. The Euroleague Basketball Company used the final four format for the first time in 2002, following the 2001 FIBA SuproLeague Final four, which was the last final four held by FIBA Europe. The first time the EuroLeague used a Final four format to decide its league champion, was at the conclusion of the 1965–66 and 1966–67 seasons, when it held the 1966 FIBA European Champions Cup Final four, and the 1967 FIBA European Champions Cup Final four. The Euroleague Final Four has been held every year since, with FIBA Europe organizing it until 2001, and the Euroleague Basketball Company organizing it since 2002. The SuproLeague, which was organized by FIBA, and the EuroLeague, which was organized by Euroleague Basketball Company. Euroleague Basketball Company's EuroLeague competition, in its inaugural year, used a playoff format, with the two professional teams from Bologna (Virtus and Fortitudo), AEK, and TAU reaching the tournament's semifinals. That season's Euroleague Basketball tournament, the Euroleague 2000–01 season, did not end with a Final four tournament. The EuroLeague now officially recognizes both the 2001 FIBA SuproLeague Final four, and the 2001 Euroleague Semifinals, as Final Fours. * The 2000–01 season was a transition year, with the best European teams splitting into two different major leagues: The SuproLeague, held by FIBA, and the EuroLeague, held by Euroleague Basketball. That season's EuroLeague Basketball tournament did not end with a Final four tournament." .

ns1:57927 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2001 Miller Lite 200 was a Championship Auto Racing Teams (CART) motor race held on 12 August 2001 at Mid-Ohio Sports Car Course in Lexington, Ohio, United States of America. Team Penske dominated the event, with Gil de Ferran taking pole position and Hélio Castroneves finishing ahead of de Ferran in the race itself." .

ns1:57939 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The American Basketball League, often abbreviated to the ABL of 1996 was the first independent professional basketball league for women in the United States. The ABL began league competition in the Fall of 1996, while the WNBA launched its first game in June 1997. Both organizations came into existence during a surge in popularity for women's basketball in the United States that followed the perfect 35–0 national championship season for the Connecticut Huskies in 1995 and the undefeated, gold medal-winning performance of the United States Women's basketball team at the 1996 Summer Olympics. The ABL lasted two full seasons: 1996–97 and 1997–98. On 22 December 1998; with almost no warning, the ABL declared Chapter 11 bankruptcy and suspended operations. The 1996–97 ABL All-Star Game was played on 1996, at the Hartford Civic Center. The 1998–99 ABL All-Star Game was scheduled to be played on January 24, 1999, in San Jose, California, but was canceled when the league ceased operations in 22 December 1998." .

ns1:57954 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Tropical Storm Lowell was a moderate tropical storm that developed during the 2008 Pacific hurricane season. It quickly intensified into Tropical Storm Lowell, peaking as a moderate tropical storm." .

ns1:57965 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Alaska Airlines Flight 779 was a contract cargo flight operated on 21 July 1961 by an Alaska Airlines Douglas DC-6A that crashed short of the runway at Shemya Air Force Base with the loss of all six crew members on board." .

ns1:57973 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2005 Badminton World Championships – Women's Doubles took place in Honda Center in Anaheim, United States of America, between 15 August 2005 and 21 August 2005." .

ns1:57974 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2000 Barnsley Metropolitan Borough Council election took place on 04 May 2000 to elect members of Barnsley Metropolitan Borough Council in South Yorkshire, England." .

ns1:57980 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Big Lightning (also sometimes The Great Lightning, Russian: Большая молния, Bolshaya molniya) is an unfinished opera sketched in 1932 by Dmitri Shostakovich." .

ns1:57983 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1962 Dwars door Vlaanderen was the 18th edition of the Dwars door Vlaanderen cycle race and was held on 21 April 1962–22 April 1962." .

ns1:57987 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1985 Porsche Classic was a women's tennis tournament played on indoor hard courts in Filderstadt, West Germany that was part of the 1985 WTA Tour. It was the eighth edition of the tournament and was held from 14 October 1985 through 20 October 1985." .

ns1:58041 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Great Bakersfield Fire of 1889 was a conflagration in Bakersfield, California." .

ns1:58042 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Latin Grammy Awards of 2000 were held in Los Angeles at the Staples Center on Wednesday, 2000. On 2000 the Latin Academy of Recording Arts & Sciences announced that the inaugural Latin Grammy Award were going to take place on 2000 in Los Angeles and the awards ceremony would be broadcast in the United States by CBS, which will also distribute it to other countries. The list of nominees for the Latin Grammy Awards of 2000, slated for 2000 in Los Angeles, was unveiled on Friday, 2000 in a Miami ceremony hosted by Michael Greene, President of the National Academy of Recording Arts & Sciences. The list of hosts for the first-ever Latin Grammy Award, included Gloria Estefan, Antonio Banderas, Jimmy Smits, Andy García and Jennifer Lopez, who also was announced to perform her hit single \"No Me Ames\" with Marc Anthony, but Anthony (with six nominations that night) did not attend the show citing complications with his (then) wife's pregnancy. On 2000 Sony Discos released the first \"Latin Grammy Nominees CD\" to coincide with the inaugural awards with nominees from three categories, Latin Grammy Award for Record of the Year, Best Female Pop Vocal Performance and Best Tropical Song, with a portion of the proceeds to support the Latin Academy Of Recording Arts and Sciences for community-outreach efforts." .

ns1:58047 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 Armenian First League season began on 05 April 2011, and ended on 10 October 2011." .

ns1:58048 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Belarusian Super Cup was held on 2017 between the 2016 Belarusian Premier League champions FC BATE Borisov and the 2015–16 Belarusian Cup winners FC Torpedo-BelAZ Zhodino." .

ns1:58064 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "A Credit default swap (CDS) is a swap agreement that the seller of the CDS will compensate the buyer in the event of a debt default (by the debtor) or other credit event. A \"Credit default swap\" (CDS) is a Credit derivative contract between two counterparties. J.P. Morgan & Co. is widely credited with creating the modern Credit default swap in 1994. Under the Duffie construct, the price of a Credit default swap can also be derived by calculating the asset swap spread of a bond. A new type of default swap is the \"loan only\" Credit default swap (LCDS)." .

ns1:58069 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1993 European Amateur Boxing Championships were held in Bursa from September 6 to September 12. The 30th edition of the normally bi-annual competition, in which 197 fighters from 32 countries participated this time, was organised by the European governing body for amateur boxing, EABA." .

ns1:58074 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Hirak movement in the Rif or The Rif Movement (Berber: ⴰⵎⵓⵙⵙⵓ ⵏ ⴰⵕⵉⴼ, Arabic: حراك الريف Movement of the Rif) is a popular mass protest movement that took place in the Berber Rif region in northern Morocco between 28 October 2016 and June 2017 as a result of the death of Mohcine Fikri, a fishmonger who was crushed to death after jumping into the back of a garbage truck, following the confiscation by local authorities of the fish which he was selling on the local market. Macron also reported that the king said to him, that \"he inaugurated the practice of spending vacations in Al Hoceima\", and the king claimed to Macron, that \"contrary to other countries, people in the Rif were free to protest\"." .

ns1:58081 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Gwangbokjeol is a holiday celebrated annually on 15 August in both South Korea and North Korea. It is notable for being the only Korean public holiday celebrated by both South Korea and North Korea. In South Korea it is known as Gwangbokjeol (광복절; literally, \"the day the light returned\"), and is one of the public holidays in South Korea. After the Korean Peninsula was liberated by the Allies in 1945, independent Korean governments were created three years later, on 15 August 1948, when the pro-U.S. Syngman Rhee was elected first President of South Korea and pro-Soviet Kim Il-sung was made first Leader of North Korea. Gwangbokjeol was officially designated a public holiday on 1 October 1949 in South Korea and is known as Chogukhaebangŭi nal (조국해방의 날; literally \"Liberation of Fatherland Day\") in North Korea. North Korea currently celebrates this holiday separately from South Korea. At 10:23 a.m., 15 August 1974, Mun Se-gwang, a Zainichi Korean and North Korea sympathizer, attempted to assassinate President Park Chung-hee at the National Theater of Korea in Seoul during a Gwangbokjeol ceremony; Park was unharmed but his wife Yuk Young-soo, First Lady of South Korea, was killed." .

ns1:58094 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 Copa Libertadores was the 49th edition of the Copa Libertadores, CONMEBOL's premier annual international club tournament. Liga Deportiva Universitaria de Quito earned a berth in the 2008 FIFA Club World Cup and 2009 Recopa Sudamericana." .

ns1:58127 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 FIM Motocross World Championship season was the 60th International Motorcycling Federation Motocross World Championship season. A 19-round calendar for the 2016 season was announced on 14 October 2015 FIM Motocross World Championship season." .

ns1:58146 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Tour des Fjords (earlier known as Rogaland Grand Prix until 2012) is a road bicycle racing held annually between Stavanger and Bergen, in the region of western Norway, Norway. From the 2019 season the race will merge with Tour of Norway to form a new six day stage race that will cover all of the southern counties of Norway. The first edition will be held from May 28th until June 2nd 2019." .

ns1:58154 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 UEFA European Under-17 Football Championship was the twelfth edition of Union of European Football Associations's UEFA European Under-17 Football Championship since the re-organising of age group competitions in 2002. Slovakia were hosts of the tournament with games taking place at four venues between 05 May 2013–17 May 2013." .

ns1:58155 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Copa América Group C was one of the three groups of competing nations in the 2015 Copa lola. Group play began on 2015 and ended on 21 June 2015." .

ns1:58161 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1994 Skate Canada International was held in Red Deer, Alberta on 03 November 1994–6." .

ns1:58197 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008–09 Montenegrin First League was the third season of the top-tier football in Montenegro. The season began on 09 August 2008 and ended on 30 May 2009." .

ns1:58207 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "El Porteñazo (02 June 1962 – 06 June 1962) was a short-lived military rebellion against the government of Rómulo Betancourt in Venezuela, in which rebels attempted to take over the city of Puerto Cabello (75.83 miles (122.04 km) from the capital)." .

ns1:58212 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Grand Prix of Indianapolis, officially known as the 2016 Angie's List Grand Prix of Indianapolis for sponsorship reasons, was the fifth round of the 2016 IndyCar Series season." .

ns1:58223 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1969 UCI Track Cycling World Championships were the World Championship for track cycling. The events of the men's sprint and the individual pursuit for professionals were held in Antwerp, Belgium the other events took place in Brno, Czechoslovakia. Eleven events were contested, 9 for men (3 for professionals, 6 for amateurs) and 2 for women between 1969 and 09 August 1969." .

ns1:58235 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1982 World Rowing Championships were World Rowing Championships that were held from 28 to 29 August 1982 at Rotsee in Lucerne, Switzerland." .

ns1:58237 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Rhunahaorine Moss was a battle between Covenanters led by General David Leslie and Royalist forces led by Alasdair Mac Colla Chiotaich (Sir Alexander MacDonald) at Rhunahaorine Point, Kintyre, Scotland on 24 May 1647." .

ns1:58255 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The alpine skiing at the 2012 Winter Youth Olympics – girls' combined competition of the alpine skiing events at the 2012 Winter Youth Olympics in Innsbruck, Austria, was held on 15 January 2012, at the Patscherkofel." .

ns1:58257 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2001 Transnistrian presidential election were held in the breakaway republic of Transnistria on 09 December 2001." .

ns1:58271 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Action of 14 September 1779 was a minor naval engagement that occurred on 14 September 1779, off the Azores between the Royal Navy frigate HMS Pearl under the command of George Montagu and the Spanish Navy frigate Santa Mónica under the command of Miguel de Nunes, with the Pearl capturing the Santa Mónica after a brief engagement. Great Britain and Spanish naval forces engaged each other several times during the war, as the conflict spilled over into Europe with the Spanish laying siege to Gibraltar. In 14 September 1779, three years into the war, George Montagu set sail on his newly commissioned ship, HMS Pearl, on a cruise in the eastern fringes of the Atlantic Ocean. Since July 1779, the Spanish Navy had conducted naval patrols near the Azores to search for Great Britain ships in the area. On 14 September 1779, while Montagu was cruising off the Azores in the early hours of the morning, he spotted a large Spanish ship." .

ns1:58272 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Mixed Team tournament in judo at the 2014 Summer Youth Olympics – mixed team was held on 21 August 2014 at the Longjiang Gymnasium." .

ns1:58281 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1961 Omloop Het Volk was the 16th edition of the Omloop Het Nieuwsblad cycle race and was held on 04 March 1961." .

ns1:58282 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1986 World Rally Championship season was the 14th season of the Fédération Internationale de l'Automobile (Fédération Internationale de l'Automobile) World Rally Championship (WRC). The season consisted of 13 rallying, including all twelve venues of the 1985 World Rally Championship season as well as the addition of the Olympus Rally. The 1986 season was notable for being the last World Rally Championship season driven with the popular Group B rally cars, which were banned after the fatal crashes at the Rally Portugal, where three spectators were killed and more than 30 injured, and at the Tour de Corse, where Henri Toivonen and his co-driver Sergio Cresto died in a fireball accident. 1986 marked the only season in which the Fédération Internationale de l'Automobile issued the World Championship for Drivers of Group A Cars." .

ns1:58292 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Akasaka was one of the earlier battles of the Genkō War between the figurehead Emperor Godaigo and the largely Hōjō controlled Kamakura shogunate during the final years of the Kamakura period in Japan. The battle in question was fought at Shimo Akasaka-jō (下赤坂城), or (English: Lower Akasaka fortress), a fortress built upon Mount Yoshino near modern-day Ōsaka in the former Kawachi Province in Ōsaka Prefecture." .

ns1:58304 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Raid on Tabasco, Capture of Villahermosa or Newport's 2nd Expedition of 1599 was an English military expedition during the Anglo–Spanish War that captured the Spanish port settlement of Villahermosa in Viceroyalty of New Spain. Their target was the port of Villahermosa (then called San Juan Bautista) in the Bay of Viceroyalty of New Spain under governor Lázaro Suárez de Córdova. Newport arrived off the Viceroyalty of New Spain coast in late October; Villahermosa lay on the southeast of Viceroyalty of New Spain, with the Gulf of Mexico to the north." .

ns1:58312 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1942 Oregon gubernatorial election took place on 03 November 1942 to elect the governor of the U.S. state of Oregon. Republican candidate and Oregon Secretary of State Earl Snell defeated state senator Lew Wallace by a more than 3–1 margin, the largest margin of victory for any Oregon governor." .

ns1:58318 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The wrestling at the 1968 Summer Olympics – men's freestyle 52 kg as part of the wrestling at the 1968 Summer Olympics were held at the Insurgentes Ice Rink." .

ns1:58322 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 Emirates Cup was a pre-season association football friendly tournament hosted by Arsenal F.C. at its Emirates Stadium, the Emirates Stadium in London. Held on the weekend of 2011–2011, the participants were Arsenal F.C., Boca Juniors, Paris Saint-Germain, and New York Red Bulls. For this event Arsenal F.C. and Paris Saint-Germain did not play each other, nor did Boca Juniors face New York Red Bulls. The Emirates Cup began in July 2007 once Arsenal F.C. finalised plans to stage a pre-season competition at its Emirates Stadium. Argentine club Boca Juniors, French side Paris Saint-Germain and American club New York Red Bulls were confirmed as participants for the 2011 edition, alongside hosts Arsenal F.C.. The involvement of New York Red Bulls had added significance because Thierry Henry, who formerly played for Arsenal F.C., faced his old club. The opening match of the 2011 Emirates Cup saw New York Red Bulls taking on Paris Saint-Germain. Gervinho assisted Robin van Persie's goal in the first half a minute before the half-hour, and substitute Aaron Ramsey scored Arsenal F.C.'s second, a long-range strike in the 46th minute. The final match of the 2011 tournament was between Arsenal F.C. and New York Red Bulls." .

ns1:58329 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Hoengsong was a battle during the Korean War that took place between 11 February 1951 - 15, 1951. As predicted, the XIII Army Group commander opened an attack on the night of 11 February 1951 toward Hoengseong County." .

ns1:58331 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Duke–Michigan basketball rivalry is a college basketball rivalry between the Duke Blue Devils men's basketball team of Duke University and Michigan Wolverines men's basketball team of the University of Michigan." .

ns1:58344 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Nanchang (simplified Chinese: 南昌会战; traditional Chinese: 南昌會戰) was a military campaign fought around Nanchang, Jiangxi between the Chinese National Revolutionary Army and the Japanese Imperial Japanese Army in the Second Sino-Japanese War." .

ns1:58346 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Central Coast Massacre was a shooting spree killing that occurred on the evening of 27 October 1992 on the Central Coast, New South Wales, Australia." .

ns1:58356 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 5th Tony Awards were held on 1951, at the Waldorf-Astoria Hotel Grand Ballroom and broadcast on radio station WOR and the Mutual Network." .

ns1:58370 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The swimming at the 1988 Summer Olympics – men's 4 × 100 metre freestyle relay event at the 1988 Summer Olympics took place on 23 September 1988 at the Jamsil Indoor Swimming Pool in Seoul, South Korea." .

ns1:58399 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1949 Australian federal election was held in Australia on 10 December 1949. Robert Menzies became Prime Minister for the second time, and the Liberal Party of Australia won government federally for the first time." .

ns1:58413 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Åsta accident was a railway accident that occurred at 13:12:25 on 4 January 2000 at Åsta in Åmot, south of Rena in Østerdalen, Norway." .

ns1:58418 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018–19 figure skating season began on 01 July 2018, and ended on 30 June 2019." .

ns1:58436 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 African Cross Country Championships was the fourth edition of the international cross country running competition for African athletes organised by the Confederation of African Athletics. It was held on 12 March at the Yaounde Golf Club in Yaounde, Cameroon – the first time a West African nation had hosted the event since its re-launch in 2011." .

ns1:58437 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1999 DFS Classic was a women's tennis tournament played on grass court at the Edgbaston Priory Club in Birmingham in the United Kingdom that was part of WTA Tier III tournaments of the 1999 WTA Tour. The tournament was held from 07 June 1999 until 13 June 1999." .

ns1:58446 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Six-red World Championship (often styled the 2012 SangSom 6-red World Championship for sponsorship and marketing purposes) was a six-red snooker tournament held between 02 July 2012 and 07 July 2012 at the Montien Riverside Hotel in Bangkok, Thailand." .

ns1:58452 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The best team who isn't playing on Campeonato Brasileiro Série A, Série B or Série C qualifies to the 2013 Campeonato Brasileiro Série D. The Federal District Football Federation allows teams from neighbouring states to play in its competition, with the condition that they are sited in cities no farther than 200km from Brasília. Brasiliense Futebol Clube won the 2013 Campeonato Brasiliense." .

ns1:58453 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1890 World Series was an end-of-the-year baseball playoff series between the National League champion Brooklyn Bridegrooms (later known as the Dodgers) and the American Association champion Louisville Colonels. After losing the 1889 World Series to their \"cross-town\" rivals, the New York Giants (Brooklyn was actually a separate city until 1898), the Bridegrooms came back strong and won the National League pennant in 1890, the first major league club to win consecutive pennants in two different leagues (the only other being the Boston Reds (1890–91)). The first four were played in Louisville (including a tie in Game 3), and the remainder of the Series was scheduled for Brooklyn." .

ns1:58460 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2003 Cricket World Cup Final was a One Day International (ODI) match played on 2003 at the Wanderers Stadium in Johannesburg, South Africa. The second semi-final was played between India and Kenya on 2003 at Kingsmead, Durban. The final was played on 2003 at the Wanderers Stadium, Johannesburg." .

ns1:58468 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Battle of Kachhi was fought between His Highness The Khan of Kalat Mir Abdullah Khan Baloch and Main Noor Mohammad Kalhoro Amir of Sindh to establish their rule in Kachhi District. In the year 1142 AH (1729 AD) Murad Kaleri Baloch was also known as Ganjah Baloch, was appointed by Main Noor Mohammad Kalhoro as an agent in charge of Siwí, and brought into subjection powerful chiefs like Jafar Khan Magsí, the land owner of Ganjobah, Ali Mardan Abro and Ibrahim Khan Abro chiefs of Nausharo, Kachhi District, Mahyan Eri and Lahná Machhi, big land owners of Bhag Nari, Kala Khan and other chiefs and owners of Dhadhar." .

ns1:58479 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Individual event for badminton at the Badminton at the 2017 Southeast Asian Games – Individual Event was held at the Axiata Arena, Kuala Lumpur from 26 to 29 August 2017." .

ns1:58480 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1980 Tanzanian general election were held in Tanzania on 26 October 1980." .

ns1:58486 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "wheelchair tennis at the 2012 Summer Paralympics were held between 01 September 2012–09 September 2012 at Eton Manor, London." .

ns1:58487 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Glenkiln Sculpture Park was a sculpture landscape in the historic county of Kirkcudbrightshire in Dumfries and Galloway, south-west Scotland." .

ns1:58503 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1972 Summer Paralympics (German: Paralympische Sommerspiele 1972), the fourth edition of the Paralympic Games, were held in Heidelberg, West Germany, from 2 to 11 August 1972. The games ended 15 days before the 1972 Summer Olympics held in Munich, also in West Germany." .

ns1:58507 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1988 Southern 500, the 39th running of the event, was a NASCAR Winston Cup Series racing event that took place on 04 September 1988, at Darlington Raceway in Darlington, South Carolina. Darlington Raceway, nicknamed by many NASCAR fans and drivers as \"The Lady in Black\" or \"The Track Too Tough to Tame\" and advertised as a \"NASCAR Tradition\", is a race track built for NASCAR racing located near Darlington, South Carolina. Lasting almost four hours, this final \"traditional\" Bojangles' Southern 500 racing event would feature Bill Elliott (employed at that time by Mr. Harry Melling of Melling Racing) defeating Rusty Wallace (employed by Raymond Beadle Blue Max Racing racing team during this era) by 0.24 seconds; the average speed of the race was 128.297 miles per hour (206.474 km/h)." .

ns1:58508 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1903 Ball in the Winter Palace (Russian: Костюмированный бал 1903 года, lit. It was held in the Winter Palace, Saint Petersburg, in two stages, on 11 February 1903 and 13." .

ns1:58518 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1962 NHRA Winternationals were a National Hot Rod Association (NHRA) drag racing event, held at Auto Club Raceway, Pomona, California on 18 February 1962." .

ns1:58527 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Kyōtoku Incident (享徳の乱, Kyōtoku no Ran) was a long series of skirmishes and conflicts fought for control of the Kantō region of Japan in the 15th century. The conflict began in 1454 with the assassination of Uesugi Noritada by Kantō kubō Ashikaga Shigeuji." .

ns1:58540 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006 Men's World Floorball Championships were the sixth men's Men's World Floorball Championship. It was held in 2006 in Sweden, with the host country defeating Finland in sudden victory in the gold medal game. Official 2006 Rankings according to the International Floorball Federation." .

ns1:58545 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1974 Michigan gubernatorial election was held on 1974. William Milliken was elected to his second term as Governor of Michigan in a rematch with Sander Levin." .

ns1:58549 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 34th Saturn Awards, honoring the best in science fiction, fantasy and horror film and television in 2007 were presented on 24 June 2008 in Universal City Hilton Hotel in Los Angeles, California." .

ns1:58582 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Mark Edmondson and Kim Warwick were the defending champions." .

ns1:58598 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2000 Idea Prokom Open was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor clay courts in Sopot, Poland that was part of the Tier III category of the 2000 WTA Tour. It was the third edition of the Orange Warsaw Open and took place from 17 July until 23 July 2000." .

ns1:58625 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Irwell Sculpture Trail is the largest public art scheme in England, commissioning regional, national and international artists. The Trail includes 28 art pieces and follows a well established 30-mile (48 km) footpath stretching from Salford Quays through Bury into Rossendale and up to the Pennines above Bacup." .

ns1:58627 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Broome flood was a severe flood that occurred in January and 2018 in and around Broome in the Kimberley region of Western Australia. Broome received 1,502 millimetres (59 in) of rainfall in the first two months of 2018, the equivalent of two years worth. The rainfall was caused by Cyclone Joyce, which struck Broome on 2018. The floodwater also caused damage to around 100 kilometres (62 mi) of the road between Broome and Derby with another 12 km (7 mi) remaining underwater south of Broome near Roebuck Plains a week after the floods." .

ns1:58629 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1985–86 Serie B was the fifty-fourth tournament of this competition played in Italy since its creation. Brescia Calcio, L.R. Vicenza Virtus, Unione Sportiva Catanzaro and Palermo FC had been promoted from Serie C, while Ascoli Calcio 1898 F.C., S.S. Lazio and Cremonese had been relegated from Serie A." .

ns1:58632 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The IFBB Arnold Sports Festival, also known as the Arnold Schwarzenegger Sports Festival, is an annual multi-sport event consisting of professional bodybuilding (Arnold Classic), strongman (Arnold Strongman Classic), fitness, figure and bikini weekend expo." .

ns1:58642 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Küstendorf Film and Music Festival (Serbian: Кустендорф филмски фестивал) is an annual event held during early January in the village of Drvengrad (also known as Küstendorf) in the Mokra Gora region of Serbia. Established in 2008, it is a film and music festival organized by Rasta International, film director Emir Kusturica's production company, in the village whose construction he financed in mid-2000s on the slopes of Mećavnik hill. The festival has no commercial sponsors and is partly financed by the Ministry of Culture of Serbia. In its inaugural edition from 14 to 2008, the festival's main guest was Nikita Mikhalkov who held a workshop, along with a retrospective of his films being shown — 12, A Slave of Love, A Few Days from the Life of I. I. Oblomov, and An Unfinished Piece for Mechanical Piano. Held against the backdrop of the 2008 Serbian presidential elections — contested between incumbent Boris Tadić from the ruling center-left Democratic Party (DS) and challenger Tomislav Nikolić from the opposition right-wing Serbian Radical Party (SRS) — as well as speculation that Albanians from Serbia's province of Kosovo were in the final stages of coordination with the United States to unilaterally declare independence, the festival received plenty of attention both in the Serbian and foreign press. In 2009, the festival was held from January until January. Top Serbian political figures also took note of Depp being in the country; right after landing in Serbia, the film star got taken in by Kusturica for a photo-op visit with Serbian president Boris Tadić at the Andrićev Venac presidential palace in Belgrade before proceeding on to Drvengrad by helicopter. The fourth edition of the festival took place from 5th until January with the opening ceremony inspired by a scene from Fellini's Amarcord featuring Nele Karajlić dressed in a bear costume and Vrelo girls followed by Serbian Minister of Culture Nebojša Bradić officially proclaiming the festival open. Serbian government's Ministry of Culture contributed RSD25 million (~€250,000) for this year's festival including the addition of Dom pisaca (Writers' Center) at Drvengrad. She and Kusturica then flew by helicopter to Kamengrad for another photo-op before finally crossing the border into Serbia for the festival site in Drvengrad. The festival was held from the 18th until the 23rd of January with Bérénice Bejo presiding over the jury that also includes screenwriters Guillermo Arriaga and Srđan Koljević." .

ns1:58649 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Olympic Airways v. Husain, 540 United States Reports 644 (2004), was a Supreme Court of the United States case related to Olympic Airways Flight 417. The Supreme Court of the United States decided the appeal in Olympic Airways v. Husain. In a 6-2 decision in favor of Husain, Clarence Thomas delivered the opinion of the Court." .

ns1:58650 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Simulation Theory World Tour was a world concert tour by English rock band Muse, in support of their eighth studio album, Simulation Theory (2018). Muse – Simulation Theory, a film containing live footage from the London 02 gigs that took place on 14 September 2019–15 September 2019, as well as additional filmed scenes, was released on 17 August 2020 in IMAX cinemas worldwide with a digital download release on 21 August." .

ns1:58675 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The finals and the qualifying heats of the European Short Course Swimming Championships 1998 – women's 200 metre freestyle event at the European Short Course Swimming Championships 1998 were held on the third and last day of the competition, on Sunday 13 December 1998 in Sheffield." .

ns1:58681 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Tajik Super Cup was the 9th Tajik Supercup, an annual Tajik association football match played between the winners of the previous season's Tajik League and Tajik Cup. The match was contested by 2017 Tajik League champions Istiqlol Dushanbe, and the 2017 Tajik Cup champions FK Khujand. It was held at the Central Stadium in Hisor on 2018. FK Khujand led a 2-1 at halftime thanks to goals from Firdavs Chakalov and Agbley Jones, with Istiqlol Dushanbe getting one back on the brink of halftime after Chakalov." .

ns1:58685 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1757 Berlin raid took place during the Third Silesian War (part of the Seven Years' War). Cavalrymen of the Holy Roman Empire attacked and briefly occupied Berlin, the capital of Prussia. On 16 October 1757 Hadik and his raiding force arrived outside of Berlin." .

ns1:58696 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014–15 FA Trophy was the 45th season of the FA Trophy, the Football Association's cup competition for teams at levels 5–8 of the English football league system. A total of 275 clubs entered the competition, which was won by Conference North club North Ferriby United A.F.C. after beating Conference team Wrexham in the final on 29 March 2015." .

ns1:58701 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1979 Ålandic legislative election were held in the Åland on 20 October 1979 to elect members of the Landstinget." .

ns1:58708 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Wakefield massacre occurred on 26 December 2000, at Edgewater Technology in Wakefield." .

ns1:58710 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Volta Limburg Classic was a one-day road cycling race that took place on 01 April 2017. It was the 44th edition of the Volta Limburg Classic and was rated as a 1.1 event as part of the 2017 UCI Europe Tour." .

ns1:58714 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2003 Croatian Football Cup Final was a two-legged affair played between NK Istra 1961 and H.N.K. Hajduk Split." .

ns1:58718 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of LaFourche Crossing (also spelled Lafourche Crossing) was a battle in Lafourche Parish, United States and fought on 20 June 1863–21, 1863, during the American Civil War as part of a campaign known as Taylor’s Operations in West Louisiana." .

ns1:58720 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 Open Seguros Bolívar was a professional tennis tournament played on clay courts. It took place in Bogotá, Colombia between 11 July 2011 and 17 July 2011." .

ns1:58722 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Festival d'Avignon, or Avignon Festival, is an annual arts festival held in the France city of Avignon every summer in July in the courtyard of the Palais des Papes as well as in other locations of the city. Founded in 1947 by Jean Vilar, it is the oldest existent festival in France and one of the world's greatest. Alongside the official festival, the \"In\" one, a number of shows are presented in Avignon at the same time of the year and are known as the \"Off\". The festival is organised by a non-profit organisation (since 1980), which is administered by a board of trustees composed of: the France state, the city of Avignon, the Département of Vaucluse, the Région Provence-Alpes-Côte d'Azur, and seven public figures competent in the field of theatre. The Festival d'Avignon was founded by Jean Vilar in 1947. The very first Festival d'Avignon in 1947 set the scene as a showcase for unknown work and modern scripts. On the crest of the wave of contemporary tendencies to revolutionize theatrical practices, in 1966 Avignon's Théâtre des Carmes, co-founded by André Benedetto and Bertrand Hurault, staged a festival « Off », unofficial and independent. Due to the anti-authoritarian protest movements of May 1968, the 22nd edition of the Festival of Avignon hosted virtually no France shows, halving the number of planned shows down from a total of 83. Archives on the works of Jean Vilar and the totality of the 3,000 representations programmed by the Festival of Avignon since its 1947 debut are conserved at Maison Jean-Vilar, located in Avignon at 8, rue de Mons, Montée Paul-Puaux." .

ns1:58723 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1980–81 FIBA European Cup Winner's Cup was the fifteenth edition of FIBA Europe's 2nd-tier level European-wide professional club basketball competition, contested between national domestic cup champions, running from 07 October 1980, to 18 March 1981. Pallacanestro Cantù defeated FC Barcelona, in the final held in Rome, winning the FIBA Europe European Cup Winner's Cup for a (European record) fourth time." .

ns1:58732 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "London Book Fair (LBF) is a large book-publishing trade fair held annually, usually in April, in London, England. Until 2006 the London Book Fair had been held at the Olympia exhibition centre, but it moved to the ExCeL Exhibition Centre in London's Docklands that year. Due to generally unfavourable feedback from attendees over the new location, such as the inconvenience of transport links or the infrastructure of the location, as well as the intervention resulting from other exhibitions and their wandering visitors, the book fair returned to west London in 2007 and took place at Earls Court Exhibition Centre from 16 to 18 April." .

ns1:58748 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Apostolicae curae is the title of a papal bull, issued in 1896 by Leo XIII, declaring all Anglican ordinations to be \"absolutely null and utterly void\". The final result was the papal bull Apostolicae curae, in which Anglican orders were declared to be invalid. Catholic World Report of 10 May 2017 published a comment by Edward Peters: The news item in The Tablet referred to Apostolicae curae as simply \"Leo XIII’s remarks\", although it was arguably an infallible exercise of papal extraordinary magisterium, or at least \"a prominent exercise of the ordinary papal magisterium which coalesced with several centuries of other ordinary exercises of papal-episcopal magisterium in rejecting the validity of Anglican orders to the point that Catholics must hold them invalid\", as indicated in the official commentary that accompanied the Apostolic Letter Ad tuendam fidem. The congregation's commentary listed Leo XIII's declaration in Apostolicae curae on the invalidity of Anglican ordinations as an example of \"those truths connected to revelation by historical necessity and which are to be held definitively, but are not able to be declared as divinely revealed\". In the essay, approved by the Congregation for the Doctrine of the Faith, Ghirlanda commented that \"the ordination of ministers coming from Anglicanism will be absolute, on the basis of the Bull Apostolicae curae of Leo XIII of 13 September 1896\"." .

ns1:58751 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 Toray Pan Pacific Open was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts. It was the 28th edition of the Pan Pacific Open, and was part of the Premier Series of the 2011 WTA Tour. It took place at the Ariake Coliseum in Tokyo, Japan, from September 26 through 2011." .

ns1:58756 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1949 FIVB Men's Volleyball World Championship was the first edition of the tournament, organised by the world's governing body, the Fédération Internationale de Volleyball. It was held in Prague, Czechoslovakia from 10 to 18 September 1949." .

ns1:58768 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2002 Islington London Borough Council election took place on 02 May 2002 to elect members of Islington London Borough Council in London, England. The whole council was up for election with boundary changes since the 1998 Islington London Borough Council election reducing the number of seats by 4." .

ns1:58776 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1963 Peruvian general election were held in Peru on 09 June 1963 to elect the President and both houses of the Congress after the results of the 1962 Peruvian general election were annulled following a military coup." .

ns1:58778 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2003 Campeonato Brasileiro Série A was the 47th edition of the Campeonato Brasileiro Série A. The first edition with only a double round-robin and no playoffs, it began on 29 March 2003, and reached its end on 14 December 2003." .

ns1:58785 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1911 Ontario general election was the 13th general election held in the Province of Ontario, Canada. It was held on 11 December 1911, to elect the 106 Members of the 13th Legislative Assembly of Ontario (titled as Members of the Provincial Parliament or M.P.P.). The Ontario Conservative Party, led by Sir James Whitney, was elected for a third consecutive term in government, with a slight reduced majority in the Legislature." .

ns1:58789 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1950 Volta a Catalunya was the 30th edition of the Volta a Catalunya cycle race and was held from 17 September 1950 to 24 September 1950." .

ns1:58803 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010–11 Liga Leumit was the twelfth season since its introduction in 1999 and the 69th season of second-tier football in Israel. A total of sixteen teams contested the league, including twelve sides from the 2009–10 Liga Leumit, two promoted teams from the 2009–10 Liga Alef and two relegated teams from the 2009–10 2010–11 Israeli Premier League. Ironi Kiryat Shmona and Hapoel Ashkelon were directly promoted to the 2010–11 Israeli Premier League after finishing the 2009–10 Liga Leumit in the two top places. Maccabi Ahi Nazareth and Hapoel Ra'anana were directly relegated to the 2010–11 Liga Leumit after finishing the 2009–10 Liga Leumit in the bottom two places." .

ns1:58807 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1956 United States presidential election in Minnesota took place on 06 November 1956 as part of the 1956 United States presidential election. Minnesota was won by the Republican Party candidate, incumbent President of the United States Dwight D. Eisenhower won the state over former Illinois governor Adlai Stevenson by a margin of 101,777 votes, or 7.6%. The 1956 presidential election was a rematch of the 1952 election, in which Eisenhower also defeated Stevenson, both nationally and in Minnesota. As of the 2020 presidential election, this is the last time Minnesota was carried twice by the Republican nominee. The Democratic nomination campaign leading into 1956 presidential election may have had a major role in the end of the political career of Coya Knutson, the first woman to be elected to the United States House of Representatives from Minnesota." .

ns1:58809 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 Finnish Championships in Athletics were held in the Tampere Stadium in Tampere from 24 July 2008 to 27 July 2008." .

ns1:58810 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Indian Wells Masters, also known as BNP Paribas Open and the WTA Indian Wells Open, is an annual tennis tournament held in early- and mid-March at the Indian Wells Tennis Garden in Indian Wells, United States. The tournament is a Masters 1000 event on the Association of Tennis Professionals and is a Premier Mandatory event on the Women's Tennis Association. The tournament is played on hardcourt and is the best-attended tennis tournament outside the four Grand Slam tournaments. The tournament is played in the Indian Wells Tennis Garden (built in 2000) which has 29 tennis courts, including the 16,100-seat main stadium, which is the second largest tennis-specific stadium in the world. After the 2013 BNP Paribas Open the Indian Wells Tennis Garden started an expansion and upgrade of its facilities that includes a new 8,000 seat Stadium 2. The revamping of the tennis center also included a \"Pro Purple\" interior court color created specifically for the ATP Masters Series and first used at Indian Wells, citing the purple color being 180 degrees and exactly opposite the yellow of the ball. In 1996, the championship became one of the few fully combined events on both the Association of Tennis Professionals and Women's Tennis Association tours. The BNP Paribas Open has become one of the largest events on both men's and Women's Tennis Association." .

ns1:58817 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Newry and Armagh by-election, 1986 was caused by the resignation of incumbent Member of Parliament Jim Nicholson. The poll was held on 23 January 1986, and was unusual for a by-election in the turnout being higher than for the preceding general election, however Nicholson was not re-elected, (despite gaining an increase in his own vote) losing his seat to the nationalist SDLP candidate Seamus Mallon." .

ns1:58829 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of the Metaurus was a pivotal battle in the Second Punic War between Roman Republic and Carthage, fought in 207 BC near the Metauro River in Italy. The battle confirmed Roman supremacy over Italy. Dexter Hoyos believes Hasdrubal's death was foolish, as he could have given organisation and leadership to the remnants of the Carthaginian army and posed a lingering threat to Roman Republic in northern Italy. It is included in Edward Shepherd Creasy's The Fifteen Decisive Battles of the World (1851), the rationale being that it effectively removed the Carthaginian threat from Roman Republic's ascendancy to global dominion by leaving Hannibal stranded in Italy. Paul K. Davis sees its importance as the \"Carthaginian defeat ended the attempt to reinforce Hannibal, dooming his effort in Italy, and Roman Republic was able to establish dominance over Spain.\" The Battle of the Metaurus is overshadowed by other battles of the Second Punic War, such as Hannibal's great victory at the Battle of Cannae or his ultimate defeat at the Battle of Zama. F. L. Lucas's short story \"The Fortune of Carthage\" (Athenaeum, 28 January 1921) is about the battle's prelude, from Claudius Nero's viewpoint." .

ns1:58862 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 13th Chess Olympiad, organized by FIDE and comprising an open team tournament, as well as several other events designed to promote the game of chess, took place between 30 September 1958 and 23 October 1958, in Munich, West Germany." .

ns1:58863 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "United Nations Security Council Resolution 698, adopted unanimously on 14 June 1991, after recalling Resolution 682 (1990) dealing with financial issues and all resolutions on Cyprus up to the most recent United Nations Security Council Resolution 697 (1991), the Council expressed its concern regarding the financial situation facing the United Nations Peacekeeping Force in Cyprus (UNFICYP), established in Resolution 186 (1964)." .

ns1:58870 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Pickens Forest was a U.S. Marine Corps operation in Quảng Nam, South Vietnam that took place from 16 July to 24 August 1970. Operation Pickens Forest concluded on 24 August 1970, the Marines had lost 4 killed and the PAVN/VC 99 killed." .

ns1:58897 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Deligrad was fought between Serbia and the Ottoman Empire in 1806. The First Serbian Uprising had begun in 1804 with the expulsion of the ruling janissary elite and the proclamation of an independent Serbia state by the revolution's leader, Karađorđe. The Serbia high command decided to meet the Ottoman Empire force under Ibrahim Bushati, pasha of Shkodër, at Deligrad. The Serbia rebels also attacked the Ottoman Empire positions numerous times and managed to capture nine Ottoman Empire cannons. The battle provided a decisive victory for the Serbs and bolstered the morale of the outnumbered Serbia rebels." .

ns1:58904 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Neretva '93 was an Army of the Republic of Bosnia and Herzegovina (ARBiH) operation against the Croatian Defence Council (HVO) in 01 September 1993 on a 200 km long front from Gornji Vakuf to south of Mostar, one of its largest of the year, on areas which were included in the Croatian Republic of Herzeg-Bosnia." .

ns1:58907 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Wild West Show were traveling vaudeville performances in the United States and Europe that existed around 1870–1920. Western Films in the first half of the 20th century filled the gap left behind by Wild West Show." .

ns1:58912 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Agadir air disaster was a chartered Boeing 707 passenger flight on Sunday, 03 August 1975, that crashed into a mountain on approach to Agadir-Inezgane Airport." .

ns1:58925 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2001 Sicilian regional election was held on 24 June 2001. The election was competed by three competitors: Salvatore Cuffaro, House of Freedoms candidate; Leoluca Orlando, candidate of The Olive Tree and former mayor of Palermo; and Sergio D'Antoni, leader of European Democracy." .

ns1:58940 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Tameside Metropolitan Borough Council election took place on 03 May 2012 to elect members of Tameside Metropolitan Borough Council in England." .

ns1:58945 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 European Athletics Championships – men's high jump at the 2018 European Athletics Championships took place at the Olympiastadion Berlin on 9 August and 11 August." .

ns1:58946 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The athletics at the 2014 Asian Games – women's 800 metres event at the athletics at the 2014 Asian Games was held at the Incheon Asiad Main Stadium, Incheon, South Korea on 30 September – 1 October." .

ns1:58948 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1970 Wisconsin gubernatorial election was held on 1970. Democrat Patrick Lucey won the election with 54% of the vote, winning his first term as Governor of Wisconsin and defeating Republican Jack B. Olson." .

ns1:58957 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012–13 Saudi Professional League (officially known as the Zain League, because of its sponsorship) was the 37th season of Saudi Professional League since its establishment in 1976. On 14, Al-Fateh SC won the title after a 1–0 home win over Al-Ahli." .

ns1:58959 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Laura Montalvo and Henrieta Nagyová were the defending champions but only Montalvo competed that year with Paola Suárez." .

ns1:58963 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013–14 Azadegan League was the 23rd season of the Azadegan League and 13th as the second highest division since its establishment in 1991. Shahr Khodro F.C. won the Azadegan League title for the first time in their history." .

ns1:58970 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Murino took place on 08 January 1880 between the Principality of Montenegro and the League of Prizren." .

ns1:58979 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Gravel and Tar is a one-day cycling race, held annually in the Manawatu region of New Zealand, since 2016, finishing in Palmerston North. It is rated 1.2 and has been part of UCI Oceania Tour since 2018." .

ns1:58984 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "rowing at the 2020 Summer Paralympics at the 2020 Summer Paralympics (now to be held in 2021) in Tokyo, Japan will take place at the Sea Forest Waterway, the same location as the paracanoeing events are located." .

ns1:58993 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Première Ligue de soccer du Québec season is the fourth season of play for the Première Ligue de soccer du Québec; the highest level of soccer based in the Canada province of Québec and one of two Division 3 semi-professional soccer leagues in the Canada soccer pyramid (the other being League1 Ontario). The season began on 02 May 2015 and ended on 18 October 2015, with the cup final taking place on 2015. The Inter-Provincial Cup Championship was a two-legged home-and-away series between the league champions of League1 Ontario and the Première Ligue de soccer du Québec – the only Division 3 men's semi-professional soccer leagues based fully within Canada." .

ns1:59004 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Shaoguan incident was a civil disturbance which took place overnight on 25/26 June 2009 in Guangdong, China. As a result of the fighting, 2 Uyghurs died and 118 people were injured, 16 of them seriously." .

ns1:59008 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Martina Navratilova and Heinz Günthardt won in the final 6–3, 6–4 against Elizabeth Smylie and John Fitzgerald." .

ns1:59012 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 World Youth Championships in Athletics was the ninth edition of the biennial international athletics competition for youth (under-18) athletes. The five-day competition took place between 15 July 2015 and 19 July at the Estadio Olímpico Pascual Guerrero stadium in Cali, Colombia." .

ns1:59016 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Woolfire Festival was an annual music festival held since 2005 on a former sheep farm in Woodmancott, Hampshire, England." .

ns1:59029 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015–16 W-League was the eighth season of the W-League, the Australia national women's association football competition. League newcomers Melbourne City FC W-League claimed both the regular season Premiership and end of season Championship, the latter awarded for their victory in the 2016 W-League Grand Final." .

ns1:59031 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Little Salkeld rail accident occurred between Little Salkeld and Lazonby railway stations in Long Meg cutting on the Settle-Carlisle Line on 19 January 1918." .

ns1:59036 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Miss World Puerto Rico 2009 pageant was held on 2009 in Puerto Rico. Ivonne Orsini, who won the title of Miss World Puerto Rico 2008 and classified as one of the semi-finalists at Miss World 2008, crowned her successor, Jennifer Colón as Miss World Puerto Rico 2009, Jennifer represented Puerto Rico at Miss World 2009." .

ns1:59054 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2003 Polish Figure Skating Championships (Polish: Mistrzostwa Polski w łyżwiarstwie figurowym 2002/2003) were held in Sosnowiec between 13 December 2002 and 15, 2002." .

ns1:59070 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006 Women's Rugby World Cup (officially IRB Rugby World Cup 2006 Canada) took place in Edmonton, Alberta, Canada. The tournament began on 31 August 2006 and ended on 17 September 2006." .

ns1:59101 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "In 1148, a Council of Reims was called by Pope Eugene III to consider a number of regulations, or canons, for the Church, as well as to debate some other issues. Originally the summons for the council went out in October 1147 and it was supposed to be held in Trier, which is now in Germany, but conditions in Trier were such that it was moved to Reims, in France, for 1148. The papal entourage arrived in Trier on 30 November 1147, but shortly after arrival the pope decided to move the proceedings because of complaints from the residents of Trier, and announced in 1148 that the council would move to Reims, but still on the date given before. The Italian bishops, however, were mostly excused from attendance, as Eugene held a council at Cremona in 1148 where the Reims decrees were announced. The attendees were from what were later the countries of France, Germany, England, and Spain, and in keeping with the times, would have included not just the prelates who were summoned, but also their servants and officials, making the true number of people in Reims impossible to know. The opening of the council took place on 1148 at Reims Cathedral on a Sunday." .

ns1:59104 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Missouri lieutenant gubernatorial election was held on 06 November 2012. Incumbent lieutenant governor Peter Kinder won the 2008 Missouri lieutenant gubernatorial election with 49.9% of the vote against Democratic candidate Sam Page. Kinder had been considered the front-runner in the 2012 Republican Gubernatorial primary, however after Peter Kinder emerged, he decided to run for re-election." .

ns1:59106 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Women's 200 metres at the athletics at the 1988 Summer Olympics – women's 200 metres in Seoul, South Korea had an entry list of 60 competitors, with eight qualifying heats (60), four second-round races (32) and two semi-finals (16), before the final (8) took off on Thursday 29 September 1988. At the 1988 Olympic Trials, her 21.77 American record already showed Florence Griffith Joyner was going to be a contender against the East Germans who had dominated the sprints for the previous decade." .

ns1:59116 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Garnock Court fire was a fire that took place on 11 June 1999 at Garnock Court, a 14-storey block of flats in Irvine, Scotland, which resulted in one fatality." .

ns1:59127 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2004 Senior League World Series took place from 15 August 2004–21 in Bangor, Maine, United States." .

ns1:59128 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "These are the official results of the athletics at the 1976 Summer Olympics – women's 4 × 100 metres relay event at the 1976 Summer Olympics in Montreal, Quebec, Canada. The event was held on 30 July 1976 and 31 July 1976." .

ns1:59142 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2002 All-Ireland Senior Hurling Championship Final was the 115th All-Ireland Hurling Final and the culmination of the All-Ireland Senior Hurling Championship 2002, a tournament for the top hurling counties. The match was held at Croke Park, Dublin, on 2002, between Kilkenny and Clare GAA." .

ns1:59151 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2001 Crystal Skate of Romania was the 3rd edition of an annual senior-level international figure skating competition held in Romania. It was held between 22 November 2001 and 25, 2001 in Bucharest." .

ns1:59156 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Junior Eurovision Song Contest 2009 was the seventh edition of the annual Junior Eurovision Song Contest and took place in Kiev, Ukraine. Both Prime Minister Yulia Tymoshenko and President of Ukraine Viktor Yushchenko were present during the final; Tymoshenko was also present and speeched during the opening ceremony on 2009. The European Broadcasting Union (EBU) invited broadcasters to bid for the rights to host the Junior Eurovision Song Contest 2009; three bids were received from Belarus, Serbia, and Ukraine. On 6 June 2008, after deliberations by the EBU, the National Television Company of Ukraine (NTU) was granted the rights to the 2009 contest and confirmed they would host it in Kiev. On 2009, Ukrainian Deputy Prime Minister Ivan Vasiunyk declared that the contest would not be postponed; (earlier) Party of Regions member of parliament Hanna Herman had called on Prime Minister Yulia Tymoshenko to cancel the song contest because of the 2009 flu pandemic in Ukraine. Junior Eurovision Song Contest 2009 - KYIV - Ukraine - All The Songs From The Show, is a compilation album put together by the European Broadcasting Union, and was released by Universal Music Group on 21 November 2009." .

ns1:59172 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "United Nations Security Council Resolution 687, adopted on 03 April 1991, after reaffirming resolutions 660, 661, 662, 664, 665, 666, 667, 669, 670, 674, 677, 678 (all 1990) and United Nations Security Council Resolution 686 (1991), the Council set the terms, in a comprehensive resolution, with which Iraq was to comply after losing the Gulf War." .

ns1:59173 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Dance of the Forty-One or the Ball of the Forty-One (Spanish: El baile de los cuarenta y uno) was a society scandal in early 20th-century Mexico, during the presidency of Porfirio Díaz. The incident revolved around an illegal police raid carried out on 17 November 1901 against a private home on Calle de la Paz (since renamed Calle Ezequiel Montes) in Colonia Tabacalera of Mexico City, the site of a dance attended by a group of men, of whom 19 were dressed in women's clothing. The party began the night of 17 November 1901 secretly in a house rented for that purpose in what were the limits of Mexico City, the Colonia Tabacalera." .

ns1:59185 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The athletics at the 1952 Summer Olympics – men's 50 kilometres walk at the 1952 Summer Olympics took place on 21 July 1952 at the Helsinki Olympic Stadium." .

ns1:59186 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2017 ESPY Awards were presented at the 25th annual ESPY Awards show, held on 2017 at 5 pm Pacific at the Microsoft Theater in Los Angeles, California and on television nationwide in the United States of America on American Broadcasting Company at 8 pm Eastern/7 PM Central. On 2017, it was announced by ESPN.com that Peyton Manning would host the show." .

ns1:59188 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1968 United States presidential election in Hawaii took place on 05 November 1968. Hawaii voters chose 4 electors to the Electoral College, which selected the President of the United States and vice President of the United States." .

ns1:59204 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2004 Torneo Clausura was the 76th Chilean Primera División, in which Cobreloa won its 8th league title after beating Unión Española in the finals." .

ns1:59206 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Ikea Effect is a cognitive bias in which consumers place a disproportionately high value on products they partially created. The researchers noted that their use of \"simple IKEA boxes and Lego sets that did not permit customization\" did not prevent participants from manifesting the Ikea Effect." .

ns1:59213 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 LINAFOOT was the inaugural season of the Chad Premier League, the top Chadian league for association football clubs. This edition of Chad Premier League was marked by many controversies, which led to proclaiming AS CotonTchad league champion, and then later giving Gazelle FC champion title back." .

ns1:59218 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1994 California State Treasurer election occurred on 08 November 1994. The Republican nominee, Board of Equalization Chairman Matt Fong, narrowly defeated the Democratic nominee, Phil Angelides, for the office previously held by incumbent Kathleen Brown, who chose not to seek re-election in favor of running for governor." .

ns1:59232 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Original High Tour was the third solo (sixth overall) concert tour by American singer/songwriter Adam Lambert, launched in support of his third studio album, The Original High." .

ns1:59239 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1983 Pioneer Life World Women's Curling Championship, the women's world curling championship, was held from April 3–9 at the Moose Jaw Civic Centre in Moose Jaw, Saskatchewan, Canada." .

ns1:59243 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Charles Stedman Garland and Richard Norris Williams defeated Algernon Kingscote and James Cecil Parke in the final, 4–6, 6–4, 7–5, 6–2 to win the Gentlemen' Doubles tennis title at the 1920 Wimbledon Championships." .

ns1:59247 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Brian Gilbert and Kitty McKane were the defending champions, but lost in the quarterfinals to eventual champions Jean Borotra and Suzanne Lenglen." .

ns1:59248 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Harold Barlow and Ernest Lewis defeated Harold Mahony and Joshua Pim 8–10, 6–3, 5–7, 11–9, 6–1 in the All Comers' Final, and then defeated the reigning champions Herbert Baddeley and Wilfred Baddeley 4–6, 6–2, 8–6, 6–4 in the Challenge Round to win the Gentlemen' Doubles tennis title at the 1892 Wimbledon Championships." .

ns1:59254 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Bernard Baruch Handicap is a Grade II American Thoroughbred horse race for three-years-old and older run over a distance of 1 1⁄16 miles on the turf annually in early August at Saratoga Race Course in Saratoga Springs." .

ns1:59256 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Winner: Frances Hardinge, The Lie Tree. Winner: Frances Hardinge, The Lie Tree." .

ns1:59257 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 London–Surrey Cycle Classic was a one-off, one-day bicycle race that served as one of three United Kingdom events in the year-long 2010–11 UCI Europe Tour and acted as the test event for the 2012 Summer Olympics. In the week preceding the race London experienced a series of riots; several football matches were called off, but the 2011 London–Surrey Cycle Classic was allowed to proceed. The crash split the peloton and in a group of just 20 riders Mark Cavendish beat Sacha Modolo of Italy and France's Samuel Dumoulin in a sprint finish on the Mall." .

ns1:59260 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1994 Antiguan general election were held in Antigua and Barbuda on 08 March 1994." .

ns1:59284 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Capitol Park, a six block site bounded by E, H, 20th and 22nd Streets (38.580°N 121.477°W / 38.580; -121.477) was purchased and was home to the state fair for the next fifty years. In addition to the annual State Fair, California Exposition hosts a number of other community, trade and business events, including the annual State Fair Gala (supporting scholarships), Sacramento County Fair, International Sportsman Expo, Western States Horse Expo and a number of other RV, auto and special collectible trade shows. The one-mile dirt racetrack at California Exposition hosts live thoroughbred racing during the annual State Fair and live harness racing (September through May), in addition to special events like the Sacramento Mile Auto-race." .

ns1:59319 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1890–91 Scottish Football League was the first season of the Scottish Football League. Rangers defeated 3rd Lanark RV 4–1 in their final league match on 9 May 1891 to move level with Dumbarton F.C. on 29 points and a play-off was organised to determine which team would be crowned champions." .

ns1:59321 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Hasim Rahman vs. Lennox Lewis II, billed as \"Final Judgment\", was a heavyweight professional boxing match contested between unified WBC, IBF, IBO and lineal champion Hasim Rahman and former undisputed heavyweight champion Lennox Lewis. The bout took place on November 17, 2001 at the Mandalay Bay Events Center in Paradise, Nevada, and served as a rematch of their Lennox Lewis vs. Hasim Rahman in which Rahman scored a major upset." .

ns1:59331 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1973 FA Trophy Final was the 4th final of the Football Association's cup competition for non-league clubs in the English football league system. The match was contested between Scarborough F.C. and Wigan Athletic F.C., and was the first time that no Southern League team had reached the final since the competition began in 1970." .

ns1:59344 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The athletics at the 2010 Asian Games – men's javelin throw event at the athletics at the 2010 Asian Games was held at the Guangdong Olympic Stadium, Guangzhou, China on 2010." .

ns1:59346 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Cleveland Classic 2012 is the women's edition of the 2012 Cleveland Classic, which is a tournament of the WSA World Tour event International (Prize money: $50,000). The event took place at the Cleveland Racket Club in Cleveland in United States of America from 29 January to 1 February. Nicol David won her first Cleveland Classic trophy, beating Laura Massaro in the final." .

ns1:59351 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1996 Porsche Tennis Grand Prix was a women's tennis tournament played on indoor hard courts at the Filderstadt Tennis Centre in Filderstadt in Germany that was part of Tier II of the 1996 WTA Tour. It was the 19th edition of the tournament and was held from 07 October 1996 until 13 October 1996." .

ns1:59365 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "November to Remember was the eighth and final November to Remember professional wrestling pay-per-view (PPV) event produced by Extreme Championship Wrestling (ECW). It took place on 05 November 2000 from the Odeum Expo Center in Villa Park, Illinois, US." .

ns1:59370 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2003–04 Top League was the first season of Japan's domestic rugby union competition, the Top League. IBM Big Blue and Toyota Verblitz won promotion to the 2004–05 Top League via the 2004 Top League Challenge Series, while Kyuden Voltex and Toyota Industries Shuttles progressed to the promotion play-offs." .

ns1:59372 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Munson Shorthand system was a form of shorthand devised by James Eugene Munson, who was an official court shorthand in New York State." .

ns1:59378 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 AdvoCare 500 was a NASCAR Sprint Cup Series stock car race held on 10 November 2013, at Phoenix Raceway in Avondale, Arizona." .

ns1:59379 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Great Storm of 1987 was a violent extratropical cyclone that occurred on the night of 15 October 1987–16 October, with hurricane-force winds causing casualties in the United Kingdom, France and the Channel Islands as a severe depression in the Bay of Biscay moved northeast. It has been said that as 15 October 1987–1987 approached, shipping weather forecasts warned of European windstorm movements in the North Atlantic, with the result that ships stayed away from North Atlantic European windstorm tracks, depriving North Atlantic meteorology of a major necessary source of weather report data." .

ns1:59390 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006 Euro Beach Soccer Cup was the eighth Euro Beach Soccer Cup, one of Europe's two major beach soccer championships at the time, held in 2006, in Naples, Italy." .

ns1:59392 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1986 Singapore Women's Open was a women's tennis tournament played on indoor hard courts in Singapore and was part of the Category 1 tier of the 1986 Virginia Slims World Championship Series. It was the inaugural edition of the Singapore Women's Open and was held from 20 October 1986 through 26 October 1986." .

ns1:59414 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Battle of Piatka (Ukrainian: П'ятка, Polish: Piątka, Piątek), (02 February 1593) was a battle of the Kosiński Uprising." .

ns1:59420 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "World Kindness Day is an international observance on 13 November 1998. World Kindness Day is to highlight good deeds in the community focusing on the positive power and the common thread of kindness for good which binds us." .

ns1:59438 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Curlew Pass was fought on 15 August 1599, during the campaign of the Earl of Essex in the Nine Years' War, between an English force under Sir Conyers Clifford and a rebel Irish force led by Aodh Ruadh Ó Domhnaill (Red Hugh O'Donnell). The English were ambushed and routed while marching through a pass in the Curlew Mountains, near the town of Boyle, in the west of Ireland." .

ns1:59439 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2001 Volta a Catalunya was the 81st edition of the Volta a Catalunya cycle race and was held from 21 June 2001 to 28 June 2001." .

ns1:59440 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 27th World Science Fiction Convention, also known as St. Louiscon, was held August 28–01 September 1969, at the Chase Park Plaza Hotel in St. Louis, Missouri, United States, under the auspices of OSFA, the Ozark Science Fiction Association." .

ns1:59449 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Rafael Osuna and Dennis Ralston defeated Davies and Wilson in the final, 7–5, 6–3, 10–8 to win the Gentlemen' Doubles tennis title at the 1960 Wimbledon Championship." .

ns1:59452 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2003 Murcian regional election was held on Sunday, 2003, to elect the 6th Regional Assembly of the autonomous community of the Region of Murcia. The term of the Regional Assembly of Region of Murcia expired four years after the date of its 1999 Murcian regional election. The 1999 Murcian regional election was held on 13 June 1999, setting the election date for the Regional Assembly on Sunday, 2003." .

ns1:59454 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The athletics at the 1972 Summer Olympics – men's 4 × 100 metres relay was held on 09 September 1972 and 10 September 1972." .

ns1:59477 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Guadix was an engagement between Castilian and Granadan forces at Guadix, then in the Emirate of Granada and now in Spain, that took place in 31 January 1362. Soon after taking power, Muhammed VI lost a battle against a Christian force at Guadix. In 31 January 1362 a force of about 2,600 Castilians attacked a force of 4,600 Muslims at Guadix." .

ns1:59483 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Tado Festival (多度祭, Tado Matsuri) is a Japanese festival that takes place every year during Japan's Golden Week on May 4 and 5 at Tado Shrine in the city of Kuwana, Mie Prefecture." .

ns1:59484 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2003 Women's African Volleyball Championship was the 11th edition of the Women's African Volleyball Championship organised by Africa's governing volleyball body, the Confédération Africaine de Volleyball. It was held in Nairobi, Kenya, from 16 to 2003. The drawing of lots was held in Nairobi, Kenya on 2003." .

ns1:59493 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1989 World Men's Curling Championship was held at the Miller High Life Theatre in Milwaukee from April 3–9, 1989." .

ns1:59509 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1997–98 Coppa Italia, the 51st Coppa Italia was an Italian Football Federation domestic cup competition won by S.S. Lazio." .

ns1:59517 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1998 Idaho Vandals football team represented the University of Idaho in the 1998 NCAA Division I-A football season. The Idaho Vandals, led by fourth-year head coach Chris Tormey, were members of the Big West Conference and played their home games at the Kibbie Dome, an indoor facility on campus in Moscow, Idaho." .

ns1:59519 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Sinking of F174 took place on the night of 25 December 1996–26, 1996, off the coast of Sicily, in the Mediterranean Sea. An old, severely overloaded wooden ship transporting illegal immigrants from India, Sri Lanka and Pakistan to Italy sank 19 nautical miles, (22 miles; 35 km) from Portopalo di Capo Passero in Sicily (Italy), under questionable circumstances, and came to rest under 108 metres (354 ft) of water." .

ns1:59539 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Cyclone Joan was an intense tropical cyclone that ravaged areas of Western Australia. The following day, the cyclone reached its peak intensity with winds of 215 km/h (130 mph) and a barometric pressure of 915 hPa (27.02 inHg). Cyclone Joan originated from a mass of convection that formed northwest of Darwin on 30 November 1975. All locations along the coast from Kuri Bay to Exmouth were issued a tropical cyclone alert or warning at least once during the duration of Cyclone Joan." .

ns1:59543 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Skate Canada International was the second event of six in the 2007–08 ISU Grand Prix of Figure Skating, a senior-level international invitational competition series. It was held at the Colisée Pepsi in Quebec City, Quebec on 01 November 2007–4." .

ns1:59548 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 NCAA Division I Men's Golf Championship was a golf tournament contested from May 26 to 2009 at the Inverness Club in Toledo, Ohio. This was the second NCAA Division I Men's Golf Championship held at the Inverness Club in Toledo, Ohio." .

ns1:59558 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Hazel Hotchkiss Wightman and Helen Wills defeated Phyllis Covell and Kitty McKane in the final, 6–4, 6–4 to win the Ladies' Doubles tennis title at the 1924 Wimbledon Championships." .

ns1:59561 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 Thüringen Rundfahrt der Frauen was the 22nd edition of the Thüringen Ladies Tour, a women's cycling stage race in Germany. It was rated by the UCI as a category 2.1 race and was held between 21 July 2009 and 26 July 2009." .

ns1:59563 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Winter Soldier: Iraq & Afghanistan was an event at which more than 200 U.S. military veterans and active duty soldiers, as well as Iraqi and Afghan civilians, provided accounts of their experiences in Iraq and Afghanistan. It was organized by Iraq Veterans Against the War, and held from 13 March 2008 to 16 March 2008, timed for the fifth anniversary of the 2003 invasion of Iraq, at the National Labor College in Silver Spring, Maryland." .

ns1:59569 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Placilla was an engagement fought during the Chilean Civil War of 1891 between Balmacedist and Congressional forces on 28 August 1891." .

ns1:59577 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Shania Now Tour was the fourth headlining concert tour by Canadian recording artist Shania Twain, in support of her fifth studio album Now (2017)." .

ns1:59596 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Nobel Peace Prize was shared, in two equal parts, between the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (IPCC) and Al Gore \"for their efforts to build up and disseminate greater knowledge about man-made climate change, and to lay the foundations for the measures that are needed to counteract such change\". The award was given immediate publicity: an Associated Press article published by USA Today on 2007 and headlined \"Gore, scientists share Nobel Peace Prize\" quoted Pachauri as saying \"All the scientists that have contributed to the work of the IPCC are the Nobel laureates who have been recognized and acknowledged by the Nobel Prize Committee\". On the same day, the Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory listed its scientists who had contributed to the IPCC's work, and said that Pachauri had sent a letter to lead authors of the 2007 IPCC Fourth Assessment Report saying that he had \"been stunned in a pleasant way with the news of the award of the Nobel Peace Prize for the IPCC. In Oslo on 2007, the presentation was made with a speech by Ole Danbolt Mjøs as Chairman of the Norwegian Nobel Committee, and followed by Nobel Lectures given by Rajendra K. Pachauri, representing the IPCC, and Al Gore. The IPCC presented scientists who had \"contributed substantially to the preparation of IPCC reports\" with personalized certificates for \"contributing to the award of the Nobel Peace Prize for 2007 to the IPCC\". The certificates, which name the individual and feature a reproduction of the Nobel Peace Prize diploma, were sent to \"coordinating lead authors, lead authors, review editors, bureau members, staff of the technical support units and staff of the secretariat from the IPCC's inception in 1988 until the award of the prize in 2007.\" It would be correct to describe a scientist who was involved with AR4 or earlier IPCC reports in this way: 'X contributed to the reports of the IPCC, which was awarded the Nobel Peace Prize in 2007.'\"" .

ns1:59597 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1884 United States presidential election in Texas was held on 04 November 1884, as part of the 1884 United States presidential election." .

ns1:59611 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1975 Chatham Cup was the 48th annual nationwide knockout association football competition in New Zealand. Christchurch United successfully defended their trophy, and also became only the second team to win the Chatham Cup/National League double." .

ns1:59615 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Dhalai was a battle fought between Pakistan and India before the start 1971 India-Pakistan War for the Bangladesh Liberation War. The battle started after an attack by Indian army on Pakistani border outpost (BOP) in East Pakistan on 28 October 1971 and lasted until 03 November 1971." .

ns1:59622 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The New Market train wreck happened when two Southern Railway passenger trains traveling at great speed collided head on near New Market, Tennessee, on Saturday, 24 September 1904, killing at least 56 passengers and crew and injuring 106." .

ns1:59645 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1795 French Directory election were held from 12 October to 1795 (20 Vendémiaire to 13 Brumaire Year IV) Constitution of the Year III. The election served as an alarm signal for the Republican political forces, as the monarchists, in a landslide, obtained 87 out of 161 elected seats inside the Legislative Body, as well a sympathizer Director: General Lazare Carnot, formerly a Jacobin and revolutionary chief." .

ns1:59650 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2000 Paris–Roubaix was the 98th running of the Paris–Roubaix single-day cycling race, often known as the Hell of the North. It was held on 09 April 2000 over a distance of 272 kilometres (169.0 miles). These are the results for the 2000 edition of the Paris–Roubaix cycling classic, in which Johan Museeuw took his second Roubaix win. 9-04-2000: Compiègne–Roubaix." .

ns1:59651 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 Tour de Georgia was a seven-stage professional bicycle race held from 21 April 2008 through 27 April 2008 across the state of Georgia." .

ns1:59652 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1977 World Rowing Championships was the 6th World Rowing Championships. The championships were held from 19 to 28 August 1977 on the Bosbaan rowing lake in Amsterdam, Netherlands." .

ns1:59653 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Roan's Tan Yard, also known as the Battle of Silver Creek, was a minor battle fought during the American Civil War on 08 January 1862, in Randolph County. In 08 January 1862, Major W. M. G. Torrence of the Union Army located a Missouri State Guard base in Randolph County and attacked it on 08 January 1862 with elements of four cavalry regiments. When the American Civil War began in early 1861, the state of Missouri was politically divided. The Missouri State Guard then formed a camp in the vicinity of Yates, Missouri, in Randolph County, to attract and train new recruits. While scouting near Silver Creek in 08 January 1862, Union major, W. M. G. Torrence, of the 1st Iowa Cavalry Regiment, learned of the presence of a Missouri State Guard camp in the area. After the battle, the camp was destroyed; with the camp unusable, Missouri State Guard recruiting activities in Randolph County ceased." .

ns1:59677 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1972 NCAA Skiing Championships were contested in Winter Park, Colorado Buffaloes at the 19th annual NCAA-sanctioned ski tournament to determine the individual and team national champions of men's collegiate alpine, cross country skiing, and ski jumping in the United States of America. This year's championships were contested at the Winter Park Resort in Winter Park, Colorado Buffaloes." .

ns1:59679 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Dual Alliance, 1879 (German: Doppelte Allianz, Hungarian: Kettős Szövetség) was a defensive alliance between Germany and Austria-Hungary, which was created by treaty on October 7th, 1879 as part of Germany's Otto von Bismarck's system of alliances to prevent or limit war. When Austria-Hungary and Germany formed an alliance in 1879, it was one of the more surprising alliances of its time." .

ns1:59685 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 World Championship of Ski Mountaineering was the fourth World Championships of Ski Mountaineering sanctioned by the International Council for Ski Mountaineering Competitions (ISMC), held in Portes du Soleil, Switzerland, from February 23 to 2008." .

ns1:59688 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2004 European Cup took place on 2004 and 2004 at the Zdzisław Krzyszkowiak Stadium in Bydgoszcz, Poland. This was the 25th European Cup and the first time the event has been hosted in Poland." .

ns1:59705 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Sport 1 Open was a professional tennis tournament played on clay courts. It took place in Scheveningen, Netherlands between 08 July 2013 and 2013." .

ns1:59708 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015–16 Football League (Greece) is the second division of the Greek professional football system and the sixth season under the name Stoiximan.gr Football League after previously being known as Beta Ethniki. Its season began on 27 September 2015 and ended on 29 01 May 2016." .

ns1:59718 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 44th Daytime Creative Arts Emmy Awards ceremony, which honors the crafts behind American daytime television programming, was held at the Pasadena Conference Center in Pasadena on 28 April 2017." .

ns1:59719 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1985 Scheldeprijs was the 72nd edition of the Scheldeprijs cycle race and was held on 30 July 1985." .

ns1:59728 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Empire Slovak Open (previously known as the Empire Trnava Cup) is a tournament for professional female tennis players played on outdoor clay court. The event is classified as a $100,000 ITF Women's Circuit tournament and has been held in Trnava, Slovakia, since 2009." .

ns1:59737 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Porcelain Tour was a 19 date concert tour by Matt Cardle in support of his 2013 album Porcelain." .

ns1:59745 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1980 United States presidential election in Montana took place on 04 November 1980, and was part of the 1980 United States presidential election. Montana overwhelmingly voted for the Republican nominee, Governor Ronald Reagan, over the Democratic nominee, President of the United States Jimmy Carter." .

ns1:59746 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2011 French cantonal elections to elect half the membership of the general councils of France's 100 departments were held on 20 March 2011 and 27 March 2011. Since cantonal councillors are electors for the senatorial elections, and considering that the forces in the Senate are, as of 20 March 2011 almost balanced, the results of this election could play a role in the September 2011 senatorial elections." .

ns1:59760 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Ronnie Thomas was crowned NASCAR Rookie of the Year in a tight battle with Roger Hamby." .

ns1:59768 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of El Adde took place on 15 January 2016. Al-Shabaab militants launched an attack on a Kenyan-run AMISOM army base in the town of El Adde, Gedo, Somalia. On 15 January 2016, at 6:30 a.m before morning prayers, a suicide bomber detonated his explosive laden armoured personnel carrier to the front gate to an AMISOM garrison base in El-Adde, Gedo, Somalia." .

ns1:59789 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Russia participated in the Russia in the Eurovision Song Contest 2008 in Belgrade, Serbia. Dima Bilan represented Russia with the song \"Believe\", which qualified from the first semi-final of the competition and won the contest, placing 1st in the final with 272 points. The final took place on 2008 at the Akademicheskiy Concert Hall in Moscow, hosted by Oxana Fedorova and Oskar Kuchera. Among the competing artists, Dima Bilan represented Russia in the Eurovision Song Contest 2006; Premyer-Ministr represented Russia in the Eurovision Song Contest 2002 and Polina Smolova represented Belarus in the Eurovision Song Contest 2006. With changes to the format of the Eurovision Song Contest coming into effect in 2008, Russia was required to compete in one of two semi-finals in order to qualify to compete in the final. In the allocation draw on 2008, Russia was drawn to compete in the first semi-final on 2008. On 2008 during the draw for running order, Russia was drawn to receive the third of three wildcards for the first semi-final, allowing the Russian head of delegation to select the starting position for the nation. Russia won the 2008 Contest placing 1st and scoring 272 points. As the winning nation of the 2008 Contest and the host of the forthcoming contest, Russia pre-qualified to compete in the final of the Eurovision Song Contest 2009." .

ns1:59792 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "United Nations Security Council Resolution 1030, adopted unanimously on 14 December 1995, after recalling previous resolutions 968 (1994) and 999 (1995) on the situation in Tajikistan, the Council extended the mandate of the United Nations Mission of Observers in Tajikistan (UNMOT) until 15 June 1996 and addressed the process of national reconciliation in the country." .

ns1:59809 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The swimming at the 2000 Summer Olympics – women's 100 metre backstroke event at the 2000 Summer Olympics took place on 17 September 2000–18 September at the Sydney Olympic Park Aquatic Centre in Sydney, Australia. At only 16 years of age, Diana Mocanu made an Olympic milestone to become Romania's first ever gold medalist in swimming." .

ns1:59810 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "TWA Flight 800 (1964) was a Boeing 707 that crashed during take off on runway 25 at Leonardo da Vinci–Fiumicino Airport, Rome at 13:09 GMT on a flight to Athens International Airport, Greece on 23 November 1964. The accident killed 50 passengers and crew on board, with 23 passengers and crew surviving." .

ns1:59819 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "United Nations Security Council Resolution 1501, adopted unanimously on 26 August 2003, after recalling all previous United Nations Security Council resolution on the situation in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, particularly United Nations Security Council resolution 1484 (2003) and 1493 (2003), authorised countries participating in Operation Artemis in Bunia to assist the United Nations Organization Stabilization Mission in the Democratic Republic of the Congo (MONUC) as it was deployed around the town." .

ns1:59824 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Karbala bombings refer to a series of bombings in Karbala, Iraq in 14 April 2007. A suicide bomber killed at least 42 people in Karbala on 14 April 2007 and injured more than 160." .

ns1:59825 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1986 English Professional Championship was a professional non-ranking snooker tournament, which took place in 04 February 1986 in Ipswich, England." .

ns1:59830 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Senior women's race at the 1992 IAAF World Cross Country Championships – senior women's race was held in Boston, Massachusetts, United States, at Franklin Park on 1992." .

ns1:59844 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Bluff Cove Air Attacks occurred 08 June 1982, during the Falklands War." .

ns1:59853 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006 Korea Open Badminton Championships in badminton was held in Seoul, from 21 August 2006 to 27 August 2006." .

ns1:59856 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Monmouth Stakes is an American thoroughbred horse race on turf at Monmouth Park Racetrack in Oceanport. Monmouth Park Racetrack created the Monmouth Stakes when IEAH Stables said they wanted their Dual Classic winner, Big Brown, to compete in a turf race in the middle of September." .

ns1:59858 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1999 HEW Cyclassics was the fourth edition of the EuroEyes Cyclassics cycle race and was held on 15 August 1999." .

ns1:59861 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Bunkhouse Stampede was the third Bunkhouse Stampede professional wrestling event produced by Jim Crockett Promotions under the National Wrestling Alliance (NWA) banner. It was the only Bunkhouse Stampede event to air as a pay-per-view (PPV) event. The event took place on 24 January 1988 from the Nassau Veterans Memorial Coliseum in Uniondale, New York. In the main event, Dusty Rhodes won a Steel Cage Bunkhouse Stampede. The event comprised four professional wrestling matches that involved wrestlers from pre-existing rivalries, plots and storylines that were played out on Worldwide, Pro and World Championship Wrestling–Jim Crockett Promotions (JCP)'s television programs. The main match heading into the event was the Steel Cage Bunkhouse Stampede. On 24 January 1988 episode of World Championship Wrestling, Flair was scheduled to defend the title against Hawk at Bunkhouse Stampede. Before the event aired live on PPV, Sting and Jimmy Garvin defeated The Sheepherders (Butch Miller and Luke Williams) by professional wrestling in a non-televised tag team match. The main event was a Steel Cage Bunkhouse Stampede. The Bunkhouse Stampede event met little success and was badly criticized." .

ns1:59863 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Hudson River derby, also known as the New York derby, is the name given to the soccer local derby between the two teams based in the New York metropolitan area, New York Red Bulls and New York City FC." .

ns1:59865 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The triathlon at the 2004 Summer Olympics – men's was part of the triathlon at the 2004 Summer Olympics programme. The competition was held on Thursday, 2004 at the Vouliagmeni Olympic Centre in Athens." .

ns1:59890 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1949 Memorial Cup final was the 31st junior ice hockey championship of the Canadian Amateur Hockey Association. The George Richardson Memorial Trophy champions Montreal Royals of the Quebec Junior Hockey League in Eastern Canada competed against the Abbott Cup champions Brandon Wheat Kings of the Manitoba Junior Hockey League in Western Canada. In a best-of-seven series, held at Shea's Amphitheatre in Winnipeg, Manitoba and the Wheat City Arena in Brandon, Manitoba, Montreal won their 1st Memorial Cup, defeating Brandon 4 games to 3, with 1 tied game." .

ns1:59898 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The swimming at the 1996 Summer Olympics – women's 200 metre butterfly event at the 1996 Summer Olympics took place on 26 July 1996 at the Georgia Tech Campus Recreation Center in Atlanta, United States." .

ns1:59915 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Nobel Peace Prize was awarded to the Tunisian National Dialogue Quartet for \"its decisive contribution to the building of a pluralistic democracy in Tunisia in the wake of the Jasmine Revolution of 2011\". Tunisian singer/songwriter Emel Mathlouthi sang Kelmti Horra during the award ceremony at City Hall in Oslo, Norway, on 2015. It is the second highest number of candidates as of 2015, after 278 in 2014 Nobel Peace Prize." .

ns1:59917 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1994–95 Umaglesi Liga was the sixth season of top-tier football in Georgia. It began on 14 August 1994 and ended on 31 May 1995." .

ns1:59919 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Kuurne–Brussels–Kuurne took place on 26 February 2012. It was the 65th edition of the international classic Kuurne–Brussels–Kuurne and was won by Mark Cavendish of Team Sky in a bunch sprint." .

ns1:59922 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Mike Tyson vs. Mitch Green, billed as \"New York Is Busting Out\", was a professional boxing match contested on 20 May 1986. Only four days later, it was announced that Tyson would next face Mitch \"Blood\" Green, who was ranked one spot ahead of Tyson on the WBC heavyweight standings, on 20 May 1986 in Madison Square Garden." .

ns1:59931 a ns1:context_narration ;
