ns1:63689 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Montijo was fought on 26 May 1644, in Montijo, Spain, between Portugal and Spain forces. Although the battle ended with a Portugal victory, the Spain saw it as a strategic success as they claimed to have prevented Matias de Albuquerque from capturing Badajoz, despite Albuquerque having no intention of attacking the city. Portugal General Matias de Albuquerque knew the Spain were commanded by the Marquis of Torrecusa, a renowned military tactician, and wanted to affirm his own presence. While on march, the Portugal were confronted by a Spain force from Torrecusa's army led by the Baron of Mollingen consisting of 4,000 infantry and 1,700 cavalry. On 26 May 1644 the two armies met not far from Montijo, Spain. The six cannons of the Portugal initiated the hostilities, the Spain side soon replied, but very ineffectively. The Spain cavalry attacked the Portugal left flank, routing the 150 Dutch cavalry commanded by Captain Piper. Led by Mollingen himself, the Spain cavalry easily opened a breach in the centre of the Portugal positions, taking the Portugal artillery. Taking advantage of the Spain lack of reserves and dispersion, Matias de Albuquerque and his officers rallied some of the scattered troops and quickly retook the Portugal artillery. D. João da Costa, a Portugal artillery officer, efficiently used the artillery to stop the Spain forces from regrouping. The Spain playwrights Pedro Francisco Lamini and Sr. Durán composed respectively the comedy El más valiente Extremeño, Bernardo del Montijo, Spain, el segundo Don Rodrigo Díaz de Vivar and the poem in his collection Romances vulgares de valentías, guapezas y desafueros in honor to the Battle. The Portugal João Soares da Gama also did it in his Batalha do Montijo, Spain." .

