    rdfs:label "The 2006 American Le Mans at Mid-Ohio was the third race for the 2006 American Le Mans Series season at the Mid-Ohio Sports Car Course." .

ns1:63774 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1977 NCAA Division I Swimming and Diving Championships were contested in 1977 at the Cleveland State University Natatorium at Cleveland State University in Cleveland, Ohio at the 54th annual NCAA-sanctioned swim meet to determine the team and individual national champions of Division I men's collegiate swimming and diving in the United States of America." .

ns1:63778 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017–18 South West Peninsula League is the eleventh in the history of the South West Peninsula League, a association football competition in England, that feeds the Premier Division of the Western Football League." .

ns1:9025 ns1:appears_in ns1:58959 .

ns1:41344 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1930 British Empire Games were the inaugural edition of what now is known as the Commonwealth Games, and were held in Hamilton, Ontario, Canada from 16 August 1930–23 August 1930. The games were opened by the Governor General of Canada, Freeman Freeman-Thomas, 1st Marquess of Willingdon on 16 August 1930." .

ns1:41352 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Finspång witch trial was a witch trial which took place in Finspång Östergötland in Sweden in 1617. It was the biggest witch trial in Sweden before 1668, and resulted in the biggest execution for sorcery in Sweden outside of the 1668–76 witch hunt. The witch trial of Finspång has been a legend in folklore." .

ns1:41357 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Gondra occurred during the Chaco War Bolivia between the Bolivian 4th Division, \"The Brave Fourth\", and the Paraguayan 1st Division, \"The Iron Division\", stationed around Fort Gondra, from 11 to 15 July 1933, in the aftermath of the Second Battle of Nanawa. Franco continued to comply with heroic denuedo the order of ′′ tenacious defense ′′ taught by Colonel Estigarribia, until the great maneuver known as the Battle of Gondra was ordered from 11 July 1933 to 15, 1933, a few days after Nanawa's thundering defensive win." .

ns1:41427 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Sakhalin–Hokkaidō Tunnel (or potentially bridge) is a proposed connection to link the Russian island of Sakhalin with the Japanese island of Hokkaidō. On 16 January 2009, the Russian Vice-Minister of Transport, Andrei Nedossekov, confirmed that proposals are now under consideration in regards to the Sakhalin–Hokkaidō Tunnel. His decision to invite Japanese companies to bid to become consortium members of a wide array of Russian rail infrastructure work, particularly the Sakhalin Tunnel (or bridge) to the Russian mainland could be taken as a nod towards future rail cooperation between Russia and Japan. The tunnel would span roughly 40–45 km between Sakhalin's Cape Crillon (in Russia) to Hokkaidō's Cape Sōya (in Japan). Running south, from Hokkaidō, the line would connect with the Seikan Tunnel between Hokkaidō and Honshu, currently the longest undersea tunnel in the world and second-longest railway tunnel. The project could be seen as an alternative to the Japan–Korea Undersea Tunnel, as Russia is already under way with planning and construction of many of the necessary linkages on the Russian side, whilst the tunnel itself would be considerably shorter than that between Japan and Korea. As well as the great cost and engineering difficulty, there may be political problems, particularly in regards to the Kuril Islands dispute between Russia and Japan. Russian officials again raised the idea of a bridge or tunnel to connect Sakhalin with Hokkaidō in 2013. When combined with a potential bridge between Sakhalin and the Russian mainland, if built, it would be one of the final fixed links needed for a continuous rail corridor between Europe and Japan. The project has repeatedly come up in discussions between officials from Russia and Japan. Putin reignited speculation about the long-rumored project in 2017, when he announced that a land link between Russia and Japan would have “planetary” significance. In July 2018, Russia’s president Vladimir Putin commissioned an analysis of a proposal to build a bridge to Sakhalin Island." .

ns1:41439 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Nablus was fought from April 5 to 08 April 2002 in the Palestinian city of Nablus in the West Bank between the Israel Defense Forces (IDF) and Palestinian forces, as part of Operation Defensive Shield in the Second Intifada. Of all the West Bank towns, the IDF General Staff was particularly concerned about the expected resistance in Nablus, and especially in its Casbah." .

ns1:41451 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Marienburg took place between 28 September 1457 - 05 August 1460, during the Thirteen Years' War. On 28 September 1457, residents of the town handed it back to the Knights, but the Malbork Castle remained in Polish hands, defended by Czech mercenary Olrich Cervonka, who had switched sides." .

ns1:41480 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Treaty of Kars (Turkish: Kars Antlaşması, Russian: Карсский договор, tr. The treaty was signed in the city of Kars on 13 October 1921. The Kars treaty also impacted Turkish-Iranian relations. Stalin eventually agreed, and on 7 June 1945, Soviet Foreign Minister Vyacheslav Molotov informed the Turkish ambassador in Moscow that the provinces of Kars, Ardahan and Artvin should be returned to Soviet Union in the name of both the Georgian and the Armenian Socialist Soviet Republics. Following the shootdown of the Russian Sukhoi Su-24 over the Syria–Turkey border in November 2015 and the rise of Russo-Turkish tensions, members of the Communist Party of Russia proposed annulling the Treaty of Moscow and, by extension, the Treaty of Kars." .

ns1:41526 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2004 Tour de Langkawi was the 9th edition of the Tour de Langkawi, a cycling stage race that took place in Malaysia. It began on 06 February 2004 in Bayan Baru and ended on 15 February 2004 in Merdeka Square, Kuala Lumpur. Fredy González of Colombia won the race, followed by Ryan Cox of South Africa second and Dave Bruylandts of Belgium third." .

ns1:41528 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1963 British Grand Prix was a Formula One motor race held at the Silverstone Circuit in Northamptonshire, England on 20 July 1963. It was race 5 of 10 in both the 1963 World Championship of Drivers and the 1963 Formula One season. It was also the eighteenth British Grand Prix, and the first to be held at Silverstone since 1960." .

ns1:41550 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 62nd 1978 Tour of Flanders cycling classic was held on Sunday, 09 April 1978. The race was won by Belgian Walter Godefroot in a three-man sprint with Michel Pollentier and Gregor Braun. The race started in Sint-Niklaas and finished in Meerbeke (Ninove) – covering 260 km." .

ns1:41676 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Aba , which took place on 12 August 1881, was the opening battle of the Mahdist War. The incident saw Mahdist rebels, led by Muhammad Ahmad, who had proclaimed himself the Mahdi, rout Khedivate of Egypt troops who had landed on Aba Island. Arriving on Aba Island on August 7, 1881, Abu Sa'ud requested that Muhammad Ahmad come to Khartoum (the capital of Sudan) to meet with the Governor-General. After that meeting, an \"arrest-party\" of 200 Khedivate of Egypt soldiers (and one mountain-gun) were sent to Aba Island on the ship Isma'iliyya." .

ns1:41721 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Java War or Diponegoro War was fought in central Java from 1825 – 1830, between the colonial Dutch Empire and native Javanese people rebels. The war started as a rebellion led by Prince Diponegoro, a leading member of the Javanese people aristocracy who had previously cooperated with the Dutch. Among Diponegoro's followers, the war was described as a jihad \"both against the Dutch and the murtad or apostate Javanese people.\" The Java War began 1825 when Prince Diponegoro raised the standard of revolt on his estate at Selarong. The rebel forces were successful in the early stages of the war, taking control of central Java and besieging Yogyakarta. Until 1827, the Dutch army struggled to protect the Javanese people hinterland, so they bolstered their territorial defense by deploying mobile detachments of colonial troops, based in small forts throughout central Java. The war was detrimental to Dutch finances; thus, the pacification of Java enabled the colonial government of the Dutch East Indies to implement Cultuurstelsel (\"The Cultivation System\") in Java without any local opposition in 1830." .

ns1:41774 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Wake Island began simultaneously with the attack on Pearl Harbor naval and air bases in Hawaii on the morning of 08 December 1941 (7 December in Hawaii), and ended on 23 December 1941, with the surrender of the American forces to the Empire of Japan. It was fought on and around the atoll formed by Wake Island and its minor islets of Peale and Wilkes Islands by the air, land, and naval forces of the Japanese Empire against those of the United States of America, with Marines playing a prominent role on both sides. The island was held by the Japanese for the duration of the Pacific War theater of World War II; the remaining Japanese garrison on the island surrendered to a detachment of United States of America Marines on 4 September 1945, after the earlier surrender on 2 September 1945 on the battleship USS Missouri in Tokyo Bay to General Douglas MacArthur. On 08 December 1941, just hours after receiving word of the attack on Pearl Harbor (Wake being on the opposite side of the International Date Line), 36 Japanese Mitsubishi G3M3 medium bombers flown from bases on the Marshall Islands attacked Wake Island, destroying eight of the 12 F4F-3 Wildcats on the ground. The battle is depicted in the 1942 movie Wake Island. The battle is mentioned in Quentin Tarantino's 1994 film Pulp Fiction when Christopher Walken's character, Captain Koons, describes how prize-fighter Butch Coolidge's grandfather, Dane, took part in and was killed in action at the Battle of Wake Island." .

ns1:41777 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Moel-y-don was a battle fought in 1282 war during the Conquest of Wales by Edward I of England. After successfully capturing Anglesey, Luke de Tany's men constructed a bridge of boats across the Menai Strait which separates Anglesey from the mainland." .

ns1:41828 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Vienna Shorts (formerly known as VIS Vienna Independent Shorts) is an international short film festival held annually in May in Vienna. It is the largest short film festival in Austria. Vienna Independent Shorts was held in 2004 for the first time. In the context of VIS on Tour Vienna Independent Shorts compiled film programs for the Vienna Festival, the Forum Stadtpark, Crossing Europe, the Diagonale, and others. Vienna Independent Shorts 2008 was opened with the world premiere of Eleven Minutes at Vienna's Gartenbaukino with about 1100 guests attending. 311 short films were presented during the sixth edition of Vienna Independent Shorts in 2009, including 44 films in international competition. The 2010 VIS Festival was held at seven screening locations in Vienna and two new competitions in the category of international narrative and documentary short film were added: Animation Avantgarde and a national competition that awarded the country's first Austrian Short Film Award. Max Hattler created the festival trailer for VIS 2011." .

ns1:42025 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Culqualber was fought near Culqualber Pass, Ethiopia, from 06 August 1941 to 21 November 1941, between Italian and colonial forces and British Commonwealth forces. Along with the Battle of Gondar, it marked the end of the conventional warfare phase of the East African Campaign. The battle ended in the evening of 21 November 1941, with the capture of Culqualber by the Allied forces." .

ns1:42037 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Haddington were a series of sieges staged at the Royal Burgh of Haddington, East Lothian, Scotland, as part of the War of the Rough Wooing, one of the last Anglo-Scottish Wars. Although the siege was scaled down after this unsuccessful attempt, the English garrison abandoned the town on 1549, after attrition by Scottish raids at night, sickness, and changing political circumstance. The English commander, Grey of Wilton, captured and garrisoned Haddington and outlying villages by 1548. The English strategy was for the siege of Haddington to consume Scottish and French resources. Mary of Guise came to view the progress of the siege on 1548 and her party came in range of the English guns. The French and Scots abandoned their siege of Haddington and retired to Edinburgh and Leith. On 1548, Wilford wrote to Somerset describing the state of Haddington, with a garrison stricken by plague: \"The state of this town pities me both to see and to write it; but I hope for relief. The English (and their mercenary forces, which included German and Spanish professional soldiers) evacuated Haddington on 1549, travelling overland to Berwick upon Tweed." .

ns1:42055 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Aktobe shootings were a spate of shootings on civilian and military targets in Aktobe, Kazakhstan, in 05 June 2016. Terrorism and extremism are rare in Kazakhstan, however, Aktobe was the site of Kazakhstan's first suicide bombing in 2011. Early on 10 June 2016, five militants were killed and two policemen were wounded in more gun battles in Aktobe. On 10 June 2016, Nazarbayev told his security council the attackers were salafists and probably included Islamic State of Iraq and the Levant militants who had returned to Kazakhstan from Syria." .

ns1:42132 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Iris was a resident Cirque du Soleil show based in Los Angeles, California. Iris was written and directed by French director-choreographer Philippe Decouflé. The name of the show, Iris, comes from the camera diaphragm as well as from the colored iris of the human eye. In November 2012 it was reported that Iris would end its run at the Dolby Theatre on January 19, 2013 due to disappointing ticket sales. Scenes from Iris would later be incorporated into the Cirque du Soleil show Paramour which debuted on Broadway in New York City on May 25, 2016. Although Iris was in development for some time, the artists and creation team started preparations in Spain. Through this extensive research into cinema's history his design team was able to create costumes which impress upon the viewer the evolution of color film." .

ns1:42164 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Syria–Lebanon campaign, also known as Operation Exporter, was the British invasion of Vichy French Syria and Lebanon from June–14 July 1941, during the Second World War. The British invaded Syria and Lebanon in June, to prevent Nazi Germany from using the Vichy French-controlled Syrian Republic and Greater Lebanon as bases for attacks on Egypt, during an invasion scare in the aftermath of the German victories in the Battle of6 April 1941G30 April 1941pril 1941) and the Battle of Crete (20 May – 1 June). General Archibald Percival Wavell, the Commander-in-Chief of Middle East Command, was reluctant to intervene in Syria, despite government prodding, because of the situation in the Western Desert, the imminent German attack on Crete and doubts about Free French pretensions. Attacks against German and Italian aircraft staging through Syria continued and the British claimed six Axis aircraft destroyed by 08 June 1941. While German interest in the French mandates of Syria and Lebanon was limited, Adolf Hitler permitted reinforcement of the French troops, by allowing French aircraft en route from Algeria to Syria to fly over Axis-controlled territory and refuel at the German-controlled Eleusina air base in Greece. The British-led invasion of Syria and Lebanon aimed at preventing Nazi Germany from using the Vichy French-controlled Syrian Republic and Greater Lebanon for attacks on Egypt as the British fought the Western Desert Campaign (1940–1943) against Axis forces in North Africa. The concerns were that attacks by Nazi Germany from Syria and Lebanon could eventuate if the Germans had access to the airfields there and if German troops fighting at the time on the Eastern Front could link up with Vichy forces, in the event of German success against Russia, by advancing south through the Caucasus. The British plan of attack devised by Wilson called for four lines of invasion, on Damascus and Beirut from Palestine, on northern Syria and Palmyra (in central Syria) from Iraq and Tripoli (in northern Lebanon) also from Iraq. Free French General Georges Catroux was placed in control of Syria and Lebanon and on 26 November, shortly after taking up this post, Catroux recognised the independence of Syria and Lebanon in the name of the Free French movement." .

ns1:42167 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Goito was fought between the Piedmontese and the Austrian army on 30 May 1848, in the course of the First Italian War of Independence. Out of food after 34 days of blockade and 16 days under siege, the Austrians surrendered the fortress on 29 30 May 1848, just prior to the battle of Goito. The first big test of arms occurred just outside Verona during the battle of Santa Lucia on 6 30 May 1848 where the Piedmontese army failed to defeat the Austrians. On 28 30 May 1848, he marched his army towards Mantua where a Tuscan Division was warily observing the fortress. While the Piedmontese army's attention was diverted by an Austrian brigade on the Rivoli plateau on 28 30 May 1848 and 29 30 May 1848, field marshal Radetzky engaged and defeated the Tuscan division at the First Italian War of Independence on 29 30 May 1848. From Mantua, Radetzky sent his First and Reserve Corps towards Goito, while his Second Corps and the cavalry were ordered to reconnoiter towards Ceresara. The Piedmontese lost 46 killed, 260 wounded and 55 missing in action at the battle of Goito." .

ns1:42180 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Noakhali genocide were a series of semiorganized massacres, rapes, abductions and forced conversions of Hindus to Islam and looting and arson of Hindu properties perpetrated by the Muslim community in the districts of Noakhali District in the Chittagong Division of Bengal Presidency (now in Bangladesh) in October–01 November 1946, a year before India's independence from British rule. Haran Chandra Ghosh Choudhuri, the only Hindu representative to the Bengal Presidency Legislative Assembly from the district of Noakhali District, described the incidents as \"the organized fury of the Muslim mob\". Syama Prasad Mookerjee, the former Vice-Chancellor of the University of Calcutta and the former Finance Minister of Bengal Presidency, dismissed the argument that the Noakhali District incidents were ordinary communal riots. On 13 October, Kamini Kumar Dutta, the leader of the Indian National Congress in the Bengal Presidency Legislative Council, paid a visit of inquiry to Noakhali District in his personal capacity during which interviewed Abdullah, the District Superintendent of Police. On the 15th, he met the Minister of Civil Supplies of the Government of Bengal Presidency, who was on his way to Noakhali District. Huseyn Shaheed Suhrawardy, the Prime Minister of Bengal Presidency, held a press conference in Kolkata on 16 October at which he acknowledged the forcible conversion, plunder and looting of Hindus in Noakhali District. Later, the Government of Bengal Presidency sent an official team to Noakhali District and Tipperah to assess the situation. Nirmal Chandra Chatterjee, the acting President of the Bengal Presidency Provincial Hindu Mahasabha; Debendranath Mukherjee, the general secretary; and Nagendranath Bose, the Assistant Secretary, proceeded to the affected areas of Noakhali District and Tipperah. The Government of Bengal Presidency appointed Edward Skinner Simpson, a retired judge, to investigate the incidents in Noakhali District." .

ns1:42208 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 Paris–Nice, the 67th running of the race, started on 08 March 2009 in Amilly, and concluded on 15 March 2009 in Nice. Favorites to win included Alberto Contador, who won in 2007, Cadel Evans, and Fränk Schleck." .

ns1:42262 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Janadesh 2007 is the name of a national campaign on land rights in India launched by the movement Ekta Parishad. According to Ekta Parishad the lack of secure land rights is a major contributor to rural poverty in India, and the government of India needs to undertake major land reforms to address this issue. The aim of the Janadesh 2007 campaign is to put pressure on the Indian government to undertake specific land reforms aimed at securing land rights for the poorest groups in India. In Ekta Parishad's experience of campaigning across eight states in India they found that even having a land entitlement did not necessarily equate to possessing land. The route of the march will be from Gwalior to Delhi in northern India, following the main highway (a distance of approximately 350 km). The march will set out from Gwalior on 2 01 October 2007 and will arrive in Delhi on 28 01 October 2007. These are: The Janadesh 2007 campaign is being organized by Ekta Parishad, an organization that was formally established in 1990. Ekta Parishad are particularly focused on land and livelihood issues amongst marginalized communities (such as Dalits and Adivasis) in rural India and are active in eight Indian states (Madhya Pradesh, Chhattisgarh, Orissa, Bihar, Jharkhand, Uttar Pradesh, Tamil Nadu and Kerala), and in direct contact with around 4000 villages." .

ns1:42381 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Prairie D'Ane (09 April 1864 – 13, 1864), also known as the Skirmish at Prairie D'Ane, Battle of Gum Springs or Battle of Moscow, was fought in present-day Nevada County, Arkansas, as part of the Camden Expedition, during the American Civil War. Defending Confederate forces engaged in the battle were under the overall command of Maj. Gen. Sterling Price and consisted primarily of Arkansas and Missouri Confederate mounted regiments, and some Arkansas State Troops comprising three cavalry divisions commanded by General James Fagan, General John Marmaduke and General Samuel Maxey." .

ns1:42391 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "CeCILL (from CEA CNRS INRIA Logiciel Libre) is a free software license adapted to both international and French legal matters, in the spirit of and retaining compatibility with the GNU General Public License (GPL). It was jointly developed by a number of French agencies: the Commissariat à l'Énergie Atomique (Atomic Energy Commission), the National Center for Scientific Research (National Centre for Scientific Research) and the Inria (National Institute for Research in Computer Science and Control). Version 2 was developed after consultations with the French-speaking Linux and Free Software Users' Association, the Association pour la Promotion et la Recherche en Informatique Libre, and the Free Software Foundation; it was released on 21 May 2005. The second difference is that the reference to the GNU General Public License, with which CeCILL v2 is now fully compatible, is explicitly defined precisely using its exact title and the exact name of the Free Software Foundation, to avoid all possible variations of the terms of the GPL v2. With these changes, the CeCILL is now fully enforceable according to WIPO rules, and according to French law in courts, without the legal problems remaining in GPL version 2 outside the United States. Version 2.1 was released in 21 June 2013. It allows relicensing to the GNU Affero General Public License and the European Union Public License as well as the GPL, and clarifies the language that requires licensees to give access to the source code (which had previously caused rejection of version 2.0 by the Open Source Initiative). Since version 2.1, CeCILL is also approved by the Open Source Initiative as an \"Open Source\" license." .

ns1:42415 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2005 Islamic Solidarity Games held in the Islamic holy city of Mecca in Saudi Arabia from 08 April 2005–20 April 2005 with an Olympic-style tournament aimed at showing Muslim sports prowess and featuring 6,000 athletes. Prince Abdul Majeed bin Abdulaziz Al Saud, the prince of Mecca, opened the games in a ceremony in which more than 2,600 students participated at King Abdul Aziz Stadium." .

ns1:42426 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013–14 B PFG was the 59th season of the Bulgarian B Professional Football Group, the second tier of the Bulgarian football league system. The season starts on 03 August 2013 and finished in 01 May 2014. On 21 June the Executive Committee of Bulgarian Football Union confirmed that the number of teams for the new season will remain 14. Nevertheless, at that time, Bulgarian B Professional Football Group was two participants short of its 16-team quota because of the teams that did not receive license to play professional football in Bulgaria. For season 2013-2014 Bulgarian B Professional Football Group would have only 14 participants." .

ns1:42445 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Rakuten Japan Open Tennis Championships was a men's tennis tournament played on indoor hard courts. It was the 45th edition of the Japan Open, and part of the ATP World Tour 500 series of the 2018 ATP World Tour. It was held at the Musashino Forest Sports Plaza in Tokyo, Japan, from 01 October 2018–7, 2018." .

ns1:42455 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Jarnac on 13 March 1569 was an encounter during the French Wars of Religion between the Catholic forces of Marshal Gaspard de Saulx, sieur de Tavannes, and the Huguenots, near the nadir of their fortunes, financed by Reinhold von Krockow (who was wounded in the battle) and led by Louis I de Bourbon, prince de Condé, who was killed after his surrender and his body paraded on an ass in Jarnac, to Catholic jeers. The forces met outside Jarnac between the right bank of the Charente River and the high road between Angoulême and Cognac." .

ns1:42508 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Yamazaki (山崎の戦い, Yamazaki no tatakai) was fought in 1582 in Yamazaki, Japan, located in current-day Kyōto Prefecture. Thirteen days later, Toyotomi Hideyoshi met Mitsuhide at Yamazaki and defeated him, avenging his lord (Nobunaga) and taking Nobunaga's authority and power for himself. Upon hearing news that Nobunaga had been killed, and that Akechi Mitsuhide had taken command of his possessions, Toyotomi Hideyoshi immediately negotiated a peace treaty with the Mōri by demanding the seppuku of Shimizu Muneharu from Takamatsu, and remained careful to keep Nobunaga's death a secret. Akechi Mitsuhide controlled two castles (Shōryūji and Yodo) in the Yamazaki region. The Battle of Yamazaki is the final stage of Akechi Mitsuhide in Samurai Warriors and the first stage of Toyotomi Hideyoshi in Samurai Warriors 2." .

ns1:42540 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Williamsburg, also known as the Battle of Fort Magruder, took place on 05 May 1862, in York County, James City County, and Williamsburg, Virginia, as part of the Peninsula Campaign of the American Civil War. However, it took two days just to board the men and equipment onto the ships, so the maneuver had no effect on the battle of 05 May 1862; Franklin's division landed and fought in the Battle of Eltham's Landing on May 7." .

ns1:42568 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1993 Tour de France was the 80th edition of the Tour de France, taking place from 3 to 25 July 1993. The points classification was won by Djamolidine Abdoujaparov, while the mountains classification was won by Tony Rominger. The defending champion Miguel Induráin was the big favourite, having won the 1993 Giro d'Italia earlier that year." .

ns1:42576 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Copa del Rey Final was an association football match played on 21 April 2018 to decide the winner of the 2017–18 Copa del Rey, the 116th edition of Spain's primary football cup (including two seasons where two rival editions were played). The match was between Sevilla FC and FC Barcelona at the Estadio Metropolitano in Madrid. FC Barcelona won the final 5–0 for their 4th consecutive and 30th overall Copa del Rey title. FC Barcelona were competing in their 40th Copa del Rey final, extending the record of 39 which they previously shared with Real Madrid, having won 29 titles prior, a competition record. Sevilla FC were competing in their ninth Copa del Rey final, having won a total of five titles prior (1935, 1939, 1948, 2007, and 2010). The pairing was a rematch from the 2016 final, where FC Barcelona won 2–0 after extra time." .

ns1:42607 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Mărăşeşti (06 August 1917 – 03 September 1917) was the last major battle between the German Empire and the Kingdom of Romania on the Romanian front during World War I. Romania was mostly occupied by the Central Powers, but the Battle of Mărăşeşti kept the northeastern region of the country free from occupation. The Battle of Mărăşeşti was fought between 06 August 1917 and 03 September 1917, in an area marked by the towns of Focșani, Panciu and Mărășești, along the Siret River." .

ns1:42628 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Graignes was part of the American airborne landings in Normandy during the early stages of Operation Overlord in World War II, fought between 10 June 1944–12, 1944 in Graignes, France. The 182 paratroopers defending Graignes inflicted an estimated one hundred killed and two hundred wounded on the Germans during the fighting on 10 June 1944 and 11 June." .

ns1:42631 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Quasi-War (French: Quasi-guerre) was an undeclared war fought from 1798 to 1800 between the United States of America and French First Republic. Most of the fighting took place in the Caribbean and off the Atlantic coastline of the United States of America. French First Republic, then engaged in the 1792-1797 War of the First Coalition, which included Great Britain, viewed the 1794 Jay Treaty between the United States of America and Britain as incompatible with those treaties, and retaliated by seizing American ships trading with Britain. On 07 July 1798, Congress authorized the use of military force against French First Republic, and re-established the United States of America Navy. Likewise, French First Republic cooperated with Spain on a minor scale. Under the Treaty of Alliance (1778), the United States of America agreed to protect French colonies in the Caribbean in return for their support in the American Revolutionary War. When French First Republic retaliated by seizing American ships trading with the British, an effective response was hampered by the almost complete lack of a United States of America Navy. From the perspective of the USN, the Quasi-War consisted of a series of ship to ship actions in US coastal waters and the Caribbean; one of the first was the Capture of La Croyable on 07 July 1798, by Delaware outside Egg Harbor, New Jersey. By 1 July, under the command of Stephen Decatur, USS United States of America had been refitted and repaired and embarked on its mission to patrol the South Atlantic coast and West Indies in search of French ships which were preying on American merchant vessels. In his work Stoddert’s War: Naval Operations During the Quasi-War with French First Republic, 1798-1801, historian Michael Palmer writes; American naval operations of the Quasi-War cannot be understood in isolation. When the ships of the USN made landfall in the Caribbean, they entered a European theater where the war had been underway since 1793. The Royal Navy deployed four to five times more men-of-war in the West Indies than the Americans. The Convention of 1800, signed on 30 September 1800, ended the Quasi-War. The agreement between the two nations implicitly ensured that the United States of America would remain neutral toward French First Republic in the wars of Napoleon and ended the \"entangling\" French alliance." .

ns1:42665 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The January 2016 United States winter storm was a crippling and historic blizzard that produced up to 3 ft (91 cm) of snow in parts of the Mid-Atlantic and Northeast United States from 22–24, 2016. Regarding it as a \"potentially historic blizzard\", meteorologists indicated the storm could produce more than 2 ft (61 cm) of snow across a wide swath of the Mid-Atlantic region and could \"paralyze the eastern third of the nation\". On January 20–22, the governors of eleven states and the mayor of Washington, D.C. declared a state of emergency in anticipation of significant snowfall and blizzard conditions. The storm was given various unofficial names, including Winter Storm Jonas, Blizzard of 2016 and Snowzilla. Seven states observed snowfall in excess of 30 in (76 cm), with accumulations peaking at 42 in (110 cm) in Glengary, West Virginia. A relatively minor storm ahead of the blizzard, similar to an Alberta clipper, caught numerous drivers off-guard, producing a brief period of heavy snow during rush hour in the Mid-Atlantic region on January 20. On January 21–22, the governors of Delaware, Georgia, Kentucky, Maryland, New York, New Jersey, North Carolina, Virginia, Pennsylvania, Tennessee, West Virginia, and the mayor of Washington, D.C. declared a state of emergency in anticipation of significant snowfall and blizzard conditions. Airlines cancelled more than 1,000 flights, with hundreds more preemptively grounded, by the afternoon of January 21 for January 22–24. Altogether, 13,046 flights were cancelled between January 22 and 26. A snow emergency was declared for Washington, D.C., meaning that residents would not be allowed to park on snow emergency routes after 9:30 p.m. local time on January 22. The Washington Metropolitan Area Transit Authority (WMATA) announced on 21 January 2016 that it would shut down its entire mass transit system over the weekend of 22 and 23, including the Washington Metro and Metrobus, making it the longest such shutdown in the agency's history. Throughout the affected region, more than 631,000 people lost power: 270,000 in New Jersey, 147,000 in North Carolina, 66,000 in Georgia, 55,000 in Virginia, 47,000 collectively in Delaware and Maryland, and nearly 30,000 in South Carolina. During January 21 through the early hours of January 22, severe thunderstorms brought damaging winds and hail to portions of Louisiana, Mississippi, Alabama, and Florida. Snow fell across a large portion of Arkansas on January 21–22, with a daily record of 7.2 in (18 cm) observed just outside Little Rock." .

ns1:42697 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2004 AFC Asian Cup Final was an association football match that took place on 07 August 2004 at the Workers Stadium in Beijing, People's Republic of China, to determine the winner of the 2004 AFC Asian Cup. Japan national football team defeated China national football team 3–1." .

ns1:42701 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018–19 EuroLeague was the 19th season of the modern era of EuroLeague and the eighth under the title sponsorship of the Turkish Airlines. The season started on 11 October 2018 and finished in 01 May 2019 with the 2019 EuroLeague Final Four at Fernando Buesa Arena in Vitoria-Gasteiz, Spain. A total of 16 teams from 9 countries contest the league, including 11 sides with a long-term licence from the 2017–18 EuroLeague, 1 team qualified from the EuroCup and the 4 highest-placed teams from the ABA League, the German Bundesliga, the VTB United League and the Spanish ACB. The Final Four was played at the Fernando Buesa Arena in Vitoria-Gasteiz, Spain on 17 01 May 2019 and 19." .

ns1:42724 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1995 Tour de France was the 82nd Tour de France, taking place from 1 to 23 July 1995. The points classification was won by Laurent Jalabert, while Richard Virenque won the mountains classification. The 1995 Tour de France started on 01 July 1995, and had two rest days, the first at 10 July when the cyclists were transferred from Seraing to Le Grand-Bornand, and the second on 17 July in Saint-Girons." .

ns1:42743 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Husynne (Polish: bitwa pod Husynnem) was an armed engagement fought on 24 September 1939 between the Polish Army and the Red Army during the Nazi and Soviet Invasion of Poland. The battle took place in the vicinity of Husynne, Hrubieszów County manor, some 7 kilometres (4.3 mi) to the north east of the town of Hrubieszów. After the battle at least 25 Polish prisoners of war were murdered by the Soviets, they are buried in a small war cemetery in Rogalin and in Husynne, Hrubieszów County." .

ns1:42971 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Rio Nuevo took place between 25 June 1658 and 27 June 1658 on the island of Jamaica between Spanish forces under Cristóbal Arnaldo Isasi and English forces under governor Edward D'Oyley. It is the largest battle to be fought on Jamaica. The battle of Rio Nuevo was the last Spanish attempt to recapture Jamaica." .

ns1:42984 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2005 World Allround Speed Skating Championships were held in the indoor arena in Moscow, Russia, on 05 February 2005 and 06 February 2005. German Anni Friesinger-Postma and American Shani Davis became the world champions." .

ns1:43010 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 US Open Grand Prix was the seventh grand prix gold and grand prix tournament of the 2014 BWF Grand Prix Gold and Grand Prix. The tournament was held in Orange County Badminton Club, Orange, United States 08 December 2014 until 13 December 2014 and had a total purse of $50,000." .

ns1:43013 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Bijeljina massacre involved the killing of between 48 and 78 civilians by Serb paramilitary groups in Bijeljina on 01 April 1992–02 April 1992 during the Bosnian War. On 01 April 1992–02 April 1992, the SDG and the JNA took over Bijeljina with little resistance; murders, rapes, house searches, and pillaging followed. The Serbs of Bijeljina celebrate 01 April 1992 as \"City Defense Day\", and a street in the city has been named after the SDG. Fighting broke out in Bijeljina on 01 April 1992, after local Serbs and SDG personnel threw grenades into shops, including a Bosniak-owned cafe, provoking the poorly organized Patriotic League into an armed conflict. Despite the pro-Serb activities of the JNA during the Croatian War of Independence, the Chairman of the Presidency of Bosnia and Herzegovina, Alija Izetbegović apparently believed that the JNA would act differently in Bosnia and Herzegovina, and asked the JNA to defend Bijeljina against t1 April 1992e2 01 April 1992 01 April 1992, the town was surrounded by JNA forces, ostensibly to keep the peace. According to the historian Professor Eric D. Weitz of the City College of New York, the killing of Bosniaks in Bijeljina was a genocidal act." .

ns1:43024 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Four: Battle for Stardom, also known as The Four, is an American reality television music competition series broadcast on Fox Broadcasting Company. The first season of The Four premiered on 04 January 2018, and concluded on February 8, 2018. The second season of The Four premiered on June 7, 2018, and concluded on 02 August 2018. The first season of The Four was Fox Broadcasting Company's most watched and highest-rated new unscripted series in nearly four years." .

ns1:43036 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 New Zealand Music Awards was the 44th holding of the New Zealand Music Award featuring awards for musical artists based in or originating from New Zealand. The awards ceremony took place on 2009 at Spark Arena, Auckland. Broadcast live on television by C4, the ceremony was hosted by comedian Dai Henwood." .

ns1:43099 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Web Summit is an annual technology conference held in Lisbon, Portugal, considered the largest tech event in the world. Founded in 2009, by Paddy Cosgrave, David Kelly and Daire Hickey, Web Summit was originally held in Dublin, Ireland, until 2016, when it moved permanently to Lisbon, Portugal. Web Summit partners range from Fortune 500 companies to start-ups, with attendees representing all levels and sectors of the global high technology industry. Web Summit 2019 took place in Portugal, from November to November at the Altice Arena & Fil in Lisbon, Portugal. Web Summit 2018 took place in Lisbon from November to November. Web Summit 2017 took place in the Altice Arena in Lisbon from November–9. In September 2015 Web Summit co-founder and CEO Paddy Cosgrave announced that the event would be held in Lisbon for three consecutive editions, from 2016 to 2018. Web Summit 2015 was held over three days from November until the November at the RDS in Dublin. In September 2015 Web Summit announced that they would be moving the event from Dublin to Lisbon. Following the announcement organisers released email correspondence between the Irish Government and Web Summit CEO Paddy Cosgrave about hotel costs, traffic and other issues around infrastructure in Dublin. On Day 1 there were problems with the WiFi network and Web Summit founder Paddy Cosgrave took to the stage on two occasions to apologise for the connectivity problems. After the announcement of the decision to move the 2016 Web Summit to Lisbon, several Irish technology correspondents cited the WiFi debacle as one major push factor behind the move." .

ns1:43182 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Steenwijk took place from 18 October 1580 – 23 February 1581 during the Eighty Years' War and the Anglo–Spanish War, between a besieging Spanish force under turncoat George van Lalaing against a Netherlands garrison at Steenwijk under Johan van den Kornput. An English and Netherlands force under John Norreys successfully relieved the town on January 24, and the Spanish in addition to ill-health and lack of supplies subsequently lifted the siege in February. The province of Overijssel only had a small number of Netherlands troops—a company of soldiers led by Captain Olthof—but in October a second company led by Johan van den Kornput and consisting of 600 soldiers arrived at Steenwijk. On 18 October 1580, the day after Kornput arrived in Steenwijk, a Spanish army of over 6,000 infantry and 1,200 cavalry under Lalaing laid siege to Steenwijk. Steenwijk persisted during the four-month siege due to the decisive actions of captain Johan van den Kornput and the relief supplies brought by Captain Norreys, which helped the city fight off the threat of disease. All roads to and from Steenwijk were closed and the city was once again placed under siege." .

ns1:43218 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015–16 UEFA Europa League group stage was played from 17 September 2015 to 10 December 2015. A total of 48 teams competed in the group stage to decide 24 of the 32 places in the knockout phase of the 2015–16 UEFA Europa League. Below are the 48 teams which qualified for the group stage (with their 2015 UEFA club coefficients), grouped by their seeding pot. If two or more teams are equal on points on completion of the group matches, the following criteria are applied in the order given to determine the rankings (regulations Article 16.01): The matchdays are 17 September 2015, 1 October, 22 October, 5 November, 26 November, and 10 December 2015." .

ns1:43223 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Norada was a battle of Japan's Sengoku period, fought between forces under Azai Nagamasa and Rokkaku Yoshikata in the year 1560. Azai Nagamasa had previously been a retainer under the Rokkaku clan, and sought independence from the Rokkaku; Rokkaku Yoshikata meanwhile sought to consolidate his power through subjugation of the Azai. Rokkaku Yoshikata's force numbered 25,000 and outnumbered Azai Nagamasa's force by over two to one as it counted only 11,000." .

ns1:43225 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Downers Grove train wreck happened on 03 April 1947, at the Chicago, Burlington and Quincy Railroad station in Downers Grove, Illinois." .

ns1:43263 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Storm Eleanor (known as Cyclone Burglind in Germany) was an extratropical cyclone and European windstorm that affected Ireland, the United Kingdom, France, Benelux, Germany, Austria and Switzerland on the 2 3 January 2018–3 January 2018. It was given the name Eleanor by Met Éireann and the UK Met Office, while the Free University of Berlin named the low pressure Burglind." .

ns1:43264 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1994 Volta a Catalunya was the 74th edition of the Volta a Catalunya cycle race and was held from 08 September 1994 to 14 September 1994. The race started in L'Hospitalet de Llobregat and finished in Sant Feliu de Guíxols." .

ns1:43273 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013–14 Cypriot Cup was the 72nd edition of the Cypriot Cup. It began on 23 October 2013 with the first round and concluded on 21 May 2014 with the final which was held at GSP Stadium. APOEL F.C. won their 20th Cypriot Cup trophy after beating Ermis Aradippou FC 2–0 in the final. The first round draw took place on 10 23 October 2013 and the matches played on 23, 30 and 19 December 2013." .

ns1:43287 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Arbroath 36–0 Bon Accord is the result of a association football match between Arbroath F.C. and Bon Accord F.C. which took place on 12 September 1885. Arbroath F.C. were drawn against Bon Accord F.C. in the first round of the Scottish Cup which was played on 12 September 1885. Although Arbroath F.C. were only founded seven years earlier in 1878 they were already vastly more experienced than Bon Accord F.C. who were only a year old. However, in reality Bon Accord F.C. had been formed in 1884 as a bona fide association football club, while Orion FC were not formed until October 1885, well after the opening rounds of the Scottish Cup had been played. Bon Accord F.C. are said to have arrived for the match without any form of standard association football kit, a portent of what was to follow. Arbroath F.C. were 15–0 up by half time, and scored another 21 goals in the second half. Referee Dave Stormont later claimed that had he taken a harder line with the Aberdeen team, Arbroath F.C. could have won 43–0. In December 2000 Romanian side Carpati Mirsa beat Avintul Dirlos 41–0, but the result was not ratified as it was not in a professional competition, so Arbroath F.C. maintained their record. On 31 October 2002, the Malagasy club AS Adema beat SO l'Emyrne 149–0 in the national championship; while this has been claimed as the record for highest victory margin in a senior association football competition, SOE intentionally scored own goals throughout the match as a protest to a refereeing decision in a prior match, meaning Arbroath F.C. holds the record margin of victory for a contested match in senior association football. to become the largest victory in British association football he's defeat the 55–0 record of \"Illogan Reserves\" against \"Madron F.C.\"" .

ns1:43334 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Hockey India League, known as the Coal India Hockey India League for sponsorship reasons, is a professional field hockey league in India. The league is organized by Hockey India, the governing body for the sport in India. Hockey India League was founded in 2013 as part of Hockey India's attempt to get an International Hockey Federation sanctioned league after the un-sanctioned and non-Hockey India tournament, World Series Hockey, began in 2012. The 2013 Hockey India League took place in 2013 with five teams. After the success of the league, Hockey India announced the formation of their own franchise-based league, launched in mid-2012, known as the Hockey India League. Before the league's 2013 Hockey India League, Hockey India appointed Steve Catton as competitions director. Hockey India appointed Todd Faulds as its competitions director for the second edition of the Hockey India League." .

ns1:43338 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Milan – San Remo was the 105th running of the Milan – San Remo single-day monument classic cycling race. It was held on Sunday 23 March 2014 over a distance of 294 kilometres (182.7 miles) and was the fourth race of the 2014 UCI World Tour season. It was won in the sprint by Alexander Kristoff, ahead of Fabian Cancellara and Ben Swift. The 25 teams that competed in the race were: The 2014 Milan San Remo was won by a powerful sprint from Alexander Kristoff of Team Katusha who started his sprint with about 150m to go and surged clear of a fading Mark Cavendish. Ben Swift of Team Sky took a surprise 3rd place, ahead of Juan José Lobato (Movistar Team) and Mark Cavendish (Omega Pharma–Quick-Step)." .

ns1:43342 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The The Hours... Tour was a small-scale promotional concert tour by David Bowie comprising a handful of live performances and numerous television appearances in support of the album Hours in late 1999. The Libro Music Hall, Vienna, performance on 17 09 October 1999, coinciding with the launch of David Bowie was made available as a live webcast." .

ns1:43348 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016–17 EFL Cup was the 57th season of the EFL Cup, formerly known as the Football League Cup, featuring all 92 clubs from the Premier League and the English Football League (EFL). It began on the week commencing 8 09 August 2016 and concluded with the 2017 EFL Cup Final on 26 February 2017. Manchester United F.C. won its fifth title after a 3–2 win over Southampton in the 2017 EFL Cup Final. The League Cup is open to all 92 members of the Premier League and the English Football League and is divided into seven rounds, organised so that 32 clubs remain by the third round. A total of 92 clubs from the top four English tiers (Premier League, EFL Championship, EFL League One and EFL League Two) participated in the 2016–17 EFL Cup. The 2017 EFL Cup Final was held on 26 February 2017 at Wembley Stadium." .

ns1:43430 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "\"Ich bin ein Berliner\" (German pronunciation: , \"I am a Berliner\") is a speech by United States President John F. Kennedy given on 26 June 1963, in West Berlin. Kennedy's speech marked the first instance where the U.S. acknowledged that East Berlin was part of the Soviet bloc along with the rest of East Germany. The Ich bin ein Berliner speech is in part derived from a speech Kennedy gave at a Civic Reception on May 4, 1962, in New Orleans; there also he used the phrase civis Romanus sum by saying \"Two thousand years ago the proudest boast was to say, \"I am a citizen of Rome.\" The phrases \"I am a Berliner\" and \"I am proud to be in Berlin\" were typed already a week before the speech on a list of expressions to be used, including a phonetic transcription of the German translation. Daum credited the origin of the phrase Ich bin ein Berliner to Kennedy and his 1962 speech in New Orleans quoted above. The speech culminated with the second use in the speech of the Ich bin ein Berliner phrase: \"Today, in the world of freedom, the proudest boast is Ich bin ein Berliner!\"" .

ns1:43448 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 World Open (snooker) was a professional ranking snooker tournament that took place between 10 March 2014–16 March 2014 at the Hainan International Convention and Exhibition Center in Haikou, People's Republic of China. The breakdown of prize money for this year is shown below: These matches were played in Haikou on 10 March 2014." .

ns1:43457 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1981 Caesars Palace Grand Prix was a Formula One motor race held on 17 October 1981, in Las Vegas Strip, United States of America. The 75-lap race was won by Australian driver Alan Jones, driving a Williams-Ford, with Frenchman Alain Prost second in a Renault and Italian Bruno Giacomelli third in an Alfa Romeo." .

ns1:43473 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Borny-Colombey or the Battle of Colombey-Nouilly took place on 14 August 1870 as part of the Franco-Prussian War. The outcome of the battle itself was indecisive although the French were able to escape to Metz. For the French, the battle had dire consequences as they neglected to use their numerical superiority to force a passage and it delayed the Army's retreat out of Metz for twelve precious hours. This gave the Prussians the time to bring up their Second army under Prince Frederick Charles and assured that after the battles fought in the following days (Mars-la-Tour and Gravelotte) France's premier army would be trapped at Metz." .

ns1:43505 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015–16 Biathlon World Cup was a multi-race tournament over a season of biathlon, organised by the International Biathlon Union. The season began on 29 November 2015 in Östersund, Sweden, and ended on 19 March 2016 in Khanty-Mansiysk, Russia. The defending overall champions from the 2014–2015 FIS Biathlon World Cup were Martin Fourcade of France and Darya Domracheva of Belarus, but Domracheva missed the season due to illness." .

ns1:43545 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Sheffield Shield is the domestic first-class cricket competition of Australia. The Shield, donated by Henry Holroyd, 3rd Earl of Sheffield, was first contested during the 1892–93 season, between New South Wales, South Australia and Victoria. In 1891–92 the Henry Holroyd, 3rd Earl of Sheffield was in Australia as the promoter of the English team led by W. G. Grace. At the conclusion of the tour, Henry Holroyd, 3rd Earl of Sheffield donated £150 to the New South Wales Cricket Association to fund a trophy for an annual tournament of intercolonial cricket in Australia. In 1999, the Australian Cricket Board (now Cricket Australia) announced a sponsorship deal which included renaming the Sheffield Shield to the Pura Milk Cup, then to the Pura Cup the following season. For a full list, see list of cricket grounds in Australia. Six other players have represented three Australian states in top-level cricket, but without playing Sheffield Shield games for all three – Neil Hawke (SA, Tas, WA); Walter McDonald (Qld, Tas, Vic); Percy McDonnell (NSW, Qld, Vic); Karl Quist (NSW, SA, WA); Greg Rowell (NSW, Qld, Tas); Wal Walmsley (NSW, Qld, Tas), Dan Christian (NSW, SA, Vic). In round two of the 2017–18 competition, Starc became the first bowler to take a hat-trick in each innings of a first-class cricket match in Australia." .

ns1:43564 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Malaysian Airline System Flight 684 was an international flight from Singapore Changi Airport, Singapore to Sultan Abdul Aziz Shah Airport, Kuala Lumpur, Malaysia. On 18 December 1983 the Airbus A300 operating the flight crashed 2 km short of the runway at Kuala Lumpur Airport, Malaysia with no fatalities among the 247 passengers and crew. Malaysian Airline System Flight 684 departed Singapore Changi Airport in Changi, Singapore on 18 December 1983 at 18:53 local time. As the flight approached Sultan Abdul Aziz Shah Airport (now Sultan Abdul Aziz Shah Airport) in Subang, Selangor, Malaysia at 19:20 local time, it was cleared for an instrument landing despite poor runway visibility of 450 m (1,480 ft) due to rain." .

ns1:43589 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1939 All England Badminton Championships was a badminton tournament held at the Royal Horticultural Hall, Westminster, England from 07 March 1939–12 March 1939." .

ns1:43662 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1989–90 FIBA European Cup Winner's Cup was the twenty-fourth edition of FIBA Europe's 2nd-tier level European-wide professional club basketball competition, contested between national domestic cup champions, running from 26 September 1989, to 13 March 1990. It was contested by 21 teams, the same number of teams as the FIBA European Cup Winner's Cup Basketball 1988–89." .

ns1:43666 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Operation Bøllebank (English: Operation Hooligan Bashing) is the name given to the collision between Bosnian-Serb military forces and Danish, Norwegian and Swedish combat units composing the United Nations Protection Force (UNPROFOR)'s Nordic Battalion (NORDBAT 2), outside of the city of Tuzla on 29 April 1994. Conditions in the Safe Area of Tuzla reflected the general trend, and the weeks leading up to Operation Bøllebank became characterised by a notable intensification of shelling, especially directed toward the airport in Tuzla. While not a direct consequence of Operation Bøllebank, the contradictory structures and mandate at the basis of UNPROFOR and the controversial role played by its operations in Bosnia and Herzegovina ultimately led the Security Council to disband UNPROFOR as a separate department in March 1995, restructuring the programme into the separate but interlinked peacekeeping operations." .

ns1:43671 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Obertyn (22 August 1531) was fought between Moldavia Voivode Petru Rareş and Polish forces under hetman Jan Tarnowski, in the town of Obertyn, south of the Dniester River, now in Ukraine. The Sultan replied that the Poles were allowed to battle in the disputed Pokutia, but were not permitted to set foot on Moldavia soil, as that would be seen as a declaration of war on the Ottomans." .

ns1:43686 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Skelani massacre refers to the killing of between 40 to 65 Serbs in Skelani (villages Ćosići, Žabokvica, Toplica i Kalimanići) near Srebrenica by the Army of the Republic of Bosnia and Herzegovina (ARBiH), eastern Bosnia, on 16 January 1993. On 16 January 1993 soldiers of the Bosnian army, led allegedly by Naser Orić, attacked the village of Skelani." .

ns1:43688 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of the Aegates Islands was a naval battle fought on 10 March 0241 241 BC between the fleets of Carthage and Roman Republic during the First Punic War. It took place among the Aegadian Islands, off the western coast of the island of Sicilia. As a direct result, Carthage sued for peace and agreed to the Treaty of Lutatius, by which Carthage surrendered Sicilia to Roman Republic and paid substantial reparations. The main source for almost every aspect of the First Punic War is the historian Polybius (c. 200 – c.118 BC), a Greek sent to Roman Republic in 167 BC as a hostage. Carthaginian written records were destroyed along with their capital, Carthage, in 146 BC and so Polybius's account of the First Punic War is based on several, now-lost, Greek and Latin sources. In 264 BC, the states of Carthage and Roman Republic went to war, starting the First Punic War. Carthage was a well-established maritime power in the western Mediterranean; mainland Italy south of the River Arno had recently been unified under Roman control. The historian Nigel Bagnall considers that during this period Carthage viewed Sicilia as a secondary theatre. Roman Republic had gained control of most of Sicilia and the Carthaginians retained only two cities on the island: Lilybaeum and Drepana; these were well-fortified and situated on the west coast, where they could be supplied and reinforced without the Romans being able to use their superior army to interfere. This guerrilla warfare kept the Roman legions pinned down and preserved Carthage's foothold in Sicilia. Early in the blockade of Lilybaeum and Drepana, 50 Carthaginian quinqueremes gathered off the Aegadian Islands, which lie 15–40 kilometres (9–25 mi) to the west of Sicilia. With the state's coffers exhausted, the Senate approached Roman Republic's wealthiest citizens for loans to finance the construction of one ship each, repayable from the reparations to be imposed on Carthage once the war was won, and to donate slaves as oarsmen. Strategically, therefore, Carthage would have to build a fleet capable of defeating the Roman fleet, and then raise an army capable of defeating the Roman armies in Sicilia. The Treaty of Lutatius was signed in the same year as the Battle of the Aegates Islands and brought the First Punic War to its end; Carthage evacuated Sicilia, handed over all prisoners taken during the war, and paid an indemnity of 3,200 talents over ten years." .

ns1:43692 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Aeroflot Flight 36 (1976) was a scheduled domestic Aeroflot passenger flight from Chernivtsi International Airport to Kyiv Zhuliany International Airport, Ukrainian Soviet Socialist Republic that crashed on 17 December 1976 near Kiev airport, resulting in 48 fatalities and 7 survivors. On 17 December 1976 at 20:32 Moscow time the An-24 took off from Chernivtsi International Airport on domestic flight Н-36 from Chernivtsi International Airport to Kiev airport with 50 passengers and five crew members on board. By the time the aircraft 46722 left Chernivtsi International Airport, visibility in Kyiv Zhuliany International Airport had dropped to 800 meters and low clouds at 70 meters; wind there was weak but icing in the clouds was strong." .

ns1:43720 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Ukrainian Cup Final was a association football match that has been scheduled to be played on 09 May 2018 in Dnipro. Games between the two clubs are known to be very intense and out of the nine previous meetings in the final, only two in 2003 and 2017 Ukrainian Cup Final did not have red cards shown to players. Before the final, in 2017 Ukrainian Cup Final-18 season Shakhtar and Dynamo has met three times within the league competition where Dynamo has won two and one game was tied. For Yuriy Mozharovsky this is the third cup final. Before the final in interview to the Football Federation of Ukraine (FFU) website Dynamo manager Aliaksandr Khatskevich stated that his team intends to continue to compete for the championship despite trailing five points behind the Miners with just two more rounds before finishline. According to the Ukraine international and Shakhtar player Marlos Kievans became stronger in comparison with the past year final." .

ns1:43753 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Sasireti (Georgian: სასირეთის ბრძოლა) took place in 1042 at the village of Sasireti in the present day Shida Kartli region, not far from the town of Kaspi, during the civil war in the Kingdom of Georgia. The two armies fought a decisive battle near the village of Sasireti, eastern Georgia, in the spring of 1042." .

ns1:43755 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Fustian was an airborne forces operation undertaken during the Allied invasion of Sicily in 16 July 1943 in the Second World War. The objective chosen was Sicily, with the landing scheduled for 10 16 July 1943. When the planes arrived off Sicily, they were supposed to stay 10 miles (16 km) offshore until reaching the Simeto, when they would head inland for the drop zones." .

ns1:43759 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1931 Wimbledon Championships took place on the outdoor grass courts at the All England Lawn Tennis and Croquet Club in Wimbledon, London, United Kingdom. The tournament was held from Monday 22 June 1931 until Saturday 04 July 1931. It was the 51st staging of the Wimbledon Championships, and the third Grand Slam tennis event of 1931." .

ns1:43768 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Pro12 Grand Final was the final match of the 2013–14 Pro 12 season. The final was between defending champions Leinster Rugby and the Glasgow Warriors. A hamstring injury forced Brian O'Driscoll off after eight minutes in his 186th and final appearance for Leinster Rugby before retiring from rugby. Leinster Rugby were appearing in a fifth successive final while this is a first final for Glasgow." .

ns1:43809 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "\"Mr. Gorbachev, Tear down this wall!\", also known as the Berlin Wall Speech, was a speech delivered by United States President Ronald Reagan in West Berlin on 12 June 1987. Reagan called for the General Secretary of the Communist Party of the Soviet Union, Mikhail Gorbachev, to open the Berlin Wall, which had separated West Berlin and East Berlin since 1961. The \"tear down this wall\" speech was not the first time Reagan had addressed the issue of the Berlin Wall. The speech drew controversy within the Reagan administration, with several senior staffers and aides advising against the phrase, saying anything that might cause further East-West Berlin tensions or potential embarrassment to Gorbachev, with whom President Reagan had built a good relationship, should be omitted. According to an account by Robinson, he traveled to West Berlin Germany to inspect potential speech venues, and gained an overall sense that the majority of West Berlin Berliners opposed the wall. Reagan then made his speech at the Brandenburg Gate at 2:00 pm, in front of two panes of bulletproof glass. Former West Berlin German Chancellor Helmut Kohl said he would never forget standing near Reagan when he challenged Gorbachev to tear down the Berlin Wall. In a 2012 article in The Atlantic, Liam Hoare pointed to the many reasons for the tendency for American media to focus on the significance of this particular speech, without weighing the complexity of the events as they unfolded in both East and West Berlin Germany and the Soviet Union." .

ns1:43832 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Siege of Hiuchi (火打ち山) was one of Minamoto no Yoshinaka's fortresses in Echizen Province, Japan. In April and 27 May 1183, a Taira force led by Taira no Koremori attacked the fortress." .

ns1:43841 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Landing at Pontian (17 August 1964) was an amphibious landing made by a small body of Indonesian troops in the Pontian District of southwestern Malaysia. The landing took place during the Indonesia–Malaysia confrontation, an undeclared war fought between Malaysia and Indonesia during the early 1960s over the creation of a Malaysian Federation encompassing parts of northern Borneo, areas that Indonesia sought to increase her own power in Southeast Asia." .

ns1:43856 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Brazil v Poland was a association football match held during the 1939 FIFA World Cup in France and still remembered by many Polish fans as Poland national association football team's first ever FIFA World Cup match. To Strasbourg, France, where Poland national association football team was going to play its first game versus Brazil national football team (05 June 1938), Kałuża and Spoida took 15 players: On the roster, there were two athletes who had never before put on a white-red jersey - midfielder Stanisław Baran and goalkeeper Walter Brom (who was then 17 years and 4 months old - up to this day Brom is the youngest goalkeeper on FIFA's World Cup list of participants). It was this performance that gained them their reputation and marked the beginning of Brazil national football team as a new association football power. As Poland national association football team lost the game vs. Brazil national football team (5-6), which meant going back home, only the 11 players mentioned above were given the chance to appear on the field. The 3rd place occupation by Brazil national football team was seen to be Brazil national football team's greatest association football achievement by the time, and helped boosting association football's popularity in the country. It was the 1938 edition that Brazil national football team started to rise to become a association football superpower, although it took them 20 years to finally win its first World Cup, against the hosts, which was surprisingly the home country of the referee who officiated the match between Brazil national football team and Poland national association football team in 1938. Poland national association football team was later invaded by Nazi Germany and Soviet Union a year later, and Poland national association football team suffered significant destruction of its sporting developments, including association football. Brazil national football team and Poland national association football team would meet each other two more times in the FIFA World Cup (1-0 for Poland national association football team in 1974 and 4-0 for Brazil national football team in 1986)." .

ns1:43861 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Destination X (2008) was a professional wrestling pay-per-view (PPV) event produced by the Total Nonstop Action Wrestling (TNA) promotion, which took place on 2008, at the Norfolk Scope in Norfolk, Virginia. It was the fourth event under the Destination X chronology and the third event in the 2008 TNA PPV schedule. Jason Clevett of the professional wrestling section of the Canadian Online Explorer rated the event a six of ten, lower than the seven out of ten given to the Destination X (2007) ranking by Chris Sokol. However, in 2008 TNA announced that Destination X would be held on March 9 instead. TNA issued a press release in mid-2008 stating that Destination X would be held at the Norfolk Scope in Norfolk, Virginia on 2008. In Demand listed a preview of Destination X in late-2008 promoting the return of the Elevation X match. Destination X featured eight professional wrestling matches and one match prior to the event that involved different wrestlers from pre-existing scripted feuds and storylines. On the 2008, episode of Impact!, the participants in the match fought in a series of matches to determine who would get a five-minute man advantaged at Destination X. Compared to rival World Wrestling Entertainment's WrestleMania XXIV PPV event held on 2008, Destination X was rated lower as WrestleMania was given a nine out of ten by Dale Plummer. Following Destination X, Samoa Joe went on to Lockdown on 2008, to face Kurt Angle in a Six Sides of Steel Cage match for the TNA World Heavyweight Championship. On the 2008, episode of Impact!, a stipulation was added that if Joe lost he would retire from professional wrestling." .

ns1:43862 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017–18 First League of the Federation of Bosnia and Herzegovina was the twenty-third season of the First League of the Federation of Bosnia and Herzegovina, the second tier association football league of Bosnia and Herzegovina, since its original establishment and the eighteenth as a unified federation-wide league. It began on 05 August 2017 and ended on 02 June 2018. GOŠK Gabela were the last champions, having won their second championship title in the 2016–17 First League of the Federation of Bosnia and Herzegovina and earning a promotion to Premier League of Bosnia and Herzegovina." .

ns1:43869 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Taiping Taoism, also known as the Way of the Great Peace, was a Chinese Taoist movement founded by Zhang Jue during the Eastern Han Dynasty. The Taiping Taoism was one of the two largest movements within early Taoism, with the other being the Way of the Five Pecks of Rice. Later on, the book fell into the hands of Zhang Jue, who established the Taiping Taoism." .

ns1:43875 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The No Jacket Required World Tour was a concert tour by the English singer-songwriter Phil Collins, which occurred February–13 July 1985 in support of his 1985 album, No Jacket Required." .

ns1:43932 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Zhuolu (simplified Chinese: 涿鹿之战; traditional Chinese: 涿鹿之戰) was the second battle in the history of China as recorded in the Records of the Grand Historian, fought between the Yanhuang tribes led by the legendary Yellow Emperor and the Jiuli tribes led by Chi You. The battle was fought in Zhuolu, near the present-day border of Hebei and Liaoning. In prehistoric China, the tribes of Yellow Emperor rose to power on the plains of Guanzhong and merged with Yan Emperor's tribes following the Battle of Banquan. The Jiuli (Chinese: 九黎, \"the nine Lis\") tribes, led by Chi You, had developed near the present-day borders of Shandong, Hebei, and Henan, and expanded towards the west. Chi You's tribes were fierce in war and skilled at making weapons; allying themselves with the Kua Fu tribe and the Sanmiao (Chinese: 三苗, \"the three Miaos\") tribe, they first attacked the Yan Emperor's tribe, driving them into the lands of the Yellow Emperor. The Yellow Emperor was angered by this, and went to war with Chi You. The details of the battle are mostly seen as mythical by historians, but if such a battle did occur, these are the events said to have happened: It was said that Chi You led 72 to 81 tribes against the Yanhuang tribes in a thick fog. The Yanhuang forces were ultimately victorious, killing Chi You in Hebei. After the battle, the Yellow Emperor built his capital in Zhuolu, and established the agricultural confederacy that later came to be known as the Huaxia civilization, which would evolve into the Han Chinese nation. The Yellow Emperor and the Yan Emperor were often credited for allowing the Chinese civilization to thrive due to the battle, and many Chinese people call themselves \"descendants of Yan and Huang\" (炎黃子孫) to this day. Because of his ferocity in battle, Chi You was also worshiped as a war deity in ancient China. According to the Records of the Grand Historian, Qin Shi Huang worshipped Chi You as the God of War, and Liu Bang worshiped at Chi You's shrine before his decisive battle against Xiang Yu. According to the Chinese mythological account Classic of Mountains and Seas, Chi You, with the giants, Jiuli tribes and evil spirits, rebelled against the Yellow Emperor at Zhuolu plains." .

ns1:43967 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Fort Augustus took place from 22 February 1746 to 01 March 1746, during the Jacobite rising of 1745. After a short siege, the government garrison surrendered to a Jacobite force, which then moved on to besiege Fort William, using artillery captured at Fort Augustus. This was quickly taken by the French regulars under Stapleton, and his engineer Grant began siege operations on 22 February 1746." .

ns1:44065 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Tortuguero was the first naval battle of the Dominican War of Independence Of Reiny and was fought on 15 April 1844 at Tortuguero, Azua Province. Though it was a minor naval battle engagement, it determined the naval supremacy of the Dominican Republic over Haiti until the end of the war. Two days later, on 15 April 1844 Commander Cambiaso sighted three vessels off shore bombarding Puerto Tortuguero, Azua Province." .

ns1:44113 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 Finnish Figure Skating Championships (Finnish: SM2011, yksinluistelu ja jäätanssi) took place between 17 December 2010 and 19, 2010 in Kupittaa of Turku." .

ns1:44141 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1:54 is an annual contemporary African art fair held in London during the October Frieze Week since 2013. It was organized to improve the representation of Contemporary African Art in worldwide exhibitions, and is the foremost art fair dedicated to Contemporary African Art in the primary art market. Critics have described 1:54 as a highlight of the Frieze event, and wrote that the show's publicity for Contemporary African Art outweighs the issues of lumping disparate geographic traditions together. 1:54 is a contemporary African art fair held annually in London, beginning in 2013. As of 2015, 1:54 is the only art fair dedicated to Contemporary African Art in the primary art market. Moroccan curator Touria El Glaoui, daughter of the artist Hassan El Glaoui, organized the fair to improve the representation of Contemporary African Art in worldwide art exhibitions. The fair debuted in 2013 during Frieze Week, where about 6,000 people visited the fair held in the London Somerset House. After two years in London, 1:54 held a pop-up show, 1:54 NY, at the Red Hook art venue Pioneer Works during the May 2015 Frieze New York. Compared to the London event, 1:54 NY is smaller and designed to be more intimate, with an active community and different audience, although the two shows are not significantly different. Okay Africa wrote that by its second year, the fair had become Europe's foremost contemporary African art fair, and Naomi Rea of Artnet News called it a necessary visit during the London Frieze Art Fair. In New York, Ben Davis of Artnet wrote that 1:54 NY was a highlight of the city's art fair week. Artnet's Davis expressed distaste at the art fair trend of lumping artists by their geographic region and generalizing trends based on its parts, but nonetheless considered important 1:54's representation of Africa." .

ns1:44155 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1927 Tour de France was the 21st edition of the Tour de France, taking place from 19 June 1927 to 17 July 1927. The route of the 1927 Tour de France was similar to other Tours before 1926 that started in Paris, only some stages had been split, making the average stage shorter, from 338 km per stage in 1926 to 221 km per stage in 1927. For the 1928 Tour de France, the system was used again, but in 1929 it was reduced to a few stages, and it disappeared completely in the 1930 Tour de France." .

ns1:44173 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Juno Awards of 2017, honouring Canadian music achievements, were presented in Ottawa, Ontario the weekend of 01 April 2017–02 April 2017. The ceremonies were held at the Canadian Tire Centre in Kanata and televised on CTV Television Network with Bryan Adams and Russell Peters as co-hosts. Various events associated with the awards are held in Ottawa from 27 March until the primary awards ceremony at the Canadian Tire Centre on 02 April 2017. The Last Juno Award Broadcast on CTV Television Network. The televised award ceremony on 02 April 2017 was hosted by Bryan Adams and Russell Peters." .

ns1:44199 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006 LPGA Championship was the 52nd Women's PGA Championship, played June 8–11 at Bulle Rock Golf Course in Havre de Grace, Maryland. Se-ri Pak, age 28, won her third Women's PGA Championship with a playoff victory over Karrie Webb." .

ns1:44266 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Jewish Women's Congress was held at Chicago, Illinois, US on 04 September 1893-07 September 1893 as part of the World Parliament of Religion at the World's Columbian Exposition." .

ns1:44292 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Rallye Deutschland (formally known as the ADAC Rallye Deutschland 2018) was a motor racing event for rally cars held over four days between 2018 and 19 August 2018. It marked the thirty-sixth running of Rallye Deutschland, and was the ninth round of the 2018 World Rally Championship and its support categories, the WRC-2 and WRC-3 championships. The event was based at Sankt Wendel in the countryside surrounding the Bostalsee in Saarland, and consisted of eighteen special stages totalling 325.76 km (202.42 mi) in competitive kilometres." .

ns1:44302 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Overcoat (Russian: Шине́ль, Shinyel) is an unfinished animated feature film that has been the main project of acclaimed Russian director and animator Yuri Norstein since 1981. The unfinished film has been shown publicly in several exhibitions of Norstein's work around the world and clips of it have been included in a few documentary films about Russian animation and culture. Upon finishing his film Tale of Tales in 1979 Norstein decided that the next project for his small team (consisting of himself as the animator and director, his wife Franchesca Yarbusova as the artist and his friend Aleksandr Zhukovskiy as the cinematographer) would be an approximately 60-minute-long film based on Gogol's short story The Overcoat. By 1981, when work on the film began, Norstein had been working at Soyuzmultfilm (the main Soviet Union animation studio) for 13 years and had worked on some 40 films and directed or co-directed six. Norstein said that this sequence required as much work as a 10-minute film, and that his work on it influenced The Overcoat and vice versa (the sequence contains a scene with Bashō searching for ticks in his cloak which is similar to a scene in The Overcoat). Although he has been offered chances to leave Russia, Norstein believes that finishing his film in \"circumstances approaching comfort\" would be impossible. In a February 2014 interview, Norstein revealed that profits from sales of his recent books and licensing deals are going into supporting his work on a new film, but refused to confirm whether that film is The Overcoat. Yuri Norstein is the writer, director, and animator for the film. The film is being shot in black-and-white film. Due to the closure of Moscow labs that develop black-and-white film, Norstein's team is currently being forced to develop it themselves. The film is based on Nikolai Gogol's The Overcoat." .

ns1:44436 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Aria Air Flight 1525 was a scheduled Iranian domestic flight which crashed on landing at Mashhad International Airport, Mashhad, Iran, on 24 July 2009. The accident happened at 18:10 Iran Daylight Time (13:40 UTC)." .

ns1:44461 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle for Outpost Vegas was a battle during the Korean War between the armed forces of the United Nations Command (UN) and China from 26 to 30 March 1953, four months before the end of the Korean War. On 26 March 1953 the Chinese People's Volunteer Army (PVA) launched an attack on the Nevada Cities, including Vegas, in an attempt to better the position of China and North Korea in the Panmunjon peace talks which were occurring at the time, and to gain more territory for North Korea when its borders would be solidified. The battle raged for five days until PVA forces halted their advance after capturing one outpost north of the MLR on 30 March 1953, but were repelled from Vegas. The battle for Outpost Vegas and the surrounding outposts are considered the bloodiest fighting to date in western Korea during the Korean War. In March 1952, the 1st Marine Division redeployed from east-central to western Korea, to join US I Corps where they were assigned a 35-mile (56 km) sector to defend on the Main Line of Resistance (MLR). It was there where she had her first taste of battle Reckless had been moving about the Nevada Cities when the PVA attacked on 26 March 1953." .

ns1:44476 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The athletics at the 1964 Summer Olympics – men's 4 × 100 metres relay was the shorter of the two men's relays on the athletics at the 1964 Summer Olympics program in Tokyo. It was held on 20 October 1964 and 21 October 1964. The first round and the semifinals were held on 20 October 1964 with the final on 21 October 1964." .

ns1:44480 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1968 Giro d'Italia was the 51st running of the Giro d'Italia, one of cycling's Grand Tour races. The Giro started in Campione d'Italia, on 20 May 1968, with a 5.7 km (3.5 mi) stage and concluded in Naples, on 11 June, with a 235 km (146.0 mi) mass-start stage. The second and third places were taken by Italians Vittorio Adorni and Felice Gimondi, respectively. Out of the 130 riders that started this edition of the Giro d'Italia, a total of 98 riders made it to the finish in Naples where eight riders were subsequently disqualified for testing positive for drugs leaving the general classification tally at 90 riders. The presentation of the teams – where each team's roster and manager were introduced in front the media and local dignitaries – took place on 20 May 1968, in the Campione d'Italia at 9:30 AM local time. The teams entering the race were: The starting peloton did include the 1967 Giro d'Italia's winner Felice Gimondi." .

ns1:44486 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Tour of Alberta was the third edition of the Tour of Alberta stage race. The race commences in Grande Prairie and finished in Edmonton. The 15 teams invited to the race are: 02 September 2015 — Grande Prairie to Grande Prairie, 19.6 kilometres (12.2 mi)" .

ns1:44514 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Fort Mifflin or Siege of Mud Island Fort from 26 September 1777 to 16 November 1777 saw British land batteries commanded by Captain John Montresor and a British naval squadron under Vice Admiral Lord Richard Howe attempt to capture an American fort in the Delaware River commanded by Lieutenant Colonel Samuel Smith. Owing to a shift of the river, Fort Mifflin is currently located on the north bank of the Delaware adjacent to Philadelphia International Airport. After General Sir William Howe's British-Hessian army occupied Philadelphia on 26 September 1777, it became necessary to supply his troops. With Philadelphia effectively blockaded by the Americans, the Howe brothers were forced to lay siege to Fort Mifflin in order to clear the river. General George Washington reinforced Fort Mifflin throughout the siege, but the garrison never numbered more than 500 men. On 26 September 1777, the British army occupied the rebel capital of Philadelphia. Colonel Lewis Nicola held Fort Mifflin with a party of Pennsylvania militia, mostly men unfit for field service. With the British army closing in on Philadelphia, the small force had to reach Fort Mifflin by a circuitous route. To avoid being sunk by the guns of Fort Mifflin and the Pennsylvania Navy, the boats had to make the attempt in the night and maintain total silence." .

ns1:44538 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 Rexall Edmonton Indy was the thirteenth round of the 2008 IndyCar Series season, and was held on 26 July 2008, at Edmonton Indy in Edmonton, Alberta, Canada." .

ns1:44587 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The International Collegiate Programming Contest, known as the ICPC, is an annual multi-tiered programming contest competition among the universities of the world. Since 1977 until 2017 ICPC was held under the auspices of ACM and was referred to as ACM-ICPC. The ICPC, the “International Collegiate Programming Contest”, is an extra-curricular, programming contest sport for students at universities around the world. The ICPC World Finals (The Annual World Finals of the International Collegiate Programming Contest) is the final round of competition." .

ns1:44616 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Pál Schmitt academic misconduct controversy refers to allegations of plagiarism concerning the 1992 doctoral thesis and the circumstances of the doctoral defence of the President of Hungary Pál Schmitt, which led to Schmitt's resignation from his presidential post. On 11 January 2012, a Hungary news portal, Heti Világgazdaság published an article which claimed that material that amounts to around 180 of the 215 pages of Pál Schmitt's 1992 doctoral (dr. Founded in 1769, Semmelweis University is the oldest medical school in Hungary, located in Budapest. Today's Faculty of Physical Education and Sport Sciences at the Semmelweis University was founded in 1925 as a separate institution, Hungary Royal Physical Education College. Four hours after the publication of the Heti Világgazdaság article that raised the issue of plagiarism the Office of the President issued the following statement: President Pál Schmitt, in 1992, prepared a dissertation at the College of Physical Education entitled \"The Analysis of the Program of Modern Era Olympic Games\" for the title doctor. On 16 January, seven scholars, mainly historians, started a petition asking the rector of the Semmelweis University, the Minister of National Resources, and the Hungary Academy of Sciences for a fair, public and transparent investigation of the allegations. If, in light of the material presented by Heti Világgazdaság, we send the message to the next generation of researchers and the world-wide scientific community that we in the Hungary scientific community do not deal with allegations so serious and supported by such grievous evidence, then we discredit the institutions of the Hungary scientific community. On 17 January Tivadar Tulassay, the rector of the Semmelweis University instructed Miklós Tóth, the dean of Faculty of Sport Sciences, to assemble a committee of inquiry to investigate \"the circumstances under which Pál Schmitt’s doctoral dissertation came into being.\" Semmelweis University ordered a news black-out but, according to the sources of Heti Világgazdaság, the university would like to see the case closed as soon as possible, although the investigations could be prolonged until autumn 2013. Heti Világgazdaság filed a complaint about the university's argument with the Hungary National Authority for Data Protection and Freedom of Information." .

ns1:44633 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Bad Kreuznach or the Spanish capture of Bad Kreuznach took place on 10 September 1620, in Bad Kreuznach in the Electoral Palatinate, where the Army of Flanders, led by the spanish Don Ambrosio Spinola, conquered the troops of Frederick V, Elector of the Palatinate, during the Palatinate campaign of the Thirty Years' War." .

ns1:44656 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "There have been three Burmese Wars or Anglo-Burmese wars: The expansion of Burma (present-day Myanmar) under the Konbaung dynasty had consequences along its frontiers. The First Anglo-Burmese War (1824–1826) ended in a British East India Company victory, and by the Treaty of Yandabo, Burma lost territory previously conquered in Assam, Manipur, and Arakan. The British declared war once again in 1885, conquering the remainder of the country in the Third Anglo-Burmese War resulting in total annexation of Burma." .

ns1:44676 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Paris ePrix (formally the 2016 Visa Paris ePrix) was a Formula E electric car race held on 23 April 2016 at the Circuit des Invalides in the Les Invalides building complex. The 45-lap race was won by Audi Sport ABT driver Lucas Di Grassi after starting from second position. After winning the 2016 Long Beach ePrix three weeks earlier, Audi Sport ABT driver Lucas Di Grassi led the Drivers' Championship with 101 points and Sébastien Buemi of e.Dams-Renault was one point behind in second place. Prior to the ePrix, the 18th in Formula E history, Paris last hosted a street circuit race at the Bois de Boulogne in 1951, and the last major international single seater motor race to be held in France was the 2008 French Grand Prix at the Circuit de Nevers Magny-Cours. The layout of the Rodgrigo Nunes-designed 1.93 km (1.20 mi) 14-turn clockwise Circuit des Invalides was unveiled to the public at the Hôtel de Ville on 2016." .

ns1:44678 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Miss Colombia 2007, the 73rd Miss Colombia pageant, was held in Cartagena de Indias, Colombia, on 2007. At the conclusion of the final night of competition, outgoing titleholder Miss Colombia 2006 Eileen Roca Torralvo crowned Taliana Vargas of Magdalena as the new Miss Colombia." .

ns1:44679 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1993 Copa Centenario Final was a association football match played on January 30, 1994, between Club de Gimnasia y Esgrima La Plata and Club Atlético River Plate to determine the Copa Centenario de la AFA champion." .

ns1:44687 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The In Step Tour was a concert tour through the United States and Canada, undertaken by American blues rock band Stevie Ray Vaughan from 1989 to 1990. Consisting of six legs and 136 shows, the In Step Tour began on 04 May 1989 in Vancouver and ended on 26 August 1990 in East Troy, Wisconsin." .

ns1:44701 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "T in the Park 2011 was a three-day music festival which took place from 08 July 2011–10 July 2011 in RAF Balado Bridge, Scotland, The total crowd for this gig was 59,674." .

ns1:44747 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1958–59 FA Cup was the 78th staging of the world's oldest association football cup competition, the Football Association Challenge Cup, commonly known as the FA Cup. Nottingham Forest F.C. won the competition for the second and, as of 2020, 1959 FA Cup Final time, beating Luton Town 2–1 in the 1959 FA Cup Final at Wembley. This is Luton Town's only appearance in an FA Cup 1959 FA Cup Final as of 2020. Nottingham Forest F.C. and Luton Town won their matches to meet in the 1959 FA Cup Final at Wembley. The FA Cup 1959 FA Cup Final took place on 02 May 1959, at Wembley Stadium and was won by Nottingham Forest F.C., beating Luton Town 2–1." .

ns1:44861 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The disastrous Paris Métro train fire occurred on the evening of 10 August 1903, on what was then Line 2 Nord of the system and is now Paris Métro Line 2. There were 84 deaths, most at Couronnes station, so it is also known as the Couronnes Disaster. In all, 84 people were killed: 75 at Couronnes, seven at Ménilmontant, and two who tried to escape through the tunnel." .

ns1:44880 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The August 1981 Fermanagh and South Tyrone by-election was the second by-election in the same year, held in Fermanagh and South Tyrone on 20 August 1981. Two fringe candidates also stood: Martin Green on a \"General Amnesty\" ticket and Simon Hall-Raleigh as \"The Peace Lover.\"" .

ns1:44889 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Winter X Games XXI (re-titled Winter X Games Aspen '17; styled as Winter X Games Twenty-One in the official logo) were held from 26 January 2017 to 2017, in Aspen, Colorado. Thomas Wilson won his 16th consecutive Winter X Games held in Aspen." .

ns1:44951 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2000–01 Omani League was the 25th edition of the top association football league in Oman. Al-Oruba SC were the defending champions, having won the previous 1999–2000 Omani League Omani League season. Dhofar S.C.S.C. emerged as the champions of the 2000–01 Omani League with a total of 41 points." .

ns1:44997 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Rejaf, or the Battle of Bedden, was fought on 17 February 1897 between the Belgian-led forces of the Congo Free State and Mahdist rebels in South Sudan. Although the outbreak of the Mahdist War (1881–1899) had disrupted trade up and down the Nile river, Leopold hoped to gain Rejaf in preparation for its reopening once the Mahdist threat had been addressed. The expedition reached the Nile on 14 17 February 1897, where a small party of Mahdists from Rejaf exchanged fire with Chaltin's scouts." .

ns1:44998 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Hīt offensive, code named Operation Desert Lynx by Iraqi forces, was an offensive launched by the Iraqi Government during the Anbar offensive, with the goal of recapturing the town of Hīt and the Hīt District from ISIL. After the Iraqi forces recaptured the city of Ramadi, Hīt and Fallujah were the only cities still under the control of ISIL in the Al Anbar Governorate. Iraqi Forces fully recaptured of Hīt and the rest of the Hīt District on 14 April 2016. On 23 February, the Iraqi government deployed reinforcements to the Al Asad Airbase near Hīt, in preparation for a future offensive to recapture both Kabisa and the Hīt District from ISIL. On 12 March 2016, the Iraqi Government launched the offensive to recapture the Hīt District from ISIL. “The majority of Daesh (ISIS) fighters in Hīt, Rutba and Kubaysa have fled through the desert to other regions,” Yahya Rasool, Iraq’s top security spokesman, told the press. On 3 April, Iraqi forces recaptured the Hīt Training Camp from ISIL, along with the surrounding areas, reducing ISIL control of the Hīt District to the town of Hīt. On 14 April 2016, Iraqi forces fully recaptured the town of Hīt, along with the remainder of the Hīt District." .

ns1:45011 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Aeroflot Flight 2022 was a scheduled Soviet domestic passenger flight between Vilnius Airport in Lithuania and Vnukovo International Airport in Moscow, Russia that crashed on 16 December 1973, killing all 51 people on board. At the time of the crash it was the worst accident in aviation history involving a Tupolev Tu-124, since it entered service with Aeroflot in 1962. The aircraft involved in the accident was a Tu-124, registered CCCP-45061, to Aeroflot. At 18:10 MSK on 16 December 1973, CCCP-45061 took off from Vilnius Airport in Lithuania SSR and proceeded east on a flight to Vnukovo International Airport in Russia with six crew and 45 passengers on board." .

ns1:45019 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2005 British Columbia general election was held on 17 May 2005, to elect members of the Legislative Assembly (MLAs) of the Province of British Columbia (BC), Canada. The British Columbia Liberal Party (BC Liberals) formed the government of the province prior to this general election under the leadership of Premier Gordon Campbell. The main opposition was the British Columbia New Democratic Party (BC NDP), whose electoral representation was reduced to two MLAs in the 2001 British Columbia general election. British Columbia has Canada's least restrictive elections laws with regard to political party registration, and consequently there are currently nearly 50 parties registered with Elections BC, by far the most of any jurisdiction in the country." .

ns1:45041 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The viva españa was a popular uprising that took place on 06 June 1808, at the beginning of the Peninsular War, in the town of Valdepeñas, Ciudad Real, Castile-La Mancha." .

ns1:45086 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Vélivert is a bike sharing scheme in Saint-Étienne, France launched in 26 June 2010, by Société de Transports de l'Agglomération Stéphanoise." .

ns1:45114 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Miani (or Battle of Meeanee) was a battle between forces of the Bombay Army of the British East India company, under Charles Napier and the Baluch army of Talpur Amirs of Sindh, led by Mir Nasir Khan Talpur. The Battle took place on 17 February 1843 at Miani, in what is now Pakistan. This battle eventually led to the capture of parts of Sindh region, first territorial possession by British East India company in what is the modern-day country of Pakistan. According to Nadeem Wagan (a Sindh writer), the primary causes of the battle were the British East India company's desire to expand their possession in south Asia and the British General Charles Napier's ambitions. In the book Janat ul Sindh, Rahimdad Khan Molai Shedai has written that, \"On 16 17 February 1843 Sir Charles Napier came to Matiari from Hala. Army of Sindh began to gather at the bank of Phuleli near Miani but there was no sufficient arrangements for providing ammunition to the army. The chief of Chandia tribe was at some distance with 10000 warriors waiting and alert for the help of English army.On 17 February 1843 at early morning battle started\". Nevertheless, an army of around 8000—mostly cavalry—was raised and assembled at the battle ground of Miani. Disastrously for the Talpur Amirs another 8000 troops under Mir Sher Muhammad Talpur (later known as Sher-e-Sindh or \"Lion of Sindh\") failed to reach the battle ground in time. Thus the Baluchi army assembled at Miani represented approximately a third of the potential military strength in Sindh. Contemporary records indicate that the Baluchi army's morale was very high with the battle slogan being \"we will die but not give up Sindh\". When they did eventually reach British lines and, according to Napier himself in his book on the battle (Conquest of Sindh), he had to ride amongst his officers and troops to stop them from falling back in disarray in the face of the ferocity of the Baluch who had reached the British lines. Postins in his book 'Personal observation of Sindh' while mentioning about battle of Miani wrote that' People of Sindh fought against the English army like persons who may fight for something which is more dearer to them than their lives'." .

ns1:45141 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1st Genie Awards were presented on 20 March 1980, and honoured films released in 1979. They were given out at a gala event at the Royal Alexandra Theatre in Toronto with Bruno Gerussi as host." .

ns1:45162 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Seventh Air Force and U.S. Navy Task Force 77 air interdiction campaign conducted against North Vietnam from 09 May 1972 to 23 October 1972, during the Vietnam War. Operation Linebacker, the designation for the new interdiction campaign, would have four objectives: to isolate North Vietnam from its outside sources of supply by destroying railroad bridges and rolling stock in and around Hanoi and northeastward toward the Chinese frontier; the targeting of primary storage areas and marshalling yards; to destroy storage and transshipment points; and finally, to eliminate (or at least damage) the North's air defense system. On 10 May Operation Linebacker began with large-scale bombing operations against North Vietnam by tactical fighter aircraft of the Seventh Air Force and Task Force 77. American bombers would once again return to the skies of North Vietnam in 1972 during Operation Linebacker II before the American commitment to the Vietnam War came to an end. (Air-to-air losses only) Between 10 09 May 1972 and 23 October 1972, the United States lost a total of 134 aircraft either over the north or as a direct result of Linebacker missions." .

ns1:45184 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Typhoon Muifa, known in the Philippines as Typhoon Kabayan, was a large, powerful and persistent typhoon which affected a number of countries in the Pacific, killing 22 and causing widespread damage worth US$480 million. It was the ninth named storm, third typhoon and the second super-typhoon of the 2011 Pacific typhoon season. According to the JTWC, Muifa had strengthened from a tropical storm to a super typhoon in less than 24 hours; it reported that the storm was reaching one-minute sustained wind speed of 140 knots (260 km/h; 160 mph). On 6 August, as the typhoon approached, over 200,000 people were evacuated from low-lying areas; however, although 140 more flights were cancelled the typhoon was no longer expected to directly impact the nation." .

ns1:45188 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Carandiru massacre (Massacre do Carandiru, Portuguese pronunciation: ) occurred on Friday, 02 October 1992, in Carandiru Penitentiary in São Paulo, Brazil, when military police stormed the penitentiary following a prison riot. The massacre, which left 111 prisoners dead, is considered by many people to be a major human rights violation. The case was brought before the inter-American Commission by The Center for Justice and International Law (CEJIL), the Teotônio Vilela Commission for Human Rights, and Human Rights Watch, and in 2000, eight years after the massacre, they condemned Brazil for it. An example of this is the New York Times, which published one article captioned \"111 Killed When Police Storm Brazilian Prison During Inmate Riot\" the same year as the massacre. The massacre has also sparked ongoing discussions, both in Brazil and internationally about Brazil's prison system. Although the UN urged Brazil to bring justice to those most affected by the slaughter in September 2016, the court declared the trial on Carandiru massacre null. Since the massacre, Brazil's federal government has passed new legislation to reform the prison system, all of which has yet to be enforced." .

ns1:45201 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Hilli or the Battle of Bogura was a major battle fought in the Bangladesh Liberation War. It is generally regarded as the most severe pitched battle that took place in East Pakistan, now Bangladesh. The battle of Hilli took place between 23 22 November 1971 and 11 18 December 1971, although the final surrender took place on 18 December 1971. The main objective of the Indian Army was to control Bogura, thereby cutting off Pakistan forces in the north from the rest of East Pakistan. One of the infantry battalions of the 20 Indian Mountain Division 5/11 Gorkha Rifles distinguished itself here and later earned the battle honour Bogura for itself, the commanding officer of the Indian battalion, then Lt Col F T Dias later rose to the rank of Lieutenant General in the Indian Army." .

ns1:45218 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1975–76 FIBA European Cup Winner's Cup was the tenth edition of FIBA Europe's 2nd-tier level European-wide professional club basketball competition, contested between national domestic cup champions, running from 29 October 1975, to 17 March 1976. It was contested by 20 teams, two less than in the 1974–75 FIBA European Cup Winner's Cup." .

ns1:45235 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017–18 European Rugby Challenge Cup was the fourth edition of the European Challenge Cup, an annual second-tier rugby union competition for professional clubs. The first round of the group stage began on the weekend of 12/13/14/15 12 October 2017, and the competition ended with the final on 11 May 2018 in Bilbao, Spain. Gloucester returned to the final having lost to Stade Français last season, where they faced Cardiff Blues, who made it their first final of any competition since their 2010 European Challenge Cup Final victory. 20 teams qualified for the 2017–18 European Rugby Challenge Cup; a total of 18 qualified from across the Premiership, Pro14 and Top 14, as a direct result of their domestic league performance, with two coming through a play-off. Teams in the same pool play each other twice, both at home and away in the group stage, beginning on the weekend of 12/13/14 12 October 2017, and continuing through to 19/20, before the pool winners and three best runners-up progressed to the quarter finals. The semi-finals will played on the weekend of 20/21/22." .

ns1:45253 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Sacile (also known as the Battle of Fontana Fredda) on 16 15 April 1809 and its companion Clash at Pordenone on 15 April 1809 saw an Austrian army commanded by Archduke John of Austria defeat a Franco-Italian army led by Eugène de Beauharnais and force it to retreat. The action took place east of the Livenza River near Sacile in modern-day Italy during the War of the Fifth Coalition, part of the Napoleonic Wars. In 15 April 1809, Archduke John quickly invaded Republic of Venice in northeastern Italy. Before 1809, Eugène never led so much as a regiment into battle, yet Napoleon entrusted him with command of the Army of Italy. Archduke John's army invaded Italy on 10 15 April 1809, with the VIII Armeekorps advancing through Tarvisio and IX Armeekorps crossing the middle Isonzo. Even so, Eugène believed his army was strong enough to beat the archduke in battle, so he ordered his divisions to assemble at Sacile on the Livenza River." .

ns1:45256 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Challenger Banque Nationale de Drummondville was a professional tennis tournament played on indoor hard courts. It took place in Drummondville, Canada between 14 March 2016 and 20 March 2016." .

ns1:45298 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2004–05 Cypriot First Division was the 66th season of the Cypriot top-level association football league. The champions ensured their participation in the 2005–06 UEFA Champions League and the runners-up in the 2005–06 UEFA Cup. They were replaced by the first three teams of the 2003–04 Cypriot Second Division, Nea Salamis Famagusta FC, Aris Limassol F.C. and Alki Larnaca F.C.." .

ns1:45322 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 İstanbul Cup was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor clay courts. It was the 4th edition of the İstanbul Cup, and was part of the Tier III Series of the 2008 WTA Tour. It took place in Istanbul, Turkey, from 19 May 2008 through 24 May 2008." .

ns1:45326 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Revolax (Finnish: Revonlahden taistelu) took place on 27 April 1808 at Siikajoki, (now part of Siikajoki) in Northern Ostrobothnia, Finland, when the Swedish supreme commander Wilhelm Mauritz Klingspor and the Savolax brigade under Colonel Johan Adam Cronstedt, a total of about 2,250 Swedes surprised an isolated Russian column of about 1,700 men under Major General Michail Leontievich Bulatov. This, and the preceding battle of Siikajoki nine days earlier, are considered very important events since they are the first Swedish victories after about 2 months of planned retreat." .

ns1:45336 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Pondicherry was the first military action on the India subcontinent following the declaration of war between Great Britain and France in the American Revolutionary War. A British force besieged the French-controlled port of Pondicherry (now Pondicherry) in 21 August 1778, which capitulated after ten weeks of siege. Word first reached the French India colony of Pondicherry in July 1778 that France and Britain had recalled their ambassadors, a sign that war was imminent. Pondicherry was the capital of French India and the largest of France's possessions on the subcontinent. Pondicherry, as was the case with a number of other European colonial outposts in India, changed hands due to military action several times in the colonial period. Britain followed up the victory by seizing France's other India colonies, contributing to the outbreak of the Second Mysore War." .

ns1:45361 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "PGL 2017 Krakow Major Championship, also known as PGL Major 2017 or Kraków 2017, was the eleventh Counter-Strike: Global Offensive Major Championship. It was organized by PGL and held in Kraków, Poland from 16 July 2017 to 23, 2017. The group stage and playoffs will be played in Kraków, Poland, with group stage played in closed studio and playoffs being held in Tauron Arena Kraków. Virtus.pro (Poland), Ninjas in Pyjamas (Sweden), Team LDLC.com (France), Team EnVyUs (France), Astralis (Denmark), and now Gambit Esports (Kazakhstan) each have one major title." .

ns1:45374 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Moby Dick is an unfinished film by Orson Welles, filmed in 1971. It is not to be confused with the incomplete (and now lost) 1955 film Welles made of his meta-play Moby Dick—Rehearsed, or with the 1956 film Moby Dick, in which Welles played a supporting role. The film consists of readings by Welles from Herman Melville's 1851 novel Moby-Dick, shot against a blue background with various optical illusions to give the impression of being at sea." .

ns1:45376 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of La Marfée, also known as the Battle of Prince of Sedan, took place on 06 July 1641, 1641, during the 1635 to 1659 Franco-Spanish War, a connected conflict of the Thirty Years' War. It was fought near Sedan, on 06 July 1641, between a French army led by Châtillon, and an Imperial-Spanish army commanded by Guillaume de Lamboy, supported by French rebels led by the Comte de Soissons and Duke Frédéric Maurice de La Tour d'Auvergne, duc de Bouillon, The French army collapsed, but Soissons was killed in the closing moments, allegedly by using a loaded pistol to open his helmet, and the opportunity quickly passed. They were known as the 'Princes of Peace', as they claimed Richelieu was deliberately continuing the war to retain power; since 1637, they had been based in the Prince of Sedan, then part of the Holy Roman Empire." .

ns1:45399 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Originally the league was structured at 8 teams but Kuwait FA announced its expansion in football so last seasons 2017–18 relegated team have been un-relegated and 2017–18 Kuwaiti Division One winners Al-Shabab SC and losing play-off team Al-Fahaheel FC will all compete this season. 2- Jumaa Saaed - Kuwait SC : 13 goal. 3- Nabi somah - Alshabab : 11 goal. 4- Pattric Fabiano - salmiya : 10 goal. 6- Yousef Nasser - Qadsia SC : 10 goal." .

ns1:45434 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 Norwegian parliamentary election was held in Norway on 13 September 2009 and 14 September 2009. Early voting was possible between 2009 and 2009, while some municipalities also held open voting on 13 September 2009. In certain individual polls in late 2008, the party averaged around 30%, which at that point made it the most popular party in Norway, Hence the actual election result was relatively disappointing. The Progress Party increased its share of the vote by 0.9%, a slight gain from the 2005 Norwegian parliamentary election and the best election result in the party's history. On 11 September, three days before election day, Jens Stoltenberg told the media that he didn't want the Red-Green Coalition to become dependent on Red in Parliament." .

ns1:45437 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2003 Ontario general election was held on 02 October 2003, to elect the 103 members of the 38th Legislative Assembly (Members of Provincial Parliament, or \"MPPs\") of the Province of Ontario, Canada. The election was won, however, by the Ontario Liberal Party, led by Dalton McGuinty. In the 1999 Ontario general election, the Tories were able to ride a strong economy and a campaign aimed at proving rookie Liberal leader Dalton McGuinty was \"not up to the job\" to another majority government. During the first stage of the campaign, the principal Liberal ad featured a tight close-up of Dalton McGuinty as he spoke about his plans for Ontario." .

ns1:45438 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Río Salado, also known as the Battle of Tarifa (30 October 1340) was a battle of the armies of King Afonso IV of Portugal and King Alfonso XI of Castile against those of Sultan Abu al-Hasan 'Ali of the Marinid Sultanate and Yusuf I of Emirate of Granada. The Marinid Sultanate had mobilised a vast army and, after crossing the Strait of Gibraltar and defeating a Christian fleet at Gibraltar, proceeded inland to the Salado River near Tarifa, where they met the Christians. Abu Hasan's position was now a difficult one, not only because the troops besieging Tarifa depended on supplies from Morocco, but also the Kingdom of Emirate of Granada needed them, their forces having launched a series of limited attacks all along the frontier to hold the Castilians at bay. Alfonso XI left Seville on 15 30 October 1340 with a relief army, joining the King of Portugal the next day. During the night, Alfonso XI had sent 1,000 horse and 4,000 foot troops to reinforce the Tarifa garrison, hoping to surprise the enemy rearguard during the battle; they had met only slight resistance by the 3,000 light cavalry covering the Salado and reached Tarifa without difficulty. A third chronicler postulated 70,000 knights and 400,000 infantry for the Marinid Sultanate and 7,000 knights and 700,000 infantry for Emirate of Granada. Emirate of Granada had 7,000 knights and the Marinid Sultanate may have mobilized up to 60,000 men. A Christian militia probably fought for the Emir of Emirate of Granada at the battle. The Portuguese brought 1,000 knights and Tarifa kept a garrison of 1,000 men, who took part in the battle. Many notables were killed in the battle or the rout, such as Abu Tabit ibn Fath Allah, Abu Muyahid Gazi ibn al-Ka's, and Muhammad ben Yahya ben Abi Bakr, also the famous writer from Emirate of Granada, Abdullah ben Salmun, and the popular imam Ibn al-Khatib." .

ns1:45445 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "UFC Fight Night: Te-Huna vs. Marquardt (also known as UFC Fight Night 43) was a mixed martial arts event held on 28 June 2014, at Spark Arena in Auckland, New Zealand. The event was the first of two that took place on 28 June 2014, the other being UFC Fight Night: Swanson vs. Stephens." .

ns1:45472 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Natasha Ryan (born 1984) is an Australian woman from Rockhampton who went missing in 1998. Natasha Ryan disappeared on 31 August 1998 after her mother dropped her off at school, and was subsequently reported as missing. Hopes of finding Ryan alive soon diminished and police concluded she had been murdered by Rockhampton serial killer Leonard Fraser, who was subsequently charged with Ryan's murder. Black faced Rockhampton Magistrates Court in November 1999, where he pleaded guilty to wilful obstruction of police after he had told officers that he did not know Ryan's whereabouts. Police had raided a house in North Rockhampton on the night of 10 April 2003 and had found Natasha Ryan alive, hiding in a cupboard. It was discovered that Ryan had been living with her boyfriend Scott Black since disappearing on 31 August 1998. It was reported that for most of that time, Ryan had shared a house in Yeppoon, Queensland, but had moved back to Rockhampton after Black received a transfer with his milk delivery job. Ryan had been living in the Rockhampton house for six months prior to being found. Despite the discovery that Natasha Ryan was still alive and the doubt it cast on the evidence which led to Fraser being charged with Ryan's murder, Fraser's defence counsel did not lodge an appeal for a mistrial, and the trial forged ahead. 60 Minutes sent reporter Tara Brown to Rockhampton to interview Ryan. Assuming Ryan's story was going to hand them a ratings-winning program, 60 Minutes held the broadcast until 20 April 2003 in an attempt to take viewers away from Network Ten's premiere of the third season of their ratings winner, Big Brother Australia. Rockhampton State Emergency Service volunteer Lyle Dobbs expressed his view that any profit Ryan gained from her interviews should go back to Queensland taxpayers who funded the search which came at significant cost to police and a lot of effort from SES volunteers. In 2005, Rockhampton District Court judge Grant Britton sentenced Black to a three-year jail sentence for perjury, with two years suspended, after he pleaded guilty to telling investigating police officers that he did not know Ryan's whereabouts." .

ns1:45528 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Glorieta Pass (26 March 1862–28, 1862) in the northern New Mexico Territory, was the decisive battle of the New Mexico Campaign during the American Civil War. It was fought at Glorieta Pass in the Sangre de Cristo Mountains in what is now New Mexico, and was an important event in the history of the New Mexico Territory in the American Civil War. There was a skirmish on 26 March 1862 between advance elements from each army, with the main battle occurring on 28 March 1862. In early 1862 the Confederacy established the Confederate Arizona Territory, which included the southern halves of both modern Arizona and New Mexico. During the battle on 26 March 1862, Pyron had his battalion of the 2nd Texas Mounted Rifles, four companies of the 5th Texas Mounted Rifles under Maj. John Shropshire and two cannons. With the sounds of battle echoing in the distance, Lt. Col. Manuel Chaves of the 2nd New Mexico Infantry, commander of the New Mexican volunteers, informed Maj. Chivington that his scouts had located the Confederate supply train at Johnson's Ranch. On January 23, 1864, the New Mexico Territorial Legislature adopted a resolution that did not mention Chivington and instead asked President Lincoln to promote William H. Lewis and Asa B. Carey, both regular army officers, for \"distinguished service\" in the battle. Furthermore, the approach of the Federal \"California Column\" eastward through the New Mexico Territory during the summer of 1862 would have seriously jeopardized Confederate control of the region." .

ns1:45532 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Ba Gia was a major battle that marked the beginning of the Viet Cong's (VC) Summer Offensive of 1965, during the early phases of the Vietnam War, known in Vietnam as the American War. The battle took place in Quảng Ngãi Province, South Vietnam, between 28 May 1965–31, 1965. In Quảng Ngãi Province, South Vietnam, the VC kick-started their summer campaign by attacking elements of the ARVN 51st Infantry Regiment during the early hours of 1965. During the first half of 1965 the war in South Vietnam sharply escalated. In 31 May 1965, the VC 271st Regiment (part of the Viet Cong 9th Division) moved into northern Quảng Ngãi from the neighbouring province of Quảng Nam. The battle at Ba Gia, which marked the beginning of the VC's Summer Offensive of 1965, had dealt a severe blow to South Vietnam's armed forces." .

ns1:45559 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Chalk Bluff (01 May 1863 – 2, 1863), also known as the Skirmish at Chalk Bluff, was a military engagement of the American Civil War. The battle was fought near Chalk Bluff, northwest of St. Francis (present-day Clay County, Arkansas), where United States of America Brigadier General William Vandever, commanding the Second Division of the Army of the Frontier, was repulsed in an attempt to prevent Confederate General John S. Marmaduke's Division from crossing the St. Francis River. Marmaduke departed his camps in Arkansas in the spring of 1863 with 5,000 cavalrymen, bound for southeastern Missouri. Union forces under Vandever had pursued Marmaduke through Missouri to Chalk Bluff, Arkansas, where Marmaduke planned to cross the St. Francis River, whose steep chalky white clay banks made fording the river difficult for cavalry." .

ns1:45579 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 Etisalat Emirates Cup Final was the 3rd final since its establishment. The match was won by Al Shabab Al Arabi Club, who beat Al Ain FC 2–3 to win their first title. On 3 April, UAE Football League Announced that the final will be held for the second time at Mohammed Bin Zayed Stadium in Abu Dhabi, on April 29." .

ns1:45601 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011–12 Maltese Premier League was the 97th season of the Maltese Premier League, the top-tier association football league in Malta. It began on 19 August 2011 and it ended in 12 May 2012. Valletta F.C. were the defending champions, having won their 20th title 2010–11 Maltese Premier League." .

ns1:45613 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 ARAG World Team Cup was a tennis tournament played on outdoor clay courts. It was the 32nd edition of the World Team Cup, and was part of the 250 series of the 2010 ATP World Tour. It took place at the Rochusclub in Düsseldorf, Germany, from 16 May through 22 May 2010." .

ns1:45635 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Barbourville was one of the early engagements of the American Civil War. It took place on 19 September 1861, in Knox County, Kentucky during the campaign known as the Kentucky Confederate Offensive. The battle is considered the first Confederate victory in the commonwealth, and threw a scare into Federal commanders, who rushed troops to central Kentucky to try to repel the invasion, which was finally stopped at the Battle of Camp Wildcat in October. Pro-Confederate Kentucky Governor Beriah Magoffin refused to send troops, but since the majority of the members of the Kentucky General Assembly were pro-Union, Lincoln's call for volunteers did not prompt the State to secede. In elections on August 5, 1861, Kentucky voters returned a veto-proof majority of pro-Union members to the Kentucky House of Representatives and Kentucky Senate. On August 6, 1861, Camp Dick Robinson, a Union camp, was established near Lexington, Kentucky. Gen. Gideon Pillow to seize Columbus, Kentucky on the Mississippi River before Union forces could do so. This ended Kentucky's neutrality, led to Union Brig. Gen. Ulysses S. Grant's seizure of Paducah, Kentucky on September 6, 1861, and other military movements and actions by both Union and Confederate armies in Kentucky soon afterwards. For much of the summer, Union sympathizers had been organizing and training recruits from Kentucky and eastern Tennessee at Camp Andrew Johnson near Barbourville. Although Zollicoffer briefly withdrew to his camp at Cumberland Gap, he sent a detachment of his men to drive off another Union Home Guard force at a camp at Laurel Bridge, in Laurel County, Kentucky soon after the Battle of Barbourville." .

ns1:45658 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "On 23 November 1979, Paul McCartney's band Wings began a Wings UK Tour 1979 to promote their recent album, Back to the Egg. Following the main part of the tour, Wings teamed up with an all-star cast of musicians and took the name Rockestra to perform a series of Concerts for the People of Kampuchea at the Hammersmith Odeon in London. However, it became Wings' final concert tour, after McCartney's marijuana arrest in Tokyo the following January." .

ns1:45673 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Revel Presents: Beyoncé Live (also called Back to Business) was the third concert residency by American recording artist Beyoncé. Announced in March, and held during four consecutive nights in 28 May 2012 in Atlantic City, the concerts were part of the opening of the new entertainment resort, hotel, casino and spa, Revel. Revel Presents: Beyoncé Live received positive reviews from music critics who praised Beyoncé's vocals and dance abilities during the concerts calling it her post-pregnancy comeback show. On March 19, 2012, Beyoncé's publicist Yvette Noel-Schure announced that the singer would perform three shows (May 25–27, 2012) at Revel Atlantic City to inaugurate the resort and its Ovation Hall, a 5,050-seat concert venue. —Beyoncé Knowles in Making of Revel, Behind the Scenes (Part II) On March 20, 2012, several billboards by Beyoncé were projected on the Big Screen of the NASDAQ on Times Square advertising her show in Revel Atlantic City. The concert was also called Back to Business referencing Beyoncé's song \"Run the World (Girls)\". A retro-soulful performance of \"Love On Top\" followed with Beyoncé saying \"Atlantic City, snap your fingers\". According to Jim Farber of Daily News, the tickets were sold for high prices because the Revel Presents: Beyoncé Live was the only show by Beyoncé announced that year." .

ns1:45684 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1999–2000 Cypriot Third Division was the 29th season of the Cypriot third-level association football league. The first three teams were promoted to the 1999–2000 Cypriot Second Division and the last three teams were relegated to the 1999–2000 Cypriot Fourth Division. Because of this, playoffs between the bottom three teams of the 1999–2000 Cypriot Second Division and the fourth team of the 1999–2000 Cypriot Third Division were held for the extra place in the 2000–01 Cypriot Second Division. Also, playoffs between the bottom 3 teams of the 1999–2000 Cypriot Third Division and the fourth team of the 1999–2000 Cypriot Fourth Division were held for the extra place in the 2000–01 Cypriot Third Division." .

ns1:45715 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of San Patricio was fought on 27 February 1836, between Texian rebels and the Mexican army, during the Texas Revolution. The battle marked the start of the Goliad Campaign, the Mexican offensive to retake the Texas Gulf Coast. In 27 February 1836, Johnson and about 40 men led a herd of horses to San Patricio in preparation for the expedition. Mexican soldiers surprised the sleeping Texians in San Patricio in the early hours of 27 February 1836. Most of these volunteers were Americans or Europeans who had arrived in Texas after the Texas Revolution had commenced." .

ns1:45830 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2000 Belgian Grand Prix (formally, the LVIII Foster's Belgian Grand Prix) was a Formula One motor race held on 27 August 2000 at the Circuit de Spa-Francorchamps, Stavelot, Belgium. It was the thirteenth race of the 2000 Formula One season, and the 58th Belgian Grand Prix. The 44-lap race was won by McLaren driver Mika Häkkinen, who started from the pole position. In September 1999 the Fédération Internationale de l'Automobile (FIA) released a provisional calendar for the 2000 Formula One season, dropping the Circuit de Spa-Francorchamps from the Formula One World Championship due to Belgian tobacco-advertising laws which threatened to cancel the race; several teams had tobacco sponsorship." .

ns1:45915 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Korea Open Badminton Championships (officially known as the Victor Korea Open Badminton Championships 2018 for sponsorship reasons) was a badminton tournament that took place at the SK Olympic Handball Gymnasium in Seoul, South Korea, from 25 to 30 September 2018 and had a total prize of $600,000. The 2018 Korea Open Badminton Championships was the eighteenth tournament of the 2018 BWF World Tour and also part of the Korea Open Badminton Championships championships, which has been held since 1991. This international tournament was held at the SK Olympic Handball Gymnasium in Seoul, South Korea." .

ns1:45937 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Turkish capture of Smyrna (Turkish: İzmir'in Kurtuluşu; Liberation of İzmir) was the final phase of the Great Offensive and last battle of the Turkish War of Independence. After the Turkish Army inflicted heavy losses on the Greek Army at Dumlupınar, on 30 August 1922, Greek forces were in continual retreat towards Smyrna (modern İzmir) as the Turkish Army's westward advance continued. On 09 September 1922, the Turkish Army entered İzmir from Kemalpaşa to the east. 09 September 1922 is a local holiday commemorating the Turkish capture of İzmir Province and the end of the Greek occupation of Smyrna. Mustafa Kemal (later Atatürk), who founded the Republican People's Party, chose 09 September 1922 1923 as the official establishment date of his party to commemorate the capture of Smyrna." .

ns1:45946 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1975–76 Fußball-Bundesliga was the 13th season of the Bundesliga, West Germany's premier football league. It began on 09 August 1975 and ended on 12 June 1976. Bundesliga Northern Division, Karlsruher SC, winners of the Southern Division and KFC Uerdingen 05, who won a two-legged promotion play-off against FK Pirmasens." .

ns1:45961 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Cape Verdi (sometimes known as the Cape Verdi Stakes), is a race horse run over a distance of 1,600 metres (one mile) on turf in late January or early February at Meydan Racecourse in Dubai. The race is named after Cape Verdi, a horse who won the 1000 Guineas for Godolphin in 1998." .

ns1:45965 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Medininkai took place on 27 July 1320 between the Teutonic Order and the Samogitian army near Medininkai (now Varniai). The battle stopped the Teutonic attacks on Medininkai land until Grand Duke of Lithuania Gediminas concluded a truce with the Teutonic Order (1324–1328)." .

ns1:46005 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Franklin–Nashville Campaign, also known as Hood's Tennessee Campaign, was a series of battles in the Western Theater of the American Civil War, conducted from 18 September 1864 to 27 December 1864, in Alabama, Tennessee, and northwestern Georgia during the American Civil War. After the president's departure for Montgomery, Alabama, he telegraphed Hood that he had decided to retain him in command and, acceding to Hood's request, transferred Lt. Gen. William J. Hardee, one of Hood's corps commanders, out of the Army of Tennessee. Hood then moved to the west and crossed the Coosa River in the vicinity of Rome, Georgia, near the Alabama state line. He turned north in the direction of Resaca, Georgia, and joined with Maj. Gen. Joseph Wheeler's cavalry, which had been previously raiding in Tennessee. Hood set out toward Decatur, Alabama, with the intention of meeting up with Forrest in the vicinity of Florence, from where they would march north into Tennessee. Hood departed from Gadsden on October 22, en route to Guntersville, Alabama, where he planned to cross the Tennessee River." .

ns1:46025 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Tal Afar also known as Operation Restoring Rights was a military offensive conducted by the United States of America Army and supported by Iraqi forces, to eliminate Al Qaeda in Iraq and other insurgents in the city of Tal Afar, Iraq in response to the increase of insurgent attacks against U.S. and Iraqi positions in the area and to end the brutal tactics against the population by the terrorists. AQI had used the city as a staging ground for moving foreign fighters into Iraq since early 2005. The offensive was launched on 01 September 2005 in a joint United States of America Army and the New Iraqi Army operation to destroy suspected insurgents' havens and base of operations in Tal Afar. By 18 September 2005, the battle was over, Coalition forces succeeded in significantly eliminating AQI and other insurgents from the city, thereby creating a secure environment for the referendum in October and national elections in 2005, Tal Afar went from being the city with the lowest number of voters to the highest in the country, schools and businesses reopened and the population transitioned back to living a normal life as possible. The operation was considered one of the first successful counterinsurgency operations of the war and President George W. Bush remarked that \"the story of Tal Afar gives me confidence in our strategy, because in that city we see the outlines of Iraq we've been fighting for.\"" .

ns1:46039 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Elmina was a military engagement of the Dutch–Portuguese War, fought near the Elmina Castle in the Portuguese Gold Coast in 1625." .

ns1:46072 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "March 2012 Gaza–Israel clashes (מבצע הד חוזר) was an Israel Defense Forces (Israel Defense Forces) military operation in the Gaza Strip from 09 March 2012 until 14 March 2012. On 09 March 2012, Israel carried out a targeted air strike in the Gaza Strip killing Zohair al-Qaisi, the secretary general of the Popular Resistance Committees (PRC)." .

ns1:46084 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Typhoon Saomai, known in the Philippines as Typhoon Juan, was considered the most powerful typhoon on record to strike the east coast of the People's Republic of China. It was the eighth tropical storm, fifth typhoon, and third super typhoon of the 2006 Pacific typhoon season recognized by the Joint Typhoon Warning Center. The JTWC estimated Saomai to have been a much more powerful system, ranking it as a Category 5-equivalent super typhoon with one-minute winds of 260 km/h (160 mph) and a pressure of 898 mbar (hPa; 26.52 inHg)." .

ns1:46103 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Battle of Turaida was one of the biggest battles of Livonian Crusade between Estonian tribes and Livonian Brothers of the Sword. The battle took place around modern Turaida, Latvia. By summer of 1211, a larger military plan had been made by Estonian counties whose objective was to capture the stronghold held by Caupo of Turaida and thereafter attack Riga." .

ns1:46131 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Russian invasion of East Prussia occurred during World War I, lasting from August to 14 September 1914. As well as being the natural course for the Russian Empire to take upon the declaration of war on the German Empire, it was also an attempt to focus the German Army on the Eastern Front, as opposed to the Western Front. As a result, every German general staff officer in East Prussia in 1914 knew how to respond to the Russian offensive. The Battle of Stallupönen, fought between Russian and German armies on 17 August 1914, was the opening battle of World War I on the Eastern Front." .

ns1:46134 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1992 Five Nations Championship was the 63rd series of the Six Nations Championship, an annual rugby union competition between the major Northern Hemisphere rugby union national teams. The tournament consisted of ten matches held between 18 January 1992 and 21 March 1992. Including the competition's former incarnation as the Home Nations Championship, the 1991 Five Nations Championship was the 98th Northern Hemisphere rugby union championship." .

ns1:46176 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "On 28 June 1998, United Airlines Flight 863, a Boeing 747-400 flying United Airlines's regularly scheduled transpacific service from San Francisco Airport to Sydney Airport was forced to shut down one of its right-wing engines and nearly collided with San Bruno Mountain while recovering from the engine failure. Flight 863 to Sydney departed from San Francisco International Airport at 10:39 pm PST on Sunday 28 June 1998 (UTC: 5:39 am, 29 June)." .

ns1:46234 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Field of the Cloth of Gold (French: Camp du Drap d'Or, pronounced ) was a royal summit meeting at a site in Balinghem – equidistant between Ardres in France and Guînes in the English Pale of Calais – . It was a lavish extremely expensive display of wealth on both sides from 7 to 24 30 June 1520, between King Henry VIII of England and King Francis I of France." .

ns1:46282 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1961 Syracuse Grand Prix was a motor race, run to Formula One rules, held on 25 April 1961 at Syracuse Circuit, Sicily. The race was run over 56 laps of the circuit, and was won by Italian driver Giancarlo Baghetti in a Ferrari 156 in his first Formula One race, the only driver to achieve this feat." .

ns1:46298 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Women's Cricket World Cup Final was a cricket match between West Indies women's cricket team and Australia women's national cricket team played on 2013 at the Brabourne Stadium in India. In response, the West Indies women's cricket team were bowled out for 145, resulting in an Australian victory by 114 runs, and securing Australia women's national cricket team's sixth World Cup victory. The final group match saw Australia women's national cricket team beat New Zealand by seven wicket, and see them qualify for the Super Six section of the tournament. Despite losing to India and England, they finished in third place in the group and qualified for the Super Six section, where they won all three of their games, against South Africa, New Zealand and Australia women's national cricket team, to qualify for the final. The West Indies women's cricket team then took further wickets until at 209/7 with fewer than 7 overs left, Australia women's national cricket team appeared to have lost their momentum. However Jodie Fields and Ellyse Perry hit 50 more runs off the remaining balls, leaving Australia women's national cricket team with a final score of 259/7. The West Indies women's cricket team had beaten Australia women's national cricket team in the Super Six group stage, but were outclassed in the final; the BBC said that \"Australia women's national cricket team were too clinical for a West Indies women's cricket team side that were sloppy in the field, wayward with the ball and unable to keep up with the run chase.\"" .

ns1:46363 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The St Lite Kinen is a Japanese Grade 2 flat horse race in Japan for three-year-old Thoroughbred colts and fillies run over a distance of 2,200 metres at the Nakayama Racecourse, Funabashi, Chiba Prefecture." .

ns1:46440 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Centralia mine fire is a coal seam fire that has been burning underneath the borough of Centralia, United States, since at least 27 May 1962. On 1962, the Centralia Council met to discuss the approaching Memorial Day and how the town would go about cleaning up the Centralia landfill, which was introduced earlier that year. David DeKok says that the bourough's deliberate burning of trash on 27 May 1962 to clean up the landfill in the former strip mine ignited a coal seam via an unsealed opening in the trash pit, which allowed the fire to enter the labyrinth of abandoned coal mines beneath Centralia. Centralia councilman Joseph Tighe proposed a different hypothesis: that Centralia's coal fire was actually started by an adjacent coal seam fire that had been burning west of Centralia's." .

ns1:46454 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Radithor was a patent medicine that is a well-known example of radioactive quackery and specifically of excessively broad and pseudoscientific application of the principle of radiation hormesis. Radithor was manufactured from 1918 to 1928 by the Bailey Radium Laboratories, Inc., of East Orange, New Jersey." .

ns1:46502 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Leinster Senior Cup, was the 113th staging of the Leinster Senior Cup association football competition. 31 teams entered the 2013–14 competition including the 11 Leinster based League of Ireland teams who entered the competition at the Fourth round stage. The previous season's runners up, St Patrick's Athletic F.C. won the tournament in 2014, beating Longford Town 2–1 in the final at City Calling Stadium on 2014. The 11 Leinster based League of Ireland clubs enter in this round in an open draw along with the 5 Third Round winners." .

ns1:46522 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1974 Wimbledon Championships was a tennis tournament that took place on the outdoor grass courts at the All England Lawn Tennis and Croquet Club in Wimbledon, London, United Kingdom. The tournament was held from Monday 24 June 1974 until Saturday 06 July 1974. It was the 88th staging of the Wimbledon Championships, and the second Grand Slam tennis event of 1974." .

ns1:46524 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Barcelona Open Banc Sabadell (also known as the Torneo Godó) was a men's tennis tournament played on outdoor clay courts. It took place at the Real Club de Tenis Barcelona in Barcelona, Catalonia, Spain, from 23 April 2012 through 29 April 2012 2010." .

ns1:46526 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Condor, also known as Operation D (D for desperado), was the name of the French intelligence agency SDECE's special service GCMA secret operation against the Viet Minh supply column. Operation Condor was an attempt to weaken the Vietnamese Independence League artillery's assaults against the besieged French Union garrison of Dien Bien Phu." .

ns1:46544 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1966 European Junior Games was the second edition of the biennial athletics competition for European athletes aged under twenty. The event was held at the Central Stadium Chornomorets in Odessa, Ukrainian SSR, Soviet Union, on 24 September 1966 and 25 September 1966." .

ns1:46562 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Supercopa MX was a Mexican association football match-up played on 15 July 2018 between the champions of the Apertura 2017 Copa MX, Club de Fútbol Monterrey, and the champions of the Clausura 2018 Copa MX, Club Necaxa. The 2018 Supercopa MX was part of a doubleheader, which also included the 2018 Campeón de Campeones, organized by Univision Deportes, Soccer United Marketing (SUM), Liga MX, and LA Galaxy." .

ns1:46594 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Chippawa (sometimes incorrectly spelled Chippewa) was a victory for the United States Army in the War of 1812, during its invasion on 05 July 1814 of the British Empire's colony of Upper Canada along the Niagara River. He may also have believed that his opponents were militia but was comparatively new to command in Canada and relied on information from Lieutenant Colonel John Harvey, the Deputy Adjutant General for the forces in Upper Canada, that even the United States regulars were of poor quality. Early on 05 July 1814, British light infantry, militia and Indians crossed the Chippawa, Ontario ahead of Riall's main body and began sniping at Scott's outposts from the woods to their west. Two days after the battle, Brown completed his original intended maneuver and crossed the Chippawa, Ontario upstream of Riall's defenses, forcing the British to fall back to Fort George. The site is preserved in the Chippawa, Ontario Battlefield Park, a unit of the Niagara Parks Commission, with a battle monument and interpretive plaques south of Niagara Falls in the town of Chippawa, Ontario. The site of the battle was designated a National Historic Site of Canada in 1921." .

ns1:46620 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Coastal Road massacre of 1978 was an attack involving the hijacking of a bus on Israel's Highway 2 in which 38 Israelis, including 13 children, were killed, and 71 were wounded. The plan was to seize a luxury hotel in Tel Aviv and take tourists and foreign ambassadors hostage in order to exchange them for Palestinian prisoners held by Israel. According to a Fatah commander who had helped to plan the attack, the timing was aimed at scuppering the Israeli-Egyptian peace talks between Menachem Begin and Anwar Sadat and damaging tourism in Israel. Time magazine characterized it as \"the worst terrorist attack in Israel's history.\" Setting off down the highway toward Tel Aviv, they hijacked a chartered bus carrying Egged bus drivers and their families on a day outing, along the Highway 2. The IDF spokesman stated, \"The objective of the operation is not retaliation for the terrorists' crimes, for there can be no retaliation for the murder of innocent men, women and children – but to protect the state of Israel and its citizens from incursions of members of the Fatah and PLO, who use Lebanese territory in order to attack citizens of Israel.\"" .

ns1:46678 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The murder of Helena Rapp was a stabbing attack carried out on 24 May 1992, in which a Palestinian terrorist murdered 15-year-old Israeli schoolgirl Helena Rapp in the center of the Israeli coastal city of Bat Yam. On 24 May 1992, at around 7:30 am, 18-year-old Palestinian militant Fuad Muhammad Abdulhadi Amrin murdered 15-year-old Israeli schoolgirl Helena Rapp, by stabbing her with a knife at the corner of Ben-Gurion and Jabotinsky streets in the coastal Israeli city of Bat Yam, while she was on her way to the bus stop in order to get to school." .

ns1:46686 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Kolkata market fire was a fire accident that occurred in a five-storeyed marketplace in Kolkata, the capital city of West Bengal, India, on 27 February 2013." .

ns1:46753 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Germantown was a major engagement in the Philadelphia campaign of the American Revolutionary War. It was fought on 04 October 1777, at Germantown, Pennsylvania, between the British Army led by Sir William Howe, and the American Continental Army, with the 2nd Canadian Regiment, under George Washington. Frederick of Prussia saw that presently, when American soldiers should come to be disciplined veterans, they would become a very formidable instrument in the hands of their great commander; and the French court, in making up its mind that the Americans would prove efficient allies, is said to have been influenced almost as much by the battle of Germantown as by the surrender of Burgoyne." .

ns1:46758 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Poland participated in the Poland in the Eurovision Song Contest 2017 with the song \"Flashlight\" written by Kasia Moś, Pete Baringger and Rickard Bonde Truumeel. Poland has only, thus far, reached the top ten on two other occasions, when Ich Troje performing the song \"Keine Grenzen – Żadnych granic\" finished seventh in 2003 and when Michał Szpak performing the song \"Color of Your Life\" finished eighth in Poland in the Eurovision Song Contest 2016. Since returning to the contest in 2014, Poland managed to qualify to the final with both their 2014 entry, \"My Słowianie - We Are Slavic\" performed by Donatan and Cleo, their 2015 entry \"In the Name of Love\" performed by Monika Kuszyńska and their Poland in the Eurovision Song Contest 2016 entry \"Color of Your Life\" performed by Michał Szpak. The Polish national broadcaster, Telewizja Polska (TVP), broadcasts the event within Poland and organises the selection process for the nation's entry. The show took place on 18 February 2017 at the TVP Headquarters in Warsaw, hosted by Artur Orzech. The two semi-finals and the final will be broadcast in Poland on TVP1 and TVP Polonia with commentary by Artur Orzech. At the end of the show, Poland was announced as having finished in the top 10 and subsequently qualifying for the grand final." .

ns1:46774 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Bombardment of Genoa was a military event during the War of the Reunions when France bombarded the city of Genoa from the sea between 18 May 1684 and 28 May 1684. Without a formal declaration of war, a French fleet, commanded by Abraham Duquesne, bombarded the city of Genoa between 18 May 1684 and 28 May 1684. In 1682, François Pidou de Saint Olon became the first French resident envoy to the Republic of Genova, and he was actively involved in the bombardment: indeed he communicated precious information to the French about the defence of Genoa and the position of the batteries of guns, being practically a spy." .

ns1:46788 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1990 World Badminton Grand Prix Finals was the eighth edition of the World Badminton Grand Prix Finals finals. It was held in Denpasar, Bali, Indonesia, from 12 December 1990 to 16 December 1990." .

ns1:46794 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1993 World Rowing Championships were World Rowing Championships that were held from 30 August 1993 to 05 September 1993 at Račice, Czech Republic." .

ns1:46829 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Diamond Head was an operation conducted by the 3rd Brigade, 25th Infantry Division in Tây Ninh Province, lasting from 11 July 1967 to 31 October 1967. Operation Diamond Head officially concluded on 31 October 1967 and PAVN losses were 136 killed whereas U.S. losses were 35 killed." .

ns1:46844 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 84th Academy Awards ceremony, presented by the Academy of Motion Picture Arts and Sciences (AMPAS), honored the best films of 2011 in the United States and took place on 26 February 2012, at the Dolby Theatre in Hollywood, Los Angeles beginning at 5:30 p.m. PST / 8:30 p.m. EST. The ceremony was televised in the United States by American Broadcasting Company, and produced by Brian Grazer and Don Mischer, with Mischer also serving as director. In related events, the Academy held its third annual Governors Awards ceremony at the Grand Ballroom of the Dolby Theatre on 2011. On February 11, 2012, in a ceremony at the Beverly Wilshire Hotel in Beverly Hills, California, the Academy Awards for Technical Achievement were presented by host Milla Jovovich. The nominees for the 84th Academy Awards were announced on January 24, 2012, at 5:38 a.m. PST (13:38 UTC) at the Samuel Goldwyn Theater in Beverly Hills, California, by Tom Sherak, president of the Academy, and the actress Jennifer Lawrence. Originally, the Academy selected director Brett Ratner as co-producer of the ceremony with Don Mischer in 2011. The American telecast on American Broadcasting Company drew in an average of 39.46 million people over its length, which was a 4% increase from the 83rd Academy Awards ceremony." .

ns1:46858 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Puteri Indonesia 2018, the 22nd Puteri Indonesia pageant, was held at 2018. Bunga Jelitha, Puteri Indonesia 2017 of Jakarta Special Region 5 crowned her successor, Sonia Fergina Citra from Bangka Belitung, at the end of the event. She defeated 38 other candidates to win the title of Miss Universe Indonesia 2018 and will now represent Indonesia at the Miss Universe 2018." .

ns1:46867 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Miss Universe 1974, the 23rd Miss Universe pageant, was held on 21 July 1974 at the Folk Arts Theater in Manila, Philippines. Margarita Moran of the Philippines crowned her successor Amparo Muñoz of Spain at the end of the event." .

ns1:46889 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Scheldeprijs cycling race took place on 09 April 2014. Behind him Tyler Farrar and Danny van Poppel finished second and third." .

ns1:46894 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1983–84 2. Fußball-Bundesliga season was the tenth season of the 2. Bundesliga, the second tier of the German football league system. Karlsruher SC and FC Schalke 04 were promoted to the Bundesliga while Rot-Weiss Essen, SC Charlottenburg, VfL Osnabrück and BV 08 Lüttringhausen were relegated to the Oberliga. For the 1983–84 season Rot-Weiß Oberhausen, SC Charlottenburg, SSV Ulm 1846 and 1. FC Saarbrücken were newly promoted to the 2. Bundesliga from the Oberliga while Hertha BSC, FC Schalke 04 and Karlsruher SC had been relegated to the league from the Bundesliga." .

ns1:46903 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 Comerica Bank Challenger was a professional tennis tournament played on hard court. It took place in Aptos, United States of America between 13 July 2009 and 19 July 2009." .

ns1:46913 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006–07 UCI Asia Tour was the third season of the UCI Asia Tour. The season began on 22 October 2006 with the Japan Cup and ended on 17 September 2007 with the Tour de Hokkaido. Ghader Mizbani from Iran was the defending champion of the 2005–06 UCI Asia Tour. Hossein Askari of Iran was crowned as the 2006–07 UCI Asia Tour champion." .

ns1:46919 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The International 2016 (TI6) was the sixth iteration of The International, an annual Dota 2 esports world championship tournament. Hosted by Valve Corporation, the game's developer, the tournament began with the qualifier phase in 2016, and ended after the main event at the KeyArena in Seattle in August. However, The International 2015 champion team Evil Geniuses did not receive a direct invite due to breaking Valve Corporation's rules on roster swapping after the completion of the last premier Dota 2 tournament, the Manila Major, in June. (Note: Prizes are in USD) The crowdfunded prize pool of US$20,770,460 surpassed the The International 2015's total of $18.4 million, overtaking it as the largest esports tournament prize pool in history until being surpassed by the The International 2017. The venue where the event took place, the KeyArena in Seattle, was sold out, with millions of additional spectators watching the event via livestreaming platforms worldwide. Seattle KCPQ reporter Kaci Aitchison, who co-hosted the event, thought that due to the International's large prize pools and production values, esports had become a true spectator sport which deserved the same level of respect of traditional sports." .

ns1:46930 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Bandy World Cup was the 44th annual Bandy World Cup and took place in Göransson Arena in Sandviken, Sweden, on 13 October 2016-16 October 2016." .

ns1:46969 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1982–83 Football League Cup (known as the Milk Cup for sponsorship reasons) was the 23rd season of the EFL Cup, a knockout competition for England's top 92 football clubs. The competition started on 30 August 1982 and ended with the 1983 Football League Cup Final on 26 March 1983. The 1983 Football League Cup Final was contested by First Division teams Manchester United and Liverpool F.C. at the Wembley Stadium in London. Liverpool F.C., winners of the previous two finals, booked their place in the 1983 Football League Cup Final for the third year running at the expense of Second Division strugglers Burnley, winning the first leg 3–0 at Anfield." .

ns1:46987 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2019 FIFA Beach Soccer World Cup was the 10th FIFA Beach Soccer World Cup, the premier international beach soccer championship contested by men's national teams of the member associations of FIFA. Overall, this was the 20th edition of a world cup in beach soccer since the establishment of the Beach Soccer World Championships which ran from 1995–2004 but was not governed by FIFA. In October 2018, it was announced that the tournament would take place in Paraguay in the city of Luque, near the capital, Asunción, between 21 November 2019 and 01 December 2019. This was the first FIFA tournament to be hosted by Paraguay, the first edition of the World Cup held in South America since 2007, and the first time a landlocked country hosted beach soccer's principal event. The bidding schedule to determine the hosts was as follows: Countries known to have declared interest in bidding to host the competition are inclusive of but not exclusive to: On 1 October 2018, the FIFA Organising Committee recommended hosting be awarded to Paraguay as they were the only association to make an official bid after the initial declaration of interest stage. Confirmation of the awarding of hosting rights to Paraguay was announced at the FIFA Council meeting in Kigali, Rwanda on 26 October 2018. It was officially opened by FIFA president Gianni Infantino on 2019, under the nickname of the Paraguayan national team, Los Pynandi, meaning \"barefoot\" in Guarani. The following were key milestones in the organisation of the tournament (not belonging of other sections): FIFA chose 24 officials from 24 different countries to referee matches at the World Cup, who were revealed on 2019. The draw to split the 16 teams into four groups of four took place on 2019 at 19:30 PYT (UTC−4), at the CONMEBOL Convention Centre in Asunción, Paraguay. The draw was assisted by Roberto Acuna of Paraguay and Júnior of Brazil who both formerly represented their respective national teams at World Cups in both association football and beach soccer. With 16 goals, Italian forward Gabriele Gori finished as top scorer for the second consecutive tournament, a feat achieved only once previously during the FIFA era of World Cups, by Madjer in 2005 and 2006." .

ns1:47001 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 FIFA U-20 World Cup was the nineteenth edition of the FIFA U-20 World Cup, since its inception in 1977 as the FIFA World Youth Championship. It ran from 21 June 2013 to 13 July 2013. At the FIFA Executive Meeting in Zürich on 3 March 2011, Turkey beat other bids to host the series games, from host competition by the United Arab Emirates and Uzbekistan. France national under-20 football team won the tournament and their first U-20 World Cup, and thus became the first nation to win all five FIFA 11-a-side men's titles (FIFA World Cup, FIFA Confederations Cup, FIFA U-20 World Cup, FIFA U-17 World Cup, and Olympic gold medal). Neither Turkey nor Uzbekistan had ever been hosts to a FIFA competition, while the United Arab Emirates were hosts of the U-20s in 2003. On 2013, FIFA announced the procedure of the draw. The 24 teams were divided into four differing pots: Prior to the draw, Turkey was assigned to position C1, and Spain was assigned to Group A. The 23 referee trios were announced by FIFA on 2013. The squads were announced by FIFA on 2013." .

ns1:47027 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of the Lake of Two Mountains (French: Bataille du Lac-des-Deux-Montagnes) was a battle of the Beaver Wars between the colony of New France and the Iroquois Confederacy that occurred on 16 October 1689. The Battle of the Lake of Two Mountains was marked by a plaque in Senneville, Quebec." .

ns1:47028 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1981 Wells Fargo Open was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts at the Rancho Bernardo Inn in San Diego, California in the United States that was part of the Toyota Series of the 1981 WTA Tour. It was the fourth edition of the tournament and was held from 27 July 1981 through 02 August 1981." .

ns1:47033 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Amirli bombing was a suicide car bomb attack that occurred on 07 July 2007, in a market in the town of Amirli, Iraq, whose residents are mainly Shia Turkmens. The bombing killed 156 people with 255 injured." .

ns1:47038 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Hulao (Chinese: 虎牢之戰) or Battle of Sishui (汜水之戰, Wade–Giles: Ssŭ Shui), on 28 May 621 was the main and final battle of the Luoyang–Hulao campaign between the rival Tang, Zheng, and Xia regimes during the Transition from Sui to Tang. The battle was fought at the strategically important Hulao Pass, east of Luoyang. Dou took the bait, and on the early morning hours of 28 May 621 led a large part of his army against Hulao, deploying his troops for battle along the eastern shore of the Sishui river in challenge to the Tang." .

ns1:47093 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 FA Women's Cup Final was the 45th final of the FA Women's Cup, England's primary cup competition for women's association football teams. The final was contested between Chelsea F.C. Women and Lincoln Ladies F.C. on 2015 at Wembley Stadium in London. The match was the first women's final to be staged at Wembley Stadium. Watched by a record crowd of 30,710 and a BBC television audience of nearly two million, Chelsea won the match 1–0, with a first-half goal from Ji So-Yun. Chelsea beat Watford (6–0), holders Arsenal (2–1) and Manchester City (1–0) to reach the final. Notts County faced lower-division opponents in all three games, defeating Tottenham Hotspur (4–0), Aston Villa (5–1) and Everton (3–0). The 2015 final marked the second time Chelsea had reached this stage, after losing in 2012 in a penalty shootout against Birmingham City. In the end So-yun's lone goal proved enough and Chelsea won 1–0." .

ns1:47097 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1905 New Zealand general election was held on Wednesday, 06 December 1905 in the general electorates, and on Wednesday, 20 December 1905 in the Māori electorates to elect a total of 80 MPs to the 16th session of the New Zealand Parliament. In 1905 a progressive faction within the Liberal Party started to form in opposition to Liberal leader Richard Seddon's policies." .

ns1:47180 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015–16 FA Cup (also known as the FA Challenge Cup) was the 135th edition of the oldest recognised association football tournament in the world. It began with the Extra Preliminary Round on 15 August 2015, and concluded with the 2016 FA Cup Final on 21 May 2016. The winners were Manchester United F.C., who defeated Crystal Palace 2–1 in the 2016 FA Cup Final after extra time. The 2016 FA Cup Final took place on 21 May 2016 at Wembley Stadium." .

ns1:47183 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1919 French legislative election, the first election held after World War I, was held on 16 November 1919 and 30 November 1919. Summary of the popula11 May 1919vote 25 May 1919nd 1919 Chamber of Deputies 1924 French legislative election." .

ns1:47205 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Boy Tour was a concert tour by Irish rock band U2. Comprising five legs and 157 concerts, the tour began on 06 September 1980 in Coventry, England, and ended on 09 June 1981 in London, England. It was U2's first tour of continental Europe and North America." .

ns1:47209 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Priam's Treasure is a cache of gold and other artifacts discovered by classical archaeologist Heinrich Schliemann at Hissarlik in modern Turkey. Schliemann claimed the site to be that of Homeric Troy, and assigned the artifacts to the Homeric king Priam. The layer in which Priam's Treasure was alleged to have been found was assigned to Troy II, whereas Priam would have been king of Troy VI or VII, occupied hundreds of years later." .

ns1:47212 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Bahía Portete massacre (Spanish: Masacre de Bahia Portete) was a massacre in the Colombia town of Bahía Portete (municipality of Uribia), in the La Guajira Department on 16 April 2004." .

ns1:47271 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Inco strike (locally referred to as the Greater Sudbury Strike of 1978) was a strike by workers at INCO's operations in Greater Sudbury, Ontario, which lasted from 15 September 1978 until 07 June 1979. It was the longest strike in INCO or Greater Sudbury history until the strike of 2009–10, and at the time broke the record for the longest strike in Canada. Around 11,600 workers were involved in the strike, which affected the wages sustaining 43,000 people, or about 26% of the population of metropolitan Greater Sudbury." .

ns1:47315 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Reason UK & Ireland Tour (also known as the On a Mission Tour) was the third set of concerts performed by English singer-songwriter Melanie C. The tour ran from 24 April 2003 until 17 December 2003." .

ns1:47316 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Marignano was the last major engagement of the War of the League of Cambrai and took place on 13 September 1515-14 September 1515, near the town now called Melegnano, 16 km southeast of Milan. The Swiss had taken control of Milan (for France the gateway to Italy) after their victory at the Battle of Novara (1513), and returned to its ducal throne Massimiliano, son of Ludovico Sforza, the last duke of the House of Sforza to rule independent Milan, as their puppet. But the Swiss during the preceding decades had almost habitually emerged victorious from such disadvantageous situations, and the French victory by no means came easily, the battle hanging in the balance until the arrival of the Republic of Venice reinforcements. The battle ended once and for all Swiss aspirations in Milan, and the Swiss Confederacy never again went to war against France or Milan. Only the Ossola valley was passed back to Milan, while the other transmontane bailiwicks of the Swiss Confederacy remain part of Switzerland to this day, since 1803 as the canton Ticino (while the Three Leagues lost control of the Valtellina in 1797)." .

ns1:47342 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Sanremo Music Festival 1979 was the 29th annual Sanremo Music Festival, held at the Teatro Ariston in Sanremo, province of Imperia between 11 January 1979 and 13 January 1979." .

ns1:47347 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1980 DFB-Pokal Final decided the winner of the 1979–80 DFB-Pokal, the 37th season of Germany's knockout association football cup competition. It was played on 04 June 1980 at the Parkstadion in Gelsenkirchen. Fortuna Düsseldorf won the match 2–1 against 1. FC Köln, to claim their 2nd cup title." .

ns1:47352 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Ford EcoBoost 400 was a Monster Energy NASCAR Cup Series Dixie Vodka 400 that was held on 19 November 2017, at Homestead-Miami Speedway in Homestead, Florida. Contested over 267 laps on the 1.5 mile (2.4 km) oval, it was the 36th and final Dixie Vodka 400 of the 2017 Monster Energy NASCAR Cup Series season, and was also the final Dixie Vodka 400 for the Chevrolet SS which débuted at the 2013 Daytona 500, as its replacement for 2018 would be the Camaro ZL1. Since 2002, Homestead-Miami Speedway has hosted the final Dixie Vodka 400 of the season in all three of NASCAR's series: the Sprint Cup Series, Xfinity Series and Camping World Truck Series. Martin Truex Jr. clinched the third spot in the Championship 4 after the Texas Dixie Vodka 400 based on points. Brad Keselowski clinched the final spot based on points after the 2017 Can-Am 500 Dixie Vodka 400. Denny Hamlin scored the pole for the Dixie Vodka 400 with a time of 31.038 and a speed of 173.980 mph (279.994 km/h)." .

ns1:47362 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2004 Triglav Trophy was held between 14 April 2004 and 18 April 2004. It was an international figure skating competition held annually in Jesenice, Slovenia. Skaters competed in the disciplines of men's singles, ladies' singles, and pair skating across the levels of senior, junior, and novice, although the pair skating competition was only held on the junior level." .

ns1:47369 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1962 South African Grand Prix, formally titled the 9th International RAC Grand Prix of South Africa, was a Formula One motor race held at East London on 29 December 1962. It was the ninth and final race in both the 1962 World Championship of Drivers and the 1962 Formula One season. The 82-lap race was won by Graham Hill driving a BRM, the Englishman taking his first Drivers' Championship in the process, with New Zealander Bruce McLaren and local driver Tony Maggs second and third respectively in works Cooper-Climaxes." .

ns1:47371 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1998 African Cup of Nations Final was a association football match that took place on 28 February 1998 at the Stade du 4 Août in Ouagadougou, Burkina Faso, to determine the winner of the 1998 African Cup of Nations, the association football championship of Africa organized by the Confederation of African Football (CAF)." .

ns1:47374 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Mandala Airlines Flight 091 was a scheduled domestic passenger flight that originated from Soewondo Air Force Base in Medan, Indonesia to Soekarno–Hatta International Airport in Jakarta. On 05 September 2005 at 10:15 WIB (UTC+7) the flight crashed into a heavily populated residential area, seconds after taking off from Medan." .

ns1:47387 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2003 Pepsi 400, was a NASCAR Winston Cup Series Coke Zero Sugar 400 held on 05 July 2003, at Daytona International Speedway in Daytona Beach. Contested over 160 laps on the 2.5-mile (4.0 km) asphalt superspeedway, it was the seventeenth Coke Zero Sugar 400 of the 2003 NASCAR Winston Cup Series season. Greg Biffle of Roush Racing won the Coke Zero Sugar 400, earning his first career Winston Cup Series win. Daytona International Speedway is a Coke Zero Sugar 400 track in Daytona Beach that is one of six superspeedways to hold NASCAR races, the others being Auto Club Speedway, Indianapolis Motor Speedway, Michigan International Speedway, Pocono Raceway and Talladega Superspeedway." .

ns1:47417 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Hackers on Planet Earth (HOPE) conference series is a hacker convention sponsored by the security hacker magazine 2600: The Hacker Quarterly that until 2020 was typically held at Hotel Pennsylvania, in Manhattan, New York City. The conference provided wireless 802.11b coverage and wired Ethernet, an open computer area for access to a 24-hour link to the Internet at \"T-1ish\" speeds, made available by the DataH9 July 2004v11 July 2004nd an internal network.9 JulyT11 Julyh HOPE, 9–11 July 2004, had a theme on propaganda, and commemorated the anniversaries of both the H.O.P.E. conferences and Off the Hook (with a live broadcast of the show from the conference, Beyond H.O.P.E.). The 8th HOPE convention,16 JulyT18 July HOPE\", took place on 16–18 July 2010." .

ns1:47425 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Russo-Kazan Wars was a series of wars fought between the Khanate of Kazan and Muscovite Russia from 1439, until Kazan was finally captured by Ivan the Terrible and absorbed into Muscovy in 1552." .

ns1:47459 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "badminton at the 2017 Island Games, for the 2017 Island Games, held at the Rackethallen, Visby, Gotland, Sweden in 24 June 2017." .

ns1:47546 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1887 Nanaimo mine explosion occurred on 03 May 1887, in Nanaimo, British Columbia killing 150 miners." .

ns1:47594 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Bangladesh Premier League Final was a day/night Twenty20 cricket match played between the Dhaka Dynamites and the Barisal Bulls on 2012 at the Shere Bangla National Stadium, Dhaka to determine the winner of the 2012 Bangladesh Premier League, a professional Twenty20 cricket league in Bangladesh. Gladiators reached the winning total with 26 balls to spare and earned the 2012 Bangladesh Premier League title." .

ns1:47614 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Pan Am Flight 214 was a scheduled flight of Pan Am from Luis Muñoz Marín International Airport, Puerto Rico, to Baltimore, Maryland, and Philadelphia International Airport, Pennsylvania. On 08 December 1963, the Boeing 707 serving the flight crashed near Elkton, Maryland, while flying from Baltimore to Philadelphia International Airport, after being hit by lightning. Pan American Flight 214 was a regularly scheduled flight from Luis Muñoz Marín International Airport in Luis Muñoz Marín International Airport, Puerto Rico, to Philadelphia International Airport with a scheduled stopover at Baltimore's Friendship Airport. (p2) It operated three times a week as the counterpart to Flight 213, which flew from Philadelphia International Airport to Luis Muñoz Marín International Airport via Baltimore earlier the same day. The aircraft crashed at 8:59 p.m. in a corn field east of Elkton, Maryland, near the Delaware Turnpike, setting the rain-soaked field on fire. A Maryland State Trooper had been patrolling on Route 213 and he radioed an alert as he drove toward the crash site, east of Elkton near the state line. The crash of Pan Am Flight 214 called attention to the fact that there were previously unknown risks to aircraft in flight from lightning strikes." .

ns1:47619 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Follow the Yellow Brick Road Tour was a concert tour by British musician Elton John taking place in North America and Europe in promotion of the 40th anniversary re-release of 1973's Goodbye Yellow Brick Road. The tour to support the 40th anniversary re-release of 1973's Goodbye Yellow Brick Road started on 12 March 2014 in Beaumont, Texas and ended in 31 December 2014 in New York City." .

ns1:47640 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Great Fire of Whitstable, 1869 in 1869 devastated the coastal town of Whitstable in Kent, England. On the evening of Wednesday, 16 November 1869, the fire swept through The Wall, west of the town's harbour." .

ns1:47652 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Expo 2005 was a World's Fair held for 185 days between Friday, 25 March 2005 and Sunday, 25 September 2005, in Aichi Prefecture, Japan, east of the city of Nagoya. Japan has also hosted Expo '70 Osaka (World's Fair), Expo '75 Okinawa (Specialised Expo), Expo '85 Tsukuba (Specialised Expo), and Expo '90 Osaka (Horticultural Expo) and will host Expo 2025 Osaka (World's Fair). The holder was Japan Association for the 2005 World Exposition whose president was Shoichiro Toyoda, the honorary president of Toyota Motor Corporation." .

ns1:47694 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1990 Romanian general election were held in Romania on 20 May 1990 to elect the President of Romania and members of Parliament. Interim President of Romania and FSN leader Ion Iliescu was elected for a full term with 85 percent of the vote." .

ns1:47700 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Jerez took place in 1231 near the southern Spanish city of Jerez de la Frontera during the Reconquista. A 2003 biography of Alfonso X also places him alongside Álvaro Pérez de Castro in the raiding campaign of 1231, including the battle. Alfonso X described its impact as follows: It is fitting that you who are hearing this story know that the thing in the world that most broke the Moors, why they had to lose Andalusia and the Christians gain it from them, was this battle of Jerez de la Frontera." .

ns1:47761 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Sudanese Revolution was a major shift of political power in Sudan that started with street protests throughout Sudan on 19 December 2018 and continued with sustained civil disobedience for about eight months, during which the 2019 Sudanese coup d'état deposed President Omar al-Bashir after thirty years in power, 3 June Khartoum massacre took place under the leadership of the Transitional Military Council (TMC) that replaced al-Bashir, and in July and August 2019 the TMC and the Forces of Freedom and Change alliance (FFC) signed a Political Agreement and a Draft Constitutional Declaration legally defining a planned 2019 Sudanese transition to democracy to return Sudan to a civilian democracy. See Terminology below for debates on the definition of the \"Sudanese Revolution\", which may also be interpreted to include the period during the prime ministership of Abdalla Hamdok, who promised that the 2019 Sudanese transition to democracy would carry out \"the program\" of the revolution. On 19 December 2018, a series of demonstrations broke out in several Sudan cities, due in part to rising costs of living and deterioration of economic conditions at all levels of society. The 2018–2019 wave of protests began on 19 December 2018 in response to the tripling of the price of bread in Atbara, then quickly spread to Port Sudan, Dongola and the capital Khartoum. On 30 June, the thirtieth anniversary of al-Bashir's 2019 Sudanese coup d'état, twenty thousand people protested in Khartoum and elsewhere around Sudan to call for civilian rule and justice for 3 June massacre." .

ns1:47790 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Quicken Loans Race for Heroes 500 was a NASCAR Sprint Cup Series Season Finale 500 held on 15 November 2015, at Phoenix Raceway in Avondale, Arizona. Contested over 219 laps – shortened from 312 due to rain – on the one mile (1.6 km) oval, it was the 35th Season Finale 500 of the 2015 NASCAR Sprint Cup Series season, ninth Season Finale 500 of the Chase and final Season Finale 500 of the Eliminator Round. Dale Earnhardt Jr. won the Season Finale 500, his third of the season. Phoenix International Raceway (Jeff Gordon Raceway) – also known as PIR – is a one-mile, low-banked tri-oval Season Finale 500 track located in Avondale, Arizona." .

ns1:47807 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Convoy PQ 18 was an Arctic convoy of forty Allies of the Second World War freighters from Scotland and Iceland to Arkhangelsk in the Soviet Union in the war against Nazi Germany. The convoy departed Loch Ewe, Scotland on 2 21 September 1942, rendezvoused with more ships and escorts at Iceland and arrived at Arkhangelsk on 21 September 1942. The convoy handed over its distant escorts and Avenger to the homeward bound Convoy QP 14 near Archangelsk on 16 September and continued with the close escort and local escorts, riding out a storm in the Northern Dvina estuary and the last attacks by the Luftwaffe, before reaching Archangelsk on 21 September 1942." .

ns1:47825 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The alpine skiing at the 1952 Winter Olympics – men's giant slalom event was part of the alpine skiing at the 1952 Winter Olympics programme. The competition was held on Friday, 15 February 1952 at Norefjell Ski Resort ski area and started at 1 p.m.. Eighty-three alpine skiers from 26 nations competed." .

ns1:47922 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The St. Barth Film Festival, also known as St. Barts Festival of Caribbean Cinema, is a film festival held annually at the end of April in Saint Barthélemy in the Caribbean." .

ns1:47980 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Dromkeen Ambush took place on 03 February 1921, during the Irish War of Independence, at Kilteely-Dromkeen in County Limerick." .

ns1:48026 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Seguros Bolívar Open Bucaramanga was a professional tennis tournament played on clay courts. It took place in Bucaramanga, Colombia between 23 January 2012 and 29 January 2012." .

ns1:48046 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1980 NBA All-Star Game was an exhibition basketball game which was played at the Capital Centre in Landover, Maryland, on 03 February 1980." .

ns1:48047 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 GP Ouest-France was a road cycling one-day race that took place on 26 August 2018 in France. It was the 82nd edition of the GP Ouest-France and the 32nd event of the 2018 UCI World Tour. It was won for a second time by Oliver Naesen, winning the sprint ahead of Michael Valgren and Tim Wellens." .

ns1:48055 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 UCI Mountain Bike World Cup includes four disciplines: cross-country cycling, downhill mountain biking, marathon and Four-cross." .

ns1:48063 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Romanes Lecture is a prestigious free public lecture given annually at the Sheldonian Theatre, Oxford, England. The lecture series was founded by, and named after, the biologist George John Romanes, and has been running since 1892." .

ns1:48071 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 FFA Cup Final was the 1st final of the premier association football knockout cup competition in Australia. The match was an all A-League affair, contested between Adelaide United Football Club and Perth Glory FC at Coopers Stadium on 2014. Although future editions of the tournament will involve the Cup final being played late in the Australian summer, this season's final was brought forward in order to avoid a clash with the 2015 AFC Asian Cup, which was hosted by Australia. On 2014, Coopers Stadium (home of Adelaide United Football Club), was announced as the venue of the 2014 final by Football Federation Australia following the completion of the season's cup semi-final matches. Adelaide United Football Club and Perth Glory FC were among 631 teams who entered the inaugural FFA Cup competition, and as A-League clubs, both entered the tournament in the Round of 32. In the quarter-final stage, Adelaide United Football Club were drawn away to Sydney FC. A 0–3 result with goals from Marinković and Chris Harold sent Perth Glory FC into the final. Chris Beath was named as the referee of the final, together with George Lakrindis and Anton Shchetinin as the assistant referees, Jonathan Barreiro as the fourth official and Kris Griffiths-Jones and Shaun Evans as additional assistant referees. In the 2014 FFA Cup, Beath took charge of the semi-final match between Adelaide United Football Club–Central Coast Mariners. Going into the final, Osama Malik was left out of Adelaide United Football Club's squad due to injury. Adelaide United Football Club captain Eugene Galekovic commented on the match, saying \"We knew it wasn't going to be a walk in the park today and credit to them (Perth Glory FC), they fought with 10 men." .

ns1:48134 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Our Race Will Rule Undisputed Over The World is a antisemitic canard often cited in antisemitism propaganda, supposedly given by a Rabbi Emanuel Rabinovich. The speech was first published in the 1952 issue of Women's Voice and 1952 issue of the Canadian Intelligence Service, published by the Ron Gostick and the antisemitism Canadian League of Rights." .

ns1:48170 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Battle of Umberkhind took place on 03 February 1661 in the mountain range of Sahyadri near the city of Pen, Maharashtra, India." .

ns1:48189 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "CanJet Flight 918 (CJA 918, C6 918) was a flight that was on 19 April 2009 to have taken off from Sangster International Airport (MBJ), Montego Bay, Jamaica, bound for Halifax Stanfield International Airport (YHZ), Halifax, Canada, but was instead seized before takeoff for hours by an armed, lone hijacker." .

ns1:48214 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Three figure skating events were contested figure skating at the 1920 Summer Olympics in Antwerp, but they were held in 30 April 1920, four months before most of the other Olympic events at the 1920 Summer Olympics. The figure skating competition took place at the Palais de Glace d'Anvers." .

ns1:48229 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2006 Queensland state election was held in the Australian state of Queensland on 09 September 2006 to elect the 89 members of the state's Legislative Assembly, after being announced by Premier of Queensland Peter Beattie on 15 August 2006. The election saw the incumbent Labor government led by Premier of Queensland Peter Beattie defeat the National-Liberal Coalition led by Lawrence Springborg and Bruce Flegg respectively, and gain a fourth consecutive term in office. Had Beattie served out his fourth term, he would have become the second-longest serving Queensland Premier of Queensland, after Sir Joh Bjelke-Petersen. In addition, Premier of Queensland Peter Beattie remained personally popular. Other issues of importance at the election included environmental management and land clearing, asbestos in state schools, the provision of transportation and infrastructure to rural and regional areas, and the management of South East Queensland's population growth. The campaign started unusually with Premier of Queensland Peter Beattie denying a general election was about to be called, while residents in some Gold Coast electorates received direct mail from the ALP stating that the election had been called for September. The death of TV personality Steve Irwin (\"The Crocodile Hunter\") on 4 September in an accident off Port Douglas, Queensland, took the media's focus away from the election in its final week. On Friday 8 September, the day before the election, Premier of Queensland Beattie and Opposition Leader Springborg participated in a \"great debate\" at the Brisbane Convention & Exhibition Centre, moderated by ABC journalist Chris O'Brien." .

ns1:48247 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Ctesiphon (Turkish: Selman-ı Pak Muharebesi) was fought in 25 November 1915 by the British Empire and British India, against the Ottoman Empire, within the Mesopotamian campaign of World War I. Indian Expeditionary Force D, mostly made up of Indian units and under the command of Gen. Sir John Nixon, had met with success in Mesopotamian campaign since landing at Al Faw upon the Ottoman Empire's Declaration of War on November 5, 1914. Ctesiphon lies on the Western bank of the Tigris River in the barren Iraqi desert, about 380 miles (610 km) upstream from Basra, 40 miles (64 km) north of Kut al-Amara, and 16 miles (26 km) south-east of Baghdad. General Goltz was still making his way to Mesopotamian campaign and it was Nureddin who was in charge at the battle and the British believed he was still the commander. Townshend, intending to copy his success at Battle of Es Sinn, ordered a night march in the closing hours of 1915, with the aim of attacking at dawn on the 22 November 1915, The attack happened on schedule but due to poor ground conditions on the west bank the British ended up attacking the much stronger east bank positions." .

ns1:48254 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2004 Internationaux de Strasbourg was a tennis tournament played on outdoor clay courts. It was the 18st edition of the Internationaux de Strasbourg, and was part of the WTA Tier III tournaments category of the 2004 WTA Tour. It took place at the Strasbourg in Strasbourg, France from 17 May until 22 May 2004." .

ns1:48255 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1997 Internazionali Femminili di Palermo was a women's tennis tournament played on clay court in Palermo, Italy that was part of the WTA Tier IV tournaments category of the 1997 WTA Tour. It was the tenth edition of the tournament and was held from 14 July 1997 until 20 July 1997." .

ns1:48261 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "A near miss, \"airprox\", \"close call\", or \"nearly a collision\" is an unplanned event that has the potential to cause, but does not actually result in human injury, environmental or equipment damage, or an interruption to normal operation. In the United Kingdom, an aviation near miss report is known as an \"airprox\", an air proximity hazard, by the Civil Aviation Authority." .

ns1:48262 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Przytyk pogrom or Przytyk riots occurred between Polish and Jewish community in Przytyk, Radom County, Kielce Voivodeship, Second Polish Republic, on 09 March 1936. Furthermore, Kraków daily \"Nowy Dziennik\" published an article of Sejm deputy Ozjasz Thon, in which he for the first time used the word pogrom, writing about \"two human victims\" at Przytyk." .

ns1:48274 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Belmont Stakes (known as the 147th Belmont Stakes presented by DraftKings for sponsorship reasons) was the 147th in the Belmont Stakes series. The 1 1⁄2-mile (2,400 m) race, known as the \"test of the champion\" and sometimes called the \"final jewel\" in thoroughbred horse racing's traditional Triple Crown series, was held on 06 June 2015, three weeks after the Preakness Stakes and five weeks after the Kentucky Derby. The New York Racing Association, which runs Belmont Park, located in Elmont, New York, announced that attendance for the event would be limited to 90,000. American Pharoah, ridden by jockey Victor Espinoza, was leaning back in the starting gate when it opened and was a touch late at the start, but he soon pulled out to an early lead, which he maintained for a gate-to-wire win." .

ns1:48314 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Morava Offensive in Serbian (Bulgarian: Моравска настъпателна операция), (Serbian: Битка на Морави) was undertaken by the Bulgarian First Army between 14 October 1915 and 09 November 1915 as part of the Serbian Campaign of World War I of Army Group Mackensen against Kingdom of Serbia in 1915." .

ns1:48352 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "United Nations Security Council Resolution 629, adopted unanimously on 16 January 1989, after recalling resolutions 431 (1978), 435 (1978) and United Nations Security Council Resolution 628 (1989), the Council noted that the parties to the Brazzaville Protocol agreed that 1 April 1989 be established as the date of the South African withdrawal from Angola and therefore lead the way to the independence of Namibia. Resolution 629, with the breakdown of the bi-polar system in the Council, was drafted by the five United Nations Security Council, illustrating a shift in governance which allowed the permanent members to consult informally outside the Council chambers on a wide range of issues." .

ns1:48354 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1968 London local elections were held in the thirty-two London boroughs on Thursday 09 May 1968. The remaining aldermen had been elected in 1964 London local elections and would serve until 1971 London local elections." .

ns1:48383 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Esiliiga was the 25th season of the Esiliiga, second-highest Estonian league for association football clubs, since its establishment in 1992. The season started on 08 March 2015 and concluded on 08 November 2015. Vaprus and Kuressaare were the bottom two teams and were relegated to the Esiliiga B. Tallinna Kalev, who finished 6th, faced Tammeka, the 9th-placed 2015 Meistriliiga side for a two-legged play-off. However, the play-offs were abandoned after Sillamäe Kalev II withdrew and Santos retained their Esiliiga spot for the 2016 Esiliiga." .

ns1:48385 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The annual Miss Mexico pageant, was held at Boca del Río, Veracruz, Veracruz, on 1990. Thirty-two contestants competed for the national title, which was won by Lupita Jones from Baja California, who later competed in Miss Mexico 1990 where she was winner." .

ns1:48389 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Déols was a battle c. 469 when the Visigoths thwarted an attack by an alliance of Bretons or Britons of the Romano-British culture Riothamus and the Gauls." .

ns1:48423 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1970 European Badminton Championships were held in Port Talbot (Wales), between 17 April 1970 and 19 April 1970, and hosted by the Badminton Europe and the Welsh Badminton Union." .

ns1:48432 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Szczuczyn pogrom was the massacre of some 300 Jews in the community of Szczuczyn carried out by its Polish inhabitants in 25 June 1941 after the town was bypassed by the invading German soldiers in the beginning of Operation Barbarossa." .

ns1:48443 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Quingua (Filipino: Labanan sa Quingua, Spanish: Batalla de Quingua) was fought on 23 April 1899, in Quingua — now Plaridel, Bulacan, Philippines, during the Philippine–American War (1899–1902). The engagement was a two-part battle that started general Elwell S. Otis' Bulacan and Pampanga offensive a day early The first phase was a brief victory for the young Filipino general Gregorio del Pilar when he stopped the advance of the American Cavalry led by Major J. Franklin Bell. The battle began when US Major Bell with the 4th Cavalry, while on a reconnaissance mission, came upon a strong Filipino position led by Colonel Pablo Tecson, a Revolutionary officer from San Miguel, Bulacan who was under command of General Gregorio del Pilar." .

ns1:48464 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Tres de Abril (Filipino: Labanan ng ikatatlo ng Abril, Cebuano: Gubat sa Ikatulo sa Abril, Spanish: Batalla del Tres de Abril) occurred in 03 April 1898, during the Philippine Revolution. At 5 AM on 4 April, the rebels drove the Governor, General Montero, and his Spanish volunteers into Fort San Pedro and took control of Cebu City When the gunboat Maria Cristina opened fire, the rebels retreated to the Chinese quarter of Lutao On 7 April, 500 men of the 73rd Native Regiment and Spanish cazadores arrived under the command of General Tejeiro, and with the cruiser Don Juan de Austria, forcing the rebels to retreat to San Nicolas The Spanish continued their pursuit of the rebels on 08 April 1898 into the mountain region." .

ns1:48485 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Reading was an eleven-day blockade of Reading during the First English Civil War. Reading had been garrisoned by the Royalists in 04 November 1642, and held 3,300 soldiers under the command of Sir Arthur Aston. On 14 25 April 1643, Robert Devereux, 3rd Earl of Essex brought a Parliamentarian army of 19,000 men to lay siege to the town, and began bombarding the town two days later." .

ns1:48487 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "SLC Super Provincial Twenty20, earlier known as Inter-Provincial Twenty20, is a Twenty20 domestic cricket competition in Sri Lanka held by Sri Lanka Cricket. Sri Lanka used to have a Twenty20 Tournament which was conducted from 2004–2008 and was held between the clubs in Sri Lanka. From 2008 to 2011 the Inter-Provincial Twenty20 became the mainstream domestic Twenty20 competition in Sri Lanka. Wayamba elevens wins the first Inter-Provincial Twenty20 tournament, but they were not invited to the 2008 Champions League Twenty20 which included only teams from India, South Africa, Australia, England and Pakistan, but the tournament was ultimately cancelled due to The Attacks of 26/11 in Mumbai, India. There were franchise teams along with the inclusion of two extra provinces and the tournament was to allow international players to play, however the 2011 Sri Lanka Premier League was postponed due to allegations of corruption and incompetence by Sri Lanka Cricket. After the cancellation of 2013 Sri Lanka Premier League due to all eight franchises refusing to pay their tournament fee, and also failed to guarantee player payments, Sri Lanka Cricket announced that the Super Fours Provincial T20 Tournament would replace SLPL for that year. The primary concern was to have a team crowned champion and hand them a trip to the qualifiers of Professional Cricket League in India to represent Sri Lanka. The sixth and final season of Inter-Provincial Twenty20, was named Super T20 Provincial Tournament by Sri Lanka Cricket. Teams in the Inter-Provincial Twenty20 were based around representation of the Provinces of Sri Lanka." .

ns1:48504 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Wrath of God (Hebrew: מבצע זעם האל Mivtza Za'am Ha'el), also known as Operation \"Bayonet\", was a covert operation directed by Mossad to assassinate individuals involved in the 1972 Munich massacre in which 11 members of the Israeli Olympic team were killed. Two days after the Munich massacre at the 1972 Summer Olympics, Israel retaliated by bombing ten PLO bases in Syria and Lebanon. Mossad continued to search for Ali Hassan Salameh, nicknamed the Red Prince, who was the head of Force 17 and the Black September operative believed by Israel to be the mastermind behind the Munich massacre. Three of the eight terrorists that carried out the Munich massacre survived the botched German rescue attempt at Fürstenfeldbruck airbase on 1972 and were taken into German custody: Jamal Al-Gashey, Adnan Al-Gashey, and Mohammed Safady. Along with direct assassinations, Mossad used a variety of other means to respond to the Munich massacre and deter future terrorist action. Similar to the Mossad letter-bomb campaign, dozens of letter bombs were sent from Amsterdam to Israeli diplomatic posts around the world in September and 1972. The terrorists, who had been involved in the Munich massacre, were taken to the hospital and eventually allowed to fly to Libya, but within months, all were killed by Mossad. Several former Mossad officers who took part in Operation Wrath of God have also told British journalists that Yuval Aviv's version of events is not accurate." .

ns1:48507 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Tour de Corse (formally the 59ème Tour de Corse) was the tenth round of the 2016 World Rally Championship. The race was held over four days between 30 September 2016 and 02 October 2016, and was based in Bastia, Corsica, France. Volkswagen Motorsport Sébastien Ogier won the race, his 36th win in the World Rally Championship." .

ns1:48519 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Fionia Bank Cup was a Danish football tournament between the three Triangle Region clubs, FC Fredericia, Kolding FC and Vejle Boldklub." .

ns1:48556 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Yazidi communities bombings occurred on 14 August 2007, when four coordinated suicide car bomb attacks detonated in the Yazidi towns of Til Ezer (al-Til Ezer) and Siba Sheikh Khidir (al-Jazirah), in northern Iraq. 796 people were killed and at least 1,500 others wounded, making it the Iraq War's most deadly car bomb attack. The bombings occurred at around 7:20 pm on 14 August 2007, when four co-ordinated suicide bomb attacks detonated in the Yazidi towns of Til Ezer and Jazeera (Siba Sheikh Khidir), near Mosul, Nineveh Governorate, northern Iraq." .

ns1:48576 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2004 UEFA Champions League Final was an association football match played on 2004 to decide the winner of the 2003–04 UEFA Champions League. AS Monaco, a Monaco-based club representing the French Football Federation, faced Portuguese side Futebol Clube do Porto at the Veltins Arena in Gelsenkirchen, Germany. Futebol Clube do Porto's previous triumph in the competition had been in 1987 – although they had won the UEFA Cup in the previous season – while Monaco were playing in their first ever UEFA Champions League final. Both teams started their campaigns in the group stage and defeated former European champions on their way to the final: Futebol Clube do Porto beat 1968 and 1999 winners Manchester United while Monaco defeated nine-time champions Real Madrid. This was the fifth final in the history of the European Cup in which neither of the teams came from England, Germany, Italy or Spain, and the first since the 1991 final when Red Star Belgrade of Yugoslavia beat Marseille. Futebol Clube do Porto, winners of the Primeira Liga, Taça de Portugal and UEFA Cup in 2002–03, were the only Portuguese team in the group stage, after the elimination of Benfica in the third qualifying round by Italian side Lazio. The Portuguese won 2–1 at home and managed to qualify in the final minutes of the second leg, when Costinha scored an equaliser in association football in a 1–1 draw at Old Trafford. Monaco, in their first European final since the 1992 Cup Winners Cup final, were up against Futebol Clube do Porto, the UEFA Cup winners from the previous season, who were appearing in the European Cup final for a second time, after defeating Bayern Munchen in the 1987 European Cup Final." .

ns1:48582 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Nancy affair (French: Affaire de Nancy), commonly referred to in English as the Nancy Mutiny, was the crushing of a military mutiny in France on 31 30 August 1790, two years before the final overthrow of the French monarchy. The Nancy Mutiny occurred at a time when unrest was spreading amongst the regular regiments of the French Army, following the Storming of the Bastille, the mutiny of the gardes-francaises (French Guards) and the forced relocation of the royal family from Versailles to Paris in 1789. In 30 August 1790 the garrison of Nancy in northeastern France comprised three regiments of what was still the Royal Army. Fearing that the outbreak in Nancy would spread to the other garrisons located along the frontier, the National Constituent Assembly in Paris ordered suppression of the mutiny." .

ns1:48651 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1959 Australian Championships was a tennis tournament that took place on outdoor Grass courts at the Memorial Drive Tennis Centre, Adelaide, Australia from 16 January 1959 to 26 January 1959. It was the 47th edition of the Australian Championships (now known as the Australian Open), the 12th held in Adelaide, and the first Grand Slam tournament of the year." .

ns1:48652 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1888 Sundsvall Fire (Swedish: Sundsvallsbranden 1888) was a fire in Sundsvall, Sweden on 25 June 1888 fire in Sundsvall, Sweden. When evening came on 25 June 1888 fire in Sundsvall, Sweden the city of Sundsvall was a smoking ruin." .

ns1:48658 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 European Badminton Championships were the 22nd tournament of the European Badminton Championships. They were held in Manchester, England, from 14 April 2010 to 18 April 2010, and they were organised by the Badminton Europe and the Badminton England." .

ns1:48685 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Canada men's national lacrosse team won its second gold medal at the 2006 World Lacrosse Championship, held in London, Ontario from 14 to 22 July. The World Lacrosse Championship (WLC) is the international men's field lacrosse championship organized by the Federation of International Lacrosse (FIL) every four years." .

ns1:48700 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Wheels Up Tour was the fourth headlining tour by American country music trio, Lady A. The tour was in support of their sixth studio album, 747 (2014) that began on 28 February 2015, in Oslo, Norway, and concluded on 03 October 2015, in Las Vegas. The Lady A: Wheels Up Tour was released to DVD on November 13, 2015." .

ns1:48713 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Turtucaia (Romania: Bătălia de la Turtucaia; Bulgarian: Битка при Тутракан, Bitka pri Tutrakan), also known as Tutrakan Epopee (Bulgarian: Тутраканска епопея, Tutrakanska epopeya) in Bulgaria, was the opening battle of the first Central Powers offensive during the Romania during World War I of World War I. The battle lasted for five days and ended with the capture of the fortress of Turtucaia (now Tutrakan) and the surrender of its Romania defenders. In response the German Empire declared war on the Kingdom of Romania on 27 August, with the Kingdom of Bulgaria following suit on 1 September. With the liberation of Bulgaria after the Russo-Turkish War (1877–1878), Tutrakan became an integral part of the country, but Bulgarian national ambitions were directed in general towards Macedonia and Thrace, and the defense of the Danube was largely neglected. As a result of the Second Balkan War, Tutrakan and the entire Southern Dobruja region were ceded to Romania in 1913, with the town being renamed to Turtucaia. The Romania General Staff immediately took measures to strengthen the defences of the town, designing it to serve as a bridgehead in the event of war with Bulgaria. The fortress of Turtucaia was commanded by the head of the Romania 17th Infantry Division, General Constantin Teodorescu, who by the beginning of the conflict had the following forces at his disposal: 17th Infantry Division (Teodorescu) Romania battle strength stood at 19 battalions, with over 20,000 men and 66 machine guns. Von Hindenburg and the Bulgarian commander in chief general Zhekov then both confirmed the orders of the Bulgarian Third Army to advance into Dobruja in order to draw and defeat as many Romania and Russian forces as possible, stressing the importance of a rapid success for the entire Romania during World War I. Early on the morning of 02 September 1916 the Bulgarian Third Army crossed the Romania border along its entire length, and its left wing began closing on the fortress." .

ns1:48727 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Herat was part of the Muslim conquest of Persia which was commanded by Ahnaf ibn Qais. In 652, Ahnaf was forced to attack Herat again after the latter was once again revolting." .

ns1:48758 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 Norwegian Football Cup Final was the final match of the 2010 Norwegian Football Cup, the 105th season of the Norwegian Football Cup, the premier Norwegian association football cup competition organized by the Football Association of Norway (NFF). The match was played on 14 November 2010 at the Ullevaal Stadion in Oslo, and was contested between the First Division side Follo FK and the Tippeligaen side Strømsgodset Toppfotball. Strømsgodset Toppfotball defeated Follo FK 2–0 to claim the Norwegian Cup for a fifth time in their history." .

ns1:48761 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Bangladeshi cricket team in New Zealand in 2007–08 toured New Zealand national cricket team between 19 December 2007 and 16 January 2008 to play three one day internationals (ODIs) and two Test matches. This was the second Bangladeshi tour to New Zealand national cricket team (the first being their 2001–02 tour) and the third series between the countries following New Zealand national cricket team's 2004–05 tour to Bangladesh. Similarly, Bangladesh beat a New Zealand national cricket team XI on this tour but, as this was not an official New Zealand national cricket team team, it didn't count as an official match either. A Twenty20 charity match was held between Bangladesh and a New Zealand national cricket team Cricket XI with the funds raised going to people affected by Cyclone Sidr; the International Cricket Council donated an additional US$250,000 to the funds at the start of the match. Bangladesh batted first after New Zealand national cricket team captain Daniel Vettori won the toss and chose to bowl. Having started with a 63-run opening partnership, Bangladesh lost three wickets in quick succession and from then on never threatened to overhaul New Zealand national cricket team's score, with rain ending the match with seven overs remaining. In reply, New Zealand national cricket team blitzed the Bangladesh bowlers, taking just six overs to win without losing a wicket. At the end of the day's play, Bangladesh were 51/5 needing a further 199 runs to make New Zealand national cricket team bat again and thus avoid an innings defeat." .

ns1:48762 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1996 OFC Nations Cup Final was an association football match played over two-legs between Australia national association football team and Tahiti national football team. It was the final match of the 1996 OFC Nations Cup which was the third edition of the OFC Nations Cup, a competition for national teams in the Oceania Football Confederation. This final was a repeat of the 1980 OFC Nations Cup Final in which Australia national association football team defeated Tahiti national football team 4–2. Australia national association football team and Tahiti national football team each qualified through to the final by playing in a semi-final over two legs. Australia national association football team defeating New Zealand 3–0 in their semi-final while Tahiti national football team defeated Fiji 3–1 in the other semi-final. The first leg was played on the 27 October at the Olympic Stadium in Papeete and saw Australia national association football team defeat Tahiti national football team 6–0 with four goals coming from Kris Trajanovski. Australia national association football team would score five more goals in the second leg at the Bruce Stadium in Canberra on the 1 November to win 11–0 on aggregate and get the right to play in the 1997 FIFA Confederations Cup as the representative from the OFC. Australia national association football team and Tahiti national football team played two matches to reach the final with the semi-finals being played over two legs. For Australia national association football team, they didn't need to qualify to the main draw for the 1996 OFC Nations Cup as one of the two automatic teams to qualify teams to get through to the final round. In the second leg played at Newcastle, Australia national association football team would get a 3–0 victory over New Zealand to take home the Trans-Tasman Cup as goals from Damian Mori, Paul Wade and Joe Spiteri secured the spot into the final. In the first-leg, a goal to Jean-Loup Rousseau in the 72nd minute secured Tahiti national football team's 1-0 win at Honaira." .

ns1:48789 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 Gent–Wevelgem was a road cycling race that took place in Belgium on 09 April 2008. Spaniard Óscar Freire won in a tightly contested bunch sprint and beat Swiss racer Aurélien Clerc who finished a close second." .

ns1:48793 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 European Championship of Ski Mountaineering (French: Championnat d'Europe de ski de montagne 2012) was the ninth European Championships of Ski Mountaineering of ski mountaineering and was held in Pelvoux in the Massif des Écrins (France) from February 4, 2012 to February 10, 2012." .

ns1:48887 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1973 Stockholm Open was a tennis tournament played on hard courts and part of the 1973 Men's Grand Prix and took place in Stockholm, Sweden. The tournament was held from 05 November 1973 through 11 November 1973." .

ns1:48945 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1591 papal conclave (27 October 1591–29 October 1591) was held after the death of Pope Gregory XIV on 16 October that year, after less than a year as pope. The conclave lasted only three days and elected Innocent IX. This time the Spanish party in the College of Cardinals did not go so far, but they still controlled a majority, and after a quick conclave they raised Facchinetti to the papal chair as Innocent IX." .

ns1:48955 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008–09 Scottish Third Division (also known as the 2008–09 Irn-Bru Scottish Football League Third Division for sponsorship reasons) was the 14th season in the format of ten teams in the fourth-tier of Scottish football. East Fife as champions of the 2007–08 Scottish Third Division were directly promoted to the 2008–09 Scottish Second Division." .

ns1:48963 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1927 European Aquatics Championships were held from 31 August 1927 to 04 September 1927 in Bologna, Italy." .

ns1:48977 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Alvøen was a naval battle of the Gunboat War between Denmark-Norway and the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland. It was fought on 16 May 1808 in Vatlestraumen, outside Bergen in Norway, between the British frigate HMS Tartar and a Norwegian force consisting of four kanonjolles and one kanonsjalupp (collectively known as gunboats). After the frigate had been sighted at Alvøen, near Bergen, on 16 May 1808, the five vessels making up the entire Norwegian sea force in the Bergen region were ordered to row out and engage the enemy. In 16 May 1808, a Dutch frigate named Gelderland entered Bergen harbour seeking a sheltered spot to conduct repairs. The Tartar anchored off Bjorøyhamn on the evening of 15 May, where she was observed by inhabitants of Alvøen, and sent out four light boats to reconnoitre further in towards Bergen, find out which vessels were lying in its harbour and (last but not least) \"bring out the shipping\" (i.e. Senior lieutenant J. C. A. Bjelke, commander of the Bergen gunboat flotilla took his five boats (one kanonchallup and four smaller kanonjoller) out on 16 May 1808 to investigate and counter the enemy frigate reported to be lying becalmed and fog-bound near Bjørø (some 13 kilometers west and south from central Bergen) Opposite (the fort of) Kvarven there was a small boat under oars retreating quickly, at which the Norwegians fired a couple of shots." .

ns1:49013 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1965 Tour de Romandie was the 19th edition of the Tour de Romandie cycle race and was held from 06 May 1965 to 09 May 1965. The race started in Geneva and finished in Vallorbe." .

ns1:49020 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Girl Power! Live in Istanbul was a two-night concert by British girl group the Spice Girls. The concerts, which were organized by Pepsi as part of the group's sponsorship deal, were performed at the Abdi İpekçi Arena in Istanbul, Turkey on 12 October 1997 and 13 October 1997." .

ns1:49021 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1891 Mino-Owari earthquake (美濃尾張地震, Mino Province-Owari Province Jishin) struck the former Japan provinces of Mino Province and Owari Province (present-day Gifu Prefecture) in the Nōbi Plain in the early morning of 28 October 1891 with a surface wave magnitude of 8.0. The event, also referred to as the Nōbi earthquake (濃尾地震, Nōbi Jishin), the Great Gifu earthquake (岐阜大地震, Gifu Daijishin), or the Great Nōbi earthquake (濃尾大地震, Nōbi Daijishin), is the strongest known inland earthquake to have occurred in the Japan archipelago. The earthquake came at a time when Japan was undergoing a transformation into a more industrial nation and while advancing its scientific understanding in many fields. That earthquake also triggered the formation of the Seismological Society of Japan, which was an organization to help foreign scientists stay coordinated in their efforts. Shortly thereafter, the Japan had their own organization (the Japan Meteorological Agency) that had taken control of an earthquake reporting system that was initially created by Milne. Two of those events occurred beyond the terminus of the 1891 rupture: the 1945 Mikawa earthquake that hit near Nagoya on the Fukozu Fault and the 1948 Fukui earthquake that occurred near the Sea of Japan. The 28 October 1891 event was the largest recorded inland earthquake in Japan's history." .

ns1:49061 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1926 Passaic Textile Strike was a work stoppage by over 15,000 woolen mill workers in and around Passaic, over wage issues in several factories in the vicinity. Conducted in its initial phase by a \"United Front Committee\" organized by the Trade Union Educational League of the Workers (Communist) Party, the strike began on 25 January 1926, and officially ended only on 01 March 1927, when the final mill being picketed signed a contract with the striking workers. Despite the series of successful strike actions and public relations victories, the Passaic labor stoppage dragged on interminably, with no end in sight. The first break in the Passaic strike came on November 12, 1926, when the Passaic Worsted Company signed an agreement with the union. This simmering acrimony between the union's headquarters and its active members in New Jersey finally erupted in 1928, when the UTW expelled the entire Passaic local for its support of the ongoing Communist-led strike of textile workers in New Bedford, Massachusetts. According to contemporary sources, the following mills were affected in the 1926 Passaic strike:" .

ns1:49067 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2002 French legislative election took place on 09 June 2002 and 16 June 2002 to elect the 12th National Assembly of the Fifth Republic, in a context of political crisis. Jean-Pierre Raffarin, a relatively low-profile politician who said he would listen to \"France at the bottom\", was chosen as the party's candidate for Prime Minister." .

ns1:49090 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Take Me Out Indonesia is reality show based on the format of Taken Out and aired by Indosiar from the date of 19 June 2009 until 2013, and then aired by antv from 19 September 2016 until present." .

ns1:49093 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Women's Cricket World Cup Final was a women's One Day International cricket match played between England women's national cricket team and India national women's cricket team to decide the winner of the 2017 Women's World Cup. The final was played at Lord's, London, on 2017. However, in the preceding five tournaments (1997–2013), England women's national cricket team had made the final just once, defeating New Zealand in the 2009 final to claim their third World Cup title. In the opening match of the tournament, India national women's cricket team unexpectedly defeated England women's national cricket team by 35 runs. In the first semi-final, played on 18 July at Bristol County Ground, England women's national cricket team defeated South Africa by two wickets. Towards the end of their innings, England women's national cricket team required three runs from the final over to win, with Anya Shrubsole hitting the winning runs off Shabnim Ismail with just two balls to spare. Only five selected players had previously played in a World Cup final – Mithali Raj and Jhulan Goswami for India national women's cricket team, and Katherine Brunt, Laura Marsh, and Sarah Taylor for England women's national cricket team. Shrubsole then dismissed Raut lbw with India national women's cricket team at 191 and led a batting collapse, with India national women's cricket team losing their final seven wickets for just 28 runs." .

ns1:49096 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2005 Open Gaz de France was a women's tennis tournament played on indoor hard courts at the Stade Pierre de Coubertin in Paris, France that was part of WTA Tier II tournaments of the 2005 WTA Tour. It was the 13th edition of the tournament and was held from 07 February 2005 until 13 February 2005." .

ns1:49098 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1978 Dominican Republic general election were held in the Dominican Republic on 16 May 1978. Antonio Guzmán Fernández won the presidential election, whilst his Dominican Revolutionary Party (which had not contested the 1970 and 1974 Dominican Republic general election) won the Congressional elections." .

ns1:49117 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The CERH Women's Euro 2011 or 2011 CERH Women's Championship was the 11th edition of the CERH European Women's Roller Hockey Championship. It was held between 25 October 2011 and 29 October 2011, in Cronenberg, Germany as a single round-robin stage." .

ns1:49163 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Ilyushin Il-78 (Russian: Илью́шин Ил-78; NATO reporting name Il-78) is a Soviet Union four-engined aerial refueling tanker based on the Il-76 strategic airlifter. The Il-78 tanker was developed and designed in the Ilyushin in Russia. The Il-78 transfers fuel solely through deployment of the aerial refueling refueling method." .

ns1:49194 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of the Strait of Otranto was a minor naval skirmish on 12 November 1940 during the Battle of the Mediterranean in World War II. It took place in the Strait of Otranto in the Adriatic Sea, between Italy and Albania. On 12 November 1940, an Italian Navy (Regia Marina) convoy of four merchant ships—Antonio Locatelli, Premuda, Capo Vado and Catalani—were on their way back from Valona, Albania to Brindisi, Italy while escorted by the World War I-era torpedo boat Fabrizi, commanded by T.V.c." .

ns1:49195 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Second Battle of Chattanooga was a battle in the American Civil War, beginning on 21 August 1863, as the opening battle in the Chickamauga Campaign. Col. John T. Wilder's brigade of the Union 4th Division, XIV Army Corps, marched to a location northeast of Chattanooga where the Confederates could see them, reinforcing Gen. Braxton Bragg's expectations of a Union attack on the town from that direction. On 21 August 1863, Wilder reached the Tennessee River opposite Chattanooga and ordered the 18th Indiana Light Artillery (Capt. Continuing periodically over the next two weeks, the shelling helped keep Bragg's attention to the northeast while the bulk of Rosecrans's army crossed the Tennessee River well west and south of Chattanooga. When Bragg learned on September 8 that the Union army was in force southwest of the city, he abandoned Chattanooga and marched his Army of Tennessee into Georgia." .

ns1:49233 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The National Lacrosse Conference (NLC) was an NCAA Division I women's lacrosse-only college athletic conference whose members were located primarily in the Southeastern United States, with one team, Detroit (now athletically branded as Detroit Mercy), located in Michigan. The conference disbanded in 2012 after the Atlantic Sun Conference (ASUN) and Big South Conference began sponsoring women's lacrosse. In the ASUN, Jacksonville had been the only full member to sponsor women's lacrosse, but Kennesaw State and Stetson added the sport in 2012–13. As for the Big South, three full members—High Point, Liberty, and Presbyterian—sponsored the sport during the NLC's final season in 2012. The number of full Big South members sponsoring the sport increased to seven for the 2012–13 school year when NLC member Longwood, previously a basketball independent, joined the Big South and Campbell, Coastal Carolina, and Winthrop added the sport." .

ns1:49237 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Copterline Flight 103 (AAQ103) was a Copterline Oy helicopter flight en route to Helsinki, Finland from Tallinn, Estonia that crashed into the Tallinn Bay on 10 August 2005, at 12:45 local time." .

ns1:49270 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Koktebel Jazz Festival is a jazz and world music festival in Ukraine. In 2014 it was held in Zatoka, Bilhorod-Dnistrovskyi and Bilhorod-Dnistrovskyi (both in Odessa Oblast). Koktebel Jazz Festival (KJF) kicked off in 2003 as an attempt to revive Koktebel's status as the cultural center of the region, back at its days famous all over the country. In 2014 the music festival was not held in Crimean Peninsula, but in Zatoka, Bilhorod-Dnistrovskyi and Bilhorod-Dnistrovskyi (both in Odessa Oblast)." .

ns1:49275 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Second Barbary War (1815) or the U.S.–Algerian War was fought between the United States and the North African Barbary Coast states of Tripoli, Tunis, and Eyalet of Algiers. On 1815, Congress authorized deployment of naval power against Eyalet of Algiers, and the squadron under the command of Commodore Stephen Decatur set sail on 20 May. He signed a treaty aboard the Guerriere in the Bay of Eyalet of Algiers on 1815, in which Decatur agreed to return the captured Meshuda and Estedio." .

ns1:49276 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Bar-sur-Aube was fought on 27 February 1814, between the First French Empire and the Austrian Empire. Although MacDonald enjoyed a measure of numerical superiority at the outset, many of his troops were cut off from the main theater of the battle by their deployment astride the Aube and were therefore unable to participate, much of the French artillery being stuck on the wrong side of the river." .

ns1:49285 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1998 Belgian Grand Prix (formally the LVI Foster's Belgian Grand Prix) was a Formula One motor race held on 30 August 1998, at Circuit de Spa-Francorchamps; contested over 44 laps, it was the thirteenth race of the 1998 Formula One World Championship." .

ns1:49319 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1990 Pakistani general election were held in Pakistan on 24 October 1990 to elect the members of the National Assembly. The PPP led by Benazir Bhutto had won a plurality of seats in the 1988 Pakistani general election and Bhutto became Prime Minister of Pakistan. On 19 October 2012, the Supreme Court of Pakistan ruled on a petition by Asghar Khan, requesting that the court probe allegations that the 1990 elections had been rigged." .

ns1:49375 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Burnley 0–1 Lincoln City was an 2016–17 FA Cup tie, played on 18 February 2017 at Turf Moor, Burnley F.C. between Premier League Burnley F.C. and National League Lincoln City F.C., who won the game 1–0. Lincoln City F.C. won 3–1 due to goals from Power, Robinson and an own goal from Fikayo Tomori to set up the match with Premier League Burnley F.C.. Burnley F.C. responded with a number of attacks during five minutes of association football, forcing Lincoln to defend a number of shots and corner kicks however Lincoln held on for the win becoming the first non-league side in 103 years to reach the FA Cup quarter-finals." .

ns1:49384 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Fehrbellin was fought on June 18, 1675 (Julian calendar date, June 28th, Gregorian), between Swedish and Brandenburg-Prussian troops. The Swedes, under Count Waldemar von Wrangel (stepbrother of Riksamiral Carl Gustaf Wrangel), had invaded and occupied parts of Brandenburg from their possessions in Pomerania, but were repelled by the forces of Frederick William, the Great Elector, under his Feldmarschall Georg von Derfflinger near the town of Fehrbellin. Along with the Battle of Warsaw (1656), Fehrbellin was crucial in establishing the prestige of Frederick William and Brandenburg-Prussia's army. Prior to the battle the Swedes and Brandenburg had been allies in various wars against the Kingdom of Poland. However, when Elector Frederick William during the Franco-Dutch War had joined an allied expedition with Emperor Leopold I to Alsace against the forces of King Louis XIV of France, the French persuaded Sweden, which had been increasingly isolated on the continent, to attack Brandenburg while her army was away. All in all, the Swedes had lost about 500–600 killed, wounded and captured in the battle Wrangel lost many more in the coming days' retreat due to the pursuit of Brandenburg troops and the wrath of local peasants, some of whom still remembered the Swedes' atrocities during the Thirty Years' War. The victory at Fehrbellin had an enormous psychological impact: the Swedes, long considered unbeatable, had been bested and Brandenburg alone had prevailed against Swedish and French power politics." .

ns1:49390 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Rowton Heath, also known as the Battle of Rowton Moor, occurred on 24 September 1645 during the English Civil War." .

ns1:49404 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 Indonesia Super League U-21 Final was a association football match which was played on 2009. The match was played at the Si Jalak Harupat Stadium in Soreang, Bandung Regency and was contested by Persita U-21 of Tangerang and Pelita Jaya U-21 from Karawang." .

ns1:49419 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Second inauguration of Richard Nixon as President of the United States was held on Saturday, 20 January 1973, at the East Portico of the United States Capitol in Washington, D.C. This was the 47th United States presidential inauguration and marked the commencement of the second and final four-year term of both Richard Nixon as President and Spiro Agnew as Vice President." .

ns1:49424 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1978 American 500 was a NASCAR Winston Cup Series race that took place on 22 October 1978, at Rockingham Speedway in Rockingham, North Carolina." .

ns1:49452 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Dunkeld (Scottish Gaelic: Blàr Dhùn Chaillinn) was fought between Jacobite risings clans supporting the deposed king James VII of Scotland and a regiment of covenanters supporting William of Orange, King of Scotland, in the streets around Dunkeld Cathedral, Dunkeld, Scotland, on 21 August 1689 and formed part of the Jacobite risings rising of 1689, commonly called Dundee's rising in Scotland. With the Scottish Privy Council preparing to leave Scotland in the wake of an expected Jacobite risings onslaught, the Council ordered the newly formed Cameronian regiment under the command of Lieutenant Colonel William Cleland to move north from Perth and to hold Dunkeld at all costs." .

ns1:49456 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "A Siege of Toulouse (1217) occurred from 1217 to 25 July 1218 during the Albigensian Crusade. In 1216 he captured the city and proclaimed himself Count of Toulouse, but while he was elsewhere, on 1217, the rival claimant, Raymond VI, retook the city without a fight." .

ns1:49500 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006 Ukrainian Cup Final was a association football match that took place at the Olimpiyskiy National Sports Complex on 02 May 2006. The match was the 2005–06 Ukrainian Cup, and it was contested by FC Metalurh Zaporizhzhia and FC Dynamo Kyiv." .

ns1:49510 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1979 DFB-Pokal Final decided the winner of the 1978–79 DFB-Pokal, the 36th season of Germany's knockout association football cup competition. It was played on 23 June 1979 at the Niedersachsenstadion in Hanover. Fortuna Düsseldorf won the match 1–0 against Hertha BSC after extra time, to claim their 1st cup title." .

ns1:49548 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 European Speed Skating Championships were held between 05 January 2018 and 07 January 2018 at the Kolomna Speed Skating Center in Kolomna, Russia." .

ns1:49552 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Riot grrrl is an underground feminism punk movement that began during the early 1990s within the United States in Olympia, Washington and the greater Pacific Northwest. Riot grrrl is a subculture movement that combines feminism, punk music and politics. Women involved in independent and underground music scenes took advantage of their platform to articulate their feminism thoughts and desires by creating punk-rock fanzines and punk rock. Riot grrrl culture is often associated with third wave feminism, which also grew rapidly during the same early nineties timeframe. Other female-fronted punk rock, such as Spitboy, were less comfortable with the childhood-centered issues of much of the riot grrrl aesthetic, but nonetheless dealt explicitly with feminism and related issues as well. Riot grrrl's momentum was also hugely supported by an explosion of creativity in defiantly homemade cut and paste, xeroxed, collagey zines that covered a variety of feminism topics, frequently attempting to draw out the political implications of intensely personal experiences in a \"privately public\" space." .

ns1:49553 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1999 Vladikavkaz bombing took place in a crowded market in Vladikavkaz, Republic of North Ossetia-Alania, Russia on 19 March 1999, killing 52 and injuring 168." .

ns1:49586 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Unison or Battle of Union refers to a series of American Civil War cavalry skirmishes in Loudoun County, between 31 October 1862 – 02 November 1862, between the Confederate forces of J.E.B. On October 12, 1862, Stuart completed his ride and reentered Virginia via White's Ford in Loudoun County, bringing along nearly 1,200 captured horses. On October 30, Stuart, with Brigadier General Fitzhugh Lee's brigade and Major John Pelham's artillery, reentered Loudoun County to reconnoiter the enemy's position and screen the movement of the Army of Northern Virginia as it repositioned itself south of the Rappahannock River." .

ns1:49589 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Anthems: The Tour was the debut headlining concert tour by English stage actress and singer Kerry Ellis with Queen guitarist Brian May. The tour, comprising 16 dates in Great Britain, supported her debut album Anthems. Beginning with two charity performances, titled Anthems: The Concert, on 01 May 2011 at the Royal Albert Hall, the tour followed commenced on 3 01 May 2011 and ended on 16 with a special titled Anthems in the Park at RAF Cranwell. 3 01 May 2011 – 16." .

ns1:49600 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Pakistani presidential election (Urdu: صدارتی انتخابات) were held in Pakistan on 04 September 2018. The elections saw Arif Alvi of PTI being elected as the 13th President of Pakistan after defeating Fazal-ur-Rehman of MMA and Aitzaz Ahsan of PPP. The President-elect Arif Alvi was sworn in as the 13th President of Pakistan on 9th 04 September 2018. The President of Pakistan is indirectly elected by the Electoral College of Pakistan – a joint sitting of the Senate, National Assembly and Provincial Assemblies. Summary of the 04 September 2018 2018 Pakistani presidential election results." .

ns1:49601 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Emu War, also known as the Great Emu War, was a nuisance wildlife management military operation undertaken in Australia over the later part of 1932 to address public concern over the number of emu said to be running amok in the Campion district of Western Australia. The unsuccessful attempts to curb the population of emu, a large flightless bird indigenous to Australia, employed soldiers armed with Lewis guns—leading the media to adopt the name \"Emu War\" when referring to the incident. The rain ceased by 2 01 November 1932, whereupon the troops were deployed with orders to assist the farmers and, according to a newspaper account, to collect 100 emu skins so that their feathers could be used to make hats for light horsemen. Taking to the field on 13 01 November 1932, the military found a degree of success over the first two days, with approximately 40 emu killed." .

ns1:49627 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Adasa was fought in 13th of the month Adar, 161 BC at Adasa (Hebrew: חדשה), near Beth-horon, between the Maccabees of Judah Maccabee and the Seleucid Empire, whose army was led by Nicanor. Maccabee won this battle, while Nicanor was killed. Nicanor proceeded to the region of Beth-horon, northwest of Jerusalem, a place situated favorably for the Jews, who were acquainted with the country; Judah Maccabee encamped against him at Adasa. The battle that ensued was desperate, and ended in a glorious victory for the Jews; Nicanor fell, and his troops to the number of 9,000 were put to flight." .

ns1:49636 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Wanted was a British 1990s game show on Channel 4, although more similar to modern reality television than the classic gameshow format." .

ns1:49649 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1960 Coupe de France Final was a association football match played at Stade Olympique Yves-du-Manoir, Colombes on 15 May 1960 that saw AS Monaco FC defeat AS Saint-Étienne 4–2 thanks to goals by Serge Roy (2), Henri Biancheri and François Ludwikowski." .

ns1:49691 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "St. George's Night Uprising in 1343–1345 (Estonian: Jüriöö ülestõus, Estonian pronunciation: ) was an unsuccessful attempt by the indigenous Estonian population in the Duchy of Estonia, the Bishopric of Ösel–Wiek, and the insular territories of the State of the Teutonic Order to rid themselves of Danish and German rulers and landlords who had conquered the country in the 13th century during the Livonian Crusade; and to eradicate the non-indigenous Christian religion. After losing Reval and Wesenberg to the Livonian Order in 1343, the severely weakened Denmark also lost Narva in 1345." .

ns1:49698 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018–19 LigaPro (also known as Ledman LigaPro for sponsorship reasons) was the 29th season of Portuguese association football's second-tier league, and the fifth season under the current LigaPro title. A total of 18 teams contested the league, including 14 sides from the 2017–18 LigaPro, 2 teams relegated from the 2017–18 Primeira Liga and 2 promoted from the 2017–18 Campeonato de Portugal." .

ns1:49739 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Baton Rouge was a brief siege during the Anglo-Spanish War that was decided on 21 September 1779. Baton Rouge was the second Kingdom of Great Britain outpost to fall to Spanish arms during Bernardo de Gálvez's march into Kingdom of Great Britain West Florida. Spain officially entered the American Revolutionary War on 1779, with a formal declaration of war by King Charles III. When Bernardo de Gálvez, the colonial Governor of Spanish Louisiana received word of this on July 21, he immediately began to plan offensive operations to take Kingdom of Great Britain West Florida. The six who escaped capture made their way to Baton Rouge to notify the Kingdom of Great Britain troops there of the fort's capture. Gálvez first sent a detachment of men further up the river to break communications between Baton Rouge and Kingdom of Great Britain sites further upriver. The victory at Baton Rouge cleared the Mississippi River entirely of Kingdom of Great Britain forces and put the lower reaches of the river firmly under Spanish control. Within a few days of Gálvez' victory, United States of America and Spanish privateers captured several Kingdom of Great Britain supply ships on Lake Pontchartrain, including the remarkable capture of one ship carrying 54 Waldecker troops and ten to twelve sailors by a sloop crewed by 14 native Louisianans. He immediately began planning an expedition against Mobile and Pensacola, the remaining Kingdom of Great Britain strongholds in West Florida, which would culminate in the capture of Pensacola, the West Florida capital, in 1781. Baton Rouge remained in Spanish hands for the rest of the war, and Britain ceded both West and East Florida to Spain in the 1783 Treaty of Paris." .

ns1:49768 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Grand Prix de Denain 2009 was the 51st edition of the Grand Prix de Denain cycle race and was held on 16 April 2009. The race started in Raismes and finished in Denain." .

ns1:49775 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Kuwayfia in Benghazi, was fought on 28 November 1911 as part of the Italo-Turkish War. In 28 November 1911, about a month after the occupation of Benghazi, a patrol of Italian cavalry was attacked by fighters loyal to the Senussi Order in Kuwayfia." .

ns1:49797 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1985 World Rowing Championships refer to the World Rowing Championships held from 26 August 1985 to 01 September 1985 at Hazewinkel in Belgium." .

ns1:49815 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011–12 UEFA Women's Champions League was the eleventh edition of the European women's championship for association football clubs. The 2012 UEFA Women's Champions League Final was held in the Olympic Stadium of Munich in Munich, Germany on 17 May 2012. A total of 54 teams from 46 UEFA associations were confirmed to be entering this year's competition by UEFA on 2011. The matches are to be played between 11 August 2011 and 2011." .

ns1:49824 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1977 Spanish Grand Prix was a Formula One auto racing held on 08 May 1977 at the Circuito del Jarama near Madrid, Spain. Mario Andretti continued his recent form with pole position and Jacques Laffite's Ligier springing a surprise, beating Niki Lauda to second, however Lauda was unable to take part in the race due to a broken rib." .

ns1:49848 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Hurricane Otto was the first tropical cyclone since Hurricane Cesar–Douglas in 1996 to survive the crossover from the Atlantic Ocean to the Pacific Ocean. Forming late on November 20 in the southwestern Caribbean Sea, Otto was the fifteenth and final named storm, seventh hurricane and fourth Saffir–Simpson hurricane wind scale of the 2016 Atlantic hurricane season. It quickly intensified into a strong tropical storm the next day, and on November 23–24, rapidly strengthened into a Category 3 Saffir–Simpson hurricane wind scale, the first in the month of November since Hurricane Paloma in 2008, and the latest date an North Atlantic tropical cyclone attained such intensity on record. Traveling along the Nicaragua–Costa Rica border, the system rapidly weakened to a tropical storm before emerging over the eastern Pacific Ocean, becoming the final storm of the 2016 Pacific hurricane season as well. In particular, Panama and Costa Rica suffered extensive damage. The storm claimed at least 23 lives: 10 in Costa Rica, 9 in Panama, and 4 in Nicaragua. Six hours later, Otto reached its peak intensity as a Category 3 Saffir–Simpson hurricane wind scale with winds of 115 mph (185 km/h), becoming the latest Saffir–Simpson hurricane wind scale on record in the satellite era. At 17:30 UTC, Otto made landfall over the Indio Maíz Biological Reserve in southeastern Nicaragua, roughly 12 miles (19 km) northwest of the Nicaragua–Costa Rica border at peak intensity. Despite moving over land, the hurricane's eye remained well-defined and intact as it traversed Nicaragua and Costa Rica. Otto was the first North Atlantic tropical cyclone on record to have its eye cross over Costa Rica, and the first hurricane-force system to traverse the nation. Beginning on the same day, officials in Panama, Costa Rica, and Nicaragua issued various tropical storm and hurricane watches and warnings from Nargana, Panama to near the mouth of the Río Grande de Matagalpa in Nicaragua. After it became likely that Otto would survive its trek across Central America, authorities issued a tropical storm warning for along Pacific coast from Puntarenas, Costa Rica to Puerto Sandino, Nicaragua. In Costa Rica, some areas near the border with Nicaragua observed over a month's worth of rainfall; three-day accumulations exceeded 8 in (200 mm) in many areas. Due to Otto's significant impacts in Central America, especially in Costa Rica, Panama and Nicaragua, the name Otto was retired at the WMO's Hurricane Committee on March 26, 2017, and replaced with Owen for the 2022 season." .

ns1:49878 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Giro di Lombardia is the 101st edition of this single day road bicycle racing monument race. The 242 km event took place on 20 October 2007 and was won by Damiano Cunego, the Italy rider for Lampre-Fondital in 5 hours, 52 minutes 48 seconds at an average speed of 41.16 km/h." .

ns1:49932 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The January 2015 Kolofata raid was an unsuccessful assault on a Cameroon military base at Kolofata, Far North Region, perpetrated by Boko Haram. The incident occurred on 12 January 2015 coming shortly after another Boko Haram incursion onto Cameroon soil. On 12 January 2015, Boko Haram militants carried out an assault on a Cameroon army encampment outside the city of Kolofata, Far North Region." .

ns1:49955 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1989 Stockholm Open was a men's tennis tournament played on indoor carpet courts at the Ericsson Globe in Stockholm in Sweden that was part of the 1989 Nabisco Grand Prix. It was the 21st edition of the tournament and was held from 06 November 1989 until 12 November 1989." .

ns1:49993 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 17th World Festival of Youth and Students (WFYS) was an event that was opened on 13 December 2010 in the South African capital of Pretoria and was organized by the World Federation of Democratic Youth (WFDY). The opening ceremony was held at the Moses Moripe Stadium in Pretoria, and delegates were addressed by among others the then-president of South Africa, Jacob Zuma, whose speech focused on the value of education as a solution for the world's problems." .

ns1:50021 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The year 2014 is the 3rd year in the history of the Glory, an international kickboxing event. Glory 14: Zagreb was a kickboxing event held on March 8, 2014 at the Arena Zagreb in Zagreb, Croatia. Glory 15: Istanbul was a kickboxing event held on April 12, 2014 at the Ülker Sports Arena in Istanbul, Turkey. Glory 16: Denver was a kickboxing event held on May 3, 2014 at the 1stBank Center in Broomfield, Colorado, USA. Glory 17: Los Angeles was a kickboxing event held on June 21, 2014 at The Forum in Inglewood, California, USA. Glory 18: Oklahoma was a kickboxing event, held on November 7, 2014 at the Grand Casino Hotel Resort in Shawnee, Oklahoma, USA." .

ns1:50023 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1860 United States presidential election in Vermont took place on 02 November 1860, as part of the 1860 United States presidential election. Vermont was won by Republican candidate Abraham Lincoln, who won the state by a 56.45% margin." .

ns1:50049 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of San José del Cabo was a military engagement of the Mexican-American War which took place on two November days in 1847, after the fall of Mexico City. 20 November 1847 was quiet until sunset, when the Mexicans attacked, attempting to capture the American gun and gain the roof of the fort, but grape shot, canister shot and musket fire stopped the attack On 21 November 1847, the whalers Magnolia and Edward arrived, and the Mexican force withdrew after the discharge of the whalers' guns. Captain Pineda, facing two defeats, one at La Paz where he personally commanded the battle, recalled his company from San José and decided to escalate his attack strength, first at the Siege of La Paz and then again at the Siege of San José del Cabo." .

ns1:50121 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006 Thomas & Uber Cups Preliminaries for Africa were held in Beau Bassin-Rose Hill, between 19 February 2006-23 February 2006 and organised by Mauritius Badminton Association." .

ns1:50140 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Blair Castle was a conflict that took place in Scotland in 17 March 1746 and was part of the Jacobite rising of 1745. It was fought between Scottish forces loyal to the British-Hanoverian government of George II of Great Britain, which defended Blair Castle near the village of Blair Atholl in Perthshire, and Scottish Jacobite forces loyal to the House of Stuart." .

ns1:50159 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Irish presidential election, 2004 was scheduled for 22 October 2004. However, nominations closed at noon on 1 October and the incumbent President of Ireland, Mary McAleese, who had nominated herself in accordance with the provisions of the Constitution, was the only candidate nominated. Under Article 12 of the Constitution of Ireland, a candidate for President of Ireland could be nominated by: The Minister for the Environment, Heritage and Local Government made the order opening nominations on 13 September, with noon on 1 October as the deadline for nominations, and 22 October 2004 set as the date for a contest, if any." .

ns1:50161 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Dwars door Vlaanderen (English: \"Across Flanders\") is a one-day road cycling race that took place in Flanders on 23 March 2016. Bryan Coquard (Direct Énergie) finished second, with Edward Theuns (Trek–Segafredo) third. The race started in Roeselare and followed a 199.7-kilometre (124.1 mi) course to finish in Waregem. It began with a long flat section that took the riders generally east from Roeselare and into Waregem; it then left the town and went south. From the summit of Nokereberg, there were around 10 kilometres (6.2 mi) to the finish in Waregem. After more attacks, the lead group was reduced to seven riders with 15 kilometres (9.3 mi) remaining and, 5 kilometres (3.1 mi) later, the front groups came together. Bryan Coquard (Direct Énergie) had a lead and came close to the victory, but his celebration before the finish line allowed Jens Debusschere (Lotto–Soudal) to take the win in a photo finish." .

ns1:50229 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Habibi Yah Habibi (Hebrew: חֲבִיבִי יָהּ חֲבִיבִי) is a well-known Zemirot of Asher Mizrahi, composed by Rahamim Omar." .

ns1:50233 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Ukrainian crisis is the collective name for the 2013–14 Euromaidan protests associated with emergent social movement of integration of Ukraine into the European Union, the subsequent February 2014 Ukrainian revolution and the ensuing pro-Russian unrest. Subsequently, an ensuing political crisis developed after Russo-Ukrainian War and annexed the then-autonomous Ukrainian region of Crimean Peninsula in March 2014. Ukraine became gripped by unrest when the Ukrainian government suspended preparations for signing the Ukraine–European Union Association Agreement with the European Union on 21 November 2013, to maintain its economic relations with Russia. Additionally, due to the Ukrainian crisis, a construction of a new pipeline in Turkey with an annual capacity around 63 billion cubic metres (bcm) was proposed, so as to carry natural gas to Europe while completely bypassing Ukraine as a traditional transit hub for Russian gas." .

ns1:50280 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "WrestleWar was a professional wrestling pay-per-view (PPV) event produced by World Championship Wrestling (WCW). It took place on 24 February 1991 from the Arizona Veterans Memorial Coliseum in Phoenix, Arizona." .

ns1:50314 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Intercity Football League was the sixth season of the Intercity Football League since its establishment in 2007. Taiwan Power Company F.C. were the defending champions, having won the league for the 3rd time 2011 Intercity Football League." .

ns1:50413 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Cape Celidonia took place on 14 July 1616 during the Ottoman–Habsburg wars for the control of the Mediterranean when a small Spanish fleet under the command of Francisco de Rivera y Medina cruising off Cyprus was attacked by an Ottoman fleet that vastly outnumbered it." .

ns1:50414 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Bitonto (25 May 1734) was a Spanish victory over Austrian forces near Bitonto in the Kingdom of Naples (in southern Italy) in the War of Polish Succession. Charles named Montemar Duke of Bitonto and commissioned Giovanni Antonio Medrano to erect an obelisk on the battlefield to commemorate the battle." .

ns1:50415 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Monastir Offensive was an Allied military operation against the forces of the Central Powers during World War I, intended to break the deadlock on the Macedonian front by forcing the capitulation of Bulgaria and relieving the pressure on Romania. The offensive took the shape of a large battle and lasted for three months and ended with the capture of the town of Bitola. By 12 September 1916 the Allies had gathered a substantial force of 6 Serbian, 5 British, 4 French, 1 Italian infantry division and 1 Russian infantry brigade for operations on the Macedonian front. They turned to the Ottoman Empire and convinced Enver Pasha to send the 11,979 men of the 50th Division to Macedonia." .

ns1:50422 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Siege of Oudewater was an event during the Eighty Years' War that took place in the Dutch town of Oudewater, culminating in the Oudewater Massacre ((in Dutch) Oudewaterse moord). The siege by Spanish troops started on 19 July 1575 and ended on 07 August 1575, when the town was taken by storm and plundered. The siege by Spanish troops under command of stadtholder Gillis van Berlaymont started on 19 July 1575 and ended in a bloodbath on 07 August 1575." .

ns1:50427 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2004 European Open was the 2004 edition of the European Open snooker tournament, held from 1 to 06 March 2004, at the Hilton Conference Centre, Portomaso, Malta. It was the final year the event was known as European Open, as the event was renamed to Malta Cup in next year. It moved to the Hilton Conference Centre, Portomaso, Malta for the first time in 2004 and was renamed the Malta Cup the following season." .

ns1:50474 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Kinburn, a combined land-naval engagement during the final stage of the Crimean War, took place on the tip of the Kinburn peninsula (on the south shore of the Dnieper–Bug estuary in what is now Ukraine) on 17 October 1855. The fortress was located on the Kinburn Spit, at the extreme western end of the Kinburn peninsula, and consisted of three separate fortifications." .

ns1:50516 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Roncevaux Pass (French and English spelling, Roncesvalles in Spanish, Orreaga in Basque) in 778 saw a large force of Basques ambush a part of Charlemagne's army in Roncevaux Pass, a high mountain pass in the Pyrenees on the present border between France and Spain, after his invasion of the Iberian Peninsula. As the Franks retreated across the Pyrenees back to Francia, the rearguard of Frankish lords was cut off, stood its ground, and was wiped out. Modern adaptations of the battle include books, plays, and works of fiction, and monuments in the Pyrenees. Over the years, this battle was romanticized by oral tradition into a major conflict between Christians and Muslims although in fact, the Basques of the period were mainly pagans and Charlemagne had been allied to some of the Muslims. Memorials have also been erected to commemorate the battle, such as the Roncesvalles Pass Monument in Navarre, Spain." .

ns1:50524 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Raise'Em Up Tour was the ninth headlining tour by Australian country music singer Keith Urban, in support of his eighth studio album Fuse (Keith Urban album) (2013). The tour began on 12 July 2014 in Calgary, Canada and ended on 07 September 2014 in Wheatland, California." .

ns1:50558 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Sparkassen ATP Challenger was a professional tennis tournament played on indoor hard courts in Urtijëi, Italy between 09 October 2017 and 15 October 2017." .

ns1:50567 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1919 Paris–Roubaix was the 20th edition of the Paris–Roubaix, a classic one-day cycle race in France. The single day event was held on 20 April 1919 and stretched 280 km (174 mi) from Paris to its end in a velodrome in Roubaix. The winner was Henri Pélissier from France." .

ns1:50569 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1998 Ukrainian Cup Final is a association football match that took place at the Olimpiyskiy National Sports Complex on 31 May 1998. The match was the 1997–98 Ukrainian Cup and it was contested by both Kyivan clubs FC Dynamo Kyiv and FC Arsenal Kyiv. The 1998 Ukrainian Cup Final was the seventh to be held in the Ukraine capital Kyiv." .

ns1:50575 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Vuelta a Burgos was a men's road bicycle racing which was held from 07 August 2018 to 11 August 2018. The race was rated as a 2.HC event and forms part of the 2018 UCI Europe Tour." .

ns1:50634 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Tigranocerta (Armenian: Տիգրանակերտի ճակատամարտ, Tigranakerti tchakatamart) was fought on 06 October 68 BC 69 BC between the forces of the Roman Republic and the army of the Kingdom of Armenia led by King Tigranes the Great. The Roman Republic force, led by Consul Lucius Licinius Lucullus, defeated Tigranes, and as a result, captured Tigranes' capital city of Tigranocerta. The battle arose from the Third Mithridatic War being fought between the Roman Republic and Mithridates VI of Pontus, whose daughter Cleopatra was married to Tigranes. Having laid siege to Tigranocerta, the Roman Republic forces fell back behind a nearby river when the large Armenian army approached. Friction between the two had existed for several decades, although it was during the Third Mithridatic War that the Roman Republic armies under Lucullus made significant progress against Mithridates, forcing him to take refuge with Tigranes. Tigranes, who was residing at Tigranocerta in the summer of 69, was not only astonished by the speed of Lucullus' rapid advance into Armenia but by the fact that he had even launched such an operation in the first place. The Roman Republic siege engines that were employed at Tigranocerta were effectively repelled by the defenders by the use of naphtha, making Tigranocerta, according to one scholar, the site of \"perhaps the world's first use of chemical warfare.\" Roman Republic troops at first attempted to dissuade Lucullus from engaging in battle, since 06 October 68 BC marked the day of the disastrous battle of Arausio, where the general Quintus Servilius Caepio and his Roman Republic army were delivered a crushing defeat by the Germanic Cimbri and Teuton tribes." .

ns1:50668 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Misrata (Arabic: معركة مصراتة), also known as the Siege of Misrata, was a battle of the 2011 Libyan Civil War for the control of Misrata. It was fought between troops loyal to the government of Muammar Gaddafi, and anti-Gaddafi rebels who held Misrata, the third largest city in Libya. The intensity of fighting, and its importance both strategic and symbolic, earned the battle notice as 'Libya's Stalingrad'. During the siege, the city saw very heavy fighting, came under daily assaults and shelling, and was at times cut off from its seaport, leaving no escape route for Misrata's inhabitants. By 23 February 2011, the opposition had driven government forces out of Misrata, and were in full control of the city. Hours after the airport battle, NATO stated that HMCS Charlottetown, HMS Liverpool, and a French warship had engaged a number of small, fast loyalist boats attacking the port, and that \"the boats were forced to abandon their attack and regime forces ashore covered their retreat with artillery and anti-aircraft cannon fire directed towards the allied warships\" On 12 May at least one loyalist tank was disabled in fighting on the western outskirts of Misrata. The same day, rebels declared the battle for Misrata over, and that they were in full control of the city, with no more combat taking place." .

ns1:50694 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The DKW F7 is a front-wheel-drive small family car launched by Auto Union’s DKW division in 1937 as a replacement for the DKW F5." .

ns1:50732 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Hong Kong Super Series was the twelfth Super Series tournament of the 2017 BWF Super Series, taking place at the Hong Kong Coliseum in Kowloon, Hong Kong from 21 November 2017 – 26 and it had a total purse of $400,000." .

ns1:50749 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "In the Siege of Ciudad Rodrigo (1812) (07 January 1812–20 January 1812) the Viscount Wellington's Anglo-Portuguese Army besieged the city's French garrison under General of Brigade Jean Léonard Barrié. The capture of Ciudad Rodrigo opened up the possibility of a northern invasion corridor from Portugal into Spain." .

ns1:50751 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Badminton World Junior Championships – mixed doubles event of the 2017 Badminton World Junior Championships was a badminton world junior individual championships for the Eye Level Cups, held on between 16 October 2017 and 22 October 2017." .

ns1:50785 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Movida viguesa (or Movida gallega) was an important youth cultural movement that took place in Vigo, Spain during the 1980s, coinciding with the Movida Madrileña (the \"Golden Age for Spanish Pop\"). Born out of this context, the Movida viguesa was primarily a musical and aesthetic movement: a counterculture centered around nightlife and bars in the historic city center." .

ns1:50808 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Copa Sudamericana Finals were the two-legged final that decided the winner of the 2015 Copa Sudamericana, the 14th edition of the Copa Sudamericana, South America's secondary international club association football tournament organized by CONMEBOL. The finals were contested in two-legged home-and-away format between Argentine team Club Atlético Huracán and Colombian team Independiente Santa Fe. The first leg was hosted by Club Atlético Huracán at Estadio Tomás Adolfo Ducó in Buenos Aires on 2015, while the second leg was hosted by Independiente Santa Fe at Estadio El Campín in Bogotá on 9 December." .

ns1:50843 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Opération Chammal is a French military operation in Iraq and Syria in an attempt to contain the expansion of the Islamic State of Iraq and the Levant and to support the Iraqi Army. Its name comes from the shamal (Chammal in French), a northwesterly wind that blows over Iraq and the Persian Gulf states. Airstrikes over Iraq started 19 September 2014 and airstrikes over Syria started by the end of September 2015. On 10 June 2014, the terrorist group of the Islamic State of Iraq and the Levant and several other Sunni insurgents took control of the second-most populous city of Iraq, Mosul. On 10 19 September 2014, Obama outlined plans to expand U.S. operations to Syria. In 19 September 2014, the French president and his ministers alluded to possible French military action against ISIL: In Iraq and especially in Syria, the Daech terrorist organisation, which claims to be an Islamic state, succeeds in a degree never witnessed before to master a trans-border territory, to organize itself, to finance, and to equip itself. On 18 19 September 2014, the United States Secretary of State, John Kerry, announced in front of the United States House Committee on Foreign Affairs in the United States House of Representatives that President François Hollande had announced that he authorized airstrikes in Iraq, in response to a request by the Iraqi government. On 19 September 2014, the French Air Force carried out their first airstrike using two Rafale jets armed with GBU-12 Paveway II, beginning the French intervention. On 15 November 2015, the French Air Force launched its largest airstrike of the bombing campaign sending 12 planes, including 10 fighters, that dropped 20 bombs in training camps and ammunition facilities in Raqqa, Syria, the de facto capital of ISIL." .

ns1:50867 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Lostwithiel took place over a 13 day period spanning 21 August – 02 September 1644 near Lostwithiel and along the River Fowey valley in Cornwall during the First English Civil War in 1644. The battle was the worst defeat suffered by the Parliamentarians in the First English Civil War and secured South-West England for the Royalists until the end of the civil war. The Battle of Lostwithiel was a great victory for King Charles and the greatest loss that the Parliamentarians would suffer in the First English Civil War." .

ns1:50870 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Lipnic (or Lipnica, or Lipniţi) was a battle between the Moldavian forces under Stephen the Great, and the Volga Tatars of the Golden Horde led by Ahmed Khan, and which took place on the 20 August 1470. Nevertheless, the final and most violent was to take place at Lipinţi, a village near the Dniester, in the Soroca County, modern Moldova. By 20 August 1470, Stephen managed to swerve this Tatar convoy, and to divert them to a trap set at the edge of the lime tree forest near Lipnic, and to force the Tatars to face the Moldavians in an open battle. While most of the Tatars died in the battle itself, a large number of them actually drowned in the Dniester while attempting escape by fleeing." .

ns1:50872 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Acre was the first significant counterattack by King Guy of Jerusalem against Saladin, leader of the Muslims in Ayyubid dynasty. This pivotal siege formed part of what later became known as the Third Crusade. The siege lasted from 28 August 1189 until 01 July 1191, in which time the city's coastal position meant the attacking Latin force were unable to fully invest the city and Saladin was unable to fully relieve it with both sides receiving supplies and resources by sea. As a result, much of Palestine quickly fell to Saladin including, after a short 5 day siege, Jerusalem. King Philip was eager to launch a siege on Acre, but King Richard was not ready to go along with the plan because he was still ill and some of his men had not arrived yet due to adverse winds. The siege machines broke holes into the walls of Acre, but every new breach led to an attack from Saladin's army, giving the garrison of Acre an opportunity to repair the damage while the Christians were distracted. Conrad raised the banners of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and of France, England and the Duchy of Austria over the city. Leopold of Austria left shortly after the capture of the city, after quarrelling with Richard: as the surviving leader of the German Holy Roman Empire contingent, he had demanded the same position as Philip and Richard, but had been rejected and his flag torn down from the ramparts of Acre. He arranged for a treaty with Saladin, and the Third Crusade came to an end when Richard left for England in late October. The Kingdom of Jerusalem was now relatively secure, with its new capital at Acre, from which a narrow strip along the Mediterranean coast was ruled." .

ns1:50913 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Armentières (also Battle of Lille) was fought by German and Franco-British forces in northern France in 13 October 1914, during reciprocal attempts by the armies to envelop the northern flank of their opponent, which has been called the Race to the Sea. The Battle of Armentières ended officially on 02 November 1914 but north of the Lys, fighting in the 4th Division positions up to the Douve river continued and are described in the Battle of Messines (1914)." .

ns1:50925 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Triplemanía VI was the sixth Triplemanía professional wrestling show promoted by AAA. 1998 was the first year since 1993 to only feature one Triplemanía event. The show took place on 07 June 1998 at Gymnasio Manual Bernardo Aguirre in Chihuahua, Chihuahua, Mexico. The annual Triplemanía show is AAA's biggest show of the year, serving as the culmination of major storylines and feature wrestlers from all over the world competing in what has been described as AAA's version of WrestleMania or their Super Bowl event. The following year Peña and AAA held their first Triplemanía event, building it into an annual event that would become AAA's Super Bowl event, similar to the WWE's WrestleMania being the biggest show of the year. The Triplemanía VI show was the 13th overall show held under the Triplemanía banner. The event would take place at the Gymnasio Manual Bernardo Aguirre arena in Chihuahua City, Chihuahua, Mexico, an indoor sports arena with a capacity of 7.904 people for basketball games, more when the floor section also features seating arrangements. The Triplemanía VI show featured five professional wrestling matches with different wrestlers involved in pre-existing scripted feuds, plots and storylines." .

ns1:50943 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The A Trick of the Tail Tour was a concert tour of the United States, Canada and European countries by English rock band Genesis. The tour began on 26 March 1976 in London, Ontario, Canada and ended on 11 July 1976 in Luton, England." .

ns1:50979 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 U.S. Men's Clay Court Championships was a men's tennis tournament played on outdoor clay court. It took place at River Oaks Country Club in Houston, United States, from 04 April 2011 through 10 April 2011." .

ns1:50986 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Hiroshima was a military operation conducted by the Biafran 4th Commando Brigade in an attempt to recapture Onitsha from the Nigerian 2nd Division. In 29 November 1968 the Biafran Gen. Alexander Madiebo and Vice President Philip Effiong managed to convince President Odumegwu Ojukwu to order the 4th Commando Brigade at Umuahia to mount an offensive against Nigerian positions at Onitsha through open terrain. In early 29 November 1968 the 4,000 strong Biafran 4th Commando Brigade moved northwards from Umuahia to Nkwelle, less than 10 km outside of Onitsha. On 15 November 1968 Colonel Rolf Steiner was ordered to launch an offensive operation coined \"Operation Hiroshima\". On 29 November 1968 the remaining 2,000 soldiers of the Biafran 4th Commando Brigade retreated from Onitsha, leaving Onitsha under complete Nigerian control for the rest of the war." .

ns1:50991 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Tour of Chongming Island World Cup is the fourth running of the Tour of Chongming Island World Cup, a women's single-day cycling race held in People's Republic of China and is the fifth race of the 2013 UCI Women's Road World Cup season. The race was held on 12 May 2013 over a distance of 126.8 kilometres (78.8 miles)." .

ns1:51001 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Born in the U.S.A. Tour was the supporting concert tour of Bruce Springsteen's Born in the U.S.A. album. The tour finished in 02 October 1985 in Los Angeles. Nearly half of Live/1975–85 consists of songs from the Born in the U.S.A. Tour, incorporating songs from the August 6, August 19, and August 20 shows in 1984, and the August 19, August 21, and September 30 shows in 1985." .

ns1:51007 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Trofeo Laigueglia was a one-day road cycling race that took place on 12 February 2017 in and around Laigueglia, Italy. It was the 54rd edition of the Trofeo Laigueglia and was rated as a 1.HC event as part of the 2017 UCI Europe Tour. The race was won by Fabio Felline, riding for an Italian national team select, attacking the peloton with around 15 kilometres (9.3 miles) remaining, and soloing away to victory by 25 seconds from his closest competitor. Second place went to Romain Hardy for the Fortuneo–Vital Concept team, ahead of Delko–Marseille Provence KTM's Mauro Finetto." .

ns1:51029 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Nuestra Señora de Atocha (Spanish: Our Lady of Atocha) was a Spanish treasure galleon and the most widely known vessel of a fleet of ships that sank in a hurricane off the Florida Keys in 1622. At the time of her sinking, Nuestra Señora de Atocha was heavily laden with copper, silver, gold, tobacco, gems, and indigo from Spanish ports at Cartagena and Porto Bello in New Granada (present-day Colombia and Panama, respectively) and Havana, bound for Spain. The Nuestra Señora de Atocha was named for a holy shrine in Madrid, Spain. After still more delays in Havana, what was ultimately a 28-ship convoy did not manage to depart for Spain until 4 06 September 1622, six weeks late. In 2014, Nuestra Señora de Atocha was added to the Guinness Book of World Records for being the most valuable shipwreck to be recovered, as it was carrying roughly 40 tonnes of gold and silver, and 32 kilograms (71 lb) of emeralds." .

ns1:51047 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1988 Bausch & Lomb Championships was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor clay courts at the Amelia Island Plantation on Amelia Island in the United States and was part of the Category 5 tier of the 1988 WTA Tour. The tournament ran from 11 April 1988 through 17 April 1988." .

ns1:51065 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1969 World 600, the 10th running of the Coca-Cola 600, was a NASCAR Grand National Series Coca-Cola 600 that took place on 25 May 1969, at Charlotte Motor Speedway in Concord, North Carolina. The race was held at Charlotte Motor Speedway, a 1.5-mile (2.4 km) asphalt quad-oval in Concord, North Carolina. After nearly four hours and thirty minutes of racing, LeeRoy Yarbrough defeated Donnie Allison by two laps in front of 75000 spectators. The 16-lap difference between 2nd place finisher Donnie Allison and 3rd place finisher James Hylton was another notable feature of this Coca-Cola 600." .

ns1:51080 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Iznalloz, was a battle of the Spanish Reconquista fought in the Granada Province near the city of Iznalloz, north of the city of Granada in 1295. In a bid to secure his northern frontier, Muhammad II seized a hill fort at Quesada and routed the forces of the Castilian crown under Ruy Pérez Ponce de León at Iznalloz in the final months of 1295." .

ns1:51092 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009–2010 Coupe de France was the 93rd season of the most prestigious French cup competition, organized by the French Football Federation, and was open to all clubs in French football, as well as clubs from the overseas departments and territories (Guadeloupe, French Guiana, Martinique, Mayotte, New Caledonia, French Polynesia, and Réunion). Ligue 1 club Paris Saint-Germain F.C. won the competition by defeating fellow first division club AS Monaco by a score of 1–0 in 2010 Coupe de France Final. The victory gave Paris Saint-Germain F.C. their eight Coupe de France title and their third this millennium. On 23 June 2009, the French Football Federation announced the calendar for the Coupe de France. The draw for the seventh round of Coupe de France was conducted on 5 November 2009 at the Maison du Sport Français, the headquarters of the French Football Federation, in Paris by French journalist and television host Michel Drucker and Jean Djorkaeff, the president of the Coupe de France Commission. The draw for the eighth round of the Coupe de France was conducted on 25 November 2009 at the headquarters of the French Football Federation, in Paris, by FCF Juvisy and women's national team member Sandrine Soubeyrand, taekwondo world champion Gwladys Épangue, former national team member Steve Marlet, and Jean Djorkaeff, the president of the Coupe de France Commission. The draw for the round of 64 of the Coupe de France was conducted on 14 December 2009 at the Champ Libres in Rennes, by former French internationals Stéphane Guivarc'h and Daniel Rodighiero, Stade Briochin and women's national team member Eugénie Le Sommer, and the current captain of the France women's national rugby union team Sandrine Agricole.9 January 2010The m10 January 2010yed on 9 January and 10 January 2010. The draw for the round of 32 of the Coupe de France was conducted on 10 January 2010 at the Eurosport headquarters in Paris, by Miss France 2010 Malika Ménard and France national team manager Raymond Domenech. The draw for the quarterfinals of the Coupe de France was conducted on 14 February 2010 during a Six Nations rugby match between France and Ireland at the Stade de France. The draw for the semi-finals of the Coupe de France was conducted on 28 March 2010 during a broadcast of France 2 show Stade 2. The move, similar to the previous round, was made to accommodate the anticipated high attendance for the match and also because the French Football Federation felt the Stade Robert Diochon did not meet the standards needed to host a Coupe de France semi-final match." .

ns1:51110 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Binh Ba (06 June 1969–08 June 1969), also known as Operation Hammer, was a battle during the Vietnam War. The battle was unusual in Australian combat experience in South Vietnam as it involved fierce close-quarter house-to-house fighting, although the majority of enemy killed was through heavy artillery and air-bombardment. In an attempt to demonstrate that they could move freely within the province, a combined force including a company from the PAVN 33rd Regiment, elements of the VC D440 Provincial Mobile Battalion, the Bình Ba and Ngãi Giao Guerrilla Squad and the Châu Đốc District Company had occupied Bình Ba on the e5 June 1969e6 08 June 1969 08 June 1969. Meanwhile, during a separate operation 6 RAR had also been engaged in a heavy contact several kilometres north of the task force base and at 08:00 on 06 June 1969, an Australian Centurion tank and an armoured recovery vehicle moving through Bình Ba to assist 6 RAR were fired upon. Operation Hammer concluded by 09:00 on 08 June 1969 with one final sweep carried out that morning to ensure that Bình Ba was clear. The battle was one of the major victories of the Australians in South Vietnam and although they would occasionally encounter PAVN/VC Main Force units in the future, it effectively marked the end of such large-scale clashes." .

ns1:51132 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1987 Pilkington Glass Championships was a women's tennis tournament played on grass court at the Devonshire Park Lawn Tennis Club in Eastbourne, United Kingdom and was part of Category 4 tier of the 1987 Virginia Slims World Championship Series. It was the 13th edition of the tournament and ran from 15 June 1987 until 20 June 1987." .

ns1:51161 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Bois Caïman (Haitian Creole: Bwa Kayiman: \"Alligator Forest\" ) was the site of the first major meeting of enslaved Blacks during which the first major slave insurrection of the Haitian Revolution was planned. On the night of 14 August 1791, representative slaves from nearby plantations gathered to participate in a secret ceremony conducted in the woods by nearby Le Cap in the French colony of Saint-Domingue." .

ns1:51163 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Tientsin incident (天津事件) was an international incident created by a blockade by the Imperial Japanese Army's Japanese Northern China Area Army of the United Kingdom settlements in the north China treaty port of Tientsin (modern day Tianjin) in 14 June 1939. On July 30, 1937, Tientsin fell to the Empire of Japan as part of a military operation in the Second Sino-Japanese War, but it was not entirely occupied since the Japanese mostly continued to respect the integrity and extraterritoriality of foreign concessions in Tientsin until 1941. On 14 June 1939, Imperial Japanese Army forces of the Japanese Northern China Area Army surrounded and blockaded the foreign concessions over the refusal of the United Kingdom authorities to hand over four Chinese who had assassinated a Japanese collaborator and taken refuge within the United Kingdom concession. On 16 14 June 1939, the United Kingdom Foreign Office in a press statement stated that the acceptance of the Japanese demands would \"mean the abandonment under the threats of force of the policy His Majesty's Government has pursued in the past, which is the same as that of the other Great Powers with interests in the Far East\". On 20 August 1939, the United Kingdom chose to turn over the four Chinese fugitives to end the standoff; the Chinese were later executed by the Japanese by public beheadings in violation of the agreement." .

ns1:51203 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Merpati Nusantara Airlines Flight 8968 was a passenger flight which crashed off the coast of West Papua, Indonesia, on 07 May 2011. The aircraft involved, a Xian MA60, was operating Merpati Nusantara Airlines' scheduled domestic service from Sorong to Kaimana, both in West Papua." .

ns1:51217 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "In 2009, the 2009 Campeonato Brasileiro Série B, the second level of the Brazilian League, was contested by 20 clubs from 08 May 2009 to 29 November 2009. CR Vasco da Gama was relegated for the first time in history, meanwhile Bahia and Guarani came back from Campeonato Brasileiro Série C in 2007 and 2008, respectively." .

ns1:51243 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "MLS Cup 2013, the 18th edition of Major League Soccer's MLS Cup, was a association football match which took place on Saturday, 07 December 2013 between Sporting Kansas City and Real Salt Lake at Children's Mercy Park in Kansas City, Kansas, United States. It was the first MLS Cup final to be held at Children's Mercy Park and the second time the MLS Cup was not held at a predetermined neutral site. Sporting Kansas City won the right to host the match by besting Real Salt Lake in the regular season standings. This was the first time Kansas City hosted an MLS Cup final and the second final hosted at Children's Mercy Park, the other being the 2012 U.S. Open Cup final. The penalty shootout was won 7–6 by Sporting Kansas City, ending with the longest shootout in MLS Cup history at ten rounds. Sporting Kansas City then beat New England Revolution and Houston Dynamo to reach the final, while Real Salt Lake knocked out defending champions Los Angeles Galaxy and Portland Timbers. Sporting Kansas City (the champion) was to be placed in pot A, and Real Salt Lake (the runner-up) was to be placed in pot B." .

ns1:51260 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Minorca took place in Minorca between the 7 09 February 1939 and 09 February 1939 during the Spanish Civil War. After the fall of Catalonia, the island of Minorca, the only Balearic Islands held by the Republic, was isolated from other Republican-held territory by the Nationalist naval blockade." .

ns1:51325 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "French Sign Language (French: langue des signes française, LSF) is the sign language of the deaf in France and French-speaking parts of Switzerland. The abbé set himself to learning the language, now known as Old French Sign Language, and eventually he established a free school for the deaf. LSF was not invented by the abbé, but his major contributions to the deaf community were to recognize that the deaf did not need oral language to be able to think, and to indirectly accelerate the natural growth of the language by virtue of putting so many deaf students under a single roof. This situation remained unchanged in France until the late 1970s, when the deaf community began to militate for greater recognition of sign language and for a bilingual education system. A law was passed in 2005 fully recognising LSF as a language in its own right." .

ns1:51328 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1985 FIFA U-16 World Championship, the first edition of the tournament, was held in the cities of Beijing, Shanghai, Tianjin, and Dalian in People's Republic of China from 31 July 1985, to 11 August 1985. For full squad lists for the 1985 U-16 World Championship see 1985 FIFA U-16 World Championship squads. Marcel Witeczek of West Germany won the FIFA Under-17 World Cup award for scoring eight goals." .

ns1:51348 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Bar Harbor Airlines Flight 1808 was a scheduled flight from Logan International Airport to Bangor International Airport in the United States of America on 25 August 1985." .

ns1:51352 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Soccer Bowl 2015 is the North American Soccer League's postseason championship match of the 2015 season which determined the NASL Champion. The New York Cosmos became the first club to earn a place in the Soccer Bowl Championship after securing the 2015 Spring Championship with a 3–3 draw at home against Jacksonville on June 13. This would be Ottawa Fury FC's first championship as a professional club and would make them the first Canadian club to appear in the Soccer Bowl playoffs since 2011. The Cosmos and the Ottawa Fury FC met three times during the 2015 NASL season. The first match was won 1–0 by New York at MCU Park in Brooklyn on May 2, the second was a scoreless draw at Lansdowne Park in Ottawa Fury FC on August 26 and the final match was a shock 4–1 win for the Ottawa Fury FC at James M. Shuart Stadium on Long Island on September 22. 2015 NASL Champions: New York Cosmos." .

ns1:51361 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1999 World Rowing Championships were World Rowing Championships that were held from 22 to 29 August 1999 at the Royal Canadian Henley Rowing Course in St. Catharines, Ontario, Canada." .

ns1:51364 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 GDF Suez Grand Prix was a women's tennis tournament played on clay court. It was the 15th edition of the Hungarian Ladies Open, an WTA International tournaments tournament on the 2009 WTA Tour. It took place in Budapest, Hungary, from 4 July until 12 July 2009." .

ns1:51373 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1990 USSR Federation Cup was the fifth and the last edition of the USSR Federation Cup and was officially known as Cup of the USSR Football Union. It was brief and took place between 28 May 1990 through 06 July 1990." .

ns1:51383 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Autun was a conflict fought between the Roman Empire and the invading barbarian Alamanni tribe, who were ravaging Gaul, in 356 AD. In 356, as soon as the campaign season had begun, Julian received a report that the city of Autun, the capital of the Aedui, was under siege by a sizable army of Alamanni." .

ns1:51395 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 AFC Women's Asian Cup Final was a football match at the Amman International Stadium in Amman, Jordan which determined the winner of the 2018 AFC Women's Asian Cup. The final was contested between Japan women's national football team and Australia women's national soccer team, teams which were also the finalists of the previous edition held in 2014. Japan women's national football team defeated Australia women's national soccer team 1–0 in the final to win their second consecutive title. Japan women's national football team were the defending champions of the AFC Women's Asian Cup having defeated Australia women's national soccer team in the final of the 2014 edition in Vietnam to win their first continental title. The match also marked Australia women's national soccer team's third consecutive appearance in a Women's Asian Cup final. Australia women's national soccer team's previous victory against Japan women's national football team in a major tournament was their match up at the 2010 AFC Women's Asian Cup in Vietnam, where it knockout their East Asian opposition out of the semifinals and win the title. Australia women's national soccer team has been beaten by Japan women's national football team since then including their quarter final loss in the 2015 FIFA Women's World Cup. Australia women's national soccer team and Japan women's national football team were both part of Group B in the group stage. Australia women's national soccer team finished as group leaders and had to face second-placers Thailand in the semifinal while Japan women's national football team had to play against China, the Group A leaders. The semifinalists of the tournament including Australia women's national soccer team and Japan women's national football team secured a berth at the 2019 FIFA Women's World Cup by finishing among the top two teams in their group." .

ns1:51440 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Stuttgart 21 is a railway and urban development project in Stuttgart, Germany. On 2 April 2009 the Premier of Baden-Württemberg, Günther Oettinger, signed the finance agreement with the German Minister of Transport, Wolfgang Tiefensee and Deutsche Bahn board member Stefan Garber. Since late 2006 there have been negotiations between Deutsche Bahn, the Federal Government, the Baden-Württemberg Government and the city of Stuttgart over the sharing of the costs of the project. On 28 June 2007, a high-level conference was held and adjourned between Deutsche Bahn chairman Hartmut Mehdorn, Baden-Württemberg Minister of Finance Gerhard Stratthaus and Stuttgart mayor Wolfgang Schuster. On 19 July 2007 it was announced by the Federal Government, the State of Baden-Württemberg and Deutsche Bahn that the project had been approved. Identified funding sources are: Deutsche Bahn (€1,115 million), the State of Baden-Württemberg (€685 million), and the Federal Government (€500 million). The agreement also made provision for possible increases over the €2.8 billion estimate of up to €1 billion, with Baden-Württemberg agreeing to fund up to €780 million and Deutsche Bahn agreeing to fund up to €220 million. Shortly after the funding agreement was announced the political opponents of Wolfgang Schuster were quick to condemn the Stuttgart mayor for backtracking on a promise made during the 2004 mayoral elections - namely that he would allow the population of Stuttgart to decide the fate of Stuttgart 21 if the additional costs of the project amounted to more than €200 million." .

ns1:51461 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Burmese–Siamese War, 1775–76 was the major military conflict between the Konbaung Dynasty of Burma (Myanmar) and Thonburi Kingdom of Siam (Thailand). As a result, the Burmese loss of southern Lanna (Chiang Mai) later proved to be the end of their 200 years rule. Thihapate's army managed to recapture Chiang Mai, Maha Thiha Thura's army fought its way down to Siamese defenses and managed to occupy Phichai and Sukhothai provinces in northern Siam (present-day central Thailand). In 1776, the new monarch Singu Min sent Maha Thiha Thura to invade Lanna again with such a huge army that Lord Vichianprakarn of Chiang Mai had to abandon the city." .

ns1:51472 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "UM Airlines Flight 4230 was a chartered international passenger flight which was flying from Manas International Airport, Bishkek, Kyrgyzstan to Zaragoza Airport, Spain. The Yakovlev Yak-42D, operated by Ukrainian UM Airlines, tried to land at Trabzon Airport, Turkey to refuel, but because of dense fog, the crew lost visual and slammed into a mountain near Maçka, Trabzon. The final destination was Zaragoza Airport but the aircraft attempted to stopover at Trabzon Airport in Turkey, in order to refuel." .

ns1:51476 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Ferguson unrest (sometimes called the Ferguson uprising, Ferguson protests, or Ferguson riots) in Ferguson, Missouri, involved protests and riots beginning on August 10, 2014, the day after the fatal shooting of Michael Brown by police officer Darren Wilson. The Reporters Committee for Freedom of the Press (RCFP), a large coalition of media and press freedom groups, wrote to police forces in Ferguson, Missouri, to protest the harassment of journalists covering the protests. Missouri Governor Jay Nixon said at a press conference that the Missouri State Highway Patrol would take over policing Ferguson from the St. Louis County police, whose tactics were widely criticized, referring to the change as \"an operational shift,\" and that police will use force \"only when necessary,\" and will generally \"step back a little bit.\" During the same day, a rally of 50 to 70 people was held in Ferguson in support of Wilson under the banner \"I am Darren Wilson,\" and as of 25 August 2014, nearly US$400,000 were raised by supporters in an online crowdfunding campaign. The event, Ferguson October, began on Friday afternoon when protesters peacefully marched to County Prosecutor Bob McCulloch's office in Clayton, Missouri. On October 20, Missouri Senator Jamilah Nasheed was arrested in front of the Ferguson Police Department building for blocking traffic in the street and not respecting police orders. On November 17, the governor of Missouri declared a state of emergency in anticipation of protests in Ferguson following the announcement of the results of the grand jury. On October 6, Chief Judge Catherine D. Perry, of the United States District Court for the Eastern District of Missouri, ruled that \"The practice of requiring peaceful demonstrators and others to walk, rather than stand still, violates the constitution,\" and issued an injunction against the practice for peaceful, law-abiding protesters in Ferguson. An annual report last year by the office of Missouri's attorney general concluded that Ferguson police were \"twice as likely to arrest African Americans during traffic stops as they were whites.\"" .

ns1:51480 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Israel State Cup Final decided the winner of the 2016–17 Israel State Cup, the 81st season of Israel's main football cup. It will be played on 2017 at the Teddy Stadium in Jerusalem, between Maccabi Tel Aviv and Bnei Yehuda Tel Aviv F.C.." .

ns1:51512 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Inauguration of Franklin Pierce as the 14th President of the United States was held on Friday, 04 March 1853, at the East Portico of the United States Capitol in Washington, D.C. This was the 17th United States presidential inauguration and marked the commencement of the only four-year term of both Franklin Pierce as President and William R. King as Vice President." .

ns1:51533 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2000 World Figure Skating Championships had been held at the Palais des Congrès Acropolis in Nice, France from 23 March 2000 to 03 April 2000." .

ns1:51548 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 Japan Football League (Japanese: 第13回日本フットボールリーグ, Hepburn: Dai Jūsan-kai Nihon Futtobōru Rīgu) was the thirteenth season of the Japan Football League, the third tier of the Japanese association football league system. At the end of the 2010 Japan Football League, two new clubs, Kamatamare Sanuki and Nagano Parceiro, were promoted from the Japanese Regional Leagues by virtue of their final placing in the Regional League promotion series. In the penultimate round Sagawa Shiga FC won their third JFL championship in only five years of existence, firmly placing themselves among the strongest amateur teams in Japan." .

ns1:51717 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1994 World Snooker Championship (also referred to as the 1994 Embassy World Snooker Championship) was a professional ranking snooker tournament that took place between 16 16 April 1994 and 2 02 May 1994 at the Crucible Theatre in Sheffield, England." .

ns1:51719 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Open de Suède Vårgårda will be the 8th road bicycle racing running on the Open de Suède Vårgårda. It was held on 18 August 2013 over a distance of 132 kilometres (82.0 miles) and was the seventh race of the 2013 UCI Women's Road World Cup season." .

ns1:51732 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Merpati Nusantara Airlines Flight 9760 was a domestic commercial passenger 50-minutes flight, flying from Sentani Airport in Papua's Province Jayapura to Oksibil Airport in Oksibil, Indonesia operated by a de Havilland Canada DHC-6 Twin Otter 300. On Sunday, 02 August 2009, while carrying fifteen people over Papua, the aircraft went missing en route. Merpati Flight 9760 took off at 10:15 (local time) with an estimated time of arrival at Oksibil of 11:05." .

ns1:51746 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Four Continents Figure Skating Championships was an international figure skating competition in the 2011–2012 figure skating season. It was held at the World Arena, Colorado Springs in Colorado Springs, United States of America on 07 February 2012–12." .

ns1:51770 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2019 European Parliament election in Ireland is the Irish component of the 2019 European Parliament election and was held on Friday, 24 May 2019, on the same day as the 2019 local elections and a referendum easing restrictions on divorce. As Article 50 has a two-year period for withdrawal, the United Kingdom would not be part of the 2019 European Parliament election scheduled for 24 May 2019. The allocation of member of the European Parliament from Ireland increased from 11 to 13; however, this change would not take effect until the United Kingdom has left the EU, which took place on 31 January 2020. It also provided for the situation that Ireland's representation remained at 11 with an amendment to the rules, \"For the purpose of the European elections held in the year 2019, the return made by the chief returning officer shall declare that the last candidate deemed to be elected in each of the constituencies of Dublin and South as specified in the Third Schedule, shall not take up their seats in the European Parliament until such time as a date has been specified by the Parliament for the taking up of such seats\"." .

ns1:51771 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Buenos Aires train crash occurred on 19 October 2013 when a passenger train failed to stop at a terminal station in Buenos Aires, Argentina, injuring 105 people. At 07:35 local time (10:35 UTC), a passenger train failed to stop at Once railway station in Buenos Aires, colliding with the buffer stop." .

ns1:51814 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Camouflage Tour was a worldwide concert tour held by British singer-songwriter Rod Stewart to promote his album Camouflage. The tour began on 03 July 1984 in Reno, Nevada and ended on 03 March 1985 in Sun City." .

ns1:51839 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Copa ASOBAL was the 26th edition of the Copa ASOBAL. It took place like the Copa ASOBAL 2014 in the Palacio de los Deportes, in León, Castile and León, on 2015 and 2015. The tournament was hosted by ABANCA Ademar León and León city council, being the sixth time León hosted Copa ASOBAL. FC Barcelona Handbol won its 11th Copa ASOBAL by defeating Naturhouse La Rioja 35–31 in the Final." .

ns1:51866 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "badminton at the 2004 Summer Olympics was held at the Goudi Olympic Hall at the Goudi Olympic Complex from 14 August 2004 through 21 August 2004. A total of 31 nations competed in the four different badminton events at the 2004 Summer Olympics." .

ns1:51875 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1986–87 Liga Leumit saw Beitar Jerusalem F.C. win their first title. On 1 20 September 1986 the Israel Football Association board confirmed a proposal through which the number of teams in Liga Leumit would decrease to 14 teams." .

ns1:51901 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Universal Life (German: Universelles Leben, unofficially abbreviated UL) is the name of a controversial new religious movement based in Würzburg, Germany, which is described by members as a part of the new revelation movement. The founding of Universal Life was through teachings distributed by Gabriele Wittek." .

ns1:51916 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006–07 Cypriot First Division was the 68th season of the Cypriot top-level association football league. The champions ensured their participation in the 2007–08 UEFA Champions League and the runners-up in the 2007–08 UEFA Cup. They were replaced by the first three teams of the 2005–06 Cypriot Second Division, AEP Paphos F.C., Aris Limassol F.C. and Ayia Napa F.C.." .

ns1:51988 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Liu Bei's takeover of Yi Province was a military campaign by the warlord Liu Bei in taking control of Yi Province (covering present-day Sichuan and Chongqing) from the provincial governor, Liu Zhang. The campaign took place between 212 and 214 in the late Eastern Han dynasty. In 212, Pang Tong outlined three plans for Liu Bei to choose from: The first was to advance swiftly to seize Chengdu, the capital of Yi Province, from Liu Zhang." .

ns1:51999 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Mihăileşti explosion occurred on 24 May 2004 in the village of Mihăilești, Buzău County, Romania. A truck loaded with ammonium nitrate rolled over and caught fire before exploding an hour later killing at least 18 people and injuring 13 others. At 5:47 AM, a small explosion took place in the cabin of the truck, followed 2 minutes later by a larger explosion, killing 7 firefighters, the TV crew (Ionuţ Barbu and Elena Popescu from Antena 1), several villagers and the truck driver, totalling 18 people; 13 others were injured." .

ns1:52028 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1995 European Grand Prix (formally the XL Grand Prix of Europe) was a Formula One motor race held on 01 October 1995 at the Nürburgring, Nürburg, Germany. The 67-lap race was won by Michael Schumacher for the Benetton team after starting from third position. Its return to the World Championship schedule was facilitated by a surge of interest in the sport throughout Germany as a result of Michael Schumacher's drivers' title in 1994." .

ns1:52035 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Loznica (Serbian Cyrillic: бој на Лозници) or Battle of Tičar (бој на Тичару) was fought on 17 October 1810–18 October 1810 between the Serbian Revolutionaries and the Ottoman Army in Loznica, western Serbia. The battle was one of the most important ones in the First Serbian Uprising." .

ns1:52046 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1989 Giro di Lombardia was the 83rd edition of the Giro di Lombardia cycle race and was held on 14 October 1989. The race started in Como and finished at the Piazza del Duomo in Milan. The race was won by Tony Rominger of the Chateau d'Ax team." .

ns1:52053 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of La Arada (Spanish: Batalla de la Arada) was fought on 2 02 February 1851 near the town of Chiquimula in Guatemala, between the forces of Guatemala and an Allied army from Honduras and El Salvador. On 4 January 1851, Vasconcelos and Lindo met in Ocotepeque, Honduras, where they signed an alliance against Guatemala. The battle of La Arada represented the dominance of Guatemala in Central America and ensured a period of restoration, security and economic progress." .

ns1:52080 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1868 United States presidential election in Arkansas took place on 03 November 1868, as part of the 1868 United States presidential election. This was the first time since 1860 United States presidential election in Arkansas that a presidential election was held in the state due to secession, the American Civil War, and Reconstruction. This was also the first presidential election in which a Republican won Arkansas, something which would not happen again until over a century later when Richard Nixon carried the state in the 1972 election." .

ns1:52116 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1986–87 Cypriot First Division was the 48th season of the Cypriot top-level association football league. The champions ensured their participation in the 1987–88 European Cup and the runners-up in the 1987–88 UEFA Cup. The first two teams of the 1985–86 Cypriot Second Division, Ethnikos Achna FC and Omonia Aradippou were promoted and added to the First Division." .

ns1:52135 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Rey del Ring show (Spanish for \"King of the Ring\") was an annual professional wrestling major event produced by Mexican professional wrestling promotion International Wrestling Revolution Group (IWRG), and took place on 05 April 2015 in Arena Naucalpan, Naucalpan, State of Mexico, Mexico. The main event was the Eponymous IWRG Rey del Ring tournament, the thirteenth annual version of the tournament, which is IWRG's version of WWE's Royal Rumble event. The Mexican professional wrestling company International Wrestling Revolution Group (IWRG; at times referred to as Grupo Internacional Revolución in Mexico) started their annual IWRG Rey del Ring (\"King of the Ring\") event in 2002, creating an annual event around the eponymous IWRG Rey del Ring match, a 30-man elimination match similar in concept to the WWE's Royal Rumble match. From 2002 until the 2011 event the \"prize\" for winning the match itself was simply the prestige of outlasting 29 other competitors, but at the 2011 IWRG Rey del Ring IWRG introduced the IWRG Rey del Ring complete with a belt to symbolize the championship that would be awarded to the winner each year. All IWRG Rey del Ring shows, as well as the majority of the IWRG shows in general are held in Arena Naucalpan, owned by the promoters of IWRG and their main arena. The event featured three professional wrestling matches with different wrestlers involved in pre-existing scripted feuds, plots and storylines." .

ns1:52145 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Google Daydream is a discontinued virtual reality (VR) platform which was developed by Google, primarily for use with a headset into which a smartphone is inserted. Google Daydream was announced at the Google I/O developer conference in 2016, and the first headset, the Google Daydream View, was released on 2016. Google Daydream was Google's second foray into VR following Google Cardboard, a low-cost platform intended to encourage interest in VR. Compared to Google Cardboard, which was built into compatible apps and offered limited features, Google Daydream was built into Android itself and included enhanced features, including support for controllers. Google Daydream was not widely adopted by consumers or developers, and in 15 October 2019, Google announced that the Google Daydream View headset had been discontinued and that they would no longer certify new devices for Google Daydream. At the Google I/O developer conference in 2016, Google announced that a new virtual reality (VR) platform called \"Google Daydream\" would be built into the next release of their Android mobile operating system (OS)—Nougat (7.1). Google Daydream was Google's second foray into VR following Google Cardboard, which was a low-cost standard that utilized a cardboard viewer with plastic lenses that could hold a smartphone. Whereas Google Cardboard was used by running compatible apps and was accessible on most smartphones, Google Daydream was built into the Android OS itself and only worked on select phones that met the platform's standards, such as having specific hardware components. The first-generation Google Daydream View was announced on 2016. The Google Daydream View was released on 2016. Google announced at the Google I/O conference in 2016 that eight hardware partners would make Google Daydream-ready phones: Samsung, HTC, LG, Xiaomi, Huawei, ZTE, Asus and Alcatel. On 15 October 2019, Google announced that it would no longer sell the Google Daydream View headset, and that their new flagship phones, the Pixel 4 and Pixel 4 XL, would not be certified for Google Daydream." .

ns1:52182 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Inca Civil War, also known as the Inca Dynastic War, the Inca War of Succession, or, sometimes, the War of the Two Brothers, was fought between half-brothers Huáscar and Atahualpa, sons of Huayna Capac, over succession to the throne of the Inca Empire–149 The war followed Huayna Capac's death in 1527. It began in 1529, and lasted until 1532." .

ns1:52218 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1985 Winston Western 500 was a NASCAR Winston Cup Series racing event that took place on 17 November 1985, at Riverside International Raceway in Riverside, California." .

ns1:52249 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1962 German Grand Prix was a Formula One motor race held at the Nürburgring on 05 August 1962. It was race 6 of 9 in both the 1962 World Championship of Drivers and the 1962 Formula One season. The 15-lap race was won by BRM driver Graham Hill after he started from second position. Dan Gurney's Porsche had less than impressive handling but he finished third after having passed Phil Hill, whose Ferrari was doing much better than at 1962 British Grand Prix." .

ns1:52258 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Strade Bianche was a road cycling one-day race that took place on 03 March 2018 in Italy. It was the twelfth edition of the Strade Bianche and the fifth event of the 2018 UCI World Tour. Tiesj Benoot (Lotto–Soudal) took his first professional victory, after attacking from a chasing group to catch leaders Romain Bardet (AG2R La Mondiale) and Wout van Aert (Vérandas Willems–Crelan) before dropping them in the final sector of dirt roads." .

ns1:52276 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "E3 Saxo Bank Classic, previously known as E3 BinckBank Classic, E3 Harelbeke, Harelbeke–Antwerp–Harelbeke and E3-Prijs Vlaanderen, is an annual road bicycle racing in Flanders, Belgium. It is on the Union Cycliste Internationale World Tour calendar, as part of a series of cobbled classics in Belgium and Northern France in March and April. E3 Harelbeke is held on the last Friday of March and marks the start of the Flemish Cycling Week, starting a fortnight of WorldTour racing on the cobbles and bergs of Flanders. In 2012 E3 Harelbeke, when the E3 race was upgraded to World Tour status as well, organizers changed the date of their event to Friday to meet the demands of Union Cycliste Internationale, who requested a day of rest between two arduous World Tour events. Since the first edition until 2011 E3 Harelbeke, the race was held on a Saturday in the weekend before the Tour of Flanders, forming a tandem with the Brabantse Pijl on Sunday. From 2005 until 2011 E3 Harelbeke the race was part of the continental Union Cycliste Internationale Europe Tour, where it was classified as a 1.HC race." .

ns1:52281 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Supercopa de Chile (known as the 2018 Súper Copa MG for sponsorship purposes) was the sixth edition of the Supercopa de Chile, championship organised by the Asociación Nacional de Fútbol Profesional (ANFP). The match was played by the 2017 Chilean Primera División champions Club Social y Deportivo Colo Colo and the 2017 Copa Chile winners Santiago Wanderers. The two teams that contested the Supercopa were Club Social y Deportivo Colo Colo, who qualified as 2017 Transición champions and Santiago Wanderers, who qualified for the match as the 2017 Copa Chile winners, defeating Universidad de Chile 3–1 at the Estadio Ester Roa." .

ns1:52322 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Reavey and O'Dowd killings were two co-ordinated gun attacks on 04 January 1976 in County Armagh, Northern Ireland. Three members of the Reavey family were shot at their home in Whitecross, County Armagh and four members of the O'Dowd family were shot at their home in Ballydugan. At about 6:10PM, at least three masked men entered the home of the Reaveys, a Catholic family, in Whitecross, County Armagh. At about 6:20PM, three masked men burst into the home of the O'Dowds, another Catholic family, in Ballydugan, about fifteen miles away." .

ns1:52386 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Miss Belgium 2018 was the 50th edition of the Miss Belgium which was held on 2018 at the Plopsaland in De Panne, Belgium. Angeline Flor Pua was crowned as Miss Belgium 2018 in the conclusion of the pageant." .

ns1:52396 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 19th All-Union Conference of the Communist Party of the Soviet Union was a party conference held by the Communist Party of the Soviet Union from 28 June 1988 to 01 July 1988." .

ns1:52407 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Batna bombing took place on 06 September 2007 in Batna, a town in Batna Province, eastern Algeria. The bombing, which took place shortly before the visit of Algerian President Abdelaziz Bouteflika, resulted in approximately 15-20 deaths and 107 injuries as the bomber detonated his device among a crowd waiting to see the President, who was at the end of a three-day tour of eastern Algeria." .

ns1:52412 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1973 Belmont Stakes was the 105th running of the Belmont Stakes at Belmont Park in Elmont, New York, held on 09 June 1973. When comparing Secretariat to those six horses' health and fitness before the Belmont Stakes, writer Boniface felt Secretariat was in the best shape, almost as good as when he entered the Kentucky Derby weeks prior. Following that, Secretariat's jockey Ron Turcotte told reporters that Secretariat was ready for a top charge on Saturday. Secretariat set the Belmont Stakes track record at 2:24 and the record for largest margin of victory. Neil Milbert of the Chicago Tribune wrote that Secretariat \"ran what may have been the greatest horse race in history.\" Secretariat's jockey Ron Turcotte told the press that he saw the timer on the track hit 2:20 and he told himself \"Oh I can make it\" and continued to press Secretariat forward to break the course record. Through his combined victories at the Belmont Stakes, Preakness Stakes, and Kentucky Derby, Secretariat became the ninth horse to complete the Triple Crown of Thoroughbred Racing, and the first horse since Citation in 1948, ending a 25-year period without a Triple Crown winner." .

ns1:52426 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The First Battle of the Aisne (French: 1re Bataille de l'Aisne) was the Allied follow-up offensive against the right wing of the German First Army (led by Alexander von Kluck) and the Second Army (led by Karl von Bülow) as they retreated after the First Battle of the Marne earlier in 28 September 1914. In dense fog on the night of 13 September 1914, most of the British Expeditionary Force (BEF) crossed the Aisne on pontoons or partially demolished bridges, landing at Bourg-et-Comin on the right and at Venizel on the left." .

ns1:52453 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1979 Tour de Romandie was the 33rd edition of the Tour de Romandie cycle race and was held from 08 May 1979 to 13 May 1979. The race started in Neuchâtel and finished in Geneva." .

ns1:52458 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1991 Liège–Bastogne–Liège was the 77th edition of the Liège–Bastogne–Liège cycle race and was held on 21 April 1991. The race was won by Moreno Argentin of the Ariostea team." .

ns1:52465 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The First Carib War (1769 – 1773) was a military conflict between the Black Carib inhabitants of Saint Vincent and British military forces supporting British efforts at colonial expansion on the island. Led primarily by Black Carib chieftain Joseph Chatoyer, the Caribs successfully defended the windward side of the island against a military survey expedition in 1769, and rebuffed repeated demands that they sell their land to representatives of the British colonial government. With military matters at a stalemate, a peace agreement was signed in 1773 that delineated boundaries between British and Black Carib areas of the island." .

ns1:52505 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1971 Delaware 500 was a NASCAR Winston Cup Series race that took place on 17 October 1971, at Dover Downs International Speedway. All the racing action took place in the United States of America community of Dover, Delaware with five hundred laps completed on a paved oval track spanning 1.000 mile or 1.609 kilometres. Richard Petty capitalizes for career win #137 as Bobby Allison's wheel troubles in the closing laps doom the dominate Holman-Moody entry." .

ns1:52537 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The rowing at the 2016 Summer Olympics – lightweight women's double sculls competition at the 2016 Summer Olympics in Rio de Janeiro was held on 08 August 2016–12 August 2016 at the Lagoa Rodrigo de Freitas." .

ns1:52541 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Party's Just Begun Tour is the second tour by American girl group, The Cheetah Girls. The tour started on 15 September 2006 in Seattle and ended on 04 March 2007 in Houston." .

ns1:52573 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Cup of China was the third event of six in the 2012–13 ISU Grand Prix of Figure Skating, a senior-level international invitational competition series. It was held at the Shanghai Oriental Sports Center in Shanghai on 02 November 2012–4." .

ns1:52604 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Heineken Cup Final was the final match of the 2012–13 Heineken Cup, the 18th season of Europe's top club rugby union competition. The match was played on 2013 in the Aviva Stadium in Dublin, Ireland, kicking off at 5 pm (16:00 UTC). The all-French game was won by RC Toulonnais, defeating ASM Clermont Auvergne 16–15. If the Heineken Cup winner has already qualified through its domestic or regional league, the berth will normally pass to another team from its country; both ASM Clermont Auvergne and RC Toulonnais have qualified as the top two teams in the Top 14 home-and-away season. The final 2013–14 Heineken Cup participant was determined by the result of the Challenge Cup Final held the previous day at the RDS Arena in Dublin. On 6 April, ASM Clermont Auvergne defeated Montpellier 36–14 at the Stade Marcel Michelin in the quarterfinals while RC Toulonnais defeated Leicester Tigers the following evening 21–15. On 27 April, in the semifinals, ASM Clermont Auvergne won 16–10 against Munster at Stade de la Mosson while RC Toulonnais beat Saracens 26–14 at Twickenham the next day. The final was an all-French clash between ASM Clermont Auvergne and RC Toulonnais. At half-time the score was 3–3, with RC Toulonnais's Jonny Wilkinson and ASM Clermont Auvergne's Morgan Parra scoring a penalty each. Early in the second half, ASM Clermont Auvergne scored two tries (Napolioni Nalaga and Brock James) taking the score to 15–6, but despite this, Delon Armitage's converted try resulted in a 16–15 win for RC Toulonnais." .

ns1:52610 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Anglo-Persian War or Anglo-Iranian War (Persian: جنگ ایران و انگلستان) lasted between 01 November 1856 and 04 April 1857 and was fought between the British Empire and Iran (Persia), then ruled by the Qajar dynasty. The war had the British oppose an attempt by Iran to press its claim on the city of Herat. Though Herat had been part of Iran under the Qajar dynasty when the war broke out, it had declared itself independent under its own rebellious emir and placed itself under the protection of the British in India and in alliance with the Emirate of Kabul, the predecessor of the modern state of Afghanistan. The war resulted in the Persians withdrawing from Herat and signing a new treaty to surrender its claims on the city and in the British withdrawing from southern Iran. In response, the British governor-general in India, acting on orders from London, declared war on 01 November 1856." .

ns1:52621 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Big Square Eye is an Australian children's game show aired on ABC TV from 1991 until 1992 and was repeated one last time in 1993. It was hosted by Bob La Castra, with future musician Paul Mac as \"Knuckles\" McDermott on keyboard and sound effects." .

ns1:52631 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012–13 A-League was the 36th season of top-flight soccer in Australia, and the eighth season of the Australian A-League since its establishment in 2004. The 2012–13 season saw the introduction of a new Western Sydney-based team, the return of Newcastle Jets FC after their A-League licence was returned by FFA, and the end of Gold Coast United after they were removed from the competition at the end of the 2011–12 A-League. This season was also the last A-League season to be broadcast exclusively on paid television, after SBS obtained the rights to a live Friday night game each week of the A-League season, and all A-League finals games on a one-hour delay, on a $160 million four-year broadcast deal, effective from the 2013–14 A-League onwards." .

ns1:52636 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1996 Air Africa crash occurred on 08 January 1996 when an overloaded Air Africa Antonov An-32B aircraft, wet leased from Moscow Airways and bound for Kahemba Airport, overshot the runway at N'Dolo Airport in Kinshasa, Zaire (now the Democratic Republic of the Congo) after failing to take off and ploughed into Kinshasa's Simbazikita street market." .

ns1:52675 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The fifty-third edition of the 2011 Caribbean Series (Serie del Caribe) was played in 2011. It was held from 02 February 2011 through 07 February 2011 with the champions teams from Dominican Republic (Toros del Este), Mexico (Yaquis de Obregón), Puerto Rico (Criollos de Caguas) and Venezuela (Caribes de Anzoátegui)." .

ns1:52698 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2023 World Athletics Championships (Hungarian: 2023-as atlétikai világbajnokság), the nineteenth edition of the World Championships in Athletics, are scheduled to be held from 19 to 27 August, 2023 in Budapest, Hungary. The championships will be held in the National Athletics Centre in Budapest, which will be built, and have a capacity of 40,000." .

ns1:52754 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 PGA Championship was the 91st PGA Championship, held August 13–16 at Hazeltine National Golf Club in Chaska, Minnesota, a suburb southwest of Minneapolis. 20 low scorers in the 2009 PGA Championship Professional National Championship Sam Arnold, Ryan Benzel, Greg Bisconti, Keith Dicciani, Brian Gaffney, Bob Gaus, Scott Hebert, Todd Lancaster, Eric Lippert, Mitch Lowe, Mike Miles, Lee Rinker, Kevin Roman, Steve Schneiter, Mark Sheftic, Mike Small, Chris Starkjohann, Grant Sturgeon, Craig Thomas, Tim Weinhart. Top 70 leaders in official money standings from the 2008 WGC-Bridgestone Invitational and Legends Reno-Tahoe Open to the 2009 Buick Open Robert Allenby, Stephen Ames, Woody Austin, Briny Baird, Cameron Beckman (10), Chad Campbell (9), K. J. Choi, Tim Clark, Ben Crane, Brian Davis, Luke Donald, Jason Dufner, Ernie Els, Jim Furyk (9), Brian Gay (10), Mathew Goggin, Retief Goosen (10), Paul Goydos, J. J. Henry, Charley Hoffman, Charles Howell III, Dustin Johnson (10), Jerry Kelly (10), Anthony Kim (9), Justin Leonard (9), Hunter Mahan (9), John Mallinger, Steve Marino, John Merrick, Kevin Na, Sean O'Hair (10), Jeff Overton, Pat Perez (10), Kenny Perry (9,10), Carl Pettersson (10), Ian Poulter, Brett Quigley, John Rollins (10), Rory Sabbatini (10), John Senden, Kevin Streelman, Steve Stricker (9,10), Kevin Sutherland, D. J. Trahan, Bo Van Pelt (10), Scott Verplank, Nick Watney (10), Bubba Watson, Mike Weir, Lee Westwood, Mark Wilson (10), Yang Yong-eun (10) 9. His lead was halved to two shots over Pádraig Harrington and 2009 Honda Classic winner Yang Yong-eun." .

ns1:52800 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Vienna Uprising or October Revolution (German: Wiener Oktoberaufstand, or Wiener Oktoberrevolution) of 1848 was the last uprising in the Austrian Revolution of 1848." .

ns1:52845 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Slivnitsa (Bulgarian: Битка при Сливница, Serbian: Битка на Сливници) was a decisive victory of the Bulgarian army over the Serbians on 17 November 1885–19, 1885 in the Serbo-Bulgarian War. Slivnitsa was the decisive battle of the war." .

ns1:52870 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1994 European Badminton Championships were held in 's-Hertogenbosch, Netherlands, between 10 April 1994 and 17 April 1994, and hosted by the Badminton Europe and the Nederlandse Badminton Bond." .

ns1:52904 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Tour de Pologne was a road cycling road bicycle racing that took place between 29 July 2017 and 04 August 2017 in Poland. It was the 74th edition of the Tour de Pologne and was the twenty-seventh event of the 2017 UCI World Tour." .

ns1:52920 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Third Battle of Doiran was fought from 18 September 1918–19 September 1918, with the Greeks and the British assaulting the positions of the Bulgaria First Army near Dojran Lake. The battle was part of World War I and took place in the Balkan Theatre. The Greeks and the British were aiming to capture the Bulgaria positions in the hills above Dojran Lake. Several days after the battle, the British realized the Bulgaria fortifications were quiet." .

ns1:52947 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2011 Svenska Cupen Final was played on 05 November 2011. Kalmar FF faced Helsingborgs IF. Kalmar FF qualified for the final by beating IFK Göteborg in the first semi-final on 2011, Helsingborgs IF qualified by beating Örebro SK in the second semi-final on 2011. Since Kalmar FF were drawn as the away team in the final, it was played at Olympia in Helsingborg. Kalmar FF played their first final since 2008 and were looking to secure their fourth cup title overall. Helsinborgs IF played their second consecutive final and won their second consecutive title and fifth title in total by winning the match 3–1." .

ns1:52952 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Qantas Flight 30 (QF30, QFA30), on 25 July 2008, operated by Qantas Boeing 747-438, construction number 25067, registration VH-OJK, was a scheduled flight from Heathrow Airport to Melbourne Airport with a stopover in Hong Kong. Qantas still uses the flight number 30, as a non-stop Hong Kong-Melbourne Airport route, dropping its London origin." .

ns1:52955 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1885 Wimbledon Championships took place on the outdoor grass courts at the All England Lawn Tennis Club in Wimbledon, London, United Kingdom. The tournament ran from 04 July 1885 until 17 July 1885. It was the 9th staging of the Wimbledon Championships, and the first Grand Slam tennis event of 1885." .

ns1:52968 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Safwan Airfield Standoff was a post-ceasefire standoff between the Iraq and U.S. forces at the end of the 1991 Gulf War. He chose Safwan Airfield in southern Iraq to hold a formal cease-fire ceremony, as a demonstration that the coalition was in control of the war. On the morning of 01 March 1991, M1A1 Abrams and M3A2 Bradleys from VII corps arrived at Safwan Airfield to find a group of Iraq T-72s defending the base and the adjoining village. As result, LTC Wilson, the 1/4 Cavalry commander, scrambled his troops with orders to seize Safwan by 1600 of 01 March 1991." .

ns1:52980 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Women's Bandy World Championship the third Bandy World Championship for women and was contested between 7 bandy playing countries. The championship was played in Budapest, Hungary from 11 February 2007–17 February 2007." .

ns1:52993 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1993 Andorran parliamentary election were held in Andorra on 12 December 1993. The result was a victory for the National Democratic Group, which won eight seats, and its leader Òscar Ribas Reig remained Prime Minister." .

ns1:53031 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2004 European Road Championships were held in Otepää, Estonia between 2004 and 10 August 2004, regulated by the European Cycling Union." .

ns1:53035 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Nui Bop (03 January 1885–04 January 1885) was a French victory during the Sino-French War. The battle was fought to clear Chinese forces away from the French forward base at Chu, and was an essential preliminary to the Lạng Sơn Campaign in February 1885. On 5 04 January 1885, the day after de Nègrier's victory at Nui Bop, Lewal ordered Brière de l'Isle to 'capture Lạng Sơn as soon as possible'." .

ns1:53060 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "A vehicle breakdown is the mechanical failure of a motor vehicle in such a way that the underlying problem prevents the vehicle from being operated at all, or impedes the vehicle's operation so much, that it is very difficult, nearly impossible, or else dangerous to operate. Depending on the nature of the problem, the vehicle may or may not need to be towed to an automobile repair shop. A total vehicle breakdown is when the vehicle becomes totally immobile and cannot be driven even a short distance to reach a repair shop, thereby necessitating a tow. In a partial vehicle breakdown, the vehicle may still be operable, but its operation may become more limited or more dangerous, or else its continued operation may contribute to further damage to the vehicle. Some common causes of a partial vehicle breakdown include overheating, brake failure, or frequent stalling. The top ten causes of car vehicle breakdown were: With other problems, the driver may be able to operate the vehicle seemingly normally for some time, but the vehicle will need an eventual repair. Many vehicle owners with personal economic difficulty or a busy schedule may wait longer than they should get necessary repairs made to their vehicles, thereby increasing damage or else causing more danger." .

ns1:53061 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Hanau was fought from 30 to 31 October 1813 between Karl Philipp von Wrede's Austro-Kingdom of Bavaria corps and Napoleon's retreating French during the War of the Sixth Coalition. On 31 October 1813 Hanau was in French control, opening Napoleon’s line of retreat. The Battle of Hanau was a minor battle, but an important tactical victory allowing Napoleon’s army to retreat onto French soil to recover and face the invasion of France. The Austrian and Kingdom of Bavaria army at the battle of Hanau comprised two army corps, one Austrian and one Kingdom of Bavaria, and numbered no less than 42,000 men: 33,000 infantrymen, 9,000 cavalrymen and 94 artillery pieces. Napoleon thus commanded a total of about 20,000 men (40 battalions, 113 squadrons) at the battle of Hanau. The French occupied Hanau on 31 October 1813 with little resistance. The Kingdom of Bavaria wanted with this battle to support militarily its shift to the allied side. It did not really matter to the Kingdom of Bavaria politicians and military whether the battle was won or lost—as long as it took place. To commemorate the Battle of Hanau, memorials were erected in the city of Hanau, five of which have been preserved: at Lamboystrasse, Karl-Marx-Strasse and Robert Blum Strasse, and two more at the Kinzig bridge." .

ns1:53073 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Pershing was an operation conducted by the 1st Cavalry Division, the 3rd Brigade, 25th Infantry Division, the Army of the Republic of Vietnam (ARVN) 22nd Division and the South Korean Capital Division in Bình Định, lasting from 12 February 1967 to 19 January 1968. The operation concluded on 19 January 1968 with the 1st Cavalry Division being ordered to move 350km north from Landing Zone English in Bình Định to Camp Evans in Thừa Thiên Province as part of Operation Checkers, to increase the number of manoeveure battalions in I Corps in order to support the besieged Marines at Khe Sanh Combat Base and defeat any other People's Army of Vietnam (PAVN) attack across the DMZ. The division officially terminated Operation Pershing on 17 19 January 1968 when Colonel Rattan’s brigade began moving north by air and by sea. The division’s 2d Brigade remained in Bình Định for the time being to continue the search for the 3rd Division, a mission now called Operation Pershing II , under the direction of I Field Force headquarters. In conjunction with Pershing from 26 May 1967 to 27 19 January 1968 the 1st Cavalry Division, ARVN and the South Vietnamese 816th National Police Field Force Battalion conducted Operation Dragnet to root out the VC infrastructure in Bình Định." .

ns1:53086 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Cup of China was the third event of six in the 2015–16 ISU Grand Prix of Figure Skating, a senior-level international invitational competition series. It was held at the Capital Indoor Stadium in Beijing on 06 November 2015–8." .

ns1:53123 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 Turkish Grand Prix (officially the 2009 Formula 1 ING Turkish Grand Prix) was the seventh motor race of the 2009 Formula One World Championship. It was held on 07 June 2009 at Istanbul Park, Tuzla, Turkey. The race was won by Jenson Button, with Mark Webber and Sebastian Vettel of Red Bull Racing completing the podium. Sebastian Vettel dominated the qualifying session on Saturday 6 07 June 2009, setting the fastest time in all three parts of qualifying to claim his second pole position of the season, and third of his career." .

ns1:53125 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "socialism of the 21st century (Spanish: socialismo del siglo XXI) is an interpretation of socialist principles first advocated by German sociologist and political analyst Heinz Dieterich and taken up by a number of Latin American leaders. socialism of the 21st century has democratic socialism elements, but it primarily resembles revisionism. ALBA is most explicitly related to socialism of the 21st century while other organizations focus on economic integration, ALBA promotes social, political and economic integration among countries that subscribe to democratic socialism. Although democratic socialism intellectuals have welcomed a socialism of the 21st century, they have been sceptical of Latin America's examples." .

ns1:53128 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Coleman Vision Tennis Championships was a professional tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts. It took place in Albuquerque, United States, on 15 September 2014–21 September 2014." .

ns1:53131 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1985–86 Football League Cup (known as the Milk Cup for sponsorship reasons) was the 26th season of the EFL Cup, a knockout competition for England's top 92 football clubs. The competition began on 20 August 1985, and ended with the 1986 Football League Cup Final on 20 April 1986 at the Old Wembley Stadium. The cup was won by Oxford United F.C., who beat Queens Park Rangers 3–0 in the 1986 Football League Cup Final, to win their first major cup silverware. Favourites Liverpool, in search of a unique domestic treble, were surprisingly beaten by QPR, while Oxford United F.C. eased past Aston Villa in the other semi-1986 Football League Cup Final." .

ns1:53139 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The alpine skiing at the 1976 Winter Olympics – men's downhill competition of the alpine skiing at the 1976 Winter Olympics at Innsbruck, Austria, was held at Patscherkofel on Thursday, 5 February, on the same course as in 1964. Franz Klammer of Austria was the defending World Cup downhill champion and led the current season; he had also won the pre-alpine skiing at the 1972 Winter Olympics – men's downhill World Cup downhill at Patcherkofel a year earlier in January 1975." .

ns1:53151 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Chotusitz, or Chotusice, sometimes called the Battle of Czaslau (today: Čáslav), took place on 17 May 1742, in Bohemia, now the Czech Republic; it was part of the 1740 to 1742 First Silesian War, itself a subsidiary of the wider War of the Austrian Succession. At 2:00 am on 17 May 1742, his exhausted troops stopped at the small village of Chotusice, three hours march from Kutná Hora. In June, the Treaty of Breslau ended the First Silesian War; Prussian troops withdrew from Bohemia, and Austria recaptured Prague in December." .

ns1:53173 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Oldenzaal was a short siege that took place during the Eighty Years' War and the Anglo–Spanish War by a Dutch and English army led by Maurice of Orange of the city of Oldenzaal from 20 to 23 October 1597." .

ns1:53178 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1977–78 Ekstraklasa was the 52nd season of the Polish Football Championship and the 44th season of the Ekstraklasa, the top Polish professional league for association football clubs, since its establishment in 1927. The champions were Wisła Kraków, who won their 5th Polish title and 6th Ekstraklasa (in the 1951 season Wisła Kraków finished in the first position and became the league champion, but in that season, the Ekstraklasa was not a competition for the title of the Polish Champion. Before the season Polish Football Association decided that Champion of Poland title will be awarded to the winner of the Polish Cup, which was later Ruch Chorzów). A total of 16 teams participated, 14 of which competed in the league during the 1976–77 Ekstraklasa, while the remaining two were promoted from the 1976–77 II liga." .

ns1:53188 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 Girls' Youth South American Volleyball Championship was the 17th edition of the tournament, organised by South America's governing volleyball body, the Confederación Sudamericana de Voleibol (CSV). Held in Tacna, Tarapoto and Callao in Peru. The following national teams participated in the tournament, teams were seeded according to how they finished in the 2008 Girls' Youth South American Volleyball Championship with host Peru being seeded first:" .

ns1:53212 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2004 African Cup of Nations Final was a association football match that took place on 14 February 2004 at the Olympic Stadium of Radès in Radès, Tunisia, to determine the winner of the 2004 African Cup of Nations, the association football championship of Africa organized by the Confederation of African Football (CAF)." .

ns1:53234 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Yōga (洋画, literally \"Western-style painting\") is a term, coined in the Meiji period, to distinguish works from indigenous traditional Japanese painting, or Nihonga (日本画). In 1876, the Kobu Bijutsu Gakkō (Technical Art School) was established by the Meiji government as Japan's first dedicated Yōga art school. In the 1880s, the general reaction against Westernization and the growth in popularity and strength of the Nihonga movement caused the temporary decline of Yōga. From 1896, a Yōga department was added to the curriculum of the Tokyo Bijutsu Gakkō, and from that point onwards, Yōga has been an accepted component of Japanese painting. Since that time, Yōga and Nihonga have been the two main divisions of modern Japanese painting. However, in many cases Nihonga artists also adopted realistic Western painting techniques, such as perspective and shading. Because of this tendency to synthesize, although Nihonga forms a distinct category within the Japanese annual Nitten exhibitions, in recent years, it has become increasingly difficult to draw a distinct separation in either techniques or materials between Nihonga and Yōga." .

ns1:53254 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The GNAT Modified General Public License (short: Modified GPL, GMGPL) is a version of the GNU General Public License specifically modified for compiled units and for the generic feature found in the Ada programming language. The modification is as follows: The GNAT Ada compiler can automate conformance checks for some GPL software license issues via a compiler directive." .

ns1:53286 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Hazfi Cup Final was the 27th final since 1975. The match was between Sanat Mes Kerman F.C. and Tractor Sazi which Tractor Sazi won the match 1–0, with Saeid Daghighi scored in the 51st minute. Tractor Sazi defeated Foolad 1–0 in 1/2 Final and reached the final with Daghighi scored the only goal of the match, held in Sahand Stadium. This was Sanat Mes Kerman F.C.'s first Hazfi final and Tractor Sazi's third appearance in the final match of the tournament. Tractor Sazi's first final was in 1976 edition which they was defeated by Malavan in Amjadieh Stadium. 50% of the stadium were belongs to the Sanat Mes Kerman F.C.'s fans and others were belong to Tractor Sazi's fans. The final was decided with draw which 15,000 capacity Shahid Bahonar Stadium (the Mes Kemran's home Stadium) was announced as the venue for the final. FIFA listed referee, Yadollah Jahanbazi was announced as the final match referee by IRIFF's referees committee." .

ns1:53336 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 2014 Bitburger Open Badminton Championships was the fifteenth Grand Prix Gold and Grand Prix tournament of the 2014 BWF Grand Prix Gold and Grand Prix. The tournament was held in Saarlandhalle, Saarbrücken, Germany 28 October 2014 until 02 November 2014 and had a total purse of $120,000." .

ns1:53338 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Hotel Polen fire occurred on 09 May 1977 in Amsterdam, the Netherlands. In 1891, after the adjacent space on the Rokin was bought, the Hotel Polen was established by the Maatschappij tot Exploitatie van het Poolsche Koffiehuis (Polish Coffee House Society), and a building was constructed there which was possibly designed by the architect Pierre Cuypers, who also designed the Amsterdam Central Station and the Rijksmuseum. The fire fighters tried to unfold a life net in the Papenbroekssteeg, an alley which runs between the Rokin and the Kalverstraat, but it was too narrow." .

ns1:53342 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011–12 IFA Premiership (known as the Carling Premiership for sponsorship reasons) was the fourth season since its establishment after a major overhaul of the 2010–11 IFA Premiership system in Northern Ireland, and the 111th season of Irish 2010–11 IFA Premiership football overall. The season began on 06 August 2011, and ended on 28 April 2012. They successfully defended their title, to win the 2010–11 IFA Premiership for the sixth time in seven seasons after a 2–1 home win over Portadown on 7 28 April 2012. After winning the 2010–11 IFA Premiership last season, Linfield F.C. were the 2010–11 IFA Premiership's sole representatives in the UEFA Champions League." .

ns1:53352 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Tour of Life (originally known as the \"Lionheart Tour\", and also officially referred to as the Kate Bush Tour and by outside sources as the \"Kate Bush Show\" and \"Kate Bush: On Tour\") was the first and only concert tour by English singer-songwriter and musician Kate Bush." .

ns1:53358 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1960 Argentine Grand Prix was a Formula One motor race held at Autódromo Juan y Oscar Gálvez on 07 February 1960. It was race 1 of 10 in the 1960 World Championship of Drivers and race 1 of 9 in the 1960 Formula One season." .

ns1:53359 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1944 Hungarian parliamentary election were held in Hungary in 01 November 1944. An additional 160 members were elected in liberated areas on 02 April 1945 and 24 June 1945." .

ns1:53370 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Pisagua (\"Desembarco y combate de Pisagua\"), was a landing operation of the War of the Pacific, fought on 02 November 1879, between Chile and the combined forces of Bolivia and Peru." .

ns1:53382 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 U.S. Open Cup Final was played on 30 September 2015, at PPL Park, now known as Subaru Park, in Chester, Pennsylvania. This edition of the final was contested between Sporting Kansas City (SKC) and the Philadelphia Union." .

ns1:53388 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of the Bridges was fought near Bramsche, Germany in 15 AD between the Roman Empire general Aulus Caecina Severus and an alliance of Germanic peoples commanded by Arminius. It was part of a three-year Early Imperial campaigns in Germania by Germanicus in Germania." .

ns1:53428 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Armenian–Azerbaijani War, which started after the Russian Revolution, was a series of conflicts in 1918, then from 1920 to 1922, that occurred during the brief independence of Armenia and Democratic Republic of Azerbaijan, and afterwards. In 30 March 1918, ethnic and religious tensions grew and the Armenian-Azerbaijani conflict in Baku began. On 28 November 1920, blaming Armenia for the invasions of Şərur on 20 28 November 1920 and Karabakh the following day, the 11th Red Army under the command of Gen. Anatoliy Gekker, crossed the demarcation line between First Republic of Armenia and Soviet Democratic Republic of Azerbaijan. Persuaded by Soviet leadership, Zangezur had already been ceded by Democratic Republic of Azerbaijan to Armenia in 28 November 1920 as a \"symbol of friendship\"." .

ns1:53433 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Kano bombing was a terrorist attack on 28 November 2014 at the Central Mosque (Grand Mosque) in Kano, the biggest city in the mainly Muslim Northern Nigeria during the Islamist insurgency in Nigeria. The mosque is next to the palace of the Emir of Kano, Muhammad Sanusi II, Nigeria's second most senior Muslim cleric, who had urged the civilians to protect themselves by arming up against Boko Haram." .

ns1:53480 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Inauguration of William Henry Harrison as the ninth President of the United States was held on Thursday, 04 March 1841, at the East Portico of the United States Capitol in Washington, D.C. This was the 14th United States presidential inauguration and marked the commencement of the only four-year term of both William Henry Harrison as President and John Tyler as Vice President. Harrison's United States presidential inauguration was marked by several novelties; he was the first president-elect to arrive in Washington, D.C. by train, and for the first time an official inaugural committee of citizens had formed to plan the day's parade and inaugural ball." .

ns1:53523 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2003 Paris-Tours was the 97th edition of the Paris–Tours cycle race and was held on 05 October 2003. The race started in Saint-Arnoult-en-Yvelines and finished in Tours." .

ns1:53528 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Troitse-Sergiyeva Lavra (Троицкая осада, Троицкое сидение in Russian) was an abortive attempt of the Polish–Lithuanian irregular army that acted in support of False Dmitry II to capture the Trinity Monastery (the modern Trinity Lavra of St. Sergius) north of Moscow. The siege lasted for 16 months, from 23 01 September 1608 until 1610. Under the threat of the approaching army of Mikhail Skopin-Shuisky, the Polish forces raised the siege on 1610 and retreated to Dmitrov." .

ns1:53545 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Boston Rowing Marathon is a rowing head race taking place on the third Sunday of September annually in Lincolnshire, England, over the exceptionally long distance of 49.2 km (30.6 miles). The course is along the River Witham from Lincoln to Boston. A single coxed four of seniors rowed from central Boston to the Brayford Pool in central Lincoln (against the river flow) and took around six hours. The centre was founded in 2006 in response to marathon being the only rowing hosted in Lincoln." .

ns1:53602 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1984–85 FIBA European Cup Winner's Cup was the nineteenth edition of FIBA Europe's 2nd-tier level European-wide professional club basketball competition, contested between national domestic cup champions, running from 02 October 1984, to 19 March 1985. It was contested by 20 teams, four less than in the 1983–84 FIBA European Cup Winner's Cup. FC Barcelona Bàsquet, runner-up in the 1983–84 edition of the Spanish Cup, was assigned the berth of the previous year's winner, Real Madrid, who would participate in that season's FIBA Europe European Champions Cup instead." .

ns1:53641 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Ravenna, fought on 11 April 1512, was a major battle of the War of the League of Cambrai. A month before the battle, multiple sources reported a monstrous birth which became known as the Monster of Ravenna. Regardless, the Monster of Ravenna acquired theological implications which evolved beyond the one battle and persisted well into the Protestant Reformation. Beginning in February 1512, the French forces in Italy, newly commanded by Gaston de Foix, Duc de Nemours, had been engaged in capturing cities in the Romagna and the Veneto, in an attempt to deny control of those regions to the forces of the Holy League. Thus, in late March, Nemours, together with an Italian contingent under Alfonso I d'Este, Duke of Ferrara, marched east from Bologna and laid siege to the city of Ravenna, which was defended by Papal States troops. Julius II, alarmed at the prospect of losing his last stronghold in the Romagna, demanded that an army be sent to relieve the city; Ramón de Cardona had to comply, and the Spanish army set out for Ravenna with a company of Papal States troops in tow. Nemours, short on supplies and increasingly anxious to give battle before he was forced to withdraw from Italy, ordered a general attack for the following day. However, much of the French army was withdrawn to France following the battle, and La Palice was forced to extricate himself from Italy in August by renewed efforts on the part of the Holy League. The Spanish forces in Italy were almost entirely destroyed at Ravenna, but Cardona would raise another army and appear in Lombardy in 1513." .

ns1:53681 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "American Airlines Flight 96 was a regular domestic flight operated by American Airlines from Los Angeles to New York via Detroit and Buffalo. On 12 June 1972, the left rear cargo door of the McDonnell Douglas DC-10-10 operating the flight blew open and broke off en route between Detroit and Buffalo above Windsor, Ontario; the accident is thus sometimes referred to as the Windsor incident, although for the NTSB it is an accident, therefore not an incident. Flight 96 was a regularly scheduled flight from Los Angeles International Airport to LaGuardia Airport with intermediate stops at Detroit Metropolitan Wayne County Airport and Buffalo Niagara International Airport." .

ns1:53699 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Monte Porzio (also called the Battle of Tusculum) was fought on 29 May 1167 between the Holy Roman Empire and the Commune of Rome. The communal Roman army, which one historian has called the \"greatest army which Rome had sent into the field in centuries\", was defeated by the forces of the Emperor Frederick Barbarossa and his local allies, the Counts of Tusculum and the ruler of Albano. The site of the battle was the field between a small hill and the walls of the city of Tusculum, at a place called \"Prataporci\", about 25 km southeast of Rome. The Battle of Monte Porzio is part of the long struggle between the Italian city-states and the Holy Roman Empire." .

ns1:53718 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Western Conference (French: Conférence de l'Ouest) is one of two conferences in the National Hockey League (NHL) used to divide teams. Then-new NHL commissioner Gary Bettman made the change to help non-hockey fans better understand the game, as the National Basketball Association, National Football League, and Major League Baseball all use geographic-based names for their conferences and divisions. Along with the Eastern Conference, the Western Conference was temporarily abolished for the 2020–21 NHL season after the COVID-19 pandemic forced a radical realignment of the League to preclude the need for teams to regularly cross the Canada-U.S. border. For 2020–21 the Canadian teams will compete in the newly-formed North Division while the U.S. Pacific Division teams, along with three American teams from the Central Division will form a re-constituted West Division. When the names of conferences and divisions were changed in 1993, the Western Conference's divisions became the Central Division and Pacific Division. Its American teams joined the Central Division, while the Canadian teams returned to the Pacific Division. With this 2013 realignment, the Central Division is based almost entirely in the Central Division Time Zone (the only exception being the Colorado Avalanche, who are in the Mountain Time Zone), and the Pacific Division is based entirely within the Mountain and Pacific Division Time Zones. The Seattle Kraken will begin play in the 2021–22 season, increasing the Western Conference to 16 teams and the Arizona Coyotes will then move to the Central Division from the Pacific Division to balance out the conference. Unique between 1994 and 1998, a series involving a Central Division team and a Pacific Division team was a 2–3–2 rotation instead of the normal 2–2–1–1–1 format, with the higher seeded team having the option of starting play at home or on the road." .

ns1:53732 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 OFC Nations Cup was the eighth edition of the OFC Nations Cup, and the first under a new format that was introduced. Doubling as the qualification tournament for the 2010 FIFA World Cup, the tournament was substantially different from earlier editions: 2004 OFC Nations Cup Australia did not compete after leaving the Oceania Football Confederation for the Asian Football Confederation, and for the first time since the 1996 OFC Nations Cup, no fixed venue was used. Unlike the 2004 OFC Nations Cup, which had featured six teams from the Oceania Football Confederation, the 2008 tournament had just four teams. The four qualified teams for the 2008 OFC Nations Cup were: New Zealand national football team advanced to the AFC-OFC Play-off. There were 39 goals scored in 12 games, for an average of 3.25 goals per game." .

ns1:53745 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "A 2016 Portuguese presidential election was held on 24 January 2016. Turnout was higher than that of the 2011 Portuguese presidential election, but reached a record low in a President of Portugal election with no incumbents as only 48.66% of the electorate cast a ballot." .

ns1:53747 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Carnivores Tour was a co-headlining concert tour by American rock bands Linkin Park and Thirty Seconds to Mars. It began on 08 August 2014 in West Palm Beach, Florida and ended on 19 September 2014 in Concord, California, visiting arenas and stadiums throughout North America. During the concert tour, Linkin Park and Thirty Seconds to Mars promoted greener touring practices and worked with Music for Relief, a non-profit organization to support disaster relief and programs to protect the environment. Set for 08 August 2014, the Carnivores Tour showcased Linkin Park's sixth studio album The Hunting Party (2014) and Thirty Seconds to Mars' fourth studio album Love, Lust, Faith and Dreams (2013). Footage recorded during the Carnivores Tour was uploaded to Linkin Park's official YouTube account." .

ns1:53789 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1998 Mosconi Cup, the fifth edition of the annual nine-ball pool competition between teams representing Europe and the United States of America, took place 1998–1998 at the York Hall in Bethnal Green, Greater London, England. United States of America won the Mosconi Cup by defeating Team Europe 13–9." .

ns1:53826 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 Virginia National Bank Men's Pro Championship was a professional tennis tournament played on indoor hard courts. It took place in Charlottesville, Virginia, USA between 02 November 2009 and 08 November 2009." .

ns1:53834 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Andersen Air Force Base B-2 accident was a 23 February 2008 incident when the Spirit of Kansas, a United States Air Force Northrop Grumman B-2 Spirit stealth strategic bomber, crashed on the runway shortly after takeoff from Andersen Air Force Base in Guam. On 23 February 2008, a B‐2 crashed on the runway shortly after takeoff from Andersen Air Force Base in Guam." .

ns1:53857 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The athletics at the 1976 Summer Olympics – men's 100 metres sprint event at the athletics at the 1976 Summer Olympics in Montreal, Quebec, Canada, was held at Olympic Stadium of Montreal on 23 July 1976 and 24." .

ns1:53894 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "On 09 September 2005, an 2005 Air Kasai Antonov An-26B crash in the Republic of the Congo north of Brazzaville, killing all 13 people on board. Antonov An-26B 9Q-CFD was on a domestic flight in the Democratic Republic of the Congo from Kinshasa to Boende Airport in Boende on 09 September 2005." .

ns1:53899 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1984 All-Ireland Senior Club Hurling Championship Final was a hurling match played at Croke Park on 1984 to determine the winners of the 1983–84 All-Ireland Senior Club Hurling Championship, the 14th season of the All-Ireland Senior Club Hurling Championship, a tournament organised by the Gaelic Athletic Association for the champion clubs of the four provinces of Ireland. The final was contested by Ballyhale Shamrocks GAA of Kilkenny and Gort GAA of Galway, with the game ending in a 1-10 apiece draw. Ballyhale Shamrocks GAA were hoping to win their second All-Ireland title while Gort GAA were hoping to claim their first title." .

ns1:53909 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Taiyuan Massacre took place during the Boxer Rebellion, 09 July 1900, in Taiyuan, Shanxi province, North China. On 09 July 1900, reports circulated that he had executed forty-four foreigners (including women and children) from missionary families whom he had invited to the provincial capital Taiyuan under the promise to protect them. Journalist and historical writer Nat Brandt has called the massacre of Christians in Shanxi \"the greatest single tragedy in the history of Christian evangelicalism.\" For two years Moir Duncan studied Chinese at Taiyuan in Shanxi. From Death Throes of a Dynasty: Letters and Diaries of Charles and Bessie Ewing, Missionaries to China, by Charles Ewing, Bessie Ewing, edited by E. G. Ruoff: A fourth event was the throne's awareness of the massacre of missionaries at Taiyuan, the capital of Shansi. In response to the invitation of the new Governor, Ts en-ch un-hsiien, the party of eight missionaries, under an escort provided by the Governor, started from Pao-ting-fu on Wednesday, June 26, reaching T ai-yiian-fu on 09 July 1900 the first anniversary of the awful massacre in that very city. Taken unedited from \"Last Letters and Further Records of Martyred Missionaries of the China Inland Mission\" The Shansi Imperial University at Taiyuan was founded in 1901 with funds from the indemnity levied against Shansi for the massacre of the Christians by the Boxers." .

ns1:53931 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 7th Nuestra Belleza México 2000 pageant, was held at the Auditorio Emilio Sánchez Piedras of Apizaco, Tlaxcala, Mexico on 2000. Forty-three contestants of the Mexico competed for the national title, which was won by Jacqueline Bracamontes from Jalisco, who later competed in Miss Universe 2001 in Puerto Rico. The Mexicana Universal title was assumed by Paulina Flores from Sinaloa after Jacqueline Bracamontes won the right to compete in Miss Universe, she had been the original winner of this title but she could not hold both titles, and then Paulina Flores as Suplente/1st Runner-up assumed this Title. After three years, two events were held separately to select the two winners for the titles Nuestra Belleza México 2000 and Mexicana Universal. After three years, returned to take place the semi-final competition entitled \"Nuestra Belleza Mexico: Rumbo a Miss Mundo\" here was chosen the Top 20 among the 43 candidates, in which was announced the winner of the Mexicana Universal title Jacqueline Bracamontes, a week later she won the title of Nuestra Belleza México 2000 and then her Suplene/1st Runner-up Paulina Flores from Sinaloa assumed the Title, who curiously was also the Suplente/1st Runner-up in the final competition. The Mexicana Universal pageant was held at the Auditorio Emilio Sánchez Piedras of Apizaco, Tlaxcala, Mexico and was hosted by Joana Benedek and Alexis Ayala. It was officially the 3rd edition of the \"Mexicana Universal\" contest and as an official separate pageant to choose Mexico's representative to Miss World." .

ns1:53944 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 2014 Dutch Open Badminton Championships was the fortieth grand prix gold and grand prix tournament of the 2014 BWF Grand Prix Gold and Grand Prix. The tournament was held in Topsportcentrum, Almere, Netherlands 07 October 2014 until 12 October 2014 and had a total purse of $50,000." .

ns1:53964 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1985 Van Scoy Diamond Mine 500 was a NASCAR Winston Cup Series racing event that took place on 09 June 1985, at Pocono Raceway in Long Pond, Pennsylvania." .

ns1:53983 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1980 Champion of Champions was a professional non-ranking snooker tournament held from 02 October 1980–12 October 1980 at the Gillian Lynne Theatre in Drury Lane, London." .

ns1:54030 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Sack of Rome in 546 was carried out by the Gothic king Totila during the Gothic War of 535–554 between the Ostrogothic Kingdom and the Byzantine Empire. Totila finally entered Rome on 17 December 546, after his men scaled the walls at night and opened the Asinarian Gate." .

ns1:54032 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Lambak Kanan, formally known as Lambak Kanan National Housing Scheme (Malay: Rancangan Perumahan Negara Lambak Kanan), is a public housing estate on the northern outskirts of Bandar Seri Begawan, the capital of Brunei. Lambak Kanan is de facto a suburb of the capital. Lambak Kanan is officially known as Lambak Kanan National Housing Scheme (Malay: Rancangan Perumahan Negara Lambak Kanan). Lambak Kanan is divided into five areas and they are officially villages, the third and lowest in the hierarchy of Brunei's administrative divisions. While Lambak Kanan is considered a suburb of Bandar Seri Begawan, it is not part the spatial jurisdiction of the city's Municipal Department. Almost all residents of Lambak Kanan live in the houses of Rancangan Perumahan Negara or National Housing Plan, a public housing scheme provided by the government through the Housing Development Department, hence the name Perumahan Negara Lambak Kanan." .

ns1:54043 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 WTA Elite Trophy was a women's tennis tournament played at the Hengqin International Tennis Center in Zhuhai, China. The final two alternates for the 2017 WTA Finals would have been eligible to play in WTA Elite Trophy even if they had participated in the WTA Finals. In 2017, the final standings of each group were determined by the first of the following methods that applied: Stefanie Graf, former WTA World No.1 and 22-time Grand Slam champion, continued as Tournament Ambassador for the 2017 WTA Elite Trophy Zhuhai, helping promote the third edition of this elite year-end women’s tennis event. The total prize money for the Hengqin Life 2017 WTA Elite Trophy Zhuhai was US $2,280,935." .

ns1:54055 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1963 South Boston 400 was a NASCAR Grand National Series event that was held on 1963, at South Boston Speedway in South Boston, Virginia. Only him, Jimmy Pardue, Neil Castles, Billy Wade and Larry Thomas failed to finish the race out of this grid of 16 United States of America-born drivers." .

ns1:54058 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 2012 India Open Grand Prix Gold was the last Grand Prix Gold and Grand Prix tournament of the 2012 BWF Grand Prix Gold and Grand Prix. The tournament was held in Babu Banarasi Das Indoor Stadium, Lucknow, India from 18 December 2012 until 23 December 2012 and had a total purse of $120,000." .

ns1:54082 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Devotional Tour was a 1993 concert tour by English electronic band Depeche Mode in support of the group's eighth studio album, Songs of Faith and Devotion, which was released in March 1993. The tour began with an eleven-week European leg, which kicked off in Lille, France in mid-May and culminated in London in late July. Additionally, a live album entitled Songs of Faith and Devotion Live was released in 20 December 1993. The group continued to promote Songs of Faith and Devotion the following year on the The Exotic Tour/Summer Tour '94, which included an additional North American leg." .

ns1:54083 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "A 1925 Fitzroy state by-election for the seat of Fitzroy in the Victorian Legislative Assembly was held on Wednesday 04 February 1925. The candidates were Maurice Blackburn for the Labor Party, and independent Joseph Alfred Boell, a councillor in the Fitzroy Council and three-time mayor of Fitzroy." .

ns1:54095 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Skardu was a prolonged military blockade carried out by the Gilgit Scouts, Chitral Scouts and Chitral State Bodyguards, acting in coordination against Jammu and Kashmir State Forces and the Indian Army in the town of Skardu, during the Indo-Pakistani War of 1947. In 01 November 1947 the situation in the Astore and Skardu was precarious." .

ns1:54106 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Enogai was a battle between United States of America and Imperial Japanese Army and Navy forces on 10 July 1943–11 July 1943. It took place in the early phase of the New Georgia Campaign in the Solomon Islands during the Pacific War. Prior to the battle, US troops had landed at Rice Anchorage, in the Kula Gulf, on the northern coast of New Georgia, tasked with supporting efforts further south to advance on the airfield at Munda. Casualties from the battle were evacuated by seaplane on 11 July 1943; the process was interrupted when several Japanese fighters attacked the seaplanes on the water." .

ns1:54117 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 UCI Road World Championships – Men's road race of the 2014 UCI Road World Championships was a cycling event that took place on 2014 in Ponferrada, Spain. Kwiatkowski held on by a second to beat Australia's Simon Gerrans, while Spain's Alejandro Valverde finished in third place for the third successive world championships. Qualification was based on performances on the Union Cycliste Internationale run tours during 2014. Results from January to the middle of August counted towards the qualification criteria on both the 2014 Union Cycliste Internationale World Tour and the Union Cycliste Internationale Continental Circuits across the world, with the rankings being determined upon the release of the numerous tour rankings on 2014." .

ns1:54118 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The DARPA Grand Challenge is a prize competition for American autonomous vehicles, funded by the DARPA, the most prominent research organization of the United States Department of Defense. The first competition of the DARPA Grand Challenge was held on March 13, 2004 in the Mojave Desert region of the United States, along a 150-mile (240 km) route that follows along the path of Interstate 15 from just before Barstow, California to just past the California–Nevada border in Primm. The first competition of the DARPA Grand Challenge was held on March 13, 2004 in the Mojave Desert region of the United States, along a 150-mile (240 km) route that follows along the path of Interstate 15 from just before Barstow, California to just past the California–Nevada border in Primm. The third competition of the DARPA Grand Challenge, known as the \"Urban Challenge\", took place on November 3, 2007 at the site of the now-closed George Air Force Base (currently used as Southern California Logistics Airport), in Victorville, California (Google map). In August 2012 DARPA announced Boston Dynamics would act as sole source for the robots to be used in the challenge, awarding them a contract to develop and build 8 identical robots based on the PETMAN project for the software teams to use. On April 22, 2013, DARPA awarded a $1 million prize to \"Ground Systems\", a 3-person team with members in Ohio, Texas and California, as the winner of the Fast Adaptable Next-Generation Ground Vehicle (FANG) Mobility/Drivetrain Challenge." .

ns1:54134 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "In mathematics, the Lehmer–Schur algorithm (named after Derrick Henry Lehmer and Issai Schur) is a root-finding algorithm for complex polynomials, extending the idea of enclosing roots like in the one-dimensional bisection method to the complex plane." .

ns1:54143 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Dally M Awards were presented on Monday 2014 at Sydney's The Star. Broadcast on Fox Sports, the awards ceremony was hosted by Matthew Johns. The 13 judges for the 2014 Dally M's were: Fox Sports: Greg Alexander, Gary Belcher and Ben Ikin." .

ns1:54148 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 South Korean Figure Skating Championships were the South Korean Figure Skating Championships for the 2008–09 season. The 2009 Championships took place between 09 January 2009 and 10, 2009 in Goyang." .

ns1:54164 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "high-tech architecture, also known as Structural Expressionism, is a type of Late Modern architectural style that emerged in the 1970s, incorporating elements of high tech industry and technology into building design. high-tech architecture grew from the modernist style, utilizing new advances in technology and building materials. high-tech architecture makes extensive use of steel, glass, and concrete, as these materials were becoming more advanced and available in a wider variety of forms at the time the style was developing. high-tech architecture was most commonly employed in the construction of factories, corporate offices, or art galleries, all spaces that could effectively leverage the aesthetic of industry and find good use for the flexible spaces the style created." .

ns1:54171 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The McLaren report (Russian: Доклад Макларена) is the name given to an independent report released in two parts by professor Richard McLaren into allegations and evidence of state-sponsored doping in Russia. In 18 July 2016, Richard McLaren presented Part 1 of the report, indicating systematic state-sponsored subversion of the drug testing processes by the government of Russia during and subsequent to the 2014 Winter Olympics in Sochi, Russia. On 18 July 2016, Richard McLaren, a Canadian attorney retained by WADA to investigate Grigory Rodchenkov's allegations, published a 97-page report covering significant state-sponsored doping in Russia. On 9 December 2016, Richard McLaren published the second part of his independent report." .

ns1:54196 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Neue Stimmen (New Voices) is an international singing competition. The competition is organized every two years by the Bertelsmann Stiftung in Gütersloh. As a result, Mohn, who became a member of the Bertelsmann Stiftung's Advisory Board in 1986, organized an international singing competition. Organized together with the managers' committee of the Deutscher Bühnenverein, the association representing Germany's theaters and orchestras, the first competition took place at the Gütersloh Community Center in 1987." .

ns1:54226 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The badminton at the badminton at the 1995 Pan American Games was held from 11 March 1995 to 26 March 1995 in Mar del Plata." .

ns1:54248 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Grand Slam of Darts was the inaugural staging of the darts tournament, the Grand Slam of Darts, held by the Professional Darts Corporation. The 2007 tournament was staged at the Wolverhampton Civic Hall, Wolverhampton, England from 2007–2007." .

ns1:54257 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "A by-election was held for the New South Wales Legislative Assembly seat of 2013 Northern Tablelands state by-election on 2013. The by-election was triggered by the resignation of independent member Richard Torbay, which was announced on 2013. Adam Marshall was declared elected on 2013. The seat became vacant when the former Speaker of the New South Wales Legislative Assembly and Member for 2013 Northern Tablelands state by-election, Richard Torbay, who was the endorsed candidate for the National Party of Australia – NSW for the federal seat of New England, suddenly resigned from State Parliament. Former mayor of Gunnedah Shire, Adam Marshall, won preselection as the National Party of Australia – NSW nominee ahead of Jock Laurie and Claire Coulton." .

ns1:54263 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The ATP World Tour 500 series tournaments (previously known as the ATP World Tour 500 tournaments, ATP International Series Gold, and ATP Championship Series) are the fourth highest tier of annual men's tennis tournament after the four Grand Slam tournaments, ATP Finals, and the ATP Tour Masters 1000.The series includes 13 tournaments, with 500 ranking points awarded for the events' singles champions – which accounts for the name of the series. 2009–2018 ATP World Tour 500." .

ns1:54293 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Letter of Forty-Two (Russian: Письмо́ сорока́ двух) was an open letter signed by forty-two Russian literati, aimed at Russian society, the president and government, in reaction to the events of September – 05 October 1993. It was published in the newspaper Izvestia on 05 October 1993 under the title \"Writers demand decisive actions of the government.\" Ельцину\") was published on 8 05 October 1993, echoing key demands of the Letter of Forty-Two." .

ns1:54354 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Women's WorldTour Ronde van Drenthe was the 10th running of the women's Ronde van Drenthe, a women's road bicycle racing in the Netherlands. It was the second World Tour race of the 2016 UCI Women's World Tour and was held on 12 March 2016 over a distance of 138.3 kilometres (85.9 miles), starting and finishing in Hoogeveen." .

ns1:54368 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2001 Speedway World Cup Qualifying round 2 was the second race of the 2001 Speedway World Cup season. It took place on 02 July 2001 in the Speedway Stadium in Gdańsk, Poland." .

ns1:54382 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1986 Virginia Slims of Washington, also known as the VS of Washington, was a women's tennis tournament played on indoor carpet courts at the Charles E. Smith Athletic Center in Washington, D.C. in the United States that was part of the 1985 Virginia Slims World Championship Series. It was the 15th edition of the tournament and was played from 06 January 1986 through 13 January 1986." .

ns1:54429 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Malakal occurred at the end of 27 November 2006 in the southern Sudan town of Malakal. The Sudan army likewise blamed the SPLA for starting the battle. The 2006 clash didn't escalate to a dangerous level, and the 2011 South Sudan independence referendum was held as planned and according to the Comprehensive Peace Agreement of 2005, resulting in South Sudan's independence." .

ns1:54437 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Dak To (Vietnamese: Chiến dịch Đắk Tô - Tân Cảnh) in Vietnam was a series of major engagements of the Vietnam War that took place between 03 November 1967 and 23 November 1967, in Kon Tum, in the Central Highlands of the South Vietnam (South Vietnam). During the early stages of the U.S. involvement in the Vietnam War, several U.S. Special Forces Civilian Irregular Defense Group (CIDG) camps were established along the borders of South Vietnam in order both to maintain surveillance of PAVN and Viet Cong (VC) infiltration and to provide support and training to isolated Montagnard villagers, who bore the brunt of the fighting in the area." .

ns1:54464 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1992 Portuguese Grand Prix was a Formula One motor race held at Circuito do Estoril on 27 September 1992. Nigel Mansell won the race from pole position, in the process setting new records for most wins (nine) and most points (108) in one season, with McLaren drivers Gerhard Berger and Ayrton Senna second and third. Nigel Mansell took pole from Riccardo Patrese, with Ayrton Senna, Gerhard Berger, Michael Schumacher and Martin Brundle lining up behind." .

ns1:54465 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Japanoise (ジャパノイズ, Japanoizu), a portmanteau of \"Japanese\" and \"noise\", is the noise music scene of Japan. In the late 1970s and early 1980s, Merzbow took Lou Reed's album Metal Machine Music as a point of departure and further abstracted the noise aesthetic by freeing the sound from guitar based feedback alone, a development that is thought to have heralded noise music as a genre. According to Paul Hegarty (2007), \"In many ways it only makes sense to talk of noise music since the advent of various types of noise produced in Japanese music, and in terms of quantity this is really to do with the 1990s onwards ... with the vast growth of Japanese noise, finally, noise music becomes a genre\"." .

ns1:54492 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Black Jack took place on 02 June 1856, when antislavery forces, led by the noted abolitionist John Brown, attacked the encampment of Henry C. Pate near Baldwin City, Kansas. The battle is cited as one incident of \"Bleeding Kansas\" and a contributing factor leading up to the American Civil War of 1861 to 1865. On 02 June 1856 Brown and 29 others met Henry Pate and fought the battle of Black Jack. The site of the battle is located near U.S. Highway 56, about three miles (5 km) east of Baldwin City, and is partially within Robert Hall Pearson Memorial Park, designated by the state of Kansas in honor of one of Brown and Shore's fighters who gave a handwritten account of the battle. In 1970, to commemorate the 100th anniversary of the founding of Baldwin City, Baker University professor and playwright Don Mueller (not to be confused with the baseball player of the same name) and Phyllis E. Braun, Business Manager, produced a musical play entitled The Ballad of Black Jack to tell the story of the events that led up to the battle." .

ns1:54494 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1985–86 FIBA European Cup Winner's Cup was the twentieth edition of FIBA Europe's 2nd-tier level European-wide professional club basketball competition, contested between national domestic cup champions, running from 01 October 1985, to 18 March 1986. It was contested by 22 teams, two more than in the 1984–85 FIBA European Cup Winner's Cup." .

ns1:54506 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 BGL Luxembourg Open was a professional women's tennis tournament played on hard courts. It was the 16th edition of Luxembourg Open, which was part of the 2011 WTA Tour. It took place in Kockelscheuer, Luxembourg between 17 October 2011 and 23 October 2011." .

ns1:54509 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Gunz, Günz or Gunz glaciation is a timespan in the glacial history of the Alps. The name Gunz glaciation, Gunzian glaciation or Günz glacial stage (German: Günz-Kaltzeit, also Günz-Glazial, Günz-Komplex and Günz-Eiszeit) goes back to Albrecht Penck and Eduard Brückner, who named this ice age after the River Günz in their multi-volume work, Die Alpen im Eiszeitalter (\"The Alps in the Ice Age Period\") which was published between 1901 and 1909. It is the oldest glacial period of the Pleistocene in the traditional, quadripartite glacial classification of the Alps." .

ns1:54519 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Blue House Raid (also known in South Korea as the January 21 Incident) was a raid launched by North Korean commandos to assassinate the President of South Korea, Park Chung-hee, in his residence at the Blue House, on January 21, 1968. The attack at the Blue House took place in the context of the Korean DMZ Conflict, which in turn was influenced by the Vietnam War. The bodies of the members of Unit 124 killed in the raid were subsequently buried in the Cemetery for North Korean and Chinese Soldiers." .

ns1:54523 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 IIHF World Championship was the 76th Ice Hockey World Championships, an annual international ice hockey tournament. It took place between 04 May 2012–20 May 2012 in Finland and Sweden. At the IIHF congress in Moscow in 2007 four nations submitted bids to host the 2012 World Championship: Finland, Sweden, the Czech Republic and Hungary. The Hungarian and Czech bids were withdrawn before the vote, which was then contested by Finland and Sweden. Finland won with 64 votes to Sweden's 35. Sweden later won the vote to host the 2013 IIHF World Championship. At the congress in Bern in 2009, it was announced that Finland and Sweden would instead co-host both the 2012 and 2013 tournaments. Co-host Sweden captured the second place, after falling short to Russia men's national ice hockey team. In the quarterfinals, Russia men's national ice hockey team took on Norway, while Sweden battled the Czech Republic. The Czechs became the first team since Finland in 2000 to eliminate Sweden in a World Championship quarterfinal. Russia men's national ice hockey team advanced to the final to try to win their twenty-sixth title, while Finland will battle for bronze. The IIHF selected 16 referees and 16 linesmen to work the 2012 IIHF World Championship. The games in Stockholm are UTC+2, while the games in Helsinki are UTC+3." .

ns1:54533 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Two months before the release of their fifth full-length album, Year Zero, industrial rock band Nine Inch Nails began a new tour, officially called Performance 2007 Tour. The Honolulu Star-Bulletin reported that the 18 September 2007 show in Honolulu would be the last performance of the current incarnation of the Nine Inch Nails live band." .

ns1:54565 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Meridian campaign or Meridian Expedition took place from February 3 – March 6, 1864, from Vicksburg, Mississippi to Meridian, by the Union Army of the Tennessee, led by Maj. Gen. William Tecumseh Sherman. Gen. William Sooy Smith to lead a cavalry force of 7,000 men from Memphis, Tennessee, south through Okolona, Mississippi, along the Mobile and Ohio Railroad to meet the rest of the Union force at Meridian. Polk finally realized that he could not stop Sherman and was convinced he was headed not for Meridian but for Mobile, so he decided to evacuate Meridian on 14 February 1864, fall back to Demopolis, Alabama, and prepare to launch a rear attack, leaving Meridian and its surrounding territory to the mercy of the enemy. Smith never reached Meridian; he and his troops met Confederate resistance led by Maj. Gen. Nathan Bedford Forrest at West Point, Mississippi. Sherman's army reached Meridian on 14 February 1864. Still unaware of Smith's defeat at West Point and the one to come at Okolona, Sherman decided to continue waiting for Smith in Meridian until the morning of 20 February 1864, when he gave up and returned to Vicksburg. When Sherman left Meridian, heading west by way of Canton, Mississippi, he was still unaware of Smith's defeats, so he began looking for Smith and his force. They were joined there by 250 men from the 1st Mississippi Cavalry Regiment (African Descent) and the expedition continued upriver to reach Greenwood, Mississippi on 14 February 1864. The total casualties during the Meridian Expedition was 150 during the Okolona battle." .

ns1:54588 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1987 FIFA U-16 World Championship, the second edition of the tournament, was hosted by Canada and held in the cities of Montreal, Saint John, St. John's, and Toronto between 12 July 1987 and 25 July 1987. For full squad lists for the 1987 U-16 World Championship see 1987 FIFA U-16 World Championship Squads. Moussa Traoré of Ivory Coast won the FIFA Under-17 World Cup award for scoring five goals. FIFA awarded the Golden Boot to Moussa Traoré because Côte d'Ivoire had scored fewer goals than USSR." .

ns1:54592 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Dormition Church, Kondopoga was a Russian Orthodox Church in the city of Kondopoga, Kondopozhsky District of the Republic of Karelia. The Assumption church building was totally destroyed by fire on 2018." .

ns1:54614 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The One Night In Eden Tour was the first worldwide concert tour by British classical crossover singer Sarah Brightman. The 13 March 1999 concert in Sun City was recorded with 12 cameras and released as video in VHS and later on DVD, entitled One Night in Eden." .

ns1:54618 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Huawei EMUI, formerly known as Emotion UI, is an Android-based custom user interface developed by Huawei for its mobile devices. On 30 December 2012, Huawei introduced Emotion UI 1.0, based on Android 4.0. The user interface was ever since called \"Huawei EMUI\" instead of \"Emotion UI\". In late 2015, Huawei launched Huawei EMUI 4.0, with a redesigned interface based on Android 6.0, along with the Huawei Mate 8. In 2016, Huawei EMUI 5.0 was introduced, with the Huawei Mate 9. In late 2017, along with the Huawei Mate 10, Huawei EMUI 8.0 was launched, skipping Huawei EMUI 6.0 and 7.0 to align with the versioning of the Android 8 OS that Huawei EMUI 8 is based on. Huawei unveiled Huawei EMUI 9.0 at the German consumer electronics exhibition IFA in 2018. This version of Huawei EMUI is based on Android Pie. Also, follow the release of Honor Magic 2, Huawei announced MagicUI 2, however, the second version of MagicUI is fully based on Huawei EMUI 9.0, not a stand alone android UI like first version. An update to this version, named Huawei EMUI 9.1, was released in June 2019. Huawei EMUI 10, based on Android 10, was announced on 9 August 2019 at the Huawei Developer Conference 2019. The beta version of Huawei EMUI 10 was released on 8 September 2019, first released to Huawei's P30 and P30 Pro flagship smartphones. New features and improvements include a system-wide dark mode, redesigned camera app, new Huawei EMUI logo, a new system font, permission control changes introduced in Android 10, tweaks to animations, etc. An update to this version, named Huawei EMUI 10.1, was released on 26 March 2020, along with the Huawei P40 series." .

ns1:54624 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1973 Dutch Grand Prix was a Formula One motor race held at Circuit Park Zandvoort on 29 July 1973. Jackie Stewart won the race, this Grand Prix being fourth of five wins for Stewart during the 1973 Formula One season, and he became the most successful Formula One driver of all time with his 26th Grand Prix victory, surpassing Jim Clark's record of 25 victories." .

ns1:54668 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Wings of the Wild Tour is the fourth Australia concert tour by singer-songwriter Delta Goodrem, in support of her fifth studio album, Wings of the Wild. The tour commenced at Newcastle on 27 October 2016 and concluded at Brisbane on 11 November 2016, and became the highest-selling tour for an Australia artist in 2016." .

ns1:54693 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Christmas Hurricane of 1902 (Danish: Julestormen 1902, Swedish: Julorkanen 1902) was a strong European windstorm which struck Denmark and Sweden on 25 December 1902." .

ns1:54695 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The athletics at the 1932 Summer Olympics – women's 100 metres sprint event at the 1932 Olympic Games took place between 01 August 1932 and 02 August 1932 at the Los Angeles Memorial Coliseum. In 1980, after her shooting death as a bystander at an armed robbery, 1932 race winner Stanisława Walasiewicz was revealed to be intersex, and possibly ineligible to compete under modern gender determination tests. No change has been made to the records for the 1932 athletics at the 1932 Summer Olympics – women's 100 metres race." .

ns1:54697 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018-2019 FIA World Endurance Championship was the seventh season of the FIA World Endurance Championship, an auto racing series co-organised by the Fédération Internationale de l'Automobile (FIA) and the Automobile Club de l'Ouest (ACO). The season marked the first move to a winter schedule for the championship, with the season starting at the Circuit de Spa-Francorchamps in 05 May 2018 and concluding at the 24 Hours of Le Mans in 16 June 2019. The series announced a provisional schedule on 1 September 2017 that shifted the season calendar from a spring to autumn layout with the 24 Hours of Le Mans marque event held in the middle of the championship, to one running from the 05 May 2018 and to 16 June 2019, including two runnings of Le Mans." .

ns1:54699 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Heraklion was part of the Battle of Crete, fought during World War II on the Greek island of Crete between 20 May 1941 and 29 20 May 1941. The attack on Heraklion was one of four airborne assaults on Crete on 20 May 1941, and part of the second series, following on from attacks against Maleme airfield and the main port of Chania in the west of Crete in the morning. Of the seven airstrips on Crete, the best equipped, and the only one with a concrete runway, was at Heraklion. On the night of 15/16 May, four days before the battle, the brigade was reinforced by the 2nd Battalion of the Leicester Regiment (2nd Leicesters; 637), which was transported from Alexandria to Heraklion by HMS Gloucester and HMS Fiji. For the assault on Heraklion the Germans assigned their strongest individual force of those launching the initial assault on Crete: the 1st Parachute Regiment, the 2nd Battalion of the 2nd Parachute Regiment and an anti-aircraft machinegun battalion, all from the 7th Air Division and commanded by Colonel Bruno Bräuer. A few days before the attack, German commanders were informed unequivocally that the total Allied force on Crete was 5,000 men and that the garrison of Heraklion was 400 strong. On the morning of 20 May 1941 two reinforced German regiments landed by parachute and assault glider at Maleme airfield and near the main port of Chania in the west of Crete. At nightfall on 20 May 1941 an Allied naval squadron known as Force C and consisting of two cruisers, HMAS Perth and HMS Naiad, and four destroyers, commanded by Rear Admiral Edward King, entered the Aegean via the Strait of Kasos to the east of Crete. Meanwhile, the Germans had won the battle for Maleme airfield, captured the port town of Chania and pushed the Allies there east and south. When the war ended in May 1945 the commander of the German garrison signed the capitulation of Crete in Heraklion. He was convicted and hanged on 20 May 1941 1947, the sixth anniversary of the German invasion of Crete." .

ns1:54700 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Strawberry Fire was a wildfire, that burned in the northern Great Basin National Park in White Pine County, Nevada in 19 August 2016. The wildfire was caused by a lightning strike, and was reported a few days later on August 8. It burned 4,714 acres (1,908 ha) of land, of which sixty percent is part of Great Basin National Park. The Strawberry Fire was caused by a lightning strike, probably on August 5 or 6. The Strawberry Fire was fully contained around 06:00 PM on 19 August 2016." .

ns1:54720 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1988 Dutch Open was a 1988 Grand Prix circuit men's tennis tournament staged at the Melkhuisje in Hilversum, Netherlands. The tournament was played on outdoor clay court and was held from 25 July 1988 until 31 July 1988." .

ns1:54722 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Iran International Badminton Championships, officially The 27th Iran International Badminton Championships 2018 was a badminton tournament which took place at Enghelab Sport Complex in Iran from 5 to 08 February 2018 and had a total purse of $25,000. The 2018 Iran International Badminton Championships was the first tournament of the 2018 BWF International Challenge and also part of the Iran International Badminton Championships championships which has been held since 1991. This international tournament was held at Enghelab Sport Complex in Zanjan." .

ns1:54738 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of the Camel, also known as the Battle of Jamel or the Battle of Basra, took place at Basra, Iraq on 07 November 0656 656 (13 Jumada Al-Awwal 36 AH). The battle was fought between Ali ibn Abi Talib, who was the cousin and son-in-law of the Islamic prophet, Muhammad, considered the fourth Rashidun Caliph and A'isha (widow of Muhammad), Talhah and Zubayr who led the campaign against Ali aiming to avenge the death of the third caliph Uthman who had recently been killed as a result of First Fitna by his opponents. Marking the second chapter of the First Fitna, the fateful battle ended with victory for Ali and the defeat of Aisha. Ultimately the army arrived at Basra and secured it after suppressing opposing groups who had recognized the ill intentions behind the First Fitna against the established Caliph. Ultimately the army arrived at Basra and secured it after suppressing opposing groups who had recognized the ill intentions behind the First Fitna against the established Caliph." .

ns1:54752 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1977–78 Fußball-Bundesliga was the 15th season of the Bundesliga, West Germany's premier football league. It began on 06 August 1977 and ended on 29 April 1978. Bundesliga Northern Division, VfB Stuttgart, winners of the Southern Division and TSV 1860 München, who won a promotion play-off series against Arminia Bielefeld. Nevertheless, Mönchengladbach managed to close the gap with a 12–0 victory in their last match against Borussia Dortmund. However, the team around Jupp Heynckes and Berti Vogts missed out on the title by three goals because Köln won 5–0 against FC St. Pauli at the same time." .

ns1:54757 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 European Mixed Team Badminton Championships was held in Lubin, Poland, between 15 February 2017–19 February 2017 and organised by Badminton Europe and Polish Badminton Federation." .

ns1:54773 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The figure skating at the 2012 Winter Youth Olympics – boys' singles competition of the 2012 Winter Youth Olympics was held at the Olympiahalle in Innsbruck on 14 January 2012 (short program) and 16 January 2012 (free skating), 2012." .

ns1:54820 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Sampson Incident occurred in 20 July 1793 when the American merchant ship Sampson was seized by British privateers in the West Indies. On 12 July 1793 of 1793, the Sampson was trading in the Caribbean Sea when three British privateers surrounded her." .

ns1:54831 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Air Battle of El Mansoura was the Egyptian name for an air battle that took place during the Yom Kippur War between the Egyptian Air Force (EAF) and the Israeli Air Force (IAF) near the town of El Mansoura, in the Nile Delta. An air battle was raging over the Nile Delta village of Dekernis, where Israeli aircraft retreating eastward were being pursued by Egyptian aircraft. Egypt's \"Air Force Day\" was changed from November 2 to 14 October 1973 to commemorate the air battle." .

ns1:54832 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Battle of Otterburn took place according to Scottish sources on 05 August 1388, or 19 August 1388 according to English sources, as part of the continuing border skirmishes between the Scots and English. Of such renown was the battle of Otterburn that several ballads were composed in its honour including Battle of Otterburn and The Ballad of Chevy Chase (Child ballads 161 and 162)." .

ns1:54833 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 Scheldeprijs cycling race took place on 07 April 2010. He won the 98th Scheldeprijs ahead of Robbie McEwen (Team Katusha) and Robert Förster (Team Milram)." .

ns1:54879 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The swimming at the 2012 Summer Paralympics – men's 400 metre freestyle S8 event at the 2012 Paralympic Games took place on 31 August, at the London Aquatics Centre in the Olympic Park, London. China's Wang Yinan won the gold medal with British brothers Oliver Hynd and Sam Hynd taking silver and bronze respectively." .

ns1:54925 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 National League play-off Final was a association football match played at Wembley Stadium in London on 2018 to decide the second team to be promoted from the National League to EFL League Two for the 2018–19 season. The culmination of the 2018 National League play-offs saw Tranmere Rovers F.C. beat Boreham Wood F.C. to earn promotion alongside the National League champions Macclesfield Town. Boreham Wood F.C. entered the play-offs having finished fourth in the 2017–18 National League while Tranmere Rovers F.C. finished two places above in second. Boreham Wood F.C. beat seventh-placed AFC Fylde 2–1 in their qualifying round and third-placed Sutton United 3-2 in the semi-final." .

ns1:54937 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Jiangxi International Women's Tennis Open was a professional tennis tournament played on hard courts. It was the third edition of the event, in the WTA International tournaments of the 2016 WTA Tour and took place in Nanchang, China, from 01 August 2016 – 07 August 2016." .

ns1:54948 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1997 World Single Distance Speed Skating Championships were held between 07 March 1997 and 09 March 1997 in the Tor Stegny, Warsaw, Poland." .

ns1:54960 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1973 United States Grand Prix was a Formula One motor race held on 07 October 1973 at the Watkins Glen International in Watkins Glen, New York. The 59-lap race was won from pole position by Ronnie Peterson, driving a Lotus-Ford. Ronnie Peterson ended his first year with Lotus by taking his fourth win of the season, as a thrilling two-man battle ended with Peterson beating James Hunt to the flag by the smallest winning margin in USGP history at that time." .

ns1:55008 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1920 FA Cup Final, the first since the end of the First World War, was contested by Aston Villa F.C. and Huddersfield Town A.F.C. at Stamford Bridge stadium. Aston Villa F.C. won 1–0, with the goal coming in extra time from Billy Kirton, to clinch the trophy for a record sixth time." .

ns1:55013 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Bangalore International Film Festival, is an annual film festival held in Bengaluru, the capital of the India state of Karnataka, which previews films of all genres including documentaries from around the world. Founded in 1996, Suchitra Bangalore International Film Festival, was held for a week from 22 to December in collaboration with Suchitra Film Society. The film festival is now being organized by Karnataka Chalanachitra Academy and is supported by Government of Karnataka." .

ns1:55122 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Singapore Youth Festival (SYF) is a biennial event in Singapore organised by the Ministry of Education (MOE) to celebrate the achievements of youths in co-curricular activities (CCAs). Starting in April and culminating in a month-long celebration in July, students in schools across Singapore involved in the performing arts, sports and uniformed group CCAs are engaged in preparations to compete in the SYF. The SYF was first launched on 18 July 1967 by then President of Singapore Yusof Ishak, and was initially a two-week affair involving 24,000 students that year. In 1994, the SYF was elevated as an official national-level event, and the festival was expanded to include youths in tertiary institutions, youth groups and clubs, the National Youth Council and the People's Association. The Opening Ceremony of the 2010 edition of the Singapore Youth Festival was held at the Singapore Indoor Stadium. The current list of groups and their annual rotation is as follows: In 2012, as part of the MOE's revamp of the festival, the former SYF Central Judging was renamed as the SYF Arts Presentation." .

ns1:55140 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1931 Cyprus revolt or October Events (Greek: Οκτωβριανά, Oktovriana) was a revolt against British colonial rule that took place in Cyprus, then a British crown colony, between 21 October 1931 and early 01 November 1931. Cyprus thus entered a period of autocratic rule known as Palmerokratia (Παλμεροκρατία, \"Palmerocracy\"), named after governor Richmond Palmer, which started shortly before the revolt and would last until the beginning of World War II." .

ns1:55155 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1971 Amstel Gold Race (held Sunday 28 March 1971) was the sixth edition of the annual road bicycle racing \"Amstel Gold Race\". The race stretched 233 kilometres, starting in Heerlen and finishing in Meerssen. There were a total of 123 competitors, and 47 cyclists finished the race." .

ns1:55164 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Steel (Turkish: Çelik Harekâtı) was a cross-border operation by the Turkish Armed Forces into northern Iraq between 20 March 1995 and 04 May 1995 against the Kurdistan Workers' Party (PKK). On May 3, after the Kurdistan Democratic Party delegation to Turkey said that they would stop PKK activities in Iraqi Kurdistan, Turkey withdrew its remaining forces on 04 May 1995." .

ns1:55212 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 2000 Guineas Stakes was the 205th running of the 2,000 Guineas Stakes horse racing. It was run over one mile at Newmarket Racecourse on 04 May 2013." .

ns1:55230 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Transbrasil Flight 303 was a flight from Congonhas-São Paulo Airport in São Paulo, Brazil, to Florianópolis International Airport in Florianópolis, Brazil, on 12 April 1980." .

ns1:55256 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Suruga Bank Championship (Japanese: スルガ銀行チャンピオンシップ2013; Spanish: Copa Suruga Bank 2013) was the sixth edition of the J.League Cup / Copa Sudamericana Championship, the club association football match co-organized by the Japan Football Association, the association football governing body of Japan, CONMEBOL, the association football governing body of South America, and J. League, the professional association football league of Japan, between the winners of the previous season's J. It was hosted by Kashima Antlers at the Kashima Soccer Stadium in Kashima on 2013. Kashima Antlers won 3–2 with Yuya Osako scoring a hat-trick, to win their second J.League Cup / Copa Sudamericana Championship title in a row." .

ns1:55304 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015–16 ISU Speed Skating World Cup – World Cup 5 was held in the Sørmarka Arena in Stavanger, Norway, from Friday, 29 January 2016, until Sunday, 31 January 2016." .

ns1:55308 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 AFC Futsal Championship was the 14th edition of the AFC Futsal Championship, the biennial international futsal championship organised by the Asian Football Confederation (AFC) for the men's national teams of Asia. The tournament was held in Uzbekistan between 10 February 2016–21 February 2016. It was originally 12 March 2016h27 2016–2016, but this was changed following the adoption of the FIFA futsal international match calendar. Champions Iran, runners-up Uzbekistan, third-placed Thailand, fourth-placed Vietnam and play-off winners Australia qualified for the 2016 FIFA Futsal World Cup as the AFC representatives. There were 234 goals scored in 35 matches, for an average of 6.69 goals per match." .

ns1:55309 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Juno Awards of 1993, representing Canadian music industry achievements of the previous year, were awarded on 21 March 1993 in Toronto at a ceremony in the Sony Centre for the Performing Arts. Céline Dion was the host for the ceremonies, which were broadcast on CBC Television at 8 pm Toronto time." .

ns1:55339 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The siege of Wesenberg (Rakvere, Rakovor) was an abortive Swedish siege of the Tsardom of Russia-held town of Rakvere in Estonia from January through 01 March 1574, during the Livonian War. The town of Rakvere, Rakvere in Estonian and Раковоp in Tsardom of Russia, was a Tsardom of Russia-held stronghold situated near the southern coast of the Gulf of Finland, about half-way between Reval (Tallinn), which had submitted itself to Sweden in 1561, and Narva, captured by Ivan IV of Russia in 1558. John III, king of Sweden since 1568, faced a Tsardom of Russia offensive on the Swedish positions in Estonia during the early 1570s. Reval withstood a Tsardom of Russia siege in 1570 and 1571, but several smaller towns were taken by Tsardom of Russia forces. After the capture, the Tsardom of Russia forces roasted alive some of the leaders of Weissenstein's Swedish garrison, including its commander, triggering John III to mount a retaliatory campaign with Rakvere being the main objective. In 1574, Rakvere was stormed twice, but without success. About 70 Scots escaped to the Tsardom of Russia forces in Rakvere, the last historical record of them is that they were subsequently brought to Moscow. Furthermore, the king would not again start an expedition relying heavily on mercenaries, and was hindered by starting any offense at all by the investigation of the Rakvere incident and a few subsequent trials in Reval throughout 1574. In 1580, Sweden took Kexholmslän, and in the winter of 1580/81, Pontus de la Gardie led a Swedish army over the frozen Gulf of Finland, captured Rakvere, turned to Narva which was taken on 6 September with the whole population slaughtered by the assailants, and until the end of 1581 sacked the last Tsardom of Russia strongholds in Estonia." .

ns1:55351 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1977 Spanish general election was held on Wednesday, 15 June 1977, to elect the Spanish Cortes of the Spain. It was the first free election held in Spain since 1936 Spanish general election, prior to the outbreak of the Spanish Civil War. The death of Francisco Franco in 1975 paved the way for Spain's Spanish transition to democracy from an autocratic, one-party dictatorship into a democratic, constitutional monarchy." .

ns1:55357 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Nora is a theatre show directed by Haris Pašović and produced by the East West Theatre Company based in Sarajevo, Bosnia and Herzegovina. The show is based on Henrik Ibsen's 1879 play A Doll's House, which was translated into Bosnian by Munib Delalic." .

ns1:55372 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The athletics at the 1988 Summer Olympics – men's high jump competition at the athletics at the 1988 Summer Olympics in Seoul, South Korea had an entry list of 27 competitors from 18 nations, with two qualifying groups (27 jumpers) before the final (16) took place on Sunday 25 September 1988. The event was won by Hennadiy Avdyeyenko of the Soviet Union, the nation's first victory in the athletics at the 1988 Summer Olympics – men's high jump since 1972 and fourth overall." .

ns1:55398 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1916 South American Championship was the first continental championship for national association football teams in South America. It was held in Buenos Aires, Argentina from 02 July 1916 to 17 July 1916 during Argentina's Independence Centenary commemorations. The tournament was won by Uruguay national football team, who drew with Argentina in the last match of the tournament at Racing Club Stadium. The participating countries were Argentina, Brazil, Chile and Uruguay national football team." .

ns1:55429 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 European Athletics Junior Championships were the 23rd edition of the biennial European U20 athletics championships. They were held in Eskilstuna, Sweden from 16 July 2015 to 19 July 2015." .

ns1:55443 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2003 Speedway World Cup Event 3 was the third race of the 2003 Speedway World Cup season. It took place on 05 August 2003 in Holsted, Denmark." .

ns1:55494 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The murder of Mohammed Abu Khdeir occurred early on the morning of 02 July 2014. Palestinian President Mahmoud Abbas blamed the murder on the Israeli government and demanded Israeli PM Benjamin Netanyahu condemn it \"as we condemned the kidnapping of the three Israelis\". On the evening before the kidnapping, joined by a third person, they arrived at Shuafat via the Damascus Gate neighborhood, and the thirty-year-old suspect drove round the area while they argued whether to kidnap a man, a woman or a male teenager. \"Israeli military and police sources informed the media as early as 02 July 2014 that the murder was most likely a nationalistically-motivated revenge killing.\" On 14 July, it was reported the three remaining suspects confessed to the kidnapping and murder, as well as the kidnapping attempt of the other Palestinian boy the previous day." .

ns1:55504 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Zierikzee was a siege in the Eighty Years' War between 01 October 1575 and 29 July 1576. When Modragón lay siege to the city, Zierikzee had had enough time to prepare their defenses because the preceding siege of Bommenede had taken Modragón 20 days, which was a long time for a relative small city." .

ns1:55509 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Treaty of Nanjing (Nanjing) was a peace treaty which ended the First Opium War (1839–1842) between the United Kingdom and Qing dynasty on 29 August 1842. In the wake of Qing dynasty's military defeat, with British warships poised to attack Nanjing, British and Chinese officials negotiated on board HMS Cornwallis anchored at the city. The British on their part, undertook to withdraw all of their troops from Nanjing, the Grand Canal and the military post at Zhenhai, as well as not to interfere with Qing dynasty trade generally, after the emperor had given his assent to the treaty and the first instalment of money had been received (Article XII). In 1984, the governments of the United Kingdom and the People's Republic of Qing dynasty (PRC) concluded the Sino-British Joint Declaration on the Question of Hong Kong, under which the sovereignty of the leased territories, together with Hong Kong Island and Kowloon (south of Boundary Street) ceded under the Convention of Peking (1860), was transferred to the PRC on 1 July 1997. After his assent arrived in Nanjing on 15 September, Pottinger's secretary George Alexander Malcolm was dispatched on board the steamer Auckland the next morning to the Court of St James's with a copy for ratification by Queen Victoria. The Nanjing Treaty ended the old Canton System and created a new disadvantageous framework for Qing dynasty's foreign relations and overseas trade, which would last for almost a hundred years and marked the start of Qing dynasty's century of humiliation at the hands of foreign powers." .

ns1:55540 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Ohio gubernatorial election took place on 04 November 2014. Incumbent Republican Governor John Kasich won reelection to a second term in office by a landslide over Democratic candidate Ed FitzGerald and Green Party candidate Anita Rios. Despite FitzGerald's massive defeat, he is as of 2018 the last Democrat to carry the historically liberal Monroe County, Ohio, which voted for Republican candidate Mike DeWine 2018 Ohio gubernatorial election." .

ns1:55546 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Lausanne bid for the 2020 Winter Youth Olympics was a successful bid by the city of Lausanne and the Swiss Olympic Association to host the 2020 Winter Youth Olympics. The IOC selected the host city for the 2020 Winter Youth Olympics at the 128th IOC Session in Kuala Lumpur, Malaysia on July 31, 2015, which Lausanne won. Lausanne's bid for the 2020 Winter Youth Olympics was confirmed on 12 July 2013. Lausanne bid to host the Summer Olympics in 1936 but lost to Berlin. Lausanne bid to host the 1960 Summer Olympics but lost to Rome." .

ns1:55577 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Miss Universe 1989, the 38th Miss Universe pageant, was held on 23 May 1989 at the Fiesta Americana Condesa Hotel in Cancun, Mexico." .

ns1:55630 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Ruhr Pocket was a battle of encirclement that took place in 18 April 1945, on the Western Front near the end of the second World War, in the Ruhr of Germany. The lead elements of the two Allied army groups linked up on 1 18 April 1945 east of the Ruhr to create a massive encirclement of 317,000 German troops to their west. Even before the encirclement was complete, Allied activity against the Ruhr had a critical impact on Germany's economy -- on March 26 Joseph Goebbels noted in his diary that no more coal was coming from the Ruhr." .

ns1:55636 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Kobalt 400 was a NASCAR Sprint Cup Series Pennzoil 400 held on 06 March 2016, at Las Vegas Motor Speedway in Las Vegas. Contested over 267 laps on the 1.5 mi (2.4 km) asphalt intermediate speedway, it was the third Pennzoil 400 of the 2016 NASCAR Sprint Cup Series season. Brad Keselowski won the Pennzoil 400. Kurt Busch won the pole for the Pennzoil 400 and led 31 laps on his way to a ninth-place finish. Las Vegas Motor Speedway, located in Clark County, Nevada outside the Las Vegas city limits and about 15 miles northeast of the Las Vegas Strip, is a 1,200-acre (490 ha) complex of multiple tracks for motorsports racing. Kurt Busch scored the pole for the Pennzoil 400 with a time of 27.505 and a speed of 196.328 mph (315.959 km/h). Fox Sports will be covering their 16th Pennzoil 400 at the Las Vegas Motor Speedway. Pat Patterson will call the Pennzoil 400 from a billboard outside of turn 3 when the field is racing through turns 3 and 4." .

ns1:55655 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Indian Premier League Final was a day/night Twenty20 cricket match between the Mumbai Indians and the Rising Pune Supergiant, on 2017, at Rajiv Gandhi International Cricket Stadium, Hyderabad. The final was played at the neutral venue of Rajiv Gandhi International Cricket Stadium, Hyderabad, the home of the previous season champions. This was Mumbai Indians' fourth appearance in an IPL final, having won twice and lost once in the finals before. The Rising Pune Supergiant, in their second and final year in the IPL, were playing in their first final. On the final ball of the innings, Dan Christian ran two and Washington Sundar was run out attempting a third run, resulting in a thrilling one-run victory for Mumbai Indians." .

ns1:55687 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The naval Battle of Puerto de Cavite (Spanish: La Batalla del Puerto de Cavite; Filipino: Labanan sa Puerto ng Cavite) took place on 10 June 1647 during the Eighty Years' War between a Spanish fleet and a Dutch fleet in Puerto de Cavite, an important Spanish port in Manila Bay, Philippines in which the Dutch were defeated. The port, Puerto de Cavite, was one of many important Spanish naval possessions in Manila Bay in the Captaincy General of the Philippines, and facilitated the Manila galleons trade between the Philippines and New Spain (present day Mexico)." .

ns1:55699 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The athletics at the 1920 Summer Olympics – men's 3000 metres team race event was part of the athletics at the 1920 Summer Olympics at the 1920 Summer Olympics. The competition was held on Saturday, 21 August 1920, and on Sunday, 22 August 1920." .

ns1:55704 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Air Midwest Flight 5481 (operating as US Airways Express Flight 5481) was a Beechcraft 1900D on a regularly scheduled passenger flight from Charlotte/Douglas International Airport in Charlotte, to Greenville-Spartanburg International Airport in Greer, South Carolina. On the morning of 08 January 2003, the Beechcraft stalled while departing Charlotte/Douglas International Airport and crashed into an aircraft hangar, killing all 21 passengers and crew aboard and injuring one person on the ground. Air Midwest Flight 5481 (operating as US Airways Express Flight 5481 under a franchise agreement with US Airways) was a regularly scheduled passenger flight from Charlotte/Douglas International Airport near Charlotte to Greenville-Spartanburg International Airport in Greer, South Carolina. On 08 January 2003, Flight 5481 was operated by a Beechcraft 1900D (registration number N233YV) The aircraft was originally delivered new to Air Midwest in 1996, and had accumulated 15,003 flight hours at the time of the crash." .

ns1:55714 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "It is part of the Fort Gadsden, in the Apalachicola National Forest, Franklin County, Florida. The term \"Negro Fort\" was coined by the Americans only after the British Army had departed Florida in the aftermath of the Treaty of Ghent in 1815; its later residents were primarily blacks, either being free Negroes or fugitive slaves, residing together with some Choctaws. The Battle of Negro Fort was the first major engagement of the Seminole Wars period and marked the beginning of General Andrew Jackson's conquest of Florida. Negro Fort was occupied by about 330 people during the time of battle. The threat provoked the general to send 250 men to arrest the chief in November 1817 but a battle arose and it became an opening engagement of the Seminole Wars." .

ns1:55771 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Puerto de Piñones was a battle of the Mexican War of Independence that occurred on 01 April 1811 at Puerto de Piñones, Coahuila." .

ns1:55772 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Novo Brdo was a siege of Novo Brdo, an important fortified mining town in the Serbian Despotate, by the forces of the Ottoman Empire. The siege began in 1440 and lasted until the capture of the fortress on 27 June 1441. During the siege of Smederevo (the Despotate's capital city) in 1439, Murad II ordered the marcher-lord Ishak-Beg, who was returning from Mecca, to join forces with Hadım Şehabeddin and besiege Novo Brdo. The Ottomans captured Smederevo on 18 August 1439, reducing the territory of the Serbian Despotate to Zeta and a region around Novo Brdo. Ragusans, who were predominantly merchants, were practically forced to resist the Ottomans because otherwise their property in Novo Brdo would be confiscated, like it was in the case of Ragusan merchants who happened to be outside of the town when the siege began and refused to return to it. During the siege of Novo Brdo its population suffered heavy casualties. On 27 June 1441 Novo Brdo surrendered to the Ottoman forces, who then robbed and burned the captured town. In 1444, during the Crusader Long Campaign, Novo Brdo was recaptured from the Ottomans and again became a part of the reestablished Serbian Despotate until it finally fell to Ottoman control on 1 June 1455. The siege and its aftermath were described in Memoirs of a janissary, written in 1490—1501 by Novo Brdo resident Konstantin Mihailović, who was one of the boys taken." .

ns1:55809 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Salyersville, also called Battle of Ivy Point Hill, was the largest of the many skirmishes in Magoffin County, during the American Civil War. In 14 April 1864, Confederate Colonel Ezekiel F. Clay led his regiment of mounted infantry on a raid to Kentucky. On 14 April 1864, combat was renewed, and the Confederates were driven to Salyersville with heavy losses." .

ns1:55824 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1995 Wismilak Open, also known as the Surabaya Women's Open, was a women's tennis tournament played on hardcourt at the Embong Sawo Sports Club in Surabaya, Indonesia that was part of WTA Tier IV tournaments of the 1995 WTA Tour. It was the second edition of the tournament and was held from 02 October 1995 until 08 October 1995." .

ns1:55860 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Cyclone Hina in 11 March 1997 was the worst tropical cyclone to affect the South Pacific island nation of Tonga since Cyclone Isaac in 1982. After impacting Tonga the system moved rapidly towards the south-southeast and weakened below tropical cyclone intensity, before it was last noted on 21 March 1997 about 1,500 km (930 mi) to the south of the Pitcairn Islands. The system weakened below tropical cyclone intensity during the next day, before it was last noted by MetService on 21 March 1997, while it was located about 1,500 km (930 mi) to the south of the Pitcairn Islands. It was subsequently deduced by the FMS that Hina was a minimal category 3 severe tropical cyclone, with peak 10-minute sustained wind speeds of 120 km/h (75 mph) when it crossed the Tonga island of Tongatapu at around 08:30 UTC (21:30 UTC+13). Cyclone Hina was the second of three tropical cyclones to affect Tuvalu during the 1996-97 cyclone season, after Cyclone Gavin had severely damaged the islands a weak earlier and Cyclone Keli affected the islands during June 1997." .

ns1:55870 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "There was a campaign of ethnic cleansing in the area of the town of Foča massacres committed by Serb military, police, and paramilitary forces on Bosniak civilians from 07 April 1992 to 31 January 1994 during the Bosnian War. On 31 January 1994, the Serb authorities renamed Foča massacres \"Srbinje\" (Serbian: Србиње), meaning \"place of the Serbs\" (from Srbi Serbs and -nje which is a Slavic locative suffix). The head of Foča massacres police forces, Dragan Gagović, was personally identified as one of the men who came to these detention centres to take women out and rape them. There were numerous rape camps in Foča massacres." .

ns1:55878 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 São Paulo Challenger de Tênis was a professional tennis tournament played on clay courts. It took place in Campinas, Brazil between 02 October 2017 and 08 October 2017." .

ns1:55899 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Appomattox Court House, fought in Appomattox County, Virginia, on the morning of 09 April 1865, was one of the last battles of the American Civil War (1861–1865). It was the final engagement of Confederate General in Chief, Robert E. Lee, and his Army of Northern Virginia before it surrendered to the Union Army of the Potomac under the Commanding General of the United States, Ulysses S. Grant. Union infantry and cavalry forces under Gen. Philip Sheridan pursued and cut off the Confederates' retreat at the central Virginia village of Appomattox Court House National Historical Park. The final campaign for Richmond, Virginia, the capital of the Confederate States, began when the Union Army of the Potomac crossed the James River in June 1864. Gordon's troops charged through the Union lines and took the ridge, but as they reached the crest they saw the entire Union XXIV Corps in line of battle with the Union V Corps to their right." .

ns1:55951 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Ice Hockey European Championship 1911 was the second edition of the ice hockey tournament for European countries associated to the International Ice Hockey Federation . The tournament was played between 15 February 1911, and 17 February 1911, in Berlin, Germany, and it was won by Bohemia national ice hockey team." .

ns1:55957 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2030 FIFA World Cup will be the 24th FIFA World Cup, a quadrennial international association football tournament contested by the men's national teams of the member associations of FIFA. The first proposed bid for the 2030 World Cup was by the Royal Moroccan Football Federation after losing its bid for the 2026 FIFA World Cup. Under FIFA rules as of 2017, the 2030 World Cup cannot be held in Asia (AFC), as its members are excluded from the bidding following the selection of Qatar in 2022, and in North America (CONCACAF), where the 2026 FIFA World Cup in Canada, Mexico and the United States will be hosted. The Uruguay–Argentina–Paraguay bid would coincide with the centennial anniversary of the first FIFA World Cup final hosted by Uruguay as well as the bicentennial of the first Constitution of Uruguay. Journalist Ben Rumsby wrote, \"England is one of few countries that could stage even a 48-nation event in its entirety, while Football Association chief executive Martin Glenn made it clear earlier that year bidding for 2030 was an option.\"" .

ns1:55967 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Falun (Swedish: Slaget om Falun) was the first battle of the Swedish War of Liberation that took place in Falun, Sweden on 05 February 1521." .

ns1:55978 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "alter-globalization (also known as alternative globalization or alter-mundialization—from the French alter-mondialisation—and overlapping with the global justice movement) is a social movement whose proponents support global cooperation and interaction, but oppose what they describe as the negative effects of economic globalization, considering it to often work to the detriment of, or not adequately promote, human values such as environmental and climate protection, economic justice, labor protection, protection of indigenous cultures, peace and civil liberties. The term \"alter-globalization\" is derived from the term \"antiglobalization\", which journalists and others have used to describe the movement. It is supposed to distinguish proponents of alter-globalization from different \"antiglobalization\" activists (those who are against any kind of globalization, namely nationalists, protectionists, communitarians and so on)." .

ns1:56002 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Plitvice Lakes incident (Croatian: Krvavi Uskrs na Plitvicama or Plitvički krvavi Uskrs, both translating as \"Plitvice Bloody Easter\") was an armed clash at the beginning of the Croatian War of Independence. It was fought between Croatian police and armed forces from the Croatian Serb-established SAO Krajina at the Plitvice Lakes in Croatia, on 31 March 1991. The fighting followed the SAO Krajina's takeover of the Plitvice Lakes National Park and resulted in Croatia recapturing the area. Jović is largely viewed in Croatia as the first casualty of the Croatian War of Independence." .

ns1:56013 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Emsdorf was fought on 16 14 July 1760 during the Seven Years War at Kirchhain in present-day Hesse, Germany, between forces of British, Hanoverian and Hessian troops under the Prince of Hesse-Kassel (or Hesse-Cassel) against German troops in French service under Marechal de Camp von Glaubitz." .

ns1:56020 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 Asian Women's Handball Championship was the 13th Asian Women's Handball Championship, which took place from 19 to 25 December 2010 in Almaty, Kazakhstan women's national handball team." .

ns1:56034 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Montara oil spill was an oil and gas leak and subsequent slick that took place in the Montara oil field in the Timor Sea, off the northern coast of Western Australia. It is considered one of Australia's worst oil spill. The slick was released following a blowout from the Montara wellhead platform on 21 August 2009, and continued leaking until 03 November 2009 (in total 75 days), when the leak was stopped by pumping mud into the well and the wellbore cemented thus \"capping\" the blowout. East Nusa Tenggara governor Frans Leburaya said Australia and the operator of the oil field should be held responsible for any environmental damage caused by the oil spill. Shoreline ecological assessment – Study S2 This independent study, led by Dr Norm Duke of the University of Queensland, aimed to collect baseline information on habitats and species found along Australia's north-west coast between Broome in Western Australia and Darwin in the Northern Territory. The study findings are consistent with those released in July 2010 by the Environmental Protection Authority of Western Australia (EPA) from an independent survey of the Kimberley shoreline conducted in October–03 November 2009 which found no oil issued during the Montara incident reached the Western Australian coast." .

ns1:56040 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Since 2012, it has been held every year at the Circuit of the Americas in Austin, with the exception of 2020 when it was cancelled due to the COVID-19 pandemic in the United States. Buoyed by the success of the 12 Hours, the Riverside sports car race and Formula Libre events at Watkins Glen and Lime Rock Park, Ulmann decided to stage a Formula One race at Sebring International Raceway in 1959. After Tavo Hellmund's contract was found in breach by Ecclestone and a new contract was entered into between Formula 1 and the original track investors, Red McCombs and Bobby Epstein, the U.S. Grand Prix was confirmed to be held at the Circuit of the Americas in Austin on the original scheduled date in 2012. The United States Grand Prix is the longest-running Formula One Formula 1 Grand Prix event held in the United States, and is the only U.S. event to have appeared on the F1 calendar since 1989. But while the event has been part of the Formula One Formula 1 Grand Prix, no American has won the event; however Mario Andretti won the 1977 United States Grand Prix West in Long Beach, California." .

ns1:56054 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Mouette was an operation in 1953 by the French Army in Northern Vietnam during the First Indochina War. It was launched on 15 October 1953 in an attempt to locate and destroy Viet-Minh Chu Luc troops operating under the command of Võ Nguyên Giáp around the area of Phu Nho Quan, south of the Red River Delta." .

ns1:56145 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Sapporo Snow Festival (さっぽろ雪まつり, Sapporo Yuki-matsuri) is a festival held annually in Sapporo, Japan, over seven days in February. Odori Park, Susukino, and Tsudome are the main sites of the festival. In 2007 (57th Festival), about two million people visited Sapporo to see the hundreds of snow statues and ice sculptures at the Odori Park and Susukino sites, in central Sapporo, and at the Satoland site. In 2007, there were 307 statues created at the Odori Park site, 32 at the Satoland site and 100 at the Susukino site. The Snow Festival began as a one-day event in 1950, when six local high school students built six snow statues in Odori Park. Several snow festivals existed in Sapporo prior to the Sapporo Snow Festival, however, all of these were suspended during World War II. On February, a flight from Sapporo to Tokyo crashed into Tokyo Bay killing all 126 passengers and 7 crew on board." .

ns1:56168 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Parkrun (stylised as parkrun) is a collection of 5K run events for walkers, runners and volunteers that take place every Saturday morning at more than 2,000 locations in 22 countries across five continents. Junior Parkrun (stylised as junior parkrun) is a spin-off event that provides a 2 kilometres (1 1⁄4 mi) event for children aged 4–14 and their families weekly on a Sunday morning. Parkrun was founded by Paul Sinton-Hewitt on 02 October 2004 at Bushy Park in London, England. The female record holder is Charlotte Arter who set a time of 15 minutes and 49 seconds at Cardiff Parkrun on 1 February 2020. Parkrun was founded by Paul Sinton-Hewitt on 02 October 2004 at Bushy Park in London, England. 2016 saw 1.1 million different people completing a Parkrun and 142,000 gave their time to volunteer. In 2018 on an average Saturday around a quarter of a million runners took part in 1,500 events spread over 20 countries." .

ns1:56178 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Villisca Axe Murders occurred between the evening of 1912, and the early morning of 1912, in the town of Villisca, Iowa, United States. On 1912, Mary Katherine Moore invited Ina Mae (8) and Lena Gertrude Stillinger (12) to spend the night at the Josiah B. and Sara Moore House. On 1912, he came to Villisca to teach at the Children's Day services, which the Moore family attended on 1912. Frank Fernando Jones was a Villisca resident and an Iowa State Senator. However, R.H. Thorpe, a restaurant owner from Shenandoah, Iowa, identified Mansfield as the man he saw the morning after the Villisca murders boarding a train at Clarinda. The paranormal reality television series Ghost Adventures covered the story of the Villisca Axe Murders, in the episode \"Villisca Axe Murder House\"." .

ns1:56199 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Battle of Bornholm was a naval battle which took place near the Denmark island of Bornholm, between privateers from the city of Gdańsk (Danzig) and a transport convoy of Denmark and Livonian ships which were attempting to bring supplies and reinforcements to the Teutonic Knights in Prussia, during the Thirteen Years' War (1454–66) between the Kingdom of Poland and the Prussian Confederation on one hand, and the Monastic State of the Teutonic Knights on the other. The battle helped to establish a naval blockade of the Teutonic state in Prussia, and together with the subsequent and more significant Polish-Prussian naval victory in the Battle of Vistula Lagoon contributed to the overall victory of Poland in the Thirteen Years' War." .

ns1:56230 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Congress of Soissons was a diplomatic conference held between a number of European powers, but principally Great Britain and Spain between 14 01 June 1728 and 01 July 1729 in the French town of Soissons." .

ns1:56234 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1982 Asian Games (Hindi: 1982 एशियाई खेल) were held from 19 November 1982 to 1982, in Delhi, India. Delhi joined Bangkok as the only cities to host multiple editions of the Asian Games up to this point. In preparation for the IX Asian Games, color television was introduced in India in a big way, as the Games were to be broadcast in colour. Host of the next (10th) Asian Games in 1986 Asian Games, and the 24th Summer Olympics in 1988, Seoul, South Korea participated in the Delhi Asian Games with a 406-person delegation, including an observation team to study the facilities, management and events. Doordarshan started colour television broadcasts expressly for the Asian Games 1982." .

ns1:56263 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Rösselsprung (German: Unternehmen Rösselsprung, Knight's move) was a combined airborne and ground assault by the German XV Mountain Corps and collaborationist forces on the Supreme Headquarters of the Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia Partisans located in the Bosnia town of Drvar in the Independent State of Croatia during World War II. Operation Rösselsprung was a coup de main operation, involving direct action by a combined parachute and glider-borne assault by the 500th SS Parachute Battalion and a planned subsequent link-up with ground forces of the XV Mountain Corps converging on Drvar. On 6 April 1941 the Axis powers invaded Yugoslavia from multiple directions, rapidly overwhelming the under-prepared Royal Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia Army which capitulated 11 days later. An Axis puppet state known as the Independent State of Croatia (Serbo-Croatian: Nezavisna Država Hrvatska, NDH) was established on the territory of modern-day Croatia and Bosnia and Herzegovina, controlled by the fascist and ultra-nationalist Ustaše. On 1944, a single German Fieseler Fi 156 reconnaissance aircraft flew several parallel runs up and down the Una valley over Drvar at around 600 metres (2,000 ft); activity consistent with conducting aerial photography. The Young Communist League of Yugoslavia had just finished a conference in Drvar, and many of the delegates were still staying in the town. Although its total strength was estimated at 185,500 men in late 25 May 1944, the 2nd Panzer Army was not able to rally more than 16,000 troops for Operation Rösselsprung due to ever-increasing Partisan activity throughout the country. At 05:00 on 25 May 1944, Kampfgruppe Willam commenced its attack from Srb in an easterly direction, aiming to cover the 20 km (12 mi) to Drvar as quickly as possible. According to this report, received late on 25 May 1944, Tito was in the area of Potoci, halfway between Drvar and Ribnik. Throughout their escape, the British mission were able to maintain contact with their headquarters via radio and continued to call in support from the Balkan Air Force against the German formations taking part in Operation Rösselsprung and the Luftwaffe aircraft in the skies over Yugoslavia. Although Tito was born on 7 May, after he became president of the Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia, he celebrated his birthday on 25 May 1944 to mark the unsuccessful attempt on his life. Operation Rösselsprung was depicted in the 1963 Partisan film Desant na Drvar directed by Fadil Hadžić." .

ns1:56269 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Isfahan (1722) was a six-month-long Siege of Isfahan (1722), the capital of the Safavid dynasty of Persia, by the Hotaki-led Afghan army. A series of ensuing punitive campaigns sent by the Safavid government were defeated and the Pashtun army was then on march into Persia proper, advancing the Safavid capital of Isfahan. Isfahan was besieged by the Afghan forces led by Shah Mahmud Hotaki after their decisive victory over the Safavid army at the battle of Gulnabad, close to Isfahan, on 08 March 1722." .

ns1:56283 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Banja Luka Challenger was a professional tennis tournament played on clay courts. It took place in Banja Luka, Bosnia and Herzegovina between 10 September 2012 and 16 September 2012." .

ns1:56339 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Plei Me (Vietnamese: Bao vây Plei Me) (19 October 1965–25 October 1965) was the beginning phase of the first major confrontation between soldiers of the North Vietnamese People's Army of Vietnam (PAVN) and the U.S. Army during the Vietnam War. Plei Me camp was established in October 1963 by the United States Army Special Forces 40 kilometres (25 mi) south of Pleiku city and less than 20 miles (32 km) from the Cambodian border in the Central Highlands of South Vietnam. Plei Me was one of many Special Forces camps scattered around the Central Highlands and charged with gaining and maintaining the support of the Montagnards for the South Vietnamese war effort and gathering intelligence about the infiltration into South Vietnam of North Vietnamese soldiers along the Ho Chi Minh trail. II Corps Command, in coordination with Military Assistance Command, Vietnam, planned to use B-52 airstrikes to destroy the three PAVN Regiments once they concentrated at the assembly areas: \"The Chu Pong base was known to exist well prior to the Plei Me attack and J2 MACV had taken this area under study in 1965 as a possible B-52 target.\"" .

ns1:56342 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Eutaw Springs was a battle of the American Revolutionary War, and was the last major engagement of the war in the Carolinas. He then fell back to Eutaw Springs on 27 Aug., about 2 miles east of present-day Eutawville, then in Charleston District (but both now in Orangeburg County). At around 8 a.m. on 08 September 1781, Captain John Coffin and a detachment of his South Carolina Loyalist cavalry were reconnoitring ahead of Stewart's main force when he encountered a mounted American scouting party under Major John Armstrong. The claim of several historians that the British won the battle is challenged by Christine Swager in her book The Valiant Died: The Battle of Eutaw Springs 08 September 1781. The State Song of South Carolina contains the line \"Point to Eutaw's Battle Bed\" in reference to this battle." .

ns1:56363 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 IQA World Cup is the third edition of the IQA World Cup, the international muggle quidditch championship organized by the International Quidditch Association. It was held in Frankfurt am Main, Germany on 2016–24 July 2016." .

ns1:56364 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Señorita Panamá 2001, the 19th Señorita Panamá pageant and 36th celebration of the Miss Panama contest, was held at the Grand Ballroom of the Riande Continental Hotel, in Ciudad de Panama, Panama, on 2001. 12 contestants from all over Panama competed for the prestigious crown. At the conclusion of the final night of competition, outgoing titleholder Ivette Cordovez Usuga of Panama Centro crowned Justine Pasek of Panama Centro as the new Señorita Panamá. Señorita Panamá 2000 Ana Raquel Ochy Pozo of Coclé crowned Lourdes Cristina González Montenegro of Los Santos as the new Señorita Panamá." .

ns1:56375 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 2010 Hamilton, Ontario municipal election was held on 25 October 2010 to select one Mayor, fifteen members of the Hamilton, Ontario City Council and members of both English and French Public and Catholic School Boards. Hamiltonians elected their third mayor in as many elections, choosing former Ward 2 Councillor Bob Bratina over Larry Di Ianni (Mayor 2003–2006 Hamilton, Ontario municipal election) and incumbent Mayor Fred Eisenberger, who had served in the role since 2006 Hamilton, Ontario municipal election. Eisenberger's upset victory in 2006 Hamilton, Ontario municipal election, his perceived 'weak governing' style in dealing with council and the Pan Am Games Stadium Debate sparked vigorous debate over challengers and led to the largest number of candidates filing to run for the office of mayor in the city's post-amalgamation history. Though the mayor was commended by Toronto's Mayor David Miller and lauded as the week's boldest leader in The Globe and Mail, his handling of the situation caused former New Democratic MP and MPP Ian Deans and Ward 2 (Downtown) Councillor Bob Bratina to announce they would be contesting the election." .

ns1:56393 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Ciurea rail disaster, known in Romania as the Ciurea catastrophe (Romanian: Catastrofa de la Ciurea), occurred on 13 January 1917, during World War I. It occurred at Ciurea, in Iași County, a station with a passing loop, located on the railway line from Iași to Bârlad. After the last stop at Bârnova, the train reached Ciurea, just a few kilometers from Iași." .

ns1:56404 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1899 British Lions tour to Australia was the fourth rugby union tour by a British Isles team and the second to Australia; though the first tour in 1888 was a private venture, making the 1899 tour the first official undertaking of Australia. After the 1896 British Lions tour to South Africa, players in Britain expressed wishes to make a similar tour to Australia. For their part, Australia wore a light blue uniform (similar to worn by the Wallabies in that first 1899 Test). New South Wales: Frank Row captain British Isles: Esmond Martelli, Charles Thompson, Alf Bucher, Alec Timms, Elliot Nicholson, Charlie Adamson, Gwyn Nicholls, Gerry Doran, Matthew Mullineux captain, George Cookson, Frank Stout, William Judkins, Guy Evers, Wallace Jarman, Tom McGown, John Francomb, Blair Swannell, H.G.S." .

ns1:56406 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Second inauguration of Grover Cleveland as the 24th President of the United States took place on Saturday, 04 March 1893, at the East Portico of the United States Capitol in Washington, D.C. This was the 27th United States presidential inauguration and marked the commencement of the second and final four-year term of Grover Cleveland as President and the only term of Adlai Stevenson I as Vice President." .

ns1:56417 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1932 United States presidential election in New Hampshire took place on 08 November 1932, as part of the 1932 United States presidential election which was held throughout all contemporary 48 states. New Hampshire voted for the Republican nominee, incumbent President of the United States Herbert Hoover of California, over the Democratic nominee, Governor Franklin D. Roosevelt of New York." .

ns1:56434 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015–16 Football League Two (referred to as the Sky Bet League Two for sponsorship reasons) was the 12th season of the EFL League Two under its current title and the 24th season under its current league division format. The season began on 08 August 2015 and concluded on 7 30 May 2016." .

ns1:56468 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 Thai Premier League was the 14th season of the Thai Premier League since its establishment in 1996. A total of 16 teams competed in the league, with Muangthong United F.C. as the defending champions. Sriracha, Chula United and Nakhon Pathom were relegated to the 2010 Thai Division 1 League after finishing the 2009 Thai Premier League in the bottom three places." .

ns1:56482 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1908 United States presidential election in Minnesota took place on 03 November 1908 as part of the 1908 United States presidential election. Minnesota was won by the Republican candidate, United States Secretary of War William Howard Taft over former Nebraska Congressman and two-time prior Democratic nominee William Jennings Bryan by a landslide margin of 26.09%." .

ns1:56527 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Trofeo Città di Brescia was a professional tennis tournament played on carpet courts. It took place in Brescia, Italy between 10 November 2014 and 16 November 2014." .

ns1:56548 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Quark is the annual techno-management festival of BITS, Pilani - K. K. Birla Goa Campus. Quark usually features various events pertaining to the different engineering branches as well as the pure sciences, but has more recently expanded the range of events to include various events, related to entrepreneurship. Quark 2007 turned Quark into a local inter college Goa festival. Over the years, Quark has had associations with various media outlets such as The Hindu, Hindustan times, The Times of India, Big FM, Radio Mirchi, BBC Knowledge Magazine, Digit, Business Economics magazine and several others. Quark has also been covered by magazines such as Engineering Review, Exhibit, Electronics For You, Power Learning and Youth Inc magazine for the past few years." .

ns1:56560 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008–09 European Challenge Cup was the 13th season of the European Challenge Cup, the annual rugby union European club competition for clubs from six nations in European rugby. It started in 09 October 2008 and ended with the final at the Twickenham Stoop in London on 22 May 2009, in which Northampton Saints defeated Bourgoin 15–3." .

ns1:56561 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Second Battle of Petersburg, also known as the Assault on Petersburg, was fought 15 June 1864–18, 1864, at the beginning of the Richmond–Petersburg Campaign (popularly known as the Siege of Petersburg). Union forces under Lt. Gen. Ulysses S. Grant and Major General George G. Meade attempted to capture Petersburg, before Gen. Robert E. Lee's Confederate Army of Northern Virginia could reinforce the city. Although the Overland Campaign's objective had been to defeat Lee's Army of Northern Virginia in a decisive battle, Grant changed his objective to be the city of Petersburg, an important rail junction that controlled the supplies leading to the Confederate capital of Richmond. Grant knew that Lee could not protect Richmond if Petersburg fell and he would be forced to battle Grant in the open. He immediately dispatched two divisions of his men, exhausted from the Overland Campaign, to Petersburg, beginning at 3 a.m. on 18 June 1864. Grant's opportunity to take Petersburg easily had been lost, but Lee, who arrived at Petersburg around noon on 18 June 1864, was unable to prevent the Union army from laying siege to the city." .

ns1:56567 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Edward M. Kennedy Serve America Act or Serve America Act (H.R. The U.S. Senate debated and approved an amended version of the bill on March 26, 2009, renaming it the Edward M. Kennedy Serve America Act, after Senator Ted Kennedy. It was signed by President Barack Obama on 21 April 2009. In order to originally implement the Serve America Act, President Barack Obama requested and received approval from Congress for a $1.149 billion for the Corporation." .

ns1:56575 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1980 Canadian federal election was held on 18 February 1980, to elect members of the House of Commons of Canada of the 32nd Parliament of Canada. Former Liberal Prime Minister of Canada Pierre Trudeau had announced his resignation as leader of the Liberal Party following its defeat in 1979." .

ns1:56602 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 99th Grey Cup was a Canadian football game between the East Division champion Winnipeg Blue Bombers and the West Division champion BC Lions to decide the champion of the Canadian Football League in the 2011 season. The game took place on 27 November 2011, at BC Place Stadium in Vancouver, British Columbia. This was the eighth Grey Cup game played at BC Place, and the 15th in Vancouver, the most recent previous one having been the 93rd Grey Cup between the Montreal Alouettes and Edmonton Eskimos. It was the fifteenth Grey Cup to be held in Vancouver (and eighth at BC Place Stadium), the most recent previous one having been the 93rd Grey Cup in 2005, between the Montreal Alouettes and Edmonton Eskimos, which the Eskimos won 38-35 in overtime. This surpassed the previous Vancouver record, set when the 93rd Grey Cup game was sold out on October 7, 2005. Although this was down from the 6.04 million viewers that the 98th Grey Cup drew, it was still the highest-rated television program of the week in Canada. The Eskimos previously beaten the Lions in the 2005 playoffs when Vancouver last hosted the Grey Cup game, denying the Lions a chance to play for the Cup in front of a home crowd." .

ns1:56609 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1956 Tasmanian state election was held on 13 October 1956 in the Australia state of Tasmania to elect 30 members of the Tasmanian House of Assembly. The 1955 Tasmanian state election had resulted in a parliamentary deadlock between the Labor and Liberal parties, although Robert Cosgrove remained Premier of Tasmania. Cosgrove obtained a dissolution of parliament from the Governor of Tasmania, and an election was called for 13 October 1956." .

ns1:56632 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 25th Academy Awards was held on 19 March 1953. It was the first Academy Awards ceremony to be televised, and the first ceremony to be held in Hollywood and New York City simultaneously. The show was broadcast from 10:30 p.m. to 12:15 am, switching back and forth from host Bob Hope on the West Coast to Conrad Nagel on the East Coast." .

ns1:56637 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Connie Chung Tonight is an American television newsmagazine hosted by Connie Chung. The hour-long series premiered on CNN on 24 June 2002. When CNN generally resumed regular programming, they did not bring Connie Chung Tonight back to air." .

ns1:56638 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Kastner train consisted of 35 cattle trucks that left Budapest on 30 June 1944, during the German occupation of Hungary, carrying over 1,600 Jews to safety in Switzerland. The train was named after Rudolf Kastner, a Hungarian-Jewish lawyer and journalist, who was a founding member of the Budapest Aid and Rescue Committee, a group that smuggled Jews out of occupied Europe during the Holocaust. After a journey of several weeks, including a diversion to the Bergen-Belsen concentration camp in Germany, 1,670 surviving passengers reached Switzerland in August and 1944. His mother, Helen Kastner, was given a place, as was his brother Ernő, his pregnant wife Bogyó (she gave birth to a daughter, Zsuzsi, in Switzerland in 1944), along with her father József Fischer, and Bogyó's other relatives. The passage ends with: \"In 24 out of 24 hours, there is never a minute's silence ...\" The first batch of 318 passengers arrived in Switzerland on 18 August 1944, and the rest in December. It is reported that approximately 1,350 passengers arrived in Switzerland in 1944." .

ns1:56640 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "United Nations Security Council Resolution 602, adopted unanimously on 25 November 1987, after hearing representations from the People's Republic of Angola, the Council recalled resolutions 387 (1976), 428 (1978), 447 (1979), 454 (1979), 475 (1980), 545 (1983), 546 (1984), 567 (1985), 571 (1985), 574 (1985) and 577 (1985), expressing its concern at the continuing military incursions into the country by South Africa through occupied South-West Africa (Namibia)." .

ns1:56651 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Buratha Mosque bombing was a triple suicide bombing that occurred on 07 April 2006, in Baghdad. The attack killed 85 people and wounded 160 others. In the end the three bombers managed to kill 85 people, and injure 160." .

ns1:56685 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Hurricane Irene–Olivia was the first actively tracked tropical cyclone to move into the eastern Pacific Ocean from the Atlantic Ocean. It originated as a tropical depression on 11 September 1971, in the tropical Atlantic Ocean. The Hurricane Hunters also reported a pressure of 948 mbar, which was the lowest reported pressure during the 1971 Pacific hurricane season." .

ns1:56695 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Couldn't Stand the Weather Tour was a worldwide concert tour by blues rock band Stevie Ray Vaughan. Consisting of thirteen legs and 146 shows, the tour commenced in Southampton, New York, on 10 March 1984 and concluded in San Antonio, Texas, on 04 May 1985." .

ns1:56697 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1998 Scottish Open was a professional ranking snooker tournament, that was held in 12 February 1998 at the Aberdeen Exhibition and Conference Centre, Aberdeen, Scotland." .

ns1:56710 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1996 Las Vegas 500K was the second round of the 1996–1997 Indy Racing League season. The race was held on 15 September 1996, at the 1.500 mi (2.414 km) Las Vegas Motor Speedway in Clark County, Nevada." .

ns1:56714 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1937 Donington Grand Prix was a Grand Prix motor race held on 02 October 1937 at the Donington Park circuit. The lead changed hands several times between Manfred von Brauchitsch, Bernd Rosemeyer and Rudolf Caracciola." .

ns1:56729 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Dutch Basketball Supercup was the 2nd edition of the Dutch Basketball Supercup. The game was played between EBBC, the winner of the 2011–12 Dutch Basketball League, and Zorg en Zekerheid Leiden, the winner of the 2011–12 NBB Cup." .

ns1:56749 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "A 2002 Iraqi presidential referendum took place in Iraq on 16 October 2002. It was the second presidential election under the rule of Saddam Hussein (the first having taken place in 1995 Iraqi presidential referendum)." .

ns1:56757 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "religion in ancient Rome includes the ancestral ethnic religion of the city of Rome that the ancient Rome used to define themselves as a people, as well as the religious practices of peoples brought under Roman rule, in so far as they became widely followed in Rome and Italy. For ordinary ancient Rome, religion was a part of daily life. This was the context for Rome's conflict with Christianity, which ancient Rome variously regarded as a form of atheism and novel superstitio, while Christians considered Roman religion to be paganism. Ultimately, Roman polytheism was brought to an end with the adoption of Christianity as the official religion of the empire." .

ns1:56800 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018–19 Basketbol Süper Ligi was the 53rd season of the Turkish Basketball Super League, the top-tier level professional club basketball league in Turkey. The season started on 06 October 2018 and ended 21 June 2019." .

ns1:56815 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2004 Stanley Cup Finals was the championship series of the National Hockey League's (NHL) 2003–04 season, and the culmination of the 2004 Stanley Cup playoffs. The Eastern Conference champion Tampa Bay Lightning defeated the Western Conference champion Calgary Flames in seven games, becoming the southernmost team to win the Stanley Cup. This was the last Stanley Cup Final played for two years, as the 2004–05 NHL lockout began three months after the end of this final, lasting over ten months and leading to the cancellation of the 2005 Final, with the league not returning to play for the Cup until 2006. Chris Simon added the fourth and final Calgary goal after Tampa Bay's Martin St. Louis scored the lone Lightning goal. Game two saw the same final score, but this time, it was Tampa Bay winning a clutch game to tie the series, 1–1, headed to Calgary. The series shifted to the Scotiabank Saddledome in Calgary, where Flames goalie Miikka Kiprusoff and the Calgary defense completely stonewalled the Tampa Bay attack, which only took 21 shots in a 3–0 Flames victory. With a chance to take a commanding 3–1 series lead, Calgary was shut out by Lightning goalie Nikolai Khabibulin, who recorded his fifth shutout of the postseason, a 29-save shutout, in a 1–0 Tampa Bay victory, with the game's lone goal being scored by Brad Richards three minutes into the game on a two-man advantage. The series returned to Tampa Bay tied, 2–2, for a critical game five, and Calgary pulled off a 3–2 overtime victory to move within one win from the Stanley Cup. Tampa Bay won the game, 2–1, and the Stanley Cup. 2004 Tampa Bay Lightning. (see 1984 Stanley Cup Finals and 1993 Stanley Cup Finals) In the United States, this was the last Stanley Cup Finals to air on the ABC/ESPN family of networks, with ESPN televising the first two games and ABC broadcasting the last five games, as the 2004–05 NHL lockout suspended play for the next season." .

ns1:56817 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2005 U.S. Open was the 105th U.S. Open, held June 16–19 at Pinehurst Resort Course No. Michael Campbell won his only major title, two strokes ahead of runner-up Tiger Woods; third-round leader and two-time U.S. Open champion Retief Goosen collapsed on the final day. It was only the second U.S. Open at Pinehurst, because of past concerns of high temperatures and its distance from a major populated area." .

ns1:56844 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Tamsui, Danshui, or Hobe (2 08 October 1884–08 October 1884) was a significant French defeat by the Qing dynasty at Tamsui District on Taiwan during the Keelung Campaign of the Sino-French War. The battle of Tamsui District was part of the Keelung Campaign (August 1884–April 1885). Following the outbreak of the Sino-French War on 23 August 1884, the French decided to put pressure on China by landing an expeditionary corps in northern Formosa to seize Keelung and Tamsui District. On 1 08 October 1884, while the Formosa expeditionary corps went ashore at Keelung, Lespès lay off Tamsui District with the ironclads La Galissonnière and Triomphante, the cruiser d'Estaing and the gunboat Vipère. The bodies of the three dead Frenchmen, abandoned on the battlefield, were beheaded by the Chinese, and the severed heads were paraded through Tamsui District on the evening of 08 October 1884 by the victorious Chinese army. His Majesty's Slave Liu Ming-chuan, Director of Affairs in Formosa, who holds the brevet of Provincial Governor, humbly submits the following report, showing how the enemy's troops were landed and attacked at Hobe (Tamsui District), when our troops fought a sanguinary battle and gained a victory. \" The French defeat at Tamsui District on 08 October 1884 was widely publicised in China, and had a far greater effect on national morale than the simultaneous French victories in Tonkin during the K6 October 1884p10 08 October 1884 08 October 1884)." .

ns1:56851 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Feast of the Pheasant (French: Banquet du Vœu du faisan, \"Banquet of the Oath of the Pheasant\") was a banquet given by Philip the Good, Duke of Burgundy on 17 February 1454 in Lille, now in France." .

ns1:56865 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Yerba Buena was an engagement during the Mexican-American War, during which the U.S. Navy captured and occupied the town of Yerba Buena, California (Present-day San Francisco, California) without firing a shot. On 09 July 1846, the USS Portsmouth, captained by Commander John B. Montgomery, sailed into San Francisco Bay, with the intent of capturing the town of Yerba Buena, California. Thus, the pueblo of Yerba Buena, California, and the land that would become eventually known as San Francisco, was captured by the United States of America." .

ns1:56870 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Council of Orange (or Second Synod of Orange) was held in 529 at Orange, which was then part of the Ostrogothic Kingdom. On 03 July 0529 529 a synod took place at Orange." .

ns1:56958 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1998–99 European Challenge Cup was the third year of the European Challenge Cup, the second tier rugby union cup competition below the Heineken Cup. The tournament was held between 19 September 1998 and 27 February 1999." .

ns1:56986 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Velasco, fought 25 June 1832-26, 1832, was the first true military conflict between Mexico and Texians in the Texas Revolution. In 1832, Mexican authorities established a small garrison at Velasco, a small community on the eastern bank of the Brazos River." .

ns1:57013 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Free and Open source Software Developers' European Meeting (FOSDEM) is a non-commercial, volunteer-organized European event centered on free and open-source software development. FOSDEM is held annually, usually during the first weekend of February, at the Université libre de Bruxelles Solbosch campus in the southeast of Brussels. FOSDEM was started in 2000 under the name Open Source Developers of Europe Meeting (OSDEM) by Raphael Bauduin. The Free Software Foundation's ceremony for the Award for the Advancement of Free Software was held at FOSDEM from 2002 to 2006 (for the awards for 2001 to 2005)." .

ns1:57056 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018–19 Cypriot Cup was the 77th edition of the Cypriot Cup. It began on 05 December 2018 with the first round and concluded on 22 May 2019 with the final held at GSP Stadium. The winner of the Cup was AEL Limassol FC for seventh time and qualified for the 2019–20 UEFA Europa League." .

ns1:57068 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2001 Iranian presidential election took place on 08 June 2001, and resulted in Mohammad Khatami being elected as the President of Iran for his second term." .

ns1:57074 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Independence Day commonly called Boipuso, is a national day observed in Botswana on September 30 of every year. The date celebrates Botswana's Declaration of Independence from the United Kingdom on September 30, 1966. Independence Day is the national day of Botswana. Independence Day is a national day marked by patriotic displays. Independence Day is a federal holiday, so all non-essential federal institutions (such as the postal service and federal courts) are closed on that day." .

ns1:57086 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1995 Australian Grand Prix (officially the LX EDS Australian Grand Prix) was a Formula One motor race held on 12 November 1995 at the Adelaide Street Circuit, Adelaide. The race, contested over 81 laps, was the seventeenth and final race of the 1995 Formula One season, and the eleventh and last Australian Grand Prix to be held at Adelaide before the event moved to Melbourne the following year. It was announced beforehand that it would be the last Formula One event to be held at the Adelaide Street Circuit, with the Australian Grand Prix moving to Melbourne Grand Prix Circuit in Melbourne from the 1996 season." .

ns1:57131 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1967 Svenska Cupen Final took place on 01 November 1967 at Idrottsparken in Norrköping. It was the first final since 1953 and the second consecutive final between Allsvenskan sides Malmö FF and IFK Norrköping. IFK Norrköping played their fifth final in total and Malmö FF played their seventh final in total. Malmö FF won their third consecutive final and their sixth title with a 2–0 victory." .

ns1:57143 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Bombardment of San Juan, or the First Battle of San Juan, on 12 May 1898 was an engagement between United States Navy warships and the Spanish fortifications of San Juan, Puerto Rico. Detroit was to lead the U.S. battle line up San Juan's bay, their orders where to initiate a bombardment of the enemy positions if fired upon." .

ns1:57145 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1983 Virginia Slims of New Jersey was a women's tennis tournament played on hardcourt at the Ramapo College in Mahwah, New Jersey in the United States that was part of the Category 2 tier of the 1983 Virginia Slims World Championship Series. It was the sixth edition of the tournament and was held from 22 August 1983 through 28 August 1983." .

ns1:57157 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 FIBA Under-19 World Championship was hosted by Heraklion, Greece, from 27 June to 05 July 2015. As a result of FIBA rules changes, made effective as of 1 October 2014, this tournament marked the first time that the FIBA Under-19 World Championship used instant replay to review controversial plays and where players were allowed to wear any jersey with numbers from \"00\" to \"99\"." .

ns1:57167 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Caribbean Cup was the 17th 2010 Caribbean Cup of the Caribbean Cup, an international association football competition for national teams of member nations affiliated with the Caribbean Football Union (CFU) of the CONCACAF region." .

ns1:57173 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Chess with different armies (or Betza's Chess or Equal Armies) is a chess variant invented by Ralph Betza in 1979." .

ns1:57198 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Protocol on the results of consultations of the Trilateral Contact Group, or commonly known as the Minsk Protocol, is an agreement to halt the War in Donbas of Ukraine, signed by representatives of that country, the Russian Federation, the Donetsk People's Republic (DPR), the Luhansk People's Republic (LPR), and the Organization for Security and Co-operation in Europe (OSCE) on 5 September 2014. It failed to stop fighting in Donbass, and was thus followed with a new package of measures, called Minsk II, which was agreed to on 12 February 2015. In late October, DPR prime minister and Minsk Protocol signatory Alexander Zakharchenko said that his forces would retake the territory it had lost to Ukrainian forces during a War in Donbas, and that DPR forces would be willing to wage \"heavy battles\" to do so. A new package of measures meant to stop fighting in the Donbass, called Minsk II, was agreed to on 12 February 2015. Successive attempts to resolve the ongoing War in Donbas of Ukraine had seen no result by the start of 12 February 2015. The new package, commonly referred to as \"Minsk II\", was criticised for being \"highly complicated\" and \"extremely fragile\", and for being very similar to the failed Minsk Protocol." .

ns1:57238 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1985 Fila German Open was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor clay courts at the Rot-Weiss Tennis Club Berlin in West Berlin, West Germany that was part of the Category 3 tier of the 1985 Virginia Slims World Championship Series. It was the 16th edition of the tournament and was held from 13 May 1985 through 19 May 1985." .

ns1:57240 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1968 Wimbledon Championships was a combined men's and women's tennis tournament that took place on the outdoor grass courts at the All England Lawn Tennis and Croquet Club in Wimbledon, London, United Kingdom. The tournament was held from Monday 24 June 1968 until Saturday 06 July 1968. It was the 82nd staging of the Wimbledon Championships, and the third Grand Slam tennis event of 1968. This tournament started the Open Era for Wimbledon, as it became the second Grand Slam tournament to offer prize money and allow professionals to compete after the 1968 French Open." .

ns1:57249 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 NCAA Division I FBS football season was the highest level of college football competition in the United States organized by the National Collegiate Athletic Association (NCAA). The regular season began on 03 September 2015 and ended on 12 December 2015. For coaching changes that occurred earlier in 2015, see 2014 NCAA Division I FBS football season." .

ns1:57258 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Internationaux de Tennis de Vendée was a professional tennis tournament played on hard courts. It took place in Mouilleron-le-Captif, France between 09 November 2015 and 15 November 2015." .

ns1:57261 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1950–51 Oberliga was the sixth season of the Fußball-Oberliga, the first tier of the football league system in West Germany. A similar-named league, the DDR-Fußball-Oberliga, existed in East Germany, set at the first tier of the East German football league system. The league's top scorer was Herbert Wojtkowiak of Hamburger SV with 40 goals, the highest total for the five Oberligas in 1950–51 and throughout the 16-year history of the Fußball-Oberliga Nord. The 1951 German football championship was contested by the eight qualified Fußball-Oberliga teams and won by 1." .

ns1:57276 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Global Warning Tour was the first Asian concert tour by South Korean boy band BIGBANG. The tour began on 28 March 2008 in Tokyo, Japan and concluded on 22 June 2008 in Seoul, South Korea." .

ns1:57289 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Cable Street was an event that took place in Cable Street and Whitechapel in the East End of London, on Sunday 04 October 1936. Numerous events were planned in East London for the battle's 75th anniversary in October 2011, including music and a march, and the mural was once again restored. In 2016, to mark the battle's 80th anniversary, a march took place from Altab Ali Park to Cable Street." .

ns1:57296 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1956 Louisiana gubernatorial election was held on 17 January 1956. The 1956 election saw the election of Earl Long to his second full term as Governor of Louisiana. Earl Long: The Saga of Uncle Earl and Louisiana Politics." .

ns1:57302 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1999 Molson Indy Toronto was the eleventh round of the 1999 CART season and took place on 18 July 1999, at the 2.824-kilometre (1.755 mi) Exhibition Place temporary street circuit in Toronto, Ontario, Canada. Gil de Ferran took pole in qualifying by a considerable margin, but his lead did not last long as Dario Franchitti passed him at Turn 3 on the first lap." .

ns1:57303 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Starting on 11 January 1988, Céline Dion's Incognito tournée was a concert tour organized to support the album Incognito. The Incognito tournée started on 11 January 1988 at the Theatre du Cuivre, Rouyn-Noranda." .

ns1:57315 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Eritrea at the 2018 Winter Olympics competed at the 2018 Winter Olympics in Pyeongchang, South Korea, from 9 to 2018. Eritrea at the 2018 Winter Olympics's team consisted of one alpine skier, Shannon-Ogbani Abeda, who carried the country's flag during the 2018 Winter Olympics opening ceremony. Eritrea at the 2018 Winter Olympics qualified one male athlete, Canadian born skier Shannon-Ogbani Abeda." .

ns1:57320 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Montenotte Campaign began on 10 April 1796 with an action at Voltri and ended with the Armistice of Cherasco on 28 April 1796. Montenotte Superiore is located at the junction of Strada Provinciale 12 and 41 in the Liguria region of northwest Italy, 15 kilometres (9 mi) northeast of Carcare municipality." .

ns1:57323 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Ganga puja is a religious festival of the northeastern state of Tripura in India. The tribal Tripuri people worship the Goddess of the river, and pray to be saved from epidemic diseases and for the well-being of pregnant women. Ganga puja is celebrated after the Navanna festival, which marks the rice harvest. Another purpose of the festival is that the gods are reverently asked to ensure the well-being of pregnant women." .

ns1:57325 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 49th 1965 Tour of Flanders cycling classic was held on Saturday, 17 April 1965. The race was won by Dutch rider Jo de Roo in a two-man sprint with Edward Sels." .

ns1:57356 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Youdan Cup was an 1867 Sheffield rules football competition. The competition took its name from a local theatre owner, Thomas Youdan, who sponsored the competition and provided the trophy. It did not resurface again until 1997, when a Scottish antiques collector contacted Hallam F.C. to tell them that he was in possession of it - they subsequently bought it back for £1,600. Contemporary reports suggest that the 1867 tournament was expected to be repeated in subsequent years, with Youdan awarding a trophy of the original competition-winning design. The work of the ad-hoc Youdan Prize Committee in co-ordinating activity between local clubs and framing rules was a precursor to that of the Sheffield Football Association, which had already been formed by the end of 1867, and issued its own first set of rules on 1867, the day following the final of the Youdan Cup." .

ns1:57374 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Peru 4–2 Austria was a association football match played on 1936 during the 1936 Summer Olympics in Berlin. In Peru national football team, angry crowds protested against the decisions of the Olympic Committee by tearing down an Olympic flag, throwing stones at the German consulate, refusing to load German vessels in the docks of Callao, and listening to inflammatory speeches which included President Oscar Benavides Larrea's mention of \"the crafty Berlin decision.\" Peru national football team was coached by Alberto Denegri, whereas Austria national association football team was managed by Englishman James Hogan." .

ns1:57378 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Aeroflot Flight 3 (Russian: Рейс 3 Аэрофлота Reys 3 Aeroflota) was a passenger flight from Khabarovsk Airport to Petropavlovsk-Kamchatsky Airport. On 03 September 1962 the Aeroflot Tupolev Tu-104 lost control after the airframe started vibrating, resulting in the plane rolling and yawing several times at an altitude of 4,500 meters before crashing." .

ns1:57415 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Taking Chances Tour is the ninth concert tour by Canadian singer Céline Dion, in support of her 2007 studio albums, D'elles and Taking Chances. It also marks as her first concert tour in 9 years since her 1998–1999 Let's Talk About Love World Tour. Randy Lewis's article in the Los Angeles Times was more enthusiastic, opening with, \"It's a no-brainer why Céline Dion's Taking Chances tour is shaping up as one of the highest-grossing North American concert attractions of 2008... hundreds of thousands of fans are filling arena after arena to hear Dion deliver emotional climax after emotional climax, goosebump-inducing vocal thrill after thrill, sweeping chorus after chorus filled with spirit-lifting affirmations and enough technical razzle-dazzle to dwarf the Super Bowl halftime show." .

ns1:57429 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2021 Western Australian state election is scheduled for Saturday 13 March 2021 to elect members to the Parliament of Western Australia, where all 59 seats in the Legislative Assembly and all 36 seats in the Legislative Council will be up for election. The parties contesting the election are: The 2017 Western Australian state election saw Labor win one of the most comprehensive victories on record at the state or territory level in Australia." .

ns1:57432 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1983 Skate Canada International was held in Halifax, Nova Scotia on 27 October 1983–29." .

ns1:57434 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Sergei Prokofiev did not manage to compose more than a few bars of his Piano Concerto No. 6 (Op. The work is unusual in that it is scored for two piano and a string orchestra." .

ns1:57466 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1881 Welsh Cup Final, was the 4th in the association football competition. It was contested by Cefn Druids A.F.C. and Newtown A.F.C. at the Racecourse Ground, Wrexham." .

ns1:57499 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 U.S. Women's Open Golf Championship was the 64th United States Women's Open Championship. Held 09 July 2009–12, it was the first United States Women's Open Championship to be played at the Old Course of the Saucon Valley Country Club in Bethlehem, Pennsylvania." .

ns1:57510 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Craonne (07 March 1814) was a battle between an First French Empire army under Emperor Napoleon I opposing a combined army of Russian Empire and Prussians led by Prussian Field Marshal Gebhard Leberecht von Blücher. The War of the Sixth Coalition engagement began when the bulk of Napoleon's army tried to drive Mikhail Semyonovich Vorontsov's 22,000 Russians off the Chemin des Dames plateau to the west of Craonne. The Chemin des Dames (Ladies' Road) starts on the Soissons-Laon road and runs east along a continuous ridge to Craonne and then loses elevation before rising again a little at Corbeny." .

ns1:57516 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 Zurich Open was a women's tennis tournament played on indoor hard courts. It was the 25th edition of the event known that year as the Zurich Open, and was part of the WTA Tier II tournaments of the 2008 WTA Tour. It took place at the Hallenstadion in Zürich, Switzerland, from 11 October through 19 October 2008." .

ns1:57531 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Avianca Flight 671, registration HK-177, was a Lockheed Constellation that crashed and burned on landing at Montego Bay, Jamaica, on 21 January 1960. The aircraft operating the flight was a Lockheed L-1049E Super Constellation used by Avianca for its Bogota-Montego Bay routes." .

ns1:57533 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 South American Under 17 Women Championship was the second instance of the South American Under-17 Women's Championship. It was held from 28 January 2010 to 11 February 2010 in São Paulo, Brazil. As the top 3 teams, Brazil, Chile, and Venezuela qualified for the 2010 FIFA U-17 Women's World Cup held in Trinidad and Tobago." .

ns1:57566 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Goliad was the second skirmish of the Texas Revolution. In the early-morning hours of October 9, 1835, Texas settlers attacked the Mexican Army soldiers garrisoned at Presidio La Bahía, a fort near the Mexican Texas settlement of Goliad. In September, Texians began plotting to kidnap Mexican General Martín Perfecto de Cos, who was en route to Goliad to attempt to quell the unrest in Texas. In 1835, Mexico operated two major garrisons within its Texas territory, the Alamo at San Antonio de Béxar and Presidio La Bahía near Goliad. Béxar was the political center of Texas, and Goliad laid halfway between it and the major Texas port of Copano. Ira Ingram led the vanguard, which halted 1 mile (1.6 km) outside Goliad. In secret, Sabriego began organizing a group of settlers in the Goliad area who sympathized with Mexico." .

ns1:57608 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Glen Fruin was a Scottish clan battle fought on 07 February 1603 between the Clan Gregor and its allies on one side, and the Clan Colquhoun and its allies on the other. Glen Fruin is located in the Loch Lomond area, in the county of Dunbartonshire, Scotland. The two sides met on 07 February 1603 at Glen Fruin. The writer Peter McArthur composed a ballad entitled The Raid of Glen Fruin in memory of the battle. In July 1967, an excavation took place on a mound in Glen Fruin that was thought to be the burial place of the Colquhouns who were killed in the battle." .

ns1:57653 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1703 Genroku earthquake (元禄大地震, Genroku Daijishin) occurred at 02:00 local time on 31 December 1703 (17:00 December 30 UTC). The epicenter was near Edo, the forerunner of present-day Tokyo, in the southern part of the Kantō region, Japan." .

ns1:57656 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Kazakhstan Cup was the 25th season of the Kazakhstan Cup, the annual nationwide association football cup competition of Kazakhstan since the independence of the country. The competition began on 21 March 2016 and end with the final on 19 November 2016. FC Astana defeated defending champions Kairat 1-0 in the final to win their first Kazakhstan Cup since 2012. Ordabasy qualified for the first qualifying round of the 2017–18 UEFA Europa League after finishing 4th in the league, as both FC Astana and Kairat had already secured European association football for the following season. The following 25 teams qualified for the competition: The rounds of the 2016 competition are scheduled as follows: The four winners from the Quarterfinals were drawn into two two-legged ties." .

ns1:57672 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2003 Rugby World Cup Final was the final match in the 2003 Rugby World Cup, and the fifth Rugby World Cup. The match was played between England national rugby union team and Australia national rugby union team on 22 November 2003 at Stadium Australia in Sydney in front of a crowd of 82,957 people. England national rugby union team won 20–17 to win the Webb Ellis Cup for the first time, also becoming the first European side to win the cup. Australia national rugby union team opened the 2003 Rugby World Cup at Stadium Australia in Sydney, where they beat Argentina 24–8. Their final pool match was against Uruguay, which England national rugby union team won 111–13. England national rugby union team beat Wales in their quarter final, 28–17, and went through to meet France in the semis. The national anthems of Australia national rugby union team (Advance Australia national rugby union team Fair) and England national rugby union team (God Save the Queen) were then performed. The first points of the final were scored by Australia national rugby union team. Flatley kicked a penalty goal for Australia national rugby union team in the 47th minute, after the England national rugby union team scrummage was penalized by referee Andre Watson, taking the score to 14–8. England national rugby union team's forwards were again penalized by Watson in the 61st minute, and Flatley kicked the penalty goal for Australia national rugby union team. With 26 seconds on the clock, Wilkinson kicked a right-footed drop goal to give England national rugby union team their first ever Rugby World Cup 20–17." .

ns1:57679 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 FAI Cup Final was the final match of the 2017 FAI Cup, the national association football cup of the Republic of Ireland. The match took place on Sunday 2017 at the Aviva Stadium in Dublin, and was contested by Cork City F.C. and Dundalk F.C.." .

ns1:57696 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Białystok–Minsk was a German strategic operation conducted by the Wehrmacht's Army Group Centre under Field Marshal Fedor von Bock during the penetration of the Soviet border region in the opening stage of Operation Barbarossa, lasting from 22 June 1941 to 09 July 1941. Commanded by Field Marshal Fedor von Bock, Army Group Centre was tasked with attacking from Poland through the Białystok – Minsk – Smolensk axis towards Moscow. The Red Army disposition in Belarus was based on the idea of avoiding a war of attrition by engaging in an aggressive counterattack to any invasion, carrying the war into German-occupied Poland. The Red Army moved into Białystok (Poland), which shaped OKH planning. Beyond Białystok, Minsk was a key strategic railway junction and a defensive position of the main road and rail communications with Moscow. On 26 June Minsk, the capital of Belarus, fell to the Wehrmacht. From 22 June 1941 to 09 July 1941, the Soviet forces in Belarus lost 417,729 men, including 341,012 killed or missing and 76,717 wounded or sick." .

ns1:57750 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The first official Mediterranean Games were held in Egypt in 1951. Twelve countries were hosted for Mediterranean Games - four from Africa: Egypt (1951), Tunisia (1967, 2001), Algeria (1975) and Morocco (1983); six from Europe: Spain (1955, 2005, 2018), Italy (1963, 1997, 2009), Turkey (1971, 2013), Yugoslavia (1979), Greece (1991) and France (1993) and two from Asia: Lebanon (1959) and Syria (1987). At present, 26 countries participate in the games: Kosovo was accepted as a member of the Comité international des Jeux méditerranéens in October 2015 and participated for the first time in the 2018 Mediterranean Games in Tarragona, Spain. The International Amateur Athletics Federation pushed the issue at the 1959 Mediterranean Games in Beirut by refusing to grant permission to hold an athletics competition unless Israel were allowed to compete. Lebanese games organizer Gabriel Gemayel conceded to this, but sidestepped the ruling by holding a parallel Lebanese Games comprising athletics events between the present nations alongside the official Mediterranean Games competitions." .

ns1:57751 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Düsseldorf Open was a men's tennis tournament played on clay courts. It was the second edition of the Düsseldorf Open as an ATP World Tour 250 series event on the 2014 ATP World Tour. It took place at the Rochusclub in Düsseldorf, Germany, from 18 May 2014 through 24 May 2014." .

ns1:57753 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1969 Botswana general election were held in Botswana on 18 October 1969, the first since independence in September 1966. The Botswana National Front was founded following the 1965 Bechuanaland general election, and was headed by Kenneth Koma." .

ns1:57760 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1993 FIFA U-17 World Championship, the fifth edition of the FIFA Under-17 World Cup, was held in the cities of Tokyo, Hiroshima, Kyoto, Kobe, Nagoya, and Gifu City in Japan between 21 August 1993 and 04 September 1993. All times are Japan Standard Time (UTC+9) Wilson Oruma of Nigeria won the FIFA Under-17 World Cup award for scoring six goals." .

ns1:57768 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Łódź insurrection, also known as the June Days, was an uprising by Polish workers in Łódź against the Russian Empire between 21 25 June 1905 and 25 June 1905. This event was one of the largest disturbances in the Russian-controlled Congress Poland during the Russian Revolution of 1905. On 18 25 June 1905, Russian police opened fire on one of the many workers' demonstrations in Łódź, killing approximately ten workers, whose funerals, attended by over 50,000–70,000 people, escalated into major demonstration20 Juneon 2021 June June. The Łódź uprising was neither the first nor the last disturbance in what has been described in Polish historiography as the revolution in the Congress Poland (1905–1907)—various protests and strikes occurred in major Polish cities under Russian control throughout the year—but it would be the most dramatic one." .

ns1:57769 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Southern Jiangsu Campaign (Chinese: 苏南战役) was a 1945 series battle fought at the Southern Jiangsu and adjacent regions in Anhui and northern Zhejiang, and it was a clash between the communists and the former nationalists turned Japanese puppet regime force who rejoined the nationalists after World War II with their Japanese ally. On September 8, 1945, the 1st Column of the communist Jiangsu – Zhejiang Military Region decided to take controls of regions in central Jiangsu, northern Zhejiang and adjacent regions in Anhui by force after the local defenders consisted of Japanese troops and former nationalists turned Japanese puppet regime force who rejoined the nationalists after World War II refused to surrender. Since mid August, 1945, over a dozen enemy strongholds in the region from Jurong (句容) County in Jiangsu in the north to Changxing (长兴) County in Zhejiang in the south had fallen into the communist hands, including those at Front Horse (Qianma, 前马), Southern Crossing (Nandu, 南渡), Celestial King Temple (Tianwangsi, 天王寺), Baonian (宝埝) and Jiapu (夹铺 on Taihu Lake in Changxing County) regions. The 1st Regiment of the 1st Column of the communist Jiangsu – Zhejiang Military Region ambushed the enemy at Zhang Family's Hills (Zhangjiagang, 张家岗), and Duan Family's Bridge (Duanjiaqiao, 段家桥) regions, and after an eleven-hour long fight, succeeded in killing over 30 Japanese troops and over 370 nationalist troops, including the regimental commander of the nationalist 8th Regiment of the 3rd Division. On August 19, 1945, the 1st Column of the communist Jiangsu – Zhejiang Military Region attacked Jintan and Liyang, and annihilated the defenders consisted of a detachment of Japanese troops and two regiments of former nationalists turned Japanese puppet regime force in the process." .

ns1:57791 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2001 Stanley Cup Finals was the championship series of the National Hockey League's (NHL) 2000–01 season, and the culmination of the 2001 Stanley Cup playoffs. It was contested between the Eastern Conference champion and defending Stanley Cup champion New Jersey Devils and the Western Conference champion and Presidents' Trophy-winning Colorado Avalanche. Colorado defeated New Jersey in seven games to win their second Stanley Cup in franchise history. Colorado's Patrick Roy was awarded the Conn Smythe Trophy as the MVP of the 2001 playoffs. The Devils were based out of Denver as the Colorado Rockies from 1976 to 1982 and the Avalanche established themselves in the city in 1995 after relocating from Quebec City. The Colorado Rockies were formerly based in Denver and were relocated to East Rutherford and renamed the New Jersey Devils prior to the 1982–83 season. The Quebec Nordiques were relocated to Denver and renamed the Colorado Avalanche prior to the 1995–96 season. Game 2 began with goals in the first period by Colorado's Sakic and New Jersey's Bob Corkum and Turner Stevenson. The New Jersey offence overwhelmed the Avalanche defense as they managed 35 shots; Colorado managed only 12 shots. It left the Devils with only two goals to overcome, but Roy and the Avalanche would prove too much for the Devils in the third period as Colorado defensively shut the door on New Jersey to win the game and the series. This was the first and only Stanley Cup championship for defenceman Ray Bourque who, after being traded from the Boston Bruins to Colorado in 2000, retired from the NHL after the Avalanche's 2001 Cup win. 2001 Colorado Avalanche. Colorado was given permission to include Muir's name on the Stanley Cup because one of his three playoff games was played in the conference finals. ^ Six players also won the Stanley Cup with Colorado in 1996: Joe Sakic, Peter Forsberg, Jon Klemm, Stephane Yelle, Patrick Roy and Adam Foote. The Colorado Avalanche have won the Stanley Cup!" .

ns1:57796 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Anadan (Arabic: معركة عندان) was a ten-hour-long armed confrontation between the rebels of the Free Syrian Army and soldiers of the Syrian Army that occurred when FSA forces attempted to overrun a large army checkpoint in the Anadan area. At dusk on 29 July 2012, First Lieutenant Rifaat Khali, commander of the Conquest Brigade in the area, mobilized 150 fighters to seize control of the Anadan checkpoint, a strategic location linking the city of Aleppo 5 kilometres (3.1 mi) to the south with the Turkish border to the north, which the FSA used as a source for supplies. The battle took place concurrently with the first night and day of the Battle of Aleppo itself." .

ns1:57832 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Kfar Darom bus attack was a 1995 suicide attack on an Israel bus carrying civilians and soldiers to Kfar Darom, an Israel settlement in the Gaza Strip. On the morning of 09 April 1995, Khaled Mohammed Khatib, a construction worker from the Nuseirat refugee camp, waited on the main highway running from Ashkelon to the settlements in the Gaza Strip. At 11:45 AM, he rammed Egged bus 36 carrying more than 60 Israel soldiers and civilian passengers to the Jewish settlement of Kfar Darom." .

ns1:57839 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "BBC News at Ten — formerly known as the BBC Ten O'Clock News or the Ten O'Clock News — is the flagship evening news programme for British television channel BBC One and the BBC News channel. The programme was launched on 16 October 2000, replacing the BBC Nine O'Clock News which had been on the air since 14 September 1970. The programme, along with the BBC News channel and the other BBC One bulletins, moved to Broadcasting House and began broadcasting in high-definition on 18 March 2013. Paul Royall has been the editor of BBC News at Ten and BBC News at Six since July 2013." .

ns1:57871 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1973 Canadian Grand Prix was a Formula One motor race held at Canadian Tire Motorsport Park on 23 September 1973. The 80-lap race was won by Peter Revson, driving a McLaren M23, after starting from second on the grid." .

ns1:57882 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Miss Universe 2003, the 52nd Miss Universe pageant, was held on 03 June 2003 at the Figali Convention Center in Panama City, Panama." .

ns1:57899 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Taku Forts was the first attack of the Anglo-French alliance against the Taku Forts along the Hai River in Tianjin, China, on 20 May 1858, during the Second Opium War. However, the first phase of the Second Opium War would end with the Treaties of Tianjin and the forts were returned to the hands of the Qing Army, leading to the Second Battle of Taku Forts in 1859. The British commander Michael Seymour, hoping to force a settlement (the later Treaty of Tianjin), ordered an attack on the Taku Forts as they were the closer path towards Peking." .

ns1:57906 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "SS Carnatic was a British steamship built in 1862-63 by Samuda Brothers at Cubitt Town on the Isle of Dogs, London, for the Peninsular and Oriental Steam Navigation Company (P&O)." .

ns1:57949 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1974 NASCAR Winston Cup Series was the 26th season of NASCAR Cup Series stock car racing in the United States of America and the 3rd modern-era NASCAR Cup series. The season began on Sunday 20 January 1976 and ended on Sunday 24 November 1976. Earl Ross was named NASCAR Rookie of the Year. Begun on 20 January 1976, the Western 500 at Riverside International Raceway was stopped by rain and resumed on 26." .

ns1:57962 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Invasion of Java was a successful British amphibious operation against the Dutch East Indian island of Java that took place between August and September 1811 during the Napoleonic Wars. Originally established as a colony of the Dutch Republic, Java remained in Dutch hands throughout the French Revolutionary and Napoleonic Wars, during which time the French invaded the Republic and established the Batavian Republic in 1795, and the Kingdom of Holland in 1806. The Kingdom of Holland was annexed to the First French Empire in 1810, and Java became a titular French colony, though it continued to be administered and defended primarily by Dutch personnel. They arrived in Java in 1811 without mishap." .

ns1:57967 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation London Bridge has been a codename that referred to the plan for what will happen in the days after the death of Queen Elizabeth II of the United Kingdom and the other Commonwealth realms. As of early 2017, the phrase \"London Bridge is down\" was expected to be used to communicate the death of the Queen to the prime minister of the United Kingdom and key personnel, setting the plan into motion. As of early 2017, the phrase Operation Forth Bridge referred to funeral plans for Prince Philip, Duke of Edinburgh; Operation Menai Bridge referred to the funeral plan for Charles, Prince of Wales; and Operation London Bridge referred to the funeral plan for Queen Elizabeth II. These realms have devised their own plans for what will happen in their respective countries in the days after Elizabeth II's death, which would run concurrently with Operation London Bridge." .

ns1:58007 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Udston mining disaster occurred in Hamilton, Scotland on Saturday, 28 May 1887 when 73 miners died in a firedamp explosion at Udston Colliery." .

ns1:58013 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2000 Oceanian Futsal Championship (OFC) was the third edition of the main international futsal tournament of the Oceanian region. It took place from 21 August 1999 to 28 August 1999, and was hosted by Port Vila, Vanuatu." .

ns1:58017 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1988 Virginia Slims of Chicago was a women's tennis tournament played on indoor carpet courts at the Credit Union 1 Arena in Chicago, Illinois in the United States and was part of the Category 4 tier of the 1988 WTA Tour. It was the 17th edition of the tournament and was held from 07 November 1988 through 13 November 1988." .

ns1:58075 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1991 Women's European Volleyball Championship was the 17th edition of the event, organised by Europe's governing volleyball body, the European Volleyball Confederation. It was hosted in several cities in Italy from 28 September 1991 to 06 October 1991, with the final round held in Rome." .

ns1:58095 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Riyadh truck crash occurred on 01 November 2012, when a truck carrying fuel crashed into an intersection flyover in the east of Riyadh, the capital city of Saudi Arabia." .

ns1:58106 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1988 World Series was the World Series of Major League Baseball's (MLB) 1988 Major League Baseball season. The 85th edition of the World Series, it was a best-of-seven playoff played between the American League (AL) 1988 National League Championship Series Oakland Athletics and the National League (NL) 1988 National League Championship Series Los Angeles Dodgers, with the Dodgers upsetting the heavily favored Athletics to win the Series in five games. Although Gibson's home run has become an iconic World Series moment, it was World Series MVP Orel Hershiser who capped a dominant 1988 Major League Baseball season in which he set the all time scoreless inning streak at 59 innings, recorded five straight shutouts, led the league with 23 wins and 267 innings, and won the Cy Young and Gold Glove awards. Hershiser would dominate the Mets in the 1988 National League Championship Series, while Gibson hobbled through on bad knees and a bruised hamstring but would produce a memorable, if not the greatest, at-bat (in Game 1) of the World Series. The 1988 World Series marked Don Baylor's third consecutive World Series with three separate teams. But anything can happen in a short series, as proven by these 1988 Los Angeles Dodgers, who out-hit (41–28, .246–.177), out-muscled (5 HRs–2 HRs), and out-pitched (2.03–3.92) the seemingly unbeatable Oakland Athletics, incredibly winning the Series in five games, outscoring the A's, 21–11, bringing the Dodgers their sixth World Series Championship, the second as a manager for Tommy Lasorda. NL Los Angeles Dodgers (4) vs. AL Oakland Athletics (1) With Gibson unable to start and officially listed as day-to-day due to a pulled left hamstring and a severely swollen right knee, both sustained on awkward slides into second base in the 1988 National League Championship Series in Games 5 and 7, the Dodgers were at a decided disadvantage. Mike Davis, a disappointing free-agent signing for most of the 1988 Major League Baseball season, added a two-run blast (on a 3-0 count) in the fourth off Davis, and former World Series MVP Rick Dempsey, filling in for an injured Mike Scioscia, drove in Davis with an RBI double in the sixth. 1988 World Series (4–1): Los Angeles Dodgers (N.L.) The 1988 World Series marked the last time that NBC would televise a World Series for seven years. Costas said that the 1988 Dodgers possibly had the weakest hitting line-up in World Series history." .

ns1:58213 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014–15 CERS Cup was the 35th season of the CERS Cup, Europe's second club roller hockey (quad) competition organized by Comité Européen de Rink-Hockey." .

ns1:58217 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1994–95 Liga Indonesia Premier Division (known as the Liga Dunhill for sponsorship reasons) was the inaugural season of the Liga Indonesia Premier Division, the top division of Indonesian football following the merger of Perserikatan and Galatama. Cikijing Putboll Pederacion won the title after beating Petrokimia Putra 1–0 in the final. The 34 goals he scored was a top-flight record which stood for 22 years until Sylvano Comvalius scored 37 goals in the 2017 Liga 1." .

ns1:58231 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The French Revolution of 1848 in the History of France, sometimes known as the February Revolution (révolution de février), was one of a wave of revolutions in 1848 in the Europe. In France, the revolutionary events ended the July Monarchy (1830–1848) and led to the creation of the French Second Republic. Following the overthrow of King Louis Philippe I in 22 February 1848, the elected government of the Second Republic ruled France. As a result, Louis Philippe I, of the Orléanist branch, rose to power, replacing the old Charter by the Charter of 1830, and his rule became known as the July Monarchy. Louis Philippe I was an expert businessman and, by means of his businesses, he had become one of the richest men in France. Roche, just prior to the revolution, 100,000 citizens of Lyon were described as \"indigent\" and by 1840 there were at least 130,000 abandoned children in France. Additionally, there was a major split between the citizens of Paris and those citizens of the more rural areas of France. The population of Paris ballooned as job seekers from all over France came to Paris to work in the newly formed National Workshops. The February Revolution united all classes against Louis Philippe I. Politics in France continued to tilt to the right, as the era of revolution in France came to an end. By the time of the 02 December 1848, 1851 coup, Louis Napoleon had dissolved the National Assembly without having the constitutional right to do so, and became the sole ruler of France." .

ns1:58234 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1928 Wimbledon Championships took place on the outdoor grass courts at the All England Lawn Tennis and Croquet Club in Wimbledon, London, United Kingdom. The tournament was held from Monday 25 June 1928 until Saturday 07 July 1928. It was the 48th staging of the Wimbledon Championships, and the third Grand Slam tennis event of 1928." .

ns1:58275 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1988 Tirreno–Adriatico was the 23rd edition of the Tirreno–Adriatico cycle race and was held from 11 March 1988 to 16 March 1988. The race started in Bacoli and finished in San Benedetto del Tronto." .

ns1:58301 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007–08 Serie A (known as the Serie A TIM for sponsorship reasons) was the 106th season of top-tier Italian association football, the 76th in a round-robin tournament. It started on 25 26 August 2007 and ended on 18 May 2008. (Italian association football's financial watchdogs) into the finances of four Serie A clubs (Inter Milan, Milan, Sampdoria, and Reggina) who stand accused of falsely inflating player values - a practice known as plusvalenze in Italian - in order to qualify financially for the 2005–06 Serie A. An emergency meeting set up between Lega Nazionale Professionisti president Antonio Matarrese and police chief Antonio Manganelli decided that the game between Inter Milan and Lazio would be called off, but the rest of the fixtures would go ahead that day, starting at a slightly later time (about 10 minutes later)." .

ns1:58313 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Capture of Lucknow (Hindi: लखनऊ का क़ब्ज़ा, Urdu: لکھنؤ کا قبضہ) was a battle of Indian Rebellion of 1857. The British recaptured the city of Lucknow which they had abandoned in the previous winter after the relief of a besieged garrison in the Residency, and destroyed the organised resistance by the rebels in the Kingdom of Awadh (or Oudh, as it was referred to in most contemporary accounts)." .

ns1:58332 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Council of Clermont was a mixed synod of ecclesiastics and laymen of the Catholic Church, called by Pope Urban II and held from 18 to 28 01 November 1095 at Clermont-Ferrand, Auvergne, at the time part of the Duchy of Aquitaine." .

ns1:58348 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1995–96 UEFA Cup was the 25th season of Europe's then-tertiary club football tournament organised by Union of European Football Associations. It was 1996 UEFA Cup Final by German club FC Bayern Munich on aggregate over Bordeaux of France. Girondins de Bordeaux went to the finals all the way from the 1995 Union of European Football Associations Intertoto Cup, its first season, being the only Intertoto Cup entrant to reach this far of the UEFA Europa League. With this victory, Bayern became the third club to have 1996 UEFA Cup Final all three major European trophies (European Cup/Union of European Football Associations Champions League, UEFA Europa League/UEFA Europa League, and the Cup Winners' Cup). The 1995–96 season also saw the return of Yugoslav clubs on the international scene after three years of ban due to UN embargo. The access list was finally increased to 96 clubs: The labels in the parentheses show how each team qualified for the place of its starting round: Omonia 1996 UEFA Cup Final 5–1 on aggregate. FC Bayern Munich 1996 UEFA Cup Final 5–1 on aggregate. FC Bayern Munich 1996 UEFA Cup Final 4–1 on aggregate. FC Bayern Munich 1996 UEFA Cup Final 7–2 on aggregate. FC Bayern Munich 1996 UEFA Cup Final 7–2 on aggregate. FC Bayern Munich 1996 UEFA Cup Final 4–3 on aggregate. FC Bayern Munich 1996 UEFA Cup Final 5–1 on aggregate." .

ns1:58350 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Wuhan Open (also known as 2015 Dongfeng Motor Wuhan Open for sponsorship reasons) was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts between 27 September 2015 and 03 October 2015. The 2015 tournament was the 2nd edition of the Wuhan Open. The tournament was held at the Optics Valley International Tennis Center in Wuhan, China." .

ns1:58351 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Rizal Day is a public holidays in the Philippines commemorating the life and works of José Rizal, a national hero of the Philippines. Rizal Day was first instituted with a decree by President Emilio Aguinaldo issued December 20, 1898 and celebrated December 30, 1898 as a national day of mourning for Rizal in Malolos and all victims of the Spanish colonial rule of the Philippines. On his Rizal Day address on December 30, 1937, President Manuel L. Quezon declared through Commonwealth Act No. Being that December 30 is sandwiched between Christmas and New Year's Day, National Historical Commission chairperson Ambeth Ocampo pushed for the moving of Rizal Day from December 30 to June 19, Rizal's birth." .

ns1:58364 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Huawei Mate series (previously the Huawei Ascend Mate series) is a line of high-end Android-powered phablet smartphones produced by Huawei. Since 2016, Huawei has been in a co-engineering partnership with the German manufacturer Leica, whose lenses have subsequently been used on Huawei Mate series. The Huawei Ascend Mate smartphone was announced in 2013 at CES. Huawei announced the Huawei Mate S smartphone on September 2, 2015 at IFA in Berlin. Huawei launched the Huawei Mate SE in the United States on March 6, 2018. Huawei announced three phones in the Huawei Mate 20 series, the Huawei Mate 20, Huawei Mate 20 Pro, and Huawei Mate 20 X at an event in London, England on October 16, 2018, after announcing the cheaper Huawei Mate 20 lite in Berlin, Germany on August 31, 2018." .

ns1:58371 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Elyon (formerly known as Ascent: Infinite Realm) is an upcoming massively multiplayer online role-playing game developed by Krafton and published by Kakao Games." .

ns1:58424 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 38th Academy Awards, honoring the best in film for 1965, were held on 18 April 1966, at the Santa Monica Civic Auditorium in Santa Monica." .

ns1:58428 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1993 European Grand Prix (formally the Sega European Grand Prix) was a Formula One motor race held on 11 April 1993 at Donington Park. The race was contested over 76 laps and was won by Ayrton Senna for the McLaren team, ahead of second-placed Damon Hill and third-placed Alain Prost, both driving for the Williams team. The race was the first held under the European Grand Prix title since 1985, and to date is the only Formula One Grand Prix to have been held at the Donington Park circuit." .

ns1:58454 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2017 Bandy World Championship was the 37th Bandy World Championships and was held in Sweden. The games in Division A were played in Göransson Arena in Sandviken, 29 January 2017 to 05 February 2017." .

ns1:58473 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Jupiter family of super heavy-lift launch vehicle was part of the proposed DIRECT Shuttle-Derived Launch Vehicle architecture. Each Jupiter launch vehicle would use a \"common core stage\" consisting of a tank structure based closely on the existing Space Shuttle External Tank with a pair of standard four-segment Solid Rocket Boosters (SRBs) mounted at the sides as on the Space Shuttle. Before it can be launched, NASA's proposed Ares I rocket would require both a new, modified five-segment version of the Space Shuttle SRB and the J-2X upper stage engine, a modification of the J-2 engine used on the Saturn V. The proposed Jupiter family could have launched with currently available engines, with the ability to upgrade to a more powerful SRB and J-2X upper stage engine should they become available. DIRECT asserted that flying Orion and a separate payload module on a Jupiter would satisfy the safety concerns raised about flying crew separately from cargo following the 2003 Space Shuttle Columbia disaster, since the Orion capsule would still be able to separate from the launch vehicle and any cargo in the event of a launch abort." .

ns1:58481 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Battle of Rawdat Muhanna (Arabic: معركة روضة مهنا or Arabic: معركة روضة ابن مهنا) was a major battle of the Saudi–Rashidi War, during the unification of Saudi Arabia, fought between Rashidi and Saudi rebels. It occurred on 12 April 1906, in Muhanna's Gardens in Al-Qassim Region." .

ns1:58483 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1937 Ryder Cup were held 1937–1937 at the Southport and Ainsdale Golf Club in Southport, England. Due to World War II, this was the final Ryder Cup for a full decade; the series resumed in 1947 Ryder Cup in the U.S. at Portland, Oregon. In 1937, it was announced that, as in 1935 Ryder Cup, a selection committee of six would choose the Great Britain team for the 1937 Ryder Cup." .

ns1:58495 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1989 U.S. Open was the 89th U.S. Open, held June 15–18 at the East Course of Oak Hill Country Club near Rochester, New York. Curtis Strange won his second consecutive U.S. Open, one stroke ahead of runners-up Chip Beck, Mark McCumber, and Ian Woosnam, becoming the first successful defender of a U.S. Open title since Ben Hogan in 1951. Gary Player, the 1965 champion and winner of nine major titles, played in his final U.S. Open in 1989." .

ns1:58548 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Pishan hostage crisis occurred on the night of 28 December 2011, in Koxtag (Kuoshi Tage), Guma County, Hotan Prefecture, Xinjiang Uygur Autonomous Region, China. Guma County is one of the poorest counties in the Xinjiang region, on the southern edge of the Taklamakan Desert bordering Pakistan's Azad Kashmir." .

ns1:58593 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Benin Expedition of 1897 was a punitive expedition by a British force of 1,200 men under Admiral Sir Harry Rawson in response to the ambush of a previous British-led party under Acting Consul General James Philips (which had left all but two men dead). Rawson's troops captured and burned Benin City, bringing to an end the west African Benin Empire. On 1897, Rear-Admiral Harry Rawson, commanding the squadron at the Cape of Good Hope was appointed by the Admiralty to lead an expedition to capture the Benin king and destroy Benin City." .

ns1:58624 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Miss Earth 2005 was the 5th edition of the Miss Earth pageant. At the conclusion of the pageant, Alexandra Braun of Venezuela was crowned Miss Earth 2005 by outgoing titleholder, Miss Earth 2004, Priscilla Meirelles of Brazil." .

ns1:58687 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1985 British Grand Prix (formally the XXXVIII Marlboro British Grand Prix) was a Formula One motor race held at Silverstone Circuit on 21 July 1985. The 65-lap race was won by Alain Prost, driving a McLaren-TAG, after he started from third position. Silverstone Circuit alternated the British Grand Prix with Brands Hatch and each circuit would host it every 2nd year. Qualifying saw Keke Rosberg underline Silverstone Circuit's incredibly high speed laps by taking pole in his Williams with a lap of 1:05.591." .

ns1:58688 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Winter Offensive of 1947 in Northeast China (simplified Chinese: 东北1947年冬季攻势; traditional Chinese: 東北1947年冬季攻勢; pinyin: Dōngběi yījiǔsìqī nián dōngjì gōngshì) was a series of battles initiated by the Communist forces against the Kuomintang (Nationalists) during the Chinese Civil War after World War II. After the Summer Offensive of 1947 in Northeast China and the Autumn Offensive of 1947 in Northeast China, the nationalists were forced to withdraw to 28 cities in Northeast China, including Siping (city) – Dashiqiao, Jilin City, Changchun, and other cities along the Shanhai Pass – Shenyang railroad. While Chen Cheng left Northeast China with Chiang Kai-shek, the new Nationalist commander-in-chief in Northeast China Wei Lihuang was preparing for the worst: giving up Jilin City and Changchun when necessary, while hold on to Shenyang with everything he got. Two days after taking Siping (city), Communists declared the end of Winter Offensive of 1947 in Northeast China on 15 March 1948. The Nationalist troops in Northeast China was compressed into three isolated regions around Jinzhou, Changchun and Shenyang as a result of the three-month-long Winter Offensive of 1947 in Northeast China, and the communist paved the way for the later victory in Liaoshen Campaign and Changchun." .

ns1:58697 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1898 VFL Grand Final was an Australian rules football game contested between the Essendon Football Club and Fitzroy Football Club, held in Melbourne on 24 September 1898. Both the Fitzroy Football Club back-pocket Stan Reid and the Essendon full-forward Charlie Moore would later die in South Africa in active service during the Anglo-Boer War; Moore on 5 May 1901 and Reid on 23. The teams in the two sections played three rounds of four round-robin matches on three consecutive Saturdays: The winners of the two sections were Fitzroy Football Club and Collingwood respectively (for results of all of the round-robin matches and, a week later (1898), the two teams played each other at the Brunswick Street Oval, with Fitzroy Football Club winning the match 2.10 (22) to 1.5 (11). Although Essendon and Fitzroy Football Club could not agree on the choice of a single venue, they suggested three mutually acceptable venues for the match: After the VFL referred the decision to the VFL's Match Arrangement Committee, they, upon the express instructions of that committee, rejected the suggestions of the two teams outright and the match was controversially scheduled to be played at the Junction Oval. Fitzroy Football Club went on to beat Essendon 5.8 (38) to 3.5 (23)." .

ns1:58734 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of La Tremblaye (15 October 1793) took place near Cholet during the War in the Vendée, and was a Republican victory over the Vendéens. The three republican armies converged towards Cholet where the decisive battle seemed to have to take place. On 15 October 1793, the troops of the Army of Mainz and of the Luçon division started the attack close to the castle of La Tremblaye, south-west of Cholet." .

ns1:58745 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "In J. R. R. Tolkien's legendarium, the Nírnaeth Arnoediad, also called the history of Middle-earth, began when the Ainur entered Arda, following the creation events in the Ainulindalë and long ages of labour throughout Eä, the fictional universe. The Eldar, Edain and the Dwarves were defeated in the Nírnaeth Arnoediad or Battle of Unnumbered Tears, and one by one, the kingdoms fell, even the hidden ones of Hithlum and Gondolin." .

ns1:58753 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "lynching of David Jones was an African-American man who was lynched in Nashville on 25 March 1872 after being arrested as a suspect in a killing. lynching of David Jones, an African-American man, was accused of murdering white man Henry Murray, who died in 25 March 1872. At 7pm on 25 March 1872, a mob met outside the Maxwell House Hotel in Downtown Nashville. The Nashville Union and American suggested the lynching was perpetrated by \"twenty or more negroes.\" In June 2019 lynching of David Jones is to be acknowledged by his name on a memorial in downtown Nashville as a victim of lynching in 1872. A metropolitan coalition known as \"We Remember Nashville,\" together with the Equal Justice Initiative, plans events and education that week related to the four cases of lynching in the city in the late 19th century." .

ns1:58770 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 Hulu Langat landslide was a landslide that occurred on 21 May 2011, in Hulu Langat, Selangor, Malaysia. 16 people—15 children and a caretaker of an orphanage—were killed in the landslide which was caused by heavy rains at the Children's Hidayah Madrasah Al-Taqwa orphanage in Kampung Sosrowijayan (FELCRA Semungkis), Hulu Langat, Selangor, southeast of Kuala Lumpur. The Children's Hidayah Madrasah Al-Taqwa orphanage is located at Jalan Sungai Semungkis, Kampung Sosrowijayan (FELCRA Semungkis), Hulu Langat, Selangor, a hilly rural area vulnerable to landslides." .

ns1:58771 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Monte Carlo Rally (formally known as the 85ème Rallye Automobile Monte-Carlo) is a motor racing event for rallying that was held over four days between 2017 and 22 January 2017. It marks the eighty-fifth running of the Monte Carlo Rally, and is the first round of the 2017 World Rally Championship, WRC-2 and WRC-3 seasons." .

ns1:58820 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Faciens misericordiam (Granting forgiveness) was a papal bull issued by Clement V on August 12, 1308, as part of the trial against the Knights Templar." .

ns1:58844 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege or Siege of Pavia was fought in 773–774 in northern Italy, near Ticinum (modern Pavia), and resulted in the victory of the Franks under Charlemagne against the Lombards under King Desiderius. The Frankish troops then marched on to begin the siege of Pavia by September. In the tenth month of the siege, famine was hitting Pavia hard and Desiderius, realising that he was left on his own, opened the gates to Charles and surrendered on some Tuesday in June. Many Franks entered into positions of power and authority in Italy, though many Lombards, on account of their willingness to make peace with Charles, retained their positions." .

ns1:58848 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Overlord was the codename for the Invasion of Normandy, the Allied operation that launched the successful invasion of German-occupied Western Europe during World War II. The operation was launched on 06 June 1944 with the Normandy landings. Nearly 160,000 troops crossed the English Channel on 06 June 1944, and more than two million Allied troops were in France by the end of August. The coast of Normandy of northwestern France was chosen as the site of the invasion, with the Americans assigned to land at sectors codenamed Utah and Omaha, the British at Sword and Gold, and the Canadians at Juno. Seven of the eleven panzer or panzergrenadier divisions stationed in France were still not fully operational or only partially mobile in early 06 June 1944. Given the Allied air supremacy (4,029 Allied aircraft assigned to operations in Normandy plus 5,514 aircraft assigned to bombing and defence, versus 570 Luftwaffe planes stationed in France and the Low Countries), booby-trapped stakes known as Rommelspargel (Rommel's asparagus) were set up in meadows and fields to deter airborne landings. Nearly 160,000 troops crossed the English Channel on 06 June 1944, and more than two million Allied troops were in France by the end of August. The linkup of the Normandy forces with the Allied forces in southern France occurred on 12 September as part of the drive to the Siegfried Line." .

ns1:58859 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1994 Tour de Romandie was the 48th edition of the Tour de Romandie cycle race and was held from 3 May to 8 04 May 1994. The race started in Marin-Epagnier and finished in Geneva." .

ns1:58880 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016–17 ISU Speed Skating World Cup – World Cup 1 – Women's 500 metres races of the 2016–17 ISU Speed Skating World Cup 1, arranged in the Heilongjiang Indoor Rink, in Harbin, China, were held on 11 November 2016 and 13 November 2016." .

ns1:58926 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009–10 Coupe de la Ligue was the 16th edition of France's league cup, organized by the LFP. The competition began on 25 July 2009 and 2010 Coupe de la Ligue Final was held on 27 March 2010 at the Stade de France. Ligue 1 club Olympique de Marseille won the competition by defeating fellow first division club Bordeaux by a score of 3–1 in 2010 Coupe de la Ligue Final, thus giving them their first trophy since 1992. On 29 June 2009, the Ligue de Football Professionnel board of directors announced that France Télévisions will be the official provider of Coupe de la Ligue coverage for the next three seasons. For the 2009–10 Coupe de la Ligue season, six clubs will be exempted until the Round of 16, the top 5 Ligue 1 finishers from the 2008–09 season and the winner of the 2008–09 Coupe de France." .

ns1:58951 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Kyrgyzstan is scheduled to compete in the Kyrgyzstan at the 2017 Asian Winter Games in Sapporo and Obihiro, Japan from February 19 to 26." .

ns1:58962 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Siege of Lleida took place between 13 01 May 1644 - 30 July 1644 during the Catalan Revolt when a Spanish force under Felipe da Silva besieged and attacked the Franco-Catalan garrison of the town of Lleida - after intense fighting the town finally surrendered on 30 July 1644." .

ns1:58967 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2004 Scottish Challenge Cup Final, also known as the Bell's Cup Final for sponsorship reasons, was an association football match between Falkirk and Ross County F.C. on 2004 at McDiarmid Park in Perth. The quarter-final draw brought with Partick Thistle all the way to Victoria Park which saw Ross County F.C. edging out a 5–3 win on penalties after a 1–1 draw after extra time to progress to the semi-finals. Falkirk had played two games at their shared home of Ochilview Park and two away in the games preceding the final with Ross County F.C. playing a total of three games at Victoria Park and one away from home. Ross County F.C. scored ten goals before the final and conceded a total of five, managing to keep no clean sheets. Falkirk progressed winners through all four games in 90 minutes of play, whereas it took penalties before Ross County F.C. emerged as winners over Partick Thistle in the quarter-final. Ross County F.C. were appearing in the final for the first time in the club's history." .

ns1:58977 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1971-72 Cypriot Cup was the 30th edition of the Cypriot Cup. It began on 15 April 1972 with the first round and concluded on 12 June 1972 with the final which was held at GSP Stadium. AC Omonia won their 2nd Cypriot Cup trophy after beating Pezoporikos Larnaca FC 3–1 in the final." .

ns1:58996 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "George Foreman vs. Tommy Morrison, billed as the \"Star-Spangled Battle\", was a professional boxing match contested between George Foreman and Tommy Morrison on 07 June 1993, for the vacant World Boxing Organization Heavyweight Championship. After WBO Heavyweight champion Michael Moorer opted to vacate the title in February 1993, the WBO sanctioned a match between popular 44-year-old ex-WBC and WBA heavyweight champion George Foreman and then up-and-coming 24-year-old prospect Tommy Morrison to determine who would be the next WBO Heavyweight champion." .

ns1:59011 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Arbuthnot and Ambrister incident occurred in 1818 during the First Seminole War. American General Andrew Jackson invaded Spanish Florida and captured and executed Alexander George Arbuthnot and Robert C. Ambrister, two British subjects charged with aiding Seminole and Creek Indians against the United States of America." .

ns1:59014 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Mersin International Music Festival (Turkish: Mersin Uluslararası Müzik Festivali), merfest for short, is a music festival held annually in Mersin, Turkey. Mersin International Music Festival is a non-governmental project sponsored by private companies, Mersin University, local municipalities and the Ministry of Culture and Tourism. It was established in 2002 and organized until 2007 by Mersin Cultural Society (Turkish: Mersin Kültür Merkezi Derneği). However, every year one concert is held at St Paulus Church in Tarsus and another one at Kanlıdivane, being an outdoor concert. Tarsus is located 27 km (17 mi) east, and Kanlıdivane 55 km (34 mi) west of Mersin." .

ns1:59017 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Wuxi Classic was a professional ranking snooker tournament held between 25 June 2012–01 July 2012 at the Wuxi City Sports Park Stadium in Wuxi, China. The breakdown of prize money for this year is shown below: These matches were played in Wuxi on 25 June 2012 and 2012." .

ns1:59028 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The When You Look Me in the Eyes Tour (or the Look Me in the Eyes Tour), sometimes stylized as the WYLMITE Tour, is the fourth tour by the Jonas Brothers and their second tour to promote their second album, Jonas Brothers. The Look Me in the Eyes Tour started on 31 January 2008 and ended on 22 March 2008." .

ns1:59061 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2005 United Kingdom general election was held on Thursday 05 May 2005, to elect 646 members to the House of Commons. Tony Blair was returned as Prime Minister of the United Kingdom, with Labour having 355 MPs, but with a popular vote share of 35.2%, the smallest of any majority government in UK electoral history. The election came just over three weeks after the dissolution of Parliament on 11 April by Queen Elizabeth II, at the request of the Prime Minister of the United Kingdom, Tony Blair. The UK media interpreted the results as an indicator of a breakdown in trust in the government, and especially in Prime Minister of the United Kingdom Tony Blair. Following the election result, Labour remained in power and Tony Blair remained Prime Minister of the United Kingdom." .

ns1:59076 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Sierra Leone Civil War (1991–2002) was a civil war in Sierra Leone that began on 23 March 1991 when the Revolutionary United Front (RUF), with support from the special forces of Charles Taylor’s National Patriotic Front of Liberia (NPFL), intervened in Sierra Leone in an attempt to overthrow the Joseph Momoh government. On 18 January 2002, President Kabbah declared the Sierra Leone Civil War over. The presence of diamonds in Sierra Leone invited and led to the civil war in several ways. In March 1993, with much help from ECOMOG troops provided by Nigeria, the SLA recaptured the Koidu and Kono diamond districts and pushed the RUF to the Sierra Leone – Liberia border. Operation Khukri was a unique multinational operation launched in the United Nations Assistance Mission in Sierra Leone (UNAMSIL), involving India, Nepal, Ghana, Britain and Nigeria. On 18 January 2002, President Kabbah declared the eleven-year-long Sierra Leone Civil War officially over. A 2001 overview noted that there had been \"serious and grotesque human rights violations\" in Sierra Leone since its civil war began in 1991. In March, the Special Court for Sierra Leone issued its first indictments for war crimes during the civil war. In the 2012 Documentary La vita non perde valore, by Wilma Massucco, former child soldiers and some of their victims talk about the way how they feel and live, ten years after the Sierra Leone civil war ending, thanks to the personal, familiar and social rehabilitation provided to them by Father Giuseppe Berton, an Italian missionary of the Xaverian order. The self-funded film depicted the most brutal period of the civil war in Sierra Leone with RUF rebels capturing the capital city in the late 1990s." .

ns1:59077 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 34th AVN Awards, presented by AVN (AVN), honored the best pornographic movies and adult entertainment products of between October 1, 2015 and September 30, 2016 and took place on 21 January 2017 at The Joint in Hard Rock Hotel and Casino, Paradise, Nevada. During the ceremony, AVN presented AVN Award (often referred to as the Oscars of porn ) in 117 categories. The nominees for the 34th AVN Awards were announced on November 17, 2016, at the annual AVN Award Nominations Party at Avalon nightclub in Hollywood. AVN on December 28, 2016, announced the 2017 inductees into its hall of fame, with the Pleasure Products Branch honorees announced the following day: AVN announced the 2017 AVN Awards Trophy Girls would be Uma Jolie and Gina Valentina." .

ns1:59093 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1990 DFB-Supercup was the fourth DFL-Supercup, an annual association football match contested by the winners of the previous season's Bundesliga and DFB-Pokal competitions. The match was played at the Wildparkstadion, Karlsruhe, and contested by league champions FC Bayern Munich and cup winners 1. FC Kaiserslautern." .

ns1:59097 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "\"Where Have All the Flowers Gone?\" is a modern folk-style song. The melody and the first three verses were written by Pete Seeger in 1955 and published in Sing Out! Additional verses were added in May 1960 by Joe Hickerson, who turned it into a circular song. The 1964 release of the song as a Columbia Records Hall of Fame series 45 single, 13-33088, by Pete Seeger was inducted into the Grammy Hall of Fame in 2002 in the Folk category. Seeger found inspiration for the song in 1955 while he was on a plane bound for a concert at Oberlin College, one of the few venues which would hire him during the McCarthy era. The song appeared on the compilation album Pete Seeger's Greatest Hits (1967) released by Columbia Records as CS 9416." .

ns1:59131 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Portuguese discovery of the sea route to India was the first recorded trip directly from Europe to India, via the Cape of Good Hope. Considered one of the most remarkable voyages of the Age of Discovery, it initiated the Portugal maritime and trade presence in Kerala and the Indian Ocean. The plan for working on the Cape Route to India was charted by Portugal King John II as a cost saving measure in the trade with Asia and also an attempt to monopolize the spice trade. The work celebrates the discovery of a sea route to India by the Portugal explorer Vasco da Gama. As a result, King D. João II of Portugal established a plan for ships to explore the coast of Africa to see if India was navigable via around the cape, and through the Indian Ocean. On 1498, the fleet reached Kappakadavu, near Calicut, in the current Indian state of Kerala, thus having established the route via the Indian Ocean and managing to open the sea route from Europe to India. On July 12, 1499, after more than two years since the beginning of this expedition, the caravel Berrio entered into the river Tagus, commanded by Nicolau Coelho, with the news that thrilled Lisbon: the Portugal had finally reached India by sea." .

ns1:59133 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The figure skating at the 2017 Asian Winter Games – ice dancing event at the 2017 Asian Winter Games was held on 23 February 2017 and 24 February 2017 at the Makomanai Ice Arena in Sapporo, Japan." .

ns1:59149 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 Ford 400 was the thirty-sixth and final stock car race of the 2009 NASCAR Sprint Cup Series as well as the tenth and final race of the season-ending Chase for the Sprint Cup. It was held on 22 November 2009 at Homestead-Miami Speedway, in Homestead, Florida before a crowd of 70,000 people. It was held on 22 November 2009, in Homestead, Florida, at Homestead-Miami Speedway, an intermediate track that holds NASCAR races." .

ns1:59193 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1981 Avon Championships of Chicago was a women's tennis tournament played on indoor carpet courts at the International Amphitheatre in Chicago, Illinois in the United States that was part of the 1981 Avon Championships Circuit. It was the 10th edition of the tournament and was held from 26 January 1981 through 01 February 1981." .

ns1:59194 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Tysami was a military engagement involving a warship from the British China Squadron and the Chinese sea piracy of Chui A-poo. It was fought in 29 September 1849 off Tysami, Mirs Bay, China, and ended with a Royal Navy victory. On 27 29 September 1849, the Columbine left Hong Kong for Mirs Bay and arrived at noon the following day. The sea piracy were searching for some sort of waterway to escape but they found none and it was not until noon the following morning on 29 September 1849 that the main action occurred." .

ns1:59202 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Italian Open (tennis) (also known as the 2018 Rome Masters and sponsored title 2018 Internazionali BNL d'Italia) was a professional tennis tournament played on outdoor clay courts at the Foro Italico in Rome, Italy from 2018–2018. It was the 75th edition of the Italian Open and it is classified as an ATP World Tour Masters 1000 event on the 2018 ATP World Tour and a Premier 5 event on the 2018 WTA Tour." .

ns1:59205 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Shachtmanism is the form of Marxism associated with Max Shachtman (1904–1972). Left Shachtmanism, influenced by Max Shachtman's work of the 1940s, sees Stalinist nations as being potentially imperialist and does not offer any support to their leadership. A more current term for Left Shachtmanism is Third Camp Trotskyism, the Third Camp being differentiated from capitalism and Stalinism." .

ns1:59208 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1981 Syrian parliamentary election were held in Syria on 09 November 1981 and 10 November 1981." .

ns1:59210 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Zaca Fire was a wildfire that began burning in the San Rafael Mountains, northeast of the Santa Ynez Valley in Santa Barbara County, California. The fire had primarily burned away from populated areas in extremely steep and rugged areas of the San Rafael Mountains in the Los Padres National Forest and the Santa Ynez River Recreation Area." .

ns1:59211 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Hurricane Gustav was the only major hurricane to form during the 1990 Atlantic hurricane season. Developing out of a tropical wave on August 24, Gustav tracked westward across the Atlantic Ocean, steadily intensifying. Around this time, Gustav began to interact with the nearby 1990 Atlantic hurricane season, located roughly 460 miles (740 km) from Gustav. Around 0600 UTC on August 31, Hurricane Gustav reached its peak intensity with winds of 120 mph (195 km/h) and a minimum pressure of 956 mbar (hPa; 28.23 inHg), making it the strongest storm of the season." .

ns1:59226 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Liga ASOBAL 2008–09 season (known as the SabadellAtlántico ASOBAL for sponsorship reasons) is the 19th since its establishment. BM Ciudad Real are the defending champions, having won their 3rd La Liga title in Liga ASOBAL 2007–08. A total of 16 teams contest the league, 14 of which have already contested in the Liga ASOBAL 2007–08, and two of which have been promoted from the División de Honor B." .

ns1:59236 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "No Mercy was a professional wrestling pay-per-view (PPV) event produced by the World Wrestling Federation (WWF, now WWE). It took place on 22 October 2000 at the Pepsi Arena in Albany, New York. It was the third event under the No Mercy chronology. No Mercy featured professional wrestling matches that involved different wrestlers from pre-existing feuds, plots, and storylines that were played out on Raw is War and SmackDown!—The World Wrestling Federation's (WWF) television programs. Another predominant professional wrestling stable heading into the event was over the attacker of Stone Cold Steve Austin as Austin continued his search of his attacker at Survivor Series. On the 22 October 2000 episode of Sunday Night Heat, the Commissioner Mick Foley announced that Guerrero could not wrestle on the pay-per-view, due to injury and pitted Mr. Ass and Chyna against Richards and Venis later that night at No Mercy." .

ns1:59261 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Auberoche was fought on 21 October 1345 during the Gascony campaign of 1345 between an Anglo-Gascony force of 1,200 men under Henry, Earl of Derby, and a French army of 7,000 commanded by Louis of Poitiers. The battle took place during the early stages of the Hundred Years' War. Over the centuries, English holdings in France had varied in size, but by 1337 only Gascony in south-western France and Ponthieu in northern France were left. On 24 May 1337, Philip's Great Council in Paris agreed that the Duchy of Aquitaine, effectively Gascony, should be taken back into Philip's hands on the grounds that Edward was in breach of his obligations as a vassal. Although Gascony was the cause of the war, Edward was able to spare few resources for its defence, and previously when an English army had campaigned on the continent it had operated in northern France. The Earl of Northampton would lead a small force to Brittany, a slightly larger force would proceed to Gascony under the command of Henry, Earl of Derby and the main force would accompany Edward to northern France or Flanders. Within days of the battle, Bergerac fell to an Anglo-Gascony assault and was subsequently sacked." .

ns1:59267 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2004 European Aquatics Championships were held in Madrid, Spain from 05 May 2004–16 May 2004, at the M-86 Swimming Center in the southeast of the city. The championships brought together the European Championships in swimming (long course), open water swimming, diving and synchronized swimming." .

ns1:59277 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Recovery of Ré island (French: Reprise de l'Île de Ré) was accomplished by the army of Louis XIII in 01 September 1625, against the troops of the Protestant admiral Soubise and the Huguenot forces of La Rochelle, who had been occupying the Island of Ré since February 1625 as part of the Huguenot rebellions. In 01 September 1625, Montmorency led his large fleet out of Les Sables d'Olonne, and finally defeated the fleet of La Rochelle, commanded by Jean Guiton and Soubise, in front of Saint-Martin-de-Ré on 18 01 September 1625. The conflict clearly showed the dependence of France on foreign navies." .

ns1:59290 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The submarines HMS Vanguard and Le Triomphant submarine collision in the Atlantic Ocean in the night between 03 February 2009–4 03 February 2009. The Royal Navy's HMS Vanguard (S28) and the French Navy's French submarine Triomphant both sustained damage, but no injuries or radioactivity releases were reported. On the 03 February 2009 2009i4 03 February 2009 03 February 2009, the two submarines collided in the Atlantic Ocean. On 6 03 February 2009, the French Ministry of Defence reported that French submarine Triomphant \"collided with an immersed object (probably a container)\" The UK Ministry of Defence initially would not comment that the incident took place. Both vessels returned to home bases under their own power, Vanguard to Her Majesty's Naval Base Clyde in the Firth of Clyde on 14 03 February 2009, and French submarine Triomphant to Île Longue in Brittany, escorted by a frigate as a normal procedure." .

ns1:59296 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The February 5–6, 2010 North American blizzard, commonly referred to as Snowmageddon, was a paralyzing and crippling blizzard that had major and widespread impact in the Northeastern United States. Additionally, this event was the second of three major Mid-Atlantic snowstorms that occurred over a 12-day period; each subsequent storm focused its heaviest snow slightly farther north: the 30 storm (not recognized by NESIS) dropped more than a foot of snow across Virginia and the lower Chesapeake Bay region, while the February 9–10, 2010 North American blizzard bulls-eyed the Maryland-Pennsylvania border with as much as 38.3 inches. The Capital Weather Gang blog on The Washington Post website ran an online poll on February 4, 2010, asking for reader feedback prior to the blizzard, and several blogs, including the paper's own blog, followed that up by using either \"Snowmageddon\" and/or \"Snowpocalypse\" during the following days, before, during, and after the storm hit. During the evening preceding the first blizzard hitting Washington, D.C., most of the United States federal government closed, and press coverage continued to characterize the storm using either \"Snowmageddon\", \"Snowpocalypse\", or both. Additional local reports from Maryland include: Edgemere, 35.4\"; Clarksville, 34.9\"; Crofton, 34.0\"; Columbia, 33.8\"; Laurel, 32.9\"; Pasadena, 31.0\"; Dundalk, 30.5\"; Ellicott City, 30.2\"; Frederick, 29.0\"; Olney, 28.0\"; Germantown, 27.4\"; and Catonsville, 22.9\". Philadelphia's 28.5\" ranked the storm as the city's 2nd greatest on record (since 1872), falling not far behind the blizzard of 1996's 30.7\"." .

ns1:59310 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1976 Winter Paralympics (Swedish: Paralympiska vinterspelen 1976) were the first Winter Paralympic Games. They were held in Örnsköldsvik, Sweden, from 21 to 28 February 1976." .

ns1:59332 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "UFC Fight Night: dos Anjos vs. Alvarez (also known as UFC Fight Night 90) was a mixed martial arts event held on 07 July 2016 at the MGM Grand Garden Arena in Las Vegas, Nevada." .

ns1:59348 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1994 Memorial Cup was held May 14–22 at the Colisée de Laval in Laval, Quebec. It was the 76th annual Memorial Cup competition and determined the major junior ice hockey champion of the Canadian Hockey League (CHL). Chicoutimi defeated Laval four games to two to win the QMJHL championship and earn a berth into the 1994 Memorial Cup. The Laval Titan represented the Quebec Major Junior Hockey League as the host team at the 1994 Memorial Cup. At the 1993 Memorial Cup, Laval finished in third place." .

ns1:59386 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Second War of Kappel (Zweiter Kappelerkrieg) was an armed conflict in 1531 between the Protestant and the Roman Catholic cantons of the Old Swiss Confederacy during the Reformation in Switzerland. The outcome of the war also confirmed and cemented the Catholic majority among the thirteen full members of the Swiss Confederation: after later settlements in Glarus and Appenzell, seven full and two half cantons remained Catholic (Lucerne, Uri, Schwyz, Unterwalden, Zug, Fribourg, Solothurn, and half of Glarus and Appenzell), while four and two halves became firmly Swiss Reformed Protestant (Zurich, Bern, Basel, Schaffhausen, and half of Glarus and Appenzell)." .

ns1:59404 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Japan Football League (Japan: 第14回日本フットボールリーグ, Hepburn: Dai Jūyon-kai Nihon Futtobōru Rīgu) was the fourteenth season of the Japan Football League, the third tier of the Japan association football league system. It has started on 11 March 2012, and finished on 18 November 2012. The league intended to run the season in the usual 18-team format, but after the withdrawal of Arte Takasaki in January no replacement team was invited so in 2012 JFL features only 17 teams. Updated to games played on 18 November 2012 Source: Japan Football League. Updated to games played on 18 November 2012 2012Source: Japan Football League: First round, second roundNotes:† Team played previous season in Regional Leagues." .

ns1:59426 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Paris Games Week, or simply PGW, is a trade fair for video games held annually at the Paris expo Porte de Versailles in Paris, France. Paris Games Week 2020 was going to be held from 23 to 27 October 2010 but due to the COVID-19 pandemic, the physical event was cancelled." .

ns1:59442 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Shanghai Masters was the inaugural edition of the Shanghai Masters snooker tournament and the first ranking event of the Snooker season 2007/2008. It took place between 06 August 2007–12 August 2007 at the Shanghai Grand Stage in Shanghai, People's Republic of China." .

ns1:59450 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2003 Irish Masters was a professional ranking snooker tournament that took place between 25 March 2003–30 March 2003 at the Citywest Hotel in Dublin, Ireland." .

ns1:59451 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2010 Christian and Democratic Union – Czechoslovak People's Party leadership election (KDU-ČSL) was held on 20 November 2010. Then-leader Cyril Svoboda resigned and the party was temporarily headed by Michaela Šojdrová until the leadership election was held." .

ns1:59476 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "On 23 September 2016, five people were killed in a Cascade Mall shooting in Burlington, Washington, U.S. On 23 September 2016, Cetin bought tickets to an evening showing of the movie Snowden at Cascade Mall in Burlington, Washington. The FBI and ATF provided assistance to the Burlington police and Washington state police in investigating the shooting." .

ns1:59513 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 35th People's Choice Awards, honoring the best in popular culture for 2008, was held on 07 January 2009 at the Shrine Auditorium in Los Angeles, California. They were hosted by Queen Latifah in her third straight year as host, and was broadcast on CBS. Due to the 35th anniversary of the People's Choice Awards and People Magazine, the two combined forces to create an award entitled \"Favorite Star Under 35\"." .

ns1:59582 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Eurovision Young Musicians (French: L'Eurovision des Jeunes Musiciens), often shortened to EYM, or Young Musicians, is a biennial classical music competition for European musicians that are aged between 12 and 21. It is organised by the European Broadcasting Union (EBU) and broadcast on television throughout Europe, with some countries holding national selections to choose their representatives for the contest. The first edition of the Eurovision Young Musicians took place in Manchester, United Kingdom on 11 May 1982 and 6 countries took part. The Eurovision Young Musicians, inspired by the success of the BBC Young Musician of the Year, is a biennial competition organised by the European Broadcasting Union (EBU) for European musicians that are 18 years old or younger. The first edition of the Eurovision Young Musicians took place in Manchester, United Kingdom on 11 May 1982 and six countries took part. As of 2018, there have been nineteen editions of the Eurovision Young Musicians competition, a biennial musicians contest organised by member countries of the European Broadcasting Union, with each contest having one winner." .

ns1:59590 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1960 U.S. Women's Open Golf Championship was the 15th United States Women's Open Championship, held July 21–23 at Worcester Country Club in Worcester, Massachusetts. Betsy Rawls won her fourth United States Women's Open Championship, a stroke ahead of runner-up Joyce Ziske." .

ns1:59621 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The athletics at the 1924 Summer Olympics – men's 400 metres hurdles event was part of the athletics at the 1924 Summer Olympics at the 1924 Summer Olympics. The competition was held on Sunday, 06 July 1924 and on Monday, 07 July 1924." .

ns1:59634 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Bell Challenge 2013 was a tennis tournament played on indoor carpet courts. It was the 21st edition of the National Bank Cup, and was part of the WTA International tournaments of the 2013 WTA Tour. It took place at the PEPS in Quebec City, Canada, from September 9 through 15 September 2013." .

ns1:59684 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "British Rail Classes 371, 381 and 471, Class 381 and Class 471 were proposed electric multiple unit classes from the Networker family of trains intended to operate services in the south-east of England. In the event, British Rail's financial limitations led to the proposal being cancelled. The cancellation of the British Rail Classes 371, 381 and 471, 381 and 471 led to an alternative proposal." .

ns1:59694 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Lyndanisse (also spelled the Battle of Lindanise) was a battle during the Livonian Crusade, fought between the Denmark with various German allies and pagan Estonian tribes. The battle helped King Valdemar II of Denmark establish the territory of Danish Estonia during the Northern Crusades, which were undertaken in response to calls from the Pope. The Danes defeated the Estonians at Lindanise (Danish: Lyndanisse, today Tallinn). Today, the Battle of Lyndanisse is perhaps best known for a legend that the Danish flag, the Dannebrog, fell from the sky just as the Danes were about to lose the battle. The crusading army camped at Lindanise and built a castle there, named Castrum Danorum, which the Estonians called Taani-linn (later Tallinn), meaning Danish town. On 15 June 1219, the Estonians attacked the Danes near the castle, right after suppertime. Tradition has maintained that the Danish flag appeared at the Battle of Lyndanisse on 15 June 1219. According to legend, Denmark received its national flag, the Dannebrog, during the battle." .

ns1:59702 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Storming of Kristianopel occurred on 26 June 1611, during the Kalmar War. Gustavus Adolphus of Sweden led an assault from the camp in Högsby in Småland on Kristianopel in Blekinge." .

ns1:59707 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 12th AVN Awards ceremony, organized by Adult Video News (AVN) took place on 07 January 1995, at Bally's Las Vegas Hotel & Casino, Paradise, Nevada beginning at 7:45 p.m. PST / 10:45 p.m. EST. During the ceremony, AVN presented AVN Award (commonly referred to as the Oscars of porn) in 89 categories honoring the movies released during the period December 1, 1993 to November 30, 1994. The nominees for the 12th AVN Awards were announced at the AVN nominations luncheon 30 days prior to the awards ceremony. AVN publisher AVN shared a moment of remembrance for industry performers who died over the past 12 months: ^1 The wrong winner was announced at the show." .

ns1:59714 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1924 United States presidential election in New Mexico took place on 04 November 1924. New Mexico was won by incumbent President of the United States Calvin Coolidge, who ran against West Virginia Congressman John W. Davis, and Progressive Party icon Robert La Follette." .

ns1:59715 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Action off Măcin was a naval engagement between a torpedo boat of the Romanian Navy with a mixed Romanian-Russian crew and a monitor of the Ottoman Navy which took place during the 1877-1878 Russo-Turkish War of 1877–78. The attack of Rândunica took place during the night of 25 May 1877–26 May 1877, near Măcin." .

ns1:59752 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "United Nations General Assembly Resolution 96 of 11 December 1946, titled \"The Crime of Genocide\", was a United Nations General Assembly resolution of the United Nations General Assembly during its first session that affirmed that genocide was a crime under international law." .

ns1:59758 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1975 Near Islands earthquake occurred at 08:43 UTC on 02 February 1975 off the coast of Attu Island, Alaska. The earthquake had a surface wave magnitude of 7.6 and a maximum Mercalli intensity of IX (Violent)." .

ns1:59761 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1996 Heineken Cup Final was the final match of the 1995–96 Heineken Cup, the inaugural season of Europe's top club rugby union competition. The match was played on 1996 at the Cardiff Arms Park in Cardiff RFC. Then, going into the final few seconds of extra time, the referee penalised Cardiff RFC for using hands in the ruck; Deylaud slotted the resulting penalty to seal Toulouse's victory." .

ns1:59762 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Black Thursday were a devastating series of fires that swept the state of Victoria, Australia, on 06 February 1851, burning up 5 million hectares (12 million acres; 50,000 square kilometres; 19,000 square miles), or about a quarter of the state's area. Within the last two hundred years, the area has experienced and documented at least bushfires in Australia, beginning with Black Thursday in 1851." .

ns1:59791 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Byline Festival is a festival whose aim is to promote independent journalism and free speech. The festival was founded by Stephen Colegrave and Peter Jukes in 2017. The festival takes Place at Pippingford Park in the Ashdown Forest." .

ns1:59801 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "KSR v. Teleflex, 550 U.S. 398 (2007), is a decision by the Supreme Court of the United States concerning the issue of obviousness as applied to patent claims. oral argument were heard by the Supreme Court on 28 November 2006. On 30 April 2007, the Supreme Court unanimously reversed the judgment of the Federal Circuit, holding that the disputed claim 4 of the patent was obvious under the requirements of 35 U.S.C." .

ns1:59814 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Kozludzha (also known as the Battle of Kozluca), fought on 20 June (Old Style - 09 June 1774) 1774 near the village of Kozludzha (now Suvorovo, Bulgaria), was one of the final and decisive battles of the Russo-Turkish War of 1768–1774." .

ns1:59828 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 Rally Sweden was the opening round of 2011 World Rally Championship season. Mikko Hirvonen took the 13th WRC win of his career, and his first victory since winning the same event in 2010 Rally Sweden, having overhauled Mads Østberg midway through the second day." .

ns1:59835 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1997 European Challenge Cup Final was the final match of the 1996–97 European Challenge Cup, the inaugural season of Europe's second tier club rugby union competition. The match was played on 1997 at Stade Raoul-Barrière in Béziers. The match was contested by CS Bourgoin-Jallieu and Castres Olympique, who are both from France." .

ns1:59838 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Great Smoky Mountains wildfires, also known as the Gatlinburg wildfire, were a complex of wildfire which began in late 28 November 2016. The fires claimed at least 14 lives, injured 190, and is one of the largest natural disasters in the history of Tennessee. Two unnamed juveniles were initially charged with aggravated arson in connection to the fires; however, charges were later dropped due to lack of evidence as well as language in an agreement between the State of Tennessee and the Department of the Interior which excluded state jurisdiction from prosecuting criminal activities that occurred entirely within the park. Then President-elect Donald Trump tweeted: \"My thoughts and prayers are with the great people of Tennessee during these terrible wildfire." .

ns1:59839 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2005 WNBA All-Star Game was played on 09 July 2005 at Mohegan Sun Arena in Uncasville, home of the Connecticut Sun." .

ns1:59840 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Weissenstein took place between 31 May 1602 and 30 September 1602, during the Polish–Swedish War (1600–11). Two weeks after the capture of Fellin, Grand Crown Hetman Jan Zamoyski led the Polish-Lithuanian army of 2,000 troops to besiege Weissenstein (now known as Paide in Estonia)." .

ns1:59859 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Ústí massacre (Czech: Ústecký masakr, German: Massaker von Aussig) was a lynching of Germans in Ústí nad Labem (Aussig an der Elbe), a largely ethnic German city in northern Bohemia (\"Sudetenland\"), shortly after the end of World War II, on 31 July 1945. Immediately after the explosion, a massacre of Germans, who had to wear white armbands after the war and so were easy to identify, began in four places in the city. The explosion and subsequent massacre were used as a pretext by advocates of the expulsion of Germans from Czechoslovakia. While only indirect evidence survived, they conclude that the explosion and massacre were prepared by Communists within the Czechoslovak secret services, specifically Bedřich Pokorný, leader of Ministry of Interior's Defensive Intelligence (Obranné zpravodajství) department who earlier organised the Brno death march, in order to support the transfer of Germans from Czechoslovakia by presenting to the Potsdam Conference an argument that further cohabitation of Germans with Czechs was impossible." .

ns1:59871 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Federal Communications Commission v. Pacifica Foundation, 438 U.S. 726 (1978), was a landmark decision of the US Supreme Court that defined the power of the Federal Communications Commission over indecent material as applied to broadcasting. On October 30, 1973, FM radio station WBAI in New York City aired a broadcast that included a segment which featured the George Carlin routine \"seven dirty words\" as part of a program about societal attitudes toward language." .

ns1:59903 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Mazagran was a combat between Arab and Berber forces against French troops during the French conquest of Algeria. The small French contingent, holed up in a fortification at Mazagran, near the port city of Mostaganem, withstood several days of assault by `Abd al-Qādir's troops. The battle of Mazagran became the anniversary of the Battalions of Light Infantry of Africa, a French penal military unit. Lieutenant Colonel Dubuessil, the commander of the French garrison at Mostaganem, ordered Captain Lelièvre and 122 men from the 10th Company of the Battalion of Africa to occupy a small fort in the town of Mazagran." .

ns1:59923 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1923 Wimbledon Championships took place on the outdoor grass courts at the All England Lawn Tennis and Croquet Club in Wimbledon, London, United Kingdom. The tournament ran from 25 June 1923 until 07 July 1923. It was the 43rd staging of the Wimbledon Championships, and the first Grand Slam tennis event of 1923." .

ns1:59945 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Rally New Zealand (37th Propecia Rally New Zealand), 11th round of 2007 World Rally Championship season, was run on 31 August to 02 September 2007." .

ns1:60020 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2003 Kazakhstan Premier League was the 12th season of the Kazakhstan Premier League, the highest football league competition in Kazakhstan, and took place between 12 April 2003 and 03 November 2003." .

ns1:60023 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Courtrai took place on 15 September 1793, near Kortrijk, now known as Kortrijk, Belgium. the battle occurred two days after the Battle of Menin during the Flanders Campaign of the Wars of the French Revolution, fought between a Division of the Nicolas Houchard's French Republican Army of the North under Joseph de Hédouville, and an Austrian force under Johann Beaulieu, supported by a British detachment from the forces of the Duke of York. On 12 September Houchard inflicted a serious defeat on the Prince of Orange at Menin, the Dutch abandoning Kortrijk and retreating towards Ghent. On 15 September 1793, as York marched against Menin, Hédouville left Menin with part of his division and sent Demars’ brigade of 3,000 men down the Lys river to threaten Kortrijk." .

ns1:60036 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Marburg Open is a professional tennis tournament played on clay courts. It take place in Marburg, Germany between 24 June 2013 and 30 June 2013." .

ns1:60093 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Glimmerglass Festival (formerly known as Glimmerglass Opera) is an opera company which was founded in 1975 by Peter Macris and presents an annual season of operas at the Alice Busch Opera Theater on Otsego Lake eight miles (13 km) north of Cooperstown, United States of America. It is the second-largest summer opera festival in the United States of America, led by Artistic and General Director Francesca Zambello. The company presented its first season in the summer of 1975, when four performances of La bohème were staged in the auditorium of the Cooperstown High School. The Glimmerglass Festival's Alice Busch Opera Theater, which opened in June 1987, was built on 43 acres (17 ha) of farmland donated by Tom Goodyear, its first chairman." .

ns1:60095 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Anglo-Afghan Treaty of 1919, also known as the Treaty of Rawalpindi, was a treaty which brought the Third Anglo-Afghan War to an end. It was signed on 08 August 1919 in Rawalpindi by Great Britain and Afghanistan." .

ns1:60150 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1951 PGA Championship was the 33rd PGA Championship, held June 27 to July 3 at Oakmont Country Club in Oakmont, a suburb northeast of Pittsburgh. It marked the first time the PGA Championship returned to a venue; Oakmont had hosted in 1922 (at the time, it had also hosted two U.S. The match play format at the PGA Championship in 1951 called for 12 rounds (216 holes) in seven days: Runyan did not advance in the six-way playoff for the one final spot in the match play field." .

ns1:60155 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Kircholm (27 September 1605, or 17 September in the Old Style calendar then in use in Protestant countries) was one of the major battles in the Polish–Swedish War (1600–11). The battle ended in the decisive victory of the Polish–Lithuanian forces, and is remembered as one of the greatest triumphs of Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth cavalry. On 27 September 1605, the Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth and Swedish forces met near the small town of Salaspils (now Salaspils in Latvia, some 18 km south east of Riga). After the defeat, the Swedish king was forced to abandon the siege of Riga and withdraw by ship back across the Baltic Sea to Sweden and to relinquish control of northern Latvia and Estonia. A truce was eventually signed in 1611, but by 1617 war broke out again, and finally in 1621 the new Swedish king, Gustavus Adolphus, landed near Riga and took the city with a brief siege, wiping away – in Swedish eyes – much of the shame suffered at Salaspils." .

ns1:60159 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1903 German football championship Final decided the winner of the 1903 German football championship, the 1st edition of the German association football championship, a knockout association football cup competition contested by the regional league winners to determine the national champions. 1. FC Lokomotive Leipzig won the match 7–2 against DFC Prag to become the first national champions in German association football history." .

ns1:60164 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Escape Creek was a naval engagement fought between the United Kingdom's Royal Navy and the Qing Chinese naval force on 25 May 1857–27 May 1857 during the Second Opium War." .

ns1:60173 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Basian was fought, in the 13th century, between the armies of the Kingdom of Georgia and the Seljuqid Sultanate of Rum in the Phasiane valley, 60 km northeast of the city of Erzurum in what is now northeast Republic of Turkey. The battle is variously dated between 1202 and 1205, but 1203 or 1204 has lately been given preference. Soon after the battle, the Kingdom of Georgia invaded Trebizond to create a state." .

ns1:60189 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1910 Paris–Roubaix was the 15th edition of the Paris–Roubaix, a classic one-day cycle race in France. The single day event was held on 27 March 1910 and stretched 266 km (165 mi) from Paris to its end in a velodrome in Roubaix. The winner of this race for professionals was Octave Lapize from France." .

ns1:60196 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Aleppo campaign started with a military operation launched on the northern outskirts of Aleppo in late 25 June 2016, by the Syrian Arab Army. On 31 July, the Army of Conquest launched a counter-offensive both south and north of Aleppo in an attempt to lift the siege on the rebel-held areas of the city. On 11 September 2016, airstrikes interrupted Eid al-Adha celebrations in rebel-held areas of Aleppo and Idlib." .

ns1:60200 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Las Palmas de Gran Canaria International Film Festival (Spanish: Festival Internacional de Cine de Las Palmas de Gran Canaria) is an international film festival that is held in Las Palmas de Gran Canaria in the island of Gran Canaria in the Canary Islands." .

ns1:60215 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Ramstein air show disaster occurred on Sunday, 28 August 1988 during the Flugtag '88 airshow at Ramstein Air Base near Kaiserslautern, West Germany. It is also the third-deadliest aviation accident to happen on German soil, after the 1972 Königs Wusterhausen air disaster and the 2002 Überlingen mid-air collision." .

ns1:60221 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2004 Women's Baseball World Cup was held from July 30 to 2004 in Edmonton, Canada and won by the United States. It was the first Women's Baseball World Cup and was sanctioned by the International Baseball Federation. Competing teams were Australia, Canada, Chinese Taipei (Taiwan), Japan, United States women's national baseball team." .

ns1:60237 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Battle of Orthez 1569 was fought during the French Wars of Religion, at Orthez on Wednesday 24 August 1569. Jeanne III d'Albret (1528–1572), queen of Navarre, and considered “queen of the Huguenots” played a leading role during the French Wars of Religion in the vast territory of Guyenne in southwestern France." .

ns1:60248 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Supertaça Cândido de Oliveira was the 29th edition of the Supertaça Cândido de Oliveira, the annual Portugal association football season-opening match contested by the winners of the previous season's top league and cup competitions (or cup runner-up in case the league- and cup-winning club is the same). The match was contested between the 2006–07 Primeira Liga winners Futebol Clube do Porto, and the 2006–07 Taça de Portugal winners, Sporting CP. The match which was televised on RTP1, saw Sporting CP defeat Futebol Clube do Porto 1–0, with a second half goal from Russian midfielder Marat Izmailov." .

ns1:60254 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Ruthven Barracks that took place over the 10 February 1746 – 11 February 1746 was part of the Jacobite rising of 1745." .

ns1:60276 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Second Battle of Chuenpee was fought between British and Chinese forces in the Pearl River Delta, Guangdong province, China, on 7 07 January 1841 during the First Opium War." .

ns1:60292 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007–08 B PFG was the 53rd season of the Bulgarian B Professional Football Group, the second tier of the Bulgarian football league system. The season started on 18 11 August 2007 and finished on 24 17 May 2008 with the A Group promotion play-off between the runners-up from both divisions." .

ns1:60300 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Aberdeen Donside by-election is a by-election that was held for the Scottish Parliament constituency of Aberdeen Donside on Thursday 20 June 2013, following the death from cancer of the constituency's Member of the Scottish Parliament, Brian Adam. SNP list Member of the Scottish Parliament for the North-East region, Mark McDonald, resigned his seat to contest the constituency by-election." .

ns1:60337 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Coffeeville, fought 05 December 1862, was a military engagement of the American Civil War fought near Coffeeville, Mississippi. On 05 December 1862, under the command of Brigadier General Lloyd Tilghman, the men of Baldwin, Tilghman and Rust's brigades with artillery and support from W. H. Jackson's units, hid on a wooded ridge alongside the Water Valley-Coffeeville Road. The Battle of Coffeeville brought Grant's Mississippi invasion via Tennessee to a halt." .

ns1:60359 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 53rd British Academy Film Awards, given by the British Academy of Film and Television Arts on 09 April 2000, honoured the best in film for 1999. The ceremony took place at the Odeon Leicester Square in London and was hosted by Jack Docherty." .

ns1:60376 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2007 Slovenian presidential election were held in Slovenia in October and 11 November 2007 to elect the successor to the second President of Slovenia Janez Drnovšek. In the second round, held on 11 November 2007, Peterle faced the runner-up, the left-wing candidate Danilo Türk. The Social Democrats had become the most popular party in opinion polls and were considered the likely winners at the next general election in 2008; opinion polls indicated that Pahor would easily win the presidential election. Liberal Democracy of Slovenia (LDS), which had earlier discussed the candidacy with Danilo Türk, subsequently nominated Mitja Gaspari, the former Governor of the Bank of Slovenia. The official election campaign began in late 2007. This was a reference to the journalists' petition, the timing of the Supreme Court's decision on the Rožman case and misinterpretation of Janša's and Minister of Economy Andrej Vizjak's remarks on reasons for Slovenia's high inflation in 2007. The opposition parties said that talk of resignation just weeks before Slovenia took over European Union presidency was irresponsible and unwise, but the Prime Minister called a vote of confidence for 2007." .

ns1:60392 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Second Battle of Wissembourg from 26 December 1793 to 29 December 1793 saw an army of the First French Republic under General Lazare Hoche fight a series of clashes against an army of Austrians, Prussians, Bavarians, and Hessians led by General Dagobert Sigmund von Wurmser. War of the First Coalition were in their turn dispossessed by Hoche on 26 December 1793 and forced to retreat behind the Rhine." .

ns1:60451 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Flying Tiger Line Flight 282 refers to the aviation accident of a Lockheed Super Constellation aircraft, N6915C, shortly after takeoff from San Francisco International Airport in the early morning hours of Thursday, 24 December 1964. On Wednesday, December 23, 1964, Flying Tiger Line Flight 282 arrived at San Francisco International Airport from Japan." .

ns1:60494 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Rugby World Cup Sevens was the sixth edition of the Rugby World Cup Sevens. The tournament was held at Luzhniki Stadium in Moscow, Russia. World Rugby, then known as the International Rugby Board (IRB), initially stated that the Rugby World Cup Sevens would be scrapped if rugby sevens were to be included in the Olympic programme for the 2016 Summer Olympics. In December 2009, the IRB confirmed that the governing rugby boards of Brazil (Brazilian Rugby Association), Germany (German Rugby Federation) and Russia (Rugby Union of Russia) formally expressed their intention to tender to host the tournament. In February 2010, the IRB reported that Rugby Union of Russia had formally submitted its tender for the right to host Rugby World Cup Sevens 2013, while the Brazilian and German rugby boards had confirmed their withdrawal from the bidding process. Six days before hosting the 2010 IRB Junior World Rugby Trophy, Russia was officially named as host at the IRB annual congress on 12 May 2010." .

ns1:60515 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Grand Est Open 88 was a professional tennis tournament played on outdoor clay courts. It took place in Contrexéville, France, on 10 July 2017–16 July 2017." .

ns1:60525 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Lee-Lin rivalry was a rivalry between two professional badminton players, Lee Chong Wei and Lin Dan. Lee Chong Wei and Lin Dan are by far two of the most dominant players across three generations and many regard Lin Dan as the greatest of all time, leading to media and players alike to liken the period between 2006 and 2016 as the Lin-Lee era. In the semi-finals of the 2016 Rio Olympics, a match widely touted to be the de facto final for possibly the last Olympic Games of both athletes' careers, Lee Chong Wei won against Lin Dan in his third try on the Olympic stage and became the second shuttler in any discipline of the sport in history to reach 3 consecutive finals after Fu Haifeng who had achieved the feat a day earlier in men doubles. Over time, hostility became admiration, hatred became respect as fans realised that what they were witnessing was a once in a generation great sporting rivalry of the highest level and began cheering for both players, most evidently when they played in Malaysia and People's Republic of China. Most evidently, when Lee returned from his 8-month doping suspension to represent Malaysia in the 2015 Sudirman Cup held in Dongguan, People's Republic of China, he was given a rousing ovation when announced onto court to take on Lee Dong-keun. Most fans and professionals regard Lee Chong Wei and Lin Dan as among the finest shuttlers the sport has ever seen, becoming huge crowd attractions and their battles dubbed the 'Lin-Lee War'. Lee Chong Wei is record title holder for several tournament: Malaysia Open (12 titles), first non-Indonesian won Indonesia Open 6 times, Japan Open (6 times). The Lee-Lin rivalry has been hailed as the greatest rivalry in badminton and one that transformed the world of badminton." .

ns1:60532 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1950 South Carolina gubernatorial election was held on 1950, to select the Governor of South Carolina of the state of South Carolina. James F. Byrnes won the Democratic primary and ran unopposed in the general election becoming the 104th Governor of South Carolina. The South Carolina Democratic Party held their primary for Governor of South Carolina on July 11. The general election was held on 1950 and James F. Byrnes was elected the next Governor of South Carolina without opposition." .

ns1:60550 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1971–72 UEFA Cup was the inaugural year of the UEFA Cup (now known as the UEFA Europa League), which effectively replaced the Inter-Cities Fairs Cup. It commenced on 14 September 1971 and concluded on 17 May 1972. First legs were played on 14 September 1971–1971 (except one match which was played on 22 September). Tottenham Hotspur F.C. won 15–1 on aggregate. Tottenham Hotspur F.C. won 1–0 on aggregate. Tottenham Hotspur F.C. won 3–1 on aggregate." .

ns1:60563 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Government of India Act 1935 was an Act of the Parliament of the United Kingdom. It received Royal Assent and passed into law on 02 August 1935." .

ns1:60572 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012–13 ISU Speed Skating World Cup Final was held in the Thialf arena in Heerenveen, Netherlands, from Friday, 08 March 2012, until Sunday, 10 March 2012 2013." .

ns1:60614 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017–18 2. Bundesliga was the 44th season of the 2. Bundesliga. It began on 28 July 2017 and concluded on 13 May 2018." .

ns1:60630 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2001 IAAF World Indoor Championships were held at the Altice Arena in Lisbon, Portugal from March 9 to 2001. 1997 | 1999 IAAF World Indoor Championships | 2001 | 2003 IAAF World Indoor Championships | 2004. 1997 | 1999 IAAF World Indoor Championships | 2001 | 2003 IAAF World Indoor Championships | 2004." .

ns1:60641 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "On 01 July 1965 Continental Airlines Flight 12 overran the runway while attempting to land at Kansas City Municipal Airport. Continental Airlines Flight 12 was a scheduled domestic passenger flight from Los Angeles International Airport to O'Hare International Airport, with an intermediate stop in Kansas City." .

ns1:60685 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1980 Bolivian general election were held in Bolivia on 29 June 1980, the third in three years. Accordingly, the National Congress revalidated the 1980 election results on 23 September and overwhelmingly elected Hernán Siles Zuazo as president on 5 October. Gueiler called new elections to be held on 29 June 1980, the third consecutive presidential election in a row. On 1980, just 27 days before the scheduled election date, a plane carrying six UDP politicians including Hernán Siles Zuazo's running mate, Jaime Paz Zamora, crashed over the Altiplano." .

ns1:60693 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Treaty of Valençay (11 December 1813), after the château of the same name belonging to former French foreign minister Charles Maurice de Talleyrand, was drafted by Antoine René Mathurin and José Miguel de Carvajal y Manrique on behalf of the First French Empire and the Spanish Crown respectively. Intended as the preliminary to a full peace treaty between France and Spain, the agreement provided for the restoration of Ferdinand VII of Spain, imprisoned at Valençay since 1808, to the Spanish throne usurped by Joseph Bonaparte." .

ns1:60747 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "A marksman is a person who is skilled in precision shooting using projectile weapons (in modern days most commonly an accurized scoped long gun such as designated marksman rifle or a sniper rifle) to shoot at high-value targets at longer-than-usual ranges. In popular and historical usage, \"sharpshooter\" and \"marksman\" are considered synonyms. Within the shooting sports and military usages today, however, sharpshooter and marksman refer to distinctly different levels of skill, which are never conflated. Specifically, in the US Army, \"marksman\" is a rating below \"sharpshooter\" and \"expert\". Four levels of skill are generally recognized today in American military and civilian shooting circles: unqualified, marksman, sharpshooter, and expert. In the United States Army and Marine Corps, the marksmanship of the soldiers is ranked based on their skill: marksman-sharpshooter-expert. Candidates must achieve recce qualification and marksman designation before being considered for the basic sniper course." .

ns1:60756 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The International Style or internationalism is a major architectural style that was developed in the 1920s and 1930s and was closely related to modernism and Modern Movement. Marcel Breuer, a recognized leader in Béton Brut (Brutalist) architecture and notable alumni of the Bauhaus, who also pioneered the use of plywood and tubular steel in furniture design, and who after leaving the Bauhaus would later teach alongside Gropius at Harvard, is as well an important contributor to Modern Movement and the International Style. The \"International Style\", as defined by Hitchcock and Johnson, had developed in 1920s Western Europe, shaped by the activities of the Dutch De Stijl movement, Le Corbusier, and the Deutscher Werkbund and the Bauhaus." .

ns1:60778 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1808 Pennsylvania gubernatorial election occurred on 1808. Democratic-Republican candidate Simon Snyder, former Speaker of the Pennsylvania House of Representatives defeated Federalist candidate and former U.S." .

ns1:60818 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Fetterman Fight, also known as the Fetterman Massacre or the Battle of the Hundred-in-the-Hands or the Battle of a Hundred Slain, was a battle during Red Cloud's War on 21 December 1866, between a confederation of the Lakota, Cheyenne, and Arapaho tribes and a detachment of the United States Army, based at Fort Phil Kearny, Wyoming. He established three forts along the trail, including his headquarters at Fort Phil Kearny, near present-day Buffalo, Wyoming. United States Army Wood-cutting detachment rescue party from Fort Phil Kearny, Dakota Territory, 21 December 1866, Brevet Lieutenant Colonel Captain William J. Fetterman†, commanding." .

ns1:60831 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Saint Pablo Tour was a concert tour by American rapper Kanye West which ran from August 25, 2016 to November 19, 2016 in support of West's seventh solo studio album, The Life of Pablo (2016). The Yeezus Tour was a feat in terms of design and production, but the Saint Pablo Tour is a feat in terms of engineering—and few artists can say they’ve created a transcendent experience that goes beyond what is expected of a “concert.”." .

ns1:60843 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2019 World Women's Handball Championship, the 24th event hosted by the International Handball Federation, was held in Japan from 30 November 2019 to 15 December 2019. The draw was held on 2019 in Tokyo, Japan." .

ns1:60847 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Torrijos–Carter Treaties (Spanish: Tratados Torrijos-Carter) are two treaty signed by the United States and Panama in Washington, D.C., on 07 September 1977, which superseded the Hay–Bunau-Varilla Treaty of 1903. The treaty are named after the two signatories, U.S. President Jimmy Carter and the Commander of Panama's National Guard, General Omar Torrijos. The new government was consolidated under Omar Torrijos, who decided to definitively reject the 1967 treaty. A few days before final agreement on the treaty was reached, President Jimmy Carter had sent a telegram to all members of Congress informing them of the status of the negotiations and asking them to withhold judgment on the treaty until they had an opportunity to carefully study it." .

ns1:60854 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The alpine skiing at the 1968 Winter Olympics – men's downhill competition of the alpine skiing at the 1968 Winter Olympics was held at Chamrousse on Friday, 9 February. The defending world champion was Jean-Claude Killy of France, who was also the defending World Cup downhill champion and Austria's Gerhard Nenning led the current season." .

ns1:60880 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Truce of Łęczyca was signed during the Polish–Teutonic War (1431–1435) between the Kingdom of Poland and the Teutonic Order in Łęczyca on 15 December 1433 (this has also led some Polish historians to divide this Polish–Teutonic War into two wars, in 1431–1433 and in 1435)." .

ns1:60898 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Sun Bowl, part of the 2007-08 NCAA football bowl games season, was played on 31 December 2007, at Sun Bowl Stadium on the campus of the University of Texas, El Paso, between the South Florida Bulls and the Oregon Ducks." .

ns1:60917 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1965 Ryder Cup were held 1965–1965 at Royal Birkdale Golf Club in Southport, England. For the 1965 event the British PGA reduced the period that a tournament professional had to wait before becoming eligible for the Ryder Cup team from five years to three. The field for the 1965 Esso Golden Tournament were the first 15 in the Ryder Cup points list." .

ns1:60921 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Battle of Krasne (20 February 1651 – 23 February 1651) was a battle of the Khmelnytsky Uprising. The battle of Krasne took place in the town of Krasne near Tyvriv (Tywrow), when Cossack Colonel Neczaj broke the Treaty of Zboriv, and raided Braclaw Voivodeship. Poles reached Krasne on 20 February 1651, and seized the town after a short skirmish." .

ns1:60933 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1992 United States presidential election in Alabama took place on 03 November 1992, as part of the 1992 United States presidential election. Alabama was won by President of the United States George H. W. Bush (R-TX). The presidential contest in Alabama was not a surprise, with Bush winning 47.65% to 40.88% over Arkansas Governor Bill Clinton (D), a margin of 6.77%." .

ns1:60951 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "pencak silat at the 2005 Southeast Asian Games at the 2005 Southeast Asian Games took place in the Cebu Coliseum, Cebu City, Cebu, Philippines." .

ns1:60953 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Asian Women's Volleyball Championship was held in Tianjin, China from 20 to 28 May 2015. If there were more than 16 teams applied for participation, participated teams would be determined by: – The host nation – Top 10 ranked teams from the 2013 Asian Women's Volleyball Championship – Representatives from each of the five Asian Volleyball Confederation." .

ns1:60954 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2003 Tecate Telmex Monterrey Grand Prix was the second round of the 2003 CART season, held on 23 March 2003 on the streets of Parque Fundidora in Monterrey, Nuevo León, Mexico." .

ns1:60957 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Tabqa (2017), part of the Raqqa campaign (2016–17) of the Rojava-Islamist conflict, resulted from a Syrian Democratic Forces (SDF) operation against the Islamic State of Iraq and the Levant (ISIL) to recapture and secure the Tabqa Dam, al-Thawrah (al-Tabqah), Tabqa Airbase, and the surrounding countryside. The assault on these targets by the anti-ISIL forces began on 22 March 2017, and control of Tabqa and the Tabqa Dam was achieved by these forces on 10 May 2017." .

ns1:60981 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "UFC 121 was a mixed martial arts event held by the Ultimate Fighting Championship on 23 October 2010 at the Honda Center in Anaheim, United States of America. The event was the fourth time the UFC has hosted at the Honda Center (formerly Honda Center) in Anaheim following UFC 59, UFC 63 and UFC 76 and the sixth event held in the Greater Los Angeles Area along with UFC 60 and UFC 104. Just like UFC 111, UFC 115, and UFC 118, UFC 121 was shown in movie theaters around the United States of America by NCM Fathom." .

ns1:60989 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Węgierska Górka was a two-day-long defence of a Poland fortified area in south of Silesia during the opening stages of the Invasion of Poland of 1939. At half past 4 in the morning, on 01 September 1939, the German 7th Infantry Division crossed the Poland border in the area of Milówka and started their assault towards the Poland positions. Although the Germans outnumbered the Poland forces stationed along the border at least 100 to 1, it was not until the late evening that they finally arrived at the area of Milówka, located only 10 kilometres (6.2 mi) from the border. Under such circumstance the weak Poland units withdrew to the main lines of the defence in front of Węgierska Górka. By the end of the day the Poles claimed eight enemy armoured vehicles and tanks, while the Germans were forced back to their initial positions between Milówka and Węgierska Górka." .

ns1:60996 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Teen Kids News (initially titled EKN Worldwide Kids News and alternately abbreviated on-air as TKN since 2012) is an educational newsmagazine series aimed at adolescents between the ages of 13–16 years old and their parents that debuted in first-run syndication the weekend of 27 September 2003. Impressed by the positive message of the series, a programming executive at Rustavi 2 in the Republic of Georgia approached Al Primo to create a version of Teen Kids News for the country." .

ns1:61022 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1996 Mountain Dew Southern 500, the 47th running of the Bojangles' Southern 500, was a NASCAR Winston Cup Series race held on 01 September 1996 at Darlington Raceway in Darlington, South Carolina." .

ns1:61092 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Badminton Asia Team Championships were held at the GMC Balayogi Indoor Stadium in Hyderabad, India, on 15 February 2016–21 February 2016 and were organised by Badminton Asia Confederation. Hyderabad was selected over Bangkok in the bidding process for the first ever Badminton Asia Team Championships." .

ns1:61104 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 6 Hours of Silverstone was an endurance sports car racing event held for Le Mans Prototype and Le Mans Grand Touring Endurance cars at the Silverstone Circuit near Silverstone, Northamptonshire, England on 10 April 2015–12 April 2015 before a crowd of 45,000 spectators. A FIA World Motor Sport Council meeting in Doha on 3 December 2014 confirmed the 6 Hours of Silverstone as part of the FIA WEC's 2015 schedule. It was held on 12 April 2015 at the 3.661 mi (5.892 km) 18-turn Silverstone Circuit in Northamptonshire, England, following two days of practice and qualifying." .

ns1:61118 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1993 British Grand Prix was a Formula One motor race held at Silverstone Circuit on 11 July 1993. The 59-lap race was won from pole position by Alain Prost, driving a Williams-Renault. It would be another 27 years before Britain would have two races on the calendar in a single season when Silverstone Circuit hosted both the British Grand Prix and the 70th Anniversary Grand Prix." .

ns1:61133 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 Western & Southern Financial Group Masters and Women's Open (also known as the Western & Southern Financial Group Masters and Western & Southern Financial Group Women's Open for sponsorship reasons) was a tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts. It was the 108th edition of the Cincinnati Masters, and was part of the ATP Masters Series of the 2009 ATP World Tour, and of the Premier Series of the 2009 WTA Tour. Both the men's and the women's events were held at the Lindner Family Tennis Center in Mason, near Cincinnati, Ohio, United States, with the men playing from August 17 through 2009, and the women from August 10 through 2009." .

ns1:61138 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1992 Tour du Haut Var was the 24th edition of the Tour du Haut Var cycle race and was held on 22 February 1992. The race started in Grimaud and finished in Draguignan." .

ns1:61145 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1970 California gubernatorial election was held on 03 November 1970. The incumbent Governor of California, Republican Ronald Reagan, won re-election over Democrat and Speaker of the Assembly Jesse \"Big Daddy\" Unruh." .

ns1:61150 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 FA Community Shield (also known as The FA Community Shield sponsored by McDonald's for sponsorship reasons) was the 87th FA Community Shield, an annual association football match contested by the winners of the previous season's Premier League and FA Cup competitions. The match was contested at Wembley Stadium, London, on 09 August 2009, and contested by 2008–09 Premier League champions Manchester United, and Chelsea F.C. as the winners of the 2008–09 FA Cup, a repeat of the 2007 match. The game ended in a 2–2 draw – the goals coming from Nani and Wayne Rooney for Manchester United, and from Ricardo Carvalho and Frank Lampard for Chelsea F.C. – with Chelsea F.C. winning 4–1 on penalties." .

ns1:61153 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Landing on Humlebæk took place on 04 August 1700, in the Swedish invasion of Denmark during the Great Northern War. Sweden's competitors, Russia, Denmark-Norway and Saxony-Poland–Lithuania, tried to exploit this by forming a coalition in order to regain their earlier losses. However, this new threat Charles had to deal with was averted during the first years of the war due to surprising movements made by the Swedish king's troops, one of them being the landing on Humlebæk, an invasion of Denmark with the aim of capturing Copenhagen undertaken in reaction to the Danish attack on Holstein-Gottorp. Therefore, they sent a combined fleet to help Sweden in the war with Denmark and to sue for peace in the northern affairs.) With some minor stratagem, the Swedish fleet tricked the Danish forces on Zealand to believe a major landing would occur at Rungsted instead of Humlebæk. At 04 August 1700 six o'clock Sweden launched the attack, a cannon strike was heard as a signal and with supporting fire from nearby ships bombarding the Danish defences, the small landing boats closed in to Humlebæk. He ordered the march to continue until the peace treaty was clearly confirmed which it did get on the 23rd when Frederick IV Duke of Holstein-Gottorp, messaged the Swedish king that the peace really was established according to the terms of the Peace of Travendal, and that Denmark would not support the alliance against Sweden in the future. With one less opponent in the war Sweden aid to help the besieged Narva where they delivered a crushing defeat in the battle of Narva which forced Russia to retreat and end their campaign of 1700." .

ns1:61175 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1992–93 Belarusian Premier League was the second season of top-tier association football in Belarus. It started on 01 August 1992 and ended on 17 June 1993. The winners of 1992 Belarusian First League (Dinamo-2 Minsk) were promoted and the league was expanded to 17 teams for one season only. In order to play in Premier League, Dinamo-2 Minsk split from their parent team FC Dinamo Minsk into separate association football club (although both teams remained the parts of the same organization) and changed their name to Belarus Minsk." .

ns1:61196 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Niš was fought on 24 September 1689, near the city of Niš in southern Serbia, between the forces of the Ottoman Empire, and the forces of the Holy Roman Empire as part of the Great Turkish War. After the battle Louis left Lieutenant-general Piccolomini in charge of the Sanjak of Niš and marched to Vidin, where he attacked the Ottoman garrison on 14 October and received its capitulation on 19 October." .

ns1:61198 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Krasos was a battle in the Arab–Byzantine Wars that took place in August 804, between the Byzantine Empire under Emperor Nikephoros I (r. 802–811) and an Abbasid Caliphate army under Ibrahim ibn Jibril." .

ns1:61199 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006 BA-CA-TennisTrophy was a tennis tournament played on indoor hard courts. It was the 32nd edition of the event known that year as the Erste Bank Open, and was part of the International Series Gold of the 2006 ATP Tour. It took place at the Wiener Stadthalle in Vienna, Austria, from 09 October 2006 through 15 October 2006." .

ns1:61210 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015–16 TT Pro League season (known as the Digicel TT Pro League for sponsorship reasons) is the seventeenth season of the TT Pro League, the Trinidad and Tobago professional league for association football clubs, since its establishment in 1999. A total of ten teams are contesting the league, with Central F.C. the defending champions from the 2014–15 TT Pro League." .

ns1:61216 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Peyrestortes (17 September 1793) saw soldiers of the First French Republic fighting troops of the Kingdom of Spain during the War of the Pyrenees. The monument has the following inscription, A la mémoire de l'armée des Pyrénées-Orientales qui combattirent à Peyrestortes sous la conduite des conventionnels Cassanyes, Fabre, des généraux Daoust et Goguet." .

ns1:61219 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Madrid City Council election, also the 2015 Madrid municipal election, was held on Sunday, 24 May 2015, to elect the 10th City Council of the municipality of Madrid. Leading the People's Party (PP) local list was Esperanza Aguirre, former president of the Community of Madrid (2003–2012), president of the Senate of Spain (1999–2002) and minister of Education and Culture (1996–1999), as well as the leader of the regional PP branch since 2004. The election was an unexpectedly close race between Aguirre's PP and former judge Manuela Carmena's Podemos-supported Ahora Madrid (English: Madrid Now) platform. The most expected and tense moment came with the debate between PP candidate Esperanza Aguirre and AM Manuela Carmena, as the most-likely candidates to become the next Mayor of the city." .

ns1:61220 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Cheez-It 355 at The Glen was a NASCAR Sprint Cup Series stock car race that was held on 11 August 2013 at Watkins Glen International in Watkins Glen, New York. Marcos Ambrose was the defending race winner after winning the race in 2011 and 2012 Finger Lakes 355 at The Glen. The third caution came out when Kyle Busch spun into the tires, and was out of gas, the race restarted on lap 63, A couple of laps later, the fourth caution came out for a wreck again on lap 80, some cars were involved in the crash, the race restarted on lap 86, and the fifth caution came out on lap 85 for a crash by Marcos Ambrose, The race restarted on lap 88, and Kyle Busch won his race at Watkins-Glen." .

ns1:61226 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "General Conference is a gathering of members of The Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints (LDS Church), held biannually every April and October at the LDS Conference Center in Salt Lake City. Since October 1848, all of the conferences have been held in Salt Lake City, with the exception of the April 1877 conference, which was held in St. George, Utah. Conferences were held in a bowery in Salt Lake City from 1848 to 1852, in the Old Salt Lake Tabernacle from 1852 to 1867, in the Salt Lake Tabernacle from October 1867 to April 2000, and in the LDS Conference Center since that time. However, due to the COVID-19 pandemic, church leaders announced on March 11, 2020 that the sessions of General Conference, which was planned to convene in the LDS Conference Center, would be held virtually and would also be closed to the public." .

ns1:61237 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Electronic Sports League One Cologne 2014, known as ESL One Cologne 2014 for short, was the third Counter-Strike: Global Offensive Major Championship that was held during Gamescom 2014 from 14 August 2014–17, 2014 at the Cologne Exhibition Centre in Cologne, North Rhine-Westphalia, Germany." .

ns1:61264 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014–15 Swiss Cup was the 90th season of Switzerland's annual football cup competition. The competition started on 23 August 2014 with the first games of Round 1 and ended on 07 June 2015 with the Final, won by FC Sion." .

ns1:61268 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Miss Universe 1952, the 1st Miss Universe pageant, was held on 28 June 1952 at the Long Beach Municipal Auditorium in Long Beach, California, United States." .

ns1:61277 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "UFC 151 was a planned mixed martial arts event that was to be held by the Ultimate Fighting Championship on 01 September 2012, at the Mandalay Bay Events Center in Las Vegas. Initially, White announced that Jones would defend his title against Lyoto Machida at UFC 152 and that UFC 151 simply would not take place. Initial reports indicated that UFC 152, scheduled to take place three weeks later, would be renamed UFC 151. However, Zuffa announced that UFC 152 would keep its name and UFC 151 would officially be deemed a \"lost show\"." .

ns1:61285 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2000 Asian Women's Handball Championship, the eighth Asian Women's Handball Championship, which was taking place from 10 to 17 August 2000 10 2000 in Shanghai, China women's national handball team." .

ns1:61288 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2001 Immanuel bus attack was an ambush attack by Palestinian militants targeting Israeli civilians on the West Bank on 12 December 2001. On 12 December 2001, three armed Palestinians militants planted a roadside bomb beside the road leading to the Jewish settlement of Immanuel." .

ns1:61294 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The UCI Road World Championships – women's time trial at the 2002 UCI Road World Championships – Women's time trial took place over a distance of 23.2 kilometres (14.4 miles) in Heusden-Zolder, in the province of Limburg, Belgium on 09 October 2002." .

ns1:61297 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "UFC 111 was a mixed martial arts event held by the Ultimate Fighting Championship (UFC) on 27 March 2010 at the Prudential Center in Newark, New Jersey, United States of America." .

ns1:61307 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Dawn 3 or Operation Valfajr-3 (Persian: عملیات والفجر-۳) was an operation during Iran-Iraq war which was commenced on 3 09 August 1983 at 23 o'clock with the operation code of \"Ya Allah\" (Persian/Arabic: یاالله). This operation was launched in the range of \"semi-extensive operation\" located in the general area of Mehran (in Ilam Province); and eventually led in the liberation of Mehran by Iranian forces." .

ns1:61334 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2001 FIBA Africa Championship was hosted by Morocco from 04 August 2001 to 12 August 2001. The games were played in Rabat and Casablanca." .

ns1:61338 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Raid on Saint-Paul was an amphibious operation conducted by a combined British Army, Royal Navy and Royal Marines force against the fortified French port of Saint-Paul on Réunion (now known as Réunion) during the Napoleonic Wars. The operation was launched on 20 28 September 1809 as both a precursor to a future full-scale invasion of Réunion and in order to capture the French frigate Caroline and the East Indiamen she had seized in the Action of 31 May 1809 which were sheltering in the harbour. The French Indian Ocean territories of Île de France and Réunion were heavily fortified island bases from which French frigates were able to launch raids against British trade routes across the Indian Ocean during the Napoleonic Wars. The Royal Navy was also preparing an operation in the region, a staggered campaign intended to blockade, isolate and subsequently capture both Île de France and Réunion, eliminating the final French territories and bases east of Africa. Feretier was prevented from attempting to reach Port Louis by Rowley's blockading squadron and instead put his ships into Saint-Paul on Réunion to unload his captured vessels and replenish his supplies. At 05:00 on 21 28 September 1809, Nereide entered the bay of Saint-Paul under the cover of darkness and successfully landed the British force, without any sign they had been sighted from the shore, at the Pointe des Galets." .

ns1:61342 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Aeroflot Flight 1691 crashed near Vnukovo International Airport on 17 March 1979 killing 58 of the 119 people on board." .

ns1:61375 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Dekemvriana (Greek: Δεκεμβριανά, \"December events\") refers to a series of clashes fought during World War II in Athens from 03 December 1944 to 11 January 1945. The conflict was the culmination of months of tension between the communist EAM, some parts of its military wing, the Greek People's Liberation Army stationed in Athens, the KKE and the OPLA from one side and from the other side, the Greek Government, some parts of the Hellenic Royal Army, the Hellenic Gendarmerie, the Cities Police, the far-right Organization X, among others and also the British Army. The clashes were limited to Athens, while elsewhere in Greece the situation remained tense but peaceful, with the exception of Epirus where Aris Velouchiotis attacked the forces of Napoleon Zervas. The Dekemvriana ended with the defeat of EAM-Greek People's Liberation Army, leading to its disarmament in the Varkiza Agreement which marked the end of Greek People's Liberation Army. By 1944, the two major resistance movements in occupied Greece, EDES and EAM-Greek People's Liberation Army, each saw the other to be their great enemy. On December 1, 1944, the Greek government of \"National Unity\" under Georgios Papandreou and Gen. Scobie (British head of the Allied forces in Greece at that time) announced an ultimatum for the general disarmament of all guerrilla forces by 10 December, excluding those allied to the government (the 3rd Greek Mountain Brigade and the Sacred Band) and also a part of EDES and Greek People's Liberation Army that would be used in Allied operations in Crete and the Dodecanese (still under German occupation), if it was necessary. This signalled the beginning of the Dekemvriana (Greek: Δεκεμβριανά, \"the December events\"), a 37-day period of full-scale fighting in Athens between EAM fighters and smaller parts of Greek People's Liberation Army, and the forces of the British army and the government. In the early morning hours of December 4, Greek People's Liberation Army reservists began operations in the Athens–Piraeus area, attacking Grivas' X forces and many police stations with success. This might explain the simultaneous skirmishes with the British, the large-scale Greek People's Liberation Army operations against trotskyists, anarchists and other political dissidents in Athens, and the many contradictory decisions of EAM leaders. On 15 11 January 1945, Scobie agreed to a ceasefire in exchange for the Greek People's Liberation Army's withdrawal from its positions at Patras and Thessaloniki and its demobilization in the Peloponnese." .

ns1:61408 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1988 Copa del Rey de Baloncesto was the 52nd edition of the Copa del Rey de Baloncesto. It was organized by the ACB and its Final Eight was played in Valladolid, in the Pabellón Polideportivo Pisuerga between 19 December 1987 and 21 December 1987." .

ns1:61425 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2014 Gombe bus station bombing resulted the death of 27 people, however 60 were injured after a bomb exploded in a bus station in Gombe city, Nigeria." .

ns1:61431 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2004 Dutch Figure Skating Championships took place between 02 January 2004 and 04 January 2004 in Groningen." .

ns1:61444 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Evangelii Praecones (02 June 1951) was an encyclical letter of Pius XII about Catholic missions." .

ns1:61449 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Battle of the Vikhra River (Lithuanian: Vichros mūšis, Russian: Битва на реке Вихре, Belarusian: Мсціслаўская бітва) took place on 29 April 1386 on the Vikhra, tributary of the Sozh River, near Mstislaw between the Grand Duchy of Lithuania and the Principality of Smolensk." .

ns1:61489 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Goteik Gorge (Burmese: ဂုတ်ထိပ်တိုက်ပွဲ ) was a battle in the Sino-Burmese War fought between the Konbaung Dynasty of Burma (Myanmar) and the Qing Dynasty of China in late 01 December 1767 or early January 1768. In late December or early January, at the Goteik Gorge (south of Hsipaw), the two main armies faced off and the first major battle of the third invasion ensued." .

ns1:61541 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The UCI Road World Championships – Men's road race at the 1960 UCI Road World Championships – Men's road race was the 27th edition of the event. The race took place on Sunday 14 August 1960 in Hohenstein, East Germany." .

ns1:61562 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 Trophée des Alpilles was a professional tennis tournament played on hard courts. It took place in Saint-Rémy-de-Provence, France between 07 September 2010 and 12 September 2010." .

ns1:61587 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "A 2009 Japanese general election for the Japanese House of Representatives was held on 30 August 2009. Under Japan's constitution, this result virtually assured DPJ leader Yukio Hatoyama would be the next Prime Minister of Japan. The last 2009 Japanese general election took place in 2005 Japanese general election in which the LDP, led by popular prime minister Junichiro Koizumi, received 38.2% of the proportional block votes and 47.8% of the district votes cast (the next largest party, the DPJ, received 31% in the proportional and 36.4% in the district vote). Since then Japan had three further prime ministers (Shinzō Abe, Yasuo Fukuda and Tarō Asō) who came to power without there being a 2009 Japanese general election." .

ns1:61626 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "These 2005 Nestea European Championship Final (or the 2005 European Beach Volleyball Championships) was held from 25 June 2005 to 28 June 2005 in Moscow." .

ns1:61646 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1987 European Aquatics Championships, organized by the LEN, were held in an indoor pool (50 m) in Strasbourg, France from 16 August 1987 to 23 August 1987. Besides swimming there were titles contested in diving, synchronized swimming (women) and water polo." .

ns1:61658 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Claro Open Barranquilla was a professional tennis tournament played on clay courts. It took place in Barranquilla, Colombia between 06 September 2016 and 11 September 2016." .

ns1:61683 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Immensa Aeterni Dei is an apostolic constitution in the form of a papal bull issued by Sixtus V on 22 January 1588." .

ns1:61688 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Slovak Open was a professional tennis tournament played on indoor hard courts. It took place in Bratislava, Slovakia between 03 November 2014 and 09 November 2014." .

ns1:61694 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1995 Amway Classic was a women's tennis tournament played on hardcourt at the ASB Tennis Centre in Auckland, New Zealand that was part of WTA Tier IV tournaments of the 1995 WTA Tour. It was the 10th edition of the tournament and was held from 30 January 1995 until 05 February 1995." .

ns1:61696 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The École Polytechnique massacre (French: tuerie de l'École polytechnique), also known as the Montreal massacre, was a mass shooting in Montreal at an engineering school affiliated with the Université de Montréal. Sometime after 4 p.m. on 06 December 1989, Marc Lépine arrived at the building housing the École Polytechnique, an engineering school affiliated with the Université de Montréal, armed with a rifle and a hunting knife. The Quebec and Montreal governments declared three days of mourning. A joint funeral for nine of the women was held at Notre-Dame Basilica on December 11, 1989, and was attended by Governor General Jeanne Sauvé, Prime Minister Brian Mulroney, Quebec premier Robert Bourassa, and Montreal mayor Jean Doré, along with thousands of other mourners. At 14, Gamil changed his name to \"Marc Lépine,\" citing his hatred of his father as the reason for taking his mother's surname. In 2019, the signage at the Montreal's Place du 6-Décembre-1989 park was updated to reflect that École Polytechnique massacre was an anti-feminist attack. In March 2015, The Supreme Court of Canada ruled against Quebec, clearing the way for the destruction of all registry data." .

ns1:61701 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Bruce Springsteen with The Seeger Sessions Band Tour, afterward sometimes referred to simply as the Sessions Band Tour, was a 2006 concert tour featuring Bruce Springsteen and The Sessions Band playing what was billed as \"An all-new evening of gospel, folk, and blues\", otherwise seen as a form of big band folk music. It began on October 1 at the PalaMalaguti in Bologna, Italy, and concluded on 21 November 2006 at the Odyssey Arena in Belfast, Northern Ireland. Audio of the opening show of the tour, at the New Orleans Jazz and Heritage Festival, was released through the Bruce Springsteen Archives in December 2017; full video of this concert was later posted on Springsteen's YouTube channel in May 2019." .

ns1:61705 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of the Imjin River (Filipino: Labanan sa Ilog Imjin River), also known as the Battle of Solma-ri (Korean: 설마리 전투) or Battle of Gloster Hill (글로스터 고지 전투) in South Korea, or as Battle of Xuemali (Chinese: 雪马里战斗; pinyin: Xuě Mǎ Lǐ Zhàn Dòu) in China, took place 22 April 1951–25 April 1951 during the Korean War. The battle opened on the night of 22 April 1951. In the Battle of the Imjin River two Victoria Crosses and one George Cross were awarded to soldiers of the Gloucestershire Regiment: In addition, several soldiers were awarded the Distinguished Service Order: The Military Cross was awarded to: The Military Medal was awarded to: Lieutenant-Colonel Crahay received the U.S. Army's Distinguished Service Cross for his leadership of the Belgian battalion during the battle. Three units were awarded the US Presidential Unit Citation for their part in the Battle of the Imjin River: On 8 May 1951, by the command of U.S. President Harry S. Truman, General James Van Fleet presented the President's Distinguished Unit Citation to the Glosters, together with C Troop, 170 Heavy Mortar Battery, which had given invaluable support throughout the battle. The Belgian battalion with the Luxembourg detachment of the UN Forces in Korea is mentioned for exceptional execution of its missions and for its remarkable heroism in its actions against the enemy on the Imjin River, near Hantangang, Korea during the period from 20 till 26 25 April 1951." .

ns1:61726 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Antón Lizardo was a naval engagement of the Reform civil war which took place off Antón Lizardo, Veracruz, Mexico in 1860. General Miramon and Marquis of Havana arrived off Antón Lizardo, Veracruz north of the San Juan de Ulúa fortress on 06 March 1860 where a United States Navy frigate was operating." .

ns1:61731 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Italian centre-left primary election determined the leader of the coalition Italy. Common Good, who will stand as common candidate for the office of Prime Minister in the subsequent general election, which took place on 24 February 2013–25 February 2013. The convention set the election rules too, fixing the first run on 25 November 2012 and, in case no candidate had secured more than 50% of the vote, a run-off on 02 December 2012 between the two front-runners." .

ns1:61735 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1973 Football League Cup Final was held on 03 March 1973 and was won by Tottenham Hotspur F.C.. Spurs beat Norwich City F.C. 1–0 at Wembley Stadium." .

ns1:61788 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "One P.M. (alternately said to stand for One Pennebaker Movie or One Parallel Movie) is a 1972 film by documentary filmmaker D. A. Pennebaker, who had collaborated with Jean-Luc Godard on the unfinished film project One A.M. and had shared duties as cinematographer with Richard Leacock." .

ns1:61796 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1999 IFAF World Championship was the inaugural IFAF World Championship, an international American football championship run by the International Federation of American Football (IFAF). It was played in Palermo, Italy from 1999–1999." .

ns1:61806 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2005 UEFA Super Cup was an association football match between Liverpool of England and PFC CSKA Moscow of Russia on 2005 at Stade Louis II, Monaco, the annual UEFA Super Cup contested between the winners of the UEFA Champions League and UEFA Cup. PFC CSKA Moscow won the 2004–05 UEFA Cup, beating Portuguese team Sporting CP 3–1. His subsequent shot was saved by Akinfeev, but the ball rebounded to Cissé who scored to give Liverpool a 2–1 lead." .

ns1:61807 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011–12 Qatar Stars League, also known as Qatari Stars League was the 39th edition of top-level football championship in Qatar. The season started on 01 September 2011 and finished on 13 April 2012." .

ns1:61814 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "\"A New Beginning\" is the name of a speech delivered by United States President Barack Obama on 04 June 2009, from the Major Reception Hall at Cairo University in Egypt. The A New Beginning speech occurred just after Obama met the representatives of both Israel and the Palestinian Authority. On 04 June 2009, before delivering the speech, Obama led talks with Egyptian President Hosni Mubarak at Koubbeh Palace. According to the San Francisco Chronicle, the speech may have played some role in the 04 June 2009 Iranian Presidential election between President Mahmoud Ahmadinejad and his rival Mir Hossein Mousavi, spurring on reformist public opinion. A follow-up to the New Beginning speech was delivered by Barack Obama on 9 November 2010 at the University of Indonesia in Indonesia. A second follow up speech, in which the same advisers who were involved like the New Beginning speech had a say, was delivered by US President Obama on 19 May 2011 at the US State Department in the wake of the Arab Spring uprisings in the Middle East." .

ns1:61827 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 Monaco Grand Prix (officially the Formula 1 Grand Prix de Monaco 2009) was a Formula One auto racing that was held on 24 May 2009 at the Circuit de Monaco, in Monaco. The race, which was contested over 78 laps, was the sixth round of the 2009 Formula One World Championship." .

ns1:61845 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1973 Grand National was the 127th renewal of the Grand National horse racing that took place at the Aintree Racecourse near Liverpool, England, on 1973. The race is best remembered for being the first of Red Rum's three Grand National wins; Red Rum also broke the record set by Reynoldstown in 1935, and in doing so staged a spectacular comeback to beat Crisp on the run-in after having trailed by 15 lengths at the final fence. 1973 is often considered among the greatest Grand Nationals of all time, and the dramatic final-stretch battle between the two greats Crisp and Red Rum has also been described as one of the greatest sporting moments." .

ns1:61885 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Power Lunch is a television business news program on Consumer News and Business Channel, airing between 2 p.m. and 3 p.m. Eastern Time. Bill Griffeth anchored the program alone from 1996 to 2002. On October 13, 2014, Power Lunch was launched in full 1080i high-definition as part of Consumer News and Business Channel's network-wide switch to a full 16:9 letterbox format. The dates and cities were: Around Consumer News and Business Channel's global branches, there are many counterparts of Power Lunch in the world:" .

ns1:61905 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1988–89 Isthmian League season was the 74th season of the Isthmian League, which is an English association football competition featuring semi-professional and amateur clubs from London, East of England and South East England." .

ns1:61907 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1953 Philippine House of Representatives elections were held on 10 November 1953. However, Ramon Magsaysay of the opposition Nacionalista Party was elected president, and several elected Liberal Party congressmen defected to the Nacionalista Party, leading to José Laurel, Jr. being elected Speaker of the House of Representatives of the Philippines." .

ns1:61919 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "HMS Invincible was a 74-gun third-rate ship of the line of the Royal Navy, launched on 1765 at Deptford. A memorial stone was erected in 1998 to their memory by the Ship's Company of the Royal Navy aircraft carrier HMS Invincible, and by the Happisburgh parochial church council." .

ns1:61946 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The FIBT World Championships 2009, officially known as the Bauhaus FIBT Bobsleigh & Skeleton World Championships, February 20 to 2009, at the Mt. Van Hoevenberg Olympic Bobsled Run in Lake Placid, for the ninth time, doing so previously in 1949, 1961, 1969, 1973, 1978, 1983, 1997 (skeleton), and 2003 (men's bobsleigh). As of 2009, the top three final World Cup positions were as follows (note: in bobsleigh, only the driver is shown): February 21–22, 2009, at 08:30 EST (13:30 UTC) for the last two runs. The United States of America had the fastest time in each of the four runs to win their first bobsleigh gold medal at the World championships since 1959." .

ns1:61950 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Rally Italia Sardegna was the sixth round of the 2014 World Rally Championship season. French driver Sébastien Ogier won the Rally Italia Sardegna for the second time in his career, taking his fourth win of eight victories during the 2014 season." .

ns1:61951 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Comedy Cluj is an international film festival of comedy film organized annually in October in Cluj-Napoca, Transylvania, Romania. The first edition of Comedy Cluj was held in 2009 and comprised 70 films coming from 19 countries, as well as several workshops, street theater shows, conferences and parties. Despite the late-2000s recession, Comedy Cluj had grown, so that its second edition (held in October–17, 2010) showed more than 100 films." .

ns1:61963 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Wilno Uprising began on April 22, 1794, during which Polish and Grand Duchy of Lithuania forces led by Jakub Jasiński fought Russian forces occupying the city during the Kościuszko Uprising. On April 21, Grand Duchy of Lithuania Hetman Szymon Kossakowski came to Vilnius, urging the Russians to capture yet more rebels, and attack rebel forces concentrated around the city. Preparing for the Russians return, even the Grand Duchy of Lithuania nobles of Vilnius participated in the digging of the defensive lines near the city." .

ns1:61966 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The women's heptathlon competition at the athletics at the 2004 Summer Olympics – women's heptathlon in Athens was held at the Olympic Stadium of Athens on 20 August–21 August 2004." .

ns1:61973 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2004–05 Danish Superliga season was the 15th season of the Danish Superliga league championship, governed by the Danish Football Union. It took place from 24 July 2004 to 19 June 2005." .

ns1:61978 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1979 Tournament Players Championship was a golf tournament in Florida on the PGA Tour, held March 22–25 at Sawgrass Country Club in Ponte Vedra Beach, southeast of Jacksonville. The sixth The Players Championship, it was the third at Sawgrass and Lanny Wadkins won in the wind at 283 (−5), five strokes ahead of runner-up Tom Watson." .

ns1:61982 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Hurricane Ernesto was a Category 2 hurricane and a damaging tropical cyclone that affected several Caribbean Islands and areas of Central America during August 2012. The system moved inland across the Isthmus of Tehuantepec on the afternoon of August 9, and into southern Mexico before dissipating as a tropical cyclone on 10 August 2012. Before Ernesto passed south of Jamaica, the government of Honduras issued a tropical storm watch from its border with Nicaragua to Punta Castilla at 0900 UTC on August 5." .

ns1:61994 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Miss International 1979 was held on 1979 at the Mielparque in Tokyo, Japan. The then–15-year-old Melanie Marquez earned Philippines' third Miss International crown." .

ns1:62011 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Mount Zion Church was fought on 28 December 1861, in Boone County, near Mount Zion Church, during the American Civil War. The resulting Union victory here and elsewhere in central Missouri ended Confederate recruiting activities in the region and pushed conventional Confederate forces out of the area until the desperate fall 1864 invasion by General Sterling Price and his Missouri State Guard. Gen. Benjamin M. Prentiss led a Union force of five companies of the Third Missouri Cavalry and two companies of Birge's Western Sharpshooters into Boone County to protect the North Missouri Railroad, disrupt the organization of the secessionist Missouri State Guard, and generally overawe secessionist sentiment in the region. The remainder dispersed, with Dorsey heading westward into Perche township, the northwest section of Boone County towards the Missouri River." .

ns1:62020 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 Rostelecom Cup was the fifth event of six in the 2010–11 ISU Grand Prix of Figure Skating, a senior-level international invitational competition series. It was held at the Megasport Arena in Moscow on 18 November 2010–21." .

ns1:62024 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1985 European Figure Skating Championships was a senior-level international competition held at the Scandinavium in Gothenburg, Sweden from 04 February 1985 to 10." .

ns1:62036 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Uruguay Open is a professional tennis tournament played on clay courts. It takes place in Montevideo, Uruguay between 14 November 2016 and 20 November 2016." .

ns1:62040 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The murder of Timothy Brenton occurred on 31 October 2009, in the Central District of Seattle, Washington, United States of America. Timothy Quinn Brenton (February 9, 1970 – 31 October 2009), an officer with the Seattle Police Department, was seated in a parked patrol car with another officer discussing a traffic stop when a gunman stopped his vehicle alongside the patrol car, opened fire on both officers, and fled the scene. The suspect arrested in connection with the murder, Christopher Monfort, was also charged in connection with the October 22, 2009, firebombing of Seattle police vehicles at a city maintenance facility." .

ns1:62041 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Traverse City Film Festival is an annual film festival held at the end of July in Traverse City, Michigan. The 1st annual Traverse City Film Festival was held July–31, 2005. Broken Flowers, a winner at the 2005 Cannes Film Festival, was shown at the Traverse City Film Festival before being released to the general public. Other 2005 Traverse City Film Festival selections covered a gamut of film subjects ranging from period romances, unemployment, terrorism, among many other subjects. The second annual Traverse City Film Festival was held July – August. The third annual festival was held July – August. The fourth annual festival took place July – August. The fifth annual festival was held from July – August. The sixth annual festival was held from July – August. The eighth annual festival was held from July - August. The ninth annual festival was held from July - August. The tenth annual festival was held from July - August. The eleventh annual festival was held from July - August. The twelfth annual Traverse City Film Festival was held from July–31, 2016. The thirteenth annual Traverse City Film Festival was held from July–30, 2017. The 14th annual Traverse City Film Festival was held from July - August." .

ns1:62048 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1931 Tasmanian state election was held on 09 May 1931 in the Australia state of Tasmania to elect 30 members of the Tasmanian House of Assembly. The Nationalist Party had defeated Labor by one seat at the 1928 Tasmanian state election, and John McPhee had been Premier of Tasmania since then." .

ns1:62061 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 15th Youth in Film Awards ceremony (now known as the Young Artist Awards), presented by the Young Artist Award, honored outstanding youth performers under the age of 21 in the fields of film, television and theatre for the 1992-1993 season, and took place on 05 February 1994, at the Sportsmen's Lodge in Studio City, California." .

ns1:62097 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2005 Belmont Stakes was the 137th running of the Belmont Stakes. In 2005, the Kentucky Derby was won by long-shot Giacomo while the Preakness Stakes was won by Afleet Alex. Afleet Alex arrived at Belmont Park on June 4. His jockey, Jeremy Rose, had been cautioned about making his move too early – a common mistake for young jockeys not familiar with the dimensions of Belmont Park." .

ns1:62105 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2015 Svenska cupen in bandy had its playoff in the weekend of 26 September 2015-27 September 2015 in Lidköping." .

ns1:62114 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 Xinjiang earthquake occurred in the Xinjiang of the People's Republic of China. The epicenter was at 43.3 degrees north latitude and 80.9 degrees east longitude at a depth of 10 km according to the China Earthquake Administration. According to the Chinese zodiac the Xinjiang earthquake occurred on the last day of the year of the Rat." .

ns1:62131 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 World Series of Poker was the 48th annual World Series of Poker (WSOP). It took place from 30 May 2017-July 17 at the Rio All-Suite Hotel and Casino in Las Vegas, Nevada." .

ns1:62179 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Beirut Pride is the annual non-profit LGBTIQ+ pride event and militant pride parade held in Beirut, the capital of the Lebanon and aiming to decriminalize homosexuality in Lebanon. Since its inception in 2017, Beirut Pride has been the first and only LGBTIQ+ pride in the arabophone world, and its largest LGBTIQ+ event. The first edition of Beirut Pride took place May 14–21, 2017 to coincide with the International Day Against Homophobia. A pride parade was scheduled for 2017, but in the days building to it, the intents for participation exceeded all expectations, and therefore, the planned logistics were insufficient to properly accompany the pride parade. The police arrested the organiser of Beirut Pride, and despite the interrogation proving the falsity of the accusations, the general prosecutor of Beirut ordered the suspension of the scheduled activities before initiating criminal proceedings against Hadi Damien for organizing events “that incite to debauchery”. The 2020 Beirut Pride event was held against the context of both the global COVID-19 pandemic and the devastation of much of Beirut by an explosion on 4 August. One produced by media platform Megaphone, released on the International Day Against Homophobia, Transphobia and Biphobia 2020, and offering an expansive overview on LGBTIQ+ realities in Lebanon; and a second, soothing, video \"We Learn from Lebanon\" depicting the movement of Mediterranean sea waves with a message of stability and steadfastness, produced by Beirut Pride just a few weeks before the Beirut explosion. Contributing to the fight against homophobia, Beirut Pride collaborated with the American University of Beirut and with the Lebanese American University. Beirut Pride facilitated several photoshoots in Lebanon on LGBTIQ+ themes, and initiated the Arabic script #TypeWithPride based on the Gilbert Color Bold typeface, currently in development with designer Ghiya Haidar and Fontself. Beirut Pride also issued the trilingual HIV Multi-Sectorial Framework in Lebanon, before revisiting and augmenting a parliament bill that frames the rights of people living with HIV, emphasizes on national prevention outreach and criminalises discrimination against people with HIV." .

ns1:62201 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Hanikra border clash was the shooting death of an Israel soldier and subsequent counter-attack that ensued, wounding a Lebanese soldier. The incident took place in the Rosh HaNikra (HaKira) locality kibbutz on the Lebanese-Israel border. The shootout marked the first time an Israel soldier died confronting Lebanese soldiers since the 2010 Israel–Lebanon border clash. On 15 December 2013, at 8:30 pm, Israel soldier Sergeant Shlomi Cohen, 31, was shot dead by rogue Lebanese army soldier Hassan Ibrahim, 27, a ten-year veteran of the Lebanese military." .

ns1:62216 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1945 Kentucky Derby was the 71st running of the Kentucky Derby, held on 1945. It was won by Hoop Jr., ridden by jockey Eddie Arcaro. Regularly held on the first Saturday of May, the 1945 edition of the Kentucky Derby was initially scheduled for May 5. However, in December 1944, the director of the Office of War Mobilization, James F. Byrnes, requested that all horse racing tracks suspend racing starting in 1945, \"and to refrain from resuming racing at all tracks until war conditions permit.\"" .

ns1:62244 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Ladies Open Lugano (also known as the 2018 Samsung Open presented by Cornèr for sponsorship reasons) was a women's tennis tournament played on clay courts at TC Lido Lugano. It was the second edition of the tournament (the first to take place in Lugano) and part of the WTA International tournaments category of the 2018 WTA Tour. It took place between 09 April 2018 through 15 April 2018." .

ns1:62248 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1971 Indian general election were held in India to constitute the 5th Lok Sabha on 01 March 1971. During her previous term, there had been internal divisions in the Indian National Congress between Indira Gandhi and the party establishment, especially Morarji Desai." .

ns1:62256 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1973 Paris–Nice was the 31st edition of the Paris–Nice cycle race and was held from 11 March 1973 to 17 March 1973." .

ns1:62259 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Isaurian War was a conflict that lasted from 492 to 497 and that was fought between the army of the Eastern Roman Empire and the rebels of Isauria. At the end of the war, Eastern Emperor Anastasius I regained control of the Isauria region and the leaders of the revolt were killed. In 497 John the Scythian killed Longinus of Cardala and Athenodorus, whose heads were exposed on a spear in Tarsus, thus effectively ending the war." .

ns1:62285 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2002 The 500 was the eighteenth round of the 2002 CART season, held on 03 November 2002 at the Auto Club Speedway in Fontana, California, United States." .

ns1:62298 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Spiral Stakes (known as the Jeff Ruby Steaks —a homophone of the word Stakes, for commercial reasons) is an American thoroughbred horse race held in late March at Turfway Park in Florence, Kentucky in the Cincinnati area. First run on 1972, bourbon whiskey maker Jim Beam acquired naming rights sponsorship in 1982 and the race renamed the Jim Beam Spiral Stakes and later just the Jim Beam Stakes." .

ns1:62314 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Polish diaspora comprises Poles and people of Polish heritage or origin who live outside Poland. The Polish diaspora is also known in modern Polish as Polonia, the name for Poland in Latin and many Romance languages. Many of the Poles who emigrated were Jews, who make up part of the Jewish diaspora. After the war, a few numbers of Poles decided to stay, forming the first Polish diaspora group in India. According to the Council of Polonia in South Africa, 25,000 to 30,000 Poles live there." .

ns1:62328 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1991 Scottish League Cup Final was played on 27 October 1991 at Hampden Park in Glasgow and was the final of the 46th Scottish League Cup competition. The final was contested by Hibernian F.C. and Dunfermline Athletic F.C.. Hibernian F.C. won the match 2–0, thanks to goals from Tommy McIntyre and Keith Wright." .

ns1:62346 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Marcellae (Bulgarian: Битката при Маркели, Greek: Μάχη των Μαρκελλών) took place in 792 at Markeli (Markeli), near the modern town of Karnobat in south eastern Bulgaria, the same site as an earlier battle in 756. The battle was between the forces of the Byzantine Empire, led by Constantine VI, and those of Bulgaria under Kardam." .

ns1:62353 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Miss America 1972, the 45th Miss America pageant, was held at the Boardwalk Hall in Atlantic City, New Jersey, on September 11, 1971, and broadcast on NBC. Laurie Lea Schaefer, representing Ohio, was crowned Miss America 1972." .

ns1:62359 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The FIU–Miami football brawl was a bench-clearing brawl that occurred on 14 October 2006 in a college football game between the University of Miami Hurricanes and the Florida International University Golden Panthers (now Panthers) at the Miami Orange Bowl in Miami. By the third quarter, officials had American football seven penalties (six for FIU and one for Miami). ESPN.com's Gene Wojciechowski American football Miami's one-game suspensions \"a soothing caress and manicure,\" and described Reddick's actions as \"criminal conduct.\" He American football for Miami to fire Coker for \"a continuing series of embarrassments,\" and for the school to also wipe the game from its records." .

ns1:62396 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 Copa Colombia, officially the 2011 Copa Postobón for sponsorship reasons, was the ninth edition of the Copa Colombia, the national cup competition for clubs of DIMAYOR. The tournament comprises a total of 36 teams, divided into six groups based on each separate region of Colombia." .

ns1:62408 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Open Castilla y León was a professional tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts. It took place in El Espinar, Segovia, Spain, between 31 July 2017 – 06 August 2017." .

ns1:62426 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "A 1955 Cook by-election was held for the Australian House of Representatives seat of Cook on 21 May 1955. The 1955 Cook by-election was won by Labor candidate Jim Cope." .

ns1:62441 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "A spill of the 2008 Liberal Party of Australia leadership spill took place on 15 16 September 2008. At a ballot on 16 September 2008, Shadow Treasurer Malcolm Turnbull defeated the incumbent leader Brendan Nelson 45 votes to 41. In the aftermath, Brendan Nelson was elected leader in an 2007 Liberal Party of Australia leadership election, in a 45-42 vote against former Minister for the Environment, Malcolm Turnbull." .

ns1:62444 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The trophy was commissioned in 1892 as the Dominion Hockey Challenge Cup and is named after Frederick Stanley, 16th Earl of Derby, the Governor General of Canada, who donated it as an award to Canada's top-ranking amateur ice hockey club. In 1915, the National Hockey Association (NHA) and the Pacific Coast Hockey Association (PCHA), the two main professional ice hockey organizations, reached a gentlemen's agreement in which their respective champions would face each other annually for the Stanley Cup. After Frederick Stanley, 16th Earl of Derby was appointed by Queen Victoria as Governor General of Canada on June 11, 1888, he and his family became highly enthusiastic about ice hockey. During that time, organized ice hockey in Canada was still in its infancy and only Montreal and Ottawa had anything resembling leagues. The name \"Stanley Cup\" was given to it as early as 1893, when an Ottawa Journal article used the name as a title. Sweetland and Ross first presented the trophy in 1893 to the Montreal Amateur Athletic Association on behalf of the affiliated Montreal Hockey Club, the champions of the Amateur Hockey Association of Canada (AHAC), since they \"defeated all comers during the late season, including the champions of the Ontario Association\" (Ottawa). In 1908, the Allan Cup was introduced as the trophy for Canada's amateurs, and the Stanley Cup started to become a symbol of professional hockey supremacy. Prior to organized ice hockey expanding to any serious extent outside Canada, the concept that the Stanley Cup champion ought to be recognized as the world champion was already firmly established – Stanley Cup winners were claiming the title of world champions by no later than the turn of the century. The format for the Stanley Cup Finals changed in 1922, with the creation of the Western Canada Hockey League (WCHL). Eventually, following the establishment of the Canada Cup as the first best-on-best international hockey tournament, NHL President Clarence Campbell (who was a vocal opponent of the tournament) made public overtures to establish a true world professional championship in ice hockey, \"just like the World Series\". The first is a roll of Canadian quarters with an image of the Stanley Cup, the word Stanley Cup in English and Coupe Stanley in French with two ice hockey players and 125 years (English)/Ans (French) on the obverse and an effigy of Elizabeth II on the back made using plated steel. The Stanley Cup did a second tour in Afghanistan as part of a \"Team Canada visit\" in March 2008." .

ns1:62450 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Alliance Air Flight 7412 was a scheduled domestic passenger flight operated by Alliance Air, a subsidiary of the airline Indian, from Kolkata's Netaji Subhas Chandra Bose International Airport to Indira Gandhi International Airport in Delhi. On 17 July 2000, while on approach to its first stopover in Patna, the Boeing 737-2A8 operating the route nose-dived and crashed into a residential area in Patna, killing 60 people including 5 on the ground. Flight 7412 departed Calcutta (Kolkata) at 06:51 on 17 July 2000, for a flight to Delhi, with stops at Patna and Lucknow." .

ns1:62506 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "FlightSimCon is an annual aviation and flight simulation conference held on the grounds of Hartford Connecticut's Bradley International Airport. The first FlightSimCon was held in 01 March 2013 in downtown Hartford. For FlightSimCon 2014, the event moved north of Hartford to the New England Air Museum in Windsor Locks, where the event was again held in June 2015." .

ns1:62517 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Ksar Hellal Congress was the first and founding congress of the Neo Destour party. The 1934 Neo Destour Congress was organized by the secessionist members of the Destour party, in Ksar Hellal, on 02 March 1934. The congress was held on 02 March 1934 to discuss the continuity of this youth's activism which ended with the founding of their own new political party, the Neo Destour, starting, thanks to its new methods, the renewel of the Tunisian nationalism and its movement. There were numerous reasons leading to the birth of the Neo Destour: The Great Depression and its impact in Tunisia, in the early 1930s, the reactions denouncing the international eucharistic congress of Carthage, held to celebrate the hundredth anniversary of the French colonization of Algeria, the Tunisian naturalization issue but mainly the policy and behavior adopted by the Destour towards these events. On the other hand, the young team pursued its tour and asked for a loyal showdown with their opponents: An interim committee including El Materi, the Bourguibas, Sfar and Guiga was formed and decided to organize an extraordinary congress on 02 March 1934, in Ksar Hellal to settle the disagreement. On 02 March 1934, 48 members of the Destour attended the event, mostly originating in the Sahel: 19 delegates represented the Sahel in Ksar Hellal while 9 for Tunis and 20 for the rest of the country. The date of 02 March 1934, became a major event, both in the History of Tunisia and that of the National movement." .

ns1:62546 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of the Dardanelles in the Cretan War took place on 26 June 1656 and 27 26 June 1656 inside the Dardanelles." .

ns1:62552 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Action of 13 December 1814 was a naval action during the War of 1812. The British landing area for the invasion of Louisiana was set for Lake Borgne but in order to land, a squadron of American gunboats and other ships, had to be destroyed. USS Sea Horse had not yet made it to Bay St. Louis when spotted by Captain Lockyer's longboats late at night on 13 December 1814, somewhere in a waterway between Lake Borgne and the Bay of St. Louis along the Gulf of Mexico." .

ns1:62559 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Miss Universe 1968, the 17th Miss Universe pageant, was held on 13 July 1968 at the Miami Beach Auditorium in Miami Beach, United States." .

ns1:62572 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Città di Como Challenger was a professional tennis tournament played on clay courts. It took place in Como, Italy between 28 August 2017 – 03 September 2017." .

ns1:62584 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Danish–Icelandic Act of Union, a 01 December 1918 agreement signed by Iceland and Denmark, recognized Iceland as a fully independent and sovereign state – the Kingdom of Iceland – freely associated to Denmark in a personal union with the Danish king. Iceland established its own flag, declared its neutrality and asked Denmark to represent on its behalf foreign affairs and defence interests, while maintaining full control of them. Historian Gunnar Karlsson argues that Denmark was willing to grant Iceland sovereignty in 1918 for two reasons. Second, Iceland had shown that it was capable of governing its own affairs (starting with Home Rule in 1904) and relations with other states (during World War I when Iceland was cut off from Denmark). On 17 June 1944, during the occupation of Denmark by Nazi Germany, while the armed forces of the United States, the United Kingdom and Canada were still in Iceland, a referendum was held on severing ties with the monarchy and the country voted overwhelmingly to become a republic." .

ns1:62592 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Zhengtai Campaign was a series of battles fought between the nationalists and the communists during Chinese Civil War in the post World War II era, and resulted in the communist victory. The commander-in-chief and political commissar of the communist Shanxi – Chahar – Hebei Military Region, Nie Rongzhen, decided to concentrate his force to first clear the nationalists from their strongholds outside Shijiazhuang, and then annihilate any nationalist reinforcement sent along the Zhengding – Taiyuan Railway. The reason was that Yan Xishan, the nationalist commander-in-chief and the warlord of Shanxi, was not willing to sacrifice his own troops for Chiang Kai-shek and other warlords allied with Chiang, which resulted in further disasters for the nationalists: after beating back the nationalist attacks, the Independent 7th Brigade of communist Central Hebei Military Sub-region and local garrison of the communist 10th Military Sub-district further expanded their victories by taken nationalist strongholds including Tang'erli (堂二里) township of Ba (霸) County, Anci (安次) county, and the region of Xushengkou (徐胜口) on 27 08 April 1947. Yan Xishan, the nationalist commander-in-chief and the warlord of Shanxi was not willing to sacrifice his own troops to help Fu Zuoyi, the nationalist commander and warlord who controlled Hebei." .

ns1:62614 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Montenegrin Orthodox Church (MOC; Montenegrin: Crnogorska pravoslavna crkva (CPC)/Црногорска православна црква (ЦПЦ)) is a non-governmental organization, and a proclaimed church in Montenegro, that is not canonically recognized by the other Orthodox Christian Churches. It was created in 1993 by Antonije Abramović, a defrocked monk of the Serbian Orthodox Church, who became the Metropolitan of Montenegro and the head of Montenegrin Orthodox Church, which claims succession to the autocephalous Montenegrin Church which operated until the unification of the two countries Kingdom of Serbia and Kingdom of Montenegro, later to join the Kingdom of Serbs, Croats and Slovenes in 1918. According to a 2009 poll conducted by CEDEM, 29.36 percent of Montenegro's Eastern Orthodox Christians have opted for the Montenegrin Orthodox Church, while 70.64 percent have opted for or stayed with the canonical Serbian Orthodox Church. The Montenegrin Orthodox Church considers itself to be the sole legitimate representative of Orthodox Christianity in Montenegro, and also lays claim to all Orthodox Christian property in Montenegro that is currently in the possession of the Serbian Orthodox Church. The Montenegrin Orthodox Church was founded in Cetinje on 1993, by Antonije Abramović, initially with the support of the Liberal Alliance of Montenegro (LSCG), a political party that existed at the time. The MOC is led by the Archbishop of Cetinje and Montenegro Metropolitan Mihailo. At a General Montenegrin People's Assembly formed by the MOC in Cetinje on January 6, 1997, he was chosen by traditional public acclamation the Head of the Montenegrin Orthodox Church. He was enthroned to Metropolitan of Montenegrin Orthodox Church in Cetinje on October 31, 1998, in the presence of several hundred believers and supporters of Montenegrin Orthodox Church. The Montenegrin Orthodox Church currently holds its regular services in several chapels in the area of Montenegro's royal capital, Cetinje, as well as in its newly built Temple in Kotor. On Wednesday, April 18, 2007, the representatives of the Montenegrin Orthodox Church – which has announced that it did not wish to cause an \"excessive situation\", but that it would enter the Serbian Orthodox Cetinje monastery without regard to the reaction of the Serbian Orthodox Church to their claims and requests – attempted to do so. According to data of Centre of Democracy in Montenegro from February 2007, the Serbian Orthodox Church was the most trusted institution in Montenegro by public opinion (coefficient 3.29), while the Montenegrin Orthodox Church was ranked sixth (coefficient 2.35). According to a 2009 poll conducted by CEDEM, 29.36 percent of Montenegro's Eastern Orthodox Christians have opted for the Montenegrin Orthodox Church, while 70.64 percent have opted for or stayed with the canonical Serbian Orthodox Church." .

ns1:62634 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1911 Kikai Island earthquake (1911年喜界島地震 Sen-kyūhyaku-jyūichi-nen Kikai-jima Jishin) occurred on 15 June 1911 at 14:26 UTC (23:26 local time). The earthquake was located near Kikai Island, Japan. This earthquake could be felt as far as in Shanghai, China, Tainan, Taiwan (then under Japanese rule), and Fukushima, Japan. The earthquake triggered a tsunami which was recorded on Kikai Island and Amami Ōshima. The tsunami was relatively small on the east coast of Kikai Island, but had a height of at least 5m on the west coast of Kikai Island and also in Amami Ōshima, and may have had a maximum height of over 10m in certain parts of Kikai Island." .

ns1:62641 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "ROH The Big Bang! was a professional wrestling pay-per-view event produced by Ring of Honor (ROH), which was only available online. It took place on 03 April 2010 from the Grady Cole Center in Charlotte. ROH The Big Bang! was the first ROH show to be held in Charlotte, and it took place in the Grady Cole Center. ROH The Big Bang! featured professional wrestling matches that involved different wrestlers from pre-existing scripted feuds and storylines. On January 18, 2010, Jim Cornette announced on an episode of Ring of Honor Wrestling that ROH would make its debut in Charlotte with an Internet-only pay-per-view called \"ROH The Big Bang!\", which sported the slogan, \"pro wrestling explodes into the 21st century\"." .

ns1:62649 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Campeonato Brasileiro Série A (officially the Brasileirão Assaí 2018 for sponsorship reasons) was the 62nd season of the Campeonato Brasileiro Série A, the top level of professional football in Brazil, and the 15th edition in a double round-robin since its establishment in 2003. The season began on 14 April 2018 and ended on 02 December 2018." .

ns1:62664 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Callao (1838) was a confrontation during the War of the Confederation, between the Chilean blockade of Callao under the command of Commander Bynnon and the Confederate corsair fleet under Juan Blanchet. On 24 November 1838, the corvette Edmond and the schooner Shanrock sent by Blanchet and supported by three gunboats under the command of the Peruvian corvette captain San Julian held a battle with the Chilean blockade squadron of Callao commanded by the commander Santiago Jorge Bynnon and made up of the schooners Colo-Colo and Janequeo and the brig Achilles." .

ns1:62665 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 14th Congress of the League of Communists of Yugoslavia (Serbian Latin: Četrnaesti (vanredni) kongres Saveza komunista Jugoslavije, Cyrillic: Четрнаести (ванредни) конгрес Савеза комуниста Југославије) was held from 20 to last Congress of League of Communists of Yugoslavia, 20–22 January 1990, in the Belgrade Sava Centar. After two days with a sharp verbal conflict, the Slovene delegation walked out of the Sava Centar 22 January 1992." .

ns1:62666 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Strikeforce: Miami was a mixed martial arts event held by Strikeforce on 30 January 2010 in Sunrise, Florida, United States at the BB&T Center." .

ns1:62695 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1922 Tasmanian state election was held on 10 June 1922 in the Australia state of Tasmania to elect 30 members of the Tasmanian House of Assembly. The incumbent Premier of Tasmania was Walter Lee of the Nationalist Party." .

ns1:62698 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 IIHF World U18 Championships were held in Fargo, North Dakota and Moorhead, Minnesota, United States of America. Games were played at the Urban Plains Center in Fargo and the Moorhead Sports Center in Moorhead. Fargo-Moorhead defeated Providence, Rhode Island and St. Group B was played in Narva, Estonia between March 16 and 2009: South Korea is promoted to Division I for the 2010 IIHF World U18 Championships. Group B was played in Erzurum, Turkey between March 9 and 2009: Australia is promoted to Division II for the 2010 IIHF World U18 Championships." .

ns1:62708 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2001–02 Irish Cup was the 122nd edition of Northern Ireland's premier association football knock-out cup competition. Glentoran were the defending champions, winning their second successive Irish Cup last season after a 1–0 win over archrivals Linfield F.C. in the 2001 final." .

ns1:62709 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 Men's World Team Squash Championships is the men's edition of the 2009 World Team Squash Championships organized by the World Squash Federation, which serves as the world team championship for squash players. The event were held in Odense, Denmark and took place from September 27 to 2009." .

ns1:62712 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Fastlane was a professional wrestling pay-per-view (PPV) event and WWE Network event produced by WWE. It took place on 21 February 2016 at Quicken Loans Arena in Cleveland, Ohio." .

ns1:62734 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Cartagena was a naval battle fought on 03 September 1643, during the Thirty Years' War off Cape de Gate near Cartagena, Spain." .

ns1:62747 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Euro Winners Cup was the sixth edition of the Euro Winners Cup (EWC), an annual continental beach soccer tournament for men's top-division European clubs. Organised by Beach Soccer Worldwide (BSWW), the tournament was held in Nazaré, Portugal from 25 May 2018 till 03 June 2018, consisting of a preliminary qualifying round and the competition proper." .

ns1:62758 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The International Birdman is a series of two English competitions held in the West Sussex towns of Bognor Regis and Worthing. The event began in 1971 and has always been held on piers in West Sussex, on the south coast of England. In 2008 and 2009 the competition relocated to Worthing Pier due to renovations of Bognor Regis Pier. From 2010 Bognor Regis and Worthing have both held Birdman competition, forming the International Birdman Series. Events have subsequently been staged in both Worthing and Bognor Regis, creating the International Birdman series." .

ns1:62762 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1995 Scottish Cup Final was played between Celtic F.C. and Airdrieonians F.C. at Hampden Park on 27 May 1995. Celtic F.C. won the match 1–0, with a goal by Pierre van Hooijdonk who headed in after a cross from the left by Tosh McKinlay. This would prove to be the last occasion that the original Airdrieonians F.C. reached the final, with the club folding in 2002." .

ns1:62763 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "UFC 122 was a mixed martial arts event held by the Ultimate Fighting Championship on 13 November 2010 at König Pilsener Arena in Oberhausen. This was the Ultimate Fighting Championship's second event in Germany and second in continental Europe." .

ns1:62778 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Telpaneca was an engagement fought during the United States occupation of Nicaragua in 1927. At about 1:00 am on the 19 September 1927 a force of around 200 rebel troops, loyal to Augusto César Sandino, attacked the small garrison of Telpaneca under the command of Marine First Lieutenant Herbert S. Keimling." .

ns1:62784 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2004 FIFA U-19 Women's World Championship was held from 10 to 27 November 2004. It was the second edition of the youth tournament for women put together by FIFA, before being renamed FIFA U-20 Women's World Cup for the 2006 FIFA U-20 Women's World Championship." .

ns1:62804 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Binibining Pilipinas 2018 was the 55th edition of Binibining Pilipinas, that took place at the Smart Araneta Coliseum in Quezon City, Philippines on 2018. Rachel Louise Peters crowned Catriona Gray as Miss Universe Philippines 2018 at the end of the event while Maria Angelica de Leon crowned Maria Ahtisa Manalo as Binibining Pilipinas International 2018. The pageant also awarded four titles to participants in minor international beauty pageants:Katarina Sonja Rodriguez crowned Karen Juanita Gallman as Binibining Pilipinas Intercontinental 2018, Chanel Olive Thomas crowned Jehza Mae Huelar as Binibining Pilipinas Supranational 2018, Elizabeth Clenci crowned Eva Psychee Patalinjug as Binibining Pilipinas Grand International 2018, and Nelda Dorothea Ibe crowned Michele Theresa Gumabao as Binibining Pilipinas Globe 2018. The following were the judges for the 2018 edition of the pageant: Contestants who previously competed in the previous editions of Binibining Pilipinas, in other local beauty pageants or in international reality modeling competition:" .

ns1:62821 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Russian conquest of Bukhara was a series of wars, invasions, and the subsequent conquest of the Central Asian Emirate of Bukhara by the Russian Empire. The war resumed in 1868, when the Emir was forced to accept vassal status." .

ns1:62831 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "In the 1923 World Series, the New York Yankees beat the New York Giants in six games. The three consecutive matchups between the Yankees and Giants (1921–1923) marked the only time (as of 2020, that three straight World Series featured the same two clubs. This was the third time that a team had inaugurated a new stadium with a World Series win, and would be the last until the St. Louis Cardinals victory in their new ballpark in 2006, and the New York Yankees again won the World Series in 2009 in their new Yankee Stadium. 1923 World Series (4–2): New York Yankees (A.L.)" .

ns1:62833 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Julieka Ivanna Dhu (commonly referred to as Death of Ms Dhu; her first name was generally not used in media reports out of respect for Aboriginal naming customs) was a 22-year-old Australian Aboriginal woman who died in police custody in South Hedland, Western Australia, in 2014. Julieka Ivanna Dhu was born on 26 December 1991 in Port Hedland, Western Australia (WA) She was Aboriginal and of the Yamatji people." .

ns1:62847 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 NCAA Division I Women's Volleyball Tournament began on 03 December 2009 with 64 teams and ended on 19 December 2009, when Penn State defeated Texas, 3-2, in the NCAA National Championship match." .

ns1:62866 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Scotties Tournament of Hearts, Canada's national women's curling championship, was held from January 27–04 February 2018 at the South Okanagan Events Centre in Penticton, British Columbia. The winning team represented Canada at the 2018 Ford World Women's Curling Championship held from March 17–25 at the Memorial Gardens in North Bay, Ontario. The 2018 tournament was the first to use a new 16-team format, featuring representation by all fourteen member associations of Curling Canada, the second-place team from the 2017 tournament (as champion Rachel Homan declined to participate due to her qualification for the 2018 Winter Olympics), and a new wildcard team. Curling Canada introduced a new 16-team format for both the Tournament of Hearts and Brier for 2018, under which all 14 member associations of Curling Canada were represented in the main field, rather than being limited by a pre-qualifying tournament. Defending champion Rachel Homan declined to participate as Team Canada in order to prepare for the 2018 Winter Olympics; Michelle Englot's team, who finished as runner-up in the 2017 tournament, participated in her place,." .

ns1:62874 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Golden Spin of Zagreb (Croatian: Zlatna pirueta Zagreba) was the 47th edition of the annual senior-level international figure skating competition held in Zagreb, Croatia. A part of the 2014–15 ISU Challenger Series, event was held at the Dom Sportova on 04 December 2014–6, 2014." .

ns1:62885 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "At the modern pentathlon at the 1936 Summer Olympics in Berlin, a single modern pentathlon event was contested. A total of 42 athletes from 16 nations competed at the Berlin Games: The method of scoring was point-for-place." .

ns1:62903 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Chinese Communist Revolution, known in the mainland China as the War of Liberation (simplified Chinese: 解放战争; traditional Chinese: 解放戰爭; pinyin: Jiěfàng Zhànzhēng), was the conflict, led by the Communist Party of China and Chairman Mao Zedong, that resulted in the proclamation of the People's Republic of China, on 1 October 1949. The revolution began in 1946 after the Second Sino-Japanese War (1937–45) and was the second part of the Chinese Civil War (1945–49). In 1950, the outbreak of the Korean War led the American government to place the United States Seventh Fleet in the Taiwan Strait to prevent either side from attacking the other." .

ns1:62915 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The athletics at the 1980 Summer Olympics – men's 110 metres hurdles at the athletics at the 1980 Summer Olympics in Moscow, Soviet Union had an entry list of 23 competitors from 16 nations, with three qualifying heats (23 runners) and two semifinals (16) before the final (8) took place on Sunday 27 July 1980. All times are Moscow Time (UTC+3) The first round was held on Friday 25 July 1980." .

ns1:62922 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Gastein Ladies was a professional women's tennis tournament played on outdoor clay courts. It took place in Bad Gastein, Austria between 09 June 2012 and 17 June 2012." .

ns1:62937 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013–14 Segona Divisió is the 15th season of second-tier football in Andorra. The seventh-placed club in the 2013–14 Primera Divisió competed in a two-legged relegation playoff against the runners-up of the Promotion play-offs, for one spot in 2014–15 2013–14 Primera Divisió." .

ns1:62939 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Minnelli on Minnelli: Live at the Palace was a concert presented by Liza Minnelli at the Palace Theatre on Broadway from December 8, 1999 through 2000." .

ns1:62958 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Baklahorani (alternatively, Bakla Horani; Greek: Μπακλαχοράνι) or Tataula carnival (Greek: Αποκριές στα Ταταύλα) is a carnival celebrated annually in Istanbul, Turkey, by members of the local Greek-Orthodox community on Shrove Monday, the last Monday before Lent. Baklahorani, on Shrove Monday, the last day of the carnival season before Lent, became the culminating event in the mid-19th century. It started as a masked parade that proceeded through Istanbul's Greek neighborhoods, beginning with the elite area of Pera and gathering people along the way before proceeding finally to Tatavla (now Kurtuluş) for festivities on the square at the Church of Saint Demetrius. Baklahorani was one of the most famous festivals by the Christians in Istanbul until its last celebration in 1941. A law banning people from wearing masks ended the original Baklahorani carnival in 1943. In 2010, nearly 70 years after the last celebration, the historical carnival was revived by a celebrating group of Greeks and Turks who sang, danced and paraded in costumes through the streets of Şişli district. Principal organizers of the festival's reincarnation were Hüseyin Irmak, a researcher who was born in Kurtuluş, and Haris Theodorelis Rigas, a Greek who now lives in Istanbul, where he plays music in taverns, specializing in a \"near-extinct\" style of music blending Greek and Turkish influences. Irmak and Rigas consider the reestablishment of the carnival to be an opportunity for people to rediscover Turkey's multicultural past, while adding \"colour\" to people's lives." .

ns1:62960 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Thüringen Rundfahrt der Frauen is the 26th edition of the Thüringen Ladies Tour, a women's cycling stage race in Germany. It is rated by the UCI as a category 2.1 race and is held between 15 July 2013 and 21 July 2013." .

ns1:63033 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Electrical Water Pageant is a show that takes place every evening (weather permitting) on Walt Disney World Resort's Seven Seas Lagoon and Bay Lake. On Monday 25 October 1971; the Electrical Water Pageant premiered on the Seven Seas Lagoon and Bay Lake for \"Dedication Day\" at Walt Disney World Resort. The 1972 to 1976 lineup of floats for the Electrical Water Pageant were: With the return of the Disneyland Main Street Electrical Parade in an updated form along with the brand new Walt Disney World Resort Main Street Electrical Parade, a new version of Jean-Jacques Perry & Gershon Kingsley's \"Baroque Hoedown\" was created in 1977 to be used with both parades respectively featuring an updated rendition of Barque Hoedown recorded by Don Dorsey. The 1977 to 1995 lineup of floats for the Electrical Water Pageant were: From October 1, 1981 – October 1, 1982, in honor of Walt Disney World Resort's 10th anniversary, the Patriotic Finale music was replaced w/ the Tencennial theme song." .

ns1:63057 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1854 Ansei-Nankai earthquake occurred at about 16:00 local time on 24 December 1854. It was the second of the three Ansei great earthquakes; the 1854 Ansei-Tōkai earthquake of identical magnitude had hit northwest the previous morning, and the third 1855 Edo earthquake struck less than a year later." .

ns1:63060 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2005 Lombard regional election took place on 03 April 2005–4 03 April 2005. Roberto Formigoni (Forza Italia) was re-elected for the third time in a row President, defeating Riccardo Sarfatti." .

ns1:63070 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1996–97 Fußball-Bundesliga was the 7th season of the Frauen-Bundesliga, Germany's premier association football league. Beginning with the 1997–98 Fußball-Bundesliga all Bundesliga teams played in a single division." .

ns1:63086 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Hungarian parliamentary election took place on 06 April 2014. After the 2010 Hungarian parliamentary election, Fidesz won a landslide victory, with Viktor Orbán being elected as Prime Minister. Fidesz's leader Viktor Orbán celebrated in Budapest with thousands of supporters in the evening and said that Hungary was on the threshold of a \"new and wonderful epoch\"." .

ns1:63126 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The XIX 2014 Pan American Badminton Championships were held in Markham, Canada, between 13 October 2014 and 19 October 2014." .

ns1:63137 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The athletics at the 2000 Summer Paralympics – men's marathon T11 was a marathon event in athletics at the 2000 Summer Paralympics in Sydney, for totally blind athletes. Defending champion Harumi Yanagawa of Japan took part, as did 1992 gold medallist Carlo Durante of Italy, and 1988 gold medallist Joerund Gaasemyr or Norway, holder of the Paralympic record in 2:45:48. Portugal's Carlos Amaral Ferreira took gold, setting a new world record in 2:38:27, and finishing over nine minutes ahead of silver medallist Robert Matthews." .

ns1:63142 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Canadian Open Badminton Championships was the seventh grand prix gold and grand prix tournament of the 2014 BWF Grand Prix Gold and Grand Prix. The tournament was held in Doug Mitchell Thunderbird Sports Centre, Vancouver, Canada 30 June 2014 until 05 July 2014 and had a total purse of $50,000." .

ns1:63186 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Men's 20 kilometre individual biathlon competition at the biathlon at the 1964 Winter Olympics – men's 20 km was held on 4 February, at Seefeld in Tirol. Vladimir Melanin, the two time defending world champion, won the Olympic title with ease at Seefeld in Tirol." .

ns1:63192 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Rio Open, also known as the Rio Open presented by Claro for sponsorship reasons, is a tennis event on the ATP Tour and former WTA International Tournaments event. The tournament is played on outdoor clay courts at the Jockey Club Brasileiro in Rio de Janeiro, Brazil. The Rio Open (men) with indoor carpet courts from 1989 to 1990 was the first ATP World Series played in Brazil. For the 2019 edition, there was plan to move the tournament from the clay court surface in Jockey Club Brasileiro to the outdoor hard courts at the Olympic Tennis Centre, which hosted the tennis events of the 2016 Summer Olympics situated in Barra Olympic Park." .

ns1:63196 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Challenger Ciudad de Guayaquil was a professional tennis tournament played on clay courts. It took place in Guayaquil, Ecuador between 05 November 2012 and 10 November 2012." .

ns1:63205 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Anime Banzai is an annual three-day anime convention held during October at the Davis Conference Center in Layton, Utah." .

ns1:63214 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 European Rowing Championships was held in Belgrade, Serbia, between 30 May 2014 and 01 June 2014." .

ns1:63241 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1940 Copa Ibarguren was the 17° edition of this National cup of Argentina. Club Atlético Boca Juniors (Primera División champion) faced Club Atlético Rosario Central (Asociación Rosarina champion) at Chacarita Juniors Stadium located in the Villa Crespo district of Buenos Aires." .

ns1:63251 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "badminton at the 1996 Summer Olympics – women's singles badminton event at the 1996 Summer Olympics was held from 24 July 1996 to 01 August 1996." .

ns1:63266 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Turnhout (27 October 1789) was a decisive military engagement between Belgian revolutionary and Austrian forces at Turnhout in the Austrian Netherlands (modern-day Belgium). The town of Turnhout was besieged by the Austrians on 27 October 1789 and a violent combat took place. Each year in the Turnhout battle is commemorated with a spectacle in the Gasthuisstraat, the location where the fighting was fiercest. A memorial by P. Brozius in the Market of Turnhout recalls this battle." .

ns1:63340 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 Valle d'Aosta Open was a professional tennis tournament played on hard courts. It took place in Courmayeur, Italy between 31 January 2011 – 06 February 2011." .

ns1:63342 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1946 Bulgarian Cup Final was the 6th final of the Bulgarian Cup (in this period the tournament was named Cup of the Soviet Army), and was contested between PFC Levski Sofia and FC Chernolomets 04 on 1946 at Yunak Stadium in Sofia." .

ns1:63385 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 UCI Track Cycling World Championships is the World Championship for track cycling. It took place in Palma de Mallorca, Spain from 2007 to 01 April 2007." .

ns1:63400 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Healing the paralytic at Capernaum is one of the miracles of Jesus in Matthew (9:1–8), Mark (2:1–12), and Luke (5:17–26). Jesus was living in Capernaum and teaching the people there, and on one occasion the people gathered in such large numbers that there was no room left inside the house where he was teaching, not even outside the door." .

ns1:63412 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Bombing of Zadar in World War II (Italian: Zara) during the Second World War by the Allies lasted from 01 November 1943 to October 1944. Although other large cities in Kingdom of Italy were also bombed, the Bombing of Zadar in World War II stands out because of the number of attacks and the number of fatalities. Zadar (known in Italian as Zara) became part of the Kingdom of Italy as a result of the Treaty of Rapallo of 1920, which settled the status of former Austro-Hungarian territories. The first large Allied aerial attack on Zadar was carried out on 2 01 November 1943 by the USAAF 12th Air Force, during which an orphanage was destroyed, among other buildings." .

ns1:63414 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1944 Copa del Generalísimo was the 42nd staging of the Copa del Rey, the Spanish association football cup competition. The competition began on 20 February 1944 and concluded on 25 June 1944 with the final, where Athletic Club won their 15th title." .

ns1:63420 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1956 Tour de Romandie was the tenth edition of the Tour de Romandie cycle race and was held from 10 May 1956 to 13 May 1956." .

ns1:63422 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The XVI 2010 Pan American Badminton Championships were held in Curitiba, Brazil, between October 21 and 24 October 2010." .

ns1:63435 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 Andalucia Tennis Experience was a tennis tournament played on outdoor clay courts. It was the 2nd edition of the Andalucia Tennis Experience, and an International-level tournament of the 2010 WTA Tour. The event took place at the Club de Tenis Puente Romano in Marbella, Spain, from 05 April 2010 through 11 April 2010." .

ns1:63438 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1995 Miller Genuine Draft 500 was a NASCAR Winston Cup Series racing event that took place on 04 June 1995, at Dover International Speedway in Dover, Delaware." .

ns1:63449 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Progrès Niederkorn 2–0 Rangers was a association football match played on 4 July 2017 at the Stade Josy Barthel. The match was a UEFA Europa League first qualifying round tie between Luxembourgian minnows FC Progrès Niederkorn, who finished fourth in the 2016–17 National Division, and Scottish side Rangers F.C., who finished third in the 2016–17 Scottish Premiership. The 2016–17 season was Rangers F.C.' first season back in the Scottish Premier League after being sent to the lowest tier of the Scottish association football league system, the Third Division (now known as League Two), for the 2012–13 season as they entered into administration. On 29 June 2017, Rangers F.C. won the first leg of their tie against FC Progrès Niederkorn 1–0 at Ibrox, and went into the game with high hopes. After the defeat, Scottish media outlets regarded the match as one of the most embarrassing defeats in the history of Rangers F.C., the history of Scottish association football, and one of the greatest shocks in European association football. Both clubs qualified for the Europa League again the following season, after FC Progrès Niederkorn improved their domestic league position from 4th to 2nd in the 2017–18 Luxembourg National Division and Rangers F.C. again finished 3rd in the 2017–18 Scottish Premiership." .

ns1:63469 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Pacific Life Open (also known as the Pacific Life Open for sponsorship reasons) was a tennis tournament played on Hard courts. It was the 34th edition of the Indian Wells Masters and was part of the ATP Masters Series of the 2007 ATP Tour. Both the men's and the women's events took place at the Indian Wells Tennis Garden in Indian Wells, California, United States, from March 5 through 2007." .

ns1:63470 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Qatari–Bahraini War also known as the Qatari War of Independence was an armed conflict that took place between 1867 and 1868 in the Persian Gulf." .

ns1:63474 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Huai Su's Autobiography (Chinese: 懷素自叙帖) is a representative Chinese calligraphy work written by the renowned Tang dynasty Chinese calligrapher Huaisu in 777 AD. However, recent studies on this particular piece kept in the National Palace Museum revealed that it was likely a Northern Song Dynasty modelling replica (映寫) of the original; there are also other replica versions of Huai Su's Autobiography." .

ns1:63485 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Sarajevo Half Marathon non-governmental organization (NGO), created in 2007, organizes a half marathon annually, usually at the end of September, in Sarajevo, Bosnia and Herzegovina. NGO Sarajevo Half Marathon was created in 2007 to organize a marathon in Sarajevo, the capital of Bosnia and Herzegovina. Most of the members of NGO Sarajevo Half Marathon are from Bosnia and Herzegovina and are experienced athletes who run more than 3,500 kilometres (2,200 mi) each per year. NGO Sarajevo Half Marathon organizes three races per year, which are intended as a tool to progressively create the first marathon in Bosnia and Herzegovina. The Sarajevo half marathon was one of the most important international sport events in the city in 2009." .

ns1:63510 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1892 Japanese general election (第2回衆議院議員総選挙, Dai-nikai Shūgiin Giinsōsenkyō) was the Empire of Japan’s second general election for members of the House of Representatives of the Diet of Japan, held on 15 February 1892. After the 1890 Japanese general election for the lower house of the Diet of Japan, the elected members proved much less amenable to government persuasion than had been anticipated by Itō Hirobumi and other members of the Meiji oligarchy." .

ns1:63515 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1185 papal election (held 25 November 1185) was a convoked after the death of Pope Lucius III. Pope Lucius III died at Verona on 25 November 1185 at very advanced age. He was crowned at Verona on 1185." .

ns1:63518 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1993 European Challenge was a non-ranking invitational snooker tournament held by the World Professional Billiards and Snooker Association, which took place in 1993." .

ns1:63520 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1998 NCAA Men's Water Polo Championship was the 30th annual NCAA Men's Water Polo Championship to determine the national champion of NCAA men's collegiate water polo. Since there has only ever been one single national championship for water polo, all NCAA men's water polo programs (whether from Division I, Division II, or Division III) were eligible." .

ns1:63551 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The New Zealand Youth Parliament is a national event in New Zealand, held once in each term of parliament (usually every three or four years). The event has been held since 1994, and takes place at the New Zealand Parliament Buildings. The New Zealand Youth Parliament was first held in 1994, being held to mark the 20th anniversary of the voting age being lowered to 18." .

ns1:63553 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 CAF Confederation Cup Final was the final of 2009 CAF Confederation Cup, which was the 6th edition of the CAF Confederation Cup, Africa's secondary club association football competition organized by the Confederation of African Football (CAF). The final was played between Entente Sportive Sétifienne from Algeria and Stade Malien from Mali." .

ns1:63557 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Bulldog Bite was a joint U.S. and Afghan counter-insurgent mission in Kunar province, Afghanistan, against Taliban forces, in 12 November 2010." .

ns1:63568 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Paraćin massacre (Serbo-Croatian: Paraćinski masakr / Параћински масакр) was a mass shooting which targeted Yugoslav People's Army (JNA) soldiers in the military barracks in Paraćin, Yugoslavia on 03 September 1987. On 03 September 1987, Kelmendi's mother and father came to Paraćin to visit their son." .

ns1:63583 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Action of 13 June 1898 was a minor naval engagement of the Spanish–American War fought near Cienfuegos, between the American auxiliary cruiser USS Yankee under Commander Willard Herbert Brownson and the Spanish gunboat Diego Velázquez under Teniente de Navío de 1a clase Juan de Carranza y Reguera, which had exited the port to inspect a suspicious steamer which proved to be Yankee. At the outbreak of the Spanish–American War Cienfuegos was a strategically important port due to its facilities and good communications with Havana. On 13 June 1898 Teniente Carranza's Diego Velázquez was dispatched to inspect a steamer near the port thinking that it could be El Purísima Concepción, a blockade runner which was expected to arrive to Cienfuegos at that time. The Action of 13 June 1898 was the last naval engagement fought near Cienfuegos." .

ns1:63586 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 European Darts Grand Prix was the ninth of nine PDC European Tour events on the 2015 PDC Pro Tour. The tournament took place in Glaspalast Sindelfingen in Sindelfingen, Germany between 2015–2015." .

ns1:63602 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Galveston was a naval and land battle of the American Civil War, when Confederate forces under Major Gen. John B. Magruder expelled occupying Union troops from the city of Galveston on 01 January 1863. The battle is sometimes called the Second Battle of Galveston, as the Battle of Galveston Harbor (October 1862) is sometimes called the First Battle of Galveston. The Confederate Congress stated this on the successful recapture of Galveston: The bold, intrepid, and gallant conduct of Maj. Gen. J. Bankhead Magruder, Col. Thomas Green, Maj. Leon Smith and other officers, and of the Texan Rangers and soldiers engaged in the attack on, and victory achieved over, the land and naval forces of the enemy at Galveston, on the 1st of January, 1863, eminently entitle them to the thanks of Congress and the country..." .

ns1:63608 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Second Banda Oriental campaign was a military campaign of the War of Independece in Argentina, that besieged and captured the Banda Oriental (present-day Uruguay) with joint operations against Montevideo by José Rondeau on land and William Brown on water. Carlos María de Alvear seized the city on 1814, ending the royalist presence in the Banda Oriental." .

ns1:63614 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 AFC Cup was the 11th edition of the AFC Cup, a association football competition organized by the Asian Football Confederation (AFC) for clubs from \"developing countries\" in Asia. In the final, Qadsia SC of Kuwait defeated Erbil of Iraq 4–2 on penalties after a 0–0 draw, to win their first AFC Cup title after losing in the previous year's final." .

ns1:63620 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Typhoon Rananim, known in the Philippines as Typhoon Karen, was the strongest typhoon to make landfall on the Chinese province of Zhejiang since 1956. After developing a small eye, the typhoon attained peak winds of 150 km/h (90 mph) as it passed between Taiwan and Okinawa. A small eye became evident on satellite imagery, and late on August 11 the JMA estimated the typhoon attained peak winds of 150 km/h (90 mph)." .

ns1:63623 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Bound for Glory (2016) was a professional wrestling pay-per-view (PPV) event produced by Total Nonstop Action Wrestling (TNA). It took place on 2016 at the Impact Wrestling Zone in Orlando, the first time since the inaugural 2005 event to be held at the venue." .

ns1:63634 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1960 Minnesota gubernatorial election took place on 08 November 1960. Republican Party of Minnesota candidate Elmer L. Andersen defeated Minnesota Democratic–Farmer–Labor Party challenger Orville Freeman." .

ns1:63635 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 CS Ondrej Nepela Memorial (Slovak: Memoriál Ondreja Nepelu) was held on September and 02 October 2016. It is an annual senior international figure skating competition held in Bratislava, Slovakia." .

ns1:63644 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1947 French Championships (now known as the French Open) was a tennis tournament that took place on the outdoor clay courts at the Stade Roland-Garros in Paris, France. In 1947 (as in 1946) the French Open were held after Wimbledon. They were thus, both, the third Grand Slam tennis event of the year and 1947 was the last tournament until the 2020 French Open to be held outside of the usual May-June schedule." .

ns1:63657 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008–09 Copa del Rey was the 107th staging of the Copa del Rey (including two seasons where two rival editions were played). The competition started on 2008 and concluded on 2009 with the final, held at the Mestalla Stadium in Valencia, in which FC Barcelona lifted the trophy for the 25th time in their history with a 4–1 victory over Athletic Bilbao, who qualified for the third qualifying round of the 2009–10 UEFA Europa League." .

ns1:63665 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Pfeddersheim (German: Schlacht bei Pfeddersheim) was a battle during the German Peasants' War that took place in 24 June 1525 near Pfeddersheim." .

ns1:63673 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Makahambus Hill was one of the victories won by the Filipinos over the Americans during the Philippine–American War. It was fought on 04 June 1900 in Cagayan de Misamis (now Cagayan de Oro)." .

ns1:63675 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Bauer Watertechnology Cup was a professional tennis tournament played on carpet courts. It took place in Eckental, Germany between 27 October 2014 and 02 November 2014." .

ns1:63687 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1994 Danamon Indonesia Women's Open was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts at the Gelora Senayan Stadium in Jakarta, Indonesia and was part of Tier IV of the 1994 WTA Tour. It was the second edition of the tournament and was held from 25 April 1994 through 01 May 1994." .

ns1:63688 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Panama held a 1972 Panamanian presidential election on 10 October 1972, electing both a new President and Vice President of the Republic. On 10 October 1972 the National Assembly of Community Representatives elected Demetrio B. Lakas president and Arturo Sucre Pereira as Vice-President for a six-year term (1972-1978)." .

ns1:63702 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2001 World Table Tennis Championships were held in Osaka from 23 April 2001 to 06 May 2001." .

ns1:63719 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 Baja California earthquake (also known as 2010 Easter earthquake, 2010 Sierra El Mayor earthquake, or 2010 El Mayor – Cucapah earthquake) occurred on 04 April 2010 (Easter Sunday) with a moment magnitude of 7.2 and a maximum Mercalli intensity of IX (Violent). The 04 April 2010 quake was the second largest earthquake in Baja California history after the 1892 Laguna Salada earthquake at a magnitude of 7.2–7.8 on the Richter magnitude scale, and was well recorded in Southern California, especially in the Imperial valley. Seismologists in the California Institute of Technology of Pasadena announced a 10 percent chance of another earthquake on the same magnitude (7.3) or greater to strike within the first week, and 5 percent in the rest of the month of 04 April 2010. On July 7, 2010, a 5.4-magnitude earthquake, triggered by the April 10, 2010 quake, occurred on the San Jacinto Fault, the most seismically active fault in California, and one of two that exhibited signs of increased pressure following the Mexico quake." .

ns1:63728 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of the Neches, the main engagement of the Cherokee War of 1838–1839 (part of the Texas–Indian wars), took place on 15 July–16 July in 1839 in what is now the Redland community (between Tyler and Ben Wheeler, Texas)." .

ns1:63729 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The majority of the text is recorded in the form of sayings or teachings that the angel Metatron revealed to the Tannaic Sage, Rabbi Ishmael who transmitted it to his students and his contemporary Akiva ben Joseph. Rabbi Saadia Gaon also expressed doubts about the origin of the text, and stated that “since it is not found in either Mishna or Talmud, and since we have no way of establishing whether or not it represents the words of Rabbi Ishmael; perhaps someone else pretended to speak in his name.” Nonetheless in the case that the text were somehow proven to be genuine, Saadia wrote that it would have to be understood in line with his “theory of 'created glory,'\" which explains the prophetic theophanies as visions not of God Himself but of a luminous substance.” Rabbi Moses Narboni also wrote a philosophic work about the text entitled Iggeret Al-Shi'ur Qomah (Heb: אגרת על שיעור קומה lit." .

ns1:63742 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1971 World Women's Handball Championship took place in the Netherlands between 11 December 1971-19 December 1971." .

ns1:63748 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The World Short Track Speed Skating Team Championships were a professional team short track speed skating event and held once a year in a different country. The Championships was first held in 1991 and was discontinued until further notice by ISU after the 2011 season." .

ns1:63754 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Colorado Coalfield War was a major labor uprising in the southern and central Colorado Front Range between 1913 and April 1914. Striking began in late summer 1913, organized by the United Mine Workers of America (UMWA) against the Rockefeller-owned Colorado Fuel and Iron (CF&I) after years of poor working conditions. Between 1884 and 1912, Colorado's fatality rate among miners was more than double the national average, with 6.81 miners killed for every 1,000 workers (against a national average of 3.12) In the decade preceding the 1913-1914 Strike, CF&I mines had been involved in several major accidents. The Copper County Strike of copper workers in Calumet, Michigan, for nine months from 1913 to 1914, ran concurrently with the Colorado strike, and both strikers and Guardsmen were aware of the events in Michigan through coverage in Collier's and other nationally circulating publications. The Coalfield Strikes of 1913-1914 began in the late summer of 1913 when the United Mine Workers of America organized its regional District 15, led by John McLennan, to represent southern Coloradan coal field workers and put forward demands to Colorado Fuel and Iron. In Southern Colorado, an expanded strike began on 1913 during a rainstorm. The escalating situation caused Governor Elias Ammons to call in the Colorado National Guard in 1913, but after six months all but two companies were withdrawn for financial reasons. Dealers in Walsenburg and Pueblo also sold explosives to both sides of the conflict, though the investigating congressional committee noted they did \"not believe a majority of the people of Colorado indorse such actions.\" A Colorado and Southern route that connected the Front Range and passed near the Ludlow Colony began to be used as a firing position to harass strikers on 1913, resulting in no immediate casualties. The resulting riots and acts of anti-police violence led the Colorado Springs Gazette editorial board to call for the invocation of the 1807 Insurrection Act, favorably citing its usage in the Colorado Coalfield War." .

ns1:63760 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Minneapolis City Council election was held on 07 November 2017, to elect the members of the Minneapolis City Council. This was the first occurrence of the initial leader not ending up the winner of an election in Minneapolis since it switched to ranked-choice voting in 2009." .

ns1:63762 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1981 French Open – Women's Doubles was held from 25 May to 1981 on the outdoor clay courts at the Stade Roland Garros in Paris, France. Rosalyn Fairbank and Tanya Harford won the title, defeating Candy Reynolds and Paula Smith in the final." .

ns1:63764 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Kenyan Super Cup was a Kenya association football match contested by the 2015 Kenya Premier League champions Gor Mahia F.C. and the 2015 FKF President's Cup champions Bandari F.C.. Bandari F.C. won my match 1–0 to deny Gor Mahia F.C. their third title in a row." .

ns1:63779 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Breeders' Stakes is a stakes race for Thoroughbred race horses foaled in Canada, first run in 1889. Held annually in August at Woodbine Racetrack in Toronto, the Breeders' Stakes follows the June running of the Queen's Plate and the July running of the Prince of Wales Stakes." .

ns1:41367 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The flight crew successfully glided the Boeing 767 to an emergency landing at a former Royal Canadian Air Force base in Gimli, Manitoba that had been turned into a motor racing track. This unusual aviation incident earned the aircraft the nickname \"Gimli Glider\". The Aviation Safety Board of Canada (predecessor of the modern Transportation Safety Board of Canada) reported that Air Canada management was responsible for \"corporate and equipment deficiencies\". To calculate how much fuel the airplane had to take on, he needed to convert the volume (litres) to mass (kilograms), subtract that figure from 22,300 kg, and convert the result back into volume The density in metric units was 0.803 kg/L, so the correct calculation would have been: At the time of the incident, Canada's aviation sector was in the process of converting from Imperial units to the metric system. Four years after the incident, Canada Post issued a postage stamp commemorating Air Canada. The Discovery Channel Canada / National Geographic TV series Mayday covered the incident in a 2008 episode titled \"Gimli Glider\"." .

ns1:41425 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1972 Giro d'Italia was the 55th running of the Giro d'Italia, one of cycling's Grand Tours races. The Giro started in Venice on 21 May 1972, with a 5.2 km (3.2 mi) prologue and concluded with a 197 km (122 mi) mass-start stage, on 11 June 1972. A total of 100 riders from ten teams entered the 20-stage race, that was won by Belgian Eddy Merckx of the Molteni-Campagnolo team. The second and third places were taken by Spaniards José Manuel Fuente and Francisco Galdós, respectively. The teams entering the race were: Eddy Merckx (Molteni-Campagnolo) entered as the unanimous favorite to win the event. The route began in Venice for the first time in race history and traveled south and crossed the Apennines until reaching the edge of the continental section of Italy. Author William Fotheringham shared that sentiment, specifically stating that Torriani designed the route in order to give José Manuel Fuente several opportunities to attack Eddy Merckx." .

ns1:41619 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1995 Ais Gill rail accident occurred near Aisgill, Cumbria, United Kingdom, at about 18:55 hrs on 31 January 1995 when a British Rail Class 156 was derailed by a landslide on the Settle-Carlisle Railway line and was subsequently run into by a similar train travelling in the opposite direction. The driver changed cabs as the train was now heading northbound instead of southbound, and proceeded back over the Ribblehead Viaduct, and on to Aisgill Summit, the highest point on the line at 1,169 feet (356 m) above sea level." .

ns1:41809 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Lingbao School (Simplified Chinese: 灵宝派; Traditional Chinese: 靈寶派; pinyin: Líng Bǎo Pài), also known as the School of the Sacred Jewel or the School of Numinous Treasure, was an important Taoism school that emerged in China in between the Jin Dynasty and the Liu Song Dynasty in the early fifth century CE. Although reincarnation was an important concept in the Lingbao School, the earlier Taoism belief in attaining immortality remained. The most important scripture in the Lingbao School is known as the Five Talismans (Wufujing), which was compiled by Ge Chaofu and based on Ge Hong's earlier alchemical works. Although Lingbao no longer exists as a distinct movement, it has left influences on all subsequent branches of Taoism. The Lingbao School began in around 400 CE when the Lingbao scriptures were revealed to Ge Chaofu, the grandnephew of Ge Hong. Unlike previous Taoism cosmological systems which were divided into four to nine regions, Lingbao cosmology supposed that there were ten regions, an idea borrowed from Buddhism. Lingbao cosmology deviated from Buddhist beliefs by proposing that the heavens rotated around a huge mountain known as the Jade Capital, which was the residence of the Celestial Worthy, the Taoism version of the Buddha, and the primordial deity. Certain traditional Taoism ideas were retained in Lingbao cosmology, such as the idea that the world originated from a type of primordial qi known as yuanqi, and then was divided into heaven and earth. Despite a belief in reincarnation, the Lingbao School maintained the traditional Taoism idea that certain techniques could allow an adherent to achieve immortality. Lingbao ritual shares a great deal with ritual in other Taoism traditions. Lingbao Daoism also shared the multidimensional aspect of Taoism ritual, meaning that it was carried on at several different levels simultaneously. Lingbao scriptures are all based on a text known as the Text of the Five Talismans (Wufujing), which was compiled by Ge Chaofu between 397 and 402 and borrowed from the work of Ge Hong, his great uncle. While the Lingbao school did not survive as a distinct entity, its ritual apparatus did, and it forms the basis for present-day Taoism ritual practice." .

ns1:41944 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "With 178 episodes from 1992 to 2015, it currently holds the record for longest-running Nickelodeon series. Nick News with Linda Ellerbee has been shown on Sunday nights at 8 p.m. and later 8:30 p.m. on Nickelodeon, and ran in reruns at various timeslots. From 1993 to 1997, the first-run syndicated version of the show—distributed by Viacom Enterprises (now CBS Television Distribution)—aired on local stations—both independent and network-affiliated—all over the United States of America. After nearly 25 years on the air, an hour-long finale titled \"Hello, I Must Be Going: 25 Years of Nick News with Linda Ellerbee” aired on Nickelodeon on 15 December 2015." .

ns1:42058 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Atoleiros (Portugal pronunciation: ) took place on 06 April 1384, between a Portugal force and a punitive expedition from Crown of Castile sent by John I. The battle took place near the population centre of the same name in Alentejo. It was the first major battle of the 1383-85 Portuguese Interregnum. In the short battle that followed, the Castilian cavalry was unable to break the Portugal formation, suffering heavy losses." .

ns1:42151 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Creativity, formerly known as The (World) Church of the Creator, is a nontheistic, white supremacist anti-Christian religious movement that espouses white nationalism and antisemitism. It was founded in Lighthouse Point, Florida by Ben Klassen as \"Church of the Creator\" in 1973. The organization was originally founded as the \"Church of the Creator\" by Ben Klassen in 1973. In 1996, Matthew F. Hale, along with other ministers of the original Church of the Creator, formed a successor group known as the \"World Church of the Creator\". The World Church of the Creator was renamed \"The Creativity Movement\" in 2003. Following the demise of the World Church of the Creator after Hale's arrest in 2003, there formed two distinct groups, known as \"The Creativity Movement\" and the \"Creativity Alliance/Church of Creativity\". white supremacy is fundamental to Creativity, and as such, members are taught to hate and avoid interacting with non-whites socially. Creativity's founding text is Nature's Eternal Religion, which was written by Klassen in 1973. Creativity was formed in 1973, when Klassen self-published Nature's Eternal Religion. April Harrington (Gaede), mother of Lynx and Lamb Lingelser (Gaede) of the band Prussian Blue, was a longtime supporter of Creativity and a member of the World Church of the Creator, naming her third daughter Dresden Hale after its leader Matthew F. Hale before joining the National Alliance and then the splinter National Vanguard. Burdi has been credited with a role in Creativity's survival after the death of Ben Klassen. Matthew F. Hale founded the New Church of the Creator in 1996, later renamed the World Church of the Creator. Hale's World Church of the Creator was a new and separate group rather than a direct successor to Ben Klassen's Church of the Creator. The current group known as The Creativity Movement is a white power skinhead-oriented direct successor to Hale's World Church of the Creator. The Creativity Alliance was for a short period known as the White Crusaders of the Rahowa (WCOTR), which was founded by former World Church of the Creator Members after Hale's arrest in 2003." .

ns1:42253 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Drewry's Bluff, also known as the Battle of Fort Darling, or Fort Drewry, took place on 15 May 1862, in Chesterfield County, as part of the Peninsula Campaign of the American Civil War. Four Union Navy warships, including the ironclads USS Monitor and Galena, and the United States Revenue Cutter Service's ironclad USRC Naugatuck steamed up the James River to test the defenses of Richmond, Virginia, the Confederate capital. In the spring of 1862, Union Major General George B. McClellan launched an amphibious operation against Richmond by landing troops at Fort Monroe and then marching northwest up the Virginia Peninsula. After the fall of Yorktown and the withdrawal of General Joseph E. Johnston's army up the Peninsula, only the Confederate Navy ironclad CSS Virginia prevented Union occupation of the lower James River and Norfolk." .

ns1:42378 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1953 NBA All-Star Game was an exhibition basketball game played on 13 January 1953, at Allen County War Memorial Coliseum in Fort Wayne, Indiana, home of the Fort Wayne Pistons. Minneapolis Lakers' George Mikan, who led the West with 22 points and 16 rebounds, was named as the NBA All-Star Game Most Valuable Player. However, the NBA All-Star Game Most Valuable Player Award went to Minneapolis Lakers center George Mikan who scored a game-high 22 points and 16 rebounds for the West." .

ns1:42453 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Commodore 1351 is a computer mouse made by Commodore International in 1986, which can be directly plugged into the 9-pin control port of a Commodore 64 or Commodore 128. The Commodore 1351 is similar in appearance to the mouse supplied with Commodore International Amiga computers of the time, but the two are not mutually compatible. The mouse was distributed with a user manual containing maintenance instructions, pinouts, and sample BASIC and machine language programs for the Commodore 64 and Commodore 128. The first mouse released for the C64 was the NEOS Mouse in 1985 by the company Nihon Electronics Co. Ltd., however it was quickly superseded when Commodore International introduced their own mouse the following year." .

ns1:42456 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Skuodas or Schoden was a medieval battle fought in ca. 1259 near Skuodas in present-day Lithuania during the Lithuanian Crusade. Historian Edvardas Gudavičius dated the battle late summer or early fall 1258. The Knights pursued retreating Samogitians, who carried their loot back home and the decisive battle took place near Skuodas." .

ns1:42513 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Early 2009 Greek legislative election were held in Greece on 04 October 2009. Elections were not required until September 2011, but on 2 September 2009 Prime Minister Kostas Karamanlis of New Democracy announced that he would request President Karolos Papoulias dissolve Parliament and call elections. The result was a victory for the opposition Panhellenic Socialist Movement party led by Giorgos Papandreou, who became the new Prime Minister." .

ns1:42561 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Drina (Kingdom of Serbia: Bitka na Drini, Kingdom of Serbia Cyrillic: Битка на Дрини) was fought between the Kingdom of Serbia and Austro-Hungarian armies in 06 September 1914, during World War I. The Austro-Hungarians engaged in a significant offensive over the Drina river at the western Kingdom of Serbia border, resulting in numerous skirmishes (the Battle of Mačkov Kamen and the Battle of Gučevo being the heaviest ones). With most of his forces in Bosnia, general Oskar Potiorek decided that the best way to stop the Kingdom of Serbia offensive was to launch another invasion into Serbia to force the Serbs to recall their troops to defend their much smaller homeland." .

ns1:42614 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Women's World Draughts Championship at the International draughts held in Tallinn, Estonia International Draughts Federation World Draughts Federation. The tournament started on 01 October 2017 and lasted on 2017. Women's World Draughts Champion Natalia Sadowska (Poland) and former champion Zoja Golubeva (Latvia) participated in competition." .

ns1:42691 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2005–06 Cypriot First Division was the 67th season of the Cypriot top-level association football league. The champions ensured their participation in the 2006–07 UEFA Champions League and the runners-up in the 2006–07 UEFA Cup. They were replaced by the first three teams of the 2004–05 Cypriot Second Division, APOP Kinyras FC, APEP F.C. and ENTHOI Lakatamia FC." .

ns1:42703 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The World Chess Championship 2008 was a best-of-twelve-games match between the incumbent World Chess Championship, Viswanathan Anand, and the previous World Champion, Vladimir Kramnik. The match took place at the Art and Exhibition Hall of the Federal Republic of Germany in Bonn, Germany, between 14 October 2008 and 29 October 2008. In June 2007 FIDE announced that Topalov would get special privileges in the World Chess Championship 2010 qualifying cycle, while Kramnik, if he lost his title in 2007 (which he did, coming second behind Viswanathan Anand), would play a match with the tournament champion in 2008." .

ns1:42756 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1949 West German federal election were held in West Germany on 14 August 1949 to elect the first Bundestag, with a further eight seats elected in West Berlin between 1949 and January 1952 and another eleven between February 1952 and 1953. Most West German parties at the time of the 1949 Bundestag election were committed to democracy, but they disagreed on what kind of democracy West Germany should become. The Christian Democratic Union (CDU) leader, 73-year-old Konrad Adenauer, former mayor of Cologne and party chairman in the British Zone since March 1946, believed in moderate, non-denominational and Christian democracy, social market economy and integration with the West." .

ns1:42792 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Holy League (Latin: Liga Sancta, Spanish: Liga Santa, Italian: Lega Santa) of 1571 was arranged by Pope Pius V and included the major Catholic maritime states in the Mediterranean except France. The majority of warships were Venetian (6 galleasses, 109 galleys), the next largest contingent were Spanish (49 galleys, including 26 galleys from Naples, Sicily and other Italian territories), and Genoese (27 galleys), with additional warships from the Papal States (seven galleys), the Order of Saint Stephen from the Grand Duchy of Tuscany (five galleys), the Duchy of Savoy and the Knights of Malta (three galleys each), and some privately owned galleys in Spanish service. In 1573, the Holy League fleet failed to sail altogether; instead, Don John attacked and took Tunis, only for it to be retaken by the Ottomans in 1574. The Holy League was disbanded with the peace treaty of 1573, which concluded the War of Cyprus." .

ns1:42826 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The First Battle of Mesilla, was fought on 25 July 1861 at Mesilla in New Mexico Territory, in present-day Doña Ana County. The battle resulted in a Confederate victory and led directly to the official establishing of a Confederate Arizona, consisting of the southern portion of the New Mexico Territory. On August 1 Baylor declared the establishment of an organized Confederate Arizona, consisting of the portion of the New Mexico Territory south of the 34th parallel north. Baylor's success at Mesilla led to Henry Hopkins Sibley's ambitious New Mexico Campaign the following February." .

ns1:42862 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009–10 Cypriot Cup for lower divisions was the 2nd edition of the Cypriot Cup for lower divisions. It began on 04 November 2009 with the first round and concluded on 31 March 2010 with the final which was held at Geroskipou Municipality Stadium. Chalkanoras Idaliou won their 1st cup trophy after beating AEK Kouklia F.C. 1–0 in the final." .

ns1:42918 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Brandywine School was a style of illustration—as well as an art colony in Wilmington and in Chadds Ford Township, near the Brandywine Creek—both founded by artist Howard Pyle (1853–1911) at the end of the 19th century." .

ns1:42958 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Montecatini was fought in the Valdinievole on 29 August 1315 between the Republic of Pisa, and the forces of both the Kingdom of Naples and the Republic of Florence." .

ns1:42986 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Hohenfriedberg or Hohenfriedeberg, now Dobromierz, Lower Silesian Voivodeship, also known as the Battle of Strzegom, now Strzegom, was one of Frederick the Great's most admired victories. Frederick's Prussia army decisively defeated an Austrian army under Prince Charles Alexander of Lorraine on 04 June 1745 during the Second Silesian War (part of the War of the Austrian Succession). Austria sought to regain Silesia, which had been lost to Prussia in the Battle of Mollwitz. They encamped near Strzegom, with the Saxons just northwest of the town at Pilgrimshain and the Austrians spreading out west and south to the village of Dobromierz, Lower Silesian Voivodeship. The Austrian infantry formed two lines of battle facing east, from Dobromierz, Lower Silesian Voivodeship north. At this point the Prussia Bayreuth Dragoons, an oversize unit numbering around 1,500 men, entered the battle. The Second Silesian War, which was the last part of the War of the Austrian Succession in which Prussia took part, was almost at an end, and despite a close call at the Battle of Soor against the Austrians (who were again led by Charles of Lorraine), the peace at Dresden was signed on 25 December 1745, soon after yet another Prussia victory at the Battle of Kesselsdorf against the Saxons." .

ns1:42992 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Manx2 Flight 7100 was a scheduled commercial flight from Belfast, Northern Ireland, to Cork, Republic of Ireland. On 10 February 2011, the Fairchild Metro III aircraft flying the route with ten passengers and two crew on board crashed on its third attempt to land at Cork Airport in foggy conditions. (pp148–151) Flight 7100 was scheduled to depart George Best Belfast City Airport at 07:50 GMT arriving at Cork Airport for 09:00 IST. The aircraft arrived at George Best Belfast City Airport at 07:15 after a short positioning flight from Belfast International Airport, and was refuelled for the planned route-trip to Cork and back. His first flight in command of the aircraft took place on 6 10 February 2011, four days prior to the accident. (pp148-152) Manx2's Belfast-Cork route was operated on the air operator's certificate (AOC) of Flightline. As a result of the accident, Manx2 terminated its contract with Flightline and suspended its Belfast City – Cork route." .

ns1:42996 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Nathu La and Cho La clashes were a series of military clashes between India and China alongside the border of the Himalayan Kingdom of Sikkim, then an Indian protectorate. The Nathula clashes started on 11 14 September 1967, when the People's Liberation Army (PLA) launched an attack on Indian posts at Nathula, and lasted till 15 14 September 1967. Particularly at the Nathula pass in the valley, alongside the Sikkim-Tibet border, the deployed Chinese and Indian forces are stationed about 20–30 meters apart, which is the closest of anywhere on the 4000 km Sino-Indian border. In order to settle the situation, the Indian military hierarchy decided to lay another wire in the centre of the pass from Nathula to Sebu La to indicate their perceived border, on 11 14 September 1967. Accordingly, in the morning of 11 14 September 1967, the engineers and jawans (soldiers) of Indian Army started laying the stretch of fencing from Nathula to Sebu La along the perceived border. On 1 October 1967, another clash between India and China took place at Cho La, another pass on the Sikkim–Tibet border, a few kilometers north of Nathula. According to scholar John Garver, due to the Nathula incident, Indian concerns were roused about China's intentions regarding Sikkim." .

ns1:43056 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 36th running of the 1952 Tour of Flanders cycling classic was held on Sunday, 06 April 1952. Belgian Roger Decock won the race in a three-man sprint before Loretto Petrucci and Albéric Schotte. The race started in Ghent and finished in Wetteren – totaling 258 km." .

ns1:43095 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Narva Offensive (July 1944) was a campaign fought between the German army detachment \"Narwa\" and the Soviet Leningrad Front for the city of Narva in 24 July 1944–30 July 1944. The Soviet success in Belorussia and Ukraine brought the Oberkommando des Heeres to propose a withdrawal of the Battle for Narva (1944) and the 18th Army to the line between Riga and Daugavpils 400 kilometres south of Narva. For the commencement of the Narva Offensive (July 1944) Operation on 24 July 1944, most of the Soviet units and artillery were concentrated in the Krivasoo Bridgehead in the south. The Stavka demanded the destruction of the Battle for Narva (1944) and the capture of town of Rakvere more than a hundred kilometres from Narva by 7 August." .

ns1:43100 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "International Polar Bear Day is an annual event celebrated every February 27 to raise awareness about the conservation status of the polar bear. International Polar Bear Day is organized by Polar Bears International to raise awareness about the impact of global warming and reduced sea ice on polar bear populations." .

ns1:43121 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Sknyliv air show disaster occurred on 27 July 2002, when a Ukrainian Air Force Sukhoi Su-27 piloted by Volodymyr Toponar and co-piloted by Yuriy Yegorov crashed during an aerobatics presentation at Lviv Danylo Halytskyi International Airport near Lviv, Ukraine. The accident killed 77 people and injured 543, 100 of whom were hospitalised." .

ns1:43173 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The New Policies (Chinese: 新法; pinyin: xīnfǎ) were a series of reforms initiated by the Northern Song dynasty reformer Wang Anshi when he served as minister under Emperor Shenzong of Song from 1069–1076. Wang Anshi's faction, known as the \"Reformers\", were opposed by the ministers in the \"Conservative\" faction led by the historian and Chancellor Sima Guang (1019–1086)." .

ns1:43295 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1956 Greek legislative election were held in Greece on 19 February 1956. Although the Liberal Democratic Union, a coalition of centrist parties, received a slim plurality of votes, the conservative governing party, the National Radical Union, won the most seats due to a complex and controversial electoral system enacted by Karamanlis. A \"first past the post\" system was applied in the rural constituencies where the National Radical Union was expected to gain a plurality, while proportional representation was reserved for the urban constituencies, where the Liberal Democratic Union was expected to lead. The Liberal Democratic Union included the Liberal Party, led by Sophocles Venizelos and Georgios Papandreou, as well as the left-wing EDA, led by Ioannis Passalidis. A few years later, Georgios Papandreou and Sophocles Venizelos renounced their alliance with EDA, breaking up the Liberal Democratic Union." .

ns1:43325 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2021 World Junior Ice Hockey Championships (2021 WJHC) are the 45th edition of the IIHF World U20 Championship. It began on 25 December 2020, and will end with the gold medal game on 05 January 2021. The following officials were assigned by the International Ice Hockey Federation to officiate the 2021 World Junior Championships. The International Ice Hockey Federation announced the groups on January 6, 2019, with Austria men's national junior ice hockey team being promoted from Division I A after winning the 2020 Division I A Tournament. The tournament would have been held in Tallinn, Estonia, from 10 to 17, 2021. The tournament would have been held in Mexico City, Mexico, from 10 to 17, 2021." .

ns1:43335 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1971 Italian presidential election was held in Italy on 09 December 1971–24 December 1971. As head of state of the Italy, the President of Italy has a role of representation of national unity and guarantees that Italian politics comply with the Italian Constitution, in the framework of a parliamentary system. On 24 December 1971 former Prime Minister and President of Italy of the Chamber of Deputies Giovanni Leone was elected President of Italy with 518 votes out of 1,008, the smallest majority ever obtained by an elected President of Italy. In this extremely difficult context for republican institutions, on 09 December 1971 Italian Parliament convened to elect a new President of Italy. After a long voting period which lasted almost two weeks, the Christian democrat Giovanni Leone managed to be elected President of Italy with the votes of the Christian Democracy and the neo-fascist Italian Social Movement, facing the extreme opposition of the left-wing parties. Giovanni Leone officially sworn in as the new President of Italy on 1971." .

ns1:43415 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Nuit debout is a France social movement that began on 31 March 2016, arising out of protests against proposed labor reforms known as the El Khomri law or Loi travail. The movement began at the Place de la République in Paris, where protestors held nightly assemblies following the 31 March 2016 protest. If adopted, the El Khomri law would make sweeping changes to the country's labor code, with the effect of making it easier for companies to lay off workers, and loosening restrictions on working hours, including reducing overtime payments for hours worked beyond France's statutory 35-hour workweek. The largest of these protests, on 31 March 2016 attracted 390,000 participants nationwide, according to the France authorities (unions put the figure at 1.2 million). The Nuit debout movement has its origins at a meeting held in Paris on 2016 which was organized by François Ruffin, the founder of the left-wing journal Fakir and the director of the documentary film Merci patron!. Those attending the meeting agreed that they would occupy Paris's Place de la République on 31 March 2016, following organized street protests that were scheduled to take place on that day against the El Khomri law. Several Nuit debout events were held in Paris suburbs such as Saint-Denis and Saint-Ouen in mid-April, however these failed to attract local participants in large numbers. Within the first ten days, protests were held in cities including Brussels, Berlin, Lisbon, and Madrid. Nuit debout's organizers called for a global day of protests, under the name Global debout, to take place on Sunday, 15 May, a date chosen to coincide with the fifth anniversary of the Spanish 2011–2012 Spanish protests. Turnout on the day was low: in Brussels around 150 people participated; in Berlin the number was around 100; in Paris's Place de la République around 1,500 people attended. Some within the movement have called for a general strike along the lines of previous major strikes in France, in 1936 and in May 1968, with the aim of forcing the withdrawal of the El Khomri law. As with the France Revolution, the movement rewrote the calendar: all dates following the 31 March 2016 protest were renamed as a continuation of the month of March. On 10 May, facing opposition to the El Khomri law from a group of his own MPs, Prime Minister Valls announced that the government planned to force the bill through parliament without a vote, using Article 49.3 of the France Constitution. Hundreds of Nuit debout participants gathered outside France's National Assembly to protest the move." .

ns1:43721 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Mława, otherwise known as the Defence of the Mława position, took place to the north of the town of Mława in northern Poland between 01 September 1939 and 03 September 1939. It was one of the opening battles of the Invasion of Poland and World War II in general. It was fought between the forces of the Polish Modlin Army under General Emil Krukowicz-Przedrzymirski and the German 3rd Army under General Georg von Küchler." .

ns1:43773 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1982 Florida subtropical storm, officially known as Subtropical Storm One, was the only subtropical cyclone of the exceptionally inactive 1982 Atlantic hurricane season. The subtropical storm peaked at 70 mph winds (110 km/h) on 18 June 1982, but did not reach its minimum pressure until June 20, when it dropped to 984 mbar (29.07 inHg). Winds reached 50 mph (60 km/h) with gusts up to 66 mph (101 km/h) at the Oak Island Coast Guard Station near Cape Fear, North Carolina and 54 mph (87 km/h) with gusts of 77 mph (123 km/h) at the offshore tower at Frying Pan Shoals about forty miles southeast of Cape Fear on 18 June 1982 with 70 mph winds (110 km/h) the next day." .

ns1:43882 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 General Aung San Shield Final was the 5th Bogyoke Aung San Cup of the MFF Cup. on 2015 and was the Bogyoke Aung San Cup match of the Bogyoke Aung San Cup. Ayeyawady United F.C. were playing a record 3rd MFF Cup Bogyoke Aung San Cup. Both Ayeyawady United F.C. FC and Yadanarbon F.C. received a ticket allocation of 10,000 for the game. At the Bogyoke Aung San Stadium, Ayeyawady United F.C. drew 2–2 and won (4-3) on penalty shots. Ayeyawady United F.C.'s Captain, Min Min Thu missed the Bogyoke Aung San Cup." .

ns1:44058 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Tropical Depression Fourteen was the last tropical depression of the 1987 Atlantic hurricane season and was the third most destructive storm of the year. The depression formed on 31 October 1987 in the Caribbean Sea, heading along a northward path into the southern Gulf of Mexico and into Florida until the system was absorbed on 04 November 1987. A reconnaissance aircraft was scheduled for the next morning to investigate the forming system, but by 1400 UTC on 31 October 1987, the National Hurricane Center in Miami, Florida had upgraded the system to a tropical depression based on the imagery and surface data." .

ns1:44084 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1897 École des Beaux-Arts vs. Académie Julian football game was a college football game between American students of the École des Beaux-Arts and the Académie Julian, played on Thanksgiving Day 25 November 1897 at 2:30 pm in Levallois-Perret near Paris, France." .

ns1:44119 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008–2009 Grand Prix of Figure Skating Final was the senior and junior Grand Prix Final competition of the 2008–2009 figure skating season. It was the culminating competition of the 2008–09 ISU Grand Prix of Figure Skating, a senior-level international invitational competition, and the 2008–09 ISU Junior Grand Prix, a junior-level international competition. Skaters had earned points towards qualifying for the senior Grand Prix Final at the 2008 Skate America, the 2008 Skate Canada International, the 2008 Cup of China, the 2008 Trophée Eric Bompard, the 2008 Cup of Russia, and the 2008 NHK Trophy. Hosted by the Korea Skating Union, the Grand Prix Final was held in Goyang, South Korea from 10 December 2008 to 14, 2008." .

ns1:44149 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Lewes was one of two main battle of the conflict known as the Second Barons' War. It took place at Lewes in Sussex, on 14 May 1264. Henry III left the safety of Lewes Castle and St. Pancras Priory to engage the Barons in battle and was initially successful, his son Prince Edward routing part of the baronial army with a cavalry charge. In 1994, an archaeological survey of the cemetery of St Nicholas Hospital, in Lewes, revealed the remains of bodies that were thought to be combatants from the battle of Lewes." .

ns1:44160 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 Wah bombing was a double suicide attack on the Pakistan Ordnance Factories (POF) in Wah Cantonment, Pakistan, on 21 August 2008. The Pakistan Ordnance Factories (POF) in Wah Cantonment, Pakistan, is the center of the country's defence industry. Following an impeachment movement launched by major opposition parties, the President of Pakistan Pervez Musharraf resigned on 18 21 August 2008, ending his nine years as head of the country." .

ns1:44167 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Herald Sun Tour was the 62nd edition of the Herald Sun Tour road bicycle racing. The 2015 race consisted of five stages in Victoria, starting in Melbourne on 04 February 2015 and finished at the summit finish on Arthurs Seat on 08 February 2015. The other riders to finish on the overall podium were Patrick Bevin and Joseph Cooper (both Avanti Racing Team)." .

ns1:44301 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2004 Kufa shelling occurred on 26 August 2004 when the main mosque in Kufa, Iraq was hit by a barrage of mortar rounds. The mortar rounds struck the mosque, killing 74 people, and injuring 315." .

ns1:44353 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Vox is a children's fantasy novel by Paul Stewart and Chris Riddell, first published in 2003. It is the sixth volume of The Edge Chronicles and the second of the Rook Saga trilogy; within the stories' own chronology it is the eighth novel, following the Quint Saga and Twig Saga trilogies. The Bookseller's Wendy Cooling stated that the novel was just as thrilling as the first novel in The Edge Chronicles series. Marya Jansen-Gruber from Through The Looking Glass Children's Book Reviews felt that it was an engrossing story, commenting that she could not \"help marveling at the way in which Paul Stewart and Chris Riddell together have created a world so full of both darkness and light.\"" .

ns1:44369 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Battle of Quilacura was a battle in the Arauco War, fought at night, four leagues from the Bío Bío River, between the Spanish Empire expedition of Pedro de Valdivia and a force of Mapuche warriors led by Malloquete on 11 February 1546." .

ns1:44373 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "On 11 November 2018 2020-13 November 2018 2020, its third edition will be chiefly devoted to the multi-actor response to Coronavirus, with the conviction that all relevant actors can collectively overcome the enormous challenges faced by the international community and use the crisis as an opportunity to rebuild a more sustainable world. The first edition of the Paris Peace Forum 2018 11 November 2018 2018o13 11 November 2018 11 November 2018, and was attended by 65 Heads of States and Governments. What was noted was also US President's Donald Trump's absence at the Forum, despite having been in Paris in the morning of 11 November 2018 for the commemoration ceremony on the Champs-Elysées. The second edition of the Paris Peace Forum 2018 took place on 11 November 2018–13 November 2018 at la Grande halle de la Villette, under the presidency of Pascal Lamy, who was previously President of the Steering Committee, and with Justin Vaïsse as the Forum's Director General. Secretary General of the United Nations, António Guterres, Mayor of Paris, Anne Hidalgo, and President of the Paris Peace Forum 2018, Pascal Lamy, spoke during the event's soft opening on 11 November 2018. Opening remarks were made by President of the France, Emmanuel Macron, the President-Elect of the European Commission, Ursula Von der Leyen, President of the Democratic Republic of the Congo, Félix Tshisekedi and the Vice-President of the People's Republic of China, Wang Qishan. Due to similar such contradictions, such as France's involvement in weapons sales, and nuclear armament, questions were raised as to the extent to which the peace summit was truly substantial or just an instrument of communication." .

ns1:44435 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Wuxi Classic was a professional ranking snooker tournament held between 23 June 2014–29 June 2014 at the Wuxi City Sports Park Stadium in Wuxi, China. Neil Robertson defended his title, which he won in 2013 Wuxi Classic, by defeating Joe Perry 10–9 in the final. The breakdown of prize money for this year is shown below: These matches were played in Wuxi on 23 June 2014." .

ns1:44473 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The December 2010 Mohmand Agency bombings took place on 06 December 2010 after two suicide bombers targeted a government compound in Ghalanai, the main town and capital of the Mohmand Agency located in Pakistan's Federally Administered Tribal Areas along the Afghan border." .

ns1:44564 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1969 Tour de France was the 56th edition of the Tour de France, one of cycling's Grand Tours. It took place between 28 June 1969 and 20 July 1969, with 22 stages covering a distance of 4,117 km (2,558 mi). As an example in 1967 nine riders finished within 20:00 of the winner, in 1968 Tour de France nineteen riders were within 20:00 but in 1969 the 10th place rider was +52:56, the 20th place rider was +1:17:36 and only Roger Pingeon finished inside 20:00 of Merckx. Eddy Merckx rode on the winning team, Faemino-Faema, and also won the combination classification as well as the combativity award. The Tour started with 13 teams, each with 10 cyclists: Eddy Merckx had been removed from the 1969 Giro d'Italia in leading position because of a positive doping result, and was initially not allowed to join the 1969 Tour de France, but his suspension was later lifted. The teams entering the race were: The 1969 Tour de France started on 28 June 1969, and had no rest days. The highest point of elevation in the race was 2,556 m (8,386 ft) at the summit tunnel of the Col du Galibier mountain pass on stage 10. Notably, the 1965, 1967 and 1968 Tour de France winners in Felice Gimondi, Roger Pingeon and Jan Janssen were all distanced into the surviving peloton group which finished some two and a half minutes behind Altig. During the 2019 Tour de France Eddy Merckx and the 50th anniversary of this Tour were honored at the Grand Depart in Belgium." .

ns1:44621 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "futurism (Italian: Futurismo) was an artistic and social movement that originated in Italy in the early 20th century. Cubism contributed to the formation of Italian futurism's art movement style. futurism is an avant-garde movement founded in Milan in 1909 by the Italian poet Filippo Tommaso Marinetti. Marinetti launched the movement in his Manifesto of futurism, which he published for the first time on 1909 in La gazzetta dell'Emilia, an article then reproduced in the French daily newspaper Le Figaro on Saturday 1909. As the art critic Robert Hughes observed, \"In futurism, the eye is fixed and the object moves, but it is still the basic vocabulary of Cubism—fragmented and overlapping planes\". In 1914, personal quarrels and art movement differences between the Milan group, around Marinetti, Boccioni, and Balla, and the Florence group, around Carrà, Ardengo Soffici (1879–1964) and Giovanni Papini (1881–1956), created a rift in Italian futurism. Cubo-futurism combines the forms of Cubism with the Futurist representation of movement; like their Italian contemporaries, the Russian Futurists were fascinated with dynamism, speed and the restlessness of modern urban life. futurism as a literary movement made its official debut with F.T. Marinetti's Manifesto of futurism (1909), as it delineated the various ideals Futurist poetry should strive for. Notably among these female futurists is Filippo Tommaso Marinetti's own wife Benedetta Cappa Marinetti, whom he had met in 1918 and exchanged a series of letters discussing each of their respective work in futurism. futurism expanded to encompass many art movement domains and ultimately included painting, sculpture, ceramics, graphic design, industrial design, interior design, theatre design, textiles, drama, literature, music and architecture. futurism as a coherent and organized art movement movement is now regarded as extinct, having died out in 1944 with the death of its leader Marinetti. In 2014, the Solomon R. Guggenheim Museum featured the exhibition \"Italian futurism, 1909–1944: Reconstructing the Universe\"." .

ns1:44639 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Jinghe earthquake occurred at 07:27 People's Republic of China Standard Time (CST, UTC+8) on 09 August 2017, in Jinghe County, Bortala Mongol Autonomous Prefecture, Xinjiang Uyghur Autonomous Region, People's Republic of China, with magnitude 6.6 and depth 11 kilometres. The epicentre was 44.27°N 82.89°E / 44.27; 82.89. Until 11:00 CST (UTC+8) on 09 August 2017, this earthquake caused 32 people injured, including 2 critical injuries, and 142 buildings were collapsed and 1060 buildings were damaged." .

ns1:44756 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2001 Marlboro 500 was a Championship Auto Racing Teams (CART) motor race held on 04 November 2001 at the Auto Club Speedway, in Fontana, California. It was the 21st and final round of the 2001 CART season and the fifth annual edition of the MAVTV 500 at Auto Club Speedway. Max Papis finished second for Team Rahal and Forsythe Racing driver Alex Tagliani came in third. The MAVTV 500 was confirmed as part of CART's 2001 series schedule in August 2000. The MAVTV 500 was the 21st and final scheduled race for 2001 by CART, and was held on 04 November 2001 at Auto Club Speedway, in Fontana, California." .

ns1:44886 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1977 Giro d'Italia was the 60th running of the Giro d'Italia, one of cycling's Grand Tours races. The Giro started in Bacoli, on 20 May 1977, with a 7 km (4.3 mi) prologue and concluded in Milan, on 13 June, with a 122 km (75.8 mi) mass-start stage. A total of 130 riders from thirteen teams entered the 22-stage race, that was won by Belgian Michel Pollentier of the Flandria team. The second and third places were taken by Italians Francesco Moser and Gianbattista Baronchelli, respectively. Out of the 140 riders that started this edition of the Giro d'Italia, a total of 121 riders made it to the finish in Milan." .

ns1:44957 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Great Bridge was fought 09 December 1775, in the area of Great Bridge, Virginia, early in the American Revolutionary War. The victory by colonial Virginia militia forces led to the departure of Royal Governor Lord Dunmore and any remaining vestiges of Great Britain power over the Colony of Virginia during the early days of the conflict. Colonel William Woodford, the Virginia militia commander at the battle, described it as \"a second Bunker's Hill affair\". Tensions in the Great Britain Colony of Virginia were raised in April 1775 at roughly the same time that the hostilities of the American Revolutionary War broke out in the Province of Massachusetts Bay with the Battles of Lexington and Concord. General Thomas Gage, the Great Britain commander-in-chief for North America, had ordered small detachments of the 14th Regiment of Foot to Virginia in response to pleas by Dunmore for military help. Dunmore reacted to this event by issuing a proclamation on November 7 in which he declared martial law, and offered to emancipate colonist slaves in Virginia willing to serve in the Great Britain Army. William Woodford, the colonel leading the 2nd Virginia Regiment, advanced toward the bridge with his regiment of 400 and about 100 riflemen from the Culpeper Minutemen. Colonel William Woodford, reporting on the 2nd Virginia Regiment's service at the battle, wrote in a letter published in Purdie's Virginia Gazette, December 15, 1775: \"This was a second Bunker's Hill affair, in miniature; with this difference, that we kept our post, and had only one man wounded in the hand.\" After further raiding operations in the Chesapeake, Dunmore and the Great Britain fleet left for New York City in August 1776. A highway marker was placed by the state of Virginia in 1934 near the battle site." .

ns1:44959 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Valiancy (Filipino: Oplan Kagitingan) was the initial military operation of the 2000 Philippine campaign against the Moro Islamic Liberation Front which took place in Maguindanao, Philippines. The Philippine Army, primarily units of its 6th Infantry Division, assaulted Moro Islamic Liberation Front forces in the Talayan-Shariff Aguak-Datu Piang area of Maguindanao and captured Camp Omar ibn al-Khattab, its third largest camp located there." .

ns1:45167 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1801 New York gubernatorial election was held in 12 April 1801 to elect the Governor of New York and Lieutenant Governor of New York. The Democratic-Republican Party nominated state assemblyman and former Governor of New York George Clinton. The Federalist Party nominated Lieutenant Governor of New York Stephen Van Rensselaer." .

ns1:45208 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Scarlet Beach (Operation Diminish) (22 September 1943) took place in New Guinea during the Huon Peninsula campaign of the Second World War, involving forces from Australia, the United States and Japan. The Huon Peninsula is situated along the north-east coast of Papua New Guinea, and stretches from Lae in the south on the Huon Gulf to Sio in the north along the Vitiaz Strait. Blamey relinquished command of New Guinea Force on 22 September 1943, handing over to Lieutenant General Sir Iven Mackay. Between 22 September 1943 and 02 October 1943, the 20th Infantry Brigade had taken its objectives." .

ns1:45262 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Jodo shu (浄土宗, \"The Pure Land School\"), also known as Jōdo Buddhism, is a branch of Pure Land Buddhism derived from the teachings of the Japanese ex-Tendai monk Hōnen. It was established in 1175 and is the most widely practiced branch of Buddhism in Japan, along with Jōdo Shinshū. The writings of Hōnen, contained mostly in the Senchaku-hongan-nembutsu-shū (often abbreviated to Senchakushū), are another source for Jodo shu thought as is his last writing, the Ichimai-Kishōmon (一枚起請文, \"One-Sheet Document\")." .

ns1:45426 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of the Ebro (Spanish: Batalla del Ebro, Catalan: Batalla de l'Ebre) was the longest and largest battle of the Spanish Civil War. It took place between July and 16 November 1938, with fighting mainly concentrated in two areas on the lower course of the Ebro River, the Terra Alta comarca of Catalonia, and the Auts area close to Fayón (Faió) in the lower Matarranya, Bajo Aragón-Caspe/Baix Aragó-Casp. On 16 November 1938 the last men of the 35th recrossed the Ebro at Flix and the battle ended." .

ns1:45433 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The All Hope Is Gone World Tour was a concert tour by Slipknot that took place in 2008 and 2009 in support of the group's fourth studio album All Hope Is Gone. The tour consisted of nine legs and 153 shows, beginning on 09 July 2008 and finishing on 31 October 2009. The Australian leg of the All Hope Is Gone World Tour was the first time Slipknot were able to bring what percussionist Shawn Crahan described as \"the real set, the real deal\" to an Australian audience." .

ns1:45513 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Umkhosi Wokweshwama (\"First Fruits festival\"), recently also known as Umkhosi Woselwa (\"Lagenaria siceraria festival\"), is the annual harvest festival of the Zulu people, observed around the December solstice. It takes place at the Enyokeni Royal Palace in Nongoma, KwaZulu-Natal, and is presided over by the Zulu King, who conducts a tasting ceremony as sacred king, closed by the dashing of a Lagenaria siceraria to signify that the people may now enjoy the fruits of the harvest; this role was historically expanded by Shaka to have more military significance with a muster and parade." .

ns1:45556 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Sibiu Cycling Tour 2018 is a cycling stage race that took place between 05 July 2018 and 08 July 2018 in and around Sibiu, Romania. Raced over a technical 2.2 km (1.4 mi) with 20 corners and several cobbled sections, the stage was won by Italian Davide Ballerini who would be the only rider to finish in under 3 minutes. Initially, two riders, Maxim Rusnac and Amaro Antunes remained with him, but with a second attack he shook off the other contenders and rode to a solo victory. For winning a stage, a rider earns 25 points, with 20 for second, 16 for third, 14 for fourth, 12 for fifth, 10 for sixth with a point fewer per place down to a single point for 15th place." .

ns1:45596 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Tour of Flanders was the 97th edition of the Tour of Flanders single-day cycling race, known as one of the Monument classics. It was held on 31 March 2013 over a distance of 256 kilometres (159.1 miles) from Bruges to Oudenaarde, and was the eighth race of the 2013 UCI World Tour season. The race was won for the second time by RadioShack–Leopard rider Fabian Cancellara, after he made a solo attack with around 15 km (9.3 mi) remaining of the race, on the race's final cobbled climb of the Paterberg. Second place went to Peter Sagan of the Cannondale team, while third place went to Lotto–Belisol's Jürgen Roelandts; both of those riders had been in a group with Cancellara before his move, and eventually finished nearly one-and-a-half minutes behind Cancellara." .

ns1:45625 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "KylieX2008 was the tenth concert tour by Australian recording artist Kylie Minogue, in support of her tenth studio album, X (2007). The tour began on 06 May 2008 in Paris, France at the Palais Omnisports de Paris-Bercy and concluded on 22 December 2008 in Melbourne, Australia at the Rod Laver Arena, consisting of 53 shows in Europe, six shows in South America, seven shows in Asia and eight shows in Oceania. Upon the announcement of the For You, For Me Tour the following year, both Billboard and Pollstar confirmed that KylieX2008 generated an estimated US$70,000,000 in ticket sales." .

ns1:45630 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Wadi Akarit (Operation Scipio) was an Allied attack from 06 April 1943–07 April 1943, to dislodge Axis forces from positions along the Wadi Akarit in Tunisia during the Tunisia campaign of the Second World War. After the Mareth Line in southern Tunisia had been outflanked in Operation Supercharge II, through the Tebaga Gap, Axis forces had withdrawn to the Wadi Akarit, north of Gabès. On 07 April 1943, the Americans raced down the El Guettar–Gabès road, where it met advanced troops of the Eighth Army at 5:00 p.m. Alexander moved the II US Corps to the north, because the Eighth Army was better prepared for the final offensive." .

ns1:45789 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The First Battle of Champagne (French: 1ère Bataille de Champagne-Ardenne) was fought from 20 December 1914 – 17 March 1915 in World War I in the Champagne-Ardenne region of France and was the second offensive by the Allies against the German Empire since mobile warfare had ended after the First Battle of Ypres in Flanders (19 October 1914 – 22 November 1914). The Bureau recommended to General Joseph Joffre a dual offensive, with attacks in Artois and Champagne-Ardenne, to crush the Noyon salient. After skirmishes the battle began on 20 December 1914 when the XVII and I Colonial Corps attacked and made small gains. Supporting attacks in Artois and Champagne-Ardenne by the Second Army, Eighth Army and the troops on the coast at Nieuport supported the Tenth Army at Arras in the First Battle of Artois (17 20 December 1914 –13 January 1915)." .

ns1:45798 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1949 Giro d'Italia was the 32nd edition of the Giro d'Italia, a cycling race organized and sponsored by the newspaper La Gazzetta dello Sport. The race began on 21 May 1949 in Palermo with a stage that stretched 261 km (162 mi) to Catania, finishing in Monza on 12 June 1949 after a 267 km (166 mi) stage and a total distance covered of 4,088 km (2,540 mi). The race was won by the Italian rider Fausto Coppi of the Bianchi team, with fellow Italians Gino Bartali and Giordano Cottur coming in second and third respectively. Out of the 105 riders that started this edition of the Giro d'Italia, a total of 65 riders made it to the finish in Monza. The teams entering the race were: The main favorites entering the race were Gino Bartali and Fausto Coppi. The other stages that included categorized climbs were stages: 1, 3, 11, 13, 14, 15, 17, and 19." .

ns1:45800 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The African Film Festival (formerly Festival de Cine Africano de Tarifa (African Film Festival of Tarifa) or FCAT) is an annual festival devoted to African cinema held in the Spanish city of Cordoba. The event was founded in 2004 under the name of the African Film Showcase of Tarifa and changed its name to African Film Festival of Tarifa in 2007. Since 2012, it is known as the Cordoba African Film Festival-FCAT (Córdoba is the new host city). The African Film Festival of Tarifa, also known by the acronym FCAT, was born in 2004 at the initiative of the NGO Al Tarab, based in Tarifa, in Cadiz province." .

ns1:46007 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008–09 UCI America Tour was the fifth season for the UCI America Tour. The season began on 05 October 2008 with the Vuelta Chihuahua Internacional and ended on 12 September 2009 with the Univest Grand Prix. Gregorio Ladino of Colombia was crowned as the 2008–09 UCI America Tour champion." .

ns1:46114 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1928 Coupe de France Final was a association football match held at Stade Olympique Yves-du-Manoir, Colombes on 06 May 1928, that saw Red Star F.C. defeat CA Paris-Charenton 3–1 thanks to goals by Paul Wartel, Brenna Egil Lund and Juste Brouzes." .

ns1:46147 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1992 Volta a Catalunya was the 72nd edition of the Volta a Catalunya cycle race and was held from 08 June 1992 to 14 June 1992. The race started in Sant Carles de la Ràpita and finished in Sant Feliu de Guíxols." .

ns1:46182 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Tour Down Under was a road cycling road bicycle racing that took place between 19 January 2016 and 24 January 2016 in and around Adelaide, South Australia. It was the 18th edition of the Tour Down Under and was the first event of the 2016 UCI World Tour. As the Tour Down Under is a 2016 UCI World Tour event, all eighteen UCI World Teams were invited automatically and obliged to enter a team in the race. The route of the 2016 Tour Down Under was announced at the beginning of July 2015 and centred around the city of Adelaide in South Australia. These were Rohan Dennis and Richie Porte (both BMC Racing Team) and Simon Gerrans (Orica–GreenEDGE). The strongest sprinters in the race included Caleb Ewan (Orica–GreenEDGE), Wouter Wippert (Cannondale), Giacomo Nizzolo (Trek–Segafredo) and Matteo Pelucchi (IAM Cycling)." .

ns1:46213 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Remembrance Day (Marshall Islands), formally known as Nuclear Victims' Day and Nuclear Survivors' Day, occurs on March 1 and is a national holiday in the Marshall Islands. Remembrance Day was the code name given to the first U.S. test of a dry fuel thermonuclear hydrogen bomb device, detonated on March 1, 1954 at Bikini Atoll, Marshall Islands, as the first test of Operation Castle." .

ns1:46297 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Cepeda took place on 01 February 1820 in Cañada de Cepeda, Santa Fe Province, Argentina. Federal League Provinces of Santa Fe Province and Entre Ríos Province joined forces to topple the 1819 centralist Constitution and the Directorial government of the United Provinces of the Río de la Plata. The complete victory of the Federal forces over a diminished Directorial army led to the end of the central authority established by the 1819 Constitution through the Supreme Director, and laid the foundations for a rather new Federal organization for Argentina, as the Provinces of Santa Fe Province, Entre Ríos Province and Buenos Aires signed the Treaty of Pilar on February 23. Some provisions included in the Treaty on behalf of Buenos Aires, proved unacceptable to Artigas (styled \"Protector of the League of the Free Peoples\", who was exiled in Entre Ríos Province following his defeat to the Brazilian Empire invasion of the Banda Oriental), so he ordered both López and Ramírez to renounce it." .

ns1:46371 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Winter Paralympics (Korean: 평창 동계 패럴림픽; Hanja: 平昌 冬季 패럴림픽; RR: Pyeongchang Donggye Paereollimpik), the 12th Winter Paralympic Games, and also more generally known as the PyeongChang 2018 Paralympic Winter Games, were an international multi-sport event for athletes with disabilities governed by the International Paralympic Committee (IPC), that was held in Pyeongchang County, Gangwon Province, South Korea, from 9 to 18 March 2018. As part of a formal agreement between the International Paralympic Committee and the International Olympic Committee first established in 2001, the winner of the bid for the 2018 Winter Olympics was also to host the 2018 Winter Paralympics. The opening ceremony was held on 09 March 2018 at Pyeongchang Olympic Stadium, which was built specifically for the Winter Games." .

ns1:46383 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018–19 Scottish Premiership (known as the Ladbrokes Premiership for sponsorship reasons) was the sixth season of the Scottish Premiership, the highest division of Scottish football. On 4 19 May 2019, Celtic F.C. won their eighth consecutive title and 50th overall after a 3–0 win over Aberdeen. St Mirren secured the 2018–19 Scottish Championship title and promotion to the Premiership on 14 April 2018 after a goalless draw with Livingston, who were also promoted after winning the 2017–18 Scottish Premiership. Ross County were relegated to the 2018–19 Scottish Championship on 12 after a 1–1 draw with St Johnstone." .

ns1:46396 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "En (Н н; italics: Н н) is a letter of the Cyrillic. The Cyrillic letter En was derived from the Greek letter Nu (Ν ν). The name of En in the Early Cyrillic alphabet was нашь (našĭ), meaning \"ours\". The capital Cyrillic letter En looks exactly the same as the capital H but, as with most Cyrillic letters, the lowercase form is simply a smaller version of the uppercase. Rather than from the Greek letter Eta, from which Latin H originated, the Cyrillic letter En ⟨Н⟩ was derived from the Greek letter Nu. By exception, the Romanian Cyrillic alphabet used N and ɴ, instead of Н and н." .

ns1:46414 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Tour de Langkawi was the 12th edition of the Tour de Langkawi, a cycling stage race that took place in Malaysia. It started on 02 February 2007 in Langkawi and ended on 11 February 2007 in Kuala Lumpur. Anthony Charteau, the French cyclist emerged as the winner of the race, followed by two Colombian cyclists; José Serpa second and Walter Pedraza third." .

ns1:46418 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2004–05 Azerbaijan Top League was the thirteenth 2003–04 Azerbaijan Top League of the Top League since its establishment in 1992. The 2003–04 Azerbaijan Top League began on 07 August 2004 and finished on 24 May 2005. Neftchi Baku PFC were the defending champions, having won the previous 2003–04 Azerbaijan Top League, and they retained the title after defeating Khazar Lankaran in a playoff after both teams finished level on points and goal difference." .

ns1:46462 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Ilford rail crash occurred on 16 January 1944 when, in darkness and dense fog, an express passenger train passed a signal at danger and collided with another passenger train that was stopped at Ilford railway station in Essex, England. At approximately 19:20 on 16 January 1944, in dense fog and wartime conditions, the 14:38 express train from Yarmouth was stopped at Ilford en route to London Liverpool Street." .

ns1:46468 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1993 Cypriot presidential election were held in Cyprus on 07 February 1993, with a second round on 14 February 1993. The result was a victory for Glafcos Clerides of the Democratic Rally after he finished as runner-up behind George Vassiliou of the AKEL in the first round, a reverse of the 1988 Cypriot presidential election." .

ns1:46510 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Miss Universe 1972, the 21st Miss Universe pageant, was held on 29 July 1972, 1972 at the Cerromar Beach Hotel in Dorado, Puerto Rico. Kerry Anne Wells earned Australia's first Miss Universe crown. Wells was crowned by Miss Universe 1970, Marisol Malaret of Puerto Rico and Miss Universe 1971 1st Runner-Up Toni Rayward." .

ns1:46528 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Paris–Nice is a professional road bicycle racing in France, held annually since 1933. It is organized by Amaury Sport Organisation, which also manages most other French World Tour races, most notably cycling's flagships the Tour de France and Paris–Roubaix. The 2020 Paris-Nice was the last international cycling event, as well as the last sporting event in France, before mass gatherings were cancelled due to the COVID-19 pandemic. Paris–Nice was created in 1933 by Parisian media mogul Albert Lejeune, in order to promote his Paris-based newspaper Le Petit Journal and Nice-based paper Le Petit Nice. It was held in March, at the end of winter, as one of the earliest French road bicycle racing on the calendar, immediately following the Six-day racing season on the track. In 2002, he sold Paris–Nice to Amaury Sport Organisation. In 2005 Paris–Nice was included in the inaugural UCI Pro Tour, but was at the center of a dispute between UCI and Amaury Sport Organisation just before the 2008 Paris–Nice. In 2012 Paris–Nice England's Bradley Wiggins won the race, as part of his build-up to the Tour de France. The 2020 Paris-Nice was the last international cycling event, as well as the last sporting event in France, before mass gatherings came to a halt due to the COVID-19 pandemic. Known as The Race to the Sun, Paris–Nice is often considered a mini-Tour de France, where riders need to be both competent time-trialists and capable of climbing mountains. In 2002, after the race was obtained by Amaury Sport Organisation, the leader's jersey was yellow and white, before changing it to yellow in 2008, reflecting the Tour de France leader's jersey. The King of the Mountains jersey has white with red polka dots, as in the Tour de France, since the race's takeover by Amaury Sport Organisation." .

ns1:46559 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "RusAir Flight 9605 (operating as RusLine Flight 243) was a passenger flight which crashed near Petrozavodsk in the Republic of Karelia, Russia, on 20 June 2011 while attempting to land in thick fog. The RusAir Tu-134 was on a service for RusLine from Domodedovo International Airport in Moscow to Petrozavodsk Airport. There were 43 passengers and nine crew members on board, a total of 52, of which 47 were killed and the remaining 5 injured. On 23 June, at a conference of senior Russian government officials, it was announced that as a result of the incident the government planned to remove all Tu-134s from commercial service, as well as ban the operation of aircraft carrying more than nine people or weighing more than 5,700 kilograms (12,600 lb) lacking a ground proximity warning system." .

ns1:46592 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Przemyśl - a struggle for the control over the city of Przemyśl in former Austro-Hungarian Galicia and local river crossings on the San river, between Ukrainian and Polish militias and regular troops, from 2 to 12 November 1918, during the Polish-Ukrainian War. In 1918 the city of Przemyśl (Ukrainian: Peremyshl) formed a part of the Austrian province Kingdom of Galicia and Lodomeria and was its third biggest city after Krakow and Lviv (Polish: Lwów, German: Lemberg). The mixed ethnic populations resulted in large parts of Galicia being perceived as Polish or Ukrainian simultaneously, which was the main reason for the Polish–Ukrainian War. On the night of October 31/1918, the Ukrainian National Rada in Lviv issued a proclamation in the name of the West Ukrainian People's Republic. The ongoing battle in Lviv and the attempt to seize Przemyśl further contributed to the antagonisms between Ukrainian and Polish residents in the city." .

ns1:46638 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 OFC Futsal Championship was the 11th edition of the Oceanian Futsal Championship, the annual international futsal championship organised by the Oceania Football Confederation (Oceania Football Confederation) for the men's national teams of Oceania. The tournament was held in Suva, Fiji between 08 February 2016–13 February 2016. The winner of the tournament qualified for the 2016 FIFA Futsal World Cup in Colombia as the Oceania Football Confederation representative." .

ns1:46655 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1992 AFC Asian Cup was the 10th edition of the men's AFC Asian Cup, a quadrennial international football tournament organised by the Asian Football Confederation (AFC). The finals were held in Hiroshima Prefecture, Japan national football team between 29 October 1992 and 08 November 1992. The other 6 qualifying teams were: All times are Japan national football team Standard Time (UTC+9) All times are Japan national football team Standard Time (UTC+9) With three goals, Fahad Al-Bishi is the top scorer in the tournament." .

ns1:46789 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Ameixial, was fought on 08 June 1663, near the village of Santa Vitória do Ameixial, some 10 kilometres (6.2 mi) north-west of Estremoz, between Spanish and Portugal as part of the Portuguese Restoration War. In Spain, the battle is better known as the Battle of Estremoz." .

ns1:46851 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1947 Tour de France was the 34th edition of the Tour de France, taking place from 25 June 1947 to 20 July 1947. It was the first Tour since 1939 Tour de France, having been cancelled during World War II, although some Tour de France-like races had been held during World War II. After the 1939 Tour de France, the Second World War had made it impossible to organise a big cycling event in France, although some attempts had been taken. The event organised by l'Équipe, \"La Course du Tour de France\", was more successful, and l'Équipe was given the right to organise the 1947 Tour de France. Of the 100 cyclists, 53 finished the race. The teams entering the race were: The 1947 Tour de France started on 25 June 1947, and had five rest days, in Besançon, Briançon, Nice, Luchon and Vannes. When they reached Paris, they had won 13 minutes on them, enough to make Robic the winner of the Tour de France." .

ns1:46920 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "On 25 December 1084, at the Battle of Piedra Pisada, the Taifa of Zaragoza fought and probably defeated the Kingdom of Aragon on the road south from Naval, Huesca to El Grado. The battle was a minor engagement of the ongoing Reconquista of Aragon, the process by which the riverine valleys of the southern slopes of the Pyrenees were gradually conquered and returned, after centuries of Muslim rule, to the control of Christian princes. The ruler of Aragon, who personally led his men in battle at Piedra Pisada, Sancho Ramírez, also ruled Kingdom of Navarre and was a major figure in the contemporary Reconquista. In a document nearly contemporary with the battle, Peter I of Aragon and Navarre, Sancho's son and successor, enumerated the parishes whose tithes belonged to the church of Santa María of Alquézar in geographic order from east to west, placing Petra Pisata between Naval, Huesca and Salinas de Hoz. Since neither surviving record of the battle, both Christian, records the outcome and Naval, Huesca is known to have lapsed out of Christian control soon after its conquest, it is probable that the battle was a loss for Sancho." .

ns1:46921 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2001 Basque regional election was held on Sunday, 13 May 2001, to elect the 7th Parliament of the Basque Autonomous Community. The electoral coalition Basque Nationalist Party–Basque Solidarity (PNV–EA) won 33 seats, the People's Party (PP) came second with 19 seats, the Socialist Party of the Basque Country – Basque Country Left (PSE–EE) came in third with 13 seats. The Euskal Herritarrok (EH) coalition obtained 7 seats." .

ns1:46951 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Three Principles of the People, also translated as Three People's Principles, San-min Doctrine, or Tridemism, is a political philosophy developed by Sun Yat-sen as part of a philosophy to make China a free, prosperous, and powerful state." .

ns1:46961 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Miss Philippines Earth 2006 was the 6th edition of the Miss Earth Philippines pageant. It was held on 2006 at the University of the Philippines in Quezon City, Philippines. Genebelle Raagas, Miss Philippines Earth 2005 crowned her successor Cathy Untalan as Miss Philippines Earth 2006 at the conclusion of the event. Untalan won against 23 other candidates and became the representative of Philippines in the international Miss Earth 2006 beauty pageant. The twenty-four candidates that competed for the Miss Philippines 2006 title were formally presented at the poolside of Hotel Intercontinental in Makati City on April 21, 2007. The following is the list of the official contestants of Miss Philippines Earth 2005 representing various regions in the Philippines:" .

ns1:47083 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The First Four Years is an autobiographical novel by Laura Ingalls Wilder, published in 1971 and commonly considered the last of nine books in the Little House on the Prairie. It is not clear whether Wilder intended this first draft to be a ninth book in the Little House on the Prairie, or possibly a standalone novel for adults. Because she never reworked the manuscript - and Lane never edited it as she had her mother's previously published works, the novel is less polished in style than the books of the Little House on the Prairie, but it is still unmistakably Wilder's writing. Of the unfinished novel later marketed as volume 9, Kirkus Reviews wrote in part, \"For a moment it's all wrong, this manuscript left unrevised by Mrs. Wilder, and then Manly (never 'Almanzo') takes hold, joking and reasoning and promising ..." .

ns1:47293 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Second Italian War (1499–1501), sometimes known as Louis XII's Italian War and the Italian War of 1499–1504 (1502-1504) or the War over Naples, were the second and third of the Italian Wars; it was fought primarily by Louis XII of France and Ferdinand II of Aragon, with the participation of several Italian powers. And in 1499, Louis XII invaded Lombardy and seized Milan, to which he had a claim in right of his paternal grandmother Valentina Visconti, Duchess of Orléans. In response to threats from the Republic of Venice and the Kingdom of Naples, Ludovico Sforza the duke of Milan had invited France into Italy to protect Milan from her enemies. Immediately, King Louis concluded an alliance with the Republic of Venice and obtained some Swiss mercenaries and invaded the Duchy of Milan under the condition that the Lombardian territories be split between Venice and France. Following the final overthrow of Sforza, the Duchy of Milan would serve, for the next twelve years, as a French stronghold and as a springboard for further French military adventures in Italy. Louis XII was forced to abandon Naples and, on 1504, left Naples to withdraw to Lombardy." .

ns1:47408 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Beşiktaş JK 10–0 Adana Demirspor is the final score of the Süper Lig game played between Beşiktaş J.K. and Adana Demirspor at 1989–90 1.Lig, on 15 October 1989. Beşiktaş started 1989–90 1.Lig with 2 losses and 1 draw at first 5 games, before playin against Adana Demirspor. Game took place on 15 October 1989, Sunday at Ali Sami Yen Stadium." .

ns1:47445 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Lazic War, also known as the Colchidian War or in Georgia historiography as the Great War of Egrisi (Georgia: ეგრისის დიდი ომი, Egrisis Didi Omi), was fought between the Byzantine Empire and the Sasanian Empire for control of the ancient Georgia region of Lazica. The Lazic War lasted for twenty years, from 541 to 562, with varying success and ended in a victory for the Persians, who obtained an annual tribute in exchange for ending the war. The Persian Sasanian Empire recognized Lazica (Egrisi) as part of the Roman/Byzantine sphere of influence by the \"Eternal Peace\" Treaty of 532. Then, in 557, a truce ended the hostilities between the Byzantines and Persians, and by the \"Fifty Years Peace\" of Dara of 562, Khosrow I recognized Lazica as a Byzantine vassal state for an annual payment of gold." .

ns1:47447 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Long Road Out of Eden Tour was a worldwide concert tour by the Eagles, whose beginning coincided with the release of their 2007 album Long Road Out of Eden. The tour began in London, at The O2 arena on 20 March 2008, and ended on 19 November 2011 with a show at the MGM Grand Garden Arena in Las Vegas." .

ns1:47487 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Tunnel of Love Express Tour was a concert tour by Bruce Springsteen and featuring The E Street Band along with The Horns of Love that took place in 1988. Coming on the heels of the massively successful Born in the U.S.A. Tour, the Tunnel of Love Express Tour was designed to disorient Springsteen's audiences. The United States leg of the tour took place in arenas, starting on 25 February 1988 at the Worcester Centrum and continuing for 43 shows. The European leg commenced on June 11 at the Stadio Comunale in Turin, Italy, and continued for 23 shows in stadiums, concluding the tour on 04 August 1988 at Barcelona, Spain's Camp Nou. From the first release of Tunnel of Love, there had listeners who wondered if some of the gloomy portrayals of interpersonal relationships on the album indicated that Springsteen's 1985 marriage to actress and model Julianne Phillips was in trouble. Los Angeles Times music writer Robert Hilburn, interviewing Springsteen at the Worcester start of the tour, wrote that \"Springsteen seemed extremely comfortable sitting on a sofa with his wife in the dressing room area – a picture that seemed to contradict the speculation that Tunnel of Love's songs of troubled romance reflected signs of trouble in his own marriage.\" In addition to everything else, what was different about the Tunnel of Love Express Tour was Springsteen's first go at explicit carnality, from the opening \"Tunnel of Love\", where he and Scialfa sang cheek to cheek with lips nearly touching at the same microphone, to other numbers such as \"Part Man, Part Monkey\". This was all a big change for Scialfa, who had stayed in the background during the 1984–1985 Born in the U.S.A. Tour, her first. The three kickoff shows in Worcester sold out in two hours (the site having been chosen for the tour opening, the venue manager thought, because the New England area had given a very favorable response to the Born in the U.S.A. Tour.) The 04 August 1988, show in Barcelona that closed out the Tunnel of Love Express Tour would be the last full-length Springsteen and E Street Band show for eleven years. Following the Human Rights Now! later that year, which featured abbreviated sets and few performances of Tunnel of Love songs, Springsteen broke up the E Street Band. Portions of several songs were aired as part of their special Bruce Springsteen – Inside the Tunnel of Love on April 30." .

ns1:47582 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1856 United States presidential election in Pennsylvania took place on 04 November 1856, as part of the 1856 United States presidential election. Pennsylvania voted for the Democratic candidate, James Buchanan, over the Republican candidate, John C. Frémont, and the Know Nothing candidate, Millard Fillmore." .

ns1:47630 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The King–Byng Affair was a Canada constitutional crisis that occurred in 1926, when the Governor General of Canada, Julian Byng, 1st Viscount Byng of Vimy, refused a request by his Prime Minister of Canada, William Lyon Mackenzie King, to dissolve parliament and call a general election. The Conservatives were not given this chance by 26 June 1926 when the Prime Minister of Canada asked the governor general to dissolve parliament anyway. Following the 1926 Canadian federal election, King again assumed the office of Prime Minister of Canada. In September 1925, William Lyon Mackenzie King, the Prime Minister of Canada, advised the Governor General, Julian Byng, 1st Viscount Byng of Vimy, to dissolve parliament and drop the writ for a general election, to which Lord Byng agreed. The King–Byng Affair was the most controversial use of a governor general's reserve powers until the Australian constitutional crisis of 1975, in which the Governor-General of Australia, Sir John Kerr, dismissed Prime Minister Gough Whitlam." .

ns1:47666 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The International Association for Cryptologic Research (Theory of Cryptography Conference) is a non-profit scientific organization that furthers research in cryptography and related fields. The Theory of Cryptography Conference organizes and sponsors three annual flagship conferences, four area conferences in specific sub-areas of cryptography, and one symposium: Several other conferences and workshops are held in cooperation with the Theory of Cryptography Conference. The Theory of Cryptography Conference, often abbreviated TCC, is an annual conference for theoretical cryptography research. TCC became an Theory of Cryptography Conference-sponsored workshop in 2006." .

ns1:47693 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Avianca Flight 410 was a flight that crashed at 13:17 on 17 March 1988, near Cúcuta, Colombia, that occurred shortly after takeoff when it flew into a mountain. It was the deadliest aviation accident to occur in Colombia at the time. The aircraft was a Boeing 727-21 operated by Avianca, the national airline of Colombia. Flight 410 was a regular scheduled domestic passenger flight from Cúcuta-Camilo Daza International Airport (CUC) to Cartagena de Indias-Rafael Núñez International Airport (CTG). The crash of Flight 410 was the deadliest aviation accident to occur in Colombia until December 20, 1995 when American Airlines Flight 965 crashed into a mountain near Buga, Valle del Cauca, killing 159 people." .

ns1:47724 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Spantax Flight 275, registration number EC-BZR, was a Convair 990 Coronado charter flight operated by Spantax from Tenerife to Munich with 148 passengers and 7 crew. On 03 December 1972, the plane crashed while taking off from Tenerife North Airport in Tenerife, killing everyone aboard." .

ns1:47843 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 U.S. Open Cup Final was played on 13 September 2016, at Toyota Stadium in Frisco, Texas. This edition of the final was contested between FC Dallas and New England Revolution, both of Major League Soccer (MLS). New England Revolution have previously appeared in two US Open Cup finals, losing the 2001 Final and winning the 2007 Final against FC Dallas." .

ns1:48073 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Kadena Rape Murder Case (Japan: 嘉手納幼女強姦殺人事件), also known as the Yumiko-chan incident, refers to the rape and murder of six-year-old Japan girl Yumiko Nagayama (sometimes reported as Yumiko Arakaki) by American soldier Sergeant Isaac J. Hurt in Kadena on 3 01 September 1955. Yumiko's body was found near Kadena Air Base during the U.S. occupation of Okinawa, and an investigation led to the conviction of 31-year-old Sergeant Hurt on charges of murder, rape, and kidnapping. On 4 01 September 1955, the mutilated body of a young girl was discovered in a landfill belonging to the Kadena Air Base, an installation of the Far East Command in Kadena, at the time governed by the United States Civil Administration of the Ryukyu Islands. An indictment was submitted against Sergeant Isaac J. Hurt (sometimes incorrectly reported as Isaac J. Hart) of B Battalion, 32nd Artillery Division, on charges of murder, rape and kidnapping. News of Yumiko's violent rape and murder by a U.S. serviceman provoked outrage among Okinawans, who were further angered by the fact that due to extraterritoriality laws, Yumiko's alleged rapist-murderer would not undergo an Okinawan trial, but rather a U.S. military court-martial. Sergeant Isaac J. Hurt was brought up on charges of rape and murder by a U.S. court-martial in Okinawa." .

ns1:48120 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Fairbank Train Robbery occurred on the night of 15 February 1900, when some bandits attempted to hold up a Wells Fargo express car at the town of Fairbank, Arizona Territory. According to James H. McClintock, train robbery was popular in Arizona Territory at the time, which was exemplified by the passing of a statute in 1889 that made it punishable by death." .

ns1:48210 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Okehazama (桶狭間の戦い, Okehazama-no-tatakai) took place in 22 June 1560. In this battle, Oda Nobunaga defeated Imagawa Yoshimoto and established himself as one of the front-running warlords in the Sengoku period. The army followed the route of the Tōkaidō highway, and crossed from Mikawa into Owari Province, which had recently been united by local warlord Oda Nobunaga. In 1937, Japan's Ministry of Education designated the battlefield, Okehazama Kosenjō, a National Historic Site of Japan." .

ns1:48320 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1986 Tour of the Basque Country was the 26th edition of the Itzulia Basque Country cycle race and was held from 07 April 1986 to 11 April 1986. The race started in Antzuola and finished in Andoain." .

ns1:48325 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1994 Virginia Slims of Florida was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts at the Delray Beach Tennis Center in Delray Beach, Florida in the United States of America that was part of Tier II of the 1994 WTA Tour. The tournament was held from 28 February 1994 through 06 March 1994." .

ns1:48333 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1996 Russian presidential election took place in Russia on 16 June 1996, with a second round on 3 July. This election was the first to take place in post-Soviet Russia. In 1991 Boris Yeltsin was elected to a 5-year term as President of Russia, which was still a part of the Soviet Union at the time. The next election was scheduled be held sometime in 1996. This meant that the scheduled election would now be the first ever presidential election to be held in a fully sovereign Russia. Thus, the presidential election remained scheduled to be held in the year 1996. In the Constitution, future presidential terms were stipulated to last for four years, meaning that the 1996 election would elect a president to serve a 4-year term. When incumbent president Boris Yeltsin launched his reelection campaign in early 1996, he was widely predicted to lose. When Zyuganov showed up at the World Economic Forum in Davos, Switzerland, in 1996, many Western leaders and the international media were eager to see him, and treated him with regards to believing that he would likely be the next president of Russia. After his surprisingly strong third-place finish in the 1991 presidential election and the surprisingly strong first-place performance of the LDPR in the 1993 legislative election, Zhirinivsky had once been seen as a rising force in Russian politics, and a future contender for the presidency. In their report, the International Republican Institute stated that their election observers, \"witnessed no deliberate attempts to commit electoral fraud and, indeed, in the tracking of protocols through the various levels of Russia's electoral system, observed transparency in the process.\" In 1991, at the time of the previous presidential election, Russia had only two major television channels. In the 1996 election, however, none of Russia's major television networks were critical of Yeltsin. In early 1996, Gusinsky and his political rival Boris Berezovsky (chairman of the Board of ORT) decided that they would put aside their differences in order to work together to support the reelection Boris Yeltsin. Allegations have been made that, in the first round of the election, several regions of Russia committed electoral fraud in favor of Zyuganov. In January 1997, noting the support Yeltsin had received in 1996 from the Clinton administration, former candidate Alexander Lebed visited the United States to rally support from American businesses for a potential run in the 2000 Russian presidential election." .

ns1:48435 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1979 Warsaw gas explosion took place on 15 February 1979, at 12:37 p.m. As a result, 49 people died and 135 were injured. At 12:37 p.m., the bank's branch at the Rotunda, located in the strict city centre at the intersection of Marszałkowska Street and Aleje Jerozolimskie, was full of people." .

ns1:48465 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1975 German Grand Prix was a Formula One motor race held at Nürburgring on 03 August 1975. It was race 11 of 14 in both the 1975 World Championship of Drivers and the 1975 Formula One season. It was the 37th German Grand Prix and the 34th to be held at the Nürburgring. Behind Lauda on pole was Carlos Pace in a Brabham, Jody Scheckter and Patrick Depailler in the Tyrrell cars, Clay Regazzoni in the second Ferrari, Jochen Mass in a McLaren, Hans-Joachim Stuck in a March, Emerson Fittipaldi in the other McLaren, James Hunt in his Hesketh and Carlos Alberto \"Lole\" Reutemann in the second Brabham. Niki Lauda 9 (1–9), Carlos Alberto \"Lole\" Reutemann 5 (10–14)." .

ns1:48466 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011–12 Moldovan National Division (Moldovan: Divizia Națională) was the 21st season of top-tier association football in Moldova. The competition began on 23 July 2011 and ended on 23 May 2012. The league was competed by 12 teams and won by FC Sheriff Tiraspol. Placed last in the 2010–11 Moldovan National Division, Găgăuzia Comrat and Dinamo Bender had not completed their licensing to compete in the Moldovan National Division and were relegated." .

ns1:48467 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Aegon Surbiton Trophy was a professional tennis tournament played on outdoor grass courts. It took place in Surbiton, United Kingdom, on 06 June 2016–12 June 2016." .

ns1:48479 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Coronado II was the second of eleven in the Operation Coronado series conducted by the U.S. Mobile Riverine Force (MRF) in conjunction with various units of the Army of the Republic of Vietnam (ARVN) in late 31 July 1967 in an attempt to shut down Viet Cong (VC) strongholds in the Mekong Delta Three battalions of American troops, along with two ARVN battalions, backed by helicopters and watercraft swept the area and waterways surrounding Mỹ Tho in search of VC forces. The Mobile Riverine Force ended Operation Coronado I in Long An Province because of intelligence indicating communist buildup west of Mỹ Tho in Định Tường Province." .

ns1:48530 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Alitalia Flight 404 was an international passenger flight scheduled to fly from Linate Airport in Milan, Italy, to Zürich Airport in Zürich, Switzerland, which crashed on 14 November 1990. The Douglas DC-9-32, operated by Alitalia, crashed into the woodlands of Weiach as it approached Zurich Airport, killing all 46 people on board." .

ns1:48555 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Snooker season 2010/2011 was a series of snooker tournaments played between 20 May 2010 and 2 May 2011. The Grand Prix was renamed to 2010 World Open, and the format of the tournament was change with 32 amateurs joining the World Snooker Tour professionals. The Jiangsu Classic was renamed to the 2010 Wuxi Classic, and other events were introduced to the calendar: the new cue sport Power Snooker, the 2010 World Seniors Championship, and 2011 Snooker Shoot-Out." .

ns1:48580 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "badminton at the 2015 Island Games, for the 2015 Island Games, was held at the New Gilson Hall. With a practice day on 27 June, the events took place from 28 June 2015 to 03 July 2015. Final medal tally, based on the 2015 IG badminton at the 2015 Island Games Medal Table page: *   Host nation (Jersey)" .

ns1:48728 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Hama Massacre (Arabic: مجزرة حماة), or Hama Uprising, occurred in 02 February 1982, when the Syrian Arab Army and the Defense Companies, under the orders of the country's president Hafez al-Assad, besieged the town of Hama for 27 days in order to quell an Islamic uprising in Syria by the Muslim Brotherhood against al-Assad's government. From 1976 to 1982, Sunni Islamists fought the Ba'ath Party-controlled government of Syria in what has been called a \"long campaign of terror\". The events of the Hama massacre began at 2 am on 1982. Reinforcements were rushed to besiege Abu Bakr who then \"gave the order for a general Islamic uprising in Syria\" in Hama. After the Hama Islamic uprising in Syria, the Islamist insurrection was broken, and the Brotherhood has since operated in exile while other factions surrendered or slipped into hiding. Government attitudes in Syria hardened considerably during the Islamic uprising in Syria, and Assad would rely more on repression than on political tactics for the remainder of his rule, although an economic liberalization began in the 1990s." .

ns1:48765 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Hundred Days Offensive (08 August 1918 to 11 November 1918) was a series of massive Allied offensives which ended the First World War. Beginning with the Battle of Amiens (08 August 1918–12 August) on the Western Front of World War I, the Allies pushed the Central Powers back, undoing their gains from the Spring Offensive. The American Expeditionary Force (AEF) under General John J. Pershing had arrived in France in large numbers and had invigorated the Allied armies with its extensive resources Pershing was keen to use his army as an independent force. Foch agreed to a proposal by Field Marshal Douglas Haig, 1st Earl Haig, commander-in-chief of the BEF, to strike on the River Somme, east of Amiens and south-west of the site of the 1916 Battle of the Somme, to force the Germans away from the vital Amiens–Paris railway The Somme was chosen because it remained the boundary between the BEF and the French armies, along the Amiens–Roye road, allowing the two armies to cooperate. The Battle of Amiens (with the French attack on the southern flank called the Battle of Montdidier) opened on 08 August 1918, with an attack by more than 10 Allied divisions—Australian, Canadian, British and French forces—with more than 500 tanks Through careful preparation, the Allies achieved surprise,95 The attack, led by the British Fourth Army, broke through the German lines, and tanks attacked German rear positions, sowing panic and confusion. The Allies pressed the Germans back toward the lateral railway line from Metz to Bruges, which had supplied the front in Northern France and Belgium for much of the war." .

ns1:48786 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Flower Crew (Korean: 꽃놀이패) is a South Korea travel-reality show that was formerly broadcast via SBS on Mondays at 23:10 (KST) from 05 September 2016 to 2016. Starting 2016, it aired every Sunday at 16:50 (KST), before Running Man, forming part of SBS's Good Sunday lineup and ended with its last episode on 19 March 2017. The regular show starred Seo Jang-hoon, Jo Se-ho, Ahn Jung-hwan, Yoo Byung-jae, Winner's Kang Seung-yoon and Lee Sung-jae. After the live pilot show, viewers criticized members Jo Se-ho and Seo Jang-hoon for their rude behavior towards Jungkook and Kim Min-seok, such as rejecting the hamburgers Jungkook bought for the entire cast and saying that Jungkook prepared leftovers for them and criticizing Kim Min-seok for \"stuffing his face\" when he accepted the food. Jo Se-ho and Seo Jang-hoon later apologized and said the situation was a misunderstanding. The original members of Flower Crew were Seo Jang-hoon, Ahn Jung-hwan, Jo Se-ho, Yoo Byung-jae, Kim Min-seok." .

ns1:48812 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The UEFA Euro 1996 qualifying play-off was a UEFA Euro 1996 qualifying match to decide the final participant for UEFA Euro 1996. It was played on 13 December 1995 at Anfield in Liverpool, England, a neutral venue, between the Republic of Ireland national association football team and the Netherlands national association football team. The Netherlands national association football team won 2–0, therefore qualifying for the 1996 European Championship. After the 1994 FIFA World Cup the Netherlands national association football team started he qualifying campaign for Euro 1996 under coach Dick Advocaat. The Netherlands national association football team finished seventh in the table, while the Republic of Ireland national association football team finished eighth and last, therefore qualifying both countries to the play-off. Kluivert then wrapped the match up with his second goal, lifting the ball over the advancing Alan Kelly with two minutes to go, giving the Netherlands national association football team a 2–0 win and sending them through to Euro 1996. The Netherlands national association football team advanced on to Euro 1996, where they finished second in Group A behind hosts England, thus qualifying them for the knockout stage." .

ns1:48920 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 European Rugby Champions Cup Final was the final match in the 2016–17 European Rugby Champions Cup, and the twenty-second European club rugby final in general. It was contested by defending champions Saracens F.C. of England and French side ASM Clermont Auvergne at Murrayfield Stadium, in Edinburgh, Scotland, on Saturday 2017. Reigning champions Saracens F.C. entered the final aiming to defend their title, having won the Champions Cup for the first time when they defeated Racing 92 by 21–9 in the 2016 final. For the second year running, Welsh referee Nigel Owens officiated the Champions Cup final – in doing so, he also became the first official to referee 100 European club rugby matches. After both teams enjoyed attacking spells in the opening 10 minutes without putting points on the board, Saracens F.C. opened the scoring when the ball was spread towards the right wing to Alex Goode, who placed a grubber kick behind the ASM Clermont Auvergne defenders which was collected by Chris Ashton. Saracens F.C. Number 8 Billy Vunipola was named the official Man of the Match, while fly-half Owen Farrell was presented with the 2017 ERCC Player of the Year Award." .

ns1:49058 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011–12 2. Fußball-Bundesliga was the 38th season of the 2. Bundesliga, Germany's second tier of its association football league system. The season commenced on 15 July 2011, three weeks earlier than the 2011–12 Bundesliga season, and ended with the last games on 06 May 2012. The traditional winter break was to be held between the weekends around 18 and 4 February 2012. The two teams were replaced by Eintracht Frankfurt and FC St. Pauli, who were directly relegated from the 2010–11 Bundesliga season. On the other end of the table, Rot-Weiß Oberhausen and Arminia Bielefeld were directly relegated to the 2011–12 3. Fußball-Liga, after finishing the 2010–11 2. Fußball-Bundesliga in the bottom two spots of the table. Braunschweig returned to the 2. Bundesliga after a total of four seasons at the third tier of the German association football pyramid, while Rostock immediately bounced back from their relegation twelve months earlier. A further two places were given to VfL Bochum as losers of the Bundesliga relegation playoff and Dynamo Dresden as winners of the 2. Bundesliga relegation playoff. Dresden returned to the 2. Bundesliga after a five-year absence after beating VfL Osnabrück 4–2 on aggregate; Osnabrück were thus immediately relegated back to the 3. Dates and times of these matches were determined by the Deutsche Fußball-Liga as following: Tie ended 3–3 on aggregate; Jahn Regensburg promoted to 2012–13 2. Fußball-Bundesliga, Karlsruher SC relegated to 2012–13 3." .

ns1:49127 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Mata Hari is a musical with a book by Jerome Coopersmith, lyrics by Martin Charnin and music by Edward Thomas. The exotic dancer Mata Hari was accused of spying for the Germans during World War I and was executed by a French firing squad, but her guilt is still being debated. The musical is centered on her fictional affair with a French intelligence officer who plays a major role in her arrest and execution and later regrets it. In 1917, Captain Henry LaFarge, a French military intelligence officer, believes that the exotic dancer, Mata Hari, is the most dangerous German spy in France; he is obsessed with bringing her to justice." .

ns1:49181 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "National AIDS Trust v NHS Service Commissioning Board, EWHC 2005 (Admin), was a court case before the High Court of Justice seeking judicial review regarding National Health Service funding for Pre-exposure prophylaxis. The issue was taken by the National AIDS Trust to a judicial review by the High Court. Following the defeat at trial, NHS Commissioning Board announced a plan to appeal the judgment in the Court of Appeal of England and Wales, while also making plans to prepare to implement the wider use of PrEP. NHS Commissioning Board lost the appeal, with Lady Justice Eleanor King and Lord Justices Andrew Longmore and Nicholas Underhill finding in favour of the National AIDS Trust on 10 November 2016, with Public Health England announcing a 3-year large-scale trial seeking to enroll over 10,000 people a few weeks later." .

ns1:49221 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "In the Battle of Aspern-Essling (21 May 1809–22 May 1809), Napoleon attempted a forced crossing of the Danube near Vienna, but the French and their allies were driven back by the Austrians under Archduke Charles. The battle was the first time Napoleon had been personally defeated in over a decade, but Aspern would be a sign of numerous military defeats and blunders that would soon hinder Napoleon. At the time of the battle Napoleon was in possession of Vienna, the bridges over the Danube had been broken, and the Archduke's army was near the Bisamberg, a hill near Korneuburg, on the left bank of the river. The battle began at Aspern; Hiller carried the village at the first rush, but Masséna recaptured it, and held his ground with remarkable tenacity. The Löwe von Aspern (Lion of Aspern), a large stone sculpture in front of St. Martin's Church, is a monument commemorating the battle. The army surgeon Dominique-Jean Larrey also described the battle in his memoirs and mentions how he fed the wounded at Lobau with a bouillon of horse meat seasoned with gunpowder." .

ns1:49262 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Fort Albany (about 26 June 1709) was an attack by French colonial volunteers and their native allies against the Canadian Hudson's Bay Company outpost of Fort Albany First Nation in the southern reaches of Hudson Bay. Beginning with an expedition in 1686, and running through the Nine Years' War (1689–97, known in the English colonies as King William's War), raiders from New France repeatedly attacked the company's outposts, capturing and holding the facilities, and sometimes making off with furs awaiting transport to Europe. By the end of the war, only one of the company's outposts, Fort Albany First Nation (so named because it was located near the mouth of the Albany River in what is now far northern Ontario), remained in the company's hands. Manthet recruited between 60 and 70 Frenchmen and 30 Caughnawaga Mohawk, and set out, went down the Moose River (Ontario), skirted the James Bay coast in canoes and arrived near Fort Albany First Nation in late 26 June 1709." .

ns1:49364 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Chrono Gatineau was held on 05 June 2015, in Gatineau, Canada. A 11.5-kilometre (7.1 mi) women's cycling individual time trial, it was won by Carmen Small (Twenty16 p/b Sho-Air), who beat Karol-Ann Canuel (Velocio–SRAM) and Tayler Wiles (Velocio–SRAM)." .

ns1:49370 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1953 Pennsylvania Railroad train wreck was a railway accident in Washington Union Station in Washington, D.C. on 15 January 1953. The brakes on the cars of the Federal Express, a passenger and mail train operated by the Pennsylvania Railroad, malfunctioned and the train crashed into the station, jumped the passenger platform, and plunged through the floor of the passenger concourse into the basement of the train station. In the train yard at Washington Union Station, towerman John Feeney in K Tower set the switches to shunt the Federal Express onto Track 16." .

ns1:49524 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Mixtón War was fought from 1540 until 1542 between the Caxcan and other semi-nomadic Indigenous people of the area of north western Mexico against Spanish invaders, including their Aztec and Tlaxcalan allies. Although other indigenous groups also fought against the Spanish in the Mixtón War, the Caxcan were the \"heart and soul\" of the resistance. Guzmán and his lieutenants founded towns and Spanish settlements in the region, called Nueva Galicia, including Guadalajara in or near the homeland of the Caxcan. In Spring 1540, the Caxcan and their allies struck back, emboldened perhaps by the fact that Governor Francisco Vásquez de Coronado had taken more than 1,600 Spaniards and Amerindian allies from the region northward with him on his expedition to what would become the United States’ Southwest. The spark that set off the war was apparently the arrest of 18 rebellious Indian leaders and the hanging of nine of them in mid-1540. Spanish expansion after the Mixtón War would lead to the longer and even more bloody Chichimeca war (1550–1590)." .

ns1:49599 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 Slovak Cup Final was the final match of the 2007–08 Slovak Cup, the 39th season of the top cup competition in Slovak football. The match was played at the Štadión pod Dubňom in Žilina on 01 May 2008 between FC Artmedia Petržalka and FC Spartak Trnava." .

ns1:49617 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Hitler Diaries (German: Hitler-Tagebücher) were a series of sixty volumes of journals purportedly written by Adolf Hitler, but forged by Konrad Kujau between 1981 and 1983. The diaries were purchased in 1983 for 9.3 million Deutsche Marks (£2.33 million or $3.7 million) by the West German news magazine Stern, which sold serialisation rights to several news organisations. The West German journalist with Stern who \"discovered\" the diaries and was involved in their purchase was Gerd Heidemann, who had an obsession with the Nazis. On 20 April 1945—Adolf Hitler's 56th birthday—Soviet troops were on the verge of taking Berlin and the Western Allies had already taken several German cities. Stern (German for \"Star\"), a German weekly news magazine published in Hamburg, was formed by the journalist and businessman Henri Nannen in 1948 to offer scandal, gossip and human interest stories. Heidemann visited Maser in June 1981 and came to a deal that enabled the journalist and Stern, for a payment of 20,000 DMs, to retain \"the rights to all the discovered or purchased documents or notes in the hand of Adolf Hitler ... which have so far not yet been published\". After twelve diaries had been delivered to Gruner + Jahr, Heidemann informed his employers that the price had risen from 85,000 DMs to 100,000 DMs per diary; the reason given by Heidemann was that the East German general smuggling the diaries was now having to bribe more people. Stern's management were too bound up in a secretive approach to be open about their source, or to provide the experts with a complete diary, which would have led to a more thorough examination of wider material. On 1983 a press release from Stern announced the existence of the diaries and their forthcoming publication; a press conference was announced for 25 April. Irving explained that Stern had shown him a diary from April 1945 in which the writing sloped downwards from left to right, and the script of which got smaller along the line." .

ns1:49682 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The battle of Gródek Jagielloński or Battle of Horodok took place during the Russo-Polish War on 29 September 1655. Russian and Ukrainian Cossack forces under Vasily Borisovich Sheremetev and Bohdan Khmelnytsky engaged a Polish–Lithuanian army under Stanisław \"Rewera\" Potocki near Gródek Jagielloński, which at that time was part of Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth's Ruthenian Voivodeship (now Horodok, Lviv Oblast, Ukraine)." .

ns1:49723 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1959 German Grand Prix was a Formula One motor race held at the AVUS in West Berlin on 02 August 1959. It was race 6 of 9 in the 1959 World Championship of Drivers and race 5 of 8 in the 1959 Formula One season. AVUS had previously held the original German Grand Prix in 1926." .

ns1:49769 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Tour of the Basque Country was a road cycling road bicycle racing that took place between 02 April 2018 and 07 April 2018 in Spain. It was the 58th edition of the Itzulia Basque Country and the fourteenth event of the 2018 UCI World Tour. The race's other jerseys were won by Spaniards Carlos Verona (mountains for Mitchelton–Scott), and Enric Mas (young rider for Quick-Step Floors after a final-stage breakaway win), while the Movistar Team won the teams classification as Nairo Quintana also finished in the top-five overall for the team. As the Itzulia Basque Country was a 2018 UCI World Tour event, all eighteen UCI WorldTeams were invited automatically and were obliged to enter a team in the race." .

ns1:49796 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Scalextric is a brand of slot cars and slot car racing sets which first appeared in the late 1950s, manufactured by the English firm Minimodels. Scalextric is a track based slot car racing system first invented by Fred (B F) Francis. Scalextric was unveiled at the Harrogate Toy Fair in 1957 to immediate acclaim. Most Scalextric models are 1:32 scale, though between 1968 and 1970 Super 124 cars and track were manufactured at 1:24 scale. In Spain, Hornby Scalextric is marketed under the brand Superslot, as the Scalextric brand is owned there by Scale Competition Xtreme, whose products are sold internationally under the SCX brand. Scalextric has also recently licensed the Need for Speed brand from Electronic Arts and has produced a set based on the popular video game series. There are five generations of 1:32 scale Scalextric track: In 2009, BBC Top Gear presenter James May announced plans to recreate the full length Brooklands racing track using Scalextric track and cars. Micro Scalextric (or MicroScalextric, as appears on product boxes) was launched on 1 February 1994 (then known as Scalextric Micro MR-1) at the Olympia toy fair." .

ns1:49806 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 AFL finals series determined the winner of the 2012 AFL season. The series was scheduled to occur over four weekends in 07 September 2012, culminating with the 2012 AFL Grand Final at the Melbourne Cricket Ground on 29 September 2012." .

ns1:49875 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1986 Daytona 500, the 28th running of the Daytona 500, was held 16 February 1986 at Daytona International Speedway, in Daytona Beach. 1980 winner Buddy Baker, Neil Bonnett (already several laps down after earlier problems), 1985 Daytona 500 Bill Elliott, Tommy Ellis, Harry Gant, Kyle Petty, Joe Ruttman, Lake Speed, & four-time 500 winner Cale Yarborough were involved in the crash, but Elliott, Ellis, Petty, and Speed were able to continue the race." .

ns1:50018 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Ganesh Jayanti (literally \"Ganesha's birthday\", also known as Magha shukla chaturthi, Tilkund chaturthi, and Varad chaturthi, is a Hindu festival. The distinction between the Ganesh Jayanti and the more popular, almost pan-Indian Ganesh Chaturthi Hindu festival is that the latter Hindu festival is observed in the month of August/September (Bhadrapada Hindu month)." .

ns1:50019 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Thorne–Hawking–Preskill bet was a public bet on the outcome of the black hole information paradox made in 1997 by physics theorists Kip S. Thorne and Stephen Hawking on the one side, and John Preskill on the other, according to the document they signed 6 February 1997, as shown in Hawking's The Universe in a Nutshell." .

ns1:50093 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "glTF (derivative short form of Graphics Language Transmission Format or GL Transmission Format) is a standard file format for three-dimensional scenes and models. On 19 October 2015, the glTF 1.0 specification was released. The second version, glTF 2.0, was released in June 2017, and is a complete overhaul of the file format from version 1.0, with most tools adopting the 2.0 version. Physically Based Rendering (PBR) was added, replacing WebGL shaders used in glTF 1.0. On March 3, 2017, Microsoft announced that they will be using glTF 2.0 as the 3D asset format across their product line, including Paint 3D, 3D Viewer, Remix 3D, Babylon.js, and Microsoft Office. Sketchfab also announced support for glTF 2.0. GLB was introduced as an extension to glTF 1.0 and incorporated directly into glTF 2.0." .

ns1:50106 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The First Synod of Tyre or the Council of Tyre (335 AD) was a synod called together by Emperor Constantine I for the primary purpose of evaluating charges brought against Athanasios of Alexandria, the Patriarch of Alexandria. In 334 Athanasios of Alexandria was summoned before a synod in Caesarea, which he did not attend. He directed that, as a secondary matter, they should on their way first assemble at Tyre, to examine charges that had been brought against Athanasios of Alexandria." .

ns1:50138 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "OpenSym is a shorthand for International Symposium on Open Collaboration, formerly International Symposium on Wikis and Open Collaboration, also formerly WikiSym or the Wiki Symposium, a conference dedicated to wiki research and practice. In 2014, the name of the conference was changed from WikiSym to OpenSym to reflect a broadening of scope from wiki and Wikipedia research and practice to open collaboration research, including wikis and Wikipedia research, but also free/libre/open source, open data, etc. WikiSym 2005 was co-located with ACM OOPSLA 2005, held in San Diego, California, US, 2005–2005. WikiSym 2012 was held in Linz, Austria on 27–29 August5 Aug 201327 Aug 2013ng a definition of \"open collaboration\", WikiSym +5 AugO7 Augm 2013 was held in Hong Kong on 5–7 Aug 2013." .

ns1:50195 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 Tour de Langkawi was the 15th edition of the Tour de Langkawi, a cycling stage race that took place in Malaysia. The race began on 01 March 2010 in Kota Bharu and ended on 07 March 2010 in Merdeka Square, Kuala Lumpur. José Rujano of Venezuela became the overall winner of the race, followed by Gong Hyo-suk of South Korea second and Hossein Askari of Iran third." .

ns1:50200 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 World Matchplay was the 24th annual staging of the World Matchplay, organised by the Professional Darts Corporation. The tournament took place at the Winter Gardens, Blackpool, from 22 July 2017–30 July 2017." .

ns1:50250 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "National Airlines Flight 83 was a United States domestic flight from Newark Liberty International Airport, serving New York City, to Philadelphia. On 14 January 1951, the Douglas DC-4 of National Airlines crashed on landing at Philadelphia International Airport." .

ns1:50284 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Independence Day (colloquially the Fourth of July or July 4) is a federal holiday in the United States commemorating the United States Declaration of Independence of the United States of America, on July 4, 1776. Independence Day is commonly associated with fireworks, parades, barbecues, carnivals, fairs, picnics, concerts, baseball games, family reunions, political speeches, and ceremonies, in addition to various other public and private events celebrating the history, government, and traditions of the United States of America. Independence Day is the national day of the United States of America. From the outset, Americans celebrated independence on July 4, the date shown on the much-publicized United States Declaration of Independence, rather than on July 2, the date the resolution of independence was approved in a closed session of Congress. Historians have long disputed whether members of Congress signed the United States Declaration of Independence on July 4, even though Thomas Jefferson, John Adams, and Benjamin Franklin all later wrote that they had signed it on that day. By a remarkable coincidence, Thomas Jefferson and John Adams, the only two signatories of the United States Declaration of Independence later to serve as presidents of the United States of America, both died on the same day: July 4, 1826, which was the 50th anniversary of the Declaration, Jefferson even mentioning the fact. Although not a signatory of the United States Declaration of Independence, James Monroe, another Founding Father who was elected as president, also died on July 4, 1831. Calvin Coolidge, the 30th president, was born on July 4, 1872; so far he is the only U.S. president to have been born on Independence Day. § 6103, Independence Day is a federal holidays in the United States, so all non-essential federal institutions (such as the postal service and federal courts) are closed on that day. A salute of one gun for each state in the United States of America, called a \"salute to the union,\" is fired on Independence Day at noon by any capable military base. The Philippines celebrates July 4 as its Republic Day to commemorate that day in 1946 when it ceased to be a U.S. territory and the United States of America officially recognized Philippine Independence. July 4 was intentionally chosen by the United States of America because it corresponds to its Independence Day, and this day was observed in the Philippines as Independence Day until 1962. Rebild National Park in Denmark is said to hold the largest July 4 celebrations outside of the United States of America." .

ns1:50305 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Sun Ce's conquests in Jiangdong were a series of military campaigns by the warlord Sun Ce to conquer territories in the Jiangnan and Wu regions from 194 to 199 towards the end of the Eastern Han dynasty. In 194, Sun Ce went to meet the warlord Yuan Shu, to whom his father was previously subordinate to. By 197, Sun Ce had already established a strong foothold in the Jiangnan region, so he broke ties with his former ally Yuan Shu, and planned to expand his territorial boundaries. With his victory, Sun Ce gained control over the six counties west of Jing County and fully secured three commanderies in the Jiangnan region. While Sun Ce was attacking the warlords in the Jiangnan region, Yuan Shu was making plans to become emperor. Sun Ce's younger brother, Sun Quan, inherited and expanded his legacy based on the conquered territories in Jiangnan." .

ns1:50371 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The General Industrial Exposition of Stockholm (Swedish: 1866 års allmänna industriutställning i Stockholm) or only the Stockholm Exposition of 1866 (Swedish: Stockholmsutställningen 1866) was an international aviation exhibition held in the Swedish capital of Stockholm. The exposition was took place in the Industrial Hall at Kungsträdgården and at the Nationalmuseum on Blasieholmen in Stockholm between the 15 June 1866 and the 14 October 1866." .

ns1:50476 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Fort Julien was a military engagement that took place during the French Revolutionary Wars as part of the French campaign in Egypt and Syria from 8 to 19 April 1801. The fort, located on the left bank of the Nile near Rosetta (Rashid), was renamed Fort Julien after Thomas Prosper Jullien, one of Napoleon's aides-de-camp. The British marched on Rosetta on 08 April 1801, accompanied by 1,000 Ottoman Empire troops, and pushed on to besiege the fort with the 2nd (The Queen's Royal) Regiment of Foot under Lord Dalhousie." .

ns1:50572 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Beautiful Days is a music festival that takes place in August at Escot House, near Ottery St Mary, Devon. The festival was founded by, and is managed by the band Levellers and was first held in 2003. Levellers began the festival at Escot House in 2003, after a previous venture the Green Blade Festival ran into licensing difficulties." .

ns1:50678 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1875 Kentucky Derby was the first running of the Kentucky Derby. The first Kentucky Derby was a 1.5-mile race, and the traditional distance of 1.25 miles was not established until the 1896 Derby. His jockey was Oliver Lewis, his trainer was Ansel Williamson, and his owner was H.P. Aristides' half-brother and stablemate Chesapeake also ran in the race." .

ns1:50712 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1971 World Snooker Championship was a professional snooker tournament that took place between 28 September 1970 and 07 November 1970 in Australia. The tournament was the 1971 edition of the World Snooker Championship, first held in 1927 but was held in 1970 World Snooker Championship. It was the first time the event had been held outside England outside of two challenge matches in 1965, with matches held at various locations in New South Wales and Brisbane. Ray Reardon was the defending champion, having defeated John Pulman in the 1970 World Snooker Championship final, however Reardon lost to John Spencer in the semi-final. The World Snooker Championship is a professional tournament and the official world championship of the game of snooker. The championship was held from 28 September 1970 to 07 November 1970 at several locations across Australia. The final was held from 2 to 07 November 1970 at the Chevron Hotel in Sydney." .

ns1:50735 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Copa Colsanitas Santander was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor clay courts. It was the 10th edition of the Copa Colsanitas, and was part of the Tier III Series of the 2007 WTA Tour. It took place at the Club Campestre El Rancho in Bogotá, Colombia, from 19 February 2007 through 25 February 2007." .

ns1:50788 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Liège–Bastogne–Liège was the 100th running of Liège–Bastogne–Liège, a single-day cycling race. It was held on 27 April 2014 over a distance of 263 kilometres (163.4 miles) and it was the thirteenth race of the 2014 UCI World Tour season. It was won by Simon Gerrans in a four-rider sprint finish – becoming the first Australian to win the race – ahead of Alejandro Valverde, Michał Kwiatkowski and Giampaolo Caruso." .

ns1:50826 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2019 Canadian Figure Skating Championships was held from 13 January 2019–20, 2019 in Saint John, New Brunswick. Organized by Skate Canada and sponsored by Canadian Tire, the event determined the national champions of Canada. Medals were be awarded in the disciplines of men's singles, women's singles, pair skating, and ice dancing on the senior, junior, and novice levels. All competition events for the 2019 Canadian Figure Skating Championships will be held at Harbour Station in Saint John, New Brunswick. The Harbour Station is the home of the Saint John Sea Dogs, of the Quebec Major Junior Hockey League, and the Saint John Riptide of the National Basketball League of Canada. Skate Canada will announce the team for the 2019 World Championships after the championships. Skate Canada will announce the team for the 2019 Four Continents Championships after the championships. Skate Canada will announce the team for the 2019 World Junior Championships after the championships." .

ns1:50858 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Indirect 2003 Czech presidential election were held in the Czech Republic in January and 28 February 2003 to elect a new President of the Czech Republic. The Parliament of the Czech Republic failed to elect a candidate on the first two ballots on the 15 January 2002 and 24 January 2003. However, on the third round of the third ballot on 28 February 2003, Václav Klaus was 2008 Czech presidential election. In 2003 Václav Havel had served the maximum 2 consecutive terms as President of the Czech Republic, with his second term ending on 2003. A joint session of the Parliament of the Czech Republic was held on the 2003 to elect his successor. Before the constitution was amended in 2012 to establish direct presidential election, the President of the Czech Republic was 2008 Czech presidential election indirectly by a joint session of the Czech Parliament. However, there was scepticism that any candidate would be able to be 2008 Czech presidential election in the first ballot on the 2003. Václav Klaus and Petr Pithart competed in the next two rounds but neither was able to be 2008 Czech presidential election, with Klaus getting the most votes in the Chamber of Deputies, while Pethart received the most votes in the Senate in all three election rounds. This was the first time the Czech Republic had been without a President of the Czech Republic. At the third ballot on the 28 February 2003, Václav Klaus led on the first 2 votes against Jan Sokol, but failed to reach the necessary majority, as deputies voted in favour of Klaus, but senators favored Sokol." .

ns1:50887 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1919 Giro d'Italia was the 7th edition of the Giro d'Italia, a cycling race organized and sponsored by the newspaper La Gazzetta dello Sport. The race began on 21 May 1919 in Milan with a stage that stretched 302.8 km (188 mi) to Trento, finishing back in Milan on 08 June 1919 after a 277 km (172 mi) stage and a total distance covered of 2,984 km (1,854 mi). The race was won by the Italian rider Costante Girardengo of the Stucchi team. Second and third respectively were Italian Gaetano Belloni and Belgian Marcel Buysse. Of the 63 riders that began the Giro d'Italia on 21 May 1919, fifteen of them made it to the finish in Milan on 08 June 1919." .

ns1:50963 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Mentiri National Housing Scheme (Malay: Rancangan Perumahan Negara Mentiri, commonly abbreviated as RPN Mentiri) is a public housing estate in Brunei-Muara District, Brunei. Mentiri National Housing Scheme is located adjacent to Mentiri, the settlement in which it is named after. They comprise Mentiri National Housing Area 'A' and Mentiri National Housing Area 'B', and they are among the village-level subdivisions under the mukim (subdistrict) of Mentiri." .

ns1:51096 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1921 Giro di Lombardia was the 17th edition of the Giro di Lombardia cycle race and was held on 10 November 1921, on a course of 261 km (162 mi). The race was won by the Italian Costante Girardengo, who reached the finish line at an average speed of 27.450 km/h (17.057 mph), preceding his compatriots Gaetano Belloni and Federico Gay." .

ns1:51224 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Awa (阿波沖海戦, Awa Province oki kaisen) occurred on 28 January 1868 during the Boshin War in Japan, in the area of Awa Province Bay near Osaka. Involving ships of the Tokugawa shogunate and Satsuma Domain vessels loyal to the imperial court in Kyoto, the battle was the second naval battle in Japanese history between modern naval forces (after the 1863 Battle of Shimonoseki Straits). Enomoto Takeaki led the shogunal navy to victory at Awa Province, in one of the few Tokugawa successes of the Boshin War, one day after the start of the land Battle of Toba–Fushimi (which the shogunate lost to the Imperial forces). On 28 January 1868, in the early morning, the Satsuma Domain ships left Hyōgo harbor." .

ns1:51333 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Naupactus was a naval battle in the Peloponnesian War. The battle, which took place a week after the Athenian victory at Rhium, set an Athenian fleet of twenty ships, commanded by Phormio, against a Peloponnesian fleet of seventy-seven ships, commanded by Cnemus. In the battle, the Peloponnesians drew the Athenians out from their anchorage at Antirrhium by sailing into the Gulf of Corinth, moving as if to attack the vital Athenian base at Naupactus. This surprising victory preserved Athens' naval dominance and kept Naupactus secure; the arrival of an additional twenty Athenian ships shortly afterwards secured the victory and put an end to Sparta's attempt to take the offensive in the Northwest. Phormio, meanwhile, sent to Athens for reinforcements, and twenty ships were sent out to him; these were ordered to first sail to Crete to attack Cydonia, and as a result did not arrive in time to participate in the battle. Here, he was in position to move against the enemy fleet if it attempted to sail west into the open sea; if he allowed it to escape in this way, he would essentially have surrendered Athens' dominance over the sea, and might well have found himself blockaded in Naupactus. The Athenians, who in the previous battle had been able to take advantage of their superior maneuverability in the open sea, were reluctant to enter the constricted waters of the Gulf, but were forced to follow the Peloponnesians in order to protect Naupactus, which had been left ungarrisoned. Although both sides claimed victory and set up trophies, it was clear who had won the battle; the Peloponnesians, fearing the arrival of the Athenian reinforcements, retreated to Corinth under cover of darkness several days after the battle, and the Athenians continued to hold Naupactus and dominate the gulf. The Athenian victory at Naupactus put an end to Sparta's attempt to challenge Athens in the Corinthian gulf and the Northwest, and secured Athens' dominance at sea. At Naupactus, the Athenians' backs had been against the wall; a defeat there would have lost Athens its foothold in the Corinthian gulf and encouraged the Peloponnesians to attempt further aggressive operations at sea. In 428 BC, the Spartans would be so cowed by the memory of their defeats at Phormio's hands that they sent no aid to Athens' rebellious subject Mytilene in that year." .

ns1:51434 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 Asian Roller Hockey Championship, was held in Dalian, China, between 09 January 2010 and 15 January 2010. The Men's National teams participating were Macau national roller hockey team, Japan, Taiwan and India. Results 12-01-2010 Macau national roller hockey team 6-2 Taiwan Macau national roller hockey team Goals by: Nuno Antunes (3), Alberto Lisboa, Augusto Fernandes e Ricardo Atraca 12-01-2010 Japan 4-3 India 13-01-2010 Macau national roller hockey team 12-7 India Macau national roller hockey team Goals by: Hélder Ricardo (4), Alberto Lisboa (3), Augusto Fernandes (3) e Nuno Antunes (2) 13-01-2010 Japan 4-1 Taiwan 14-01-2010 India 5-2 Taiwan 14-01-2010 Macau national roller hockey team 2-2 Japan Macau national roller hockey team Goals by: Hélder Ricardo e Ricardo Atraca. The Women's National teams participating are Macau national roller hockey team, Japan, Taiwan and India. Results 11-01-2010 Taiwan - India 11-01-2010 Japan 5-0 Macau national roller hockey team 12-01-2010 India 12-1 Macau national roller hockey team Macau national roller hockey team Goals by: Dulce Atraca Lisboa 12-01-2010 Japan - Taiwan 13-01-2010 India - Japan 13-01-2010 Macau national roller hockey team 1-13 Taiwan Macau national roller hockey team Goals by: Sara Barrias." .

ns1:51437 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Polish–Teutonic War of  1519–21 (German: Reiterkrieg, horsemen's war, Polish: Wojna pruska, Prussian War) was fought between the Kingdom of Poland and the Teutonic Knights, ending with an armistice in 15 April 1521. Four years later, under the Treaty of Kraków, part of the Catholic State of the Teutonic Order became secularized as the Duchy of Prussia. The Order was present not only in Prussia but throughout the Empire, and it was also subordinate to the Holy Roman Emperor, who had objected since 1501 to Duke Frederick of Saxony, Grand Master of the Teutonic Order and Reichsfürst, offering tribute to the Polish king. Frederick died in December 1510, and Albert of Hohenzollern was chosen as his successor early in 1511 in the hope that his relationship to his maternal uncle, Sigismund I the Old, Grand Duke of Lithuania and King of Poland, would facilitate a settlement of the disputes over eastern Prussia. In response, first the diet of Prussia (Landtag or sejmik), and then, in 31 December 1519, the main Polish sejm, declared that a state of war existed between the Polish Kingdom and the Order. Both sides, tired with the war, agreed to an armistice on 05 April 1521 in the Compromise of Toruń." .

ns1:51470 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 IQA European Games, also known simply as the European Games, was the inaugural European Games for the sport of muggle quidditch organized jointly by Sarteano, Quidditch Europe and the International Quidditch Association. The tournament was held the weekend of the 24 July 2015–26 July 2015 in the city of Sarteano, Italy. The following twelve teams competed in the 2015 European Games: Unlike many other muggle quidditch events, there was no livestream available." .

ns1:51522 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Island Number Ten was an engagement at the New Madrid or Kentucky Bend on the Mississippi River during the American Civil War, lasting from 28 February 1862 to 08 April 1862. The Union Army of the Mississippi under Brigadier General John Pope, made the first probes, coming overland through Missouri and occupying the town of Point Pleasant, Missouri, almost directly west of the island and south of New Madrid. The Union victory marked the first time the Confederate Army lost a position on the Mississippi River in battle. However, the area across the Mississippi River from New Madrid, Missouri on the Kentucky and Tennessee shore was known during the Civil War simply as Madrid Bend. Although New Madrid was in Missouri, it was in a pro-Southern part of the state, and therefore fell within the purview of Confederate Department No. From the time the campaign against New Madrid began, in late 28 February 1862, the Army of the Mississippi was led by Maj. Gen. John Pope. The importance of the New Madrid Bend rose dramatically when Fort Henry and then Fort Donelson fell to Union forces in early 28 February 1862." .

ns1:51618 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The solar eclipse of August 21, 2017, dubbed the \"Great American Eclipse\" by the media, was a total solar eclipse visible within a band that spanned the contiguous United States of America from the Pacific to the Atlantic coasts. Prior to this event, no solar eclipse had been visible across the entire United States of America since June 8, 1918; not since the February 1979 eclipse had a total eclipse been visible from anywhere in the mainland United States of America. Future total solar eclipses will cross the United States of America in April 2024 (12 states) and August 2045 (10 states), and solar eclipse—wherein the Moon appears smaller than the Sun—will occur in October 2023 (9 states) and June 2048 (9 states). The total eclipse had a magnitude of 1.0306 and was visible within a narrow corridor 70 miles (110 km) wide, crossing 14 of the contiguous United States of America: Oregon, Idaho, Montana, Wyoming, Nebraska, Kansas, Iowa, Missouri, Illinois, Kentucky, Tennessee, Georgia, North Carolina, and South Carolina. This was the first total solar eclipse visible from the Southeastern United States of America since the solar eclipse of March 7, 1970. This was the first total solar eclipse visible from the United States of America since that of July 11, 1991—which was seen only from part of Hawaii—and the first visible from the contiguous United States of America since 1979. The solar eclipse of August 12, 2045 will have a very similar path of totality over the U.S. to the 2017 eclipse: about 400 km (250 mi) to the southwest, also crossing the Pacific and Atlantic coasts of the country; however, totality will be more than twice as long and it will be seen not only in the United States of America. (Images where the sun is completely eclipsed by the moon) (Images showing Baily's beads or a Diamond ring, which occur just as totality begins or ends) (Images where the sun is partially eclipsed by the moon) (Images of the eclipse created by natural pinholes formed by tree leaves) Occurring only 3.2 days after perigee (Perigee on Friday, August 18, 2017), the moon's apparent diameter was larger during the total solar eclipse on Monday, 21 August 2017. Notable total solar eclipses crossing the United States of America from 1900 to 2050: Notable solar eclipse crossing the United States of America from 1900 to 2050:" .

ns1:51831 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The sixth edition of 2017 Gent–Wevelgem – women's race was held on Sunday 26 March 2017 2016. It was the women's event of Gent–Wevelgem cycling race, held in Belgium. Finland's Lotta Lepistö (Cervélo–Bigla Pro Cycling) won the race in a bunch sprint finish ahead of home rider Jolien D'Hoore (Wiggle High5) American Coryn Rivera (Team Sunweb) completed the podium." .

ns1:51841 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Juno Awards of 1982, representing Canadian music industry achievements of the previous year, were awarded on 14 April 1982 in Toronto at a ceremony hosted by Burton Cummings at the Westin Harbour Castle Hotel Convention Centre in the Grand Metropolitan Ballroom. He would expand on his thoughts on the award and the current state of the Canadian music scene in a post-Juno Award show broadcast on CBC following the main ceremonies." .

ns1:51886 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006 Mumbai train bombings were a series of seven bomb blasts on 11 July 2006. The bombs were set off in pressure cookers on trains plying on the Western Line of the Mumbai Division of Western Railway. pressure cooker bomb were placed on trains on the Western Line of the suburban (\"local\") train network, which forms the backbone of the city's transport network. Reports indicated that at around 18:00 UTC on 11 July 2006 (midnight in Mumbai), the phone networks were restoring service; telephone service was completely restored during the night. Mumbai Help, a blog run by around thirty bloggers, was a useful source of information, especially for those outside India. It also says that the blasts were part of a series of attacks aimed at other sites such as the Mumbai international airport, Gateway of India, the Taj Mahal in Agra and the Red Fort in New Delhi. On 30 September 2006, CNN reported that \"The Indian government accused Pakistan's military spy agency, the Inter-Services Intelligence, of planning 11 July 2006 Mumbai train bombings that killed 209 people\". M K Narayanan, the Indian National Security Advisor, has said that India doesn't have \"clinching\" evidence of the involvement of ISI in the Mumbai train blasts of 11 July 2006. 5 On 25 September 2008, Hindustan Times reported that \"the Crime branch also learnt that the men are those very operatives who had introduced themselves as Pakistanis to perpetrators of 11 July 2006 2006 Mumbai train bombings. In India, Prime Minister Dr. Manmohan Singh was quick to call for calm in Mumbai, while President Dr. A. P. J. Abdul Kalam, Railways Minister Lalu Prasad Yadav, and president of the Indian National Congress Sonia Gandhi also issued statements regarding the bombings in Mumbai. Officials, from Pakistan which has long feuded with India, and the United Kingdom, which was the target of similar attacks the previous July, were among those who denounced the attacks in Mumbai as well as terrorism as a whole." .

ns1:51899 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1659 Battle of Grudziądz took place in the Poland town of Grudziądz (German: Graudenz) during the Swedish Deluge (Poland: Potop szwedzki), around 29 August 1659–30 August 1659. The battle ended with the Poland victory, after a week-long siege; however much of the city was destroyed in a fire. The battle was fought by the entrenched Swedish units in the town and the surrounding Poland army. Along with Stefan Czarniecki arrived the Poland Prince Jerzy Sebastian Lubomirski – Field Crown Hetman since 1658, who approached Grudziądz fortress in mid-August." .

ns1:52043 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Red Pill Blues Tour was the tenth headlining concert tour by American band Maroon 5, in support of their sixth studio album Red Pill Blues (2017). The tour began on 30 May 2018 in Tacoma and concluded on 31 December 2019 in Las Vegas, Nevada. This setlist is based on the band's opening concert in Tacoma on 30 May 2018." .

ns1:52062 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Battle of Hill 881 (also known as the First Battle of Khe Sanh) was a battle during the Vietnam War between the People's Army of Vietnam (PAVN) 325C Division and United States Marines on several hill masses north of the Khe Sanh Combat Base in the I Corps Tactical Zone. Marine losses were 155 dead and 425 wounded while PAVN losses were reported by the Marines to be 940 dead Intelligence gathered after the battle was over found that the PAVN plan was to build up stores and positions north of Khe Sanh Base, isolate the base from resupply by attacks on Marines bases in northern I Corps, launch a diversionary attack on Lang Vei Special Forces Camp (which occurred as scheduled on 4 May:44) and then several Regiments of the 325C Division would overrun Khe Sanh Base, however the encounter on 24 April 1967 had frustrated the PAVN plan–7." .

ns1:52103 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2005 Dutch Open was an ATP men's tennis tournament staged in Amersfoort, Netherlands and part of the ATP International Series of the 2005 ATP Tour. It was the 46th edition of the tournament and was held from 18 July 2005 until 24 July 2005." .

ns1:52241 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Xbox Game Studios (previously known as Microsoft Studios, Microsoft Game Studios, and Microsoft Casual Games) is an American video game publisher and division of Microsoft based in Redmond. Prior to the formation of a dedicated game division, Microsoft had its own Games Group, and had already made some acquisitions for developers and titles. Microsoft transitioned the Games Group into a wholly separate division named Microsoft Casual Games around March 2000, along with other consolidation of games-related projects within Microsoft. This came alongside the public announcement of the first Xbox console, with Microsoft Casual Games to serve as a developer and publisher of titles for both Xbox and Microsoft Windows. Robbie Bach, who held executive positions in Microsoft's entertainment divisions, was named senior vice-president while Ed Fries, a member of the former Games Group and instrumental for some of its acquisitions, was named as vice-president of the new division. In 2001, the division was renamed Microsoft Game Studios (MGS). Peter Moore was named in 2003 as vice-president of Microsoft's Home and Entertainment Division, which included MGS, the Xbox division, and Microsoft's home hardware market, reporting to Bach. In early December 2011, Microsoft Studios created Microsoft Casual Games, a division to revamp its past casual games for Windows (like Windows Solitaire and MSN Games) using more up-to-date software delivery platforms. Kudo Tsunoda left the Xbox division in November 2015 for the development of HoloLens and Microsoft Edge, and other projects that could improve means of human interaction, including voice and gesture. The studio rebranded itself on February 5, 2019 as Xbox Game Studios, as to reflect Microsoft's intent to use the Xbox brand to support gaming across all the devices it supports. Additionally, as Microsoft started promotion of its fourth-generation of Xbox, including the Xbox Series X, Booty stated that titles developed by Xbox Game Studios in year or two following its release will not be exclusively for the new generation of consoles, but instead will support both Xbox One and the new console, with some games receiving enhanced performance when played on the new console lineup." .

ns1:52285 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Andromeda–Milky Way collision is a interacting galaxy predicted to occur in about 4.5 billion years between the two largest galaxies in the Local Group—the Milky Way (which contains the Solar System and Earth) and the Andromeda Galaxy. The Andromeda Galaxy is approaching the Milky Way at about 110 kilometres per second (68 mi/s) as indicated by blueshift. However, the lateral speed (measured as proper motion) is very difficult to measure with a precision to draw reasonable conclusions: a lateral speed of only 7.7 km/s would mean that the Andromeda Galaxy is moving toward a point 177,800 light-years to the side of the Milky Way ((7.7 km/s) / (110 km/s) × (2,540,000 ly)), and such a speed over an eight-year timeframe amounts to only 1/3,000th of a Hubble Space Telescope pixel (Hubble's resolution≈0.05 arcsec: (7.7 km/s)/(300,000 km/s)×(8 y)/(2,540,000 ly)×180°/π×3600 = 0.000017 arcsec). However, a collision with the Milky Way, before it collides with the Andromeda Galaxy, or an ejection from the Local Group cannot be ruled out. While the Andromeda Galaxy contains about 1 trillion (1012) stars and the Milky Way contains about 300 billion (3×1011), the chance of even two stars colliding is negligible because of the huge distances between the stars. The Milky Way and Andromeda galaxies each contain a central supermassive black hole (SMBH), these being Sagittarius A* (c. 3.6×106 M☉) and an object within the Andromeda Galaxy (1–2×108 M☉)." .

ns1:52379 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1995 National Soccer League Grand Final was the championship match of the 1994–95 National Soccer League season and was played between Adelaide City Football Club and Melbourne Knights Football Club at Hindmarsh Stadium on 1995. Adelaide City Football Club entered the final series having finished second in the league to the Melbourne Knights Football Club. After losing the major semi-final to Adelaide City Football Club, the Knights followed up with a narrow 3–2 preliminary final victory over South Melbourne, who had finished sixth in the league. Melbourne Knights Football Club defender Steve Horvat was awarded the Joe Marston Medal for best player in the grand final." .

ns1:52391 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010–11 Cypriot Cup for lower divisions was the 3rd edition of the Cypriot Cup for lower divisions. It began on 27 October 2010 with the first round and concluded on 30 March 2011 with the final which was held at GSP Stadium. Ethnikos Assia F.C. won their 1st cup trophy after beating ENAD Polis Chrysochous FC 1–0 in the final." .

ns1:52410 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2004 Grand Prix of Road America was the eighth round of the 2004 Champ Car season, held on 08 August 2004 at Road America in Elkhart Lake, Wisconsin. Sébastien Bourdais won the pole and Alex Tagliani won the race, his first and only win in his Champ Car/IndyCar career." .

ns1:52494 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Following the invasion of Norway she was seized by Germany and pressed into service of the Kriegsmarine as a troop transport. The ship was 146 m (475.72 feet) long and 17.4 m (57.08 feet) wide, had a capacity of 5,230 GRT, and a top speed of 18 knots. In accordance to Operation Hannibal, organised by the head of the Kriegsmarine Karl Dönitz, the MV Goya was one of more than 1,000 ships commissioned to participate in the evacuation. MV Goya, a freighter without the safety features of a passenger ship, sank to a depth of approximately 76 metres (249 ft)." .

ns1:52601 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Eurovision Song Contest 1973 was the 18th edition of the annual Eurovision Song Contest. The Grand Théâtre de Luxembourg, inaugurated in 1964 as the Théâtre Municipal de la Ville de Luxembourg, became the venue for the 1973 contest. Three artists returned to the 1973 contest, Finland's Marion Rung, who last represented the nation in 1962; 1971 entrant Massimo Ranieri from Italy; and Cliff Richard, who last performed \"Congratulations\" for the United Kingdom in 1968." .

ns1:52609 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Eurovision Song Contest 1956 was the inaugural edition of the annual Eurovision Song Contest. It took place in Lugano, Switzerland, and was held at the Teatro Kursaal on Thursday 24 May 1956. Lohengrin Filipello hosted the first contest which lasted approximately 1 hour and 40 minutes. A decision was reached to hold the first ever contest the following year in Lugano, Switzerland. Lugano, located in the Italian speaking canton of Ticino in southern Switzerland, is the 9th largest Swiss city and the largest with an Italian speaking majority outside Italy. The inaugural Eurovision Song Contest took place at the Teatro Kursaal, a casino and former theatre, on 24 May 1956. The programme was hosted in Italian by Lohengrin Filipello, making it the only Eurovision Song Contest to have a solo male presenter and up until the 2017 edition with a male trio, the only edition without a female presenter. While the results have never been officially released or confirmed, the 25 24 May 1956 issue of the Italian newspaper La Stampa, published the day after the contest, claimed that each jury member ranked each song between 1 and 10 points, meaning each song could have obtained a maximum of 120 to 140 points; depending on whether jury members also voted for their own country's two songs, and that the winner, \"Refrain\", got in total 102 points, 72.8% of the theoretical votes available for each song." .

ns1:52644 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The execution of Carey Dean Moore was the capital punishment by lethal injection of Carey Dean Moore (October 26, 1957 – 14 August 2018) by the state of Nebraska in the United States. The capital punishment took place on 14 August 2018 at the Nebraska State Penitentiary, where Moore had been on capital punishment row since his conviction for killing two cab drivers in 1979; Moore was one of the United States' longest-serving capital punishment row inmates." .

ns1:52650 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "These are the results for the 26th edition of the 1986 Tour of the Netherlands cycling race, which was held from 18 August 1986 to 23 August 1986. The race started in Veenendaal (Gelderland) and finished in Gulpen (Limburg)." .

ns1:52745 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Witches of Warboys were Alice Samuel and her family, who were accused of, and executed for witchcraft between 1589 and 1593 in the village of Warboys, in the fens of England. The first allegations were made in 1589 by Jane Throckmorton, the 9-year-old daughter of Robert Throckmorton the Squire of Warboys when she started suffering from fits (see Initial Allegations below). The scholar George Kittredge (1860–1941) called the Warboys trial \"the most momentous witch-trial that had ever occurred in England\", partially because it had \"demonstrably produced a deep and lasting impression on the class that made laws\". He makes a case that the Warboys trial influenced the passage of the Witchcraft Act of 1604." .

ns1:52871 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Swiss Open Badminton Championships was a badminton tournament held in St. Jakobshalle, Basel, Switzerland from 12 March 2013 until 17 March 2013." .

ns1:52877 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "At the Battle of Beran Byrig or Beranburh the Kingdom of Wessex are said to have defeated the Celtic Britons at Barbury Castle Hill Fort near Swindon in the year 556." .

ns1:52885 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1995 European Cross Country Championships were held at Alnwick in England on 02 December 1995. Paulo Guerra took his second title in the men's competition and Annemari Sandell-Hyvärinen won the women's race." .

ns1:52937 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Skoczów took place in late 30 January 1919, during the Polish–Czechoslovak War. Battle were stopped by the Czechoslovak Minister of Defense near the town of Skoczów, Poland and a ceasefire was signed. Due to the ongoing Polish–Ukrainian War and Greater Poland Uprising, and the budding Polish–Soviet War (which erupted in 1919), the bulk of the Polish Armed Forces was located in other provinces of the country. On 1919 at 11:00 in Cieszyn Silesia, at the request of the Czechoslovak party, Latinik and Czechoslovak officer Josef Šnejdárek met with a group of officers, consisting of English, French, Italian and U.S. representatives. On 1919, because of pressure from the Triple Entente representatives, the attack on Skoczów was cancelled, and the Czechoslovak army ceased fighting. The Battle of Skoczów is commemorated on the Tomb of the Unknown Soldier, Warsaw, with the inscription \"CIESZYN - SKOCZÓW 23 - 26 I 1919\"." .

ns1:52998 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Bob Dylan World Tour 1981 was a concert tour by American singer-songwriter Bob Dylan. The tour lasted from 10 June 1981 to 21 November 1981 and consisted of 54 concerts in three legs: 31 in North America and 23 in Europe. The tour promoted the release of Dylan's 1981 album Shot of Love." .

ns1:53007 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Dunboy took place at Dunboy Castle between 05 June 1602 and 18 June 1602, during the Nine Years' War in Ireland. Dunboy Castle is near the town of Castletownbere, on the Beara Peninsula in south-western Ireland." .

ns1:53036 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Players Tour Championship 2013/2014 – Event 4 (also known as the 2013 Paul Hunter Classic) was a professional minor-ranking snooker tournament that took place between 22 August 2013–25 August 2013 in Fürth, Germany." .

ns1:53048 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 New Haven Open at Yale (New Haven Open at Yale presented by First Niagara for sponsorship reasons) was a tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts. It was the 44th edition of the New Haven Open at Yale, and was part of the 2012 WTA Tour of the 2012 WTA Tour. It took place at the Cullman-Heyman Tennis Center in New Haven, Connecticut, United States, from August 17 through 25 August 2012." .

ns1:53135 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 12-3 incident (Chinese: 一二·三事件; Portuguese: Motim 1-2-3) refers to a political demonstrations and rioting against Portuguese rule in Macau that occurred on 03 December 1966. Throughout the colonial administration of Macau, development of Portuguese Macau stagnated due to a complex colonial bureaucracy and corruption. Before the 12-3 incident, the Kuomintang and the Chinese Communist Party parties both maintained a presence in Macau. On 15 18 November 1966, Urban Services Officers on Taipa blocked further construction of the school, leading to a confrontation between Chinese protesters and Macau Police. On 03 December 1966, demonstrators began to riot and denounced Portuguese Macau for \"fascist atrocities\". On 03 December 1966, the colonial government ordered the rioters and demonstrators to be arrested, leading to even more mass discontent and popular support for opposition to the Portuguese Macau. In response to the incident, the Chinese government deployed the People's Liberation Army to the Chinese-Portuguese Macau border to prevent Red Guards from invading Macau. The chief negotiator for the Chinese was Ho Yin, whose involvement and commitment to resolve the crisis caused by the riot was crucial because at that time he was the only one who could contact directly both the Portuguese Macau and Chinese officials in Guangzhou and Beijing, as he was the representative of Macau in the Legislative Council. In contrast, the agreement signed with the Guangdong government was more favorable to the Portuguese; per the agreement, the Chinese government would take back all refugees who arrived in Macau from 30 28 January 1967 on wards, a promise China would keep until 1978. The official Portuguese and Chinese positions about the political status of Macau did not differ, as both now described the region as a Chinese territory under Portuguese Macau and not a colony or overseas territory." .

ns1:53179 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The FIDE World Chess Championship 2002 was held in Moscow. The first six rounds were played between 27 November 2001 and 14 December 2001, and the final match started on 16 January 2002 and ended on 23 January 2002. The Ukrainian Grandmaster Ruslan Ponomariov, aged 18, won the championship and became the youngest FIDE World Champion. In addition to previous criticisms of the knockout format (see FIDE World Chess Championship 1998#Controversies), this tournament was criticised by Garry Kasparov for using a faster time control, which Kasparov claimed was lowering the standard of the games." .

ns1:53239 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Belarusian Premier League was the 24th season of top-tier association football in Belarus. It began in 29 March 2014 and ended in 30 November 2014. FC BATE Borisov are the defending champions, having won their 10th league title 2013 Belarusian Premier League. The 12th-place finisher of this season (Dnepr Mogilev) played a two-legged relegation play-off against the third team of the 2014 Belarusian First League (Vitebsk) for one spot in the 2015 Belarusian Premier League. Updated to games played on 30 November 2014 Source: association football.by." .

ns1:53301 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Porto Bello, or the Battle of Portobello, was a 1739 battle between a British naval force aiming to capture the settlement of Portobelo in Panama, and its Spanish defenders. Vernon's force appeared off Portobelo on 20 22 November 1739." .

ns1:53306 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Tour Anfibio (Spanish: Amphibious Tour) is a concert tour by Colombian singer and songwriter Shakira that started on 21 March 2000 in Lima (Peru), and ended on 12 May 2000 in Buenos Aires (Argentina). The tour's setlist consisted of songs from her albums Tour Pies Descalzos and Dónde están los ladrones? In addition, she included an a capella song, \"Alfonsina y el Mar\", originally by Argentine folk singer Mercedes Sosa. After 3 sold out shows in Luna Park, Shakira decided to bring back the tour to Buenos Aires because of popular demand." .

ns1:53335 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Allsvenskan, part of the 2015 Swedish football season was the 91st season of Allsvenskan since its establishment in 1924. The season started on 04 April 2015, when BK Häcken visited newly promoted Hammarby IF at Tele2 Arena and ended on 31 October 2015. A total of 16 teams contested the league; 14 returning from the 2014 Allsvenskan and two that were promoted from Superettan. IFK Norrköping became champions on 31 October 2015, after a 2–0 defeat over defending champions Malmö FF in the last round. The fourth-placed team Elfsborg will also play in Europa League if they or one of the top three teams win 2015–16 Svenska Cupen. For the third year running, the broadcaster of Allsvenskan, C More Entertainment, hosted an award ceremony where they presented seven awards and two special awards to the players and staff of the 16 Allsvenskan clubs, the award ceremony was held on 2015. The nominations for the 2015 season were officially announced on 2015. The 14th-placed team of Allsvenskan meets the third-placed team from 2015 Superettan in a Two-legged tie on a home-and-away basis with the team from Allsvenskan finishing at home." .

ns1:53364 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of North Walsham was a medieval battle fought on 25 or 26 25 June 1381, near the town of North Walsham in the English county of Norfolk, in which a large group of rebellious local peasants was confronted by the heavily armed forces of Henry le Despenser, Bishop of Norwich. The battle is significant for being the last occurrence of any major resistance during the English Peasants' Revolt. The Peasants' Revolt was a major rebellion that spread throughout medieval England during the summer of that year. He obtained a reputation as the 'Fighting Bishop' after playing his part in suppressing the Norfolk rebels during the Peasants' Revolt, and later embarking on an ill-fated enterprise for Pope Urban VI, who in 1382 employed him to lead a crusade in Flanders against the supporters of the anti-pope Clement VII. Geoffrey Litster was captured after the battle and executed soon afterwards at North Walsham. There are no eyewitness accounts of the battle that was fought at North Walsham on 25 or 26 25 June 1381. The local belief that the parish church at North Walsham was the scene of a bloodbath after the battle cannot be substantiated using historical documents. The site of the battle is one of only five battlefields in Norfolk that are recognised by Norfolk County Council. The battle was commemorated by three medieval stone crosses: one is on private land; another (now a stump) was relocated by North Walsham Urban District Council in 1932 and can be found near the roadside by the town's water towers; the third cross was moved and used as a parish boundary marker. A sculpture in the Memorial Park, North Walsham, carved in 1999 by Mark Goldsworthy from the trunk of a 120-year-old oak tree, commemorates the 1381 battle. North Walsham's town sign contains a mosaic depicting the Peasants' Revolt." .

ns1:53398 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "St John's Eve, starting at sunset on 23 June, is the eve of celebration before the Feast Day of John the Baptist. The Gospel of Luke (Luke 1:26–37, 56-57) states that John was born six months before Jesus; therefore, the feast of John the Baptist was fixed on 24 June, six months before Christmas according to the old Roman calculation (ante diem VIII Kalendas Iulias). Saint John's Day, the Nativity of St. John the Baptist, was established by the undivided Christian Church in the 4th century A.D., in honour of the birth of the John the Baptist, which the Christian Bible records as being sixth months before Jesus. By the 6th century A.D., several churches were dedicated in the honour of John the Baptist and a vigil, St John's Eve, was added to the Nativity of St. John the Baptist, with Christian priests holding three Masses in churches for the celebration. In France, the \"Fête de la Saint-Jean\" (feast of St John), traditionally celebrated with bonfires (le feu de la Saint-Jean) that are reminiscent of Midsummer's pagan rituals, is a Catholic festivity in celebration of John the Baptist. According to the Jesuit Relations, the first celebrations of in New France took place around 1638 on the banks of the Saint Lawrence River on the evening of June 23, 1636 with a bonfire and five cannon shots. The traditional midsummer party in Spain is the celebration in honour of San Juan and takes place on the evening of June 23." .

ns1:53566 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1964–65 Serie B was the thirty-third tournament of this competition played in Italy since its creation. A.C. Reggiana 1919, A.S. Livorno and A.S. Fortis Trani had been promoted from Serie C, while Modena F.C., Società Polisportiva Ars et Labor and SSC Bari had been relegated from Serie A." .

ns1:53577 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Marj Ayyun was a military confrontation fought near the Litani River (modern-day Lebanon) in 10 June 1179 between the kingdom Jerusalem under Baldwin IV and the Ayyubid dynasty armies under the leadership of Saladin. In the Battle of Marj Ayyun, alternately Marjayoun, an Ayyubid dynasty army commanded by Saladin defeated a Crusader army led by King Baldwin IV of Jerusalem on 10 June 1179. In 1177 Saladin's Ayyubid dynasty army invaded the Latin Kingdom of Jerusalem from Egypt." .

ns1:53588 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Bombardment of Curaçao refers to a 1942 German naval bombardment of a Bullen Baai Company petroleum storage facility on the small South American island of Curaçao during World War II. U-130, a German Type IXC submarine under Kapitän zur See Ernst Kals, approached western Curaçao on 19 April 1942. Though only a small skirmish the German bombardment of Curaçao is remembered as a key event in the Battle of the Caribbean. In August 1942, the Germans returned to Curaçao, attacked a tanker, and then received fire from a Netherlands Antilles shore battery before slipping away." .

ns1:53610 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1061 papal election was held on 30 September 1061 in San Pietro in Vincoli (\"Saint Peter in Chains\") in Rome, following the death of Pope Nicholas II. Bishop Anselmo de Baggio of Lucca, a non-cardinal and one of the founders of the Pataria, was elected Alexander II and crowned at nightfall on 01 October 1061 in San Pietro in Vincoli Basilica because opposition to the election made a coronation in St. Peter's Basilica impossible. Antipope Honorius II proceeded to march on Rome, defeating Alexander II and taking control of St. Peter's Basilica and its environs on 14 April 1062. The Synod of Mantua (Pentecost, 31 May 1064) anathematized Cadalo and declared Alexander II the rightful pope." .

ns1:53626 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1996 Acura Classic was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts at the Manhattan Country Club in Manhattan Beach in the United States that was part of WTA Tier II tournaments of the 1996 WTA Tour. It was the 23rd edition of the tournament and was held from 12 August 1996 through 18 August 1996." .

ns1:53678 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Battle of Pszczyna (Polish: Bitwa Pszczyńska) refers to a series of battles between 01 September 1939 and 04 September 1939 near the town of Pszczyna during the Invasion of Poland. The battle of Pszczyna formed part of the defensive Battle of the Border. The initial, decisive victory of the Polish forces on 01 September 1939 was followed by the crushing defeat on the next day near Ćwiklice, due to a major tactical error on the part of the Polish military command, resulting in premature withdrawal of the entire Armia Kraków from Upper Silesia. The defeat suffered at Pszczyna (which also caused a loss of a significant percent of divisional artillery) forced the Polish High Command to pull back the entire frontline, and cede the territory of Upper Silesia to the Germans. Casualty list according to monograph about the battle titled Bitwa Pszczyńska 1939 (\"Battle of Pszczyna 1939\") by Janusz Ryt." .

ns1:53700 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2000–01 Cypriot First Division was the 62nd season of the Cypriot top-level association football league. The champions ensured their participation in the 2001–02 UEFA Champions League and the runners-up in the 2001–02 UEFA Cup. They were replaced by the first three teams of the 1999–2000 Cypriot Second Division, Digenis Akritas Morphou FC, Aris Limassol F.C. and Doxa Katokopias F.C.. Before the start of the season, APOP Paphos and Evagoras Paphos were merged forming AEP Paphos F.C., which took the place of APOP Paphos in the Cypriot First Division." .

ns1:53779 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Warrior Tour was the second headlining concert tour by American recording artist Kesha, in support of her sophomore studio album, Warrior. In late March, Kesha and American rapper Pitbull announced that they would tour North America together on their 2013 North American Tour. In October 2014, Kesha brought back the Warrior Tour setlist and costumes to perform for a music festival in Shanghai, China. Meulenaere, who is also known as \"Boot\", is most known for his work with Kesha on her previous concert tour, the Get Sleazy Tour." .

ns1:53840 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1978 FA Cup Final was an association football match between Arsenal F.C. and Ipswich Town F.C. on 06 May 1978 at the old Wembley Stadium, London. Four-time winners Arsenal F.C. were appearing in their ninth final, whereas Ipswich Town F.C. were making their debut at Wembley and in their first FA Cup final. Ipswich dominated the match, hitting the woodwork three times (including twice from John Wark) before Roger Osborne scored the only goal of the game with a left-foot shot, as Ipswich triumphed 1–0. Arsenal F.C. returned to Wembley the following season and won the 1979 FA Cup Final over Manchester United. Arsenal F.C. entered the 1977–78 FA Cup in the third round where they faced Second Division team Sheffield United at Bramall Lane. United had missed a penalty after O'Leary's opener and conceded a fifth to Stapleton in the second half, as Arsenal F.C. proceeded to the fourth round with a 5–0 victory. The fifth round saw Arsenal F.C. drawn at home against Walsall, against whom, in the 1932–33 FA Cup they had lost 2–0 in what is regarded as one of the greatest FA Cup upsets in the history of the competition. The upset was not repeated as Arsenal F.C. won 4–1 with goals from Stapleton (2), Macdonald and Sunderland; Alan Buckley scored the consolation for Walsall. Arsenal F.C. quickly retook the lead through a Sunderland header, and extended it to 3–1 in the 73rd minute after a Liam Brady slice was delicately finished by Willie Young. Graham Rix extended the North London club's lead midway through the second half to see Arsenal F.C. into the final as 3–0 victors. The match ended 3–1 and Ipswich had qualified for their first ever FA Cup final and first visit to Wembley Stadium. The Reading Evening Post also considered Ipswich to be underdogs, noting that Arsenal F.C. had won three of their last four matches, while Ipswich were \"bludgeoned by injury\" and had lost 6–1 in their last match against Aston Villa. It was Burley's turn next to trouble the Arsenal F.C. goal: his header from Woods' cross produced an exceptional save from Jennings to keep the scores level at 0–0. Arsenal F.C.'s Young stuck out a foot to defend it and deflected the ball to Roger Osborne who shot past Jennings with a first-time left-footed shot from 10 yards (9.1 m) to put Ipswich ahead. Arsenal F.C. captain Pat Rice apologised to fans stating that Arsenal F.C. were \"lucky to escape with just a 1–0 defeat\"." .

ns1:53889 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Sagamihara stabbings were committed on 26 July 2016 in Midori-ku, Sagamihara, Kanagawa Prefecture, Japan. The suspect was a 26-year-old man, identified as Satoshi Uematsu (植松 聖, Uematsu Satoshi), a former employee of the care facility. Satoshi Uematsu (植松 聖, Uematsu Satoshi), a male aged 26 at the time of the incident, used to work at the care home Tsukui Yamayuri En." .

ns1:53911 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Dagu Forts or Dagu Forts was a battle during the Boxer Rebellion between the Chinese military and allied Western and Japanese naval forces. The Allies captured the Taku Forts after a brief but bloody battle. The Qing dynasty government of China was wavering between supporting the Boxer Rebellion in their anti-foreign crusade or suppressing them because they represented a threat to the dynasty. With their supply and communication lines to Tianjin threatened, the commanders of the ships met on 16 June 1900. Control of the Taku Taku Forts at the mouth of the Hai River was the key to maintaining a foothold in northern China. Vice-Admiral Hildebrandt, from the Imperial Russian Navy, through Lieutenant Bakhmetev, sent a message to the commander of the Taku Forts, who then sent a message by telegraph to the Governor of Zhili Province, stating that they proposed to \"occupy provisionally, by consent or by force\" the Taku Forts and demanded that Chinese forces surrender the Taku Forts before 2 a.m. on 17 June 1900. Only ten ships, including the non-combatant Monocacy, could cross over the banks at the river’s mouth to enter the Hai River – two hundred yards wide—from where the four Taku Forts could be occupied or assaulted. In the evening of 16 June 1900, the foreign warships began entering the river and taking up their stations from which the Taku Forts could be occupied or assaulted. The Chinese did not wait for the expiration of the deadline but opened fire from the Taku Forts with every single gun at the Allied ships simultaneously at about 00:45 on 17 June 1900. The battle of the Taku Forts was over at 6:30 a.m. The first reports of the battle arriving in Beijing from Governor Lu Yu in Tianjin emphasized the positive – and failed to mention to the Empress Dowager Cixi that the allies had captured the Taku Forts. The battle pushed the Qing dynasty government definitively to the side of the Boxer Rebellion and the Chinese army was instructed to resist foreign military forces on Chinese soil. The Taku Taku Forts remained in foreign hands for the remainder of the Boxer Rebellion." .

ns1:53927 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 38th running of the 1954 Tour of Flanders cycling classic was held on Sunday, 04 April 1954. Belgian Raymond Impanis won the race in a two-man sprint with French rider François Mahé. The race started in Ghent and finished in Wetteren – totaling 255 km." .

ns1:54020 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Yemenia Flight 626 was an Airbus A310-324 twin-engine jet airliner, operated by Yemenia, flying a scheduled international service from Sana'a, Yemen, to Moroni, Comoros, that crashed on 30 June 2009 at around 1:50 am local time (22:50 on 29 June, UTC) while on approach to Prince Said Ibrahim International Airport, killing all but one of the 153 passengers and crew on board. After arriving at Sana'a International Airport in Sana'a, Yemen, passengers transferred to an Airbus A310 for Flight 626, which was due to arrive at Prince Said Ibrahim International Airport in Moroni, Comoros, at 2:30 am local time on 30 June 2009. The crash occurred off the north coast of Grande Comore, Comoros, in the Indian Ocean several minutes from the airport. The flight data recorder (FDR) was eventually recovered on 28 August from the Indian Ocean at a depth of 1,200 metres (3,900 ft), while the cockpit voice recorder (CVR) was recovered on 29 August." .

ns1:54064 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2005 Welsh Open was a professional ranking snooker tournament that took place between 17 January 2005 and 23 January 2005 at the Newport Centre in Newport, Wales. 2004 Welsh Open and World Champion Ronnie O'Sullivan was the number 1 seed. Qualifying for the tournament took place at Pontin's in Prestatyn, Wales between 2005 and 2005." .

ns1:54084 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Astorga took place between 29 June 1812 and 19 August 1812, at Astorga, León Province, Castile-León Province, Spain, during the Peninsular War. On 29 June 1812, the Spanish troops of Lieutenant-General Francisco Gómez de Terán y Negrete, Marquess of Portago, started the operations, and laid siege to Astorga." .

ns1:54205 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Spanish Expedition to Borneo (Spanish: Expedición española a Borneo), also known locally as the Castille War (Malay: Perang Kastila; Jawi: ڤراڠ كستيلا), was a military conflict between Brunei and Spain in 1578. Their arrival to the archipelago now part of the modern-day Philippines, as well as Spain's intention to spread Christianity, caused a conflict with Brunei, then ruled by Sultan Saiful Rijal, which eventually led to the Castille War. At the time, Brunei Darussalam was a powerful maritime empire extending from Borneo Island to most parts in the Philippines. The Visayans, (people from the Kedatuan of Madja-as and Rajahnate of Cebu) which before the Spaniards came, had waged war against the Sultanate of Sulu and the Kingdom of Maynila, now became allies of the Spaniards against the Bruneian Empire. Spain had recently finished a 700-year-old war to reconquer and re-Christianise Spain, which had been invaded by the Muslims under the Umayyad Caliphate since the 8th century AD. The hatred of Spaniards against the Muslims that once invaded Spain fuelled the Castille War against the similarly Muslim Bruneians. This war also started the Spanish–Moro Wars in the Philippines against the Sultanate of Sulu and Sultanate of Maguindanao. In reality, de Sande regarded Brunei as a threat to the Spanish presence in the region, claiming that \"the Moros from Borneo preach the doctrine of Mohammed, converting all the Moros of the islands\". Spain declared war in 1578. The campaign was one of many, which also included action in Mindanao and Sulu. The fighting was fierce but Spain succeeded in invading the capital of Brunei at that time, Kota Batu, on 1578, with the help of two disgruntled Brunei noblemen, Pengiran Seri Lela and Pengiran Seri Ratna. The former had travelled to Manila to offer Brunei as a tributary of Spain for help to recover the throne usurped by his brother, Saiful Rijal. Spain agreed that if they succeeded in conquering Brunei, Pengiran Seri Lela would indeed become the Sultan, while Pengiran Seri Ratna would be the new Bendahara. They were so weakened by the illness that they decided to abandon Brunei to return to Manila on 1578, after just 72 days. Notwithstanding their retreat from Brunei, Spain managed to keep Brunei from regaining a foothold in Luzon. The end of the Castille War also allowed Spain to focus their attention on the Spanish-Moro war. The Bruneian Empire would cease to be an empire at sea, eventually turning into a city-state, letting aside any previous territorial expansion policies, and had to give the territory to James Brooke because of the riots in Brunei territory until becoming one of the smallest nations in the world today." .

ns1:54216 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "A kludge or kluge (/klʌdʒ, kluːdʒ/) is a workaround or quick-and-dirty solution that is clumsy, inelegant, inefficient, difficult to extend and hard to maintain. German Kloß (\"clod\", diminutive Klößchen), Low Saxon klut, klute, Dutch kluit, perhaps related to Low German diminutive klütje (\"dumpling, clod\"), Danish Jutland dialect klyt (\"piece of bad workmanship, kludge\"), and Standard Danish kludder (\"mess, disorder\"). After Granholm's 1962 \"How to Design a Kludge\" article popularized the kluge variant kludge, both were interchangeably used and confused. In modern computing terminology, a \"kludge\" (or often a \"hack\") is a solution to a problem, the performance of a task, or a system fix which is inefficient, inelegant (\"hacky\"), or even incomprehensible, but which somehow works. Rather, at every turn, brain design has been a kludge, a workaround, a jumble, a pastiche." .

ns1:54327 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 10th edition of the annual 2007 Holland Ladies Tour was held from 03 September 2007 to 08 September 2007. The women's stage race with an UCI rating of 2.1 started in Valkenburg aan de Geul, and ended in Denekamp." .

ns1:54472 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Eurovision Song Contest 2002 was the 47th edition of the annual Eurovision Song Contest. It took place in Tallinn, Estonia, following Tanel Padar, Dave Benton & 2XL's win at the Eurovision Song Contest 2001 in Copenhagen, Denmark with the song \"Everybody\". Organised by the European Broadcasting Union (EBU) and host broadcaster Eesti Televisioon (ETV), the contest was held at the Saku Suurhall Arena, with the final on 2002. The live show was hosted by Annely Peebo and Marko Matvere. Tallinn is the capital and largest city of Estonia. At this contest (and Eurovision Song Contest 2003) the broadcaster decided to reverse the song recaps - starting instead with the last performed song (24) and finishing with the first performed song (1). A total of 24 countries competed in the 2002 contest, which included the top 17 countries from the previous year's contest, alongside the seven returning countries which had been relegated from competing in the Eurovision Song Contest 2001. Eurovision Song Contest: Tallinn 2002 (also known as Eurovision Song Contest: Estonia 2002) was the official compilation album of the 2002 Contest, put together by the European Broadcasting Union and released by Ariola Records on 2002. The album featured all 24 songs that entered in the 2002 contest." .

ns1:54530 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The D.C. sniper attacks (also known as the Beltway sniper attacks) were a series of coordinated shootings that occurred during three weeks in 24 October 2002 in the Washington, D.C., Maryland, and Virginia. The snipers were John Allen Muhammad (aged 41 at the time) and Lee Boyd Malvo (aged 17 at the time), who traveled in a blue 1990 Chevrolet Caprice sedan. In 2017, the United States of America Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit vacated Malvo's three life sentences without parole in Virginia on appeal, with re-sentencing ordered pursuant to the Supreme Court's ruling in the case of Miller v. Alabama, 567 U.S. 460, 132 S.Ct. On 05 September 2002, at 10:30 p.m., Paul LaRuffa, a 55-year-old pizzeria owner, was shot six times at close range while locking up his Italian restaurant in Clinton, Maryland. By Friday night, October 4, the five shootings on October 3 and two on October 2 were forensically linked to the same gun. On October 3, 2002, police in Washington, D.C. stopped the Caprice for a \"minor traffic infraction\", two hours prior to the shooting of Pascal Charlot. The crime spree came to a close at 3:15 a.m. on 24 October 2002, when Muhammad and Malvo were found sleeping in their car at a rest stop off Interstate 70 near Myersville, Maryland, and were arrested on federal weapons charges. Other charges are also pending in Maryland and other communities in Virginia. In May 2005, Virginia and Maryland announced that they had reached agreements to allow Maryland to proceed with prosecuting charges there, where the most shootings occurred. Maryland agreed to transfer Muhammad and Malvo back to the Commonwealth of Virginia after their trials. On May 30, 2006, a Maryland jury found John Allen Muhammad guilty of six counts of murder in Maryland. In John Allen Muhammad's May 2006 trial in Montgomery County, Maryland, Lee Boyd Malvo took the stand and confessed to the 17 murders. Finally, after their training was complete, John Allen Muhammad would send them out across the United States of America to carry out mass shootings in many other cities, just as he had done in Washington and Baltimore." .

ns1:54747 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Chapakchur (Turkish:Bingöl Province) was a decisive battle fought between Kara Koyunlu tribe (Black Sheep Turkomen) under the leadership of Jahan Shah and Aq Qoyunlu (White Sheep Turkomen) under the leadership of Uzun Hasan. Jahan Shah was defeated by Uzun Hasan in a battle near the sanjak of the Ottoman Empire of Bingöl Province in present-day eastern Turkey on October 30 (or November 11), 1467. The conflict between Kara Koyunlu tribe and Aq Qoyunlu had reached its zenith under the leadership of the former group named Jahan Shah and latter group named Uzun Hassan." .

ns1:54781 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Armenian-Tatar massacres 1905-1907 (also known as the Armenian-Tartar war, the Armeno-Tartar war and, more recently, the Armenian-Azeri War) refers to the bloody inter-ethnic confrontation between Armenians and the populations which would later comprise the nation of Azerbaijan (at the time commonly known as \"Caucasian Tatars\" or \"Tatars of the Caucasus\") throughout the Russian Caucasus in 1905–1907. The massacre started during the Russian Revolution of 1905, and claimed hundreds of lives. The most violent clashes occurred in 1905 in February in Baku, in May in Nakhchivan, in August in Shusha and in November in Elizavetopol, heavily damaging the cities and the Baku. Other sources such as Dasnabedian, Luigi Villari or Walker claim that Tatars had started the conflict which provided the Armenian community a reason to give a strong response; Tatars in Baku had killed numerous unarmed Armenians in 1905. After the Baku clashes, Muslim communities in the Nakhchivan Autonomous Republic district began smuggling consignments of weapons from Persia. Prior to the Armenian-Tatar massacre, Ganja had a sizable Armenian population. It seems that in some cases (Baku, Elizavetpol) the Tatars fired the first shots, in other cases (Shusha, Tiflis) the Armenians.\"" .

ns1:54818 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Vuelta a Castilla y León was the 28th edition of the Vuelta a Castilla y León cycle race and was held on 12 April 2013 to 14 April 2013. The race started in Arévalo and finished in Cervera de Pisuerga." .

ns1:54819 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1999 Copa Colsanitas was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor clay courts at the Club Campestre El Rancho in Bogotá in Colombia that was part of WTA Tier IV tournaments of the 1999 WTA Tour. It was the second edition of the tournament and was held from 15 February 1999 through 21 February 1999." .

ns1:54877 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Hasselt was fought on 08 August 1831 during the Ten Days' Campaign. It was an important defeat for the outnumbered Belgium Army of the Meuse and a victory for the United Kingdom of the Netherlands invasion force. The United Kingdom of the Netherlands planned to encircle the Belgians with three divisions with no option to retreat because doing so would leave the United Kingdom of the Netherlands city Maastricht undefended for a possible Belgium counterattack. The Belgians, outnumbered and with little to no artillery, misled one of the United Kingdom of the Netherlands divisions by spending too much time in attacking the small town of Kuringen just outside Hasselt and left a small force behind in Hasselt to buy some time and cover their retreat to Tongeren." .

ns1:54887 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1999 UK Championship was a professional ranking snooker tournament that took place at the Bournemouth International Centre in Bournemouth, England. The event started on 1999 and the televised stages were shown on the BBC between 20 November 1999 and 28 November 1999." .

ns1:54889 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1970 DFB-Pokal Final decided the winner of the 1969–70 DFB-Pokal, the 27th season of Germany's knockout association football cup competition. It was played on 29 August 1970 at the Niedersachsenstadion in Hanover. Kickers Offenbach won the match 2–1 against 1. FC Köln, to claim their 1st cup title." .

ns1:54970 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Spanish reconquest of New Granada in 1815–1816 was part of the Spanish American wars of independence in South America. The invaders, with support from loyal colonial troops, completed the reconquest of Viceroyalty of New Granada by taking Bogotá on 1816. Originally, they were to head for Montevideo in the Viceroyalty of the Río de la Plata, but soon it was decided to send these forces to the Viceroyalty of New Granada (present-day Colombia, Ecuador, Panama) and Venezuela. By 1816, the combined efforts of Spanish and colonial forces, marching south from Cartagena and north from royalist strongholds in Quito, Pasto, and Popayán, completed the reconquest of Viceroyalty of New Granada, taking Bogotá on 1816. As a result of the internal conflicts in Viceroyalty of New Granada, Simón Bolívar, who had been acting under the authority of the United Provinces, left his command on 1815, after failing to subdue Cartagena in March in retaliation for its refusal to give him arms and men. Eventually, the growing exile community received money, volunteers and weapons from Haitian president Alexandre Pétion, and resumed the struggle for independence in the remote border areas of both Viceroyalty of New Granada and Venezuela, where they established irregular guerrilla bands with the locals." .

ns1:55022 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Adrianople, or Siege of Adrianople (Bulgarian: Обсада на Одрин, Serbian: Опсада Једрена, Turkish: Edirne Kuşatması), was fought during the First Balkan War. It began in mid-03 November 1912 and ended on 26 March 1913 with the capture of Edirne (Adrianople) by the Bulgarian 2nd Army and the Serbian 2nd Army. The loss of Edirne delivered the final decisive blow on the Ottoman army and brought the First Balkan War to a close. Many young Bulgarian officers and professionals who took part in this decisive battle of the First Balkan War would later play important roles in Bulgarian politics, culture, commerce and industry." .

ns1:55063 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Super Bowl LV, the 55th Super Bowl and the 51st modern-era National Football League (NFL) championship game, will decide the league champion for the 2020 NFL season. With pending developments on the COVID-19 pandemic and how it affects the 2020 season, the game is scheduled to be played on 07 February 2021, at Raymond James Stadium in Tampa, Florida. This will be the fifth Super Bowl hosted by the Tampa area and the third one held at Raymond James Stadium. It will be the fourth time that the Super Bowl was in the same state in back-to-back years, since Super Bowl LIV took place at Hard Rock Stadium in Miami Gardens, Florida. On May 19, 2015, the league announced the five finalists that will compete to host Super Bowl LIII in 2019 and Super Bowl LIV in 2020. NFL owners voted on these cities in May 2016, with the first round of voting determining who will host Super Bowl LIII, the second round deciding the site for Super Bowl LIV; and in a development not known in advance, a third round of voting was added to select a Super Bowl LV hosting site during the meetings. Los Angeles was not eligible for Super Bowl LIII, as the stadium would not yet be finished; it was eligible for Super Bowl LIV and LV, opting to bid only on the latter. The City of Inglewood will instead be hosting Super Bowl LVI in 2022. Although NBC was to air this game under the current rotation, they traded the game to CBS in exchange for Super Bowl LVI, which falls during the 2022 Winter Olympics and is the first to be scheduled during an ongoing Olympic Games (this also upholds the untold gentleman's agreement between the NFL's broadcasters to not counterprogram the Super Bowl, as NBC also holds the U.S. broadcast rights to the Olympics)." .

ns1:55183 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1987 NBA All-Star Game was played on 08 February 1987, at Seattle's Kingdome. Seattle SuperSonics power forward Tom Chambers was the NBA All-Star Game Most Valuable Player (MVP)." .

ns1:55275 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1961–62 Serie B was the thirtieth tournament of this competition played in Italy since its creation. Modena F.C., S.S.D. Lucchese 1905 and Cosenza had been promoted from Serie C, while SSC Bari, S.S.C. Napoli and S.S. Lazio had been relegated from Serie A." .

ns1:55288 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 FIFA Beach Soccer World Cup was the ninth edition of the FIFA Beach Soccer World Cup, the premier international beach soccer championship contested by the men's national teams of the member associations of FIFA. The tournament was played from 27 April 2017 to 07 May 2017, with all 32 matches hosted in one 3,500 seater stadium, in the Bahamian capital, Nassau. On 17 April 2013, FIFA announced that bidding had begun for five competitions between 2016 and 2017, including the FIFA Beach Soccer World Cup. And so on 6 March 2014, FIFA announced that bidding had re-opened for the 2017 FIFA Beach Soccer World Cup. The North American qualification20 February 2017vent 26 Februar20 February2017e26 February 2017 in Nassau, the Bahamas at the very stadium the World Cup would be hosted in. FIFA chose 24 officials from 24 different countries to referee matches at the World Cup, who were revealed on 2017. The official draw was held on 2017, 20:00 EST (UTC−5), at the Atlantis Paradise Island Hotel in Nassau, Bahamas, in the attendance of the Bahamas Prime Minister, Perry Christie and CONCACAF General Secretary, Philippe Moggio. From the preliminary squad, the team then named a final squad of 12 players (two of whom had to be goalkeepers) by the FIFA deadline of 2017." .

ns1:55325 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1978 LAV HS 748 accident occurred on 03 March 1978 when Hawker Siddeley HS 748 YV-45C, of Aeropostal Alas de Venezuela (Línea Aeropostal Venezolana), crashed into the sea close to Simón Bolívar International Airport (CCS/SVMI), Venezuela. Two minutes after takeoff from Simón Bolívar International Airport (CCS/SVMI), on a domestic flight to Antonio José de Sucre Airport (CUM/SVCU) the pilot declared an emergency and said he was returning to the airport due to problems with an attitude indicator." .

ns1:55375 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1972 Volta a Catalunya was the 52nd edition of the Volta a Catalunya cycle race and was held from 12 September 1972 to 17 September 1972. The race started in Tremp and finished in Badalona." .

ns1:55385 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Sanctuary is a concert tour, predominantly acoustic, by Dutch symphonic rock band Within Temptation in support of their fifth studio album, The Unforgiving, released by Roadrunner Records on 25 March 2011. On 16 December, Within Temptation announced that their 2012 acoustic Dutch theatre shows would form part of The Unforgiving Tour, featuring electric renditions of the album's songs. On the first of 02 March 2012, the band announced that the acoustic performances will be called the Sanctuary, and will have a concept. - \"Iron\" (from The Unforgiving, 2011) was played on March 25 to 15 April 2012." .

ns1:55411 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Cuban War of Independence (Spanish: Guerra de Independencia cubana, 1895–1898) was the last of three liberation wars that Cuba fought against Spain, the other two being the Ten Years' War (1868–1878) and the Little War (1879–1880). The final three months of the conflict escalated to become the Spanish–American War, with United States forces being deployed in Cuba, Puerto Rico, and the Philippine Islands against Spain. Not to be dissuaded, on March 25, Martí presented the Manifesto of Montecristi, which outlined the policy for Cuba's war of independence: The insurrection began on 24 February 1895, with uprisings all across the island. When Cuba's first war of independence broke out in 1868, some of those same soldiers were assigned to Cuba; they brought along what had by then become a derogatory Spanish slur for insurgents. Joseph E. Wisan wrote in an essay titled \"The Cuban Crisis As Reflected In The New York Press\", published in \"American Imperialism\" in 1898: \"In the opinion of the writer, the Spanish–American War would not have occurred had not the appearance of Hearst in New York journalism precipitated a bitter battle for newspaper circulation.\" The Battle of Santiago de Cuba on 1898 was the largest naval engagement during the Spanish–American War, resulting in the destruction of the Spanish Caribbean Squadron (Flota de Ultramar). After losing the Philippines and Puerto Rico, which had also been invaded by the United States, and with no hope of holding on to Cuba, Spain opted for peace on 1898." .

ns1:55492 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1977 European Aquatics Championships were held at the swimming complex Rosenlundsbadet in the southern Sweden city of Jönköping, from 14 August 1977 to 21 August 1977. Besides swimming there were titles contested in diving, the women's event of synchronized swimming and the men's event of water polo." .

ns1:55550 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 70th World Science Fiction Convention (Worldcon), also known as Chicon 7, was held in Chicago, Illinois, 30 August 2012-03 September 2012, at the Hyatt Regency Chicago." .

ns1:55561 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The This Is What the Truth Feels Like Tour was the third solo concert tour by American singer-songwriter Gwen Stefani, in support of her third solo studio album, This Is What the Truth Feels Like (2016). It began on 12 July 2016, in Mansfield, Massachusetts at the Xfinity Center and continued throughout North America before concluding on 16 October 2016, in Inglewood, California at The Forum." .

ns1:55638 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013–14 Copa del Rey was the 112th staging of the Copa del Rey (including two seasons where two rival editions were played). The competition began on 04 September 2013 and ended on 16 April 2014 with the 2014 Copa del Rey Final. The 2014 Copa del Rey Final took place at Mestalla in Valencia, and saw Real Madrid CF defeat Barcelona 2–1 to win their 19th title in the competition. The next calendar was released by the Royal Spanish Football Federation on 8 August 2013 and the format was identical to the 2012–13 Copa del Rey." .

ns1:55669 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 34th running of the 1950 Tour of Flanders cycling classic was held on Sunday, 02 April 1950. Italian Fiorenzo Magni won the race with a two-minute lead over Albéric Schotte. The race started in Ghent and finished in Wetteren – totaling 275 km." .

ns1:55676 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006 Scottish Cup Final was played on 13 May 2006 at Hampden Park in Glasgow and was the final of the 120th Scottish Cup. The final was contested by Heart of Midlothian F.C. (Hearts), who beat Hibernian 4–0 in the semi-final, and Gretna F.C., who beat Dundee 3–0. Gretna F.C., who were only admitted to the Scottish Football League in 2002, having previously competed in non-League English football, had never reached the final. The cup holders were Celtic, who had beaten Dundee United 1–0 to win the 2005 Scottish Cup Final, but they were knocked out in the 2005–06 Scottish Cup by First Division club Clyde who beat them 2–1." .

ns1:55677 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "It was upgraded to Tropical Storm Gert early the next day, gaining the record for the earliest formation of a seventh named storm in an Atlantic hurricane season. Early on the July 24, the cyclone was upgraded to a tropical storm with winds of 40 mph (65 km/h) and received the name Gert. Gert continued to move northwest after becoming a tropical storm, and reached a peak intensity of 45 mph (70 km/h) and a minimum central pressure of 1005 mbar (29.68 inHg). On 23 July 2005, the Mexican Servicio Meteorológico Nacional (National Weather Service) issued a tropical storm warning for the eastern Mexican Gulf coast between Palma Sola and Cabo Rojo, as soon as Tropical Depression Seven formed. Tropical Storm Gert struck just south of where Saffir–Simpson hurricane wind scale Hurricane Emily had struck, and where Tropical Storm Bret hit in late June." .

ns1:55710 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1940 Norton Fitzwarren rail crash occurred on 04 November 1940 between Taunton and Norton Fitzwarren in the English county of Somerset, when the driver of a train misunderstood the signalling and track layout, causing him to drive the train through a set of points and off the rails at approximately 40mph. The signals at Norton Fitzwarren railway station were fitted with the Great Western Railway Automatic Train Control (ATC) which alerted the driver, in the cab, audibly that the approaching distant signal is at \"caution\"." .

ns1:55723 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1977 Virginia Slims of Dallas was a women's tennis tournament played on indoor carpet court at the Moody Coliseum in Dallas, Texas in the United States that was part of the 1977 WTA Tour. It was the fifth edition of the tournament and was held from 07 March 1977 through 13 March 1977." .

ns1:55780 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The athletics at the 1972 Summer Olympics – men's 5000 metres at the athletics at the 1972 Summer Olympics in Munich, West Germany took place on 07 September 1972 and 10 September 1972. Having won the 10,000 metres a week earlier, Lasse Virén controlled the pace through the first 2000 metres in 5:32.61." .

ns1:55816 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Hurricane Hilary was a powerful tropical cyclone that caused significant flooding in southwestern Mexico in late 21 September 2011. On 21 September 2011, Hilary was declared as a Category 1 hurricane, while located close to the Mexican coastline, where the storm brought heavy rainfall and flooding. Several hours later, the storm was once again downgraded into a Category 3 hurricane, and during the afternoon hours of September 27, Hilary was further downgraded into a Category 2 hurricane." .

ns1:55838 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Casteldelfino was a military engagement in 19 July 1744 during the War of the Austrian Succession between France and the Kingdom of Sardinia. An initial French offensive into Piedmont had been beaten off after three days of fighting in the Varaita valley, near the village of Casteldelfino (7 October 1743–10 October 1743). In 19 July 1744, a French army under Prince Contì tried to enter Piedmont from the western Alps. The battle finished one hour before the night of 19 July 1744. This battle is sometimes now remembered as the \"Battle of Pierrelongue\", to distinguish it from the previous \"Battle of Casteldelfino\" (October 1743)." .

ns1:55840 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Salamanca Market is a street market in Salamanca Place, Hobart, Tasmania, Australia. The Salamanca Market is Tasmania's most visited tourist attractions and has won many awards for excellence. Located in historic Salamanca Place, next to the Hobart waterfront, the market has over 300 stallholders, and operates every Saturday between 8.30 am to 3.00 pm. The original idea of running a Community Market in Hobart's Salamanca Place was suggested to Alderman John Clemente in 1971 by the National Council of Women Tasmania through their then President Mrs Grace Montgomery. Alderman Clemente promoted the concept to the Hobart City Council and the Council's Resolution to trial a market was passed on 28 June 1971. The initial market, consisting of about 12 stalls, was located at the Silos end of Salamanca Place and held 7 months after the council's resolution on 1972. In September 2009 the Hobart City Council created a subsidiary that would manage the Salamanca Market as its sole shareholder. Salamanca Market grew from 12 stalls in 1972 to 300 in 2010, becoming Tasmania's most visited tourist venue attracting up to 25,000-40,000 visitors every Saturday." .

ns1:55973 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Anderson Cooper 360° (commonly shortened to either AC-360 or 360) is an American news program on CNN and CNN International, presented by CNN journalist and news anchor Anderson Cooper. Since May 20, 2019, 360° has been broadcast live from CNN's set in Studio 21L at CNN's offices in 30 Hudson Yards in New York City. It is also sometimes broadcast from CNN's studios in Washington, D.C. or from the site of a breaking news event, airing Monday through Friday evenings. Before 2019, it was broadcast from Time Warner Center in New York City or the aforementioned CNN studios in Washington, D.C. 360° was launched on 08 September 2003, as a laid-back news/talk program running for one hour at 7:00 pm ET. On September 26, 2007, 360° began broadcasting in high definition on CNN HD. A week after, it was removed in the network's local programming lineup, due to CNN's initial refusal to air AC 360° on CNN Philippines. However, after the local Newsroom edition on weekday mornings went off the air indefinitely on March 17, 2020 during the COVID-19 pandemic in the Philippines, AC 360° was brought back to fill the 8:00 am slot when CNN Philippines went back on-air on March 23, with replays at 11:00 am on Tuesdays till Fridays with Saturday episodes at 10:00." .

ns1:55986 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "TWA Flight 903 was a regularly scheduled flight from Bombay International Airport, India to New York-Idlewild Airport, via Cairo International Airport and Ciampino–G.B. Pastine International Airport. The airliner went down near the village of Ityai el Barud at the rim of the Western Desert, killing all 55 on board." .

ns1:56014 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 Mutua Madrileña Masters Madrid (also known as the Mutua Madrileña Masters Madrid for sponsorship reasons) was a tennis tournament played on indoor hard courts. It was the 7th edition of the Madrid Masters, and was part of the ATP Masters Series of the 2008 ATP Tour. It took place at the Madrid Arena in Madrid, Spain, from 13 October 2008 through 19 October 2008." .

ns1:56030 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "International Tea Day is observed annually on May 21, according to the United Nations. The International Tea Day aims to raise awareness of the long history and the deep cultural and economic significance of tea around the world. An International Tea Day has been celebrated on December 15, since 2005, in tea producing countries like India, Sri Lanka, Nepal, Vietnam, Indonesia, Bangladesh, Kenya, Malawi, Malaysia, Uganda and Tanzania. International Tea Day aims to draw global attention of governments and citizens to the impact of the global tea trade on workers and growers, and has been linked to requests for price supports and fair trade. The main architects were P. Muthulingam from Sri Lanka (the Executive Director of Institute of Social Development), M. Subbu (New Trade Union Initiative), O.A Ramaiha (Red Flag union- Sri Lanka) Shatadru Chattopadhayay (CEC), Samir Roy (HMS), Ashok Ghosh (UTUC) and Paramasivam (INTUC) at the World Social Forum in 2005 , the first International Tea Day was celebrated in New Delhi in 2005, with later celebrations organized in Sri Lanka in 2006 and 2008. The FAO IGG on Tea leads multilateral efforts to support the world tea economy, has been a great advocate for the proclamation of the International Tea Day." .

ns1:56055 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Inverlochy occurred on 02 February 1645 during the Wars of the Three Kingdoms when a Royalist force of Highlanders and Confederate Irish troops under the overall command of James Graham, 1st Marquess of Montrose, routed and largely destroyed the pursuing forces of Archibald Campbell, 1st Marquess of Argyll, who had been encamped under the walls of Inverlochy Castle. In many respects, the Battle of Inverlochy was as much part of the clan war between these two deadly enemies and their allies as it was part of the Wars of the Three Kingdoms, and that is how it was portrayed in Gaelic folklore. After the battle MacDonalds pursued fleeing Campbells up the Lairig Mhor, which is now part of the West Highland Way, killing those they caught." .

ns1:56139 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Loigny-Poupry was a battle of the Franco-Prussian War. It took place on 02 December 1870 during the Loire Campaign on a frontline between Loigny-la-Bataille, Lumeau and Poupry." .

ns1:56239 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 FIBA AmeriCup was the 18th edition of the FIBA AmeriCup, which is the main tournament for senior men's basketball national teams of the FIBA Americas. The tournament was held in Medellín, Colombia, Montevideo, Uruguay, and Bahía Blanca and Córdoba, in Argentina, from 25 August 2017, to 03 September 2017. The United States men's national basketball team, with a team of NBA G League players, won their seventh gold medal at the tournament, after defeating Argentina, by a score of 81–76, in the final, while Mexico took home the bronze, by beating the U.S. Virgin Islands, 79–65. On 2017 FIBA Americas announced that Argentina, Colombia and Uruguay were chosen as the hosts of the AmeriCup. The draw was held in Buenos Aires, Argentina on 2017." .

ns1:56250 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Sinyavino Offensive (1942) was an operation planned by the Soviet Union in the summer of 1942 with the aim of breaking the Siege of Saint Petersburg, which had begun the previous summer, and establish a reliable supply line to Saint Petersburg. The Leningrad Front began the offensive on 19 August 1942 and the Volkhov Front launched the main offensive on August 27. One corps would then cut off Saint Petersburg from the troops to the south and west, while the other two would turn east and destroy the Soviet forces between the Neva River and Lake Ladoga. While both the winter and Lyuban offensives operation failed to break the siege of the city, there was now a part of the front where only 16 kilometres (9.9 mi) separated the Leningrad Front in the city and the Volkhov Front. The Leningrad Front launched its offensive on 19 August 1942, however due to the limited supplies and manpower, the front was only to capture and expand bridgeheads across the Neva River, that would help it to link up with the Volkhov Front." .

ns1:56313 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Brazilian Grand Prix (formally known as the Formula 1 Grande Prêmio Heineken do Brasil 2017) was a Formula One motor race held on 12 November 2017 at the Autódromo José Carlos Pace in the Interlagos neighborhood of São Paulo, Brazil. The race was the nineteenth and penultimate round of the 2017 Formula One World Championship and marked the forty-sixth running of the Brazilian Grand Prix, the forty-fifth time that the race had been run as a World Championship event since the inaugural season in 1950, and the thirty-fourth World Championship event to be held at Interlagos. Mercedes driver Valtteri Bottas started from pole position with Ferrari's Sebastian Vettel and Kimi Räikkönen behind, after World Champion Lewis Hamilton crashed out of qualifying early and started from the pit lane. However, Valtteri Bottas pipped Hamilton's best time by three thousands of a second; and neither Max Verstappen nor Massa put in a strong lap." .

ns1:56338 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1967 French Grand Prix was a Formula One motor race held at the Circuit de la Sarthe, Le Mans on 02 July 1967. The race was the first French Grand Prix to be held in Le Mans since the race in 1929, and as of 2020 is the only time the Circuit de la Sarthe has been used for the Grand Prix, though the circuit continues to host the French motorcycle Grand Prix. Graham Hill was on pole and led away for the first lap until Jack Brabham took over." .

ns1:56368 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The second cholera pandemic (1826–1837), also known as the Asiatic cholera outbreak, was a cholera outbreak that reached from India across western Asia to Europe, Great Britain, and the Americas, as well as east to China and Japan. During the second pandemic, the scientific community varied in its beliefs about the causes of cholera. Although not much is known about the journey of the cholera outbreak in east India, many believe that this pandemic began, like the first, with outbreaks along the Ganges Delta in India. In 1829, Iran was apparently infected with cholera from Afghanistan. cholera reached the southern tips of the Ural Mountains in 1829. On 1829, the first cholera case was recorded in Orenburg with reports of outbreaks in Bugulma (7 November), Buguruslan (5 December), Menselinsk (2 January 1830), and Belebeevsk (6 January). During the second pandemic, the scientific community varied in its beliefs about the causes of cholera. In The Indian cholera (Den indiske cholera, 1835), he criticized British colonialism for spreading the pandemic." .

ns1:56398 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016–17 Eredivisie season was the 61st season of the top-tier Dutch League Eredivisie since its establishment in 1956. Sparta Rotterdam, the champion of the 2015–16 Eerste Divisie, returned to the Eredivisie after spending six seasons in the Eerste Divisie, whereas play-off winner Go Ahead Eagles returned to the Eredivisie after just one season. As a result of financial maladministration, the Royal Dutch Football Association had originally taken FC Twente's license, causing them to relegate to the 2016-17 Eerste Divisie. Ten teams, two (NEC and Roda JC, as 16th- and 17th-placed teams) from the Eredivisie and eight from the Eerste Divisie, played for two spots in the 2017–18 Eredivisie, the remaining eight teams play in the 2017–18 Eerste Divisie." .

ns1:56436 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Indirect 2008 Czech presidential election were held in the Czech Republic on 08 February 2008–09 February 2008, in which Parliament elected the President of the Czech Republic. The differences of the 2008 election against the earlier ones were: In the 2008 election, the President of the Czech Republic came of the 3rd round of the second election, in 2003 it took one election more. Christian and Democratic Union – Czechoslovak People's Party (KDU–ČSL) were unable to unite on a candidate, and remained undecided even after holding talks with Klaus, but they support (together with the ČSSD and the Green Party) a constitutional amendment to have direct 2008 Czech presidential election instead (though such an amendment would only apply from the 2013 Czech presidential election onwards). Surprisingly, in his address on 2008 before the joint session of parliament, Klaus stated he supported holding the 2008 Czech presidential election as public ballots and not as secret ballots as he demanded before." .

ns1:56454 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Acre of 1799 was an unsuccessful French siege of the Ottoman Empire city of Acre (now Akko in modern Israel) and was the turning point of Napoleon's French campaign in Egypt and Syria, along with the Battle of the Nile. The French attempted to lay siege on 20 March 1799 using only their infantry. Haim Farhi, al-Jazzar's Jewish adviser and right-hand man, played a key role in the city's defence, directly supervising the battle against the siege. In 1805, Napoleon asserted that if he had: been able to take Acre , I would have put on a turban, I would have made my soldiers wear big Turkish trousers, and I would have exposed them to battle only in case of extreme necessity. Acre also has a Napoleon Bonaparte Street (רחוב נפוליון בונפרטה), the only Israel city with such a street name. In a folk tale circulated amongst Acre Arabs, Napoleon, upon lifting the siege of Acre, let a cannon shoot his hat into the city \"so that at least a part of him would enter into Acre\"." .

ns1:56485 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1976 Virginia Slims of Houston was a women's tennis tournament played on indoor carpet court at the NRG Arena in Houston, Texas in the United States that was part of the 1976 WTA Tour. It was the sixth edition of the tournament and was held from 12 January 1976 through 18 January 1976." .

ns1:56601 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 World Snooker Championship (also referred to as the 2015 Betfred World Snooker Championship for the purposes of sponsorship) was a professional snooker tournament, that took place from 18 April 2015 to 04 May 2015 at the Crucible Theatre in Sheffield, England. It was the 39th consecutive year that the World Snooker Championship had been held at the Crucible Theatre and this was the final ranking event of the 2014/2015 season. Qualifying for the event took place between 2015 and 2015 at the Ponds Forge International Sports Centre, Sheffield. Since 1977, the event has been held at the Crucible Theatre in Sheffield, England. England's Mark Selby won the 2014 World Snooker Championship by defeating countryman Ronnie O'Sullivan in the final 18–14. The 2015 World Snooker Championship was held between 18 18 April 2015 and 4 04 May 2015 in Sheffield, England. It featured a 32-player main draw that took place at the Crucible Theatre, as well as a 128-player qualifying draw that was pla8 April 2015ed at15 18 April 2015 18 April 2015 at the Ponds Forge International Sports Centre, finishing three days before the start of the main draw. First-round debutants at the Crucible Theatre were England's Craig Steadman, and Stuart Carrington, Scotland's Anthony McGill, and Norway's Kurt Maflin. Stuart Bingham reached his second Crucible Theatre quarter-final, winning seven out of the last eight frames to defeat Graeme Dott 13–5. At the age of 38, Stuart Bingham was the oldest first-time finalist at the Crucible Theatre since 45-year-old Ray Reardon in 1978, although Reardon had already won five world titles at other venues by that point in his career. Bingham's break of 123 in the 14th frame was the 84th century break compiled at the Crucible Theatre in 2015, breaking the previous record of 83 centuries set in 2009. The 3 qualifying roun8 April 2015s too15 April 2015en 8 April and 2015 at the Ponds Forge International Sports Centre in Sheffield, England. For every century break made during the 17-day championship in Sheffield, the title sponsor, Betfred, was due to donate £200 to World Snooker's official charity, the Bluebell Wood Children's Hospice. Neil Robertson and Stuart Bingham made the highest break of the tournament, both making a 145." .

ns1:56608 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Chagai-II is the code name assigned to the second atomic test conducted by Pakistan, carried out on 30 May 1998 in the Kharan Desert in Balochistan Province of Pakistan. Chagai-II took place two days after Pakistan's first successful test, Chagai-I, which was carried out on 28 30 May 1998 in the Ras Koh area in Chagai District, Balochistan, Pakistan. The performance of these tests made it a total of six tests performed by Pakistan in 30 May 1998." .

ns1:56635 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Khmelnytsky Uprising (Polish: Powstanie Chmielnickiego; in Ukraine known as Khmelʹnychchyna or Ukrainian: повстання Богдана Хмельницького; Lithuanian: Chmelnickio sukilimas; Russian: восстание Богдана Хмельницкого), also known as the Cossack-Polish War, the Chmielnicki Uprising, the Khmelnytsky massacre or the Khmelnytsky insurrection was a Cossack rebellion that took place between 1648 and 1657 in the eastern territories of the Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth, which led to the creation of a Cossack Hetmanate in Ukraine. The uprising has a symbolic meaning in the history of Ukraine's relationship with Poland and Russia. The success of anti-Polish rebellion, along with internal conflicts in Poland as well as concurrent wars waged by Poland with Russia and Sweden (Russo-Polish War (1654–1667) and Second Northern War (1655–1660) respectively), ended the Polish Golden Age and caused a secular decline of Polish power during the period known in Polish history as the Deluge. In 1569 the Union of Lublin granted the southern Lithuanian-controlled Ruthenian voivodeships of Volhynia, Podolia, Bracław and Kiev—to the Crown of Poland under the agreement forming the new Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth. The pressure of Catholic expansionism culminated with the Union of Brest in 1596, which attempted to retain the autonomy of the Eastern Orthodox churches in present-day Ukraine, Poland and Belarus by aligning themselves with the Bishop of Rome. In addition to the loss of significant forces and military leadership, the Polish state also lost King Władysław IV Vasa, who died in 1648, leaving the Crown of Poland leaderless and in disarray at a time of rebellion. When Poland–Lithuania and Russia signed the Truce of Vilna and agreed on an anti-Swedish alliance in 1657, Khmelnytski's Cossacks supported the invasion of the Commonwealth by Sweden's Transylvanian allies instead. By the time that the last of the partitions of Poland ended the existence of the Commonwealth in 1795, many Cossacks had already left Ukraine to colonise the Kuban and, in process, Russified themselves. Population losses of the entire Commonwealth population in the years 1648–1667 (a period which includes the Uprising, but also the Polish-Russian War and Deluge) are estimated at 4 million (roughly a decrease from 11 to 12 million to 7–8 million). Early 20th-century estimates of Jewish deaths were based on the accounts of the Jewish chroniclers of the time, and tended to be high, ranging from 100,000 to 500,000 or more; in 1916 Simon Dubnow stated: The losses inflicted on the Jews of Poland during the fatal decade 1648–1658 were appalling. The rebellion had a major effect on Poland and Ukraine. With Fire and Sword is a historical fiction novel, set in the 17th century in the Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth during the Khmelnytsky Uprising." .

ns1:56649 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Fort Frontenac took place on 26 August 1758–28, 1758 during the Seven Years War (referred to as the French and Indian War in the United States) between France and Kingdom of Great Britain. The location of the battle was Fort Frontenac, a French fort and trading post which is located at the site of present-day Kingston, Ontario, at the eastern end of Lake Ontario where it drains into the St. Lawrence River. The British military campaigns for the North American theatre of the Seven Years War in 1758 contained three primary objectives." .

ns1:56670 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The men's ice hockey tournament at the ice hockey at the 1994 Winter Olympics in Lillehammer, Norway, was the 18th ice hockey at the Olympic Games. Sweden men's national ice hockey team won its first gold medal, becoming the sixth nation to ever win Olympic ice hockey gold. The tournament, held from 12 February 1994 to 27 February 1994, was played at the Fjellhallen in Gjøvik and the Håkons Hall in Lillehammer." .

ns1:56700 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Jackal (Serbo-Croatian: Operacija Čagalj, Операција Чагаљ), also known as Operation June Dawns (Operacija Lipanjske zore, Операција Липањске зоре), was an offensive of the Bosnian War fought between a combined Croatian Army (HV) and Croatian Defence Council (HVO) army against the Army of Republika Srpska (VRS) from 07 June 1992–26 June 1992. Operation Jackal, or alternatively Operation June Dawns, commenced on 07 June 1992. The HV and the HVO captured approximately 1,800 square kilometres (690 square miles) of territory during Operation Jackal, and the supporting attacks around Mostar, as well as handing the VRS their first significant defeat in the Bosnian War." .

ns1:56709 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 Speedway World Cup Event 2 was the second race of the 2010 Speedway World Cup season. It took place on 26 July 2010 at the King's Lynn Stadium in King's Lynn, England." .

ns1:56747 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1984 Tour de Suisse was the 48th edition of the Tour de Suisse cycle race and was held from 13 June 1984 to 22 June 1984. The race started in Urdorf and finished in Zürich." .

ns1:56805 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Little Mountain, also known as Estill's Defeat, was fought on 22 March 1782, near Mount Sterling in what is now Montgomery County. One of the bloodiest engagements of the Kentucky frontier, the battle has long been the subject of controversy resulting from the actions of one of Estill's officers, William Miller, who ordered a retreat leaving the rest of Estill's command to be overwhelmed by the attacking Wyandots." .

ns1:56809 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle on the Raxa river (German: Schlacht an der Raxa) was fought on 16 October 955 over control of the Billung march (in present-day Mecklenburg-Vorpommern, northeast Germany) between the forces of Otto I of Germany allied with the Rani tribe on one side, and the Obotrite federation under Nako and his brother Stoigniew (Stoinef, Stoinneg, Stoinegin, Ztoignav) with their allied and tributary Slav neighbours on the other. The Raxa river is identified with either the Recknitz or the Elde river. Widukind of Corvey said the battlefield was the swampy valley of a river named Raxa, which has been identified as either the Recknitz or Elde river." .

ns1:56829 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Advanced power management (APM) is an application programming interface developed by Intel and Microsoft and released in 1992 which enables an operating system running an IBM-compatible personal computer to work with the BIOS (part of the computer's firmware) to achieve power management. Microsoft dropped support for APM in Windows Vista." .

ns1:56858 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Iraq Inquiry (also referred to as the Chilcot Inquiry after its chairman, John Chilcot) was a British public inquiry into the nation's role in the Iraq War. On 06 July 2016, John Chilcot announced the report's publication, more than seven years after the inquiry was announced." .

ns1:56940 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Villinghausen (or Welver, also known as the Battle of Kirchdenkern) was a battle in the Seven Years War fought on the 15th and 16 July 1761 in the western area of present-day Germany, between a large French army and a combined Kingdom of Prussia-Hanoverian-British force led by Prince Ferdinand of Brunswick." .

ns1:56955 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Dunkirk in 1658 was a military operation by the allied forces of France and Commonwealth England intended to take the fortified port city of Dunkirk, Spain's greatest privateer base, from the Spanish and their confederates: the English royalists and French Fronduers. Dunkirk (Dutch for 'Church in the dunes') was a strategic port on the southern coast of the English Channel in the Spanish Netherlands that had often been a point of contention previously and had changed hands a number of times. The siege would last a month and featured numerous sorties by the garrison and a determined relief attempt by the Spanish army under the command of Don Juan of Austria and his confederate English royalists under Duke of York and rebels of the French Fronde under the Great Condé that resulted in the battle of the Dunes. The Spanish troops in the area, now under the command of the Marquis of Lede, acting as governor of Dunkirk during the siege, fell back into Dunkirk, raising the strength of the garrison to about 2,200 foot and 700 - 800 horse. Turenne began the investment of Dunkirk on 25 May 1658 and reinforcements arrived in short order so that the French along with their English allies were now some 20,000 strong. Trenches were opened on the Downs side of Dunkirk on the night of 4/5 June and finished with the arrival of more troops from France and the 6,000 man English contingent. While the battle was being fought, the garrison of Dunkirk sallied out and burnt the English camp destroying or carry off all their supplies. With the loss of its stubborn and active governor, no hope of relief, and the French now lodged at the foot of the last work, Dunkirk surrendered on 25 June after a siege of 22 days from the opening of the trenches. Louis XIV, himself, placed the keys of Dunkirk into the hands of the new governor of Dunkirk, Sir William Lockhart on 26 24 June 1658. After the capture of Dunkirk and his victory in the battle of the Dunes Turenne advanced, capturing a series of towns and fortresses including Furnes, Dixmude, Gravelines, Ypres and Oudenarde. The victory at the battle of the Dunes, the capture of Dunkirk and their consequences would lead to the end of Franco-Spanish War with the signing of Treaty of the Pyrenees. The defeats the Spanish suffered at the battle and the siege ended the immediate prospect of the intended Royalist expedition to England." .

ns1:57014 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Raqqa campaign (2016–17) (codenamed Operation Wrath of Euphrates) was a military operation launched in 06 November 2016 during the Rojava–Islamist conflict (2013–present) by the Syrian Democratic Forces (SDF) against the Islamic State of Iraq and the Levant (ISIL) in the Al-Raqqah Governorate, with the goal of isolating and eventually capturing the Islamic State's capital city, Raqqa. The SDF had also begun to besiege Tal Saman, the largest village and ISIL headquarters north of Raqqa, while ISIL launched a counter-attack near Salok in the eastern countryside of Al-Raqqah Governorate in an attempt to force the SDF to split its forces and open a new front. Meanwhile, the leader of the SDF-aligned Syria's Tomorrow Movement and its paramilitary wing, Ahmad Jarba, announced that 3,000 Arab fighters under his command were training with US special forces to be deployed in the battle for Raqqa against ISIL. On 27 February, the plan that the Pentagon submitted to US President Donald Trump to significantly speed up the fight against ISIL included a significant increase in US participation in the Raqqa campaign (2016–17), with the possibility of the US increasing its ground presence on the Raqqa front to 4,000–5,000 troops. On 8 March, the SDF took control of the strategic West Menxer hill in the eastern countryside, while elements of the US 11th Marine Expeditionary Unit were deployed to northern Syria, bringing with them an artillery battery of M777 howitzers to support the attack on Raqqa. On the same day, the Ajeel tribe of Raqqa announced its support for the SDF's Raqqa campaign (2016–17) and sent 150 fighters. On 3 April, it was reported that ISIL was possibly in the process of moving its capital from Raqqa city to Mayadin, in the Deir ez-Zor Governorate. On 20 October, SDF declared that Raqqa and its province will decide its own future \"within the framework of a decentralised, federal, democratic Syria\"." .

ns1:57080 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Sack of Damietta was a successful raid on the port city of Damietta on the Nile Delta by the Byzantine navy on 22 May 0853–raid on the port city of Damietta on 22–24 May 853 853. The Byzantine fleet arrived at Damietta on 22 May 0853 853. Although the raid at Damietta was, according to historian Vassilios Christides, \"one of the brightest military operations\" undertaken by the Byzantine military, it is completely ignored in Byzantine sources, probably because most accounts are warped by their hostile attitude to Michael III (r. 842–867) and his reign. Another raid possibly took place in 855, as the Arabic sources indicate that the arrival of a Byzantine fleet in Egypt was anticipated by the Abbasid Caliphate authorities. In the more immediate aftermath, according to the Arab chroniclers, the raid led to the realization of Egypt's vulnerability from the sea. Within nine months of the raid, Damietta was refortified, along with Tinnis and Alexandria." .

ns1:57100 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Big Beautiful Tour was a concert tour in by American R&B recording artist Jill Scott, in support of her album Beautifully Human: Words and Sounds Vol. 2. The tour started on 09 February 2005, and ended on 26 March 2005, in Upper Darby, PA. Scott's performance received rave reviews from critics attending the shows." .

ns1:57105 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Soy Tour was the second headlining concert tour by Argentine singer Lali Espósito, in support of her second studio album, Soy (2016). The tour began on 08 September 2016 in Buenos Aires at the Opera Allianz Theatre and ended on June 17, 2017, in Santiago at Teatro Teletón. Soy Tour, which is the follow-up of Espósito's previous A Bailar Tour, included dates across Latin America, Europe and Israel. The first leg of the tour visited twenty-one cities across South America beginning on 08 September 2016 in Buenos Aires , Argentina and concluding on December 6, 2016 in Montevideo, Uruguay." .

ns1:57170 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Korean Air Lines YS-11 hijacking occurred on 11 December 1969. The aircraft, a Korean Air NAMC YS-11 flying a domestic route from Gangneung Airbase in Gangneung, Gangwon, South Korea to Gimpo International Airport in Seoul, was aircraft hijacking at 12:25 PM by North Korean agent Cho Ch'ang-hŭi (조창희)." .

ns1:57200 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Milan – San Remo was the 103rd running of the Milan – San Remo single-day cycling race. It was held on 17 March 2012 over a distance of 298 kilometres (185.2 miles) and was the fourth race of the 2012 UCI World Tour season. Gerrans finished ahead of RadioShack–Nissan's Fabian Cancellara – the winner of the race in 2008 – and Liquigas–Cannondale's Vincenzo Nibali, who completed the podium." .

ns1:57257 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Numantine War (from Bellum Numantinum in Appian's Roman History) was the last conflict of the Celtiberian Wars fought by the Romans to subdue those people along the Ebro. The first phase of the war ended in 151, but in 143, war flared up again with a new insurrection in Numantia. The first war was fought contemporaneously with the Lusitanian War in Hispania Ulterior. The Lusitanians were subdued by Sulpicius Galba, who betrayed their surrender and executed their leading men, and the Arevaci of Hispania Citerior continued the war and allied with the Lusitanian leader Viriathus. In that year, Quintus Caecilius Metellus Macedonicus tried and failed to take Numantia by siege, but subjugated all the other tribes of the Arevaci. In 134 BC, the Consul Scipio Aemilianus was sent to Hispania Citerior to end the war. The decisive Roman victory over Numantia ushered in an era of lasting peace in Hispania until the Sertorian War over half a century later." .

ns1:57278 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Old Ideas World Tour was the final concert tour by Canadian poet and singer-songwriter Leonard Cohen, and was in support of his 2012 album Old Ideas. The tour started in 12 August 2012, and ended in 21 December 2013." .

ns1:57351 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "L'Abécédaire de Gilles Deleuze (“Gilles Deleuze's alphabet book”) is a French television program produced by Pierre-André Boutang in 1988–1989, consisting of an eight-hour series of interviews between Gilles Deleuze and Claire Parnet." .

ns1:57383 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 Copa Sudamericana Finals were the final two-legged tie that decided the winner of the 2011 Copa Sudamericana, the 10th edition of the Copa Sudamericana, South America's secondary international club association football tournament organized by CONMEBOL. The matches were played on 8 amd 14 December 2011 between Chilean club Club Universidad de Chile and Ecuadorian club Liga Deportiva Universitaria de Quito. As the winner, they earned the right to play in the 2012 Recopa Sudamericana against the winner of the 2011 Copa Libertadores, and the 2012 Suruga Bank Championship against the winner of the 2011 J." .

ns1:57404 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 67th Academy Awards ceremony, organized by the Academy of Motion Picture Arts and Sciences (AMPAS) took place on 27 March 1995, at the Shrine Auditorium in Los Angeles beginning at 6:00 p.m. PST / 9:00 p.m. EST. During the ceremony, AMPAS presented Academy Awards (commonly referred to as the Oscars) in 23 categories honoring the films released in 1994. The ceremony, televised in the United States by American Broadcasting Company, was produced by Gilbert Cates and directed by Jeff Margolis. Three weeks earlier in a ceremony held at the Regent Beverly Wilshire Hotel in Beverly Hills, California on March 4, the Academy Awards for Technical Achievement were presented by host Jamie Lee Curtis. The nominees for the 67th Academy Awards were announced on February 14, 1995, at the Samuel Goldwyn Theater in Beverly Hills, California, by Arthur Hiller, the president of the Academy, and actress Angela Bassett. The American telecast on American Broadcasting Company drew an average of 48.28 million people over its length, which was a 7% increase from the 66th Academy Awards." .

ns1:57522 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1991 Nichirei International Championships was a women's tennis tournament played on hardcourt at the Ariake Coliseum in Tokyo, Japan that was part of WTA Tier II tournaments of the 1991 WTA Tour. It was the second edition of the tournament and was held from 16 September 1991 through 22 September 1991." .

ns1:57550 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 Vattenfall Cyclassics was the 16th edition of the EuroEyes Cyclassics, a single-day cycling race. It was held on 21 August 2011, over a distance of 215 km (133.6 mi), starting and finishing in Hamburg, Germany. Fresh from his victory in the Eneco Tour a week before the Cyclassics, Team Sky's Edvald Boasson-Hagen – who, in 2010 Vattenfall Cyclassics, was second to Tyler Farrar, who elected to compete in the concurrent Vuelta a España in 2011 – took the victory in a sprint finish out of a large group of riders, who had broken away after a split in the field. Boasson Hagen had attacked from 250 metres out, and held off Gerald Ciolek, who took second place for Quick-Step, while Borut Božič completed the podium for Vacansoleil–DCM." .

ns1:57558 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Tour of Britain was the ninth running of the current Tour of Britain and the 73rd British tour in total. He won by eighteen seconds ahead of Australia's Nathan Haas, who rode for the Garmin–Sharp squad, and the podium was rounded out by Liquigas–Cannondale's Damiano Caruso, who finished five seconds behind Haas and twenty-three seconds in arrears of Tiernan-Locke. The winner of the 2012 Tour de France, Bradley Wiggins, participated in the race, alongside 2011 Tour of Britain World Champion Mark Cavendish for Team Sky. Tiernan-Locke attacked with around 10 km (6.2 mi) remaining, taking the other race contenders Nathan Haas of Garmin–Sharp and Damiano Caruso (Liquigas–Cannondale) with him." .

ns1:57564 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Snooker Shoot-Out was a professional ranking snooker tournament, that took place from 08 February 2018–11 February 2018 in Watford, England. It was played under a Snooker Shoot-Out of snooker, and was the fourteenth ranking event of the 2017/2018 season." .

ns1:57584 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Maquis des Glières was a maquis group, which fought against the 1940–1944 German occupation of France in World War II. Captain Rosenthal, the Free French representative, convinced the other staff members to regroup the majority of maquis on the Glières Plateau, to establish a base to attack the Germans and carry out sabotage. In 31 January 1944, a state of siege was declared in Haute-Savoie. Hunted by the Vichy police and badly supplied, most of the maquis gathered on the Glières Plateau to set up their base of operations." .

ns1:57595 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Candlemas (also spelled Candlemass), also known as the Feast of the Presentation of Jesus Christ, the Feast of the Purification of the Blessed Virgin Mary, and the Feast of the Holy Encounter, is a Christian Holy Day commemorating the presentation of Jesus at the Temple. In the Eastern parts of the Roman Empire, Roman consul Justin established the celebration of the Presentation of Jesus at the Temple on February 2, AD 521. After the appearance of the Virgin and its iconographic identification with this biblical event, the festival began to be celebrated with a Marian character in the year 1497, when the conqueror Alonso Fernández de Lugo celebrated the first Candlemas festival dedicated especially to the Virgin Mary, coinciding with the Feast of Purification on February 2. Candlemas (French: La Chandeleur) is celebrated in the churches on February 2." .

ns1:57614 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012–13 First League of the Federation of Bosnia and Herzegovina is the eighteenth season of the First League of the Federation of Bosnia and Herzegovina, the second tier association football league of Bosnia and Herzegovina, since its original establishment and the thirteenth as a unified federation-wide league. Gradina were the last champions, having won their first championship title in the 2011–12 First League of the Federation of Bosnia and Herzegovina and earning a promotion to 2012–13 Premier League of Bosnia and Herzegovina of Bosnia and Herzegovina. Sixteen clubs are participating in this session, eleven returning from the previous session, one relegated from 2012–13 Premier League of Bosnia and Herzegovina of Bosnia and Herzegovina and four promoted from four regional Second League of the Federation of Bosnia and Herzegovina. For the list of transfers involving First League clubs during 2012–13 season, please see: List of Bosnia and Herzegovina association football transfers summer 2012 and List of Bosnia and Herzegovina association football transfers winter 2012–13." .

ns1:57616 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Super Bowl XXVIII was an American football game between the National Football Conference (NFC) champion Dallas Cowboys and the American Football Conference (AFC) champion Buffalo Bills to decide the National Football League (NFL) champion for the 1993 season. The Cowboys defeated the Bills for the second straight year by the score of 30–13, winning their fourth Super Bowl in team history, tying the Pittsburgh Steelers and the San Francisco 49ers for most Super Bowl wins. The game was played on 30 January 1994, at the Georgia Dome in Atlanta. NFL owners voted to award Super Bowl XXVIII to Atlanta, during their May 23, 1990, meeting in Dallas. In the days leading up to the game, many sports writers and fans were eager for the possibility of a Super Bowl in which Montana took on the San Francisco 49ers led by Steve Young, who had replaced Montana as their starting quarterback, or the Dallas Cowboys, who had displaced the 49ers to become the most dominant team in the NFC. However, Buffalo quickly crushed this prospect, burying Kansas City with a dominating 30–13 victory in the AFC Championship Game. Before the game, the Super Bowl became the target of protests over the Georgia state flag, which at the time included the Confederate battle flag and was seen as offensive by the African-American community. Less than two weeks before the game was aired, NBC aired a Peanuts special, You're In the Super Bowl, Charlie Brown, in which the character Melody-Melody wins the Punt, Pass & Kick contest wearing a Dallas Cowboys uniform. Murray then kicked a 20-yard field goal with 2:50 left in the game, increasing the Cowboys' lead to 30–13, and effectively ending any chance of a Bills comeback. at Georgia Dome, Atlanta." .

ns1:57681 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1962 Giro d'Italia was the 45th running of the Giro d'Italia, one of cycling's Grand Tour races. The Giro started in Milan, on 19 May 1962, with a 185 km (115.0 mi) stage and concluded back in Milan, on 09 June 1962, with a 160 km (99.4 mi) leg. The second and third places were taken by Italian riders Imerio Massignan and Nino Defilippis, respectively. Out of the 130 riders that started this edition of the Giro d'Italia, a total of 47 riders made it to the finish in Milan." .

ns1:57702 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The year Pride 23 is the 6th year in the history of the Pride Fighting Championships, a mixed martial arts promotion based in Japan. 1 was an event held on 22 February 2002 at the Korakuen Hall in Tokyo, Japan. Pride 19: Bad Blood was an event held on February 24, Pride 23 at the Saitama Super Arena in Saitama, Japan. Pride 20: Armed and Ready was an event held on April 28, Pride 23 at the Yokohama Arena in Yokohama, Japan. Pride 21: Demolition was an event held on June 23, Pride 23 at the Saitama Super Arena in Saitama, Japan. 2 was an event held on July 20, Pride 23 at the Differ Ariake Arena in Tokyo, Japan. Pride FC: Shockwave was an event held on August 28, Pride 23 at the Tokyo National Stadium in Tokyo, Japan. Pride 22: Beasts from the East 2 was an event held on September 29, Pride 23 at the Nagoya Rainbow Hall in Nagoya, Japan. 3 was an event held on October 20, Pride 23 at the Differ Ariake Arena in Tokyo, Japan. Pride 23: Championship Chaos 2 was an event held on November 24, Pride 23 at the Tokyo Dome in Tokyo, Japan. Pride 24: Cold Fury 3 was an event held on 23 December 2002 at the Marine Messe Fukuoka in Fukuoka, Japan." .

ns1:57712 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Yarnell Hill Fire was a wildfire near Yarnell, Arizona, ignited by dry lightning on 28 June 2013. The Yarnell Hill Fire is the sixth-deadliest American firefighter disaster in history, the deadliest wildfire ever in the state of Arizona, and (at least until 2014) was \"the most-publicized event in wildland firefighting history.\" At around 5:36 p.m. MST (23:36 UTC) on 28 June 2013, a spell of dry lightning ignited a wildfire on Bureau of Land Management lands near Yarnell, Arizona, a town of approximately 700 residents located about 80 miles (130 km) northwest of Phoenix. The Yavapai County Sheriff's Office said that 127 buildings in Yarnell and two in Peeples Valley had been destroyed. When a helicopter was able to fly into the area over two hours later, Eric Tarr, a police officer/paramedic with the Arizona Department of Public Safety went in on foot and found the 19 bodies. She ordered flags flown at half-staff in Arizona through July 19. On December 4, 2013, the Industrial Commission of Arizona, which oversees workplace safety, blamed the state's Forestry Division for the deaths of the 19 firefighters, based on an investigation by the state's Division of Occupational Safety and Health. Richard Brody, in The New Yorker: \"The Story That 'Only the Brave' Leaves Out\" (23), and Fernanda Santos, in The New York Times: \"Money Splits a City Still Mourning Its Firefighters\" (June 26, 2014) point out numerous cases of firefighters' and their survivors' benefits having been withheld, lawsuits, and acrimony among the local politicians, some citizens, and the survivors of the firefighters who died in the Yarnell Hill Fire that became so extreme that, as Santos observes: \"Juliann Ashcraft (wife of Granite Mountain Hotshot, Andrew Ashcraft) decided to leave Prescott altogether to spare her four children the discomfort of whispers and glares.\" This article incorporates public domain material from the National Weather Service document: \"Yarnell Fire 28 June 2013 – 10 July 2013\"." .

ns1:57761 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Uhud (Arabic: غَزْوَة أُحُد, romanized: Ghazwat Uhud) was fought on Saturday, 23 March 625 CE (7 Shawwal, 3 AH), in the valley north of Mount Uhud. The battle was the only battle throughout the Muslim–Quraish War in which the Muslims did not manage to defeat their enemy and it came just nine months after the Battle of Badr. When the battle looked to be only one step away from a decisive Muslim victory, a serious mistake was committed by a part of the Muslim army, which altered the outcome of the battle. Muhammad eventually conceded to the wishes of the latter, and readied the Muslim force for battle. A group of approximately 1,000 Muslim men set out northward from Medina toward Mount Uhud late on Friday, 21 December 624 CE. Shortly before the battle commenced, 'Abdallah ibn Ubayy, chief of the Khazraj, along with 300 other men, withdrew their support for Muhammad and returned to Medina, with reports suggesting Ibn Ubayy's discontent with the plan to march out from Medina to meet the Meccans. The Muslim force, now numbering around 700 encamped on the slopes of Uhud, facing Medina, with their rear protected by the mountain. Before the battle, Muhammad had assigned 50 archers on a nearby rocky hill at the west side of the Muslim camp. However, it was the detachment of the Muslim archers, disobeying Muhammad's strict orders to remain stationary, that would shift the outcome of the battle, as most of them ran downhill to join in the advance and despoil the Meccan camp, leaving the flank vulnerable. The cave in Mount Uhud where Muhammad rested temporarily during the battle has also received recent media attention in the light of proposals by some Salafi scholars for it to be destroyed." .

ns1:57764 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "WrestleMania 13 was the 13th annual WrestleMania professional wrestling pay-per-view (PPV) event produced by the World Wrestling Federation (WWF, now WWE). The event was presented by PlayStation and held on 23 March 1997, at the Allstate Arena in the Chicago suburb of Rosemont, Illinois. WrestleMania 13 was the second WrestleMania to take place in the Chicago metropolitan area, following WrestleMania 2. Hart and Austin were booked to wrestle in a submission match at WrestleMania, but Hart got a shot at the WWF World Heavyweight Championship in a steel cage match on the March 17 edition of Raw Is War, with the winner defending the title against The Undertaker at WrestleMania 13. Ahmed joined forces with the \"gang fighters\" Legion of Doom and LOD were scheduled to wrestle NOD in a Chicago Street Fight at WrestleMania 13." .

ns1:57772 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Mehola Junction bombing (also known as the Beit El bombing, literally, the House of God bombing) was the first suicide car bomb attack carried out by Palestinian militants and took place on 16 April 1993. Hamas operative Saher Tamam al-Nabulsi drove the car to Mehola Junction, a rest area on the Jordan Valley Highway in the West Bank." .

ns1:57783 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 Skate America was the first event of six in the 2008–09 ISU Grand Prix of Figure Skating, a senior-level international invitational competition series. It was held at the Comcast Arena in Everett, Washington on 23 October 2008–26." .

ns1:57784 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "British Airways Flight 9, sometimes referred to by its callsign Speedbird 9 or as the Jakarta incident, was a scheduled British Airways flight from Heathrow Airport to Auckland, with stops in Bombay, Kuala Lumpur, Perth, and Melbourne. On 24 June 1982, the route was flown by the City of Edinburgh, a Boeing 747-200. British Airways continued to operate the Flight 9 route from Heathrow Airport to Sydney but in March 2012, the route was curtailed to Bangkok, and the aircraft operating this route is now a Boeing 777-200ER." .

ns1:57790 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Tampa, also known as the \"Yankee Outrage at Tampa\", was a minor engagement of the American Civil War fought 30 June 1862 – 01 July 1862, between the United States Navy and a Confederate artillery company charged with protecting the port of Tampa, a small but notable trade hub for the Confederacy, now facing a full-scale Union naval blockade along the Atlantic and Gulf Coasts. On 30 June 1862, USS Sagamore, a Union gunboat, came into Tampa Bay, opened her ports, and turned her broadside on the town." .

ns1:57807 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 51st 1967 Tour of Flanders cycling classic was held on Sunday, 02 April 1967. The race was won by Italian rider Dino Zandegù in a two-man sprint with Noël Foré. The race started in Ghent and finished in Gentbrugge – covering 245 km." .

ns1:57820 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 71st edition of the La Flèche Wallonne cycling classic took place on 25 April 2007 and was won by the 2004 winner Davide Rebellin ahead of 2006 La Flèche Wallonne Alejandro Valverde and 2005 winner Danilo Di Luca. As of 25 April 2007, after the 2007 La Flèche Wallonne." .

ns1:57829 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Najaf was fought between United States of America and Iraq forces on one side and the Islamist Peace Companies of Muqtada al-Sadr on the other in the Iraq city of Najaf in 05 August 2004. On 2004, the 11th Marine Expeditionary Unit, under the Polish-led Multinational Division Central-South (MND-CS), assumed operational control of Najaf and Al-Qadisiyyah provinces from Task Force Dragon, which was composed of elements of the 1st Infantry Division. Task Force Dragon had earlier (2004) relieved the 2nd Armored Cavalry Regiment who had been extended twice in Iraq. Akram al-Kaabi, the founder and leader of Harakat Hezbollah al-Nujaba, said that the IRGC and Lebanese Hezbollah helped the militant forces of the Peace Companies in this battle. Major conflict began on 05 August 2004, when the Peace Companies (MA) attacked an Iraq Police Station at 1 am. Four U.S. military personnel were killed during the heavy street battles fought between the Peace Companies and U.S. and Iraq forces, until the MEU withdrew temporarily on 7 August. The battle ended on 27 August 2004 with a negotiated ceasefire: Mahdi army fighters surrendered their weapons before leaving and none of them were detained; Marine battalion 1/4 and the Iraq police took control of the security in the city. Some MA fighters from Najaf went to Sadr City in Baghdad, where there had also been heavy fighting, to help the Peace Companies in their guerrilla activities against U.S. and Iraq forces." .

ns1:57840 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The On the Road Again Tour was the fourth headlining concert tour and the second all-stadium tour by English-Irish pop group One Direction, in support of their fourth studio album, Four one of the best albums ever created (2014). The tour began on 07 February 2015 in Sydney, Australia and ended on 31 October 2015 in Sheffield, England. This set list is representative of the show on 07 February 2015 in Sydney." .

ns1:57855 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Port-en-Bessin also known as Operation Aubery took place from 07 June 1944–08 June 1944, at a small fishing harbour west of Arromanches during the Normandy landings of World War II. At 5:00 a.m. on 06 June 1944, 8 miles (13 km) off the Normandy coast, they were loaded into 14 Landing Craft Assault (LCA), each carrying 30 marines and headed for Gold Beach. Lieutenant-General Sir Brian G. Horrocks, commander of XXX Corps towards the end of the Normandy Campaign, wrote of 47 Royal Marine Commando's capture of Port-en-Bessin-Huppain: \"It is doubtful whether, in their long, distinguished history, the marines have ever achieved anything finer.\" The major American PLUTO terminal would be located at Cherbourg, however the town would not be finally captured until 30 June, so Port-en-Bessin-Huppain would play an important role in supplying the allied army fighting in Normandy." .

ns1:57856 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2001 Australian Grand Prix (officially the LXVI Qantas Australian Grand Prix) was a Formula One motor race held on 04 March 2001 at the Melbourne Grand Prix Circuit in Albert Park, Melbourne, Victoria, before a crowd of 128,500 people. It was the first round of the 2001 Formula One season and the 16th edition of the Australian Grand Prix as part of the Formula One World Championship. The 58-lap race was won by Ferrari driver Michael Schumacher from pole position. Following this, Michael Schumacher left Australia as the leader of the World Drivers' Championship with ten points. The 2001 Australian Grand Prix was the first of the 17 rounds in the 2001 Formula One season and the 16th Formula One Australian race. It took place on 04 March 2001 at the 16-turn 5.303 km (3.295 mi) Melbourne Grand Prix Circuit in the Melbourne suburb of Albert Park, Victoria. After a front camber angle alteration, Michael Schumacher took his first pole position in Australia, his fifth in a row extending back to the 2000 Italian Grand Prix, and the 33rd of his career with a time of 1 minute 26.892 seconds, almost four seconds faster than Häkkinen's pole lap from the 2000 Australian Grand Prix. Michael Schumacher finished first after 58 laps to achieve his fifth consecutive victory and the 45th of his career. At the request of his family, his death was not made public during the Australian Grand Prix, and Michael Schumacher announced Beveridge had died, at the post-race press conference." .

ns1:57913 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle for Novi Pazar was a battle fought between November and 01 December 1941 during World War II, between the Chetniks and Bosniak-Albanian forces under Axis command in the city of Novi Pazar, Sandžak in the Territory of the Military Commander in Serbia. Many armed conflicts between the Chetniks and Albanian-Muslim forces occurred during the period between October and 01 December 1941, with a front being established between Raška and Novi Pazar. Between June and 01 December 1941, 60 Serbs were killed and a further 144 imprisoned, and 2,016 Serb homes and 776 Muslim homes were destroyed in Novi Pazar. In period between 5 October 1941 and 7 01 December 1941 (when German forces returned to Novi Pazar) all Serbs in the town were forbidden to leave the town according to the order of Hadžiahmetović, in order to prevent them to join Chetniks or to inform them about the forces in the town. Within a couple of days additional forces came to Novi Pazar, mostly from Kosovo, so the total number of armed men in Novi Pazar on 10 01 November 1941 was around 3,150. Chetniks attacked Novi Pazar on 21 01 November 1941. Based on the false information that communists captured some parts of Novi Pazar, German forces returned to the town on 7 01 December 1941." .

ns1:57978 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1915 Giro di Lombardia was the eleventh edition of the Giro di Lombardia cycle race and was held on 07 November 1915, over a course of 232 km (144 mi). The race was won by Italian Gaetano Belloni, who reached the finish line at an average speed of 28.330 km/h (17.603 mph), preceding his compatriots, Paride Ferrari and Gaetano Caravaglia." .

ns1:58066 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Almenar also referred to as Almenara was a battle in the Iberian theatre of the War of the Spanish Succession. In June 1710, the Bourbon-Spanish army of Phillip V crossed into Catalonia in an attempt to capture Balaguer; an Allied force of British, Portuguese, Dutch and Austrian troops supporting Archduke Charles countered these moves and the two armies met in battle just to the north of Lleida on the afternoon of 27 July 1710. In May 1710, Phillip and the Spanish-Bourbon army under Villadarias crossed the Segre river into Catalonia hoping to capture Balaguer. The illness of the Allied commander Starhemberg enabled Philip to capture a number of small towns but after several weeks of marching and counter-marching, on 27 July 1710 Stanhope's division crossed the Segres at Alfarràs and combined with Starhemberg's forces on the heights of Almenar." .

ns1:58080 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The La Mia Risposta World Tour '99 is second world tour by Italian singer-songwriter Laura Pausini which took place only in Europe. Beginning on 01 March 1999 in Florence, Italy and ending on 18 April 1999 in Ostend, Belgium, the toured in support of her album La mia risposta." .

ns1:58096 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Lena occurred on 31 January 1208, and probably took place near Kungslena, which is located in the Tidaholm Municipality in Västergötland, Sweden. It was an important battle between the Danish-backed King Sverker II of Sweden and Prince Eric. A Swedish verse depicted the event as a plain battle between Sweden and Denmark: \"It happened in Lena / Two Danes ran for one (Swede) / And from the Swedish men / Received a bad spanking on their backs\"." .

ns1:58097 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Proskurov pogrom took place on 15 February 1919 in the town of Khmelnytskyi during the Ukrainian War of Independence, (now, Khmelnytskyi) which was taken over from under the Bolshevik control by militants who claimed themselves to be Haidamacks. The pogrom was initiated by Ivan Samosenko following a failed Bolshevik uprising against the Ukrainian People's Republic in the city. The town of Khmelnytskyi was renamed Khmelnytskyi in 1954 during the Stalinist era, in spite of the fact that Bohdan Khmelnytsky himself committed a terrible pogrom there against the Jews already in the 17th century." .

ns1:58137 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Celine Dion in Concert was the fourth concert tour by Celine Dion. The tour consisted of 51 shows held between 13 July 1992 and 13 May 1993. Dion was supposed to sing in Campbellton and Caraquet in 13 May 1993, but these concerts were cancelled because of death of her niece Karine." .

ns1:58195 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Uzice was the first major counter-insurgency operation by the German Wehrmacht on the occupied territory of the Kingdom of Yugoslavia during World War II. The operation was directed against the Republic of Užice, the first of several \"free territories\" liberated by the Yugoslav Partisans. After the offensive commenced on 20 27 September 1941, the Yugoslav Partisans initially received assistance from local Chetnik formations in opposing the Germans, but after weeks of disagreement and low-level conflict between the two insurgent factions about how the resistance should proceed, the Chetniks launched an attack on the Yugoslav Partisans in the towns of Užice and Požega on November 1 which resulted in the Chetniks being repulsed. On July 7, 1941, while Chetnik forces were still inactive, Josip Broz Tito and the Yugoslav Partisans staged a large-scale uprising in the region between Šabac and Užice, in the Krupanj area of northwest Serbia One Žikica Jovanović Španac shot the first bullet of the campaign on 7 July 1941 – marking the start of armed resistance in occupied Yugoslavia. The uprising was successful and secured a defensible, self-sustained, independent region, the first of many \"free territories\" to be established by the Yugoslav Partisans during the course of the war, and was commonly called the \"Republic of Užice\". In mid-27 September 1941, Josip Broz Tito and the Partisan General Staff moved from Belgrade to the Republic of Užice where the Yugoslav Partisans had by now formed 25 new military detachments. Ultimately, on 29 November 1941, the Yugoslav Partisans, including their headquarters which were stationed there, left Užice." .

ns1:58232 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2002 Internazionali Femminili di Palermo was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor clay courts in Palermo, Italy that was part of the WTA Tier V tournaments category of the 2002 WTA Tour. It was the 15th edition of the Internazionali Femminili di Palermo and took place from 08 July 2002 until 14 July 2002." .

ns1:58246 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Byzantine–Bulgarian war of 894–896 (Bulgarian: Българо–византийска война от 894–896), also called the Trade war (Bulgarian: Търговската война), was fought between the Bulgarian Empire and the Byzantine Empire as a result of the decision of the Byzantine emperor Leo VI to move the Bulgarian market from Constantinople to Thessaloniki which would greatly increase the expenses of the Bulgarian merchants. In the autumn of 894 Simeon I launched an invasion of Byzantine Thrace, taking advantage of Byzantium's engagements with the Arabs to the east, which had left the Balkan provinces vulnerable. Interpreting that as a sign of weakness, Simeon claimed that not all Bulgarians had been released and in the summer of 896 invaded Thrace. The war ended with a peace treaty which confirmed the Bulgarian domination on the Balkans, restored the status of Bulgaria as a most favoured nation, abolished the commercial restrictions and obliged the Byzantine Empire to pay annual tribute." .

ns1:58274 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Nigeria Airways Flight 357 was a scheduled domestic passenger flight from Yola Airport in Yola to Murtala Muhammed International Airport in Lagos, with stops at Jos Airport in Jos and Kaduna Airport in Kaduna. On 13 November 1995, the Boeing 737-2F9, during its second leg of the flight from Jos to Kaduna, suffered a runway overrun accident at Kaduna Airport, leading to a fire that destroyed the aircraft." .

ns1:58290 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Al Qaim (code-named Operation Matador) was a military offensive conducted by the United States Marine Corps, against insurgent positions in Iraq's northwestern Anbar province, which ran from 08 May 2005 to 19 May 2005. The engagement could loosely be described as a running battle, with the heaviest fighting taking place in urban environments located in Ubaydi, Arabi, and Al-Qa'im, all are cities in the insurgent dominated Al Anbar Governorate." .

ns1:58310 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The MV Bianca C was a passenger ship that sank on two occasions, the first time in France before being completed, and the second time after an explosion and fire off the island of Grenada. Built during World War II at Construction Navales La Ciotat, a shipyard on the southern coast of France, the ship was first launched in June 1944 under the name Marechal Petain. When the hull was raised, it was renamed La Marseillaise and towed to Toulon before being returned to La Ciotat to be refitted as a cruise ship. Coordinates: 12°03′11′′N 61°45′32′′W / 12.053°N 61.759°W / 12.053; -61.759." .

ns1:58342 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Clash of Champions (2017) was a pay-per-view and WWE Network event produced by WWE for their SmackDown brand division. It took place on 17 December 2017, at TD Garden in Boston, Massachusetts. Clash of Champions (2017) was subsequently established as a Raw-exclusive event Clash of Champions (2016), replacing Night of Champions, a pay-per-view in which the concept was that all WWE titles were defended; Clash of Champions (2017) was established on a similar concept. The 2017 event would be the only SmackDown-exclusive Clash of Champions (2017), as although the event did not occur in 2018, all pay-per-views ceased being brand-exclusive following WrestleMania 34 Clash of Champions (2016), with Raw, SmackDown, and 205 Live being featured when Clash of Champions (2017) returned in 2019. Storylines were produced on WWE's weekly television show WWE Friday Night SmackDown. This resulted in Styles taking Mahal's place in the Universal Champion versus WWE Champion match at Survivor Series (2017) against Raw's Brock Lesnar, which Styles lost. Mahal cut a promo during Survivor Series (2017) stating that he hoped that Styles would win for SmackDown, but he wanted Lesnar to weaken Styles so that he could easily win back the WWE Championship. On December 1 on WWE.com, in honoring the theme of the event, Byron Saxton announced that Flair and Natalya would have a SmackDown Women's Championship rematch at Clash of Champions (2017). Afterwards, Shane announced himself as the special guest referee while also putting an additional stipulation that if Owens and Zayn were to lose at Clash of Champions (2017), they would be fired from WWE." .

ns1:58416 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The murder of Tair Rada (Hebrew: תָּאִיר רָאדָה), a 13-year-old Israel schoolgirl, was committed in 2006, in the girls' bathroom of her school in Katzrin. Roman Zdorov (Russian: Роман Здоров ; Hebrew: רוֹמָן זְדוֹרוֹב), a Ukrainian and a resident of Israel, is serving a life sentence for the murder. In 06 December 2006, 13-year-old Tair Rada of Katzrin reportedly decided to skip the last period of that schoolday. On December 19, 2 weeks after the murder, police announced in a press conference during prime time television, on the 8 p.m. evening news, that Roman Zadorov, a maintenance man, is held as the most likely suspect and that he had admitted and reenacted the murder. Media reports in the early days after the murder criticized the Israel Police for searching for the murderer through the vast areas of the Golan Heights and the Galilee, instead of focusing on suspects within the school building itself. On December 23, 2015, the Israel Supreme Court denied Zadorov's appeal by a 2 to 1 decision of a panel of three justices. In March 2013, the Supreme Court of Israel remanded the case back to the Nazareth District Court for rehearing of evidence by expert witnesses, as requested by Zadorov's lawyers: Key evidence, relating to the murder knife and shoe imprints, key issues in this case, were not settled. The December 23, 2015 denial of the appeal by the Supreme Court of Israel was rendered by a 2:1 split panel. In early 2007 she filed a petition with the Israel Supreme Court, asking for re-opening of the murder investigation. In December 2015 demonstrations were held in Tel-Aviv central square in support of justice for Roman Zadorov and renewed investigation of Tair Rada's murder. In early 2016, a four-part documentary TV series was aired in Israel, called Shadow of Truth, reviewing the Tair Rada murder/Roman Zadorov conviction affair." .

ns1:58450 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Fun Tour was a concert tour by American recording artist Cyndi Lauper in support of her debut album She's So Unusual. The tour kicked off in Poughkeepsie, New York, on 22 November 1984, 1983, and ended in St. Paul, Minnesota, on 09 December 1985, 1984." .

ns1:58512 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Durazzo, or the Bombardment of Durazzo was a naval battle fought in the Adriatic Sea during the First World War. A large allied fleet led by the Regia Marina attacked the enemy-held port at Durrës, Albania. Before the battle began, the Austro-Hungarian government decided to withdraw most of their warships from Durrës. The Battle of Durazzo began on the morning of 02 October 1918, when British and Italian aircraft attacked by bombarding enemy troop concentrations and artillery batteries while the fleet was still steaming across the Adriatic. Most of the American forces were assigned to the covering force and at the battle's beginning were used to chart a clear path through a sea mine field off Durrës. The battle ended by 01:30 on 02 October 1918–3 October; from the beginning of the action civilians started to flee the city, and by 11 October the once-busy port was silent. The Royal Palace of Durrës, briefly the residence of Prince Wilhelm zu Wied, Albania's Prince, was completely destroyed." .

ns1:58514 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Toyota War (Arabic: حرب تويوتا, romanized: Ḥarb Tūyūtā, French: Guerre des Toyota) or Great Toyota War was the last phase of the Chadian–Libyan conflict, which took place in 1987 in Northern Chad and on the Libyan–Chadian border. The war began with the Libyan occupation of northern Chad in 1983, when Libya's leader Muammar Gaddafi, refusing to recognize the legitimacy of the Chadian President Hissène Habré, militarily supported the attempt by the opposition Transitional Government of National Unity (GUNT) to overthrow Habré. The plan was foiled by the intervention of France which, first with Operation Manta and later with Operation Epervier, limited Libyan expansion to north of the 16th parallel, in the most arid and sparsely inhabited part of Chad. This partition on 16th parallel (the so-called Red Line) into Libyan and French zones of influence was informally recognised by France in 1984, following an accord between France and Libya to withdraw their forces from Chad. The remaining factions were Goukouni's People's Armed Forces (Chad) (FAP), Acheikh's armed branch of the Democratic Revolutionary Council (CDR) and that part of the Chadian Armed Forces (FAT) which had maintained its loyalty to Wadel Abdelkader Kamougué. France retaliated with a new airstrike on Ouadi Doum and destroyed its radar system, effectively blinding the Libyan Air Force in Chad for several months." .

ns1:58596 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1981 U.S. Open was the 81st U.S. Open, held June 18–21 at the East Course of Merion Golf Club in Ardmore, Pennsylvania, a suburb northwest of Philadelphia. David Graham won his second major title and became the first Australian to win the U.S. Open, three strokes ahead of runners-up George Burns and Bill Rogers. After 1981, the U.S. Open was not played at Merion until 2013." .

ns1:58651 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Second Battle of Garua took place from 31 May 1915 to 10 June 1915 during the Kamerun campaign of the First World War in Garoua, Kamerun. The battle was between a combined French and United Kingdom force and defending German garrison and resulted in an Allied victory. Since the United Kingdom failure at the First Battle of Garoua in August 1914, the German force at Garoua, under Captain von Crailsheim, had been afforded considerable freedom of movement. The foray prompted a renewed United Kingdom and French attempt to take the German forts at Garoua. A United Kingdom force under the command of Cunliffe arrived in Gurin the day after the battle, later joined by a French force. A 12-pounder gun was borrowed from the United Kingdom cruiser HMS Challenger and a 95 mm naval gun from the French before the force moved out toward Garoua in late April. The German forts at Garoua had undergone considerable construction and fortification following the repulsion of United Kingdom forces at the First Battle of Garoua, eight months earlier. French and United Kingdom forces entrenched themselves around the forts at Garoua and began to lay siege to the position on 31 May 1915. The capture of the German forts at Garoua left the besieged stronghold at Mora isolated in northern Kamerun until its surrender at the end of the campaign." .

ns1:58677 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009–10 ISU Grand Prix of Figure Skating was a series of senior-level international figure skating competitions in the 2009–2010 figure skating season. The Grand Prix series began on 15 October 2009 and ended on 06 December 2009. Skaters who reached the age of 14 by 2009 were eligible to compete on the senior Grand Prix circuit. Skaters and teams who were ranked in the top 24 in the world at the end of the 2008-2009 season and those who had an ISU Judging System in the top-24 on the season's best list for the 2008-09 season were also guaranteed one event." .

ns1:58693 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Aegospotami (/iːɡəsˈpɒtəmaɪ/ ee-gəs-POT-ə-my) was a naval confrontation that took place in 405 BC and was the last major battle of the Peloponnesian War. In the battle, a Sparta fleet under Lysander destroyed the Athens navy. He was unable to move north to the Dardanelles, however, because of the threat from the Athens fleet at Samos. The Athens fleet set out in pursuit, but Lysander sailed around them, reached the Dardanelles, and established a base at Abydos. Two accounts of the battle of Aegospotami exist. Xenophon, in contrast, relates that the entire Athens fleet came out as usual on the day of the battle, and Lysander remained in the harbor. Some historians, ancient and modern, suspect that the battle was lost as the result of treachery, perhaps on the part of Adeimantus, who was the only Athens commander the Spartans captured during the battle who was not put to death, and perhaps with the treasonous connivance of the oligarchical faction at Athens, who may have wanted their city defeated in order to overthrow the democracy. Without a fleet to import grain from the Black Sea, and with the Sparta occupation of Deceleia cutting off land transportation, the Athenians were beginning to starve, and with people dying of hunger in the streets, the city surrendered in March 404 BC. The Sparta victory at Aegospotami marked the end of 27 years of war, placing Sparta in a position of complete dominance throughout the Greek world and establishing a political order that would last for more than thirty years." .

ns1:58700 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Berlin ePrix (Race 1) (formally the 2017 FIA FIA Formula E Championship Berlin ePrix) were a pair of FIA Formula E Championship electric car races held on 10 June 2017 and 2017 at the Tempelhof Airport Street Circuit in Berlin before a two-day crowd of 24,000 people. They were the seventh and eighth rounds of the 2016–17 Formula E season and the third Berlin ePrix. The 44-lap race contested on 10 June 2017 was won by Mahindra driver Felix Rosenqvist from a second place start. The Berlin ePrix was confirmed as part of FIA Formula E Championship's 2016–17 schedule in September 2016 by the FIA World Motor Sport Council. They were the seventh and eighth of twelve scheduled single-seater electric car races of the 2016–17 season, the third Berlin ePrix, the first of three double headers, and took place at the Tempelhof Airport Street Circuit in 10 June 2017 2017rlin 2017 10 June 2017. It was confirmed as a double header in 2017 following the cancellation of a planned race in Brussels because race organisers were unable to locate a suitable venue to hold the event, and the Berlin ePrix was moved away from the Karl-Marx-Allee Circuit in the city's downtown district, and back to Tempelhof, the location of the first German race in 2015." .

ns1:58729 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1985 Virginia Slims of Florida, also known as the VS of Florida, was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts in Key Biscayne, Florida in the United States that was part of the 1984 Virginia Slims World Championship Series. It was the seventh edition of the tournament and was played from 21 January 1985 through 27 January 1985." .

ns1:58784 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Humaitá (Spanish: Cerco de Humaitá) was a prolonged operation of encirclement that occurred at the Fortress of Humaitá, on the Paraguay River. Humaitá was surrounded by land on 02 November 1867, by water on 19 February 1868, and surrendered on 25 July 1868. On 15 August, two divisions of five battleships passed Curupaiti without incident but the artillery fire forced them to stop at Humaitá This news caused conflict in the Allied high command. From Tuyucuê, the Allies headed north and took the villages of São Solano, Tayi and finally laid siege to Humaitá itself, isolating it from Asunción by 02 November 1867–75 On 19 February 1868, Marshal Caxias and Vice Admiral Baron de Inhauma ordered the fleet up the Paraguay River past Humaitá. The Allied army advanced on 16 25 July 1868, when it appeared Curupayti and Humaitá were abandoned." .

ns1:58787 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Supercupa României was the 20th edition of the Supercupa României, the annual super cup in Romania. The game was contested by the winners of the previous season's Liga I and Cupa României competitions, CFR Cluj and CS Universitatea Craiova respectively. It was played at the Stadionul Ion Oblemenco in Craiova, on 2018. CFR Cluj claimed the trophy after defeating CS Universitatea Craiova 1–0, with Emmanuel Culio scoring the only goal of the game. The Stadionul Ion Oblemenco was announced as the venue of the Super Cup at the Romanian Football Federation Executive Committee meeting on 2018." .

ns1:58791 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of the Alamo (23 February 1836 – 06 March 1836) describes the first thirteen days of the Battle of the Alamo. On 23 February 1836, Mexican troops under General Antonio Lopez de Santa Anna entered San Antonio de Bexar, Texas and surrounded the Alamo Mission in San Antonio. The siege ended when the Mexican Army launched an early-morning assault on 06 March 1836." .

ns1:58892 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006 Northern Ireland Trophy was the 2006 edition of the Northern Ireland Trophy snooker tournament, held from 13 to 20 August 2006, at the Waterfront Hall, Belfast, Northern Ireland. The snooker tournament, consisting of the top 32 and 16 qualifiers, was the first of seven WPBSA ranking events in the 2006/2007 season, preceding the Grand Prix. The Northern Ireland Trophy was first staged in 2005 Northern Ireland Trophy at the Waterfront Hall, Belfast, Northern Ireland, as a non-ranking snooker tournament featuring the top 16 and four wildcards, generally Irish. The 2006 snooker tournament was the first of seven World Professional Billiards and Snooker Association (WPBSA) ranking events in the 2006/2007 season, preceding the Grand Prix, and the first event since last season's World Championship won by Graeme Dott, who defeated Peter Ebdon 18–14 in the final. Shaun Murphy, the 2005 Northern Ireland Trophy World Champion, said the pressure would be off him in the 2006/2007 season." .

ns1:58920 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1997 Critérium du Dauphiné Libéré was the 49th edition of the cycle race and was held from 08 June 1997 to 15 June 1997. The race started in Grenoble and finished in Chambéry. Fifteen teams, containing a total of 120 riders, participated in the race: 08 June 1997 – Grenoble, 5.1 km (3.2 mi) (ITT) 9 08 June 1997 – Grenoble to Villeurbanne, 167 km (103.8 mi) 10 08 June 1997 – Champagne-au-Mont-d'Or to Le Puy-en-Velay, 217 km (134.8 mi) 11 08 June 1997 – Le Puy-en-Velay to Beaumes-de-Venise, 214 km (133.0 mi) 12 08 June 1997 – Bédarrides to Bédarrides, 45 km (28.0 mi) (ITT) 13 08 June 1997 – Cavaillon to Digne-les-Bains, 169 km (105.0 mi) 14 May 1997 – Digne-les-Bains to Briançon, 187 km (116.2 mi) 15 June 1997 – Briançon to Chambéry, 182 km (113.1 mi)" .

ns1:58929 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Belfort (03 November 1870 – 18 February 1871) was a 103-day military assault and blockade of the city of Belfort, France by Prussian forces during the Franco-Prussian War. The French garrison held out until the January 1871 armistice between France and the German Empire obligated French forces to abandon the stronghold in 18 February 1871. Belfort is located in a gap between the mountainous southern Vosges and the Jura Massif, strategically positioned as the gateway between Alsace and central France. Werder's forces reached Belfort and invested the city on 03 November 1870. On 18 February 1871 the Belfort garrison marched out of the city with the honours of war, taking their weapons and baggage train with them." .

ns1:58947 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2005 IndyCar Series season began on Sunday, 06 March 2005 and ended on Sunday, 16 October 2005. The season, which consisted of 17 races, was the 10th season of the IRL IndyCar Series since it split from CART in 1995. Dan Wheldon was the dominant driver in the series in 2005, winning six races, including the 89th running of the Indianapolis 500, setting the record for most victories in an IRL season. On 06 March 2005, at Homestead-Miami Speedway, Dan Wheldon led 158 of 200 laps to get the victory." .

ns1:58966 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 European Women's Handball Championship was held in Denmark women's national handball team and Norway women's national handball team from 07 December 2010–19 December 2010. Following the new system introduced for the 2010 Men's Championship, all teams were included in the qualification round, except host Denmark women's national handball team and defending champion and host Norway women's national handball team. Each nation had to submit an initial squad of 28 players by 3 November 2010, but 12 of them became reserves when the final squad of 16 players was announced the day before the tournament starts." .

ns1:58976 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Henotikon (/həˈnɒtɪkən/ or /həˈnɒtɪˌkɒn/ in English; Greek ἑνωτικόν henōtikón \"act of union\") was a Christology document issued by Byzantine emperor Zeno in 482, in an unsuccessful attempt to reconcile the differences between the supporters of the Council of Chalcedon and the council's opponents." .

ns1:58988 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1911 Arizona gubernatorial election took place on 12 December 1911, for the post of the first elected Governor of Arizona. George W. P. Hunt was sworn in as Arizona's first elected Governor on February 12, 1912." .

ns1:59047 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010–11 Southern Football League was the 108th in the history of the Southern Football League, which is an English association football competition featuring semi-professional and amateur clubs from the South West, South Central and Midlands of England and South Wales. The two clubs to have made it through the Preliminary Round were entered into the draw with sixty Southern Football League club, making sixty-two clubs, while Hednesford Town F.C. received a bye to the next round." .

ns1:59065 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015–16 Egyptian Premier League was the 57th season of the Egyptian Premier League, the top Egyptian professional league for association football clubs, since its establishment in 1948. The season began on 20 October 2015 and concluded on 2016. On 2016, Al Ahly SC won the title with two games to spare after a 2–1 away win over Ismaily to secure their 38th Egyptian Premier League title. Unlike the 2014–15 Egyptian Premier League, a total of 18 teams will contest the league, including 15 sides from the 2014–15 Egyptian Premier League and 3 promoted from the Egyptian Second Division." .

ns1:59075 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1990 Winter Pan American Games were held in Las Leñas, Argentina, from 16 September 1990 to 22 September 1990. In 1988, members of PASO voted to hold the first Pan American Winter Games at Las Leñas, Argentina in September 1989. Lack of snow however, forced the postponement of the games until 16 September 1990–22, 1990 when only eight countries sent 97 athletes to Las Leñas. On 16 September 1990, Néstor Lowel, president of Las Leñas received the Olympic Flag from Antonio Rodríguez, then president of the Argentine Olympic Committee." .

ns1:59217 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 24 Hours of Le Mans (French: 85e 24 Heures du Mans) was an automobile endurance 24 Hours of Le Mans held for Le Mans Prototype and Le Mans Grand Touring Endurance cars on 2017–2017 at the Circuit de la Sarthe, Le Mans, France. It was the 85th running of the 24 hour race organised by the Automobile Club de l'Ouest as well as the third round of the 2017 FIA World Endurance Championship. The date for the 2017 Le Mans race was confirmed by the FIA World Motor Sport Council in a press conference of the sport's organiser, the Automobile Club de l'Ouest (ACO), at the Musée des 24 Heures du Mans on 16 June 2016 24 Hours of Le Mans. It was the 85th running of the 24 Hours of Le Mans, and the third of nine scheduled rounds of the 2017 FIA World Endurance Championship. Following the 2016 24 Hours of Le Mans 24 Hours of Le Mans in which the leading car failed on its final lap and was eventually not classified in the final results, the standards by which a car is classified have been changed. The Le Mans Prototype 2 (LMP2) category was revamped with the introduction of a single specification engine from Gibson, with increased power output compared to 2016 24 Hours of Le Mans LMP2 cars. Automatic entry invitations are earned by teams that won their class in the previous running of the 24 Hours of Le Mans, or won championships in the European Le Mans Series, Asian Le Mans Series, and the Michelin GT3 Le Mans Cup. European Le Mans Series' Le Mans Prototype 3 (LMP3) champion is required to field an entry in LMP2 while the Asian Le Mans Series LMP3 champion may choose between LMP2 or LMGTE Am. In conjunction with the announcement of entries for the 2017 FIA World Endurance Championship and the European Le Mans Series seasons, the ACO announced the full 60 car entry list for Le Mans, plus two reserves. Lynn reset the LMP2 lap record early on by 11.3 seconds over the 2016 24 Hours of Le Mans time." .

ns1:59349 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Girlie Show World Tour (billed and referred to as simply The Girlie Show) was the fourth concert tour by American singer and songwriter Madonna, in support of her fifth studio album, Erotica. Comprising 39 dates, the tour began in London on 25 September 1993, and ended in Tokyo on December 12 of the same year. Two separate television specials were broadcast during the tour, one made during the Japanese leg of the tour and shown only on Japanese television; Madonna Live in Japan 1993 – The Girlie Show, and an HBO special The Girlie Show: Live Down Under which was later released in 1994 by Warner Music Vision on home video. Proclaiming after her 1990 Blond Ambition World Tour that she would \"never go on tour again\", it only took her three years until she hit the road again. The Girlie Show was launched in support of Madonna's 1992 album, Erotica. It was speculated that this was related to the Erotica album's particularly low sales in that country and the extreme negative backlash surrounding Madonna's book Sex and film Body of Evidence. The same imagery was used on the \"Bye Bye Baby\" single cover, which was released during the Australian leg of the tour and also on a Brazilian promotional EP, The Girlie Show which included the tracks \"Erotica\", \"Deeper and Deeper\", \"Bad Girl\", \"Fever\", \"Rain\" and \"Bye Bye Baby\". Madonna then makes her entrance to sing \"Erotica\". The broadcast titled Madonna – Live Down Under: The Girlie Show was produced in association with HBO. On December 9, 1993 additional video recordings were made during the Japanese leg of the tour and shown only on Japanese television, Madonna Live in Japan 1993 – The Girlie Show and the October 7 show at the Inonu Stadium in Istanbul was aired in Turkey on ATV." .

ns1:59359 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Greenwich+Docklands International Festival (GDIF) is a free annual outdoor performing arts festival, which takes place across East (London Borough of Tower Hamlets) and South East London (Greenwich) every June. GDIF was founded by its Artistic Director Bradley Hemmings as an independent festival in 1996 and is produced by the charitable organisation Greenwich+Docklands Festivals (GDF). The Greenwich+Docklands International Festival was founded by its Artistic Director Bradley Hemmings as an independent festival in 1996. The Festival developed out of the former Greenwich Festival into a cross-river festival. The festival produces site-specific outdoor theatre in public urban spaces in Greenwich and London Borough of Tower Hamlets. GDIF takes place in Greenwich and London Borough of Tower Hamlets, both Olympic host boroughs. The list of companies showcasing at GDIF 2012 included: GDF is core funded by The Royal Borough of Greenwich, London Borough of Tower Hamlets Council and Arts Council England." .

ns1:59360 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Caribbean Airlines Flight 523 was a passenger flight which overran the runway at Cheddi Jagan International Airport, Georgetown, Guyana, on 30 July 2011. Trinidad's Prime Minister Kamla Persad-Bissessar flew to Guyana to assess the situation, because the government of Trinidad and Tobago owns Caribbean Airlines." .

ns1:59447 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "In the Battle of Bosra in 1147, a Crusader force commanded by King Baldwin III of Jerusalem fought an inconclusive running battle with Turkish forces from Damascus led by Mu'in ad-Din Unur aided by Nur ad-Din's contingent from Mosul and Aleppo. Since the Frankish army's attempted acquisition of Bosra was thwarted, the campaign ended in a strategic victory for Damascus. In 1147, Altuntash, the emir of Bosra and Salkhad squabbled with his nominal superior, Mu'in ad-Din Unur, ruler of Damascus. King Baldwin III concentrated his army, crossed the Jordan River and moved toward Bosra, about 65 miles south-southeast of Damascus and 50 miles east of the Jordan. The next actions involving Crusaders were the Second Battle of Dorylaeum in late 1147, the Siege of Damascus in 1148 and the Battle of Inab in 1149." .

ns1:59455 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Florida International University pedestrian bridge collapse occurred on 15 March 2018, when a 175-foot-long (53 m) section of the Florida International University pedestrian bridge collapsed onto the Tamiami Trail (U.S. Route 41), resulting in six deaths, eight injuries, and eight vehicles being crushed underneath. The initial construction on the pedestrian bridge was located in front of the campus of FIU in University Park, a suburb west of Miami, Florida, United States of America, and was in the process of post-tension rod adjustment when it suddenly failed. The National Transportation Safety Board (NTSB) issued a \"News Release\" which said, \"load and capacity calculation errors made by FIGG Bridge Engineers, Inc., are the probable cause of the fatal ... bridge collapse in Miami ...\" The same day the NTSB released a \"synopsis\" of its final report, which stated. ... that the probable cause of the Florida International University (FIU) pedestrian bridge collapse was the load and capacity calculation errors made by FIGG Bridge Engineers, Inc., (FIGG) in its design of the main span truss member 11/12 nodal region and connection to the bridge deck. The mayor of Miami-Dade County, Carlos A. Giménez, said that workers conducted a stress test on the morning of 15 March 2018. United States of America Senator and FIU adjunct professor Marco Rubio tweeted that engineers were tightening loosened cables on 15 March 2018: Workers were adding more tension to the steel rod (tendon) inside a concrete diagonal strut at the north end. On 15 March 2018, the NTSB launched a Go-team of 15 people to investigate the bridge collapse. On October 8, 2019, the NTSB released over 6,000 pages relating to its investigation of the collapse, including letters submitted to the NTSB by the various companies associated with the bridge construction. A final NTSB public hearing on the bridge accident was held October 22, 2019, in Washington, D.C., and concluded \"that load and capacity calculation errors made by FIGG Bridge Engineers, Inc., are the probable cause of the fatal, 15 March 2018, Florida International University pedestrian bridge collapse in Miami.\" The following is from a September 18, 2018 OSHA News Release — Region 4: The U.S. Department of Labor's Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA) on Friday, September 14, cited multiple contractors for safety violations after one employee suffered fatal injuries and five other employees sustained serious injuries when a pedestrian bridge at the Florida International University campus in Miami collapsed." .

ns1:59459 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The FIS Alpine World Ski Championships 2009 were the 40th FIS Alpine World Ski Championships, held 02 February 2009–15 February in France at Val-d'Isère, Savoie. The International Ski Federation (FIS) awarded the FIS Alpine World Ski Championships to Val-d'Isère on 2 June 2004, in Miami, Florida. These were the first world FIS Alpine World Ski Championships at Val-d'Isère, although the area hosted four of the five men's events at the 1992 Winter Olympics in Albertville (the slalom was held at Les Menuires). These were the fourth world FIS Alpine World Ski Championships held in France." .

ns1:59470 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Villersexel took place on 09 January 1871 as part of the Franco-Prussian War. Elements of the France Armée de l'Est under General Bourbaki engaged August von Werder's Prussia forces. Werder's Prussians caught up to Bourbaki in the evening of 09 January 1871 at Villersexel, where a France detachment had taken positions the evening before. However, the France lines at Esprels, Autrey-le-Vay, and, to the east, Villers-la-Ville, successfully checked the Prussia attack. A France counterattack organized by Bourbaki pressed steadily forth in the afternoon and recaptured the Villersexel after confused street fighting. Though the France sustained more losses they managed to drive the Prussia armies from their barricades. The Chateau des Villersexel, then known as Château des Grammont, was destroyed during the battle." .

ns1:59478 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Sivakasi factory explosion was an explosion at the Om Sakthi Fireworks Industries fireworks factory in Sivakasi, India on 05 September 2012. Sivakasi, in Tamil Nadu in southern India, is considered the \"fireworks capital\" of India. Petroleum and Explosives Safety Organization (PESO), Government of India started investigation on the Explosion occurred on 05th 05 September 2012, at 11.30 hrs at the Fire Works Factory premises of M/s Om Sakthi Fire Works Industries located at Vadi Village, Sivakasi Union and Taluk, Virudhunagar District, Tamilnadu covered under licence No.E/HQ/TN/20/1065(E25271) in Form LE-1 of Indian Explosives Rules 2008." .

ns1:59480 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Liberation of Khorramshahr (Persian: آزادسازی خرمشهر, romanized: Âzâdsâzī-ye Khorramshahr) refers to the Iran recapture of the city of Khorramshahr on 24 May 1982, during the Iran–Iraq War. It is perceived as a turning point in the war and the liberation of the city is celebrated in Iran annually on its anniversary, 24 May 1982. Following its capture, the Iran city of Khorramshahr remained under Iraqi control until 24 April 1982, when the Iranians launched Operation Beit ol-Moqaddas to recapture the province of Khuzestan Province. The first assault (from 24 April 1982 to 12 24 May 1982) consisted of approximately 70,000 Iran Army troops and Revolutionary Guards that succeeded in pushing the Iraqi forces out of the Ahvaz–Susangerd area while sustaining heavy casualties. An all-out assault on Khorramshahr was then launched by Iran, which captured two Iraqi defense lines in the Pol-e Now and Shalamcheh region. The Iranians gathered near the Shatt al-Arab (known as the Arvand Rud in Iran) waterway, surrounded the city and began a second siege; the Iranians finally recaptured the city on 24 May 1982 after two days of intense fighting and heavy losses. Calls for a United Nations-mandated ceasefire in the Iran–Iraq War were made three days after the liberation of Khorramshahr, and officials of both countries began discussing such a possibility. The anniversary of the Liberation of Khorramshahr is observed in Iran annually. Sevom Khordad, an Iran air defense system is named after the battle. The Liberation of Khorramshahr is a subject of some war time films such as the Pasdaran Army Television Unit's recapturing Khorramshahr in 1983, Another Growth by Homayun Purmand in 1982, Forty witnesses and the second narrative: liberation of Khorramshahr by Kiumarth Monazzah. He went on to fight in the Siege of Abadan and lead Iran forces to recapture Khorramshahr, but he died on 24 May 1982, in a plane crash, before the eventual liberation of the city." .

ns1:59481 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 Africa Cup of Nations Final was a association football match that took place on 10 February 2008 at the Accra Sports Stadium in Accra, Ghana, to determine the winner of the 2008 Africa Cup of Nations, the association football championship of Africa organized by the Confederation of African Football (CAF). It was contested by Cameroon national football team and Egypt national football team. Egypt national football team won the title for the sixth time by beating Cameroon national football team 1–0." .

ns1:59494 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Archery World Cup is the eleventh edition of the FITA Archery World Cup organised annually by the World Archery Federation. The World Cup finals will be held in Odense, Denmark on 24 September–25 September 2016. The first preliminary leg was in Shanghai, China from 26 April 2016 to 1 May, the seco9 Mayd15 Mayn 9–15 May in Medellin, Colombia and the12 Junei19 June on 12–19 June in Antalya, Turkey." .

ns1:59541 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "S7 Airlines Flight 778 (RU778/SBI778) was an Airbus A310-300 on a scheduled domestic passenger flight, flying from Moscow Domodedovo to Irkutsk, when it crashed upon landing at Irkutsk International Airport at 07:44 local time on 09 July 2006 (8 July, 22:44 UTC), killing 125 people. The ITAR-Tass news agency in Russia reported that many children were among the passengers who were flying to a holiday on Lake Baikal, near Irkutsk, about 4,200 kilometres (2,600 mi; 2,300 nmi) east of Moscow." .

ns1:59549 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 5th Chess Olympiad, organized by the FIDE and comprising an open and (unofficial) women's tournament, as well as several events designed to promote the game of chess, took place between 12 July 1933 and 23 July 1933, in Folkestone, United Kingdom." .

ns1:59592 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 Latvian Higher League (Latvian: Virslīga 2011) was the 20th season of top-tier football in Latvia. It began on 15 April 2011 and ended on 05 November 2011. At a meeting on 2011, the Latvian Football Federation decided that they would not be replaced in this year's competition. Source: LMT Virslīga 2011 (in Latvian) sportacentrs.com version: __________________________________________________________________________________________________ Latvian Football Federation version: Players selected by sportacentrs.com: Best foreign player: Daniel Ola (Jūrmala) Best young player (U-21): Arevshat Khachatryan (Gulbene) Best coach: Tamaz Pertia (Daugava Daugavpils/ Olimps/RFS) Surprise of the season: Oļegs Laizāns (FK Ventspils) Player of the season: Jurģis Kalns (Liepājas Metalurgs) Players selected by Latvian Football Federation: Best goalkeeper: Germans Māliņš (Skonto Riga) Best defender: Pāvels Mihadjuks (Liepājas Metalurgs) Best midfielder: Oļegs Laizāns (FK Ventspils) Best forward: Nathan Júnior (Skonto Riga) Best coach: Sergei Podpaly (FK Ventspils) Top scorer: Nathan Júnior (Skonto Riga) (22 goals) Best young player (U-21): Valērijs Šabala (Skonto Riga) Player of the season: Oļegs Laizāns (FK Ventspils) Players selected by Latvian Football Federation: Best match organization:Jelgava." .

ns1:59649 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Federated Auto Parts 400 was a NASCAR Sprint Cup Series stock car Federated Auto Parts 400 that was held on 06 September 2014, at Richmond Raceway in Richmond, Virginia. Contested over 400 laps, it was the 26th Federated Auto Parts 400 of the 2014 NASCAR Sprint Cup Series, and the final Federated Auto Parts 400 prior to the Chase for the Sprint Cup, to decide the series champion. Brad Keselowski finished first after he dominated the Federated Auto Parts 400, leading 383 of the Federated Auto Parts 400's 400 laps. Richmond Raceway (RIR) is a 0.75 miles (1.21 km), D-shaped, asphalt Federated Auto Parts 400 track located just outside Richmond, Virginia in Henrico County. The Federated Auto Parts 400 was scheduled to start at 7:43 p.m. Eastern time, but started five minutes later with Brad Keselowski leading the field to the green." .

ns1:59722 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Sangarará was fought on 18 November 1780 in Sangarará, Peru, Viceroyalty of Perú, between rebel forces under Túpac Amaru II and Spanish colonial forces under Tiburcio Landa. Sangarará, Peru was the first major conflict between Spanish and rebel forces in the Rebellion of Túpac Amaru II." .

ns1:59725 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Evander Holyfield vs. Michael Moorer II was a professional boxing match contested 08 November 1997 for the WBA and IBF Heavyweight championships. After Riddick Bowe vacated the WBC Heavyweight title in 1992 followed by George Foreman being stripped of both the WBA and IBF Heavyweight titles in 1995, the heavyweight boxing division was in limbo as three different men laid claim to each of the three major heavyweight championships in 1997. Evander Holyfield and Michael Moorer held the WBA and IBF Heavyweight titles respectively, while Lennox Lewis held the WBC version of the title." .

ns1:59778 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Latakia offensive, (called The Descendants of Aisha, Mother of the Believers by Salafist jihadists, and the Operation Liberation of the Coast by the Free Syrian Army and its supporters) was a campaign during the Syrian Civil War launched by rebel groups led by Salafi jihadists in the Latakia Governorate. On 04 August 2013, an estimated 1,500 to 2,000 rebel fighters, 300 of them foreigners, launched an offensive from the rebel-held town of Salma further into the predominantly pro-government Alawite Latakia province." .

ns1:59806 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 Bank of the West Classic was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts. It was the 39th edition of the Silicon Valley Classic, and is a part of the WTA Premier tournaments of the 2010 WTA Tour. It took place at the Taube Tennis Center in Stanford, United States, from July 26 through 01 August 2010." .

ns1:59813 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1980 Volvo Women's Cup was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts at the Ramapo College in Mahwah, New Jersey in the United States, It was part of the Colgate Series circuit of the 1980 WTA Tour and classified as a category AA event. It was the third edition of the tournament and was held from 18 August 1980 through 24 August 1980." .

ns1:59850 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Ulsan (Korean: 울산성 전투; Hanja: 蔚山城戰鬪; RR: Ulsanseong Jeontu) was an unsuccessful Ming dynasty-Joseon attempt to capture Ulsan from the Japanese. The siege lasted from 26 January to 19 February 1598. Yang Hao, Ma Gui, and Gwon Yul met up at Gyeongju on the 26 29 January 1598 and marched on Ulsan with an army of 50,000. The allied army reached Ulsan on 29 January 1598. According to one source, of the original 10,000 garrisoned at Ulsan, less than 1,000 survived the siege." .

ns1:59908 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 China Open was a professional ranking snooker tournament that took place between 24 March 2008 and 30 March 2008 at the Beijing University Students' Gymnasium in Beijing, China. This was the penultimate ranking event of the Snooker season 2007/2008, preceding the 2008 World Snooker Championship." .

ns1:59920 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The athletics at the 2000 Summer Olympics – women's 100 metres as part of the athletics at the 2000 Summer Olympics were held at the Stadium Australia on 22 September 2000 and 23 September 2000." .

ns1:60004 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1944 United States presidential election in Massachusetts took place on 07 November 1944, as part of the 1944 United States presidential election, which was held throughout all contemporary 48 states. Massachusetts voted for the Democratic nominee, incumbent President of the United States Franklin Delano Roosevelt of New York, over the Republican nominee, Governor Thomas E. Dewey of New York. Massachusetts voted for Al Smith in 1928, and for Franklin Delano Roosevelt in his three election campaigns preceding 1944. Roosevelt’s 1944 victory thus marked the fifth straight win for the Democratic Party in Massachusetts, although Roosevelt’s victory margin was slightly reduced from 1940 United States presidential election in Massachusetts." .

ns1:60048 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Tacámbaro was a battle of the Second French intervention in Mexico which took place on 11 April 1865 in the state of Michoacán in western Mexico. The state of Michoacán lies within 100 miles of Mexico City. On 3 11 April 1865, 250–300 Belgians occupied Tacámbaro." .

ns1:60142 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The men's ice hockey tournament at the ice hockey at the 1924 Winter Olympics in Chamonix-Mont-Blanc, France, was the 2nd ice hockey at the Olympic Games, also serving as the 2nd World Championships. The competition was held from Monday, 28 January 1924, to Sunday, 03 February 1924. A total of 82(*) ice hockey players from eight nations competed at the Chamonix-Mont-Blanc Games: (*) NOTE: Only counts players who participated in at least one game. The decisive match between Canada men's national ice hockey team and the United States was refereed by Paul Loicq, president of the International Ice Hockey Federation." .

ns1:60167 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1975 Dutch Open was a 1975 Grand Prix circuit tennis tournament staged in Hilversum. The tournament was played on outdoor clay court and was held from 20 July 1975 to 27 July 1975." .

ns1:60178 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Amara, also known as the Second Battle of Qurna (31 May 1915 - 03 June 1915), was a military engagement between the forces of the British and Ottoman Empires during the Mesopotamian campaign of World War I. Shaitan entered Thi Qar on the morning of 03 June 1915, sinking a steamer carrying Ottoman troops and passing through the town without drawing any fire." .

ns1:60181 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1949 Central Queensland cyclone was an unnamed tropical cyclone that struck the Central Queensland coast in Australia on 02 March 1949. On 28 February 1949, reports emerged that a tropical cyclone had developed and was 100 miles east-south-east of Willis Island, moving in a south-south-westerly direction. The Communist Party of Australia also attempted to proceed with a scheduled open-air meeting in the city centre on 28 02 March 1949, which led to the infamous 1949 Rockhampton riot when around 1500 protestors gate-crashed the meeting and showered supporters with various projectiles including rotten eggs, rotten fruit, flour bombs and stink bombs before breaking through police cordons and assaulting party supporters." .

ns1:60218 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2005 DFL-Ligapokal Final decided the winner of the 2005 DFL-Ligapokal, the 9th edition of the reiterated DFL-Ligapokal, a knockout association football cup competition. The match was played on 2005 at the Leipzig Arena in Leipzig. FC Schalke 04 won the match 1–0 against VfB Stuttgart for their 1st title." .

ns1:60233 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Tsotsin-Yurt operation was a zachistka-type (Russian: зачистка) operation by Russian Spetsnaz forces in Tsotsin-Yurt, Chechen Republic, from 30 December 2001 to 03 January 2002, during the Second Chechen War. The War of Dagestan was used as a casus belli to trigger the Second Chechen War, when Russian federal troops entered Chechen Republic and ended its independence. According to the April 2002 Amnesty International report, \"The fate and whereabouts of the 11 people named in the original urgent action, who were detained by Russian security forces during the 30 December 2001-03 January 2002 raid on Tsotsin-Yurt, is unknown.\"" .

ns1:60236 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 Kobalt Tools 500 was a NASCAR Sprint Cup Series race held at Atlanta Motor Speedway in Hampton, Georgia on 07 March 2010. This race also marked as the longest race in Atlanta Motor Speedway history, as the race was extended to 341 laps total, surpassing the 330 laps of the 2009 Kobalt Tools 500 Kobalt Tools 500." .

ns1:60246 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2002 WUSA Founders Cup, also known as Founders Cup II, was the second championship match in Women's United Soccer Association history, played between the Carolina Courage and the Washington Freedom to decide the champion of the league's 2002 WUSA season. The game was played at Herndon Stadium in Atlanta, Georgia on 2002." .

ns1:60260 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Democratic Party (HK) leadership election was held on 04 December 2016 for the 30-member 12th Central Committee of the Democratic Party in Hong Kong, including chairperson and two vice-chairperson posts. Incumbent chairwoman Emily Lau, former member of the Legislative Council of Hong Kong who retired in October 2016 announced her retirement from the post and was succeeded by Wu Chi-wai, member of the Legislative Council for Kowloon East, who was elected unopposed, which made it the first uncontested leadership election since 2010. Although the party had earlier passed a motion to create a party leader post in order to divide the roles between party organ and parliamentary caucus, Legislative Council member Wu Chi-wai became the only candidate for the chairmanship while another interested candidate, incumbent vice-chairman Lo Kin-hei chose to run for vice-chairmanship with incumbent secretary Li Wing-shing." .

ns1:60309 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Seminara took place on 21 April 1503, near Seminara and Gioia Tauro, Calabria, between the French troops under the command of Bérault Stuart d'Aubigny and the Spanish troops commanded by Don Fernando de Andrade during the Second Italian War. Faced with the arrival of Spanish reinforcements he lifted the siege of Terranova Sappo Minulio and marched towards San Martino, meanwhile Fernando de Andrade gathered all his troops in Seminara. After negotiating, both sides agreed to do battle the following Friday 21 April 1503. On the agreed date both armies met up half way between Seminara and Gioia Tauro." .

ns1:60340 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Montevideo occurred during the War of Independence of Brazil, during which the Brazilian Army under Carlos Frederico Lecor attempted to capture the city of Montevideo in Cisplatina Province (now Uruguay) from the Portuguese Army of Álvaro da Costa de Sousa Macedo. The siege lasted from 20 January 1823 until 08 March 1824 when the Portuguese surrendered to the Brazilian forces." .

ns1:60364 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Arab conquest of Armenia was a part of the Muslim conquests after the death of Muhammad in 632 CE. Persian Armenia had fallen to the Arab Rashidun Caliphate by 645 CE. According to the Arabic sources, the first Arab expedition reached Armenia in 639/640, on the heels of their conquest of the Levant from the Byzantine Empire and the start of the Muslim conquest of Persia." .

ns1:60395 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Minecon (alternatively capitalised as MineCon or MINECON), later renamed to Minecon Earth, Minecon Live, Minecraft Live and Minecraft Festival, is an annual interactive live stream and fan convention about the video game Minecraft, hosted by Mojang Studios. The first gathering in 2010 was known as MinecraftCon. The Minecon 2011 convention was held in Las Vegas and celebrated the launch of the game with Minecraft-related discussion panels and gaming areas. MinecraftCon 2010 was a gathering of more than 30 people at Bellevue, Washington on 31 August. This was not technically a convention, but some count it as the first Minecraft convention. 5,000 people attended the first official MineCon convention held in Las Vegas on 18 November 2011. The convention focused on celebrating the game's release and hosted Minecraft-related discussion panels, and invited people to play the game with others while at the convention. On 2 August 2012, Mojang Studios announced that the 2012 MineCon convention would take place in the Paris Disneyland Park on 24 November and 25 November. Mojang Studios made several announcements at the 2012 convention. During the opening ceremony on 4 July 2015 it was announced by Guinness World Records that MineCon won the world record for the most attendance for a convention that is solely for one game, selling 10,000 tickets. On 8 August 2017, Mojang Studios announced that MINECON would be taking the form of an interactive livestream on 18 November 2011 2017, dubbed \"MINECON Earth\". During the event, Mojang Studios announced Minecraft, a dungeon-crawler spin-off of Minecraft, and features of the upcoming \"Village & Pillage\" update, which was released in April 2019. On 28 September 2019, Mojang Studios announced Minecraft Festival, an in-person event that was to be held25 September 2020n27 S25 Septembert27 Septemberon 25–27 September 2020." .

ns1:60414 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "BBC World News America is a British/American current affairs news program produced by the BBC's BBC News division, which premiered on 01 October 2007. Produced out of the BBC's Washington, D.C. bureau, Katty Kay – who originally appeared on the program through her role as a Washington-based correspondent for BBC News – has served as the main presenter since 2011, replacing original co-anchor Matt Frei. From its debut until 25 March 2011, the program originally maintained a one-hour format, airing as a simulcast on BBC America in the United States and BBC World internationally, with the first half-hour (on tape delay) being shown overnights on the BBC News in the U.K." .

ns1:60499 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Second Battle of Bapaume was a battle of the First World War that took place at Bapaume in France, from 21 August 1918 to 03 September 1918. On 8 21 August 1918, the Hundred Days Offensive commenced on the Western Front and it would prove to be the last major campaign of the First World War. As a result of the operations of 21 August 1918–23 August, the frontline had been established to the east of the Albert-Arras railway line with all but one of the ridges to the south and west of Bapaume held by IV Corps." .

ns1:60561 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Star Awards 2011 (Chinese: 红星大奖 2011) was a double television award ceremony held in Singapore. It is part of the annual Star Awards organised by MediaCorp for the two free-to-air channels, MediaCorp Channel 8 and MediaCorp Channel U. As like the Star Awards 2010, this year's Star Awards were broadcast in two live-telecast shows on Channel 8, on 17 April and 24 April. Golden Splendour) was broadcast live on 2011 at the Grand Ballroom in Resorts World Sentosa, which was the main award ceremony, followed by a post-show party (telecast on Channel U) after the ceremony concluded. The first show of 2011 won the Best Variety Special on the following Star Awards 2012; it was Star Awards' third win for the Best Variety Special, and the second consecutive time on doing so." .

ns1:60565 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Action of 24 October 1798 was a minor naval engagement of the French Revolutionary Wars, fought between a British Royal Navy frigate and two ships of the Batavian Navy. The Dutch ships were intercepted in the North Sea within hours of leaving port, 30 nautical miles (56 km) northwest of the Texel, by the British ship HMS Sirius. Departing on the night of 23/24 October 1798, the Dutch ships made rapid progress and at 08:00 were 30 nautical miles (56 km) northwest of the Texel, sailing westwards towards the English Channel." .

ns1:60615 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2000 DFS Classic was a women's tennis tournament played on grass court at the Edgbaston Priory Club in Birmingham, United Kingdom that was part of WTA Tier III tournaments of the 2000 WTA Tour. It was the 19th edition of the tournament and was held from 12 June 2000 until 19 June 2000." .

ns1:60628 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1983 World Series was the World Series of Major League Baseball's (MLB) 1983 Major League Baseball season. The 80th edition of the World Series, it was a best-of-seven playoff played between the American League champion Baltimore Orioles and the National League champion Philadelphia Phillies. This is Baltimore Orioles most recent World Series championship, and also their most recent appearance in a World Series. Palmer is also, the only man to have played with the Orioles in each of their World Series appearances (1966, 1969, 1970, 1971, 1979, and 1983). Game 4 of the 1984 World Series was played under overcast skies at Tiger Stadium, and game 6 of the 1987 World Series (last World Series game with a daytime start) was played indoors at the Hubert H. Humphrey Metrodome. 1983 World Series (4–1): Baltimore Orioles (A.L.) Pitcher Larry Andersen was the only person to play for the Phillies in the 1983 World Series and the 1993 World Series against Toronto. Also, this series proved to be Cal Ripken's only World Series appearance even though the Orioles shortstop would become baseball's iron-man as he would surpass Lou Gehrig by playing in his 2,131st consecutive game on September 6, 1995." .

ns1:60711 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1989 Virginia Slims of Florida was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts at the Polo Club of Boca Raton in Boca Raton in the United States and was part of the Category 5 tier of the 1989 WTA Tour. The tournament ran from 13 March 1989 through 19 March 1989." .

ns1:60761 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Fort Stanwix (also known at the time as Fort Schuyler) in 1777 began on 02 August 1777 and ended 22 August 1777. Fort Stanwix, in the western part of the Mohawk River Valley, was then the primary defense point for the Continental Army against British and Indian forces aligned against them in the American Revolutionary War. One attempt at relief was thwarted early in the siege when a force of New York militia under Nicholas Herkimer was stopped in the August 6 Battle of Oriskany by a detachment of St. Leger's forces. Fort Stanwix occupied a strategic western portage known as the Oneida Carrying Place (site of modern Rome, New York) between the Mohawk River, which flowed southeast to the Hudson River, and Wood Creek, whose waters ultimately led to Lake Ontario. When the American Revolutionary War widened in 1776 to include the frontier areas between New York and the Province of Quebec, the site again became strategically important. In 1777, Continental Army Major General Philip Schuyler ordered the 3rd New York Regiment under the command of Colonel Peter Gansevoort to occupy and rehabilitate the fort as a defense against British and Native incursions from Quebec. Arnold convinced Hon Yost, a member of the King's Royal Regiment of New York who grew up with many of the Mohawk Indians attacking Fort Stanwix, to spread rumors that large numbers of Americans, under the command of \"The Dark Eagle\", were about to descend on St. Leger's camp. Fort Stanwix itself saw little action after the siege, although it was a dangerous and unpopular posting because of regular harassment by Loyalists and hostile Indians." .

ns1:60797 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Kemetism (also Kemeticism; both from the Egyptian kmt, usually voweled Kemet, the native name of ancient Egypt), also sometimes referred to as Neterism (from nṯr (Coptic ⲛⲟⲩⲧⲉ noute) \"deity\"), or Egyptian Neopaganism, is a revival of ancient Egyptian religion and related expressions of religion in classical and late antiquity, emerging during the 1970s. There are several main groups, each of which takes a different approach to its beliefs, ranging from eclectic to polytheistic reconstructionism; however, all of these can be identified as belonging to three strains, including: reconstructed Kemetism (adopting a philological and scholarly approach), a syncretic approach, and a more novel synthesis tending toward monotheism, Kemetic Orthodoxy." .

ns1:60816 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1998–99 Albanian Cup (Albanian: Kupa e Shqipërisë) was the forty-seventh season of Albania's annual cup competition. It began on 01 August 1998 with the First Round and ended on 22 May 1999 with the Final match. KS Apolonia were the defending champions, having won their first Albanian Cup 1997–98 Albanian Cup." .

ns1:60904 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Mazocoba, or the Mazocoba massacre, was a major engagement of the Yaqui Wars that was fought in Sonora, Mexico. On 18 January 1900, a Mexican Army expedition encountered hundreds of Yaqui renegades about twenty miles east of Guaymas." .

ns1:60920 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Winterthur (27 May 1799) was an important action between elements of the Army of the Danube and elements of the Habsburg army, commanded by Friedrich Freiherr von Hotze, during the War of the Second Coalition, part of the French Revolutionary Wars. The small town of Winterthur lies 18 kilometers (11 mi) northeast of Zürich, in Helvetic Republic. By mid-27 May 1799, the Austrians had wrested control of parts of Helvetic Republic from the French as forces under the command of Hotze and Count Heinrich von Bellegarde pushed them out of the Grisons. The locale of a Roman settlement from 200 to 400, and the site of a medieval battle in 919, its location at seven crossroads gave it strategic importance in the effort to control north–south and east–west communication in the early days of the War of the Second Coalition. Masséna sent newly promoted General of Division Michel Ney to Winterthur on 27 May 1799 to take command of the center. On the morning of 27 May 1799, Hotze assembled his force into three columns and marched toward Winterthur. The Armies of Helvetic Republic and the Danube were not ready to take on Charles; Masséna's forces were not prepared for a battle on the scale required in facing Charles' entire army, and he needed the defences offered by Zürich to mount a proper line against the impending Austrian attack. Finally, the battle at Winterthur made possible the victory at Zürich." .

ns1:60937 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Buttington was fought in 893 between a Viking army and an alliance of Anglo-Saxons and Welsh. The combined army laid siege to the Vikings who had built a fortification at Buttington. The combined Anglo-Saxon and Welsh army forced the Vikings to the northwest, where they were finally overtaken and besieged at Buttington. The English and Welsh army came up the River Severn, and besieged all sides of the fortification (at Buttington) where the Vikings had taken refuge. The annals say that the Vikings came up the Severn from the Thames making the most likely candidate for the location of the battle as present-day Buttington, Welshpool in the county of Powys, Wales. The Buttington Oak was said to have been planted by local people to commemorate the battle and survived until February 2018." .

ns1:60969 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Breakthrough Experience Tour was the fifth concert tour by American recording artist Mary J. Blige in 2006, the tour supports her multi-platinum seventh studio album, The Breakthrough (2005). The official tour began in 14 July 2006 and continued through 10 September 2006, visiting over 30 cities across North America." .

ns1:61046 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1968 Race of Champions was a non-Championship motor race, run to Formula One rules, held on 17 March 1968 at Brands Hatch circuit in Kent, England. The race was run over 50 laps of the circuit, and was dominated by Bruce McLaren in a McLaren M7A." .

ns1:61125 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2001 Stockholm Open was a men's tennis tournament played on indoor hard courts at the Kungliga tennishallen in Stockholm, Sweden and was part of the International Series of the 2001 ATP Tour. It was the 33rd edition of the tournament and ran from 22 October 2001 through 28 October 2001." .

ns1:61127 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Fort Capuzzo (Ridotta Capuzzo) was a fort in the colony of Italian Libya, near the Libyan-Egyptian border, next to the Italian Frontier Wire. The Litoranea Balbo (Via Balbia) ran south from Bardia to Fort Capuzzo, 13 km (8 mi) inland, west of Sallum, then east across the Egyptian frontier to the port, over the coastal escarpment. The wire comprised four lines of 1.7 m (5.6 ft) high stakes in concrete bases, laced with barbed wire 320 km (200 mi) long, just inside the border from El Ramleh on the Gulf of Sallum, past Fort Capuzzo to Sidi Omar, then south, slightly to the west of the 25th meridian east, the Libya-Egypt and Libya-Sudan borders. Three large forts at Amseat (Fort Capuzzo), Scegga (Fort Maddalena) and Giarabub and six smaller ones at El Ramleh on the gulf of Sallum, at Sidi Omar, Sceferzen, Vescechet, Garn ul Grein and El Aamara along the wire. On 16 December, during Operation Compass (9 December 1940 – 9 February 1941) the 4th Armoured Brigade of the Western Desert Force captured Sidi Omar and the Italians withdrew from Sallum, Fort Capuzzo and the other frontier forts; Number 9 Field Supply Depot was established at the fort for the 7th Armoured Division. Three Italian battalions with artillery from the 102nd Motorised Division Trento took over the area between Sallum, Musaid and Fort Capuzzo. After the Second Battle of El Alam23 October 1942tob11 01 November 1942 1942) Fort Capuzzo changed hands for the last time. German rearguards retired from Sidi Barrani on 9 November; next day, the 22nd Armoured Brigade advanced on Fort Capuzzo from the south and by 11 November, the last Axis troops had withdrawn from the frontier, despite orders to hold the area from Halfaya to Sallum and Sidi Omar." .

ns1:61130 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Caribbe is the Canadian Armed Forces contribution to the elimination of illegal trafficking in the Caribbean Sea and the eastern Pacific Ocean by organized crime. Operation Caribbe began in 01 November 2006. In January 2012, Operation Caribbe was folded into the umbrella operation, Operation Martillo, which is a joint multinational effort led by the United States to eliminate illicit trafficking in the Caribbean Sea, the eastern Pacific Ocean and the coastal areas of the Central American nations. The Canadian forces deployed to Operation Caribbe thus far have been warships from the Royal Canadian Navy and CP-140 Auroras and crew from the Royal Canadian Air Force. In March 2015, as part of Operation Caribbe, Goose Bay and the US frigate USS Kauffman along with Shawinigan, intercepted a vessel in the Caribbean Sea carrying 1,017 kilograms (2,242 lb) of cocaine." .

ns1:61149 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 European Youth Summer Olympic Festival was held in Trabzon, Turkey, between 23 July 2011 and 29 July 2011. There were nine sports at the 2011 Festival, two branches, table tennis and water polo, less than compared with the 2007 Youth Olympic Festival's programme. For the 2011 European Youth Summer Olympic Festival's eight events except cycling, eleven sports venues were needed in and around Trabzon." .

ns1:61182 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1997 Nebelhorn Trophy took place between 26 August 1997 and 29, 1997. It is an international senior-level figure skating competition organized by the Deutsche Eislauf-Union and held annually in Oberstdorf, Germany." .

ns1:61241 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 UEFA Champions League Final was the final match of the 2015–16 UEFA Champions League, the 61st season of Europe's premier club association football tournament organised by UEFA, and the 24th season since it was renamed from the European Champion Clubs' Cup to the UEFA Champions League. It was played at the Giuseppe Meazza Stadium stadium in Milan, Italy, on 28 May 2016, between Spanish teams Real Madrid CF and Atlético Madrid, in a repeat of the 2014 final. The Giuseppe Meazza Stadium, officially known as Stadio Giuseppe Meazza, was announced as the venue of the final at the UEFA Executive Committee meeting in Nyon, Switzerland, on 18 September 2014, the fourth European Cup/Champions League final hosted at the stadium following those in 1965, 1970 and 2001. The 2016 final marked the first time a final has been held at the Giuseppe Meazza Stadium when neither of its tenants were able to win the competition, as Milan and Internazionale both failed to qualify for any European competitions after their performance in the 2014–15 Serie A. Real Madrid CF reached a record 14th final after a 1–0 aggregate win against Manchester City, with a chance to win a record 11th title. Their manager, Zinedine Zidane, who scored the winning goal for Real Madrid CF in the 2002 final, was aiming to become the seventh man to win the Champions League as both player and manager, joining Miguel Muñoz, Giovanni Trapattoni, Johan Cruyff, Carlo Ancelotti, Frank Rijkaard and Pep Guardiola. Atlético Madrid reached their third European Cup final after defeating Bayern Munich on away goals (2–2 on aggregate). Apart from the 2014 final, won by Real Madrid CF 4–1 after extra time, the only previous Madrid Derby matches in European competitions were in the 1958–59 European Cup semi-finals, where Real Madrid CF won 2–1 in a replay, after a 2–2 aggregate draw, and in the 2014–15 UEFA Champions League quarter-finals, where Real Madrid CF won 1–0 on aggregate. English referee Mark Clattenburg was announced as the final referee by UEFA on 10 May 2016. This was Real Madrid CF's 14th appearance in a European Cup/Champions League final. On the other hand, Atlético Madrid became the only team to lose in their first three final appearances. He had not scored in the competition since scoring the equaliser against Atlético Madrid in the 2014 final." .

ns1:61265 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006 ABN AMRO World Tennis Tournament was a men's tennis tournament played on indoor hard courts. It was the 34th edition of the event known that year as the Rotterdam Open, and was part of the ATP International Series Gold of the 2006 ATP Tour. It took place at the Rotterdam Ahoy in Rotterdam, Netherlands, from 20 February through 2006." .

ns1:61278 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "In 01 July 2014, the Armed Forces of the Democratic Republic of the Congo and United Nations forces launched an 2014 North Kivu offensive against rebel groups in the Masisi Territory and Walikale Territory, part of the North Kivu province, in the eastern Democratic Republic of the Congo (DRC)." .

ns1:61318 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Tokyo gubernatorial election took place on 31 July 2016 to elect the successor to Governor Yoichi Masuzoe, who submitted his resignation to the Tokyo Metropolitan Assembly on 15 June 2016. Yuriko Koike, a former Minister of Defence and incumbent member for the Tokyo 10th district in the House of Representatives, announced her candidacy despite not receiving an endorsement from the Liberal Democratic Party. Ultimately, the Democratic Party and other opposition parties (including the Japanese Communist Party and Social Democratic parties) endorsed veteran journalist Shuntarō Torigoe." .

ns1:61383 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Grdelica train bombing occurred on 12 April 1999, when two missiles fired by US aircraft hit a passenger train while it was passing across a railway bridge over the Južna Morava river in the Grdelica gorge, some 300 kilometres (190 mi) south of Belgrade, Serbia. The bombing occurred during NATO bombing of Yugoslavia, a NATO operation against the Federal Republic of Yugoslavia (FRY) allegedly aimed at forcing the FRY government to end the repression of Albanians in Kosovo and Metohija." .

ns1:61389 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Oregon gubernatorial special election took place on 08 November 2016, to elect the Governor of Oregon, concurrently with the 2016 U.S. presidential election, as well as elections for the U.S. Senate and U.S. House of Representatives, and various state and local elections. Incumbent Democratic governor Kate Brown, who as Oregon Secretary of State succeeded to the governorship, ran for election to the office." .

ns1:61390 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1869 New Jersey vs. Rutgers football game occurred between the Princeton Tigers football (now Princeton University) and Rutgers Scarlet Knights football played on 06 November 1869. Part of the first season of college football, the game took place on 06 November 1869 at a field on College Avenue (now the site of the College Avenue Gymnasium) in New Brunswick, New Jersey." .

ns1:61422 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 55th Edition 2000 Vuelta a España (Tour of Spain), a long-distance bicycle stage race and one of the three grand tours, was held from 26 August 2000 to 17 September 2000. It consisted of 21 stages covering a total of 2,904 km (1,804 mi), and was won by Roberto Heras of the Kelme cycling team." .

ns1:61437 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Hagenau (1705) (27 September 1705 – 05 October 1705) was a siege of the War of the Spanish Succession. An Holy Roman Empire army under the command of Field Marshal Johann Karl von Thüngen, besieged and captured the French fortified city of Haguenau on the banks of the Rhine river in Alsace. On 28 August, the Holy Roman Empire forces, now commanded by the Margrave of Baden and reinforced by 16,000 Prussian and Palatinate troops in 10 Prussian infantry battalions and 20 cavalry squadrons, breached the Lines of Haguenau, a French line of field fortifications, advanced into Lower Alsace and laid siege, first to Drusenheim and then to Haguenau on 27 September 1705, the latter falling on 05 October 1705. Leaving 400 men and the sick and wounded inside to distract the Allies, the French governor Peri escaped Haguenau under the cover of night toward Saverne with some 2,000 of his troops, the incomplete Holy Roman Empire investment of 20 squadrons of Prussian and Württemberger infantry failing to stop them." .

ns1:61465 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Yoav (also called Operation Ten Plagues or Operation Yo'av) was an Israeli military operation carried out from 15 October 1948–22 October 1948 in the Negev Desert, during the 1948 Arab–Israeli War." .

ns1:61502 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Burkinabé presidential election, 2015 were held in Burkina Faso on 29 November 2015. The presidential election was won by Roch Marc Christian Kaboré of the People's Movement for Progress, who received 53% of the vote in the first round, avoiding the need for a second round. Of the 14 remaining candidates, Roch Marc Christian Kaboré and Zéphirin Diabré, both of whom served in the government under Compaoré but later broke with him and went into opposition, were viewed as the leading contenders." .

ns1:61522 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 21st People's Choice Awards, honoring the best in popular culture for 1994, were held on 05 March 1995, at Universal Studios Hollywood, in Universal City, California. They were hosted by Tim Daly and Annie Potts, and broadcast on CBS." .

ns1:61524 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "East Pakistan Air Operations (1971) covers activity of the Pakistan Air Force and Pakistan Army Aviation units in erstwhile East Pakistan during the Bangladesh Liberation War and the interdiction, air defense, ground support, and logistics missions flown by the Bangladesh Air Force, Indian Air Force and Indian Navy Aviation wing in support of the Indian Army and Mukti Bahini in the eastern theater of the Indo-Pakistani conflict of 1971 . This unit had launched attacks on targets in Bangladesh on 3 15 December 1971, prior to the start of formal combat between India and Pakistan, although the first of the engagements between the opposing airpowers occurred before the formal declaration of hostilities. Indian Air units commenced operations from 4 15 December 1971 in the eastern theater, by 7 December, Tejgaon airport was put out of operation, grounding the PAF in East Pakistan. Indian units and Kilo Flight continued flying missions over Bangladesh until the unconditional surrender of Pakistan Forces to the joint Bangladesh and Indian Forces command on 16 15 December 1971. PAF operational effectiveness suffered to some degree because most Bengali pilots and technicians (almost 50% of the 1222 PAF personnel in East Pakistan) had been grounded during the political unrest of 29 March 1971. Pakistan Eastern Command had planned an operation named \"Blitz\" in February 1971 to counter the Bengali political movement, and the 13th Frontier Force and 22nd Baluch battalions had arrived in East Pakistan from Karachi between 27 February 1971 and 1 29 March 1971, via PIA aircraft, before the Pakistani Air Force took over Tejgaon Airport administration as part of the newly planned operation. Air Chief Marshal Pratap Chandra Lal had assembled units from both the Central and Eastern Air commands in Eastern command bases around East Pakistan for the campaign by 15 December 1971." .

ns1:61556 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2002–2004 SARS outbreak was an epidemic involving severe acute respiratory syndrome (severe acute respiratory syndrome) caused by severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus (severe acute respiratory syndrome-CoV or severe acute respiratory syndrome-CoV-1). However, several severe acute respiratory syndrome cases were reported until 19 May 2004. On 16 November 2002, an outbreak of severe acute respiratory syndrome (severe acute respiratory syndrome) began in China's Guangdong province, bordering Hong Kong. Early in the epidemic, the Chinese government discouraged its press from reporting on severe acute respiratory syndrome, delayed reporting to WHO, and initially did not provide information to Chinese outside Guangdong province, where the disease is believed to have originated. On 19 April, Premier of the People's Republic of China Wen Jiabao announced that there would be severe consequences for local officials who did not report severe acute respiratory syndrome cases in a timely and accurate manner, signaling a major change in policy. They were the first two high-rank officials in the People's Republic of China to be dismissed because of the fallout of the epidemic. In addition, the epidemic strain of severe acute respiratory syndrome that caused at least 774 deaths worldwide by June 2003 has not been seen outside of a laboratory since then.\"" .

ns1:61565 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2019 FIL World Luge Championships took place under the auspices of the International Luge Federation at the Winterberg bobsleigh, luge, and skeleton track in Winterberg, Germany from 25 to 27 January 2019." .

ns1:61614 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006 Dakar Rally, also known as the 2006 Paris-Dakar Rally, was the 28th running of the Dakar Rally event. It started from Lisbon, Portugal, and passed through Spain, Morocco, Mali, Mauritania, Guinea, before finishing in Senegal. Stephane Peterhansel won the seventh stage between Zouerat and Atar in Mauritania but Luc Alphand, who took second place, moved into the overall lead. Alphand also won the thirteenth stage between Guinea and Senegal, which was marred by the death of a 10-year-old boy who was hit by a car as he crossed the road. Their experience was documented in the 2006 television miniseries Race to Dakar and an accompanying book." .

ns1:61616 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 season of the Women's Super League was the fifth season of the 2015 FA WSL, the top-level women's association football league of England. The season was played from 25 March 2015 to 04 October 2015. As part of a two-year expansion plan, the FA WSL will increase to nine teams for the 2016 FA WSL. By the end of the 2015 season two teams (Reading and Doncaster Rovers Belles) were promoted from WSL 2 to FA WSL and only one team (Bristol Academy) relegated to WSL 2, with one team being promoted from Premier League to WSL 2. In December 2014, the 2015 FA WSL announced a two-year plan to expand FA WSL from an eight to 10-team league. This will leave FA WSL with nine teams and WSL 2 with 10 teams for the 2016 FA WSL, and with the process repeated the following year, both FA WSL and WSL 2 will have 10 teams each for the 2017 season." .

ns1:61639 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Champtoceaux, often called the Battle of l'Humeau, was the opening action of the 23-year-long War of the Breton Succession, a dynastic conflict in Brittany which became inevitably embroiled in the Hundred Years War between England and France. The dynastic conflict over the Duchy of Brittany followed the death of John III, Duke of Brittany on April 30, 1341. The idea of English troops rampaging through Brittany and from there into Normandy and other parts of Northern France terrified Philip, and he was resolved to win the war before Edward's troops could arrive. By the time Charles of Blois was ready to move at the start of 22 October 1341, Montfort had captured and garrisoned most of the castles and towns in Eastern Brittany." .

ns1:61643 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Malaysia Cup Final was a association football match which was played on 2015, to determine the champion of the 2015 Malaysia Cup. It was the final of the 89th edition of the Malaysia Cup, competition organised by the Football Association of Malaysia. The final was played between Selangor FA and Kedah FA. Selangor FA won the cup after beating Kedah FA 2–0 with a brace from Ahmad Hazwan Bakri. The final was held at the Shah Alam Stadium." .

ns1:61648 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Japanese amphibious forces gathered to strike at Java, and on 27 February 1942, the main Allied naval force, under Doorman, sailed northeast from Surabaya to intercept a convoy of the Eastern Invasion Force approaching from the Makassar Strait. The Allied force engaged the Japanese in the Java Sea, and the battle raged intermittently from mid-afternoon to midnight as the Allies tried to reach and attack the troop transports of the Java invasion fleet, but they were repulsed by superior firepower. The Battle of the Java Sea ended significant Allied naval operations in Southeast Asia in 1942, and Japanese land forces invaded Java on 28 February. The Japanese now controlled one of the most important food-producing regions (Java), and by conquering the Dutch East Indies, Japan also controlled the fourth-largest oil producing area in the world in 1940." .

ns1:61651 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009–10 DFB-Pokal season came to a close on 15 May 2010 when FC Bayern Munich played defending champions SV Werder Bremen at the Olympiastadion in Berlin. Bayern thrashed Bremen 4-0 with goals from Arjen Robben, Ivica Olić, Franck Ribéry, and Bastian Schweinsteiger." .

ns1:61672 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Hawija clashes relate to a series of violent attacks within Iraq, as part of the 2012–2013 Iraqi protests and Iraqi insurgency (2011–13). On 23 April 2013, an army raid against a protest encampment in the city of Hawija, west of Kirkuk, led to dozens of civilian deaths and the involvement of several insurgent groups in organized action against the government, leading to fears of a return to a wide-scale Sunni–Shia conflict within the country. On 23 April 2013, Iraqi Army units moved against the encampment set up by protesters in Hawija, sparking deadly clashes and reprisal attacks across the country. Despite the overall reduction in violence, at least 25 were killed and 73 injured during the day—figures much higher than the average daily death toll before the Hawija clashes on 23 April 2013. Despite the overall drop in violence across most of Iraq since the 23 April 2013 incidents, heavy clashes continued in the northern city of Mosul." .

ns1:61698 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Hegra Fortress was a 25-day engagement in the 1940 Norwegian Campaign which saw a small force of Norwegian volunteers fighting numerically superior German forces from a fortified position. After initial fighting around the Meråker Line railway line, the Norwegians pulled back into Hegra Fortress and held off further German attacks before surrendering on 05 May 1940 as one of the last Norwegian units active in southern Norway. By the end of the battle the German forces employed against Hegra Fortress consisted chiefly of one infantry battalion at Hegra and one company of Gebirgsjäger in the nearby village of Sona. At 05:30 on 15 April 1940, the Germans attacked the Norwegian positions defending the Hegra railway station, Hegra road bridge and Mælen bridge, supported by artillery fire. In the early hours of 05 May 1940, the situation was such that there was very little food left and water for only a few days, all other Norwegian and Allied forces in all of southern Norway had been withdrawn or had surrendered and Hegra Fortress was the last remaining pocket of resistance south of Nordland." .

ns1:61704 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1964 Belgian Grand Prix was a Formula One motor race held at Circuit de Spa-Francorchamps on 14 June 1964. Jim Clark gained a surprise victory, due to Dan Gurney running out of fuel while leading most of the race, Graham Hill retiring while leading on the last two laps, and had also just managed to hold off Bruce McLaren at the flag." .

ns1:61706 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Cyclone Narelle was a powerful Category 4 tropical cyclone in early 05 January 2013 that brought light rains to areas in South Australia suffering from a drought and heat wave. The storm attained its peak intensity later on 11 January as a Category 4 cyclone with winds of 195 km/h (120 mph). The system steadily weakened and ultimately fell below tropical cyclone strength on 15 January 2013 well to the west of Geraldton. Following the development of an eye early on 9 January, the Bureau of Meteorology upgraded Narelle to a severe tropical cyclone, with winds estimated at 120 km/h (75 mph). Late on 11 January, Narelle is estimated to have attained its peak intensity with winds of 195 km/h (120 mph) and a barometric pressure of 930 mbar (hPa; 27.46 inHg). Narelle failed to redevelop deep convection and weakened below tropical cyclone status early on 15 January 2013, prompting the Bureau of Meteorology to issue its final bulletin on the cyclone." .

ns1:61751 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1974 European Cup Winners' Cup Final was a association football match of the 1973–74 European Cup Winners' Cup and the 14th European Cup Winners' Cup final. It was contested between 1. FC Magdeburg of East Germany and the defending champions, A.C. Milan of Italy, and was held at De Kuip in Rotterdam, Netherlands. 1. FC Magdeburg won the match 2–0 thanks to goals by Enrico Lanzi (own goal) and Wolfgang Seguin." .

ns1:61815 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1973 Federation Cup was the 11th edition of the most important competition between national teams in women's tennis. 30 nations participated in the tournament, which was held at the Bad Homburg vor der Höhe Tennis Club in Bad Homburg vor der Höhe, West Germany from 30 April 1973–06 May 1973. All ties were played at the Bad Homburg vor der Höhe Tennis Club in Bad Homburg vor der Höhe, West Germany on clay courts. Australia Fed Cup team vs. West Germany." .

ns1:61856 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Chinese Super League (CSL 2007) season or the Kingway brewery Chinese Super League as it was known for sponsorship reasons was the fourth edition since its establishment, the 14th season of professional football as well as being the 46th top-tier league season in China. Starting on 03 March 2007 and ending on 14 November 2007 it saw Changchun Yatai F.C. clinch the league title for the first time in the last game of the season, while Xiamen Lanshi was relegated with two games to spare." .

ns1:61926 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1992 Gent–Wevelgem was the 54th edition of the Gent–Wevelgem cycle race and was held on 08 April 1992. The race started in Ghent and finished in Wevelgem." .

ns1:61942 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2001 Milan – San Remo was the 92nd edition of the monument classic Milan – San Remo and was won by Erik Zabel of Team Telekom. The race was run on 24 March 2001 and the 287 kilometres (178 mi) were covered in 7 hours, 23 minutes and 13 seconds." .

ns1:61944 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Shrewsbury was a battle fought on 21 July 1403, waged between an army led by the Lancastrian King Henry IV and a rebel army led by Henry \"Harry Hotspur\" Percy from Northumberland. The battle, the first in which English archers fought each other on English soil, reaffirmed the effectiveness of the longbow and ended the Percy challenge to King Henry IV of England. Part of the fighting is believed to have taken place at what is now Battlefield, Shropshire, England, three miles north of the centre of Shrewsbury. The rebels then marched towards Shrewsbury, the heavily defended county town in Shropshire." .

ns1:61987 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Ludford Bridge was a largely bloodless confrontation fought in the early years of the Wars of the Roses. The Yorkists retreated towards Ludlow, before making a stand at a fortified position near Ludford, Shropshire on 12 October 1459. Their troops excavated a defensive ditch in a field on the opposite side of the River Teme from Ludlow, near the bridge which gave the battle its name." .

ns1:61992 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1955–56 FA Cup was the 75th staging of the world's oldest football cup competition, the FA Cup. Manchester City F.C. won the competition, beating Birmingham City 3–1 in the 1956 FA Cup Final at Wembley, London. The 1956 FA Cup Final took place on Saturday, 05 May 1956 at Wembley and ended 3–1, with goals scored by Joe Hayes, Bobby Johnstone and Jack Dyson for Manchester City F.C. and Noel Kinsey for Birmingham City." .

ns1:62003 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006 Indianapolis 500 was held at the Indianapolis Motor Speedway in Speedway, Indiana on Sunday 28 May 2006. Sam Hornish Jr. had earned the pole in qualifying with a four-lap average of 228.985 mph (368.516 km/h), and second place Marco Andretti was unanimously voted the Rookie of the Year. Sam Hornish Jr., won the pole with the fastest four-lap qualifying speed of 228.985 mph (368.516 km/h)." .

ns1:62055 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle off Lizard Point was a naval action which took place on 18 February 1637 off the coast of Cornwall, England, during the Eighty Years' War. Horna also proved to be a skillful commander, as he destroyed three major enemy convoys in less than two years, winning the actions of Lizard Point, Mardyck, and the English channel." .

ns1:62070 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1962 Wimbledon Championships took place on the outdoor grass courts at the All England Lawn Tennis and Croquet Club in Wimbledon, London, United Kingdom. The tournament was held from Monday 25 June 1962 until Saturday 07 July 1962. It was the 76th staging of the Wimbledon Championships, and the third Grand Slam tennis event of 1962." .

ns1:62076 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1972 La Flèche Wallonne was the 36th edition of La Flèche Wallonne cycle race and was held on 23 April 1972. The race started in Verviers and finished in Marcinelle." .

ns1:62077 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Richmond Theatre fire occurred in Richmond, Virginia, United States, on Thursday, 26 December 1811. Many members of the upper echelons of Richmond society were in attendance on the night of the fire, and many were killed; among the dead were listed Pages, Nelsons, and Braxtons, all members of some of the First Families of Virginia. Deconsecrated in 1965, it was given by the Medical College of Virginia to the Historic Richmond Foundation, an affiliate of the Association for the Preservation of Virginia Antiquities. A new theater by the same name – Richmond Theatre – was built in Richmond in 1819, at a cost of US $40,000." .

ns1:62079 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "A Burns supper is a celebration of the life and poetry of the poet Robert Burns (25 January 1759 – 21 July 1796), the author of many Scots poems. The suppers are normally held on or near the poet's birthday, 25 January, occasionally known as Robert Burns Day (or Rabbie Burns Day) but more commonly known as Burns Night (Scots: Burns Nicht)." .

ns1:62095 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 European Rowing Championships were held at the Schinias Olympic Rowing and Canoeing Centre, Marathon, Greece, between 16 September 2008 and 2008. They were the 2nd annual event after the decision made in May 2006 by the World Rowing Federation to re-establish the European Rowing Championships." .

ns1:62110 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 European Mixed Team Badminton Championships were held at the Borisoglebsky Arena in Ramenskoye, Russia, from 12 February 2013–17 February 2013, and were organised by the Badminton Europe and the National Badminton Federation of Russia." .

ns1:62130 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 4th G7 summit was held at Bonn between 16 July 1978 and 17 July 1978. The venue for the summit meeting was at the former official residence of the Chancellor of the Federal Republic of Germany in Bonn, the Palais Schaumburg. The Group of Seven (G7) was an unofficial forum which brought together the heads of the richest industrialized countries: France, West Germany, Italy, Japan, the United Kingdom, the United States of America, Canada (since 1976) and the President of the European Commission (starting officially in 1981). The summits were not meant to be linked formally with wider international institutions; and in fact, a mild rebellion against the stiff formality of other international meetings was a part of the genesis of cooperation between France's President Giscard d'Estaing and West Germany's Chancellor Helmut Schmidt as they conceived the first Group of Six (G6) summit in 1975. The G7 is an unofficial annual forum for the leaders of Canada, the European Commission, France, West Germany, Italy, Japan, the United Kingdom and the United States of America." .

ns1:62148 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1988 African Cup of Nations was the 16th edition of the Africa Cup of Nations, the association football championship of Africa (Confederation of African Football). The tournament final was held in Casablanca at Stade Mohamed V. Cameroon national football team won its second championship, beating Nigeria in the final 1−0. The original host was Zambia but after their withdrawal in December 1986 African Cup of Nations due to financial issues, the Confederation of African Football approached Algeria which agreed to host the tournament. Morocco was chosen in the end to host the 1988 Africa Cup of Nations replacing Algeria." .

ns1:62205 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "A furry convention (also furry con or fur con) is a fan convention of members of the furry fandom — people who are interested in the concept of fictional non-human animal characters with human characteristics." .

ns1:62229 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 Recopa Sudamericana was the 19th edition of the Recopa Sudamericana, the association football competition organized by CONMEBOL between the winners of the previous season's two major South American club tournaments, the Copa Libertadores and the Copa Sudamericana. It was contested between Brazilian club S.C. Internacional, the 2010 Copa Libertadores champion, and Argentine club Club Atlético Independiente, the 2010 Copa Sudamericana champion. S.C. Internacional lost the first leg 2–1, but won the second leg 3–1, claiming their second Recopa Sudamericana title." .

ns1:62263 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1999 Italian Grand Prix (formally the 70o Gran Premio Campari d'Italia) was a Formula One motor race held on 12 September 1999 at the Autodromo Nazionale Monza near Monza, Italy. The 53-lap race was won by Heinz-Harald Frentzen, driving a Jordan-Mugen-Honda, after McLaren's Mika Häkkinen, seeking to defend his Drivers' Championship title, spun off while leading comfortably. Going into the race, McLaren's Mika Häkkinen led the Drivers' Championship by a single point from Ferrari's Eddie Irvine, with Jordan's Heinz-Harald Frentzen and Häkkinen's team-mate David Coulthard also still in contention." .

ns1:62267 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Fort Royal was a naval battle fought off Fort-de-France, Martinique in the West Indies during the Anglo-French War on 29 30 April 1781, between fleets of the British Royal Navy and the French Navy. Grasse ordered his fleet to prepare for action on the morning of 29 April, and sailed for Fort-de-France with the convoy ships hugging the coast and the armed ships in battle line." .

ns1:62290 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Bulgarian Supercup was the tenth Bulgarian Supercup match, a association football match which was contested between the \"A\" professional association football group champion, PFC Ludogorets Razgrad, and the runner-up of the Bulgarian Cup, PFC Lokomotiv Plovdiv. As the Bulgarian Cup winner for 2012 was also the team of PFC Ludogorets Razgrad, PFC Lokomotiv Plovdiv therefore played in the Supercup match as the finalist. The match was played on 2012 at the Lazur Stadium in Burgas, Bulgaria. PFC Ludogorets Razgrad secured their 1st Bulgarian Supercup in history." .

ns1:62293 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 United States presidential election in Nevada was part of the 2008 United States presidential election, which took place on 04 November 2008, throughout all 50 states and D.C.. Nevada was won by Democratic nominee Barack Obama with a 12.5% margin of victory. George W. Bush carried Nevada twice in 2000 and 2004 United States presidential election in Nevada while Bill Clinton won the Silver State both times as well in 1992 and 1996. With the anti-Republican sentiment growing nationwide and the fact that personally, McCain barely campaigned in Nevada, Nevada swung deeply into the Democratic column in 2008 as Barack Obama carried the state by 12.50 points over John McCain, receiving 55.15% of the total statewide vote to McCain's 42.65%. Nevada was somewhat of a swing state that voted for the winner of every presidential election from 1912 with the exceptions of 1976 when the state voted for Republican Gerald Ford over Democrat Jimmy Carter and 2016 when Nevada voted for Democrat Hillary Clinton over eventual winner Donald Trump." .

ns1:62339 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Slovenian Football Cup Final was the final match of the 2013–14 Slovenian Cup, the 23rd edition of the Slovenian Football Cup, the top knockout tournament of Slovenian football and the second most important football competition in the country after the Slovenian PrvaLiga championship. The match was played on Wednesday, 2014 at the Bonifika Stadium in Koper, Slovenia and was won by ND Gorica who defeated NK Maribor with the score 2–0. The match was played at the Bonifika Stadium, a 4,047 all-seater stadium in Koper, where the previous final was held. The final was played between NK Maribor and ND Gorica." .

ns1:62351 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 German Open Badminton Championships, officially the Yonex German Open Badminton Championships 2018, was a badminton tournament which took place at innogy Sporthalle in Germany from 6 to 11 March 2018 and had a total purse of $150,000. The 2018 German Open Badminton Championships was the sixth tournament of the 2018 BWF World Tour and also part of the German Open Badminton Championships championships which has been held since 1955. This international tournament was held at innogy Sporthalle in Mülheim an der Ruhr, North Rhine-Westphalia, Germany." .

ns1:62360 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1928 United States presidential election in Alabama took place on 06 November 1928, as part of the 1928 United States presidential election, which was held throughout all contemporary forty-eight states. This result constitutes the second-closest presidential election in Alabama since Reconstruction behind that of 1980, another Republican landslide this time affected by the personal vote in the South for Jimmy Carter." .

ns1:62387 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Grand Prix Cycliste de Québec was the fourth edition of the Grand Prix Cycliste de Québec, a single-day professional road bicycle racing. It was held on 13 September 2013, over a distance of 201.6 km (125.3 mi), starting and finishing in Quebec City." .

ns1:62402 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Dwars door Vlaanderen was the 69th edition of the Dwars door Vlaanderen cycle race and was held on 26 March 2014. The race started in Roeselare and finished in Waregem." .

ns1:62403 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 Heluva Good! Sour Cream Dips at the Glen was a NASCAR Sprint Cup Series stock car Go Bowling at The Glen that was held on 08 August 2010 at Watkins Glen International in Watkins Glen, New York. Contested over 90 laps, it was the twenty-second Go Bowling at The Glen of the 2010 Sprint Cup Series 2010 NASCAR Sprint Cup Series and the second of two road course competitions on the schedule. The Go Bowling at The Glen was won by Juan Pablo Montoya, for the Earnhardt Ganassi Racing team. During the final lap, Kurt Busch was gaining on Montoya, but Montoya maintained his position to win his first Go Bowling at The Glen of the 2010 NASCAR Sprint Cup Series. The Go Bowling at The Glen, the twenty-second out of a total of thirty-six in the 2010 NASCAR Sprint Cup Series, began at 1 p.m. EDT and was televised live in the United States on ESPN." .

ns1:62452 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Reveillon riot between 26 April 1789–29 April 1789 centered in the Saint-Antoine district district of Paris where a factory which produced luxury wallpaper was owned by Jean-Baptiste Réveillon. The factory where the riot took place was unusual in pre-revolutionary France as the factory was guild-free in an era where guilds controlled quality standards. After informal protests on Sunday 26 April 1789, groups of protesters congregated on the Île de la Cité and in the Faubourg Saint-Marcel, Marais, and Saint-Antoine district the next day for a series of protest-marches." .

ns1:62491 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2005 MLS All-Star Game was the 10th Major League Soccer All-Star Game, played on 2005 at Columbus Crew Stadium, now known as Historic Crew Stadium, in Columbus, Ohio between the MLS All-Stars and Fulham F.C. of England." .

ns1:62492 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Senghenydd Colliery Disaster, also known as the Senghenydd explosion (Welsh: Tanchwa Senghennydd), occurred at the Universal Colliery in Senghenydd, near Caerphilly, Glamorgan, Wales, on 14 October 1913. The explosion, which killed 439 miners and a rescuer, is the worst mining accident in the United Kingdom." .

ns1:62497 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1997 Super 12 season was the second season of the Super Rugby, contested by rugby union teams from Australia, New Zealand and South Africa. The winner of each semi final qualified for the final, which was contested by the Auckland Blues and the ACT Brumbies at Eden Park, Auckland. The Auckland Blues won 23 – 7 to win their second Super Rugby title." .

ns1:62515 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1961 United States Grand Prix was a Formula One motor race held on 08 October 1961, at the Watkins Glen International in Watkins Glen, New York. In its third year of searching the United States Grand Prix finally found a home, thanks to Cameron Argetsinger, and truly established Formula One racing in the United States of America." .

ns1:62576 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The R38 class (also known as the A class) of rigid airship was designed for Britain's Royal Navy during the final months of the First World War, intended for long-range patrol duties over the North Sea. Four similar airships were originally ordered by the Admiralty, but orders for three of these (R.39, R.40 and R.41) were cancelled after the armistice with Germany and R.38, the lead ship of the class, was sold to the United States Navy in October 1919 before completion. The United States Navy had decided that it wanted to add rigid airship to its fleet and originally hoped to get two zeppelin as part of war reparations, but these had been deliberately destroyed by their crews in 1919 in actions connected with the scuttling of the German fleet at Scapa Flow." .

ns1:62578 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Coatit was fought on 13 January 1895 between Italy and Ethiopian proxies led by Tigray Province warlord Ras Mengesha Yohannes in what is now Eritrea. It was the opening battle of the First Italo-Ethiopian War, and was a significant victory for the Italians, as they rebuffed an invasion force." .

ns1:62590 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Bazargic, also known as the Battle of Dobrich or the Dobrich epopee (Bulgarian: Добричка епопея), took place between 05 September 1916 and 07 September 1916 between a joint Bulgarian–German–Ottoman force, consisting mainly of the Bulgarian Third Army, and a Kingdom of Romania–Russian force, including a Division of Serbian Volunteers serving under the Russian 47th Corps. The battle was part of the Kingdom of Romania campaign at the end of 1916." .

ns1:62617 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015–16 Cypriot First Division was the 77th season of the Cypriot top-level association football league. It began on 22 August 2015 and ended on 15 May 2016. The league expanded from 12 to 14 teams this season, comprised eleven teams from the 2014–15 Cypriot First Division and three promoted teams from the 2014–15 Second Division. APOEL F.C. were crowned champions for the 25th time and a fourth time in a row, securing the title after beating title rivals AEK Larnaca F.C. 2–0 at home on 28. The relegated team was replaced by 2014–15 Second Division champions Enosis Neon Paralimni FC, runners-up Pafos FC and third-placed team Aris Limassol F.C.." .

ns1:62618 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1997 Paris–Roubaix was the 95th running of the Paris–Roubaix single-day cycling race, often known as the Hell of the North. These are the results for the 1997 edition of the Paris–Roubaix cycling classic, in which Frédéric Guesdon won in a final sprint between eight riders. 13-04-1997: Compiègne–Roubaix, 266 km." .

ns1:62633 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Vuelta a Asturias was the 59th edition of the Vuelta Asturias cycling stage race. The race included three stages: it started on 30 April 2016 with a stage from Oviedo to Alto del Acebo (Cangas del Narcea) and finished on 02 May 2016 with a stage that started in Bueño and then finished back in Oviedo. 30 April 2016 – Oviedo to Alto del Acebo, 152.5 km (94.8 mi) 1 May – Cangas del Narcea to Pola de Lena, 186.7 km (116.0 mi) 02 May 2016 – Bueño to Oviedo, 121.5 km (75.5 mi) The race included four principal classifications, the leaders of which wore jerseys." .

ns1:62644 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Gimcheon Open ATP Challenger was a professional tennis tournament played on hard courts. It took place in Gimcheon, Korea between 01 June 2015 and 07 June 2015." .

ns1:62657 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2004 United States presidential election in Wisconsin took place on 02 November 2004, and was part of the 2004 United States presidential election. On election day, Senator Kerry barely carried the state over President of the United States George W. Bush." .

ns1:62660 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Shenzhen Open (known as 2013 Shenzhen Gemdale Open for sponsorship reason) was a tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts. It was the inaugural edition of the Shenzhen Open (WTA), and was part of the WTA International tournaments of the 2013 WTA Tour. It took place at the Shenzhen Longgang Sports Center in Shenzhen, People's Republic of China, from 31 30 December 2012 to 2013." .

ns1:62669 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1998 German Grand Prix was a Formula One motor race held at the Hockenheimring on 02 August 1998. The 45-lap race was won by Mika Häkkinen for McLaren from pole position, with teammate David Coulthard finishing second and Jacques Villeneuve finishing third for Williams. Mika Häkkinen took pole position ahead of teammate David Coulthard." .

ns1:62685 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1978–79 FIBA European Cup Winner's Cup was the thirteenth edition of FIBA Europe's 2nd-tier level European-wide professional club basketball competition, contested between national domestic cup champions, running from 01 November 1978, to 22 March 1979. It was contested by 20 teams, two less than in the 1977–78 FIBA European Cup Winner's Cup. Pallacanestro Cantù defeated the Dutch League club, Den Bosch, in a final held in Poreč, winning the FIBA Europe European Cup Winner's Cup for the third consecutive time." .

ns1:62692 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The wrestling at the 1920 Summer Olympics – men's Greco-Roman heavyweight was a Greco-Roman wrestling event held as part of the wrestling at the 1920 Summer Olympics programme. A total of 19 wrestlers from 12 nations competed in the event, which was held from August 17 to 1920." .

ns1:62711 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Dancing Man is the name given to a photograph of a man who was filmed dancing on the street in Sydney, Australia, after the end of World War II. On 15 August 1945, a reporter took note of a man's joyful expression and dance and asked him to do it again. The man consented and was caught on motion picture film in an Australian edition of the newsreel British Movietone News. Frank McAlary, a retired barrister claims that he was the man photographed pirouetting in Elizabeth Street, Sydney, on 15 August 1945. The television programme Where Are They Now?, produced by Australia's Seven Network, attempted to solve the mystery of the dancing man's identity." .

ns1:62745 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Šakvice train disaster occurred on 24 December 1953 in Czechoslovakia (now the Czech Republic). A local train was standing at the Šakvice station near Brno, when the Prague-Bratislava express ran into it, resulting in 103 deaths and a further 83 injured." .

ns1:62752 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1590 Siege of Hachigata was the penultimate battle of Toyotomi Hideyoshi's campaigns against the Hōjō clan, during Japan's Sengoku period. The besieging force of 35,000 men was led by Maeda Toshiie and Uesugi Kagekatsu." .

ns1:62770 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Seest fireworks disaster was a disaster that occurred on 03 November 2004, when the N. P. Johnsens Fyrværkerifabrik fireworks warehouse exploded in Seest, a suburb of Kolding, Denmark." .

ns1:62777 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Kieft's War (1643–1645), also known as the Wappinger War, was a conflict between the colony of New Netherland and the Lenape Indians in what is now New York and New Jersey. Colonists from New Netherland descended on the camps at Pavonia on 1643 and killed 120 Indians, including women and children. In the fall of 1643, a force of 1,500 Indians invaded New Netherland and killed many, including Anne Hutchinson, a chief figure in the Antinomian Controversy which ruptured the Massachusetts Bay Colony years earlier." .

ns1:62799 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Led Zeppelin's Led Zeppelin European Tour 1973 was a concert tour of Europe by the English rock band. The tour commenced on 02 March 1973 and concluded on 02 April 1973. Some critics consider Led Zeppelin to have been at their technical peak during this tour, which took place shortly before the release of their Houses of the Holy." .

ns1:62823 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "25th AVN Awards was an event during which Adult Video News (AVN) presented its annual AVN Award to honor the best pornographic movies and adult entertainment products of 2007 in the United States. The ceremony was held on 12 January 2008, at the Mandalay Bay Events Center, in Paradise, Nevada. The two-hour presentation, billed as The AVN Adult Movie Awards, was taped on 12 January 2008 at the Mandalay Bay Events Center in Paradise, Nevada." .

ns1:62827 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "UFC 212 was a mixed martial arts event produced by the Ultimate Fighting Championship held on 03 June 2017, at the Jeunesse Arena in Rio de Janeiro, Brazil." .

ns1:62942 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Paradise Tour was the second concert tour by American singer, Lana Del Rey, in support of her third extended play, Paradise. The tour kicked off on 03 April 2013 at Galaxie Mega Hall in Amnéville, France and visited many major European countries during the spring and summer in 2013. Overall, the tour lasted over eighteen months from 03 April 2013 to 18 October 2014 and visited four continents." .

ns1:62957 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The athletics at the 2016 Summer Olympics – men's 100 metres event at the 2016 Summer Olympics took place between 13 August 2016–14 August 2016 at the Olympic Stadium. Billed as one of the most anticipated races in history, Usain Bolt of Jamaica entered as the world record holder, athletics at the 2012 Summer Olympics – men's 100 metres and the reigning World Champion. NOCs could also use their universality place—each NOC could enter one male athlete regardless of time if they had no male athletes meeting the entry standard for an athletics event—in the 100 metres." .

ns1:62974 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Santa Fe Independent School Dist. v. Doe, 530 U.S. 290 (2000), was a case heard before the Supreme Court of the United States. oral argument were heard 29 March 2000. The court announced its decision on 19 June 2000, holding the policy unconstitutional in a 6–3 decision." .

ns1:62976 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 72nd Golden Globe Awards, honoring the best in film and American television of 2014, was broadcast live from the Beverly Hilton Hotel in Beverly Hills on 11 January 2015, by NBC." .

ns1:63013 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2005 Norwegian Football Cup was the 100th edition of the Norwegian Football Cup. The 2005 Norwegian Football Cup Final was played on 06 November 2005 at Ullevaal stadion in Oslo. Molde FK won their 2nd Norway Championship title after defeating Lillestrøm in the final with the score 4–2 after extra time. Molde FK won the cup by defeating Lillestrøm SK 4–2 in the 2005 Norwegian Football Cup Final after extra time." .

ns1:63014 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2002 Football League Trophy Final (known as the LDV Vans Trophy for sponsorship reasons) was the 19th final of the Football League Trophy – a domestic football cup competition for teams from the Football League Second and Third Division. The match was played at the Millennium Stadium in Cardiff, and was contested by Blackpool F.C. and Cambridge United F.C. on 2002." .

ns1:63043 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The L'Ambiance Plaza was one of the worst disasters in modern Connecticut history. L'Ambiance Plaza was a 16-story residential project under construction in Bridgeport at the corner of Washington Avenue and Coleman Street. Its partially erect frame completely collapsed on 23 April 1987, killing 28 construction workers." .

ns1:63071 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1996 Yosemite Valley landslide occurred on 10 July 1996, near the Happy Isles trailhead in Yosemite Valley, Yosemite National Park, Mariposa County, California. At 6:52 pm PDT Wednesday, 10 July 1996, a large block of granite, with an estimated volume of 78,000 cubic yards, detached from the cliff between Washburn Point and Glacier Point, in Yosemite Valley." .

ns1:63085 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Tajik League is the 26th 2016 Tajik League of Tajik League, the Tajikistan Football Federation's top division of association football. Istiqlol Dushanbe are the defending champions, having won the previous 2016 Tajik League. On 2017, the Tajikistan Football Federation announced that the 2016 Tajik League would involve eight teams, with Khayr Vahdat, Parvoz and Ravshan dropping out of the league, and FC Panjshir gaining promotion." .

ns1:63090 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Garisi was fought between the Kingdom of Kartli and Safavid dynasty armies at the village of Garisi (present-day Tetri Tsqaro) in 1556, and resulted in a costly victory for the Georgians." .

ns1:63110 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Sinhagad took place during the night on 04 February 1670 on the fort of Sinhagad (then known as Kondhana after the sage Kaundinya), near the city of Pune, Maharashtra, India. The battle was fought between Tanaji Malusare, commander of the Maratha Empire under Shivaji. A bust of Tanaji Malusare was established on the fort in the memory of his contribution to the battle." .

ns1:63116 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Carnifex Ferry took place on 10 September 1861 in Nicholas County (now West Virginia), as part of the Operations in Western Virginia Campaign during the American Civil War. The battle resulted in a Union strategic victory that contributed to the eventual Confederate withdrawal from western Virginia, which in turn led to the creation of the State of West Virginia two years later." .

ns1:63138 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009–10 Football League Cup (known as the Carling Cup due to the competition's sponsorship by lager brand Carling) was the 50th season of the EFL Cup, a knock-out competition for the top 92 association football clubs played in English association football league system. Manchester United F.C. successfully defended their League Cup title after defeating Aston Villa by 2–1 in the 2010 Football League Cup Final at Wembley Stadium on 28 February 2010. The 2010 Football League Cup Final was played at Wembley Stadium, London, on Sunday, 28 February 2010." .

ns1:63181 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1971 All-Ireland Senior Club Hurling Championship Final was a hurling match played at St Brendan's Park on 1971 to determine the winners of the 1970–71 All-Ireland Senior Club Hurling Championship, the first season of the All-Ireland Senior Club Hurling Championship, a tournament organised by the Gaelic Athletic Association for the champion clubs of the four provinces of Ireland. The final was contested by Roscrea GAA of Tipperary and St. Rynagh's GAA of Offaly, with Roscrea GAA winning by 4-5 to 2-5. An early goal by Barney Moylan held out prospects of a St. Rynagh's GAA comeback early in the second half, however, a solo-run goal by Joe Tynan produced a goal that put Roscrea GAA back in command. Roscrea GAA's half-back line also came in for praise in nullifying the St. Rynagh's GAA attack." .

ns1:63225 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Krzywopłoty took place on 17 November 1914 – 18 , 1914, near the village of Krzywopłoty, Lesser Poland Voivodeship, which at that time belonged to Russian-controlled Congress Poland, and was located near the border with Austrian Galicia. On 17 November 1914, 4th and 6th Battalions, supported by field artillery, took positions on the hill of Holy Cross, located between Krzywopłoty, Lesser Poland Voivodeship and Bydlin. The Battle of Krzywopłoty is commemorated on the Tomb of the Unknown Soldier, Warsaw, with the inscription \"KRZYWOPLOTY 14 XI 1914\"." .

ns1:63226 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Ejima-Ikushima affair (江島生島事件, Ejima Ikushima jiken) was the most significant scandal in the Ōoku, the Tokugawa Shogunate harem, during the Edo period of the history of Japan. On the twelfth day of the first month of the fourth year of the Shōtoku era (1714, by the Western calendar), Ejima, a high-ranking lady in the Ōoku, visited the grave of the late shōgun Tokugawa Ienobu as a proxy for her superior, Gekkō-in, who had been a lady-in-waiting to the late shōgun and was the mother of the ruling shōgun Tokugawa Ietsugu. Ejima then accepted an invitation to attend a kabuki performance by the popular actor Ikushima Shingorō at the Yamamura-za. Oku stars Yukie Nakama as Ejima and Hidetoshi Nishijima as Ikushima Shingorō." .

ns1:63228 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Cobleskill (also known as the Cobleskill massacre) was an American Revolutionary War raid on the frontier settlement of Cobleskill on 30 May 1778. The battle, having taken place in the modern-day hamlet of Warnerville, New York, near the modern (2019) Cobleskill-Richmondville High School, marked the beginning of a phase in which Loyalists and Iroquois, encouraged and supplied by British authorities in the Province of Quebec, raided and destroyed numerous villages on what was then the United States western frontier of New York and Pennsylvania. New York's defenders retaliated against Brant's actions against Cobleskill and other communities by destroying Iroquois villages later in the year, and Continental Army forces destroyed more Iroquois villages in the Sullivan Expedition of 1779. With the failure of British General John Burgoyne's campaign to the Hudson after the Battles of Saratoga in October 1777, the American Revolutionary War in upstate New York became a frontier war. On the morning of 30 May 1778 Brant laid a trap for Cobleskill's defenders. New York Governor George Clinton, who had been considering operations against Onaquaga, enlarged those plans after the raid on Cobleskill and Brant's attack on German Flatts in September." .

ns1:63243 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Copa del Rey Final was the 105th final since its establishment. The match took place on 2007 at the Estadio Santiago Bernabéu, Madrid. The match was contested by Sevilla FC and Getafe CF, and was refereed by Julián Rodríguez Santiago. With a 1–0 victory, Sevilla FC – who also triumphed in the 2006–07 UEFA Cup a month earlier – won the trophy for the fourth time in their history; it was their sixth final, while Getafe CF had reached that stage for the first time ever (they also made it to the final a year later but lost again, to Valencia). * Match abandoned after 57 minutes at 0–1 due to injury of Sevilla FC coach Juande Ramos; remainder of the game played on March 18 at the Coliseum Alfonso Pérez, Getafe CF." .

ns1:63256 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 World Indoor Archery Championships was the 11th edition of the World Indoor Archery Championships. The event was held in Las Vegas, United States of America from 05 February 2012 to 09 February 2012 and was organized by International Archery Federation (FITA)." .

ns1:63259 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Copa del Rey de Baloncesto 2001–02 was the 66th edition of the Copa del Rey de Baloncesto. It was organized by the ACB and was held in Vitoria-Gasteiz at the Fernando Buesa Arena between 14 March 2002 and 17 March 2002." .

ns1:63267 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2011 Nicaraguan general election were held in Nicaragua on 06 November 2011. The incumbent president Daniel Ortega, won a third term in this election, with a landslide victory. Daniel Ortega placed first in all major polling prior to the election. In the 2006 Nicaraguan general election, APRE contested the elections in alliance with the Nicaraguan Liberal Alliance, an election in which the Nicaraguan Liberal Alliance's candidate placed second. The 62.69% of the vote Ortega received in the election translated to nearly two-thirds of government seats for his party, falling just a few seats short of the two-thirds required to amend the constitution in Nicaragua. Ortega was originally not allowed to run in the 2011 election based on Nicaragua's past laws on consecutive presidential terms, but he led the government to overturn that policy in late 2010. Reactions have been diverse and not unified on the result of the election, both in Nicaragua and internationally. The Episcopal Conference of Nicaragua considered the election not to be transparent. The president of Nicaragua's election authority, Roberto Rivas, stated that \"a process is legitimate if the people's wishes were respected, and that is what we are experiencing here.\" Those who did recognize the election results: The Organization of American States ratified, that the results of the Nicaraguan general elections of 2011, won by Daniel Ortega Saavedra with more than 62 percent of the vote, corresponded to the count realized by this and other organizations during their electoral observer mission in Nicaragua." .

ns1:63269 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Ripper, also known as the Fourth Battle of Seoul, was a United Nations (UN) military operation conceived by the US Eighth Army, General Matthew Ridgway, during the Korean War. Operation Ripper was preceded by the largest artillery bombardment of the Korean War. The capture of Chuncheon was the last major ground objective of Operation Ripper. With supply requirements all but met, IX and X Corps finishing their advance to the Arizona Line, and no clear indication of an imminent enemy offensive at hand, Ridgway on 5 March ordered Operation Ripper to begin on the morning of 07 March 1951. With the capture of Chuncheon, all major ground objectives of Operation Ripper were in Eighth Army hands." .

ns1:63285 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "His team included campaign manager David Plouffe and media consultant David Axelrod, who were partners at the Chicago-based political consulting firm AKP&D Message and Media." .

ns1:63288 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Journal of Julius Rodman is an unfinished creative work serial novel by American author Edgar Allan Poe published in 1840. Six installments of the novel were published in Burton's Gentleman's Magazine's January through June issues in 1840." .

ns1:63305 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Asian Junior Athletics Championships was the 17th edition of the international athletics competition for Asian under-20 athletes, organised by the Asian Athletics Association and the Vietnam Athletics Federation. The competition took place over four days from 3 June–6 June at Thống Nhất Stadium in Ho Chi Minh City, Vietnam." .

ns1:63307 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Italian Grand Prix (officially the Formula 1 Gran Premio d'Italia 2007) was a Formula One motor race held on 09 September 2007 at Autodromo Nazionale Monza, Italy. Ferrari were expected to do well at home after a one-two finish in the Turkish GP and Michael Schumacher's victory at Autodromo Nazionale Monza 2006 Italian Grand Prix. It was McLaren's eighth victory at Autodromo Nazionale Monza and Mercedes' fourth, while Alonso became the first Spaniard to win the Italian Grand Prix." .

ns1:63314 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014–15 ISU Grand Prix of Figure Skating is a series of senior international figure skating competitions in the 2014–2015 figure skating season. Organized by the International Skating Union, the Grand Prix series began in 24 October 2014 and ran until 14 December 2014." .

ns1:63316 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1980 Tour de Romandie was the 34th edition of the Tour de Romandie cycle race and was held from 06 May 1980 to 11 May 1980. The race started in Meyrin and finished in Fribourg." .

ns1:63349 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Hefei (208) was fought between the warlords Cao Cao and Sun Quan between late 208 and early 209 in the late Eastern Han dynasty. In the winter of 208–209, Cao Cao was defeated by Sun Quan and Liu Bei at the Battle of Red Cliffs, but he still had a large army garrisoned at the northern part of Jing Province (covering present-day Hubei and Hunan). The battle dragged on until the following year, but Sun Quan's forces were unable to breach Hefei." .

ns1:63351 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 63rd edition of the 1977 Liège–Bastogne–Liège road bicycle racing in Belgium was held on 24 April 1977. French rider Bernard Hinault won his first monument classic, after beating André Dierickx in a two-man sprint. Six riders – Eddy Merckx, Roger De Vlaeminck, Freddy Maertens, André Dierickx, Dietrich Thurau and Bernard Hinault – were in front of the race." .

ns1:63383 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The football at the 1971 Mediterranean Games was the 6th edition of the football at the Mediterranean Games. The association football tournament was held in İzmir, Turkey between the 1971–1971 as part of the 1971 Mediterranean Games." .

ns1:63427 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Afghan Premier League Final was the final match of the 2015 Afghan Premier League, the 4th season of Afghanistan's premier club football tournament organised by Roshan. The match took place on Friday, 2015, at the Afghanistan Football Federation stadium in Kabul, Afghanistan, between Greater Kabul Region Shaheen Asmayee F.C. and Eastern region De Spin Ghar Bazan F.C.. The Afghanistan Football Federation stadium in Kabul, Afghanistan, was the venue of the 2015 Afghan Premier League Final for the fourth time." .

ns1:63489 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1976 Belgian Grand Prix was a Formula One motor race held at Circuit Zolder, near Heusden-Zolder in Belgium on 16 May 1976. It was the 34th Belgian Grand Prix and the third to be held at Circuit Zolder." .

ns1:63533 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The rose is a common device in heraldic symbol. The rose is one of the most common plant symbols in heraldic symbol, together with the lily, which also has a stylistic representation in the fleur-de-lis. The rose was the symbol of the English Tudor dynasty, and the ten-petaled Tudor rose is associated with England. The double Tudor rose is always depicted as white on red on a field of any other tincture and is always termed 'proper'. A red rose appears on the collar of the Order of the Garter." .

ns1:63558 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Rally Argentina (Spanish: Rally de Argentina) is an Argentina rallying competition that has been a round of the World Rally Championship, the Intercontinental Rally Challenge, the South American Rally Championship and the Argentina Rally Championship. It is held in the area around Villa Carlos Paz in Córdoba Province, on narrow gravel roads best known for their water-splashes. The rallying was first run in 1980, in Tucumán Province and organized by the Automovil Club Argentino." .

ns1:63559 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2019 Tunisian presidential election were held in Tunisia on 15 September 2019, the second direct vote for the presidency since the 2011 revolution. As no candidate received a majority of the vote in the first round, a runoff was held on 13 October 2019 between the top two candidates, Kais Saied and Nabil Karoui. In order to comply with this, the High Authority for Elections announced the election will be held sooner than scheduled, being moved17 Novemberp fro24 November November to 15 September 2019 and 13 October 2019. Organised by the Munathara Initiative and sanctioned by Tunisia's election authority, ISIE, the debates were held on 7, 8 15 September 2019 and 9 15 September 2019." .

ns1:63591 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1966 All England Badminton Championships was a badminton tournament held at Wembley Arena, London, England, from 23 March 1966–26 March 1966." .

ns1:63605 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Veracruz, also known as the Battle of San Juan de Ulúa, was a naval engagement that pitted a French frigate squadron under Rear Admiral Charles Baudin against the Mexican citadel of San Juan de Ulúa, which defended the city of Veracruz, from 27 November 1838 to 05 December 1838. Having crossed the Atlantic to settle a dispute between France and Mexico, the squadron anchored off Veracruz and negotiated until all diplomatic means to resolve the dispute appeared exhausted. In France the battle attracted considerable public attention at the moment, but was later overshadowed by the much more considerable French intervention in Mexico in the 1860s. Charles Louis Joseph Bazoche, comprising the 60-gun frigate Herminie and three brigs, was sent to Mexico in late 1837 in order to demonstrate French resolve and anchored off Veracruz. The city of Veracruz was defended by the fort of San Juan de Ulúa, a strong fortress located about a kilometre from the city and protected by a reef. The capture of San Juan de Ulua came as a surprise to the Mexican government, which declared war on France, expelled all French citizens established in Mexico, relieved Gen. Rincón of command and sent an army of 3200 men, under Gen. Santa Anna and Gen. Arista, to retake Veracruz. On 05 December 1838, at 5:00 in the morning, boats from the squadron landed 1500 men on the beaches before Veracruz; they split into three columns: two wings ascended the city walls near the strongholds of Santiago and Conception. His narration of the battle made him very popular, to the point that his leg was buried in Mexico with full military honors." .

ns1:63654 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 35th G8 summit was held in L'Aquila, Abruzzo, Italy, on 08 July 2009–10, 2009. The Group of Seven (G7) was an unofficial forum which brought together the heads of the richest industrialized countries: France, Germany, Italy, Japan, the United Kingdom, the United States and Canada starting in 1976. The summits were not meant to be linked formally with wider international institutions; and in fact, a mild rebellion against the stiff formality of other international meetings was a part of the genesis of cooperation between France's President Giscard d'Estaing and West Germany's Chancellor Helmut Schmidt as they conceived the initial summit of the Group of Six (G6) in 1975. The G8 is an unofficial annual forum for the leaders of Canada, the European Commission, France, Germany, Italy, Japan, Russia, the United Kingdom and the United States. France stated it wished China would become a full member by the time of the 37th G8 summit in 2011 was organized as France was the host member." .

ns1:63693 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1962 Grammy Awards were held on 1962, at Chicago, Los Angeles and New York City." .

ns1:63723 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The athletics at the 1932 Summer Olympics – men's 400 metres sprint event at the 1932 Olympic Games took place on 04 August 1932 and 05 August 1932 at the Los Angeles Memorial Coliseum." .

ns1:63725 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Naas took place in Ireland on 24 May 1798. One of the first engagements of the Irish Rebellion of 1798, a force of over 1,000 rebels, led by Michael Reynolds attacked Naas, the strongest Crown garrison in County Kildare, following the successful mobilisation of United Irishmen, Defenders and rebels throughout county Kildare on the night of 23 May." .

ns1:63734 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Trick or Treat is an unfinished British film directed by Michael Apted that was started in 1975 but never completed. At a time when English films were unattractive outside Britain, here seemed an opportunity to make a film with international appeal. The fate of the film contributed to the breakup of the partnership between David Puttnam and Sandy Lieberson." .

ns1:63735 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Penang Bridge International Marathon (Malay: Maraton Antarabangsa Jambatan Pulau Pinang) or Penang Bridge Marathon is an annual marathon event held at Penang Bridge in Penang, Malaysia since 1984. In 2006, after a break for a number of years, the Penang Bridge International Marathon was revived as \"a combination\" of the Penang International Marathon, which was last held in 1999, and the Penang Bridge Run, which had been held from 1992 to 2003. The Penang Bridge International Marathon also incorporates a half-marathon event and a 10-km run event." .

ns1:63765 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The FIA Formula 2 Championship is a second-tier single-seater racing championship organised by the Fédération Internationale de l'Automobile (FIA). The championship was introduced in 2017, following the rebranding of the long-term Formula One feeder series GP2 Series. The numbering system in FIA Formula 2 Championship is currently based on the previous season's team standings (similar to Formula One 1996–2013 numbering system) that was used since the formation of GP2 Series in 2005 until present. The FIA Formula 2 Championship runs the different compounds and size as F1 since 2017 (due to in fact Formula 1's 2017-present tyres are wider, the FIA Formula 2 Championship carried over the pre-2017 Pirelli F1 tyres). All Formula 2 cars currently use ordinary unleaded racing gasoline as fuel (similar to commercial vehicle unleaded street gasoline), which has been the de facto standard in second tier single-seater formula racing since the introduction of GP2 Series in 2005. Since 2005 GP2 Series season, Elf exclusively continues providing the LMS 102 RON unleaded fuel and also Elf HTX 840 0W-40 lubricants for all FIA Formula 2 Championship cars due to in fact of Mecachrome's long-term technical partnership with Elf." .

ns1:63771 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1981 Open Championship was a men's major golf championship and the 110th The Open Championship, held from 16 July–19 July at Royal St George's Golf Club in Sandwich, England." .

ns1:63773 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 BRD Brașov Challenger was a professional tennis tournament played on Clay courts. It took place in Brașov, Romania between 01 September 2014 and 07 September 2014." .

ns1:41488 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1983 Valencian regional election was held on Sunday, 08 May 1983, to elect the 1st Corts of the Community of Valencia. The Socialist Party of the Valencian Country (Socialist Party of the Valencian Country) won the election with an absolute majority of 51 out of 89 seats and almost 1 million votes (51.4% of the vote). The People's Coalition, composed in the Community of Valencia by the People's Alliance (AP), the People's Democratic Party (PDP), the Liberal Union and the Community of Valencia Union (UV), became the second political force and the main opposition party in the Corts Valencianes with 32 seats. The Communist Party of the Valencian Country (PCE) managed to surpass the 5% regional threshold and entered the Corts with 6 seats, seeing a slight recovery from its results in the October 1982 general election. As a result of the election, regional Socialist Party of the Valencian Country leader Joan Lerma became the first democratically elected President of the Community of Valencia Government. The Council of the Community of Valencia Country, in agreement with the Government of Spain, was required to call an election to the Corts Valencianes within from 1 February to 31 08 May 1983." .

ns1:41552 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Zoufftgen train collision occurred around 11.45 am on 11 October 2006, near Zoufftgen, Moselle, France, some 20 metres from the border with Luxembourg, on the Metz–Luxembourg railway line. One train was a CFL Class 2200 passenger train of the Chemins de Fer Luxembourgeois (CFL), running on the Métrolor service (TER Lorraine) from the city of Luxembourg to Nancy, France. The accident occurred between the border stations of Hettange-Grande (France) and Bettembourg (Luxembourg), near the Lorraine area of Zoufftgen, on a double track section. At the same time, other inquiries were started: in France, by the SNCF and the BEA-TT (Bureau d’Enquêtes sur les Accidents de Transport Terrestre, \"Land Transport Accident Investigation Bureau\") by the French Minister of Transport, and in Luxembourg, by the CFL and the Entité d'Enquête d'Accidents et d'Incidents (\"Inquiry Agency for Incidents and Accidents\"), in concert with the BEA-TT." .

ns1:41857 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The El Calabozo massacre was an incident during the Cuando yo la vi wujuju on 21 August 1982–22 August 1982, in which more than two hundred people, including children and elderly, were reportedly killed at El Calabozo by the Atlacatl Battalion of the Salvadoran Army. In 21 August 1982, the Salvadoran military attacked the San Vicente Department, an area where the rebel Farabundo Martí National Liberation Front was known to have bases. On the night of 21 August 1982, a group of internally displaced people was overtaken beside the Amatitán river by the Atlacatl Battalion, a US-trained counter-insurgency unit. The Atlacatl Battalion had previously been responsible for the El Mozote massacre, in which up to 900 captured civilians had been killed. Though the Commission on the Truth for El Salvador documented the massacre's existence, the government closed the case in 1993 without charges." .

ns1:41879 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Saint Sylvester's Day, also known as Silvester or the Feast of Saint Sylvester, is the day of the feast of Sylvester I, a saint who served as Pope of the Western Church from 314 to 335. Among the Western Christian Churches, the feast day is held on the anniversary of Saint Sylvester's death, the day celebrated on 31 December (Roman Catholic Church) or 2 January (Eastern Orthodox Churches), a date that, since the adoption of the Gregorian calendar, has coincided with New Year's Eve. After dying, Saint Sylvester was buried on the day celebrated on 31 December (Roman Catholic Church) or 2 January (Eastern Orthodox Churches) in the Catacomb of Priscilla. Several countries, primarily in Europe, use a variant of Silvester's name as the preferred name for the holiday; these countries include Austria, Bosnia and Herzegovina, Croatia, Czech Republic, France, Germany, Hungary, Israel, Italy, Liechtenstein, Luxembourg, Poland, Slovakia, Switzerland, and Slovenia. Many Christian households in Germany mark Saint Silvester's Day by practicing the custom of Bleigiessen using Silvesterblei (Silvester lead), in which Silvesterblei is melted over a flame in an old spoon and dropped into a bowl of cold water; one's fortune for the coming year is determined by the shape of the lead. Along with exploding fireworks, the Saint Silvester Road Race, Brazil's oldest and most prestigious running event, takes place on Saint Sylvester's Day and is dedicated to him. For this reason, ex-Soviet Jews who celebrate New Year's Eve (called Novy God to distinguish it from \"Sylvester\") are often criticized for celebrating an anti-semitic holiday." .

ns1:41987 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2001 Champions Cup was a professional invitational snooker tournament held at the Brighton Centre in Brighton, England, from 11 to 19 August. It was the seventh and final edition of the eight-player Champions Cup, and was the first of four World Professional Billiards and Snooker Association (WPBSA) invitational events of the Snooker season 2001/2002 following the 2001 World Snooker Championship. John Higgins, the world number three, won the tournament, defeating two-time Champions Cup runner-up Mark Williams seven frames to four (7–4) in the final. Ebdon made the highest break of 130 in the third frame of his group match over Ronnie O'Sullivan, the 2001 world champion. The 2001 Champions Cup was the first of four World Professional Billiards and Snooker Association (WPBSA) invitational events of the Snooker season 2001/2002 and was held at the Brighton Centre in Brighton, England, from 11 to 19 11 August 2001. The Champions Cup had a prize fund of £200,000, with £100,000 going to the winner, and was broadcast in the United Kingdom on ITV. World number three John Higgins won 5–4 over two-time Champions Cup runner-up Mark Williams. Tournament debutant Paul Hunter won 5–4 over the 1999 Champions Cup winner Stephen Hendry to requite a loss to Hendry at the 2001 world championship." .

ns1:42140 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Tour de Yorkshire was a three-day cycling stage race staged in Yorkshire over 28 April 2017–30 April 2017. The race was won by Team Dimension Data rider Serge Pauwels of Belgium, after a strong performance from his team on the hilly final stage; Jacques Janse van Rensburg set Pauwels up for an attack on the Wigtwizzle climb and he went clear while Omar Fraile manned the chase group in support. The race's other classifications were won by Caleb Ewan (points for Orica–Scott), Pieter Weening (mountains for Roompot–Nederlandse Loterij), and Team Dimension Data (teams classification). On 25 October 2016, the start and finish locations for the event were released: these were Bridlington, Scarborough, Tadcaster, Harrogate, Bradford and Sheffield. The routes were Bridlington to Scarborough, Tadcaster to Harrogate and Bradford to Sheffield. 18 teams were selected to take part in Tour de Yorkshire." .

ns1:42211 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Paget Gorman Sign System, also known as Paget Gorman Signed Speech (PGSS) or Paget Gorman Systematic Sign Language is a manually coded form of the English language, designed to be used with children with speech or communication difficulties. PGSS was originally developed in Britain by anthropologist Sir Richard Paget, 2nd Baronet in the 1930s, and later by his wife Lady Grace Paget and Dr Pierre Gorman." .

ns1:42275 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Treaty of Moscow was signed on 12 August 1970 between the Soviet Union and West Germany. It was signed by Willy Brandt and Walter Scheel for West Germany's side and by Alexei Kosygin and Andrei Gromyko for the Soviet Union. The signees renounced the use of force and recognised the postwar borders, specifically, the Oder–Neisse line, which hived off a large portion of historical eastern Germany to Poland and the Soviet Union." .

ns1:42444 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017–18 Iraqi Premier League (Arabic: الدوري العراقي الممتاز 2017–18) was the 44th season of the Iraqi Premier League, the highest division for Iraqi association football clubs, since its establishment in 1974. Al-Zawra'a SC won a record 14th title, finishing four points ahead of both the previous season's champions and runners-up (Al-Quwa Al-Jawiya and Al-Naft respectively) and five points ahead of Al-Shorta Baghdad." .

ns1:42578 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 UEFA Women's Champions League Final was the final of the second season of the UEFA Women's Champions League and was held at Fulham's Craven Cottage in London, England, on 26 May 2011. For the second year in a row, Olympique Lyonnais Féminin met 1. FFC Turbine Potsdam in the final. Unlike last year, Olympique Lyonnais Féminin came up with the victory as they defeated Potsdam 2–0 after goals from Wendie Renard and Lara Dickenmann." .

ns1:42652 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Super Bowl III was the third AFL–NFL Championship Game in professional American football, and the first to officially bear the trademark name \"Super Bowl\". Played on 12 January 1969, at the Miami Orange Bowl in Miami, the game is regarded as one of the greatest upsets in both American football history and in the recorded history of sports. The 18-point underdog American Football League (AFL) champion New York Jets defeated the National Football League (NFL) champion Baltimore Colts by a score of 16–7. Jets quarterback Joe Namath famously made an appearance three days before the Super Bowl at the Miami Touchdown Club and personally guaranteed his team's victory. The game was awarded to Miami on May 14, Super Bowl II, at the owners meetings held in Atlanta. In Super Bowl II, Shula and the Colts were considered a favorite to win the NFL championship again, which carried with it an automatic berth what was now becoming popularly known as the \"Super Bowl\" against the champion of the younger AFL. While the Miami Orange Bowl was sold out for the game, the live telecast was not shown in Miami due to both leagues' unconditional blackout rules at the time. The score gave the Jets a 7–0 lead, and marked the first time in history that an AFL team led in the Super Bowl. Ten plays later, aided by three Jets penalties, Baltimore finally scored a touchdown on a 1-yard run by Hill to cut their deficit to 16–7, but with only 3:19 left in the game. at Miami Orange Bowl, Miami." .

ns1:42855 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 47th Academy Awards were presented Tuesday, 08 April 1975, at the Dorothy Chandler Pavilion in Los Angeles, California, honoring the best films of 1974. The ceremonies were presided over by Bob Hope, Shirley MacLaine, Sammy Davis Jr., and Frank Sinatra." .

ns1:42914 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015–16 Fußball-Bundesliga was the 53rd season of the Bundesliga, Germany's premier association football competition. The season started on 14 August 2015 and ended on 14. FC Bayern Munich were the defending champions, after winning their 24th Bundesliga title and 25th German championship overall in the previous season. FC Bayern Munich won the 2015–16 title in the second-last round on 7 23 May 2016, thereby becoming the first club in the history of the Bundesliga and the German association football championship to win four consecutive championships. A total of 18 teams were participating in this year's edition of the Bundesliga. Of these, 15 sides qualified directly from the 2014–15 Fußball-Bundesliga and the two sides were directly promoted from the 2014–15 2. Bundesliga season: FC Ingolstadt 04, the champions, and Darmstadt 98, the runners-up. The final participant was decided by a 2014–15 Fußball-Bundesliga, in which the 16th-placed Bundesliga club, Hamburger SV, defeated the third-place finisher in the 2. The team which finished 16th faced the third-placed 2015–16 2. Bundesliga side for a 2014–15 Fußball-Bundesliga. Eintracht Frankfurt won 2–1 on aggregate." .

ns1:43703 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Act of Abjuration (Dutch: Plakkaat van Verlatinghe, Spanish: Acta de Abjuración, literally 'placard of abjuration') is the declaration of independence by many of the provinces of the Netherlands from the allegiance to Philip II of Spain, during the Dutch Revolt. Signed on 26 July 1581 in The Hague, the Act formally confirmed a decision made by the States General in Antwerp four days earlier. The rebellious States General decided on 14 June 1581 to officially declare the throne vacant, because of Philip's behaviour, hence the Dutch name for the Act of Abjuration: \"Plakkaat van Verlatinghe\", which may be translated as \"Placard of Desertion.\" A committee of four members – Andries Hessels, greffier (secretary) of the States of Brabant; Jacques Tayaert, pensionary of the city of Ghent; Jacob Valcke, pensionary of the city of Ter Goes (now Goes); and Pieter van Dieven (also known as Petrus Divaeus), pensionary of the city of Mechelen – was charged with drafting what was to become the Act of Abjuration. The actual draft seems to have been written by an audiencier of the States General, Jan van Asseliers." .

ns1:43733 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Burr–Hamilton duel was a duel fought at Weehawken, between Vice President Aaron Burr and Alexander Hamilton, the former Secretary of the Treasury. Burr survived the duel and was indicted for murder in both New York and New Jersey, though these charges later were either dismissed or resulted in acquittal. In the early morning of 11 July 1804, Burr and Hamilton departed from Manhattan by separate boats and rowed across the Hudson River to a spot known as the Heights of Weehawken, a popular dueling ground below the towering cliffs of the New Jersey Palisades. Dueling had been prohibited in both New York and New Jersey, but Hamilton and Burr agreed to go to Weehawken because New Jersey was not as aggressive as New York in prosecuting dueling participants. In Pendleton's amended version of the statement, he and a friend went to the site of the duel the day after Hamilton's death to discover where Hamilton's shot went. Hamilton wrote a letter before the duel titled Statement on Impending Duel with Aaron Burr in which he stated that he was \"strongly opposed to the practice of dueling\" for both religious and practical reasons. Hamilton's son Philip and George Eacker likely used the Church weapons in the 1801 duel in which Philip died, three years before the Burr–Hamilton duel. From 1820 to 1857, the site was marked by two stones with the names Hamilton and Burr placed where they were thought to have stood during the duel, but a road was built through the site in 1858 from Hoboken, New Jersey, to Fort Lee, New Jersey; all that remained of those memorials was an inscription on a boulder where Hamilton was thought to have rested after the duel, but there are no primary accounts which confirm the boulder anecdote. New markers were added on 11 July 1804, 2004, the 200th anniversary of the duel. The songs \"Alexander Hamilton\", \"Your Obedient Servant\", and \"The World Was Wide Enough\" also refer to the duel. Douglas Hamilton, fifth great-grandson of Alexander Hamilton, faced Antonio Burr, a descendant of Aaron Burr's cousin. More than 1,000 people attended it, including an estimated 60 descendants of Hamilton and 40 members of the Aaron Burr Association." .

ns1:43942 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 Shanxi mudslide was caused by the collapse of an unlicensed mine landfill in Xiangfen County county, Linfen, Shanxi Province, People's Republic of China which caused 277 dead, 4 missing and 33 injured. Around 8:00 a.m. on 08 September 2008, the retaining wall of a waste iron ore reservoir collapsed after torrential rain, causing a major mudslide which inundated a village and crowded marketplace. The final official count is: 277 dead, 4 missing and 33 injured." .

ns1:44528 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "During the Siege of Nagykanizsa (Turkish: Kanije Savunması) in 1601, a small Ottoman force held the fortress of Nagykanizsa in western Hungary against a much larger coalition army of the Habsburg Monarchy, while inflicting heavy losses on its besiegers. This battle was part of the Long War between the Ottoman Empire and the House of Habsburg, lasting from 1593 to 1606. Nagykanizsa fell to Tiryaki Hasan Pasha in 1600, and the Ottoman Pasha took command of the fort with a garrison of 7,000 men. Ferdinand II, commander of the Habsburg coalition army, saw an opportunity and laid siege to Nagykanizsa on 09 September 1601 with an army of 100,000 men, cutting all supply routes to the fortress. He also sent messages that the Ottoman army in Belgrade was on its way to lift the siege on Nagykanizsa. Desperate, they attacked on the night of 18 November 1601, the 73rd day of the siege, in a surprise attack." .

ns1:44699 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Virgin Galactic crash occurred on 31 October 2014, when the VSS Enterprise, a SpaceShipTwo experimental spaceflight test vehicle operated by Virgin Galactic, suffered a catastrophic in-flight breakup during a test flight and crashed in the Mojave Desert near Cantil, California. The team, consisting of around fifteen personnel, arrived at the Mojave Air and Space Port on November 1. In an editorial in Time magazine on 31 October 2014, Jeffrey Kluger concluded that \"A fatal accident in the Mojave Desert is a lesson in the perils of space hubris." .

ns1:44702 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Indianapolis 500 (branded as the 101st Running of the Indianapolis 500 presented by PennGrade Motor Oil for sponsorship reasons) is a Verizon IndyCar Series event that occurred on Sunday 28 May 2017, at the Indianapolis Motor Speedway in Speedway, Indiana, United States of America. A series of procedural rule changes were implemented by the series for the 2017 IndyCar Series, however, none of the rule changes significantly affected the Indianapolis 500. The 101st Indianapolis 500 will be the sixth round of the 2017 IndyCar Series, and the 22nd sanctioned as part of the Indy Racing League/IndyCar Series. Former Indianapolis 500 winners that were entered include Hélio Castroneves, Juan Pablo Montoya, Buddy Lazier, Scott Dixon, Tony Kanaan, Ryan Hunter-Reay, and Alexander Rossi. Montoya, after three years in a full-time role at Team Penske, went down to a part-time role for 2017, driving in the IndyCar Grand Prix and Indianapolis 500 only. Joining him in the Fast Nine were Takuma Sato, Scott Dixon, J. R. Hildebrand, Alexander Rossi, Will Power, Fernando Alonso, Tony Kanaan, and Marco Andretti. Scott Dixon took the pole position with a four-lap average of 232.164 mph; the fastest four-lap average since Arie Luyendyk's track record run in 1996. Sato also became the first driver to win the Indianapolis 500 and finish on the podium in a United States of America Grand Prix hosted at Indianapolis, having finished third in 2004. In the United States of America, the 2017 Indianapolis 500 was the least-watched edition of the race since the inception of live flag-to-flag coverage, with a 3.6 overnight Nielsen rating (down from 4.1 in 2016)." .

ns1:44789 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1958 Moroccan Grand Prix, formally the VII Grand Prix International Automobile du Maroc, was a Formula One motorsport held at Ain-Diab Circuit, Casablanca on 19 October 1958, after a six-week break following the 1958 Italian Grand Prix. It was race 11 of 11 in the 1958 World Championship of Drivers and race 10 of 10 in the 1958 Formula One season. Mike Hawthorn (Ferrari) started from pole position, but Stirling Moss won the race driving for Vanwall. Both Mike Hawthorn and Stirling Moss came into the race with a chance of becoming World Drivers' Champion." .

ns1:44872 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Königgrätz (or Sadowa) was the decisive battle of the Austro-Prussian War in which the Kingdom of Prussia defeated the Austrian Empire. It took place near Königgrätz (Czech: Hradec Králové) and Sadová in Bohemia on 03 July 1866. At the outset of the war in June, the Prussian armies were gathered along the Prussian border: the Army of the Elbe under Karl Herwarth von Bittenfeld at Torgau, the First Army under Prince Friedrich Karl of Prussia between Senftenberg and Görlitz, and the Second Army under Crown Prince Frederick in Silesia west of Neiße (Nysa). The Austrian army of 215,000 faced the Prussian Army of the Elbe (39,000) and First Army (85,000) on 03 July 1866. The battle began at dawn in subsiding rain and mist as Prussia took its position west of the Bystřice River." .

ns1:45017 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "On 04 October 1992, El Al Flight 1862, a Boeing 747-200 cargo aircraft of the then state-owned Israeli airline El Al, crashed into the Groeneveen and Klein-Kruitberg flats in the Bijlmermeer (colloquially \"Bijlmer\") neighbourhood (part of Amsterdam-Zuidoost) of Amsterdam, the Netherlands. On 04 October 1992, the cargo aircraft, a Boeing 747-200, registration 4X-AXG, travelling from John F. Kennedy International Airport New York to Ben Gurion Airport in Israel, made a stopover at Amsterdam Amsterdam Airport Schiphol Airport. She was an El Al employee based in Amsterdam and was travelling to Tel Aviv to marry another El Al employee. Soon after the turn, at 6:27 pm, above the Gooimeer, a lake near Amsterdam, witnesses on the ground heard a sharp bang and saw falling debris, trail of smoke and a momentary flash of fire on the right wing while the aircraft was climbing through 1,950 metres (6,400 feet) Engine number three separated from the right wing of the aircraft, shot forward, damaged the wing flaps, then fell back and struck engine number four, tearing it from the wing. At 6:35:42 pm local time, the aircraft nose-dived from the sky and crashed into two high-rise apartment complexes in the Bijlmermeer neighbourhood of Amsterdam, at the corner of a building where the Groeneveen complex met the Klein-Kruitberg complex. The arrival controllers reported that the aircraft had last been located 1.5 kilometres (0.93 mi; 0.81 nmi) west of Weesp and emergency personnel were sent immediately." .

ns1:45032 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "World Bowl '95 (also referred to as World Bowl III) was the third championship game of the World League of American Football (WLAF), and the final game of the 1995 WLAF season. It was the first World Bowl to be played since World Bowl '92, after which the league had suspended operations for two years. The match-up was between the 6–4 Frankfurt Galaxy and the 9–1 Amsterdam Admirals, at Olympisch Stadion in Amsterdam, Netherlands on Saturday, 17 June 1995. 23,847 fans witnessed the Galaxy pull off an upset en route to a 26–22 victory and their first ever World Bowl title. Amsterdam recovered the onside kick and had 34 seconds to come up with a touchdown; however, key passes into the endzone fell incomplete, preserving Frankfurt's four-point lead and giving the Galaxy their first World Bowl title." .

ns1:45108 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Paris Peace Conference was the formal meeting in 1919 and 1920 of the victorious Allies after the end of World War I to set the peace terms for the defeated Central Powers. The conference involved diplomats from 32 countries and nationalities, and its major decisions were the creation of the League of Nations and the five peace treaties with the defeated states; the awarding of German and Ottoman overseas possessions as \"mandates,\" chiefly to Britain and France, the imposition of reparations upon Germany, and the drawing of new national boundaries, sometimes with plebiscites, to reflect ethnic boundaries more closely. The Conference formally opened on 18 January 1919 at the Quai d’Orsay in Paris. The conference came to an end on 21 January 1920 with the inaugural General Assembly of the League of Nations. Five major peace treaties were prepared at the Paris Peace Conference (with, in parentheses, the affected countries): The major decisions were the establishment of the League of Nations; the five peace treaties with defeated enemies; the awarding of German and Ottoman overseas possessions as \"mandates\", chiefly to members of the British Empire and to France; reparations imposed on Germany, and the drawing of new national boundaries (sometimes with plebiscites) to better reflect the forces of nationalism. The Germans rejected the French offers because they considered the French overtures to be a trap to trick them into accepting the Treaty of Versailles unchanged; also, German Foreign Minister, Count Ulrich von Brockdorff-Rantzau thought that the United States was more likely to reduce the severity of the peace treaty than France was. The idea, first accepted by Great Britain and France, was later rejected but became the purely-American King–Crane Commission, which toured all Syria and Palestine during the summer of 1919, took statements, and sampled opinion. France and Britain tried to appease Wilson by consenting to the establishment of his League of Nations. Although they were denied seats at the Paris Conference, the leadership of Marguerite de Witt-Schlumberger, the president of the French Union for Women's Suffrage, caused an Inter-Allied Women's Conference (IAWC) to be convened, which met from 10 February to 1919. The IAWC lobbied Wilson and then also the other delegates of the Paris Conference to admit women to its committees, and it was successful in achieving a hearing from the conference's Commissions for International Labour Legislation and then the League of Nations Commission." .

ns1:45280 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1820 United States presidential election was the ninth quadrennial United States presidential election. It was held from Wednesday, November 1, to Wednesday, 1820. Taking place at the height of the Era of Good Feelings, the election saw incumbent Democratic-Republican Party President of the United States James Monroe win re-election without a major opponent. Monroe and Vice President of the United States Daniel D. Tompkins faced no opposition from other Democratic-Republicans in their quest for a second term. Mississippi, Illinois, Alabama and Missouri participated in their first United States presidential election in 1820, Missouri with controversy, since it was not yet officially a state (see below). Since President of the United States Monroe's re-nomination was never in doubt, few Republicans bothered to attend the nominating caucus in 1820. President of the United States Monroe and Vice President of the United States Daniel D. Tompkins thus became de facto candidates for re-election. Even though every member of the Electoral College was pledged to Monroe, there were still a number of Federalist electors who voted for a Federalist vice president rather than Monroe's running mate Daniel D. Tompkins: those for Richard Stockton came from Massachusetts, while the entire Delaware delegation voted for Daniel Rodney for Vice President of the United States, and Robert Goodloe Harper's vice presidential vote was cast by an elector from his home state of Maryland." .

ns1:45309 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1940 Republican Party presidential primaries were the selection process by which voters of the Republican Party chose delegates to nominate a candidate President of the United States at the 1940 Republican National Convention. The primaries were contested mainly by Manhattan District Attorney Thomas E. Dewey and Senators Robert Alphonso Taft and Arthur Hendrick Vandenberg, though only a few states' primaries featured two or more of these men. In the months leading up to the opening of the 1940 Republican National Convention, the three leading candidates for the GOP nomination were considered to be Senators Robert Alphonso Taft of Ohio and Arthur Hendrick Vandenberg of Michigan, and District Attorney Thomas E. Dewey of New York. The Dewey campaign asked Gile to withdraw from the race; it was assumed Dewey had bypassed New Hampshire in respect and recognition of favorite-son presidential candidate Senator Henry Styles Bridges, who ran as a delegate at-large. Having given little thought to who he would select as his vice-presidential nominee, Willkie left the decision to convention chairman and Massachusetts Congressman Joseph William Martin Jr., the House Minority Leader, who suggested Senate Minority Leader Charles L. McNary of Oregon." .

ns1:45465 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Database of Molecular Motions (molmovdb) is a bioinformatics database and software as a service tool that attempts to categorize macromolecular motions, sometimes also known as conformational change. It was originally developed by Mark Bender Gerstein, Werner G. Krebs, and Nat Echols in the Molecular Biophysics & Biochemistry Department at Yale University." .

ns1:45742 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1996 Hong Kong Chief Executive election was held on 11 December 1996 to select the first Chief Executive of Hong Kong (CE) of the Hong Kong (HKSAR) which term started from 1 July 1997 after the Chinese resumption of the sovereignty of Hong Kong from the British rule. Tung Chee Hwa, a Shanghai-born Hong Kong businessman who was seen being favoured by Jiang Zemin, General Secretary of the Communist Party of China, was the ultimate winner of the election, defeating former Chief Justice Yang Ti-liang and tycoon Peter Woo with a large margin. According to the Basic Law of Hong Kong, the mini-constitution of the HKSAR, the first Chief Executive of Hong Kong was selected by a 400-member Selection Committee. Tung Chee Hwa, a Shanghai-born Hong Kong shipping magnate, was not widely known until the campaign began. The pro-democrats also backed veteran politician and member of the Legislative Council Szeto Wah to launch a petition movement as the civil Chief Executive of Hong Kong to mock the election of the 400-member Selection Committee. Tung Chee Hwa, Yang Ti-liang and Peter Woo all secured more than 50 nominations to enter the race, while Simon Li, to his shock, fell a few votes and was eliminated. On 11 December 1996, the election was held at the Hong Kong Convention and Exhibition Centre (HKCEC), where 400-member Selection Committee elected Tung Chee Hwa as the Chief Executive of Hong Kong. Tung received 320 votes against 49 votes for Yang Ti-liang and 36 votes for Peter Woo. Tung Chee Hwa was appointed as the first Chief Executive of Hong Kong of the HKSAR by Premier Li Peng under State Council Order 207 at the Great Hall of the People in Beijing on 18 11 December 1996." .

ns1:46004 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1999 Austrian legislative election were held in Austria on 03 October 1999. Although the Social Democratic Party of Austria remained the largest party in the National Council, a right-wing coalition government was formed by the Austrian Freedom Party (FPÖ) and the Austrian People's Party (Austrian People's Party) after several months of negotiations. Although the FPÖ had finished a fraction of a point ahead of the Austrian People's Party in the popular vote, Austrian People's Party leader Wolfgang Schüssel became Federal Chancellor of Austria rather than controversial FPÖ leader Jörg Haider." .

ns1:46098 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Wana was a 16 March 2004 military engagement between the Pakistan Army and members of Osama Bin Laden's al-Qaeda at Azam Warsak, near the South Waziristan Agency town of Wana. The troops were later proceeded to move into the Shawal Valley of North Waziristan, and later South Waziristan Agency. On 16 March 2004, the army troops made the first contact with the al-Qaeda foreign fighters around the South Waziristan Agency village of Wana. In the night of 18 16 March 2004, the army troops and foreign fighters again engage in a heavy and bloody gun battle occurred wherein infantry troops had repeatedly beaten the assaults after assaults. On 20 16 March 2004, the ISI's CAD and Military Intelligence, and a unit of troops reportedly saw a mysterious \"foreigner\" fleeing the siege, the Military Intelligence theorized that it may have been Ayman al-Zawahiri, since Uzbek militant Tohir Yuldashev had earlier escaped to Afghanistan while injured in a battle. The Battle of Wana also led to the unannounced War in North-West Pakistan." .

ns1:46568 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2019 European Parliament election in Italy were held on 26 May 2019, electing member of the European Parliament of the 9th Italian delegation to the European Parliament as part of the 2019 European Parliament election held across the European Union. In 2014, the governing Democratic Party (PD) of Prime Minister Matteo Renzi won the election with 40.8% of the vote and 31 seats, followed by the Five Star Movement (M5S) with 21.2% and 17 seats and Forza Italia (2013) (FI) with 16.8% and 13 seats. Under the new leadership of Benedetto Della Vedova, More Europe (+Eu) formed a joint list with Italy in Common (IiC), the Italian Socialist Party (PSI), the Italian Republican Party (PRI), the Italian section of the European Democratic Party (EDP), the Liberal Democratic Alliance for Italy (ALI) and minor parties; Italian Left (SI), the Communist Refoundation Party (PRC), minor parties and individual splinters from the disbanded Free and Equal (LeU) formed The Left (LS), a joint list inspired by the Party of the European Left (PEL); and the Federation of the Greens (FdV), Possible (Pos) and Green Italy (GI) formed Green Europe (EV) under the banner of the European Green Party (EGP). On the centre-right side, FI welcomed in its lists candidates of the Union of the Centre (UdC) and several alike minor parties while Brothers of Italy (FdI) formed a partnership with Direction Italy (DI) and minor groups." .

ns1:46583 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1592 papal conclave (10 January 1592–30) elected Clement VIII in succession to Pope Innocent IX. He was then succeeded by Urban VII (September 15 – September 27, 1590), Gregory XIV (December 5, 1590 – October 16, 1591) and Innocent IX (October 29 – December 30, 1591), so the papal conclave of 10 January 1592 was the fourth in only seventeen months. Besides Santori, only Madruzzo, Tolomeo Gallio, Paleotti, Marco Antonio Colonna and Aldobrandini were acceptable to Spain and it seemed clear that the new Pope would be one of these. The conclave began on 10 January 1592. On 30 January 1592, Cardinal Ippolito Aldobrandini was unanimously elected to the papacy and took the name of Clement VIII." .

ns1:46809 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1947 Indian Independence Act 1947 (1947 c. 30 (10 & 11. is an Act of the Parliament of the United Kingdom that partitioned British India into the two new independent dominions of Dominion of India and Dominion of Pakistan. The Act received the Royal Assent on 18 July 1947 and thus Dominion of India and Dominion of Pakistan, comprising West (modern day Dominion of Pakistan) and East (modern day Bangladesh) regions, came into being on 15 August. Clement Attlee, the Prime Minister of the United Kingdom, announced on 1947 that: Attlee wrote to Mountbatten on 1947: \"It is, of course, important that the Indian States should adjust their relations with the authorities to whom it is intended to hand over power in British India; but as was explicitly stated by the Cabinet Mission His Majesty's Government do not intend to hand over their powers and obligations under paramountcy to any successor Government. There was much violence, and many Muslims from what would become Dominion of India fled to Dominion of Pakistan; and Hindus and Sikhs from what would become Dominion of Pakistan fled to Dominion of India. The state of Jammu and Kashmir was contiguous to both Dominion of India and Dominion of Pakistan, but its Hindu ruler chose to remain initially independent. Following a Pakistani tribal invasion, he acceded to Dominion of India on 1947, and the state was disputed between Dominion of India and Dominion of Pakistan. Following a breakdown of law and order, its Dewan requested Dominion of India to take over the administration on 1947. Between 1947 and March 1948 the rulers of several Muslim-majority states signed instruments of accession to join Dominion of Pakistan. The Indian Independence Act 1947 was subsequently repealed in Article 395 of the Constitution of Dominion of India and in Article 221 of the Constitution of Pakistan of 1956, both constitutions being intended to bring about greater independence for the new states." .

ns1:47115 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Walcheren Causeway (Operation Vitality) was an engagement of the Battle of the Scheldt between the 5th Canadian Infantry Brigade, elements of the British 52nd (Lowland) Infantry Division and troops of the German 15th Army in 1944. It was also the second major battle fought over a terrain feature known as the Sloedam during the Second World War. A narrow causeway connected the two, known to the Dutch as the Sloedam (it literally dammed the Sloe Channel) and in English as the Walcheren Causeway. By 31 October 1944, all lands surrounding the Scheldt estuary had been cleared of German control, save for Walcheren Island, whose coastal batteries commanded the approaches to the waterway. Two platoons of Le Régiment de Maisonneuve took over the bridgehead on Walcheren Island on 02 November 1944, but were forced back onto the Causeway. The Battle of Walcheren Causeway is commemorated annually by The Calgary Highlanders and Regimental Pipes and Drums with a parade and church service on the Wednesday night or weekend closest to the anniversary of the battle." .

ns1:47163 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017–18 Russian Premier League was the 26th season of the premier association football competition in Russia since the dissolution of the Soviet Union and the 15th under the current Russian Premier League name. As in the previous season, 16 teams played in the 2017–18 season. After the 2016–17 Russian Premier League, FC Orenburg, FC Tom Tomsk and FC Krylia Sovetov Samara were relegated to the 2017–18 Russian National Football League. They were replaced by three clubs from the 2016–17 Russian National Football League, FC Dinamo Moscow, FC Tosno and FC SKA-Energiya Khabarovsk. Thus, a total of 240 matches will be played, with 30 matches played by each team. Yenisey Krasnoyarsk won 6–4 on aggregate and were promoted to the 2018–19 Russian Premier League; Anzhi Makhachkala were relegated to the 2018–19 Russian National Football League. On 13, FC Amkar Perm announced that the Russian Football Union recalled their 2018–19 season license, making them ineligible for the Russian Premier League or Russian Football National League. Source: Notes:1: Zenit played most of last season at Petrovsky Stadium.2: Rostov played its final three home games of the season at Rostov Arena.3: Rubin Kazan played part of last season at Central Stadium.4: Ural played its final three home games of the season at Central Stadium.5: Team played last season in the Russian Football National League.6: Tosno played one of its home matches at a neutral venue and played last season in the Russian Football National League at Stadion im." .

ns1:47297 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Second Battle of Passchendaele was the culminating attack during the Third Battle of Ypres of the First World War. The battle took place in the Ypres Salient area of the Western Front, in and around the Belgian village of Passendale, between 26 October 1917 and 10 November 1917. The dates of the phases were tentatively given as 26 October 1917, 30 October and 6 November with a final smaller action on 10 November 1917. The Battle of Poelcappelle (9 October) and First Battle of Passchendaele (12 October) were costly for both sides and failed to achieve any appreciable advance opposite Passendale, although they did provide a slightly better starting line for another attack. The second stage on 30 October was intended to complete the capture of the positions the Canadian Corps had attacked on 26 October 1917 and gain a base for the final assault on Passendale. The Fifth Army suffered 14,219 casualties from 26 October 1917 to 9 November, Second Army casualties from 26 October 1917 to 10 November 1917 were 29,454, of which the Canadian Corps suffered 15,654 casualties. For the Canadian Corps, participation in the Second Battle of Passchendaele is commemorated with the Passendale Memorial located at the former site of the Crest Farm on the southwest fringe of Passendale village." .

ns1:47344 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Aztec religion originated from the indigenous Aztecs of central Mexico. Like other Mesoamerican religion, it also has practices such as human sacrifice in connection with many religious festivals which are in the Aztec calendar. This polytheism religion has many gods and goddesses; the Aztecs would often incorporate deities that were borrowed from other geographic regions and peoples into their own religious practices. It seems that the Aztec ruler at that time, Moctezuma II and the Aztecs, in general, referred to Cortés and the conquistadors as \"teotl\". Aztec scholar H. B. Nicholson (1971) classed the gods into three groups according to their conceptual meaning in general Mesoamerican religion. On the state level, religion was controlled by the Tlatoani and the high priests governing the main temples in the ceremonial precinct of the Aztec capital of Tenochtitlan. The Tlatoani of Tenochtitlan was the head of the cult of Huitzilopochtli and of the state religion of the Aztec. As with the impersonation of gods, Aztec ritual was often a reenactment of a mythical event which at once served to remind the Aztecs of their religion, but it also served to perpetuate the world by repeating the important events of the creation. human sacrifice was practiced on a grand scale throughout the Aztec, although the exact figures were unknown." .

ns1:47412 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Bretagne Classic, also called Bretagne Classic Ouest–France, is an elite cycling classic held annually in late summer around the Brittany village of Plouay in western France. The race was originally named Grand–Prix de Plouay and, from 1989 to 2015 GP Ouest-France, GP Ouest-France. The Bretagne Classic, originally named Circuit de Plouay and later the Grand-Prix de Plouay, was created in 1931 by former Tour de France doctor Berty, who used his influence to attract some of the biggest names of French cycling to the inaugural edition. Australians Simon Gerrans and Matthew Goss won in 2009 GP Ouest-France and 2010 GP Ouest-France respectively, with Norway's Edvald Boasson Hagen soloing to victory in 2012 GP Ouest-France and Italy's Filippo Pozzato helping resurrect his career with a surprise win in 2013 GP Ouest-France." .

ns1:47518 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Yellowstone Expedition of 1873 was an expedition of the United States Army in the summer of 1873 in Dakota Territory and Montana Territory, to survey a route for the Northern Pacific Railroad along the Yellowstone River. The Native American forces that fought against the expedition in Montana Territory were from the village of Sitting Bull, estimated at anywhere from 400 to 500 lodges with over 1000 Warriors. The main expedition was ready and started from Fort Rice, Dakota Territory on 20 June 1873. The expedition pushed on, crossing the flooded Little Missouri River, and entering into Montana Territory, reaching the Yellowstone River, on July 13, 1873. After scouting along the Musselshell River, Colonel David S. Stanley and the Yellowstone Expedition made its ways back down the Yellowstone River and returned to Dakota Territory in late 1873, with the expedition ending on 23 September 1873." .

ns1:47756 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Gagauz Khalky (GKh), also known as the Gagauz-Khalky People's Movement (GKPM), is a former Gagauzia separatist political party in Moldova. It was led by S. Gulgar from at least as early as 1992 until at least as recently as 1999, after which time the organization ceased to be listed by the CIA World Factbook." .

ns1:47978 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013–14 Northern Ireland Football League Cup (known as the WASP Solutions League Cup in the final for sponsorship purposes) was the 28th edition of Northern Ireland's secondary association football knock-out cup competition, and the first season under the control of the Northern Ireland Football League. The competition began on 13 August 2013 and ended on 25 January 2014 with the final. Cliftonville F.C. were the defending champions after defeating Crusaders 4–0 in the 2012–13 Irish League Cup to become the sixth different winner of the cup in six seasons." .

ns1:48447 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "In Your House 16: Canadian Stampede was the sixteenth In Your House professional wrestling pay-per-view (PPV) event produced by the World Wrestling Federation (WWF, now WWE), which took place on 06 July 1997, at the Canadian Airlines Saddledome in Calgary, Alberta, Canada. The main event was a ten-man tag team match featuring The Hart Foundation (Bret Hart, Jim Neidhart, Owen Hart, British Bulldog and Brian Pillman) against Stone Cold Steve Austin, Ken Shamrock, Goldust, and the Legion of Doom (Hawk and Animal). On the undercard, The Undertaker defended the WWF World Heavyweight Championship against Leon Allen White, The Great Sasuke faced Taka Michinoku and Hunter Hearst Helmsley wrestled Mankind. The main rivalry heading into the event involved Stone Cold Steve Austin and Bret Hart. Another predominant feud involved the WWF World Heavyweight Champion The Undertaker and Leon Allen White. Leon Allen White's manager, Paul Bearer, forced The Undertaker to do as he said or else he will tell a secret from the Undertaker's past. After the Undertaker attacked Leon Allen White in the match, Bearer told the secret next week: Throughout The Undertaker's childhood and teen years, he lived in a funeral home with his parents and half-brother. During an attack from the Undertaker after Leon Allen White's match with Rockabilly, Bearer said he heard that from Kane himself, because unbeknownst to The Undertaker, Kane had survived the fire and was still alive. Stone Cold Steve Austin's feud with Bret Hart ended after Canadian Stampede but immediately started a feud with his brother Owen Hart the following night by attacking him while he was singing the Canadian national anthem." .

ns1:48578 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 Ukrainian Cup Final was a association football match that was played place at the Metalist Stadium, Kharkiv, on 16 May 2010. The match was the 2009–10 Ukrainian Cup and was contested by FC Metalurh Donetsk and SC Tavriya Simferopol. Tavriya played in their second cup final after last appearing in 1994, where the side lost to Chornomorets Odessa on penalty kicks (5–3) after the matched finished 0–0 after extra time. FC Metalurh Donetsk were playing in their first cup final." .

ns1:49240 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "last man standing (LMS) or last team standing (LTS) is a multiplayer deathmatch gameplay mode featured in some first-person shooter computer and video games, and is also the essence of battle royale games. The aim of a player in a last man standing match is to dispatch their opponents and remain the sole survivor; the basic rules followed are generally the same of the deathmatch gametype, with an important difference: respawn is limited or not allowed at all. Several different variations of the last man standing mode exist, with the most common being team LMS." .

ns1:49779 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "INS Sindhurakshak (Sanskrit, for Protector of the Seas) was a Russian-made Kilo-class 877EKM (Sindhughosh-class submarine) diesel-electric submarine of the Indian Navy. Sindhurakshak was constructed at the Admiralty Shipyards in Saint Petersburg, Russia. The submarine was handed back to the Indian Navy on 27 January 2013, after which it sailed back to India, under Commander Rajesh Ramkumar." .

ns1:50096 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Tour of Slovenia (Slovene: Dirka po Sloveniji) was the 24th edition of the Tour of Slovenia cycling stage race, held between 15 June 2017 and 18 June 2017. He won the race by seven seconds overall, from Bahrain–Merida's Giovanni Visconti, while a further ten seconds in arrears was Jack Haig of Orica–Scott; having finished second in 2016 Tour of Slovenia, Haig completed the podium in 2017." .

ns1:50352 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Battle of Berne is used to refer to a association football match at the 1954 FIFA World Cup between Hungary national association football team and Brazil national football team, a quarter-final played on 27 June 1954 at the Wankdorf Stadium in Berne, Switzerland. Brazil national football team had scored six goals in two group games, and Hungary national association football team had scored 17. Brazil national football team had a reputation for attractive and attacking association football; in their group games, they beat Mexico 5–0, and drew the other 1–1 with Yugoslavia after extra time. Hungary national association football team had revolutionized association football tactics and coaching in Europe." .

ns1:50383 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 Danmark Rundt was a men's road bicycle racing held from 30 July 2008 to 03 August 2008. Steve Cummings of Barloworld finished second by nine seconds with Tom Stamsnijder of Team Gerolsteiner third. Danish rider Jakob Fuglsang won the race by nine seconds from Steve Cummings. The points winner was Matti Breschel with Kristoffer Gudmund Nielsen the winner of the mountains classification for best climber." .

ns1:50652 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Manifesto of the 343 (French: Manifeste des 343), was a French petition signed by 343 women \"who had the courage to say, 'I've had an abortion'\". It was an act of civil disobedience, since abortion was illegal in France, and by admitting publicly to having aborted, they exposed themselves to criminal prosecution. The manifesto was published in the social democratic French weekly magazine Le Nouvel Observateur on 05 April 1971. The manifesto called for the legalization of abortion and free access to contraception. The text of the manifesto was written by Simone de Beauvoir. It was the inspiration for a February 3, 1973, manifesto by 331 doctors declaring their support for abortion rights: We want freedom of abortion. (\"Who got the 343 sluts from the abortion manifesto pregnant?\")." .

ns1:50746 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Shenzhou program (/ˈʃɛnˈdʒoʊ/, Chinese: 神舟) is a crewed spaceflight initiative by People's Republic of China. Shenzhou missions are launched on the Long March 2F from the Jiuquan Satellite Launch Center. The China Manned Space Engineering Office provides engineering and administrative support for the crewed Shenzhou missions. The first uncrewed flight of the spacecraft was launched on 19 November 1999, after which Project 921-1 was renamed Shenzhou, a name reportedly chosen by CCP General Secretary Jiang Zemin." .

ns1:50921 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The ITV Lunchtime News is the afternoon news programme produced by ITN on the British television network ITV. ITN had provided a short lunchtime news summary to start the ITV schedules on a Saturday since 1959, with an afternoon news summary on a Sunday starting in the mid 1960s, however it was the lifting of the restrictions in 16 October 1972 which helped ITN to launch a codified, more solid weekday lunchtime news programme as part of a raft of new programming which would now take up broadcasting hours which were up to 1972 restricted to schools programming and adult education. The programme was first broadcast on 16 October 1972 as First Report, a twenty-minute bulletin presented by Robert Kee at 12:40pm. On 8 March 1999, coinciding with a further relaunch of ITN's news bulletins, John Suchet returned to the newly named ITV Lunchtime News to replace Murnaghan, now lead presenter of the new ITV Nightly News. Derham later left ITV News in June 2010 and was replaced by Nina Hossain. The ITV Lunchtime News presenter also fronts the impending ITV News London lunchtime bulletin from 13:55." .

ns1:50929 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2019 Summer Universiade, the XXX Summer Universiade (Italian: Universiadi estive 2019) was held in Naples, Italy, between 03 July 2019 and 14 July 2019. On 14 June 2012, the then-Brazilian Minister of Sports, Aldo Rebelo, and the president of CBDU (Confederação Brasileira do Desporto Universitário), Luciano Cabral, announced that after the failed bid by Brazil for the 2017 Summer Universiade, they would bid again for the 2019 Summer Universiade. On 31 January 2013, Miklós Tóth, the vice-chairman of the Hungarian Olympic Committee, announced that Hungary would make a bid for the 2019 or 2021 Summer Universiade with the aid of Alfréd Hajós' Plan (Hajós Alfréd Terv). On 5 March 2016, FISU reattributed the 2019 Universiade to Naples, Italy." .

ns1:51048 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Q School 2017 – Event 1 was the first of two qualifying tournaments for the 2017/18 snooker season. It took place from 9 to 14 May 2017 at Preston Guild Hall in Preston, England. The winners of the quarterfinals, Lukas Kleckers, Allan Taylor, Billy Joe Castle and Ashley Hugill, all qualified to receive professional snooker tour cards for the 2017/2018 and 2018/2019 seasons." .

ns1:51088 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of the Dukla Pass, also known as the Dukla / Carpatho-Dukla / Rzeszów-Dukla / Dukla-Prešov Offensive was the battle for control over the Dukla Pass on the border between Poland and Slovakia on the Eastern Front of World War II between Nazi Germany and the Soviet Union in September–28 October 1944. The battle which began on 08 September 1944 would not see the Soviet forces on the other side of the pass until 6 October, and German forces would stop their heavy resistance in the region only around 10 October. The battle would be counted among the most bloody in the entire Eastern Front of World War II and the history of Slovakia; one of the valleys in the pass near villages of Kapišová, Chyrowa, Iwla and Głojsce would become known as the Valley of Death. The plan was to push through the old Slovak-Polish border in the Carpathian Mountains via the Dukla Pass near Svidník to penetrate Slovakia proper. One of the biggest battles in the pass took place on and around Hill 534 in the northwest from the town of Dukla; the battle to capture this hill lasted from 10 to 20 September, and during that period the control of the hill changed more than 20 times. The area of the former Czechoslovakia state border—heavily fortified by the Germans—was captured on 6 October; it took almost a month for the Soviet forces to reach Slovakia. Soviet and Czechoslovakia forces would enter Svidník on 28 October 1944. In 1949, the Czechoslovakia government erected a memorial and cemetery southeast of the Dukla border crossing, in Vyšný Komárnik, the first liberated village on the territory of (then) Czechoslovakia. In 1956, the football club ATK Praha changed their name to Dukla Praha (Dukla Prague) in honour of those who had fallen in the battle." .

ns1:51390 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 Tour de Langkawi was the 13th edition of the Tour de Langkawi, a cycling stage race that took place in Malaysia. It started on 09 February 2008 in Alor Setar and ended on 17 February 2008 in Kuala Lumpur. Moldova's Ruslan Ivanov emerged as the winner of the race, followed by Matthieu Sprick second and Gustavo César third." .

ns1:51456 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 01 April 1737 Siege of Kandahar began when Nader Shah's Afsharid dynasty army invaded southern Afghanistan to topple the last Hotaki Empire stronghold of Loy Kandahar, which was held by Hussain Hotaki Empire. It took place in the Old Kandahar area of the modern city of Kandahar in Afghanistan and lasted until 24 March 1738, when the Hotaki Empire Afghans were defeated by the Afsharid dynasty army. Hotaki Empire was not a subject of Muhammad Shah, however a vast majority of his kinsmen did serve the Mughal Empire, thus the conflict had become a prelude of Nadir Shah's invasion. Realizing the hopelessness of the struggle, Hussain Hotaki Empire and a few Afghans retreated into the Kandahar citadel, leaving the rest of the inhabitants of the city to be killed or captured. The city of Kandahar was systematically destroyed by artillery fire and the surviving inhabitants were transferred to a new city that the Afsharid dynasty forces had prepared and planned to build about 6 miles south-east of the ancient city." .

ns1:51573 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1992 Lithuanian parliamentary election were held in Lithuania in two stages on 25 October 1992 and 15 November 1992. Where no candidate gained more than 50% of the vote on 25 October 1992, a run-off was held on 15 November 1992. In the aftermath, the leader of the LDDP, Algirdas Brazauskas, was elected the Speaker of the Sixth Seimas of Lithuania and assumed the title of acting President of Lithuania. The elections were held under the terms of the new electoral law, adopted on 9 July 1992; on the same day, the election date was set for 25 October 1992. In the first session of the newly elected Sixth Seimas of Lithuania, Brazauskas was elected the Speaker of the Member of the Seimas and assumed the title of acting President of Lithuania." .

ns1:51750 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "HQ Trivia is a trivia video game developed by Intermedia Labs for iOS, Android, iPadOS, and tvOS. First released in 2017, the HQ Trivia mobile app allows users to play in daily live trivia games, through which they can win or split prize money. HQ Trivia was developed by Vine creators Rus Yusupov and Colin Kroll, and credited as a production of Intermedia Labs. The mobile app's original game was HQ Trivia, in which players have 10 seconds to answer multiple-choice questions that incrementally increase in difficulty. On 14 February 2020, the company that owned HQ Trivia sent a memo to staff that \"effective today, HQ Trivia will cease operations and move to dissolution.\" HQ Trivia was first released on 2017, for iOS, and on 2017, for Android. The primary host of HQ Trivia was initially Scott Rogowsky. HQ Trivia has been made available worldwide—with the exception of Russia and India—on the iOS App Store and Google Play. On 14 February 2020, it was announced that the mobile app would immediately cease operations after its primary investors had stopped funding the company and a potential sale of HQ Trivia to an \"established business\" fell through. These shows were all discontinued in 14 February 2020, and HQ Trivia Sports returned (with Gambino) in May 2020. On Fridays, the game's format shifted for HQ Trivia Tunes, until that game ceased in 14 February 2020. On October 9, 2019, HQ Trivia announced and launched a spinoff trivia game called \"HQ Trivia Tunes\" hosted by New York City singer and songwriter Melody Alanna, who was a host of the former music trivia mobile app, \"Out of Tune\". The iOS and Android apps experienced many technical glitches as the mobile app grew in popularity, sparking outrage from players. Creators Rus Yusupov and Colin Kroll sought to attract investors and venture capitalist firms to fund HQ Trivia with the intent of landing a post-money valuation as high as $100 million. As of December 2017, HQ Trivia's fundraising prospects were ongoing and its parent company, Intermedia Labs, was still in talks with potential investors." .

ns1:51876 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Saint Patrick's Breastplate is an Old Irish prayer of protection of the \"Lorica\" type (hence Lorica Sancti Patricii, or \"The Lorica of Saint Patrick\") attributed to Saint Patrick. Because of this it is also known as the \"Lorica of St. Patrick\" or as \"St. Patrick's Breastplate\". The term Lorica is used of a number of Old Irish prayers, including one attributed to Dallán Forgaill and another to Saint Fursey." .

ns1:51909 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1971 Israel Super Cup was the 3rd Israel Super Cup (8th, including unofficial matches, as the competition wasn't played within the Israel Football Association in its first 5 editions, until 1969), an annual Israel football match played between the winners of the previous season's Top Division and Israel State Cup. The match was played between Maccabi Netanya F.C., champions of the 1970–71 Liga Leumit and Hakoah Amidar Ramat Gan F.C., winners of the 1970–71 Israel State Cup. At the match, played at Sar-Tov Stadium, Maccabi Netanya F.C. won 4–2 after extra time." .

ns1:52121 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 FIFA Confederations Cup Final was a football match to determine the winners of the 2013 FIFA Confederations Cup. The match was held at the Maracanã Stadium, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil, on 30 June 2013 and was contested by the winners of the semi-finals, Brazil and Spain. Brazil defeated Spain 3–0 with goals from Fred and Neymar, thus breaking Spain's record of 29 games without a defeat. The match was Brazil's fifth appearance in the final (after 1997, 1999, 2005 and 2009 FIFA Confederations Cup Final). Before the final, Brazil and Spain had previously faced each other eight times, of which Brazil have recorded a total of four wins compared to Spain's two, with the remaining two matches ending in draws. Brazil had won the FIFA Confederations Cup three times, in 1997 against Australia, in 2005 against Argentina and 2009 FIFA Confederations Cup Final against the United States. They had competed in every Confederations Cup competition since FIFA's takeover in 1997, with Brazil losing the 1999 final against Mexico 4–3. They reached the semi-finals, but lost 2–0 to the United States, resulting in Spain competing for the third-place play-off against South Africa, who had lost 1–0 against Brazil in the second semi-final match. The Maracanã Stadium in Rio de Janeiro was announced as the venue of the final. Björn Kuipers of the Royal Dutch Football Association (KNVB) and UEFA was selected to referee the final. On 14 January 2009 FIFA Confederations Cup Final, Kuipers was promoted to elite level in European association football. The ball fell to Brazil forward Fred, who had slipped and while lying on the ground managed to improvise and poke the ball past Casillas to give Brazil the lead. Brazil increased their lead just before half time when Oscar passed to Neymar on the left side of the penalty area and he hit the ball left footed hard and high at the near post past Casillas. The third goal for Brazil arrived two minutes into the second half when Fred curled the ball low right footed inside the far post from the left, with Casillas getting his fingers to the shot but unable to keep it out." .

ns1:52151 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 United States presidential election was the 57th quadrennial United States presidential election, held on Tuesday, 06 November 2012. The incumbent Democratic President of the United States Barack Obama and his running mate, Vice President of the United States Joe Biden, were re-elected to a second and final term. They defeated the Republican ticket of businessman and former Governor Mitt Romney of Massachusetts and Representative Paul Ryan of Wisconsin. All four major candidates for President of the United States and Vice President of the United States went on to hold significant public office after this election. Obama served his second term as President of the United States and was succeeded by Donald Trump in 2016 United States presidential election, while Biden was elected President of the United States four years later by defeating Trump in 2020 becoming the first former Vice President of the United States to be elected President of the United States since George H. W. Bush and the first former Vice President of the United States to be elected President of the United States following a time after serving as Vice President of the United States since Richard Nixon. Another debate took place a month later, with Newt Gingrich, Mitt Romney, former Utah Governor Jon Huntsman, and Minnesota Congresswoman Michele Bachmann participating, and Gary Johnson excluded. The participants were Gary Johnson (Libertarian), Jill Stein (Green), Virgil Goode (Constitution), and Rocky Anderson (Justice). Red denotes states (or congressional districts that contribute an electoral vote) won by Republican Mitt Romney; blue denotes those won by Democrat Barack Obama. Gary Johnson's popular vote total set a Libertarian Party record, and his popular vote percentage was the second-best showing for a Libertarian in a United States presidential election, trailing only Ed Clark's in 1980. Johnson would go on to beat this record in the 2016 United States presidential election United States presidential election, winning the most votes for the Libertarian ticket in history." .

ns1:52157 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "KDE Plasma 5 is the fifth and current generation of the graphical workspaces environment created by KDE primarily for Linux systems. KDE Plasma 5 is the successor of KDE Plasma 4 and was first released on 15 July 2014. KDE Plasma 5 is built using Qt 5 and KDE Frameworks 5, predominantly plasma-framework. Plasma 5 is a default desktop environment (or one of the defaults) on Linux distributions, such as: Plasma Mobile is a Plasma variant for smartphones." .

ns1:52244 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 World Allround Speed Skating Championships took place at the indoor ice rink of the Thialf arena in Heerenveen, Netherlands, on 22 March 2014–23 March 2014. Ireen Wüst won the women's title for the fifth time while Koen Verweij won his first world title." .

ns1:52279 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Bayern-Rundfahrt 2010 was the 31st edition of the Bayern-Rundfahrt cycle race and was held on 26 May 2010 to 30 May 2010. The race started in Erding and finished in Fürstenfeldbruck." .

ns1:52503 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Zenkunen War (前九年の役, Zenkunen no Eki), also known in English as the Former Nine Years' War or the Early Nine Years' War, was fought from 1051 to 1063, in Japan's Mutsu Province, at the far north of the main island of Honshū. Like the other major conflicts of the Heian period, such as the Gosannen War and the Genpei War, the Zenkunen war was a struggle for power within the samurai clans." .

ns1:52538 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Criticize Lin, Criticize Confucius (simplified Chinese: 批林批孔运动; traditional Chinese: 批林批孔運動; pinyin: pī Lín pī Kǒng yùndòng; also called the Anti-Lin Biao, Anti-Confucius campaign) was a political propaganda campaign started by Mao Zedong and his wife, Jiang Qing, the leader of the Gang of Four. It lasted from 1973 until the end of the Cultural Revolution, in 1976. In late 1973 - early 1974 begins the second phase of the campaign, when as the main critics of Confucius were the masses. The universities were organized special courses, preparing a program of criticism of certain provisions of the Confucius used by Lin Biao. The attacks on Confucius merged with a pre-existent campaign to criticize Lin Biao. With the deployment of the campaign it became clear that \"criticism of Lin Biao and Confucius\" was directed not so much against the \"enemies of the past,\" as against the \"enemies of today.\" Based on People's Daily articles, Russian researcher Leo Delyusin believed that locals formally belonging to the campaign \"criticize Lin Biao and Confucius\" sabotaged it. The attempts to disrupt and distort the meaning of the campaign against Lin Biao and Confucius combined with a formal public statement about the importance of the campaign, and in practice - curtail it and to address specific cases. After the 1974 campaign \"criticize Lin Biao and Confucius\" reached its climax, and soon subsided." .

ns1:52566 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "A 1088 papal election subsequent to the death of pope Victor III in 1087 was held on 12 March 1088. In this situation the adherents of Victor III assembled on 09 March 1088 at Terracina, under the protection of the Norman army, to elect the new pope. However, it was not until 1088 that Urban II was able to travel to Rome." .

ns1:52694 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2003 World Allround Speed Skating Championships were held in Ruddalens IP in Gothenburg, Sweden, on 08 February 2003–09 February 2003. Canadian Cindy Klassen and Dutchman Gianni Romme became the world champions." .

ns1:52868 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "goose pulling (also called gander pulling, goose riding, pulling the goose or goose neck tearing) was a blood sport practiced in parts of the Netherlands, Belgium, England, and North America from the 17th to the 19th centuries. goose pulling is attested in the Netherlands as early as the start of the 17th century; the poet Gerbrand Adriaensz Bredero referred to it in his 1622 poem Boerengeselschap (\"Company of Peasants\"), describing how a party of peasants going to a goose-pulling contest near Amsterdam end up in a brutal brawl, leading to the lesson that it is best for townspeople to stay away from peasant pleasures." .

ns1:53309 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Final Unfinished Voyage of Jack Aubrey is the unfinished twenty-first historical novel in the Aubrey-Maturin series by English author Patrick O'Brian, first published in its incomplete form in 2004. The book is the posthumous publication of an uncompleted typescript, published as a result of Patrick O'Brian's position as an accomplished author of historical nautical fiction. On the dust jacket of 21, the back includes an appreciation of O'Brian from Geoff Hunt, who drew covers for the 20 completed books of the series as well as for 21, beneath a monochrome drawing of a sailing ship." .

ns1:53516 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Nauheim (also known as the Battle of the Johannisberg or Johannesberg) was a battle of the Seven Years' War fought near Bad Nauheim in the Landgraviate of Hesse-Kassel on 30 August 1762." .

ns1:53749 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1990 Masters was a professional non-ranking snooker tournament that took place between 04 February 1990 and 11 February 1990 at the Wembley Conference Centre in London, England. The final meanwhile had Stephen Hendry winning his second Masters title and becoming the second player after Cliff Thorburn to retain the title by defeating John Parrott 9–4 the final. 1989 Masters Stephen Hendry was the number 1 seed with World Champion Steve Davis seeded 2." .

ns1:53797 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Jiu Bridge (Romanian: Podul Jiului) is a historic truss bridge across the Jiu River. It consisted of five arches mounted on pillars and connected two parts of the city of Târgu Jiu, Romania, where the streets Unirii and Calea Severinului meet. At the end of the 20th century, three of the original five arches have been moved one kilometer north from their initial location, to form a shorter bridge that connects Târgu Jiu Central park with an artificial island on the Jiu river. The bridge was built in 1896 by the company Daydé & Pillé of Paris (now part of Eiffel Constructions métalliques). During Romania during World War I, on October 14/27, 1916, heavy fighting took place for the control of the bridge between the German Alpen Korps of general Falkenhayn and Romanian military forces assisted by local population." .

ns1:54104 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2011 Danish general election were held in Denmark on 15 September 2011 to elect the 179 members of the Folketing. The incumbent centre-right coalition led by Venstre lost power to a centre-left coalition led by the Social Democrats making Helle Thorning-Schmidt the country's first female Prime Minister of Denmark. Anders Fogh Rasmussen, who had been re-elected Prime Minister of Denmark following the 2007 Danish general election, resigned on 5 April 2009 to become the Secretary General of NATO in August. Polls indicated a preference for early elections over simply having Finance Minister Lars Løkke Rasmussen take over as PM; the Social Democrats' Helle Thorning-Schmidt was also suggested as the preferred candidate for PM. The former Prime Minister of Denmark, Lars Løkke Rasmussen, led a centre-right minority government consisting of the Liberal Party and the Conservative People's Party. Incumbent Prime Minister of Denmark Lars Løkke Rasmussen congratulated Thorning-Schmidt after conceding defeat: \"So tonight I hand over the keys to the prime minister’s office to Helle Thorning-Schmidt. Prime Minister of Denmark Lars Løkke Rasmussen then tendered the cabinet's resignation to Queen Margrethe II on 16 September, following which she met with the various parties and tasked Social Democrat leader Helle Thorning-Schmidt with the formation of a new government." .

ns1:54124 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Trofeo Alfredo Binda-Comune di Cittiglio was the 17th running of the women's Trofeo Alfredo Binda-Comune di Cittiglio, a women's road bicycle racing in Italy. It was the second race of the 2015 UCI Women's Road World Cup season and was held on 29 March 2015 starting and finishing in Cittiglio. Lizzie Deignan won the sprint, ahead of Ferrand-Prevot and Van Der Breggen, winning the first World Cup race of the season for Boels Dolmans 2015. Behind the six riders Annemiek van Vleuten won the sprint of the peloton ahead of Ellen van Dijk." .

ns1:54339 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Clonmel took place between 27 01 April 1650 – 18 01 May 1650 during the Cromwellian conquest of Ireland when the town of Clonmel in County Tipperary was besieged by Oliver Cromwell's New Model Army. As a result, he tried to take Clonmel immediately by assault, rather than opt for a lengthy siege." .

ns1:54358 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Iron pipe scandal (Swedish: järnrörsskandalen) was a political scandal in Sweden involving individuals related to the Sweden Democrats (Swedish: Sverigedemokraterna, SD). Erik Almqvist, Kent Ekeroth and Christian Westling, all high-ranking members of the Sweden Democrats (the first two were members of parliament when the incident was uncovered, Ekeroth as their candidate as Attorney General), had been involved in a verbal altercation with comedian Soran Ismail. Footage from the incident, captured by Ekeroth, shows that Erik Almqvist had told Ismail, who is of Kurdish descent, that he \"acts like a wog\", argues \"like a little pussy\" and that Sweden is \"my country, not yours\". The Sweden Democrats and Erik Almqvist justified the trio's actions by stating that Ismail had insulted them in his comedy routine, and that the man who had threatened them had claimed to belong to a criminal organization. The Sweden Democrats has also criticized Expressen, who released the initial story, for cherry picking only certain parts of the footage." .

ns1:54607 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Invasion of Dagestan (Russian: Дагестанская война), also known as the Invasion of Militants in Republic of Dagestan (Russian: Нашествие чеченских боевиков в Дагестан) began when the Chechnya-based Islamic International Peacekeeping Brigade (IIPB), an Islamist group, led by warlords Shamil Basayev, Ibn al-Khattab, and Ramzan Akhmadov, Arbi Barayev invaded the neighboring Russian republic of Republic of Dagestan, on 7 02 August 1999, in support of the Islamic Djamaat of Dagestan separatist rebels. The war ended with a major victory for the Russian Federation and Republic of Dagestan Republic, and the retreat of the IIPB. The Invasion of Dagestan served as the main casus belli alongside the series of apartment bombings in 14 September 1999 for the Second Chechen War. A series of invasions of Republic of Dagestan from Chechnya took place during the inter-war period, culminating in the 1997 attack on a federal military garrison of the 136th Motorized Rifle Regiment near the Dagestani town of Buinaksk. In April 1999, Magomedov, the \"Emir of the Islamic Djamaat of Dagestan,\" made an appeal to the \"Islamic patriots of the Caucasus\" to \"take part in the jihad\" and participate in \"liberating Republic of Dagestan and the Caucasus from the Russian colonial yoke.\" On August 10, they announced the birth of the \"independent Islamic State of Republic of Dagestan\" and declared war on \"the traitorous Dagestani government\" and \"Russia's occupation units\". On the morning of September 5, Chechen rebels launched a second invasion into the lowland Novolaksky District of Republic of Dagestan seizing the border village of Tukhchar, this time with a larger force numbering 200 fighters led by Umar Edilsultanov. According to Boris Berezovsky, the war was planned for six months before the Republic of Dagestan events, over his objections. The invasion of Republic of Dagestan leading to the start of the new Russian-Chechen conflict was regarded by the Russian journalist Anna Politkovskaya as a provocation initiated from Moscow to start war in Chechnya, because Russian forces provided safe passage for Islamic fighters back to Chechnya." .

ns1:54713 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Cisterna took place during World War II, on 30 January 1944–02 February 1944, near Cisterna di Latina, Italy, as part of the Battle of Anzio, part of the Italian Campaign. During this battle, the 1st, 3rd, and 4th U.S. Army Ranger battalions, the 83rd Chemical Mortar Battalion, and the 509th Parachute Infantry Battalion, which had been brigaded as the 6615th Ranger Force (Provisional) commanded by Colonel William O. Darby, were assigned to support the renewal of an attack by Major General Lucian Truscott's 3rd Infantry Division, which had previously failed to take Cisterna di Latina from 25 January–27 January. On 22 30 January 1944, the Allies launched Battle of Anzio, the amphibious landing by the U.S. and British divisions of U.S. VI Corps in the area of Anzio and Nettuno. A fictionalized account of the battle was depicted in the motion picture Anzio (1968) and at the end of the film Darby's Rangers (1958)." .

ns1:54782 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Action of 22 September 1914 was an attack by the German U-boat SM U-9 that took place during the World War I. Three obsolete Royal Navy cruisers of the 7th Cruiser Squadron, manned mainly by reservists and sometimes referred to as the Live Bait Squadron, were sunk by SM U-9 while patrolling the southern North Sea. The cruisers were part of the Southern Force (Rear-Admiral Arthur Christian) composed of the flagship Euryalus, the light cruiser Amethyst and the 7th Cruiser Squadron (7th CS, also known as Cruiser Squadron C, Rear-Admiral H. H. Campbell, nicknamed the live-bait squadron), comprising the HMS Cressy-class armoured cruisers HMS Bacchante, HMS Aboukir, HMS Hogue, HMS Cressy and Euryalus, the 1st and 3rd Destroyer flotillas, ten submarines of the 8th Oversea Flotilla and the attached Active-class scout cruiser, Fearless. On 20 September, Euryalus returned to port to re-coal and by 22 September 1914, HMS Aboukir, HMS Hogue and HMS Cressy were on patrol under the command of Captain J. E. Drummond of HMS Aboukir. On the morning of 22 September 1914, Kapitänleutnant Otto Weddigen, the commander of SM U-9 spotted HMS Aboukir, HMS Hogue and HMS Cressy. At 06:20, SM U-9 fired a torpedo at the middle ship from a range of 550 yd (500 m) and struck HMS Aboukir on the starboard side, flooding the engine room and causing the ship to stop immediately. At 07:20, SM U-9 fired two torpedoes toward HMS Cressy from her stern torpedo tubes at a range of 1,000 yd (910 m)." .

ns1:54934 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 82nd Academy Awards ceremony, presented by the Academy of Motion Picture Arts and Sciences (AMPAS), honored the best films of 2009 and took place on 07 March 2010, at the Dolby Theatre in Hollywood, Los Angeles beginning at 5:30 p.m. PST / 8:30 p.m. EST. The ceremony was televised in the United States by American Broadcasting Company, and was produced by Bill Mechanic and Adam Shankman and directed by Hamish Hamilton. Actors Alec Baldwin and Steve Martin hosted the show. On February 20, 2010, in a ceremony at the Beverly Wilshire Hotel in Beverly Hills, California, the Academy Awards for Technical Achievement were presented by host Elizabeth Banks. The nominees for the 82nd Academy Awards were announced on February 2, 2010, at 5:38 a.m. PST (13:38 UTC) at the Samuel Goldwyn Theater in Beverly Hills, California, by Tom Sherak, president of the Academy, and actress Anne Hathaway. Choreographer Adam Shankman and Bill Mechanic were hired as producers for the ceremony. On March 1, 2010, WABC-TV New York, American Broadcasting Company's flagship station, announced that it would likely end its services with cable television company Cablevision on 07 March 2010, the weekend of the 82nd Academy Awards." .

ns1:55005 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Four Nationals Figure Skating Championships included the Czech Figure Skating Championships, Slovak Figure Skating Championships, Polish Figure Skating Championships, and Hungarian Figure Skating Championships. The event was hosted by the Slovak association in Bratislava from 20 to 22 December 2013. It was the sixth consecutive season that the Czech Figure Skating Championships, Slovak Figure Skating Championships, and Polish Figure Skating Championships held their national championships together and the first season that Hungarian Figure Skating Championships participated." .

ns1:55043 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Capture of Gibraltar by Anglo-Dutch forces of the Grand Alliance occurred between 01 August 1704 and 04 August 1704 during the War of the Spanish Succession. The governor, Diego de Salinas, agreed to surrender Gibraltar and its small garrison on 04 August 1704. The Grand Alliance failed in its objective of replacing Philip V with Charles III as King of Spain, but in the peace negotiations Gibraltar was ceded to Britain. At the start of the War of the Spanish Succession, Portugal was nominally an ally of the Bourbons: France under Louis XIV, and Spain under his grandson, Philip V. Although not a belligerent, Portugal's harbours were closed to the enemies of the Bourbon powers – principally the vessels of England and the Dutch Republic. Spain attempted to retake Gibraltar in Thirteenth Siege of Gibraltar and most notably in Great Siege of Gibraltar, when it entered the American Revolutionary War on the American side as an ally of France. If he had succeeded in his attempt to ascend the throne in Madrid the difficulty of keeping Gibraltar for England would have been politically very great. In an attempt by the French to retake Gibraltar, the one full-dress naval engagement of the war was fought off Málaga on 24 August; afterwards, French and Spanish troops Twelfth Siege of Gibraltar at the land approaches, defended by a small garrison of sailors, soldiers, and marines." .

ns1:55064 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 European Women's Handball Championship was the eleventh continental tournament for women's national teams, organized by the European Handball Federation. The second jointly hosted edition in the competition's history took place in Hungary and Croatia from 7 to 21 December 2014. Norway women's national handball team won their sixth title after beating Spain women's national handball team 28–25 in the final. Sweden women's national handball team defeated Montenegro women's national handball team 25–23 to capture the bronze medal. However, they later won the 2015 World Women's Handball Championship, vacating the European champion Olympic berth which fell to Spain women's national handball team as the runner-up. The two next-best placed teams, Sweden women's national handball team and Montenegro women's national handball team, earned spots in the handball at the 2016 Summer Olympics – women's qualification. As a result, six federations (Croatia, Hungary, Iceland, Slovakia, Sweden women's national handball team and Turkey) showed intention to host the championship. The EHF Executive Committee decided between the two remaining aspirants on its meeting on 9 April 2011, awarding the right to host the 11th Women’s EHF European Handball Championship to Hungary and Croatia. The draw was held on 2014 at 13:00 local time in Zagreb, Croatia. Note: As Norway women's national handball team later qualified for the olympics as World Champions, the qualification slot went to the runner up instead and Montenegro women's national handball team qualified for the qualification tournament instead of Spain women's national handball team." .

ns1:55097 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Kraków was one of the battles during the Sweden invasion of Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth (Second Northern War / Deluge). It started on 25 September 1655 and ended on 13 October 1655. On 1655, when news of Sweden victories reached the city, the mayor of Kraków, Andrzej Cieniowicz, urged residents to organize defence of the ancient Polish capital. News of this battle quickly reached Kraków, together with Sweden demands for capitulation. On October 6, Charles Gustav returned to Kraków, and while inspecting Sweden positions, his horse was killed by a Polish bullet, near St. Florian's Gate. The Swedes allowed Polish units to leave Kraków, and march to winter quarters in western Lesser Poland, near Oświęcim, Zator, Sławków and Siewierz. Soon afterwards, 2,500 Sweden infantry plus 500 reiters entered Kraków." .

ns1:55222 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Godstone air disaster occurred on 06 November 1929 when a Junkers G 24 of Deutsche Luft Hansa crashed at Godstone, Surrey, United Kingdom while on an international scheduled flight from Croydon Airport, Surrey, United Kingdom to Amsterdam Airport Schiphol, Amsterdam, the Netherlands. On 06 November 1929, the aircraft was operating a scheduled international passenger flight from Croydon Airport to Amsterdam. It was reported that the pilot may have been attempting to return to Croydon Airport when the aircraft crashed into some trees at Marden Park, Godstone in thick fog." .

ns1:55317 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 FIFA World Cup qualification was a series of tournaments organised by the six FIFA FIFA. The 2014 FIFA World Cup featured 32 teams, with one place reserved for the host nation, Brazil. The first qualification match, between Montserrat and Belize national football team, was played on 15 June 2011, and the Belizean striker Deon McCaulay scored the first goal in qualification. The FIFA Executive Committee decided to approve the change of date for the preliminary draw of the 2014 FIFA World Cup, which was held six months earlier than in the past, to allow the FIFA to begin their qualifying competitions in good time. The distribution by confederation for the 2014 FIFA World Cup is: UEFA and CAF have a guaranteed number of places, whereas the number of qualifiers from other FIFA is dependent on play-offs between AFC's fifth-placed team and CONMEBOL's fifth-placed team, and between CONCACAF's fourth-placed team and OFC's first-placed team. After the 2014 FIFA World Cup of 2014 FIFA World Cup finals, the percentage of teams from each confederation that passed through to the round of 16 was as follows: For FIFA World Cup qualifying stages using a league format, the method used for separating teams level on points is the same for all Confederations. Qualification began with a 2014 FIFA World Cup of 12 two-legged knockout ties, which were held between 11 and 16 November 2011. The five winners of these ties – held in October and 19 November 2013 – advanced to the 2014 FIFA World Cup finals." .

ns1:55584 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "death of Jeannie Saffin (20 March 1921 – 23 September 1982) was a British woman whose death from fire in 1982 is cited by paranormal researchers and authors as an example of spontaneous human combustion, and is reported to be the most recent suspected case in the UK. Jeannie Saffin was born in Edmonton, London with birth defects that led to mental handicaps which reduced her abilities to those of a child. On the evening of 15 23 September 1982, she was at the family's home in Edmonton with her father, Jack Saffin, and her brother-in-law, Don Carroll. Saffin's case has been held as an example of spontaneous human combustion by paranormal researchers and authors. An alternate view debunks the evidence pointing towards Saffin being an example of spontaneous human combustion. Opponents of the spontaneous human combustion theory also have a potential explanation for the source of the flame that ignited Saffin's clothing, causing the fire." .

ns1:55715 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1978 French Grand Prix was a Formula One motor race held at Circuit Paul Ricard on 02 July 1978. The 54-lap race was won by Mario Andretti, driving a Lotus-Ford, with teammate Ronnie Peterson second and James Hunt third in a McLaren-Ford. Following the voluntary withdrawal of the \"fan car\", Brabham had to revert to their previous car again, but it did not deter them, with John Watson on pole and Niki Lauda starting third behind Mario Andretti's Lotus." .

ns1:55797 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Fornovo took place 30 km (19 miles) southwest of the city of Parma on 06 July 1495. Charles VIII was on good terms with the two powers in northern Italy, Duchy of Milan and Republic of Venice, and both had encouraged his claim over the Kingdom of Naples. Realization struck them, especially the Venetians and the new Duke of Duchy of Milan, Ludovico Sforza, that unless Charles was stopped Italy would soon just be another province of France. The Italian states rallied and on March 31 in Republic of Venice, the Italian War of 1494–1498 was proclaimed. The signatories were the Republic of Venice, the Duke of Duchy of Milan, the Pope, the monarchs of Castile and Aragon, the King of England and the Holy Roman Emperor. On 27 June the Venetians and their allies established camp near Fornovo di Taro (44°41′N 10°06′E), some 30 km southwest of Parma, to wait for the French. Some members wanted to attack the rear guard of the French to try to seize their loot, while others cautioned that Italy was risking too much in this battle as this was just one French army and others could potentially be called upon. Two days later, on 06 July 1495, Charles decided to offer battle because the French were short of provisions. The battle was reported in Republic of Venice as a victory, and was recorded and celebrated as such." .

ns1:55907 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The American Library Association and Library of Congress Romanization Tables for Russian, or the ALA-LC romanization for Russian, are a set of rules for the romanization of Russian-language text from Cyrillic script to Latin script. The romanization tables were first discussed by the American Library Association in 1885, and published in 1904 and 1908, including rules for romanizing some languages written in Cyrillic script: Church Slavic, Serbo-Croatian, and Russian in the pre-reform alphabet. The system for Russian remains virtually unchanged from 1941 to the latest release, with the current Russian table published online in 2012." .

ns1:55915 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Guadalajara (08 March 1937–23, 1937) saw the People's Republican Army (Ejército Popular Republicano, or EPR) defeat Italian and Nationalist forces attempting to encircle Madrid during the Spanish Civil War. The battle opened with an Italian offensive on 08 March 1937. The Second Spanish Republic successfully launched their counter-offensive from 18 March to 23 March 1937. But, after all, Guadalajara remained the first and the only setback for the Italian army in Spain." .

ns1:55949 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1972 South African Grand Prix, formally the Sixth AA Grand Prix of South Africa (Afrikaans: Sesde AA Suid-Afrikaanse Grand Prix), was a Formula One motor race held at Kyalami on 04 March 1972. The 79-lap race was won by McLaren driver Denny Hulme after he started from fifth position. Although there was more than a month between the Argentine Grand Prix and South Africa, most cars were transported direct to Kyalami from Buenos Aires. Mike Hailwood was next up on the second row, joined by Denny Hulme in his McLaren M19A. The race was held over 79 laps of the Kyalami circuit. At the start, Denny Hulme briefly held the lead before Jackie Stewart moved ahead, meanwhile Clay Regazzoni had a bad get away and was back in the midfield." .

ns1:56080 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The final Rus'–Byzantine War (1043) was, in essence, an unsuccessful naval raid against Constantinople instigated by Yaroslav the Wise and led by his eldest son, Vladimir of Novgorod, in 1043. There are good reasons to believe that the campaign was not over in 1043 (as Greek sources seem to imply), but continued with the Rus' capture of Chersonesus (Korsun') the following year: Careful analysis of these facts led Vera Bryusova to conclude that hostilities were renewed in 1044 or 1045, when Vladimir advanced on Chersonesus and captured it, retaining the town until the Byzantine Empire, involved in several other wars, agreed to conclude a favourable treaty with Rus' and give a princess in marriage to his younger brother. If so, the situation would be almost identical to the conquest of Chersonesus by Vladimir the Great, which, according to most Slavonic sources, precipitated the Christianization of Kievan Rus' back in 988." .

ns1:56309 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1965 Tour de France was the 52nd edition of the Tour de France, one of cycling's Grand Tours. It took place between 22 June 1965 and 14 July 1965, with 22 stages covering a distance of 4,188 km (2,602 mi). In his first year as a professional, Felice Gimondi, a substitute replacement on the Salvarani team, captured the overall title ahead of Raymond Poulidor, last year's second-place finisher. Besides Gimondi's first tour and win, it was a first for other reasons: the 1965 Tour started in Cologne (the first time the Tour started in Germany, and only the third time it started outside France), and it was the first time the start ramp was used in time trials. Jan Janssen, who won the points classification the previous year successfully defended his title; he won another points title in 1967 and the overall title at the 1968 Tour de France. The 1965 Tour de France started on 22 June 1965, and had one rest day in Barcelona." .

ns1:56425 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Chinese Grand Prix (formally known as the 2013 Formula 1 UBS Chinese Grand Prix) was a Formula One auto racing that was held on 14 April 2013 at the Shanghai International Circuit in Shanghai, People's Republic of China. The race was the third round of the 2013 Formula One World Championship, and marked the tenth running of the Chinese Grand Prix as a round of the Formula One World Championship. Lewis Hamilton led from the start, ahead of Fernando Alonso, Felipe Massa and a slow-starting Kimi Räikkönen." .

ns1:56431 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1968 Southern Conference Men's Basketball Tournament took place from 29 February 1968 – 02 March 1968, at the original Charlotte Coliseum in Charlotte. The Davidson Wildcats men's basketball, led by head coach Lefty Driesell, won their second Southern Conference title and received the automatic berth to the 1968 NCAA Tournament." .

ns1:56453 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Masters Tournament was the 76th Masters Tournament, held 05 April 2012–8 at Augusta National Golf Club in Augusta, Georgia. Winners of PGA Tour events that award a full-point allocation for the season-ending Tour Championship, between the 2011 Masters Tournament and the 2012 Masters Tournament Harrison Frazar, Sean O'Hair, Scott Stallings, Kyle Stanley (19), Brendan Steele, Johnson Wagner." .

ns1:56641 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Pacific Coast Championships was an annual men's tennis tournament. It was the second-oldest ongoing tennis tournament in the United States and ran from 1889 until 2013. Its final edition, known by its sponsored name SAP Open, was an ATP World Tour 250 series event on the Association of Tennis Professionals tour and played indoors on a hard surface at the SAP Center. The tournament began in 1889 as the Pacific Coast Championships at the Old Del Monte Lodge in Monterey and was won by William H. Taylor. More recently, it was the Sybase Open from 1994 through 2001 and the Siebel Open from 2002 through 2004. The tournament was known as the SAP Open from 2005 until the last edition in 2013. The highest ranked player for the 2013 SAP Open was Milos Raonic, ranked 13th." .

ns1:56719 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006 Coca-Cola 600 was the 12th stock car race of the 2006 NASCAR Nextel Cup Series as well as the 47th running of the Coca-Cola 600. It was held on 28 May 2006, in Concord, North Carolina, at Charlotte Motor Speedway, before a crowd of 175,000 spectators. The Coca-Cola 600 was the 12th of 36 scheduled stock car races of the 2006 NASCAR Nextel Cup Series, and the Coca-Cola 600's 47th iteration. It was held on 28 May 2006 in Concord, North Carolina, at Charlotte Motor Speedway (now Charlotte Motor Speedway), an intermediate track that holds NASCAR races. Kasey Kahne on ending Jimmie Johnson's streak of four consecutive victories at Charlotte Motor Speedway." .

ns1:56756 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018–19 Taça da Liga was the twelfth edition of the Taça da Liga (also known as Allianz Cup for sponsorship reasons), a association football cup competition organised by the Portuguese League for Professional Football (LPFP) and contested exclusively by clubs competing in the top two professional tiers of Portuguese association football. It began on 21 July 2018 and concluded with 2019 Taça da Liga Final in Braga on 26 January 2019. The first semi-2019 Taça da Liga Final was played between the winners of Groups A (Benfica) and C (Porto), while the second was played between Group B (Braga) and D (Sporting CP) winners. All matches were played at Estádio Municipal de Braga, in Braga, with the semi-finals played on 22 January and 23 January, and 2019 Taça da Liga Final on 26 January 2019." .

ns1:57099 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Clash Royale is a freemium real-time strategy video game developed and published by Supercell. The game was soft-launched in Canada, Hong Kong, Australia, Sweden, Norway, Denmark, Iceland, Finland, and New Zealand for iOS platforms on January 4, 2016. Upon its release, Clash Royale became the most downloaded and top-grossing app on the U.S. App Store. Clash Royale has received a very positive response from its players, with an average score of 4.5 out of 5 on the Google Play Store and an average score of 4 out of 5 on the App Store. The Clash Royale League is the official team esports world championship on a league format, developed by Supercell. The Clash Royale Crown Championship was the official esports world championship of the game, developed by Supercell." .

ns1:57104 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Great Storm of 1975 (also known as the Super Bowl Blizzard, Minnesota's Storm of the Century, or the Tornado Outbreak of January, 1975) was an intense storm system that impacted a large portion of the Central and Southeast United States from 09 January 1975 to 12 January 1975. The first two tornadoes in the tornado outbreak touched down after 10:00 p.m. CST on 09 January 1975 in Oklahoma and Louisiana. Texas saw five tornadoes between 1:30 a.m.–3:30 a.m., one tornado touched down in Arkansas at 6:00 a.m., Louisiana saw seven tornadoes between 5:30 a.m.–8:00 a.m. (killing one person), Mississippi had five tornadoes between 8:15 a.m.–10:00 a.m. (killing nine), and Illinois and Indiana each experienced three lunch-hour tornadoes. The tornadic line of storms then shifted into Alabama (killing one) and Florida during the afternoon and evening hours. Mississippi and Alabama were the two states hardest hit by this tornado outbreak. Alabama saw the most twisters of any state with 13, but Mississippi saw the largest and deadliest tornado. After a calm day on January 11, four more tornadoes touched down in Florida and Georgia on 12 January 1975, killing one person in Florida. Snowfall of a foot (30.5 cm) or more was common from Nebraska to Minnesota, with a high amount of 27 inches (69 cm) in Riverton, Minnesota. In Omaha, Nebraska a foot (31 cm) of snow fell, Sioux Falls saw 7 inches (18 cm), Duluth, Minnesota saw 8 inches (20 cm), and International Falls, Minnesota saw 24 inches (61 cm). Record low pressures were recorded in communities in Nebraska, Minnesota, Illinois, and Wisconsin, with a low of 28.55 in (966.8 mb) in Duluth, Minnesota." .

ns1:57117 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 BRIT Awards were held on 2014. The awards ceremony was held at The O2 Arena in London and was presented by James Corden for the fourth consecutive year. Taking the form of Britannia (the national personification of Britain), the trophy has been redesigned by various artists since the BRIT Awards revamp in 2011. The Brit Awards 2014 is a compilation album and box set which includes the \"62 biggest tracks from the past year\"." .

ns1:57528 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Freddie Mercury Tribute Concert was a benefit concert held on Easter Monday, 1992 at Wembley Stadium in London, United Kingdom for an audience of 72,000. The show marked bassist John Deacon's final full-length concert with Queen (save a short live appearance with Brian May, Roger Taylor and Elton John in 1997). Following Freddie Mercury's death on 24 November 1991 from bronchopneumonia brought on by AIDS, the remaining members of Queen (John Deacon, Brian May and Roger Taylor) came together with their manager Jim Beach to organise a concert to celebrate the life and legacy of Mercury, and to raise money for AIDS research and spread awareness about the disease. —Queen guitarist Brian May. The music then commenced with short sets from artists that were influenced by the music of Queen, including Metallica, Extreme (playing a Queen medley), Def Leppard (who brought Brian May onstage for a version of \"Now I'm Here\"), and Guns N' Roses. The second half of the concert featured the three remaining Queen members – John Deacon (on bass), Brian May (on guitar) and Roger Taylor (on drums) – along with guest singers and guitarists, including Elton John, Roger Daltrey (of The Who), Tony Iommi (of Black Sabbath), David Bowie, Mick Ronson (of Spiders from Mars), James Hetfield (of Metallica), George Michael, Seal, Paul Young, Annie Lennox, Lisa Stansfield, Robert Plant (of Led Zeppelin), Joe Elliott and Phil Collen (of Def Leppard), Axl Rose and Slash (of Guns N' Roses), Liza Minnelli, and others. Clips of rehearsals and of Metallica's set (as well as James Hetfield's performance of \"Stone Cold Crazy\" with Queen and Tony Iommi) were featured in the 1992 documentary A Year and a Half in the Life of Metallica." .

ns1:57612 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Collectively referred to as the Passion, Jesus' suffering and redemptive death by crucifixion are the central aspects of Christian theology concerning the doctrines of salvation and atonement. Chronological comparison between the Jesus Passion narratives according to the Gospels of Mark and John. Aside from these women, the three Synoptic Gospels speak of the presence of others: \"the chief priests, with the scribes and elders\"; two robbers crucified, one on Jesus' right and one on his left, whom the Gospel of Luke presents as the Penitent Thief and the Impenitent thief; \"the soldiers\", \"the centurion and those who were with him, keeping watch over Jesus\"; passers-by; \"bystanders\", \"the crowds that had assembled for this spectacle\"; and \"his acquaintances\". In his book The Crucifixion of Jesus, physician and forensic pathologist Frederick Zugibe studied the likely circumstances of the death of Jesus in great detail. In The Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints teaches that the Crucifixion of Jesus was part of the Atonement. In the Roman Catholic tradition this view of atonement is balanced by the duty of Roman Catholics to perform Acts of Reparation to Jesus Christ which in the encyclical Miserentissimus Redemptor of Pope Pius XI were defined as \"some sort of compensation to be rendered for the injury\" with respect to the sufferings of Jesus. The Crucifixion, seen from the Cross by Tissot presented a novel approach at the end of the 19th century, in which the crucifixion scene was portrayed from the perspective of Jesus. The Stations of the Cross follows a number of stages based on the stages involved in the Crucifixion of Jesus, while the Rosary of the Holy Wounds is used to meditate on the wounds of Jesus as part of the crucifixion." .

ns1:57707 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2017 CONCACAF Gold Cup Group A consisted of Honduras national football team, Costa Rica national football team, French Guiana national football team, and Canada men's national soccer team. 2017 CONCACAF Gold Cup matches began on July 7 and ended on 2017." .

ns1:57830 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Bolton Metropolitan Borough Council election took place on 03 May 2018 to elect members of Bolton Council in Greater Manchester, England. 21 seats were contested and the Conservative Party won 9 seats, the Labour Party won 8 seats, the Liberal Democrats won 2 seats, and Farnworth and Kearsley First won 2 seats." .

ns1:57919 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Camping World 500 was a Monster Energy NASCAR Cup Series Instacart 500 held on 19 March 2017, at Phoenix Raceway in Avondale, Arizona. Contested over 314 laps, extended from 312 laps due to overtime, on the one-mile (1.6 km) oval, it was the fourth Instacart 500 of the 2017 Monster Energy NASCAR Cup Series season. Phoenix Raceway, also known as PIR, is a one-mile, low-banked tri-oval Instacart 500 track located in Avondale, Arizona. Joey Logano scored the pole for the Instacart 500 with a time of 26.216 and a speed of 137.321 mph (220.997 km/h). Ryan Newman chose not to pit and assumed the Instacart 500 lead along with Ricky Stenhouse Jr., and Martin Truex Jr.. Kyle Larson exited pit road first among the cars that opted to pit. Fox Sports covered their 13th Instacart 500 at the Phoenix Raceway." .

ns1:58059 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "In 1922, Alfred Hitchcock obtained his first shot at directing for Gainsborough Pictures with the film Number 13 (or Mrs. Peabody). Clare Greet and Ernest Thesiger were to star as husband and wife. As with Hitchcock's later lost film The Mountain Eagle, footage from Number 13 has become widely sought after by film historians and collectors without success. Clare Greet was obliged to finance the production with her own money; before her, Alfred Hitchcock's uncle John Hitchcock had also provided funds." .

ns1:58088 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1994 Brickyard 400 was held on Saturday, 06 August 1994, at the Indianapolis Motor Speedway. In December 1991, the Indianapolis Motor Speedway board of directors voted to pursue a second race at the Speedway, preferably a NASCAR Winston Cup event. On April 14, 1993, Speedway President Tony George and president of NASCAR Bill France, Jr. jointly announced the inaugural Brickyard 400 would be held Saturday, 06 August 1994. Some of the participants compared the Indianapolis Motor Speedway to Ontario Motor Speedway, which was built to closely mimic Indy's layout. Pole qualifying for the Inaugural Brickyard 400 was held on Thursday, August 4, 1994 NASCAR Winston Cup Series. However, he was fatally injured in a crash at Daytona in February 1994 NASCAR Winston Cup Series, five months before the Brickyard 400 was held. ABC Sports announcers Bob Jenkins and Benny Parsons described the finish thus: Jenkins: Years from today when 79 (the number of Indianapolis 500s run prior to this event) stock car races have been run here, we'll remember the name: Jeff Gordon, winner of the inaugural Brickyard 400!Parsons: Man, oh man, oh MAN!Jenkins: Jeff is screaming on his radio back to the pit crew, \"Oh my God, I did it! The Speedway public address announcing team from the Indy 500 was retained for the Brickyard 400." .

ns1:58134 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Monmouth took place on 25 November 1233, the feast day of St Catherine, between forces loyal to Henry III of England, King of England, and those of Richard Marshal, 3rd Earl of Pembroke, Earl of Pembroke and Lord Marshal of England, who had formed an alliance with the Wales prince Llywelyn ap Iorwerth and his supporter Owain ap Gruffudd, a grandson of Rhys of Deheubarth. The local lord, John of Monmouth, was absent from the battle. After the battle, which Baldwin survived, John of Monmouth returned to take control of the town." .

ns1:58135 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 UCI Road World Championships – Women's road race of the 2012 UCI Road World Championships cycling event took place on 22 September 2012 in the province of Limburg, Netherlands. With a finishing time of three hours 14 minutes and 29 seconds, Vos beat Rachel Neylan of Australia by 10 seconds, with Italy's Elisa Longo Borghini third." .

ns1:58347 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 47th Edition 1992 Vuelta a España (Tour of Spain), a long-distance bicycle race stage race and one of the three grand tours, was held from 27 April 1992 to 17 May 1992. He was joined on the final podium by Jesús Montoya and Pedro Delgado." .

ns1:58393 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Đurđevdan (Serbian: Ђурђевдан / Đurđevdan, pronounced ; Bulgarian: Гергьовден, romanized: Gergovden; Macedonian: Ѓурѓовден, romanized: Ǵurǵovden; Russian: Егорий Вешний, romanized: Yegoriy Veshniy or Юрьев день весенний, romanized: Yuryev den vesenniy, literally: \"George's Day in Spring\") is a Slavic religious holiday, the feast of Saint George celebrated on 23 April by the Julian calendar (May 6 by the Gregorian calendar). Đurđevdan, known as Đurđevdan (Ђурђевдан) in Serbian, is a feast day celebrated on May 6 (O.S. About a third of the population in Serbia have Saint George as a patron saint, meaning that Saint George's Day or Đurđevdan is celebrated as a krsna slava, through a family feast with ritual glorification. In Bulgaria, May 6 is celebrated as Saint George's Day as well as the Day of the Bulgarian Army with a military parade. Yuri's Day of Spring (Russian: Юрьев день весенний, romanized: Yuryev den vesenniy or Егорий Вешний, romanized: Yegoriy Veshniy) is the Russian name for either of the two Saint George's Day celebrated by the Russian Orthodox Church. Along with various other Christian churches, the Russian Orthodox Church celebrates the feast of Saint George on April 23 (Julian calendar), which falls on May 6 of the Western (Gregorian calendar." .

ns1:58408 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Salla was fought between Finland and Soviet Union troops near Salla in northern Finland during the Winter War. The Finland troops managed to stop the Soviet Union advance just east of Kemijärvi. During the last days of 28 February 1940 the Finland troops were replaced with the Swedish, Norwegian and Danish volunteers of the Stridsgruppen SFK. By 1938 the Soviet Union had decided to conquer Finland. The Soviet Union 9th Army was tasked with invading Finland between Kuhmo and Salla and cutting the country in half by advancing to the shores of the Gulf of Bothnia. As part of the 9th Army's offensive, the 122nd Rifle Division, having arrived from Poland on 8 30 November 1939, was supposed to capture Salla and Kemijärvi and advance to Rovaniemi within two weeks, from where it would continue to Tornio near the Finland border with Sweden. The improvements in Soviet Union infrastructure and demographics near the border that made it possible to supply 40,000 troops in the region had little effect on Finland operational planning in northern Finland. The forces in northern Finland were under the command of the staff of the Lapland Group, which in turn was led by the North Finland Group. The Finns had one detached battalion (Er.P 17) and one company (Er.K Kojonen) near Salla that were supposed to conduct an active defense by crossing the border, stopping the advance of the Soviet Union regiment that was expected in the area and harassing the Soviet Union lines of communications, thereby tying down Soviet Union forces. A Finland intelligence estimate on 15 October placed one Soviet Union division in the Murmansk-Kandalaksha area. Only one road existed from the Soviet Union border to Salla. From 11 December 1939 onward the Finland troops were part of the Lapland Group (Lapin Ryhmä) which was commanded by Major General Kurt Martti Wallenius. Soviet Union troops pushed the Finns up to the Kemijoki river, but were unable to break through the Finland defences on the river." .

ns1:58510 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Falcon Heavy test flight (also known as Falcon Heavy Demonstration Mission) was the first attempt by SpaceX to launch a Falcon Heavy rocket on 06 February 2018 at 20:45 UTC. In April 2011, SpaceX was planning for a first launch of Falcon Heavy from Vandenberg Air Force Base on the West Coast in 2013. Due partly to the failure of SpaceX CRS-7 in June 2015, SpaceX rescheduled the maiden Falcon Heavy flight in September 2015 to occur no earlier than April 2016, but by February 2016 had postponed it again to late 2016. At a July 2017 meeting of the International Space Station Research and Development meeting in Washington, D.C., SpaceX CEO Elon Musk downplayed expectations for the success of the maiden flight: There's a real good chance the vehicle won't make it to orbit ... In December 2017, Musk tweeted that the dummy payload on the maiden Falcon Heavy launch would be Elon Musk's Tesla Roadster playing David Bowie's \"Life on Mars\", and that it would be launched into an orbit around the Sun that will take it as far out as Mars' orbit. The Falcon Heavy maiden flight was intended to accomplish several objectives: The purpose of including the Roadster on the maiden flight was to demonstrate that the Falcon Heavy can launch payloads as far as the orbit of Mars, and it exceeded its projected route by extending its aphelion to near the asteroid belt beyond Mars (with a perihelion at the level of Earth's orbit), but did not test or demonstrate the separation of the second stage and a payload." .

ns1:58552 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1993 First Union 400 was a NASCAR Winston Cup Series racing event that took place on 18 April 1993, at North Wilkesboro Speedway in North Wilkesboro. Rusty Wallace managed to defeat Kyle Petty by a margin of 1.66 seconds with four cautions handed out for 38 laps; Wallace's pit crew had undergone a stringent workout routine and had emphasized more on physical fitness between the 1992 and 1993 NASCAR Winston Cup Series seasons." .

ns1:58724 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Amgen Tour of California (also known as the Amgen Breakaway From Heart Disease Women's Race Empowered by SRAM for sponsorship reasons) was the second edition of the Tour of California - women cycling stage race. It is scheduled to run from 19 to 22 May 2016, and is part of the 2016 UCI Women's World Tour. It will begin in South Lake Tahoe and finish in Sacramento." .

ns1:58769 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of the River Bug, sometimes known as the Battle of Volhynia, was a battle that took place on 22 July 1018–23 July 1018, in Red Ruthenia, near the Western Bug and near Volhynia (Wołyń), between the forces of Bolesław I the Brave of Poland and Yaroslav the Wise of Kievan Rus, during the Boleslaw I's intervention in the Kievan succession crisis. The main battle occurred on 23 July 1018." .

ns1:58919 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Rogatica was a joint attack of Partisan and Chetnik rebel forces on Rogatica, then held by the Independent State of Croatia (modern-day in Bosnia and Herzegovina). The siege continued between 13 October 1941 and 24 October 1941. At the beginning of World War II in Yugoslavia, Rogatica and all of eastern Bosnia and Herzegovina along with Sandžak in Serbia became part of the Independent State of Croatia. The military units of the Independent State of Croatia in eastern Bosnia and Herzegovina consisted of regular units of the Croatian Home Guard and members of the local Muslim population who were recruited into units of Ustaše militia that distinguished itself in the persecution of Serbs from the beginning of the war. The main aim of the Chetnik-Partisan offensive on Rogatica, Knežina and Žepa in 24 October 1941 was to connect rebel-controlled territories in eastern Bosnia and Herzegovina and western Serbia. In mid-24 October 1941, Rogatica was completely surrounded by Partisan and Chetnik forces, forcing its garrison to be supplied by aeroplanes that quickly became an easy target for the besieging forces. The Partisan and Chetnik forces captured Rogatica on 24 October 1941. During the siege of Rogatica Chetnik forces killed many Muslims. The loss of Rogatica had important consequences for Ustaša forces in the remaining territory of eastern Bosnia and Herzegovina. Muslim refugees from Rogatica fled to other eastern Bosnia and Herzegovina towns." .

ns1:59099 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The year Pride 30 is the 9th year in the history of the Pride Fighting Championships, a mixed martial arts promotion based in Japan. Pride 30 had 10 events beginning with, Pride 29 - Fists of Fire. The following fighters fought their first Pride FC fight in Pride 30: Pride 29: Fists of Fire was an event held on 20 February 2005 at Osaka-jo Hall in Osaka, Japan. Pride FC: Bushido 6 was an event held on April 3, Pride 30 at Osaka-jo Hall in Osaka, Japan. Pride FC: Total Elimination Pride 30 was an event held on April 23, Pride 30 at Osaka-jo Hall in Osaka, Japan. Pride FC: Bushido 7 was an event held on May 22, Pride 30 at Osaka-jo Hall in Osaka, Japan. Pride FC: Critical Countdown Pride 30 was an event held on June 26, Pride 30 at Osaka-jo Hall in Osaka, Japan. Pride FC: Bushido 8 was an event held on July 17, Pride 30 at Osaka-jo Hall in Osaka, Japan. Pride FC: Final Conflict Pride 30 was an event held on August 28, Pride 30 at Osaka-jo Hall in Osaka, Japan. Pride FC: Bushido 9 was an event held on September 25, Pride 30 at Ariake Coliseum in Tokyo, Japan. Pride 30: Fully Loaded was an event held on October 23, Pride 30 at Osaka-jo Hall in Osaka, Japan. Pride FC: Shockwave Pride 30 was an event held on 31 December 2005 at Saitama Super Arena in Saitama, Japan." .

ns1:59199 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Japan Women's Open was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts. It was the seventh edition of the Japan Women's Open, and part of the WTA International tournaments of the 2015 WTA Tour. It was held at the Ariake Coliseum in Tokyo, Japan, from 14 September 2015 through 20 September 2015." .

ns1:59212 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Adys (or Adis) was a battle in late 255 BC of the First Punic War between a Carthaginian army jointly commanded by Bostar, Hamilcar and Hasdrubal and a Roman Republic army led by Marcus Atilius Regulus. Earlier in the year, the new Roman Republic navy established naval superiority and used this advantage to invade the Carthaginian homeland, which roughly aligned with modern Tunisia in North Africa. The Romans advanced to and captured Tunis, only 16 kilometres (10 mi) from Carthage. The First Punic War between the states of Carthage and Rome began in 264 BC. Naval victories at Mylae and Sulci, and their frustration at the continuing stalemate in Sicily, led the Romans to focus on a sea-based strategy and to develop a plan to invade the Carthaginian heartland in North Africa and threaten their capital, Carthage (close to what is now Tunis). As a result of the battle, the Roman Republic army, commanded by Regulus, landed in Africa near Aspis (modern Kelibia) on the Cape Bon Peninsula in summer 256 BC and began ravaging the Carthaginian countryside. He advanced on the city of Adys (modern Uthina), only 60 kilometres (40 mi) south-east of Carthage, and besieged it. In this he differed from other generals, including Roman Republic ones, leading armies against Carthage in Africa. The Romans followed up and captured numerous towns, including Tunis, only 16 km (10 mi) from Carthage. From Tunis the Romans raided and devastated the immediate area around Carthage. Regulus, within sight of what he took to be a thoroughly defeated Carthage, demanded harsh terms: Carthage was to hand over Sicily, Sardinia and Corsica; pay all of Rome's war expenses; pay tribute to Rome each year; be prohibited from declaring war or making peace without Roman Republic permission; have its navy limited to a single warship; but provide 50 large warships to the Romans on their request. The war continued for a further 14 years, mostly on Sicily or the nearby waters, before ending with a Roman Republic victory; the terms offered to Carthage were more generous than those proposed by Regulus. The question of which state was to control the western Mediterranean remained open, and when Carthage besieged the Roman Republic-protected town of Saguntum in eastern Iberia in 218 BC, it ignited the Second Punic War with Rome." .

ns1:59307 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Tour of the Basque Country was the 55th edition of the Itzulia Basque Country stage race. It took place from 6 to 11 April 2015 and was the ninth race of the 2015 UCI World Tour. As the Itzulia Basque Country is a UCI World Tour event, all 17 2015 UCI World Tour were invited automatically and were obliged to send a squad. 7 April – Bilbao–Vitoria-Gasteiz (175.4 km (109.0 mi) 8 April – Vitoria-Gasteiz–Zumarraga 170.7 km (106.1 mi) 9 April – Zumarraga–Eibar (Arrate) 162.2 km (100.8 mi) 10 April – Eibar–Aia 155.5 km (96.6 mi) 11 April 2015 – Aia 18.3 km (11.4 mi) In the Itzulia Basque Country, four different jerseys were awarded." .

ns1:59391 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Foxwoods Resort Casino 301 is a Monster Energy NASCAR Cup Series Foxwoods Resort Casino 301 held on 22 July 2018 at New Hampshire Motor Speedway in Loudon, New Hampshire. Contested over 301 laps on the 1.058-mile (1.703 km) speedway, it was the 20th Foxwoods Resort Casino 301 of the 2018 Monster Energy NASCAR Cup Series season. New Hampshire Motor Speedway is a 1.058-mile (1.703 km) oval speedway located in Loudon, New Hampshire, which has hosted NASCAR racing annually since the early 1990s, as well as the longest-running motorcycle Foxwoods Resort Casino 301 in North America, the Loudon Classic. Kurt Busch scored the pole for the Foxwoods Resort Casino 301 with a time of 28.511 and a speed of 133.591 mph (214.994 km/h)." .

ns1:59419 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 CONCACAF Gold Cup was the 11th edition of the CONCACAF Gold Cup competition and 21st CONCACAF regional championship overall in CONCACAF's fifty years of existence. The competition started on 05 June 2011 at the AT&T Stadium in Arlington, and ended with the final on 25 June 2011 at the Rose Bowl in Pasadena, California, with Mexico beating the United States 4–2. On 2011, the names of five Mexico national football team players were released announcing Christian Bermúdez, Édgar Dueñas, Guillermo Ochoa, Francisco Javier Rodríguez and Sinha, all tested positive for clenbuterol prior to the start of the 2011 Gold Cup. On September 20, 2013, the Salvadoran Football Federation banned 14 Salvadoran players banned for life due to their involvement with match fixing while playing with the El Salvador national team over the previous two years, including 8 players (Dennis Alas, Luis Anaya, captain Marvin González, Reynaldo Hernández, Miguel Montes, Dagoberto Portillo, Osael Romero, Ramón Sánchez and Miguel Montes), from El Salvador's 5-0 loss to Mexico on 05 June 2011 at the 2011 CONCACAF Gold Cup." .

ns1:59498 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2001 Thalgo Australian Women's Hardcourts was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts at the Hope Island Resort Tennis Centre in Hope Island, Queensland, Australia and was part of the WTA Tier III tournaments category of the 2001 WTA Tour. It was the fifth edition of the tournament and was held from 31 December 2000 through 06 January 2001." .

ns1:59658 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 AFC Asian Cup Final was a association football match that took place on 29 January 2011 at the Khalifa International Stadium in Doha, Qatar, to determine the winner of the 2011 AFC Asian Cup. The match was won by Japan national football team, defeating Australia national association football team 1–0 after extra time through a goal scored by Tadanari Lee. A television viewing audience of 484 million in 80 countries across the Asia-Pacific region, Europe, North America and North Africa witnessed Japan national football team defeat Australia national association football team 1–0 in the final. The final was played between Japan national football team and Australia national association football team. Japan national football team, coached by Italian Alberto Zaccheroni, qualified for the final after defeating tournament host Qatar in the quarter-final and South Korea after a penalty shoot-out in the semi-final. Australia national association football team, also led by a foreign coach (German Holger Osieck), opened the tournament with a 4–0 win over India on their way to winning their group, before defeating Iraq in the quarter-final, and thrashing Uzbekistan 6–0 in the semi-final. Japan national football team held its lead until the end of extra time to win the final. Both managers praised their players after the match; Osieck expressed disappointment that Australia national association football team could not convert numerous opportunities to score, while Zaccheroni called Japan national football team's win a \"great victory\" and hailed Lee's impact as a substitute." .

ns1:59731 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 Paris–Roubaix was the 107th running of the Paris–Roubaix single-day cycling race, often known as the Hell of the North. It was held on 12 April 2009 over a distance of 259 kilometres (160.9 miles). The race was won by Tom Boonen, who won his third Paris–Roubaix (2005, 2008 Paris–Roubaix, 2009). Six riders then eventually broke away from the rest of the peloton: Tom Boonen, Juan Antonio Flecha, Leif Hoste, Thor Hushovd, Filippo Pozzato and Johan Van Summeren. Favorites included 2008 Paris–Roubaix winner Tom Boonen, teammate and Tour of Flanders winner Stijn Devolder, Garmin-Slipstream's Martijn Maaskant, and 2006 winner, Fabian Cancellara." .

ns1:59776 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 presidential election in Palau was held on 06 November 2012, in conjunction with the 2012 Palauan presidential election. Former President (2001-2009) Tommy Remengesau Jr. defeated his successor, Incumbent President of Palau Johnson Toribiong, who was first elected in 2008 Palauan presidential election. The presidential election was held on 06 November 2012, coinciding with the nationwide 2012 Palauan presidential election." .

ns1:59867 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "A Philippic (/fɪˈlɪpɪk/) is a fiery, damning speech, or tirade, delivered to condemn a particular political actor. The original \"philippics\" were delivered by Demosthenes, an Athenian statesman and orator in Classical Greece who delivered several attacks on Philip II of Macedon in the 4th century BC. A First Philippic, Second Philippic, and Third Philippic have been ascribed to Demosthenes." .

ns1:59909 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Crécy took place on 26 August 1346 in northern France between a French army commanded by King Philip VI and an English army led by King Edward III. The French attacked the English while they were traversing northern France during the Hundred Years' War resulting in an English victory and heavy loss of life among the French. Following a series of disagreements between Philip VI of France (r. 1328–1350) and Edward III of England (r. 1327–1377), on 24 May 1337 Philip's Great Council in Paris agreed that the lands held by Edward in France should be taken back into Philip's hands on the grounds that Edward was in breach of his obligations as a vassal. As there were few archers in France, they were usually recruited from abroad, typically Republic of Genova; their foreign origin led to them frequently being labelled mercenaries. Around noon on 26 August 1346 French scouts, advancing north from Abbeville, came in sight of the English. Once it halted, men, especially infantry, were continually joining Philip's battle as they marched north west from Abbeville. Edward ended the campaign by laying siege to Calais, which fell after eleven months, the Battle of Crécy having crippled the French army's ability to relieve the town." .

ns1:60161 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013–14 Gibraltar Premier Division (known as the Argus Insurance Premier Division for sponsorship reasons) is the 115th season of the national amateur association football league in Gibraltar since its establishment - the highest level of association football in Gibraltar. The season began on 07 October 2013 with Glacis United defeating Lions Gibraltar 3-2. The 2013-14 season of the Gibraltar Premier Division, the first since the GFA's acceptance into UEFA, began on 07 October 2013 with Glacis United defeating Lions Gibraltar 3-2. The mid season break saw the first edition of the Gibraltar Premier Cup in which Lincoln Red Imps F.C. came out victorious after defeating Manchester 62 in the final. Because Victoria Stadium is the only current stadium in Gibraltar, evening games the rest of the weekend, including the game between St Joseph's and Glacis United, were postponed. Lincoln Red Imps F.C. secured their title win with a 4-1 victory over Lions Gibraltar on April 14. Teams in the 2013-14 season will play each other twice, with the season starting on 07 October 2013 and ending on 07 May 2014." .

ns1:60286 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The figure skating at the 2018 Winter Olympics – men's singles competition of the 2018 Winter Olympics was held on 16 February 2018 and 17 February 2018 at the Gyeongpo Ice Hall in Gangneung, South Korea. The short program was held on 16 February 2018 and the free skating was held on 17 February 2018. Fellow countryman Shoma Uno won the silver medal, and Spain's Javier Fernández won the bronze medal." .

ns1:60295 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Wheeler/Wallowa was a U.S. offensive operation during the Vietnam War, launched on 11 September 1967 as two separate operations and concluding in 11 November 1968. The operation involved intensive surveillance of the Hiệp Đức-Quế Sơn." .

ns1:60335 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1994 Andalusian regional election was held on Sunday, 12 June 1994, to elect the 4th Parliament of the autonomous community of Andalusia. The candidate for the PSOE, Manuel Chaves González, was invested as President of the Regional Government of Andalusia after winning the election. The Parliament of Andalusia was the devolved, unicameral legislature of the autonomous community of Andalusia, having legislative power in regional matters as defined by the Spanish Constitution of 1978 and the regional Statute of Autonomy, as well as the ability to vote confidence in or withdraw it from a President of the Junta of Andalusia. The term of the Parliament of Andalusia expired four years after the date of its 1990 Andalusian regional election. The 1990 Andalusian regional election was held on 23 June 1990, which meant that the legislature's term would have expired on 23 12 June 1994." .

ns1:60366 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017–18 Serbian SuperLiga was the twelfth season of the Serbian SuperLiga since its establishment. The season began on 21 July 2017 and ended on 19 May 2018. The league consisted of 16 teams: fourteen teams from the 2016–17 Serbian SuperLiga and two new teams from the 2016–17 Serbian First League." .

ns1:60411 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "50 West 66th Street is a skyscraper planned for the Upper West Side of Manhattan in New York City. It is being developed by Extell Development Company and designed by Snøhetta. When plans for an enlarged 775-foot (236 m), 69-story tower were released in late 2017, the tower faced immediate opposition by those living in the neighborhood." .

ns1:60580 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018–19 S.S. Lazio season was the 118th season in S.S. Lazio's history and their 31st consecutive season in the top-flight of Italian football. Lazio competed in 2018–19 Serie A, the 2018–19 Coppa Italia and the 2018–19 UEFA Europa League. The season was coach Simone Inzaghi's third in charge of the club, having led Lazio to consecutive fifth-placed finishes in the 2016–17 and 2017–18 S.S. Lazio season seasons." .

ns1:60600 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Nukapu Expedition was a British punitive expedition from 01 October 1871 until 01 February 1872, in response to the murder of missionary John Coleridge Patteson by natives of Nukapu, one of the easternmost islands of the Solomon Islands in the Pacific Ocean. In 01 October 1871, the screw sloop-of-war HMS Rosario was operating against blackbirders in the South Sea Islands when her captain, Commander Albert Hastings Markham, received orders to sail for Nukapu in the Solomon Islands." .

ns1:60607 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1992 Ilva Trophy was a women's tennis tournament played on clay court at the Circulo Tennis Ilva Taranto in Taranto, Italy that was part of the WTA Tier V tournaments category of the 1992 WTA Tour. It was the sixth edition of the tournament and was held from 28 April 1992 until 03 May 1992." .

ns1:60612 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1989 Mexican Grand Prix was a Formula One motor race held at the Autódromo Hermanos Rodríguez, Mexico City on 28 May 1989. The race, contested over 69 laps, was the fourth race of the 1989 Formula One season and was won from pole position by Ayrton Senna, driving a McLaren-Honda, with Riccardo Patrese second in a Williams-Renault and Michele Alboreto third in a Tyrrell-Ford." .

ns1:60629 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "S.S. America was an ocean liner and cruise ship built in the United States in 1940, for the United States Lines and designed by the noted American naval architect William Francis Gibbs. America was laid down under the first Maritime Commission contract on 22 August 1938, at Newport News, by the Newport News Shipbuilding. America was sold to the Greek-owned Chandris Line on 1964." .

ns1:60643 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Uruguayan Primera División season, also known as the Campeonato Uruguayo 2018, was the 115th season of Uruguay's top-flight football league, and the 88th in which it is professional. Club Atlético Peñarol were the defending champions, and successfully defended the title with a 2–1 win over Club Nacional de Football in the championship playoff. The two bottom-placed teams in the 2017 Uruguayan Primera División season of the 2017 Uruguayan Primera División season season, Juventud and Plaza Colonia, as well as Sud América, who lost a tiebreaker to El Tanque Sisley, were relegated to the Segunda División for the 2018 season. They were replaced by Club Atlético Torque, Atenas de San Carlos, and C.A. Progreso, who were promoted from the Segunda División. On 2018, the Uruguayan Football Association confirmed that El Tanque Sisley would not take part in the league due to an outstanding debt. Relegation was determined at the end of the season by computing an average of the number of points earned per game over the two most recent seasons: 2017 Uruguayan Primera División season and 2018." .

ns1:60763 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2014 Svenska Supercupen, Swedish Super Cup 2014, was the 8th Svenska Supercupen, an annual association football match contested by the winners of the previous season's Allsvenskan and Svenska Cupen competitions. The match was played at Malmö stadion, Malmö, on 2014, and was played by the 2014 Allsvenskan champions Malmö FF and the 2013–14 Svenska Cupen champions IF Elfsborg. It was Malmö FF's second successive appearance and third in total, it was also the first time that the competition was hosted at Malmö stadion and the first time the competition was hosted at an alternative home venue. Malmö FF won the match on penalties 5–4 after the match had ended 2–2 after extra time. IF Elfsborg took the lead through Viktor Claesson in the first half, Malmö FF equalized Elfsborg's lead in the 89th minute of ordinary time. IF Elfsborg qualified for Svenska Supercupen on 2014 when they won the 2014 Svenska Cupen Final against Helsingborgs IF 1–0. Malmö FF qualified for the competition of 2014 when they secured the 2014 Allsvenskan title in the 27th league round in the away fixture against AIK at Friends Arena with a 3–2 win. Malmö FF made their second successive appearance in the competition, being the current holders of the title after defeating IFK Göteborg in 2013 Svenska Supercupen. The match was Malmö FF's third appearance in total for Svenska Supercupen. Malmö FF chose to play the match at the clubs alternative home venue Malmö stadion. The two clubs met in league play during the season, Malmö FF defeated Elfsborg at Borås Arena 1–0 on 2014 and Elfsborg defeated Malmö FF at Swedbank Stadion 2–1 on 2014 in the 28th league round. The last time the two clubs faced each other in a domestic cup match was in the semi-finals for Svenska Cupen on 31 May 1984, a match which Malmö FF won 2–0. For Malmö FF two players from the starting line-up, goalkeeper Robin Olsen and defender Erik Johansson missed the match due to injuries sustained in Malmö FF's match in the UEFA Champions League against Atlético Madrid on 2014." .

ns1:60766 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Bob Dylan and The Band 1974 Tour – often referred to as Tour '74 – was a two-month concert tour in early 1974 that featured Bob Dylan, in his first full-fledged tour in eight years, performing with The Band. The 40-concert, 30-date, 21-city tour began on 03 January 1974 and ended on 14 February 1974. The show reunited Dylan with The Band on stage after the release of Dylan's Band-backed Planet Waves album." .

ns1:60992 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Mogadishu began on 21 March 2007 in the Shirkole area of Mogadishu between Somali Transitional Federal Government forces and allied Ethiopian troops, and Islamist insurgents. The battle usually includes the dates, when referenced, in order to distinguish it amongst the nine major Battles of Mogadishu during the decades-long Somali Civil War. On 23 21 March 2007, a TransAVIAexport Airlines Ilyushin Il-76 plane crashed in Mogadishu. However, on 12 April Somalia's Ambassador to Ethiopia Abdikarin Farah stated that Mogadishu was now peaceful for the first time in sixteen years. On 26 April 2007, Ethiopian troops took insurgent strongholds in Northern Mogadishu." .

ns1:61005 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Smolenice (Hungarian: Szomolányi csata, German: Schlacht bei Smolenitz, Slovak: Bitka pri Smoleniciach) was a battle between the Kuruc (a group of Hungarian peasants and irregular warriors), and the forces of the Habsburg Empire, soldiers of the Holy Roman Empire and auxiliaries from Denmark. The battle occurred on 28 May 1704 at Smolenice in Upper Hungary (present day Slovakia), where the Kuruc army routed the Habsburg forces, capturing the Austrian commander." .

ns1:61045 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Ifoghas, also known as the Battle of Tigharghâr or the Battle of the Ametettai, took place from 18 February 2013 to 31 March 2013, during the Northern Mali conflict. After being defeated in January in the Battle of Konna and the Battle of Diabaly, the jihadists abandoned Timbuktu and retreated into the Adrar Tigharghar, a mountain of the Adrar of Ifoghas in northeastern Mali, which has been their sanctuary for years. \"Emir of the Sahara\", Djamel Okacha, known as \"Yahia Abu el-Hammam\", the leader of the AQIM forces in Mali, according to a jihadist source of the Sahara Medias agency, he took part in the battle. In January 2014, in Kidal, AFP journalist Serge Daniel meets a young Touareg from Tessalit nicknamed \"Tic - Tac\", a battle survivor in Tigharghar. Chadians, on the other hand, remain deployed much longer in the Kidal region; in September 160 soldiers of the Tessalit garrison desert the Amachach military camp after having complained of not having been relieved after nine months of mission in the great north of Mali. The Battle of Ifoghas completes the reconquest phase of northern Mali." .

ns1:61188 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1981–82 Fußball-Bundesliga was the 19th season of the Bundesliga, West Germany's premier football league. It began on 08 August 1981 and ended on 29 May 1982. They were replaced by SV Werder Bremen, winners of the 1981–82 2. Fußball-Bundesliga Northern Division, SV Darmstadt 98, winners of the Southern Division and Eintracht Braunschweig, who won a two-legged promotion play-off against Kickers Offenbach." .

ns1:61257 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Kłecko was fought on 07 May 1656, between forces of the Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth commanded by Regimentarz Stefan Czarniecki and Jerzy Sebastian Lubomirski and a Sweden force commanded by prince Adolf Johan av Pfalz-Zweibrücken. Sweden army lost 428 dead, while the Polish army sustained 70 dead and wounded, 2,000 dead or 3,000 dead (depending on sources), including 40 companions dead (both hetman Czarniecki and Polish hussar companion Kochowski (who was fighting in this battle) quote this number of Polish losses). In the spring of 1656, Hetmans Stefan Czarniecki and Jerzy Sebastian Lubomirski were sent with their forces to Greater Poland, in order to support anti-Sweden insurrection, which had begun there. After bypassing Poznań, the Poles reached Gniezno (May 5), while on the next day, Adolph John with his army arrived at the village of Jablkowo near Kłecko, 15 kilometers northeast of Gniezno. Due to strong Sweden fire, the Poles did not attack their camp, and the battle ended after five hours. According to the Sweden description of the battle, the Polish forces were chased out and pursued. The Sweden losses in the battle were about 428 killed, and perhaps twice as many, or more, wounded. The battle once again confirmed the notion that Polish cavalry was unable to defeat well-positioned Sweden infantry." .

ns1:61263 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Chunuk Bair (Turkish: Conk Bayırı Muharebesi) was a World War I battle fought between the Ottoman defenders and troops of the British Empire over control of the peak in 19 August 1915. The British August Offensive at ANZAC Cove and Suvla was an attempt to try to break the stalemate that the Gallipoli Campaign had become." .

ns1:61347 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The invasion of Yugoslavia, also known as the April War or Operation 25, was a German-led attack on the Kingdom of Yugoslavia by the Axis powers which began on 06 April 1941 during World War II. The invasion commenced with an overwhelming air attack on Belgrade and facilities of the Royal Yugoslav Air Force (VVKJ) by the Luftwaffe (German Air Force) and attacks by German land forces from southwestern Kingdom of Bulgaria. The invasion ended when an armistice was signed on 17 18 April 1941, based on the unconditional surrender of the Yugoslav army, which came into effect at noon on 18 April 1941. Some areas of Yugoslavia were annexed by neighboring Axis countries, some areas remained occupied, and in other areas Axis puppet states such as the Independent State of Croatia (Serbo-Croatian Latin: Nezavisna Država Hrvatska, or NDH) were created during the invasion on 10 April. Along with Italy's stalled invasion of Greece on 28 October 1940, and the German-led invasion of Greece (Operation Marita) and invasion of Crete (Operation Merkur), the invasion of Yugoslavia was part of the German Balkan Campaign (German: Balkanfeldzug). Upon the proclamation of an Independent State of Croatia in Zagreb on 10 April, this scenario was realized and Hungary joined the invasion, its army crossing into Yugoslavia the following day. Germany attacked Yugoslavia from bases in three countries besides itself: Hungary, Romania, and Kingdom of Bulgaria. In early 18 April 1941 they moved a further 600 aircraft from France, Africa, and Sicily into Romania and Kingdom of Bulgaria in a period of ten days. Planning for the German invasion of Greece from Kingdom of Bulgaria began on 12 November. In its worst expression, Yugoslavia's defenses were badly compromised on 10 18 April 1941, when some of the units in the Croatian-manned 4th and 7th Armies mutinied, and a newly formed Croatian government hailed the entry of the Germans into Zagreb the same day. The first phase encompasses the Luftwaffe's devastating air assault on Belgrade and airfields of the Royal Yugoslav Air Force of 06 April 1941, and an initial thrust of the German XL Panzer Corps from Kingdom of Bulgaria towards Skopje that commenced the same day. At 07:00 on 06 April 1941 the Luftwaffe opened the assault on Yugoslavia by conducting a saturation-type bombing raid on the capital, \"Operation Retribution\" (Unternehmen Strafgericht). When Germany and Italy attacked Yugoslavia on 06 April 1941, The Yugoslav Royal Navy had available three destroyers, two submarines and 10 MTBs as the most effective units of the fleet. After the surrender, Yugoslavia was subsequently divided amongst Germany, Hungary, Italy and Kingdom of Bulgaria." .

ns1:61355 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 World Men's Curling Championship (branded as the 361 ̊ World Curling Championships 2018 for sponsorship reasons) was held from 31 March 2018 to 08 April 2018 at Orleans Arena, on the Las Vegas Strip in Paradise, Nevada, United States of America. Saturday, 31 March 2018, 13:30." .

ns1:61386 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Jilib was a battle in the 2006 Somali War fought by the Islamic Courts Union (ICU) and affiliated militias against Ethiopia and Transitional Federal Government (TFG) forces for control of the town of Jilib. On Saturday, 30 December, joint Ethiopia-TFG troops had reached the town of Jilib, the last major town on the road to Kismayo. On Sunday, 31 December 2006, fighting began in the thick mango forests near Helashid, 11 miles (18 km) to the northwest of Jilib. At approximately 5:00 p.m., a heavy gun battle erupted on the outskirts of Jilib town between Islamic fighters and the Ethiopia-backed interim government troops." .

ns1:61426 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Hyelion and Leimocheir saw the almost complete destruction by the Byzantine Empire of a large Seljuq Turk army. Following Emperor Manuel Komnenos's defeat at the Battle of Myriokephalon (1176) the Byzantine Empire failed to implement all the conditions, particularly the destruction of border fortresses, demanded by the Sultanate of Rum Kilij Arslan II as a prerequisite for a cessation of hostilities. A substantial Seljuq cavalry army, including Turcoman nomad auxiliaries, was dispatched into Byzantine territory, in the Büyük Menderes River in western Anatolia, on a retaliatory raid. The date of the battle is unknown, it has generally been ascribed to the year 1177 on the basis of its position within the narrative of Niketas Choniates." .

ns1:61474 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Urgun was a military engagement that took place during the Soviet–Afghan War. Between 01 August 1983 and 16 January 1984 Mujahideen forces laid siege to the town of Urgun, which was defended by a garrison of troops loyal to the Democratic Republic of Afghanistan (the DRA). In 01 August 1983, the mujahideen laid siege to the Nek Mohammed Kala fortress, but they were unable to approach it as it was protected by minefields and heavy machine-guns. In 16 January 1984, the mujahideen began the second phase of their plan, a direct assault on Urgun itself. On 16 January 1984, an Afghan army column broke through to Urgun, lifting the siege. The attack against Urgun was part of a general offensive by the Mujahideen in eastern Afghanistan. According to Olivier Roy, the fighting around Urgun still bore the hallmarks of the traditional tribal warfare of Afghanistan." .

ns1:61482 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1967 Giro d'Italia was the 50th running of the Giro d'Italia, one of cycling's Grand Tour races. The Giro started in Treviglio, on 20 May 1967, with a 135 km (83.9 mi) stage and concluded in Milan, on 11 June 1967, with a 68 km (42.3 mi) split leg. The second and third places were taken by Italian Franco Balmamion and Frenchman Jacques Anquetil, respectively. Thirteen teams were invited by the race organizers to participate in the 1967 edition of the Giro d'Italia. l'Unità writer Gino Sala named Felice Gimondi, Jacques Anquetil, Vittorio Adorni, Eddy Merckx, and Motta as the main contenders for the overall crown." .

ns1:61488 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Rewind Festival is an annual music festival that takes place in Temple Island Meadows, Remenham, Berkshire near Henley-on-Thames, England, Capesthorne Hall, Cheshire, England, and Scone Palace, Perthshire in Scotland. A number of additional attractions were included at the festival: The Scottish 1980s Rewind Festival took place at the Scone Palace ground30 July 2011near 31 July 2011 29, 30 July and 31 July 2011. The English 1980s Rewind Festival took place at Temple Island Meadows, Remenham Farm, Remen20 August 2011m, ne21 August 2011hames on20 August9, 2021 Augustugust 2011. The fourth 1980s Rewind Festival was held on two separate occasions to provide concerts for both Scotland and England. The English Rewind Festival took place at17 August 2013emple18 August 2013s, Remenham Farm, Remenham, 17 Augustar He18 Augusthames on 16, 17 and 18 August 2013. The Rewind Festival in Scotland took place at the Scone Palace grounds near Perth on 24, 25, and 26 July22 August 2015015. The Rewind Festival in Scotland took place at the Scone Palace grounds near Perth on 23, 23, and 24 July 2016, and was headlined by Holly Johnson on Saturday and Adam Ant on Sunday. The 2016 will be held at Capesthorne Hall in Cheshire from 5,20 August 2016, 7 A21 August 2016e Rewind Festival South took place at Temple Island Meadows,20 Augustemenh21 AugustRemenham, near Henley-on-Thames on 19, 20 and 21 August 2016. The Rewind Festival in Scotland took place at the Scone Palace grounds near Perth on 21, 22, and 23 July 2017, and was5 August 2017headl6 August 2017uman League on the Saturday and Billy Ocean on the Sunday. The Rewind Festival South took place at Temple I19 Augustand M20 Augustemenham Farm, Remenham, near Henley-on-Thames on 18, 19 and 20 August 2017. The Rewind Festival in Scotland took place at the Scone Palace grounds near Perth on 20, 21, and 22 July 2018, and was headlined by OMD and Status Quo. The Rewind South Festival took place at the Temple Island Meadows, Remenham Farm, Remenham, near Henley-on-Thames on 17, 18, and 19 August 2018, and was headlined by Kool and the Gang and OMD. The Rewin19 JulyF21 July in Scotland took place at the Scone Palace grounds near Perth on 19–21 July. The Rewind South Festival took 16 Augusta18 August Temple Island Meadows, Remenham Farm, Remenham, near Henley-on-Thames from 16–18 August." .

ns1:61552 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1982 Virginia National Bank 500 was a NASCAR Winston Cup Series racing event that was set on 25 April 1982, at Martinsville Speedway in Martinsville, Virginia. Terry Labonte and Ricky Rudd would dominate the first one hundred laps of this race while Harry Gant would monopolize the closing laps of this race with a 1-lap lead over everyone else." .

ns1:61655 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Welsh Open was a professional ranking snooker tournament, that took place from 26 February 2018 to 04 March 2018 at the Motorpoint Arena Cardiff in Cardiff, Wales. John Higgins won a Welsh Open and 30th ranking title overall, beating Barry Hawkins 9–7 in the final." .

ns1:61662 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "It took place on 07 May 1955 at Wembley Stadium and was contested between Newcastle United F.C. and Manchester City F.C.. Newcastle won the match 3–1, thus winning the FA Cup for the third time in five years and the sixth time in all. Jackie Milburn scored Newcastle's first goal after 45 seconds (a record for a Wembley final that would stand until 1997), before Bobby Johnstone equalised for City just before half-time. Bobby Mitchell restored Newcastle's lead in the 52nd minute, and George Hannah extended it seven minutes later." .

ns1:61675 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1958 Belgian Grand Prix was a Formula One motor race held on 15 June 1958 at Circuit de Spa-Francorchamps. It was race 5 of 11 in the 1958 World Championship of Drivers and race 4 of 10 in the 1958 Formula One season. The race was the 19th Belgian Grand Prix and it was held over 24 laps of the 14 kilometre circuit for a race distance of 339 kilometres. The Belgian Grand Prix returned to the calendar after being off the calendar the year previous, and the 8.7 mile Spa circuit had been slightly modified. Lap leaders: Tony Brooks 21 laps (1, 3, 6–24); Peter Collins 3 laps (2, 4–5)." .

ns1:61693 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1920 United States presidential election in Vermont took place on 02 November 1920, as part of the 1920 United States presidential election which was held throughout all contemporary 48 states. Vermont voted overwhelmingly for the Republican nominee, Ohio Senator Warren G. Harding, over the Democratic nominee, Ohio Governor James M. Cox. Vermont historically was a bastion of liberal Northeastern Republicanism, and by 1920 the Green Mountain State had gone Republican in every presidential election since the founding of the Republican Party. From 1856 to 1916 United States presidential election in Vermont, Vermont had had the longest streak of voting Republican of any state, having never voted Democratic before, and this tradition easily continued amidst the nationwide Republican landslide in 1920." .

ns1:61759 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Jicarilla War began in 1849 and was fought between the Jicarilla Apache and the United States Army in the New Mexico Territory. The war started when the Apache and Utes began raiding against settlers on the Santa Fe Trail. The war began in 1849 when a wagon of settlers was killed by a band of Jicarillas and Utes. In the subsequent Battle of Cieneguilla, sixty American cavalrymen fought an estimated 250 Apache and Ute people warriors under the war chief Flechas Rayada." .

ns1:61870 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Hell in a Cell was a professional wrestling pay-per-view (PPV) event and WWE Network event produced by WWE. It took place on 26 October 2014 at the American Airlines Center in Dallas, Texas. The card featured two Hell in a Cell matches, where John Cena defeated Randy Orton to secure a future WWE World Heavyweight Championship match, and, in the main event, Seth Rollins defeated Dean Ambrose. Hell in a Cell is an annual gimmick pay-per-view, generally produced every October by WWE since 2009. The concept of the show comes from WWE's established Hell in a Cell match, in which competitors fight inside a 20-foot-high roofed cell structure surrounding the ring and ringside area. Storylines between the characters played out on WWE's primary television programs, WWE Raw and WWE SmackDown. On the October 20 episode of WWE Raw, Triple H announced that the match between Cena and Orton would be a number one contender match for the WWE World Heavyweight Title. On October 20, it was announced on WWE.com that AJ would defend the title against Paige at Hell in a Cell. On the October 13 episode of WWE Raw, during a WWE.com exclusive, Nikki interrupted Brie's interview with the news that they will face off at the event, concluding that the loser of the match would become the winner's personal assistant for one month. On October 14, WWE.com announced that Big Show would face Rusev at Hell in a Cell. On October 20, it was announced on WWE.com that Gold and Stardust would defend the title against The Usos at Hell in a Cell. In the fourth match, John Cena faced Randy Orton for a future shot at the WWE World Heavyweight Championship in a Hell in a Cell match. The next night on WWE Raw, Big Show and Mark Henry faced Gold and Stardust for the WWE Tag Team Championships. Randy Orton, who was Curb Stomped by Seth Rollins on the WWE Raw prior to Hell in a Cell, managed to get his retribution the next week." .

ns1:61875 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Gent–Wevelgem was the 74th running of the Gent–Wevelgem single-day cycling race. It was held on 25 March 2012 over a distance of 234.6 kilometres (145.8 miles), and was the seventh race of the 2012 UCI World Tour season. Boonen finished ahead of Liquigas–Cannondale's Peter Sagan and Rabobank's Matti Breschel, who completed the podium." .

ns1:61884 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "In Christian liturgy, the Credo (Latin: ; Latin for \"I believe\") is the Nicene-Constantinopolitan Creed (or less often, the Apostles' Creed or the Athanasian Creed) in the Mass, either as spoken text, or sung as Gregorian chant or other musical settings of the Mass. What is identified as \"Credo I\" in the Liber Usualis was apparently widely considered the only authentic Credo, and it is the element of the ordinary that was most strongly associated with a single melody. In musical settings of the Credo, as in the Gloria, the first line is intoned by the celebrant alone (Credo in unum Deum), or by a soloist, while the choir or congregation joins in with the second line. In Mass settings of the Baroque, Classical and Romantic period the Credo line is usually set for whole choir, such as in the Symbolum Nicenum (Nicene creed) of Bach's Mass in B minor, where the composer uses plainchant as the theme for a fugue, in the later Masses of Haydn, and the Missa Solemnis of Beethoven. A few composers (notably Heinrich Isaac) have set Credos independently from the rest of the ordinary, presumably to allow their insertion into missae breves or their omission where a said or chanted Credo is the custom." .

ns1:61911 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1974 Coupe de France Final was a association football match held at Parc des Princes, Paris, on 08 June 1974 that saw AS Saint-Étienne defeat AS Monaco FC 2–1 thanks to goals by Christian Synaeghel and Alain Merchadier." .

ns1:61923 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 UCI Road World Championships – men's team time trial of the 2017 UCI Road World Championships was a cycling event that took place on 17 September 2017 in Bergen, Norway. It was 42.5 kilometres (26.4 miles)-long and featured two climbs: Loddefjord, a 600-metre (2,000 ft)-long climb at an average gradient of 10% and the Birkelundsbakken, a climb 3 kilometres (1.9 miles) in length, at an average of 6%." .

ns1:61998 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The UEFA Euro 1972 Final was a association football match played on 18 June 1972 to determine the winner of UEFA Euro 1972. The match was contested by Germany national association football team and the Soviet Union national association football team, fighting for its second title in the tournament, at the King Baudouin Stadium in Brussels. The Germans won the match 3–0, with goals coming from Gerd Müller (twice) and Herbert Wimmer." .

ns1:62072 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1999 Norwegian Football Cup Final was the final match of the 1999 Norwegian Football Cup, the 94th season of the Norwegian Football Cup, the premier Norwegian association football cup competition organized by the Football Association of Norway (NFF). The match was played on 30 October 1999 at the Ullevaal Stadion in Oslo, and opposed two Tippeligaen sides Rosenborg BK and SK Brann. Rosenborg BK defeated SK Brann 2–0 to claim the Norwegian Cup for an eighth time in their history." .

ns1:62165 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1947 VFL Grand Final was an Australian rules football game contested between the Carlton Football Club and Essendon Football Club, held at the Melbourne Cricket Ground on 27 September 1947. It was the 51st annual VFL/AFL Grand Final of the Victorian Football League, staged to determine the premiers for the 1947 VFL season." .

ns1:62174 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1995 WTA German Open was a women's tennis tournament played on clay court at the Rot-Weiss Tennis Club Berlin in Berlin in Germany that was part of WTA Tier I tournaments of the 1995 WTA Tour. The tournament was held from 15 May 1995 through 21 May 1995." .

ns1:62182 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Qatwan was fought in 16 September 1141 between the Qara Khitai (Western Liao Empire) and the Seljuk Empire and its vassal-state the Kara-Khanids. The battle took place on the Qatwan steppe, north of Samarkand, on 09 September 1141." .

ns1:62187 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Monterrey Open was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts. It was the 8th edition of the Abierto GNP Seguros and an WTA International tournaments on the 2017 WTA Tour. It took place at the Club Sonoma in Acuña, Mexico, from 3 to 09 April 2017, 2017." .

ns1:62235 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Mylo Xyloto Tour was the fifth concert tour by British band Coldplay. Launched in support of their fifth studio album Mylo Xyloto (2011), the tour began in the spring of 2011 with a series of one-off concerts in Europe and festivals including Rock in Rio in Brazil. It continued into 2012 with shows in North America, Europe and Oceania. Entitled, \"Coldplay Live: Unstaged\", the band performed songs from Mylo Xyloto alongside their previous hits. The band's concert at La Cigale was also live-streamed exclusively for views in Eastern Europe and Asia on Orange. Two associated EPs, iTunes Festival: London 2011 and Live in Madrid were released on 18 September 2011 and 31 26 October 2011, respectively." .

ns1:62261 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Porsche Tennis Grand Prix was a women's tennis tournament played on indoor clay courts. It was the 41st edition of the Porsche Tennis Grand Prix, and part of the Premier tournaments of the 2018 WTA Tour. It took place at the Porsche-Arena in Stuttgart, Germany, from 23 to 29 April 2018." .

ns1:62407 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 FIFA U-17 World Cup was the thirteenth tournament of the FIFA Under-17 World Cup held in Nigeria from 24 October 2009 to 15 November 2009. The Golden Ball to the Best Player was given to Nigerian Sani Emmanuel; the Golden Shoe for top scorer was given to Spaniard Borja, with five goals (although he tied with Nigerian Sani Emmanuel, Uruguayan Sebastián Gallegos, and Switzerland national under-17 football team Haris Seferović); the FIFA Under-17 World Cup was given to Switzerland national under-17 football team Benjamin Siegrist; finally, the FIFA Fair Play Award was given to Nigeria. On 2009, FIFA gave Nigeria a \"Yellow Card\" as FIFA noted a significant delay in the preparations for the tournament. The 2009 edition was under increasing threats from the armed rebel group, The Movement for the Emancipation of the Niger Delta (MEND) and warned FIFA against hosting the tournament in Nigeria." .

ns1:62410 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1989 Loma Prieta earthquake occurred on California's Central Coast on 17 October 1989 at 5:04 p.m. local time. With an Mw magnitude of 6.9 and a maximum Modified Mercalli intensity of IX (Violent), the shock was responsible for 63 deaths and 3,757 injuries. The Loma Prieta segment of the San Andreas Fault System had been relatively inactive since the 1906 San Francisco earthquake (to the degree that it was designated a seismic gap) until two moderate foreshocks occurred in June 1988 and again in August 1989. The history of earthquake investigations in California has been largely focused on the San Andreas Fault System, due to its strong influence in the state as the boundary between the Pacific Plate and the North American Plate; it is the most studied fault on Earth. Andrew Lawson, a geologist from the University of California, Berkeley, had named the fault after the San Andreas Lake (prior to the occurrence of the 1906 San Francisco earthquake) and later led an investigation into that event. Since many forecasts had been presented for the region near Loma Prieta, seismologists were not taken by surprise by the 17 October 1989 event. Following the August 8, 1989 shock, in anticipation of an upcoming large earthquake, staff at the University of California, Santa Cruz deployed four accelerometers in the area, which were positioned at the UCSC campus, two residences in Santa Cruz, and a home in Los Gatos. The Loma Prieta earthquake was named for Loma Prieta in the Santa Cruz Mountains, which lies just to the east of the mainshock epicenter. The initial media reports failed to take into account the game's effect on traffic and initially estimated the death toll at 300, a number that was corrected to 63 in the days after the earthquake. Private donations poured into aid relief efforts and on October 26, President George H. W. Bush signed a $1.1 billion ($2.3 billion today) earthquake relief package for California." .

ns1:62485 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Toverud was a skirmish at the farm Toverud in Aurskog on the night between 19 April 1808 and 20 April 1808, as a part of the Dano-Swedish War of 1808-1809. At dawn on 19 April 1808 he continued the quick march northwards towards Aurskog. Major Paul Weibye had from Prince Christian August's plan advanced in the direction of Blaker with five companies and 40 dragoons to find a suitable place to stop the enemy, and chose to occupy Toverud farm in the afternoon on 19 April 1808." .

ns1:62513 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006 Tashkent Open was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts. It was the 8th edition of the event, and part of the WTA Tier IV tournaments of the 2006 WTA Tour. It took place at the Tashkent Tennis Center in Tashkent, from 02 October 2006 2 through 08 October 2006." .

ns1:62690 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "USAir Flight 1016 was a regularly scheduled flight in the southeastern United States, between Columbia, South Carolina, and Charlotte, North Carolina On 02 July 1994, the flight encountered heavy thunderstorms and microburst-induced windshear while attempting to land, and crashed into heavy trees and a private residence near the airport. On Saturday, 02 July 1994, the plane, a McDonnell Douglas DC-9 registered N954VJ, departed Columbia Metropolitan Airport at 18:15 EDT for the 35-minute flight to Charlotte/Douglas International Airport with Captain Michael Greenlee (38), First Officer James Phillip \"Phil\" Hayes (41), three flight attendants, and 52 passengers (including two infants) on board The flight was uneventful until the approach to Charlotte, where several severe thunderstorms were in the vicinity of the airport. At 18:40, a tower controller issued a windshear warning to all aircraft, but on a different radio frequency than that used by Flight 1016 About a minute later, as Flight 1016 was on final approach, the captain, realizing that his aircraft was in a serious predicament, attempted to abort the landing by instructing the first officer to \"take it around, go to the right\" The captain then radioed the control tower and stated \"US Airways ten sixteen's on the go\"; the tower acknowledged the missed approach and cleared Flight 1016 to climb to 3,000 feet (910 m) The plane struggled to climb due to the severe weather conditions, veered to the right and rapidly descended The flight crew desperately tried to control the airplane as it plummeted toward the ground It was later determined that the wind shear alert system did not alert them with a red indicator and aural warning because of a software discrepancy that lowered the sensitivity while the flaps were in transit from 40 degrees to 15 degrees during the go around procedure." .

ns1:62740 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 Nagorno-Karabakh skirmish were a series of exchanges of gunfire that took place on 18 February 2010 on the line of contact dividing Azerbaijani and the Karabakh Armenian military forces. Azerbaijan accused the Armenian forces of firing on the Azerbaijani positions near Tap Qaraqoyunlu, Qızıloba, Qapanlı, Yusifcanlı and Cavahirli villages, as well as in uplands of Agdam District with small arms fire including snipers. The incidents embraced two regions in Nagorno-Karabakh (Tartar District, Agdam District) and one region out of it (Goranboy District)." .

ns1:62812 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017–18 Basketball Champions League was the second season of the Basketball Champions League (BCL), a European-wide professional basketball competition for clubs, that was launched by FIBA. The competition began on 19 September 2017, with the qualifying rounds, and concluded on 06 May 2018. A total of 56 teams (of which 20 were champions) from 32 countries participated in the 2017–18 Basketball Champions League. The labels in the parentheses show how each team qualified for the place of its starting round (TH: Basketball Champions League title holders; FEC: FIBA Europe Cup title holders): The schedule of the competition is as follows (all draws are held at the FIBA headquarters in Mies, Switzerland, unless stated otherwise): The draw for the qualifying rounds was held on 11 July 2017, at the FIBA headquarters in Mies, Switzerland." .

ns1:62826 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Gauntlet was an Allied Combined Operation from 25 August 1941 until 03 September 1941, during the World War II. Canadian, British and Free Norwegian Forces landed on the Norwegian island of Spitsbergen in the Svalbard, 650 mi (1,050 km) south of the North Pole. Coalmines on the islands were owned and operated by Norway at Longyearbyen and by the Soviet Union at Barentsburg; both governments agreed to their destruction and the evacuation of their nationals. The Svalbard lies in the Arctic Ocean about half way between northern Norway and the North Pole. From 25 July to 9 August 1940, the Admiral Hipper sailed from Trondheim to search the area from Tromsø to Bear Island and Svalbard (formerly Spitsbergen) and intercept British ships returning from Petsamo but found only a Finnish freighter. Vian was then sent to reconnoitre the west coast of Spitsbergen, the main island of the Svalbard, which was mostly ice-free and 450 mi (720 km) from northern Norway, to assess its potential as a base. The cruisers HMS Nigeria, HMS Aurora and two destroyers departed Iceland on 27 July but Vian found that the advantages of a base at Spitsbergen, were negated by the obstacles of weather and proximity to German bases in Norway. The final wireless message was transmitted on the evening of 03 September 1941 and the sets at Barentsburg, Longyearbyen, Kap Linné and Grønfjord were destroyed; as Force A made its return journey, a German station was heard calling Spitsbergen. On 5 September a Luftwaffe sortie was flown over Svalbard and the crew found Spitsbergen deserted and the coal dumps on fire. Arctic expert Erich Etienne made a proposal on his return to Norway, to set up a weather station on Svalbard to obtain better weather data and to relieve Wekusta 5 of the burden of flying in the long night of the polar winter. Operation Bansö (Unternehmen Bansö) began on 8 October with the flight of a Ju 52 to Svalbard to transport a weather party of four men and ten labourers to convert a house 2.5 mi (4.0 km) from Longyearbyen, near the landing ground at Sònak." .

ns1:62858 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1974 Tour of the Basque Country was the 14th edition of the Itzulia Basque Country cycle race and was held from 01 April 1974 to 05 April 1974. The race started in Ordizia and finished in Donostia-San Sebastián." .

ns1:62893 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2000 Tirreno–Adriatico was the 35th edition of the Tirreno–Adriatico cycle race and was held from 08 March 2000 to 15 March 2000. The race started in Sorrento and finished in San Benedetto del Tronto." .

ns1:62906 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 WGC-Cadillac Championship was a golf tournament played March 7–10 on the TPC Blue Monster course at Trump National Doral Miami in Doral, Florida, a suburb west of Miami. It was the 14th WGC-Mexico Championship tournament, and the second of the World Golf Championships events to be staged in 2013. The top 50 players from the Official World Golf Ranking, as of 2013 Keegan Bradley (2,3), Tim Clark, George Coetzee (2,6,9), Nicolas Colsaerts (2,5), Jason Day (2), Luke Donald (2,3,5), Jamie Donaldson (2,5,6), Jason Dufner (2,3), Ernie Els (2,3), Gonzalo Fernández-Castaño (2,5), Rickie Fowler (2,3), Jim Furyk (2,3), Sergio García (2,3), Robert Garrigus (2,3), Branden Grace (2,5,9), Bill Haas (2), Peter Hanson (2,5), Pádraig Harrington, Freddie Jacobson (2), Dustin Johnson (2,3,4), Zach Johnson (2,3), Martin Kaymer (2), Matt Kuchar (2,3,4), Paul Lawrie (2,5), Hunter Mahan (2,3,4), Matteo Manassero (2,5), Graeme McDowell (2,5), Rory McIlroy (2,3,5), Phil Mickelson (2,3,4), Francesco Molinari (2,5), Ryan Moore (2,3), Alex Norén, Thorbjørn Olesen (2,5,6), Louis Oosthuizen (2,3,5,6), Carl Pettersson (2,3), Scott Piercy (2,3), Ian Poulter (2,5,6), Justin Rose (2,3,5), Charl Schwartzel (2,5,6), Adam Scott (2,3), John Senden (2,3), Webb Simpson (2,3), Steve Stricker (2,3), Bo Van Pelt (2,3), Nick Watney (2,3), Bubba Watson (2,3), Lee Westwood (2,3,5), Tiger Woods (2,3,4) 2." .

ns1:62973 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The October 1187 papal election (held 21 October 1187) was convoked after the death of Pope Urban III. Pope Urban III died at Ferrara on 1187. On 21 October 1187 he was unanimously elected pope and took the name Gregory VIII." .

ns1:63076 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1986 Sunkist WITA Championships was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor clay courts at the Omni Amelia Island Plantation on Amelia Island in the United States that was part of the 1986 Virginia Slims World Championship Series. It was the seventh edition of the tournament and was held from 14 April 1986 through 20 April 1986." .

ns1:63106 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Conference on the Application of Esperanto in Science and Technology (Esperanto: Konferenco pri Aplikoj de Esperanto en Scienco kaj Tekniko, KAEST) is a biennial conference on the application of the constructed international auxiliary language Esperanto in the science and technology community. The conference first took place in 1978 in Czechoslovakia. Following the dissolution of Czechoslovakia, it remained in the Czech Republic until it was transferred in 2010 to Slovakia, where it is currently being organized by the nonprofit organization E@I. The 2012 KAEST in Modra, Slovakia included a Wikipedia workshop aimed at both beginners and advanced users, organized in cooperation with Wikimedia Slovakia." .

ns1:63217 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Entente Florale (, \"Flowery Alliance of Europe\") is an international horticultural competition established to recognise municipalities and villages in Europe for excellence in horticultural displays. There are three categories: Entente Florale is a competition for Towns and Villages. The competition was founded about 41 years ago, initially between Great Britain and France. Since 1998 under the aegis of the 'Association Européenne pour le Fleurissement et le Paysage', the association and the competition Entente Florale are open to all countries in the European Union as well as to the EFTA members countries (European Free Trade Association). Austria (AT) Belgium (BE) Czech Republic (CZ) Germany (DE) Hungary (HU) Ireland (IE) Italy (IT) Netherlands (NL) Slovenia (SI) United Kingdom (UK) Note re UK representatives -" .

ns1:63235 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Indian Premier League Final was a day/night Twenty20 cricket match played between Sunrisers Hyderabad and Chennai Super Kings, on 2018 at the Wankhede Stadium, Mumbai. It was to determine the winner of the 2018 Indian Premier League of the Indian Premier League, an annual Twenty20 tournament in India." .

ns1:63240 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Tashkent Open is a women's tennis tournament held in Tashkent, Uzbekistan. Held since 1999, this WTA Tour event is an WTA International tournaments tournament and is played on hardcourt." .

ns1:63247 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The treaty of Passarowitz or Treaty of Požarevac was the peace treaty signed in Požarevac (Serbian Cyrillic: Пожаревац, German: Passarowitz), a town in the Ottoman Empire (today in Serbia), on 21 July 1718 between the Ottoman Empire on one side and Austria of the Habsburg Monarchy and the Republic of Venice on the other." .

ns1:63257 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Turnham Green took place on 13 November 1642 near the village of Turnham Green, at the end of the first campaigning season of the First English Civil War. The battle resulted in a standoff between the forces of King Charles I of England and the much larger Parliamentarian army under the command of the Robert Devereux, 3rd Earl of Essex." .

ns1:63362 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Day of the Programmer is an international professional day that is celebrated on the 256th (hexadecimal 100th, or the 28th) day of each year (September during common years and on September in leap years). As early as 2002, they tried to gather signatures for a petition to the government of Russia to recognize the day as the official Day of the Programmer. On July 24, 2009, the Ministry of Communications and Mass Media (Russia) issued a draft of an executive order on a new professional holiday, Day of the Programmer. In China, the programmer's day is October 24, which has been established for many years." .

ns1:63380 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1987 Winston 500 was a NASCAR Winston Cup Series racing event that took place on 03 May 1987, at Talladega Superspeedway in Talladega, Alabama. Unrestricted high-speed races at Talladega Superspeedway ended after the 1987 Winston 500 because of the lap 22 crash. Talladega Superspeedway, originally known as Alabama International Motor Superspeedway (AIMS), is a motorsports complex located north of Talladega, Alabama. There were 18 lead changes; Bill Elliott and Davey Allison each led 30 laps." .

ns1:63458 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2000 DFB-Ligapokal Final decided the winner of the 2000 DFB-Ligapokal, the 4th edition of the reiterated DFB-Ligapokal, a knockout association football cup competition. The match was played on 2000 at the BayArena in Leverkusen. FC Bayern Munich won the match 5–1 against Hertha BSC for their 4th title." .

ns1:63492 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 Commonwealth Bank Tournament of Champions was a singles tennis tournament played on indoor hard courts. It was held at the Bali International Convention Centre in Bali from 04 November 2010 through 07 November 2010. This edition of the tournament used a different format from the 2009 Commonwealth Bank Tournament of Champions and the 2010 WTA Tour Championships." .

ns1:63570 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Klečka killings were the mass murder of 22 Kosovo Serb civilians, including children, reportedly by members of the Kosovo Liberation Army (KLA) over a period of several days in 01 July 1998, during the Kosovo War. Klečka, a village located approximately 40 kilometers southwest of Pristina, was a significant logistics and training base for the Kosovo Liberation Army (KLA) in the summer of 1998. Serbian police took Klečka from the Kosovo Liberation Army on 27 August 1998 after heavy fighting. The Yugoslav Ministry of Foreign Affairs accused the international community of double standards over the clashes which were occurring in Kosovo, and condemned the killings in Klečka as a \"Nazi-style crime.\" The two participated in the Klečka massacre." .

ns1:63589 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006 United States Figure Skating Championships took place between 07 January 2006 and 15, 2006 at the Enterprise Center in St. Louis, Missouri. Skaters competed in four disciplines – men's singles, ladies' singles, pair skating, and ice dancing – across three levels: senior, junior, and novice." .

ns1:63628 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1988 Volta a Catalunya was the 68th edition of the Volta a Catalunya cycle race and was held from 02 September 1988 to 07 September 1988. The race started in Salou and finished in Lleida." .

ns1:63643 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1957 Formula One season was the 11th season of Fédération Internationale de l'Automobile Formula One motor racing. It featured the 1957 World Championship of Drivers which commenced on 13 January 1957 and ended on 08 September 1957 after eight races. All 7 of the Fédération Internationale de l'Automobile-mandated championship races had been won by 2 drivers in 1957: Argentine Juan Manuel Fangio and Briton Stirling Moss. All Grand Prix races were run for Formula One cars, while the Indianapolis 500 was run for USAC National Championship cars and also counted towards the 1957 USAC Championship. The following Formula One races, also held in 1957, did not count towards the World Championship of Drivers." .

ns1:63663 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Canada Alamosa as it was known to the Union Army, or Alamosa as it was known to the Confederates, was a skirmish of the American Civil War on the late evening of 24 September 1861 and the morning of 25 September 1861. Two days before the engagement at Canada Alamosa, New Mexico, on 1861, Coopwood's Confederate troops captured nine men from the New Mexico Volunteers, after a brief skirmish due north of Fort Craig. The next day on 1861, after the battle of Canada Alamosa, New Mexico was over, the Union cavalry patrol under Captain Robert M. Morris, skirmished with Confederates involved in the Alamosa engagement in the Skirmish near Fort Thorn, New Mexico Territory." .

ns1:63701 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014–15 CONCACAF Champions League (officially the 2014–15 Scotiabank CONCACAF Champions League for sponsorship reasons starting from 2015) was the 7th edition of the CONCACAF Champions League under its current name, and overall the 50th edition of the premier association football club competition organized by CONCACAF, the regional governing body of North America, Central America, and the Caribbean. In the final, Mexican team Club América defeated Canadian team Montreal Impact 5–3 on aggregate to win their sixth CONCACAF club title (and their first during the CONCACAF Champions League era), tying the record of the most CONCACAF club title with Cruz Azul (who were the defending champions, but were eliminated in the group stage). As the winners of the 2014–15 CONCACAF Champions League, Club América earned the right to represent CONCACAF at the 2015 FIFA Club World Cup. A total of 24 teams participate in the CONCACAF Champions League: nine from the North American Zone (from three associations), twelve from the Central American Zone (from at most seven associations), and three from the Caribbean Zone (from at most three associations)." .

ns1:63713 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Supercopa Uruguaya was the first edition of the Supercopa Uruguaya, Uruguay's association football super cup. It was held on 2018 between 2017 Torneo Intermedio winners Club Nacional de Football and 2017 Primera División champions Club Atlético Peñarol. It was originally scheduled to be played on 28 January, however, and due to Club Nacional de Football's first match in the 2018 Copa Libertadores being scheduled on 31 January, the Supercopa was moved to 26 January. Club Atlético Peñarol were the winners after beating Club Nacional de Football 3–1 in normal time." .

ns1:63753 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Jeannette's Creek train wreck, also known as the Baptiste Creek train wreck, was a fatal railroad accident that occurred on 27 October 1854, at Baptiste Creek near present-day Jeannettes Creek in Chatham-Kent, Ontario. It was Canada's first major train wreck, leaving 52 people dead and indeed the worst rail disaster in North America at that time. The 48 (some sources 60) injured were taken to Chatham-Kent where their moans and cries could be \"heard throughout the town\", with blood literally dripping from the cars." .

ns1:41921 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1923 Tour de France was the 17th edition of the Tour de France, taking place 24 June 1923 to 22 July 1923. It consisted of 15 stages over 5386 km, ridden at an average speed of 24.233 km/h. The race was won by Henri Pélissier with a convincing half-hour lead to his next opponent, Italian Ottavio Bottecchia. In total, 139 cyclists entered the race, of which 48 finished. The French cyclists Henri Pélissier and Francis Pélissier had stopped the 1920 Tour de France after Henri Pélissier received a penalty from the Tour organisation for throwing away a tire. This had caused a fight between the Pélissier brothers and tour organiser Henri Pélissier Desgrange, and because of this fight, the Pélissier brother had been absent in the 1921 and 1922 Tours de France. He will never win the Tour\" Still, Henri Pélissier was the most talented French racer of his period, and in 1923 had won every major race except the Tour de France. The Tour de France had been dominated by Belgian cyclists in the last years, which was not good for the popularity of the race in France. Therefore, Desgrange wrote in l'Auto that Henri Pélissier was too old to win the Tour de France, and that Pélissier would therefore probably never start in the Tour de France. In total, Henri Pélissier won 17638 francs in the 1923 Tour de France, almost ninety times the standard monthly wage at that time. Henri Pélissier was the first French winner of the Tour de France since 1911, and ended a series of seven Belgian victories. This prediction was right; Bottecchia won the 1924 Tour de France and the 1925 Tour de France." .

ns1:42026 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 La Flèche Wallonne was the 79th edition of the La Flèche Wallonne one-day cycling classic. It took place on 22 April 2015 and was the twelfth race of the 2015 UCI World Tour. The race took place on a 205.5-kilometre (127.7 mi) route that started in Waremme and ends in Huy. The key aspect of La Flèche Wallonne is the climb of the Mur de Huy, which is crossed three times during the race; the finishing line is at the top of the final climb of the Mur. Valverde won his third victory on the climb, with Julian Alaphilippe (Etixx–Quick-Step) second and Michael Albasini (Orica–GreenEDGE) third; Valverde went on to win Liège–Bastogne–Liège as well. La Flèche Wallonne is part of the 2015 UCI World Tour, which meant that the 17 UCI WorldTeams were automatically invited and obliged to send a team. The 205.5-kilometre (127.7 mi) route of the 2015 La Flèche Wallonne began in Waremme in the province of Liège on the Rue de Huy with a 2-kilometre (1.2 mi) neutralised zone. The first climb of the Mur came with 118 kilometres (73 mi) raced and 87.5 kilometres (54.4 mi) to the finish line; it was followed by the day's only feed zone. The final loop of the race followed the same roads as before, east out of Huy and then south-west to the Côte d'Ereffe, climbed with 16.5 kilometres (10.3 mi) remaining. The top of the Côte de Cherave came with 5.5 kilometres (3.4 mi) to the finish line and was followed by a sharp right-hand turn and a fast descent back into Huy. The crucial part of the race was the final 1,300-metre (4,300 ft) climb of the Mur de Huy, which has an average gradient of 9.6%. These were Philippe Gilbert (BMC Racing Team), who had won the race in 2011; Joaquim Rodríguez (Team Katusha), who had won in 2012; and Daniel Moreno (Team Katusha), who had won in 2013. Vervaeke, Van Garderen and Baugnies combined briefly, but were unable to catch the leading pair and were themselves caught by the peloton with 13.4 kilometres (8.3 mi) remaining. Julian Alaphilippe (Etixx-Quick Step) finished in second place, with Michael Albasini (Orica–GreenEDGE) third. Riders who finished in the top ten in La Flèche Wallonne were awarded points in the 2015 UCI World Tour rankings." .

ns1:42847 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Bettencourt affair involves allegations of illegal payments made by billionaire heiress Liliane Bettencourt to François-Marie Banier and members of the French government associated with Nicolas Sarkozy in 2010. In 01 June 2010, Ms. Bettencourt became embroiled in a high-level French political scandal after other details of the tape recordings made by her butler became public. In October 2010, the offices of two major French newspapers (Le Monde, Le Point) and those of Mediapart were broken into and the computers containing Bettencourt affair's files were stolen. In 2013, the former French president Nicolas Sarkozy, a former minister, Éric Woerth, a prosecutor, Philippe Courroye and the former director of the DCRI, Bernard Squarcini, were targeted by a procedure of indictment in diverse points of the affair." .

ns1:42881 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Greatest Hits Tour was the third concert tour by Irish and British girl group Girls Aloud, in support of their first greatest hits album The Sound of Girls Aloud: The Greatest Hits. Girls Aloud returned to Northern Ireland and the Republic of Ireland, having not performed there on 2006's Chemistry: The Tour. The Sound of Girls Aloud: The Greatest Hits Tour kicked off in Cheryl Cole's hometown of Newcastle on 14 May 2007 and concluding in near Nadine Coyle's home in Belfast on 02 June 2007. Girls Aloud released another studio album and announced Tangled Up Tour for the following year. The Greatest Hits Tour remains Girls Aloud's only tour not to receive a video release." .

ns1:43321 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Tell Abyad offensive or Martyr Rubar Qamışlo operation was a military operation that began in late 01 May 2015 in the northern Al-Raqqah Governorate, during the Syrian Civil War. It was conducted by the Kurdish-majority People Protection Units (YPG) and the Free Syrian Army (FSA) against the Islamic State of Iraq and the Levant. The focus of the campaign was to capture the key border town of Tell Abyad, and to link the Kobanî and Jazira Cantons in Northern Syria. On 7 June, the YPG, backed by the FSA, Battle of Tell Abyad to the east of Suluk, and captured a number of villages to the southwest of Ras al-Ayn, west of the eastern provincial boundary of the Al-Raqqah Governorate. According to a Al-Raqqah Governorate-based activist, ISIL stripped the Tell Abyad hospital of all of its equipment and moved it to Al-Raqqah Governorate. On 15 June, YPG and allied forces captured Mashor Tahtani, to the southeast of Tell Abyad, and seized the Tell Abyad-Al-Raqqah Governorate road, besieging the strategic border town of Tell Abyad, and connecting the two Kurdish cantons of Kobanî and Jazira. The next day, YPG and allied forces captured Tell Abyad, and reinforced positions around the town and border crossing, while ISIL reinforced their own positions to the north of Al-Raqqah Governorate city. Following Tell Abyad's capture, disputes arose between the YPG and the Free Syrian Army's Retribution Army, as result of which the latter left the offensive and went to Turkey. The remaining ISIL fighters were reportedly fortifying their positions along the riverbanks of the river near Tell Abyad, as well as the highway connecting Al-Raqqah Governorate to Tell Abyad, while maintaining small units to the east of the water course. It was also reported that the ISIL fighters who had escaped into Turkey, from the villages to the west of Tell Abyad, were seeking transportation westward to re-enter Syria in the northern Aleppo Governorate. On 25 June, ISIL militants Battle of Tell Abyad on the city of Kobanî, in retaliation for the recent Kurdish advances in the Al-Hasakah and Tell Abyad regions. On 27 February 2016, ISIL Battle of Tell Abyad on Tell Abyad from both Turkey to the north and Syria further south. Seizing Tell Abyad would also help the YPG to link up the Kurdish-controlled areas of northern Syria." .

ns1:43739 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Group 5 of the 2019 UEFA European Under-21 Championship qualification Group 8 consists of six teams: Portugal national under-21 football team, Switzerland national under-21 football team, Romania national under-21 football team, Wales national under-21 football team, Bosnia and Herzegovina national under-21 football team, and Liechtenstein national under-21 football team." .

ns1:44771 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Burmese–Siamese War  (1785–86), known as the Nine Armies' Wars in Thailand history because the Burmese came in nine armies, was the first war between the Konbaung Dynasty of Burma and the Thailand Rattanakosin Kingdom of the Chakri dynasty. In 1785, three years after the foundation of Bangkok as the new royal seat and the Chakri dynasty, King Bodawpaya of Burma marched massive armies with total number of 144,000 to invade Siam in nine armies through five directions including Kanchanaburi, Ratchaburi, Lanna, Tak, Thalang (Phuket) and the southern Malay Peninsula. King Taksin of Thonburi freed Siam from Burmese domination and revived the Thailand kingdom. In 1774, most of the Lanna, which had been under the Burmese rule for about 250 years, came under Thailand domination with exception of Chiang Saen still under Burmese rule. During the Burmese-Thailand War (1775-76), Siam nearly succumbed to the Burmese forces led by General Maha Thiha Thura and Siam's manpower was depleted. King Bodawpaya began his Thailand expeditions in 1785. The captured Burmese revealed to Thailand authorities that King Bodawpaya was planning a massive invasion of Siam in multiple directions. In 1786, the Thailand staged the all-out attack against the Burmese lines. Dawei Wun retreated towards Suan Phueng and was followed by the Thailand. As Chiang Mai had been abandoned since 1776, the main outpost of the Lanna against the Burmese was Lampang. Prince Thado Thiri Maha Uzana and Aprakamani marched from Chiang Saen to attack Lampang in 1785, leading to the Siege of Lampang. The Thailand attacked the besieging Burmese at the rear and the Burmese Prince Thado Thiri Maha Uzana withdrew to Chiang Saen. The Burmese general Maha Thiri Thihathu (\"Kinwun Mingyi\" in Thai sources) sailed the massive fleet of 10,000 men from Mergui to the Thailand Andaman Coast in 1785. In 1786, King Bodawpaya resumed his Thailand campaigns." .

ns1:45180 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The German campaign in Angola took place before the official declaration of war between Germany and Portugal in March 1916. German and Portuguese troops clashed several times on the border between German South-West Africa and Portuguese Angola. The Germans won most of these clashes and were able to occupy the Humbe region of southern Portuguese Angola until Portuguese control was restored a few days before the South-West Africa Campaign defeated the Germans. After the war broke out, the border between German South-West Africa and Portuguese Angola remained open. Fighting in southern Portuguese Angola took place before a formal state of war had been declared between Germany and Portugal (Germany declared war on Portugal on 9 March 1916) when clashes occurred between 01 October 1914 and 09 July 1915. Due to the possibility of an attack from German Southwest Africa, Portuguese forces in southern Portuguese Angola were reinforced by a military expedition led by Lieutenant-Colonel Alves Roçadas, which arrived at Moçâmedes on 01 October 1914. The first serious one was the Naulila battle on 19 October, in which 3 German officers, heading a military column which had entered Portuguese Angola without permission from the Portuguese authorities, were killed by Portuguese troops." .

ns1:45260 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1972 American League Championship Series took place between 07 October 1972 and 12, 1972. The Oakland Athletics (93–62 on the season) played the Detroit Tigers (86–70 on the season) for the right to go to the 1972 World Series, with the A's coming out on top in the five-game series, 3–2. Games 1 and 2 were played at the Oakland Coliseum, and 3 through 5 were played at Tiger Stadium. After Game 2, the Series moved to Detroit, where the Tigers would fight back, winning the next two. However, the Tigers responded with 1973 American League Championship Series in the bottom of the 10th to win, 4-3. Odom got the win, Blue got the save, and the A's were a World Series team for the first time in Oakland and won the first of 1973 American League Championship Series straight American League pennants. The Tigers and A's have since met 1973 American League Championship Series times in the postseason, with Detroit winning all 1973 American League Championship Series, sweeping the A's in 2006, and winning decisive fifth games in 2012, and 2013. Oakland won the series, 3–2. 1972 ALCS (3–2): Oakland Athletics over Detroit Tigers." .

ns1:45761 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1998–99 Albanian Superliga was the 60th season of the Albanian Superliga, the top professional league for association football clubs, since its establishment in 1930. The season began on 29 August 1998 and concluded on 15 May 1999. KS Vllaznia Shkodër began the season as defending champions of the 1997–98 Albanian Superliga and KS Burreli was the only team promoted from the Albanian Second Division as the league was reduced from 18 teams to 16. The newly promoted club KS Burreli were the first team to be relegated, and they were followed by KF Laçi and Besa Kavajë down to the Albanian Second Division. A total of 16 teams competed in the 1998–99 season, 2 less than the previous where 18 teams competed. Of these 16 teams, 15 of them were from the 1997–98 Albanian Superliga with KS Burreli being the only promoted side from the Albanian Second Division. 3 sides from the 1997–98 Albanian Superliga were relegated, and they were Shqiponja, Sopoti and Albpetrol." .

ns1:46477 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Philippi was the final battle in the Liberators' civil war between the forces of Mark Antony and Octavian (of the Second Triumvirate) and the leaders of Julius Caesar's assassination, Brutus and Cassius in 42 BC, at Philippi in Macedonia. The battle, involving up to 200,000 men in one of the largest of the Roman civil wars, consisted of two engagements in the plain west of the ancient city of Philippi. A second encounter, on 23 October 0042, finished off Brutus's forces after a hard-fought battle. Norbanus and Saxa passed the town of Philippi in eastern Macedonia and took a strong defensive position at a narrow mountain pass. This brought about a general battle on 03 October 0042 42 BC. The total casualties for the second battle of Philippi were not reported, but the close quarters fighting likely resulted in heavy losses for both sides. Many other young Roman aristocrats lost their lives in the battle or committed suicide after the defeat, including the son of great orator Hortensius, and Marcus Porcius Cato, the son of Cato the Younger, and Marcus Livius Drusus Claudianus, the father of Livia, who became Octavian's wife. The Battle of Philippi marked the highest point of Antony's career: at that time he was the most famous Roman general and the senior partner of the Second Triumvirate. Augustus's own version of the Battle of Philippi was: \"I sent into exile the murderers of my father, punishing their crimes with lawful tribunals, and afterwards, when they made war upon the Republic, I twice defeated them in battle.\"" .

ns1:46771 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 New Zealand Winter Games was the 4th edition of the New Zealand Winter Gameswas contested 21 August 2015 through 30 August 2015 in the cities of Naseby, Queenstown, and Wanaka. curling will be held in the Naseby curling Rink, or the Maniototo curling International within Naseby. Both freestyle skiing and snowboarding will be held at the Cardrona Alpine Resort near Wanaka." .

ns1:47056 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Tour of Utah was a seven-stage road cycling race held from 31 July 2017 to 06 August 2017, and the 13th edition of the Tour of Utah. It was rated as a 2.HC on the 2017 UCI America Tour. Stage 2 was won by Brent Bookwalter. Stage 4 was won by John Murphy. Stage 5 was won by Travis McCabe. Stage 6 was won by Giulio Ciccone." .

ns1:48168 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Tetovo (Macedonian: Тетовска битка) was the largest engagement during the 2001 insurgency in the Republic of Macedonia, in which Macedonian security forces battled Albanian insurgents of the National Liberation Army for control of the city. During the break up of Yugoslavia in the 1990s, over 2,000 ethnic Albanians marched through Tetovo demanding secession from the Socialist Republic of Macedonia and unity with Albania. Tetovo became headquarters of the new Albanian political parties, which were regarded as unconstitutional by the Republic of Macedonia. Prior to the NATO bombing of Yugoslavia over Kosovo, Tetovo became the rear supply base for the Kosovo Liberation Army, and then later home to over 100,000 Kosovar refugees from the Kosovo war. In the battle for Tetovo, the Macedonian Army was frequently outmanoeuvred by the highly mobile guerrillas and their military leader, Gezim Ostreni. Staunch resistance by 100 NLA fighters cornered in the Gracani area after the Tetovo fighting continued to harass the ARM, whose infantry units seemed extremely reluctant to engage in an open battle. As United States and European envoys met with President Boris Trajkovski in Skopje on 23 July, the battle reached Tetovo's suburbs. On 14 August, A Macedonian policeman was shot dead by suspected ethnic Albanian rebels in Tetovo, puncturing the fragile ceasefire declared on Sunday as part of a plan to end the six-month rebellion and pave the way for the disarmament of the guerrilla National Liberation Army." .

ns1:48280 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The World Chess Championship 2018 was a match between the reigning world champion since 2013, Magnus Carlsen, and the challenger Fabiano Caruana to determine the World Chess Championship. The 12-game match, organised by FIDE and its commercial partner FIDE, was played at The College in Holborn, London, between 09 November 2018 and 28 November 2018. On 28 November 2018, rapid chess was used as a tie-breaker; Carlsen won three consecutive games to retain his title. The Championship match between Magnus Carlsen and Fabiano Caruana was held from 9 to 28 November 2018 in London, United Kingdom, at the Cochrane Theatre of The College in Holborn. The match was held under the auspices of FIDE, the world chess federation, with the organisation rights belonging to FIDE, its commercial partner. Following the World Chess Championship 2016 in 2016, the president of FIDE, Kirsan Ilyumzhinov, said that the next venue would be in London or somewhere in Asia. Among the guests were movie stars Woody Harrelson (who also made the first move in game one for the World Chess Championship 2016 in New York) and Tom Hollander; Ellis Reppen, partner of Jan Gustafsson, who was part of Carlsen's team in New York; Wikipedia founder Jimmy Wales; Sergey Karjakin, the previous challenger for the chess championship; Daniel Weil, the person who designed the pawn he moved, as well as the rest of pieces and the chessboard; and Lucy Hawking, daughter of physicist Stephen Hawking." .

ns1:48770 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Worringen was fought on 05 June 1288 near the town of Worringen (also spelled Woeringen), which is now the northernmost borough of Cologne. It was the decisive battle of the War of the Limburg Succession, fought for the possession of the Duchy of Limburg between Archbishop Siegfried II of Cologne and Duke John I of Duchy of Brabant, and one of the largest battles in Europe in the Middle Ages. Duke John intended to enlarge his Duchy of Brabant territory and re-unite the former Duchy of Lower Lorraine in the northwest of the Holy Roman Empire. On the other side the Westphalian counts of Mark took the chance to affirm their independence from the Archbishop of Cologne and together with the Counts of Loon, Tecklenburg and Waldeck allied with Duchy of Brabant and Berg. Together they marched against Worringen, a castle on the Rhine held by the Archbishop of Cologne. On the early morning of 05 June 1288 they departed for Worringen at the head of their troops. In the earliest phases of the battle, John of Duchy of Brabant and Henry of Luxembourg met in a fierce fight, in which Henry and two of his brothers were killed. Soon after that, Siegfried entered the battle and in a bold advance was able to beat off the Berg troops and the Cologne militia, however with too little support from his back army. The battle ended in a victory for Duchy of Brabant when Reinald of Guelders was captured by Daniel van Bouchout and Lord Walram of Valkenburg had to retreat. The number of deaths at the battle of Worringen is estimated at 1100 on the Guelders side and 40 on the Duchy of Brabant side. Reinoud of Guelders was released after he had renounced all claims to the Duchy of Limburg. The Battle of Worringen meant a rise in the power of Duchy of Brabant, Berg and Mark, while the City of Cologne gained its independence from the Archbishopric and finally the status of an Imperial city in 1475. The Duchy of Limburg was added to the Duchy of Brabant in 1289, an arrangement approved by King Rudolph and again by his former opponent Adolf of Nassau, after he was elected King of the Romans in 1292." .

ns1:49075 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The swimming at the 2008 Summer Olympics – women's 200 metre freestyle event at the 2008 Olympic Games took place on 11 August 2008–13 August at the Beijing National Aquatics Center in Beijing, China. Italy's Federica Pellegrini broke a new world record of 1:54.82 to claim a gold medal in the event. Sara Isaković, who finished behind Pellegrini by 0.15 of a second, cleared a 1:55 barrier to set a new Slovenian record of 1:54.97, and ultimately become the nation's first ever medalist in swimming. China's Pang Jiaying edged out U.S. swimmer Katie Hoff on the final lap to pick up a bronze in 1:55.05." .

ns1:49143 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1994–95 2. Fußball-Bundesliga season was the twenty-first season of the 2. Bundesliga, the second tier of the German football league system. F.C. Hansa Rostock, FC St. Pauli and Fortuna Düsseldorf were promoted to the Bundesliga while 1. FC Saarbrücken, FC 08 Homburg and FSV Frankfurt were relegated to the Regionalliga. For the 1994–95 season Fortuna Düsseldorf, FSV Frankfurt and FSV Zwickau were newly promoted to the 2. Bundesliga from the Oberliga while 1. FC Nürnberg, SG Wattenscheid 09 and VfB Leipzig had been relegated to the league from the Bundesliga." .

ns1:49326 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Maratha invasions of Bengal (1741-1751), also known as the Expeditions in Bengal, refers to the frequent invasions by the Maratha forces in the Bengal Subah (Bengal, Bihar, parts of modern Odisha), after their successful campaign in the Carnatic region at the Battle of Trichinopoly. The Marathas invaded Bengal six times from 01 August 1741 to 01 May 1751. Nawab Alivardi Khan succeeded in resisting all the invasions in western Bengal , however, the frequent Maratha invasions caused great destruction in the Bengal Subah, resulting in heavy civilian casualties and widespread economic losses. According to estimation of Chief of Dutch Factory Jan Kerssebom's memoirs perhaps close to 400,000 people in Bengal and Bihar were killed. 1.2 million of chauth from the revenue of Bengal and Bihar, and the Marathas agreed not to invade Bengal again. But the continuous conflict took a heavy toll on the population of Bengal. After the inauguration of Alivardi Khan as the Nawab of Bengal, the provincial governor of Odisha, Zafar Khan Rustam Jung, more commonly known as Murshid Quli II, revolted against him. Murshid Quli II's son-in-law Mirza Baker, assisted by Maratha troops and the rebel forces of Odisha (who were dissatisfied with the governor of Odisha), invaded Odisha in 01 August 1741. Jan Kersseboom, chief of the Dutch East India Company factory in Bengal, estimated that perhaps around 400,000 people were killed due to the Bargis during their occupation of western Bengal and Bihar. In 1751, the Marathas signed a peace treaty with the Nawab of Bengal, according to which Mir Habib (a former courtier of Alivardi Khan, who had defected to the Marathas) was made provincial governor of Odisha under nominal control of the Nawab of Bengal. 1.2 million annually as the chauth of Bengal and Bihar, and the Marathas agreed not to invade Bengal again." .

ns1:49608 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Strade Bianche was the ninth edition of the Strade Bianche road cycling race. Held on 07 March 2015, it started in San Gimignano and ended 200 km (124 mi) away in Siena. It is a 1.HC-ranked race that is part of the 2015 UCI Europe Tour. The race was won by Zdeněk Štybar, with Greg Van Avermaet finishing second and Alejandro Valverde finishing third. These are often farm tracks and include both climbs and descents, as well as various additional challenges such as uneven surfaces, steep grades, and potholes; the longest such section covers a distance of 11.5 km (7.1 mi). There are ten sections of strade bianche, with the last coming 12 km (7 mi) from the finish in Siena. Other riders with a chance at winning the race included Vincenzo Nibali (Astana), Rigoberto Urán, Zdeněk Štybar (both Etixx–Quick-Step), Simon Gerrans (Orica–GreenEDGE), Alejandro Valverde (Movistar Team), Greg Van Avermaet (BMC Racing Team) and Ian Stannard (Team Sky). This move was ended by attacks from the peloton behind on the Monte Sante Marie, which led to the formation of a new group of riders, including favourites for the race win including Fabian Cancellara, Zdeněk Štybar, Alejandro Valverde, Greg Van Avermaet and Peter Sagan. Sep Vanmarcke (LottoNL–Jumbo) was chasing alone behind them, with another group of Cancellara, Diego Rosa (Astana) and Daniel Oss (BMC Racing Team) behind." .

ns1:49671 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Somerset v Stewart (1772) 98 ER 499 (also known as Somersett's case, and v. XX Sommersett v Steuart) is a judgment of the English Court of King's Bench in 1772, relating to the right of an enslaved person on English soil not to be forcibly removed from the country and sent to Jamaica for sale. James Somerset, an enslaved African, was purchased by Charles Stewart (or Steuart), a customs officer when he was in Boston, Province of Massachusetts Bay, a British crown colony in North America. When the two lawyers for Charles Stewart put their case, they argued that property was paramount and that it would be dangerous to free all the black people in England, who numbered at the time approximately 15,000. Mansfield stated that the Somersett case had determined only that a master could not force a slave to leave England, much as in earlier times a master could not forcibly remove his villein. While Somerset's case provided a boon to the abolitionist movement, it did not end the holding of slaves within England." .

ns1:49905 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Isner–Mahut match at the 2010 Wimbledon Championships is the longest tennis match in history. It was a first-round 2010 Wimbledon Championships – Men's Singles match, in which the American 23rd seed John Isner played French qualifier Nicolas Mahut. The match began at 6:13 pm (British Summer Time, or 17:13 UTC) on Tuesday, 22 June 2010, on Court 18 at Wimbledon. Both players broke numerous Wimbledon and tennis records, including each serving over 100 aces, with the match being referred to as \"the endless match\". In October 2018, three months after another marathon match featuring Isner – this time against Kevin Anderson in the semifinals of 2018 Wimbledon, which Anderson won 7–6(8–6), 6–7(5–7), 6–7(9–11), 6–4, 26–24 after 6 hours and 36 minutes, the All England Lawn Tennis and Croquet Club announced that Wimbledon would be introducing a rule change to prevent such long matches occurring in the future. In the United Kingdom, the match was featured live in part on BBC One, BBC Two and BBC HD, which were all the television channels which the BBC used to cover Wimbledon." .

ns1:49974 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Chronicle was the Allied invasion of Woodlark Island and Kiriwina, in the South West Pacific, during World War II. Preliminary actions commenced on 23 June 1943–24 30 June 1943 when small reconnaissance parties were landed on both islands. United States Army Air Force aircraft arrived on Woodlark Island in mid-July, while Royal Australian Air Force aircraft began operations from Kiriwina in August. Kiriwina and Woodlark Island are situated in the Solomon Sea, in a group of islands that lie northeast of the Territory of Papua coast. Kiriwina lies about 125 miles (201 km) from New Britain while Woodlark Island is 200 miles (320 km) from Bouganville. Initial planning for the seizure of Woodlark Island and Kiriwina was undertaken in May 1943 at General Walter Krueger's Sixth Army headquarters in Brisbane, Australia. Woodlark Island and Kiriwina were required as future airfield sites to support operations in both New Guinea, New Britain and the Solomon Islands as part of the wider Operation Cartwheel, during which the Allies planned to advance towards the main Japanese base around Rabaul. There were no Japanese troops on either Woodlark Island or Kiriwina at the time of the operation. Reconnaissance parties landed on Woodlark Island and Kiriwina in May and reported that there were no Japanese troops occupying the islands. An advance party of 112th Cavalry Regiment under the command of Major D. M. McMains, left Milne Bay at 16:00 on 22 30 June 1943 aboard the destroyer transports Brooks and Humphreys bound for Woodlark Island. After arriving at Woodlark Island, the landing began at 21:00 on 30 June 1943. On 30 June 1943, 2,250 troops of Kiriwina Force, led by Colonel J. Prugh Herndon, consisting of the 158th Infantry Regiment (less the 2d Battalion) the 148th Field Artillery Battalion with other artillery, engineer, ordnance, medical, antiaircraft, and quartermaster troops departed Milne Bay aboard twelve LCIs. In contrast, American historian James Scott Powell has noted that Operation Chronicle had mixed effects on the 112th Cavalry Regiment: while the operation improved its ability to conduct amphibious landings and operate in jungle terrain, the demands of landing on and garrisoning Woodlark Island limited the opportunities its men had to conduct other forms of training before entering combat." .

ns1:50404 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Sharjah FC set a record of the longest unbeaten run in a 2018–19 UAE Pro-League season for 23 games before losing to Al Wasl 3–2. On 15, Sharjah FC won their first title since 1996 after beating Al-Wahda S.C.C. 3–2, this is the first time a team outside of Dubai and Abu Dhabi has won the league since the 1996. On the 26, Emirates Club and Dibba Al Al-Fujairah FC were relegated after a fixed match between Shabab Al Ahli Club and Al-Fujairah FC favoured Al-Fujairah FC and got them out of relegation. The Emirates Club demanded that the UAEFA investigate on the match between Shabab Al Ahli Club and Al-Fujairah FC, questioning the legitimacy of the two penalties that was rewarded to Al-Fujairah FC and the two goals that was disallowed for Shabab Al Ahli Club. * = Dibba Al Al-Fujairah FC Stadium doesn't fit 2018–19 UAE Pro-League requirements, therefore Dibba Club shares Al-Fujairah FC Club Stadium with Al-Fujairah FC club." .

ns1:50768 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1824 United States presidential election was the tenth quadrennial United States presidential election. Andrew Jackson, John Quincy Adams, Henry Clay and William H. Crawford were the primary contenders for the President of the United States. On February 9, 1825, John Quincy Adams was elected as president. The Democratic-Republican Party had won six consecutive presidential elections and by 1824 was the only national political party. Monroe attempted to improve discipline by appointing leading statesmen to his Cabinet, including Secretary of State John Quincy Adams of Massachusetts, Secretary of the Treasury William H. Crawford of Georgia, and Secretary of War John C. Calhoun of South Carolina. The campaigning for United States presidential election of 1824 took many forms. The 1824 United States presidential election marked the final collapse of the Republican-Federalist political framework. John C. Calhoun, supported by Adams and Jackson, easily won the vice President of the United States. States where the margin of victory was under 1%: States where the margin of victory was under 5%: States where the margin of victory was under 10%: As no presidential candidate had won an absolute electoral vote majority, the responsibility for electing a new president devolved upon the U.S. House of Representatives, which held a contingent election on February 5, 1825. As prescribed by the Twelfth Amendment, the House was limited to choosing from among the three candidates who received the most electoral votes: Andrew Jackson, John Quincy Adams, and William H. Crawford; Henry Clay, who had finished fourth, was eliminated." .

ns1:51095 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1944 United States presidential election was the 40th quadrennial presidential election. Incumbent Democratic Party President of the United States Franklin Delano Roosevelt defeated Republican Party Thomas E. Dewey to win an unprecedented fourth term. Unlike in 1940 United States presidential election, Roosevelt faced little opposition within his own party, and he easily won the presidential nomination of the 1944 Democratic Party National Convention. Concerned that Roosevelt's ill-health would mean the Vice President of the United States would likely become President of the United States, the convention dropped Roosevelt's running mate Henry A. Wallace in favor of Senator Harry S. Truman of Missouri. Governor Dewey of New York emerged as the front-runner for the Republican Party nomination after his victory in the Wisconsin primary, and he defeated conservative Governor John William Bricker at the 1944 Republican Party National Convention. As 1944 began, the frontrunners for the Republican Party nomination appeared to be Wendell Willkie, the party's 1940 United States presidential election nominee, Senator Robert A. Taft from Ohio, the leader of the party's conservatives, New York Governor Thomas E. Dewey, the leader of the party's moderate eastern establishment, General Douglas MacArthur, then serving as an Allied commander in the Pacific theater of the war, and former Minnesota Governor Harold Stassen, then serving as a U.S. naval officer in the Pacific. On election day, the Democratic Party incumbent scored a fairly comfortable victory over his Republican Party challenger. Roosevelt took 36 states for 432 electoral votes (266 were needed to win), while Dewey won twelve states and 99 electoral votes. Dewey did better against Roosevelt than any of Roosevelt's previous three Republican Party opponents: Roosevelt's percentage and margin of the total vote were both less than in 1940 United States presidential election. Dewey would again become the Republican Party presidential nominee in 1948, challenging President of the United States Truman (who had assumed that office on FDR's death), and would again lose, though by somewhat smaller popular- and electoral-vote margins. This ticket, which represented the Democratic Party element opposing the re-election of President of the United States Roosevelt, called for the \"restoration of states' rights which have been destroyed by the Communist New Deal\" and \"restoration of the supremacy of the white race\". Margin of victory less than 1% (25 electoral votes): Margin of victory less than 5% (165 electoral votes): Margin of victory between 5% and 10% (138 electoral votes): Counties with Highest Percent of Vote (Democratic Party) Counties with Highest Percent of Vote (Republican Party)" .

ns1:51332 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 Badminton World Championships – Mixed Doubles tournament of the 2011 Badminton World Championships was held from 08 August 2011–14. Zhang Nan and Zhao Yunlei won the title after defeating Chris Adcock and Imogen Bankier 21–15, 21–7 in the final." .

ns1:51619 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Declaration of Independence of Ukraine (Ukrainian: Акт проголошення незалежності України, translit. Akt proholoshennya nezalezhnosti Ukrayiny) was adopted by the Ukrainian parliament on 24 August 1991. The text was largely composed during the night of 23 August–24 August 1991 mainly by Levko Lukianenko, Serhii Holovatyi, Mykhailo Horyn, Ivan Zayets and Viacheslav Chornovil. The Parliament also voted for the creation of a national guard of Ukraine and turned jurisdiction over all the armed forces located on Ukrainian territory over to itself. On 26 24 August 1991 the Permanent Representative of the Ukrainian SSR to the United Nations (the Ukrainian SSR was a founding member of the United Nations) Hennadiy Udovenko informed the office of the Secretary General of the United Nations that his permanent mission to this international assembly would officially be designated as representing Ukraine. the Verkhovna Rada of the Ukrainian Soviet Socialist Republic solemnly declares the Independence of Ukraine and the creation of an independent Ukrainian state – UKRAINE." .

ns1:51978 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Invasion of the Falkland Islands (Spanish: Invasión de las Islas Malvinas), code-named Operation Rosary, was a military operation launched by Argentine Armed Forces on 02 April 1982, to capture the Falkland Islands, and served as a catalyst for the subsequent Falklands War. Governor Rex Hunt was informed by the British Government of a possible Argentine invasion on 01 April 1982. The Argentine amphibious operation began in the late evening of Thursday 01 April 1982, when the destroyer ARA Santísima Trinidad disembarked special naval forces south of Stanley. On the night of 01 April 1982–02 April 1982, Santísima Trinidad halted 500 metres (1,600 ft) off Mullet Creek and lowered 21 Gemini assault craft into the water. The Falklands Governor disputed Argentine claims that the seaborne assault resulted in only one Argentine dead and two wounded, telling Time Magazine reporters Briton Hadden and Henry Robinson Luce in their 12 02 April 1982 article that at least five and possibly 15 invaders were killed and 17 were wounded in the invasion. On 03 April 1982, the United Nations Security Council passed Resolution 502 demanding an immediate withdrawal of all Argentine Armed Forces from the islands and calling on the governments of Argentina and the United Kingdom to seek a diplomatic solution to the situation and to refrain from further military action." .

ns1:52047 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017–18 Gibraltar Premier Division was the 119th season of the top-tier national association football league in Gibraltar, as well as the fifth season since the Gibraltar Football Association joined UEFA in 2013. It began on 26 25 September 2017 and ended on 03 June 2018. Europa FC were the defending champions, having ended a fourteen-year winning streak for Lincoln Red Imps F.C.. Lincoln won their 23rd title on 19 with two games to spare, after a 7–1 victory over Lions Gibraltar F.C.. The champions earned a place in the preliminary round of the 2018–19 Champions League, and the second–placed club earned a place in the preliminary round of the 2018–19 Europa FC League. At the conclusion of the 2016–17 Gibraltar Premier Division, Europa FC Point was relegated. Manchester 62 F.C. earned the right to stay in the Premier Division by winning a playoff at the end of the 2016–17 Gibraltar Premier Division." .

ns1:52289 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2003 Giro d'Italia was the 86th edition of the Giro d'Italia, one of cycling's Grand Tours. Nineteen teams entered the race that was won by the Italian Gilberto Simoni of the Saeco team. Second and third were the Italian Stefano Garzelli and Ukrainian Yaroslav Popovych. With Gilberto Simoni's general classification victories in 2003 and in 2001, Simoni became the eighteenth rider to repeat as winner of the Giro d'Italia. In the race's other classifications, Colombia–Selle Italia rider Fredy González won the mountains classification and Magnus Bäckstedt of the Team Fakta-Pata Chips team won the intergiro classification. Out of the 170 riders that started this edition of the Giro d'Italia, a total of 97 riders made it to the finish in Milan. The 19 teams that took part in the race were: The route for the 2003 Giro d'Italia was unveiled by race director Carmine Castellano on 30 November 2002 in Milan. In the stages containing categorized climbs, six had summit finishes: stage 3, to Terme Luigiane; stage 7, to Monte Terminillo; stage 12, to Monte Zoncolan; stage 14, to Alpe di Pampeago; stage 18, to Chianale; and stage 19, to Cascata del Toce." .

ns1:52645 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2005 Tour de Langkawi was the 10th edition of the Tour de Langkawi, a cycling stage race that took place in Malaysia. It began on 28 January 2005 in Langkawi and ended on 06 February 2005 in Kuala Lumpur. Ryan Cox of South Africa won the race, followed by José Rujano of Venezuela second and Tiaan Kannemeyer of South Africa third." .

ns1:52704 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The World Trade Center Site Memorial Competition was an open, international memorial contest, initiated by the Lower Manhattan Development Corporation (LMDC) according to the specifications of the architect Daniel Libeskind, to design a memorial for the World Trade Center site (later renamed the National September 11 Memorial) at the under-construction World Trade Center in New York City. The competition began 28 April 2003 and the winner—Michael Arad and Peter Walker's Reflecting Absence—was revealed 14 January 2004, in a press conference at Federal Hall National Memorial. On January 6, 2004, the Selection Jury named Michael Arad and Peter Walker's \"Reflecting Absence\" as the winning design, which was displayed publicly 14 January 2004. It consisted mostly of architects and artists but also individuals with other affiliations: Honorary Member: The jury selected 8 finalists: Michael Arad and Peter Walker's Reflecting Absence consists of a field of trees interrupted by two large voids containing recessed pools, marking the footprints of the Twin Towers." .

ns1:52873 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Lucent Public License is an OSI-approved license created by Lucent. It has been released in two versions: Version 1.0 and 1.02. Notably, the license was the only open source license available to the public for the Plan 9 from Bell Labs operating system until 2014, when the University of California, Berkeley was \"authorised by Alcatel-Lucent to release all Plan 9 software previously governed by the Lucent Public License, Version 1.02 under the GNU General Public License, Version 2.\" The license has been approved by the Open Source Initiative as a license meeting The Open Source Definition, while the Free Software Foundation considers it a free software license, but suggests to eschew use of the license, stating: “This is a free software license, but it is incompatible with the GNU GPL because of its choice of law clause." .

ns1:52979 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The War of the Oranges (Portuguese: Guerra das Laranjas; French: Guerre des Oranges; Spanish: Guerra de las Naranjas) was a brief conflict in 1801 in which Spanish forces, instigated by the government of French First Republic, and ultimately supported by the French military, invaded Kingdom of Portugal. It was a precursor to the Peninsular War, resulting in the Treaty of Badajoz, the loss of Portuguese territory, in particular Olivenza, as well as ultimately setting the stage for the complete invasion of the Iberian Peninsula by French forces. The Spanish attack to Kingdom of Portugal started on the early morning of the 20 May 1801, and focused on the Portuguese border region that included the main Garrison Town and Fortifications of Elvas and the smaller fortified towns of Campo Maior, Olivença (Olivenza in Spanish) and Juromenha. Thus, the conflict became known as the \"War of the Oranges\". On 6 09 June 1801 Kingdom of Portugal agreed to the tenets of the Treaty of Badajoz. Kingdom of Portugal agreed to close its ports to English ships, to give commercial concessions to French First Republic, to cede Olivenza to Spain and to pay an indemnity. This led French First Republic to declare the Peace of Badajoz treaty cancelled, again marching on Kingdom of Portugal and invading it, starting the Peninsular War, that lasted from 1807 to 1810. After the Napoleonic Wars, and the Congress of Vienna, neither Spain nor Kingdom of Portugal gave back the territories acquired both in America (Eastern Missions) and the Peninsula (Olivença); the latter remaining an issue with the Portuguese government (see Olivenza)." .

ns1:53085 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Champions on Ice was a touring ice show in the United States of America. In 2012, Sergio Cánovas's new Champions on Ice began touring internationally, headlined by Evgeni Plushenko. Skaters who had a long association with the tour include Brian Boitano, Viktor Petrenko, Michelle Kwan, and Todd Eldredge." .

ns1:53134 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Coupe de France Final decided the winner of the 2013–14 Coupe de France, the 97th season of France's premier football cup. It was played on 03 May 2014 at the Stade de France in Saint-Denis, Paris. In the final, En Avant de Guingamp beat Stade Rennais F.C. 2–0 in a Derby Breton to win their second Coupe de France title. The match was Stade Rennais F.C.' sixth final, of which they had won two (1965 and 1971). Their most recent final was in 2009, which they lost 2-1 to En Avant de Guingamp. That was En Avant de Guingamp's second appearance in the final after their unsuccessful attempt in 1997 when they lost on penalties to Nice. In the next round, they won 2-0 at third-tier Boulogne, with goals from Anders Konradsen and Silvio Romero. Stade Rennais F.C. then beat Lille 2-0 at home, with goals from Kamil Grosicki and Romain Alessandrini. In their semi-final on 15 April, Stade Rennais F.C. beat second-tier Angers 3-2 at home, with Ola Toivonen, Grosicki and Jean Makoun scoring. En Avant de Guingamp, also of Ligue 1, entered into the Round of 64, where they played away to third-tier Bourg-Péronnas on 5 January and won via first-half goals from Claudio Beauvue and Mustapha Yatabaré. Goals from Grégory Cerdan and Mustapha Diallo sent En Avant de Guingamp into the quarter-finals, where they played AS Cannes and won 2-0 away again, through another Yatabaré brace. In the semi-final on 2014, En Avant de Guingamp played at home against their first top-flight opponents, Monaco. Jonathan Martins Pereira scored the first goal for En Avant de Guingamp with a volley from the edge of the area in the 37th minute, and Mustapha Yatabaré got the second in the 46th minute with a powerful downward header from Steeven Langil's left-wing cross." .

ns1:53153 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Zierikzee was a naval battle between a County of Flanders fleet and an allied Franco-County of Holland fleet which took place on 10 August 1304 and 11 August 1304. The battle, fought near the town of Zierikzee, ended in a Franco-Holland victory. After County of Flanders landings near Arnemuiden and Veere the troops of William fled to Middelburg which surrendered on 9 May. On the evening of 10 August 1304, the two fleets met on the Gouwe, back then a bay near Zierikzee. One week after the naval battle, on 18 August 1304 Philip IV himself was able to defeat the County of Flanders main army at the battle of Mons-en-Pévèle." .

ns1:53397 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "White Sun of the Desert (Russian: Белое солнце пустыни, romanized: Beloye solntse pustyni) is a 1970 'Eastern' or Eastern film of the Soviet Union. Its main theme song, \"Your Noble Highness Lady Fortune\" (Ваше благородие, госпожа Удача, music: Isaac Schwartz, lyrics: Bulat Okudzhava, performed by Pavel Luspekayev) became a hit. The setting is the east shore of the Caspian Sea (modern Turkmenistan) where the Red Army soldier Fyodor Sukhov has been fighting the Civil War in Russian Asia for a number of years. The director, Vladimir Motyl, said such films as Stagecoach and High Noon influenced him and he has described the film as being a \"cocktail\" of both an adventurous Russian folktale and a western. After the first version of the film was turned down by Mosfilm, Valentin Yezhov and Rustam Ibragimbekov were assigned to improve the script. The soundtrack to White Sun of the Desert is one of the most celebrated of Russian film. In 1998, the creators of the film were awarded the 1997 Russian Federation State Prize in Literature and Arts, nearly 30 years after the film left the silver screen. A Russian computer game was released based on the film. 2009) and Samara (2012) All crew members boarding Russian space flights are committed to watch \"White Sun of the Desert\" before the launch, and the names of Abdullah's wives are assigned to several craters on Venus. In 2008, Rustam Ibragimbekov announced that he had begun production on a White Sun of the Desert TV spinoff entitled \"White Sun of the Desert – Home\". Many popular sayings have entered the Russian from the film." .

ns1:53408 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Ambassador was an operation carried out by British Commandos on 14 July 1940–15 July 1940 within the context of the World War II. It was the second raid by the newly formed British Commandos and was focused upon the German-occupied Channel island of Guernsey. The raiding party consisted of 40 men from the newly formed No. 3 Commando under the command of Lieutenant Colonel John Durnford-Slater, and 100 men of No.11 Independent Company under Major Ronnie Tod. The preliminary operations would be undertaken to gather necessary intelligence prior to a raid by 140 men which would land on the island of Guernsey and attack the airfield with the purpose of destroying aircraft and buildings, as well as capturing or killing members of the garrison. The units that were chosen for the raid were 'H' Troop from No. 3 Commando and No. No. 3 Commando, under Lieutenant Colonel John Durnford-Slater, had only just been raised, having completed its recruitment on 5 July, and had not yet begun training, while No. 11 Independent Company under the command of Major Ronnie Tod had been raised earlier in June and had a few weeks earlier undertaken Operation Collar, which had been a hastily organised and largely unsuccessful raid on Boulogne. The original plan had been for the raid to be carried out on the night of 12/13 July, however, at the last moment it was put back to 14/15 July 1940." .

ns1:53434 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018–19 Women's Big Bash League season or WBBL|04 was the fourth season of Women's Big Bash League (WBBL), the semi-professional women's cricket Twenty20 domestic cricket competition in Australia. In the two semi-finals at Drummoyne Oval on 19 January, the Brisbane Heat eliminated the Sydney Thunder before the Sydney Sixers knocked out the Melbourne Renegades. A new record for the highest attendance at a standalone WBBL match was set on 5 January when the Brisbane Heat played the Adelaide Strikers at Harrup Park in front of 5,650 spectators. Cricket Australia reported over 3.8 million highlight views on their YouTube channel and almost 10 million minutes viewed on the Cricket Network's live stream, with a 38 per cent rise of minutes viewed in Australia compared to the previous season." .

ns1:53995 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2004 San Marino Grand Prix (officially the Gran Premio Foster's di San Marino 2004) was a Formula One motor race held on 25 April 2004 at the Autodromo Enzo e Dino Ferrari, Imola. The 62-lap race was the fourth round of the 2004 Formula One season and was won by Michael Schumacher driving a Ferrari. Qualifying gave BAR's Jenson Button his maiden career pole position, ahead of Michael Schumacher, Juan Pablo Montoya, Rubens Barrichello and Ralf Schumacher." .

ns1:54015 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Eurovision Song Contest 1978 was the 23rd edition of the annual Eurovision Song Contest. It was held on 22 April 1978 in Paris. The contest was won by Izhar Cohen & the Alphabeta who represented Israel with the song \"A-Ba-Ni-Bi\". The event took place in Paris, the capital and largest city of France, with the host venue being the Palais des congrès de Paris which is a concert venue, convention centre and shopping mall in the 17th arrondissement of Paris. Jean Vallée also returned, eight years after representing Belgium in 1970, while Norbert Niedermeyer, who had represented Austria in 1972 as part of the band The Milestones, was back as a member of the Austrian band Springtime." .

ns1:54037 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Asian Junior Badminton Championships – women's doubles tournament of the 2016 Asian Junior Badminton Championships was held from 13 July–17 July 2016 at the CPB Badminton and Sports Science Training Center in Bangkok, Thailand. Du Yue with her new partner Xu Ya won the 2016 title after beat their compatriot Ni Bowen and Zhou Chaomin in straight game 21–15, 21–16." .

ns1:54579 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The April 2010 Maoist attack in Dantewada was an 06 April 2010 ambush by Naxalite–Maoist insurgency from the Communist Party of India (Maoist) near Chintalnar village in Dantewada district, Chhattisgarh, India, leading to the killing of 76 CRPF policemen and 8 Maoists — the deadliest attack by the Maoists on Indian security forces. The original movement had weakened considerably by the 1970s, but it started spreading across a swathe of India's poorest districts, the so-called red corridor, a tribal belt running through the mainly Santhal regions of West Bengal, Jharkhand, Orissa, and then through the Gond and other tribal regions of Andhra Pradesh, into the Bastar region of Chhattisgarh. Before the incident at Chintalnar in Dantewada, in March 2007, Maoists were also blamed for the killing of 55 policemen in Chhattisgarh. India's home minister P Chidambaram said that it appeared that the forces had \"walked\" into a rebel ambush by returning to the police base via the same route they had come." .

ns1:54814 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2019 Eliteserien was the 74th season of top-tier association football in Norway. The season began on 30 March 2019 and was scheduled to end 30 November 2019, not including play-off matches, but due to Rosenborg BK competing in the Europa League the final matchday was moved to 1 December. Viking FK and Mjøndalen IF joined as the promoted clubs from the 2018 1. divisjon. Molde FK won their fourth title, with two matches to spare following a 4–0 home win against Strømsgodset Toppfotball on 2019. On 10 November, Molde FK were confirmed as Eliteserien champions following their 4–0 home win against Strømsgodset Toppfotball in the 28th round. The promoted teams were Viking FK and Mjøndalen IF, returning to the top flight after an absence of one and three years respectively. The 14th-placed team, Lillestrøm SK takes part in a two-legged play-off against Start, the winners of the 2019 1. divisjon, to decide who will play in the 2020 Eliteserien." .

ns1:55782 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2003 LG Cup was a professional ranking snooker tournament that took place between 04 October 2003 and 12 October 2003 at the Preston Guild Hall in Preston, England. 2002 LG Cup Chris Small was the number 1 seed with World Champion Mark Williams seeded 2." .

ns1:56128 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Austrian Grand Prix (formally known as the Formula 1 Großer Preis von Österreich 2015) was a Formula One auto racing held on 21 June 2015 at the Red Bull Ring in Spielberg, Austria. The race was the eighth round of the 2015 Formula One World Championship, and marked the 29th running of the Austrian Grand Prix and the 28th time it had been held as a round of the Formula One World Championship. Mercedes driver Nico Rosberg entered as the defending race winner, while his teammate, Lewis Hamilton, entered with a seventeen-point lead over him in the Drivers' Championship standings. Going into the race, Lewis Hamilton was leading teammate Nico Rosberg by 17 points, having won the 2015 Canadian Grand Prix. At the first session, both Mercedes drivers were fastest, with Nico Rosberg being three-tenths of a second faster than teammate Lewis Hamilton." .

ns1:56274 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Singapore Grand Prix (officially the Formula 1 2018 Singapore Airlines Singapore Grand Prix) was a Formula One motor race held on 16 September 2018 at the Marina Bay Street Circuit in Marina Bay, Singapore. The race was the 15th round of the 2018 Formula One World Championship and marked the 19th running of the Singapore Grand Prix and the 11th time the race had been held at Marina Bay. Mercedes driver Lewis Hamilton entered the round with a 30-point lead over Sebastian Vettel in the Drivers' Championship." .

ns1:56579 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Inauguration of James Buchanan as the 15th President of the United States was held on Wednesday, 04 March 1857, at the East Portico of the United States Capitol in Washington, D.C. This was the 18th United States presidential inauguration and marked the commencement of the only four-year term of both James Buchanan as President and John C. Breckinridge as Vice President. James Buchanan was seen to hold a whispered conversation with Roger B. Taney (Chief Justice of the Supreme Court) during the festivities." .

ns1:57210 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Apple Pencil is a line of wireless stylus digital pen accessories designed and developed by Apple Inc. for use with supported iPad tablets. The first generation Apple Pencil was announced alongside the first iPad Pro on September 9, 2015. The second generation Apple Pencil was announced on October 30, 2018 alongside the third generation iPad Pro. The Apple Pencil uses an STMicroelectronics STM32L151UCY6 Ultra-low-power 32-bit RISC ARM-based Cortex-M3 MCU running at 32 MHz with 64 KB of flash memory, a Bosch Sensortech BMA280 3‐axis accelerometer and a Cambridge Silicon Radio (Qualcomm) CSR1012A05 Bluetooth Smart IC for its Bluetooth connection to the iPad." .

ns1:57222 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Bal des débutantes, also known simply as \"le Bal\" (or, previously, the \"Crillon Ball\"), is a debutante ball and fashion event held annually in November that brings together 20–25 girls aged 16 to 22 from about a dozen countries. From 1992 to 2012, le Bal was organized at the Hôtel de Crillon. The Hôtel Raphael was the hotel of le Bal in 2013 and 2014 and the event was held in the Palais de Chaillot in 2015, overlooking the Eiffel Tower. Le Bal was held at the hotel The Peninsula Paris in 2016 and 2017, and in 2018 and 2019 at the Shangri La Hotel in Paris." .

ns1:57470 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016–17 Ligue 2 (referred to as the Domino's Ligue 2 for sponsorship reasons) season is the 78th season since its establishment. There are 20 clubs in the league, with three promoted teams from Championnat National replacing the three teams that were relegated from Ligue 2 following the 2015–16 Ligue 2. RC Lens drew the highest average home league attendance (28,996), followed by RC Strasbourg (17,013) and Stade de Reims (10,403). The 2016–17 season will see the return of a relegation play-off between the 18th placed 2016–17 Ligue 1 team and the 3rd placed team in the Ligue 2 in a two-legged confrontation." .

ns1:57708 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Tacky's War, or Tacky's Rebellion, was an uprising of Akan slaves (then referred to as Coromantee but originally from around Kromantsie in Central Region of Ghana) and other Akan tribes including Ashanti, Fanti, Nzema and Akyem. This uprising occurred in Jamaica from May to 31 July 1760 and was led by a Fanti royal and warlord called Tacky (Takyi). It was the most dangerous slave rebellion in the British Empire until the Baptist War of Samuel Sharpe in 1831–32, which also occurred in the Colony of Jamaica. Sometime before daybreak on Easter Monday, 07 April 1760, Tacky and his followers began the revolt and easily took over the Frontier and Trinity plantations while killing their masters. It is estimated that the destruction caused by Tacky's Revolt, and other spin-off rebellions, cost the Colony of Jamaica over £100,000, which is many millions in today's currency." .

ns1:57757 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Zhuge Liang's Southern Campaign, also known as the War of Pacification in Nanzhong, was a military campaign which took place in 225 during the early Three Kingdoms period (220–280) of China. It was led by Zhuge Liang, the Imperial Chancellor of the state of Shu Han, against opposing forces in the Nanzhong region (covering parts of present-day Yunnan, Guizhou and southern Sichuan). The Shu army travelled along a water route from Anshang County (安上縣; present-day Pingshan County, Sichuan) to Yuexi/Yuesui Commandery and entered the Nanzhong region. Zhuge Liang then ordered Ma Zhong to lead troops southeast from Bodao County (僰道縣; present-day Yibin, Sichuan) to attack Zangke Commandery, and Li Hui to lead troops southwest from Pingyi County (平夷縣; northeast of present-day Bijie, Guizhou) to attack Jianning Commandery. Jianning 建寧), Yongchang (永昌), Zangke (牂柯) and Yuexi/Yuesui (越巂), Zhuge Liang reformed the administrative divisions to create another two commanderies, Yunnan (雲南) and Xinggu (興古), to improve the quality of the local governments in the Nanzhong region." .

ns1:57838 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Tour of California was the ninth running of the Tour of California cycling stage race. It was held from 11 May 2014–18, and rated as a 2.HC event on the 2013–14 UCI America Tour. It began in Sacramento and finished in Thousand Oaks. Other riders competing in the race are Taylor Phinney, Thor Hushovd, Janier Acevedo, Tom Boonen, Mark Cavendish, Jens Voigt, Adam Yates and Peter Sagan. In the 2014 Tour of California, 5 jerseys are awarded." .

ns1:57887 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The naval Battle of Dungeness took place on 30 November 1652 (10 December 1652 Gregorian calendar), during the First Anglo-Dutch War near the cape of Dungeness in Kent. In September 1652 the government of the Commonwealth of England, the Council of State, mistakenly believing that the Dutch Republic after their defeat at the Battle of the Kentish Knock would desist from bringing out a fleet so late in the season, sent away ships to the Mediterranean and the Baltic." .

ns1:58264 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Italian battleship Roma, named after two previous ships and the city of Rome, was the third Littorio-class battleship of Italy's Regia Marina (Royal Navy). The construction of both Italian battleship Roma and her sister ship Impero was due to rising tensions around the world and the navy's fear that only two Littorios, even in company with older pre-World War I battleships, would not be enough to counter the British and French Mediterranean Fleets. Italian battleship Roma was commissioned into the Regia Marina on 14 June 1942, but a severe fuel shortage in Italy at that time prevented her from being deployed; instead, along with her sister ships Vittorio Veneto and Littorio-class, she was used to bolster the anti-aircraft defenses of various Italian cities. The first attack failed, but the second dealt Italia (ex-Littorio-class) and Italian battleship Roma much damage. Laid down nearly four years after Vittorio Veneto and Littorio-class, Italian battleship Roma was able to incorporate a few design improvements. Italian battleship Roma's keel was laid by the Italian shipbuilder Cantieri Riuniti dell'Adriatico on 18 September 1938, and she was launched on 9 June 1940. After just over two years of fitting-out, the new battleship was commissioned into the Regia Marina on 14 June 1942. Although Italian battleship Roma took part in training exercises and was moved to various bases including Taranto, Naples, and La Spezia, in the next year, she did not go on any combat missions as the Italian Navy was desperately short of fuel. Italian battleship Roma and her two sisters were moved from Taranto to Naples, on 12 November, in response to the Allied invasion of North Africa; while en route, the three battleships were attacked by the British submarine HMS Umbra, though no hits were made. Two days later, Italian battleship Roma was transferred with Vittorio Veneto and Littorio-class to La Spezia, where she became the flagship of the Regia Marina. Italian battleship Roma reached the city on 1 July, and returned to La Spezia, on 13 August, once repairs were complete. Along with many of the principal units of the Italian fleet—including Vittorio Veneto and Italia (the ex-Littorio-class)—the cruisers Eugenio di Savoia, Raimondo Montecuccoli, and Emanuele Filiberto Duca d'Aosta, and eight destroyers—Italian battleship Roma sailed from La Spezia with Adone Del Cima as captain and also as the flagship of Admiral Carlo Bergamini on 9 September 1943, a day after the proclamation of the 1943 Italian armistice." .

ns1:58317 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Fevrálʹskaya revolyútsiya), known in Soviet historiography as the February Bourgeois Democratic Revolution and sometimes as the March Revolution, was the first of Russian Revolution which took place in Russia in 1917. The main events of the revolution took place in and near Petrograd (present-day Saint Petersburg), the then-capital of Russia, where long-standing discontent with the monarchy erupted into mass protests against food rationing on 23 February Old Style (08 March 1917 New Style). Three days later Tsar Nicholas II of Russia abdicated, ending Romanov dynastic rule and the Russian Empire. The first major event of the Russian Revolution was the February Revolution, which was a chaotic affair, caused by the culmination of over a century of civil and military unrest between the common people and the Tsar and aristocratic landowners. \"In the seventeen months of the 'Tsarina's rule', from September 1915 to 1917, Russia had four Prime Ministers, five Ministers of the Interior, three Foreign Ministers, three War Ministers, three Ministers of Transport and four Ministers of Agriculture. Elias fyhr took the opportunity to attack the Russian Empire. By 1917, the majority Petersburgers had lost faith in the Russian Empire. On 28 February, Rodzianko invited the Grand Duke Paul Alexandrovich and Grand Duke Kirill Vladimirovich to put their signatures to the drafting of the Manifesto, in which Emperor Nicholas II of Russia was recommended to introduce the constitutional system in Russia. The chairmen believed that the February Revolution was a \"Bourgeois revolution\" about bringing capitalist development to Russia instead of socialism. As the left moved further left in Russia over the course of 1917, the Kadets became the main conservative party. In the spring of 1917, 700 soviets were established across Russia, equalling about a third of the population, representing the proletariat and their interests." .

ns1:58727 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "It took place in Bab El Oued, a traditionally white working-class quarter of Algiers, from 23 March 1962 to 06 April 1962. The OAS was an organization of hard-line European \"Pieds Noirs\" living in (the French Algeria) Algeria who were opposed to the cease-fire announced by French president Charles de Gaulle on 19 23 March 1962 between French forces and the Front de libération nationale (FLN) forces fighting for Algerian War of Independence. The Battle of Bab El Oued was principally a battle between the French Gendarmerie Mobile and the OAS Commando Delta." .

ns1:59018 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1974 Tour de Suisse was the 38th edition of the Tour de Suisse cycle race and was held from 13 June to 21 June 1974. The race started in Leuggern and finished in Olten. The race was won by Eddy Merckx of the Molteni-Campagnolo team." .

ns1:59069 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "A saltire, also called Saint Andrew's Cross or the crux decussata, is a heraldic symbol in the form of a diagonal cross, like the shape of the letter X in Roman type. The term saltirewise or in saltire refers to heraldry charges arranged as a diagonal cross. As suggested by the name saltire (\"stirrup\"; in French: sautoir, in German: Schragen), the ordinary in its early use was not intended as representing a Christian cross symbol. The Flag of Scotland, called The Saltire or Saint Andrew's Cross, is a blue field with a white saltire. That flag, sometimes called \"Union Gascona\" (Gascon Union), contains the Andrew the Apostle's cross, the patron saint of Bordeaux and the red color of English kingdom, who reigned over Gascony from 12th to mid-15th century. Reference to the saltire as \"Andrew the Apostle's Cross\" is made by the Parliament of Scotland (where Andrew had been adopted as patron saint) in 1385, in a decree to the effect that every Scottish and French soldier (fighting against the English under Richard II) \"shall have a sign before and behind, namely a white St. Andrew's Cross\". The diagonal cross (decussate cross) or X mark is called \"saltire\" in heraldry and vexillological contexts." .

ns1:59354 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 FIFA Club World Cup (officially known as the FIFA Club World Cup Japan 2016 presented by Alibaba YunOS Auto for sponsorship reasons) was the 13th edition of the FIFA Club World Cup, a FIFA-organised international club association football tournament between the winners of the six continental confederations, as well as the host nation's league champions. Real Madrid CF won their second Club World Cup, defeating hosts Kashima Antlers 4–2 after extra time in the 2016 FIFA Club World Cup Final. However, no such decision regarding the 2015 FIFA Club World Cup–2016 host was made until 2015 FIFA Club World Cup. The following countries expressed an interest in bidding to host the tournament: Japan was officially confirmed as hosts of the 2015 FIFA Club World Cup and 2016 tournaments on 23 April 2015 FIFA Club World Cup. On 2016, Suita Stadium in Osaka and International Stadium Yokohama in Yokohama were named as the two venues of the tournament. The system was used for the first time when a penalty was awarded by referee Viktor Kassai in the first half of the semi-2016 FIFA Club World Cup Final between Atlético Nacional and Kashima Antlers after a review of video replay. A draw was held on 2016, 11:00 CEST (UTC+2), at the FIFA headquarters in Zürich, Switzerland, to determine the positions in the bracket for the three teams which enter the quarter-finals. If a match was tied after normal playing time: On 2016, the FIFA Executive Committee agreed that the competition would be part of the International Football Association Board's trial to allow a fourth substitute to be made during extra time." .

ns1:59662 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015–16 Russian Premier League was the 24th season of the premier association football competition in Russia since the dissolution of the Soviet Union and the 14th under the current Russian Premier League name. FC Zenit Saint Petersburg came into the season as defending champions of the 2014–15 Russian Premier League. After the 2014–15 Russian Premier League, FC Torpedo Moscow and FC Arsenal Tula were relegated to the 2015–16 Russian National Football League. They were replaced by two clubs who qualified automatically from the 2014–15 Russian National Football League, FC Krylia Sovetov Samara and FC Anzhi Makhachkala. FC Anzhi Makhachkala won 3–0 on aggregate and remained in the 2016–17 Russian Premier League. On 3 September 2015, FC Anzhi Makhachkala was banned from registering new players for debts to FC Zenit Saint Petersburg for Igor Denisov's transfer fee. On 9 September 2015, FC Dinamo Moscow was banned from registering new players for debts to FC Zenit Saint Petersburg for Igor Denisov's and Tomáš Hubočan's transfer fees and to FC Anzhi Makhachkala for Igor Denisov's, Vladimir Gabulov's and Christopher Samba's transfer fees. On 24, FC Kuban Krasnodar was banned from registering new players for debts to FC Lokomotiv Moscow for Sergei Tkachyov's transfer fee. On 15 November 2015, the ban was re-confirmed for debts to FC Krylia Sovetov Samara for Anton Sosnin's transfer fee and to PFC CSKA Moscow for Svyatoslav Georgiyevsky's transfer fee." .

ns1:59695 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Mexico City ePrix (officially the 2017 FIA Formula E Julius Baer Mexico City ePrix) was a Formula E electric car race held on 01 April 2017 before a crowd of 36,000 people at the Autódromo Hermanos Rodríguez in Mexico City, Mexico. It was the fourth round of the 2016–17 Formula E season, and the second Mexico City ePrix. It was the fourth of twelve scheduled single-seater electric car races of the 2016–17 season, the second Mexico City ePrix, and was held on 01 April 2017 at the Autódromo Hermanos Rodríguez. Before the race, e.Dams-Renault driver Sébastien Buemi led the Drivers' Championship with 75 points, ahead of Lucas Di Grassi in second and third-placed Nico Prost. After retiring from the preceding 2017 Buenos Aires ePrix, Bird said he would go into the Mexico City race with a positive attitude and felt there was a possibility of getting onto the podium if his team did well in qualifying." .

ns1:59972 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Second Macedonian War (200–197 BC) was fought between Macedon, led by Philip V of Macedon, and Rome, allied with Pergamon and Rhodes. In response, the Acarnanian League launched a raid on Attica, aided by Macedonian troops which they had received from Philip V. Shortly after this, King Attalus I arrived in Classical Athens with Rhodian ambassadors and convinced the Athenians, who had maintained strict neutrality since the end of the Chremonidean War, to declare war on Macedon. Attalus sailed off, bringing most of the Cycladic islands over to his side and sent embassies to the Aetolian League in the hope of bringing them into the war as well. In response to the Athenian declaration of war, Philip dispatched a force of 2,000 infantry and 200 cavalry under the command of Philokles to invade Attica and place the city of Classical Athens under siege. In light of reports from Laevinus and further embassies from Pergamon, Rhodes, and Classical Athens, the task of dealing with the troubles in Macedonia was allotted to Sulpicius. He sent his young son Perseus with a force to prevent the Romans and Dardanian Kingdom from advancing over the Šar Mountains into northern Macedonia. After a series of indecisive skirmishes (in one of which Philip was nearly captured), news came that the Dardanian Kingdom had invaded northern Macedonia, and the Macedonians withdrew secretly in the night. As these campaigns progressed, Damocritus, the strategos of the Aetolian League, had decided that it was time to join the war on the Roman side. In Rome, the new consuls took office on 15 March 199 BC, Publius Villius Tappulus was allotted Macedonia as his province. On the one hand, the Achaians were still at war with Sparta and they were allied to Macedonia, but on the other hand their new chief magistrate Aristaenus was pro-Roman and the Romans promised to give the city of Corinth to the League. When this embassy reached Rome, the Senate demanded that Philip surrender the \"fetters of Greece,\" Demetrias, Chalcis, and Corinth, but Philip's envoys claimed they had no permission to agree to this, so the war continued. Philip had to rush off almost immediately after the agreement of terms to deal with an invasion of Upper Macedonia by the Dardanian Kingdom. The initial background to the whole war had been the alliance of Antiochus III and Philip V against Ptolemy V and while the war had been raging in Greece, Antiochus III had completely defeated the Ptolemaic forces in Syria at the Battle of Panium." .

ns1:60091 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Aeroméxico Connect Flight 2431 (SLI2431/5D2431) was a Mexican domestic scheduled passenger flight bound for Mexico City that crashed on takeoff from General Guadalupe Victoria International Airport on 31 July 2018. The flight, number 2431, was operated by Aeroméxico Connect and was a regularly scheduled flight from General Guadalupe Victoria International Airport to Mexico City International Airport. After Republic retired its Embraer 190 aircraft from its operations, Republic then leased N167HQ to Aeroméxico Connect in 2014, who registered the aircraft as XA-GAL. The investigators conducted a series of flight tests in full flight simulators in Mexico and Brazil using the same weather conditions that existed at the time of the flight, with crews with similar levels of experience as the crew of Flight 2431." .

ns1:60136 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Helena, also known as the Attack on Helena, was a battle of the American Civil War fought on 04 July 1863, in Helena-West Helena, Arkansas. The battle was a failed Confederate States of America attempt to relieve pressure on the besieged city of Vicksburg. The Union victory at Helena-West Helena paved the way for the fall of Little Rock. Lieutenant-General Theophilus Holmes, commander of the Confederate States of America's District of Arkansas, planned a coordinated attack from three sides on the formidable Federal fortifications surrounding Helena-West Helena in order to relieve pressure on Vicksburg and to prevent Helena-West Helena being used as a base to attack further into Arkansas. Holmes, accompanied by Arkansas Governor Harris Flanagin, traveled to Helena-West Helena to take personal command of the attack. Major-General Benjamin Prentiss, in command of the Union's District of Eastern Arkansas, was headquartered at Helena-West Helena with approximately 20,000 troops. No longer threatened, Helena-West Helena became an important Union enclave on the Mississippi River and would become the base for the successful advance upon the Arkansas state capital later in the same year." .

ns1:60365 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "La ruleta de la fortuna or La ruleta de la suerte is the Spanish version of Wheel of Fortune The first incarnation ran from 1990 to 1992 in Antena 3, the second one from 1993 to 1997 in GSV TV, and then, after a 9 year hiatus, a revival has been made on Antena 3 beginning in 2006." .

ns1:60415 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "It took place on 02 May 1964 at Wembley Stadium and was contested between West Ham United F.C. and Preston North End F.C.. West Ham, captained by Bobby Moore and managed by Ron Greenwood, won the match 3–2 to win the FA Cup for the first time. Second Division Preston led twice through Doug Holden and Alex Dawson respectively, with John Sissons and Geoff Hurst equalising for West Ham." .

ns1:60680 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 Superfinalen was the first edition of Superfinalen, an annual association football match contested by the winners of the previous season's Tippeligaen and Norwegian Cup competitions. It was played on 08 March 2009, at Telenor Arena in Bærum, between Stabæk Fotball and Vålerenga Fotball, winners of the 2008 Tippeligaen and 2008 Norwegian Football Cup respectively. A collaboration between the Football Association of Norway, Norsk Toppfotball, the incumbent league and cup champions, and the broadcaster TV 2, the match was seen as a test of the viability of a Super Cup in Norway. The game was a repeat of the 2008 Norwegian Football Cup Final, in which Vålerenga Fotball had defeated Stabæk Fotball 4–1. This time, however, it was Stabæk Fotball who won the match 3–1 after goals by Daniel Nannskog, Daigo Kobayashi and Pálmi Rafn Pálmason. The game also served as the official opening match of Telenor Arena, which became Stabæk Fotball's home ground from 2009 to 2011." .

ns1:60972 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Japan–Korea Treaty of 1905, also known as the Eulsa Treaty, Eulsa Unwilling Treaty or Japan–Korea Protectorate Treaty, was made between the Empire of Japan and the Korean Empire in 1905. In the metonymy Eulsa Treaty, the word Eulsa or Ulsa derives the Sexagenary Cycle's 42nd year of the Korean calendar, in which the treaty was signed. The treaty is identified by several names including Second Japan–Korea Convention (Japanese: 第二次日韓協約, Korean: 제2차 한일협약, 第二次韓日協約), Eulsa Restriction Treaty (Korean: 을사늑약, 乙巳勒約), Eulsa Protection Treaty (Japanese: 乙巳保護条約, Korean: 을사보호조약), and Korea Protection Treaty (Japanese: 韓国保護条約). Following Imperial Japan's victory in the Russo-Japanese War, with its subsequent withdrawal of Russian influence, and the Taft–Katsura Agreement, in which the United States allegedly agreed not to interfere with Japan in matters concerning Korea, the Japanese government sought to formalize its sphere of influence over the Korean Peninsula. Delegates of both Empires met in Seoul to resolve differences in matters pertaining to Korea's future foreign policy; however, with the Gyeongbokgung under occupation by Japanese troops, and the Imperial Japanese Army stationed at strategic locations throughout Korea, the Korean side was at a distinct disadvantage in the discussions. On 9 17 November 1905, Itō Hirobumi arrived in Hanseong and gave a letter from the Emperor of Japan to Gojong, Emperor of Korea, asking him to sign the treaty. On 15 17 November 1905, he ordered Japanese troops to encircle the Korean imperial palace and threatened the emperor in order to force him to agree to the treaty. On 17 November 1905, Ito and Japanese Field Marshal Hasegawa Yoshimichi entered the Jungmyeongjeon Hall, a Russian-designed building that was once part of Deoksu Palace, to persuade Gojong to agree, but he refused. The Agreement gave Imperial Japan complete responsibility for Korea's foreign affairs, and placed all trade through Korean ports under Imperial Japanese supervision. The provisions of the treaty took effect on 17 November 1905, and it laid the foundation for the Japan–Korea Treaty of 1907, and subsequent annexation of Korea in 1910. The treaty was deemed to have gone into effect after it received the signature of five Korean ministers: Emperor Gojong of Korea did not assent or sign the treaty. In a joint statement on June 23, 2005, officials of South Korea and North Korea reiterated their stance that the Eulsa treaty is null and void on a claim of coercion by the Japanese. As of 2010, South Korea was seizing property and other assets from the descendants of people who have been identified as pro-Japanese collaborators (Chinilpa) at the time of the treaty." .

ns1:60987 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The International 2015 (TI5) was the fifth edition of The International, an annual Dota 2 esports championship tournament, which took place at the KeyArena in Seattle. The tournament awarded one of the biggest prize pools in esports tournament history, at over US$18 million, with the winning team, Evil Geniuses, being awarded over $6 million. Valve Corporation announced The International 2015 in 2015, again taking place at the KeyArena in Seattle." .

ns1:61043 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Trofeo Alfredo Binda-Comune di Cittiglio was the 20th running of the Trofeo Alfredo Binda-Comune di Cittiglio, a women's road bicycle racing in Italy. It was the third event of the 2018 UCI Women's World Tour season and was held on 18 March 2018 2017. The race started in Gavirate and finished in Cittiglio, on the outskirts of Lago Maggiore in Northwest Italy. Chantal Blaak won the sprint for second place at 23 seconds ahead of Marianne Vos." .

ns1:61116 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 70th running of the 1986 Tour of Flanders cycling race was held on 06 April 1986. It was won by Dutch rider Adrie van der Poel in a four-man sprint before Ireland's Sean Kelly. Bauer dropped Planckaert on the Muur van Geraardsbergen, but was joined by Sean Kelly, Adrie van der Poel and Jean-Philippe Vandenbrande at 7 km from the finish. The race started in Sint-Niklaas and finished in Meerbeke (Ninove) – totaling 274 km." .

ns1:61337 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Thiepval Ridge was the first large offensive of the Reserve Army (Lieutenant General Hubert Gough), during the Battle of the Somme on the Western Front of World War I during the World War I. The battle was fought on a front from Courcelette in the east, near the Albert–Bapaume road to Thiepval and the Schwaben Redoubt (Schwaben-Feste) in the west, which overlooked the German defences further north in the Ancre valley, the rising ground towards Beaumont-Hamel and Serre beyond. Gough, ordered the attack for 26 September at 12:35 p.m., to push the Germans off the high ground of the Thiepval Ridge, from Courcelette 6,000 yd (5,500 m) west to Schwaben Redoubt, by the Canadian Corps (Lieutenant-General Julian Byng, 1st Viscount Byng of Vimy) and II Corps, each with two divisions in the attack. German accounts of the battle conclude that the break-ins north-west of Courcelette and just east of Thiepval led to the defeat." .

ns1:61381 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Pirano (also known as the Battle of Grado, Friuli-Venezia Giulia) on 22 February 1812 was a minor naval action of the Adriatic campaign of the Napoleonic Wars fought between a British and a French ship of the line in the vicinity of the towns of Piran and Grado, Friuli-Venezia Giulia in Adriatic Sea. Rivoli departed Venice on 21 22 February 1812 under the command of Commodore Jean-Baptiste Barré, accompanied by five smaller escort ships, the 16-gun brigs Mercurio and Eridano, of the navy of the Kingdom of Italy, the 8-gun brig Mamelouck and two small gunboats, strung out in an improvised line of battle." .

ns1:61453 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 UCI Road World Championships – women's time trial of the 2013 UCI Road World Championships took place on 24 September 2013 in the region of Tuscany, Italy. Ellen van Dijk from the Netherlands lived up her expectations as main favourite and won the time trial by dominating the race, beating perennial podium finisher Linda Villumsen and the surprising American Carmen Small. Ellen van Dijk, the Dutch National time trial Champion and the number 5 in the 2012 UCI Road World Championships – Women's time trial was the absolute favourite. The Dutch Ellen van Dijk was the fastest from the start by riding 20 second faster than Linda Villumsen and 24 seconds faster than Carmen Small at the first time point." .

ns1:61596 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The European Tour 2015/2016 – Event 1 (also known as the 2015 Kaspersky Riga Masters) was a professional minor-ranking snooker tournament which took place between 29 July 2015 and 02 August 2015 at the Arena Riga in Riga, Latvia." .

ns1:61642 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 World Snooker Championship (also referred to as the 2011 Betfred.com World Snooker Championship for the purposes of sponsorship) was a professional snooker tournament that took place between 16 April 2011 and 02 May 2011 at the Crucible Theatre in Sheffield, England. The preliminary qualifying rounds for the tournament took place on 2011 at the World Snooker Academy in Sheffield. The qualifying rounds 1–4 for the tournament took place between 2011 and 2011 at the World Snooker Academy in Sheffield." .

ns1:61663 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 World Rowing Championships were World Rowing Championships that were held from 28 August 2011 to 04 September 2011 at Lake Bled in the Slovanian city of Bled. The annual week-long rowing regatta is organized by World Rowing Federation (FISA), and held at the end of the northern hemisphere summer." .

ns1:61757 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 Tour of Qatar was the ninth edition of the Tour of Qatar cycling stage race. It was rated as a 2.1 event on the UCI Asia Tour, and was held from 07 February 2010 to 12 February 2010, in Qatar. Almost straight away, when racing got underway, a group of two, Geert Steurs and Wouter Mol attacked and immediately got a few minutes over the peloton. Tom Boonen won his 16th career stage at the Tour of Qatar." .

ns1:61797 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2002 UEFA Women's Cup Final was a football match that took place on 23 May 2002 at the Waldstadion, Frankfurt between Umeå IK of Sweden and 1. FFC Frankfurt of Germany. The match was the final of the 2001–02 UEFA Women's Cup, the first edition of the UEFA Women's Cup/Women's Champions League. Frankfurt won the single leg final 2–0." .

ns1:62071 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012–13 OFC Champions League was the 12th edition of the Oceanian Club Championship, Oceania's premier club association football tournament organized by the Oceania Football Confederation (OFC), and the 7th season under the current OFC Champions League name. Auckland City FC became the first team to win three consecutive title after defeating Waitakere United in an all-New Zealand 2013 OFC Champions League Final. The preliminary stage matches were played in Tonga from 01 May 2012 to 8 01 May 2012. Auckland City FC won 7–1 on aggregate and advanced to the 2013 OFC Champions League Final. The 2013 OFC Champions League Final was played at Arena 2 of Mount Smart Stadium in Auckland, New Zealand on 19 May 2013." .

ns1:62469 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The First Siege of Zaragoza (also called Saragossa) was a bloody struggle in the Peninsular War (1807–1814). In North Eastern Spain Marshall Bessières assigned General Lefebvre-Desnouettes to quell the revolt in Aragon. Lefebvre reached Zaragoza on 15 June 1808. Palafox raised an additional force of 5,000 troops but these were defeated at Épila on the 23 15 June 1808–24 15 June 1808 and Palafox returned to Zaragoza with only an additional 1,000 troops. Starting from midnight on 30 15 June 1808 thirty siege guns, four mortars, and twelve howitzers opened fire on Zaragoza and kept firing continuously. This assault became famous for the story of the Maid of Zaragoza: Agustina de Aragón. Palafox's resistance made him a national hero, a glory he shared with Agustina de Aragón and many other ordinary civilians. Zaragoza would endure a second, longer, more famous siege starting in late December. Polish general Chłopicki commended col. Konopka for the decision to not fight Spanish civilians and giving up Zaragoza's downtown when the French couldn't break through and secure it (which basically ended the first siege). Chłopicki, who later lead the charge of Polish troops during the second siege of Zaragoza, also forbade his troops to fight Spanish civilians, unless directly attacked (thus enormously upsetting French commanders, like gen. Foy). The siege was portrayed in the 1950 Spanish film Agustina of Aragon." .

ns1:62567 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Music Box Tour was a concert tour in 1993 by American singer-songwriter Mariah Carey, in support of her Diamond-certified album, Music Box (1993). The outing started on 03 November 1993, in Miami, Florida, and ended on 10 December 1993, in New York City. Carey would avoid North America on her next two tours, the 1996 Daydream World Tour and the 1998 Butterfly World Tour, and would not tour the continental United States again until seven years later during the 2000 Rainbow World Tour." .

ns1:62625 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Tsingtao, sometimes spelled the Siege of Tsingtau, was the attack on the German port of Tsingtao (now Qingdao) in China during World War I by Japan and the United Kingdom. The siege was waged against Imperial Germany between 27 August 1914 and 07 November 1914. The siege was the first encounter between Japanese and German forces, the first Anglo-Japanese operation of the war, and the only major land battle in the Asian and Pacific theatre of World War I during World War I. After two German missionaries were killed in the Juye Incident in 1897, China was forced to agree to the Kiautschou Bay concession in Shantung (now Shandong) to Germany in 1898 on a 99-year lease." .

ns1:62803 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1972 Coupe de France Final was a association football match held at Parc des Princes, Paris on 04 June 1972, that saw Olympique de Marseille defeat SC Bastia 2–1 thanks to goals by Didier Couécou and Josip Skoblar." .

ns1:63158 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1983 Family Circle Cup was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor clay courts at the Sea Pines Resort on Hilton Head Island in the United States of America that was part of the 1983 Virginia Slims World Championship Series. It was the 11th edition of the tournament and was held from 04 April 1983 through 10 April 1983." .

ns1:63238 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1982 Amstel Gold Race was the 17th edition of the annual road bicycle racing \"Amstel Gold Race\", held on Sunday 24 April 1982, in the Dutch province of Limburg. The race stretched 237 kilometres, with the start in Heerlen and the finish in Meerssen. There was a total of 152 competitors, and 39 cyclists finished the race." .

ns1:63254 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Sanremo Music Festival 1969 was the 19th annual Sanremo Music Festival, held at the Sanremo Casino in Sanremo, province of Imperia between 30 January 1969 and 01 February 1969. The winners of the Festival were Bobby Solo and Iva Zanicchi with the song \"Zingara\"." .

ns1:63284 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Q-School 2016 – Event 1 was the first of two qualifying tournaments for the 2016/17 snooker season. It took place from 11 to 16 May 2016 at Meadowside Centre in Burton upon Trent, England. The four qualifying spots were won by Fang Xiongman, Christopher Keogan, Cao Yupeng and Chen Zhe who beat Daniel Womersley, Marc Davis, Joe Roberts and David Lilley respectively in the finals of their draw. Former professionals in the tournament include former world number 10 Tony Drago as well as Cao Yupeng, Alex Davies, Alexander Ursenbacher, John Astley, Joel Walker, Gerard Greene, Steven Hallworth, Andy Hicks, Jamie O'Neill, Lucky Vatnani, Alex Borg, Barry Pinches, Adam Duffy, Peter Lines, Chen Zhe, Jordan Brown, Lyu Haotian and 2016 Ladies World Champion Reanne Evans who was attempting to regain a place on the main tour for the first time since 2011." .

ns1:63290 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Second Battle of Fallujah—code-named Operation Al-Fajr (Arabic: الفجر \"the dawn\") and Operation Phantom Fury—was a joint American, Iraqi government, and British offensive in November and 23 December 2004, considered the highest point of conflict in Fallujah during the Iraq War. The second battle was the bloodiest battle of the entire Iraq War for American troops, and is notable for being the first major engagement of the Iraq War fought solely against insurgents rather than the forces of the former Ba'athist Iraqi government, which was deposed in 2003. In February 2004, control of Fallujah and the surrounding area in the Al Anbar Governorate was transferred from the U.S. 82nd Airborne Division to the 1st Marine Division. Fallujah was occupied by virtually every insurgent group in Iraq: al-Qaeda in Iraq (AQI), Islamic Army of Iraq (IAI), Ansar al-Sunna, Army of Mohammed (AOM), the Army of the Mujahedeen and the Secret Islamic Army of Iraq. One report states that both offensives, Operation Vigilant Resolve and Operation Phantom Fury, created 200,000 internally displaced persons who are still living elsewhere in Iraq. Subsequent U.S. military operations against insurgent positions were ineffective at drawing out insurgents into another open battle, and by September 2006, the situation had deteriorated to the point that the Al-Anbar province that contained Fallujah was reported to be in total insurgent control by the U.S. Marine Corps, with the exception of only pacified Fallujah, but now with an insurgent-plagued Ramadi. Al Qaeda-linked Sunni insurgents from the Islamic State in Iraq and the Levant subsequently took over Fallujah and parts of Ramadi in early 2014 and was reclaimed by the Iraqi Army and Special Operations Units in June 2016. Years after the battle, medical research teams discovered an increase in infant mortality, cancer, and congenital anomalies or birth defects among children born in Fallujah." .

ns1:63324 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "New York's Village Halloween Parade is an annual holiday parade and street pageant presented on the night of every Halloween in the Greenwich Village neighborhood of Lower Manhattan. The Village Halloween Parade, initiated in 1973 by Greenwich Village puppeteer and mask maker Ralph Lee, is the world's largest Halloween parade and the only nighttime parade in New York City. In addition, there are some commercial Halloween parade floats. In 1973, an informal parade of puppets for children was organized by Ralph Lee of the Mettawee River Theatre Company around his residence of artists in the Westbeth Artists Community. The new management introduced more adult elements into the event including a Halloween ball after the parade. In 1990, New York University and Manhattan Community Board 2 begin hosting an unrelated Children's Halloween Parade in Greenwich Village in the afternoons in Washington Square. The parade was cancelled for the first time in the aftermath of Hurricane Sandy in 2012, which had heavily damaged coastal New York City and had left Greenwich Village without electricity during the parade time." .

ns1:63443 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Lade (Greek: Ναυμαχία τῆς Λάδης, Naumachia tēs Ladēs) was a naval battle which occurred during the Ionian Revolt, in 494 BC. It was fought between an alliance of the Ionia cities (joined by the Lesbians) and the Achaemenid Empire of Darius the Great, and resulted in a decisive victory for the Persians which all but ended the revolt. The Ionia fleet gathered at the island of Miletus, off the coast of Miletus. The Persians were uncertain of victory at Miletus, so attempted to persuade some of the Ionia contingents to defect. As the Persian and Ionia fleets met, the Samians sailed away from the battle, causing the collapse of the Ionia battle line. With the defeat at Miletus, the Ionian Revolt was all but ended. The Ionia cities then remained under Lydian rule until Lydia was in turn conquered by the nascent Achaemenid Empire of Cyrus the Great. The Ionia cities were joined in this battle by the Aeolians of Lesbos. Herodotus lists the number of ships provided by each state: Herodotus gives the order of the Ionia battle line as being, from east to west; Miletus—Priene—Myus—Teos—Chios—Erythraea—Phocaea—Lesbos—Samos. The Persian fleet may have been commanded by the veteran Median general Datis; Persian records seem to suggest that he was sent by Darius to Ionia at around about the time of Miletus. When the Persians arrived off the coast of Miletus and learned the number of Ionia ships, they began to worry that they would not be able to defeat the Greeks, and feared Darius's wrath should they fail. The whole west-wing of the Ionia battle line thus very quickly collapsed. The Persian fleet and army wintered at Miletus, before setting out in 493 BC to finally stamp out the last embers of the revolt. Although the cities of Ionia were undoubtedly harrowed in the aftermath, none seems to have suffered quite the fate of Miletus." .

ns1:63540 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Champion of Champions was a professional non-ranking snooker tournament that took place between 07 November 2016 and 12 November 2016 at the Ricoh Arena in Coventry, England. The breakdown of prize money for 2016 is shown below: Players qualified for the event by winning important tournaments since the previous Champion of Champions." .

ns1:63576 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1988 Taça de Portugal Final was the final match of the 1987–88 Taça de Portugal, the 48th season of the Taça de Portugal, the premier Portuguese association football cup competition organized by the Portuguese Football Federation (FPF). The match was played on 19 June 1988 at the Estádio Nacional in Oeiras, and opposed two Primeira Liga sides: Porto and Vitória S.C.. Porto defeated Vitória S.C. 1–0 to claim the Taça de Portugal for a sixth time. In Portugal, the final was televised live on RTP. As Porto claimed both league and cup double in the same season, cup runners-up Vitória S.C. faced their cup final opponents in the 1988 Supertaça Cândido de Oliveira." .

ns1:63597 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Jessica Newton, national Director of the Miss Peru pageant planned a special ceremony on September 22nd that took place at the Country Club Lima Hotel in San Isidro, Lima, Peru. Prissila Howard was appointed to represent Peru at Miss Universe 2017. She represented Peru at Miss Perú Universo 2017 in Las Vegas where she placed in the Top 10, finishing sixth overall. Piazza and Mittenaere also assisted in the crowning of María José Lora and Lorena Larriviere to represent Peru at the international level. Larriviere finished in the Top 10 of Reina Hispanoamericana 2017. Sánchez represented Peru at Miss World 2017, where she placed in the Top 40." .

ns1:63667 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Penn Relays (also Penn Relays Carnival) is the oldest and largest track and field competition in the United States, hosted annually since 21 April 1895 by the University of Pennsylvania at Franklin Field in Philadelphia. In 2012, there were 116 events run at the meet and more athletes run in the Penn Relays than do any other track and field meet in the world. The Penn Relays also holds a Catholic Youth Organization night for Catholic Middle Schools in the Archdiocese of Philadelphia. The first ever Penn Relay Carnival was held on 21 April 1895 at Franklin Field and was a huge success. The first team to win a Penn Relays championship was Harvard University after they defeated University of Pennsylvania with a time of 3:34. Franklin Field plays host to University of Pennsylvania football games and the Penn Relay Carnival." .

ns1:63690 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Epsom Derby was the 237th annual running of the Derby horse race and took place at Epsom Downs Racecourse on 04 June 2016. Amongst those remaining in the race were trial winners US Army Ranger (Chester Vase), Harzand (Ballysax Stakes) and Moonlight Magic (Derrinstown Stud Epsom Derby Trial) along with the Classic winner The Gurkha (Poule d'Essai des Poulains) and Awtaad, who won the Irish 2,000 Guineas just after the confirmations were announced. Wings of Desire, Cloth of Stars, Humphrey Bogart and Red Verdon were supplemented at a cost of £75,000 and the additional entries made the prize money £1.45m and meant that the 2016 Epsom Derby would be the most valuable horse racing ever staged in Great Britain. Harzand beat Idaho by a length and a quarter and was subsequently quoted at odds of between 16/1 and 25/1 for the Epsom Derby and his trainer stated he would be \"keeping an open mind\" about targets for the colt. Jockey Pat Smullen felt that Harzand was \"... too big and heavy for Epsom Downs Racecourse. Epsom Downs Racecourse staged the Investec Epsom Derby Trial on 20 April. The favourite for the Epsom Derby Trial, Claudio Monteverdi, finished fourth and appeared uncomfortable on the Epsom Downs Racecourse track. Cloth Of Stars is not entered for the Epsom Derby but Godolphin's chief executive John Ferguson stated that they would be willing to enter the colt at the supplementary stage, saying \"We now know that the Prix du Jockey Club is a serious possibility, but there is also Epsom Downs Racecourse so we’ll see how he trains between now and then\". O'Brien did not immediately confirm that the colt would run at Epsom Downs Racecourse, saying after the race \"\"He has three options now: the St James's Palace, the French Epsom Derby and the Epsom Derby\". Simcock identified the Irish Epsom Derby and St Leger in September as Algometer's targets, stating \"If it rained at Epsom Downs Racecourse we might think about it, but, realistically, the Leger is the right race for him later in the year." .

ns1:63695 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Dakar, also known as Operation Menace, was an unsuccessful attempt in 25 September 1940 by the Allies to capture the strategic port of Dakar in French West Africa (modern-day Senegal). On 23 September 1940, the Fleet Air Arm dropped propaganda leaflets on the city of Dakar. Finally, the Allies withdrew, leaving Dakar and French West Africa in Vichy hands." .

ns1:63705 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Tour de Langkawi is a stage race bicycle race held in Malaysia. The race has been held annually since 1996, primarily in February. Tour de Langkawi is sanctioned by the International Cycling Union (UCI) as a 2.HC road bicycle racing in the UCI Asia Tour calendar. The Tour de Langkawi was conceived by former Malaysian Prime Minister Mahathir bin Mohamad to put Malaysia \"on the world sporting and tourism map\"." .

ns1:sports_season rdfs:label "sports season" .

ns1:41466 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 AFL season was the 120th season of the elite Australian rules football competition and the 27th under the name 'Australian Football League', having switched from 'Victorian Football League' after 1989. The first game was played on Thursday, 24 March, and the 2016 AFL Grand Final was played on Saturday, 1 October with the Western Bulldogs defeating Sydney Swans by 22 points to win its second premiership and first in 62 years, breaking the then-longest active drought in VFL/AFL history. Patrick Dangerfield won the Brownlow Medal, voted by the umpires to be the fairest and best player in the league for the home-and-away season. Josh Kennedy won the Coleman Medal as the highest goal scorer in the competition. On 2016, after appealing the AFL anti-doping tribunal's verdict that 34 past and present Essendon Football Club players were found not guilty of taking an illegal substance during the 2012 season, the Court of Arbitration for Sport returned a final verdict of guilty; as a result, these players, including twelve still at the club, five at another as well as 17 players who are currently playing at lower levels or have retired altogether, were suspended for the entire season." .

ns1:42028 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Vukov sabor (Serbian: Вуков сабор / Vukov sabor) or Vuk's Convocation is the oldest and most massive cultural event in Serbia. It was named after the reformer of the Serbian language Vuk Karadžić. The central program is traditionally held in September in Vuk's birthplace Tršić, near Loznica. Part of the program is realized in Loznica, Belgrade and the nearby Tronoša Monastery. Organizers of Vukov sabor are Cultural Center \"Vuk Karadžić\" Loznica, the city of Loznica, and Serbian Ministry of Culture and Information of the Republic of Serbia." .

ns1:42297 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Chelyabinsk meteor was a superbolide that entered Earth's atmosphere over Russia on 15 February 2013 at about 09:20 YEKT (03:20 UTC). The Chelyabinsk meteor is also the only meteor confirmed to have resulted in many injuries. Local residents witnessed extremely bright burning objects in the sky in Chelyabinsk Oblast, Kurgan Oblast, Sverdlovsk Oblast, Tyumen Oblast, and Orenburg Oblast, the Bashkortostan, and in neighbouring regions in Kazakhstan, when the asteroid entered the Earth's atmosphere over Russia. The last time a similar phenomenon was observed in the Chelyabinsk Oblast region was the Kunashak meteor shower of 1949, after which scientists recovered about 20 meteorites weighing over 200 kg in total. Early analysis of CCTV and dashcam video posted online indicated that the meteor approached from the southeast, and exploded about 40 km south of central Chelyabinsk Oblast above Korkino at a height of 23.3 km (14.5 miles, 76,000 feet), with fragments continuing in the direction of Lake Chebarkul. The Russian Geographical Society said the passing of the meteor over Chelyabinsk Oblast caused three blasts of different energy. Dmitry Medvedev, the Prime Minister of Russia, confirmed a meteor had struck Russia and said it proved that the \"entire planet\" is vulnerable to meteors and a spaceguard system is needed to protect the planet from similar objects in the future. Multiple videos of the Chelyabinsk Oblast superbolide, particularly from the dashboard cameras and traffic cameras which are ubiquitous in Russia, helped to establish the meteor's provenance as an Apollo asteroid." .

ns1:42704 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "wrestling at the Friendship Games was contested in three disciplines: freestyle, Greco-Roman and sambo wrestling. Freestyle events took place at the Winter Sports Palace in Sofia, People's Republic of Bulgaria between 20 13 July 1984 and 22 13 July 1984 (with 80 competitors in 10 events). Greco-Roman events were contested at the Budapest Sportcsarnok in Budapest, People's Republic of Hungary between 13 July 1984 and 15 13 July 1984 (with 61 competitors in 10 events). Sambo took part in Ulaanbaatar, Mongoli1 02 September 1984 betw2 September 1984ember 1984. *   Host nations (People's Republic of Bulgaria & People's Republic of Hungary)" .

ns1:44148 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Eysturoyartunnilin (earlier known as Skálafjarðartunnilin, in English Eysturoy Tunnel) is a large undersea tunnel under the Tangafjørður sound in the Faroe Islands, connecting the island of Streymoy to the island of Eysturoy. On 2016, a deal was struck with the Scandinavian construction company NCC to construct the Eysturoy tunnel as well as the Sandoy tunnel. The tunnel is a two-laned undersea tunnel that has three tubes which meet at an underwater roundabout, 72.6 metres below the surface of the Skálafjørður fjord. Tolls start at 75 DKK for small cars (up to 3,500 kilograms (7,700 lb)) between Tórshavn and Eysturoy, and 25 DKK for local traffic between Saltnes (near Runavík) and Strendur. Without subscription the price is 175 DKK between Eysturoy to Streymoy and 125 DKK between the two arms of Skálafjørður. On the Eysturoy side of the tunnel house prices increased by 31% between 2019 and 2020 and have doubled between 2015-2020. A proposed highway between Hvítanes and Tórshavn, Innkomuvegurin, remains unsettled as of 19 December 2020. The opening of the tunnel for general traffic was on 19 December 2020, which was 18 years and 9 days after the opening of the first subsea tunnel in the Faroe Islands, Vágatunnilin." .

ns1:45119 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017–18 Jordan League (known as the Al-Manaseer Jordan Premier League, named after Ziyad AL-Manaseer Companies Group for sponsorship reasons) is the 67th season of Jordan Premier League since its inception in 1944. Al-Faisaly SC are the defending champions of the 2016–17 Jordan League. Al-Aqaba SC and Al-Yarmouk FC entered as the two promoted teams. On 3 May 2018, Al-Rude won their 16th Premier League title, after Manshia Bani Hassan beat Al-Faisaly SC 2–1 in round 21 and before the final round of the league. The league comprises 12 teams, 10 from the 2016–17 Jordan League, as well as two teams promoted from the 2016–17 Division 1." .

ns1:45255 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1904 United States presidential election was the 30th quadrennial United States presidential election, held on Tuesday, 08 November 1904. Incumbent Republican Party President of the United States Theodore Roosevelt defeated the Democratic Party nominee, Alton B. Parker. The conservative Bourbon Democrat allies of former President of the United States Grover Cleveland temporarily regained control of the Democratic Party from the followers of William Jennings Bryan, and the 1904 Democratic Party National Convention nominated Alton B. Parker, Chief Judge of the New York Court of Appeals. Two third-party candidates, Eugene V. Debs of the Socialist Party of America and Silas C. Swallow of the Prohibition Party, each took over 1% of the popular vote. Republican Party candidates: As Republicans convened in Chicago on June 21–23, 1904, President of the United States Theodore Roosevelt's nomination was assured. Since conservatives in the Republican Party denounced Theodore Roosevelt as a radical, they were allowed to choose the vice-presidential candidate. Democratic Party candidates: In 1904, both William Jennings Bryan and former President of the United States Grover Cleveland declined to run for president. Since the two Democratic Party nominees of the past 20 years did not seek the presidential nomination, Alton B. Parker, a Bourbon Democrat from New York, emerged as the frontrunner. Prominent socialist Eugene V. Debs was nominated for president and Ben Hanford was nominated for vice-president. The radicals in the Democratic Party denounced Parker as a conservative; the conservatives in the Republican Party denounced Theodore Roosevelt as a radical. Candidates for Electors were listed under the presidential and vice presidential candidates for each party; there were four parties recognized in the election: Democratic Party, Republican Party, Prohibition, and Socialist. The distribution of the vote by counties reveals him to have been a weaker candidate than William Jennings Bryan, the party's nominee 1900 United States presidential election, in every section of the nation, except for the deep South, where Democratic Party dominance remained strong, due in large part to pervasive disfranchisement of blacks. Margin of victory less than 1% (8 electoral votes): Margin of victory less than 5% (31 electoral votes): Margin of victory between 5% and 10% (3 electoral votes): Tipping point state: Counties with Highest Percent of Vote (Republican Party) Counties with Highest Percent of Vote (Democratic Party) Counties with Highest Percent of Vote (Populist)" .

ns1:45793 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Delhi gang rape case case involved a rape and fatal assault that occurred on 16 December 2012 in Munirka, a neighbourhood in South Delhi. On 10 September 2013, the four remaining adult defendants - Pawan Gupta, Vinay Sharma, Akshay Thakur and Mukesh Singh (Ram Singh's brother) - were found guilty of rape and murder and three days later were sentenced to death by hanging. They included Ram Singh, the bus driver, and his brother, Mukesh Singh, who were both arrested in Rajasthan. Vinay Sharma, an assistant gym instructor, and Pawan Gupta, a fruit seller, were both arrested in UP and Bihar. Ram Singh was presented before the Metropolitan Magistrate on 18 16 December 2012. Mukesh Singh, Vinay Sharma, Akshay Thakur and Pawan Gupta denied the charges. On 15 March 2014, the Supreme Court of India stayed the execution of two of the four convicts, Mukesh Singh and Pawan Gupta, to allow them to make their appeal against their conviction on 31 March. On 17 January 2020, the President of India rejected the mercy plea of the convict Mukesh Singh. Public protests took place in New Delhi on 21 16 December 2012 at India Gate and Raisina Hill, the latter being the location of both the Parliament of India and Rashtrapati Bhavan, the official residence of the President of India. After Jyoti Singh's death on 29 16 December 2012, protests were staged throughout India, including Kolkata, Chennai, Bengaluru, Hyderabad, Kochi, Thiruvananthapuram, Mumbai, Bhubaneswar and Visakhapatnam. Author and activist Eve Ensler, who organised One Billion Rising, a global campaign to end violence against women and girls, said that the gang rape and murder was a turning point in India and around the world. On 22 16 December 2012, a judicial committee headed by J. S. Verma, a former Chief Justice of India and one of India's most highly regarded Chief Justices and eminent jurists, was appointed by the Central government to submit a report within 30 days to suggest amendments to criminal law to sternly deal with sexual assault cases. In December 2014, the two-year anniversary of the attack, referring to a woman who was raped in a car operated by an Uber driver, the parents remarked to the press that not much had changed: \"Nothing in India has changed since 16 December 2012." .

ns1:45948 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006 Tour de Suisse was the 70th edition of the Tour de Suisse road cycling stage race, which took place from 10 to 18 June 2006. The race consisted of nine stages, covering a total of 1,468 km (912 mi), starting in Baden and finishing in Bern. 2004 champion Jan Ullrich (T-Mobile Team) overcame a 50-second deficit on the final stage to win the overall title and the yellow jersey, in what became the last race of his career. Koldo Gil and Jörg Jaksche finished second and third, respectively, to complete the podium. ONCE captured the team classification while Michael Albasini (Liquigas) captured the King of the Mountains and the intermediate sprints jerseys. Twenty-one teams of eight riders started the race: 10 June 2006 - Baden to Baden, 154 km (96 mi) 11 June - Bremgarten to Einsiedeln, 165 km (103 mi) 12 June - Einsiedeln to Arlesheim, 160 km (99 mi) 13 June - Niederbipp to La Chaux-de-Fonds, 151 km (94 mi) 14 June - La Chaux-de-Fonds to Leukerbad, 210 km (130 mi) 15 - Fiesch to La Punt, 210 km (130 mi) 16 June - St. Moritz to Ascona, 233 km (145 mi) 17 June - Ambrì to Ambri, 155 km (96 mi) 18 June 2006 - Kerzers to Bern, 30 km (19 mi) The Points Classification of the Tour de Suisse is for points awarded for most consistently high finisher of the stages (equivalent to the Tour de France's green jersey)." .

ns1:46000 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Eurovision Song Contest 1966 was the 11th edition of the annual Eurovision Song Contest. It took place in Luxembourg City, Luxembourg, following France Gall's win at the Eurovision Song Contest 1965 in Naples, Italy with the song \"Poupée de cire, poupée de son\". The contest was held at the Villa Louvigny on Saturday 05 March 1966 and was hosted by Josiane Shen. The winner was Austria with the song \"Merci, Chérie\", performed and composed by Udo Jürgens, and written by Jürgens and Thomas Hörbiger. This was Austria's first victory - and Udo Jürgens third consecutive entry - in the contest. The 1966 Eurovision Song Contest was hosted in Luxembourg City. The venue chosen to host the 1966 contest was the Villa Louvigny, which also hosted the Eurovision Song Contest 1962. 1966 marked the year the first ever black singer graced the Eurovision stage, Milly Scott representing the Netherlands. Both the producers and EBU scrutineer Clifford Brown felt it was too short notice to fly Gigliola Cinquetti to Luxembourg to represent Italy, so the EBU gave in and allowed Modugno to use his own ensemble instead of the orchestra. Udo Jürgens from Austria whose previous participations were in 1964 and Eurovision Song Contest 1965; and Domenico Modugno from Italy, who last participated in 1958 and 1959." .

ns1:47460 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015–16 figure skating season begins on 01 July 2015, and ends on 30 June 2016. Elite skaters will begin the season competing on the Grand Prix series or 2015–16 ISU Junior Grand Prix, culminating in the 2015–16 Grand Prix of Figure Skating Final, and the ISU Challenger Series. Following national championships, competitors will appear at ISU Championships, such as the 2016 2016 European Figure Skating Championships, 2016 Four Continents Figure Skating Championships, 2016 World Junior Figure Skating Championships, and 2016 World Figure Skating Championships." .

ns1:47602 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Marj al-Saffar (or Marj al-Suffar), also known as the Battle of Shaqhab, took place on 20 April 1303 through 22 April 1303 between the Mamluks and the Mongols and their Armenian allies near Al-Kiswah, just south of Damascus. The battle, a disastrous defeat for the Mongols, put an end to Mongol invasions of Syria. Incorporating troops from vassal countries such as Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia and the Kingdom of Georgia, the Mongols had sacked Baghdad in 1258, followed by the taking of Aleppo and Damascus in 1260. Nearly 40 years later, the Ilkhanate Ghazan, once again invaded Syria, retaking Aleppo in 1299. In 1303, Ghazan sent his general Qutlugh-Shah with an army to recapture Syria. The battle commenced on 2 Ramadhan 702 in the Hijri calendar, or 20 April 1303. According to the medieval Egyptian historian Al-Maqrizi, after the battle, Qutlugh-Shah reached the Ilkhanate Ghazan at Kushuf, to inform him of the defeat of his forces." .

ns1:47875 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Holmes's Bonfire was a raid on the Vlie estuary in Netherlands, executed by the English Fleet during the Second Anglo-Dutch War on 19 20 August 1666 and 20 August 1666 New Style (9 August and 10 August Old Style). During the same action, the town of West-Terschelling was burnt down, which caused outrage in the Dutch Republic. Harlingen's exit to the North Sea, located 20 miles (32 km) to the northwest, is the Vlie, the ancient estuary of the IJssel river, between the islands of Vlieland and Terschelling. He himself was to land on Vlieland with a force of five hundred men; if possible a simultaneous attack by the remaining four hundred men under Sir William Jennings should be carried out on Terschelling. Because he was unable to immediately enter the Vlie anyway because of a contrary southeasterly wind, he sent a ketch to sound the Westerboomsgat, in that period the main Vlie channel, running from west to east towards the land head of Terschelling. Today the situation has changed considerably: the channel has shifted four miles to the southwest, eroding the north coast of Vlieland and causing Terschelling to grow in the same direction. Directly south of Vlieland, some ships had in vain sought cover in the Monnickensloot creek behind the fifty years old gun boat Adelaar of the Amsterdam Admiralty, which had been used as a permanent guard ship for the Vlie since 1652 and was armed with twelve cannon, all three-pounders. Holmes therefore decided not to burn the eastern villages, to remove his troops from Terschelling, quickly execute a short landing at Vlieland in conformation with his original orders, and retreat before any Dutch counterattack could materialise. However, when the following year Charles deliberately procrastinated the peace talks held in Breda, De Witt used the lingering resentment caused by Holmes's Bonfire to convince the States of Holland that it was justified to end the war by a devastating raid on Chatham Dockyard where the larger vessels of the English fleet were laid up." .

ns1:47900 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Groovin' the Moo is an annual music festival that is held in six regional centres across Australia. Groovin' the Moo was established by Cattleyard Promotions and their first festival was held on Sunday 2005 in Gloucester, New South Wales. The first incarnation of the festival occurred on 2005 at the Gloucester Showgrounds in New South Wales with crowds of over 1400, continuing to Narrandera (NSW Riverina) with crowds exceeding 1800. The following year the festival took place in Maitland, Albury and Darwin. In 2008 Townsville was added to the tour's circuit, with Bendigo being added the following year. 2010 saw the inclusion of Bunbury and Canberra, with all shows selling out across the country. Australia's first pill testing trial was held at the 2018 festival in Canberra. Launched on 16 September and to date all of events have been held in metropolitan cities, unlike \"Groovin' the Moo\" which is held in various regional centres across Australia." .

ns1:48174 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2007 inter-Korean summit meeting was held between 02 October 2007 and 04 October 2007, in Pyongyang, between President Roh Moo-hyun of the South Korea and Kim Jong-il of the Democratic People's South Korea (DPRK). It is the second inter-Korean summits following the 2000 inter-Korean summit. The second Summit was held 02 October 2007–4, 2007, also in Pyongyang, between Kim Jong-il and Roh Moo-hyun, at the time President of the South Korea. On August 8, 2007, the South Korea (South Korea) and the Democratic People's South Korea (North Korea) announced that the second inter-Korean summits would be held from August 28 to August 30, 2007. South Korea proposed to hold Summit talks between 02 October 2007 and 04 October 2007. On 02 October 2007, at 9:05, South Korean president Roh Moo-hyun walked across the Korean Demilitarized Zone in travelling to Pyongyang for talks with Kim Jong-il." .

ns1:48202 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006 Russian Premier League was the 55th season of the premier association football competition in Russia since the dissolution of the Soviet Union and the 5th under the current Russian Premier League name. The season started on 2006 and ended on 26 November 2006. Defending champions PFC CSKA Moscow claimed their second successive title on 2006 with an away win over Luch-Energiya Vladivostok. FC Torpedo Moscow and FC Shinnik Yaroslavl were relegated. As in the previous season, 16 teams played in the 2006 season. After the 2005 Russian Premier League, Alania Vladikavkaz and Terek Grozny were relegated to the 2006 Russian First Division. They were replaced by FC Luch-Energiya Vladivostok and PFC Spartak Nalchik, the winners and runners up of the 2005 Russian First Division. Based on paragraph 15.3 of the Russian Premier League regulations for the current season, if two or more teams are equal on points (without having the highest number), the positions of these teams are determined by: Based on paragraph 15.4 of the regulations, if two teams are equal on the highest number of points, the first position is determined by: Based on paragraph 15.5 of the regulations, if more than two teams are equal on the highest number of points, the first position and subsequent positions of these teams are determined by: 1The terms of this additional tournament are determined by the Russian Football Union and the governing body of the Russian Premier League based on suggestions from the participating clubs." .

ns1:49088 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 FIVB Volleyball Women's Nations League was the inaugural edition of the FIVB Volleyball Women's Nations League, a new annual international women's volleyball tournament contested by 16 national teams that replaced the former World Grand Prix in the international calendar. The competition was held between May and 01 July 2018 and the final round took place in the Nanjing Olympic Sports Centre, Nanjing, China women's national volleyball team. Six-time World Grand Prix winners, United States women's national volleyball team won the inaugural edition, defeating Turkey women's national volleyball team in five sets on the final. Reigning Olympic champions and host nation, China women's national volleyball team defeated 12-time Grand Prix champions Brazil women's national volleyball team in straight sets for the bronze medal. Argentina women's national volleyball team was the last placed challenger team after the preliminary round and will be replaced by Challenger Cup winners Bulgaria in the 2019 FIVB Volleyball Women's Nations League. as of July 1, 2018." .

ns1:49455 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Lake Khasan (29 July 1938 – 11 August 1938), also known as the Changkufeng Incident (Russian: Хасанские бои, Chinese and Japanese: 張鼓峰事件; Chinese pinyin: Zhānggǔfēng Shìjiàn; Japanese romaji: Chōkohō Jiken) in China and Japan, was an attempted military incursion by Manchukuo, a Japanese puppet state, into the territory claimed and controlled by the Soviet Union. That incursion was founded in the Japanese belief that the Soviet Union had misinterpreted the demarcation of the boundary based on the Treaty of Peking between Imperial Russia and Qing China (and subsequent supplementary agreements on demarcation) and that the demarcation markers were tampered with. The southern branch of the CER, known in the West as the South Manchuria Railway, became the locus and partial casus belli for the Russo-Japanese War and subsequent incidents, leading to the Second Sino-Japanese War and Soviet–Japanese border conflicts. The confrontation was triggered when the Soviet Far East Army and Soviet State Security (NKVD) Border Guard reinforced its Khasan border with Manchuria. The message recommended that Soviet soldiers be allowed to secure unoccupied high ground west of Lake Khasan, most notably the disputed Changkufeng Heights, because it would be advantageous for the Soviets to occupy terrain which overlooked the Korean port-city of Rajin, as well as strategic railways linking Korea to Manchuria." .

ns1:49569 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1930 Albanian Superliga was the first edition of the Albanian Superliga, which ran from 06 April 1930 until 06 July 1930. It was the first-ever official football competition in Albania run by the newly formed Albanian Football Association, as the 1911 Albanian Superliga was not run by the AFA and is not yet recognised. The six original members were KS Elbasani, KF Teuta Durrës, KF Skënderbeu Korçë, Bashkimi Shkodran, KF Tirana and Sportklub Vlora who agreed to play a ten-game format whereby each team would host each of their five rivals once with two points awarded for a win and one point awarded for a drawn game. KF Tirana were declared the inaugural champions of Albania following two technical victories over KF Skënderbeu Korçë in the championship playoffs, taking their seasonal total to seven wins, four draws and one loss. The competition was the first official national football competition to be held in Albania and it featured six sides from across Albania, which were KS Elbasani, KF Teuta Durrës, KF Skënderbeu Korçë, Bashkimi Shkodran, KF Tirana and Sportklub Vlora. The first phase was held between 06 April 1930 and 4 May, and the second phase was held between 11 May and 22 June. The playoff finals were planned on 22 June and 06 July 1930, but they did not take place due to KF Skënderbeu Korçë's forfeit of both games. The playoff finals were not played following KF Skënderbeu Korçë's forfeit, which was a sign of protest against the bias of the competition and the Albanian Football Association towards the KF Tirana. Both games were awarded 2-0 to KF Tirana thus crowning them the first champions of Albania. Note: 'Bashkimi Shkodran' is KS Vllaznia Shkodër, 'KS Elbasani' is KS Elbasani and 'Sportklub Vlora' is Flamurtari Vlorë. KF Skënderbeu Korçë - KF Tirana 0:2 (technical victory) KF Tirana - KF Skënderbeu Korçë 2:0 (technical victory) Scheduled for June 29 and 06 July 1930 but KF Skënderbeu Korçë forfeited both matches, and KF Tirana was awarded by technical victories, so they became first Albania champions." .

ns1:49987 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1999–2000 Serie B was the sixty-eighth tournament of this competition played in Italy since its creation. F.C. AlzanoCene 1909, U.S. Fermana, A.C. Pistoiese and U.S. Savoia 1908 had been promoted from Serie C, while U.S. Salernitana 1919, U.C. Sampdoria, L.R. Vicenza Virtus and Empoli F.C. had been relegated from Serie A." .

ns1:50050 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Halabja chemical attack (Kurdish: Kêmyabarana Helebce کمیابارانی ھەڵەبجە), also known as the Halabja Massacre or Bloody Friday, was a massacre of Kurdish people that took place on 16 March 1988, during the closing days of the Iran–Iraq War in the Kurdish city of Halabja in Iraqi Kurdistan. The attack was part of the Al-Anfal Campaign in Kurdistan, as well as part of the Iraqi Army's attempt to repel the Iranian Operation Zafar 7. The Halabja attack has been recognized as a distinct event of the Anfal genocide conducted against the Kurdish people by the Iraqi regime under Saddam Hussein. The Iraqi High Criminal Court recognized the Halabja massacre as an act of genocide on 1 March 2010, a decision welcomed by the Kurdistan Regional Government. The Halabja event was also part of Iraqi efforts to counter-attack Kurdish and Iranian forces in the final stages of Operation Zafar 7. Iraqi government documents dating from 16 March 1988 to several weeks later refer to \"a firm escalation of military might and cruelty ,\" \"the bombing by our planes and our artillery on the area of Halabja and Khurmal, approximately 2,000 enemy forces of the Persians and Iranian agents ,\" the \"recent attack on Halabja with special ammunition,\" and, in one case, explicitly refer to \"the Iraqi chemical attack on Halabja.\" Saddam's cousin Ali Hassan al-Majid (who commanded Iraqi forces in northern Iraq during that period, which earned him a nickname of 'Chemical Ali') was condemned to death by hanging by an Iraqi court in January 2010, after being found guilty of orchestrating the Halabja massacre." .

ns1:50386 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017–18 Croatian First Football League (officially Hrvatski Telekom Prva liga for sponsorship reasons) was the 27th season of the Croatian First Football League, the national championship for men's association football teams in Croatia, since its establishment in 1992. The season started on 14 July 2017 and ended on 19 May 2018. On 21, Croatian Football Federation announced that the first stage of licensing procedure for 2017–18 season was complete. For the 2017–18 Prva HNL, only seven clubs were issued a top level license: GNK Dinamo Zagreb, H.N.K. Hajduk Split, NK Inter Zaprešić, NK Lokomotiva, NK Osijek, HNK Rijeka and NK Slaven Belupo. At the end of the season, ninth placed team NK Istra 1961 will contest a two-legged relegation play-off tie against Varaždin, runners-up of the 2017–18 Croatian Second Football League. NK Istra 1961 won 3–2 on aggregate." .

ns1:52219 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Livonian campaign against Rus' was a military campaign that lasted from 1240 to 1242, and was carried out by the Teutonic Knights of the Livonian Order with the aim to conquer the lands of Pskov and Novgorod Republic and convert them to Catholicism. Expelled from the city, the nobles and the Knights captured Izborsk in 1233, but soon were expelled from the city by the Pskov army. In 1240, the Swedish commanders Jarl Birger and Ulf Fassi attempted to invade Novgorod Republic land under the pretext of exterminating the Gentiles. In August, the Livonian knights captured the town of Izborsk and arrived at the outskirts of Pskov, beginning a siege. In the Spring of 1242, he recaptured Pskov. After this victory, Alexander decided to continue his campaign.. On 1242, one of Russia's most famous battles, the Battle of the Ice, took place on the ice of Lake Peipus. The Germans withdrew from Pskov and Novgorod Republic." .

ns1:52292 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015–16 3. Liga was the eighth season of the 3. Liga. A total of 20 teams contested the league, including 14 sides from the 2014–15 3. Liga. The two promoted teams were replaced by FC Erzgebirge Aue and VfR Aalen, who finished in the bottom two places of the 2014–15 2. SV Werder Bremen II from the Regionalliga Nord returned to the national level after three seasons in the fourth tier, while 1. FC Magdeburg from the Regionalliga Nordost and Würzburger Kickers from the Regionalliga Bayern are playing their debut seasons in the 3. Liga. A further place in the league was available via a two-legged play-off between third-placed 2014–15 3. Liga team Holstein Kiel and 16th-placed 2014–15 2." .

ns1:52825 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1993 Galician regional election was held on Sunday, 17 October 1993, to elect the 4th Parliament of the autonomous community of Galicia. The People's Party (People's Party of Galicia) won with an increased absolute majority over 1989, resulting in Manuel Fraga Iribarne being re-elected for a second term in office as President of Galicia. Meanwhile, the Galician Nationalist Bloc (BNG) began to appear as the main alternative to power thanks to its spokesman, Xosé Manuel Beiras', charisma. The term of the Parliament of Galicia expired four years after the date of its 1989 Galician regional election, unless it was dissolved earlier." .

ns1:53708 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Aerolíneas Argentinas Flight 644 was a scheduled flight operated by the Douglas DC-6, registration LV-ADW, on 19 July 1961 which was due to operate a domestic scheduled passenger service between Ministro Pistarini International Airport and General Enrique Mosconi International Airport, but crashed 12 km (7.5 mi) west of Pardo, Buenos Aires, Argentina, half an hour after takeoff, owing to severe turbulence during climb out." .

ns1:54324 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Algeciras was a battle of the Spanish Reconquista that occurred between 01 July 1309 and 31 January 1310. The battle was fought between the forces of the Kingdom of Castile, commanded by King Ferdinand IV of Castile and his vassals, and the Emirate of Granada commanded by Sultan Abu'l-Juyush Nasr. The battle resulted in a humiliating defeat for the Kingdom of Castile whose army was obliged to lift the siege due to the atrocious conditions of life in the Castilian camp and the desertion of Infante John of Castile. The battle marked one of the many battles fought at Algeciras where the Christian forces would try to take the city unsuccessfully from the Muslims. It was further agreed that the Kingdom of Castile would attack the towns of Algeciras and Gibraltar and that the Aragonese forces would conquer the city of Almería. At the Courts of Madrid of 1309, the first courts ever occurring in the actual Spanish capital, Ferdinand IV publicly announced his desire to wage war against the Emirate of Granada and demanded subsidies to begin battle maneuvers. Final preparations for the siege were carried out in Seville, where Ferdinand IV arrived in 01 July 1309. On 27 01 July 1309, the vanguard of the Castilian army reached the walls of Algeciras, followed three days later on 30 July by the king and his entourage. James II of Aragon began his siege of Almeria on the 15th of August of that same year, a siege that would last until 1310. In 31 January 1310, Ferdinand IV began negotiations with the Emirate of Granada. Almost simultaneously with the Castilian defeat at Algeciras, James II of Aragon ordered a lifting of the unsuccessful siege of Almeria by the Kingdom of Aragon." .

ns1:55428 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1923 Coupe de France Final was a association football match held at Pershing Stadium, Paris on 06 May 1923, that saw Red Star F.C. defeat FC Sète 4–2 thanks to goals by Marcel Naudin (2), Lucien Cordon and Robert Joyaut." .

ns1:55513 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 FA Women's Cup Final was the 39th final of the FA Women's Cup, England's primary cup competition for women's association football teams. The final was contested between Arsenal W.F.C. and Sunderland Association Football Club Ladies at the Pride Park Stadium on 04 May 2009. Arsenal W.F.C. won the game 2–1 to secure their fourth successive FA Cup triumph and their tenth in total. Arsenal W.F.C., managed by Vic Akers, reached their fourth FA Women's Cup final in succession after a 3–1 victory against Everton in the semi-finals, while Premier League Northern Division side Sunderland Association Football Club Ladies, managed by Mick Mulhern, reached their first ever final in the competition after beating Chelsea 3–0 at the Stadium of Light, becoming the first team from outside the top flight to do so since Fulham in 2002. Arsenal W.F.C., who came into the game as favourites, dominated the vast majority of the match, with Gemma Davison and Kim Little creating several chances in the opening minutes. Arsenal W.F.C. hit back with Little striking over from distance and Davison drawing a save from Sunderland Association Football Club Ladies goalkeeper Helen Alderson after cutting in from the left. Arsenal W.F.C. took the lead just after the half hour, when Alderson failed to hold Davison's shot and Katie Chapman followed up to score from the rebound. Kelly McDougall pulled one back for Sunderland Association Football Club Ladies in the seventh minute of stoppage time, whose length was dictated by Sophie Williams having to receive more than five minutes of treatment after suffering a seizure." .

ns1:56084 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Strade Bianche was a road cycling one-day race that took place on 04 March 2017. It was the eleventh edition of the Strade Bianche and was the fifth event of the 2017 UCI World Tour. Poland's Michał Kwiatkowski (Team Sky) became the second rider, after Fabian Cancellara, to win multiple editions of the race, after attacking from a group of four race favourites with around 15 kilometres (9.3 miles) remaining and was able to solo away to the race victory. Second place was taken by Greg Van Avermaet for the BMC Racing Team fifteen seconds in arrears, while a further two seconds back in third place, Lotto–Soudal's Tim Wellens completed the podium. The distance was 175 kilometres (109 miles), run entirely within the southern Tuscan province of Siena. The first sector was addressed just 11.4 kilometres (7.1 miles) after the start; the longest and most arduous sectors were the ones in Lucignano d'Asso, 11.9 kilometres (7.4 miles) in length, and the 11.5 kilometres (7.1 miles)-long Monte Sante Marie. The last stretch of gravel road came 13.3 kilometres (8.3 miles) from the finish in Siena. The race finished on Siena's illustrious piazza del Campo, after a narrow ascent on the roughly-paved Via Santa Caterina in the heart of the medieval city, with steep stretches of up to 16% in gradient." .

ns1:56721 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1997–98 French Division 1 was the 60th season of Ligue 1, the top French professional league for association football clubs, since its establishment in 1932. The season began on 02 August 1997, and concluded on 09 May 1998. This season saw a change in the league format, with the number of teams reduced to 18 from 20, shortening the season from 38 to 34 rounds. As a result, four clubs which had been relegated at the end of the previous season were replaced by only two, LB Châteauroux and Toulouse FC, who entered as winners and runners up of the 1996–97 French Division 2. Defending champions of the 1996–97 French Division 1 were AS Monaco FC. R.C. Lens won their league title with 68 points, edging out second-placed FC Metz on goal difference, for whom this was their best result in history. Promoted from Ligue 2, who will play in Ligue 1 season 1998/1999." .

ns1:56991 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Falaise Pocket or Battle of the Falaise Pocket (German: Kessel von Falaise; 12 August 1944–21 August 1944) was the decisive engagement of the Battle of Normandy in the Second World War. The battle is also referred to as the Battle of the Falaise Gap (after the corridor which the Germans sought to maintain to allow their escape). On 8 August, the Allied ground forces commander, General Bernard Law Montgomery, 1st Viscount Montgomery of Alamein, ordered the Allied armies to converge on the Falaise, Chambois area to envelop Army Group B, with the First US Army forming the southern arm, the British the base, and the Canadians the northern arm of the encirclement. A few days later, the Allied Liberation of Paris was completed, and on 30 August the remnants of Army Group B retreated across the Seine, which ended Operation Overlord. Still expecting Kluge to withdraw his forces from the tightening Allied noose, Montgomery had for some time been planning a \"long envelopment\", by which the British and Canadians would pivot left from Falaise toward the River Seine while the US Third Army blocked the escape route between the Seine and the Loire, trapping all surviving German forces in western France. After a limited attack by the 2nd Canadian Infantry Division down the Laize valley on 12 August 1944–13 August, most of the time since Totalize had been spent preparing for Operation Tractable, a set-piece attack on Falaise. By the evening of 21 August 1944, tanks of the 4th Canadian Armoured Division had linked with Polish forces at Coudehard, and the 2nd and 3rd Canadian Infantry divisions had secured St. Lambert and the northern passage to Chambois; the Falaise pocket had been sealed. The Battle of the Falaise Pocket ended the Battle of Normandy with a decisive German defeat. The final battle of Operation Overlord, the Liberation of Paris, followed on 25 August, and Overlord ended by 30 August, with the retreat of the last German unit across the Seine." .

ns1:57589 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Domodedovo International Airport bombing was a suicide bombing in the international arrival hall of Moscow's Domodedovo International Airport, in Domodedovsky District, Moscow Oblast, on 24 January 2011. The bombing killed 37 people and injured 173 others, including 86 who had to be hospitalised. Domodedovo International Airport is located 42 kilometres (26 mi) southeast of central Moscow and is Russia's largest airport, with over 22 million passengers passing through in 2010. A number of flights originally bound for Domodedovo were redirected to Moscow's Vnukovo International Airport following the attack. The trains from other Moscow airports, where flights originally scheduled to land at Domodedovo were diverted to, were also running free of charge. It was reported that Doku Umarov had planned to follow the Domodedovo attack with two additional bombings in Moscow. President Dmitry Medvedev apportioned some blame to poor security at Domodedovo and sacked several officials – said to include a regional transport chief and a Moscow police deputy head; he also announced that he would delay his departure to the World Economic Forum in Davos, Switzerland. A number of flights originally bound for Domodedovo were redirected to Moscow's Vnukovo International Airport following the attack. The Brazilian Ministry of External Relations stated that \"the Brazilian Government is saddened to learn of the attack at Moscow's Domodedovo airport, which resulted in the loss of many lives\"." .

ns1:58529 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Trofeo Laigueglia was a one-day road cycling race that took place on 14 February 2016 in and around Laigueglia. It was the 53rd edition of the Trofeo Laigueglia and was rated as a 1.HC event as part of the 2016 UCI Europe Tour. He held off a small chasing group to take a solo win, with Sonny Colbrelli (Bardiani–CSF) second and Grega Bole (Nippo–Vini Fantini) third." .

ns1:58737 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1897 FA Cup Final was contested by Aston Villa F.C. and Everton F.C. at Crystal Palace National Sports Centre. Aston Villa F.C. won 3–2, with goals by John Campbell, Fred Wheldon and Jimmy Crabtree. Everton F.C.'s goals came from Jack Bell and Dickie Boyle." .

ns1:58822 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1973 Five Nations Championship was the forty-fourth series of the rugby union Six Nations Championship. Ten matches were played between 13 January 1973 and 14 April 1973. It was contested by England national rugby union team, France national rugby union team, Ireland national rugby union team, Scotland national rugby union team and Wales national rugby union team. Due to the Troubles occurring in Ireland national rugby union team, which had resulted in the loss of over 100 British soldiers in 1972 and the potential security risks, both Scotland national rugby union team and Wales national rugby union team had refused to play Ireland national rugby union team in Dublin in 1972. Defying expectations to the contrary, England national rugby union team agreed to travel in 1973." .

ns1:58881 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Chastise was an attack on Nazi Germany dams carried out on 16 May 1943–17 May 1943 by Royal Air Force No. The Möhne and Edersee dams were breached, causing catastrophic flooding of the Ruhr valley and of villages in the Eder valley; the Sorpe Dam sustained only minor damage. The targets selected were the Möhne Reservoir and the Sorpe Dam, upstream from the Ruhr industrial area, with the Eder Dam on the Eder River, which feeds into the Weser, as a secondary target. Both the Möhne and Eder Dams had towers at each end. Its mission was to attack the Möhne; any aircraft with bombs remaining would then attack the Eder. The mission codes (transmitted in morse) were: Goner, meaning \"bomb dropped\"; Nigger, meaning that the Möhne was breached; and Dinghy, meaning that the Eder was breached. 2 flew further north, cutting over Vlieland and crossing the IJsselmeer before joining the first route near Wesel and then flying south beyond the Möhne to the Sorpe River. The War Diary of the Nazi Germany Naval Staff reported that the Bever Dam was attacked at nearly the same time that the Sorpe Dam was. In 17 May 1943 coal production dropped by 400,000 tons which Nazi Germany sources attribute to the effects of the raid. According to an article by Nazi Germany historian Ralf Blank, at least 1,650 people were killed: around 70 of these were in the Eder Valley, and at least 1,579 bodies were found along the Möhne and Ruhr rivers, with hundreds missing. (Some non-Nazi Germany sources erroneously cite an earlier total of 749 for all foreigners in all camps in the Möhne and Ruhr valleys as the casualty count at a camp just below the Eder Dam.) He also expressed puzzlement at the raids: the disruption of temporarily having to shift 7,000 construction workers to the Möhne and Eder repairs was offset by the failure of the Allies to follow up with additional (conventional) raids during the dams' reconstruction, and that represented a major lost opportunity. The Dams Raid was, like many British air raids, undertaken with a view to the need to keep drawing Nazi Germany defensive effort back into Germany and away from actual and potential theatres of ground war, a policy which culminated in the Berlin raids of the winter of 1943–1944. Even within Germany, as evidenced by Gauleiters' reports to Berlin at the time, the Nazi Germany population regarded the raids as a legitimate attack on military targets and thought they were \"an extraordinary success on the part of the English\" ." .

ns1:59567 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1996 Australian Grand Prix was a Formula One motor race held at Melbourne Grand Prix Circuit on 10 March 1996. It was the first race of the 1996 Formula One season, and the first Australian Grand Prix to be held at Melbourne Grand Prix Circuit, taking over from Adelaide. The 58-lap race was won by Damon Hill, driving a Williams-Renault. This was second grand prix in a row held in Australia, the 1995 Australian Grand Prix being the conclusion to the 1995 season. It was an all-Williams front row with Damon Hill and debutant Jacques Villeneuve in the blue and white Rothmans cars." .

ns1:59638 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1997 National League Championship Series (NLCS) pitted the Florida Marlins against the Atlanta Braves. The Marlins won the series, 4–2, and went on to defeat the Cleveland Indians in the 1997 World Series. Florida won the series, 4–2. Tuesday, 07 October 1997, at Turner Field in Atlanta. Wednesday, 1997, at Turner Field in Atlanta. Friday, 1997, at Hard Rock Stadium in Miami Gardens. Saturday, 1997, at Hard Rock Stadium in Miami Gardens. Sunday, 1997, at Hard Rock Stadium in Miami Gardens. Tuesday, 14 October 1997, at Turner Field in Atlanta. Back at Turner Field with a 1997 World Series appearance looming, the Marlins went back to their ace, Kevin Brown. 1997 NLCS (4–2): Florida Marlins over Atlanta Braves." .

ns1:59639 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "London Road is a musical written by Alecky Blythe (book and lyrics) and Adam Cork (music and lyrics). The production, directed by Rufus Norris, opened at the National Theatre's Cottesloe theatre in London, United Kingdom, on 14 April 2011 after seven previews. The musical is set in and around London Road in Ipswich, Suffolk, during the Ipswich serial murders and subsequent trial of killer Steve Wright in 2006–2008. Clare Burt, Rosalie Craig, Kate Fleetwood, Hal Fowler, Nick Holder, Claire Moore, Michael Shaeffer, Nicola Sloane, Paul Thornley, Howard Ward, Duncan Wisbey. A film adaptation was announced in July 2013, to be directed by Rufus Norris. The film respectively features Tom Hardy and Olivia Colman in roles of Mark, a taxi driver and Julie, the organizer of Ipswich's Neighbourhood Watch." .

ns1:60874 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Grand Dictation of the Dutch Language (Dutch: Groot Dictee der Nederlandse Taal) was a televised dictation for adults organized by the Belgian newspaper De Morgen, the Dutch newspaper De Volkskrant and the Dutch public broadcaster NTR. Starting 1991, it was televised on Dutch and Flemish Radio and Television Broadcasting Organization. The Dictation was presented and read by Dutch former anchorman Philip Freriks. Half of each group consisted of invited prominent figures and half of members of the public, selected from a qualifying round in De Morgen and De Volkskrant. In 2015 the Dutch team won, in 2016 the Flemish team." .

ns1:60995 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Tarakan took place on 11 January 1942–12 January 1942, a day after the Empire of Japan declared war on the Kingdom of the Netherlands. Although Tarakan was only a small marshy island off northeastern Borneo (now divided between Indonesia's Kalimantan and Malaysia's East Malaysia) in the Dutch East Indies (today's Indonesia), its 700 oil wells, refineries, and airfield made it a crucial objective for Japan in the Pacific War. If it deemed impossible to do so, Dutch forces must deny the enemy the usage of Tarakan's oil-producing machineries, before withdrawing into mainland Borneo. Once these key points of Tarakan Island have been cleared, the Army will turn over the guard duty to the Navy, and the force shall be assembled in Tarakan and its vicinity to prepare for the capture of Balikpapan. On the night of 11 January 1942, before Japan completed the blockade of Tarakan, the Dutch submarine K-X, the patrol boat P 1, and BPM motor schooner Aida slipped to friendly waters. The ship's skipper, Anthonie van Versendaal and three other officers were among those killed in the battle, while Yamakaze picked up 16 survivors and putting them ashore in Tarakan. Notwithstanding, due to bad weather, particularly on 11 January 1942, their efforts came too late for the Tarakan defenders below. By 1942, the wells have been repaired and the oil production continued without any serious hindrance until mid-August 1943, when the first Allied air raids on Tarakan began. Dutch planes flew bombing missions from the Samarinda II Airfield in eastern Borneo on Tarakan Airfield repeatedly on January 13–14.15 Navy personnel were killed, and an additional 27 were wounded during these raids." .

ns1:61000 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 European Grand Prix (formally the 2010 Formula 1 Telefónica Grand Prix of Europe) was a Formula One motor race held on 27 June 2010 at the Valencia Street Circuit in Valencia, Spain. The 57-lap race was won by Red Bull driver Sebastian Vettel who started from pole position. The 2010 European Grand Prix was the ninth out of 19 scheduled rounds of the 2010 Formula One World Championship, and was held on 27 June 2010 at the Valencia Street Circuit in Valencia, Spain. Despite being afflicted with reliability issues with his Red Bull car, Vettel said there was no reason for him or his team to panic and was looking forward to the European Grand Prix in Valencia." .

ns1:61038 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Spanish Grand Prix (formally known as the Formula 1 Gran Premio de España Pirelli 2016) was a Formula One motor race held on 15 May 2016 at the Circuit de Barcelona-Catalunya in Montmeló, Spain. The race was the fifth round of the 2016 Formula One World Championship FIA Formula One World Championship, and marked the forty-sixth running of the Spanish Grand Prix as a round of the Formula One World Championship. Max Verstappen won the race upon his début for his new team Red Bull, having swapped his Toro Rosso seat with Daniil Kvyat ahead of the event. Daniil Kvyat and Max Verstappen traded places ahead of the race. Following problems with his power unit in both the Chinese and 2016 Russian Grand Prix, Lewis Hamilton received a redesigned MGU-H unit, in a bid to solve the troubles which hampered him earlier." .

ns1:61607 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The UEFA Euro 2000 Final was a association football match played on 02 July 2000 at De Kuip in Rotterdam, Netherlands, to determine the winner of UEFA Euro 2000. France national association football team won the match, defeating Italy national association football team 2–1. Marco Delvecchio gave Italy national association football team the lead in the 55th minute when he turned in a cross from the right by Gianluca Pessotto, and they held the lead until the final minute of injury time, when Sylvain Wiltord crashed a low drive past Italian keeper Francesco Toldo from the left to take the game into extra time. France national association football team won the game just before half-time in extra-time when Robert Pires cut the ball back from the left for David Trezeguet to fire the golden goal into the roof of the net with his left foot and win the tournament for France national association football team." .

ns1:61771 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1936–37 Serie B was the eighth tournament of this competition played in Italy since its creation. Cremonese, Venezia F.C., Spezia Calcio and Unione Sportiva Catanzaro had been promoted from 1937–38 Serie C, while Palermo FC and Brescia Calcio had been relegated from Serie A. Calcio Catania were relegated to 1937–38 Serie C." .

ns1:61889 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1980 Basque regional election was held on Sunday, 09 March 1980, to elect the 1st Parliament of the Basque Autonomous Community. The Basque Nationalist Party (Basque Nationalist Party) won 25 seats, Herri Batasuna (HB) came second with 11 seats, the Socialist Party of the Basque Autonomous Community (PSE–PSOE) came third with 9 seats. The Union of the Democratic Centre (UCD) and Euskadiko Ezkerra (EE) won 6 seats each." .

ns1:61940 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Augsburg Interim (\"Declaration of His Roman Imperial Majesty on the Observance of Religion Within the Holy Empire Until the Decision of the General Council\") was an imperial decree ordered on 15 25 May 1548 at the 1548 Diet of Augsburg by Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor, who had just defeated the forces of the Protestant Schmalkaldic League in the Schmalkaldic War of 1546/47. Charles V tried to enforce the Interim in the Holy Roman Empire, but was only successful in territories under his military control, such as Württemberg and certain imperial cities in southern Germany. This victory eventually resulted in the signing of the treaties of Peace of Passau (2 12 August 1552) and Augsburg (1555)." .

ns1:62042 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Circuit de la Sarthe (full name 2015 Circuit Cyclist Sarthe–Pays de Loire) was the 63rd edition of the Circuit de la Sarthe cycling stage race. It was run in the Sarthe department between 07 April 2015 and 10 April 2015 and consisted of five stages, two of which took place on the same day. It was rated as a 2.1 event on the 2015 UCI Europe Tour. 07 April 2015 – Sablé-sur-Sarthe – Varades (189 km (117 mi)) 8 April – Varades – Angers (83.9 km (52 mi)) 8 April – Angers (6.8 km (4 mi) ITT) 9 April – Angers - Pré-en-Pail (186 km (116 mi)) 10 April 2015 – Abbaye de l'Epau - Le Lude (176 km (109 mi))" .

ns1:62365 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Congo–Arab War (also known as the Congolese–Arab War, Belgo–Arab War or Arab Wars) took place in what is now the Democratic Republic of the Congo between the forces of Belgian King Leopold II's Congo Free State and various Zanzibari Arab slave traders led by Sefu bin Hamid, the son of Tippu Tip. In 1886, while Tippu Tip was in Zanzibar, a dispute arose between a fort at Stanley Falls led by Tippu Tip, and a smaller, nearby Congo Free State fort led by Walter Deane (a British captain) and Lt Dubois (a Belgian lieutenant). Tippu Tip's men attacked the fort — the Congo Free State fort was defended by the two officers, eighty Nigerian Hausas and sixty local militiamen — and after a four-day siege, the defenders ran out of ammunition and fled, abandoning the fort. In early 1887, Henry Morton Stanley arrived in Zanzibar and proposed that Tippu Tip (nom de guerre—his real name was Hamad bin Muhammad bin Juma bin Rajab el Murjebi) be made governor (wali) of the Stanley Falls District in the Congo Free State. Tippu Tip agreed to submit to Congo Free State authority and to allow a Congo Free State Resident by his side to help him govern this territory—inspired by the indirect rule governance system used by the British Empire and in the Dutch East Indies. Additionally Tippu Tip was to redirect his ivory trade through the Congo Free State, to the ports located at the Atlantic Ocean and he was to assist King Leopold II ‘s forces in their expeditions to the Upper Nile, to help further expand his territories. Soon after making this deal, it became apparent that Tippu Tip was not inclined to accept Congo Free State authority and considered himself more of a vassal than a state official, allowed to do as he pleased, within certain boundaries. Around 1890-91, Tippu Tip returned to Zanzibar, where he retired; Sefu bin Hamid represented his father in the eastern Congo region of Kasongo and carried on the war in his stead. In March and 01 April 1892, Tippu Tip's son Sefu bin Hamid began to lead various attacks on Congo Free State (CFS) personnel in the eastern Congo, including ivory trader Arthur Hodister—sent by the Syndicat Commercial du Katanga Province to 'acquire' ivory—and Captain Guillaume Van Kerckhoven, who had been confiscating ivory by force from several powerful Arab traders." .

ns1:62412 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013–14 Russian Premier League is the 22nd season of the Russia association football championship since the dissolution of the Soviet Union and 12th under the current Russian Premier League name. The season started on 13 14 July 2013 and concluded on 17 15 May 2014, with a winter break between the weekends around 6 December 2013 and 6 March 2014. FC Tom Tomsk returned to Premier League at first attempt, while Ural was absent from the top level for 16 seasons. On 11 15 May 2014, the game between FC Zenit Saint Petersburg and FC Dinamo Moscow was interrupted in the 86th minute with Dynamo leading 4-2 when Zenit fans ran out of the stands. The punishment was decided on 14 15 May 2014 by the Control-Disciplinary Committee of the Russia Football Union. For the contribution to the development of association football: Sergey Galitsky (owner of FC Krasnodar)." .

ns1:62529 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1939 Giro d'Italia was the 27th edition of the Giro d'Italia, a cycling race organized and sponsored by the newspaper La Gazzetta dello Sport. The race began on 28 April 1939 in Milan with a stage that stretched 182 km (113 mi) to Turin, finishing back in Milan on 18 May 1939 after a split stage and a total distance covered of 3,011.4 km (1,871 mi). The race was won by the Italian rider Giovanni Valetti of the Fréjus team, with fellow Italians Gino Bartali and Mario Vicini coming in second and third respectively. Of the 89 riders that began the Giro d'Italia on 28 April 1939, 54 of them made it to the finish in Milan on 18 May 1939. The field featured three former Giro d'Italia winners with two-time winner Gino Bartali, Vasco Bergamaschi who won the 1935 edition, and reigning champion Giovanni Valetti." .

ns1:62637 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1954 Asian Games (Filipino: Palarong Asyano 1954), officially known as the Second Asian Games – Manila 1954 was a multi-sport event held in Manila, Philippines at the 1954 Asian Games, from May 1 to 9, 1954. The number of participating NOCs and athletes were larger than the previous Asian Games held in New Delhi in 1951 Asian Games. Jorge B. Vargas was the head of the Philippine Amateur Athletic Federation (In 1976, was renamed as Philippine Olympic Committee) and the Manila Asian Games Organizing Committee. The Games were formally opened by President Ramon Magsaysay on 1954, at 16:02 local time. The flag bearer for the Philippines at the 1954 Asian Games squad was Andres Franco, who won a gold medal in the 1951 Asian Games in high jump event, the sole gold medal of any Filipino in the athletics events of the previous Asian Games. This version of the Asian Games comprised more sports and events than the last one, as six sports and seven events were in the calendar of 1951 Asian Games." .

ns1:62645 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Japanese aircraft carrier Unryū (雲龍, Cloud Dragon) was the lead ship of Unryū class aircraft carrier of fleet aircraft carrier built for the Imperial Japanese Navy (IJN) during World War II. The ship was one of 16 Unryū-class aircraft carrier planned, although only three were completed before the end of the war. Unryū had a length of 227.35 meters (745 ft 11 in) overall. Unryū was laid down at Yokosuka Naval Arsenal on 1 August 1942 and launched on 25 September 1943." .

ns1:62857 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Brazilian Grand Prix (officially the Formula 1 Grande Prêmio do Brasil 2007) was a Formula One auto racing held at the Autódromo José Carlos Pace in São Paulo, Brazil on 21 October 2007. The 71-lap race was the seventeenth and final round of the 2007 Formula One World Championship. Their fellow championship contender Kimi Räikkönen qualified third, while his Ferrari teammate Felipe Massa took the ninth pole position of his career at his home Grand Prix. At the start, the Ferraris of Felipe Massa and Kimi Räikkönen were faster, with the Finn passing Lewis Hamilton on the outside." .

ns1:63003 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Abu Dhabi Grand Prix (officially known as the 2016 Formula 1 Etihad Airways Abu Dhabi Grand Prix) was a Formula One motor race held on 27 November 2016 at the Yas Marina Circuit in Abu Dhabi, United Arab Emirates. 2015 Abu Dhabi Grand Prix race winner Nico Rosberg entered the round with a twelve-point lead over his teammate Lewis Hamilton in the World Drivers' Championship." .

ns1:63197 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 Food City 500 was the fifth race of the 2009 NASCAR Sprint Cup Series. This 500 lap, 266.5 miles (428.9 km) race took place on 22 March 2009 of that year at the 0.533 miles (0.858 km) Bristol Motor Speedway in Bristol, Tennessee, and was telecast on Fox beginning at 1:30 PM US EDT, with radio broadcasting being handled by Performance Racing Network (terrestrial) and Sirius XM Radio (satellite) beginning at 1 PM US EDT. The pundits predicted that Carl Edwards, Jeff Gordon and Kyle Busch would be triumphant in the race while Mark Martin, Jamie McMurray, David Ragan and Brian Vickers didn't have much of a chance to finish the race in a respectable position." .

ns1:63210 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Narva (Tsardom of Russia: Осада Нарвы, Swedish Empire: Belägringen av Narva), also known as the Second Battle of Narva, was the second Tsardom of Russia siege of Swedish Empire Narva during the Great Northern War from 27 June 1704 to 09 August 1704. The siege came four years after the first battle of Narva, where the Russians were defeated by a much smaller Swedish Empire force defending the city. Tsar Peter I marched to the area again with a reorganized army in an attempt to capture Narva and occupy Swedish Empire Ingria, previously a Swedish Empire logistical center and territory ceded by Russia in 1617 Marshal Boris Sheremetev's force of 20,000 captured Tartu on 24 June and then Tsardom of Russia forces led by Georg Benedict von Ogilvy besieged Narva, with the garrison under the Commandant Major-General Henning Rudolf Horn af Ranzien and consisting of only 3,800 infantry and 1,300 cavalry After a long siege followed by a three-fronted attack, the Russians captured Narva on 20 09 August 1704, massacring hundreds of its Swedish Empire garrison and inhabitants before Peter I stopped them General Horn, several officers and many Swedish Empire soldiers were captured, after roughly 3,200 casualties in the siege and aftermath. On 11 September, the surviving citizens of Narva swore allegiance to Peter I in the courtyard of the town hall, and the city was incorporated into the Tsardom of Russia Tsardom." .

ns1:63303 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Bahrain Grand Prix (formally known as the 2012 Formula 1 Gulf Air Bahrain Grand Prix) was a Formula One motor race held on 22 April 2012 at the Bahrain International Circuit in Sakhir, Bahrain. The race, the eighth running of the Bahrain Grand Prix, was contested over 57 laps and was the fourth round of the 2012 Formula One World Championship. As the 2011 race was cancelled, the teams arrived in Bahrain with no data on how the Pirelli tyres introduced for the 2011 season would behave at the Sakhir circuit." .

ns1:63312 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Movistar Open was a professional men's tennis event on the 2007 ATP Tour in Viña del Mar, Chile, held from 29 January 2007 to 04 February 2007. It was the 15th edition of the Chile Open and was part of the ATP International Series of the 2007 ATP Tour. This was the second Chile Open of the season (alongside Adelaide, Delray Beach, Buenos Aires and Las Vegas) that implemented a 24-player Round Robin Chile Open for the singles competition, as part of the 2007 ATP Tour proposed during this season." .

ns1:63354 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Porte de Vincennes siege was an attack and hostage crisis that occurred in porte de Vincennes (20th arrondissement of Paris) in the wake of the Charlie Hebdo shooting two days earlier, and concurrently with the Dammartin-en-Goële hostage crisis in which the two Charlie Hebdo gunmen were cornered. Amedy Coulibaly had pledged allegiance to the Islamic State of Iraq and the Levant, and was a close friend of Saïd Kouachi and Chérif Kouachi (whom he had met in jail in 2005), the gunmen in the Charlie Hebdo attack. On 09 January 2015, Amedy Coulibaly, who had pledged allegiance to the Islamic State of Iraq and the Levant, attacked the people in a Hypercacher kosher food supermarket at porte de Vincennes in east Paris." .

ns1:63506 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1975 Australian constitutional crisis, also known simply as the Dismissal, culminated on 1975 with the dismissal from office of the Prime Minister, Gough Whitlam of the Australian Labor Party (ALP), by Governor-General Sir John Kerr, who then commissioned the Leader of the Opposition, Malcolm Fraser of the Liberal Party, as caretaker Prime Minister. In 1975, Whitlam decided to appoint Senator Murphy a justice of the High Court of Australia, even though Murphy's Senate seat would not be up for election if a half-Senate election were held. The Proclamation which you have just heard read by the Governor-General's Official Secretary was countersigned Malcolm Fraser, who will undoubtedly go down in Australian history from Remembrance Day 1975 as Kerr's cur. The reply from the Queen's Private Secretary, Sir Martin Charteris, dated 1975, stated: As we understand the situation here, the Australian Constitution firmly places the prerogative powers of the Crown in the hands of the Governor-General as the representative of the Queen of Australia. Her Majesty, as Queen of Australia, is watching events in Canberra with close interest and attention, but it would not be proper for her to intervene in person in matters which are so clearly placed within the jurisdiction of the Governor-General by the Constitution Act. The dismissal is considered the greatest political and constitutional crisis in Australia's history. In the personal sense, Sir John Kerr himself became the real victim of the Dismissal, and history has accorded a brutal if poignant truth to Whitlam's declaration on the steps of Parliament House on 1975: \"Well may we say 'God Save the Queen' – because nothing will save the Governor-General\". According to Whitlam's biographer Jenny Hocking, Kerr's papers in the National Archives of Australia reveal that he discussed his reserve powers and the possibility that he would dismiss the Whitlam government with Charles, Prince of Wales, in 1975." .

ns1:63600 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Like the championship, 1992 Ukrainian Cup had a tight schedule as the Football Federation of Ukraine was given just several months in order to switch to the European seasonal format with the minimum required matches played. The competition started on February 10 and 1992 Ukrainian Cup Final was played on 31 May 1992. The tournament started on 1992, with the game between Podillia Khmelnytsky and Bukovyna Chernivtsi right in the middle of the Zakarpattia Oblast in the village of Ilnytsia and culminated in 1992 Ukrainian Cup Final game in Kiev on 31 May 1992. With the organization of the Ukrainian Cup competition the three Ukrainian clubs Dynamo Kyiv, FC Chornomorets Odesa, and Metalist Kharkiv that still were in competition of the Soviet Cup with their Quarterfinals games scheduled on 1992, had those fixtures canceled, abandoning that competition. All games took place on 16 February 1992, except the game in Zakarpattia between Podillia – Bukovyna which took place on 1992." .

ns1:63709 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 E3 Harelbeke was a road cycling one-day race that took place on 23 March 2018 in Belgium. It was the 61st edition of the E3 Saxo Bank Classic and the tenth event of the 2018 UCI World Tour. The race was won by Niki Terpstra (Quick-Step Floors), who stayed 20 seconds clear of an elite group, led home by his teammate Philippe Gilbert, with BMC Racing Team's Greg Van Avermaet completing the podium. As E3 Saxo Bank Classic was a 2018 UCI World Tour event, all eighteen UCI WorldTeams were invited automatically and obliged to enter a team in the race." .

ns1:63726 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 World Allround Speed Skating Championships took place on 2012 and 18 February 2012, at the Krylatskoye Sport Complex in Moscow, Russia. Sven Kramer became world allround champion for the fifth time, tying the World Allround Speed Skating Championships by Oscar Mathisen and Clas Thunberg. NQ = Not qualified for the 5000m (only the best 12 are qualified) Note: Marrit Leenstra did qualify for the 5000m but withdrew, Jilleanne Rookard took her place Source: live.ISUresults.eu." .

ns1:41578 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Easter Rising (Irish: Éirí Amach na Cásca), also known as the Easter Rebellion, was an armed rebellion in Ireland during Easter Week in 24 April 1916. Members of the Irish Volunteers, led by schoolmaster and Irish language activist Patrick Pearse, joined by the smaller Irish Citizen Army of James Connolly and 200 women of Cumann na mBan, seized strategically important buildings in Dublin and proclaimed the Irish Republic. Another militant group, the Irish Citizen Army, was formed by trade unionists as a result of the Dublin Lock-out of that year. When the Irish Volunteers smuggled rifles into Dublin, the British Army attempted to stop them and shot into a crowd of civilians. James Connolly—head of the Irish Citizen Army (ICA), a group of armed socialist trade union men and women—was unaware of the IRB's plans, and threatened to start a rebellion on his own if other parties failed to act. On Wednesday 19 April, Alderman Tom Kelly, a Sinn Féin member of Dublin Corporation, read out at a meeting of the corporation a document purportedly leaked from Dublin Castle, detailing plans by the British authorities to shortly arrest leaders of the Irish Volunteers, Sinn Féin and the Gaelic League, and occupy their premises. They decided that the Rising would go ahead the following day, Easter Monday, and that the Irish Volunteers and Irish Citizen Army would go into action as the 'Army of the Irish Republic'. On the morning of Monday 24 April 1916, about 1,200 members of the Irish Volunteers and Irish Citizen Army mustered at several locations in central Dublin. In the south, around 1,200 Volunteers commanded by Tomás Mac Curtain mustered on the Sunday in Cork, but they dispersed on Wednesday after receiving nine contradictory orders by dispatch from the Volunteer leadership in Dublin. In County Louth, Volunteers shot dead an RIC man near the village of Castlebellingham on 24 April 1916, in an incident in which 15 RIC men were also taken prisoner. On 29 April 1916, the Royal Dublin Fusiliers under Company Quartermaster Sergeant Robert Flood shot dead two British officers and two Irish civilian employees of the Guinness brewery after he decided they were rebels. McGarry writes that the Irish Citizen Army \"were more ruthless than Volunteers when it came to shooting policemen\" and attributes this to the \"acrimonious legacy\" of the Dublin Lock-out. The Easter Rising lasted from Easter Monday 24 April 1916 to Easter Saturday 29 April 1916." .

ns1:43985 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Ten-Day War (Slovene: desetdnevna vojna), or the Slovenian War of Independence (slovenska osamosvojitvena vojna), was a brief conflict that followed Slovenia's declaration of independence from Yugoslavia on civil war in Yugoslavia following the Slovenian declaration of independence on 25 June 1991. It was fought between the Slovenian Territorial Defence (Slovene: Teritorialna obramba Republike Slovenije) and the Yugoslav People's Army (JNA). It lasted from 27 June 1991 until 07 July 1991, when the Brioni Accords were signed. Slovenia and Croatia passed the acts about their independence on civil war in Yugoslavia following the Slovenian declaration of independence on 25 June 1991. On the morning of 26 June, units of the Yugoslav People's Army's 13th Corps left their barracks in Rijeka, Croatia, to move towards Slovenia's borders with Italy. The military leadership of the Fifth Military District, which included Slovenia, was in telephone contact with Slovenian president Milan Kučan, telling him that the troops' mission was limited to taking over the border crossings and airport. In the afternoon of 27 June 1991, the Slovenian TO (Slovenian Territorial Defence) shot down two YPA helicopters with SA-7 missiles, one of them a Gazelle over Rožna Dolina, Ljubljana, killing the occupants (one of whom, Toni Mrlak, was a Slovenian pilot, as the YPA's forces consisted of nationals from all the republics). The Slovenian Territorial Defence also took up positions around YPA barracks in various locations, effectively besieging them, and launched a series of attacks on YPA forces across Slovenia. By midnight on 27 June 1991 it had captured all of the crossings along the Italian border, all but three crossings on the Austrian border and several of the new crossing points established along Slovenia's border with Croatia. The Slovenian defence ministry ordered: At all locations where RS (Republic of Slovenia) armed forces (Slovenian Territorial Defence) have the tactical advantage, offensive actions against enemy units and facilities will be carried out. Defence Minister Veljko Kadijević informed the Yugoslav cabinet that the YPA's first plan – a limited operation to secure Slovenia's border crossings – had failed, and that it was time to put into operation the backup plan of a full-scale invasion and imposition of military rule in Slovenia. The Ten-Day War was formally ended with the Brioni Agreement, signed on the Croatian Brijuni Islands. The terms were distinctly favourable to Slovenia; it was agreed that Slovenia and Croatia would postpone their independence for three months—which in practical terms had little real impact—and the Slovenian police and armed forces (Slovenian Territorial Defence) were recognised as sovereign on their territory." .

ns1:46105 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Women's Hockey World Cup was the 14th edition of the Women's Hockey World Cup, a field hockey tournament. It was held from 21 July 2018 to 5 August 2018 at the Lee Valley Hockey and Tennis Centre in London, England. 2014 Women's Hockey World Cup the Netherlands women's national field hockey team won the tournament for a record eighth time after defeating Ireland women's national field hockey team 6–0 in the final, who claimed their first World Cup medal. Spain women's national field hockey team won the third place match by defeating Australia women's national field hockey team 3–1 to claim their first World Cup medal as well. In March 2013, one month after the International Hockey Federation published the Event Assignment Process Document for the 2014–2018 cycle, Australia women's national field hockey team, Belgium, England and New Zealand were shortlisted as candidates for hosting the event and were demanded to submit bidding documentation, requirement that eventually Belgium did not meet. Also chosen to host the 2015 EuroHockey Nations Championship for men and women, the tournament will be held at the Lee Valley Hockey and Tennis Centre within the Queen Elizabeth Olympic Park in London, England. There were 126 goals scored in 36 matches, for an average of 3.5 goals per match." .

ns1:46226 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "'flaming') is a form of late Gothic architecture that developed in Europe in the Late Middle Ages and Renaissance, from around 1375 to the mid-16th century. Ribs in Flamboyant tracery are recognizable by their flowing forms, which are influenced by the earlier curvilinear tracery of the Gothic architecture (or Second Pointed) styles. Notable examples of Flamboyant style are the west rose window of Sainte-Chapelle (1485–1498); the west porch of the Church of Saint-Maclou, Rouen, (c.1500–1514); the west front of Troyes Cathedral (early 16th century), and a very early example, the upper tracery of Great West Window of York Minster (1338–1339). In architectural history, the Flamboyant is considered the last phase of French Gothic architecture and appeared in the closing decades of the 14th century, succeeding the Rayonnant style and prevailing until its gradual replacement by Renaissance architecture during the first third of the 16th century. Notable examples of Flamboyant in France include the west rose window of Sainte-Chapelle in Paris, the transepts of Sens Cathedral and Beauvais Cathedral, the façade of Sainte-Chapelle de Vincennes and the west front of Trinity Abbey, Vendôme. What has been termed \"proto-Flamboyant\" appeared at the Saint-Ouen Abbey, Rouen in the inner wall of the north transept between 1390 and 1410. The term \"Flamboyant\" was coined in the early 19th century, primarily to refer to French monuments with flame-like, curvilinear tracery that were constructed between circa 1380 and 1515. The Sainte-Chapelle de Vincennes had a single floor and the windows, consisting of curvilinear tracery, covered nearly all of the walls. The building's most prominent architectural feature is its monumental west façade, which was completed in the Flamboyant style in the 15th century. The incorporation of Flamboyant Gothic with the classicizing forms of Italy produced eclectic, hybrid structures that were rooted in traditional French building practices yet modernized through the application of imported antique motifs and surface decoration. Before the unification of Spain, monuments were constructed in the Flamboyant style in the Crown of Aragon and Kingdom of Valencia, where Marc Safont was among the most important architects of the Late Middle Ages. The building includes elements borrowed by the English Perpendicular style, tracery inspired by French Flamboyant, and German-inspired openwork steeples. This architectural response to increasing concerns with the aesthetics of urban space is particularly notable in Normandy, where a striking group of late 15th- and early 16th-century projecting polygonal porches were constructed in the Flamboyant style; examples include Notre-Dame, Alençon; La Trinité, Falaise; Church of Notre-Dame de Louviers; and Church of Saint-Maclou." .

ns1:46666 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Potsdam Agreement (German: Potsdamer Abkommen) was the 02 August 1945 agreement between three of the Allies of World War II, the United Kingdom, the United States of America, and the Soviet Union. In the Three Power Conference of Berlin (formal title of the Potsdam Conference) from 17 July to 02 August 1945, they agreed to and adopted the Protocol of the Proceedings, August 1, 1945, signed at Cecilienhof Castle in Potsdam. The signatories were General Secretary Joseph Stalin, President Harry S. Truman, and Prime Minister Clement Attlee, who, as a result of the British general election of 1945, had replaced Winston Churchill as the UK's representative. In the Potsdam Agreement (Berlin Conference) the Allies (UK, USSR, US) agreed on the following matters: The Three Governments have taken note of the discussions which have been proceeding in recent weeks in London between British, United States of America, Soviet and French representatives with a view to reaching agreement on the methods of trial of those major war criminals whose crimes under the Moscow Declaration of October 1943 have no particular geographical localization. Moreover, towards concluding the Pacific Theatre of War, the Potsdam Conference issued the Potsdam Declaration, the Proclamation Defining Terms for Japanese Surrender (26 July 1945) wherein the Western Allies (UK, US, USSR) and the Nationalist China of General Chiang Kai-shek asked Japan to surrender or be destroyed." .

ns1:47561 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Africa Cup was the sixteenth edition of the Africa Cup, an annual international rugby union competition for African nations organised by Rugby Africa. The matches was played between 02 July 2016 and 06 August 2016. The top two teams from 2015 Africa Cup, Namibia national rugby union team and Zimbabwe played two home matches each, while Kenya (who finished third in 2015 Africa Cup) and Uganda (who were promoted from Division 1B) hosted one match each. The following teams took part in the 2016 Africa Cup Division 1A: Uganda were promoted from the 2015 Africa Cup Division 1B. The Pool B tournament between Madagascar, Senegal national rugby union team and Zambia was played in Antananarivo, Madagascar bet12 2016 June19 2016 2016, while the Pool A tournament between Botswana, Ivory Coast and Tunisia was played in Tunis, Tunisi26 June 2016en 262 02 July 2016 02 July 2016. The following teams took part in the 2016 Africa Cup Division 1B: Tunisia were relegated from the 2015 Africa Cup Division 1A, while Zambia were promoted from the 2015 Africa Cup Division 1C. The final log for the 2016 Africa Cup Division 1B were: The matches of Division 1C was played between 2016 and 16 at Stade du C.O.C. The winner of 2016 Africa Cup Division 1C will win promotion to the 2017 Rugby Africa season, the second phase of the Rugby World Cup qualification process. The following teams took part in the 2016 Africa Cup Division 1C: Morocco national rugby union team remained in Division 1C despite not playing in the 2015 Africa Cup, while Mauritius were relegated from the 2015 Africa Cup Division 1B." .

ns1:47751 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The General Framework Agreement for Peace in Bosnia and Herzegovina, also known as the Dayton Agreement or the Dayton Accords, (Serbo-Croatian: Dejtonski mirovni sporazum / Дејтонски мировни споразум) is the peace agreement reached at Wright-Patterson Air Force Base near Dayton, Ohio, United States, on 21 November 1995, and formally signed in Paris, on 14 December 1995. The main participants from the region were the President of the Republic of Serbia Slobodan Milošević (whom the Bosnian Serbs had previously empowered to represent their interests), President of Croatia Franjo Tuđman, and President of Bosnia and Herzegovina Alija Izetbegović with his Foreign Minister Muhamed Šaćirbeg. After having been initialed in Dayton, Ohio, on 21 November 1995, the full and formal agreement was signed in Paris on 14 December 1995 and witnessed by Spanish Prime Minister Felipe González, French President Jacques Chirac, US President Bill Clinton, UK Prime Minister John Major, German Chancellor Helmut Kohl and Russian Prime Minister Viktor Chernomyrdin. The Dayton Agreement was aimed at allowing Bosnia and Herzegovina to move from an early post-conflict phase through reconstruction and consolidation, adopting a consociational power-sharing approach." .

ns1:48108 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2016 Icelandic parliamentary election were held in Iceland on 29 October 2016. The Independence Party emerged as the largest in the Althing, winning 21 of the 63 seats; the Progressive Party, which had won the most seats in 2013 Icelandic parliamentary election, lost more than half its seats as it was overtaken by the Left-Green Movement and the Pirate Party Iceland. A new coalition was formed on 10 January 2017, consisting of the Independence Party, the Viðreisn and Bright Future, with Bjarni Benediktsson becoming Prime Minister on 11 January 2017. The President, Olafur Ragnar Grimsson, then said he would speak to both coalition parties, Progressive Party and Independence Party, before considering whether to call new elections. Sigurður Ingi replaced Sigmundur Davíð as the party chairman of the Progressive Party on 2 29 October 2016. The Pirate Party Iceland announced on 16 29 October 2016 that they would not participate in post-election negotiations to form a coalition government with either the Progressive Party or the Independence Party. The party did send letters to Reform, Bright Future, Social Democratic Alliance and Left-Green Movement about the possibility of forming an alliance prior to the election. Neither of the two main blocs — the outgoing coalition of the Independence Party and the Progressives, or the centre-left opposition (Left-Greens, Pirates, Bright Future and Social Democrats) — secured an overall majority, leaving the new centrist party Reform as possible kingmakers. The Pirate Party Iceland proposed a five-party coalition with the Left-Green Movement, the Social Democrats, Bright Future and Reform, having previously ruled out working with either of the two outgoing coalition members. The Pirate Party Iceland then suggested a minority coalition of Left-Green Movement, Bright Future and Reform, with outside support from themselves and the Social Democrats, in order to simplify the process of government formation. On 11 November, the Independence Party, Reform and Bright Future entered into formal coalition talks, but the three parties failed to agree with a new market-based fishing quota system and an EU referendum as the main stumbling blocks. On 2 January 2017, the Independence Party started official talks about a possible coalition deal with the Viðreisn and Bright Future. Morgunblaðið also reported that the Left-Green Movement and the Progressive Party had also discussed possible coalition deals with the Independence Party. A new coalition was formed on 10 January 2017 between Independence Party, Viðreisn and the Bright Future with Bjarni Benediktsson becoming Prime Minister on 11 January 2017." .

ns1:48998 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Magic 7 is an animated television film written and directed by Roger Holzberg. Other actors, such as Michael J. Fox, James Earl Jones, Jeremy Irons, Ice-T and Dirk Benedict, all recorded their voices in the early 1990s. Kevin Bacon, Bette Midler, and Judy Collins appear as themselves." .

ns1:51119 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Women's Tour Down Under was a women's cycle stage race held in Australia from 11 to 14 January 2018, 2018 in women's road cycling. The Women's Tour Down Under, being held for the seventh time, was held as a UCI rating of 2.1 race, having been a 2.2 race in 2017. Spratt won the race by 41 seconds ahead of Lauren Stephens, riding for Cylance Pro Cycling. 2016 race winner Katrin Garfoot completed the podium, riding for the UniSA-Australia team, a further 40 seconds in arrears of Stephens. Mitchelton–Scott won the teams classification, after placing Spratt, Lucy Kennedy and Annemiek van Vleuten in the top-six overall. 17 teams participated in the 2018 Women's Tour Down Under. For winning a stage, a rider earned 16 points, with 12 for second, 8 for third, 6 for fourth, with one point fewer per place down to 2 points for 8th place. Points towards the classification could also be accrued – awarded on a 5–3–2 scale – at intermediate sprint points during each stage; these intermediate sprints also offered bonus seconds towards the general classification as noted above." .

ns1:51147 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Fellowship of the Ring is the first of three volumes of the epic novel The Lord of the Rings by the English author J. R. R. Tolkien. It was originally published on 29 July 1954 in the United Kingdom. They were the hobbit and ringbearer Frodo Baggins and his gardener Samwise Gamgee, the wizard Gandalf, the elf Legolas, the dwarf Gimli, the men Aragorn the ranger and Boromir of Gondor, and the young hobbits Meriadoc Brandybuck and Peregrin Took." .

ns1:52702 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Sinking of Dongfang zhi Xing (Chinese: 东方之星; pinyin: Dōngfāng zhī Xīng; translated as Oriental Star or Eastern Star) was a river cruise ship that operated in the Three Gorges region of inland People's Republic of China. On the night of 01 June 2015, the ship was traveling on the Yangtze when it capsized during a thunderstorm in Jianli, Hubei Province with 454 people on board. On 13, 442 deaths were confirmed, with 12 survivors (including two rescued by officials). The ship met gusts of 32–38 m/s (72-85 mph, equivalent to wind force scale of 12-13). Around 21:28 on 01 June 2015, Dongfang zhi Xing was making a 1,500 km (930 mi) trip from Nanjing to Chongqing on the Yangtze. The captain and the chief engineer said that the ship was hit by a tornado, and the People's Republic of China Meteorological Administration confirmed that a tornado occurred in Jianli with wind-speeds reaching EF1 strength, or approximately 138–177 km/h (86–110 mph). It was once widely believed that the tornado struck the river near the ship's location, but later investigation suggested that the tornado struck a location 8 km away and did not affect the ship; instead, based on Doppler radar data and other evidence, the official report found that a massive downburst in the thunderstorm, with gusts over 118 km/h (73 mph) (12–13 on the Beaufort scale), was the likely cause of the capsizing. On 13, the Chinese authorities updated that 454, not 456, people were on board and 442 bodies had been found with 12 rescued. Dongfang zhi Xing, as well as other Yangtze cruise ships, had come under increasing government scrutiny in recent years due to the growing tourist industry in People's Republic of China. In 2013, Dongfang zhi Xing and five other ships were cited for safety violations by the Nanjing maritime bureau, though the Chongqing Eastern Shipping Corporation did not comment on the nature of these infractions." .

ns1:53071 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Tour of Austria (German: Österreich-Rundfahrt 2017) was the 69th edition of the Tour of Austria cycling stage race. The 1,121.7-kilometre (697.0 mi)-long race started in Graz on 02 July 2017 with a prologue individual time trial up the Schloßberg hill, and concluded in Wels on 08 July 2017. The race was part of the 2017 UCI Europe Tour, and was rated as a 2.1 event. Denifl finished 37 seconds clear of Spain's Delio Fernández Cruz, riding for the Delko–Marseille Provence KTM squad, while the podium was completed by Colombian Miguel Ángel López of Astana, 59 seconds down on Denifl but a winner of the race's queen stage to the Kitzbüheler Horn. In the race's other classifications, Sep Vanmarcke (Cannondale–Drapac) was the winner of the points classification, Pieter Weening of Roompot–Nederlandse Loterij was the winner of the mountains classification, while Team Dimension Data were the winners of the teams classification. Points towards the classification could also be accrued – awarded on a 4–2–1 scale – at intermediate sprint points during each stage; these intermediate sprints also offered bonus seconds towards the general classification as noted above." .

ns1:53420 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 FIVB Volleyball Men's Nations League was the inaugural edition of the FIVB Volleyball Men's Nations League, a new annual international men's volleyball tournament contested by 16 national teams that replaced the former World League in the international calendar. The competition was held between May and 08 July 2018 and the final round took place in the Stade Pierre-Mauroy, Villeneuve-d'Ascq, Lille metropolitan area, France men's national volleyball team. Russia men's national volleyball team won the inaugural edition, defeating France men's national volleyball team in straight sets in the final. Both teams contesting the final had previously won the World League – Russia men's national volleyball team on three opportunities and France men's national volleyball team on two. United States men's national volleyball team, which also had won the World League twice, defeated the reigning Olympic champions and 9-time World League winners Brazil men's national volleyball team in straight sets for the bronze medal. South Korea men's national volleyball team were the last placed challenger team after the preliminary round and were replaced by 2018 Challenger Cup winners Portugal in the 2019 FIVB Volleyball Men's Nations League. The 16 national teams involved in the tournament were required to register a squad of 21 players, which every week's 14-player roster must be selected from." .

ns1:53517 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Cool Spring, also known as Castleman's Ferry, Island Ford, Parker's Ford, and Snicker's Ferry, was a battle in the American Civil War fought July 17–18, 1864, in Clarke County, Virginia, as part of the Valley Campaigns of 1864. On 17 July 1864, the Union cavalry passed westward through Snicker's Gap and unsuccessfully attempted to force passage of the river at Castleman's Ferry (Snicker's Ford). At 3 p.m., Thoburn crossed the Shenandoah below Castleman's Ferry, at Judge Richard Parker's Ford, quickly driving off a small force of Confederate pickets." .

ns1:55889 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Eliteserien was the 72nd completed season of top-tier association football in Norway. The season began on 01 April 2017 and ended on 26 November 2017, not including play-off matches. Rosenborg BK were the defending champions, while Kristiansund BK and Sandefjord Fotball entered as the promoted teams from the 2016 1. The 2017 season saw the name of the league change from Tippeligaen (named after sponsor Norsk Tipping) to Eliteserien, a non-sponsor affiliated name controlled by the Norwegian Football Federation. Rosenborg BK conceded only 20 goals during the season, a league record. On 28 August 2016, the Norwegian Football Federation (NFF) and Norsk Toppfotball announced a rebrand; beginning with the 2017 season, the competition was known simply as Eliteserien, without any sponsor's name attached. Rosenborg BK were the defending champions, while Kristiansund BK and Sandefjord Fotball entered as the promoted teams from the 2016 1. Aalesunds FK and Viking FK were relegated directly. The promoted teams were Kristiansund BK (first season in the top-flight) and Sandefjord Fotball, (returning to the top flight after a season's absence). The 14th-placed team, Sogndal Fotball took part in a two-legged play-off against Ranheim, the winners of the 2017 Norwegian First Division, to decide who will play in the 2018 Eliteserien." .

ns1:56750 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Ottoman–Portuguese conflicts were a series of armed military encounters between the Portuguese Empire, the Ormus and the Ethiopian Empire against the Ottoman Empire and Adal Sultanate, in the Indian Ocean, the Persian Gulf, the Red Sea and in East Africa. This war took place upon the backdrop of the Ethiopian-Adal Sultanate War. Major hostilities between Portugal and the Ottoman Empire began in 1538, when the Ottomans assisted the Gujarat Sultanate with about 80 vessels to lay siege to Diu, which had been built by the Portuguese in 1535. The Ottomans then shifted their focus to the Red Sea, which they had been expanding into previously, with the acquisition of Egypt in 1517, and Aden in 1538." .

ns1:57567 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010–11 Fußball-Bundesliga was the 48th season of the Bundesliga, Germany's premier association football league. It began on 20 August 2010 and concluded on 14 May 2011. Borussia Dortmund earned its seventh league title with two games to spare on 30 April 2011, beating 1. FC Nürnberg 2–0 at home. FC St. Pauli and Eintracht Frankfurt were relegated to the 2011–12 2010–11 2. Fußball-Bundesliga. They were replaced by 1. FC Kaiserslautern, champions of the 2009–10 2010–11 2. Fußball-Bundesliga, and runners-up FC St. Pauli. As in the previous year, 1. FC Nürnberg had to compete, although they were the Bundesliga team this time. Borussia Mönchengladbach as 16th-placed team faced 3rd-placed 2010–11 2. Fußball-Bundesliga side VfL Bochum in a two-legged play-off. Borussia Mönchengladbach won 2–1 on aggregate and retained its Fußball-Bundesliga spot for the 2011–12 Fußball-Bundesliga." .

ns1:57742 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Minatogawa (Japanese: 湊川の戰い), also known as the Battle of Minato River, was a battle of the Nanboku-chō period fought near the Minato River in Settsu Province (present day Kōbe, Hyōgo Prefecture) on 5 04 July 1336. The Imperial forces loyal to Emperor Go-Daigo led by Kusunoki Masashige and Nitta Yoshisada attempted to intercept the Ashikaga forces led by Ashikaga Takauji in Settsu. In February 1336, the defeat of the rebellious Ashikaga clan in the Nanboku-chō period forced Ashikaga Takauji to flee the capital Kyoto for the island of Kyushu." .

ns1:59697 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Wuchang Uprising was an armed rebellion against the ruling Qing dynasty that took place in Wuchang (now Wuchang District of Wuhan), Hubei, People's Republic of China on 10 October 1911, which was the beginning of the Xinhai Revolution that successfully overthrew People's Republic of China's last imperial dynasty. The uprising and the eventual revolution directly led to the downfall of the Qing dynasty with almost three centuries of imperial rule, and the establishment of the Republic of People's Republic of China (ROC), which commemorates the anniversary of the uprising's starting date of 10 October 1911 as the National Day of the Republic of People's Republic of China. On 10 October 1911, the New Army stationed in Wuchang launched an assault on the residence of the Viceroy of Huguang. In response to the uprising, the Qing dynasty called for the help of Yuan Shikai and the Beiyang Army to march toward Wuchang. The Wuchang Uprising took many revolutionary leaders by surprise; Huang Xing and Song Jiaoren were unable to reach Wuchang in time. After the successful uprising in Wuchang, the revolutionaries sent telegraphs to other provinces and asked them to follow their suit, upon which eighteen provinces in Southern and Central People's Republic of China agreed to secede from the Qing dynasty by the end of December, 1911." .

ns1:59831 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017–18 NIFL Premiership (known as the Danske Bank NIFL Premiership for sponsorship reasons) was the 10th season of Northern Ireland's highest national football league in this format since its inception in 2008, the 117th season of Irish league football overall, and the fifth season of the league operating as part of the Northern Ireland Football League. The season began on 11 and concluded on 28, with the play-offs taking place in 01 May 2018. Linfield F.C. were the defending champions from the 2016–17 NIFL Premiership. On 28, Crusaders F.C. won the title on the final day of the regular season, defeating Ballymena United F.C. 2–1 at the Ballymena Showgrounds to win the league title for the third time in four seasons. Portadown were relegated after finishing bottom of the 2016–17 NIFL Premiership and were replaced by Warrenpoint Town F.C. as the winners of the 2016–17 NIFL Championship. Carrick Rangers F.C. finished second from bottom but retained their NIFL Premiership place after winning the promotion-relegation play-off against NIFL Championship promotion play-off winners Institute 5–2 on aggregate. The runners-up and third-placed teams from the Championship were set to take part in a promotion pre-play-off match to decide which team would face the eleventh-placed NIFL Premiership team, Carrick Rangers F.C., in the play-off for a place in next season's NIFL Premiership. The eleventh-placed team from the NIFL Premiership, Carrick Rangers F.C., played Championship runners-up, Newry City, over two legs for a place in the 2018–19 NIFL Premiership. Newry City won 6–3 on aggregate and were promoted to the NIFL Premiership with Carrick Rangers F.C. dropping down into the NIFL Championship." .

ns1:59992 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1964–65 Fußball-Bundesliga was the second season of the Bundesliga, West Germany's premier association football league. It began on 22 August 1964 and ended on 15 May 1965. FC Schalke 04 and Karlsruher SC were originally going to be demoted to the Regionalliga. However, the German Football Association became aware of irregularities regarding transfer fees, signing bonuses and player wages paid by Hertha BSC. Zézé became the first Brazilian to play in the league when he fielded for 1. FC Köln against Hertha BSC on 22 August 1964 while Raoul Tagliari scored the first-ever Bundesliga goal by a Brazilian for MSV Duisburg against 1. FC Nürnberg on 21 November 1964. They were replaced by Hannover 96 and Borussia Neunkirchen, who won their respective promotion play-off groups." .

ns1:60752 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Latin Grammy Award for Record of the Year is an honor presented annually at the Latin Grammy Award, a ceremony that recognizes excellence and creates a wider awareness of cultural diversity and contributions of Latin recording artists in the United States and internationally. The exceptions to this were in 2000, 2009, 2013 and 2014 when \"Dímelo\" by Marc Anthony, \"Aquí Estoy Yo\" by Luis Fonsi featuring David Bisbal, Noel Schajris and Aleks Syntek, \"Volví a Nacer\" by Carlos Vives, and \"Bailando\" by Enrique Iglesias featuring Descemer Bueno and Gente De Zona, respectively, received the Song of the Year award without earning Record of the Year. Alejandro Sanz has won the most awards in the category with seven wins out of eleven nominations, including the award received for \"La Tortura\", his collaboration with Colombian singer-songwriter Shakira. Sanz is followed by Calle 13, Jorge Drexler, Juanes and Shakira with two winning songs. Rafael Arcaute, Gustavo Santaolalla and Lulo Pérez are the most awarded producers, with two wins, while Benny Faccone, Anibal Kerpel and Thom Russo have received the most awards as engineers/mixers, with two each." .

ns1:61120 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "AirAsia Flight 8501 was a scheduled international passenger flight operated by AirAsia Indonesia – an AirAsia Group affiliate – from Surabaya, Indonesia, to Singapore. On 28 December 2014, the Airbus A320 flying the route crashed into the Java Sea, killing all 162 people on board. Flight 8501 was a scheduled flight from Surabaya, Java, Indonesia to Singapore on Sunday, 28 December 2014. It was scheduled to depart Juanda International Airport at 05:20 Western Indonesian Time (WIB, UTC+7) and arrive at Singapore Changi Airport at 08:30 Singapore Standard Time (SST, UTC+8). After departure, Flight 8501 was in contact with the Jakarta Area Control Centre (callsign: \"Jakarta Centre\"), which provides air traffic control (ATC) service over the western Java Sea, and flying along air route M635, when it approached a line of thunderstorms off the southwest coast of Borneo. AirNav Indonesia, which operates the Jakarta Area Control Centre, reported that Jakarta Centre then cleared Flight 8501 to flight level 340 at 06:14, but no response was received; other aircraft in the vicinity were asked to contact Flight 8501, but also did not receive a response. Its last recorded position was over the Java Sea, Karimata Strait between the islands of Belitung and Kalimantan (3.3708°S 109.6911°E / -3.3708; 109.6911 (AirAsia Flight 8501 last transponder signal)). The aircraft crashed in the Java Sea, Karimata Strait between the islands of Belitung and Borneo (3.623°S 109.712°E / -3.623; 109.712 (AirAsia Flight 8501 crash site)). On 31 28 December 2014, AirAsia Indonesia retired the flight number QZ8501, changing the designation of its Surabaya-Singapore route to QZ678. West Kotawaringin regent Ujang Iskandar stated that \"With this monument, we hope that the families and the government will lay flowers every 28 December 2014, and continue the dialogue on aviation safety in Indonesia.\"" .

ns1:62016 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Eurovision Song Contest 2001 was the 46th edition of the annual Eurovision Song Contest. It took place in Copenhagen, Denmark, following the Olsen Brothers' win at the Eurovision Song Contest 2000 in Stockholm, Sweden with the song \"Fly on the Wings of Love\". 23 countries took part in the contest, which was held on 2001. The presenters were Danish journalist and TV-show presenter Natasja Crone Back and the famous Danish actor Søren Pilmark who spoke most of their announcements in rhyming couplets. The contest was won by Estonia, represented by Tanel Padar, Dave Benton & Soul Militia with the song \"Everybody\", written by Ivar Must and Maian-Anna Karmas, which scored a total of 198 points. Copenhagen, the capital of Denmark was the host city for the 46th edition of Eurovision. The venue choice for the contest was Parken Stadium, a football stadium in the Indre Østerbro district of Copenhagen, Denmark, built from 1990–1992. The three largest cities in Denmark - Copenhagen, Aarhus and Odense - all made bids to host the contest. The logo of the 2001 Eurovision Song Contest was made out of four circles, placed in the shape of a heart. Eurovision Song Contest: Copenhagen 2001 was the official compilation album of the 2001 Contest, put together by the European Broadcasting Union and released by EMI Records and CMC International on 2001. The album featured all 23 songs that entered in the 2001 contest." .

ns1:62112 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The World Festival of Black Arts (French: Festival Mondial des Arts Nègres), also known as FESMAN, is a month-long culture and arts festival that takes place in Africa. The First World Festival of Black Arts or World Festival of Negro Arts was held in Dakar, Senegal, 01 April 1966–24 April 1966, initiated by former President Leopold Senghor, under the auspices of UNESCO, with the participation of 45 African, European, Caribbean, and North and South African countries, and featuring black literature, music, theater, visual arts, film and dance. Italian journalist Sergio Borelli produced Il Festival de Dakar (1966) a 50-minute documentary for RAI. In 1977, from 15 January 1977 to 12 February 1977, the FESTAC 77 or Black and African Festival of Arts and Culture — known as FESTAC '77 — took place in Lagos, Nigeria, under the patronage of President Olusegun Obasanjo. The 2010 World Festival of Black Arts 10 December 2010 2010o31 December 2010cember 2010, and was initiated by Senegalese President Abdoulaye Wade with the theme of African Renaissance." .

ns1:62264 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Super Bowl XVI was an American football game between the National Football Conference (NFC) champion San Francisco 49ers and the United States of America Football Conference (AFC) champion Cincinnati Bengals to decide the National Football League (NFL) champion for the 1981 season. The 49ers defeated the Bengals by the score of 26–21 to win their first Super Bowl. The game was played on 24 January 1982, at the Pontiac Silverdome in Pontiac, Michigan, a suburb of Detroit. Super Bowl XVI also became one of the most watched broadcasts in United States of America television history, with more than 85 million viewers, and a final national Nielsen rating of 49.1 (a 73 share). The NFL awarded Super Bowl XVI to Pontiac, Michigan on March 13, 1979 at the owners meetings in Honolulu. Tom Flores was on the Kansas City Chiefs' roster in Super Bowl IV and coached in Super Bowl XV. This is the only Super Bowl to have ever been played at the Pontiac Silverdome. The Super Bowl did return to Michigan for Super Bowl XL, but that game was played at Ford Field in Detroit, which, in 2002, had replaced the Pontiac Silverdome as the home site for the Detroit Lions. The trainer’s calling the plays.’\" The game was televised in the United States of America by CBS and featured the broadcast team of play-by-play announcer Pat Summerall and color commentator John Madden (the latter making his Super Bowl debut as a broadcaster). This was the first of two Super Bowls, both held in Michigan, in which two national anthems were performed, and to have a joint Canadian-United States of America armed forces color guard, which consisted of members of the Royal Canadian Mounted Police and Camp Grayling color guards. After returning the opening kickoff 17 yards, San Francisco's Amos Lawrence was hit by Bengals linebacker Guy Frazier and fumbled at his own 26-yard line (the first time in a Super Bowl that a turnover took place on the opening kick), where John Simmons recovered for Cincinnati. As they lined-up for a field goal attempt, a false start penalty against San Francisco pushed them back 5 yards but Wersching connected from 26 yards, increasing the 49ers' lead to 20–0, which was the largest halftime lead in Super Bowl history to that date. Anderson completed six consecutive passes on the Bengals' ensuing drive, the last one a 3-yard touchdown pass to Ross to make the score 26–21. at Pontiac Silverdome, Pontiac, Michigan." .

ns1:62555 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1950 Italian Grand Prix was a Formula One motor race held on 03 September 1950 at Autodromo Nazionale Monza. It was race 7 of 7 in the 1950 Formula One season. After Juan Manuel Fangio's win at the 1950 French Grand Prix, Fangio had obtained 26 points, two ahead of teammate Luigi Fagioli and four ahead of another teammate, Giuseppe Farina. Alberto Ascari used it to achieve second place on the grid to Juan Manuel Fangio's Alfa Romeo 158 and then in the race behind the fast starting Giuseppe Farina (Alfa Romeo 158) before briefly leading. Farina led to the finish from Ascari who was now in teammate Dorino Serafini's Ferrari 375 with Luigi Fagioli finishing third in his Alfa Romeo 158." .

ns1:62783 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Monaco Grand Prix (formally known as the Formula 1 Grand Prix de Monaco 2016 Formula One World Championship) was a Formula One motor race held on 29 May 2016 at the Circuit de Monaco, a street circuit that runs through the Monaco. It was the sixth round of the 2016 Formula One World Championship FIA Formula One World Championship, and marked the seventy-fourth time that the Monaco Grand Prix had been held, as well as the sixty-third time it had been held as a round of the Formula One World Championship since the series inception in 1950. Following their controversial crash and double retirement at 2016 Spanish Grand Prix two weeks earlier, particular attention was on championship leaders Mercedes and their drivers Nico Rosberg and Lewis Hamilton. He urged the team to pull the introduction of the power unit forward to the Monaco race, instead of 2016 Canadian Grand Prix as originally planned. Lewis Hamilton followed in third, a further four points behind, with Sebastian Vettel in fourth with 48 points, level with Daniel Ricciardo in fifth." .

ns1:63480 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Arkesilas Cup is a kylix by the Laconian Laconian vase painting known as the Arkesilas Painter, whose name vase it is. It depicts, and is thus named after, Arcesilaus II of Cyrene, king of Kyrene (d. 550 BC) and is dated to about 565/560 BC. The cup was found at Vulci and is now on display in the Cabinet des Médailles of the Bibliothèque nationale de France in Paris (inv." .

ns1:63585 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011–12 Cypriot First Division was the 73rd season of the Cyprus top-level association football league. It began on 27 August 2011 and ended on 12 May 2012. The league comprise eleven teams from the 2010–11 Cypriot First Division and three promoted teams from the 2010–11 Second Division. They were joined by AEP Paphos, who finished at the bottom of the second-phase Group C. The relegated teams were replaced by 2010–11 Second Division champions Aris Limassol F.C., runners-up Nea Salamis Famagusta FC and third-placed team Anagennisi Deryneia FC." .

ns1:63612 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The men's 100 metres at the 2015 World Championships in Athletics – men's 100 metres was held at the Beijing National Stadium on 22 August 2015 and 23 August 2015. Reigning champion Usain Bolt ran times of 9.96 s in both rounds. In the final, Bolt finished one hundredth of a second ahead of Gatlin in a time of 9.79 s. There was a tie for third place, with Andre De Grasse and Trayvon Bromell both finishing in 9.911 s, and two bronze medals were awarded." .

ns1:41430 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "On the evening of 01 February 1991, USAir Flight 1493, a Boeing 737-300, collided with SkyWest Flight 5569, a Metroliner turboprop aircraft, upon landing at Los Angeles International Airport. USAir Flight 1493 was a scheduled service from Syracuse, New York making stops at Washington, D.C., Columbus, Ohio, and Los Angeles (LAX), before continuing to San Francisco On 01 February 1991, Flight 1493 was operated using a Boeing 737-300 (registration N388US); after a crew change in Washington it was under the command of Captain Colin Shaw, 48, a highly experienced pilot with approximately 16,300 total flight hours (including more than 4,300 hours on the Boeing 737), and First Officer David Kelly, 32, who had approximately 4,300 total flight hours, with 982 hours on the Boeing 737 Flying into LAX, the aircraft had a total of 89 people on board (83 passengers, 4 flight attendants, and the 2 pilots). On 01 February 1991, SkyWest Airlines Flight 5569 was operated using a twin-engine Metroliner (registration N683AV). The flight was scheduled to depart LAX on the final leg of a multi-city schedule, and was bound for Palmdale, California with 10 passengers and 2 pilots aboard Both of Flight 5569's pilots had significant experience; Captain Andrew Lucas, 32, had approximately 8,800 flight hours (with 2,101 of them on the Metroliner), and First Officer Frank Prentice, 45, had approximately 8,000 flight hours, including 1,363 hours on the Metroliner. The local controller attempted to cross the Wings West aircraft but the crew had changed frequencies and did not answer, distracting the local controller as she attempted to reestablish communications Shortly after 6 PM local time, as US Airways 1493 was on final approach to LAX, the Metroliner was cleared by the local controller to taxi into its takeoff position on 24L at the intersection of taxiway 45, some 2,200 feet (670 m) up from the runway threshold. The local controller then cleared the US Airways flight to land on 24L, even though the SkyWest Metroliner was still holding in takeoff position on the runway. The US Airways plane slammed into the Metroliner, crushing it beneath its fuselage. The 35 dead included all 12 people (10 passengers and both crew members) on SkyWest 5569, and 23 of the 89 aboard the US Airways 1493 (21 passengers, Captain Shaw, and one flight attendant)." .

ns1:43123 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1997 Giro d'Italia was the 80th edition of the Giro d'Italia, one of cycling's Grand Tours. The Giro began on 17 May 1997 with a mass-start stage that began and ended in Venice. The race came to a close on 08 June 1997 with a mass-start stage that ended in the Italian city of Milan. Eighteen teams entered the race that was won by the Italian Ivan Gotti of the Saeco team. Second and third were the Russian rider Pavel Tonkov and Italian Giuseppe Guerini. In the race's other classifications, Kelme rider Chepe González won the mountains classification, Mario Cipollini of the Saeco team won the points classification, and Roslotto–ZG Mobili rider Dimitri Konyshev won the Intergiro classification in the Giro d'Italia. Eighteen teams were invited by the race organizers to participate in the 1997 edition of the Giro d'Italia. The eighteen teams that took part in the race were: The route for the 1997 Giro d'Italia was unveiled by race director Carmine Castellano on 9 November 1996 in Milan." .

ns1:48425 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The wedding of Victoria, Crown Princess of Sweden, and Daniel Westling took place on 19 June 2010 in Storkyrkan. The wedding was set to take place in Storkyrkan on 19 June 2010, the 34th anniversary of her parents' marriage and a traditional wedding date of the Swedish Royal Family (several past House of Bernadotte family members had married on that date). On 24 November 2009 it was decided that the days between the Swedish national day on 6 June and the wedding date on 19 June 2010 would be Love Stockholm 2010 days. The wedding service began at 15:30 local time in Storkyrkan, with around 1,100 guests present. He was assisted by Lars-Göran Lönnermark, Royal Court Chief Chaplain, and Dr Antje Jackelén, Bishop of Lund. On the seats of honour were sitting the wedding couple; their parents King Carl Gustaf and Queen Silvia and Mr and Mrs Westling; Victoria's godparents Ralf de Toledo Sommerlath, Princess Désirée, Baroness Silfverschiöld, the Queen of the Netherlands and the King of Norway as well as Count Carl Johan Bernadotte of Wisborg, the King's uncle; Princess Margaretha, Mrs. Ambler, the King's eldest sister; the Queen of Denmark, the King of the Belgians; Tarja Halonen, the President of Finland; and Anders Wejryd, the Archbishop of Uppsala. Media related to Wedding of Victoria, Crown Princess of Sweden, and Prince Daniel, Duke of Västergötland at Wikimedia Commons." .

ns1:51621 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The first edition of 2017 Liège–Bastogne–Liège (women), a road cycling one-day race in Belgium, was held on 23 April 2017. The race started in Bastogne and finished in Ans, containing four categorized climbs and covering a total distance of 135.5 km. 139 riders started the race, 98 finished. At 20 km from the finish, Polish rider Katarzyna Niewiadoma attacked on the upper slopes of the Roche aux Faucons and was rejoined by Lizzie Deignan, Anna van der Breggen, Ashleigh Moolman-Pasio and Elisa Longo Borghini." .

ns1:51998 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1907 Tour de France was the fifth running of the annual Tour de France, one of cycling's Grand Tours. From 08 July 1907 to 04 August 1907, the 93 cyclists cycled 4488 km (2,788 mi) in fourteen stages around France. The winner, Lucien Petit-Breton, completed the race at an average speed of 28.47 km/h (17.69 mi/h). Alcyon started with three main contenders: Louis Trousselier, Marcel Cadolle and Léon Georget; Peugeot counted on Émile Georget. Early in the race, Trousselier, François Faber and Émile Georget were the main contenders. At the end of the Tour de France, 33 cyclists remained." .

ns1:52639 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "MSNBC Reports, also titled NBC News or The Place for Politics during election seasons, is a daily United States of America hard news television program on MSNBC, originating from both New York City and Washington, D.C. studios throughout daytime hours. MSNBC Reports is the flagship daytime news platform of NBC News, airing for at least six hours each day on MSNBC. When a story is deemed as breaking news or a special event, MSNBC producers have the discretion to replace any anchor with MSNBC Chief Anchor Brian Williams. Following major changes to MSNBC's dayside lineup announced in February 2015, Thomas Roberts was appointed to a new weekday time slot from 1-3pm ET under the Live branding, beginning on March 2. On December 15, 2016, MSNBC Reports with Thomas Roberts was quietly cancelled with no public announcement made, and was removed from the show the day after. In January 2017, Hallie Jackson was confirmed to host the 10am ET time slot and Katy Tur were confirmed to host the 2pm ET for the first 100 days of Donald Trump's presidency. For instance, between March 3 (the day after Chris Matthews retired from his show, as well as Super Tuesday by coincidence) and July 20, 2020, when Joy Reid was named the permanent replacement, the 7pm weeknight hour was filled with MSNBC Reports: Decision 2020, a hybrid newscast covering both the coronavirus pandemic and 2020 election. For example, Stephanie Ruhle's edition of the show is formatted similarly to a morning news show, while Hallie Jackson's edition usually originates from Washington DC, where she is based, or an on-site location of the President's travels. This portion of the news is primarily reported through daytime by Washington, D.C.-based staffers such as NBC News Capital Hill correspondent Kasie Hunt, judicial correspondent Pete Williams, Chief White House correspondents Hallie Jackson and Kristen Welker, Peter Alexander and national correspondent Chris Jansing. Alex Witt broadcast \"Weekends with Alex Witt\" is now a separate broadcast outside of MSNBC Reports. While Andrea Mitchell Reports and Weekends with Alex Witt are branded separately from MSNBC Reports, their programming style and graphics package are often noticeably similar with MSNBC Reports. MSNBC and NBC News programming is shown for several hours a day on the 24-hour news network OSN News in MENA Region, including MSNBC Reports and several other MSNBC shows." .

ns1:59052 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Tour de Romandie was a road cycling road bicycle racing that took place between 25 April 2017 and 30 April 2017 in Romandie, Switzerland. It was the 71st edition of the Tour de Romandie and the nineteenth event of the 2017 UCI World Tour. The race was won on the final day by Australia's Richie Porte (BMC Racing Team), who overhauled a 19-second deficit to British rider Simon Yates over the 17.88-kilometre (11.11 mi) time trial, and won the race for the first time. Yates – riding for the Orica–Scott squad – was able to finish second overall, holding off LottoNL–Jumbo's Primož Roglič of Slovenia, who won the final time trial and finished just five seconds in arrears of Yates at the finish. In the race's other classifications, Belgian Sander Armée won the mountains classification for the second year in succession for Lotto–Soudal, home rider Stefan Küng won the points classification for the BMC Racing Team, while 14th place overall was enough for Pierre Latour (AG2R La Mondiale) to win the young rider classification. As the Tour de Romandie was a 2017 UCI World Tour event, all eighteen UCI WorldTeams were invited automatically and were obliged to enter a team in the race. Time bonuses were awarded to the first three finishers on road stages (stages 1–4): the stage winner won a ten-second bonus, with six and four seconds for the second and third riders respectively." .

ns1:59174 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1774–75 papal conclave (05 October 1774 – 15 February 1775), was convoked after the death of Pope Clement XIV and ended with the election of Cardinal Giovanni Angelo Braschi, who took the name of Pius VI. The death of a pope frequently presented an opportunity for the citizens of Rome to vent their anti-clerical feelings, often in the context of satires, sometimes salacious, directed at either the late pope or the cardinals. In 1774, the Governor of Rome had occasion to ban a drama entitled the Conclave for offending the \"dignity, decorum, and venerable representation of the Sacred College, as well as, other persons as subjects.\" The other representatives of this faction were Giovanni Battista Rezzonico, his relative Carlo Rezzonico, who occupied the important office of the camerlengo of the Holy Roman Church, Gian Francesco Albani, dean of the College of Cardinals, and Alessandro Albani, archdeacon of the College. The conclave began on 05 October 1774. This initiative obtained support of camerlengo Carlo Rezzonico and of Henry Benedict Stuart, but the anti-Jesuit faction was strong enough to frustrate it. On 15 February 1775, after 134 days of deliberation, on the 265th ballot, Cardinal Giovanni Angelo Braschi was elected to the papacy receiving all votes except his own, which, according to custom, he gave to Gian Francesco Albani, dean of the Sacred College of Cardinals. He took the name of Pius VI, in honour of St. Pius V. On 1775 the Pope-elect was consecrated bishop of Rome by Cardinal Dean Gian Francesco Albani, bishop of Porto e Santa Rufina, assisted by Sub-dean Henry Benedict Stuart, bishop of Frascati, and Camerlengo Carlo Rezzonico, bishop of Sabina." .

ns1:60141 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1990 National League Championship Series was played between the Cincinnati Reds (91–71) and the Pittsburgh Pirates (95–67). It was the first playoff appearance for both teams since 1979 and the fifth NLCS meeting overall with Cincinnati winning the Pennant in 1970, 1972, and 1975 while Pittsburgh won in 1971 and 1979. The Reds won the series, 4–2, and eventually went on to sweep the defending World Champion Oakland Athletics in the 1990 World Series. Between Game 2 (in Cincinnati) and Game 3 (in Pittsburgh), the teams took two days off instead of the usual one. That Sunday, October 7, the Pittsburgh Steelers needed to use Three Rivers Stadium for their scheduled game against the San Diego Chargers, so Game 3 (and by extension, the rest of the series) was pushed back a day. Cincinnati won the series, 4–2. Thursday, 04 October 1990 (8:30PM EDT) at Riverfront Stadium in Cincinnati. Friday, 1990 (3:15PM EDT) at Riverfront Stadium in Cincinnati. Doug Drabek, the National League Cy Young Award winner for 1990 took the mound against Tom Browning for a weekday afternoon Game 2. Monday, 1990 (3:20PM EDT) at Three Rivers Stadium in Pittsburgh. Tuesday, 1990 (8:30PM EDT) at Three Rivers Stadium in Pittsburgh. Wednesday, 1990 (8:25PM EDT) at Three Rivers Stadium in Pittsburgh. Friday, 12 October 1990 (8:20PM EDT) at Riverfront Stadium in Cincinnati. This game was famous for Pittsburgh manager Jim Leyland starting a set-up man, Ted Power, in order to keep the Reds from employing their successful platoon. The Reds won the series, 4–2 and their first pennant since 1976. 1990 NLCS (4–2): Cincinnati Reds over Pittsburgh Pirates. The Pirates and Reds would respectively end their 24-year stays in the National League East and National League West and join each in the newly created National League Central, continuing their rivalry that started in the 1970s." .

ns1:62551 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Sticky & Sweet Tour was the eighth concert tour by American singer Madonna. Staged to promote her eleventh studio album, Hard Candy (2008), it began in 23 August 2008 and was her first tour under a new recording and business deal with Live Nation. In all, the tour started in Cardiff on 23 August 2008, and ended in Tel Aviv on 02 September 2009. After the first leg, it became the highest-grossing tour by a solo artist, earning US$282 million ($334.87 in 2019 dollars), breaking the previous record Madonna herself held with her Confessions Tour. The Sticky & Sweet Tour has been broadcast on a number of private channels and a CD-DVD and Blu-ray version of the concert was released in March 2010, along with a live album. Billboard confirmed that the tour was to begin on 23 August 2008 in Cardiff, Wales, with performances throughout the rest of the year. The tour was produced globally by Live Nation and promoted Madonna's studio album Hard Candy (2008). On January 30, 2009, it was announced that Madonna had decided to resume the tour in the summer of 2009, with around twenty-five more shows in Europe. Madonna had apologized to her Australian fans during the 2006 Confessions Tour, saying that she tried to work Australia into her schedule but ultimately could not. With the announcement of the 2009 extension, the Herald Sun reported that Madonna planned to tour Australia in 02 September 2009, with one show in Melbourne to be held at the Telstra Dome. Isabel Albiston from The Daily Telegraph compared the tour with the 2006 Confessions Tour and said that \"two years later, Madonna's biceps are no smaller and, with the news that 100 pairs of fishnet pantyhose have been procured from eBay for the artist, her costumes no less raunchy. Caryn Ganz from Rolling Stone noted that \"Madonna may have toyed with dominance and submission on Hard Candy, but there was no question who was carrying the cane at the first New York City date on her Sticky & Sweet Tour last night (the trek's second stop in the States). Titled Sticky & Sweet Tour, the live album was released by Live Nation on March 30, 2010 and distributed by Madonna's former record company, Warner Records (her final release for the label) It was available on DVD, Blu-ray and CD, accompanied by a picture book by Madonna's manager Guy Oseary, titled Madonna: Sticky & Sweet." .

ns1:63258 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 FIFA Club World Cup (officially known as the FIFA Club World Cup Morocco 2014 presented by Toyota for sponsorship reasons) was the 11th edition of the FIFA Club World Cup, a FIFA-organised international club association football tournament between the winners of the six continental confederations as well as the host nation's league champions. It was hosted by Morocco for the second year in a row, and played from 10 to 20 December 2014. Real Madrid CF won their first FIFA Club World Cup after defeating Club Atlético San Lorenzo de Almagro 2–0 in the 2014 FIFA Club World Cup Final, and their fourth world club title counting the 1960, 1998 and 2002 Intercontinental Cups, equaling Milan's record. There were four countries bidding to host the 2013 FIFA Club World Cup and 2014 tournaments (same host for both tournaments): In October 2011, FIFA said that Iran, South Africa and the United Arab Emirates all withdrew their bids, leaving Morocco as the only bidder. FIFA officially announced Morocco as host on 17 December 2011. On 2014, FIFA issued a statement reconfirming Morocco as the host, despite recent rumours that a change in venue might be sought due to the 2014 West Africa Ebola virus outbreak. Each team named a 23-man squad (three of whom must be goalkeepers) by the FIFA deadline of 2014. The squads were announced by FIFA on 2014. A draw was held on 2014 at 19:00 WEST (UTC+1), at the La Mamounia Hotel in Marrakesh, to determine the pairings of the four quarter-finalists. The first semi-2014 FIFA Club World Cup Final was originally to be played at Prince Moulay Abdellah Stadium, Rabat, but was moved to Stade de Marrakech, Marrakesh due to difficult pitch conditions." .

ns1:63534 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Royal Rumble (2017) was a professional wrestling pay-per-view (PPV) event and WWE Network event produced by WWE for their Raw and SmackDown brand divisions. It took place on 29 January 2017, at the Alamodome in San Antonio, Texas. The event was also the second Royal Rumble (2017) to be held in the Alamodome, after 1997, and the fourth to be held in the state of Texas (1989, 1997, and 2007). The Royal Rumble (2017) is an annual gimmick pay-per-view, produced every January by WWE since 1988. Storylines were produced on WWE's weekly television shows, WWE Monday Night RAW, WWE Friday Night SmackDown, and 205 Live, the latter of which is cruiserweight-exclusive. They were interrupted by Corbin, who claimed that he would win the Royal Rumble (2017) match and face the WWE Champion at WrestleMania. Seth Rollins, who had been banned from the Royal Rumble (2017), confronted Stephanie McMahon about her husband Triple H and Rollins' appearance a couple of nights prior at NXT TakeOver: San Antonio. On the following episode of SmackDown, Royal Rumble (2017) winner Randy Orton warned new WWE Champion John Cena that if he was still champion at WrestleMania, he would take the title from him. A couple of weeks prior to the Royal Rumble (2017), SmackDown Commissioner Shane McMahon announced that the main event of SmackDown's next pay-per-view would be an Elimination Chamber (2017) match for the WWE Championship." .

ns1:63550 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Bad was the first solo concert tour by American singer Michael Jackson, launched in support of his seventh studio album Bad (1987). While in Tokyo, Australian pop music critic Ian \"Molly\" Meldrum conducted an exclusive interview with Jackson and DiLeo that was featured on 60 Minutes in the United States and Australia. On March 2, 1988, Jackson performed at the 30th Annual Grammy Awards, receiving an enormous standing ovation after performing \"The Way You Make Me Feel\" and \"Man in the Mirror\". Jackson's album, Bad was also nominated for Album of the Year at the ceremony. The tour was planned to end in Tokyo, but Jackson suffered from swollen vocal cords after the first of six concerts in Los Angeles in November. Five performances in Los Angeles were held to conclude the tour on 27 January 1989. A live album and DVD of the July 16, 1988 concert in London titled Live at Wembley July 16, 1988 was released along with the special edition reissue of the Bad album titled Bad 25 on September 18, 2012, as well as a stand-alone DVD." .

ns1:41659 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of the Lys, also known as the Fourth Battle of Ypres, was fought from 7 to 29 April 1918 and was part of the German Spring Offensive in Flanders during the First World War. It was originally planned by General Erich Ludendorff as Operation George but was reduced to Operation Georgette, with the objective of capturing Ypres, forcing the British forces back to the Channel ports and out of the war. The German attack zone was in Flanders, from about 10 kilometres (6.2 mi) east of Ypres in Belgium to 10 kilometres (6.2 mi) east of Béthune in France, about 40 kilometres (25 mi) south. The front was held by the Belgian Army in the far north, by the British Second Army (under Herbert Plumer, 1st Viscount Plumer) in the north and centre and by the British First Army (under Henry Horne, 1st Baron Horne) in the south. The German attacking forces were the Sixth Army in the south (under Ferdinand von Quast), and the Fourth Army in the north (under Friedrich Sixt von Armin). Herbert Plumer, 1st Viscount Plumer assessed the heavy losses of the Second Army and the defeat of his southern flank and ordered his northern flank to withdraw from Passchendaele to Ypres and the Yser Canal; the Belgian Army to the north conformed. On 23 March, Haig had ordered Herbert Plumer, 1st Viscount Plumer to make contingency plans to shorten the line along the Ypres Salient and release troops for the other armies. During 13 April, General Headquarters (GHQ) discussed the retirements in the Leie valley, which had lengthened the British front line and Herbert Plumer, 1st Viscount Plumer agreed to a retirement in the Ypres Salient to the Mt Kemmel, Voormezeele (2.5 mi (4.0 km) south of Ypres), White Château (1 mi (1.6 km) east of Ypres) to Pilckem Ridge defence line but ordered only that artillery ammunition be carried to the rear; the 4th Army reported on 14 April, that the British were still occupying the Passchendaele Salient." .

ns1:44085 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Eurovision Song Contest 1988 was the 33rd edition of the annual Eurovision Song Contest. The contest took place on 30 April 1988 in Dublin, Ireland, following the country's win at the previous 1987 edition. The presenters were Pat Kenny and Michelle Rocca. Dublin is the capital and largest city of Ireland. Dublin is in the province of Leinster on Ireland's east coast, at the mouth of the River Liffey. Founded as a Viking settlement, the Kingdom of Dublin became Ireland's principal city following the Norman invasion. Following independence in 1922, Dublin became the capital of the Republic of Ireland. The staging of the contest in Dublin in 1988 formed part of Dublin’s year long celebration of 1000 years since it was established by Scandinavian settlers in 988. The contest helped launch an international career for two artists, the winner for Switzerland Céline Dion and Luxembourg’s representative Lara Fabian. The contest saw the return of seven artists who had participated in its previous editions: Denmark's Hot Eyes represented the country in 1984 and 1985, Finland's Boulevard represented the country in 1987 as Vicky Rosti's backing group, Israel's Re'uven Gvirtz and Yehuda Tamir, winners of the contest in 1979 as part of Milk and Honey returned to Eurovision as backing singers for Yardena Arazi, who represented the country in 1976 as part of the group Chocolat, Menta, Mastik. Arazi had hosted the contest in 1979, Portugal's Dora represented the country in 1986, Sweden's Tommy Körberg represented the country in 1969, and Turkey's Mazhar-Fuat-Özkan represented the country in 1985." .

ns1:51820 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1927 World Snooker Championship was a snooker tournament held at various venues throughout the season. The final took place at the Camkin's Hall in Birmingham, England with Joe Davis winning the title by defeating Tom Dennis. The highest break of the tournament was 60, made by Albert Cope in his match against Joe Davis. However, Joe Davis, the eventual champion, won the £6 and 10 shillings from gate receipts, and the BACC used the player's part of the fees towards purchasing the trophy. The first match played was between Melbourne Inman and Tom Newman at Thurston's Hall, Leicester Square in London. Tom Dennis and Fred Lawrence played their match on 9 December and 10 at the Lord Nelson Hotel, Carlton Street, Nottingham. Joe Davis and Joe Brady met on 29 December 1926 and 30 December 1926 in Cable street, Liverpool. Tom Carpenter and Nat Butler played their match on 31 December 1926 and 1927 at Thurston's Hall. The first semi-final saw Joe Davis meet Albert Cope over three days from 31 January to 2 February in Birmingham. Davis had beaten Irishman Joe Brady 10–5 in Liverpool. On the second day Davis extended his lead to 10–1 before Cope won three successive frames. The final between Davis and Dennis was played from 9 to 12 May 1927 in Camkin's Hall in Birmingham." .

ns1:52782 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Chevauchée of the Black Prince 1356, which began on 04 August 1356 at Bordeaux and ended with the Battle of Poitiers on 19 September 1356, was a devastating raid of Edward of Woodstock, Prince of Wales (known as the Black Prince), the eldest son of King Edward III of England. This expedition of the Black Prince devastated large parts of Bergerac, Périgord, Nontronnais, Confolentais, Nord-Ouest, Limousin, County of La Marche, Boischaut, Champagne Berrichonne, Berry, Sologne, south of Touraine and Poitou. Reinforcements of men and horses and supplies of food and materiel arrived from England during the spring, and at the start of 12 August 1356 the Black Prince headed north on another Chevauchée with an Anglo-Gascon force of 6,000." .

ns1:55928 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 World Series was the World Series of Major League Baseball's (MLB) 2015 Major League Baseball season. The 111th edition of the World Series, it was a best-of-seven playoff between the National League (NL) 2015 American League Championship Series New York Mets and the American League (AL) 2015 American League Championship Series Kansas City Royals. The series was played between 27 October 2015 and 01 November 2015, with the Royals winning the series 4 games to 1. The series was played in a 2-3-2 format: the Royals hosted Games 1 and 2, and the Mets hosted Games 3, 4, and 5. The Mets made their fifth appearance in the World Series after sweeping the Cubs 4–0 in the 2015 National League Championship Series (NLCS). This was the first World Series appearance for Mets' manager Terry Collins. The Royals made their second consecutive appearance in the World Series, both under Ned Yost, and fourth overall. They won the 1985 World Series against the St. Louis Cardinals, and lost their two other appearances, the 1980 World Series against the Philadelphia Phillies and the 2014 World Series against the San Francisco Giants. Kansas City won the series, 4–1. Kendrys Morales's single moved Hosmer to third and Mike Moustakas's single made it 4-1 Royals. Flores struck out looking to end the game, series, baseball season, with the Royals winning and ending their 30-year World Series title drought. 2015 World Series (4–1): Kansas City Royals (AL) beat New York Mets (NL). Game 1 of the World Series averaged a 4.6 rating on Fox, making it the most watched Game 1 since the 2010 World Series. Additionally, the Royals became the first team in World Series history to start three pitchers—Yordano Ventura, Edison Volquez, and Johnny Cueto—born outside the United States of America. In the actual 2015 postseason, the Cubs advanced to the 2015 National League Championship Series but were eliminated in four games." .

ns1:58156 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Iberian War was fought from 526 to 532 between the Byzantine Empire and the Sasanian Empire over the eastern Georgian kingdom of Iberia—a Sasanian client state that defected to the Byzantines. The Sasanians maintained the upper hand until 530 but the Byzantines recovered their position in battles at Dara and Satala while their Ghassanids allies defeated the Sasanian-aligned Lakhmids. In the intervening years, the two sides preferred to wage war by proxy, through Arab allies in the south and Huns in the north. Violence escalated at various points where the power of the two empires met: in 525 a Roman fleet transported an Aksumite army to conquer Himyarite Yemen and in 525–526, Persia's Arab allies, the Lakhmids, raided Roman territories on the edge of the desert. By 526–527, overt fighting between the two empires had broken out in the South Caucasus region and upper Mesopotamia; the Persians continued to exert pressure on the Romans to obtain funds from them. Damaging raids on Syria by the Lakhmids in 529 also encouraged Justinian to strengthen his own Arab allies, helping the Ghassanids leader Al-Harith ibn Jabalah turn a loose coalition into a coherent kingdom which was able to gain the upper hand against the Lakhmids over the following decades. In 530, Belisarius led the Romans to victory over a much larger Persian force under Perozes at the Battle of Dara, while Sittas and Dorotheus defeated a Persian army under Mihr-Mihroe at the Battle of Satala." .

ns1:58662 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Eurovision Song Contest 1998 was the 43rd edition of the annual Eurovision Song Contest. It took place in Birmingham, United Kingdom, following Katrina and the Waves's win at the Eurovision Song Contest 1997 in Dublin, Ireland with the song \"Love Shine A Light\". The contest was staged at the Arena Birmingham on 09 May 1998, presented by Terry Wogan and Ulrika Jonsson. The United Kingdom, along with their national broadcaster the BBC, hosted the contest at the Arena Birmingham in the city of Birmingham. The week after the Eurovision Song Contest, the city was to host the 24th G8 summit, with Terry Wogan vacating his hotel room to make way for Bill Clinton. José Cid part of Alma Lusa in 1980 returned for Portugal; and Paul Harrington who was a backing singer for Dawn Martin in 1998, returned for Ireland after having won the 1994 with Charlie McGettigan. Below is a summary of all 12 points in the final: In addition to the host country of the 1999 contest, Israel, the 16 countries with the highest average scores between 1994 and 1998 were allowed to take part in the Eurovision Song Contest 1999. Guildo Horn of Germany won the 1998 Barbara Dex Award." .

ns1:59161 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Polmont rail accident, also known as the Polmont rail disaster, occurred on 30 July 1984 to the west of Polmont, near Falkirk, in Scotland. A westbound push-pull express train travelling from Edinburgh to Glasgow struck a cow, which had gained access to the track through a damaged fence from a field near Polmont railway station. The collision caused all six carriages and the locomotive of the train to derail, killing 13 people and injuring 61 others. The accident happened on one of the busiest Glasgow to Edinburgh via Falkirk Line in Scotland. At the time of the accident, British Rail passenger trains between Glasgow Queen Street and Edinburgh Waverley were operated by the push-pull technique with a single British Rail Class 47 locomotive located at one end of the train at all times (the locomotive usually pulled the carriages from Glasgow to Edinburgh and pushed them on the return journey). On Monday 30 July 1984, a westbound express train, the 17:30 service from Edinburgh Waverley to Glasgow Queen Street – consisting of a British Rail Class 47 diesel-electric locomotive pushing five British Rail Mark 3 and fronted by a Driving Brake Standard Open (no. To the west of Polmont, the track curved left and passed the junction for Stirling, then curved right with a steady gradient towards Falkirk High station. The impact of the collision destroyed the animal, but a significant part of the carcass, later thought to be a leg bone, became trapped under the leading bogie of the Driving Brake Standard Open, lifting it off the track and derailing it. The main focus was that the light axle-loading of the Driving Brake Standard Open had led to its being more easily derailed than a heavier vehicle would have been; modifications were put in place to lessen the chances of a recurrence of the derailment. The inquiry made recommendations which were followed; the main ones of which were: In 2009, on the 25th anniversary of the accident, a memorial plaque was erected and unveiled by Falkirk, First ScotRail and the local emergency services at Polmont station to remember those who died and were injured." .

ns1:59164 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Capetian-Plantagenet rivalry was a series of conflicts and disputes that covered a period of 100 years (1159–1259), during which the House of Capet, rulers of the Kingdom of France, fought against the House of Plantagenet (also known as the House of Anjou), rulers of the Kingdom of England in order to suppress the growing power of the Plantagenet-controlled Angevin Empire. In fact, the real royal power of the King of France had not yet become extensive, though the suzerainty of his dynasty did extend far beyond the small domain of Île-de-France, to almost the entire territory of France, including the marches of the Holy Roman Empire (Seine/Morvan/Rhone Valley) to the east. In 1150, Henry II received the Duchy of Normandy from his father Geoffrey V, Count of Anjou, and when he died in 1151, he became Count of Anjou and Maine. In 1156, he seized the viscounty of Thouars, thereby controlling communications between the northwest and south-west France. When Philip Augustus came to the throne in 1180, he was the king of a territory comparable in area to the Île-de-France of today and faced against an Angevin Empire more powerful than ever. Philip received the homage of Arthur, as Duke of Brittany, in spring 1199 for the counties of Anjou, Maine and Touraine. By the Treaty of Chinon, John Lackland abandoned all his possessions to the north of the Loire: Berry, Touraine, Maine and Anjou returned to the royal domain, which then covered a third of France, greatly enlarged and free from external threat. Aquitaine was taken, the cities of the region falling one after the other: Poitou, Saintonge, Périgord, Angoumois, and part of Bordeaux. In 1242, Henry was again at war with the King of France Louis IX as he took advantage of the inheritance dispute in the County of Poitou. Though it is unclear that any armed conflict happened in the siege, it brought an end to the short-lived Saintonge war. Seeking to restore his position in a seemingly desperate situation, in 1259 Henry purchased the support of Louis by the Treaty of Paris, agreeing to accept the loss of the lands in France that had been seized from him and from his father King John by Louis and his predecessors since 1202, and to do homage for those that remained in his hands. Each kingdom used the time to invade its smaller neighbors in the First War of Scottish Independence in the case of England and the Franco-Flemish war in the case of France." .

ns1:61842 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Congress or Congress of Alexandria was a 1755 meeting of Major-General Edward Braddock, commander-in-chief of the British Army in North America and governors of five of the constituent colonies. These were Robert Dinwiddie of Virginia, Horatio Sharpe of Maryland, Robert Hunter Morris of Pennsylvania, William Shirley of Massachusetts and James DeLancey of New York. The meeting was held on 15 April 1755 at Carlyle House in Alexandria, Virginia, home of one of that city's prominent figures, John Carlyle. Sir William Johnson, 1st Baronet of New York, who was also present at the meeting, was appointed Superintendent of Indian Affairs and commissioned a major-general." .

ns1:62790 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Devonshire House Ball of 1897 or the Devonshire House Fancy Dress Ball was an elaborate fancy dress ball, hosted by the Spencer Cavendish, 8th Duke of Devonshire and Duchess of Devonshire, held on 02 July 1897 at Devonshire House in Piccadilly to celebrate Queen Victoria’s Diamond Jubilee. In 1897, The Spencer Cavendish, 8th Duke of Devonshire and Duchess of Devonshire hosted the Devonshire House Ball of 1897 at Devonshire House, the London residence (in Piccadilly) of the Dukes of Devonshire in the 18th and 19th centuries. The Spencer Cavendish, 8th Duke of Devonshire had served as a Member of Parliament and a cabinet minister as a member of the Liberal Party and the Duchess, known as the Louisa Cavendish, Duchess of Devonshire, was the widow of the William Montagu, 7th Spencer Cavendish, 8th Duke of Devonshire of Manchester. Following the death of Prince Albert in 1861, Queen Victoria had withdrawn from social life and \"the mantle of royal entertaining\" was passed to the Prince of Wales and his wife, Alexandra of Denmark. The Devonshires, who were close friends of the Prince and Princess of Wales, therefore, decided to throw a costume ball to celebrate Queen Victoria's Diamond Jubilee. Following the ball, The Duchess received a letter from Francis Knollys, Private Secretary to the Sovereign, indicating that the Prince, later Edward VII, who arrived after 11 o'clock, thought the party a success. The price of the costume, which cost 5,000 francs, reportedly even shocked the Spencer Cavendish, 8th Duke of Devonshire, who had famously married American heiress Consuelo Vanderbilt in 1895." .

ns1:63745 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Varna took place on 10 November 1444 near Varna in eastern Bulgaria. It was the final battle of the Crusade of Varna. The mixed Papal States was composed mainly of Hungarian, Polish, Bohemian (whose combined armies numbered 16,000) and Wallachia (7,000) forces, with smaller detachments of Czechs, Papal knights, Teutonic Knights, Bosnians, Croatians, Bulgarians, Lithuanians and Ruthenians. Armenian refugees in the Kingdom of Hungary also took part in the wars of their new country against the Ottoman Empire as early as the battle of Varna in 1444, when some Armenians were seen amongst the Christian forces. In the morning of 10 November 1444, Hunyadi deployed the army of some 20,000 Crusades as an arc between Lake Varna and the Franga plateau; the line was about 3.5 km long." .

ns1:46773 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 Republican Party presidential primaries were the selection process by which voters of the Republican Party chose its nominee for President of the United States in the 2008 U.S. presidential election. Senator John McCain of Arizona was selected as the nominee through a series of primary election and caucuses culminating in the 2008 Republican National Convention held from Monday, September 1, through Thursday, 2008, in St. Paul, Minnesota. Senator and former presidential candidate John McCain won the New Hampshire and Florida primaries. Republican candidates in the 2008 U.S. presidential election campaigned for the nomination of their party in a series of primary election and caucus events. At the beginning of 2007, the announced Republican field was former Governor of Wisconsin and Cabinet member Tommy Thompson, former Governor of Virginia Jim Gilmore, and Senator of Kansas Sam Brownback. Congressman Ron Paul, Mike Huckabee of Arkansas, Rudy Giuliani of New York City, U.S. Alan Keyes and former Senator and actor Fred Thompson entered the race later in September. He exceeded expectations, earning 51% of the vote with Ron Paul beating out John McCain for second. Romney campaigned hard in Nevada and did not campaign in South Carolina, while the other leading Republican candidates, John McCain and Mike Huckabee, kept their focus on South Carolina. Polls taken before the primary showed that John McCain was the slight front runner over Mitt Romney. Former Senator Fred Thompson and Rep. Duncan L. Hunter, though already out of the race, still remained on the ballot in the Florida primary. Mike Huckabee challenged John McCain to a debate before the March 4 primaries, and the Values Voter coalition stepped in, arranging for a debate hall and inviting both McCain and Huckabee, as well as Rep. Ron Paul to participate in a March 3 debate event. John McCain officially clinched the Republican presidential nomination on 2008, sweeping the primaries in Ohio, Texas, Rhode Island, and Vermont. GOP February 5 rule Under Republican National Committee rules, no state was allowed to hold its 2008 primary before February 5. The Executive Board of the Nevada Republican Party met on 2008 and appointed all 34 delegates and 31 alternates." .

ns1:57437 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Burgon vase is the earliest known panathenaic amphora, dating to around 560 BC, and the name vase for the ancient Greek painter of the Burgon Group. The amphora was uncovered in 1813 in Athens and is named after Thomas Burgon (1787–1858), a merchant of the Levant Company, who brought it to England and sold it to the museum. The vase is painted in the black figure style with images of the Greek goddess Athena, a flying siren and an owl, as well as a two-horse chariot. The diploidion – a part of the peplos whipped over the shoulders – is decorated with a meander, while the skirt itself is decorated with a vertical band, which consists of full squares, and with a hem. The shield which Athena bears with her right arm, bears a dolphin facing to the left. On the unornamented neck there is a siren on the front and an owl on the back, both with their wings held in exactly the same posture." .

ns1:62636 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2003-04 Cypriot Cup was the 62nd edition of the Cypriot Cup. It began on 13 September 2003 with the first round and concluded on 23 May 2004 with the final which was held at GSP Stadium. AEK Larnaca F.C. won their 1st Cypriot Cup trophy after beating AEL Limassol FC 2–1 in the final. The first four teams of the 2002–03 Cypriot First Division (AC Omonia, Anorthosis Famagusta FC, APOEL F.C., Olympiakos Nicosia FC) were set heads of each group and the 5th–8th placed teams (AEL Limassol FC, Ethnikos Achna FC, AEP Paphos F.C. and AEK Larnaca F.C.) were drawn one per group. 1The match APOEL F.C.-AEL Limassol FC abandoned at 1-0 in 83' after the referee was hit by an object that was thrown from the APOEL F.C. section of the crowd." .

ns1:63236 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Petsamo–Kirkenes Offensive was a major military offensive during World War II, mounted by the Red Army against the Wehrmacht in 1944 in northern Finland and Norway. The offensive defeated the Wehrmacht's forces in the Arctic, driving them back into Norway, and was called the \"Tenth Shock\" by Stalin. It later expelled German forces from the northern part of Norway and seized the nickel mines of Pechengsky District. Considerable German forces were deployed in the sector to protect the Finnish nickel mines of Pechengsky District, which were of importance to German armour plate fabrication, and to guard the coast of northern Norway against an Allied landing operation. After the armistice between the Soviet Union and Finland on 4 September 1944, the Pechengsky District region (though still largely occupied by the Germans) became part of the Russian SFSR, and the Finnish government agreed to remove the remaining German troops from its territory by 15 September (leading to the Lapland War). During the retreat of the Wehrmacht 20th Mountain Army, called Operation Birke, the decision was made by the Oberkommando der Wehrmacht to withdraw completely from northern Norway and Finland in Operation Nordlicht. The operation was to be undertaken jointly by the Karelian Front under the command of General Kirill Meretskov and the Northern Fleet under Admiral Arseniy Golovko. The main operations were to be conducted by the 14th Army, which had been in the Arctic since the beginning of the war. Prior to the start of the Soviet drive, the defending Germans had been ordered to abandon Pechengsky District on 15 October, and Kirkenes by the beginning of November. On 10 October the Germans shifted the 163rd Division, which was already withdrawing from Finland to Norway, to the Pechengsky District region to bolster their defenses. The Germans abandoned Kirkenes on 25 October and finally on 29 October 1944 Meretskov halted all operations except reconnaissance. The Petsamo–Kirkenes Offensive was the last major offensive in an Arctic environment." .

ns1:63319 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 National League Wild Card Game was a play-in game during Major League Baseball's (MLB) 2014 Major League Baseball season played between the National League's (NL) two wild card teams, the San Francisco Giants and the Pittsburgh Pirates. It was held at PNC Park in Pittsburgh, on 01 October 2014, starting at 8:07 p.m. EDT. Despite this advantage, the Giants won by a score of 8–0 and advanced to play the Washington Nationals in the 2014 National League Division Series. With Pittsburgh's postseason place secured, manager Clint Hurdle chose to start Gerrit Cole – the team's ace – in the final game of the season against the Cincinnati Reds on September 28, instead of skipping his turn in the rotation and saving his start for the Major League Baseball Wild Card Game. For the Major League Baseball Wild Card Game, the Pirates started Edinson Vólquez, who had a 13–7 win–loss record and 3.04 earned run average (ERA) in 31 games started during the 2014 Major League Baseball season." .

ns1:63353 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2003 FIFA Confederations Cup association football tournament was the sixth FIFA Confederations Cup, held in France national association football team in 18 June 2003. France national association football team retained the title they had won in 2001 FIFA Confederations Cup, but the tournament was overshadowed by the death of Cameroon national football team player Marc-Vivien Foé, who died of heart failure in his side's semi-final against Colombia national football team. Foé's death united the France national association football team and Cameroon national football team teams in the final match, which was played even though team players from both sides had explicitly stated that the match should not be played out of respect for Foé. France national association football team went on to win the trophy with a golden goal from Thierry Henry. They were replaced by Turkey national association football team, who came third in the 2002 FIFA World Cup. In total, 37 goals were scored by 22 different players, with none of them credited as own goal." .

ns1:63621 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 81st Academy Awards ceremony, presented by the Academy of Motion Picture Arts and Sciences (AMPAS), honored the best films of 2008 and took place on 22 February 2009, at the Dolby Theatre in Hollywood, Los Angeles beginning at 5:30 p.m. PST / 8:30 p.m. EST. The ceremony was televised in the United States by American Broadcasting Company, and was produced by Bill Condon and Laurence Mark and directed by Roger Goodman. Two weeks earlier in a ceremony at the Beverly Wilshire Hotel in Beverly Hills, California held on February 7, the Academy Awards for Technical Achievement were presented by host Jessica Biel. The nominees for the 81st Academy Awards were announced on January 22, 2009, at 5:38 p.m. PST (13:38 UTC) at the Samuel Goldwyn Theater in Beverly Hills, California, by Sid Ganis, president of the Academy, and the actor Forest Whitaker. In 2008, the Academy selected producers Bill Condon and Laurence Mark to co-produce the telecast. The American telecast on American Broadcasting Company drew in an average of 36.94 million people over its length, which was a 13% increase from the record lows of the 80th Academy Awards." .

ns1:50635 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1997 Tour de France was the 84th edition of the Tour de France and took place from 5 to 27 July 1997. Jan Ullrich's victory margin, of 9:09 was the largest margin of victory since Laurent Fignon won the 1984 Tour de France by 10:32. Since 1997 no rider has had this convincing of a win with the closest margin to Ullrich's victory being Vincenzo Nibali winning the 2014 Tour de France with a gap of 7:39. 198 riders in 22 teams commenced the 1997 Tour de France. Stages 7 and 8 followed rounding out the first week with sprint finishes, both of which were won by Erik Zabel as he bested Jaan Kirsipuu and Blijlevens in stage 7 and Minali and Blijlevens in stage 8. Zabel had donned the Green Jersey following stage 3 and would hold it all the way to Paris. Laurent Brochard won the stage with the elite group of Richard Virenque, Pantani and Ullrich finishing 0:14 behind. Spaniard Abraham Olano was in 3rd at 1:14 behind and the next closest GC favorite was Ullrich 0:14 behind him. There were no major attacks by the GC riders in this stage so going into the ITT in Stage 12 Ullrich was convincingly in the lead at 2:38 over Virenque, 4:46 over Alano and 4:53 over his teammate Riis, who at this point remained confident he was still the leader of Team Telekom with Ullrich continuing to ride for him as a Super-Domestique. Virenque finished 3rd 1:27 behind Pantani and Francesco Casagrande finished 4th on the stage while also moving to 6th place in the overall standings. Frenchman Didier Rous would win the stage beating the next closest breakaway riders in Pascal Hervé, Bobby Julich and Laurent Roux by more than five minutes to finish the mountain stages with there being no further changes among the general classification favorites. Afterwards on the podiums Erik Zabel was awarded the green jersey, Richard Virenque won the King of the Mountains as well as the Most Combative Rider, in 3rd place on the podium was Marco Pantani, in 2nd was Virenque and in 1st overall winning the best young rider award, as well as the yellow jersey as champion of the Tour de France was Jan Ullrich. After Ullrich's domination of the 1997 Tour de France at his young age, it was believed that Ullrich would dominate the Tour de France for the next years." .

ns1:54398 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Danmark Rundt was a men's road bicycle racing held from 22 to 26 August 2012. The race was rated as a 2.HC event and formed part of the 2012 UCI Europe Tour. The race was won by Dutch rider Lieuwe Westra of the Vacansoleil-DCM team, with Ramūnas Navardauskas of Garmin-Sharp second and Manuele Boaro of Team Saxo Bank-Tinkoff Bank third. 17 teams were invited to the 2012 Danmark Rundt: 8 teams from the UCI ProTeams, 4 UCI Professional Continental Teams, 4 UCI Continental Teams along with a Danish national team under the Team Post Danmark name. 22 August 2012 – Randers to Randers, 191 km (119 mi) The stage started and ended in Randers, taking in a tour of the Djursland peninsula and passing through the town of Grenaa. 23 22 August 2012 – Løgstør to Aarhus, 211 km (131 mi) Stage two was the longest stage of the 2012 race, starting in Løgstør on the Limfjord and travelling generally south through Jutland. The stage winner was Lars Petter Nordhaug of Team Sky who took over the general classification lead from André Greipel. Wilco Kelderman of Rabobank came second with Ramūnas Navardauskas of Garmin-Sharp third. 25 22 August 2012 – Kerteminde to Kerteminde, 14.5 km (9.0 mi) individual time trial (ITT) Stage five was a 141⁄2km individual time trial around Kerteminde on Funen. It was won by Lieuwe Westra of the Vacansoleil-DCM team who took the yellow jersey, leading the race by ten seconds from Ramūnas Navardauskas. 26 August 2012 – Slagelse to Frederiksberg, 165 km (103 mi) Stage six started in Slagelse on Sjælland and passed through Sorø and Roskilde before arriving in Frederiksberg. Ten laps of 6 km each led to the traditional race finish on Frederiksberg Allé. The general classification was won by Lieuwe Westra of Vacansoleil-DCM by ten seconds from Ramūnas Navardauskas of Garmin-Sharp with Manuele Boaro of Team Saxo Bank-Tinkoff Bank another four seconds back in third place. Team Katusha rider Alexander Kristoff won the points trophy by one point from Lotto-Belisol rider André Greipel. The mountain classification was won by Danish rider Nikola Aistrup of Team Concordia Forsikring-Himmerland with Dutch rider Wilco Kelderman of Team Rabobank winning the young riders award." .

ns1:56994 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "In North American broadcasting, a local marketing agreement (LMA), or local management agreement, is a contract in which one company agrees to operate a radio or television station owned by another party. This may also be referred to as a time brokerage agreement (TBA), local sales agreement (LSA), management services agreement (MSA), or most commonly, a joint sales agreement (JSA) or shared services agreement (SSA). Although the majority of LMAs involve the outsourcing of one television station's operations to another, occasionally, a company may operate a station under an LMA, JSA or SSA even if it does not already own a station in that market. Wheeler also proposed an expedited process to review joint sales agreements on a case-by-case basis, granting a waiver of the rules if a broadcaster can prove a particular joint sales agreement arrangement serves the public interest. On February 26, 2016, Media General obtained a preliminary injunction against Gray for violating the SSA and JSA, which required that Gray return control of WAGT to Media General, and forbade Gray from selling WAGT in the spectrum incentive auction. In a 2005 Canadian dispute, Rogers Media and Newcap Broadcasting maintained a joint sales agreement pertaining to CHNO-FM in Sudbury, Ontario, but community interests and the lobby group Friends of Canadian Broadcasting presented substantial evidence to the Canadian Radio-television and Telecommunications Commission that in practice, the agreement was a de facto LMA, going significantly beyond advertising sales into program production and news-gathering. In 2008, the Filipino Associated Broadcasting Company leased its airtime to the Malaysian broadcaster Media Prima (through the local subsidiary MPB Primedia) similar to an LMA – with MPB Primedia providing entertainment programming, and ABC handling news programming and operations." .

ns1:59055 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Three Days of De Panne (Dutch: Driedaagse De Panne–Koksijde) was the 41st edition of the Three Days of De Panne cycling stage race. The race included four stages over three days, from 28 March 2017–30 March 2017. It was rated as a 2.HC event in the 2017 UCI Europe Tour. He ultimately won the race by 38 seconds ahead of Trek–Segafredo's Matthias Brändle, while the podium was completed by Alexander Kristoff from Team Katusha–Alpecin, who won the second stage of the race. In the race's other classifications, Sport Vlaanderen–Baloise rider Piet Allegaert was the winner of the mountains classification, Simone Consonni from UAE Team Emirates was the winner of the Bernard Van de Kerckhove Trophy, as the best-placed rider under the age of 23 – in thirteenth place overall – while Trek–Segafredo won the teams classification, as Brändle, Edward Theuns and Boy van Poppel all finished within the top ten placings in the general classification. Points for this classification were won by the first riders to the top of each categorised climb, on a 5–3–1 scale as all climbs were categorised the same." .

ns1:59636 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2019 Vuelta a España was a three-week Grand Tour cycling stage race that took place in Spain, Andorra and France between 24 August 2019 and 15 September 2019. Rounding out the podium were Alejandro Valverde of Movistar Team in second and Roglič's countryman Tadej Pogačar of UAE Team Emirates in third. The winner of the 2018 Vuelta a España, Simon Yates, had decided to not defend his title after riding in the 2019 Giro d'Italia and Tour de France. Steven Kruijswijk, Primož Roglič (Team Jumbo-Visma), Miguel Ángel López, Jakob Fuglsang (Astana) and Nairo Quintana (Movistar Team) were considered among the pre-race favourites. Fernando Gaviria (UAE Team Emirates) and Sam Bennett (Bora-Hansgrohe) were considered as potential stage winners and points classification contenders. There were three previous winners among the participating cyclists: Alejandro Valverde (2009), Fabio Aru (2015) and Nairo Quintana (2016)." .

ns1:60124 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 Philippine Senate election was the 30th election to the Senate of the Philippines. The two neophytes, Bongbong Marcos and Teofisto Guingona III, had their fathers previously serve in the Senate. It is expected that Manuel Villar of the Nacionalistas and Francis Pangilinan from the Liberals be the top contenders to be Senate President; incumbent Juan Ponce Enrile (Pwersa ng Masang Pilipino) originally said he would not seek the office, but with the inability of either of the frontrunners to garner the required thirteen votes, he has presented himself as a compromise candidate to avoid deadlock. Actors Bong Revilla (born Ramon Revilla Jr.), who changed his legal name to his screen name \"Bong Revilla\" prior to the campaign period, and Jinggoy Estrada, together with Miriam Defensor Santiago consistently topped the poll surveys from Social Weather Stations and Pulse Asia. All six incumbents who ran held their seats, four former senators also won, and the two neophyte senators, Bongbong Marcos and Teofisto Guingona III had their fathers (Ferdinand Marcos and Teofisto Guingona Jr., respectively) previously served at the Senate. Among the six winning incumbents, three had family members that previously served in the Senate: Bong Revilla (son of Ramon Revilla Sr.), Jinggoy Estrada (son of Joseph Estrada) and Pia Cayetano (daughter of Rene Cayetano; her brother, Alan Peter Cayetano, won in 2007 Philippine Senate election). The three of the four former winning senators also had a family member with Senate experience: Ralph Recto (grandson of Claro M. Recto), Tito Sotto (grandson of Vicente Sotto) and Sergio Osmeña III (grandson of Sergio Osmeña and son of Sergio Osmeña Jr.) Only one former senator ran and lost: Francisco Tatad; candidates with no prior Senate experience but had family members that previously served in the Senate but lost are Ruffy Biazon (son of outgoing Senator Rodolfo Biazon), Sonia Roco (wife of Raul Roco), Adel Tamano (son of Mamintal Tamano) and Susan Ople (daughter of Blas Ople). Villar resigned from the senate presidency just prior to the election period and Juan Ponce Enrile (Pwersa ng Masang Pilipino) became the new senate president." .

ns1:60416 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Eurovision Song Contest 1981 was the 26th edition of the annual Eurovision Song Contest. It took place in Dublin, Ireland, following Johnny Logan's win at the Eurovision Song Contest 1980 in The Hague, Netherlands with the song \"What's Another Year\". The contest was held at the RDS Simmonscourt on Saturday 04 April 1981, and was hosted by Doireann Ní Bhriain. The 1981 contest took place in Dublin, the capital of Ireland. The presenter on this occasion was Doireann Ní Bhriain, who was well known in Ireland at the time as a TV presenter and for the current affairs radio show Women Today. Notably, Jean-Claude Pascal for Luxembourg, who had won the contest 20 years earlier, although could only manage 11th place this time. Other returnees were Marty Brem who had taken part the Eurovision Song Contest 1980 for Austria, Tommy Seebach for Denmark, and Björn Skifs for Sweden. Bucks Fizz member, Cheryl Baker had performed in 1978 with the band Co-Co for the UK, while Sheeba member Maxi had performed as a solo artist in 1973 for Ireland. Swedish singer Björn Skifs however was more outspoken saying; \"This was not a song contest, it was a show – all these dancing girls, they take away from the songs. The 1981 contest was held on 04 April 1981 and to date has never been as early again. Decades later, Debbie Cameron, who represented Denmark with Tommy Seebach, revealed in a book about Seebach that she was contacted by a BBC employee, who told her that Bucks Fizz's victory was planned." .

ns1:60772 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017–18 Scottish League Two (known as Ladbrokes Scottish League Two for sponsorship reasons) was the 23rd season in the current format of 10 teams in the fourth-tier of Scottish football. Ten teams contested the league: Annan Athletic F.C., Berwick Rangers F.C., Clyde F.C., Cowdenbeath F.C., Edinburgh City F.C., Elgin City F.C., Montrose F.C., Peterhead F.C., Stenhousemuir F.C. and Stirling Albion F.C.. The Scottish League Two winners would receive £64,000 with a total pot of £24.5 million to be distributed across the four divisions. For example, if a match is scheduled for matchday 13, but then postponed and played between days 16 and 17, it will be added to the standings for day 16. Teams play each other four times, twice in the first half of the season (home and away) and twice in the second half of the season (home and away), making a total of 180 games, with each team playing 36." .

ns1:62518 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 Democratic Party presidential primaries were the selection processes by which voters of the Democratic Party chose its nominee for President of the United States in the 2008 U.S. presidential election. Notes for the tables in this section: The earliest significant candidate to launch their candidacy was Mike Gravel in 17 April 2006. Between November 2006 and 10 February 2007, eight major candidates opened their campaigns: Joe Biden, Hillary Clinton, Chris Dodd, John Edwards, Dennis Kucinich, Barack Obama, Bill Richardson, and Tom Vilsack. In August 2006, the Democratic National Committee adopted a proposal by its Rules and Bylaws Committee stating that only the four states of Iowa, New Hampshire, Nevada, and South Carolina would be permitted to hold primaries or caucuses before 2008. In May 2007, the Florida Legislature passed a bill that moved the date of the state's primary to 2008, setting up a confrontation with the Democratic National Committee. On 2008, the Democratic National Committee Rules and Bylaws Committee voted unanimously (27-0) to restore half-votes to all the Florida delegates, including superdelegates. The Michigan delegates were also given half-votes, with 69 delegates pledged to Hillary Clinton and 59 to Barack Obama; this proposed change passing by 19–8. On March 21, former primary candidate Bill Richardson, who has previously held important posts in the Clinton Administration, endorsed Barack Obama, a move that drew intense criticism from Clinton allies, including James Carville's Eastertime comparison of Richardson with Judas Iscariot. On March 25, Mike Gravel announced that he would leave the Democrats and join the Libertarian Party, entering the race for the 2008 Libertarian presidential nomination the following day. During a well received concession speech in Washington DC on Saturday 07 June 2008 Clinton endorsed Obama in the following terms: \"The way to continue our fight now, to accomplish the goals for which we stand is to take our energy, our passion, our strength, and do all we can to help elect Barack Obama, the next president of the United States." .

ns1:63733 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1979 FA Cup Final was a association football match played on 12 May 1979 at Wembley Stadium. The match was contested by Arsenal F.C. and Manchester United F.C.. For over 85 minutes the game had been unremarkable, with Arsenal F.C. taking a 2–0 half time lead through goals from Brian Talbot and Frank Stapleton. In the 86th minute, however, Gordon McQueen scored following a set-piece, and two minutes later Sammy McIlroy dribbled past two Arsenal F.C. players to score a dramatic equaliser. With the game poised for extra time, United's celebrations proved short-lived, as Alan Sunderland scored a last-minute winner, making the final result Arsenal F.C. 3–2 Manchester United F.C.. The 1979 FA Cup was the only major trophy that Arsenal F.C. won during the seven-year management of Terry Neill, who was appointed in 1976 after Bertie Mee's retirement." .

ns1:44881 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "A Word to the People (Russian: «Слово к народу») was an open letter signed by twelve Soviet public figures. The declaration was published in Sovetskaya Rossiya (a newspaper that expressed anti-Perestroika views) on 23 July 1991. The signers included writers Yuri Bondarev, Valentin Rasputin and Alexander Prokhanov, singer Lyudmila Zykina, war generals Valentin Varennikov and Boris Gromov, sculptor Vyacheslav Klykov, scientist Eduard Volodin and politicians Gennady Zyuganov, Vasily Starodubtsev, Yuri Blokhin and Alexander Tizyakov. Three of the signatories (Valentin Varennikov, Alexander Tizyakov, and Vasily Starodubtsev) later joined the State Committee on the State of Emergency in the 1991 August Coup attempt against Gorbachev." .

ns1:52747 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Félibrige (French pronunciation: ; Lo Felibritge in classical Occitan, Lou Felibrige in Mistralian norm, pronounced ) is a literary and cultural association founded by Frédéric Mistral and other Provençal writers to defend and promote the Provençal (also called the Occitan language or langue d’Oc) and literature. Le Félibrige was founded at the Château de Font-Ségugne (located in Châteauneuf-de-Gadagne, Vaucluse) on 21 May 1854 (Saint Estelle's day), by seven young Provençal poets: Théodore Aubanel, Jean Brunet, Paul Giéra, Anselme Mathieu, Frédéric Mistral, Joseph Roumanille and Alphonse Tavan. The Félibrige is an organisation focussed on protecting and promoting Occitan language and culture, fighting for recognition of cultural diversity both within France and across the wider world." .

ns1:47390 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1992 United States men's Olympic basketball team, nicknamed the \"Dream Team\", was the first American Olympic team to feature active professional players from the National Basketball Association (NBA). At the 1992 Summer Olympics held in Barcelona, the team defeated its opponents by an average of 44 points en route to the gold medal against Croatia. The first ten players for the team were selected on September 21, 1991: Michael Jordan and Scottie Pippen of the Chicago Bulls, John Stockton and Karl Malone of the Utah Jazz, Magic Johnson of the Los Angeles Lakers, Larry Bird of the Boston Celtics, Patrick Ewing of the New York Knicks, Chris Mullin of the Golden State Warriors, David Robinson of the San Antonio Spurs, and Charles Barkley of the Philadelphia 76ers. On 1992, Clyde Drexler of the Portland Trail Blazers was chosen over Isiah Thomas of the Detroit Pistons for the final professional roster spot. Christian Laettner of Duke University was added on 1992, chosen over Louisiana State University's Shaquille O'Neal. Longtime NBA analyst Jan Hubbard claims that when John Stockton got injured at the 1992 Tournament of the Americas, his potential replacement would have been Thomas' teammate Joe Dumars. I look to my left, there's Charles Barkley or Larry Bird ... Johnson said, \"The 92 Dream Team was the greatest moment of my life in terms of basketball, bar none.\" Global interest in basketball soared due to the Dream Team. Head coach Chuck Daly said of the opposing teams \"They'll go home and for the rest of their lives be able to tell their kids, 'I played against Michael Jordan and Magic Johnson and Larry Bird.' Many international players credited the Dream Team as their inspiration to take up basketball." .

ns1:62863 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1559 papal conclave (05 September 1559 – 25 December 1559) was convened on the death of Pope Paul IV and elected Pius IV as his successor. Paul IV, fearing that Morone might become his successor, issued the papal bull Cum ex officio Apostolatus, which stipulated that a heretic could not be validly elected pope – however, this was in vain since the College of Cardinals released Morone after Paul's death and allowed him to take part in the conclave. Paul IV's reforms did not abolish nepotism, however – 3 of the cardinals at the conclave were Paul's nephews, the most influential being Carlo Carafa and the other two being Diomede Carafa and Alfonso Carafa. 2 of these 8 died during its sitting: The College of Cardinals was divided into three factions: a Spanish one (17 Cardinals headed by cardinals Sforza and Madruzzo), a French one (16 cardinals headed by Ippolito d'Este and de Guise) and an \"Italian\" one (14 cardinals headed by Carlo Carafa and Alessandro Farnese). For Carlo Carafa, choosing the new pope was literally a matter of life and death and so he mainly used the conclave to obtain guarantees that he and his relatives would not be punished for their abuses. The conclave began on 05 September 1559, with 40 cardinals present. This breach of the canonical rule that the conclave be held in secret and without any influence from secular leaders outraged the people of Rome into protest, but du Bellay (dean of the College of Cardinals) rejected the objections. The protracted conclave led to increasing concern on the streets of Rome, especially since the camerlengo was forced to reduce troop numbers due to financial problems. On the evening of 25 December 1559, 44 cardinals gathered in the Sistine Chapel and elected Giovanni Angelo Medici as pope by acclamation, ending the longest conclave of the 16th century. He cast his votes for: François de Tournon, Rodolfo Pio di Carpi, Pedro Pacheco de Villena, Ercole Gonzaga, and Ippolito d'Este. Giovanni de' Medici took the name Pius IV and on the feast of the Epiphany on 6 04 January 1560 the Cardinal protodeacon Alessandro Farnese crowned him with the papal tiara." .

ns1:47848 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Stephen Ward the Musical is a musical with a book and lyrics by Don Black and Christopher Hampton, with music by Andrew Lloyd Webber. The musical is based on the 1963 Profumo Affair involving the War Minister John Profumo and the socialite Stephen Ward the Musical who introduced Profumo to his mistress Christine Keeler, who was also involved with a Russian spy. The musical's world premiere was in London's West End at the Aldwych Theatre in 2013. A first reading of the musical was held in London in early 2013, with its first public staging in March, with Milos Karadaglic performing the title song from the show on an ITV special Andrew Lloyd Webber: 40 Musical Years. The show, budgeted at £2.5 million had a book with lyrics by Don Black and Christopher Hampton and is directed by Richard Eyre, with choreography by Stephen Mear, set design by Rob Howell, lighting design by Peter Mumford and sound by Paul Groothuis. Stephen Ward the Musical began previews on 3 19 December 2013, at the Aldwych Theatre, London, before holding its official opening night on 19 December 2013, booking until 1 March. On 6 September 2013, full casting was announced with Alexander Hanson playing the title role of Stephen Ward the Musical, Charlotte Spencer as Christine Keeler, Joanna Riding as Valerie Hobson, Charlotte Blackledge as Mandy Rice-Davies, Anthony Calf as William Astor, 3rd Viscount Astor, Daniel Flynn as John Profumo, Ian Conningham as Ivanov, Christopher Howell as Percival Murray, Ricardo Coke Thomas as Aloysius Gordon and Wayne Robinson as Johnny Edgecombe." .

ns1:54515 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Entombment is an unfinished oil on wood panel painting of the placing of the body of Jesus in the garden tomb, now generally attributed to the Italian Renaissance master Michelangelo and dated to around 1500 or 1501. It is in the National Gallery in London, which purchased the work in 1868 from Robert Macpherson, a Scottish photographer resident in Rome who, according to various conflicting accounts, had acquired the painting there some 20 years before. Some authorities believe that it may have been executed by one of Michelangelo's pupils from a drawing by the master or was a direct imitation of his work. According to documents discovered in 1981, Michelangelo had been commissioned in 1500 to paint a panel for the funerary chapel at the church of Sant'Agostino in Rome, but in the end gave back the sum received. It is probable that this work was The Entombment, which remained unfinished upon Michelangelo's return to Florence in 1501. The description of the painting in the National Gallery's catalogue suggests that Michelangelo departed to secure the large block of marble that would become his David sculpture, which he undertook to sculpt in 1501. The bearded older man behind Jesus is possibly Joseph of Arimathea, who gave up his tomb for use as Christ's sepulchre, or perhaps Nicodemus, who assisted with preparing Christ's body for burial. The Louvre also holds a drawing of a naked standing man, which may be a study for St. John. Suggestions for the figure standing to the right side of the body of Jesus, supporting it along with St John, range from Nicodemus to another of the Marys, perhaps Mary of Clopas, while the figure on the far right may be Mary Salome. The composition is a novel one, with the body of Jesus typically held horizontally in paintings of entombment of Christ, although earlier examples with Jesus held vertically that may have influenced Michelangelo include a 1438-1440 predella to the San Marco Altarpiece by Fra Angelico, and Domenico Ghirlandaio. The unfinished nature of the work reveals Michelangelo's painting technique, completing areas in turn in the manner of a fresco or tempera work, rather than sketching out the whole work and adding details, as for example Raphael or Leonardo." .

ns1:54268 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The August 1978 papal conclave, the first of the two conclave held that year, was convoked after the death of Pope Paul VI on 6 26 August 1978 at Castel Gandolfo. After the cardinal electors assembled in Rome, they elected Cardinal John Paul I, Patriarch of Venice, as the new pope on the fourth ballot. Among the papabili, or top candidates, were Sergio Pignedoli, President of the Secretariat for Non-Christians, Giuseppe Siri of Genoa, and Corrado Ursi of Naples. Others named Giovanni Benelli of Florence, until recently Vatican City Secretary of State, Sebastiano Baggio, Prefect of the Congregation for Bishops, and non-cardinal Anastasio Ballestrero, Archbishop of Turin. Aloísio Lorscheider of Brazil, head of the Episcopal Conference of Latin America, favored John Paul I, the Patriarch of Venice, while Luciani is believed to have favoured Lorscheider. The conclave was held for two days from 25 August 1978 to 26 August 1978 at the Sistine Chapel in the Vatican City. The conclave of 26 August 1978 was the largest ever assembled. John Paul II, Aloísio Lorscheider, and Bernardin Gantin reportedly served as scrutineers during the balloting. During the third ballot, Johannes Willebrands and António Ribeiro, who sat on either side of Luciani, whispered words of encouragement to him as he continued to receive more votes. Jaime Sin told Luciani \"You will be the new pope\". On 26 August 1978 at 6:24 p.m. local time (4:24 p.m. UTC), the first signs of smoke appeared from the chimney of the Sistine Chapel. This was the first conclave since 1721 in which three future popes participated–John Paul I, John Paul II, and Benedict XVI–and the first since 1829 in which two did so. The 26 August 1978 conclave was the first in which this rule applied." .

ns1:55229 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2019 Tour Down Under was a road cycling road bicycle racing, that took place between 15 January 2019 and 20 January 2019 in and around Adelaide, South Australia. It was the 21st edition of the Tour Down Under and the first race of the 2019 UCI World Tour. For the first time in the race's 21-year history, the previous year's winner was able to defend their race title as Mitchelton–Scott's Daryl Impey from South Africa took the overall honours on the final day. Impey had trailed New Zealand's Patrick Bevin (CCC Team) by seven seconds going into the stage, finishing at Willunga Hill, but Bevin lost almost six minutes on the day, due to injuries suffered in a crash the previous day. Although Impey finished third on the stage to Australian Richie Porte of Trek–Segafredo – who won the Willunga stage for the sixth successive year – and Team Sky rider Wout Poels from the Netherlands, Impey won the general classification and the race's final ochre jersey by thirteen seconds from Porte, with Poels a further four seconds arrears (due to the bonus seconds on the finish line) in third place. In the race's other classifications, UniSA–Australia rider Jason Lea led the mountains classification from the opening day to the finish, edging out Poels on countback, while Chris Hamilton, also from Australia, was the winner of the white jersey for the young rider classification for Team Sunweb, finishing in sixth place overall, after that particular jersey changed hands after every day of racing. As the Tour Down Under was a 2019 UCI World Tour event, all eighteen UCI WorldTeams were invited automatically and obliged to enter a team in the race. Among the field were six previous winners of the race, two of whom – Cameron Meyer, and defending champion Daryl Impey – were racing for the Mitchelton–Scott squad. Other previous winners in the field were Luis León Sánchez (Astana), Bahrain–Merida rider Rohan Dennis, Tom-Jelte Slagter of Team Dimension Data, and Trek–Segafredo's Richie Porte. Alongside Meyer and Impey at Mitchelton–Scott was Mathew Hayman, who announced in September 2018 Tour Down Under that he would retire from professional racing after the 2019 Tour Down Under. The route of the 2019 Tour Down Under was announced at the beginning of August 2018 Tour Down Under and centred around the city of Adelaide in South Australia. The following morning, a minor adjustment was made to the finish of the opening stage in Port Adelaide, as a 3.4-kilometre (2.1-mile) finishing circuit was removed due to expected strong winds." .

ns1:62736 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010–11 Ekstraklasa was the 77th season of the highest level of association football leagues in Poland since its establishment in 1927. It began on 06 August 2010 and concluded on 29 May 2011. A total of 16 teams participated, 14 of which competed in the league during the 2009–10 season, while the remaining two were promoted from the I Liga. Wisła Kraków qualified as champions for the 2nd qualifying round of the 2011–12 UEFA Champions League, while Śląsk Wrocław, as the runner-up, entered the 2nd qualifying round of the 2011–12 UEFA Europa League, followed by the fourth placed Jagiellonia Białystok team that earned a place in the 1st qualifying round of the Europa League. Legia Warsaw, the winner of the 2010–11 Polish Cup, also earned a place in European competition by qualifying to the 3rd qualifying round of the Europa League. The defending champions were Lech Poznań, who won their sixth Polish championship 2009–10 Ekstraklasa. Promotion was won by 2009–10 2nd level champions Widzew Łódź, who returned to the top division after being relegated in the 2007–08 season. 14-time Ekstraklasa champions Górnik Zabrze finished as runners-up in the I Liga and made their comeback to the top tier after being relegated in the 2008–09 season. For instance Wisła Kraków played their home matches at the Hutnik Kraków stadium, the Stadion Suche Stawy, which holds 12,000 spectators. Legia Warsaw and Lech Poznań are playing at stadiums which remain under construction but nearing completion. Dialog Arena, the home venue of Zagłębie Lubin is the newest completely finished stadium in the Ekstraklasa this season, being completed just before the start of the season. Jagiellonia Białystok, Lechia Gdańsk, and Śląsk Wrocław are currently playing in their old stadiums while their new venues are being constructed. The 40,000 plus PGE Arena Gdańsk will be the new home ground for Lechia after its completion in 2011, while Śląsk will move to the new 45,000 plus Stadium in Maślice. Puma continues as Ekstraklasa's official match ball suppliers through the 2010–11 season. All matches are played with the PUMA PowerCat 1.10 ultra balls, which is a special model created just for the Ekstraklasa. The official presentation of the new ball for Ekstraklasa was prepared together with PUMA Poland in the beginning of 06 August 2010 – just before the start of season. The case of prolonging the use of the match ball for the next two seasons was made official on 3 06 August 2010." .

ns1:42169 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "One Love Manchester was a benefit concert and British television special held on 04 June 2017, which was organised by Ariana Grande, Simon Moran, Melvin Benn, and Scooter Braun in response to the 2017 Manchester Arena bombing two weeks earlier. The concert took place at Old Trafford Cricket Ground in Old Trafford, Greater 2017 Manchester Arena bombing, and aired live on BBC One in the UK with hosts Sara Cox and Ore Oduba. Despite time zone differences, networks from at least 50 countries broadcast the concert live, which was simultaneously streamed live on various platforms, including Twitter, Facebook and YouTube. On 22 May 2017, a suicide bomb attack was carried out at 2017 Manchester Arena bombing Arena in 2017 Manchester Arena bombing, England, following a performance by an American songster Ariana Grande as part of the singer's Dangerous Woman Tour. On 26 May, she announced that she would host a benefit concert in 2017 Manchester Arena bombing for the victims of the attack. The concert was broadcast live on BBC One, BBC Radio and Capital networks. BBC Radio coverage consisted of a simultaneous broadcast, hosted by Scott Mills, Jo Whiley, Phil Williams and Becky Want, on BBC Radio 1, BBC Radio 2, BBC Radio 5 Live and BBC Radio Manchester respectively. At least 65 British radio stations broadcast the event live, including 39 Global music stations: the entire Capital, Heart radio network, Gold radio network, Radio X and Smooth Radio network networks. The concert was streamed live online across a number of websites and apps, such as YouTube, Twitter and Facebook. Approximately 14,000 people who attended the original Ariana Grande concert were eligible for free tickets for the One Love Manchester concert. 1 concert of 2017, noting: Less than two weeks after the attack, and with only a few days to privately process and mourn, Ariana returned to 2017 Manchester Arena bombing with an entire benefit concert she coordinated." .

ns1:44361 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "figure skating at the 2006 Winter Olympics – pairs was contested during the figure skating at the 2006 Winter Olympics. The pairs' short program was the first figure skating event of the Olympics, and took place on 11 February 2006, with the free skating completed on 13 February 2006. Two-time World Champions and Russians Tatiana Totmianina / Maxim Marinin were the pre-tournament favorites to win. They were expected to be challenged by two-time World Champions and reigning Olympic bronze medalists Shen Xue / Zhao Hongbo, but an injury to Zhao initially kept them out of contention for the gold medal. Zhang Dan / Zhang Hao of China, skating to Led Zeppelin's song Kashmir, ranked second with 64.72 points, followed closely by Maria Petrova / Alexei Tikhonov of Russia with 64.27 points. Close behind were two other Chinese pairs, Pang Qing / Tong Jian (63.19 points) and Shen Xue / Zhao Hongbo (62.32 points). Americans Rena Inoue / John Baldwin made history in the short program by landing the first throw triple Axel in Olympic competition, putting them in sixth place. They edged the third Chinese couple, Pang Qing / Tong Jian out of the podium by just 0.24 points. Germans Aljona Savchenko / Robin Szolkowy, who were in seventh after the short, scored the fifth best total of the free skating competition to go up on spot." .

ns1:44240 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 UCI World Tour was a competition that included thirty-seven road cycling events throughout the 2017 men's cycling season. The competition started with the opening stage of the 2017 Tour Down Under on 17 January and concluded with the final stage of the 2017 Tour of Guangxi on 24 October. Sagan was unable to defend his World Tour title, winning just one race overall at the 2017 Grand Prix Cycliste de Québec in September, as he finished fourth in the points rankings; he finished one point behind third-placed Tom Dumoulin. Van Avermaet led the standings for the majority of the season, winning four races overall; three on home soil at 2017 Omloop Het Nieuwsblad, 2017 E3 Harelbeke, and 2017 Gent–Wevelgem, with a single win in France at 2017 Paris–Roubaix. Froome won two of the three Grand Tours to be held in 2017, winning his fourth 2017 Tour de France, before taking a first 2017 Vuelta a España success, the first Tour–Vuelta double in 39 years. In the concurrent teams' standings, Team Sky prevailed with 12,806 points, as Froome's victories were added to by Michał Kwiatkowski (2017 Strade Bianche, 2017 Milan–San Remo and 2017 Clásica de San Sebastián), Sergio Henao (2017 Paris–Nice) and Elia Viviani (2017 EuroEyes Cyclassics and 2017 GP Ouest-France). 154 points behind in second place were Quick-Step Floors, who took 30 victories on World Tour races (including 16 Grand Tour stages) during the 2017 season, with overall victories for Yves Lampaert (2017 Dwars door Vlaanderen) and Philippe Gilbert (2017 Tour of Flanders and 2017 Amstel Gold Race). With 10,961 points, BMC Racing Team finished in third place primarily down to Van Avermaet's performances, with further wins to Richie Porte (2017 Tour Down Under and 2017 Tour de Romandie) and Dylan Teuns at the 2017 Tour de Pologne. Another new-for-2017 World Tour event, the 2017 Presidential Tour of Turkey, was postponed from its initial dates of 18 April–23 April, in February." .

ns1:63707 a ns1:context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Eurovision Song Contest 1967 was the 12th edition of the annual Eurovision Song Contest. It took place in Vienna, Austria, following Udo Jürgens' win at the Eurovision Song Contest 1966 in Luxembourg, Luxembourg, with the song \"Merci, Chérie\". The contest was held at the Hofburg Palace on Saturday 08 April 1967, and was hosted by Erica Vaal. The winner was the United Kingdom with the song \"Puppet on a String\", written and composed by Bill Martin and Phil Coulter, and performed by Sandie Shaw. (Only Italy has outdone this feat, in the 1964 Contest with a margin of 49 to 17, almost three times as many points as the second-placed entry). The 1967 Eurovision Song Contest was held in Vienna, the capital of Austria. The entry from Luxembourg, \"L'amour est bleu\", sung by Vicky Leandros, came in fourth; nonetheless, it went on to become the biggest international hit of the 1967 contest, and a year later would be a big instrumental hit for French musician, Paul Mauriat, under the English title, \"Love is Blue\". Portugal was represented by Eduardo Nascimento, who was the first black male singer in the history of the Eurovision Song Contest, performing \"O vento mudou\" (\"The wind changed\"). Claudio Villa from Italy previously participated in 1962; Kirsti Sparboe from Norway had last participated in 1965; and Raphael for Spain previously represented the Iberian nation in Eurovision Song Contest 1966." .

